E["npc:anduin-wrynn"] = {t="npc",n="Anduin Wrynn",z="multi",s="Anduin Llane Wrynn is the ten-year-old crowned King of Stormwind, reigning in the absence of his missing father, King Varian Wrynn. Due to his young age, the true administration of the kingdom rests in the hands of Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and the royal court.",h="Anduin Llane Wrynn is the ten-year-old crowned King of Stormwind, reigning in the absence of his missing...",sec={{t="A Boy on the Throne",b="Born in the peaceful interlude following the Second War, Prince Anduin was named in honor of both his grandfather King Llane and the legendary knight Sir Anduin Lothar. Following the mysterious disappearance of his father en route to a diplomatic summit in Theramore, the child was abruptly crowned to maintain stability throughout the kingdom. Though intelligent and empathetic beyond his years, the ten-year-old monarch has little actual say in state affairs, leaving rule of the realm divided between Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and royal councilor Lady Katrana Prestor.",sp=0},{t="A Pawn in the Court",b="Within the gilded walls of Stormwind Keep, young Anduin is surrounded by escalating political tension. While Highlord Bolvar manages military defenses and strives to maintain the integrity of the kingdom, the noblewoman Katrana Prestor constantly whispers counsel into the court's ear, actively undermining troop deployments to outlying human settlements. Anduin senses the precarious nature of his court and misses his father dearly, yet for now, he remains a figurehead surrounded by scheming nobles and watchful guardians.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is a child ruling Stormwind?",a="King Varian Wrynn vanished under mysterious circumstances while sailing toward a diplomatic summit in Theramore. To avoid a succession crisis and retain order across the realm, ten-year-old Prince Anduin was formally crowned king, while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon was named regent to govern in his stead.",al={"why is anduin king","where is varian","who is the kid king"},sp=1},{q="Who actually runs the kingdom right now?",a="Highlord Bolvar Fordragon serves as Regent of Stormwind, commanding the realm's armies and holding ultimate executive power until Anduin comes of age. However, the noblewoman Lady Katrana Prestor serves as a senior advisor, wielding significant influence over the royal court and the House of Nobles.",al={"who rules stormwind","who is in charge","stormwind regent"}},{q="Who is Anduin named after?",a="He is named after two heroes of Stormwind's history: his grandfather, the fallen King Llane Wrynn, and Sir Anduin Lothar, the Lion of Azeroth who led the Alliance during the First and Second Wars.",al={"anduin name origin","anduin namesake","why named anduin"}},{q="Where is Anduin located in the city?",a="King Anduin can be found in the throne room of Stormwind Keep, seated on the royal throne flanked closely by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"where is anduin","anduin location","anduin wrynn location"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="To the citizens and soldiers of the Alliance, King Anduin is an innocent symbol of the realm's resilience, even as many worry that his youth leaves the throne vulnerable to shadowy court intrigue.",["race:Human"]="For the people of Stormwind, the boy-king represents the survival of the Wrynn dynasty, standing in for his missing father Varian."},kw={"anduin","bolvar","boy-king","prestor","prince","stormwind","throne room","wrynn"},rel={"subzone:stormwind-keep","npc:bolvar-fordragon","topic:varian-wrynn","npc:onyxia","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:stormwind-kingdom","quest:1431"},src={"Anduin Wrynn"}}
E["npc:blanchy"] = {t="npc",n="Blanchy",z="multi",s="Old Blanchy is the loyal, hard-working mare belonging to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, currently resting beside their wagon near the Jansen Stead in Westfall. Renowned for her gentle disposition and hefty appetite, she remains a steady companion to her owners amidst the turmoil gripping the countryside.",h="Old Blanchy is the loyal, hard-working mare belonging to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, currently resting...",sec={{t="A Faithful Farmhand",b="Long before pulling the Furlbrows' wagon through the dust of Westfall, Blanchy worked the Furlbrows' farm in the breadbasket of Stormwind, where she earned a reputation as an exceptionally sweet-tempered, well-behaved mare. Years of heavy labor have worn her down to 'Old Blanchy,' and the arduous journey fleeing troubled homesteads has left her exhausted. Though visibly weary, her appetite remains hearty, and travelers who bring her nourishing handfuls of oats scattered across the surrounding fields are rewarded by Verna with Blanchy's own treasured blanket and feed pouch.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who owns Blanchy?",a="Blanchy belongs to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, homesteaders traveling through Westfall. She serves as their steadfast draft horse and beloved family companion.",al={"blanchy owner","who does blanchy belong to","furlbrow horse"}},{q="Where did Blanchy originally come from?",a="No record tells where Blanchy was foaled. As far as anyone in Westfall knows, she has long been the Furlbrows' horse, working their farm until the family had to take to the road.",al={"blanchy origins","where is blanchy from","young blanchy"}},{q="Why does Verna Furlbrow need oats for Blanchy?",a="Years of labor and the long haul along Westfall's dangerous roads have left Old Blanchy completely drained. Verna asks passing adventurers to scavenge remaining bags of oats from nearby farmlands to restore the faithful horse's strength.",al={"blanchy oats","why feed blanchy","furlbrows horse tired"}},{q="Where can Blanchy be found?",a="Old Blanchy stands hitched beside the Furlbrow wagon along the main road near the Jansen Stead in northern Westfall.",al={"blanchy location","where is old blanchy","blanchy map"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance travelers making the journey south from Elwynn Forest into Westfall, helping feed Old Blanchy is often one of the first quiet acts of charity they perform for the struggling local refugees."},kw={"blanchy","furlbrow","jansen stead","oats","old blanchy","verna furlbrow","westfall"},rel={"npc:verna-furlbrow","subzone:jansen-stead","zone:westfall"},src={"Blanchy"}}
E["quest:1050"] = {t="quest",n="Mythology of the Titans",z="general",s="In Ironforge, Librarian Mae Paledust seeks Mythology of the Titans, a book said to have rested in Tirisfal's old Monastery. Its study may show what the past can teach about the unknown ahead.",h="In Ironforge, Librarian Mae Paledust seeks Mythology of the Titans, a book said to have rested in Tirisfal's...",sec={{t="A Lost Volume",b="Before trouble came to Lordaeron, the old Monastery in Tirisfal Glades kept a library of knowledge. Mae asks you to bring her a book called Mythology of the Titans. It is said to be there, and she hopes its study will uncover secrets of the past.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for the book?",a="Librarian Mae Paledust in Ironforge asks you to bring her Mythology of the Titans.",al={"who wants it","Mae Paledust book","librarian Mae"}},{q="Where is Mythology of the Titans said to be?",a="The book is rumored to be in the library of the old Monastery in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where book","monastery book","titan book location"}},{q="What is Mythology of the Titans?",a="Mythology of the Titans is a book said to have been kept in the old Monastery's library in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what's the book","titan mythology","mythology book"}},{q="Why does Mae want the book?",a="Mae hopes studying the book may reveal secrets of the past and help prepare for the unknown.",al={"why get it","why Mae needs it","what's it for"}}},kw={"book","ironforge","librarian mae paledust","mae paledust","mythology of the titans","old monastery","tirisfal glades","titans"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","zone:ironforge","subzone:library-ironforge","topic:titan","topic:lordaeron"},m={id=1050},src={"In-game quest text: Mythology of the Titans"},fv=true}
E["quest:106"] = {t="quest",n="Young Lovers",z="general",s="Maybell Maclure loves Tommy Joe Stonefield, though their families are bitter rivals. She asks you to carry her letter to him by the river.",h="Maybell Maclure loves Tommy Joe Stonefield, though their families are bitter rivals.",sec={{t="A Forbidden Romance",b="The Stonefields and the Maclures have held their grudge for years in southern Elwynn's farm country. Maybell cannot visit Tommy Joe without stirring her family's anger. So she trusts travelers to carry her letters across the line between the two farms.",sp=0},{t="The Riverbank Rendezvous",b="Tommy Joe stays beyond his relatives' sight on the quiet riverbank just west of Stonefield Farm. A letter from Maybell brings relief and fresh longing. Being apart grows more than he can bear, and the pair begin seeking ways to meet in secret.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Young Lovers?",a="Maybell Maclure gives it at the Maclure Vineyards in Elwynn Forest. She loves Tommy Joe Stonefield, but their families are bitter rivals, so they cannot meet openly.",al={"who gives young lovers","who starts young lovers","young lovers questgiver"}},{q="Where can Tommy Joe Stonefield be found?",a="Tommy Joe Stonefield waits on the riverbank just west of Stonefield Farm, which lies due west of the Maclure Vineyards. There he stays out of his family's sight.",al={"where is tommy joe stonefield","find tommy joe","tommy joe location"}},{q="Why are Maybell and Tommy Joe kept apart?",a="The Stonefields and the Maclures have held a grudge against each other for years. Neither family lets its children mix, so the young couple writes secret letters.",al={"why are families fighting","stonefield maclure feud","why can't maybell and tommy joe see each other"}},{q="What item do you deliver during this quest?",a="You carry Maybell's Love Letter to Tommy Joe Stonefield at the river. It tells him of her affection and devotion, feud or no feud.",al={"what is maybell's letter","what do you deliver in young lovers","love letter elwynn"}},{q="How does Tommy Joe react to the letter?",a="Tommy Joe is thrilled and calls Maybell the light of his dull life. Her words make being apart more than he can bear, so he begins looking for a way for them to meet.",al={"how does tommy joe react","tommy joe response","what happens when you deliver the letter"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The story unfolds in southern Elwynn Forest, between the Maclure Vineyards and the river by Stonefield Farm. This quiet farming corner of the human kingdom is troubled by a petty family quarrel.",al={"where does young lovers take place","young lovers zone","stonefield farm location"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member passing through Elwynn, you may carry messages between the feuding Stonefields and Maclures. Even with the kingdom in danger, small heartaches go on.",["race:Human"]="As a human who knows Elwynn, you have heard of the old grudge between the Stonefields and the Maclures. Maybell and Tommy Joe's romance is a delicate scandal among the neighbors."},kw={"elwynn forest","feud","love letter","maclure vineyards","maybell maclure","stonefield farm","tommy joe stonefield","young lovers"},rel={"npc:tommy-joe-stonefield","subzone:stonefield-farm"},m={id=106},src={"In-game quest text: Young Lovers"},fv=true}
E["quest:107"] = {t="quest",n="Note to William",z="general",s="\"Note to William\" is an errand undertaken for Gramma Stonefield in southern Elwynn Forest to help two secret lovers from feuding families. By delivering Mildred's message to the apothecary William Pestle in Goldshire, adventurers seek an alchemical solution to unite young Maybell and Tommy Joe.",h="\"Note to William\" is an errand undertaken for Gramma Stonefield in southern Elwynn Forest to help two secret...",sec={{t="Star-Crossed in Elwynn",b="The Stonefield and Maclure families have lived as neighbors in southern Elwynn Forest for years, yet their bitter feuding has made normal relations impossible. Caught squarely in the middle are young Tommy Joe Stonefield and Maybell Maclure, whose romance must be conducted in utter secrecy. While their parents harbor relentless resentment, Gramma Mildred Stonefield takes pity on the young lovers and seeks an unconventional remedy to unite them.",sp=0},{t="An Appeal to Goldshire",b="Believing that mundane persuasion will never sway the feuding families, Gramma Stonefield dispatches a courier north to the Lion's Pride Inn in Goldshire. Her message seeks out William Pestle, an experienced alchemist and apothecary whom Mildred has known for many years. Taking the missive in hand, William is moved by the plight of Maybell and Tommy Joe, resolving to put his alchemical knowledge to work on their behalf.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is William Pestle?",a="William Pestle is an alchemist and apothecary residing at the Lion's Pride Inn in Goldshire. He is an old acquaintance of Gramma Mildred Stonefield and is known for concocting herbal remedies and specialized draughts.",al={"who is william pestle","william pestle goldshire","alchemist goldshire"}},{q="Who sent the note to William?",a="Gramma Mildred Stonefield, the matriarch of the Stonefield farm, wrote the note. Sympathetic to the forbidden romance between Maybell Maclure and Tommy Joe Stonefield, she hopes William Pestle can devise a solution to help them.",al={"who sent note","gramma stonefield note","mildred stonefield"}},{q="Why are Maybell and Tommy Joe unable to be together openly?",a="Maybell Maclure and Tommy Joe Stonefield are deeply in love, but their families are locked in a bitter, longstanding rivalry. Gramma Stonefield believes William Pestle may know of a potion or plan to allow the two sweethearts to be together despite the family hostility.",al={"why help maybell and tommy joe","young lovers quest elwynn","stonefield maclure feud"}},{q="Where can William Pestle be found?",a="William Pestle can be found staying inside the Lion's Pride Inn, the central tavern and gathering hub in the town of Goldshire within Elwynn Forest.",al={"where is william pestle","pestle location","lion's pride inn alchemist"}},{q="What is Gramma Stonefield's real name?",a="Her first name is Mildred, as revealed by William Pestle when he mentions that he has not seen Mildred in years upon receiving her letter.",al={"what is gramma stonefield first name","gramma stonefield name"}},{q="How does William Pestle react to the note?",a="Upon reading Mildred's letter, William remarks that he has not seen Mildred in years and wonders what her note has to say, since she hopes his alchemical craft might help the star-crossed lovers.",al={"what does william do with note","william pestle reaction"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers traveling through Elwynn Forest often assist the local farming families with personal crises when official guards focus solely on military threats.",["race:Human"]="The ongoing feud between the Stonefields and Maclures divides human neighbors in Elwynn, making sympathetic elders rely on discreet couriers."},kw={"elwynn forest","goldshire","gramma stonefield","lion's pride inn","maybell maclure","mildred stonefield","note to william","tommy joe stonefield","william pestle"},rel={"npc:william-pestle","npc:gramma-stonefield","quest:106","subzone:lion-s-pride-inn","subzone:goldshire"},m={id=107},src={"In-game quest text: Note to William"},fv=true}
E["quest:1136"] = {t="quest",n="Frostmaw",z="general",s="In the Alterac Mountains, a rare yeti hunt calls you far from home. Melor Stonehoof sends you after Frostmaw and awaits his mane in Thunder Bluff.",h="In the Alterac Mountains, a rare yeti hunt calls you far from home.",sec={{t="A Distant Hunt",b="Melor Stonehoof sends you far from home to hunt Frostmaw in the Alterac Mountains. The yeti’s white coat blends with the mountain snow, and he rarely shows himself. Melor gives you a scroll explaining how to lure him. Bring Frostmaw’s mane back to Melor in Thunder Bluff, and your journey will be complete.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Frostmaw?",a="Frostmaw is a yeti in the Alterac Mountains. His white coat blends with the snow, and he rarely appears.",al={"frostmaw who","what is frostmaw"}},{q="Where can I find Frostmaw?",a="You can find Frostmaw in the Alterac Mountains, where his white coat blends with the snow.",al={"frostmaw location","where is frostmaw"}},{q="How do I lure Frostmaw?",a="Melor Stonehoof gives you a scroll with instructions for luring Frostmaw.",al={"lure frostmaw","frostmaw scroll"}},{q="What does Melor want from Frostmaw?",a="Melor Stonehoof asks you to bring Frostmaw’s mane to him in Thunder Bluff.",al={"melor frostmaw","frostmaw mane"}}},kw={"alterac mountains","frostmaw","mane","melor stonehoof","scroll","thunder bluff","yeti"},rel={"npc:melor-stonehoof","zone:alterac","zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=1136},src={"In-game quest text: Frostmaw"},fv=true}
E["quest:1164"] = {t="quest",n="To Steal From Thieves",z="general",s="Genavie Callow in Undercity asks you to confront the Dabyrie family at their farmstead in Arathi. Her grievance turns a dispute over stolen belongings into a demand for vengeance.",h="Genavie Callow in Undercity asks you to confront the Dabyrie family at their farmstead in Arathi.",sec={{t="A Bitter Grievance",b="Genavie Callow says the plague took her family and the war left her estate looted and burned. She accuses Kenata and her children of taking the few belongings she still treasured. Now she asks you to bring her the heads of Kenata, Fardel, and Marcel Dabyrie from the Dabyrie Farmstead in Arathi.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Genavie Callow in Undercity asks you to bring her the heads of Kenata, Fardel, and Marcel Dabyrie.",al={"who gave this","who wants this","genavie"}},{q="Why is Genavie angry at the Dabyries?",a="Genavie says the plague took her family, the war cost her estate, and the Dabyries stole the belongings she had left.",al={"why mad at them","genavie grievance","dabyrie trouble"}},{q="Where is the Dabyrie Farmstead?",a="The Dabyrie Farmstead is in Arathi, northeast of Refuge Point.",al={"dabyrie farm location","where is the farm","farmstead"}},{q="Who are Kenata, Fardel, and Marcel Dabyrie?",a="They are the three people Genavie names in her demand: Kenata, Fardel, and Marcel Dabyrie.",al={"who are dabyries","kenata family","dabyrie names"}}},kw={"arathi","dabyrie farmstead","fardel","genavie callow","kenata","marcel dabyrie","refuge point","to steal from thieves"},rel={"zone:undercity","topic:forsaken"},m={id=1164},src={"In-game quest text: To Steal From Thieves"},fv=true}
E["quest:1248"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Diplomat",z="general",s="You follow the trail of a kidnapped Stormwind diplomat to Menethil Harbor. An intercepted docket confirms the abduction but leaves the diplomat's identity and the conspirators' destination unknown.",h="You follow the trail of a kidnapped Stormwind diplomat to Menethil Harbor.",sec={{t="A Trail to the Wetlands",b="You have a docket that confirms a diplomat was taken from Stormwind. It names neither the diplomat nor the conspirators' destination. So the trail leads beyond Stormwind's gates to Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands, where you can search among the harbor crowds and their secret dealings.",sp=0},{t="Discreet Contacts in Menethil",b="Mikhail, a former associate of Elling Trias, retired from Stormwind several years ago and now tends the tavern in Menethil. You seek him to find Slim, who may know where the captive was taken or who arranged the abduction.\n\nIn the crowded tavern, discretion matters. Mention Trias's name, and Mikhail urges immediate silence before the conspirators notice your inquiry.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Mikhail?",a="Mikhail was a former associate of Elling Trias who left Stormwind several years ago to retire in Menethil Harbor. He now works there as a bartender, and Trias points you to him as a trustworthy contact who can help find Slim.",al={"who is mikhail","mikhail wetlands","menethil bartender"}},{q="Who is Slim and why are they looking for him?",a="Slim is a figure in Menethil Harbor believed to know something about the kidnapped diplomat. Mikhail can help you find Slim and learn what they know.",al={"who is slim","find slim","missing diplomat slim"}},{q="Who is the kidnapped diplomat?",a="The kidnapped diplomat's identity remains uncertain, even to the investigators. The intercepted docket confirms the abduction but leaves out the diplomat's identity and the conspirators' plans after the capture.",al={"who was kidnapped","kidnapped diplomat identity","who is missing diplomat"}},{q="Why does the trail lead to Menethil Harbor?",a="The trail leads to Menethil Harbor because Elling Trias's retired contact Mikhail tends bar there. The harbor is a port town on the coast of the Wetlands, and leads about the abduction pass through it.",al={"where is menethil harbor","why wetlands","why go to menethil"}},{q="What does the docket reveal about the plot?",a="The intercepted docket confirms the diplomat's abduction but leaves out where the captive was taken and what the abductors intend to do next.",al={"what docket","trias docket","what does the docket say"}},{q="What is Trias's involvement in this mission?",a="Elling Trias directs the investigation from Stormwind. He sends you to Mikhail, his old associate, who promised to help on Trias's word alone and without drawing public attention.",al={"who sent us to mikhail","trias connection","why mention trias"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The kidnapped diplomat's abduction touches Stormwind's high-level security and reaches of conspirators across Alliance territories.",["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you can draw on covert skills to unravel secret dockets, find retired contacts, and question suspects discreetly in crowded taverns."},kw={"docket","menethil harbor","mikhail","slim","stormwind","the missing diplomat","trias","wetlands"},rel={"zone:wetlands","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1248},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Diplomat"},fv=true}
E["quest:1249"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Diplomat",z="general",s="Stormwind's envoy is missing, and you follow the trail from the capital to Menethil Harbor. There, the inquiry crosses paths with underworld figures and operatives tied to Stormwind's intelligence network.",h="Stormwind's envoy is missing, and you follow the trail from the capital to Menethil Harbor.",sec={{t="Undercurrents in Menethil Harbor",b="You follow the missing Stormwind envoy's trail north to Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands. Its docks and taverns hide shady dealers and covert runners, all working under the local guard's nose. To follow the leads through this port, you must deal with people who know the darker side of Alliance territory.",sp=0},{t="A Familiar Rogue",b="Catching Slim brings a sudden answer to the search. Local informants are surprised to learn he was entangled in the affair. His capture gives you critical answers, but it also stirs old memories for retired operatives in the Wetlands. They must weigh helping the Crown against being drawn back into Stormwind's underworld schemes.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Slim?",a="Slim is a shady contact in Menethil Harbor whom you catch during the search for the missing diplomat. Local contacts are surprised to learn he was involved.",al={"who is slim","slim missing diplomat","caught slim"}},{q="Who is Elling?",a="Elling Trias is an influential figure in Stormwind City who keeps an underground network of former rogues and informants across Alliance lands.",al={"elling trias","who is elling","elling stormwind"}},{q="Where does this part of the investigation take place?",a="You follow this part of the investigation in Menethil Harbor, a Wetlands port connecting Ironforge to distant shores and Stormwind trade routes.",al={"where does this happen","menethil harbor missing diplomat","wetlands diplomat"}},{q="What is the Missing Diplomat quest chain about?",a="You investigate the disappearance of an important Stormwind envoy, following covert operatives, conspirators, and underworld contacts across the kingdom and beyond.",al={"what is the missing diplomat","missing diplomat quest","who is missing"}},{q="Why does the contact warn against mentioning this to Elling?",a="A Menethil Harbor contact once worked with Elling Trias in Stormwind's underground circles. He avoids telling Elling that he misses the old life, for fear Elling will summon him back to the capital.",al={"why is elling mentioned","trias cheese","elling trias connection"}},{q="What happened with the investigation in Menethil Harbor?",a="You follow leads to Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands and catch Slim. The encounter confirms local suspicions about illicit underground activity in town.",al={"what happened in menethil harbor","menethil lead","slim caught"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance adventurer, you follow the missing diplomat's trail into an underworld linking Stormwind's political circles to the Wetlands docks.",["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you know that operatives and handlers like Elling Trias rely on quiet legwork and street contacts to find those who slip through the cracks."},kw={"elling trias","menethil harbor","missing diplomat","slim","stormwind","the missing diplomat","wetlands"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1249},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Diplomat"},fv=true}
E["quest:1250"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Diplomat",z="general",s="Jahn says the Defias took the king from his ship while it lay moored. You are sent to Mikhail in the Wetlands as the search for Hendel continues.",h="Jahn says the Defias took the king from his ship while it lay moored.",sec={{t="A Witness Speaks",b="Jahn says the Defias put a man named Hendel aboard the king's ship while it was moored. The king was bound for Theramore to meet Jaina Proudmoore. Jahn believes the Defias worked with someone else, so you are asked to speak with Mikhail in the Wetlands as the search for Hendel begins.",sp=0},{t="Mikhail's Response",b="Mikhail is startled to hear the king is missing and sends word to Elling. Jahn, he says, was never someone he expected to betray Stormwind for coin. Still, Mikhail offers help finding Hendel.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Jahn?",a="Jahn is a Defias conspirator who helped put Hendel aboard the king's ship.",al={"jahn who","who is jahn","defias jahn"}},{q="Who is Hendel?",a="Hendel is the man Jahn says he placed aboard the king's ship.",al={"hendel guy","who's hendel","king ship man"}},{q="What happened to the king?",a="Jahn says the Defias took the king from his ship while it was moored.",al={"where king","stormwind king missing","king kidnapped"}},{q="Why was the king going to Theramore?",a="The king was on his way to Theramore to meet Jaina Proudmoore.",al={"king theramore","jaina meeting","why theramore"}},{q="Who does Jahn say is involved besides the Defias?",a="Jahn believes the Defias worked with someone else, but says he does not know who.",al={"who working with defias","defias partner","who else involved"}},{q="Who is Mikhail?",a="Mikhail is the person you are asked to speak with in the Wetlands. He offers help finding Hendel.",al={"mikhail wetlands","who mikhail","talk to mikhail"}}},kw={"defias","hendel","jahn","jaina proudmoore","mikhail","stormwind king","the missing diplomat","theramore","wetlands"},rel={"zone:wetlands","npc:jaina-proudmoore","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:rogue"},m={id=1250},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Diplomat"},fv=true}
E["quest:1264"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Diplomat",z="general",s="A tavern keeper sends you to Commander Samaul in Theramore, where Hendel may still be stationed.",h="A tavern keeper sends you to Commander Samaul in Theramore, where Hendel may still be stationed.",sec={{t="A Lead in Theramore",b="The tavern keeper believes Hendel is among the Kul Tiras Marines who visit regularly. They are paid at the beginning of each month and usually leave Theramore afterward. Commander Samaul may know if Hendel is still stationed there. Mention Elling's name, and Samaul may help discreetly.",sp=0},{t="Samaul's Warning",b="Commander Samaul is the officer in charge of Miss Proudmoore's forces in Theramore. He is busy, but offers to answer your questions. The marsh beyond the city holds ogres, orcs and murlocs. Black dragons dwell in its southern reaches.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Hendel?",a="Hendel is a Kul Tiras Marine who may be among the regular visitors to a tavern. The tavern keeper thinks Commander Samaul in Theramore may know if Hendel is still stationed there.",al={"hendel","marine Hendel","who's Hendel"}},{q="Who is Commander Samaul?",a="Commander Samaul is in charge of Miss Proudmoore's forces in Theramore.",al={"Samaul","commander guy","Theramore commander"}},{q="Why am I looking for Samaul?",a="The tavern keeper believes Samaul may help you find Hendel if Hendel is still stationed in Theramore.",al={"find Samaul","why Samaul","missing diplomat quest"}},{q="Who is Elling?",a="The tavern keeper advises you to mention Elling's name when you ask Commander Samaul about Hendel.",al={"Elling","drop Elling's name","Elling name"}},{q="When do the Kul Tiras Marines leave Theramore?",a="The Kul Tiras Marines are paid at the beginning of each month and usually leave Theramore afterward.",al={"Marines leave","payday","when do they go"}},{q="What dangers does Samaul mention?",a="Samaul warns of ogres, orcs and murlocs in the marsh beyond the city, and black dragons in its southern reaches.",al={"Theramore dangers","what's in the swamp","marsh threats"}}},kw={"black dragons","commander samaul","dustwallow marsh","elling","hendel","kul tiras marines","miss proudmoore","theramore"},rel={"zone:dustwallow","topic:kul-tiras","topic:murloc"},m={id=1264},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Diplomat"},fv=true}
E["quest:1265"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Diplomat",z="general",s="Elling sends you to find Private Hendel, and a local officer fears the reason may be troubling. Hendel was stationed at Sentry Point, the first tower outside Theramore, where you are asked to seek signs of him.",h="Elling sends you to find Private Hendel, and a local officer fears the reason may be troubling.",sec={{t="At the Tower",b="A local officer knows Private Hendel and says he was stationed at Sentry Point, the first tower outside Theramore. The officer fears Elling sent you there first because your business may be troubling. Tell the officer what you can share. The watch must protect these lands, so any disturbance matters.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Private Hendel?",a="Private Hendel is the man Elling sent you to find. A local officer says Hendel was stationed at the first tower outside Theramore.",al={"private hendel","hendel who","hendel backstory"}},{q="Where should I look for Hendel?",a="Look for signs of Private Hendel at Sentry Point, the first tower outside Theramore.",al={"hendel location","where hendel","sentry point hendel"}},{q="Who sent me to find Hendel?",a="Elling sent you to find Private Hendel.",al={"who sent me","elling hendel","hendel search"}},{q="Why is the officer concerned?",a="The officer fears Elling sent you there first because your business may be troubling. The officer wants to know what you can share to protect the lands.",al={"why concerned","officer worried","what's troubling"}}},kw={"elling","hendel","officer","private hendel","sentry point","theramore","tower"},rel={"subzone:sentry-point"},m={id=1265},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Diplomat"},fv=true}
E["quest:1302"] = {t="quest",n="James Hyal",z="general",s="\"James Hyal\" is an Alliance quest concerning a human settler who sailed to Kalimdor with Lady Jaina Proudmoore's expedition. After setting out to open an inn on the frontier, his family in the Eastern Kingdoms lost touch with him and seeks news of his fate through Theramore's administrative records.",h="\"James Hyal\" is an Alliance quest concerning a human settler who sailed to Kalimdor with Lady Jaina...",sec={{t="Across the Great Sea",b="During the Third War, countless souls left their ancestral homes in the Eastern Kingdoms to follow Lady Jaina Proudmoore across the Great Sea. Among them was James Hyal, remembered fondly by his kin as a gifted cook. After the fighting subsided and Theramore Isle was founded, James chose to carve out a living on the rugged continent of Kalimdor by opening an inn of his own to serve travellers and fellow settlers.",sp=0},{t="In Search of a Brother",b="Back in the Eastern Kingdoms, James's brother has grown concerned after receiving no recent correspondence from across the sea. Hoping that James is merely consumed by the demands of managing a bustling roadside taproom, he asks passing travellers to track him down. The first step in finding him lies in Theramore, where Clerk Lendry oversees local records and keeps track of expedition members who have ventured out into the wilderness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is James Hyal?",a="James Hyal is a human settler and talented cook who journeyed across the Great Sea with Lady Jaina Proudmoore's fleet. Seeking a new livelihood in Kalimdor after the fighting subsided, he set out to establish an inn on the continent.",al={"who is james hyal","james hyal lore","who is james"}},{q="Where can I find James Hyal?",a="James set out to establish an inn somewhere on the Kalimdor continent after arriving with Lady Proudmoore's expedition. His family has not heard from him recently, but Clerk Lendry in Theramore Isle keeps the records and may know his exact whereabouts.",al={"where is james hyal","find james hyal","james hyal location"}},{q="Who is Clerk Lendry?",a="Clerk Lendry is an Alliance official stationed in Theramore Isle who handles local town administration and citizen inquiries. Travellers looking for settlers like James Hyal are directed to Lendry to check Theramore's records.",al={"who is clerk lendry","clerk lendry location","theramore clerk"}},{q="Why did James travel to Kalimdor?",a="James sailed with Lady Jaina Proudmoore's fleet during the Third War as part of the human exodus from the Eastern Kingdoms. Once the major conflicts settled down, he chose to stay and build an inn for travellers and settlers.",al={"why did james go to kalimdor","james hyal expedition"}},{q="Who is looking for James Hyal?",a="The inquiry comes from James's brother in the Eastern Kingdoms, who has not received word from him in a while. Remembering James as a fine cook, his brother assumes he is simply occupied with establishing his new inn.",al={"who gives james hyal quest","who is james brother"}},{q="Is James Hyal in any danger?",a="His brother believes he is simply occupied with running his new business, assuming that peace on the continent has allowed him to thrive. However, his family has not received direct word from him for some time.",al={"is james hyal safe","what happened to james hyal"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="James sailed west alongside Lady Jaina Proudmoore during the Third War, reflecting the personal ties that bind many Alliance families across the Great Sea to Theramore Isle.",["profession:Cooking"]="Known back home as a remarkably skilled cook, James ventured to Kalimdor to build a living running an inn and feeding travellers."},kw={"clerk lendry","cook","innkeeper","jaina proudmoore","james hyal","kalimdor","theramore"},m={id=1302},src={"In-game quest text: James Hyal"},fv=true}
E["quest:1505"] = {t="quest",n="Veteran Uzzek",z="general",s="Veteran Uzzek is a Horde quest that sends promising young warriors to seek out a seasoned combat instructor. Stationed just south of Far Watch Post between Durotar and the Barrens, Uzzek teaches new recruits that true martial greatness requires disciplined study as well as strength.",h="Veteran Uzzek is a Horde quest that sends promising young warriors to seek out a seasoned combat instructor.",sec={{t="The Path of the Warrior",b="In the culture of the Horde, martial strength is unquestioned and revered above all else. Yet brute force alone does not make a champion. Seasoned commanders know that the greatest warriors are also humble students of the craft, willing to hone their instincts through discipline and study. Aspiring fighters ready to advance beyond basic training are directed to seek out those who have survived the crucible of war.",sp=0},{t="The Veteran at the Border",b="To receive advanced combat guidance, young fighters travel to the arid borderlands between Durotar and the Barrens. Just south of Far Watch Post, the fortified orc garrison watching the eastern frontier, resides Uzzek. A veteran of countless bloody conflicts, Uzzek now dedicates his years to mentoring promising new recruits in the vital arts of combat and tactical survival.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Veteran Uzzek?",a="Uzzek is a hardened Horde battle veteran who instructs young warriors in combat. Having survived countless engagements, he now passes down his hard-earned martial wisdom from his camp near the frontier of Durotar and the Barrens.",al={"who is uzzek","uzzek lore","who is the veteran"}},{q="Where can Uzzek be found?",a="Uzzek is located south of Far Watch Post, the orc garrison positioned along the border between Durotar and the Barrens to the east.",al={"where is uzzek","uzzek location","find uzzek"}},{q="What is Far Watch Post?",a="Far Watch Post is an orc garrison marking the eastern frontier of the Barrens, guarding the crossing from Durotar and serving as a defensive station for the Horde.",al={"what is the far watch post","far watch post purpose"}},{q="Why are young warriors sent to Uzzek?",a="Aspiring warriors seek out Uzzek for combat instruction. Within the Horde, while strength is unquestioned, the truly great warriors must also master the discipline of study and martial technique.",al={"why visit uzzek","what does uzzek teach","warrior instruction"}},{q="What does Uzzek say when you arrive?",a="When arriving at his post, Uzzek greets the young warrior warmly, ready to begin evaluating their commitment and teaching them the ways of war.",al={"what happens when you speak to uzzek","uzzek quest turn in"}},{q="How does the Horde view its warriors?",a="The Horde reveres its warriors for their undeniable strength and martial devotion, but cultural tradition emphasizes that genuine mastery requires discipline and learning from seasoned veterans.",al={"who honors warriors","horde warrior culture"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Young warriors are sent to Uzzek to learn that brute force alone is not enough, and that true strength comes from being a dedicated student of combat.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde honors martial prowess above all, relying on experienced battle veterans stationed at frontier outposts to mentor the next generation."},kw={"barrens","combat instruction","durotar","far watch post","horde","uzzek","veteran uzzek","warrior"},rel={"npc:uzzek","subzone:far-watch-post","zone:barrens","zone:durotar","topic:warrior","topic:horde"},m={id=1505},src={"In-game quest text: Veteran Uzzek"},fv=true}
E["quest:1519"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="The earth calls a shaman to begin a patient trial of communion. By accepting an earthen sapta, the seeker steps onto a path of spiritual rebirth and learns to hear the foundation of life.",h="The earth calls a shaman to begin a patient trial of communion.",sec={{t="The Foundation of All Things",b="A shaman learns to commune with the primal force of earth through a formative rite. Earth is the most stubborn and wise of the elements, the steady bedrock beneath existence. It moves through slow cycles of erosion and rebirth. Mountains wear into deserts, and rivers carve canyons over ages. To hear the earth's voice, an initiate needs that same patience.",sp=0},{t="The Sapta and Rebirth",b="Answering the call changes a seeker beyond return to former ignorance. When the initiate proves readiness and respect for the laws of the Earth Mother, they receive an earthen sapta. The elixir opens a new way of seeing, and the shaman begins to grasp earth's secrets of strength, stamina, and elemental protection.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Earth?",a="Call of Earth tests an aspiring shaman's patience and readiness to commune with earth. The seeker must heed its enduring wisdom.",al={"what is call of earth","earth quest shaman","purpose of call of earth"}},{q="What does the earth element represent in shamanic tradition?",a="Earth is the foundation of all things, embodying strength, stamina, and patience. It is the most stubborn and wise element, teaching that all things erode and are reborn over time.",al={"what does earth represent","element of earth lore","earth meaning shaman"}},{q="What is a sapta?",a="A sapta is an elemental draught prepared by a shaman mentor. Accepting it opens a spiritual path and attunes the seeker's senses to elemental spirits.",al={"what is a sapta","earth sapta","what does sapta do"}},{q="Who is the Earth Mother mentioned in the trial?",a="The Earth Mother is the source of life and balance, especially revered by tauren shamans. She protects those who honor her laws and grants wisdom to seekers who respect the land.",al={"who is earth mother","earth mother lore","earth mother connection"}},{q="What happens upon answering the call?",a="Answering the call prepares the seeker for the next step of their path. It brings spiritual rebirth, after which a shaman cannot return to ignorance and new secrets of earth come into view.",al={"what happens when you complete it","after call of earth","call of earth rebirth"}},{q="Why is patience required to commune with the earth?",a="Earth moves through cycles of erosion and rebirth. Mountains wear into deserts and rivers carve canyons, so a shaman needs patience and stamina to attune to these enduring rhythms.",al={"why does earth require patience","patience and earth"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, this rite marks a turning point in your training. Communion with earth brings strength, stamina, and deep spiritual insight.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, reverence for the Earth Mother lies at the heart of your culture. This trial honors beliefs held by your ancestors.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, your shamanic traditions draw on the elements. Your path begins with the grounded resilience of earth."},kw={"call of earth","earth mother","elements","patience","rebirth","sapta","shaman","stamina"},rel={"topic:earth-mother"},m={id=1519},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:1522"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="A shaman sees your strength in earth and sends you north to Kranal Fiss. There, you can learn the ways of fire.",h="A shaman sees your strength in earth and sends you north to Kranal Fiss.",sec={{t="Kranal Fiss",b="Kranal Fiss lives north of the Crossroads. The shaman says he is easy to find, but warns you not to go too far, lest you reach the elves' lands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Kranal Fiss?",a="Kranal Fiss is a shaman in the Barrens who can teach you the ways of fire.",al={"who's Kranal","Kranal"}},{q="Where can I find Kranal?",a="Kranal lives north of the Crossroads in the Barrens.",al={"Kranal location","where's Kranal"}},{q="Why am I being sent to Kranal?",a="The shaman sees your strength in earth and believes you are ready to speak with Kranal, who can teach you the ways of fire.",al={"why Kranal","fire training"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you are sent to Kranal Fiss to learn the ways of fire after showing strength in earth."},kw={"barrens","call of fire","crossroads","earth","fire","kranal fiss","shaman"},rel={"zone:barrens","subzone:crossroads"},m={id=1522},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:1642"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Stormwind, The Tome of Divinity guides the first steps of paladin training. Its teachings call champions of the Holy Light to humility, compassion, and patience, not godhood or strength alone.",h="In Stormwind, The Tome of Divinity guides the first steps of paladin training.",sec={{t="The Nature of Divinity",b="The Tome of Divinity’s sacred texts teach paladin initiates what it means to serve the Light. Divinity means neither seeking godhood nor claiming rule over others. Instead, champions reflect the Light’s grace through pure intent and goodness in every deed. Paladins take up arms against evil, but strength without moral purpose means little. Their true distinction lies in how they treat ordinary people across Azeroth, keeping power bound to righteousness and humility.",sp=0},{t="Trials of Compassion and Patience",b="Scripture is only the beginning of a paladin’s service. After an initiate studies the Tome of Divinity, Duthorian Rall in Stormwind tests whether its lessons have taken root in the candidate’s heart and mind. The Light asks for more than the slaying of beasts. It calls for patience, bravery, and understanding.\n\nIn the city and the surrounding lands, people suffer and humble tasks go without glory. By aiding the less fortunate and comforting those in despair, an initiate proves they have the sound mind and selfless spirit to bear the Light’s blessings responsibly.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Tome of Divinity?",a="Paladins in Stormwind study the Tome of Divinity as a sacred guide to their calling. Its teachings reject godhood and personal glory, urging them to reflect the Holy Light through goodness, compassion, and patience.",al={"what is tome of divinity","tome of divinity quest","paladin tome of divinity"}},{q="Who is Duthorian Rall?",a="Duthorian Rall is a paladin leader in Stormwind who guides young paladins’ study of the Tome of Divinity. He oversees their spiritual and martial trials, testing their resolve through practical service to the kingdom and its people.",al={"who is duthorian rall","duthorian rall location","stormwind duthorian rall"}},{q="What virtues does the Tome of Divinity emphasize?",a="The Tome of Divinity teaches that compassion, patience, and bravery matter as much to a holy warrior as skill in combat. Paladins must smite wickedness, but their nobility lies in aiding the vulnerable and reflecting the Light’s benevolence.",al={"paladin virtues tome of divinity","what does the tome teach","virtues of the light"}},{q="Why does Duthorian Rall test aspiring paladins?",a="Duthorian Rall tests initiates to see whether they have truly taken the Tome of Divinity’s teachings to heart. They must show sound judgment and patience in serving the less fortunate, proving their devotion rests on service rather than pride.",al={"why does duthorian test paladins","duthorian rall trials","purpose of paladin trials"}},{q="Does the Tome of Divinity teach paladins to seek godhood?",a="No. The Tome of Divinity rejects the pursuit of godhood and teaches paladins to seek goodness in every deed. The Light grants them strength to protect and support others, not to place themselves above fellow mortals.",al={"do paladins seek godhood","strive for godhood paladin","meaning of divinity paladin"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The trials take place in the human kingdom of Stormwind. Initiates study the sacred teachings and report to Duthorian Rall in the city, then undertake trials of charity, patience, and valor through the streets and surrounding lands.",al={"where is the tome of divinity quest","tome of divinity stormwind location","where to turn in tome of divinity"}},{q="Why is patience so important for a paladin of the Light?",a="Patience shows that a holy warrior is disciplined, humble, and sound of mind. Serving the downtrodden often means taking on humble, difficult, or unglamorous tasks, which call for emotional steadfastness and genuine empathy as well as courage in battle.",al={"why patience paladin","importance of patience light","paladin patience test"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For a paladin, the Tome of Divinity sets out the moral heart of the calling: compassion and patience matter as much as readiness for combat.",["faction:Alliance"]="Stormwind’s paladins form the Alliance’s spiritual and martial backbone. They anchor the realm’s defense in moral integrity and humble service to ordinary citizens.",["race:Human"]="For Stormwind’s humans, studying the holy tome is a sacred rite of passage that reinforces civic duty and devotion to the Holy Light."},kw={"cathedral of light","compassion","divinity","duthorian rall","holy light","paladin","patience","stormwind","the tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:duthorian-rall","topic:light","quest:1641","quest:2997","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1642},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1643"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Stormwind, initiates of the Light are sent to help someone in need. They seek a citizen of pure heart and noble motive, and find Stephanie Turner in the Trade District.",h="In Stormwind, initiates of the Light are sent to help someone in need.",sec={{t="A Lesson in Humility and Service",b="Initiates of the Light begin this sacred trial by walking Stormwind’s streets. They seek someone of noble intent and pure motive who depends on the kindness of strangers. Before deeper instruction in holy duties, they learn that service to the vulnerable matters more than glorious combat.",sp=0},{t="Aid in the Trade District",b="In Stormwind’s Trade District, Stephanie Turner makes clothing for children in the city’s orphanage with her husband. More tradesmen have come to the city, and competition for cloth and leather has made donations harder to find. Her husband has gone to Ironforge to seek support. Stephanie remains in Stormwind, carrying on their work.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of The Tome of Divinity trial?",a="The trial teaches aspiring paladins to serve the Holy Light through humble aid. Initiates seek someone in Stormwind who is pure of heart and needs help, rather than proving themselves in combat or through grand deeds.",al={"what is the tome of divinity","tome of divinity purpose","why do the tome of divinity"}},{q="Who is Stephanie Turner?",a="Stephanie Turner lives in Stormwind’s Trade District and sews clothing for children at the city’s orphanage. She depends on cloth and leather donations to provide for them.",al={"who is stephanie turner","stephanie turner stormwind","where is stephanie turner"}},{q="Why has Stephanie Turner's husband gone to Ironforge?",a="Stephanie Turner’s husband has traveled to Ironforge to seek support for their work at the orphanage. While he is away, she gathers supplies and sews clothes in Stormwind.",al={"stephanie turner husband","who is stephanies husband","husband in ironforge"}},{q="What difficulties is Stephanie Turner facing?",a="More tradesmen in Stormwind have increased competition for cloth and leather. With fewer people willing to donate those materials, Stephanie struggles to gather enough to make clothes for the orphans.",al={"why is stephanie struggling","cloth shortage stormwind","orphanage clothing problem"}},{q="Why does this trial specifically direct initiates to Stephanie Turner?",a="Stephanie Turner has noble intent, a pure heart, and a real need for help. Aiding her gives an initiate a chance to serve someone devoted to caring for others.",al={"why help stephanie turner","why does the quest send you to stephanie"}},{q="What lesson does this trial impart to servants of the Light?",a="The trial teaches servants of the Light to show compassion and serve the vulnerable. Before using holy power in battle, they must learn to be kind to citizens in need.",al={"what does the light teach here","lesson of the tome of divinity"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin learning to serve the Holy Light, you begin with humble charity rather than martial glory.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance citizen, you see Stormwind’s struggle to support its orphans and the ties with Ironforge as people seek help amid economic strain."},kw={"holy light","ironforge","orphanage","paladin","stephanie turner","stormwind","the tome of divinity","tome of divinity","trade district"},rel={"subzone:trade-district","topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom","zone:ironforge"},m={id=1643},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1644"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="Stephanie Turner in Stormwind needs help clothing children orphaned by the Scourge. Her husband John is gathering supplies as an aspiring paladin brings linen cloth to her home.",h="Stephanie Turner in Stormwind needs help clothing children orphaned by the Scourge.",sec={{t="The Orphans of War",b="Stephanie Turner cares for children whose families were lost to the Scourge in the northern lands over the last decade. Her husband John is away gathering supplies. So Stephanie must keep the children fed, warm, and sheltered while finding time to make them clothes.",sp=0},{t="A Trial of Compassion",b="An aspiring paladin proves compassion by bringing linen cloth to Stephanie. With the cloth, she can sew shirts and pants for the children while tending to their food and shelter. Such humble aid shows how the Light's protectors guard the weak.",sp=0},{t="Strains on the Capital",b="Stephanie says this year has grown harsher. John must make long, difficult journeys as far as Ironforge to gather supplies. Even within Stormwind's high stone walls, the pain of recent tragedies in the north is still felt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Stephanie Turner?",a="Stephanie Turner lives in Stormwind and cares for children orphaned by the Scourge. She works to feed, clothe, and shelter them, with help from charity and her husband John.",al={"who is stephanie turner","stephanie turner stormwind","orphanage caretaker"}},{q="Why does Stephanie Turner need linen cloth?",a="Stephanie needs linen cloth to sew shirts and pants for the orphaned children. With food and shelter to manage, she has little time to gather materials herself.",al={"why linen cloth","what is linen used for","cloth for stephanie"}},{q="Who is John and where has he gone?",a="John is Stephanie Turner's husband. He travels from Stormwind to gather supplies, going as far as Ironforge, though he dislikes being away from his family.",al={"who is john","where is john","stephanie turners husband"}},{q="Why are there so many orphaned children in Stormwind?",a="Most of the children in Stephanie's care lost their families in Scourge attacks across the northern kingdoms over the past decade. Refugees and survivors brought many of them south to Stormwind.",al={"why are there orphans in stormwind","scourge orphans stormwind","what happened to children parents"}},{q="How does helping Stephanie relate to the Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity tests an aspiring paladin's virtues, especially charity and compassion. Giving basic supplies to a caretaker shows a servant of the Light will protect the humble as well as face monsters.",al={"tome of divinity meaning","why is this a paladin quest","paladin charity quest"}},{q="What does Stephanie mean about 'something in the air'?",a="Stephanie says this year has felt rougher than past years, as if something in the air signals a great change. She cannot say what it is.",al={"bad year stormwind","something in the air","stormwind troubles"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you show compassion and devotion to the Light by caring for the vulnerable and offering charity in Stormwind.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Stephanie Turner sews shirts and pants for the orphaned children with bolts of linen cloth.",["faction:Alliance"]="War orphans in Stormwind show the human cost of recent Scourge attacks across the northern Alliance realms."},kw={"charity","john turner","linen cloth","orphans","paladin","scourge","stephanie turner","stormwind","tome of divinity"},rel={"topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:scourge","zone:ironforge"},m={id=1644},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1646"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Ironforge, Tiza Battleforge teaches young paladins what the Light asks of them. The Tome of Divinity says devotion calls for patience, compassion, and humble service to the needy, alongside martial strength.",h="In Ironforge, Tiza Battleforge teaches young paladins what the Light asks of them.",sec={{t="The Measure of Divinity",b="For a paladin, strength in battle must serve a higher purpose. Tiza Battleforge in Ironforge gives initiates the Tome of Divinity to teach them why they wield their arms. It turns their thoughts from dominion and godhood toward moral goodness. A paladin's calling is to serve as a steady instrument of the Light.",sp=0},{t="Trials of Service and Compassion",b="The tome teaches that smiting evil is not enough to make a paladin. Compassion, patience, and humility set these warriors apart from common combatants. After studying its words, initiates undertake trials in Ironforge and nearby lands. Through charity and service, they show sound judgment and unwavering devotion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity teaches novice paladins that divinity means striving to do good, aid the less fortunate, and show compassion, patience, and bravery rather than seeking godhood.",al={"what is the tome of divinity","tome of divinity paladin","what does the tome teach"}},{q="Who is Tiza Battleforge?",a="Tiza Battleforge is a paladin mentor in Ironforge. She guides novices through their first trials and teaches them to balance righteous combat with selfless service.",al={"who is tiza battleforge","tiza battleforge location","where is tiza"}},{q="Why must paladins study patience and compassion?",a="A paladin's power reflects the Light, so battle strength alone is not enough. Patience and compassion help paladins defend and uplift others.",al={"why read the tome","paladin virtues","why patience and compassion"}},{q="What tests follow reading the book?",a="After reading the tome, paladins demonstrate their virtues through practical trials. Mentors send them across Ironforge and nearby lands to help the needy and prove their devotion to the Light.",al={"what happens after reading the tome","tome of divinity trials","next trial after reading"}},{q="Where does this trial take place?",a="After studying the tome, paladins return to Tiza Battleforge in Ironforge, the dwarven capital.",al={"where is this quest turned in","ironforge paladin quest"}},{q="What does it mean to strive for divinity?",a="Striving for divinity means choosing to do good in all things, not seeking godhood. Paladins use the Light to support and protect the people of Azeroth.",al={"what does striving for divinity mean","paladin godhood"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you learn that compassion and service matter as much as martial skill.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you receive this instruction in Ironforge from mentors such as Tiza Battleforge, who teach you to guide strength with patience and virtue."},kw={"compassion","holy light","ironforge","paladin","patience","the tome of divinity","tiza battleforge","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:tiza-battleforge","topic:light","zone:ironforge"},m={id=1646},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1647"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="At Ironforge's temple, an initiate seeks a lesson in the Light through service. Sent by a mentor into the outer ring, you search for someone with pure intent and a genuine need. There, John Turner shows how compassion and selfless work belong on the path of the Light.",h="At Ironforge's temple, an initiate seeks a lesson in the Light through service.",sec={{t="A Lesson in Compassion",b="Initiates of the Light stationed in Ironforge face a trial of compassion. Their mentor sends them into the dwarven city to find someone truly in need of aid. The search calls for discernment: you must recognize a pure heart, noble intent, and a real need for kindness. Humble service lies at the heart of this calling.",sp=0},{t="The Burden of John Turner",b="In Ironforge's outer ring, a humble worker named John Turner plies his trade. Startled by your approach, he calls out about the Orphanage of Stormwind, then tells you why he works so hard. Separated from his beloved wife by long hours of toil, Turner labors so orphaned children can have clothes and food. Help him, then return to the temple to continue your journey in the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Tome of Divinity quest?",a="The Tome of Divinity is a sacred trial for aspiring servants of the Light in Ironforge. You seek out and help someone of pure heart and noble motive before continuing your spiritual teachings.",al={"what is tome of divinity","tome of divinity purpose","what is this quest"}},{q="Who is John Turner?",a="John Turner is a hardworking man in Ironforge's outer ring, where he plies his trade. He works long hours away from his beloved wife to gather resources and feed and clothe orphans.",al={"who is john turner","john turner ironforge","where is john turner"}},{q="Why does the mentor send you to find someone in need?",a="The mentor sends you to test your understanding of compassion and service. You learn to recognize noble intent, offer kindness, and guide those who labor quietly for others.",al={"why find someone in need","purpose of helping john turner","why do this test"}},{q="Where is John Turner located in Ironforge?",a="John Turner walks along Ironforge's outer ring as he goes about his demanding daily work for the orphanage.",al={"where to find john turner","john turner location","how to find john turner"}},{q="What happens once you speak with John Turner?",a="After hearing John Turner's plight, you return to the mentor in the temple. There, you reflect on what it means to follow the Light and prepare for the next step of your calling.",al={"what happens after finding john turner","return to temple of the light","tome of divinity next step"}},{q="Why was John Turner shouting about an orphanage?",a="When first approached, John Turner shouts about the Orphanage of Stormwind, then catches himself and apologizes. He works to provide food and clothing for the orphaned children who rely on charity.",al={"what orphanage is john turner shouting about","stormwind orphanage ironforge","why did john turner shout"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you learn that devotion to the Light begins with selfless help for ordinary people in need.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you take this trial in Ironforge, your own capital, among the halls of your home city.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you see compassion, charity, and care for the vulnerable among the virtues upheld in cities like Ironforge."},kw={"ironforge","john turner","light","orphanage","paladin","temple","the tome of divinity","tome of divinity"},rel={"zone:ironforge","topic:light"},m={id=1647},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1648"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Ironforge, The Tome of Divinity calls servants of the Light to show compassion. John Turner seeks aid for the Stormwind orphanage he runs with his wife, caring for children left parentless by the Scourge. The trial asks an initiate to choose charity over martial glory.",h="In Ironforge, The Tome of Divinity calls servants of the Light to show compassion.",sec={{t="Charity in the Great Forge",b="For an initiate walking the path of the Light, the trial in Ironforge calls for charity rather than arms. John Turner has come to the dwarven halls seeking help. He and his wife oversee an orphanage in Stormwind, where they care for children left without parents by the Scourge's rampages over the past decade. His journey to Ironforge speaks to the hardships shared by the Alliance's common folk.",sp=0},{t="The Scars of the Scourge",b="Provisions are hard to gather across borders. More tradesmen have come to work across Azeroth, and local supplies have grown scarce. John Turner has spent weeks in Ironforge appealing to civic groups and citizens, with little to show for it. His wife needs linen cloth to make clothes for the children. Giving it freely shows the selfless devotion at the heart of a servant of the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is John Turner?",a="John Turner is a human caretaker who runs a Stormwind orphanage with his wife. About once a month, he travels to Ironforge to seek supplies and charity from dwarven citizens and merchant guilds for children orphaned by war.",al={"who is john turner","john turner lore","ironforge turners"}},{q="Why does John Turner need linen cloth?",a="John Turner needs linen cloth so his wife in Stormwind can sew warm clothes for the orphaned children. More tradesmen across Azeroth and shifting trade have made basic cloth scarce and hard to find.",al={"why linen","why does john turner need cloth","linen for orphanage"}},{q="Why are there so many orphans in Stormwind?",a="The children in the Turners' care lost their parents in the Scourge's devastating assaults over the past decade. The Third War shattered families across the human kingdoms and left many young people without homes.",al={"stormwind orphanage orphans","why are there orphans","scourge orphans"}},{q="Why did John Turner travel to Ironforge?",a="John Turner travels across the mountains to Ironforge to ask dwarven citizens and trade groups for donations and supplies that are hard to find in Stormwind.",al={"why is john turner in ironforge","why ironforge for stormwind orphanage"}},{q="How does helping an orphanage relate to the Holy Light?",a="The Holy Light calls for selflessness, mercy, and service to those in despair. Helping a weary caretaker provide for war orphans tests an aspiring paladin's humility and charity.",al={"what does this have to do with the light","paladin connection","why do paladins do this quest"}},{q="Why has John Turner's trip to Ironforge been so difficult?",a="John Turner's trip has been slow and unproductive because supplies are scarce and few groups have offered help. He cannot return home until he secures a shipment.",al={"is john turner stuck","why is the trip slow"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="A paladin faces this trial through selfless aid to the innocent, not steel or combat. Such compassion is a virtue at the heart of the Holy Light.",["faction:Alliance"]="For the Alliance, this reveals the Scourge's deep wounds in human homes and the reliance between Stormwind and Ironforge."},kw={"holy light","ironforge","john turner","linen cloth","paladin","scourge orphans","stormwind orphanage","the tome of divinity"},rel={"topic:light","zone:ironforge","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:scourge"},m={id=1648},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1656"] = {t="quest",n="A Task Unfinished",z="general",s="A Task Unfinished is a simple delivery quest highlighting the strain young tauren endure during their coming-of-age rites on Red Cloud Mesa. Overburdened by the demands of their trials and a filial chore, an exhausted youth asks a passing traveler to carry a bundle of furs down to Innkeeper Kauth in Bloodhoof Village.",h="A Task Unfinished is a simple delivery quest highlighting the strain young tauren endure during their...",sec={{t="Trials on the Mesa",b="High upon Red Cloud Mesa, young tauren face rigorous trials of passage to prove their readiness for the wider world. Balancing traditional rites against ordinary tribal chores can push a youth to the brink of physical exhaustion. Faced with an unfinished Rite of Strength and an urgent chore from their father, one young tauren seeks the aid of a passing traveler to carry a bundle of furs down the mesa.",sp=0},{t="Hearth and Shelter",b="The destination for the furs is Bloodhoof Village, a welcoming haven resting in the open plains below. Innkeeper Kauth eagerly receives the delivery, intending to turn the pelt bundle into warm blankets for weary wanderers. The errand highlights both the hospitality of the plains tauren and the importance of rest, showing that community support underpins every solitary trial of strength.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest A Task Unfinished?",a="A Task Unfinished is an errand given by an exhausted young tauren on Red Cloud Mesa. Overwhelmed by the ongoing trials of their Rite of Strength, the youth asks a passing traveler to deliver a bundle of furs to the innkeeper in Bloodhoof Village on their behalf.",al={"what is a task unfinished","task unfinished quest info","who gives a task unfinished"}},{q="Who is Innkeeper Kauth?",a="Innkeeper Kauth runs the local inn in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He provides a welcoming resting place, warm blankets, and hospitality to tired travelers making their way across the plains from the high mesas.",al={"who is innkeeper kauth","where is kauth","bloodhoof village innkeeper"}},{q="Why are the furs being delivered to the inn?",a="The bundle of furs is a delivery requested by the young tauren's father for Innkeeper Kauth. Kauth uses these sturdy furs as warm blankets for guests seeking shelter and rest at his inn in Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why deliver furs","what are the furs for","bundle of furs mulgore"}},{q="Why couldn't the young tauren deliver the furs themselves?",a="The young tauren is currently undertaking the Rite of Strength on Red Cloud Mesa. The physical demands of the trial leave them far too exhausted to travel all the way down to Bloodhoof Village without failing their sacred rite or collapsing from fatigue.",al={"why can't the quester deliver the furs","why does the youth need help"}},{q="What is the Rite of Strength?",a="The Rite of Strength is one of the foundational coming-of-age trials for young tauren on the mesa. It tests their physical stamina, resilience, and capability as rising protectors of their tribe.",al={"what is the rite of strength","rite of strength tauren trial"}},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village situated?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a primary settlement located in the rolling plains of Mulgore, down from the sheltered heights of Red Cloud Mesa. It features central tribal amenities including an open inn, crafting supplies, and trade outposts.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof village location"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Young tauren undergo demanding trials on Red Cloud Mesa to prove their maturity and strength, where tribal and family obligations often test their endurance as much as ritual challenges.",["faction:Horde"]="Bloodhoof Village serves as a vital gathering hub for travelers in Mulgore, offering welcoming shelter and warm hearths for members of the Horde traversing the plains."},kw={"a task unfinished","bloodhoof village","bundle of furs","innkeeper kauth","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","tauren"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:757"},m={id=1656},src={"In-game quest text: A Task Unfinished"},fv=true}
E["quest:1678"] = {t="quest",n="Vejrek",z="general",s="In the foothills south of Frostmane Hold, you face Vejrek to prove your mettle. Muren Stormpike wants proof you can handle real foes. Your success begins a lesson in strong defense.",h="In the foothills south of Frostmane Hold, you face Vejrek to prove your mettle.",sec={{t="A Test of Mettle",b="Muren Stormpike says you need toughness before he can teach you new moves. Vejrek is the toughest troll in these parts, and his hut lies in the foothills south of Frostmane Hold. Muren asks you to bring back Vejrek’s head as proof you can handle real foes.",sp=0},{t="The Lesson",b="Muren accepts Vejrek’s head and says you have what it takes. He is ready to teach you strong defense, which helps you stay standing against foes that would fell others and supports your allies.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Vejrek?",a="Vejrek is described as the toughest troll in these parts. His hut lies in the foothills south of Frostmane Hold.",al={"who's vejrek","vejrek troll","that troll"}},{q="Why does Muren send me after Vejrek?",a="Muren wants proof that you can handle real foes before he teaches you new moves.",al={"why kill vejrek","muren's test","prove mettle"}},{q="Where is Vejrek's hut?",a="Vejrek’s hut is in the foothills south of Frostmane Hold.",al={"vejrek location","where's his hut","foothills troll"}},{q="What does Muren want brought back?",a="Muren asks you to bring back Vejrek’s head as proof of your mettle.",al={"vejrek head","what to bring","muren wants what"}},{q="What does Muren teach after the test?",a="Muren begins teaching strong defense after accepting Vejrek’s head. The lesson covers how it helps you stay standing against foes.",al={"muren lesson","what's the lesson","strong defense"}}},kw={"frostmane hold","ironforge","mettle","muren stormpike","strong defense","troll","vejrek"},rel={"quest:1679","quest:287","zone:ironforge","topic:troll"},m={id=1678},src={"In-game quest text: Vejrek"},fv=true}
E["quest:1680"] = {t="quest",n="Tormus Deepforge",z="general",s="In Ironforge, Tormus Deepforge waits for fighters sent by Muren Stormpike. Before he forges a weapon for them, he asks them to face a perilous trial.",h="In Ironforge, Tormus Deepforge waits for fighters sent by Muren Stormpike.",sec={{t="The Blacksmith's Anvil",b="Tormus Deepforge is a master dwarven weaponsmith in Ironforge. Warriors who show their growing skill to Muren Stormpike are sent across the city to consult him. Tormus is known for sturdy, potent arms, but he does not give fine blades or bludgeons to every passerby. A warrior must earn his work through action.",sp=0},{t="A Perilous Trial",b="Before Tormus agrees to forge a weapon, he asks the adventurer to promise him a favor. He speaks plainly about the danger ahead and warns that it could cost a life. To him, peril is no reason to turn back. It is the proper test of a warrior's mettle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tormus Deepforge?",a="Tormus Deepforge is an accomplished weaponsmith in Ironforge. He can forge exceptional weapons for warriors who prove their courage and ability through dangerous deeds.",al={"who is tormus","tormus deepforge ironforge","who is tormus deepforge"}},{q="Who sends you to speak with Tormus Deepforge?",a="Muren Stormpike sends promising fighters to Tormus Deepforge when they are ready for a weapon suited to their growing martial skill.",al={"who sent me to tormus","why talk to tormus","muren stormpike referral"}},{q="What does Tormus Deepforge ask in return for making a weapon?",a="Tormus agrees to forge a fine weapon after the adventurer does him a favor. He asks for a perilous undertaking that could mean death, the kind of challenge he believes an aspiring warrior seeks.",al={"tormus favor","what weapon will tormus make","what does tormus want"}},{q="Why does Tormus emphasize that the job is dangerous?",a="Tormus warns that the undertaking is perilous because it could cost a life. He believes danger is the proper test for anyone worthy of his craft.",al={"is tormus deepforge dangerous","what is tormus task","ironforge warrior quest tormus"}},{q="Where can Tormus Deepforge be found?",a="Tormus Deepforge works near the Great Forge in Ironforge. Fellow smiths, anvils, and roaring smelters surround him.",al={"where is tormus","tormus deepforge location"}},{q="Which adventurers does Tormus Deepforge work with?",a="Tormus works with warriors sent by their trainers. He seeks fighters with the grit and resolve to face life-threatening dangers.",al={"which class gets tormus quest","is tormus warrior only"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Muren Stormpike sends promising warriors to Tormus Deepforge in Ironforge. There, they prove their mettle and seek a weapon forged for their calling.",["faction:Alliance"]="Ironforge's master smiths test promising Alliance fighters with dangerous undertakings before they forge fine weapons for them."},kw={"dwarf smith","great forge","ironforge","muren stormpike","tormus deepforge","warrior quest","weaponsmith"},m={id=1680},src={"In-game quest text: Tormus Deepforge"},fv=true}
E["quest:1712"] = {t="quest",n="Cyclonian",z="general",s="Cyclonian is a mighty wind lord whose defeat has been sought for ages. To challenge him, a champion must gather the components required to summon the elemental and strike him down to claim his whirlwind heart. Defeating the wind lord allows the heart's power to be infused into a formidable weapon.",h="Cyclonian is a mighty wind lord whose defeat has been sought for ages.",sec={{t="The Slumbering Wind Lord",b="Cyclonian is a raging elemental wind lord whose presence commands reverence and caution. For ages, an attentive watcher has kept vigil over the summoning site, waiting for a combatant possesses enough raw martial prowess to challenge and destroy this tempestuous entity.",sp=0},{t="Summoning the Tempest",b="Calling forth Cyclonian requires a ritual of summoning using rare materials detailed upon a special parchment. Once summoned, the wind lord must be faced in direct combat, testing whether the challenger is truly a champion or merely another victim of his howling gale.",sp=0},{t="The Whirlwind Heart",b="The ultimate goal of confronting the wind lord is to claim his whirlwind heart. Once severed from the vanquished elemental, the heart's fierce elemental energies can be bound and infused into a weapon fit for an accomplished champion.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Cyclonian?",a="Cyclonian is a powerful elemental wind lord. He represents a severe elemental threat that has long been watched by observers waiting for a champion strong enough to challenge and defeat him.",al={"who is cyclonian","cyclonian lore","what is cyclonian"}},{q="Why must Cyclonian be summoned?",a="Cyclonian is summoned to be challenged and slain by a worthy champion. Defeating the wind lord allows a proven fighter to claim his whirlwind heart, which can then be infused into a powerful weapon.",al={"why summon cyclonian","cyclonian purpose","why fight cyclonian"}},{q="What is the Whirlwind Heart?",a="The Whirlwind Heart is the core essence of the wind lord Cyclonian. When torn from the defeated elemental, its tempestuous energies can be forged directly into a weapon of great martial power.",al={"what is whirlwind heart","heart of cyclonian","whirlwind heart purpose"}},{q="Who has been watching Cyclonian?",a="Bath'rah the Windwatcher in the Alterac Mountains has spent ages keeping vigil over the site, searching endlessly for a true champion capable of toppling the elemental lord and mastering his tempestuous heart.",al={"who wants cyclonian dead","who watches cyclonian"}},{q="How is Cyclonian brought forth?",a="Summoning Cyclonian requires gathering specific rare reagents recorded on a special parchment, which serve to draw the wind lord out into the world so he can be contested.",al={"how to summon cyclonian","cyclonian ingredients"}},{q="What weapon is forged from Cyclonian?",a="A champion who gathers the necessary components, summons Cyclonian, and slays him earns a weapon infused with the latent fury of the elemental's whirlwind heart.",al={"cyclonian reward","what weapon from cyclonian"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Defeating the wind lord Cyclonian is a defining trial of strength, proving martial mastery by claiming his whirlwind heart to forge a potent weapon."},kw={"champion","cyclonian","elemental","summoning","whirlwind heart","wind lord"},m={id=1712},src={"In-game quest text: Cyclonian"},fv=true}
E["quest:1713"] = {t="quest",n="The Summoning",z="general",s="At Bath'rah Windwatcher's camp, a warrior faces a trial against Cyclonian, the wind lord. Slay the elemental and claim the Whirlwind Heart, whose storm force can be bound into a weapon.",h="At Bath'rah Windwatcher's camp, a warrior faces a trial against Cyclonian, the wind lord.",sec={{t="Calling the Tempest",b="Bath'rah Windwatcher has prepared an ancient ritual to summon Cyclonian into the mortal realm. The fearsome elemental is known as the wind lord. There will be no bargain with the tempest. An able champion must strike it down as soon as it takes form.",sp=0},{t="The Heart of the Storm",b="Cyclonian's Whirlwind Heart sustains his physical form. When the wind lord falls, Bath'rah wrenches the heart free and harnesses the gale within. Its volatile power can imbue a weapon with the force of the storm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Cyclonian?",a="Cyclonian is a powerful elemental known as the wind lord. Bath'rah Windwatcher's ritual calls him forth as a fierce tempest, and he must be defeated to claim the essence sealed in his core.",al={"who is cyclonian","what is cyclonian","cyclonian lore"}},{q="What is the Whirlwind Heart?",a="The Whirlwind Heart is Cyclonian's elemental core. Bath'rah Windwatcher needs it to bind the wind lord's swirling, destructive power into a weapon.",al={"what is whirlwind heart","why need whirlwind heart","whirlwind heart use"}},{q="Who is Bath'rah Windwatcher?",a="Bath'rah Windwatcher is a troll mystic who lives in the wilderness and knows how to summon Cyclonian. He guides warriors through the ritual, then uses the fallen elemental's heart to forge mighty weapons.",al={"who is bathrah","bathrah windwatcher","quest giver the summoning"}},{q="Why is Cyclonian being summoned?",a="Cyclonian is summoned so a warrior can face and slay the wind lord, then claim his elemental heart. Its power can be bound into a weapon.",al={"why summon cyclonian","purpose of the summoning","reason to summon"}},{q="What happens after Cyclonian is defeated?",a="After Cyclonian falls, Bath'rah can harness the elemental power in his Whirlwind Heart to empower a weapon with Cyclonian's stormcraft.",al={"what happens after defeating cyclonian","cyclonian reward lore","after the summoning"}},{q="Where does the summoning take place?",a="The ritual takes place at Bath'rah Windwatcher's camp. There, the mystic gathers the primal focus needed to draw Cyclonian from the elemental realm into the physical world.",al={"where does the ritual happen","the summoning location"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, facing Cyclonian and claiming the Whirlwind Heart is a proving ground. It yields a weapon infused with the raw power of the tempest.",["race:Troll"]="As a troll, you know Bath'rah Windwatcher's primal arts reach far beyond civilization. His command of wind elementals reflects the deep mystical traditions of troll shamans and witch doctors."},kw={"bath'rah windwatcher","bathrah","cyclonian","elemental","the summoning","warrior quest","whirlwind heart","wind lord"},rel={"quest:1712"},m={id=1713},src={"In-game quest text: The Summoning"},fv=true}
E["quest:1714"] = {t="quest",n="Essence of the Exile",z="general",s="At the cauldron, Essence of the Exile draws travelers with its beckoning vapors. Charms set off a violent reaction that reveals molten rock.",h="At the cauldron, Essence of the Exile draws travelers with its beckoning vapors.",sec={{t="A Beckoning Cauldron",b="A bubbling cauldron sends out vapors that draw travelers to its churning depths. Specific enchanted charms stir an intense reaction within the vessel.",sp=0},{t="The Molten Crucible",b="The charms sink slowly through the liquid. Then a large bubble rises and bursts, revealing a churning lump of molten rock forged from the combined materials.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Essence of the Exile?",a="Essence of the Exile is a transmutation ritual performed at a bubbling cauldron. Charms set off a violent reaction that yields a churning lump of molten rock.",al={"what is essence of the exile","essence of the exile quest"}},{q="What happens when charms are placed in the cauldron?",a="The cauldron's vapors draw travelers close. Charms sink to the bottom, then a large bubble bursts at the surface and leaves molten stone behind.",al={"what happens at the cauldron","how does the cauldron work"}},{q="What does the cauldron produce?",a="When a large bubble bursts at the surface, a churning lump of molten rock is revealed inside the cauldron.",al={"what is produced from the cauldron","what do you get from essence of the exile"}},{q="Where is the cauldron used for this ritual?",a="The ritual takes place at a boiling cauldron whose hot vapors beckon travelers. There, the vessel transforms its contents.",al={"cauldron location","where do i find the cauldron"}},{q="Why are charms placed into the cauldron?",a="The charms are consumed as reagents in the cauldron's depths. Their presence in the heated liquid helps form the molten rock.",al={"what are the charms for","why do charms go in the cauldron"}},{q="Why is the cauldron constantly bubbling?",a="The bubbling liquid and beckoning vapors mark an active reaction within the vessel. It is ready to transform whatever enters its depths.",al={"why is it bubbling","what are the vapors"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Alchemical cauldrons and elemental transmutations can bind separate essences into solid form through volatile magic.",["class:Shaman"]="Even when bubbling cauldrons churn molten rock, working with elemental essences calls for understanding the primal forces at play."},kw={"bubble","cauldron","charms","essence of the exile","molten rock","transmutation","vapors"},rel={"quest:1712"},m={id=1714},src={"In-game quest text: Essence of the Exile"},fv=true}
E["quest:1778"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Ironforge, Tiza Battleforge teaches initiates what service to the Holy Light demands. They aid someone in need, then return to tell her how they served.",h="In Ironforge, Tiza Battleforge teaches initiates what service to the Holy Light demands.",sec={{t="Lessons of the Holy Light",b="Paladins begin their trials by learning humility, duty, and charity. Tiza Battleforge does not ask for feats of arms. A servant of the Holy Light must understand the troubles ordinary people face and offer help without seeking praise or gain.",sp=0},{t="Accounting Before Ironforge",b="After helping someone in Ironforge, an aspiring paladin returns to Tiza Battleforge. The initiate tells her how they spent their time away and whose burdens they helped bear. When she is satisfied that they acted with charity and duty, Tiza commends them. Such selfless service is what it means to follow the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity tests novice paladins in compassion, charity, and self-sacrifice. Its trials ask more of them than strength in battle.",al={"what is the tome of divinity","tome of divinity trial","what does this quest do"}},{q="Who is Tiza Battleforge?",a="Tiza Battleforge is a paladin mentor in Ironforge. She guides initiates and judges whether their deeds follow the Holy Light.",al={"who is tiza battleforge","where is tiza battleforge","ironforge paladin trainer"}},{q="Who is Stephanie?",a="Stephanie Turner is John Turner's wife in Stormwind. They run an orphanage together, and John hopes to return to her after gathering supplies in Ironforge.",al={"who is stephanie","stephanie stormwind"}},{q="What does Tiza Battleforge ask of returning initiates?",a="Tiza Battleforge listens to how a returning initiate served others. She asks how they helped a noble cause and aided ordinary citizens before granting her blessing.",al={"what lesson does tiza test","what does tiza want","paladin charity test"}},{q="Why must initiates report to Ironforge?",a="Initiates return to Ironforge to tell Tiza Battleforge what they did. By helping John Turner in the city, they show compassion and selflessness.",al={"why speak to tiza in ironforge","ironforge tome of divinity"}},{q="Why is self-sacrifice central to this trial?",a="Paladin teachings place the welfare of others above one's own. Tiza teaches that selfless sacrifice will be rewarded in time.",al={"how does the light reward sacrifice","paladin sacrifice"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you learn that the Holy Light calls for selflessness, charity, and lasting duty to ordinary citizens before martial glory.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf paladin, you report to Tiza Battleforge in Ironforge after your trials in Dun Morogh. You account for your deeds in the wider world.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you see close ties between Ironforge and Stormwind in the mentors and travelers who pass between these great cities."},kw={"charity","holy light","ironforge","paladin trial","stephanie","stormwind","tiza battleforge","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:tiza-battleforge","zone:ironforge","topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1778},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1779"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Ironforge, The Tome of Divinity tests paladin initiates in holy power and martial devotion. Entrusted with the Symbol of Life, an initiate is sent to aid Muiredon Battleforge, wounded after a deadly encounter with Dark Iron dwarves.",h="In Ironforge, The Tome of Divinity tests paladin initiates in holy power and martial devotion.",sec={{t="A Trial of Faith and Power",b="In Ironforge, paladin initiates face a trial of martial skill and holy power. They have already shown humility, patience, and charity. Now they are entrusted with the Symbol of Life and sent into the city to help those suffering from conflicts beyond its gates.",sp=0},{t="A Brother Fallen",b="Muiredon Battleforge lies badly wounded after a clash with Dark Iron dwarves. A priest tends his immediate injuries as he prepares to tell what became of his expedition and his companion Narm. The initiate must answer that need with the Light’s gift of renewal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity is a rite of passage for young paladins learning the ways of the Holy Light. After proving charitable and patient, an initiate receives the Symbol of Life and is sent to aid a wounded dwarf in Ironforge.",al={"what is this quest","tome of divinity purpose","what does this quest do"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a sacred token entrusted to paladin initiates during their trials. It carries the Holy Light’s power to heal and restore those fallen while defending their people.",al={"what is symbol of life","symbol of life item"}},{q="Who is Muiredon Battleforge?",a="Muiredon Battleforge is a dwarven fighter wounded in a clash with Dark Iron dwarves. A priest tends him in Ironforge while his wife sends promising paladins to hear his tale.",al={"who is muiredon battleforge","who is the wounded dwarf"}},{q="Who gives you the Symbol of Life?",a="Muiredon Battleforge’s wife, a paladin mentor in Ironforge, gives you the Symbol of Life. After seeing your charity and patience, she sends you to aid her wounded husband and test your power, readiness for combat, and willingness to help.",al={"who sends you to muiredon","who gives this quest"}},{q="What happened to Muiredon Battleforge?",a="Muiredon and his companion Narm were ambushed by Dark Iron dwarves. Muiredon escaped and returned to Ironforge with grave injuries that need spiritual and physical mending.",al={"who attacked muiredon","what happened to muiredon"}},{q="Who is Narm?",a="Narm is Muiredon Battleforge’s best friend and fellow paladin. He accompanied Muiredon into battle against the Dark Iron dwarves, but did not escape the clash, leaving a paladin’s aid needed on his behalf.",al={"who is narm","what happened to narm"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you carry the Symbol of Life to comfort the wounded. The trial prepares you for the sacred duty of resurrection.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you face this trial in the heart of Ironforge. It recalls the close bonds among defenders who brave ambushes by hostile Dark Iron clans."},kw={"dark iron","holy light","ironforge","muiredon battleforge","narm","paladin","symbol of life","tome of divinity"},rel={"zone:ironforge","topic:light","topic:priest"},m={id=1779},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1780"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, Duthorian Rall guides paladins through a trial of devotion to the Holy Light. They prove their worth through charity, selflessness, and a duty to protect those in need.",h="In Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, Duthorian Rall guides paladins through a trial of devotion to the Holy...",sec={{t="A Trial of Virtue",b="Paladins in the service of the Holy Light face a defining trial in Stormwind City. At the Cathedral of Light, Duthorian Rall sends initiates to prove their devotion through moral conduct and selfless deeds for the kingdom's citizens. Martial glory alone will not suffice.",sp=0},{t="The Virtue of Selflessness",b="After aiding the vulnerable, initiates return to Duthorian Rall. They have brought needed relief to children and earned their heartfelt gratitude. Their mentor sees the value in putting others first. He teaches that charity, compassion, and duty to the people of Azeroth mark the path of the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity is a series of trials for aspiring paladins. Guided by mentors at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, initiates show moral virtue, charity, and duty by helping citizens in need across the kingdom.",al={"what is tome of divinity","tome of divinity quest","paladin trial quest"}},{q="Who is Duthorian Rall?",a="Duthorian Rall is a senior paladin trainer at the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City. He guides fledgling paladins in their spiritual education and moral trials, judging whether their deeds uphold the virtues of the Holy Light.",al={"who is duthorian rall","paladin trainer stormwind","duthorian rall role"}},{q="What is the lesson behind this trial?",a="The trial teaches that wielding the Light calls for humility, selfless sacrifice, and service to others. It prizes compassion, charity, and moral duty to the people of Azeroth alongside martial skill.",al={"tome of divinity purpose","why do paladins do this","what does this quest teach"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The trial centers on the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City. Initiates go out to help the populace, then return to report their deeds to Duthorian Rall at the cathedral.",al={"where does this quest take place","where to turn in tome of divinity","cathedral of light quest"}},{q="Who did the quest help?",a="The trial brings aid to those in dire need, including young children. The recipient gives heartfelt thanks and reminds paladins in Stormwind never to turn away from the innocent and destitute.",al={"who was helped in this quest","children charity quest","who received aid"}},{q="Why does Duthorian Rall emphasize charity?",a="Duthorian Rall emphasizes charity because the Holy Light calls for generosity and the defense of the innocent. He teaches that a paladin must sacrifice for others and value their well-being above their own.",al={"why does charity matter to paladins","paladins and the poor","charity holy light"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you prove your devotion to the Holy Light through charity and protection for ordinary citizens. The Tome of Divinity tests your commitment to serve others.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance defender, you are reminded that Stormwind's spiritual strength rests in safeguarding the vulnerable among your people."},kw={"cathedral of light","charity","duthorian rall","holy light","paladin trial","stormwind","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:duthorian-rall","topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1780},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1781"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="At Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, a trial awaits paladin initiates: they must focus the holy Symbol of Life. Those who prove their virtue are sent to aid Gazin Tenorm, who barely escaped a Defias ambush near Heroes' Vigil in Elwynn Forest.",h="At Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, a trial awaits paladin initiates: they must focus the holy Symbol of Life.",sec={{t="A Test of Devotion",b="In Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, patient and charitable paladin initiates receive the Symbol of Life. Duthorian Rall entrusts them with this holy vessel to test their devotion to the Light. Only a worthy champion can focus one of the Light's greatest gifts, the power to restore life to the fallen.",sp=0},{t="Ambush at Heroes' Vigil",b="Gazin Tenorm shows why the Symbol is needed. He crawled back to Stormwind after Defias bandits attacked him near Heroes' Vigil in Elwynn Forest. Priests tend his severe wounds at the cathedral altar. His companion Henze remains in danger where the outlaws struck.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gazin Tenorm?",a="Gazin Tenorm is an associate of Duthorian Rall. He barely escaped a Defias ambush near Heroes' Vigil in Elwynn Forest and was brought to Stormwind's Cathedral of Light with severe wounds. Local priests are treating him.",al={"who is gazin tenorm","gazin elwynn defias","wounded man stormwind altar"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a holy relic entrusted to patient and charitable paladin initiates. It tests whether a rising champion has the spiritual focus and fortitude to channel one of the Holy Light's greatest gifts: the restoration of life.",al={"what does symbol of life do","symbol of life paladin","paladin resurrection artifact"}},{q="What happened at Heroes' Vigil?",a="At Heroes' Vigil in Elwynn Forest, Defias outlaws ambushed Gazin Tenorm and his companion Henze. Gazin fled to Stormwind with grievous wounds, while Henze remains in danger.",al={"where is heroes vigil","heroes vigil elwynn defias","gazin ambush location"}},{q="Who is Henze?",a="Henze is Gazin Tenorm's companion. Defias bandits attacked them near Heroes' Vigil in Elwynn Forest, and Henze was left behind while Gazin reached Stormwind alive.",al={"who is henze","henze faulk","gazin friend defias"}},{q="Who sends you to speak with Gazin Tenorm?",a="Duthorian Rall sends you to speak with Gazin Tenorm. A leader among Stormwind's Cathedral of Light paladins, he first confirms that an initiate has shown patience and charity, then directs them to Gazin at the altar.",al={"who sends you to gazin","duthorian rall tome of divinity","stormwind paladin trainer quest"}},{q="Why are Defias bandits attacking travelers near Heroes' Vigil?",a="Defias outlaws and highwaymen ambush travelers across Elwynn Forest, even within striking distance of the capital. Gazin Tenorm's attack shows how brazen and deadly their operations have become.",al={"defias threat elwynn","why are defias near heroes vigil","defias brotherhood stormwind"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you face a milestone in your training at Stormwind. The trial tests whether you have the spiritual strength to wield the Symbol of Life.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you know Defias attacks near Heroes' Vigil threaten travelers and citizens across Elwynn Forest. The church must intervene."},kw={"cathedral of light","defias","duthorian","duthorian rall","gazin tenorm","henze","heroes vigil","symbol of life","the tome of divinity","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:gazin-tenorm","subzone:heroes-vigil","topic:light","topic:priest","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1781},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1783"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="A paladin initiate carries the Symbol of Life to the frigid shores south of Helm's Bed Lake in Dun Morogh. There, the initiate must find Narm Faulk, a brave scout who fell holding off Dark Iron dwarves, and call him back with the Light.",h="A paladin initiate carries the Symbol of Life to the frigid shores south of Helm's Bed Lake in Dun Morogh.",sec={{t="Ambush at the Lake",b="South of Helm's Bed Lake, dwarven scouts Muiredon and Narm Faulk watched Dark Iron dwarves entrench themselves. When they saw an enemy commander issuing orders, they struck at several foes and tried to secure a script. The Dark Irons overwhelmed them with unexpected ferocity. Muiredon fled for reinforcements while Narm held the line.",sp=0},{t="A Test of Faith",b="With the Symbol of Life, a paladin initiate searches Dun Morogh's snowy reaches for Narm Faulk. Narm fell beneath the Dark Iron assault, but the holy relic draws his spirit back just as the Light was ready to embrace him fully. In that moment, the initiate answers the first call of a paladin: restoring life to the fallen.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="The Tome of Divinity tests a young paladin's devotion and worthiness. An initiate receives the Symbol of Life and is sent to save a fallen scout, calling on the Light to bring someone back from death.",al={"what is the tome of divinity","tome of divinity purpose","paladin resurrection quest"}},{q="Who is Narm Faulk?",a="Narm Faulk is an Alliance scout ambushed by Dark Iron dwarves south of Helm's Bed Lake. He gave up his chance to flee so his partner Muiredon could escape, and fell in battle before the Light restored him.",al={"who is narm faulk","where is narm","fallen dwarf narm"}},{q="Who is Muiredon?",a="Muiredon is the scout who fled the Dark Iron ambush at Narm Faulk's urging. He returns for reinforcements and directs the paladin to save his fallen comrade with the Symbol of Life.",al={"who is muiredon","quest giver muiredon","who gives the quest"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="Tiza and Muiredon bestow the Symbol of Life upon the paladin with instructions. This sacred relic can resurrect the dead when a worthy servant of the Light channels its power.",al={"what is the symbol of life","resurrection item","symbol of life lore"}},{q="What were Narm and Muiredon doing near Helm's Bed Lake?",a="Muiredon and Narm Faulk were spying on Dark Iron dwarves entrenched south of Helm's Bed Lake. They saw an enemy leader issuing orders and tried to recover sacred scripts before the dwarves overwhelmed them.",al={"what were they doing south of helms bed lake","why spy on dark iron","dark iron helms bed lake"}},{q="Who is Tiza?",a="Tiza helps dispatch aid and prepares the Symbol of Life with Muiredon for the rescue.",al={"who is tiza","tiza paladin"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you face a trial of the Light. The Symbol of Life lets you restore a fallen ally.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you face the Dark Iron dwarves entrenched south of Helm's Bed Lake in Dun Morogh.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you help protect dwarven scouts and keep sacred scripts from the Dark Iron dwarves."},kw={"dark iron","dun morogh","helm's bed lake","muiredon","narm faulk","paladin","resurrection","symbol of life","tome of divinity"},rel={"zone:dunmorogh","subzone:helm-s-bed-lake","topic:light"},m={id=1783},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1784"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Dun Morogh, injured scouts need help exposing Dark Iron spies near Helm's Bed Lake. Their orders may persuade Ironforge's skeptical council to heed the danger.",h="In Dun Morogh, injured scouts need help exposing Dark Iron spies near Helm's Bed Lake.",sec={{t="A Trial of Service",b="Young dwarven paladins answer the call to defend Dun Morogh when an expedition near Helm's Bed Lake goes wrong. Narm Faulk and Muiredon Battleforge set out to ambush Dark Iron scouts. Narm fell wounded, while Muiredon returned to Ironforge to recover.",sp=0},{t="Proof for the Council",b="Rumors of Dark Iron infiltrators have reached Ironforge, yet some council members refuse to believe they have entered Dun Morogh. Near Helm's Bed, the paladin can track down the spies and recover a Dark Iron Script from their captain. The orders offer proof of enemy movements and plots near the capital's gates.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Hearth",b="Bringing the intercepted orders to Muiredon Battleforge in Ironforge brings relief. Narm has been rescued, and the documents go to Muiredon's wife to present before the council. The paladin's bravery and devotion to the Light help guard the dwarven realm from hidden treachery.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives The Tome of Divinity quest?",a="Narm Faulk gives you the charge to retrieve enemy scripts and report to his companion Muiredon Battleforge in Ironforge. He is an injured dwarf, wounded fighting Dark Iron spies near Helm's Bed Lake.",al={"who gives this quest","tome of divinity questgiver","who is narm"}},{q="Who is Muiredon Battleforge?",a="Muiredon Battleforge is a dwarf in Ironforge. He scouted the Dark Iron spies near Helm's Bed Lake with Narm Faulk, then returned to the city to recover from his wounds.",al={"who is muiredon battleforge","where is muiredon","muiredon battleforge location"}},{q="What is the Dark Iron Script?",a="The Dark Iron Script contains written orders passed between Dark Iron spies and their captain near Helm's Bed Lake. It offers evidence of hostile infiltration for Ironforge's skeptical council.",al={"what is dark iron script","why get dark iron script","dark iron script purpose"}},{q="Why does the council in Ironforge need proof of the Dark Irons?",a="Some members of Ironforge's council doubt Dark Iron dwarves have entered Dun Morogh. The recovered scripts prove their movements and reveal their secret plans.",al={"why council deny dark irons","ironforge council dark iron proof"}},{q="Where are the Dark Iron spies located?",a="The Dark Iron spies operate near Helm's Bed Lake in eastern Dun Morogh, east of Kharanos. They work near a small house, passing covert battle orders.",al={"where is helms bed lake","helms bed dark irons","dark iron location dun morogh"}},{q="What role does this quest play for paladins?",a="The Tome of Divinity trials test an aspiring paladin's service, compassion, and courage. By helping fallen comrades such as Narm and defending Dun Morogh, you prove your devotion to the Light.",al={"what does the tome of divinity teach","paladin tome of divinity lore"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you face an early trial in the Tome of Divinity. Protecting the kingdom and rescuing comrades in need show your devotion to the Light.",["race:Dwarf"]="For dwarves, uncovering Dark Iron spies near Ironforge is vital to the kingdom's defense. It proves to skeptical councilors that an old enemy has entered Dun Morogh.",["faction:Alliance"]="For the Alliance, exposing Dark Iron sabotage near Ironforge protects the dwarf capital and strengthens northern defenses against covert threats."},kw={"dark iron dwarves","dark iron script","dun morogh","helm's bed lake","ironforge","muiredon battleforge","narm faulk","tome of divinity"},rel={"subzone:helm-s-bed-lake","zone:ironforge","zone:dunmorogh","topic:light"},m={id=1784},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1785"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Ironforge, holy champions face a final trial before Tiza Battleforge. The Tome of Divinity shows whether they are ready to wield the Symbol of Life and call fallen allies back to life.",h="In Ironforge, holy champions face a final trial before Tiza Battleforge.",sec={{t="A Sacred Rite in Ironforge",b="You have crossed the kingdom's trials, and now you come to Tiza Battleforge in Ironforge. She is a revered teacher of the holy arts. Her husband watches with pride as you approach, eager to see the dwarven people acknowledge your readiness before the Holy Light.",sp=0},{t="The Blessing of Resurrection",b="Tiza Battleforge's final blessing tests whether your heart is ready to wield the Symbol of Life. The relic answers only to one prepared to channel the Light's greatest gifts. Prove your worth, and Tiza grants you a sacred scroll that lets you call fallen comrades back from death. Ironforge needs this power against the Scourge and the Dark Iron clan.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity is a sacred trial for holy champions in Ironforge. By proving your spiritual readiness, you learn to channel the Light and bring fallen companions back to life.",al={"what is tome of divinity","tome of divinity quest","what does this quest do"}},{q="Who is Tiza Battleforge?",a="Tiza Battleforge is a revered dwarven teacher of the Light in Ironforge. She gives the final blessing to champions ready to wield the power of resurrection.",al={"who is tiza battleforge","where is tiza battleforge","find tiza battleforge"}},{q="Who sends you to Tiza Battleforge?",a="After you complete an earlier trial in the field, Tiza Battleforge's husband directs you to her. He is already in Ironforge and sends you to his wife with pride and encouragement.",al={"who gives this quest","tiza husband","who sent me to tiza"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a holy item used in a paladin's trials. It answers only to a champion whose devotion makes them worthy to call a fallen ally back from death.",al={"what is symbol of life","symbol of life paladin","how does resurrection work"}},{q="What ability do you learn from completing this quest?",a="When you present yourself to Tiza Battleforge, she blesses you with a sacred scroll that teaches Redemption, the power to restore fallen allies to life.",al={"what power do i learn","resurrect spell quest","paladin revive quest"}},{q="Why are champions of the Light needed by Ironforge?",a="Ironforge needs champions of the Light against the Scourge and the Dark Iron dwarves. Their healing and holy defense help protect the realm.",al={"who are ironforge enemies","why fight dark irons and scourge","battlefield enemies"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you complete a sacred rite in Ironforge and receive the Light's power to raise the fallen.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you undertake this trial in Ironforge and earn the Battleforge family's gratitude on behalf of the kingdom.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance champion, you train in the Light to face the Scourge and the Dark Iron clan."},kw={"ironforge","paladin","redemption","resurrection","symbol of life","the tome of divinity","tiza battleforge","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:tiza-battleforge","zone:ironforge","topic:light","topic:scourge"},m={id=1785},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1786"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Elwynn, faith and sacrifice are tested when a Defias ambush leaves Henze Faulk dead at Heroes' Vigil. With the Symbol of Life, you are sent to restore him and hinder the brotherhood's schemes.",h="In Elwynn, faith and sacrifice are tested when a Defias ambush leaves Henze Faulk dead at Heroes' Vigil.",sec={{t="Ambush at Heroes' Vigil",b="Henze Faulk and Gazin traveled to Heroes' Vigil after hearing that Defias Brotherhood agents had crossed into northeastern Elwynn Forest. The island lies in the middle of Stone Cairn Lake. The scouts sought proof to present to the king's royal advisors, so they tried to ambush two conspirators reviewing plans.\n\nDefias spellcasters soon joined the fight, and the scouts were overwhelmed. Henze stood his ground against the mages, sacrificing himself to give Gazin time to escape with his life.",sp=0},{t="The Gift of Resurrection",b="Ordinary medicine cannot restore Henze; only the Holy Light can. With the Symbol of Life, you are sent to find his fallen body on the island in Stone Cairn Lake.\n\nYour faith, channeled through the sacred artifact, brings Henze back to life. This test shows a paladin's spiritual bond and readiness to serve the Light in Stormwind's defense.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Henze Faulk?",a="Henze Faulk is a defender who went with Gazin to Heroes' Vigil on Stone Cairn Lake to find proof of Defias infiltration. He held off the mages so Gazin could escape, and fell to their spells. The Symbol of Life later restored him to life.",al={"who is henze faulk","henze faulk","where is henze"}},{q="Where is Heroes' Vigil?",a="Heroes' Vigil is an island in the middle of Stone Cairn Lake in northeastern Elwynn Forest. Defias mages and rogues have made an outpost there, where they pore over plans.",al={"where is heroes vigil","heroes vigil location","heroes vigil island"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a holy item that channels a paladin's faith to restore the fallen. It needs true spiritual conviction, making its use on Henze Faulk a test of a paladin's calling.",al={"what is symbol of life","how to rez henze","symbol of life use"}},{q="Who directs the rescue of Henze Faulk?",a="Gazin Tenorm asks for help after escaping the Defias on the island. Wounded, he lies near the altar in Stormwind's Cathedral of Light. He is a friend of Duthorian Rall, the paladin leader who sent the initiate to him.",al={"who sent me to henze","duthroian or gazin","quest giver tome of divinity"}},{q="What are the Defias doing at Heroes' Vigil?",a="Defias operatives have crossed into northeastern Elwynn and gathered at Heroes' Vigil to study documents and coordinate their operations. Henze and Gazin sought those documents as proof for the king's advisors.",al={"why defias at heroes vigil","what are defias doing stone cairn lake","defias plans elwynn"}},{q="Why did Henze and Gazin go to Stone Cairn Lake?",a="Henze Faulk and Gazin went to Stone Cairn Lake after hearing reports of Defias infiltrators. They meant to ambush the conspirators and recover written proof of their plans for King Anduin's advisors in Stormwind.",al={"why were henze and gazin there","henze and gazin mission"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you prove your faith and bond with the Light by wielding the Symbol of Life to restore a fallen ally.",["faction:Alliance"]="Defias spellcasters in northeastern Elwynn threaten Stormwind's heartland. Gathering intelligence at Heroes' Vigil matters to the Alliance."},kw={"defias","duthorian","gazin","henze faulk","heroes vigil","paladin","resurrection","stone cairn lake","symbol of life","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:henze-faulk","subzone:heroes-vigil","subzone:stone-cairn-lake"},m={id=1786},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1787"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="You are proving your devotion to the Light through a trial for Alliance paladins. After helping an injured scout in Elwynn Forest, you face Defias Rogue Wizards and seek papers that threaten the realm.",h="You are proving your devotion to the Light through a trial for Alliance paladins.",sec={{t="A Paladin's Trial",b="As a paladin in training, you are being tested in Elwynn Forest. Henze was injured while watching illicit activity on an island. Help him learn how far the Defias have spread there.",sp=0},{t="Infiltrators in Elwynn",b="Henze found Defias Rogue Wizards studying secret papers on the island, but his injuries leave him unable to fight. You are sent to defeat the rogue spellcasters and recover their script. Then bring the intelligence safely to Gazin Tenorm in Stormwind.",sp=1},{t="Defending the Kingdom",b="The Defias Script confirms the brotherhood threatens nearby lands, including Heroes' Vigil. Bring the stolen intelligence to the Cathedral of Light. Your return proves your devotion to the Light and that Henze survived.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is involved in this quest?",a="You meet Henze Faulk, who lies injured on an island in Elwynn Forest. Afterward, report to Gazin Tenorm in the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind.",al={"who gives this quest","who gives tome of divinity","where do i get tome of divinity"}},{q="Who is Henze?",a="Henze is an ally in the paladin trials who was injured while watching Defias activity. Unable to fight, he sends you to secure proof of the brotherhood's infiltration.",al={"who is henze","henze faulk lore","who is the injured guy"}},{q="Who is Gazin Tenorm?",a="Gazin Tenorm is Henze's friend and a friend of Duthorian Rall. He can be found near the altar in Stormwind's Cathedral of Light. He barely escaped the Defias at Heroes' Vigil and wants to know Henze is safe.",al={"who is gazin tenorm","where is gazin tenorm","gazin stormwind"}},{q="Why are Defias Rogue Wizards operating on the island?",a="The Defias Brotherhood has positions throughout Elwynn Forest. Its rogue wizards study plans and coordinate operations, and their documents show what they intend in the region.",al={"why are defias in elwynn","defias rogue wizards","defias script"}},{q="What is Heroes' Vigil?",a="Heroes' Vigil is a place in Elwynn Forest threatened by Defias operations. Gazin Tenorm hopes that stopping nearby rogue wizards and taking their papers will protect it from further harm.",al={"what is heroes vigil","heroes vigil elwynn","is heroes vigil safe"}},{q="What is the Defias Script?",a="The Defias Script is written orders or correspondence carried by Defias Rogue Wizards. It proves their infiltration and details their immediate schemes in Elwynn Forest.",al={"what is defias script","what are the papers","defias papers elwynn"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you face a trial of devotion and courage. It tests your resolve to protect the innocent and uphold the Light against threats such as the Defias Brotherhood.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you help uncover Defias movements and documents in Elwynn Forest. This intelligence goes to Stormwind's defenders."},kw={"defias rogue wizards","defias script","elwynn forest","gazin tenorm","henze faulk","heroes' vigil","paladin quest","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:gazin-tenorm","subzone:heroes-vigil","topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1787},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1788"] = {t="quest",n="The Tome of Divinity",z="general",s="In Stormwind, paladins face a trial of faith in The Tome of Divinity. An initiate proves mastery of the Symbol of Life by restoring a fallen ally, then earns Duthorian Rall's blessing to wield resurrection.",h="In Stormwind, paladins face a trial of faith in The Tome of Divinity.",sec={{t="Return to the Cathedral",b="After restoring the fallen Henze with the Symbol of Life in the field, an aspiring paladin returns to the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind. The Defias papers recovered from the island are to go to the King's advisors through Gazin Tenorm. First, Duthorian Rall awaits word of the initiate's success.",sp=0},{t="The Gift of Resurrection",b="Calling on the Light to restore the dead is no small gift. Duthorian Rall sees mastery of the Symbol of Life as proof that an initiate has the faith and spiritual strength for one of the paladin order's highest callings: resurrection. With this rite mastered, the paladin can safeguard allies on battlefields across the kingdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Tome of Divinity?",a="The Tome of Divinity is a culminating trial for paladins learning to wield the Holy Light. Here, an initiate reports to Duthorian Rall after proving capable of channeling the Light to restore the fallen.",al={"tome of divinity","what is this quest","tome of divinity paladin"}},{q="Who is Duthorian Rall?",a="Duthorian Rall is a high-ranking paladin trainer at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light. He oversees paladin initiates and judges their readiness to wield sacred rites.",al={"who is duthorian","duthorian rall role","where is duthorian"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a sacred focus for calling on the Light to restore the dead. Mastering its power proves an initiate is ready to wield the paladin's power of resurrection.",al={"symbol of life purpose","what did symbol of life do"}},{q="Who was Henze?",a="Henze is an ally who fell in the field and was restored with the Symbol of Life during an initiate's trials. The deed showed the paladin's resolve and faith to reverse death.",al={"who is henze","henze lore","henze paladin quest"}},{q="How does a paladin earn the power of resurrection?",a="Resurrection is one of a paladin's highest honors. Through deep faith and attunement to the Holy Light, a paladin learns to call fallen companions back from death's threshold.",al={"paladin resurrection lore","how do paladins revive"}},{q="Why is the script being taken to the King's advisors?",a="The script is the Defias papers taken from the island. Once Duthorian Rall has looked them over, Gazin Tenorm will carry them to the King's advisors as proof that the Defias have infiltrated Elwynn.",al={"kings advisors script","what script goes to stormwind"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, this trial marks your passage into true service to the Light. You master the sacred power to return fallen companions to life.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance defender, you train among Stormwind's paladins at the Cathedral of Light. Their work preserves the lives of Alliance defenders and strengthens the kingdom's defenses."},kw={"duthorian","duthorian rall","henze","paladin","resurrection","stormwind","symbol of life","the tome of divinity","tome of divinity"},rel={"npc:duthorian-rall","topic:light","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=1788},src={"In-game quest text: The Tome of Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:1792"] = {t="quest",n="Whirlwind Weapon",z="general",s="A warrior's chosen weapon awaits the storm Cyclonian left behind. The whirlwind heart can bind the elemental's fury into a forged vessel.",h="A warrior's chosen weapon awaits the storm Cyclonian left behind.",sec={{t="Infusing the Storm",b="After Cyclonian falls and the warrior claims its heart, a craftsperson prepares a weapon to hold the storm's power. The heart is fused into the chosen arm, turning forged metal into a channel for elemental fury.",sp=0},{t="The Power of Cyclonian",b="Cyclonian's heart holds the violence of howling winds and tempestuous gales. Bound into a forged weapon, it lends the wielder the living might of the storm alongside their own martial skill. Each strike carries the force of the defeated elemental, whose storm now rages in the hands of its vanquisher.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Whirlwind Weapon quest?",a="A warrior can bind Cyclonian's whirlwind heart into a chosen weapon, joining the elemental's power with the warrior's own strength.",al={"what is whirlwind weapon","whirlwind weapon lore","what is this quest"}},{q="Who is Cyclonian?",a="Cyclonian is an elemental manifestation of wind and storm. Its defeat yields the whirlwind heart, which can bind primal wind energy into a physical weapon.",al={"who is cyclonian","cyclonian lore","what is cyclonian"}},{q="What is the whirlwind heart?",a="The whirlwind heart is Cyclonian's core, claimed when the elemental is defeated. It holds the tempest's fury and can be bound into a forged vessel chosen by the warrior.",al={"what is whirlwind heart","cyclonian heart","heart of cyclonian"}},{q="How is the weapon empowered?",a="The warrior chooses a forged weapon and has Cyclonian's whirlwind heart bound into it. The elemental's fury then courses through the steel.",al={"how is whirlwind weapon made","vessel of choice","forging whirlwind weapon"}},{q="What choices are offered for the weapon?",a="A warrior chooses the weapon that will receive Cyclonian's storm essence.",al={"which weapon can i pick","whirlwind choices","what weapons are offered"}},{q="Why is Cyclonian's power sought after?",a="Warriors seek Cyclonian's power to command the fury of a living gale through their weapons.",al={"why does warrior want whirlwind weapon","whirlwind weapon importance"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, you can claim a weapon infused with the tempestuous force of Cyclonian."},kw={"cyclonian","elemental","storm","vessel","warrior weapon","whirlwind heart","whirlwind weapon"},rel={"quest:1712"},m={id=1792},src={"In-game quest text: Whirlwind Weapon"},fv=true}
E["quest:1884"] = {t="quest",n="Ju-Ju Heaps",z="general",s="On the Echo Isles, Un'thuwa asks you to destroy Zalazane's ju-ju heaps. Their corruption threatens Durotar's flow of magic.",h="On the Echo Isles, Un'thuwa asks you to destroy Zalazane's ju-ju heaps.",sec={{t="Tainted Heaps",b="From Sen'jin Village, Un'thuwa asks you to destroy the heaps Zalazane placed among his huts on the main Echo Isle. The tainted fetishes corrupt wildlife and strengthen warlocks and infernal creatures. Left alone, they may change Durotar's flow of magic forever.",sp=0},{t="Un'thuwa's Sense",b="Un'thuwa senses the heaps have been destroyed. With time and luck, she believes Durotar's magic will return to its normal flow. She praises your deed for your people and says you have proved your worth to the Horde's classes.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who placed the ju-ju heaps?",a="Zalazane, a warlock of the Echo Isles, placed the tainted heaps among his huts on the main isle.",al={"who made the heaps","zalazane heaps","ju ju source"}},{q="What do the heaps do?",a="The heaps corrupt wildlife and strengthen warlocks and infernal creatures.",al={"what are juju heaps","heaps effect","why destroy juju"}},{q="Where are the ju-ju heaps?",a="Zalazane placed them among his huts on the main Echo Isle.",al={"where heaps","echo isle heaps","ju ju location"}},{q="Who asks me to destroy them?",a="Un'thuwa in Sen'jin Village asks you to destroy the ju-ju heaps.",al={"who gives this","unthuwa request","who wants heaps gone"}},{q="Why does Durotar's magic matter here?",a="The heaps disrupt Durotar's normal flow of magic. If they remain, that flow may change forever.",al={"magic flow","durotar magic","why care about magic"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Un'thuwa calls this a deed for your people and says you have proved your worth to the Horde's classes."},kw={"durotar","echo isles","ju-ju heaps","magic","sen'jin village","tainted fetishes","un'thuwa","zalazane"},rel={"quest:826","subzone:echo-isles","subzone:sen-jin-village","zone:durotar","topic:horde","topic:warlock"},m={id=1884},src={"In-game quest text: Ju-Ju Heaps"},fv=true}
E["quest:1947"] = {t="quest",n="Journey to the Marsh",z="general",s="Your class sends you to Dustwallow Marsh, where Tabetha’s knowledge can help you earn your wand.",h="Your class sends you to Dustwallow Marsh, where Tabetha’s knowledge can help you earn your wand.",sec={{t="Tabetha’s Cottage",b="West of Theramore and just north of the Stonemaul Ruins, Tabetha lives in a cottage. Your class asks you to speak with the hermit and learn from her vast knowledge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tabetha?",a="Tabetha is a hermit in Dustwallow Marsh, known for her vast knowledge.",al={"tabetha","marsh hermit","who is the hermit"}},{q="Where is Tabetha’s cottage?",a="Tabetha’s cottage is west of Theramore and just north of the Stonemaul Ruins.",al={"tabetha location","where is tabetha","tabetha shack"}},{q="Why am I being sent to Tabetha?",a="Speaking with Tabetha begins your effort to earn your class’s wand.",al={"why tabetha","wand quest","earn my wand"}}},kw={"cottage","dustwallow marsh","journey to the marsh","stonemaul ruins","tabetha","theramore","wand"},rel={"npc:tabetha","zone:dustwallow","subzone:stonemaul-ruins"},m={id=1947},src={"In-game quest text: Journey to the Marsh"},fv=true}
E["quest:201"] = {t="quest",n="Investigate the Camp",z="general",s="Investigate the Camp is an expedition task originating in Booty Bay to scout a newly established hunters' campsite in Stranglethorn Vale. Commissioned by Krazek, it tasks travelers with confirming rumors that the legendary dwarven marksman Hemet Nesingwary has arrived near Lake Nazferiti.",h="Investigate the Camp is an expedition task originating in Booty Bay to scout a newly established hunters'...",sec={{t="Reports in the Jungle",b="Reports circulating through the docks of Booty Bay speak of an expedition of big-game hunters encamped in the northern jungles of Stranglethorn Vale. Word has reached Krazek that the camp lies along the river just past the Venture Company's busy mining claim near Lake Nazferiti. In a lawless jungle teeming with dangers and competing interests, unexplained armed encampments always demand a watchful eye.",sp=0},{t="A Renowned Marksman",b="What makes this particular expedition notable is the rumored presence of Hemet Nesingwary himself. The renowned dwarven marksman is known throughout the lands as an elite explorer with influential ties. Krazek prefers to take no chances with powerful figures operating in the hinterlands, sending scouts upriver to confirm whether the legendary hunter has truly arrived.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the task to investigate the camp?",a="Krazek, an operative stationed in the neutral goblin port of Booty Bay, commissions travelers to seek out the encampment and report back its exact whereabouts.",al={"who wants camp investigated","who gave this quest","krazek booty bay"}},{q="Who is Hemet Nesingwary?",a="Hemet Nesingwary is a renowned dwarven big-game hunter and explorer. Word has reached Booty Bay that he is among the hunters camping near Lake Nazferiti, sparking interest due to his extensive reputation and connections.",al={"hemet nesingwary stranglethorn","who is hemet nesingwary","famous dwarf hunter stranglethorn"}},{q="Where is the hunters' camp located?",a="The hunters have established their campsite upriver from the Venture Company's mining operations along Lake Nazferiti in northern Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"hunters camp location","where is nesingwary camp","where is the camp upriver"}},{q="Why is Krazek interested in this hunting camp?",a="Normally a remote hunters' camp wouldn't draw much interest from Booty Bay, but reports that a figure as influential and well-connected as Hemet Nesingwary is present raised caution and curiosity.",al={"why does krazek care","why investigate hunters","krazek motive"}},{q="What is the hunters' relationship to the Venture Company?",a="The hunters are not part of the Venture Company; their campsite simply lies upriver from the goblin syndicate's mining outfit near Lake Nazferiti, serving as a geographic landmark.",al={"venture company connection","are hunters with venture co","venture co lake nazferiti"}},{q="To whom do you report after finding the camp?",a="Once the campsite along the river has been located and scouted, you return south to Booty Bay to deliver the report directly to Krazek.",al={"what do you do after finding camp","who to report to","where to turn in investigate camp"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="A rumored gathering of big-game hunters led by the renowned Hemet Nesingwary naturally draws tracking and marksmanship enthusiasts looking to test their mettle in the jungle.",["race:Dwarf"]="Hemet Nesingwary is among the most famous dwarven marksmen and adventurers, making his presence in the distant southern wilds of Stranglethorn Vale a point of pride and curiosity."},kw={"booty bay","hemet nesingwary","hunters camp","investigate the camp","krazek","lake nazferiti","stranglethorn vale","venture company"},rel={"quest:5762","subzone:lake-nazferiti","subzone:booty-bay","topic:venture-company","topic:hunter"},m={id=201},src={"In-game quest text: Investigate the Camp"},fv=true}
E["quest:2158"] = {t="quest",n="Rest and Relaxation",z="general",s="Rest and Relaxation is an introductory Alliance quest directing newly trained adventurers from Northshire south along the road to the Lion's Pride Inn. Run by Innkeeper Farley, the bustling Goldshire tavern offers food, drink, and a warm hearth for those seeking a reprieve from the road.",h="Rest and Relaxation is an introductory Alliance quest directing newly trained adventurers from Northshire...",sec={{t="A Haven on the Road",b="After cutting their teeth in Northshire, weary travelers are pointed south toward the Lion's Pride Inn in Goldshire. The road south leads directly to the beloved tavern, run by Innkeeper Farley. For new adventurers stepping out into the wider forest, it represents the first major outpost offering hearth, shelter, and hot meals away from the Abbey.",sp=0},{t="Farley's Hearth and Hospitality",b="Innkeeper Farley takes pride in his establishment's hospitality, operating under the motto of providing rest and relaxation for the tired and cold. Adventurers who mention their referral can take advantage of Farley's good graces, settling by the open fire to enjoy a warm meal, sturdy ale, and a much-needed respite before journeying further into Elwynn.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find the Lion's Pride Inn?",a="The Lion's Pride Inn is located south along the main road leading out of Northshire Valley, situated at the heart of the town of Goldshire. Travelers heading south cannot miss the welcoming tavern.",al={"where is lions pride inn","how to get to lion's pride inn","find lions pride"}},{q="Who is Innkeeper Farley?",a="Innkeeper Farley is the hospitable proprietor of the Lion's Pride Inn in Goldshire. A close friend of local contacts in Northshire, he welcomes weary adventurers with food, drink, and a warm hearth.",al={"who is innkeeper farley","farley lion's pride"}},{q="What is the purpose of Rest and Relaxation?",a="Rest and Relaxation directs novice adventurers leaving Northshire toward Goldshire's central hub, introducing them to the services, refreshments, and lodgings offered by Innkeeper Farley at the Lion's Pride Inn.",al={"what is the purpose of this quest","why go to lion's pride inn"}},{q="Does Innkeeper Farley really offer discounted food and drink?",a="Mentioning your referral to Innkeeper Farley allows travelers to inquire about special discounted rates on the inn's fine food and drink while recovering from their travels.",al={"discount at lions pride","do i get cheaper food farley"}},{q="Why is rest important for traveling adventurers?",a="Adventuring across Elwynn is grueling and fraught with danger. Inns provide safe shelter from the wilderness, allowing travelers to warm their bones by the fire, recuperate, and replenish their supplies before taking on new tasks.",al={"why do adventurers need rest","rest and relaxation lore"}},{q="What is the motto of the Lion's Pride Inn?",a="According to Innkeeper Farley, the inn's motto is 'Rest and relaxation for the tired and cold.' He strives to ensure every weary guest finds comfort and hospitality by his hearth.",al={"what is lions pride inn motto","lions pride slogan"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Lion's Pride Inn serves as the primary haven and social hub for Alliance travelers venturing out into Elwynn Forest.",["race:Human"]="Fresh recruits departing Northshire Valley naturally follow the southern road toward Goldshire, where Innkeeper Farley offers familiar hospitality close to Stormwind."},kw={"alliance tavern","elwynn forest","goldshire","innkeeper farley","lion's pride inn","northshire","rest and relaxation"},rel={"npc:innkeeper-farley","subzone:lion-s-pride-inn"},m={id=2158},src={"In-game quest text: Rest and Relaxation"},fv=true}
E["quest:2360"] = {t="quest",n="Mathias and the Defias",z="general",s="In Westfall, SI:7 has found the Defias unusually active. You are sent to Agent Kearnen near an abandoned Alliance watch tower for a debriefing.",h="In Westfall, SI:7 has found the Defias unusually active.",sec={{t="The Watch Tower",b="The Defias have seized an abandoned Alliance watch tower southeast of Sentinel Hill. SI:7 reports they use it as a base to smuggle goods from the Redridge supply route. You are sent to Westfall to find Agent Kearnen, hidden near the tower, and hear her debriefing.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is happening in Westfall?",a="SI:7 reports unusually heavy Defias activity in Westfall. The Defias have taken an abandoned Alliance watch tower and use it to smuggle goods from the Redridge supply route.",al={"defias in westfall","westfall defias activity"}},{q="Where is the Defias base?",a="The Defias base is an abandoned Alliance watch tower southeast of Sentinel Hill.",al={"defias tower","where is their base"}},{q="Who is Agent Kearnen?",a="Agent Kearnen is the SI:7 agent you are sent to find near the tower for a debriefing.",al={"kearnen","who is kearnen"}},{q="What does SI:7 want me to do?",a="SI:7 sends you to Westfall to find Agent Kearnen, hidden near the tower, and receive a debriefing.",al={"si7 task","find kearnen"}},{q="What are the Defias smuggling?",a="The Defias are smuggling goods from the Redridge supply route.",al={"defias goods","redridge supplies"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Defias have taken an abandoned Alliance watch tower in Westfall and use it as a base for smuggling."},kw={"agent kearnen","defias","mathias","redridge","sentinel hill","si:7","watch tower","westfall"},rel={"quest:2300","zone:westfall","subzone:sentinel-hill","topic:alliance"},m={id=2360},src={"In-game quest text: Mathias and the Defias"},fv=true}
E["quest:2383"] = {t="quest",n="Simple Parchment",z="general",s="A message waits for you in the Valley of Trials, sent while you were away. Frang, your class trainer, is outside the Den.",h="A message waits for you in the Valley of Trials, sent while you were away.",sec={{t="A Message from Frang",b="The parchment asks you to read its words and speak with Frang. Your class trainer waits outside the Den in the Valley of Trials.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sent the parchment?",a="The parchment appears to have come from Frang, your class trainer.",al={"parchment sender","who wrote it"}},{q="Where can I find Frang?",a="Frang waits outside the Den in the Valley of Trials.",al={"where's Frang","Frang location"}},{q="What does Frang want?",a="Frang has words for you when you are ready.",al={"why Frang wants me","parchment message"}},{q="What should I do with the parchment?",a="Read the parchment, then speak with Frang in the Valley of Trials.",al={"read parchment","simple parchment"}}},kw={"class trainer","den","frang","parchment","simple parchment","valley of trials"},rel={"subzone:valley-of-trials"},m={id=2383},src={"In-game quest text: Simple Parchment"},fv=true}
E["quest:272"] = {t="quest",n="Trial of the Sea Lion",z="general",s="Druids in Moonglade seek the sea lion’s spirit through a sacred rite. They recover two scattered pendant halves for agility and endurance, then unite them at the Shrine of Remulos to prepare for Aquatic Form.",h="Druids in Moonglade seek the sea lion’s spirit through a sacred rite.",sec={{t="The Path to Aquatic Form",b="Druid initiates in Moonglade undergo trials of attunement as they prepare to navigate Azeroth’s deep waters. The Trial of the Sea Lion follows earlier preparations in their spiritual training. Dendrite Starblaze oversees it in Nighthaven, where initiates learn to balance the sea lion’s graceful speed with its resilient stamina. Both are needed to take its form and withstand the ocean depths.",sp=0},{t="Agility and Endurance",b="The trial centers on reuniting the two halves of the Pendant of the Sea Lion. One represents the sea lion’s agility as it glides through turbulent currents. The other represents its endurance, the fortitude to dive long into freezing water without faltering. Initiates seek guidance from Moonglade’s inhabitants as they search across the world for the scattered relics.",sp=0},{t="The Shrine of Remulos",b="Once both halves are found, the initiate brings them near the Shrine of Remulos, where they can be joined into one talisman. The shrine’s energies bind agility and endurance in harmony. The initiate then presents the restored pendant to Dendrite Starblaze in Nighthaven. With the pendant, the druid shows readiness to embrace the sea lion aspect and awaken Aquatic Form.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Trial of the Sea Lion?",a="Druids in Moonglade recover two pendant halves and unite them at the Shrine of Remulos. The halves represent agility and endurance, and the restored pendant is presented to Dendrite Starblaze as part of the path to Aquatic Form.",al={"trial of the sea lion quest","what is trial of the sea lion","sea lion trial"}},{q="Who gives the Trial of the Sea Lion quest?",a="Warden Tajarri at the Shrine of Remulos in Moonglade gives the trial. Initiates seek guidance from local residents, join the pendant halves near the shrine, then take the finished pendant to Dendrite Starblaze in Nighthaven.",al={"who gives trial of the sea lion","where to start trial of the sea lion","trial of the sea lion quest giver"}},{q="What do the two pendant halves represent?",a="The halves represent the sea lion’s agility and endurance. One reflects the grace and swiftness to glide through deep water; the other, the stamina and resilience for long journeys underwater. Druids must respect and embody both traits equally to master their shapeshifting arts.",al={"what are the pendant halves","pendant halves meaning","agility and endurance halves"}},{q="Where are the pendant halves located?",a="Initiates learn where the Half Pendant of Aquatic Agility and the Half Pendant of Aquatic Endurance are hidden by speaking with Moonglade’s residents. Local druids and villagers give clues about the distant waters where each half was lost or concealed.",al={"how to find pendant halves","where to find the pendant halves","pendant of the sea lion halves location"}},{q="Where do you assemble the Pendant of the Sea Lion?",a="The halves must be joined near the Shrine of Remulos in Moonglade. The shrine’s sacred aura helps unite agility and endurance in one talisman.",al={"how to combine pendant halves","where to fuse pendant halves","where is shrine of remulos pendant"}},{q="Why must druids balance agility and endurance?",a="Dendrite Starblaze teaches that agility and endurance must be balanced beneath the water. Speed without stamina brings exhaustion, while endurance without swiftness leaves a swimmer helpless against strong currents. Druids need both traits to embody the sea lion’s spirit.",al={"why agility and endurance","sea lion traits druid","meaning of the sea lion trial"}},{q="What is gained by completing the trial?",a="Completing the trial earns the restored Pendant of the Sea Lion and fulfills a major trial required to earn Aquatic Form. Mastering agility and endurance gives the initiate a spiritual foundation for taking a form that can breathe and swim swiftly underwater.",al={"what do you get from trial of the sea lion","sea lion trial reward","does this give aquatic form"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="This rite is exclusive to druids. It tests their spiritual maturity and balance before they may commune with aquatic spirits and adopt the sea lion’s form.",["race:NightElf"]="As long-standing custodians of Moonglade and the Cenarion Circle, night elf druids revere Dendrite Starblaze’s teachings and the sacred ground around the Shrine of Remulos.",["race:Tauren"]="For tauren druids newly welcomed into the Cenarion Circle, undertaking the trial in Moonglade strengthens their communion with nature spirits and deepens their ties with their night elf mentors."},kw={"aquatic form","dendrite starblaze","half pendant of aquatic agility","half pendant of aquatic endurance","moonglade","nighthaven","pendant of the sea lion","shrine of remulos","trial of the sea lion"},rel={"item:pendant-of-the-sea-lion","npc:dendrite-starblaze","quest:31","npc:remulos","subzone:nighthaven","quest:5921"},m={id=272},src={"In-game quest text: Trial of the Sea Lion"},fv=true}
E["quest:284"] = {t="quest",n="The Search Continues",z="general",s="The search for the Menethil Statuette continues among nearby Murloc hovels. These shelters hold no sign of it, so you turn to two more.",h="The search for the Menethil Statuette continues among nearby Murloc hovels.",sec={{t="Empty Hovels",b="You search this cluster of Murloc hovels, but find no trace of the Menethil Statuette. Two nearby shelters to the north and northeast may hold clues.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What am I searching for?",a="You are searching for the Menethil Statuette.",al={"statuette search","menethil statue","what statuette"}},{q="What do I find in these hovels?",a="You find no trace of the Menethil Statuette in this cluster of Murloc hovels.",al={"murloc hovels","empty hovels","anything here"}},{q="Where should I look next?",a="Look in the nearby hovels to the north and northeast for more clues.",al={"next hovels","where look","north huts"}},{q="What is in the barrel?",a="The barrel holds damp, pungent cheese that is still tasty.",al={"barrel contents","cheese barrel","what's in barrel"}}},kw={"barrel","cheese","hovels","menethil statuette","murloc","search"},rel={"item:menethil-statuette","topic:murloc"},m={id=284},src={"In-game quest text: The Search Continues"},fv=true}
E["quest:285"] = {t="quest",n="Search More Hovels",z="general",s="Among the Murloc hovels, you have found no sign of the Menethil Statuette. So you press farther north, seeking the next clue.",h="Among the Murloc hovels, you have found no sign of the Menethil Statuette.",sec={{t="Northward Search",b="You believe the Menethil Statuette lies in one of the hovels to the north. So you continue your search among the Murloc dwellings.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What am I searching for?",a="You are searching the Murloc hovels for the Menethil Statuette.",al={"statuette search","menethil statue","what am i looking for"}},{q="Did these hovels have the statuette?",a="A thorough search of this group of hovels reveals nothing.",al={"empty hovels","find anything here","what did the hovels show"}},{q="Where might the statuette be?",a="You believe one of the Murloc hovels farther north may hold the Menethil Statuette.",al={"where is the statuette","north hovels","statuette location"}}},kw={"barrel","menethil statuette","murloc hovels","north hovels","search more hovels"},rel={"topic:murloc","item:menethil-statuette"},m={id=285},src={"In-game quest text: Search More Hovels"},fv=true}
E["quest:286"] = {t="quest",n="Return the Statuette",z="general",s="Karl Boran in Menethil Harbor needs the Menethil Statuette brought back. It was found inside a barrel pulled from the muck, and its return will ease the Blind Princess's captain.",h="Karl Boran in Menethil Harbor needs the Menethil Statuette brought back.",sec={{t="The Figurine",b="The statuette lay packed in fine straw inside the barrel. It shows a young, long-haired Paladin raising a hammer. Karl Boran in Menethil Harbor is waiting for it. The Blind Princess's captain will be relieved to know the cargo is safe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Menethil Statuette?",a="The Menethil Statuette is a silvery figurine showing a young, long-haired Paladin with a raised hammer.",al={"statuette","menethil figurine","what's in the barrel"}},{q="Where was the statuette found?",a="The statuette was packed in fine straw inside a barrel pulled from the muck.",al={"barrel statuette","where was it","straw barrel"}},{q="Who is waiting for the statuette?",a="Karl Boran in Menethil Harbor is waiting for the Menethil Statuette.",al={"who gets the statuette","return statuette","Karl Boran"}},{q="Who will be relieved when it is returned?",a="The captain of the Blind Princess will be relieved that the precious cargo has been recovered.",al={"captain relieved","Blind Princess cargo","who owns the cargo"}},{q="What does the statuette depict?",a="The statuette depicts a young, long-haired Paladin with a hammer raised.",al={"who is on the statuette","Paladin figurine","hammer statuette"}}},kw={"barrel","blind princess","karl boran","menethil harbor","menethil statuette","paladin","statuette"},rel={"item:menethil-statuette","subzone:menethil-harbor","topic:paladin"},m={id=286},src={"In-game quest text: Return the Statuette"},fv=true}
E["quest:29"] = {t="quest",n="Trial of the Lake",z="general",s="In Moonglade, an initiate faces the Trial of the Lake, a timed test of speed and dexterity. You dive into Lake Elune'ara for an unstable Shrine Bauble, then hurry to the Shrine of Remulos before it decays. Success earns Tajarri's recognition and honors the Cenarion Circle's devotion to balance.",h="In Moonglade, an initiate faces the Trial of the Lake, a timed test of speed and dexterity.",sec={{t="A Test of Dexterity",b="Moonglade's guardians oversee this rite for initiates. You dive into Lake Elune'ara to find a delicate Shrine Bauble resting along the sunken lakebed. It begins to decay as soon as you disturb it, so you need a sure touch, keen senses, and swift movement to carry it overland before its magic fades.",sp=0},{t="Tribute to the Keeper",b="Once you have the bauble, you must hurry to the Shrine of Remulos in northwestern Moonglade. The monument honors Keeper Remulos, who guides the Cenarion Circle in preserving nature and world balance. Activating the bauble there marks its importance to the Circle. Warden Tajarri greets initiates who beat the time limit and the competition.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Trial of the Lake?",a="The Trial of the Lake tests an initiate's agility and focus in Moonglade. You dive into the cold depths of Lake Elune'ara to retrieve a Shrine Bauble that decays rapidly, then race across the haven to the Shrine of Remulos before its energy dissipates.",al={"what is trial of the lake","purpose of trial of the lake","what is this quest"}},{q="Where is the Shrine of Remulos located?",a="The Shrine of Remulos stands in northwestern Moonglade. It honors Keeper Remulos, who guides the Cenarion Circle, and warden Tajarri guards and maintains it.",al={"where is shrine of remulos","shrine location","remulos shrine moonglade"}},{q="Who is Tajarri?",a="Tajarri is a trusted warden of Moonglade who tends the Shrine of Remulos. She oversees the Trial of the Lake and welcomes initiates who bring the bauble to honor the shrine.",al={"who is tajarri","tajarri moonglade","shrine warden tajarri"}},{q="What is a Shrine Bauble?",a="A Shrine Bauble is a relic found along the bed of Lake Elune'ara. Once taken from the water, it decays rapidly, so its bearer must race to activate it at the Shrine of Remulos as tribute before its energy dissipates.",al={"what is shrine bauble","why does bauble decay","shrine bauble purpose"}},{q="Why is Keeper Remulos honored at this shrine?",a="Keeper Remulos is a revered keeper of the grove who guides the Cenarion Circle in preserving nature and maintaining balance. The shrine in northwestern Moonglade honors his leadership and principles.",al={"who is keeper remulos","remulos lore","why honor remulos"}},{q="What does offering the bauble at the shrine signify?",a="Activating the bauble at the Shrine of Remulos pays tribute to the sacred balance the Cenarion Circle maintains and acknowledges the shrine's lasting spiritual importance in Moonglade.",al={"what does using bauble do","why activate bauble at shrine"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you face a test of swiftness and adaptability in Moonglade. The rite of passage honors Keeper Remulos."},kw={"cenarion circle","lake elune'ara","moonglade","remulos","shrine bauble","shrine of remulos","tajarri","trial of the lake"},rel={"subzone:lake-elune-ara","npc:remulos","topic:cenarion-circle","quest:5921","topic:elune"},m={id=29},src={"In-game quest text: Trial of the Lake"},fv=true}
E["quest:297"] = {t="quest",n="Gathering Idols",z="general",s="At an excavation site, troggs clutch the carved idols Magmar Fellhew seeks. The relics have driven them into a violent frenzy.",h="At an excavation site, troggs clutch the carved idols Magmar Fellhew seeks.",sec={{t="The Unearthed Relics",b="Researchers uncovered a large cache of strange, carved stone idols shortly before savage troggs surfaced and overran the excavation site. The attack drove the expedition from the ruins before any formal study could begin. Now the troggs clutch the relics and prowl the site, barring a peaceful return.",sp=0},{t="A Savage Frenzy",b="The troggs seem deeply affected by the idols, beyond merely taking them. Their strange carvings exert an unsettling power that drives the creatures into an uncontrollable rage. Magmar Fellhew wants to study the idols safely and quell the frenzy gripping the area, so he seeks to take them from trogg hands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Magmar Fellhew?",a="Magmar Fellhew is an excavator seeking to recover carved stone idols from an excavation site troggs overran.",al={"who is magmar fellhew","quest giver","magmar"}},{q="What are the carved stone idols?",a="The idols are mysterious carved stone relics unearthed at an excavation site. Their unsettling power drives nearby troggs into a berserk frenzy.",al={"what are carved stone idols","strange idols","excavated idols"}},{q="Why do the idols make the troggs go berserk?",a="The idols exert a strange, inexplicable influence on troggs. When the creatures possess them, wild fury drives them berserk.",al={"why troggs berserk","idols make troggs crazy","trogg rage"}},{q="Why does Magmar Fellhew want the idols collected?",a="Magmar Fellhew wants the idols recovered safely so he can study their origins and properties, and remove the volatile relics from trogg hands.",al={"why get idols","purpose of gathering idols","magmar study"}},{q="What happened at the excavation site?",a="Troggs surfaced abruptly from the excavation site shortly after the idols were discovered. They drove the excavators from the ruins.",al={"who surfaced at the site","trogg invasion","excavation overrun"}},{q="Where are the idols found?",a="Troggs carry the carved stone idols at the excavation site where the relics were first unearthed.",al={"where to find idols","who has idols","carved stone idol location"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you join excavators seeking ancient artifacts in trogg-infested ruins and the secrets they hold.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance adventurer, you help dwarven expeditions secure dangerous relics before savage troggs cause more havoc."},kw={"berserk","carved stone idols","excavation","gathering idols","magmar fellhew","ruins","troggs"},rel={"topic:trogg"},m={id=297},src={"In-game quest text: Gathering Idols"},fv=true}
E["quest:3090"] = {t="quest",n="Tainted Parchment",z="general",s="A marked parchment waits for you after your absence, and your teacher Nartok asks to speak with you. Its words challenge Thrall’s rule and warn of the dangers warlocks face.",h="A marked parchment waits for you after your absence, and your teacher Nartok asks to speak with you.",sec={{t="A message waits",b="The marked parchment is believed to come from Nartok, your new teacher. Read it, then speak with him in the Den in the Valley of Trials. He calls the orcs a proud people and says Thrall turns away from power that could make the land theirs.",sp=0},{t="A place among them",b="Nartok says you have joined their ranks, yet warlock power will not make you welcome. Thrall allows warlocks into Orgrimmar because they remain orcs. Nartok warns that recklessness can be fatal. He remains ready to teach you spellcasting if you need instruction.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who sent the parchment?",a="The parchment is believed to come from Nartok, your new teacher.",al={"who sent this","parchment from who"}},{q="Where can I speak with Nartok?",a="Nartok is in the Den in the Valley of Trials.",al={"where is nartok","nartok location"}},{q="What does Nartok think of Thrall?",a="Nartok says Thrall turns away from power that could make the land theirs.",al={"thrall opinion","why criticize thrall"}},{q="Why does Thrall allow warlocks into Orgrimmar?",a="Nartok says Thrall allows warlocks into Orgrimmar because they are still orcs.",al={"warlocks in orgrimmar","why thrall allows warlocks"}},{q="What warning does Nartok give?",a="Nartok warns that warlock power does not ensure acceptance, and recklessness can be fatal.",al={"nartok warning","warlock danger"}},{q="Can Nartok teach me spells?",a="Nartok remains available if you need instruction in spellcasting.",al={"spell lessons","warlock training"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="As a warlock, you face the danger Nartok warns of: wielding your power recklessly may prove fatal."},kw={"besudeltes pergament","den","nartok","orgrimmar","spellcasting","thrall","valley of trials","warlock"},rel={"npc:thrall","zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=3090},src={"In-game quest text: Besudeltes Pergament"},fv=true}
E["quest:3091"] = {t="quest",n="Simple Note",z="general",s="At Camp Narache, Harutt Thunderhorn calls young tauren warriors to defend their people. His summons reminds them that warriors stand beside druids and shamans in guarding the tribal borders from centaur.",h="At Camp Narache, Harutt Thunderhorn calls young tauren warriors to defend their people.",sec={{t="A Summons to Battle",b="At Camp Narache, a messenger brings young warriors a summons from Harutt Thunderhorn. The veteran trainer calls each fighter to report and begin training to defend the tribe.",sp=0},{t="The Defender's Burden",b="The Shu'halo revere their spiritual leaders, yet Harutt teaches that warriors bear an equal burden. They patrol tribal borders and hunt predatory centaur packs to protect their peaceful kin from attack. Battle may bring glory, but warriors remain humble and complete the same traditional rites as shamans and druids.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who wrote the Simple Note?",a="Harutt Thunderhorn wrote the summons and sent it through a messenger to young tauren ready for martial training.",al={"who sent simple note","who wrote the note","sender simple note"}},{q="Who is Harutt Thunderhorn?",a="Harutt Thunderhorn is a tauren warrior trainer at Camp Narache in Mulgore. He trains new warriors in battle and the ancestral ways of tribal warfare.",al={"who is harutt","harutt thunderhorn","where is harutt"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Simple Note?",a="The note calls newly awakened warriors to meet their trainer. It reminds them that defenders and hunters are as vital to the tribe as druids and shamans.",al={"why is the note important","what does the note say"}},{q="Why are warriors called upon to hunt centaur?",a="Hostile centaur packs threaten Mulgore's borders. Harutt trains warriors to hunt them and protect the camp.",al={"why fight centaur","tauren centaur conflict"}},{q="Where is Camp Narache located?",a="Camp Narache lies at the edge of Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. It is the ancestral training camp and starting settlement of the young tauren of the Hawkwind tribe.",al={"camp narache location","where is camp narache"}},{q="What are the tribal rites Harutt mentions?",a="The text does not name the rites. It says every tribal member, from warriors and hunters to shamans and druids, must undergo them to prove strength, humility, and dedication to their people.",al={"tauren rites of passage","what rites do tauren take"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren beginning in Red Cloud Mesa, the note shows that protecting the tribe matters as much as spiritual callings such as shamanism and druidism.",["class:Warrior"]="Harutt Thunderhorn teaches novice tauren warriors to temper their pride and defend Camp Narache from centaur."},kw={"camp narache","centaur","harutt thunderhorn","mulgore","red cloud mesa","simple note","tauren","warrior"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","topic:centaur","topic:shaman","topic:druid"},m={id=3091},src={"In-game quest text: Simple Note"},fv=true}
E["quest:3095"] = {t="quest",n="Simple Scroll",z="general",s="A sealed scroll bearing Dannal Stern’s mark finds you while you deal with the Scourge. It calls you to Deathknell, where Dannal speaks of your freedom and offers to teach you.",h="A sealed scroll bearing Dannal Stern’s mark finds you while you deal with the Scourge.",sec={{t="A Sealed Message",b="While you were away dealing with the mindless Scourge, a scroll arrived for you. Its seal bears Dannal Stern’s mark. He is the warrior trainer in Deathknell, and the message asks you to read it and speak with him before setting out again.",sp=0},{t="Freedom and Training",b="Dannal says others escaped death and fled to town. He sees promise in their resilience. He credits Sylvanas with freeing you in body and spirit, though the plague still afflicts you. He offers to teach you as you gain experience. Sylvanas will need people of your class to help save the Forsaken.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sent me the scroll?",a="The scroll bears Dannal Stern’s seal. He is the warrior trainer in Deathknell.",al={"scroll sender","who sent this","Dannal scroll"}},{q="Why did the scroll arrive?",a="The scroll asks you to read it and speak with Dannal Stern in Deathknell.",al={"why scroll","what's this scroll for","simple scroll"}},{q="What does Dannal say about Sylvanas?",a="Dannal credits Sylvanas with freeing you in body and spirit, though the plague still afflicts you.",al={"Sylvanas freedom","what did Sylvanas do","Sylvanas and me"}},{q="What does Dannal offer?",a="Dannal offers to teach you as you gain experience and invites you to seek him out.",al={"Dannal teaching","can Dannal train me","warrior trainer"}},{q="What does Sylvanas need from me?",a="Dannal says Sylvanas will need people of your class to help save the Forsaken.",al={"Sylvanas needs","my class role","Forsaken future"}}},kw={"dannal stern","deathknell","einfache rolle","forsaken","scourge","simple scroll","sylvanas","training"},rel={"subzone:deathknell"},m={id=3095},src={"In-game quest text: Einfache Rolle"},fv=true}
E["quest:3099"] = {t="quest",n="Tainted Scroll",z="general",s="The Tainted Scroll is an early quest in Deathknell that directs newly awakened Forsaken warlocks to their trainer, Maximillion. Through this missive, Maximillion invites the initiate to embrace power and dark servitude as a means of securing genuine freedom.",h="The Tainted Scroll is an early quest in Deathknell that directs newly awakened Forsaken warlocks to their...",sec={{t="A Dark Summons",b="Shortly after newly risen Forsaken begin clearing out mindless Scourge remnants around Deathknell, an ominous missive arrives bearing the seal of Maximillion, the local warlock trainer. The scroll beckons the recipient to contemplate the nature of servitude and autonomy before returning to the ruined settlement to speak with him directly.",sp=0},{t="The Philosophy of Control",b="Upon answering the summons, Maximillion reveals his philosophy on power and subjugation. Having broken free from the Lich King's mindless grasp, he argues that a slave remains a slave regardless of the master unless they seize true authority. In his eyes, mastering demonic servants is not treachery against Sylvanas, but rather the natural culmination of the Forsaken struggle for self-determination and respect.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who sent the Tainted Scroll?",a="The scroll is delivered bearing the personal seal of Maximillion, the warlock trainer stationed in Deathknell, who seeks out newly awakened practitioners of the dark arts.",al={"who sends tainted scroll","who gave me tainted scroll","tainted scroll sender"}},{q="Who is Maximillion?",a="Maximillion is the warlock trainer in Deathknell who instructs newly raised Forsaken in the dark arts of summoning and controlling demonic entities.",al={"who is maximillion","maximillion deathknell","warlock trainer deathknell"}},{q="What is written on the Tainted Scroll?",a="The scroll is a missive sent to newly awakened warlocks, reflecting on freedom, power, and the nature of servitude, urging the reader to visit Maximillion for deeper instruction.",al={"what is tainted scroll","what does scroll say","scroll message"}},{q="Is Maximillion a traitor to Lady Sylvanas?",a="Maximillion explicitly states he is no traitor to Lady Sylvanas. He claims the Banshee Queen would appreciate his perspective, arguing her own pursuit of self-determination broke their Scourge shackles.",al={"is maximillion a traitor","does maximillion betray sylvanas","maximillion loyalty"}},{q="Where can I find Maximillion?",a="Maximillion resides in Deathknell, the ruined settlement in Tirisfal Glades where newly risen Forsaken first emerge from their graves.",al={"where to find maximillion","maximillion location"}},{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="The quest serves as the introductory calling for newly raised Forsaken warlocks, initiating them into their class training and guiding them to their local mentor in Deathknell.",al={"why warlock quest tainted scroll","tainted scroll purpose"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="Maximillion initiates newly awakened Forsaken warlocks in Deathknell, framing demonology and subjugation as the ultimate assertion of mastery and freedom.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken warlocks draw a dark parallel between breaking free from the Scourge and seeking out demonic servants to command for their own survival."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken","maximillion","scourge","sylvanas","tainted scroll","tirisfal glades","warlock"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},m={id=3099},src={"In-game quest text: Tainted Scroll"},fv=true}
E["quest:3100"] = {t="quest",n="Simple Letter",z="general",s="The Simple Letter is an introductory directive that guides novice warriors to trainer Llane Beshere at Northshire Abbey. It marks the beginning of their formal martial instruction and urges them to assist the troubled citizens of the valley.",h="The Simple Letter is an introductory directive that guides novice warriors to trainer Llane Beshere at...",sec={{t="A Call to Arms",b="The Simple Letter serves as an initial introduction for aspiring warriors arriving in Northshire Valley. Directed to Llane Beshere, the resident warrior trainer at Northshire Abbey, the letter prompts newly arrived fighters to make contact with their instructor, familiarize themselves with their surroundings, and prepare for the demands of martial life.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Valley",b="Although Northshire Abbey enjoys protection under the watchful presence of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the peaceful facade of the valley belies deeper trouble. As Beshere notes upon receiving the letter, the surrounding residents face numerous plights that require able-bodied protectors, urging fresh recruits to master their weapons and step forward in defense of the realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Simple Letter?",a="The Simple Letter is an introductory missive handed to warriors in Northshire Valley once they return from the kobold camps. It directs them to introduce themselves to the local warrior trainer, Llane Beshere, to begin their formal martial training.",al={"what is simple letter","simple letter quest","starter letter human warrior"}},{q="Who is Llane Beshere?",a="Llane Beshere is the warrior trainer stationed in Northshire Abbey. He instructs novice warriors in combat techniques, welcoming recruits who arrive in the valley bearing orders or recommendations to seek him out.",al={"who is llane beshere","beshere location","where is warrior trainer northshire"}},{q="Where do I deliver the Simple Letter?",a="The letter is brought directly to Llane Beshere, who can be found in Northshire Abbey within Northshire Valley, surrounded by the sheltered grounds protected by the holy order.",al={"where to turn in simple letter","simple letter hand in","llane beshere location"}},{q="What role do the Knights of the Silver Hand play in Northshire?",a="The Knights of the Silver Hand help guard Northshire Valley, keeping the abbey and its immediate perimeter relatively secure so that recruits, farmers, and clergy can go about their duties despite growing local tensions.",al={"what are paladins doing in northshire","silver hand northshire abbey","paladins elwynn"}},{q="Why are the local citizens in need of assistance?",a="While the abbey itself remains fairly defended, local residents face constant encroachments and shortages. Beshere advises new arrivals to explore the surrounding land and lend their martial strength to those in need.",al={"why does beshere want me to help citizens","northshire problems","who needs help northshire"}},{q="When should I return to Llane Beshere after delivering the letter?",a="Llane Beshere remains available at the abbey day and night, ready to instruct warriors as they gain combat experience and require advanced martial skills.",al={"when should i return to llane beshere","more warrior training northshire","beshere training"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Llane Beshere serves as the resident warrior trainer at Northshire Abbey, making this simple letter the initial summons to martial discipline for newly arrived fighters.",["race:Human"]="Northshire Abbey is the cradle of human adventurers setting forth into Elwynn Forest, where local recruits receive their opening guidance.",["faction:Alliance"]="Even within the relative sanctuary protected by the Silver Hand, fresh recruits are immediately called upon to aid the troubled citizens of the Alliance."},kw={"elwynn forest","knights of the silver hand","llane beshere","northshire abbey","northshire valley","simple letter","warrior trainer"},rel={"subzone:northshire-abbey","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand"},m={id=3100},src={"In-game quest text: Simple Letter"},fv=true}
E["quest:3101"] = {t="quest",n="Consecrated Letter",z="general",s="The Consecrated Letter is an introductory summons carried by initiates of the Holy Light arriving in Northshire Valley. It directs the bearer to report to their class trainer at Northshire Abbey to begin their training and embrace their role as a visible symbol of faith.",h="The Consecrated Letter is an introductory summons carried by initiates of the Holy Light arriving in...",sec={{t="A Sacred Calling",b="The Consecrated Letter marks the formal beginning of an initiate's journey in Northshire Valley. Addressed to newly arrived wielders of the Holy Light, the document calls the bearer to seek out their trainer at Northshire Abbey to formalize their instruction and accept their responsibilities within the Kingdom of Stormwind.",sp=0},{t="Carrying the Light",b="Upon presenting the letter, the initiate is reminded of the weight carried by their vocation. The Holy Light shines visibly within those who channel it, making them immediate symbols of hope to allies and clear targets for their adversaries. The trainer emphasizes that such power demands disciplined conduct and an awareness of one's public example.",sp=0},{t="Guidance and Discipline",b="Beyond spiritual obligations, the letter establishes an ongoing relationship between student and mentor. As the initiate tests their mettle against the local threats confronting Northshire, they are expected to return regularly to the abbey grounds to unlock deeper understandings of combat and divine grace.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Consecrated Letter?",a="The Consecrated Letter is an introductory summons directed to an initiate of the Holy Light in Northshire Valley. It directs the bearer to their class trainer at Northshire Abbey to establish their training and remind them of their sacred duties.",al={"what is consecrated letter","consecrated letter quest"}},{q="Where does the Consecrated Letter come from?",a="Once a new paladin returns from the kobold camps, someone at Northshire Abbey hands over the letter, which is sealed with Brother Sammuel's insignia and tells the paladin to come see him.",al={"who gives consecrated letter","who sends consecrated letter"}},{q="Who receives the Consecrated Letter?",a="The letter is delivered to Brother Sammuel, the paladin trainer stationed near the entrance of Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest.",al={"who turns in consecrated letter","who reads consecrated letter"}},{q="What role does the trainer play after reading the letter?",a="The trainer serves as a mentor in the ways of the Holy Light, instructing the initiate in martial techniques, defensive blessings, and sacred spells as they gain practical combat experience in the valley.",al={"what does the trainer teach","why train with the trainer"}},{q="Why does the letter mention being a symbol?",a="Those who channel the Holy Light are seen as visible paragons of faith and virtue. Both friends and foes recognize the power of the Light, making its wielders prominent figures whose conduct reflects upon the faith itself.",al={"why am i a symbol","holy light symbol"}},{q="Where does this training take place?",a="Northshire Abbey lies in the peaceful northern valley of Elwynn Forest, serving as a sanctuary of faith and the primary recruitment grounds for defenders of the Kingdom of Stormwind.",al={"where is northshire abbey","where do i take the letter"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As wielders of the Holy Light, paladins are charged with representing its virtues and serving as beacons of hope to their allies in Northshire and beyond.",["race:Human"]="For humans beginning their service at Northshire Abbey, the letter emphasizes their responsibility to act as honorable symbols in a Kingdom troubled by recent trials.",["faction:Alliance"]="Representatives of the Holy Light are expected to conduct themselves with honor, upholding the moral standing and defense of the Alliance."},kw={"brother sammuel","consecrated letter","elwynn forest","holy light","northshire","northshire abbey","paladin","trainer"},rel={"topic:light"},m={id=3101},src={"In-game quest text: Consecrated Letter"},fv=true}
E["quest:3102"] = {t="quest",n="Encrypted Letter",z="general",s="At Northshire Abbey, Jorik Kerridan summons promising rogues to meet him in secret. His letter points them toward a quiet stable and counsel for the dangers ahead.",h="At Northshire Abbey, Jorik Kerridan summons promising rogues to meet him in secret.",sec={{t="A Discreet Summons",b="Northshire Abbey looks peaceful, yet covert talents are recruited within its walls. After dealing with the kobolds, promising novices receive a letter bearing Jorik Kerridan's seal. It is addressed discreetly, away from the priests and paladins. The bearer is sent to a secluded meeting in the stables behind the Abbey.",sp=0},{t="Shadows in Northshire",b="Jorik Kerridan keeps to the stables because they are quiet and draw little notice. When newcomers arrive unseen, he speaks plainly about the wider world. Stormwind's SI:7 and the Defias Brotherhood both seek stealthy operatives. Jorik urges each novice to keep their freedom and resist powerful groups that would push them around. A clever rogue holds the cards.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sent the Encrypted Letter?",a="Jorik Kerridan sent the letter, which bears his personal insignia. Promising novices receive it after scouting nearby kobold camps.",al={"who sent encrypted letter","who gave me encrypted letter","who wrote the encrypted letter"}},{q="Where is Jorik Kerridan located?",a="Jorik Kerridan stays in the quiet stable behind Northshire Abbey. He keeps out of sight while tending to his covert work.",al={"where is jorik","where to find jorik kerridan","jorik location"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Encrypted Letter?",a="The letter discreetly invites rising rogues to meet their local trainer. Its encrypted words keep training matters from prying eyes around the Abbey.",al={"why encrypted letter","what is encrypted letter for","why is letter encrypted"}},{q="Which organizations are looking to recruit rogues?",a="Jorik Kerridan says many factions seek skilled rogues, including Stormwind's covert agency SI:7 and the Defias Brotherhood. The Defias seek spies inside Stormwind.",al={"who wants rogues","factions interested in rogues","who covets rogue skills"}},{q="What advice does Jorik Kerridan give?",a="Jorik urges you to remain your own person and refuse to be bullied into work against your will. Rogues hold the cards before the game is done.",al={"jorik advice","what does jorik tell you","rogue advice jorik"}},{q="Why does Jorik Kerridan stay in the stable?",a="Jorik Kerridan stays in the quiet stable behind Northshire Abbey because it draws little attention. There, trainers and operatives can meet beyond the notice of the Abbey's clergy and guards.",al={"why hide in stables","why is jorik behind abbey","why quiet in stables"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you meet Jorik Kerridan and hear his counsel on dealing with SI:7 and the Defias Brotherhood while keeping your independence.",["faction:Alliance"]="Even within Northshire Abbey, Stormwind's shadow operatives recruit and train promising agents for the conflicts ahead."},kw={"defias brotherhood","encrypted letter","jorik kerridan","northshire abbey","northshire valley","rogue trainer","si:7"},rel={"subzone:northshire-abbey","quest:2300","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:kobold","quest:65"},m={id=3102},src={"In-game quest text: Encrypted Letter"},fv=true}
E["quest:320"] = {t="quest",n="Return to Bellowfiz",z="general",s="Return to Bellowfiz is a short delivery quest in Dun Morogh where an adventurer brings a cask of local Evershine to Pilot Bellowfiz at Steelgrill's Depot. It concludes a local favor, rewarding the thirsty driver with a welcome drink.",h="Return to Bellowfiz is a short delivery quest in Dun Morogh where an adventurer brings a cask of local...",sec={{t="A Cask for the Depot",b="Return to Bellowfiz is an errand in the snowy reaches of Dun Morogh. After aiding with local hunting, an adventurer receives a freshly brewed cask of Evershine with instructions to carry it straight to Pilot Bellowfiz at Steelgrill's Depot. It is a straightforward favor that highlights the informal camaraderie and mutual support that keep the cold frontier running.",sp=0},{t="Thirsty Aviators",b="Steelgrill's Depot serves as a busy center of mechanical testing, vehicle maintenance, and gnomish craft just outside Kharanos. For pilots stationed out in the freezing winds, nothing hits the spot quite like a draught of strong local drink. Pilot Bellowfiz wastes no time upon delivery, eager to uncork a bottle on the spot and enjoy the fruits of local labor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Return to Bellowfiz?",a="Return to Bellowfiz is a delivery task in Dun Morogh where an adventurer brings a freshly acquired cask of Evershine directly to Pilot Bellowfiz at Steelgrill's Depot.",al={"what is return to bellowfiz","bellowfiz quest"}},{q="Who is Pilot Bellowfiz?",a="Pilot Bellowfiz is a dwarven driver of a siege engine, stationed at Steelgrill's Depot just outside Kharanos in Dun Morogh. He eagerly awaits a fresh delivery of Evershine to sample.",al={"who wants evershine","who is bellowfiz"}},{q="What is the cask of Evershine?",a="Evershine is a potent local brew crafted in Dun Morogh, secured in a sturdy cask and prized by thirsty pilots and locals alike.",al={"what is evershine","cask of evershine"}},{q="Where do you deliver the cask?",a="Pilot Bellowfiz is located at Steelgrill's Depot, an engineering hub and workshop situated just east of Kharanos in Dun Morogh.",al={"where is bellowfiz","bellowfiz location"}},{q="Why was the Evershine sent to Bellowfiz?",a="After helping out with hunting tasks in the region, an adventurer is entrusted with a cask of Evershine to deliver to Pilot Bellowfiz, who has been eagerly anticipating a taste.",al={"why deliver evershine","evershine delivery reason"}},{q="What happens when Bellowfiz receives the Evershine?",a="Upon receiving the cask, Pilot Bellowfiz is thoroughly satisfied, declaring that it will do just fine and immediately moving to crack open a bottle for a taste.",al={"what does bellowfiz do with brew","bellowfiz reaction"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="A stout dwarven brew like Evershine is always in high demand across Dun Morogh, making its safe delivery to thirsty comrades second nature.",["race:Gnome"]="Pilot Bellowfiz is a dwarven siege-engine driver stationed at Steelgrill's Depot, and keeping hardworking engineers refreshed keeps the machines running smoothly.",["faction:Alliance"]="Running errands between dwarven hunters and dwarven pilots helps maintain morale and camaraderie among Alliance forces stationed in Dun Morogh."},kw={"cask of evershine","dun morogh","evershine","kharanos","pilot bellowfiz","return to bellowfiz","steelgrill's depot"},rel={"npc:pilot-bellowfiz","subzone:steelgrill-s-depot","quest:318"},m={id=320},src={"In-game quest text: Return to Bellowfiz"},fv=true}
E["quest:34"] = {t="quest",n="An Unwelcome Guest",z="general",s="An Unwelcome Guest is a local task in Lakeshire where resident Martie Jainrose seeks retribution against Bellygrub, a destructive wild boar. The beast has rampaged through her garden and trampled her seasonal daffodils, prompting a bounty on the creature's tusk.",h="An Unwelcome Guest is a local task in Lakeshire where resident Martie Jainrose seeks retribution against...",sec={{t="Trouble in the Garden",b="In the lakeside town of Lakeshire, Martie Jainrose tends to her domestic grounds amidst the gathering shadows over the Redridge Mountains. Her quiet routine is shattered by a persistent local terror: a notoriously aggressive wild boar that has repeatedly invaded her garden. Having trampled her prized patch of daffodils beyond recovery for the season, the distraught resident turns to capable travelers to put an end to the rampaging animal.",sp=0},{t="The Menace of Bellygrub",b="The beast responsible is known to the locals as Bellygrub, an apt title coined by Lakeshire's town guard leader, Marshal Marris. Bellygrub spends the bulk of his time rooting and foraging through the brush in the fields southwest of the town. However, his voracious appetite and boldness often draw him dangerously close to civilized homes, culminating in the complete ruin of Martie's floral beds and demanding his definitive removal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest An Unwelcome Guest?",a="Martie Jainrose offers the quest in the lakeside town of Lakeshire within the Redridge Mountains. Distressed by the constant destruction of her floral garden, she asks passing adventurers to hunt down the culprit.",al={"who gives an unwelcome guest","where to get an unwelcome guest","martie jainrose quest"}},{q="Who or what is Bellygrub?",a="Bellygrub is an aggressive, destructive boar known to terrorize the local grounds around Lakeshire. The town's local guard leader, Marshal Marris, originally bestowed the beast with its moniker.",al={"who is bellygrub","what is bellygrub","why is bellygrub called that"}},{q="Where can Bellygrub be found?",a="Bellygrub spends most of his time foraging in the open fields southwest of Lakeshire, though he frequently wanders closer to town borders and into resident gardens.",al={"bellygrub location","where is bellygrub","find bellygrub","where does bellygrub spawn"}},{q="Why does Martie Jainrose want Bellygrub killed?",a="Bellygrub repeatedly trampled and pillaged Martie Jainrose's carefully tended garden, entirely ruining her daffodils so that none can be replanted until next season. Fed up with the recurring damage, she wants him eliminated.",al={"why does martie want bellygrub dead","why kill bellygrub","what did bellygrub do"}},{q="What proof is needed to complete the quest?",a="Martie Jainrose requires the tusk of Bellygrub as proof that the menacing beast has finally been dealt with before she will reward you.",al={"bellygrub proof","unwelcome guest turn in","what to bring martie jainrose"}},{q="Who is Marshal Marris and what is his connection to the beast?",a="Marshal Marris is the local marshal stationed in Lakeshire. While Martie Jainrose seeks personal vengeance for her ruined daffodils, Marris is the one who named the nuisance boar Bellygrub.",al={"who is marshal marris","marshal marris bellygrub"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Helping Martie Jainrose protect her home and garden brings a small measure of order and relief to the beleaguered town of Lakeshire, where townspeople are already strained by larger threats in Redridge.",["class:Hunter"]="Bellygrub is a notorious named beast roaming southwest of Lakeshire, making him a prominent tracking and hunting challenge for any marksman passing through Redridge Mountains.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Martie Jainrose's pride was her cultivated floral patch, and Bellygrub's destructive rampage ruined her seasonal daffodils, striking at the heart of Redridge's delicate domestic plant life."},kw={"an unwelcome guest","bellygrub","boar","daffodils","lakeshire","marshal marris","martie jainrose","redridge","redridge mountains"},rel={"npc:martie-jainrose","subzone:lakeshire","npc:marshal-marris"},m={id=34},src={"In-game quest text: An Unwelcome Guest"},fv=true}
E["quest:359"] = {t="quest",n="Forsaken Duties",z="general",s="Forsaken Duties is a reconnaissance mission assigned by Magistrate Sevren in Brill to assess Scourge activity along the eastern perimeter of Tirisfal Glades. It establishes the constant threat posed by the Scourge slipping past The Bulwark and endangering local settlements.",h="Forsaken Duties is a reconnaissance mission assigned by Magistrate Sevren in Brill to assess Scourge activity...",sec={{t="Vigilance on the Border",b="Magistrate Sevren oversees the security of Brill and the surrounding countryside, keeping a nervous eye on the eastern borders of Tirisfal Glades. With the Scourge swarming across the Western Plaguelands, Sevren requires constant reconnaissance from frontline sentries to ensure undead horrors do not slip past the perimeter unnoticed. Travelers willing to assist are dispatched south and east down the main thoroughfare to gather an urgent tactical assessment from Deathguard Linnea.",sp=0},{t="Reports from the Front",b="Stationed at an eastern outpost along the road, Deathguard Linnea observes the frontier firsthand. Her findings confirm the magistrates fears: while the fortified position at The Bulwark successfully holds off the main Scourge host, small groups of undead manage to bypass the defenses on occasion. These stray forces have begun congregating around the nearby Balnir Farmstead, posing a localized but dangerous threat to Tirisfal Glades.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Forsaken Duties?",a="Magistrate Sevren, stationed in Brill, tasks travelers with retrieving an updated reconnaissance report from the eastern Deathguard outpost.",al={"who gives forsaken duties","who starts forsaken duties","sevren quest"}},{q="Who is Deathguard Linnea?",a="Deathguard Linnea is stationed at the Deathguard waystation east of Brill, monitoring movements along the road and guarding the approaches toward the Plaguelands.",al={"who is linnea","deathguard linnea location","where is linnea"}},{q="What is The Bulwark?",a="The Bulwark is the fortified border outpost separating Tirisfal Glades from the Western Plaguelands, manned to prevent the Scourge from overrunning Forsaken lands.",al={"what is the bulwark","bulwark defense","why is the bulwark important"}},{q="Where are Scourge slipping past the border?",a="While defenders at The Bulwark hold back the bulk of the undead army, stray Scourge forces slip through and gather around the Balnir Farmstead east of the waystation.",al={"scourge in tirisfal","balnir farmstead scourge","where are the scourge leaking through"}},{q="Why does Magistrate Sevren want a status report?",a="Magistrate Sevren needs to ensure that the Scourge cannot establish a strong foothold within Tirisfal Glades, demanding constant vigilance from the Deathguard.",al={"why does sevren need a report","sevren concern"}},{q="How do you get to Deathguard Linnea's post?",a="To reach the waystation from Brill, travelers follow the road south and turn east at the fork toward the eastern reaches of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is the waystation","how to reach linnea"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Maintaining vigilance against the Scourge is vital for the Forsaken, as any breach along the border directly threatens Brill and the newly claimed Undercity.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Tirisfal Glades against Scourge incursions ensures the Horde maintains a reliable foothold in northern Lordaeron."},kw={"balnir farmstead","brill","deathguard linnea","forsaken duties","magistrate sevren","scourge","the bulwark","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:magistrate-sevren","npc:deathguard-linnea","subzone:bulwark","subzone:balnir-farmstead","topic:scourge","quest:1882"},m={id=359},src={"In-game quest text: Forsaken Duties"},fv=true}
E["quest:3633"] = {t="quest",n="Goblin Engineering",z="general",s="Among master inventors, Goblin Engineering offers a path to command explosive power and volatile concussive ordnance. Its craft relies on high-yield blasting powders and relentless firepower, making it a destructive choice beside intricate tinkering.",h="Among master inventors, Goblin Engineering offers a path to command explosive power and volatile concussive...",sec={{t="The Art of the Detonation",b="Azeroth's engineers follow two distinct philosophies. Goblin tinkerers favor violent combustion, while other inventors make delicate clockwork and subtle devices. Goblins harness blasting powder to build tools of great devastation, turning fire, shrapnel, and concussive force toward practical problems.",sp=0},{t="Volatility and Power",b="Goblin engineers make no secret of the hazards. Recruits work with volatile compounds, heavy incendiaries, and prototypes that may singe their creator's eyebrows or level an opposing barricade. They prize maximum yield and destructive power, and welcome engineers who choose decisive firepower over fragile safety rules.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Goblin Engineering?",a="Goblin Engineering is a discipline devoted to high-yield explosions. Its practitioners craft powerful explosives, bombs, and heavy destructive devices instead of the intricate gadgets favored by gnomish engineers.",al={"what is goblin engineering","goblin spec","goblin engineering quest"}},{q="How does goblin engineering differ from gnomish engineering?",a="Gnomish engineering favors strange utility devices, complex tinkering, and inventive gadgets. Goblin engineering puts firepower, blasting powders, and destructive yield first. Goblin inventions are aggressive and effective, though hazardous to handle.",al={"goblin vs gnome engineering","gnomish or goblin","difference goblin gnome engineering"}},{q="Who teaches the goblin engineering discipline?",a="Master goblin engineers teach the discipline to recruits willing to work with explosions and blasting powder. Master Engineer Nixx Sprocketspring in Gadgetzan is a prominent trainer who tests candidates before teaching them the trade.",al={"who teaches goblin engineering","where to learn goblin engineering","goblin engineering trainer"}},{q="Is goblin engineering dangerous to practice?",a="Goblin inventors accept danger as part of the craft. Their designs maximize explosive output and concussive force, bringing unpredictable malfunctions, sudden detonations, and personal risk to the artisan handling them.",al={"goblin engineering danger","do goblin explosives blow up","is goblin engineering safe"}},{q="Can both factions learn goblin engineering?",a="Engineers of both the Alliance and Horde can learn goblin engineering once they are sufficiently skilled. Goblin tinkerers work mainly through neutral cartels such as the Steamwheedle Cartel, which welcomes anyone with steady hands, enough coin, and a love of explosive force.",al={"can anyone learn goblin engineering","alliance goblin engineering","horde goblin engineering"}},{q="Why would an engineer choose the goblin discipline?",a="Engineers choose goblin engineering for its unmatched destructive capability. It produces lethal combat explosives, mortar guns, land mines, and portable rockets made to obliterate opposition on the battlefield.",al={"why pick goblin engineering","what does goblin engineering make","goblin engineering items"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Goblin engineering is one of the two major specialized traditions open to master engineers. It focuses on volatile munitions, heavy explosive yield, and catastrophic destruction.",["profession:Mining"]="Engineers who pursue goblin craft rely heavily on refined ores, blasting powders, and solid stones to make incendiaries and bombs."},kw={"blasting powder","bombs","engineering","explosives","gadgetzan","goblin engineering","nixx sprocketspring","steamwheedle cartel"},m={id=3633},src={"In-game quest text: Goblin Engineering"},fv=true}
E["quest:416"] = {t="quest",n="Rat Catching",z="general",s="In the western foothills, tunnel rat kobolds trouble the roads into Thelsamar. Mountaineer Kadrell calls on travelers to drive them back and bring proof of the cull for a bounty.",h="In the western foothills, tunnel rat kobolds trouble the roads into Thelsamar.",sec={{t="Vermin in the Foothills",b="West of Thelsamar, tunnel rat kobolds have made dens in the foothills of Loch Modan. They foul the surrounding ground and trouble the roads leading into town. Their warrens threaten the safety and cleanliness of those roads.",sp=0},{t="A Mountaineer's Bounty",b="Mountaineer Kadrell has set a bounty on the tunnel rat kobolds. He asks passing travelers to bring their severed ears as proof of the cull. Then he pays coin and compensation, though the trophies are foul to inspect.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the Rat Catching quest?",a="Mountaineer Kadrell offers the bounty in Thelsamar. He asks travelers to cull the vermin coming from the nearby mountainside.",al={"who gives rat catching","where to get rat catching","kadrell quest"}},{q="What are tunnel rat kobolds?",a="Tunnel rat kobolds are a breed of kobolds that have dug dens in the western foothills near Thelsamar. They foul the mountainside.",al={"what are tunnel rats","kobolds in loch modan","tunnel rat kobold"}},{q="Where can the tunnel rat kobolds be found?",a="Tunnel rat kobolds and their dens lie directly west of Thelsamar, scattered along the mountainside and foothills.",al={"where are the tunnel rats","rat catching location","dens west of thelsamar"}},{q="Why does Kadrell ask for Tunnel Rat Ears?",a="Mountaineer Kadrell asks for tunnel rat ears as proof of the kill before paying the town's bounty.",al={"why collect ears","why does kadrell want ears","proof of kill loch modan"}},{q="Why is the Mountaineer concerned about the kobold infestation?",a="The kobolds have moved into the foothills outside Thelsamar, where they make dens and foul the surrounding dwarven lands. Kadrell wants them driven out to secure the area.",al={"why clear tunnel rats","thelsamar rat problem","why kill kobolds in loch modan"}},{q="Does Rat Catching lead to a larger questline?",a="This is a local bounty to clear pests around Thelsamar. Kadrell pays once the ears are delivered.",al={"does rat catching have follow up","rat catching chain","quest after rat catching"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="As defenders of Khaz Modan, Dwarves have a direct stake in keeping the foothills around Thelsamar clear of vermin and subterranean invaders.",["faction:Alliance"]="Mountaineer Kadrell speaks for Loch Modan's local dwarven authorities. He offers bounties to Alliance travelers willing to safeguard their settlements."},kw={"bounty","kobold","loch modan","mountaineer kadrell","rat catching","thelsamar","tunnel rat","tunnel rat ears"},rel={"npc:mountaineer-kadrell","subzone:thelsamar","topic:kobold"},m={id=416},src={"In-game quest text: Rat Catching"},fv=true}
E["quest:490"] = {t="quest",n="Bounty: Gnarlpine Furbolg",z="general",s="Bounty: Gnarlpine Furbolg is an Alliance quest authorized by the Sentinels to curb the aggressive furbolg population corrupting Teldrassil. Shayla Nightbreeze recruits adventurers outside Darnassus to hunt the corrupted beasts and collect their fangs as proof of deed.",h="Bounty: Gnarlpine Furbolg is an Alliance quest authorized by the Sentinels to curb the aggressive furbolg...",sec={{t="A Decree from the Sentinels",b="On the road outside Darnassus, Shayla Nightbreeze acts on direct orders from the Sentinels to place a bounty on the Gnarlpine furbolg. Once peaceful inhabitants of the ancient forests, the Gnarlpine have succumbed to a spreading corruption that stripped away their reason, turning them into vicious threats to the safety of travelers and the natural sanctity of Teldrassil.",sp=0},{t="Purging the Encampments",b="To curb the Gnarlpine aggression, hunters are dispatched across the boughs of the World Tree to cull their ranks and collect their fangs as proof. The bulk of their corrupted population resides in two major strongholds: Ban'ethil Hollow, set south of the road west of Dolanaar, and the ruins of Starbreeze Village toward the east.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the bounty on the Gnarlpine furbolg?",a="Shayla Nightbreeze commissions this bounty. She is stationed along the road leading outside the capital city of Darnassus on Teldrassil.",al={"who gives bounty gnarlpine furbolg","where to get gnarlpine bounty","shayla nightbreeze location"}},{q="Why are the Gnarlpine furbolg being hunted?",a="The Gnarlpine furbolg have succumbed to corruption, turning them into mindless and aggressive threats to travelers, night elf settlements, and the natural wildlife of Teldrassil.",al={"why kill gnarlpine","why are gnarlpine hostile","what happened to gnarlpine furbolg"}},{q="Where can the Gnarlpine furbolg be found?",a="Major Gnarlpine concentrations can be found at Ban'ethil Hollow, located west of Dolanaar and south of the main path, as well as in Starbreeze Village further to the east.",al={"gnarlpine furbolg locations","where are gnarlpine on teldrassil","where to find furbolg"}},{q="What proof is required to claim the bounty?",a="Shayla Nightbreeze requires adventurers to collect and present Gnarlpine fangs as physical proof of removing the furbolg threats from the boughs of the World Tree.",al={"proof of bounty gnarlpine","what proof shayla wants","gnarlpine fangs proof"}},{q="Who authorized the bounty on the Gnarlpine?",a="The bounty was commissioned under strict orders decreed directly by the Sentinels, the military protectors of the night elves, to secure the boughs of Teldrassil.",al={"who ordered gnarlpine bounty","who authorized furbolg bounty"}},{q="Were the Gnarlpine always hostile to the night elves?",a="Historically, furbolgs were allies and friends of the forest who coexisted peacefully with the night elves, but corruption has severed their peaceful nature and twisted their minds.",al={"were furbolgs always enemies","did furbolg used to be friendly"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Gnarlpine were once peaceful neighbors and allies to the Kaldorei, making their fall to corruption and the resulting bounty a tragic necessity for the defense of Teldrassil.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Sentinels are actively securing the night elf homeland from internal corruption, calling on Alliance adventurers to purge dangerous furbolg encampments across the boughs.",["class:Druid"]="Watching the furbolg lose their harmony with nature to become hostile beasts threatens the balance of the glade that druids are sworn to preserve."},kw={"ban'ethil hollow","bounty","corruption","darnassus","furbolg","gnarlpine","gnarlpine fangs","sentinels","shayla nightbreeze","starbreeze village","teldrassil"},rel={"topic:furbolg","topic:sentinels","zone:darnassus","subzone:ban-ethil-hollow","subzone:starbreeze-village","subzone:dolanaar"},m={id=490},src={"In-game quest text: Bounty: Gnarlpine Furbolg"},fv=true}
E["quest:4921"] = {t="quest",n="Lost in Battle",z="general",s="At the Crossroads, Mankrik searches for his wife, lost in a Bristleback ambush at a tauren camp. Wounded and unable to look for her himself, he asks travelers to search the Barrens for any sign of her.",h="At the Crossroads, Mankrik searches for his wife, lost in a Bristleback ambush at a tauren camp.",sec={{t="Ambush at the Camp",b="Mankrik, an orc warrior at the Crossroads, fought beside his wife at a small tauren camp. Bristleback quilboar suddenly swarmed them. His wife held off three attackers alone, but fresh reinforcements cut her off from Mankrik in the fight.",sp=0},{t="A Desperate Search",b="Mankrik faced the advancing quilboar in a blind rage, but their numbers soon brought him down. A passing tauren druid found him near death on the Gold Road and tended his wounds. Still too injured to search for his wife, Mankrik asks travelers passing through the Crossroads to look across the Barrens and bring back any sign of her.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Mankrik?",a="Mankrik is an orc warrior recovering at the Crossroads in the Barrens. Quilboar separated him from his wife, and he asks travelers to search the scrublands for any sign of her.",al={"who is mankrik","who gives this quest","mankrik backstory"}},{q="What happened to Mankrik's wife during the battle?",a="Mankrik and his wife were fighting together at a small tauren camp when Bristleback quilboar ambushed them. She held off three attackers alone before fresh reinforcements overwhelmed her and separated her from Mankrik.",al={"what happened to mankriks wife","where is his wife","mankrik wife attack"}},{q="How did Mankrik survive the quilboar ambush?",a="A wandering tauren druid found Mankrik on the Gold Road after the quilboar overwhelmed him. The druid tended his life-threatening wounds and saved him.",al={"who saved mankrik","how did mankrik survive","druid on gold road"}},{q="Who attacked Mankrik and his wife?",a="Bristleback quilboar swarmed a small tauren camp and attacked Mankrik and his wife. Their numbers overcame them despite their fierce resistance.",al={"who attacked them","quilboar in the barrens","bristleback attackers"}},{q="Where should travelers look for Mankrik's wife?",a="Mankrik was found on the Gold Road after falling near a small tauren camp overrun by Bristlebacks. Travelers can search the quilboar-infested areas across the Barrens for his wife.",al={"where do i look for her","where to search for mankriks wife"}},{q="Where can Mankrik be found now?",a="Mankrik is recovering at the Crossroads in the Barrens. He waits for news or any sign of his missing wife.",al={"why crossroads","where is mankrik now"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Mankrik's plea shows the danger Horde settlers and patrols face from Bristleback quilboar while traveling through the Barrens.",["race:Orc"]="Mankrik fought fiercely in an orcish battle-rage to defend his mate, but the quilboar swarm overwhelmed him.",["race:Tauren"]="The ambush happened at a small tauren camp. A wandering tauren druid tended Mankrik's wounds along the Gold Road and saved his life.",["class:Druid"]="A tauren druid passing along the Gold Road found Mankrik as he collapsed. The druid tended his severe wounds and kept him alive."},kw={"bristleback","crossroads","gold road","lost in battle","mankrik","mankriks wife","quilboar","tauren camp"},rel={"npc:mankrik","subzone:crossroads","subzone:gold-road","topic:tauren","topic:druid"},m={id=4921},src={"In-game quest text: Lost in Battle"},fv=true}
E["quest:54"] = {t="quest",n="Report to Goldshire",z="general",s="Marshal McBride sends you south to Goldshire with dispatches for Marshal Dughan. Beyond Northshire's sheltered valley, Stormwind's nobles give Elwynn Forest less and less thought.",h="Marshal McBride sends you south to Goldshire with dispatches for Marshal Dughan.",sec={{t="From Northshire to the Greater Forest",b="You've proved yourself against Northshire's troubles, and McBride releases you from the valley's guard. His transfer papers send you past the border gates and down the road to Goldshire. From there, your duty reaches across the wider forest.",sp=0},{t="The Burden of the Marshals",b="Marshal Dughan waits in Goldshire to receive McBride's dispatches and make you an Acting Deputy of the Stormwind Marshals. His welcome carries a grim warning: beside the dangers roaming Elwynn, Northshire is a peaceful garden. Stormwind's royal army is bound up in distant nobles' politics, instead of guarding home ground.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Marshal Dughan?",a="Marshal Dughan commands the Stormwind Marshals in Goldshire. He oversees Elwynn Forest's defenses and patrols.",al={"who is marshal dughan","where is dughan","dughan role"}},{q="Why are recruits sent to Goldshire?",a="When recruits have dealt with trouble in Northshire Valley, Marshal McBride sends those who prove capable south to Goldshire. The dangers there stretch across all of Elwynn Forest.",al={"why go to goldshire","leaving northshire","next step after northshire"}},{q="What does Acting Deputy Status mean?",a="Acting Deputy is a field rank granted with Marshal McBride's papers. It brings promising recruits into the Stormwind Marshals to help keep the law.",al={"acting deputy","what is acting deputy status","deputy status meaning"}},{q="Where is the Stormwind army?",a="Marshal Dughan says most of Stormwind's standing army is away. The court nobles keep it busy with their duties instead of patrolling the towns and farms at home.",al={"where is stormwind army","stormwind army missing","why is elwynn unprotected"}},{q="Who is Marshal McBride?",a="Marshal McBride commands the garrison at Northshire Abbey. He trains new defenders and sends those who prove themselves to the front line at Goldshire.",al={"who is mcbride","mcbride role"}},{q="How do you find Goldshire from Northshire?",a="Follow the main road south out of Northshire Valley, past the border gates and into Elwynn Forest's heart. Goldshire lies along the road.",al={"goldshire road","how to get to goldshire","direction to goldshire"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="As a human, your move from Northshire to Goldshire is your first real recognition from Stormwind's military.",["faction:Alliance"]="With Stormwind's army sent elsewhere, Elwynn's defense rests more and more on newly deputized recruits and volunteers like you."},kw={"acting deputy","elwynn forest","goldshire","marshal dughan","marshal mcbride","northshire","report to goldshire","stormwind marshals"},rel={"npc:marshal-dughan","npc:marshal-mcbride","subzone:goldshire","zone:elwynn","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=54},src={"In-game quest text: Report to Goldshire"},fv=true}
E["quest:565"] = {t="quest",n="Bartolo's Yeti Fur Cloak",z="general",s="In Southshore, Bartolo Ginsetti gathers materials for Bartolo's Yeti Fur Cloak. The eccentric artisan claims his mantle is renowned throughout the Alliance and calls for tailoring supplies, a leatherworker's cloak, and raw yeti fur.",h="In Southshore, Bartolo Ginsetti gathers materials for Bartolo's Yeti Fur Cloak.",sec={{t="The Grand Tailor of Southshore",b="Travelers in coastal Southshore can commission a Yeti Fur Cloak from Bartolo Ginsetti. The artisan boasts that no finer garment exists anywhere on the planet. His work draws on materials gathered from the local wilderness and the skill of allied trades.",sp=0},{t="Components of the Craft",b="Bartolo needs more than a pelt and needle to make his cloak. Bring him a Bolt of Wool, Fine Thread, a Hillman's Cloak, and ten clumps of Yeti Fur from beasts roaming the foothills. With those goods in hand, he works the raw materials into his cold-weather mantle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Bartolo Ginsetti?",a="Bartolo Ginsetti is an eccentric, boastful craftsman in Southshore, in the Hillsbrad Foothills. He claims to have made an unrivaled, world-famous Yeti Fur Cloak.",al={"who is bartolo","bartolo ginsetti","who gives this quest"}},{q="What materials does Bartolo require to make the cloak?",a="Bartolo needs a Bolt of Wool, a Hillman's Cloak made by a leatherworker, Fine Thread, and ten clumps of Yeti Fur.",al={"cloak ingredients","what items for bartolo","yeti fur cloak materials"}},{q="Where does Bartolo make his cloaks?",a="Bartolo makes his cloaks in Southshore, a coastal human town and the main Alliance hub in the Hillsbrad Foothills. He welcomes travelers passing through the region.",al={"why southshore","where is southshore","where does this happen"}},{q="Where do adventurers obtain Yeti Fur in Hillsbrad?",a="Wild yetis in the foothills and mountains of Hillsbrad and Alterac carry clumps of Yeti Fur. Their coats are dense and insulating.",al={"where to get fur","which yetis","where do yeti furs drop"}},{q="How is the Hillman's Cloak acquired?",a="A skilled leatherworker patterns and stitches the Hillman's Cloak that Bartolo uses as his base garment. He adds wool, Fine Thread, and fur to it.",al={"who makes hillmans cloak","hillmans cloak craft","leatherworking cloak"}},{q="Why does Bartolo claim his cloak is world famous?",a="Bartolo says no finer cloak exists anywhere on the planet. He boasts that it is renowned throughout the Alliance and offers unmatched warmth, luxury, and prestige.",al={"why is the cloak famous","best cloak in the world","is bartolo's cloak good"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Bartolo boasts that his Yeti Fur Cloak is renowned throughout the Alliance. He offers his tailoring from Southshore, an Alliance stronghold.",["profession:Tailoring"]="The garment calls for a Bolt of Wool and Fine Thread, along with exotic beast pelts."},kw={"bartolo ginsetti","bolt of wool","fine thread","hillmans cloak","hillsbrad foothills","southshore","yeti fur","yeti fur cloak"},rel={"subzone:southshore","topic:alliance"},m={id=565},src={"In-game quest text: Bartolo's Yeti Fur Cloak"},fv=true}
E["quest:5925"] = {t="quest",n="Heeding the Call",z="general",s="Mathrengyl Bearwalker calls a young night elf druid to Darnassus to continue their training. As Archdruid Fandral Staghelm's direct assistant, he prepares the initiate for the trials of the bear.",h="Mathrengyl Bearwalker calls a young night elf druid to Darnassus to continue their training.",sec={{t="A Summons to Darnassus",b="As your basic instruction among Teldrassil's boughs comes to an end, word arrives that Mathrengyl Bearwalker wishes to speak with you. He is the direct assistant to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and one of the Cenarion Enclave's wisest elders. His summons calls you beyond basic wardenship and toward the deeper arts of nature.",sp=0},{t="The Path of the Bear",b="You travel to the Cenarion Enclave in Darnassus and climb to the second floor of Archdruid Staghelm's great tree. Bearwalker welcomes you and reminds you that those who answer Cenarius' call must honor plants and beasts as companions and charges. To preserve nature's balance, you need more than quiet reflection. You must learn the ways of the wild bear and master strength of body and heart.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Mathrengyl Bearwalker?",a="Mathrengyl Bearwalker is a wise druid and the direct assistant to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm. He oversees advanced instruction for promising druids in Darnassus.",al={"who wants to talk to me","who is mathrengyl","who is mathrengyl bearwalker"}},{q="Where is Mathrengyl Bearwalker located?",a="Mathrengyl Bearwalker is on the second floor of Fandral Staghelm's tree in the Cenarion Enclave in Darnassus. Senior members of the Cenarion Circle convene there on Teldrassil.",al={"where do i find bearwalker","where is mathrengyl","cenarion enclave location"}},{q="Why was I summoned to speak with Mathrengyl?",a="Your basic training is complete, and your path as a druid is ready to widen. Mathrengyl will guide you into nature's deeper disciplines.",al={"why am i being called","what is this quest for","why heed the call"}},{q="What will I learn from Mathrengyl Bearwalker?",a="Mathrengyl begins teaching you the ways of the bear. You learn strength of body and heart to help protect nature's balance.",al={"what will mathrengyl teach me","what do i learn next","what form do i get"}},{q="What does it mean to be among 'Cenarius' children'?",a="Cenarius is the patron demigod of nature and teacher of the first druids. His followers believe all druids share a duty to protect Azeroth's wild plants and beasts.",al={"who is cenarius","children of cenarius meaning"}},{q="What is Fandral Staghelm's tree?",a="Fandral Staghelm's tree is a grand, hollow living tree in Darnassus's Cenarion Enclave. Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and his inner circle direct Cenarion affairs across Teldrassil from there.",al={"what tree is he in","fandral tree location"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you leave basic novice training and begin learning nature's deeper forms under Archdruid Staghelm's direct assistant.",["race:NightElf"]="At the heart of Darnassus, the Cenarion Enclave shows how deeply Cenarion druidism is woven into Kaldorei society.",["faction:Alliance"]="As a member of the Alliance, you join night elf druids answering the Cenarion call in Darnassus. Their work strengthens the Alliance's ancient magical and natural foundations on Kalimdor."},kw={"bear form","cenarion enclave","darnassus","druid training","fandral staghelm","heeding the call","mathrengyl bearwalker","teldrassil"},rel={"npc:mathrengyl-bearwalker","npc:fandral-staghelm","subzone:cenarion-enclave","zone:darnassus","quest:6001","quest:6381","quest:940","topic:druid"},m={id=5925},src={"In-game quest text: Heeding the Call"},fv=true}
E["quest:5929"] = {t="quest",n="Great Bear Spirit",z="general",s="In Moonglade, druids are sent north to seek the Great Bear Spirit. Communion with this ancient guide teaches the bear’s nature and strength, a vital part of a druid’s spiritual and martial training.",h="In Moonglade, druids are sent north to seek the Great Bear Spirit.",sec={{t="The Noble Mentor of Moonglade",b="In northwestern Moonglade, the Great Bear Spirit dwells apart from Nighthaven’s quiet paths. This ancient and noble mentor teaches every initiate who seeks to understand the bear’s nature. Druids have long sought its blessing and wisdom as part of the path of the wild.",sp=0},{t="A Rite of Primal Focus",b="Dendrite Starblaze guides young druids from Nighthaven into the hills to meet the spirit. Communion brings primal wisdom and an awareness of natural strength. When the druids return, Dendrite tests their focus. The encounter gives them raw strength and knowing intent, but they must learn to direct that power in their daily work.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can the Great Bear Spirit be found?",a="The Great Bear Spirit dwells in the northwestern wilds of Moonglade, apart from Nighthaven and its settled paths.",al={"where is the bear spirit","find bear spirit moonglade","bear spirit location"}},{q="Who sends druids to seek out the Great Bear Spirit?",a="Dendrite Starblaze, an elder druid in Nighthaven, directs initiates to commune with the spirit as part of their natural training.",al={"who sends you to bear spirit","dendrite starblaze role","quest giver bear spirit"}},{q="What is the Great Bear Spirit?",a="The Great Bear Spirit is a wise and noble guide, tutor, and mentor who teaches practicing druids the primal nature of the bear.",al={"what is the bear spirit","bear spirit lore","who is great bear spirit"}},{q="Why must druids commune with the Great Bear Spirit?",a="Initiates commune with the spirit to learn the bear’s nature and take in its raw strength, resilience, and wisdom before returning to put those qualities to work.",al={"why talk to bear spirit","bear spirit quest purpose","what do you learn from bear spirit"}},{q="What happens after the Great Bear Spirit is satisfied?",a="After communing with the spirit, initiates return to Dendrite Starblaze in Nighthaven. Dendrite recognizes their new understanding and prepares them to focus that power.",al={"what happens after bear spirit","dendrite after spirit","after visiting bear spirit"}},{q="Do all druids need to seek the spirit's wisdom?",a="Every druid must seek the Great Bear Spirit’s counsel. Communion is a fundamental step toward attuning to animal forms and natural power.",al={"do all druids visit bear spirit","is bear spirit mandatory","who visits bear spirit"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you commune with the Great Bear Spirit to gain natural wisdom and the spiritual grounding needed to take on the bear’s resilience."},kw={"bear form","dendrite starblaze","druid quest","great bear spirit","moonglade","nighthaven"},rel={"npc:dendrite-starblaze","topic:druid","subzone:nighthaven","quest:5921"},m={id=5929},src={"In-game quest text: Great Bear Spirit"},fv=true}
E["quest:6393"] = {t="quest",n="Elemental War",z="general",s="Elemental War is a task undertaken for Tsunaman, an envoy of the water elementals residing at Sun Rock Retreat in Stonetalon Mountains. Guided by the will of Neptulon the Tidehunter, adventurers are sent into the scorched depths of the Charred Vale to extinguish blazing fire elementals and collect their smoldering Incendrite cores. It provides a rare glimpse into the ancient, perpetual rivalry that divides the elemental planes.",h="Elemental War is a task undertaken for Tsunaman, an envoy of the water elementals residing at Sun Rock...",sec={{t="Primal Rivalry in the Mountains",b="In the rugged reaches of Stonetalon Mountains, an envoy named Tsunaman has arrived at Sun Rock Retreat on behalf of the Tribunal of the Tides. Acting under the supreme will of Neptulon the Tidehunter, the lord of water elementals, Tsunaman seeks to carry out an ancient vendetta against their fiery kin. Rather than a dispute over mortal borders, this conflict represents the unending clash of Azeroth's primal forces.",sp=0},{t="The Ash and the Flame",b="The primary battleground for this struggle is the Charred Vale, a scarred wasteland smoldering in southern Stonetalon Mountains. Fire elementals thrive amidst the ashes, threatening the surrounding regions. Adventurers are enlisted to descend into the vale, snuff out these flame-wreathed entities, and gather Incendrites—blazing remnants left behind upon an elemental's defeat—to prove their destruction to the Tribunal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tsunaman?",a="Tsunaman is an emissary sent by the Tribunal of the Tides to Sun Rock Retreat in Stonetalon Mountains. Acting under the authority of Neptulon the Tidehunter, he seeks to wage war on fire elementals and recruits adventurers to help destroy them.",al={"who is tsunaman","tsunaman quest giver","sun rock retreat elemental"}},{q="Why does Neptulon want the fire elementals destroyed?",a="Neptulon the Tidehunter, the Elemental Lord of water, holds an ancient enmity against fire elementals and has taken a personal interest in extinguishing them. Tsunaman was dispatched by the Tribunal of the Tides to carry out this campaign in Stonetalon Mountains.",al={"why fight fire elementals","elemental war reason","neptulon fire elementals"}},{q="Where is the Charred Vale located?",a="The Charred Vale is a devastated, ash-covered basin located in southern Stonetalon Mountains. It is overrun by fiery elemental creatures and other dangerous beasts born of flame and ruin.",al={"where is charred vale","fire elemental location stonetalon","where to find incendrites"}},{q="What are Incendrites?",a="Incendrites are burning remnants left behind when the fire elementals of the Charred Vale are extinguished. Tsunaman collects these cores as physical proof of their destruction to build favor with the Tribunal of the Tides.",al={"what are incendrites","fire elemental drops","incendrite lore"}},{q="What is the Tribunal of the Tides?",a="The Tribunal of the Tides is an authority among water elementals serving Neptulon the Tidehunter. They direct elemental forces and envoys like Tsunaman to advance the Tidehunter's will against rival elemental domains.",al={"tribunal of tides lore","who are tribunal of tides","water elemental faction"}},{q="Who is Neptulon the Tidehunter?",a="Neptulon the Tidehunter is the Elemental Lord of water, ruling over the oceans, seas, and all water elementals. Like the other elemental lords, he wages an ancient, perpetual conflict against rival elements, especially fire.",al={"who is neptulon","water elemental lord","neptulon tidehunter"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Shamans revere the balance of the elements, making this direct conflict between Neptulon's servants and fire elementals a vivid demonstration of primal elemental strife.",["faction:Horde"]="Given that Tsunaman is stationed at Sun Rock Retreat, Horde adventurers passing through Stonetalon Mountains are directly positioned to assist in this elemental clash."},kw={"charred vale","elemental war","fire elementals","incendrites","neptulon","stonetalon mountains","sun rock retreat","tribunal of the tides","tsunaman"},rel={"npc:tsunaman","subzone:charred-vale","subzone:sun-rock-retreat"},m={id=6393},src={"In-game quest text: Elemental War"},fv=true}
E["quest:6394"] = {t="quest",n="Thazz'ril's Pick",z="general",s="Foreman Thazz'ril accidentally left his favorite enchanted mining pick deep inside the northern cavern during a mineral survey. Now overrun by dangerous beasts, the cave known as the Burning Blade Coven holds the glowing tool awaiting recovery.",h="Foreman Thazz'ril accidentally left his favorite enchanted mining pick deep inside the northern cavern during...",sec={{t="A Lost Tool",b="Foreman Thazz'ril, the supervisor overseeing work details in the Valley of Trials, previously ventured into the northern cavern to prospect for mineral veins. While surveying the rocky interior, he accidentally left behind his prized mining pick. By the time he realized his mistake and returned to fetch it, vicious beasts and hostile inhabitants had overtaken the cavern, forcing the foreman to seek assistance from capable recruits to recover his lost tool.",sp=0},{t="Glow in the Dark",b="To make his prized pick easier to use in dim subterranean depths, Thazz'ril enchanted it with a spell that causes it to glow in the dark. This magical illumination allows adventurers to easily spot the implement resting inside a waterfall chamber within the Burning Blade Coven, though retrieving it requires facing whatever dangerous creatures prowl the cavern shadows. Once returned, Thazz'ril hopes to put the pick back to work uncovering new mining opportunities across the valley.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Foreman Thazz'ril?",a="Foreman Thazz'ril oversees labor and resource gathering in the Valley of Trials, directing peons as they clear timber and prepare the land for mining operations.",al={"who is thazzril","thazzril role","foreman thazzril"}},{q="Why does Thazz'ril's pick glow?",a="Thazz'ril placed a spell on his favorite pick so that it glows in the dark, allowing it to be easily spotted even in pitch-black cavern depths.",al={"why is the pick special","glowing pick","enchanted pick"}},{q="Where is the Burning Blade Coven?",a="The Burning Blade Coven is a dark cave network located to the north of the peon lumber camp in the Valley of Trials.",al={"where is burning blade coven","coven location","pick cave"}},{q="How did Thazz'ril lose his pick?",a="Thazz'ril left his favorite pick behind while surveying the northern cave for minerals. When he went back to retrieve it, vicious beasts and dark cultists had infested the cavern.",al={"how did thazzril lose his pick","why leave the pick","pick left behind"}},{q="Where inside the cave was the pick left?",a="The pick was left deep inside the cave in a subterranean chamber featuring waterfalls, resting where Thazz'ril was last surveying the rock walls.",al={"where inside cave","pick room","waterfall chamber"}},{q="What does Thazz'ril plan to do after getting his pick back?",a="Thazz'ril plans to find a productive cave to mine once his peons finish cutting down the surrounding timber in the Valley of Trials.",al={"thazzril future plans","what will thazzril mine"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="Foreman Thazz'ril relies on his enchanted pick for mineral surveys across Durotar, hoping to open new mining prospects once the local timber is cleared.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing basic tools and clearing out hostile threats like the Burning Blade Coven helps the Horde establish a firm foothold in the Valley of Trials."},kw={"burning blade coven","durotar","foreman thazz'ril","mining","pick","thazz'ril","valley of trials","waterfall"},rel={"npc:foreman-thazz-ril","subzone:burning-blade-coven"},m={id=6394},src={"In-game quest text: Thazz'ril's Pick"},fv=true}
E["quest:642"] = {t="quest",n="The Princess Trapped",z="general",s="The Princess Trapped begins when adventurers uncover an iridescent crystal shard in the Arathi Highlands and hear the plea of Myzrael, a self-styled princess of the earth imprisoned deep underground. Claiming she was trapped by hostile giants, she begs travelers to confront their kobold allies in Drywhisker Gorge and restore power to her crystalline communication network.",h="The Princess Trapped begins when adventurers uncover an iridescent crystal shard in the Arathi Highlands and...",sec={{t="A Voice from the Stone",b="Scattered across the rolling plains of the Arathi Highlands, strange crystal shards hum with distant power. Approaching them reveals a desperate voice echoing from deep subterranean caverns: Myzrael, a self-proclaimed princess of the earth. She claims that ancient giants cast her down and bound her far beneath the soil, cutting her off from the sunlit lands above. The crystals embedded in the earth represent her sole means of contacting the surface, carrying her pleas for salvation to any adventurer willing to listen.",sp=0},{t="The Drywhisker Connection",b="To maintain her tenuous contact and begin breaking through her subterranean isolation, Myzrael directs travelers eastward toward Drywhisker Gorge. The gorge is infested with Drywhisker kobolds, creatures she identifies as allies of her giant captors. These kobolds carry resonant Motes of Myzrael and guard a prominent cluster of iridescent shards. By wresting these motes from the kobolds and applying their energy to the formation, rescuers can empower the crystal cluster and deepen their communication with the trapped princess.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Myzrael?",a="The voice speaking through the crystal belongs to Myzrael, an entity who claims to be a princess of the earth imprisoned deep beneath the Arathi Highlands. Unable to break free on her own, she reaches out to passing adventurers to help her commune with the surface.",al={"who is the trapped princess","who is talking through the crystal","who is myzrael"}},{q="Who trapped Myzrael beneath the highlands?",a="According to Myzrael, she was imprisoned beneath Arathi by giants. She claims they bound her deep underground, forcing her to rely on crystal shards scattered across the region to communicate with anyone walking above.",al={"who imprisoned myzrael","who trapped the princess","why is myzrael underground"}},{q="What are the Motes of Myzrael?",a="Motes of Myzrael are crystalline or elemental fragments carried by the Drywhisker kobolds. Myzrael explains that these motes hold enough latent earth power to activate and empower the shard clusters needed to strengthen her link to the surface.",al={"why do the kobolds have motes","drywhisker gorge motes","what are motes of myzrael"}},{q="How are the Drywhisker kobolds connected to Myzrael's captors?",a="Myzrael states that the Drywhisker kobolds living in Drywhisker Gorge are allies of the giants who imprisoned her. They guard a key crystal shard cluster in the gorge, standing between the princess and the surface.",al={"why are kobolds involved","drywhisker kobolds giants","kobolds working with giants"}},{q="How is Myzrael able to speak from underground?",a="Myzrael channels her voice through mysterious iridescent crystal shards protruding from the soil of the Arathi Highlands. These formations act as geomantic transmitters, allowing her spirit or voice to pierce the subterranean depths and contact surface dwellers.",al={"how does myzrael speak","iridescent shards arathi","what are the crystal shards"}},{q="Where is Drywhisker Gorge located?",a="Drywhisker Gorge is a rugged cleft situated in eastern Arathi Highlands. It is inhabited by the Drywhisker kobolds, who surround the iridescent shard cluster that Myzrael asks you to activate with the gathered motes.",al={"where is drywhisker gorge","where do i take the motes","drywhisker shards location"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As practitioners who commune with the spirits of the elements, shamans will recognize Myzrael's claim of being an earth princess, though her imprisonment beneath the earth warrants careful elemental scrutiny.",["class:Mage"]="The use of resonant crystal shards and motes of earth energy to communicate and channel power across great distances presents a curious display of geomantic magic.",["profession:Mining"]="Miners venturing near Drywhisker Gorge and underground veins in Arathi will find themselves crossing paths with both the kobold infestations and the enchanted crystal shards tied to the princess."},kw={"arathi highlands","drywhisker gorge","drywhisker kobolds","earth princess","iridescent shards","motes of myzrael","myzrael","princess of the earth","the princess trapped"},rel={"zone:arathi","subzone:drywhisker-gorge","topic:kobold"},m={id=642},src={"In-game quest text: The Princess Trapped"},fv=true}
E["quest:655"] = {t="quest",n="Hammerfall",z="general",s="Hammerfall recounts the tragic fall of Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer during the liberation of an Alliance internment camp in the Arathi Highlands. Through the grief-stricken memories of Gor'mul, travelers learn how the camp was freed and why the stronghold bears its solemn name.",h="Hammerfall recounts the tragic fall of Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer during the liberation of an Alliance...",sec={{t="Blood and Broken Chains",b="Hammerfall was once a grim Alliance internment camp where captive orcs were kept behind wood and steel after the Second War. That changed when young Thrall and Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer launched an assault to shatter the enclosures and free their kin. Though the raid succeeded in freeing many captives and bringing down the camp's defenses, it ended in tragedy when Doomhammer was struck from behind by a mounted lance, dying on the very soil where the Horde settlement now stands.",sp=0},{t="The Lingering Shadow of Defeat",b="Not every captive found freedom on that fateful day. In the chaos of battle and the tremors of the earth, an orc named Gor'mul watched the legendary Warchief fall and lost all will to fight. Left behind as his brethren fled, Gor'mul spent years wandering the highlands in directionless grief. Even now that the Horde has reclaimed the site as a fortified base, the memory of that cowardly strike continues to haunt those who survived it.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gor'mul?",a="Gor'mul was an orc captive held in the internment camp that once stood where Hammerfall now rests. Although Thrall and Doomhammer led an assault to liberate the camp, Gor'mul was left behind in the chaotic escape and wandered alone for years in despair.",al={"who is gormul","gor mul backstory","what happened to gormul"}},{q="How did Orgrim Doomhammer die?",a="Orgrim Doomhammer was slain during the battle to liberate the Arathi internment camp. While leading the attack in his black plate armor, he was struck from behind by an Alliance knight charging on horseback who ran him through with a lance.",al={"how did doomhammer die","who killed doomhammer","doomhammer death story"}},{q="Why is the outpost named Hammerfall?",a="The outpost was named Hammerfall in honor of Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer, who fell at this exact location while freeing his people from human captivity. The Horde later returned to the ruined camp to establish a permanent base.",al={"why is it called hammerfall","origin of hammerfall name","hammerfall history"}},{q="Who is Tor'gan?",a="Tor'gan is a Horde representative stationed in Hammerfall who helps look after the encampment and understands the lingering trauma suffered by former captives like Gor'mul.",al={"who is tor'gan","torgan hammerfall","torgan quest"}},{q="Did Thrall take part in the battle here?",a="Yes. Thrall fought alongside Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer in the assault on this Arathi internment camp. Together, they shattered the camp's defenses to liberate the captive orcs, though the victory came at the cost of Doomhammer's life.",al={"thrall and doomhammer arathi","liberation of hammerfall","did thrall fight at hammerfall"}},{q="Why is Gor'mul so despondent?",a="Gor'mul witnessed Doomhammer struck down before his eyes, crushing his spirit. When the other captives escaped into the wilderness, he was left behind, wandering aimlessly without hope until the Horde returned to build Hammerfall.",al={"why is gormul so sad","gormul depression","why did gormul lose hope"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Hammerfall stands as a solemn monument to the liberation of the orcish internment camps and the sacrifice of Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer.",["race:Orc"]="For orcs, the fall of Doomhammer in Arathi is one of the most painful moments in their history, marking the end of an era and the rise of Thrall's new Horde.",["class:Warrior"]="Orgrim Doomhammer remains an iconic warrior of legend, and walking the grounds where he fell in battle carries heavy martial significance."},kw={"arathi highlands","doomhammer","gor'mul","hammerfall","internment camp","orgrim doomhammer","thrall","tor'gan"},rel={"subzone:hammerfall","npc:thrall"},m={id=655},src={"In-game quest text: Hammerfall"},fv=true}
E["quest:65601"] = {t="quest",n="Love Hurts",z="general",s="Gan'rul sends you along Orgrimmar's main drag to find Magar. His longing for Krilge may reveal the object Gan'rul needs to lure an incubus into the world.",h="Gan'rul sends you along Orgrimmar's main drag to find Magar.",sec={{t="Magar's Search",b="East of the Cleft of Shadow, Magar longs for Krilge to return. He asks you to search for something precious. Bring that object to Gan'rul, who needs a token of love and devotion to summon an incubus.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Magar?",a="Magar lives along Orgrimmar's main drag and longs for Krilge to return.",al={"Magar who","who's Magar"}},{q="Who is Krilge?",a="Krilge is Magar's mate, and Magar longs for Krilge's return.",al={"Krilge who","Magar's mate"}},{q="What is an incubus?",a="An incubus is a male sayaad, profoundly evil and single-minded.",al={"incubus meaning","sayaad male"}},{q="What does Gan'rul need?",a="Gan'rul needs an object that represents great love and devotion to lure an incubus into the world.",al={"Gan'rul needs what","incubus item"}},{q="Where can I find Magar?",a="Magar lives east of the Cleft of Shadow along Orgrimmar's main drag.",al={"Magar location","where Magar"}}},kw={"cleft of shadow","gan'rul","incubus","krilge","love hurts","magar","orgrimmar","sayaad"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar","subzone:cleft-of-shadow","subzone:drag"},m={id=65601},src={"In-game quest text: Love Hurts"},fv=true}
E["quest:65610"] = {t="quest",n="Wish You Were Here",z="general",s="A mooncloth maker in Ashenvale asks you to find Krilge, who vanished while guarding civilians. His comrades call him a deserter, but the maker believes he held his post.",h="A mooncloth maker in Ashenvale asks you to find Krilge, who vanished while guarding civilians.",sec={{t="A Missing Friend",b="A mooncloth maker met Krilge while making mooncloth in Ashenvale. Krilge was stationed there to protect civilians, then disappeared. They often met at Fallen Sky Lake, until aggressive bog beasts overran it. The maker still hopes to learn what became of him.",sp=0},{t="At the Lake",b="You are asked to search Fallen Sky Lake and report your findings to Gan'rul Bloodeye in Orgrimmar. Krilge's comrades have stopped looking for him. Perhaps the lake still holds a sign of what became of him.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Krilge?",a="Krilge was stationed in Ashenvale to protect civilians before he disappeared. He and the mooncloth maker often met at Fallen Sky Lake.",al={"krilge","missing mooncloth maker's friend"}},{q="Why is Krilge being called a deserter?",a="Krilge vanished from his post. Those who served with him stopped searching and call him a deserter.",al={"krilge deserter","why they stopped looking"}},{q="Who asks you to look for Krilge?",a="A mooncloth maker who met Krilge in Ashenvale asks you to search for a sign of him.",al={"who sent me","mooncloth maker"}},{q="Where did Krilge and the maker meet?",a="Krilge and the maker often met at Fallen Sky Lake in Ashenvale, before aggressive bog beasts overran the area.",al={"krilge meeting place","where is fallen lake"}},{q="Where should you report your findings?",a="You are asked to report your findings to Gan'rul Bloodeye in Orgrimmar.",al={"report to gan'rul","where do i report"}}},kw={"ashenvale","bog beasts","deserter","fallen sky lake","gan'rul bloodeye","krilge","missing","mooncloth"},rel={"zone:ashenvale","zone:orgrimmar","npc:gan-rul-bloodeye"},m={id=65610},src={"In-game quest text: Wish You Were Here"},fv=true}
E["quest:6661"] = {t="quest",n="Deeprun Rat Roundup",z="general",s="Deeprun Rat Roundup is an Alliance task in the Deeprun Tram where players assist Monty with pest control. Using a charming musical flute, players round up living Deeprun rats to supply Monty's brother with fresh meat for rat kabobs.",h="Deeprun Rat Roundup is an Alliance task in the Deeprun Tram where players assist Monty with pest control.",sec={{t="Vermin Beneath the Rails",b="Down in the subterranean tube of the Deeprun Tram, a colorful character named Monty struggles to keep the underground transit line clear of pests. Speaking in salty sea slang and treating the tramway like a ship's deck, Monty enlists passing travelers to help round up the scurrying vermin infesting the station.",sp=0},{t="A Questionable Delicacy",b="Rather than exterminating the rodents outright, Monty equips helpers with a Rat Catcher's Flute that plays a charming melody to entice the Deeprun rats into following along. The creatures must be brought back alive and kicking, as Monty's brother needs fresh specimens for culinary use: specifically, turning them into rat kabobs before the meat turns sour.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the Deeprun Rat Roundup quest?",a="Monty, a pirate-talking figure stationed down in the Deeprun Tram, hands out the task. He is looking for extra hands to help manage a vermin infestation along the subterranean transit line.",al={"who gives deeprun rat roundup","monty deeprun tram","rat quest guy"}},{q="Why does Monty need the rats captured alive?",a="Monty needs the Deeprun rats captured alive so they do not go sour before his brother can prepare them into rat kabobs. If the vermin die prematurely, the meat spoils.",al={"why catch rats alive","what does monty do with rats","rat kabob lore"}},{q="How do you charm and round up the rats?",a="Monty provides an enchanted Rat Catcher's Flute. Playing its melody near the vermin charms them, causing the rats to follow behind the player back to Monty's station.",al={"how to catch deeprun rats","what is the flute for","rat catcher flute deeprun"}},{q="Where does this task take place?",a="The quest takes place inside the Deeprun Tram, the subterranean engineering marvel connecting the Alliance cities of Stormwind and Ironforge. Monty is stationed along the tram platforms.",al={"where is deeprun rat roundup","monty location","deeprun tram rats"}},{q="Who is Monty's brother?",a="Monty mentions having a brother who cooks rat kabobs, but does not name him directly. His brother depends on a steady supply of fresh, living rats to avoid serving rancid meat.",al={"who is montys brother","monty brother cook","kabob brother"}},{q="Why does Monty talk like a pirate?",a="While Monty speaks in heavy nautical pirate slang, treating the tram like a ship's deck, he has taken up pest control duties down in the underground railway.",al={"why is a pirate in the tram","monty pirate deeprun"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Monty's brother uses freshly captured Deeprun vermin to prepare rat kabobs, emphasizing that the rodents must remain alive until cooking lest the meat spoil."},kw={"deeprun rat","deeprun rat roundup","deeprun tram","monty","rat catchers flute","rat kabob","vermin"},m={id=6661},src={"In-game quest text: Deeprun Rat Roundup"},fv=true}
E["quest:6662"] = {t="quest",n="Me Brother, Nipsy",z="general",s="Me Brother, Nipsy is an Alliance delivery quest that takes travelers across the Deeprun Tram between Ironforge and Stormwind. A gnome named Monty tasks commuters with transporting a crate of live rats to his brother Nipsy, who runs a rat kabob stall at the opposite terminal. Beneath the quirky errand lies a touching look at Monty's fractured mind following the tragedy of Gnomeregan.",h="Me Brother, Nipsy is an Alliance delivery quest that takes travelers across the Deeprun Tram between...",sec={{t="A Subterranean Delivery",b="Me Brother, Nipsy is a brief courier errand that introduces commuters to the wonders—and hazards—of the Deeprun Tram. Stationed on the Ironforge side of the tramway, an odd gnome named Monty tasks travelers with ferrying a carton of fresh critters through the subterranean transit corridor to his brother Nipsy at the Stormwind depot. The delivery highlights the bizarre everyday commerce taking place beneath the mountains connecting the dwarven and human capitals.",sp=0},{t="The Rat Kabob Trade",b="While labeled as a Carton of Mystery Meat, the parcel is actually packed with live, squirming Deeprun rats. Nipsy operates a rat kabob enterprise at the Stormwind terminal and requires his stock delivered before it goes bad. When the crate arrives intact and twitching, Nipsy is relieved to keep his culinary operation stocked for hungry travelers passing through the transit hub.",sp=0},{t="Scars of Gnomeregan",b="Beneath the humorous delivery lies a somber glimpse into the displaced gnomes of Ironforge. Monty speaks in exaggerated pirate slang, warning travelers to keep their limbs aboard lest they end up peglegged. Upon receiving the rats, Nipsy laments that Monty has never seen an ocean in his life; his eccentric pirate persona is merely a coping mechanism born from the lingering trauma of the radioactive fall of Gnomeregan.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Me Brother, Nipsy?",a="Monty is an eccentric gnome found on the Ironforge side of the Deeprun Tram. Affected deeply by the catastrophe at Gnomeregan, Monty has adopted the speech and mannerisms of a pirate despite having never seen an ocean, catching live rats to ship across the tramline.",al={"who gives me brother nipsy","who is monty","monty tram"}},{q="Who is Nipsy?",a="Nipsy is Monty's brother who operates a food stall at the Stormwind Deeprun Tram Depot. He relies on Monty to send shipments of live rats across the tram tunnel to keep his rat kabob business supplied with fresh ingredients.",al={"who is nipsy","where is nipsy","nipsy location"}},{q="What is inside the Carton of Mystery Meat?",a="Despite the tongue-in-cheek label of Mystery Meat, the crate is filled with live, twitching Deeprun rats caught by Monty. Nipsy insists they arrive alive and kicking so that the meat does not spoil before being skewered into kabobs.",al={"what is carton of mystery meat","mystery meat crate","what is in the box"}},{q="Why does Monty speak like a pirate?",a="Monty talks like a pirate because the trauma of the Gnomeregan disaster shattered his mind. As his brother Nipsy laments, Monty has never even seen an ocean, yet he clings to pirate affectations and warns travelers against losing limbs over the tram rails.",al={"why does monty talk like a pirate","is monty a pirate","monty pirate lore"}},{q="What does Nipsy do with the rats?",a="Nipsy prepares and sells rat kabobs to commuters passing through the Stormwind depot. Keeping a steady supply of fresh, twitching critters arriving on the tram is what keeps his unconventional underground eatery running.",al={"what does nipsy do with the rats","rat kabob operation","nipsy food"}},{q="Where do you deliver the shipment?",a="You take the shipment across the Deeprun Tram from the Ironforge depot to the Stormwind terminal. Stepping aboard one of the high-speed tram cars carries you through the underwater tunnel straight to Nipsy's station on the Stormwind side.",al={"how to get to nipsy","where do i take the meat","tram ride to nipsy"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Monty's eccentric delusions and pirate affectations stem directly from the trauma of the Gnomeregan incident, showing the deep personal toll the fall of their city took on surviving gnomes.",["profession:Cooking"]="Nipsy's culinary venture revolves around skewering fresh Deeprun rats into edible kabobs for commuters traveling between Ironforge and Stormwind.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Deeprun Tram serves as an essential, high-speed engineering marvel linking Stormwind and Ironforge, complete with quirky local vendors serving travelers at either depot."},kw={"deeprun tram","gnomeregan","ironforge depot","me brother, nipsy","monty","mystery meat","nipsy","rat kabob","stormwind depot"},rel={"subzone:deeprun-tram","zone:gnomeregan","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:pirate"},m={id=6662},src={"In-game quest text: Me Brother, Nipsy"},fv=true}
E["quest:671"] = {t="quest",n="Foul Magics",z="general",s="Foul Magics is a Horde quest originating in Hammerfall within the Arathi Highlands. Tor'gan senses a disturbing demonic presence to the west and discovers traces of dark summoning rituals at Northfold Manor. He asks adventurers to venture into the manor and retrieve bloodstone amulets used in these summonings so he can study their dark magic.",h="Foul Magics is a Horde quest originating in Hammerfall within the Arathi Highlands.",sec={{t="A Simmering Rage",b="Tor'gan, an orc stationed at the Horde outpost of Hammerfall, began noticing an unsettling disturbance drifting through the Arathi Highlands. For an orc, demonic energies are not merely an abstract threat; they evoke a visceral, biological reaction rooted in the history of the blood curse. As Tor'gan tracked the source of his agitation westward toward Northfold Manor, the ambient corruption caused his blood to boil and stoked an involuntary rage within him. Recognizing the telltale aftermath of demonic summoning, he retreated before the dark influence overwhelmed his senses, seeking outside help instead of returning himself.",sp=0},{t="The Bloodstone Amulets",b="At Northfold Manor, Tor'gan discovered physical evidence of dark rituals: an amulet carved from bloodstone lying on the ground. These magical charms are actively employed to facilitate and stabilize demonic summoning rituals. The specific amulet Tor'gan managed to recover was already depleted of its magical reserves, making thorough magical analysis impossible. In order to investigate the full extent of the summoning and determine what entities were brought forth, he charges Horde travelers with heading into Northfold Manor to retrieve ten intact bloodstone amulets from the practitioners residing there.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Foul Magics?",a="The quest is offered by Tor'gan, an orc located in Hammerfall, the Horde stronghold in the Arathi Highlands. Tor'gan senses a disturbing demonic presence nearby and asks adventurers to investigate Northfold Manor to discover what dark powers are at work.",al={"foul magics quest giver","who starts foul magics","torgan quest arathi"}},{q="Where is Northfold Manor located?",a="Northfold Manor is located to the west of Hammerfall in the northern hills of the Arathi Highlands. Once a human homestead, the estate has become a site for covert demonic summonings using bloodstone charms.",al={"northfold manor location","where is northfold manor","find northfold manor arathi"}},{q="What are Bloodstone Amulets used for?",a="Bloodstone amulets are magical foci used to assist in demonic summoning rituals. Tor'gan discovered that practitioners at Northfold Manor employ these stones to channel dark magic, though individual amulets become depleted once their power is drained during a summoning.",al={"what is bloodstone amulet","bloodstone amulet purpose","why bloodstone amulets"}},{q="Why does Tor'gan feel rage near Northfold Manor?",a="Tor'gan experiences a violent physical reaction near Northfold Manor due to the lingering demonic summoning energies. For orcs, exposure to fel magic agitates the remnants of the blood curse, causing their blood to boil and igniting uncontrollable rage.",al={"why torgan blood boils","torgan rage northfold","why does torgan get angry"}},{q="Why does Tor'gan refuse to return to Northfold Manor?",a="Tor'gan refuses to return to Northfold Manor because the residual demonic power stirs a dangerous rage within him. Fearing that he might lose control or succumb to the corruptive influence, he enlists outside adventurers to recover the amulets on his behalf.",al={"why wont torgan go to northfold","why torgan doesnt go back","why cant torgan get amulets"}},{q="How many Bloodstone Amulets are needed?",a="Tor'gan asks for ten bloodstone amulets from Northfold Manor. Because the single amulet he found on the ground was completely depleted, he requires multiple charged specimens to properly examine and understand the rituals taking place there.",al={"how many bloodstone amulets","bloodstone amulet count","foul magics objective count"}},{q="Who is summoning demons at Northfold Manor?",a="Dark practitioners have taken over Northfold Manor to conduct illicit demonic summonings. Their dark rituals and the resulting fel residue drew Tor'gan's attention from Hammerfall, prompting the Horde's investigation into the estate.",al={"who is at northfold manor","who is summoning demons northfold","northfold manor cultists"}}},ang={["race:Orc"]="Orcs have a deep, painful history with demonic corruption, and residual fel magic can physically cause their blood to boil and stir unnatural rage. Tor'gan's visceral reaction near Northfold Manor highlights the enduring sensitivity orcs share toward demonic summoning.",["faction:Horde"]="With Hammerfall standing as the Horde's lone outpost in the Arathi Highlands, the presence of dark summoners operating just to the west poses a direct security threat to the settlement.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks are intimately familiar with the rituals and binding reagents required to summon demons, making the bloodstone amulets found at Northfold Manor objects of particular interest."},kw={"arathi highlands","bloodstone","bloodstone amulet","demonic summoning","foul magics","hammerfall","northfold manor","tor'gan"},rel={"subzone:northfold-manor","subzone:hammerfall"},m={id=671},src={"In-game quest text: Foul Magics"},fv=true}
E["quest:672"] = {t="quest",n="Raising Spirits",z="general",s="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest in the Arathi Highlands where Tor'gan seeks to lift the spirits of a despondent orc named Gor'mul. When tales of Orgrimmar and Warchief Thrall fail, Tor'gan turns to newly rediscovered shamanic traditions to craft a revitalizing amulet from highland raptor eyes.",h="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest in the Arathi Highlands where Tor'gan seeks to lift the spirits of a...",sec={{t="A Heavy Heart in Hammerfall",b="At the remote outpost of Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands, the orcs face enemies from without and quiet battles within. One of their own, Gor'mul, has fallen into a deep, unshakable melancholy. His companions attempted to rally him with proud tales of Kalimdor, the mighty fortress city of Orgrimmar, and the visionary leadership of young Warchief Thrall. Yet none of these grand victories or distant wonders could pierce Gor'mul's gloom or give him reason to hope.",sp=0},{t="Spirits of the Wild",b="Recognizing that mere words are powerless against Gor'mul's despondency, Tor'gan turns to the Horde's revived spiritual heritage. By gathering the eyes of fierce highland raptors roaming Arathi, Tor'gan crafts an amulet blessed with the predators' spirits. Rather than relying on brute force or fel power, this ritual invokes the restorative strength of newly embraced shamanistic traditions, hoping the beast's vital flame can awaken a brother's dormant will to live.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tor'gan?",a="Tor'gan is an orc stationed at the Horde outpost of Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands. Seeking to help a dispirited companion named Gor'mul, Tor'gan turns to the Horde's newly rediscovered shamanistic magic to craft an amulet capable of restoring his comrade's inner fire.",al={"who is tor'gan","torgan hammerfall","quest giver raising spirits"}},{q="Who is Gor'mul and why does he need cheering up?",a="Gor'mul is an orc resident in Hammerfall who is suffering from severe despondency and lethargy. Despite efforts by his fellow orcs to cheer him up with tales of Orgrimmar and Thrall's leadership, nothing has managed to move him or spark his spirits.",al={"who is gormul","why is gormul sad","orc depressed hammerfall"}},{q="Why does Tor'gan need highland raptor eyes?",a="Tor'gan uses the eyes of Highland Striders and Highland Thrashers to create a shamanic amulet. By channeling the spirits of these fierce predators, Tor'gan hopes to impart the raptors' inherent strength and tenacity into Gor'mul to reignite his fighting spirit.",al={"why raptor eyes","raptor eyes amulet","purpose of raptor eyes"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="Hammerfall is the primary Horde outpost in the Arathi Highlands, named in honor of the former Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. It serves as a fortified haven for orcs remaining in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"where is hammerfall","hammerfall location","orcs in arathi"}},{q="What is the newly found shamanistic magic mentioned by Tor'gan?",a="During their time under demonic corruption and subsequent internment, orcs lost their spiritual connection to the elements and ancestor spirits. Under Warchief Thrall's leadership, the Horde has reclaimed this ancient shamanistic path, using spiritual harmony rather than fel magic to guide their people.",al={"shaman magic orcs","orc shamanism new horde","why new shaman magic"}},{q="What stories failed to cheer up Gor'mul?",a="His companions tried to inspire Gor'mul with grand stories of the new capital city of Orgrimmar across the sea in Kalimdor, as well as the valor of Warchief Thrall wielding the armor and legendary warhammer of Orgrim Doomhammer.",al={"stories about thrall","what stories did they tell gormul","orgrimmar tales gormul"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This effort demonstrates how the Horde's revived shamanic traditions are applied to heal emotional wounds and listlessness, using animal spirits to rekindle a brother's will to live.",["race:Orc"]="Many veterans like Gor'mul remain burdened by the lethargy and despair following the internment camps and the loss of their former ways, finding it difficult to embrace Thrall's new Horde.",["faction:Horde"]="The quest reflects the compassionate side of Thrall's Horde, where comrades in remote outposts like Hammerfall look out for despondent brothers-in-arms rather than discarding them."},kw={"arathi highlands","gor'mul","hammerfall","highland raptor eyes","orgrimmar","raising spirits","shamanism","thrall","tor'gan"},rel={"subzone:hammerfall","npc:thrall","zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=672},src={"In-game quest text: Raising Spirits"},fv=true}
E["quest:674"] = {t="quest",n="Raising Spirits",z="general",s="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest aimed at rekindling the fighting will of Gor'mul, a broken warrior consumed by despair. An emissary delivers a Raptor Talon Amulet in hopes of awakening the fierce spirit he once possessed. However, Gor'mul remains bitter, questioning whether any simple trinket could ever mend his sorrow or restore glory to the Horde.",h="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest aimed at rekindling the fighting will of Gor'mul, a broken warrior consumed...",sec={{t="A Spark for a Fallen Flame",b="Raising Spirits involves delivering a Raptor Talon Amulet to an orc named Gor'mul. His allies hope that the wild strength and ferocity of raptors, channeled through the talisman, will reawaken the warrior's flame that once burned fiercely within his veins.",sp=0},{t="Bitterness and Despair",b="Upon receiving the talisman, Gor'mul offers only bitter grief. Tormented by the memory of the mightiest champions being struck down by cowardly hands, he rejects the token as mere polished bone, questioning how any trinket could restore the Horde to its former glory.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Raising Spirits?",a="Raising Spirits is a quest tasked with delivering a Raptor Talon Amulet to a despondent warrior named Gor'mul in hopes of rekindling his fighting spirit.",al={"what is raising spirits","raising spirits quest"}},{q="Who is Gor'mul?",a="Gor'mul is a broken-spirited warrior who has succumbed to despair after witnessing the mightiest of champions fall to a coward's hand. He dismisses tokens and questions the future of the Horde.",al={"who is gormul","gor mul"}},{q="Why is the Raptor Talon Amulet brought to Gor'mul?",a="The Raptor Talon Amulet is crafted from polished bone and raptor talons, offered in the hope that channeling the natural strength and ferocity of raptors can awaken Gor'mul's inner flame.",al={"why bring amulet","what does raptor talon amulet do"}},{q="Why is Gor'mul despondent?",a="Gor'mul has lost all hope after seeing the mightiest warriors slain by cowardice. The tragedy left him convinced that honor and strength can easily be snuffed out, breaking his will to fight.",al={"why is gormul sad","why is gormul depressed"}},{q="How does Gor'mul react to the amulet?",a="Gor'mul rejects the gift, bitterly dismissing the amulet as mere polished bone that cannot possibly restore the Horde or undo the tragedy he laments.",al={"does gormul like the amulet","gormul reaction"}},{q="What does Gor'mul say about the Horde?",a="Gor'mul openly doubts whether the Horde can ever reclaim its former glory, seeing little hope when foul treachery can strike down even the greatest heroes.",al={"can the horde be restored","gormul horde glory"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Gor'mul mourns the fallen might of the Horde, questioning whether a simple trinket could ever restore the faction to its lost glory.",["class:Warrior"]="This task centers on reigniting the extinguished warrior spirit within an orc paralyzed by despair and grief over a cowardly strike."},kw={"gor'mul","gormul","horde","raising spirits","raptor amulet","raptor talon amulet","warrior"},rel={"topic:horde","topic:warrior"},m={id=674},src={"In-game quest text: Raising Spirits"},fv=true}
E["quest:675"] = {t="quest",n="Raising Spirits",z="general",s="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest that addresses the lingering emotional trauma of an old orc veteran. Through the guidance of Tor'gan, a shaman attempts to reach the embittered Gor'mul using ancestral magic to restore his lost pride and will to live.",h="Raising Spirits is a Horde quest that addresses the lingering emotional trauma of an old orc veteran.",sec={{t="A Broken Spirit",b="In the wake of past wars, many orcish veterans struggle with the burdens of survival and perceived dishonor. Gor'mul is one such warrior, languishing in bitter despondency because he was denied what he considers an honorable death alongside his fallen Warchief. Tor'gan, an empathetic shaman, seeks to alleviate Gor'mul's suffering by reminding him of his people's spiritual strength. Believing that ancestral energies can heal emotional wounds, Tor'gan prepares an enchanted amulet intended to rekindle the old fighter's pride.",sp=0},{t="The Limits of the Amulet",b="When delivered to Gor'mul, the talisman fails to produce the desired effect. In an outburst of resentment, Gor'mul casts the amulet to the dirt, questioning why anyone would show him mercy when fate denied him an honorable end. Tor'gan takes the setback in stride, recognizing that a single charm possessed insufficient power to overcome years of entrenched sorrow. Even so, the shaman remains steadfast in his mission, resolute that the burgeoning practice of shamanism will ultimately guide Gor'mul back to his former honor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Gor'mul?",a="Gor'mul is an embittered orc veteran consumed by grief and survivor's guilt. Having survived the conflicts of the older Horde when his Warchief fell, he views living on as a cruel twist of fate and rejects conventional sympathy.",al={"who is gormul","gormul backstory","what is wrong with gormul"}},{q="Who is Tor'gan and why is he helping Gor'mul?",a="Tor'gan is a shaman trying to lift Gor'mul out of his bitter despair. Believing that reconnecting with the ancestral ways can restore broken spirits, Tor'gan crafts infused charms to remind former warriors of their people's spiritual rebirth.",al={"who is torgan","what does torgan want","torgan purpose"}},{q="What was the purpose of the amulet?",a="The amulet was an enchanted talisman prepared by Tor'gan to reawaken Gor'mul's warrior spirit through shamanic energy. When presented to Gor'mul, he rejected it and threw it to the ground, feeling its magic was insufficient to dispel his deep bitterness.",al={"what is the amulet","why did gormul throw the amulet","did the amulet work"}},{q="Which Warchief does Gor'mul mourn?",a="While Gor'mul curses fate for denying him death alongside his Warchief in battle, he does not name him directly. His words reflect the tragic perspective of the old Horde's defeated remnants who longed for an honorable battlefield demise.",al={"which warchief died","who was gormuls warchief","who did gormul serve"}},{q="What does Tor'gan plan to do after the amulet fails?",a="Tor'gan remains patient despite the initial failure. He acknowledges that the amulet was not potent enough on its own, but maintains faith that demonstrating the true power of shamanism will eventually rekindle Gor'mul's lost pride.",al={"does torgan give up","what happens after raising spirits","will gormul recover"}},{q="Why does Tor'gan use shamanism to help Gor'mul?",a="The effort highlights the shaman's vital role within the new Horde as spiritual healers, working not merely to channel the elements for war, but to tend to the wounded minds and pride of their people.",al={"what does this show about shamans","why use shaman magic here"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This task illustrates the restorative and spiritual purpose of modern orcish shamanism, using empowered relics to help heal lingering trauma among broken veterans.",["race:Orc"]="Gor'mul embodies the deep spiritual malaise and dishonor felt by old veterans denied what they considered an honorable death alongside their past Warchief.",["faction:Horde"]="Under Thrall's leadership, the Horde actively seeks to reintegrate and uplift embittered veterans rather than abandon them to despair."},kw={"amulet","gor'mul","mercy","orc","raising spirits","shaman","tor'gan","veteran","warchief"},rel={"topic:shaman"},m={id=675},src={"In-game quest text: Raising Spirits"},fv=true}
E["quest:677"] = {t="quest",n="Call to Arms",z="general",s="Call to Arms is a Horde military initiative directed by Drum Fel from the outpost of Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands. Threatened by an alliance between the local Witherbark forest trolls and Boulderfist ogres, the Horde launches a preemptive campaign against the Witherbark to secure their foothold in the region.",h="Call to Arms is a Horde military initiative directed by Drum Fel from the outpost of Hammerfall in the Arathi...",sec={{t="Defending Hammerfall",b="From the fortified sanctuary of Hammerfall, Drum Fel organizes a preemptive military strike against the hostile native powers of the Arathi Highlands. Surrounded by old human ruins and aggressive local factions, the Horde settlement faces an imminent threat from a cooperative pact formed between the indigenous Witherbark forest trolls and the brute Boulderfist ogres. Rather than allowing their enemies to dictate the terms of survival, Drum Fel calls on champions to take the offensive.",sp=0},{t="The Witherbark Threat",b="The initial offensive focuses south of Hammerfall, targeting the heart of Witherbark territory. The Witherbark have turned their backs on any diplomacy, choosing instead to conspire with the Boulderfist ogres to purge the Horde from the highlands. By culling their specialized axe throwers, headhunters, and witch doctors, Hammerfall seeks to shatter their martial leadership and demonstrate that the Horde is here to stay.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Call to Arms?",a="Call to Arms is given by Drum Fel, an orc stationed at the Horde outpost of Hammerfall in the northeastern Arathi Highlands. He coordinates defensive and offensive strikes to ensure the survival of the encampment against neighboring threats.",al={"who gives call to arms","where is drum fel","quest giver call to arms"}},{q="Why is Hammerfall targeting the Witherbark trolls?",a="The Witherbark tribe of forest trolls has aligned with the Boulderfist ogres to purge the Horde from the Arathi Highlands. Having rejected peaceful coexistence and turned their backs on the Horde, their aggressive posture leaves Hammerfall with no choice but to strike first.",al={"why kill witherbark","why attack trolls arathi","what did witherbark do"}},{q="Where are the Witherbark trolls located?",a="The Witherbark trolls inhabit several settlements and camps located south of Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands, including deep within Witherbark Village near the mountains.",al={"where are witherbark trolls","witherbark camp location","where to find axe throwers arathi"}},{q="What role do the Boulderfist ogres play in this conflict?",a="The Boulderfist ogres are a brutal clan residing in the Arathi Highlands. According to Drum Fel, they have entered an alliance of convenience with the Witherbark trolls to eradicate the Horde's presence in the highlands.",al={"who are boulderfist","ogres in arathi","boulderfist troll alliance"}},{q="What is Hammerfall and why does it need defending?",a="Hammerfall is the primary Horde outpost in the Arathi Highlands, named in memory of Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. Situated on the site of an old Alliance internment camp, it serves as a hard-won sanctuary and military bastion for the Horde in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"what is hammerfall","hammerfall lore","why is horde in arathi"}},{q="Are the Witherbark trolls part of the Horde?",a="No. While the jungle trolls of the Darkspear tribe are members of the Horde, the Witherbark are forest trolls native to the Eastern Kingdoms. They harbor deep hostility toward outsiders and have violently rejected any overtures from the Horde.",al={"are witherbark in the horde","are forest trolls friendly to horde","darkspear vs witherbark"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Hammerfall stands isolated in the Arathi Highlands, surrounded by hostile forces; defending the outpost from aggressive native factions is essential to securing a permanent Horde foothold in human-dominated lands.",["race:Orc"]="Built on the grounds of a former Second War internment camp, Hammerfall is a symbol of liberation for orcs, making the defense of their new settlement against hostile neighbors deeply personal.",["race:Troll"]="For Darkspear trolls serving Thrall's Horde, fighting the hostile Witherbark forest trolls highlights the sharp divide between their people and tribes that rejected the new Horde."},kw={"arathi highlands","axe thrower","boulderfist","call to arms","drum fel","hammerfall","headhunter","witch doctor","witherbark"},rel={"npc:drum-fel","subzone:hammerfall","topic:troll"},m={id=677},src={"In-game quest text: Call to Arms"},fv=true}
E["quest:678"] = {t="quest",n="Call to Arms",z="general",s="Call to Arms is a Horde martial trial issued by Drum Fel at the Hammerfall outpost in the Arathi Highlands. Having proven their basic combat readiness, adventurers are ordered southwest toward the dwarven bridge to engage the dangerous Boulderfist ogres. Eliminating both front-line brutes and spellcasting magi tests the warrior's resolve while protecting the Horde's vulnerable foothold in Arathi.",h="Call to Arms is a Horde martial trial issued by Drum Fel at the Hammerfall outpost in the Arathi Highlands.",sec={{t="A Test of Mettle",b="At the isolated Horde redoubt of Hammerfall, survival demands unwavering martial strength. Drum Fel, recognizing the growing capabilities of travelers aiding the outpost, refuses to let anyone rest on easy victories. Rather than facing minor skirmishers, warriors must prove their mettle against what Fel considers a genuine threat: the hulking Boulderfist ogres. These brutes have consistently harassed the Horde expedition and tested the resolve of Hammerfall’s defenders. Fel offers simple terms for the task—warriors are expected to return successful in flesh or honorably in spirit, with praise promised for sheer tenacity in battle.",sp=0},{t="The Boulderfist Threat",b="The Boulderfist clan has established an entrenched lair southwest of Hammerfall, extending their territory toward the grand dwarven bridge connecting Arathi to the Wetlands. Unlike undisciplined beasts, the ogres command both crushing physical power and lethal mystical arts, fielding durable brutes alongside cunning magi. Their aggressive presence poses a direct challenge to the Horde's supply lines and regional dominance. By ordering the destruction of ten Boulderfist brutes and four magi, Drum Fel seeks to blunt their strength, ensuring the Boulderfists understand that the warriors of Hammerfall will neither yield ground nor tolerate rivals in Arathi.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the Call to Arms quest?",a="The quest is offered by Drum Fel, an officer stationed within the fortified Horde outpost of Hammerfall in the northeastern Arathi Highlands. Seeking capable champions to safeguard the stronghold's fragile position, Fel tasks proven fighters with striking out against dangerous regional adversaries to demonstrate their martial discipline and raw strength.",al={"who gives call to arms","drum fel quest","call to arms questgiver"}},{q="Where is the Boulderfist lair located?",a="The Boulderfist ogre settlement lies to the southwest of Hammerfall, situated along the path leading toward the Thandol Span, the great dwarven bridge to the Wetlands. The ogres occupy earthen dens and earthen mounds in the highlands, where both their physical brawlers and spellcasters congregate in force.",al={"boulderfist location","where are the ogres","boulderfist lair arathi"}},{q="Which enemies need to be defeated for this task?",a="Drum Fel instructs warriors to eliminate ten Boulderfist Brutes and four Boulderfist Magi before returning to Hammerfall. The brutes rely on heavy melee assaults, while the magi support them from afar with arcane spells, making them significantly more dangerous than standard highway skirmishers in Arathi.",al={"call to arms kill count","which ogres to kill","call to arms targets"}},{q="Why are the Boulderfist ogres considered a threat to Hammerfall?",a="The Boulderfists have established a fortified foothold in Arathi and have repeatedly challenged the Horde garrison at Hammerfall. Their proximity to key transit routes toward the southern dwarven bridge threatens Horde scouting and supply lines. Drum Fel considers them a genuine rival force that must be actively contained through regular martial strikes.",al={"why fight boulderfists","are boulderfists hostile to horde","ogre threat in arathi"}},{q="What does Drum Fel mean by returning in body or in spirit?",a="Drum Fel’s grim remark reflects the uncompromising warrior ethos of the Horde in Arathi. He expects his fighters to succeed and return alive, but respects anyone who falls fighting valiantly against formidable foes. In his eyes, showing tenacity and fearlessness against overwhelming ogre strength brings honor, regardless of whether one survives the bloody encounter.",al={"drum fel quote","return in spirit meaning","come in body or spirit"}},{q="Can Alliance players undertake this quest?",a="No, this Call to Arms is strictly a Horde quest issued by Drum Fel inside Hammerfall. Alliance forces operate out of Refuge Pointe to the west and view both Hammerfall's defenders and the Boulderfist ogres as hostile enemies competing for dominance over the Arathi Highlands.",al={"is call to arms horde only","alliance call to arms arathi","can alliance do this quest"}},{q="Why does Drum Fel target both brutes and magi?",a="Targeting both types of ogres neutralizes the Boulderfists' combined-arms tactical advantage. The brutes form the muscular frontline that absorbs blows, while the magi wield destructive magic from the rear. Defeating both types ensures the clan cannot mount an organized counteroffensive against Hammerfall.",al={"why kill magi","boulderfist magi importance","boulderfist brute and magus"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Securing Hammerfall is vital for the Horde's foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms, and culling the aggressive Boulderfist ogres keeps the outpost from being overwhelmed.",["race:Orc"]="Hammerfall is named after Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer, giving orcs a deeply personal stake in driving out any rival brutes who threaten this memorial stronghold.",["class:Warrior"]="Drum Fel's demand for tenacity against brute force and arcane power offers warriors a direct test of combat endurance and martial honor.",["class:Mage"]="Confronting Boulderfist Magi allows mages to counter brute-force spellcasters who bend arcane energies to primitive, destructive ends."},kw={"arathi highlands","boulderfist","boulderfist brute","boulderfist magi","call to arms","drum fel","hammerfall","ogres","thandol span"},rel={"npc:drum-fel","subzone:hammerfall"},m={id=678},src={"In-game quest text: Call to Arms"},fv=true}
E["quest:684"] = {t="quest",n="WANTED: Marez Cowl",z="general",s="At Stromgarde, Marez Cowl advises Syndicate leaders and traffics with demons. Her influence threatens Alliance forces in the Arathi Highlands.",h="At Stromgarde, Marez Cowl advises Syndicate leaders and traffics with demons.",sec={{t="A Price on Syndicate Leadership",b="At Refuge Pointe, Alliance commanders offer a bounty on a high-ranking Syndicate leader. Marez Cowl advises the Syndicate and directs its work in Stromgarde’s ruins as a field operative. The bounty aims to break the group’s local command.",sp=0},{t="The Warlock of Stromgarde",b="Marez Cowl is a warlock among the Syndicate’s brigands and dispossessed nobles. She deals openly with demonic powers to enforce her will and defend Syndicate territory. To reach her lair in Stromgarde, you must face armed criminals and the demons she commands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Marez Cowl?",a="Marez Cowl is a high-ranking Syndicate advisor and operative. She wields influence in the organization and is a dangerous warlock who traffics with demons.",al={"who is marez cowl","marez cowl background","who is she"}},{q="Where is Marez Cowl located?",a="Marez Cowl is in the Syndicate-held part of ruined Stromgarde, in the Arathi Highlands. Criminal forces are entrenched there, and you must find your way through them to reach her lair.",al={"where to find marez cowl","marez cowl location","where is marez"}},{q="Who posted the bounty on Marez Cowl?",a="The Alliance military in the Arathi Highlands offers the bounty. Captain Nials, commander of the Alliance stronghold at Refuge Pointe, oversees it.",al={"who posted bounty marez cowl","who wants marez cowl dead","who pays bounty"}},{q="Why is there a bounty on her head?",a="Marez Cowl is wanted for her Syndicate leadership and her use of demonic summoning in Stromgarde. She threatens the Alliance garrison trying to reclaim the ruined kingdom.",al={"why is marez cowl wanted","why bounty on marez cowl","why kill marez cowl"}},{q="What kind of magic does Marez Cowl use?",a="Marez Cowl uses warlock magic to summon and bargain with demons. Take extreme care when confronting her and the demons she commands.",al={"what makes marez cowl dangerous","marez cowl powers","is marez cowl strong"}},{q="Where do you turn in the bounty for Marez Cowl?",a="After Marez Cowl is slain, deliver her head to Captain Nials at Refuge Pointe, the Alliance outpost in the Arathi Highlands.",al={"where turn in marez cowl bounty","where is captain nials","turn in marez head"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance garrison at Refuge Pointe, led by Captain Nials, fights to reclaim Stromgarde. High-ranking Syndicate leaders like Marez Cowl are strategic priorities.",["class:Warlock"]="Marez Cowl belongs to the Syndicate’s darker occult wing. Her use of demonic summoning shows how political renegades turn to demons to extend their influence.",["class:Paladin"]="As a warlock who deals openly with demons in the ruins of an ancient human kingdom, Cowl affronts the Holy Light and Stromgarde’s defense."},kw={"arathi highlands","bounty","captain nials","marez cowl","refuge pointe","stromgarde","syndicate","warlock"},rel={"subzone:refuge-pointe","topic:syndicate","topic:warlock"},m={id=684},src={"In-game quest text: WANTED: Marez Cowl"},fv=true}
E["quest:685"] = {t="quest",n="WANTED: Otto and Falconcrest",z="general",s="A bounty posted by the Stromgarde Militia marks Lord Falconcrest, leader of the local Syndicate forces, and his deadly bodyguard Otto for death. Eliminating them strikes a crucial blow against the criminal organization's foothold in the Arathi Highlands. Proof of their demise must be delivered to Captain Nials at Refuge Pointe.",h="A bounty posted by the Stromgarde Militia marks Lord Falconcrest, leader of the local Syndicate forces, and...",sec={{t="A Desperate Bounty",b="The Stromgarde Militia has issued an official bounty targeting two key figures in the Arathi Highlands: Lord Falconcrest and his ferocious bodyguard, Otto. Stationed out of Refuge Pointe, the beleaguered defenders of Stromgarde seek to break the Syndicate's hold over their ancestral lands. Hunters must bring the heads of both targets to Captain Nials to claim the reward.",sp=0},{t="Commander and Enforcer",b="Lord Falconcrest serves as the mastermind behind the Syndicate's coordinated push into the Arathi Highlands. His death represents a vital strategic victory for the Alliance, promising to severely disrupt the criminal network's operations in the region. Beside him stands Otto, a brutally effective bodyguard whose blades have already cut down dozens of Stromgarde's remaining militia soldiers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Lord Falconcrest?",a="Lord Falconcrest is a noble and commander heading the Syndicate's operations in the Arathi Highlands. His presence organizes their opportunistic grab for the broken kingdom of Stromgarde, making his removal a primary objective for the local human militia.",al={"who is lord falconcrest","falconcrest lore","who is falconcrest"}},{q="Who is Otto?",a="Otto is the deadly personal bodyguard of Lord Falconcrest. While not a strategic planner like his master, he is a ferocious combatant responsible for slaying dozens of Stromgarde defenders in the highlands.",al={"who is otto","falconcrest bodyguard","otto syndicate"}},{q="Why has the Stromgarde Militia placed a bounty on them?",a="The Stromgarde Militia posted bounties on them to cripple the Syndicate's leadership in the Arathi Highlands. Eliminating Falconcrest dismantles their regional command structure, while taking down Otto removes a lethal threat who has butchered many Alliance fighters.",al={"why bounty on falconcrest","why kill otto and falconcrest","stromgarde bounty"}},{q="Who offers the reward for Otto and Falconcrest?",a="The bounties for both Lord Falconcrest and Otto are collected by Captain Nials, an officer of the Stromgarde Militia stationed at Refuge Pointe in the Arathi Highlands.",al={"where turn in otto falconcrest","who is captain nials","falconcrest bounty turn in"}},{q="What is the Syndicate doing in the Arathi Highlands?",a="The Syndicate consists of fallen Alteraci nobles and rogue elements looking to claim land and riches amidst northern Lordaeron's collapse. In the Arathi Highlands, they have moved into the ruins of Stromgarde to wrest control from its surviving defenders.",al={"what is the syndicate arathi","syndicate role arathi"}},{q="What proof is required to claim the bounty?",a="To claim the reward, hunters must bring the severed heads of both Lord Falconcrest and Otto directly to Captain Nials at Refuge Pointe as undeniable proof of their demise.",al={"where to take otto head","proof of bounty"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Lord Falconcrest and his Syndicate operatives threaten the remnants of Stromgarde, making this bounty vital to securing Refuge Pointe and reclaiming the highlands.",["race:Human"]="For the defenders of Stromgarde, cutting down traitorous human nobles in the Syndicate is a personal fight to avenge their fallen kingdom."},kw={"arathi highlands","bounty","captain nials","falconcrest","otto","refuge pointe","stromgarde militia","syndicate"},rel={"topic:syndicate","subzone:refuge-pointe","zone:arathi"},m={id=685},src={"In-game quest text: WANTED: Otto and Falconcrest"},fv=true}
E["quest:701"] = {t="quest",n="Guile of the Raptor",z="general",s="Guile of the Raptor is a quest in which Tor'gan seeks to awaken the dormant warrior spirit within Gor'mul. By harvesting the hearts of Highland Fleshstalkers, Tor'gan plans to fashion an enchanted item imbued with the cunning and predatory power of the raptor.",h="Guile of the Raptor is a quest in which Tor'gan seeks to awaken the dormant warrior spirit within Gor'mul.",sec={{t="Awakening the Dormant Warrior",b="Tor'gan works to restore a listless compatriot named Gor'mul, whose warrior spirit has fallen dormant. Rather than relying on simple persuasion, Tor'gan turns to primal magic to shock Gor'mul back into action. By crafting a charm imbued with the essence of wild predators, he hopes to reintroduce Gor'mul to martial resolve and demonstrate the strength of their new power.",sp=0},{t="The Heart of the Predator",b="To channel the predatory instincts of the wild, Tor'gan requires the hearts of Highland Fleshstalkers. In shamanic and primal traditions, the heart of a cunning hunter carries its ferocity and guile. Tor'gan weaves both a physical token and a spell from these trophies, aiming to infuse Gor'mul directly with the predator's cunning so he can rediscover his true calling.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tor'gan?",a="Tor'gan is a shamanistic or spiritual figure seeking to aid Gor'mul. Believing that Gor'mul has lost touch with his inner strength, Tor'gan gathers reagents to craft an item imbued with beast magic to reawaken him.",al={"who is tor'gan","torgan","quest giver"}},{q="Who is Gor'mul?",a="Gor'mul is an individual who has lost his fighting spirit and let the warrior within him go dormant. Tor'gan intends to infuse him with primal predator essence so he can witness new power and rise to fight again.",al={"who is gormul","why help gormul","dormant warrior"}},{q="Why does Tor'gan need raptor hearts?",a="Tor'gan requires twelve raptor hearts to fashion both a physical talisman and the enchantment needed to imbue Gor'mul. Primal magic often uses the hearts of predators to channel their inherent instincts, guile, and predatory power.",al={"why raptor hearts","what are hearts for","raptor magic"}},{q="Where do the raptor hearts come from?",a="The required hearts are taken from Highland Fleshstalkers, predatory raptors known for their relentless hunting prowess and cunning. Tor'gan considers their hearts potent enough to fuel the awakening spell.",al={"where to get hearts","which raptors","highland fleshstalkers"}},{q="What does the guile of the raptor signify?",a="The guile of the raptor represents the cunning, swift reflexes, and lethal instinct of the predator. Tor'gan believes channeling this specific nature will cut through Gor'mul's lethargy and revive his combat instincts.",al={"what is guile of the raptor","what power","raptor guile meaning"}},{q="What is the ultimate goal of the ritual?",a="Tor'gan crafts the item and weaves the spell using the gathered hearts, aiming to demonstrate new power to Gor'mul and awaken his dormant warrior identity so he can reclaim his path.",al={"is gormul cured","what happens after ritual","does it work"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Tor'gan seeks to reignite the dormant warrior spirit in Gor'mul, using the cunning and ferocity of raptors to remind him of martial strength.",["faction:Horde"]="This ritual reflects Horde traditions of drawing upon primal beasts and natural spirits to restore purpose to a listless or troubled comrade."},kw={"dormant warrior","gor'mul","guile of the raptor","highland fleshstalkers","raptor hearts","tor'gan","warrior"},rel={"topic:warrior"},m={id=701},src={"In-game quest text: Guile of the Raptor"},fv=true}
E["quest:736"] = {t="quest",n="The Star, the Hand and the Heart",z="general",s="The Star, the Hand and the Heart is an Undercity quest wherein Keeper Bel'dugur tests an aspiring leader seeking to establish a new chapter of their order. To earn the essential Yagyin's Digest, the petitioner must track down three items of power whose names Bel'dugur dares not speak aloud.",h="The Star, the Hand and the Heart is an Undercity quest wherein Keeper Bel'dugur tests an aspiring leader...",sec={{t="A Test of Brotherhood",b="Within the shadowed corridors of the Undercity, ambitious practitioners seeking to lead their own circle turn to Keeper Bel'dugur. To establish a new flock and open a chapter of their order, an aspiring leader requires Yagyin's Digest, an authoritative text of ritual lore guarded by the keeper. Bel'dugur stands ready to support his fellow brother, but demands proof of worthiness before yielding such authority.",sp=0},{t="The Unspoken Relics",b="Keeper Bel'dugur will not part with Yagyin's Digest lightly. He requires three distinct items of power to fulfill his side of the exchange. These relics carry such perilous or sacred significance that their names cannot even be spoken aloud; Bel'dugur instead pens them onto a private note. Only a practitioner capable of tracking down and retrieving all three unspoken artifacts proves worthy of founding their own chapter.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Keeper Bel'dugur?",a="Keeper Bel'dugur is an occult figure residing in the Undercity who assists members of his dark brotherhood. He guards potent lore and requires candidates to prove their competence before entrusting them with sacred texts.",al={"who is keeper bel'dugur","bel'dugur role","beldugur undercity"}},{q="What is Yagyin's Digest?",a="Yagyin's Digest is a specialized instructional text necessary for founding and leading a new chapter or gathering a fresh flock within a secretive occult order.",al={"what is yagyin's digest","yagyins digest purpose","yagyin digest book"}},{q="What items does Keeper Bel'dugur ask for?",a="Bel'dugur requires three specific items of power. Because their names are too forbidden or perilous to utter aloud, he sets them down quietly in a written note for the petitioner to recover.",al={"what items does bel'dugur need","beldugurs note items","three items of power"}},{q="Why are the names of the requested items not spoken aloud?",a="Keeper Bel'dugur explicitly notes that he cannot utter the names of these three relics aloud, indicating they possess dark, sacred, or volatile power that demands discretion.",al={"why can't bel'dugur say the item names","cannot utter names","unspoken item names"}},{q="What does it mean to gather another flock?",a="Gathering a flock refers to recruiting followers and establishing a functioning independent chapter of their brotherhood, proving one's readiness to guide other practitioners of the craft.",al={"what does gathering a flock mean","opening a chapter","our order chapter"}},{q="Where is this quest centered?",a="The quest centers around Keeper Bel'dugur, who is stationed within the subterranean Forsaken capital of the Undercity.",al={"where does this quest take place","bel'dugur location","quest hand in location"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="Acquiring Yagyin's Digest to establish a new flock represents a pivotal step in expanding the reach and occult mastery of your dark order.",["race:Undead"]="Operating out of the Undercity, Keeper Bel'dugur guides Forsaken practitioners seeking to delve deeper into forbidden rites under the city's shadowed spires."},kw={"bel'dugur's note","keeper bel'dugur","order","the star the hand and the heart","three items of power","undercity","yagyin's digest"},rel={"npc:keeper-bel-dugur","zone:undercity"},m={id=736},src={"In-game quest text: The Star, the Hand and the Heart"},fv=true}
E["quest:744"] = {t="quest",n="Preparation for Ceremony",z="general",s="Eyahn Eagletalon is making a headdress for his brother, who soon stands before Chief Cairne Bloodhoof. He asks you to gather the vibrant feathers from harpies beyond Thunder Bluff while he tans the leather bands.",h="Eyahn Eagletalon is making a headdress for his brother, who soon stands before Chief Cairne Bloodhoof.",sec={{t="Honoring the Chieftain",b="Among the tauren, standing before Chief Cairne Bloodhoof brings pride and reverence. Families honor the occasion by preparing proper attire. The traditional headdress is made from treated leather and symbolic plumage, with each part chosen to show dignity and respect before the leader of the united tribes.",sp=0},{t="Feathers of the Crags",b="A ceremonial headdress needs a leather band and feathers for its crown. While the leather straps are tanned, long, pristine feathers must be gathered. Harpies roost in the craggy hills north and northwest of Thunder Bluff, so you must venture into their hostile nests to find feathers of the needed size and color.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What ceremony is being prepared for?",a="The ceremony marks a tauren appearing before Chief Cairne Bloodhoof. His sibling is making a headdress from tanned leather and vibrant feathers for the occasion.",al={"what ceremony is this","what is the ceremony","who stands before bloodhoof"}},{q="Who is Chief Bloodhoof?",a="Chief Cairne Bloodhoof leads the united tauren tribes from Thunder Bluff. Standing before him is an honor for any tauren.",al={"who is chief bloodhoof","cairne bloodhoof","who is bloodhoof"}},{q="Why are harpy feathers needed for the headdress?",a="The headdress needs feathers of a particular size and color. Its design calls for azure and bronze plumage while the leather straps are tanned.",al={"why do we need harpy feathers","why harpy feathers","why feathers"}},{q="Where are the harpies located?",a="The harpies live in rugged lands north and northwest of Thunder Bluff, in Mulgore.",al={"where do harpies live","where to find harpies","where are azure and bronze feathers"}},{q="What role do harpies play in Mulgore?",a="Harpies are vicious, avian-humanoid creatures that nest in Mulgore’s rocky crags. They threaten travelers and the tribes, while their large, colorful plumage provides material for ceremonial ornaments.",al={"why are harpies hunted","what are harpies"}},{q="Who is crafting the ceremonial headdress?",a="Eyahn Eagletalon tans the leather straps and makes the headdress for his brother, who is preparing to appear before the chieftain.",al={"who makes the headdress","who crafts the headdress"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know that natural headdresses are bound to honor and rites of passage before the high chieftain. Helping make such regalia shows the tribes’ shared pride and spirit.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you honor the customs of your tauren allies atop Thunder Bluff’s mesas when you help with their ceremonial duties."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","ceremony","feathers","harpy","headdress","mulgore","preparation for ceremony","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff","topic:harpy"},m={id=744},src={"In-game quest text: Preparation for Ceremony"},fv=true}
E["quest:750"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunt Continues",z="general",s="The Hunt Continues is an early rite of passage assigned by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache. It tasks aspiring hunters with gathering mountain cougar pelts from the southern hills to clothe the tribe's children and mend their tents.",h="The Hunt Continues is an early rite of passage assigned by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="The Purpose of the Hunt",b="In Camp Narache, young hunters are taught that taking a beast is never an idle pursuit or a search for trophies. Grull Hawkwind charges fledgling adventurers with venturing into the southern hills of Red Cloud Mesa to track down mountain cougars. Rather than proving martial prowess alone, the task emphasizes responsibility to the community, showing that true hunters must provide essential provisions for their people.",sp=0},{t="Shelter for the Tribe",b="Life on the mesa demands durable materials to withstand the open elements. Grull explains that the cougar hides are urgently needed to sew clothing for the tribe's children and to mend worn hide tents throughout Camp Narache. Proving oneself capable of securing these prized pelts earns the gratitude of the elders, and Grull promises to bring word of the hunter's selfless contributions directly to his father, Chief Hawkwind.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does the tribe need mountain cougar pelts?",a="The pelts of the mountain cougars are vital practical resources for Camp Narache. Grull Hawkwind explains that the hides are needed to craft warm clothing for the tribe's children and to repair and reinforce the village's tents.",al={"why hunt cougars","what are cougar pelts for","mountain cougar pelts use"}},{q="Who offers this quest?",a="The hunt is assigned by Grull Hawkwind, a prominent hunter in Camp Narache and the son of Chief Hawkwind. He guides novice adventurers in learning the essential duties of supporting the settlement.",al={"who gives the hunt continues","quest giver camp narache"}},{q="Where are the mountain cougars found?",a="The mountain cougars prowl the rocky ridges and rolling hills lying just to the south of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where are the cougars","mountain cougars location","where to find mountain cougar"}},{q="What is Grull Hawkwind's connection to Chief Hawkwind?",a="Grull Hawkwind is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the tribal leader at Camp Narache. Grull promises to speak to his father on your behalf and share word of your generous deeds if you supply the needed pelts.",al={"how to impress chief hawkwind","chief hawkwind son"}},{q="What does this hunt teach about tauren tradition?",a="Among the tauren, hunting is never treated as sport or trophy taking. Every beast taken from the land is meant to sustain the community, honoring the plains by using hide, meat, and bone to preserve the tribe's survival.",al={"tauren hunting philosophy","why hunt in mulgore"}},{q="Why are mountain cougar pelts considered highly valued?",a="Cougar pelts are thick, durable, and well suited to withstand the elements on the windswept plains. Because they provide warmth for young children and material strong enough to patch dwellings, they are prized by village elders.",al={"are cougar pelts valuable","why are cougar pelts prized"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren coming of age in Red Cloud Mesa, learning that every part of a beast must serve the tribe—from food to clothing and shelter—is an essential rite of passage.",["class:Hunter"]="This task reinforces the core hunting philosophy of the plains: stalking beasts with purpose, taking only what is needed, and never viewing animals as simple sport or trophies."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mountain cougar pelts","red cloud mesa","tauren hunting","the hunt continues"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","npc:chief-hawkwind"},m={id=750},src={"In-game quest text: The Hunt Continues"},fv=true}
E["quest:755"] = {t="quest",n="Rites of the Earthmother",z="general",s="Rites of the Earthmother marks the beginning of an initiate's spiritual journey on Red Cloud Mesa. Directed by Chief Hawkwind, travelers seek out Seer Graytongue in the southern hills of Camp Narache. Undertaking these venerable ceremonies is considered a vital trial of passage for tauren coming of age.",h="Rites of the Earthmother marks the beginning of an initiate's spiritual journey on Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="The Threshold of the Trials",b="Upon the Red Cloud Mesa, Chief Hawkwind directs young tauren seeking to prove themselves to Seer Graytongue. Residing in the quiet hills directly south of Camp Narache, Graytongue oversees the spiritual beginnings of the youth. This simple journey marks the formal threshold between everyday camp life and the serious commitments demanded by tribal custom.",sp=0},{t="Embracing the Earthmother",b="The Earthmother is central to the creation beliefs and everyday existence of the tauren. Her rites serve as sacred trials of maturity, resilience, and spiritual depth. As Seer Graytongue cautions all who arrive at his sanctuary, embarking upon the Rites of the Earthmother is no light endeavor, requiring total dedication to the traditions of the mesa.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The quest is initiated by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache, who directs young adventurers to seek out Seer Graytongue in the surrounding hills.",al={"who starts rites of the earthmother","quest giver"}},{q="Where can I find Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue dwells in the hills located directly south of Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore.",al={"where is seer graytongue","graytongue location","find graytongue"}},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are traditional sacred trials of passage undertaken by tauren youths to prove their wisdom, strength, and devotion to their people and beliefs.",al={"what are rites of the earthmother","earthmother trials lore"}},{q="Who is Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue is a respected spiritual elder residing south of Camp Narache who oversees and guides initiates through the sacred trials of the Earthmother.",al={"who is seer graytongue","graytongue role"}},{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the tribal leader presiding over Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa, tasked with preparing young tauren before sending them to the seers.",al={"who is chief hawkwind","hawkwind role"}},{q="What is required to complete this task?",a="You only need to journey south from Camp Narache into the hills and speak with Seer Graytongue to report your readiness for the trials.",al={"what do i need to do","how to complete quest"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rites of the Earthmother represent the foundational spiritual journey for young tauren, testing their readiness to serve their tribe and revere the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="Understanding these sacred trials offers insight into the spiritual heart of the tauren allies who have recently joined the Horde.",["class:Shaman"]="For aspiring shamans, undertaking rites blessed by tribal seers is an essential initial step toward communion with the elements and the spirits of Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="Druidic candidates begin their path of harmony with nature by respecting the sacred guidance of the tribal elders and seers."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","earthmother","red cloud mesa","rites of the earthmother","seer graytongue","tauren"},rel={"npc:seer-graytongue","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","quest:763"},m={id=755},src={"In-game quest text: Rites of the Earthmother"},fv=true}
E["quest:7792"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="In Darnassus, wool cloth is gathered to support the city's stores. Your sixty pieces earn recognition for your generosity.",h="In Darnassus, wool cloth is gathered to support the city's stores.",sec={{t="Wool Stores",b="Darnassus needs sixty pieces of wool cloth from you. Others across the realm are expected to provide the rest of its stores. Your donation earns full recognition for your generosity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Darnassus need?",a="Darnassus needs wool cloth to support its stores.",al={"wool donation","what cloth does Darnassus want"}},{q="How much wool do I need to donate?",a="You need to donate sixty pieces of wool cloth.",al={"how many wool","sixty wool"}},{q="Why is Darnassus collecting wool?",a="Darnassus is gathering wool cloth to support its stores.",al={"why wool","wool drive"}},{q="What do I get for donating wool?",a="Your donation of sixty pieces earns full recognition from Darnassus for your generosity.",al={"wool donation recognition","donate wool reward"}}},kw={"darnassus","donation","recognition","stores","wool","wool cloth"},rel={"zone:darnassus"},m={id=7792},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:7802"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="At Ironforge, wool donations help support its stores. Sixty pieces bring full recognition for your generosity.",h="At Ironforge, wool donations help support its stores.",sec={{t="Wool Stores",b="Ironforge asks you for sixty pieces of wool cloth. Its stores need only that much from you. Others are expected to give enough to support the drive.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How much wool does Ironforge need from me?",a="Sixty pieces of wool cloth, for Ironforge.",al={"wool donation amount","how many wool","sixty wool"}},{q="What do I get for donating wool?",a="Give Ironforge sixty pieces of wool cloth, and you earn full recognition for your generosity.",al={"wool donation reward","what for wool","ironforge recognition"}},{q="Why is Ironforge collecting wool?",a="Ironforge gathers wool cloth to support its stores.",al={"wool drive","why wool donation","ironforge wool"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, your wool donation earns recognition from Ironforge."},kw={"donation","ironforge","recognition","sixty pieces","stores","wool","wool cloth"},rel={"zone:ironforge"},m={id=7802},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:7807"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="Across the realm, A Donation of Wool calls for aid to the Gnomeregan Exiles. Driven from Gnomeregan, they need wool cloth to sustain their displaced people. Sixty pieces earn formal recognition among the gnomes.",h="Across the realm, A Donation of Wool calls for aid to the Gnomeregan Exiles.",sec={{t="A Displaced People's Needs",b="Gnomeregan's fall to trogg invaders and radioactive contamination drove its surviving gnomes from their high-tech city. They now live as refugees, hosted chiefly by their dwarven cousins in Ironforge.\n\nOutside their ancestral halls, the Gnomeregan Exiles face severe strain in supplying their people. Regular drives for cloth and other basics help keep them clothed, supplied, and organized.",sp=0},{t="The Textile Drive",b="The drive asks each volunteer for exactly sixty pieces of wool cloth. By gathering modest contributions from across the realm, the exiles can collect the textiles they need for bedding, bandages, and clothing without overburdening any one supplier. Those who aid their recovery receive formal recognition among their people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do the Gnomeregan Exiles need wool cloth?",a="The Gnomeregan Exiles are gathering basic textile supplies. Driven from their home city, their refugee population needs wool cloth to sustain its people and ongoing operations.",al={"why wool","what is the wool for","wool donation purpose"}},{q="How much wool cloth is needed?",a="The drive asks each contributor for sixty pieces of wool cloth. The organizers count on contributions from across the realm to meet their supply needs.",al={"how much wool","donation amount","wool required"}},{q="Who is organizing this donation drive?",a="Representatives of the Gnomeregan Exiles organize the collection and receive donations for the displaced gnomes sheltering among their allies.",al={"who takes donation","who is accepting wool","donation recipient"}},{q="Can you donate wool cloth more than once?",a="Each donor is asked for one contribution of sixty pieces. The exiles need that fixed amount from you and rely on contributors across the realm to meet their quota.",al={"can i donate again","is it repeatable","more wool donations"}},{q="What recognition do the Gnomeregan Exiles offer?",a="Donating sixty pieces earns recognition and gratitude from the Gnomeregan Exiles. It shows your loyalty and support for their displaced people in a time of crisis.",al={"gnomeregan exiles standing","gnome reputation","exile recognition"}},{q="Why are the gnomes currently in exile?",a="The gnomes fled their subterranean home in Gnomeregan after a catastrophic disaster and trogg invasion. As refugees, they depend on temporary encampments, aid from allies, and material drives.",al={"why are gnomes in exile","where is gnomeregan","gnome refugee crisis"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Contributing raw materials strengthens Alliance solidarity and helps sustain a vital ally displaced from its mountain home.",["race:Gnome"]="For exiled gnomes, gathering wool helps resupply your displaced kin as they survive and rebuild outside Gnomeregan.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Tailors who gather or weave wool cloth have an ample supply of textiles that refugee populations need for basic garments and supplies."},kw={"cloth drive","donation of wool","gnome","gnomeregan","gnomeregan exiles","textiles","wool cloth"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan"},m={id=7807},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:78124"] = {t="quest",n="Nar'thalas Almanac",z="general",s="Scholars seek the Nar'thalas Almanac to preserve ancient magical study. Its recovery brings forgotten lore into a library where researchers can study it.",h="Scholars seek the Nar'thalas Almanac to preserve ancient magical study.",sec={{t="A Relic of Ancient Study",b="A rare work of arcane study, the Nar'thalas Almanac comes from the ancient traditions of pre-Sundering night elven scholarship. For caretakers of great archives, securing the volume rescues centuries-old lore from obscurity and adds an important piece of history to their shelves. Those who uncover neglected works help bridge forgotten antiquity and modern scholarship.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Archives",b="Across Azeroth, archivists and researchers seek lost treatises, field notes, and chronicles. Bringing the Nar'thalas Almanac to a dedicated library lets its keepers safeguard, catalog, and study it, rather than leave it to rot in ruins or fall into careless hands. Each recovered volume offers fresh insight into the arcane arts of past eras.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Nar'thalas Almanac?",a="The Nar'thalas Almanac is a rare ancient manuscript of magical scholarship sought by curators for their historical archives. Those who recover it place its insights in scholarly care, where they can be preserved and cataloged.",al={"what is the narthalas almanac","narthalas almanac quest","almanac info"}},{q="Who is collecting the Nar'thalas Almanac?",a="Library scholars interested in ancient magical texts and historical archives collect the almanac. Curators actively seek additions to expand their shared knowledge of the world's arcane heritage.",al={"who wants the almanac","where does the almanac go","almanac turn in"}},{q="What does the name Nar'thalas refer to?",a="Nar'thalas was an ancient Kaldorei center of learning and magical study before the Great Sundering. Texts bearing its name preserve fragments of pre-Sundering arcane arts and elven knowledge.",al={"what is narthalas","narthalas lore","where is narthalas"}},{q="Why are magical texts being collected for the library?",a="Historical records and magical treatises were displaced, lost, or buried in ruins across Azeroth. Scholars study recovered writings to broaden their understanding of arcane currents and guard fragile records against being lost entirely.",al={"why collect magical works","library purpose","why books matter"}},{q="Can other magical works be brought to the library?",a="Treatises, tomes, and forgotten manuscripts can shed light on arcane theory and ancient history. Scholars continually seek additions to enrich their shelves.",al={"more magical books","other tomes","more texts"}},{q="Is the Nar'thalas Almanac dangerous to handle?",a="The almanac is a compiled record of study, not a volatile forbidden artifact. Historians and practitioners can use it as a valuable reference on historical magic.",al={"is the almanac dangerous","does almanac have dark magic","safe to read"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you know the scholarly value of texts on ancient arcane traditions. Adding recovered works to a library's magical collection matters deeply.",["race:NightElf"]="As a Night Elf, you know Nar'thalas as an ancient center of Kaldorei arcane scholarship before the Sundering shattered the old world. An almanac bearing its name links you to ancient night elven history.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance adventurer, you can help scholars and curators in Alliance archives seek rare manuscripts and expand the realm's gathered lore."},kw={"almanac","arcane","book","library","magical works","nar'thalas","narthalas","tome"},m={id=78124},src={"In-game quest text: Nar'thalas Almanac"},fv=true}
E["quest:78127"] = {t="quest",n="The Dalaran Digest",z="general",s="The librarian asks you to bring magical works for the collection. Each book adds knowledge to the library.",h="The librarian asks you to bring magical works for the collection.",sec={{t="The Collection",b="The librarian welcomes magical works that may benefit the library. You bring a book to join the collection, helping its knowledge grow.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the librarian want?",a="The librarian asks you to bring magical works that may benefit the collection.",al={"what to bring","library book","magical works"}},{q="Where do I bring the book?",a="Bring the book to the librarian who asked you to add it to the collection.",al={"where is the librarian","library location"}},{q="Why is the librarian collecting magical works?",a="Your contribution brings more knowledge to the collection.",al={"why collect books","library purpose"}}},kw={"collection","dalaran digest","knowledge","librarian","library","magical works"},m={id=78127},src={"In-game quest text: The Dalaran Digest"},fv=true}
E["quest:78142"] = {t="quest",n="Bewitchments and Glamours",z="general",s="In Ironforge, the library is seeking magical works for its shelves. You can help its keepers widen their knowledge by bringing in books that may benefit the collection.",h="In Ironforge, the library is seeking magical works for its shelves.",sec={{t="A Growing Collection",b="Bring magical works to the library in Ironforge, where each addition can broaden what its keepers know.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the library want?",a="The library seeks magical works that may benefit its collection.",al={"what do they want","library books","magical works"}},{q="Where do I bring the magical works?",a="Bring the magical works to the library in Ironforge.",al={"where bring books","where is the library","ironforge library"}},{q="Why are they collecting magical works?",a="The library says the works will expand its knowledge.",al={"why collect magic books","what is the collection for"}}},kw={"bewitchments and glamours","books","collection","ironforge","library","magical works"},rel={"subzone:library-ironforge"},m={id=78142},src={"In-game quest text: Bewitchments and Glamours"},fv=true}
E["quest:78143"] = {t="quest",n="Secrets of the Dreamers",z="general",s="A library's scholars seek writings tied to the Dreamers and other magical lore. Travelers bring forgotten works from across the realm, where they can be studied and kept in the archives.",h="A library's scholars seek writings tied to the Dreamers and other magical lore.",sec={{t="Recovering Forgotten Lore",b="Scholars across the realm depend on travelers to recover rare magical texts and historical treatises. These works lie in ancient ruins or private hoards, far from the archives. So adventurers carry them safely to those who study Azeroth's arcane mysteries and preserve its lore.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Archives",b="When travelers bring in treatises, archivists add them to the library's permanent collection. Researchers seek obscure writings on the Emerald Dream and arcane theory. Their work continues, and they welcome further magical texts found in the field.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the quest Secrets of the Dreamers?",a="Travelers recover magical writings and obscure knowledge, then bring them to a library's scholars to enrich the archives.",al={"what is secrets of the dreamers","secrets of dreamers quest","what do you do in this quest"}},{q="Who collects these magical texts?",a="A scholar overseeing a library's collection gathers the texts, asking travelers to bring in magical works.",al={"who wants secrets of the dreamers","who gives this quest","where to turn in secrets of the dreamers"}},{q="Where are the recovered works stored?",a="The writings go into an institutional library's growing archives, where Alliance scholars and researchers can catalog and preserve rare lore and magical discoveries.",al={"what is the collection","which library is this for","where do the books go"}},{q="What does the title refer to by \"the Dreamers\"?",a="The Dreamers refers to druids attuned to the Emerald Dream or ancient sleeping entities bound to nature's slumber. Writings bearing their secrets preserve mysterious primal power.",al={"who are the dreamers","dreamers lore","why is it called dreamers"}},{q="Can you bring more magical works after finishing the quest?",a="The archivists welcome further contributions. When you find magical writings that may aid the collection, you can bring them in for continued research.",al={"can i turn in more books","is this repeatable","more magical works"}},{q="Why is collecting these magical writings important to scholars?",a="Scholars seek to widen their understanding of arcane, natural, and obscure lore. Lost or scattered treatises may hold insights into the world's deeper history and mysteries.",al={"why gather magical books","why does the library need magic tomes"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you can seek obscure tomes and magical texts to enrich scholarly archives.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, writings tied to nature magic and the Emerald Dream hold deep meaning in the Cenarion arts.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance scholars, especially those who keep great repositories, depend on champions to recover lost knowledge from dangerous places."},kw={"arcane","dreamers","library","magical works","scholars","secrets of the dreamers","tomes"},m={id=78143},src={"In-game quest text: Secrets of the Dreamers"},fv=true}
E["quest:78146"] = {t="quest",n="Goaz Scrolls",z="general",s="The librarian asks you to bring Goaz Scrolls for the collection. Your contribution adds to the library’s knowledge.",h="The librarian asks you to bring Goaz Scrolls for the collection.",sec={{t="A Growing Collection",b="The librarian thanks you for contributing Goaz Scrolls. More magical works could help the library too, and the librarian welcomes them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Goaz Scrolls for?",a="Goaz Scrolls add to the library’s collection and knowledge.",al={"goaz scrolls purpose","why bring scrolls","scrolls library"}},{q="Who asks for the Goaz Scrolls?",a="A librarian asks you to bring Goaz Scrolls for the collection.",al={"who wants goaz scrolls","scrolls giver","library person"}},{q="Where are the scrolls going?",a="The Goaz Scrolls are for the library’s collection.",al={"where bring scrolls","goaz scrolls location","library scrolls"}},{q="What else would benefit the library?",a="The librarian welcomes other magical works that might benefit the library.",al={"other magical works","more library books","what else to bring"}}},kw={"collection","goaz scrolls","knowledge","library","magical works","scrolls"},rel={"subzone:library-ironforge"},m={id=78146},src={"In-game quest text: Goaz Scrolls"},fv=true}
E["quest:7820"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="Thunder Bluff seeks wool cloth, and you can earn its full recognition by giving sixty pieces. Other donors are expected to bring enough supplies to support the drive.",h="Thunder Bluff seeks wool cloth, and you can earn its full recognition by giving sixty pieces.",sec={{t="A Cloth Donation",b="Thunder Bluff is gathering wool cloth. Give sixty pieces to earn full recognition for your generosity. Other donors in the realm are expected to bring enough to support the drive.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Thunder Bluff want donated?",a="Thunder Bluff is accepting wool cloth donations.",al={"wool donation","what cloth do they need","donate wool"}},{q="How much wool cloth should I give?",a="Give sixty pieces of wool cloth to earn full recognition from Thunder Bluff.",al={"how many wool","sixty cloth","donation amount"}},{q="What do I get for donating wool?",a="Your gift of sixty pieces of wool cloth earns full recognition from Thunder Bluff.",al={"wool donation recognition","what's the recognition","donation reward"}},{q="Why is Thunder Bluff collecting wool?",a="Thunder Bluff expects other donors in the realm to provide enough wool cloth to support the donation drive.",al={"wool drive why","what is wool for","why collect cloth"}}},kw={"cloth drive","donation","recognition","sixty pieces","thunder bluff","wool","wool cloth"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=7820},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:7826"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="In Orgrimmar, A Donation of Wool asks for help gathering cloth. The city has wool in its stores and expects more donors to bring in enough for the drive.",h="In Orgrimmar, A Donation of Wool asks for help gathering cloth.",sec={{t="Wool for Orgrimmar",b="Orgrimmar asks you to give sixty pieces of wool cloth. In return, the city promises recognition. Its stores already hold some wool, and other donors are expected to help support the drive.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Orgrimmar asking for?",a="Orgrimmar is accepting donations of wool cloth.",al={"what do they want","wool donation"}},{q="How much wool do I need to donate?",a="Orgrimmar asks for sixty pieces of wool cloth.",al={"how many wool","sixty wool"}},{q="What do I get for donating wool?",a="You earn full recognition from Orgrimmar for donating sixty pieces of wool cloth.",al={"donation recognition","wool reward"}},{q="Why is Orgrimmar collecting wool?",a="Orgrimmar is gathering wool to support its donation drive.",al={"wool drive","why wool"}},{q="Does Orgrimmar already have wool?",a="Yes. Orgrimmar has wool in its stores and expects other donors to bring in enough for the drive.",al={"wool stores","how much wool"}}},kw={"a donation of wool","donation","orgrimmar","recognition","sixty pieces","wool","wool cloth"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=7826},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:7833"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="A Darkspear troll gathers wool donations, and your sixty pieces of Wool Cloth join a larger collection.",h="A Darkspear troll gathers wool donations, and your sixty pieces of Wool Cloth join a larger collection.",sec={{t="A Shared Collection",b="The Darkspear trolls are gathering Wool Cloth to replenish their stores. The collector can take your sixty pieces now, while help from others is expected to complete the collection.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is being collected?",a="The Darkspear trolls are gathering Wool Cloth.",al={"what do they need","wool donation"}},{q="How much Wool Cloth do they need from me?",a="The collector needs sixty pieces of Wool Cloth from you.",al={"how much wool","60 cloth"}},{q="Why are the trolls collecting wool?",a="The trolls are gathering wool to replenish their stores.",al={"why wool","what for"}},{q="Can I give the cloth now?",a="Yes. The collector can accept your sixty pieces of Wool Cloth now.",al={"where give wool","donate now"}}},kw={"collection","darkspear","donation","stores","trolls","wool","wool cloth"},m={id=7833},src={"In-game quest text: Spendet Wolle"},fv=true}
E["quest:789"] = {t="quest",n="Sting of the Scorpid",z="general",s="Sting of the Scorpid is an early trial assigned by Gornek to Horde recruits at the Den in the Valley of Trials. Novices are challenged to hunt venomous scorpid workers northwest of camp and return ten tails to prove their combat readiness. The task serves as a vital lesson in surviving battlefield hazards and respecting even the smallest adversaries.",h="Sting of the Scorpid is an early trial assigned by Gornek to Horde recruits at the Den in the Valley of...",sec={{t="Trial of the Stinger",b="In the sun-baked canyon of the Valley of Trials, Gornek tests novice Horde recruits by sending them against the local wildlife. The venomous scorpids roaming the arid dirt northwest of the Den have humbled seasoned warriors and clumsy initiates alike. To prove their battlefield competence, young champions must venture into the rocks, slay scorpid workers, and return with ten of their severed tails as proof of prowess.",sp=0},{t="Venom and Vigilance",b="While deadly, the venomous predators also supply the very salvation of injured initiates. The Den maintains stockpiles of antivenom brewed directly from extracted scorpid venom to treat fallen novices. Beyond surviving the venom, Gornek emphasizes a core tactical truth: in the chaos of battle, arrogance is fatal, and even the smallest foe wields enough lethal venom to bring down a mighty warrior.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who assigns Sting of the Scorpid?",a="Gornek, the battlemaster overseeing novice training at the Den in the Valley of Trials, tasks young warriors and adventurers with this trial.",al={"who gives sting of the scorpid","scorpid quest giver","gornek quest"}},{q="Where are the scorpids located?",a="Scorpid workers dwell in large numbers directly northwest of the Den in the Valley of Trials, roaming the arid red canyon terrain.",al={"where to find scorpids","scorpid location","valley of trials scorpids"}},{q="What does Gornek require from the hunt?",a="Gornek demands ten Scorpid Worker Tails as tangible proof that a recruit has faced the venomous beasts and emerged victorious in battle.",al={"what do scorpids drop","scorpid quest item","scorpid tails"}},{q="How is the antidote for scorpid venom created?",a="The antidote for scorpid stings is brewed directly from venom extracted from the creatures' own stingers. The Den keeps ample stores on hand to treat novices who underestimate their prey.",al={"scorpid venom cure","antidote lore","how is scorpid antidote made"}},{q="What lesson does Gornek want recruits to learn?",a="Gornek uses the hunt to teach novice fighters that any opponent, large or small, possesses lethal potential. Overconfidence in battle against seemingly minor creatures can quickly prove fatal.",al={"gornek lesson","why hunt scorpids","scorpid trial purpose"}},{q="Does Gornek offer further training after this hunt?",a="Upon delivering the tails, Gornek declares he has nothing left to teach you at the Den, sending you forward into Durotar to continue your service to the Horde.",al={"what after sting of the scorpid","gornek next quest","does gornek teach more"}}},ang={["race:Orc"]="For young orcs coming of age in the Valley of Trials, hunting venomous scorpids is an essential trial of endurance and battlefield awareness overseen by Gornek.",["race:Troll"]="Darkspear trolls training alongside the Horde in the Valley of Trials must also prove their prowess against the local scorpids before venturing deeper into Durotar.",["class:Warrior"]="Gornek treats the scorpid hunt as a fundamental lesson in martial resolve, reminding warriors that decisive strikes overcome tough carapaces and that arrogance against smaller foes invites defeat."},kw={"antidote","durotar","gornek","scorpid","scorpid worker","sting of the scorpid","the den","valley of trials","venom"},rel={"npc:gornek","topic:warrior"},m={id=789},src={"In-game quest text: Sting of the Scorpid"},fv=true}
E["quest:79007"] = {t="quest",n="... and that note you found",z="general",s="At a recent campsite, cooling embers and scattered supplies are all that remain. A scorched parchment bears a riddle about finding a twin land, the only clue left behind.",h="At a recent campsite, cooling embers and scattered supplies are all that remain.",sec={{t="Embers in the Wild",b="A recent campfire is dying beside scattered provisions and a blistered piece of paper. Whoever stayed here left recently, and the parchment narrowly escaped the flames. The supplies remain exposed, as though the traveler departed in haste or was interrupted.",sp=0},{t="A Cryptic Riddle",b="The surviving scrap bears one instruction: “Not all that burns is lost. Find the twin land, find the place.” No signature, coordinates, or further context survived the fire. The riddle is the only trail left for whoever finds it.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the note found at the campsite say?",a="The charred, blistered note reads: “Not all that burns is lost. Find the twin land, find the place.” It is the only clue left at the abandoned campsite.",al={"what does the note say","note text","blistered note"}},{q="Who left the note behind?",a="The note's author is unknown. It was found among scattered provisions beside the dying embers of a recent campfire, suggesting whoever wrote or carried it left recently or was forced to depart in haste.",al={"where did the note come from","who wrote the note","who made the fire"}},{q="What does 'the twin land' refer to?",a="The note's “twin land” means a matching or mirrored place. The charred fragment gives no further explanation, leaving its meaning for travelers to unravel.",al={"what is twin land","twin land clue"}},{q="How do you follow the clues on the note?",a="The charred note is your only guide, with no destination marked on a map. Its riddle points you to seek a land or place that echoes its description.",al={"what to do with the note","where does this quest go"}},{q="Why was the campsite abandoned?",a="The campsite shows signs of a sudden departure or disturbance. Scattered provisions lie beside fading embers, and no other identifying gear or signs remain beyond the blistered scrap.",al={"who abandoned the provisions","scattered provisions campsite"}},{q="Are there any other clues at the fire?",a="No other writings or markers remain intact at the campfire. The flames destroyed almost everything, leaving only the blistered paper as evidence of whoever rested there.",al={"does the fire reveal anything else","campsite clues"}}},kw={"and that note you found","blistered note","charred note","embers","recent fire","scattered provisions","twin land"},m={id=79007},src={"In-game quest text: ... and that note you found"},fv=true}
E["quest:79091"] = {t="quest",n="Archmage Antonidas: The Unabridged Autobiography",z="general",s="In Ironforge, you seek Archmage Antonidas: The Unabridged Autobiography. The rare volume holds the late Kirin Tor leader's own account and offers insight into Dalaran's history and high arcane scholarship.",h="In Ironforge, you seek Archmage Antonidas: The Unabridged Autobiography.",sec={{t="The Words of the Grand Magus",b="Archmage Antonidas led the Kirin Tor for decades and ruled Dalaran. One of Azeroth's most brilliant minds, he rose through the ranks of the Violet Citadel and mastered the arcane. His unabridged autobiography records his leadership during the Second and Third Wars. For scholars and practitioners of magic, it offers a firsthand view of Dalaran's inner workings and the arcane arts.",sp=0},{t="From the Great Library of Ironforge",b="Before reaching its new home, the rare manuscript rested in Ironforge's grand library. In the Hall of Explorers, dwarven archivists kept it beside ancient titan relics and dwarven histories. Their vast collections hold texts gathered from ruins and foreign kingdoms across Azeroth. Yet arcane scholars elsewhere seek such works for their own collections, sure the busy archivists will never notice one missing volume.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Archmage Antonidas?",a="Archmage Antonidas led the Kirin Tor for many years and ruled the magical city-state of Dalaran. A legendary master of the arcane, he was a key councilor to the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. He warned King Terenas about the plague before falling in defense of Dalaran during the Third War.",al={"who was antonidas","archmage antonidas lore","antonidas backstory"}},{q="What is the unabridged autobiography?",a="Archmage Antonidas wrote the comprehensive autobiography himself, recounting his life, scholarship, and leadership within Dalaran. As head of the Kirin Tor, his writings are an invaluable record of history and magic.",al={"what is this book","unabridged autobiography book","antonidas tome"}},{q="Where did this book come from?",a="The volume came from the library in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. Dwarven explorers and archivists often collect historical and magical texts from across the eastern continents.",al={"where is the book from","did this come from ironforge","ironforge library book"}},{q="Why is this book being sought after?",a="Scholars and librarians seek powerful or historic magical texts to expand their archives and preserve arcane knowledge. Works by legendary magi are especially sought after, lest they be lost or hidden in foreign collections.",al={"why collect magical books","who wants antonidas book","why does the library want this"}},{q="What happened to Archmage Antonidas?",a="Archmage Antonidas perished during the Third War when the fallen Prince Arthas Menethil and the Scourge invaded Dalaran. Antonidas raised a powerful magical barrier to defend the city and its secrets. Arthas breached the defenses and struck him down to claim the Book of Medivh.",al={"did antonidas die","how did antonidas die","what happened to antonidas"}},{q="Will the Ironforge library notice the book is gone?",a="The recipient dismisses concern that the dwarves will notice the book's absence. The Ironforge archives are so vast that one missing tome will likely go unnoticed.",al={"will ironforge miss the book","stealing from ironforge library"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Archmage Antonidas was one of the Kirin Tor's greatest leaders and a paragon of arcane scholarship. His life and personal writings offer vital insight to any practitioner of the arcane arts.",["race:Dwarf"]="The tome comes from the great library in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. The scholars receiving it seem unconcerned about depriving the dwarven archivists of such a rare volume.",["race:Human"]="Antonidas was a revered human archmage who led the Kirin Tor and Dalaran through the Second and Third Wars. His unabridged autobiography is a precious piece of human history.",["faction:Alliance"]="Antonidas was a pillar of the Alliance of Lordaeron against the Orcish Horde and the Scourge. His personal memoirs are a treasured historical record across Alliance lands."},kw={"antonidas","arcane","archmage antonidas","autobiography","dalaran","hall of explorers","ironforge","kirin tor","library","mage"},rel={"subzone:library-ironforge","zone:ironforge"},m={id=79091},src={"In-game quest text: Archmage Antonidas: The Unabridged Autobiography"},fv=true}
E["quest:79092"] = {t="quest",n="Archmage Theocritus' Research Journal",z="general",s="At the Tower of Azora, Archmage Theocritus keeps a journal of his investigations. Now you carry its magical insights to the scholars of the Ironforge Library, where they can join the dwarven archive.",h="At the Tower of Azora, Archmage Theocritus keeps a journal of his investigations.",sec={{t="A Master's Field Notes",b="Archmage Theocritus is an esteemed spellcaster known throughout the Alliance. He is stationed at the Tower of Azora in eastern Elwynn Forest, where he has studied alone for years. His investigations range from curious artifacts and ley line anomalies to nearby magical threats.\n\nHe records his findings in detailed journals. This volume offers a rare chance to study the personal observations of a senior Kirin Tor archmage.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Ironforge Archives",b="The Explorers' League and the caretakers of the Ironforge Library maintain one of the continent's greatest archives. You bring Archmage Theocritus' Research Journal to them, helping preserve and catalog its arcane insights beside the historic volumes of Khaz Modan. The librarians welcome further magical discoveries that can broaden their shared wisdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Archmage Theocritus' Research Journal?",a="Archmage Theocritus' Research Journal is a scholarly record of his research. You bring it to the Ironforge Library to preserve arcane knowledge and enrich its archives.",al={"theocritus journal turn in","what is theocritus journal"}},{q="Who is Archmage Theocritus?",a="Archmage Theocritus is an esteemed human mage stationed at the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest. He studies ancient magical disturbances and artifacts.",al={"who is theocritus","archmage theocritus lore"}},{q="Where is this journal brought?",a="You bring the journal to the archivists at the Library in Ironforge. They collect rare documents and magical manuscripts.",al={"where do i turn in theocritus journal","who wants theocritus journal"}},{q="Why does the library want this journal?",a="The Ironforge Library's scholars want to preserve arcane knowledge and study rare works by master spellcasters. These works expand their historical records.",al={"why collect magical journals","purpose of theocritus journal"}},{q="Can more magical works be delivered to the library?",a="Yes. The curators encourage you to seek more magical works and historical texts for their archives.",al={"more journals to turn in","are there other books for library"}},{q="What kind of research is contained in the journal?",a="The journal holds notes and observations from Archmage Theocritus' extensive investigations into magical phenomena and arcane lore.",al={"what is in theocritus journal","contents of theocritus journal"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you know that notes from prominent figures like Archmage Theocritus help safeguard historical magical works in Ironforge's archives.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you help enrich Ironforge's great repository of knowledge for Alliance scholars."},kw={"arcane knowledge","archmage theocritus","ironforge","ironforge library","research journal","theocritus","tower of azora"},rel={"subzone:library-ironforge"},m={id=79092},src={"In-game quest text: Archmage Theocritus' Research Journal"},fv=true}
E["quest:79093"] = {t="quest",n="Rumi of Gnomeregan: The Collected Works",z="general",s="A compiled work by Rumi of Gnomeregan is sought for a library’s collection. Its pages preserve gnomish writings and knowledge that might otherwise be lost.",h="A compiled work by Rumi of Gnomeregan is sought for a library’s collection.",sec={{t="Preserving Gnomish Thought",b="Gnomish writings were scattered, buried, or left in danger when troggs overran Gnomeregan. Now scholars and archivists seek surviving manuscripts, treatises, and compiled works. They hope to keep the wisdom and ingenuity of gnomish thinkers alive.",sp=0},{t="Archival Recovery",b="When travelers find collected works in the field and bring them to the archives, curators add them to their growing collections. Each recovered manuscript widens what scholars can study. Archivists welcome more magical and historical texts, keeping them within reach of scholars, mages, and historians who guard Azeroth’s learning.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Rumi of Gnomeregan: The Collected Works?",a="Rumi of Gnomeregan: The Collected Works is a compilation of gnomish writings sought for a library. An adventurer brings it to a scholar so its knowledge can be preserved and studied.",al={"rumi of gnomeregan quest","what is rumi of gnomeregan","who is rumi"}},{q="Where are these collected works delivered?",a="The collected works go to a curator or scholar who tends a library’s collection. They seek significant writings and magical treatises for the archives.",al={"where do i turn in rumi works","who takes rumi books","rumi collection turn in"}},{q="Why does the library want these collected works?",a="The library wants the works to preserve important scholarship. Scholars and archivists gather rare texts, historical treatises, and magical manuscripts, especially writings from Gnomeregan.",al={"why collect rumi books","purpose of rumi of gnomeregan","why library wants rumi"}},{q="Who is Rumi of Gnomeregan?",a="Rumi of Gnomeregan is an intellectual or magical author whose collected writings archivists seek. With Gnomeregan largely overrun, surviving works preserve the gnomes’ scholarly traditions.",al={"who was rumi of gnomeregan","rumi lore","rumi author"}},{q="Can more books be brought to the library after this?",a="Yes. Archivists welcome more discoveries, and curators encourage travelers to bring rare magical treatises and historical works for the collection.",al={"are there more books to find","can i bring more texts","more magical works library"}},{q="Who can complete this task?",a="Alliance adventurers can help scholars safeguard historical and arcane writings. Anyone who recovers these texts can bring them to the archives to aid research.",al={"rumi quest requirements","who can turn in rumi works","alliance only rumi"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="As a gnome, preserving the collected writings of thinkers like Rumi helps keep your culture and intellectual heritage alive after Gnomeregan’s fall.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you value rare writings from across Azeroth that deepen understanding of magical theory and history.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you can help scholars and curators protect valuable literature and magical texts for the Alliance’s shared knowledge."},kw={"collected works","gnomeregan lore","gnomish writings","library","magical works","rumi of gnomeregan"},m={id=79093},src={"In-game quest text: Rumi of Gnomeregan: The Collected Works"},fv=true}
E["quest:79095"] = {t="quest",n="The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer",z="general",s="Curators seek The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer to enrich their archives. Its delivery helps preserve rare alchemical and arcane theory for researchers and spellcasters.",h="Curators seek The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer to enrich their archives.",sec={{t="A Scholar's Acquisition",b="Curators welcome The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer for its study of esoteric and magical principles. Owen Thadd adds the text directly to his working collection of magical references.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Archives",b="Owen Thadd's collection in Undercity preserves specialized treatises. By bringing him rare primers on arcane and alchemical theory, you help keep metaphysical insights safe in the archives and accessible to researchers instead of leaving them lost in the wilderness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer?",a="Curators seek The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer as a rare scholarly text for their collection of magical literature and study of metaphysical sciences.",al={"what is the apothecaries metaphysical primer","metaphysical primer quest","what is this book"}},{q="Who wants this primer delivered to them?",a="Owen Thadd in Undercity wants the primer delivered to him. He seeks magical works and arcane treatises to preserve knowledge for researchers and spellcasters.",al={"who wants this book","where do i turn in the primer","who takes the primer"}},{q="Why does the library collect works like this?",a="Libraries and archives gather metaphysical and magical works to expand their lore. These works help scholars, alchemists, and arcanists study arcane principles.",al={"why library want primer","purpose of collecting primer","why collect magical books"}},{q="Can you bring more magical works to the library?",a="Scholars who catalog magical literature welcome further arcane and scholarly discoveries you find for their archives.",al={"are there more books to turn in","can i bring other books","more library books"}},{q="What kind of lore is contained in a metaphysical primer?",a="In magical scholarship, metaphysics concerns the nature of magical energies, the unseen forces governing reality, and how physical reagents interact with arcane forces.",al={"what does metaphysical mean wotlk classic","metaphysics lore wow","what is apothecary metaphysics"}},{q="Who wrote The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer?",a="The records do not name the apothecary who wrote the primer. Apothecaries and scholars often compile primers on practical alchemy, herbal mixtures, and mystical formulas.",al={"who wrote apothecary primer","apothecary primer author","who is the apothecary"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you can help Owen Thadd in Undercity recover rare treatises for the library's arcane collections.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you value metaphysical primers for their insights into magical theory, alchemical interactions, and the foundations of spellcraft."},kw={"alchemy","apothecary","undercity","library","magical works","metaphysical primer","tome"},rel={"zone:undercity"},m={id=79095},src={"In-game quest text: The Apothecary's Metaphysical Primer"},fv=true}
E["quest:79096"] = {t="quest",n="Ataeric: On Arcane Curiosities",z="general",s="Ataeric seeks rare magical writings to enlarge a library's collection. Scholars hope these works will deepen their study of the arcane and keep its lore safe.",h="Ataeric seeks rare magical writings to enlarge a library's collection.",sec={{t="Preserving Arcane Knowledge",b="Across Azeroth, scholars rely on travelers to recover rare tomes and treatises from dangerous ground. Ataeric seeks magical writings to broaden an institution's study of the arcane. Each volume can help scholars piece together practices that might otherwise be scattered or lost.",sp=0},{t="An Expanding Archive",b="The curator adds each curiosity to the archive. The library remains eager for further magical writings found across the world, so its collection can grow with ongoing scholarship.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="Ataeric seeks magical writings to add to a library's collection of arcane lore.",al={"what is this quest","ataeric quest","arcane curiosities turn in"}},{q="Who collects these arcane writings?",a="Ataeric wrote the treatise. A researcher or curator gathers magical works and rare volumes for a library's archives.",al={"who wants this","who gets the book","who is ataeric"}},{q="Which library benefits from these curiosities?",a="The curiosities are sought for an academic collection at a scholarly library.",al={"what library is this","where do these books go"}},{q="Are there more magical works to collect?",a="Yes. The scholar welcomes any further magical works found during your travels to broaden the library's lore.",al={"can you turn in more","repeatable magical works","more books for library"}},{q="Why does the library seek arcane curiosities?",a="The library seeks rare treatises to deepen the study of the arcane and preserve mystical knowledge.",al={"what does the library study","why collect arcane books"}},{q="Why is recovering arcane works important?",a="Recovering arcane works brings obscure knowledge into a library where it can be kept and studied instead of remaining lost in the field.",al={"is this dangerous","arcane magic risk"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you have a natural interest in arcane study. Recovering magical treatises and contributing to scholarly libraries suit your craft.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you know the value of written records, artifacts, and obscure lore. Dwarven scholars and the Explorers' League prize them."},kw={"arcane","arcane curiosities","ataeric","library","magical works","scholars"},m={id=79096},src={"In-game quest text: Ataeric: On Arcane Curiosities"},fv=true}
E["quest:79097"] = {t="quest",n="Baxtan: On Destructive Magics",z="general",s="At the library, Baxtan: On Destructive Magics joins the works scholars gather to understand dangerous arcane power.",h="At the library, Baxtan: On Destructive Magics joins the works scholars gather to understand dangerous arcane...",sec={{t="A Tome of Ruinous Arts",b="Scholars seek to preserve what is known of arcane lore. When you find a text on destructive magic, its place is in careful academic keeping. There, its power and peril can be understood instead of lost.",sp=0},{t="Curating the Library's Lore",b="The library holds works on history, science, and the arcane. Its scholar welcomes travelers who recover forgotten treatises from dangerous corners of the world. Each volume adds to the library's knowledge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest 'Baxtan: On Destructive Magics'?",a="You bring Baxtan's treatise on destructive magics to a library scholar, adding a work of magic to the collection.",al={"what is baxtan on destructive magics","what is this quest","baxtan quest"}},{q="Who is Baxtan?",a="Baxtan wrote On Destructive Magics. The scholar who receives it gathers important tomes and magical treatises for the library.",al={"who is baxtan","baxtan npc","where is baxtan"}},{q="What kinds of books is Baxtan looking for?",a="The library's scholar seeks works of learning and magic for the collection, including treatises you find across the world.",al={"what kind of books does baxtan want","baxtan book collection","more books for baxtan"}},{q="Where does this magical knowledge end up?",a="The gathered texts enter the library's collection, where scholars preserve them for study of the arcane and its dangers.",al={"where does the book go","which library","ironforge library collection"}},{q="Why does the library collect works on destructive magic?",a="Scholars catalog destructive magic to understand the hazards, workings, and forms of potent arcane energies.",al={"why study destructive magic","destructive magics lore"}},{q="Can you bring more magical works to Baxtan?",a="Yes. The scholar welcomes more magical works you uncover as part of an ongoing effort to gather lore.",al={"are there more quests for baxtan","can i turn in more books"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you may value treatises on destructive magic as you study arcane power and its dangers.",["class:Warlock"]="As a warlock, you may recognize why scholars seek to understand volatile or forbidden powers.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you benefit when Alliance libraries expand the knowledge held for all."},kw={"arcane","baxtan","book","destructive magics","library","magic","tome"},m={id=79097},src={"In-game quest text: Baxtan: On Destructive Magics"},fv=true}
E["quest:79236"] = {t="quest",n="Cherry for Your Thoughts?",z="general",s="In Booty Bay, a mercenary named Tokal is seeking a drink in exchange for the truth behind recent attacks on human and dwarven outposts. While rumors point the finger at the Horde, Tokal insists her group was operating under a private mercenary contract.",h="In Booty Bay, a mercenary named Tokal is seeking a drink in exchange for the truth behind recent attacks on...",sec={{t="The Whispers in Booty Bay",b="In the bustling port of Booty Bay, an individual named Tokal is keeping a low profile while watching out for law enforcement. A series of aggressive raids recently devastated human and dwarven camps, fueling rumors that the Horde had orchestrated the attacks. When approached by travelers who clearly do not work for the inquiring marshal, Tokal is quick to push back against the widespread narrative, offering a different version of events in exchange for a drink.",sp=0},{t="Private Mercenaries",b="Tokal readily admits to participating in the pillaging of human and dwarven outposts, but firmly denies that the raids were carried out on behalf of the Horde. Instead, she claims her band was operating under private contract for an undisclosed third party. Before revealing the full account of who financed the operation and why, she insists on being brought a refreshing Cherry Grog.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Tokal?",a="Tokal is an operative or mercenary found taking refuge in the pirate haven of Booty Bay. Facing scrutiny over recent raids on human and dwarven settlements, she offers to tell the full story of who she was working for to anyone willing to buy her a Cherry Grog.",al={"who is tokal","tokal booty bay","cherry grog quest giver"}},{q="Why does Tokal want a Cherry Grog?",a="Tokal is reluctant to share the sensitive details of her past contracts while dry. Buying her a Cherry Grog loosens her tongue, getting her to explain who privately funded the raids against the Alliance camps.",al={"why buy cherry grog","cherry grog tokal","what does tokal want"}},{q="Was the Horde responsible for pillaging the human and dwarven camps?",a="According to Tokal, the raids on human and dwarven encampments were not ordered by the Horde. While rumors claimed the Horde was responsible, she insists her group was working under a private mercenary contract.",al={"who hired tokal","did horde attack alliance camps","was horde behind the raids"}},{q="Why is Tokal worried about a marshal?",a="Tokal fears pursuit by a marshal investigating the pillaging of human and dwarven settlements. She is careful to make sure anyone approaching her in Booty Bay is not an agent sent to bring her to justice.",al={"who is the marshal","is tokal hiding from marshal","marshal looking for tokal"}},{q="Where do you get Cherry Grog for Tokal?",a="Cherry Grog is a beverage that can be procured from tavern keepers and barkeepers in Booty Bay. Delivering a mug of this drink to Tokal satisfies her demand and prompts her to disclose her tale.",al={"where to get cherry grog","cherry grog location","booty bay grog"}},{q="Why is Tokal staying in Booty Bay?",a="Booty Bay serves as a neutral, freebooter port outside the strict jurisdiction of both the Alliance and the Horde. For a mercenary lying low after pillaging human and dwarven encampments, it provides an ideal hideout.",al={"why is tokal in booty bay","tokal hiding place"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance members will be interested to learn that the raiders striking human and dwarven encampments were not operating under the authority of the Horde, but were hired by a private patron.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers can find vindication in Tokal's admission, which clarifies that the attacks carried out against Alliance camps were private mercenary contracts rather than official Horde aggression."},kw={"booty bay","cherry for your thoughts","cherry grog","marshal","mercenary","pillaging","private contract","tokal"},rel={"subzone:booty-bay","topic:horde","topic:human"},m={id=79236},src={"In-game quest text: Cherry for Your Thoughts?"},fv=true}
E["quest:79974"] = {t="quest",n="Wet Job",z="general",s="On a mountainside, Wet Job sends you to recover an emergency reserve stash. Someone prepared it for a hasty escape and hid it near a high cliffside perch.",h="On a mountainside, Wet Job sends you to recover an emergency reserve stash.",sec={{t="An Emergency Cache",b="In dangerous country, a reserve can make a quick escape possible. You search the rugged heights for a stash someone left there in case plans went wrong. Its supplies are waiting to be recovered.",sp=0},{t="The Eagle's Nest",b="The directions lead to a high mountain perch, described as carved from the waterfalls of time. There, at the eagle's nest, a weathered messenger bag lies partly buried in the earth. Inside, provisions and parchment have endured beneath the soil, protected by dwarven craft.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Wet Job about?",a="You recover a hidden emergency stash, prepared with supplies for a hasty escape.",al={"what is wet job","wet job quest","what kind of quest is wet job"}},{q="Who left the reserve stash?",a="Someone left the stash as an emergency reserve in case they had to flee in a hurry. It holds a messenger bag with parchment and supplies, made with dwarven craft.",al={"who left the stash","whose supplies are these","who hid the reserve stash"}},{q="Where is the stash hidden?",a="The stash lies high on a mountainside, at an eagle's nest on the rock face described as carved from the waterfalls of time. A messenger bag is partly buried there.",al={"where is the stash","wet job location","eagles nest location"}},{q="What is found in the messenger bag?",a="The buried messenger bag holds provisions and parchment, protected by dwarven craft.",al={"what is inside the stash","wet job items","what do you find"}},{q="Why is the task called Wet Job?",a="The name refers to the mountain landmarks, with waterfalls of time and damp soil around the hidden bag.",al={"why is it called wet job","wet job name meaning"}},{q="How is dwarven engineering involved?",a="The cache's construction and reliability show dwarven engineering, made to withstand burial outdoors.",al={"dwarven engineering connection","is this dwarven"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="As an engineer, you can admire the dwarven ingenuity behind a dependable emergency stockpile hidden in rugged terrain.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you can take pride in the enduring quality of the mountain craft praised when the cache is recovered.",["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you know the value of hidden emergency stashes, kept ready in case a hasty flight is needed."},kw={"dwarven engineering","eagles nest","messenger bag","mountainside","parchment","reserve stash","supplies","wet job"},m={id=79974},src={"In-game quest text: Wet Job"},fv=true}
E["quest:79975"] = {t="quest",n="Eagle's Fist",z="general",s="At a misty overlook, Eagle's Fist keeps a lonely watch over a weathered barricade. A letter speaks of loyalty to the Old Kingdoms and the Eagle and Fist, though its writer has left in haste.",h="At a misty overlook, Eagle's Fist keeps a lonely watch over a weathered barricade.",sec={{t="A Hidden Perch",b="A folded letter lies inside a toolbox at a high overlook. From here, its writer says, one can call the shots or launch a parachute glider across the land. Misty spray dampens this lonely post, but the view reaches across historic lands.",sp=0},{t="Loyalty to the Old Kingdoms",b="The letter's writer pledges lasting loyalty to the Eagle and Fist. To serve that cause well, the writer says, one must keep a measured distance. The nearby barricade has fallen into disrepair, like the Old Kingdoms it once shielded from danger. Still, the watcher vows never to abandon the post.",sp=0},{t="A Hasty Departure",b="The sentry left the overlook in a hurry. The campfire was fed with fresh wood only recently, yet the watcher is gone. Tools, burning embers and a cryptic letter remain behind.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Eagle's Fist?",a="Eagle's Fist is a lonely lookout over a weathered barricade, where a toolbox holds a folded letter. Its writer has left in haste.",al={"what is eagle's fist","eagles fist quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Who wrote the letter found in the toolbox?",a="An unidentified scout or sentry wrote the letter. The writer serves the Old Kingdoms and the Eagle and Fist, keeping watch at a lonely barricade.",al={"who wrote the note","who left the letter","toolbox letter author"}},{q="What does 'the Eagle and Fist' refer to?",a="The Eagle and Fist is an allegiance named in the letter. It calls for keeping a measured distance in service, and is tied to guarding what remains of the Old Kingdoms.",al={"what is the eagle and fist","eagle and fist meaning","who is eagle and fist"}},{q="Why is the campsite abandoned?",a="The scout left in haste, though the campfire had only recently been fed with fresh wood. The writer praised the overlook as a place to launch a parachute glider.",al={"where did the scout go","why did they leave","who fed the campfire"}},{q="What barricade is being defended?",a="The letter's writer says the barricade is worn, like the fallen Old Kingdoms of humanity it once defended. Still, the watcher vows never to abandon it.",al={"what barricade","which old kingdoms","barricade lore"}},{q="What kind of lookout post is this?",a="The perch offers an incredible view, and water spray dampens the air. It may be a high cliff or overlook near a great waterfall or cascading river.",al={"where is this located","why is there water spray","eagles fist location"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="The lookout's note calls the spot an ideal place to launch a parachute glider. Engineers may appreciate the chance for aerial reconnaissance and mechanical flight."},kw={"barricade","campfire","eagle's fist","folded letter","lookout","old kingdoms","parachute glider","toolbox"},m={id=79975},src={"In-game quest text: Eagle's Fist"},fv=true}
E["quest:79976"] = {t="quest",n="This Must Be The Place",z="general",s="At This Must Be The Place, a camper has vanished moments before anyone could catch up. A torn note says the writer has taken their work underground to keep it from the wrong hands.",h="At This Must Be The Place, a camper has vanished moments before anyone could catch up.",sec={{t="A Hasty Departure",b="Someone broke camp at this secluded spot only moments ago. An inadequately secured sleeping bag unrolls underfoot, while heavy canvas lies bundled over assorted gear. A small scrap of paper holds a hurried parting message, but whoever camped here has vanished before anyone could catch up.",sp=0},{t="Into the Underground",b="The torn note says its author has taken their work underground to keep it from the wrong hands. Whether these are final words or a clue to the researcher's next hidden shelter remains unknown. The equipment lies abandoned for whoever found the campsite.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who wrote the torn note?",a="The note does not name its author. The writer says only that they have taken their work underground to keep it from the wrong hands, leaving the campsite and equipment behind.",al={"who wrote the note","note author","who left the message"}},{q="What work is being taken underground?",a="The note does not reveal what work the author is taking underground. The writer says they want to keep it safe from unspecified adversaries.",al={"what underground work","why take work underground","what work"}},{q="Where did the campsite's occupant go?",a="The occupant left moments before anyone arrived, and their destination is unknown. The note hints they may have gone to another hidden shelter.",al={"did i miss them","where did they go","where is the author"}},{q="What was left behind at the campsite?",a="The traveler left an inadequately secured sleeping bag that unrolls on the ground, heavy canvas bundled over assorted gear, and a discarded pack containing miscellaneous gear.",al={"what is left behind","supplies at camp","what was in the pack"}},{q="Is this a clue leading to another hideout?",a="The note could be a farewell or a clue to the author's next underground shelter. It does not settle which.",al={"is there another hideout","next camp","more clues"}},{q="Who are the 'wrong hands' mentioned in the note?",a="The note does not identify the wrong hands. The author feared pursuit or theft and left before their work could be compromised.",al={"who are the wrong hands","wrong hands meaning","who is chasing the author"}}},kw={"abandoned camp","equipment pack","hideout","sleeping bag","this must be the place","torn note","underground"},m={id=79976},src={"In-game quest text: This Must Be The Place"},fv=true}
E["quest:79980"] = {t="quest",n="Scramble",z="general",s="A scavenger's note sends travelers down a steep cliff in search of Scramble's hidden supplies. One hundred paces north, they find provisions and a dirty parchment.",h="A scavenger's note sends travelers down a steep cliff in search of Scramble's hidden supplies.",sec={{t="The Northward Trail",b="A note points the way to a hidden supply cache. Its writer offers a few informal words of encouragement, then gives precise directions: go one hundred paces due north down the treacherous cliffside. Provisions wait there to keep a traveler going.",sp=0},{t="Unearthing the Messenger Bag",b="Following the directions down the steep cliff face leads to a buried messenger bag. Inside are practical survival supplies and another dirty parchment. The note points onward along a larger trail.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest Scramble?",a="Scramble sends travelers along a scavenged trail to a hidden cache. A note directs them one hundred paces north down a treacherous cliff to buried supplies.",al={"scramble quest","what is scramble","scramble stash"}},{q="Where is the hidden stash located?",a="The note places the cache about one hundred paces due north, down a steep cliffside. Careful footing is needed on the way down.",al={"where is stash","how to find stash","stash location"}},{q="Who left the note and the cache?",a="The note does not name its author. Its informal, practical directions help an explorer find emergency provisions, and the stash remains intact.",al={"who wrote scramble note","who left stash","scramble author"}},{q="What is inside the buried stash?",a="The buried messenger bag holds survival supplies and a dirty parchment tucked among them.",al={"scramble rewards","what is in scramble cache","bag contents"}},{q="What is the dirty parchment found with the supplies?",a="The dirty parchment is inside the buried messenger bag, alongside the provisions. It is found when the bag is pulled from the ground.",al={"dirty parchment scramble","what does parchment say","scramble note"}},{q="What hazards are involved in reaching the cache?",a="The steep northern descent is the chief hazard. A misstep can mean broken ankles or a serious fall.",al={"danger scramble","cliff scramble","scramble cliffside"}}},kw={"cache","cliffside","messenger bag","parchment","scramble","stash","supplies"},m={id=79980},src={"In-game quest text: Scramble"},fv=true}
E["quest:815"] = {t="quest",n="Break a Few Eggs",z="general",s="Break a Few Eggs is an errand assisting a harried cook who must keep up with endless meal preparations. Securing fresh, fragile ingredients without breaking them keeps the kitchen running and the cooking on schedule.",h="Break a Few Eggs is an errand assisting a harried cook who must keep up with endless meal preparations.",sec={{t="Endless Meals",b="Feeding a crowd is an exhausting, never-ending endeavor. Between breakfast, lunch, and dinner, there is scarcely any time to step away from the stove, let alone leave the kitchen to scrounge for raw provisions. In Break a Few Eggs, a hard-pressed cook turns to passing adventurers to secure fresh eggs before the next meal service falls behind.",sp=0},{t="A Cook's Gratitude",b="Transporting eggs requires a gentle touch that brute force cannot replace. The cook may offer sharp words and little praise, but intact ingredients speak for themselves. Once the eggs are delivered safe and unbroken, the cook wastes no time sending the gatherer off and returning straight to work over the fire.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Break a Few Eggs?",a="Break a Few Eggs is a culinary task where an adventurer aids a busy cook who is overwhelmed by the constant demand to prepare meals. The task involves delivering eggs intact to ensure the cooking doesn't fall behind schedule.",al={"break a few eggs quest","what is break a few eggs","egg quest"}},{q="Who is the cook in Break a Few Eggs?",a="The cook is Cook Torka of Razor Hill in Durotar, who prepares meals around the clock. Though blunt and hurried in manner, Torka relies on capable hands to fetch Taillasher eggs from the Echo Isles without cracking them before mealtime.",al={"who gives break a few eggs","egg quest cook","who is cooking"}},{q="Why does the cook need eggs?",a="The cook needs them for ongoing meal preparations, whether breakfast, lunch, or dinner. Feeding people is an endless chore, and fresh eggs are an essential kitchen staple needed immediately at the stove.",al={"why eggs","what are eggs for","why need eggs"}},{q="Why does the cook comment on not being clumsy?",a="Eggs are fragile and easy to ruin during transit. The cook explicitly values someone who is not clumsy, as cracked shells and lost yolks ruin ingredients needed for meal service.",al={"why careful with eggs","dont break eggs","why not clumsy"}},{q="What does the title Break a Few Eggs refer to?",a="The name references the common proverb that you cannot make an omelet without breaking a few eggs, fitting a task focused on gathering fragile kitchen ingredients for a busy meal service.",al={"break a few eggs meaning","phrase meaning","why is it called break a few eggs"}},{q="Why is the cook so impatient?",a="The cook is completely occupied keeping up with non-stop meals for breakfast, lunch, and dinner. With so much cooking left to do, the cook has little patience for idle chitchat and simply wants to get back to the stove.",al={"cook attitude","why is the cook rude","grumpy cook"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Every working cook knows the relentless pressure of keeping people fed. Fetching fragile ingredients without smashing them is half the battle in any kitchen."},kw={"break a few eggs","breakfast","cook","cooking","eggs","kitchen","meals"},m={id=815},src={"In-game quest text: Break a Few Eggs"},fv=true}
E["quest:8312"] = {t="quest",n="Hallow's End Treats for Spoops!",z="general",s="For Hallow's End Treats for Spoops!, an ailing child asks travelers to gather holiday sweets. Innkeepers in the cities and Kali Remik in Sen'jin Village share treats, and Spoops offers magical candy in return.",h="For Hallow's End Treats for Spoops!, an ailing child asks travelers to gather holiday sweets.",sec={{t="A Bedridden Holiday",b="Spoops is too ill to join Hallow's End outdoors. While others celebrate, Spoops asks travelers to gather holiday treats. In return, Spoops offers candy that changes whoever eats it into strange shapes.",sp=0},{t="Tricks and Treats Across the Realm",b="Travelers can find holiday treats in settlements across the realm. Innkeepers in major cities ask visitors to perform tricks before sharing their sweets. Kali Remik also gives out treats in Sen'jin Village on the coast of Durotar.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Spoops?",a="Spoops is a sick young person who cannot go trick-or-treating during Hallow's End. Hoping to take part, Spoops asks travelers to collect seasonal treats from innkeepers and vendors across the world.",al={"who is spoops","spoops","what is spoops"}},{q="Why can't Spoops go trick-or-treating?",a="Spoops is too ill to go outside for Hallow's End. Bedridden by illness, Spoops relies on travelers to visit city innkeepers and gather holiday sweets.",al={"why cant spoops go trick or treating","why is spoops staying in","spoops sick"}},{q="What candy does Spoops offer in exchange?",a="Spoops offers magical candies that transform whoever eats them. They can turn you into shapes that are both funny and frightening.",al={"what candy does spoops give","spoops reward candy","special candy"}},{q="Where do you find the treats for Spoops?",a="Innkeepers in the capital cities share holiday candy after travelers perform tricks. Kali Remik also hands out sweets in Sen'jin Village.",al={"where do i get candy","who gives treats","candy locations"}},{q="Who is Kali Remik?",a="Kali Remik is a troll in Sen'jin Village, Durotar. During Hallow's End, Kali Remik gives holiday candy to travelers who stop by.",al={"who is kali remik","kali remik candy","senjin village candy"}},{q="Why do innkeepers ask for tricks?",a="During Hallow's End, innkeepers in major settlements ask visitors to show their holiday spirit with silly tricks or gestures before sharing their sweets.",al={"what do innkeepers want","innkeeper tricks","how to get innkeeper candy"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde traveler, you gather treats in the major cities and visit Sen'jin Village, the Darkspear settlement, on behalf of an ailing child.",["race:Troll"]="As a Darkspear troll, you can find Kali Remik handing out seasonal sweets in Sen'jin Village during Hallow's End."},kw={"candy","hallow's end","innkeeper","kali remik","sen'jin village","spoops","treats","trick or treat"},rel={"npc:kali-remik","subzone:sen-jin-village","topic:troll"},m={id=8312},src={"In-game quest text: Hallow's End Treats for Spoops!"},fv=true}
E["quest:837"] = {t="quest",n="Encroachment",z="general",s="Encroachment is a Horde quest directed by Gar'Thok at Razor Hill in central Durotar. It charges champions with striking deep into the western bramble dens to eliminate the entrenched Razormane quilboar threatening the borders of the young orc nation.",h="Encroachment is a Horde quest directed by Gar'Thok at Razor Hill in central Durotar.",sec={{t="A Thorn in Durotar's Side",b="When Thrall led the orcs to Kalimdor and founded Durotar, they found much of the harsh, arid land already inhabited by the Razormane quilboar. These aggressive, boar-like creatures proved a constant thorn in the side of the new settlement. Through sustained military efforts, the Horde drove back the bulk of their numbers, yet fortified pockets of Razormane continue to cling stubbornly to their thorned redoubts west of Razor Hill.",sp=0},{t="Gar'Thok's Offensive",b="Gar'Thok, an officer stationed at Razor Hill, oversees the effort to purge the lingering Razormane threat once and for all. He tasks seasoned defenders with striking directly into the western bramble camps to eliminate their scouts, warriors, and dustrunners. For Gar'Thok and the Horde leadership, allowing the quilboar any foothold near Razor Hill invites disaster, and striking them here is seen as the first step toward securing Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Clearing the Brambles",b="Eliminating the quilboar defenders clears the path for Horde forces to torch the bramble dens, preventing the Razormane from reclaiming the western foothills. By burning these structures to the ground, the Horde removes an enduring menace and secures the western perimeter of Durotar, safeguarding vital supply roads between Razor Hill and the wider realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Encroachment?",a="The quest is given by Gar'Thok, an orc officer stationed at Razor Hill in central Durotar.",al={"who gives encroachment","where to get encroachment","encroachment questgiver"}},{q="Why is the Horde fighting the Razormane quilboar in Durotar?",a="When the orcs arrived in Durotar, the Razormane quilboar held much of the region. Although the Horde pushed most of them back, fortified pockets remain west of Razor Hill, threatening the safety and borders of the new orc homeland.",al={"why fight quilboar durotar","why attack razormane","quilboar conflict durotar"}},{q="Where are the Razormane camps located?",a="The Razormane encampments lie to the west of Razor Hill. You can easily spot their territory by looking for dense tangles of thorny brambles.",al={"where are the quilboar camps","how to find razormane camps","encroachment location"}},{q="Who are the Razormane?",a="The Razormane are a hostile tribe of quilboar native to the arid plains of Kalimdor. They build defensive bramble dens and fiercely resist the Horde's settlement of Durotar and the Barrens.",al={"who are the razormane","razormane lore","what is razormane"}},{q="What happens once the quilboar are defeated?",a="Upon turning in the quest, Gar'Thok sends word to burn down the cleared quilboar brambles and structures, cementing Horde control over the area and securing Durotar's borders.",al={"what happens after encroachment","encroachment turn in","fate of quilboar camps"}},{q="What is Razor Hill's role in this conflict?",a="Razor Hill is a fortified orc settlement in central Durotar that serves as a military and logistical hub between the Valley of Trials and Orgrimmar.",al={"where is razor hill","what is razor hill"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Securing Durotar from the native Razormane quilboar is vital to establishing a safe permanent homeland for the nascent Horde.",["race:Orc"]="Having claimed Durotar as their new sovereign land, the orcs view the lingering Razormane bramble dens as an unacceptable threat to their settlements."},kw={"brambles","durotar","encroachment","gar'thok","quilboar","razor hill","razormane"},rel={"npc:gar-thok","subzone:razor-hill","topic:quilboar","zone:durotar"},m={id=837},src={"In-game quest text: Encroachment"},fv=true}
E["quest:8373"] = {t="quest",n="The Power of Pine",z="general",s="During Hallow’s End, Sergeant Hartman asks you to clear Forsaken stink bombs from Southshore. A pine-scented cleaner helps keep the village clean.",h="During Hallow’s End, Sergeant Hartman asks you to clear Forsaken stink bombs from Southshore.",sec={{t="Southshore’s Stink",b="During Hallow’s End, the Forsaken foul Southshore with stink bombs. The bombs mar the celebration of liberation. Sergeant Hartman asks you to remove any that have landed in the village with a Stink Bomb Cleaner.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who asks me to clean up the stink bombs?",a="Sergeant Hartman in Southshore asks you to clear the Forsaken stink bombs.",al={"who gave me this","sergeant hartman"}},{q="Where are the stink bombs?",a="The stink bombs are in Southshore.",al={"stink bombs location","where clean"}},{q="Who uses stink bombs on Southshore?",a="The Forsaken use stink bombs on Southshore during Hallow’s End.",al={"who stink bombs","forsaken bombs"}},{q="What does the cleaner do?",a="The Stink Bomb Cleaner uses pine to fight foul smells.",al={"pine cleaner","stink bomb cleaner"}},{q="Why is Southshore being cleaned?",a="The Forsaken stink bombs make a smelly mess during Southshore’s Hallow’s End celebration.",al={"why clean southshore","hallow's end stink"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, you can help Sergeant Hartman keep Southshore clean of Forsaken stink bombs."},kw={"forsaken","hallow's end","pine cleaner","sergeant hartman","southshore","stink bomb cleaner","stink bombs"},rel={"subzone:southshore","topic:forsaken","topic:horde"},m={id=8373},src={"In-game quest text: The Power of Pine"},fv=true}
E["quest:86576"] = {t="quest",n="Bloodfury Trinkets",z="general",s="Mor'rogal asks you to bring him sunstones from the Bloodfury harpies in the Charred Vale. He says they are bound to the lights of the skies.",h="Mor'rogal asks you to bring him sunstones from the Bloodfury harpies in the Charred Vale.",sec={{t="Harpies' Stones",b="The Bloodfury harpies gather shiny things, among them golden-yellow sunstones. Mor'rogal is up on the hills above you and has mentioned using them. He asks you to bring him any Glittering Sunstones you find.",sp=0},{t="Light in Stone",b="Mor'rogal says sunstones reflect the sun's majesty. They can adorn shrines and homes with small motes of light. At night, the moon lends them its silver beauty. To him, they recall a bond with the lights of the skies.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the Bloodfury harpies collecting?",a="The Bloodfury harpies gather shiny objects, including golden-yellow sunstones.",al={"harpy shiny things","bloodfury trinkets","what do harpies gather"}},{q="Who wants the sunstones?",a="Mor'rogal, who is up on the hills above you, has mentioned using sunstones.",al={"who needs sunstones","mor rogal trinkets","sunstone giver"}},{q="What does Mor'rogal use sunstones for?",a="Mor'rogal says sunstones can adorn shrines and homes with small motes of light.",al={"why morrogal wants stones","sunstone use","what are stones for"}},{q="What do the sunstones mean to Mor'rogal?",a="The sunstones remind Mor'rogal of a bond with the lights of the skies.",al={"sunstone meaning","lights of skies stones","why are stones special"}}},kw={"bloodfury harpies","bloodfury trinkets","charred vale","glittering sunstones","lights of the skies","mor'rogal","shrines","sunstones"},rel={"subzone:charred-vale","npc:mor-rogal","topic:harpy"},m={id=86576},src={"In-game quest text: Bloodfury Trinkets"},fv=true}
E["quest:86585"] = {t="quest",n="Banner of the Fallen",z="general",s="In trogg territory, Mountaineer Ylva’s search for missing comrades ended beneath the trampled standard of Ironforge. Raising it again draws out the trogg that struck her down.",h="In trogg territory, Mountaineer Ylva’s search for missing comrades ended beneath the trampled standard of...",sec={{t="The Trampled Standard",b="Mountaineer Ylva’s trampled Ironforge standard marks where the Alliance scout met her end. She entered trogg territory to find comrades who had vanished on patrol, carrying the city’s banner as a sign of defiance. A powerful trogg struck with enough force to shatter her defenses. The standard was left in the dirt.",sp=0},{t="Vengeance for Ylva",b="Headsplitter, the brute responsible for Ylva’s death, recognized the Ironforge standard and the threat it posed. Raising the fallen banner openly challenges the trogg and draws it back to defend its kill. Once Headsplitter is slain, the battered standard can be returned to Captain Rugelfuss. He arranges for Ylva’s weapon to be passed on and the banner to be cleaned and preserved in her honor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was the fallen mountaineer?",a="Mountaineer Ylva was an independent-minded dwarf who entered trogg country to search for mountaineers who never returned from patrol. She carried an Ironforge standard and sought to show dwarven resolve against the troggs before they struck her down.",al={"who was the mountaineer","ylva lore","who died with the banner"}},{q="What is the quest Banner of the Fallen about?",a="You find Mountaineer Ylva’s body and the trampled Ironforge standard she carried. Raising the banner draws out her killer, the trogg Headsplitter, so you can avenge her and return the standard to Captain Rugelfuss.",al={"what is banner of the fallen","what happens in banner of the fallen","ironforge banner quest"}},{q="Who killed Mountaineer Ylva?",a="A trogg known as Headsplitter killed Ylva. It may have used a heavy spiked club to deliver the fatal blow to her skull and knock off her helm. Raising the Ironforge standard provokes the creature to return.",al={"who killed ylva","which trogg killed the mountaineer","headsplitter trogg"}},{q="Who is Captain Rugelfuss?",a="Captain Rugelfuss is the dwarven officer overseeing mountaineer operations, and he receives Ylva’s recovered banner. He recognizes her bravery and fierce independence, and promises to clean and display the standard in her honor.",al={"who is rugelfuss","where do i turn in the banner","captain rugelfuss banner"}},{q="Why did Mountaineer Ylva go into trogg territory alone?",a="Ylva went alone to investigate the disappearance of mountaineers who had not returned from a patrol through trogg country. Fiercely independent, she carried the banner to show that Ironforge would never bow to troggs.",al={"why did ylva go into trogg territory","why was ylva alone","ylva missing patrol"}},{q="How is the trogg Headsplitter lured out?",a="Headsplitter remembers the Ironforge standard from striking down Mountaineer Ylva. Planting and raising the trampled banner again provokes the trogg to return and finish what it started.",al={"how do you summon headsplitter","call out headsplitter","raising the standard"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Mountaineer Ylva died defending Ironforge’s honor while searching for missing kin. Recovering her trampled banner and avenging her death strikes at the heart of dwarven pride and duty.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Ironforge standard represents dwarven allegiance and defiance in trogg territory. Securing it honors the fallen soldiers who hold the line for the Alliance."},kw={"banner of the fallen","captain rugelfuss","headsplitter","ironforge banner","ironforge standard","mountaineer ylva","troggs","ylva"},rel={"npc:captain-rugelfuss","topic:trogg","zone:ironforge"},m={id=86585},src={"In-game quest text: Banner of the Fallen"},fv=true}
E["quest:86614"] = {t="quest",n="Silver of the Waves",z="general",s="A lost silver hair clip waits among fishing debris, and its owner is caught up in daily chores. Its return brings relief and restores a keepsake thought lost to the water.",h="A lost silver hair clip waits among fishing debris, and its owner is caught up in daily chores.",sec={{t="A Keepsake in the Flotsam",b="Among discarded fishing debris, you find an ornate silver hair clip marked with the initials K.D. Rusted hooks and broken wire spools lie nearby. Moisture has tarnished the clip, but its fine work still shows beneath the corrosion.",sp=0},{t="Restoring the Shine",b="The clip belongs to a hardworking local, busy with daily chores. You return the keepsake, which its owner had thought lost to the water for good. A little polishing will restore its shine, and the grateful owner welcomes its return.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Silver of the Waves?",a="You find the tarnished silver hair clip among discarded fishing debris, then seek its owner. The piece bears the initials K.D.",al={"silver of the waves","what is silver of the waves","hair clip quest"}},{q="Whose hair clip is found in the debris?",a="The hair clip bears the initials K.D. Its owner recognizes it when you return it, and is grateful to recover a keepsake thought gone forever.",al={"kd monogram","whose hair clip is it","who is kd"}},{q="Where was the hair clip lost?",a="The hair clip lies among discarded fishing debris, near broken wire spools and rusty hooks. The entry does not say exactly where it was lost.",al={"where was the hair clip found","hair clip location"}},{q="What condition is the hair clip in?",a="The ornate silver hair clip is tarnished by moisture and weather, but its craftsmanship remains visible. A little polishing will restore its shine.",al={"silver clip condition","is the hair clip broken"}},{q="How does this quest begin?",a="You find the monogrammed hair clip among discarded fishing refuse, broken wire spools, and rusty hooks. Then you seek its owner.",al={"who gives the quest","how do you start silver of the waves"}},{q="Why is returning the hair clip significant?",a="The owner had thought the keepsake lost to the water for good. Its return brings relief, and the grateful owner welcomes the recovery of a treasured possession.",al={"hair clip reward","why return the hair clip"}}},kw={"chores","fishing debris","hair clip","k.d.","monogram","silver keepsake","silver of the waves"},m={id=86614},src={"In-game quest text: Silver of the Waves"},fv=true}
E["quest:86667"] = {t="quest",n="Snowbound",z="general",s="In Loch Modan, Grenhild studies the local elementals while Norric Lochthane asks for snow from South Gate Pass. His request carries a deeper hope: the aging shaman longs to see his former student again.",h="In Loch Modan, Grenhild studies the local elementals while Norric Lochthane asks for snow from South Gate...",sec={{t="An Unusual Request",b="Grenhild is studying Loch Modan's local elementals when a minor elemental arrives with a message from Norric Lochthane, her former mentor. He urgently needs fresh, unmelted snow for his shamanic workings. Grenhild asks a traveler to fetch it so she can stay with her studies. The snow comes from South Gate Pass, where cold drafts preserve the drifts year-round, and must be sealed in a specially crafted ceramic jar to keep the valley heat at bay.",sp=0},{t="Teacher and Pupil",b="Norric Lochthane lives in a quiet cottage on the Loch's northwest coast, away from the settlements. When the jar arrives, he is pleased to have the cold reagent for his mystical research. Yet he laments that Grenhild did not come herself. His request was for more than a magical ingredient: the aging shaman hoped to reconnect with his former student, now following her own path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Norric Lochthane?",a="Norric Lochthane is an experienced shaman who lives in a cottage on the northwest coast of the Loch in Loch Modan. He once taught Grenhild and still pursues shamanic workings in private. He can call on minor elementals to carry messages through the wilderness.",al={"norric lochthane lore","who is norric","norric loch modan"}},{q="Who is Grenhild?",a="Grenhild is an elemental researcher stationed in Loch Modan. She studied magic under Norric Lochthane and respects his craft, but devotes her fieldwork to local elementals and has little time to travel.",al={"who is grenhild","grenhild loch modan","grenhild mentor"}},{q="Why does Norric need a jar of snow?",a="Norric needs the snow for his shamanic workings and mystical research. He also hoped Grenhild would take the request as a reason to visit him in person.",al={"why does norric want snow","snow for shamanic workings","jar of snow purpose"}},{q="Where is the snow gathered for this quest?",a="Gather snow near the tunnel entrance at South Gate Pass, high in the mountains between Dun Morogh and Loch Modan. The cold elevation keeps snow there throughout the year, so travelers can pack it in an insulated ceramic jar.",al={"where to find snow","south gate pass snow location","how to get jar of snow"}},{q="Why couldn't Grenhild deliver the snow herself?",a="Grenhild is busy observing and studying Loch Modan's local elementals. Her fieldwork keeps her at her post, away from the trek to South Gate Pass and down to Norric's lakeside home.",al={"why cant grenhild go","why does grenhild need help","grenhild busy"}},{q="How did Norric send his request to Grenhild?",a="Norric summoned a minor elemental and sent it to carry his message to Grenhild. The unusual courier shows his ease with elemental arts and shamanic practice.",al={"norric courier","elemental messenger","how did norric contact grenhild"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Norric Lochthane's use of minor elemental couriers and frozen reagents reflects the shamanic traditions and elemental studies found in Khaz Modan.",["race:Dwarf"]="Norric Lochthane and Grenhild's exchange offers a glimpse of dwarven mentorship and elemental scholarship around Loch Modan's peaks and shores."},kw={"grenhild","jar of snow","loch modan","minor elemental","norric lochthane","shamanic workings","snowbound","south gate pass","the loch"},rel={"topic:shaman","subzone:the-loch","subzone:south-gate-pass"},m={id=86667},src={"In-game quest text: Snowbound"},fv=true}
E["quest:86776"] = {t="quest",n="Ingredients for the Forge",z="general",s="In Thelsamar, Morhan Coppertongue needs bronze bars to fill his dwindling workshop stores. Far from Ironforge's steady trade, the settlement depends on travelers to bring the metals its forge needs.",h="In Thelsamar, Morhan Coppertongue needs bronze bars to fill his dwindling workshop stores.",sec={{t="Supply Strains in Thelsamar",b="Thelsamar keeps a quieter pace than Ironforge's Great Forge. Far from the capital, supplies can be hard to come by. Morhan Coppertongue, the local blacksmith, makes the hardware, pots, and tools the township needs. Yet bronze and other raw metals often run short. Without regular shipments from the city, passing travelers and prospectors help keep the local anvils at work.",sp=0},{t="Keeping the Fires Burning",b="Bronze bars let Morhan resume making goods for daily use. But the forge still faces shortages, and Loch Modan has no reliable merchant route bringing coal to town.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers Ingredients for the Forge?",a="Morhan Coppertongue, a dwarven craftsman, offers it at the forge in Thelsamar, in Loch Modan.",al={"who gives ingredients for the forge","morhan coppertongue quest","thelsamar blacksmith quest"}},{q="What materials does Morhan Coppertongue need?",a="Morhan needs bronze bars for his workshop. He uses them to make everyday goods such as pots, kettles, and tools for local residents.",al={"what does morhan need","materials for ingredients for the forge","what bars for thelsamar forge"}},{q="Why is Thelsamar running out of metal?",a="Thelsamar lacks Ironforge's great workshops and steady trade. Its craftspeople can run short of raw metal.",al={"why does thelsamar lack bronze","why doesn't thelsamar have metal"}},{q="What does Morhan forge with the bronze?",a="Morhan uses bronze to make useful goods for the home and workplace, including cookware, kettles, pots, and tools for the townsfolk.",al={"what is bronze used for in thelsamar","what does morhan make"}},{q="What other supplies does the forge struggle to get?",a="Morhan also needs a regular trader to bring coal into town. The forge depends on fuel to keep its fires burning.",al={"what fuel does morhan need","thelsamar coal problem"}},{q="Where is this quest completed?",a="It takes place in Thelsamar, Loch Modan's chief dwarven settlement in the hills east of Dun Morogh.",al={"where does ingredients for the forge take place","ingredients for the forge location"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Morhan Coppertongue depends on smiths and metalworkers to supply Thelsamar with everyday goods, from kettles and cooking pots to field tools.",["profession:Mining"]="Miners who smelt copper and tin into bronze bars provide the materials that keep Loch Modan's rural workshops working.",["race:Dwarf"]="Even a small dwarven settlement like Thelsamar depends on its forge, so a shortage of metal concerns the whole community."},kw={"blacksmith","bronze bars","forge","ingredients for the forge","loch modan","morhan coppertongue","thelsamar"},rel={"subzone:thelsamar","zone:ironforge"},m={id=86776},src={"In-game quest text: Ingredients for the Forge"},fv=true}
E["quest:87288"] = {t="quest",n="Soft Saber Pelts",z="general",s="Soft Saber Pelts represents a trade agreement aimed at keeping wilderness roads secure from predatory cats. Hunters bring in the thick furs of local sabers so they can be crafted into warm, protective blankets and shawls.",h="Soft Saber Pelts represents a trade agreement aimed at keeping wilderness roads secure from predatory cats.",sec={{t="Securing the Wilderness Roads",b="Traversing the untamed paths of Azeroth requires constant vigilance against prowling predators. Large sabers frequently roam near well-traveled thoroughfares, presenting a persistent hazard to couriers, merchants, and lone adventurers. While local inhabitants admire the grace and majesty of these great cats, allowing their numbers to swell unchecked along trade routes invites deadly ambushes. Gathering hunters to keep the local roads safe serves as a necessary measure for communal survival.",sp=0},{t="Warmth from the Hunt",b="Hunting such impressive beasts is not done merely for sport or eradication. In frontier encampments, every resource is valued, and the dense, velvety coats of wild sabers are prized for their warmth and durability. Once brought in by capable hunters, the pelts are tailored into fine shawls and heavy blankets capable of fending off harsh weather, ensuring that nothing taken from the wild goes to waste.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are travelers asked to hunt sabers?",a="While sabers are revered as majestic beasts of the wilderness, their presence along established thoroughfares poses a constant hazard to travelers and merchants. Culling their numbers keeps the roads safe for ordinary travelers passing through the region.",al={"why hunt sabers","why kill sabers","saber threat"}},{q="What are the saber pelts used for?",a="The soft and thick fur harvested from sabers is well suited for winter garments. The pelts collected from the hunt are turned into durable, warm blankets and shawls that protect against the elements.",al={"what are pelts for","saber pelt craft","blankets and shawls"}},{q="Who seeks to trade for these saber pelts?",a="Local merchants and crafters regularly look to trade with hunters for useful materials. In exchange for quality pelts, they offer sturdy, soft items fashioned from earlier craftwork to reward travelers who aid them.",al={"saber pelt trade","who takes pelts"}},{q="Do wild sabers pose a real threat to the roads?",a="Yes. Large predatory cats naturally claim expansive hunting territories, often overlapping with frontier pathways. Unwary couriers and pack caravans risk sudden ambush unless the saber population near the roads is controlled.",al={"are sabers dangerous","saber road danger"}},{q="What is given in return for the pelts?",a="The recipient presents a keepsake crafted to be both soft and durable as a gesture of appreciation, serving as a practical reminder of the majestic beasts harvested along the frontier.",al={"soft and sturdy reward","remembrance reward"}},{q="How do local traders view the hunting of such majestic beasts?",a="Even those trading for the furs acknowledge that resorting to violence against such regal creatures is unfortunate. However, protecting travelers on the roads and making full practical use of their warm pelts justifies the necessity of the hunt.",al={"is hunting sabers justified","regret hunting sabers"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="The quest highlights the practical craft of turning wild saber pelts into soft, sturdy blankets and shawls to endure cold frontier travel.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters know the delicate balance between respecting majestic predators like sabers and culling them when they encroach upon travel routes."},kw={"blankets","pelts","roads","saber","shawls","soft saber pelts","trade"},m={id=87288},src={"In-game quest text: Soft Saber Pelts"},fv=true}
E["quest:87491"] = {t="quest",n="Spark of Freedom",z="general",s="The fire elemental has spent its strength in a blazing display. The Dark Irons held its attention, and the rest of the group is relieved it never turned on them.",h="The fire elemental has spent its strength in a blazing display.",sec={{t="After the Flames",b="The fire elemental's display leaves the onlooker amazed, then relieved when the creature runs out of strength. The Dark Irons had drawn its attention. Had it turned on the rest of the group, they too could have been in danger.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to the fire elemental?",a="The fire elemental ran out of strength after its pyrotechnic display.",al={"fire elemental","elemental ran out of steam","what happened here"}},{q="Who was the elemental going after?",a="The fire elemental had been going after the Dark Irons.",al={"dark irons target","elemental and dark irons","who was it attacking"}},{q="Why is the onlooker relieved?",a="The onlooker is relieved because the elemental did not turn on the rest of the group.",al={"why relieved","elemental danger","could it attack us"}}},kw={"dark irons","fire elemental","pyrotechnic display","spark of freedom"},m={id=87491},src={"In-game quest text: Spark of Freedom"},fv=true}
E["quest:88756"] = {t="quest",n="Bring Back a Bang",z="general",s="A construction site has lost blasting powder to Dark Iron dwarves camped east on Direforge Hill. Their marked barrels could bring danger to the surrounding lands.",h="A construction site has lost blasting powder to Dark Iron dwarves camped east on Direforge Hill.",sec={{t="Theft at the Construction Site",b="Dark Iron dwarves camp east on Direforge Hill. They raided a nearby construction site and carried its vital blasting powder back to their tents.",sp=0},{t="Volatile Hands",b="Blasting powder in malicious Dark Iron hands threatens the surrounding lands. The stolen barrels bear red markings that set them apart from common supplies. Recover them before the dwarves put the powder to destructive use.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who stole the explosives?",a="Dark Iron dwarves raided a nearby construction site and carried barrels of blasting powder to their tents east on Direforge Hill.",al={"who stole explosives","who took the barrels","stolen powder"}},{q="Where did the Dark Irons take the barrels?",a="The Dark Iron dwarves hauled the stolen barrels east to their camps on Direforge Hill.",al={"where are explosives","direforge hill camps","where is stolen powder"}},{q="How can you identify the stolen explosives?",a="Red markings distinguish the stolen construction barrels from ordinary supplies.",al={"what do barrels look like","how to identify powder","red marked barrels"}},{q="Why do the explosives need to be recovered?",a="The construction workers cannot spare the materials, and blasting powder in malicious Dark Iron hands threatens everyone nearby.",al={"why retrieve powder","what is dark iron threat","why stop dark irons"}},{q="What is Direforge Hill?",a="Direforge Hill lies east of the local worksite, where hostile Dark Iron dwarf camps have taken hold.",al={"what is direforge hill","direforge hill lore"}},{q="Who are the Dark Iron dwarves?",a="Dark Iron dwarves are a treacherous faction, known for malice, dark sorcery, and lasting hostility toward their kin and neighbors.",al={"who are dark iron dwarves","why are dark irons hostile"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you know the destructive power of mining explosives, especially when they fall into the hands of your bitter Dark Iron kin.",["profession:Engineering"]="As an engineer, you know blasting powder and volatile compounds need careful handling and containment. Unsecured stores can bring disaster."},kw={"blasting powder","bring back a bang","construction site","dark iron dwarf","direforge hill","stolen explosives"},rel={"subzone:direforge-hill","topic:dark-iron-dwarf","topic:dwarf"},m={id=88756},src={"In-game quest text: Bring Back a Bang"},fv=true}
E["quest:91282"] = {t="quest",n="A Second Home",z="general",s="Newly risen Forsaken are being invited to join an order in Tirisfal Glades. They are directed to Bandarion Keep, a monastery on the bluffs northwest of the Solliden Farmstead near the Whispering Woods. The order offers community, shared faith, and mutual support as they make a place in their new unlife.",h="Newly risen Forsaken are being invited to join an order in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Gathering of the Risen",b="Newly risen Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades are urged to find fellowship among their peers. Together, they can face unlife through mutual faith and community, and discover their full potential.",sp=0},{t="Sanctuary on the Bluffs",b="Travelers are directed to Bandarion Keep, a monastery on the bluffs northwest of the Solliden Farmstead. Beside the Whispering Woods, it offers the new order a refuge to study, practice, and establish roots in Tirisfal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest A Second Home?",a="Newly risen Forsaken are invited to join a new class order in Tirisfal Glades. They are directed to a monastery where like-minded practitioners can gather, find fellowship, and explore their shared potential.",al={"what is a second home","quest a second home","a second home quest"}},{q="Where can Bandarion Keep be found?",a="Bandarion Keep stands on the bluffs northwest of the Solliden Farmstead in Tirisfal Glades, beside the Whispering Woods.",al={"where is bandarion keep","bandarion keep location","how to get to bandarion keep"}},{q="Who established the monastery at Bandarion Keep?",a="Bandarion Keep is the monastery of Tyr's Watch, a Forsaken paladin order in Tirisfal Glades. It gathers newly risen brethren around community and shared faith.",al={"who runs bandarion keep","who formed bandarion keep","bandarion keep order"}},{q="Why are the Forsaken being invited to Bandarion Keep?",a="The founders believe newly risen undead can reach their full potential through community and shared purpose. Bandarion Keep offers their order a sanctuary and a place to learn.",al={"why go to bandarion keep","bandarion keep purpose"}},{q="What is the connection to the Solliden Farmstead?",a="The Solliden Farmstead lies in western Tirisfal Glades, southeast of the bluffs where Bandarion Keep overlooks the edge of the Whispering Woods.",al={"where is solliden farmstead","solliden farmstead bandarion"}},{q="What kind of order is gathering at Bandarion Keep?",a="Tyr's Watch is a newly formed Forsaken paladin order gathering at Bandarion Keep. It aims to support its kin as they adjust to their new unlife.",al={"what order is at bandarion keep","bandarion keep class order"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Newly risen Forsaken often feel adrift after breaking free from the Scourge. An order founded on community and shared faith offers a rare chance to rebuild.",["faction:Horde"]="Stronger ties among Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades help secure the Horde's presence in the northern Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"a second home","bandarion keep","forsaken order","monastery","solliden farmstead","tirisfal glades","whispering woods"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","subzone:solliden-farmstead"},m={id=91282},src={"In-game quest text: A Second Home"},fv=true}
E["quest:91285"] = {t="quest",n="Murlocs at the Gates",z="general",s="Breton Samuels asks you to drive back murlocs troubling the keep. Their attacks make rebuilding harder.",h="Breton Samuels asks you to drive back murlocs troubling the keep.",sec={{t="The Murloc Problem",b="Murlocs still trouble the keep after you and the others drove them from their spawning grounds. Their attacks are growing harder to manage, so Breton Samuels asks you to drive back some of them. They lurk in the bluffs along the eastern road and in camps around the lake to the west.",sp=0},{t="The Attacks",b="Breton Samuels expects the attacks to continue. He hopes pushing back the Vile Fin attackers and seers will slow them down. The keep is already hard to rebuild while murlocs strike at its people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Breton Samuels asks you to drive back some of the murlocs troubling the keep.",al={"who gives this","breton samuels","who wants help"}},{q="Why are the murlocs a problem?",a="The murlocs have troubled the keep since its people drove them from their spawning grounds. Their attacks are becoming harder to manage.",al={"why murlocs","murloc problem","what are they doing"}},{q="Where are the murlocs?",a="They lurk in the bluffs along the road to the east and in camps around the lake to the west.",al={"where murlocs","murlocs location","where to find them"}},{q="Which murlocs are involved?",a="The Vile Fin attackers and Vile Fin seers are troubling the keep.",al={"murloc types","vile fins","who to fight"}},{q="Why does the keep need help?",a="The keep is being rebuilt, and murloc attacks make that work harder.",al={"why help","keep rebuilding","what's at stake"}}},kw={"attacker","breton samuels","keep","murloc","murlocs at the gates","seer","spawning grounds","vile fin"},rel={"topic:murloc"},m={id=91285},src={"In-game quest text: Murlocs at the Gates"},fv=true}
E["quest:91294"] = {t="quest",n="Touring the Grounds",z="general",s="Breton asks you to walk Bandarion Keep after the murloc attacks come under control. Meeting Danitha Morr is your first step toward settling among the residents.",h="Breton asks you to walk Bandarion Keep after the murloc attacks come under control.",sec={{t="At the Keep",b="Breton asks you to meet Bandarion Keep’s residents and look around the stronghold. Once you have finished, Danitha Morr can help you settle in. The keep is still small, but its people call it home.",sp=0},{t="A Commander",b="Danitha Morr introduces herself as the closest thing Bandarion Keep has to a commander. She welcomes those seeking fellowship with people still loyal to the Light. The keep needs help before it can be fully established.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to look around?",a="Breton encourages you to explore Bandarion Keep after the murloc attacks come under control.",al={"who sent me around","breton touring grounds"}},{q="Who leads Bandarion Keep?",a="Danitha Morr is the closest thing Bandarion Keep has to a commander.",al={"who is danitha","keep leader"}},{q="Why should I speak to Danitha Morr?",a="Danitha Morr can help you settle in among the keep’s residents.",al={"where do i go after touring","talk to danitha"}},{q="What is Bandarion Keep like?",a="Bandarion Keep is a small stronghold whose residents call it home.",al={"what's the keep like","bandarion keep"}},{q="Who is welcome at the keep?",a="Danitha welcomes those seeking fellowship with people who remain loyal to the Light.",al={"who can join","loyal to the light"}}},kw={"bandarion keep","breton","danitha morr","grounds","light","murloc attacks","residents"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:light","topic:murloc"},m={id=91294},src={"In-game quest text: Touring the Grounds"},fv=true}
E["quest:91316"] = {t="quest",n="Making Repairs",z="general",s="At the fortress, a shortage of building materials has made restoration difficult. You are sent to Shadowvale to search for lumber.",h="At the fortress, a shortage of building materials has made restoration difficult.",sec={{t="Supplies at Shadowvale",b="Shadowvale is a ruined village in the Whispering Forest, southwest of the fortress. Search an old root cellar in one of its buildings for usable materials. The fortress lies far from settled lands, so bringing in supplies is difficult. Its repairs depend on what can be found there.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are building materials needed?",a="The fortress needs building materials to help restore it. Its distance from the Undercity makes supplies difficult to bring in.",al={"why need lumber","fortress repairs"}},{q="Where is Shadowvale?",a="Shadowvale lies in ruins in the Whispering Forest, southwest of the fortress.",al={"shadowvale location","where is the village"}},{q="Where might usable materials be found?",a="Usable materials may be stored in an old root cellar in one of Shadowvale's buildings.",al={"root cellar materials","where to look"}},{q="What is the fortress short of?",a="The fortress is short of building materials needed for its restoration.",al={"what supplies are missing","lumber shortage"}}},kw={"building materials","fortress","making repairs","root cellar","shadowvale","sturdy lumber","undercity","whispering forest"},rel={"subzone:whispering-forest","zone:tirisfal","zone:undercity"},m={id=91316},src={"In-game quest text: Making Repairs"},fv=true}
E["quest:91317"] = {t="quest",n="The Tarnished",z="general",s="At Shadowvale in Tirisfal Glades, the Tarnished threaten a fledgling order's place among the Forsaken. Their attacks deepen mistrust and put the order's future at risk.",h="At Shadowvale in Tirisfal Glades, the Tarnished threaten a fledgling order's place among the Forsaken.",sec={{t="A Tarnished Order",b="Some risen still bear prejudices from their former lives, and now attack their fellow Forsaken. The Tarnished have gathered at Shadowvale, southwest of here. Their violence must end, and Commander Rudolph Gelhardt’s head must be brought back.",sp=0},{t="A Place Among Them",b="The Tarnished’s attacks give those who distrust the order more reason to turn against it. Stopping them may help the order earn a place among the Forsaken. Acceptance could still take years or decades.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Tarnished?",a="The Tarnished are risen who kept prejudices from their former lives and now attack fellow Forsaken.",al={"tarnished who","who are these tarnished"}},{q="Where are the Tarnished?",a="A number of the Tarnished have settled at Shadowvale, southwest of here in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"tarnished location","where tarnished"}},{q="Why are the Tarnished a threat?",a="Their attacks on fellow Forsaken deepen mistrust of the order among the Forsaken.",al={"why stop tarnished","tarnished attacks"}},{q="Who leads the Tarnished?",a="Commander Rudolph Gelhardt leads the Tarnished.",al={"tarnished leader","rudy gelhardt"}},{q="Why does the order need the Forsaken's trust?",a="The order is mistrusted because the Forsaken remember persecution by the living in the name of the Light. The Tarnished’s attacks deepen that mistrust.",al={"order trust","forsaken mistrust"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As a Forsaken, you face risen who attack their own people. Their violence worsens mistrust toward a fledgling order.",["faction:Horde"]="The order hopes to earn a place among the Horde, but its leader expects acceptance may take years or decades."},kw={"commander rudolph gelhardt","fledgling order","forsaken","mistrust","shadowvale","tarnished drudges","tarnished zealots","the tarnished","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:horde","topic:light"},m={id=91317},src={"In-game quest text: The Tarnished"},fv=true}
E["quest:91723"] = {t="quest",n="Delicate Instruments",z="general",s="Delicate Instruments is a quest in Elwynn Forest given by Hamish Bergwort at the Tower of Azora. Hamish's sensitive arcane equipment is being disrupted by erratic magical surges radiating from the north. To restore stability, travelers must enter Jasperlode Mine and cull the kobold geomancers responsible for the disturbances.",h="Delicate Instruments is a quest in Elwynn Forest given by Hamish Bergwort at the Tower of Azora.",sec={{t="Disrupted Calibrations",b="Inside the Tower of Azora, Hamish Bergwort oversees delicate arcane apparatus that requires fine calibration. Unfortunately for the scholar, the surrounding countryside is far from quiet. Repeated surges of uncontrolled energy constantly throw off his measurements, rendering careful magical adjustments impossible until the source of the interference is addressed.",sp=0},{t="The Interference from Jasperlode",b="Investigation traces the magical pulses directly northward to Jasperlode Mine. The subterranean tunnels have been claimed by kobolds, whose geomancers channel crude earth magic within the stone. While their sorcery lacks scholarly refinement, the sheer frequency of their crude rituals generates elemental ripples potent enough to travel through the earth and unsettle Azora's sensitive instruments.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Hamish Bergwort?",a="Hamish Bergwort is an arcane researcher stationed at the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest. He works with sensitive instruments that require precise calibration, which are prone to disruption from ambient magical fluctuations.",al={"who is hamish bergwort","quest giver delicate instruments","hamish bergwort tower of azora"}},{q="Why are the arcane instruments malfunctioning?",a="The instruments are being disrupted by surges of magical energy radiating from Jasperlode Mine to the north, generated by primitive kobolds practicing crude earth magic.",al={"why are instruments disrupted","cause of arcane surges","what is interfering with azora"}},{q="What is Jasperlode Mine?",a="Jasperlode Mine is an infested mine north of the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest. Kobolds have overrun the tunnels, where their spellcasters conduct chaotic geomantic rituals that ripple across the surrounding woods.",al={"what is jasperlode mine","where are the geomancers","jasperlode kobolds"}},{q="What are Kobold Geomancers?",a="Kobold geomancers are subterranean spellcasters among the kobold packs who wield crude, elemental earth magic. Though primitive, their collective channeling creates notable magical disturbances that interfere with sensitive arcane equipment.",al={"what are kobold geomancers","kobold magic users","geomancy kobold"}},{q="How does eliminating the geomancers resolve the issue?",a="Culling the geomancers inside Jasperlode Mine halts the continuous elemental energy spikes, allowing researchers at the Tower of Azora to properly calibrate their sensitive arcane devices without interference.",al={"why kill geomancers","delicate instruments purpose","stopping the surges"}},{q="What is the Tower of Azora?",a="The Tower of Azora is a prominent spire of arcane study located in eastern Elwynn Forest. Scholars and mages there observe regional phenomena and maintain finely tuned magical apparatus.",al={"what is tower of azora","azora tower lore","elwynn mage tower"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The delicate arcane apparatus in the Tower of Azora requires extreme environmental stability, demonstrating how crude geomantic interference can disrupt disciplined arcane studies.",["faction:Alliance"]="Assisting the researchers at the Tower of Azora helps safeguard Elwynn Forest's scholarly enclaves from the chaotic meddling of subterranean pests."},kw={"arcane","delicate instruments","elwynn forest","geomancy","hamish bergwort","jasperlode mine","kobold","kobold geomancer","tower of azora"},rel={"subzone:tower-of-azora","subzone:jasperlode-mine","topic:kobold"},m={id=91723},src={"In-game quest text: Delicate Instruments"},fv=true}
E["quest:91724"] = {t="quest",n="Delicate Instruments",z="general",s="Delicate Instruments is a quest in eastern Elwynn Forest focused on restoring magical stability to the Tower of Azora. Hamish Bergwort's sensitive equipment is being disrupted by erratic spellcasting practiced by nearby Defias rogue wizards. Resolving the disturbance requires putting an end to the amateur sorcery around Stone Cairn Lake.",h="Delicate Instruments is a quest in eastern Elwynn Forest focused on restoring magical stability to the Tower...",sec={{t="Interference at the Tower",b="Within the Tower of Azora, Hamish Bergwort attempts to conduct delicate magical experiments, only to be repeatedly interrupted by unexplained ambient disruptions. In an arcane sanctuary where precise measurements are everything, even slight ripples in the local magical weave can ruin hours of careful work. Bergwort suspects the disruptions are not natural, but rather the result of crude spells cast nearby.",sp=0},{t="Banditry and the Arcane",b="The source of the magical static traces back to Stone Cairn Lake, where Defias rogue wizards have been practicing unchecked spellcraft. The Defias Brotherhood's reliance on rogue spellcasters introduces unstable, amateur magic into the Elwynn countryside. Dispatching these rogue wizards silences the magical noise, finally allowing Bergwort's sensitive instruments to register clean readings.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Delicate Instruments?",a="Hamish Bergwort offers this quest from within the Tower of Azora in eastern Elwynn Forest. He is conducting arcane research that requires stable magical conditions.",al={"who gives delicate instruments","delicate instruments questgiver","where is hamish bergwort"}},{q="What is disrupting Hamish Bergwort's instruments?",a="The delicate instruments in the Tower of Azora are suffering from arcane interference caused by local Defias bandits who have taken up crude spellcasting near Stone Cairn Lake.",al={"what is disrupting the instruments","why are instruments disrupted","delicate instruments interference"}},{q="Where can the Defias Rogue Wizards be found?",a="The Defias rogue wizards gather along the shores and surrounding grounds of Stone Cairn Lake, located just north of the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest.",al={"where are the rogue wizards","stone cairn lake rogue wizards","where to find defias rogue wizards"}},{q="Why are Defias bandits practicing magic?",a="While primarily consisting of disgruntled laborers, highwaymen, and thieves, the Defias Brotherhood has recruited renegade dabblers in the arcane. These rogue wizards practice magic without sanction, causing erratic disruptions in the region.",al={"why do defias have wizards","defias spellcasters","rogue wizards lore"}},{q="What is the Tower of Azora?",a="The Tower of Azora is an arcane spire situated in eastern Elwynn Forest, serving as a haven for human magi and scholars studying ley lines, magical anomalies, and regional security.",al={"what is tower of azora","azora tower elwynn","who runs tower of azora"}},{q="Does eliminating the rogue wizards fix the problem?",a="Yes. Once you cull the Defias rogue wizards casting at Stone Cairn Lake, the ambient magical resonance calms down, allowing Hamish Bergwort's apparatus to function properly once more.",al={"does stopping the wizards work","delicate instruments outcome","hamish bergwort instruments fixed"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Practitioners of the arcane will recognize how careless spellcasting by amateurs like the Defias rogue wizards can send wild fluctuations rolling across Elwynn, throwing sensitive magical research into chaos.",["faction:Alliance"]="Maintaining order around the Tower of Azora is vital for securing eastern Elwynn Forest against Defias encroachment."},kw={"defias brotherhood","defias rogue wizard","delicate instruments","elwynn forest","hamish bergwort","stone cairn lake","tower of azora"},rel={"subzone:tower-of-azora","subzone:stone-cairn-lake"},m={id=91724},src={"In-game quest text: Delicate Instruments"},fv=true}
E["quest:91725"] = {t="quest",n="Stolen Enchanting Supplies",z="general",s="Stolen Enchanting Supplies is an errand undertaken near the Tower of Azora in eastern Elwynn Forest. An apprentice named Blixie Fitzwink lost a shipment of magical reagents during a gnoll ambush, and adventurers must retrieve the stolen goods from camps around Stone Cairn Lake. Recovering the materials spares the apprentice severe chore duty and keeps Master Theocritus's arcane studies supplied.",h="Stolen Enchanting Supplies is an errand undertaken near the Tower of Azora in eastern Elwynn Forest.",sec={{t="An Ambush on the Road",b="A routine supply run turns perilous when Blixie Fitzwink, an apprentice assisting Master Theocritus, is ambushed by hostile gnolls between the trading post and the Tower of Azora. Fleeing for her life, she drops her cargo, leaving valuable enchanting reagents scattered among the gnoll camps dotting the shores of Stone Cairn Lake. Desperate to avoid weeks of disciplinary cleanup duty following arcane polymorph experiments, she appeals to passing adventurers to recover the missing goods.",sp=0},{t="Arcane Errands and Local Hazards",b="The Tower of Azora serves as an arcane bastion in eastern Elwynn Forest, where Master Theocritus conducts sensitive magical research. Sourcing delicate reagents requires regular traffic with local trading posts, but the rising boldness of gnoll packs around Stone Cairn Lake makes transport hazardous. Recovering the lost supplies ensures that Theocritus's studies continue uninterrupted while keeping his beleaguered assistant out of trouble.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Stolen Enchanting Supplies?",a="Blixie Fitzwink offers this task near the Tower of Azora. She is an assistant running errands for Master Theocritus who was ambushed by gnolls while transporting essential supplies back from a local trading post.",al={"who gives stolen enchanting supplies","who is blixie fitzwink","quest giver blixie"}},{q="Who is Master Theocritus?",a="Master Theocritus is a senior human wizard residing at the Tower of Azora in eastern Elwynn Forest. He oversees arcane investigations in the region and relies on assistants to retrieve enchanting reagents and research materials.",al={"who is master theocritus","theocritus role","tower of azora mage"}},{q="What supplies were taken by the gnolls?",a="The package contains enchanting reagents collected from a trading post for Master Theocritus's arcane work. After an ambush scattered the cargo, the items were taken to gnoll camps around Stone Cairn Lake.",al={"what are the supplies","what did gnolls steal","enchanting reagents"}},{q="Where did the gnolls take the supplies?",a="Stone Cairn Lake lies in eastern Elwynn Forest, north of the Tower of Azora. Gnoll packs have established camps along its shores and surrounding islands, posing a regular hazard to travelers on nearby roads.",al={"where is stone cairn lake","where are gnolls holding supplies","lake gnolls elwynn"}},{q="Why is Blixie Fitzwink worried about losing the package?",a="Blixie fears Master Theocritus will punish her with weeks of cleaning up after polymorph experiments. Apprentices at the tower are given mundane or messy chores whenever errands or magical duties go awry.",al={"what happens if blixie fails","blixie punishment","polymorph cleanup"}},{q="Why are gnolls attacking travelers in eastern Elwynn?",a="Gnoll packs prowl eastern Elwynn Forest, setting up hostile camps around Stone Cairn Lake and raiding nearby roads. Their presence disrupts trade and ambushes isolated travelers moving between settlements and arcane outposts.",al={"why are gnolls near tower of azora","gnoll threat elwynn","riverpaw stone cairn lake"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Maintaining a secure supply route to the Tower of Azora keeps Stormwind's frontline mages equipped against threats in Elwynn Forest and beyond.",["class:Mage"]="The arcane research conducted at the Tower of Azora demands delicate materials, making recovered enchanting reagents vital to mages studying under Master Theocritus.",["race:Gnome"]="Gnome apprentices like Blixie Fitzwink often find themselves handling menial tasks for human archmages, where mistakes can lead to humiliating chore duty."},kw={"blixie fitzwink","elwynn forest","enchanting supplies","gnolls","master theocritus","polymorph","stone cairn lake","tower of azora"},rel={"subzone:tower-of-azora","subzone:stone-cairn-lake","npc:theocritus","topic:gnoll"},m={id=91725},src={"In-game quest text: Stolen Enchanting Supplies"},fv=true}
E["quest:91732"] = {t="quest",n="Good Steel",z="general",s="Good Steel is an Alliance quest originating at Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest. In the wake of recent conflicts, high-quality metal has become scarce, leaving the camp's woodcutting equipment worn and difficult to replace. Craftsman Hagar Lowe tasks adventurers with delving into Jasperlode Mine to salvage well-crafted pickaxes so their steel can be repurposed.",h="Good Steel is an Alliance quest originating at Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest.",sec={{t="A Kingdom's Scarcity",b="Following the devastating conflicts that have battered the human kingdoms, high-grade steel has become an increasingly scarce luxury in Elwynn Forest. At Eastvale Logging Camp, daily timber harvesting relies heavily on durable axes, saws, and hardware, but the tools currently available have suffered badly from substandard metal and constant wear. Without reliable shipments coming from Stormwind's foundries, camp craftsmen like Hagar Lowe are forced to find inventive solutions to keep the forestry camp functioning.",sp=0},{t="Salvaging the Past",b="To replace worn equipment, Hagar sets his sights on Jasperlode Mine, an abandoned excavation north of the camp. The miners who worked the rich veins before the mine's fall used finely crafted picks forged from an era when steel was plentiful and expertly tempered. Rather than letting those sturdy implements rust away in the dark, adventurers are dispatched to venture into the infested tunnels, recover the abandoned picks, and bring them back to Eastvale so their superior metal can be melted down and repurposed for the camp's lumberjacks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Good Steel?",a="Hagar Lowe is a woodworker or craftsman stationed at Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest. Facing a shortage of decent tools, he asks adventurers to salvage quality steel equipment from nearby abandoned operations.",al={"who gives good steel","where do i turn in good steel","who is hagar lowe"}},{q="Why does Hagar Lowe need mining tools from Jasperlode Mine?",a="Following recent wars, quality steel is in short supply across the kingdom, leaving the camp's work tools badly worn. Hagar seeks the finely-crafted pickaxes left behind in Jasperlode Mine so he can melt down and repurpose their steel into usable lumbering tools.",al={"why get picks","why does hagar want mining tools","purpose of mining tools"}},{q="What is Jasperlode Mine?",a="Jasperlode Mine is an old mining site located in north-eastern Elwynn Forest, north of Eastvale Logging Camp. Formerly an active operation for the kingdom, it was overrun and abandoned, leaving its finely-crafted mining equipment scattered in the depths.",al={"what is jasperlode mine","where are the tools","jasperlode lore"}},{q="Why is good steel in short supply?",a="The Third War and prolonged conflicts severely disrupted production, supply lines, and trade across the human kingdoms. With resources stretched thin defending and rebuilding the realm, peripheral settlements like Eastvale must make do by salvaging durable metals from older eras.",al={"why is steel scarce","steel shortage elwynn","after the war metal shortage"}},{q="What is Eastvale Logging Camp?",a="Eastvale Logging Camp is Stormwind's primary timber harvesting settlement, located on the eastern border of Elwynn Forest near Redridge Mountains. Its woodcutters supply raw lumber to Stormwind and surrounding communities, making reliable tools vital to their work.",al={"where is eastvale logging camp","eastvale camp purpose","eastvale lumber camp"}},{q="What makes the tools in Jasperlode Mine so special?",a="The tools left behind in Jasperlode Mine were forged during an era of higher craftsmanship before recent wartime shortages compromised metal supplies. As Hagar notes, their enduring shine and durability prove they were crafted with far better steel than what is available today.",al={"who makes the picks","why are the picks good","mining tools quality"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="High-grade steel remains scarce in post-war Elwynn, forcing smiths and craftsmen to scrounge and repurpose metal from old, abandoned industrial tools.",["profession:Mining"]="The sturdy pickaxes left behind in Jasperlode Mine showcase an older, superior standard of metalworking that modern Elwynn workers struggle to match.",["faction:Alliance"]="Supply strains across Stormwind's outlying territories mean local settlements like Eastvale Logging Camp must salvage abandoned equipment to keep their operations running."},kw={"eastvale logging camp","elwynn forest","good steel","hagar lowe","jasperlode mine","mining tools","post-war shortages","repurposing steel"},rel={"subzone:jasperlode-mine","subzone:eastvale-logging-camp"},m={id=91732},src={"In-game quest text: Good Steel"},fv=true}
E["quest:91733"] = {t="quest",n="Downstream",z="general",s="Downstream is a quest in eastern Elwynn Forest tasked by Ormin Pelford at Eastvale Logging Camp. After Ormin's wagon accidentally overturns into the river, adventurers are asked to brave murlocs and wolves downstream to recover waterlogged timber supplies.",h="Downstream is a quest in eastern Elwynn Forest tasked by Ormin Pelford at Eastvale Logging Camp.",sec={{t="A Mishap on the Road",b="While transporting a shipment of gear back from a local trading post, laborer Ormin Pelford suffered a mishap when his wagon tipped over along the waterway near Eastvale Logging Camp. The cargo tumbled into the current and was quickly swept downstream before he could secure it. Without these vital woodworking tools, the loggers face delays in their timber operations.",sp=0},{t="Perils Along the Riverbank",b="Retrieving the waterlogged cargo is complicated by the hazardous local wildlife. Aggressive murlocs claim the muddy shoals downstream, while predatory wolves roam the surrounding woods. Unable to fend off these threats alone, Ormin relies on hardy travelers to braze the riverbanks, salvage the submerged saw, axe, and toolbox, and bring them back safely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Downstream?",a="Ormin Pelford offers this task near Eastvale Logging Camp. He is a worker who accidentally dumped his supply wagon into the river while hauling goods from the trading post.",al={"who gives downstream","downstream quest giver","who is ormin pelford"}},{q="What supplies were lost in the water?",a="The lost supplies consist of a Waterlogged Axe, a Waterlogged Saw, and a Waterlogged Toolbox that slipped into the water when Ormin Pelford's wagon tipped over.",al={"what fell in the river","lost supplies downstream","what items downstream"}},{q="Where did the supplies wash up?",a="The crates and tools drifted downstream along the river running south of the Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest.",al={"where are the tools","where do supplies wash up","downstream location"}},{q="Why can't Ormin Pelford retrieve the supplies himself?",a="Ormin cannot brave the riverbanks alone because predatory river murlocs and hungry forest wolves prowl the waterways where the cargo drifted.",al={"why can't ormin get supplies","why ormin pelford needs help"}},{q="What dangers lurk along the riverbanks?",a="The riverbanks downstream are infested with aggressive murlocs and prowling wolves, making salvage work dangerous for an unarmed laborer.",al={"downstream river hazards","threats downstream"}},{q="Why are these tools important to Eastvale?",a="The axes, saws, and toolboxes are essential equipment for the woodsmen of Eastvale Logging Camp, who provide raw timber for Elwynn Forest and Stormwind.",al={"why are tools important eastvale","logging camp timber tools"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Keeping Eastvale Logging Camp equipped with tools ensures vital timber supplies continue flowing toward Stormwind and Elwynn's settlements."},kw={"downstream","eastvale logging camp","elwynn forest","murloc","ormin pelford","waterlogged axe","waterlogged saw","waterlogged toolbox"},rel={"subzone:eastvale-logging-camp","topic:murloc"},m={id=91733},src={"In-game quest text: Downstream"},fv=true}
E["quest:91736"] = {t="quest",n="Applejack Still",z="general",s="The Applejack Still is a distillation apparatus used to process fermented apples into high-proof liquor. Approaching the device reveals a stinging vapor of concentrated fruit, which the machine condenses and dispenses as a clear spirit.",h="The Applejack Still is a distillation apparatus used to process fermented apples into high-proof liquor.",sec={{t="The Distillation Device",b="The Applejack Still is a functional distillation device constructed to refine fermented cider into high-proof spirits. Anyone stepping close to the apparatus is immediately greeted by a pungent aroma of concentrated apples sharp enough to sting the nostrils. Its piping and condensing coils efficiently extract and concentrate the essence of the orchard harvest into potent liquor.",sp=0},{t="A Clear Pour",b="Once activated and allowed to run its course, the machine condenses the vaporized alcohol into a clear, purified spirit. The finished applejack dispenses neatly into a waiting bottle at the outlet, ready for consumption or storage. Despite the rough-and-ready nature of field distilling, the apparatus reliably produces a clear and potent brew.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Applejack Still?",a="The Applejack Still is a mechanical distillation apparatus designed to produce applejack, a potent alcoholic spirit made from apples. When examined and operated, the machine condenses fermented apple mash or cider into a clear, distilled liquor that pours directly into a bottle.",al={"what is applejack still","what is this device","what does the still do"}},{q="Why is the apple scent so strong around the still?",a="Inspecting the device reveals a sharp, pungent scent of concentrated apples that strongly burns the nostrils. The intensity comes from the high vapor concentration produced as alcohol evaporates and re-condenses inside the metal coils.",al={"what does it smell like","why does it burn","still scent"}},{q="What kind of liquid does the machine dispense?",a="Upon operating the machine, it dispenses a clear liquid directly into a bottle. This distilled spirit is applejack, a clear and potent fruit brandy produced through the still's distillation process.",al={"what liquid comes out","clear liquid still","what does it dispense"}},{q="How does the distillation device operate?",a="The still functions by heating fermented apple cider or wash, separating volatile alcohol vapors from the water and fruit solids, and condensing the vapor back into liquid. The resulting distilled spirit collects at the spout and fills an awaiting bottle.",al={"how does applejack still work","distillation process"}},{q="What exactly is applejack?",a="Applejack is a traditional strong alcoholic drink made by distilling fermented apple cider. The resulting spirit is clear, aromatic, and notably higher in alcohol content than standard cider or ale.",al={"what is applejack","applejack drink"}},{q="Is operating the still hazardous?",a="The still produces strong alcoholic vapors potent enough to sting the nose upon inspection. While functional enough to dispense clear liquor without exploding, working with high-proof alcohol vapors and pressure requires caution.",al={"is the still safe","is it dangerous"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Distilling and brewing strong spirits from harvested orchard fruit is a staple craft among rustic cooks and tavern keepers across Azeroth.",["profession:Engineering"]="The apparatus uses mechanical condensation and plumbing to process fermented cider into concentrated liquor automatically."},kw={"alcohol","apparatus","applejack","brewing","cider","distillery","liquor","still"},m={id=91736},src={"In-game quest text: Applejack Still"},fv=true}
E["quest:91740"] = {t="quest",n="Croaky's Head",z="general",s="Croaky's Head is the severed bounty from a menacing murloc leader who terrorized the roads of eastern Elwynn Forest. Bringing proof of the creature's death to the Stormwind guard at Ridgepoint Tower offers grim relief to a garrison grieving their fallen comrades. It marks an essential victory for local defenders struggling to secure Elwynn's contested waterways.",h="Croaky's Head is the severed bounty from a menacing murloc leader who terrorized the roads of eastern Elwynn...",sec={{t="Terror on the Eastvale Roads",b="Murlocs along the rivers and streams of Elwynn Forest have grown increasingly bolder, ambushing unwary travelers and threatening vital trade routes. Among these aquatic raiders, Croaky stood out as a particularly aggressive chieftain, rallying other murlocs and leading attacks dangerously close to local roads. For merchants, farmers, and travelers moving between Elwynn and Redridge, the creature's presence turned peaceful roadways into deadly hazard zones. Striking down this aggressive raider halts a relentless series of ambushes, removing a brazen menace that openly challenged Stormwind's hold over the eastern woodland outskirts.",sp=0},{t="The Watch at Ridgepoint Tower",b="Ridgepoint Tower stands as Stormwind's easternmost sentinel post in Elwynn Forest, guarding the strategic crossing into Redridge Mountains. The guards stationed here, led by Officer Merell Ross, have found themselves stretched thin against the surrounding dangers. The escalating murloc aggression recently claimed the lives of two watchmen on patrol, shaking the outpost and highlighting just how vulnerable the border has become. Presenting Croaky's severed head to Officer Ross delivers tangible proof of retribution, lifting the beleaguered garrison's morale and giving the defenders an opening to finally drive the aquatic raiders back from Elwynn's borders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Croaky?",a="Croaky was a notorious murloc leader who terrorized travelers and local patrols along the waterways of eastern Elwynn Forest. Known for coordinating aggressive ambushes near the roadways, the creature posed a severe threat to regional peace until an adventurer hunted the beast down.",al={"who is croaky","croaky lore","what kind of murloc is croaky"}},{q="Who is Merell Ross?",a="Merell Ross is a member of the Stormwind city watch stationed at Ridgepoint Tower on the eastern border of Elwynn Forest. Tasked with protecting the high road into Redridge Mountains, Ross oversees security in the borderland and seeks to rally the garrison after losing guards to aggressive murloc raids.",al={"merell ross","who is officer ross","ridgepoint tower commander"}},{q="Where is Ridgepoint Tower located?",a="Ridgepoint Tower is located at the far eastern edge of Elwynn Forest, guarding the road that crosses into the Redridge Mountains. Situated east of the Eastvale Logging Camp, this stone garrison serves as a forward defensive fortification for Stormwind's guards against hostile creatures and highway bandits.",al={"where is ridgepoint tower","ridgepoint tower location","how to find ridgepoint tower"}},{q="Why did the guards at Ridgepoint Tower want Croaky dead?",a="The Stormwind watchmen stationed at Ridgepoint Tower recently suffered heavy losses, with murlocs killing two patrol members in the surrounding woods and waters. The guards wanted the murloc leader eliminated to stop ongoing ambushes along the road and initiate a concerted effort to drive the creatures out of Elwynn.",al={"why kill croaky","why do guards want croakys head","murloc attack on ridgepoint watch"}},{q="What does turning in Croaky's Head accomplish for Elwynn Forest?",a="Delivering Croaky's Head to Ridgepoint Tower provides the garrison with confirmation that a dangerous organizer of roadside raids has fallen. With the murloc leader dead, the guards hope to regain the offensive, secure the eastern trade paths, and gradually push the encroaching murloc tribes out of Elwynn's riverbanks.",al={"croakys head purpose","what happens when you kill croaky","turn in croakys head"}},{q="Why are murlocs such a threat along Elwynn's roads?",a="Murlocs commonly nest along the rivers, lakes, and streams running through Elwynn Forest. Because key roads run alongside these waterways, aggressive tribes frequently ambush unsuspecting travelers, trade caravans, and isolated military patrols, overwhelming defenders with coordinated numbers and fierce, unpredictable raids.",al={"why are murlocs attacking roads","elwynn murloc problem","murloc threat in elwynn"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As protectors of the kingdom's heartland, Alliance champions directly aid Stormwind's overextended forces by eliminating dangerous leaders who prey on travelers. Securing the eastern border road reinforces Alliance authority between Elwynn and Redridge.",["race:Human"]="For the humans of Elwynn, murloc incursions threaten their homesteads and claim the lives of local town guardsmen. Slaying Croaky brings justice for the two fallen watchmen and defends the realm's heartland.",["class:Warrior"]="Warriors understand the brutal reality of frontline combat and the cost paid by the fallen watchmen. Stepping in to fell an aggressive tribal chieftain relieves a hard-pressed defensive line."},kw={"croaky","croaky's head","eastvale","elwynn forest","merell ross","murloc","ridgepoint tower","stormwind guard"},rel={"subzone:ridgepoint-tower","topic:murloc"},m={id=91740},src={"In-game quest text: Croaky's Head"},fv=true}
E["quest:91741"] = {t="quest",n="Nibbled-On Book",z="general",s="The Nibbled-On Book is a volume from Northshire Abbey's library recovered from kobold scavengers in Northshire Valley. Bearing the tooth marks of a confused or ravenous creature, it must be returned to Brother Paxton to preserve the abbey's modest collection.",h="The Nibbled-On Book is a volume from Northshire Abbey's library recovered from kobold scavengers in...",sec={{t="A Chewed Tome",b="Among the subterranean pests troubling Northshire Valley, kobolds are notorious scavengers known for dragging off anything left unguarded. When an adventurer recovers a chewed tome dropped by a slain kobold, it becomes evident that the creature was not merely hoarding shiny trinkets, but actively trying to gnaw through the cover and parchment. The text clearly originates from the library shelves of nearby Northshire Abbey, bearing marks from the pests' voracious appetites.",sp=0},{t="Brother Paxton's Plight",b="Inside Northshire Abbey, Brother Paxton diligently tends to a small library intended for the quiet contemplation and instruction of the Brotherhood of Northshire. Unfortunately, the brazen vermin creeping around the valley have developed a troublesome habit of slipping in and snatching volumes straight off the shelves. While Brother Paxton is grateful to recover this particular nibbled volume, he remains anxious about the rest of his scattered collection still missing in the hands of the valley's pests.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Nibbled-On Book?",a="The Nibbled-On Book is a library volume stolen from Northshire Abbey by infesting kobolds. When recovered, it bears distinct teeth marks, suggesting the subterranean pests may have mistaken parchment and leather binding for a source of nourishment.",al={"what is this book","nibbled book lore","what book did the kobold drop"}},{q="Why did the kobolds try to eat the book?",a="While kobolds are primarily subterranean scavengers obsessed with candles and mining, they frequently hoard or gnaw on whatever items they can scavenge. Brother Paxton laments that the pests seem to actively steal his books with the intention of chewing on or eating them.",al={"why do kobolds want books","why are kobolds eating books","kobold diet books"}},{q="Who is Brother Paxton?",a="Brother Paxton is a cleric residing inside Northshire Abbey who maintains the abbey's modest library and collection of texts. He is distressed by kobolds sneaking into the abbey to pilfer books from his shelves.",al={"who is brother paxton","paxton northshire","abbey librarian"}},{q="Where did the book originally come from?",a="The book belongs on the shelves of the small library maintained by Brother Paxton within Northshire Abbey, located in the secluded Northshire Valley of Elwynn Forest.",al={"where did the book come from","where does paxton keep books","northshire library"}},{q="Are there other missing books from the abbey's library?",a="Yes, upon receiving the returned volume, Brother Paxton notes with concern that the kobolds have been repeatedly stealing from his shelves and wonders what he will do about the rest of his missing collection.",al={"are more books missing","rest of the collection","did kobolds steal more books"}},{q="Who should the book be returned to?",a="The recovered book must be returned directly to Brother Paxton inside Northshire Abbey, who inspects the tooth-marked cover and places it back into his care.",al={"how to get the book back","who wants the nibbled book","return book to paxton"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Northshire Abbey serves as the cradle for fresh Alliance recruits in Elwynn Forest, where even small errands like recovering stolen abbey property help secure the local valley.",["race:Human"]="The library inside Northshire Abbey preserves local clerical and historical knowledge for the kingdom of Stormwind, making the recovery of missing tomes personal to the residents of Northshire."},kw={"abbey library","brother paxton","elwynn forest","kobold","nibbled-on book","northshire abbey","stolen book"},rel={"topic:kobold","quest:344"},m={id=91741},src={"In-game quest text: Nibbled-On Book"},fv=true}
E["quest:91743"] = {t="quest",n="Rascally Rodents",z="general",s="Rascally Rodents is a quest in Northshire Valley where Brother Paxton tasks adventurers with recovering stolen books from the local kobolds. Though Paxton initially fears the vermin are devouring his library, retrieving the texts reveals an unsettling mystery behind the creatures' motives.",h="Rascally Rodents is a quest in Northshire Valley where Brother Paxton tasks adventurers with recovering...",sec={{t="A Threat to the Library",b="Brother Paxton in Northshire Valley is in distress over the local kobold infestation, which has spilled over from the surrounding hills and mines into the sacred grounds. Convinced that the ravenous creatures have begun devouring the texts from his collection, he enlists the aid of young adventurers to hunt down the vermin and salvage whatever volumes remain before the entire library is lost.",sp=0},{t="An Unexplained Appetite",b="Upon retrieving eight stolen volumes from the valley's kobolds, Brother Paxton discovers something unexpected: the creatures have not actually been eating the paper or bindings. Instead, the kobolds are deliberately taking and hoarding the books for reasons unknown, leaving the brother deeply unsettled as many volumes remain unaccounted for in the valley.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Rascally Rodents?",a="Brother Paxton, an abbey resident who is desperate to safeguard the abbey's library from the vermin infesting the valley, offers this task to adventurers in Northshire Valley.",al={"who gives rascally rodents","quest giver rascally rodents","paxton book quest"}},{q="Why are the kobolds stealing books?",a="Brother Paxton initially believes the kobolds are literally eating his books. However, upon inspecting the recovered volumes, he realizes they are not being devoured at all, leaving their true purpose and interest in the texts an unsettling mystery.",al={"why kobolds taking books","do kobolds eat books","why do kobolds want books"}},{q="Where do you find the Stolen Books?",a="The Stolen Books are found on the kobolds infesting Northshire Valley, who have pillaged the texts from the local collection and carried them into their camps and mine workings.",al={"where to find stolen books","stolen books location","kobold books northshire"}},{q="Who is Brother Paxton?",a="Brother Paxton is a member of the Northshire brotherhood who cares deeply for the written works housed in the valley and depends on passing initiates to rescue them from local scavengers.",al={"who is brother paxton","brother paxton lore"}},{q="What does Brother Paxton give you as a reward?",a="Upon returning eight of the stolen volumes, Brother Paxton gratefully gives you one of his how-to books that he claims he has no need for, while lamenting the many volumes that remain missing.",al={"what reward paxton gives","how to book reward"}},{q="Are all the stolen books recovered?",a="No. Even after recovering eight stolen books, Brother Paxton notes that many volumes remain missing throughout the valley, with the kobolds' strange interest in them still unexplained.",al={"are all books recovered","are all books saved"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="Human initiates beginning their training in Northshire Valley are called upon to defend the abbey's treasures and scholarly works from opportunistic pests and invaders.",["class:Priest"]="Priests aiding the Abbey of Northshire help maintain the sanctity and intellectual heritage of the Holy Light's scholars, protecting valuable texts from destruction.",["class:Mage"]="Scholars of the arcane understand the irreplaceable value of written knowledge and written works, even when under threat from unlikely thieves like kobolds."},kw={"brother paxton","kobolds","library","northshire abbey","northshire valley","rascally rodents","stolen books"},rel={"quest:344","subzone:northshire-valley","topic:kobold"},m={id=91743},src={"In-game quest text: Rascally Rodents"},fv=true}
E["quest:91745"] = {t="quest",n="Mining Consultant",z="general",s="Mining Consultant is a Northshire Valley quest that sends adventurers to confer with Kelsey Fargo, a mining apprentice stationed outside Echo Ridge Mine. Her observations reveal an unusual development: the local kobolds have begun plundering books from Northshire Abbey's library.",h="Mining Consultant is a Northshire Valley quest that sends adventurers to confer with Kelsey Fargo, a mining...",sec={{t="Trouble at the Mine",b="The kobold infestation at Echo Ridge Mine has taken an unexpected turn. Rather than merely defending subterranean claims and gathering candles, the creatures have caught the attention of local authorities due to strange, coordinated disturbances. Adventurers are directed to seek out Kelsey Fargo, a mining apprentice stationed near the mine's entrance, to ascertain what she has witnessed from her vantage point outside the tunnels.",sp=0},{t="Scholarly Scavengers",b="Kelsey Fargo confirms that the mine's pests are displaying bizarre behavior: kobolds have been spotted scampering to and fro carrying heavy tomes and bickering amongst themselves. Kelsey deduces that these volumes were looted directly from the library housed within Northshire Abbey, raising concerns about what illiterate tunnel-dwellers could possibly want with stolen scholarly texts.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Kelsey Fargo?",a="Kelsey Fargo is an apprentice miner stationed just outside the entrance to Echo Ridge Mine in Northshire Valley. Because of her work post, she is in prime position to observe the unusual movements and habits of the local kobolds.",al={"who is kelsey fargo","kelsey fargo role","mining apprentice northshire"}},{q="Why are kobolds carrying books?",a="According to Kelsey Fargo, kobolds have been seen running back and forth while arguing and carrying books. She suspects they are stealing volumes directly from the library within Northshire Abbey, though their exact purpose remains a mystery.",al={"why are kobolds stealing books","kobolds reading","books in echo ridge mine"}},{q="Where is Echo Ridge Mine?",a="Echo Ridge Mine is situated in northwestern Northshire Valley, tucked into the hills northwest of Northshire Abbey. It has become heavily infested with kobolds who disrupted mining operations.",al={"where is echo ridge mine","echo ridge mine location"}},{q="Where are the kobolds getting these books?",a="The stolen books are believed to have been taken from the library inside Northshire Abbey, which holds records, scriptures, and historical volumes maintained by the local human clergy and scholars.",al={"where do the books come from","abbey library kobolds"}},{q="What is the purpose of consulting the mining apprentice?",a="The task directs adventurers to check in with Kelsey Fargo outside the mine to investigate reports of peculiar kobold activities and learn what she has witnessed while stationed on-site.",al={"mining consultant quest purpose","why talk to kelsey"}},{q="What strange behavior have the kobolds exhibited?",a="Beyond their standard territorial scavenging and digging, kobolds around Echo Ridge Mine have been observed yelling at each other and carting off written texts, demonstrating unusually erratic coordination.",al={"what are the kobolds doing","kobold behavior northshire"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="Kelsey Fargo serves as a mining apprentice stationed right outside Echo Ridge Mine, offering a practical local contact for prospective miners seeking to understand mineral work and nearby conditions.",["race:Human"]="Northshire Valley is the training ground for new human adventurers, where securing the abbey's valuable archives and nearby mines against kobold intrusion is essential to regional stability."},kw={"echo ridge mine","kelsey fargo","kobold","library","mining","mining consultant","northshire abbey","northshire valley"},rel={"subzone:echo-ridge-mine","subzone:northshire-valley","topic:kobold"},m={id=91745},src={"In-game quest text: Mining Consultant"},fv=true}
E["quest:91746"] = {t="quest",n="Elmpaw's Head",z="general",s="Elmpaw's Head is a trophy quest rewarding an adventurer for hunting down the formidable beast Elmpaw. The creature's head is traded to an artisan who prepares mounted trophies for wealthy collectors.",h="Elmpaw's Head is a trophy quest rewarding an adventurer for hunting down the formidable beast Elmpaw.",sec={{t="The Fallen Beast",b="Elmpaw's Head marks the conclusion of a hunt against the creature known as Elmpaw. Felling such a beast is regarded as an impressive accomplishment by wilderness trackers and collectors alike, who value tangible proof of victory over dangerous wildlife.",sp=0},{t="A Collector's Prize",b="Upon presenting Elmpaw's severed head, the hunter is rewarded directly by an enterprising handler. Rather than letting the remains go to waste, the recipient prepares and mounts the trophy to sell to affluent patrons looking to adorn their mantles with fierce wild prizes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who or what is Elmpaw?",a="Elmpaw is a formidable creature whose defeat is considered an impressive feat. While specific details on its species are scarce, hunters seek out the beast to claim its head as a prized trophy.",al={"who is elmpaw","what is elmpaw","elmpaw boss"}},{q="Why does the quest giver want Elmpaw's head?",a="The quest giver prepares the head to be mounted and displayed, noting that wealthy collectors pay handsomely to have such exotic trophies decorating their mantles.",al={"why want head","elmpaw head turn in","what is trophy for"}},{q="Who are the collectors interested in the trophy?",a="The quest giver deals with wealthy collectors who value exotic beast trophies for display in their homes. Once properly prepared, the mounted head fetches a high price among these enthusiasts.",al={"who buys trophy","elmpaw buyers","who wants elmpaw head"}},{q="Is Elmpaw a dangerous target?",a="The quest giver describes taking down Elmpaw as very impressive, suggesting the beast is a notable challenge or rare prowler rather than an ordinary wild creature.",al={"how hard is elmpaw","is elmpaw tough"}},{q="What kind of task is Elmpaw's Head?",a="It functions as a bounty and trophy trade. An adventurer defeats Elmpaw in the wild, harvests the head, and brings it to an interested party who rewards them before preparing the mount for resale.",al={"is elmpaw head a bounty","who posted elmpaw quest"}},{q="Where can Elmpaw be found?",a="Elmpaw roams the wilderness where adventurers and big-game trackers cross paths with dangerous beasts. Bring its head back to the collector once you have triumphed.",al={"elmpaw location","where to find elmpaw"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Taking down a formidable beast like Elmpaw and claiming a physical trophy is the quintessential mark of a skilled tracker and hunter."},kw={"beast","collector","elmpaw","head","hunter","mantle","trophy"},m={id=91746},src={"In-game quest text: Elmpaw's Head"},fv=true}
E["quest:91751"] = {t="quest",n="Rough Wolf Pelts",z="general",s="Rough Wolf Pelts is an introductory skinning quest in Elwynn Forest offered by Helene Peltskinner near Goldshire. With her husband away assisting near Northshire Abbey, Helene relies on capable skinners to harvest raw hides from local wolves to complete her pending leatherworking orders.",h="Rough Wolf Pelts is an introductory skinning quest in Elwynn Forest offered by Helene Peltskinner near...",sec={{t="A Skinner's Need",b="Just outside Goldshire, Helene Peltskinner works diligently to supply travelers and residents with cured leather and furs. While her husband typically hunts the prowling timber wolves of Elwynn Forest to keep her workshop supplied, duties near Northshire Abbey have taken him away from the workbench. Left with a backlog of customer orders to finish, Helene enlists the aid of passing apprentices willing to turn their skinning knives toward the local wildlife.",sp=0},{t="Prowlers of the Forest",b="The dense woodlands of Elwynn Forest teem with timber wolves that stalk the edges of farmlands and roadways. For novice skinners, gathering rough wolf pelts serves as practical field experience in the trade of hide harvesting. Delivering these skins allows Helene to maintain her business and keep trade flowing through Goldshire, while providing wandering hunters a chance to hone their woodcraft.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Helene Peltskinner?",a="Helene Peltskinner is a local skinner and leatherworker based just outside Goldshire in Elwynn Forest. She prepares hides and fulfills craft orders for travelers and locals alike. With her husband occupied helping near Northshire Abbey, she relies on passing adventurers to gather the fresh wolf hides she needs to complete her trade work.",al={"who is helene peltskinner","helene peltskinner","who gives rough wolf pelts"}},{q="Why does Helene need wolf pelts?",a="Helene is working through a backlog of leather orders near Goldshire and requires raw materials to keep her business running. Normally, her husband hunts wolves to supply her workbench, but his commitments elsewhere in Elwynn have left her short on rough pelts. Adventurers help fill the gap by skinning local wolves for her craft.",al={"why need rough wolf pelts","why collect pelts","what does helene do with pelts"}},{q="Where is Helene's husband?",a="Helene's husband usually assists her with hunting wolves and procuring skins, but he is currently occupied north by Northshire Abbey. Because of his absence, Helene must contract local adventurers to bring in wolf hides so she can keep up with her workshop's ongoing pelt orders.",al={"where is helene husband","helene husband","who is helene husband"}},{q="Where are the wolves found in Elwynn Forest?",a="Rough wolf pelts are acquired from the timber wolves that roam the woodlands of Elwynn Forest surrounding Goldshire and Northshire. Those practicing the skinning trade harvest these pelts directly from beasts across the forest floor to deliver back to Helene's leatherworking post.",al={"where to find rough wolf pelts","which wolves drop pelts","wolf locations elwynn"}},{q="Is skinning required to harvest these pelts?",a="Helene explicitly offers this task as an opportunity for travelers to practice the skinning trade. The objective requires actually skinning slain wolves rather than simply hunting them for casual trophy drops, making it tailored specifically for apprentices learning to harvest animal hides.",al={"do i need skinning for rough wolf pelts","rough wolf pelts skinning requirement","skinning quest goldshire"}},{q="Where can Helene Peltskinner be found?",a="Helene Peltskinner sets up her work near Goldshire, the central settlement of Elwynn Forest. She conducts her leather trade close to the township, where hunters and travelers moving along the King's Road can readily bring her freshly harvested forest hides.",al={"where is helene peltskinner located","helene peltskinner location","where to find helene"}}},kw={"elwynn forest","goldshire","helene peltskinner","leatherworking","pelts","rough wolf pelts","skinning","timber wolves"},rel={"subzone:goldshire","zone:elwynn"},m={id=91751},src={"In-game quest text: Rough Wolf Pelts"},fv=true}
E["quest:91752"] = {t="quest",n="The Big Picture",z="general",s="The Big Picture is a Northshire Valley task concerning a thieving kobold named Shinyfinder Narf, who has been hoarding stolen books deep within the local mine. Adventurers are dispatched by local authorities to recover the illustrated volumes and return them to Marshal McBride at Northshire Abbey.",h="The Big Picture is a Northshire Valley task concerning a thieving kobold named Shinyfinder Narf, who has been...",sec={{t="The Kobold Book Thief",b="A peculiar incident has disrupted the peace of Northshire Valley: kobolds lurking around the region have been seen scurrying away with books, crying out 'Picture for Narf!' While kobolds are typically infamous for scavenging candles and shiny trinkets, one particular cave-dweller known as Shinyfinder Narf has developed an appetite for illustrated tomes, accumulating a whole sack of them in his subterranean hideout.",sp=0},{t="Recovering the Abbey's Tomes",b="The deeper shafts of the mine shelter the tougher, meaner breeds of kobold who fiercely guard Narf and his odd hoard. Recovering the stolen literature requires braving these darker tunnels to confront Narf directly. Once pried from the creature's clutches, the sack of books is brought to Marshal McBride at Northshire Abbey, who ensures the valuable volumes are safely returned to Brother Paxton.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Shinyfinder Narf?",a="Shinyfinder Narf is a kobold leader located deep in the back of the mine near Northshire Valley. He has been hoarding stolen books and calling out for picture books, prompting local authorities to order his cache recovered.",al={"who is narf","shinyfinder narf lore","narf kobold"}},{q="Why are the kobolds stealing books?",a="Kobolds venturing from the mine have been seen shouting 'Picture for Narf!' while snatching reading material. Rather than scholarly interest, Narf appears obsessed with gathering illustrated works, stuffing them into a sack deep within the tunnels.",al={"why do kobolds want picture books","what is picture for narf","why books stolen"}},{q="Whose books were stolen?",a="Brother Paxton is a resident of Northshire Abbey to whom the stolen books belong. Once the recovered sack of books reaches Marshal McBride, they are arranged to be returned directly to Brother Paxton's collection.",al={"who is brother paxton","paxton books"}},{q="Who is Marshal McBride?",a="Marshal McBride is the local law enforcement officer stationed just inside Northshire Abbey, responsible for maintaining order, protecting the abbey grounds, and organizing defense against the local kobold menace.",al={"who is marshal mcbride","where is mcbride"}},{q="Where can Shinyfinder Narf be found?",a="Narf resides in the deeper, more dangerous recesses of the local mine in Northshire Valley, surrounded by meaner kobolds who defend their scavenged hoard.",al={"where is narf","where to find narf","mine location"}},{q="How dangerous is the mine where Narf lives?",a="While common kobolds outside the mine are nuisance scavengers, the kobolds dwelling deeper in the tunnels alongside Narf are far meaner and more hostile, making Narf and his den dangerous for solitary adventurers.",al={"is narf dangerous","can you solo narf"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Northshire Valley serves as the training ground and sanctuary for fledgling Alliance champions, where keeping peace and recovering stolen goods from encroaching vermin is vital work.",["race:Human"]="Northshire Abbey is the cradle for newly recruited human adventurers stepping into service to protect Stormwind's outlying territories."},kw={"brother paxton","kobold","marshal mcbride","narf","northshire abbey","picture books","shinyfinder narf","the big picture"},rel={"npc:marshal-mcbride","quest:344","topic:kobold"},m={id=91752},src={"In-game quest text: The Big Picture"},fv=true}
E["quest:91753"] = {t="quest",n="An Enchanting Lesson",z="general",s="At the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest, Kitta Firewind gives An Enchanting Lesson to apprentice enchanters. Kobold geomancers bind simple magic into crude wax effigies, showing why practice matters alongside arcane theory.",h="At the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest, Kitta Firewind gives An Enchanting Lesson to apprentice enchanters.",sec={{t="Theory and Practice",b="Kitta Firewind, an enchanter at the Tower of Azora, challenges apprentice enchanters to look beyond polished libraries. She asks them to examine magic where it appears naturally. Even the uneducated kobolds beneath Elwynn Forest channel cantrips into everyday talismans, proof that magical instinct can thrive without formal schooling.",sp=0},{t="Primitive Cantrips",b="Kobold geomancers shape Crude Wax Effigies with simple folk enchantments. Students obtain the effigies and carefully unravel their bound magic through disenchantment, extracting Luminous Residue. By taking these primitive charms apart, they learn how raw magic behaves in the field. Enchantment's fundamental threads are found through observation as well as in grimoires.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest An Enchanting Lesson?",a="Kitta Firewind, an enchanter at the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest, offers this lesson to adventurers. She teaches that scholarly theory and hands-on practice belong together.",al={"who gives an enchanting lesson","quest giver enchanting lesson","kitta firewind quest"}},{q="Where is the Tower of Azora located?",a="The Tower of Azora stands in eastern Elwynn Forest. It is a bastion of magical study and wizardry amid the farmlands and woods of the human kingdom.",al={"where is tower of azora","tower of azora location"}},{q="How do kobolds know how to use magic?",a="Kobolds have no formal schooling or scholarly traditions, yet geomancers have an instinctive knack for binding basic cantrips and enchantments into crude wax effigies.",al={"can kobolds do magic","kobold geomancers magic","do kobolds have enchantments"}},{q="What are the wax effigies used for?",a="Crude Wax Effigies are primitive items fashioned by kobold geomancers. Though unsophisticated, they hold bound magic that can be unraveled and studied through disenchanting.",al={"what are wax effigies","crude wax effigies","kobold wax magic"}},{q="What is Luminous Residue?",a="Luminous Residue is the essence obtained by deconstructing and disenchanting the crude wax effigies carried by kobold geomancers. It is tangible proof of their innate cantrips.",al={"what is luminous residue","how to get luminous residue"}},{q="What is the main takeaway of Kitta Firewind's lesson?",a="Kitta Firewind teaches that theoretical knowledge and practical experimentation are inseparable. She urges students to study practical magic wherever it appears.",al={"lesson of the quest","what does kitta firewind teach"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Scholarly mages often scoff at subterranean vermin, yet Kitta Firewind reminds them that even primitive creatures can reveal unexpected truths about raw magical currents.",["faction:Alliance"]="Studying at the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest gives Alliance adventurers insight into academic wizardry and the surprisingly magical pests infesting their lands."},kw={"an enchanting lesson","disenchanting","elwynn forest","kitta firewind","kobold geomancer","luminous residue","tower of azora","wax effigy"},rel={"subzone:tower-of-azora","topic:kobold"},m={id=91753},src={"In-game quest text: An Enchanting Lesson"},fv=true}
E["quest:91758"] = {t="quest",n="Follow That Kobold!",z="general",s="Follow That Kobold! is an Alliance quest in Northshire Valley dealing with a bizarre theft from the local mines. A kobold has fled with a sack of Brother Paxton's books, prompting the abbey to recruit the dwarven hunter trainer Tordrin Sternblade to assist in tracking the thief.",h="Follow That Kobold!",sec={{t="The Book Thief of Northshire",b="Just when the workers and clerics of Northshire Abbey believed the vermin problem in the mines was under control, an unusual report arrived from Kelsey: a lone kobold was spotted fleeing the tunnels hauling a heavy sack of books. The stolen volumes belong to Brother Paxton, who urgently requires their return to the abbey's collection.",sp=0},{t="Calling in the Mountaineer",b="Tracking a nimble subterranean scavenger through the wooded grounds of Northshire requires specialized skill. To find the thief, adventurers are directed behind the abbey to the stables to seek out Tordrin Sternblade, a dwarven Hunter trainer newly arrived from Ironforge who possesses the wilderness knowledge needed to pick up the scent.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Follow That Kobold! about?",a="Follow That Kobold! is a quest in Northshire Valley tasked with pursuing a rogue kobold who fled the local mine carrying a heavy sack of stolen books belonging to Brother Paxton.",al={"what is follow that kobold","quest follow that kobold"}},{q="Who is Tordrin Sternblade?",a="Tordrin Sternblade is a dwarven Hunter trainer recently arrived from Ironforge. He is stationed behind Northshire Abbey near the stables and lends his tracking expertise to help recover the stolen books.",al={"who is tordrin sternblade","where is tordrin","hunter trainer northshire"}},{q="What did the kobold steal?",a="According to reports from Kelsey, a kobold slipped out of the Northshire mine hauling a large sack of books. The abbey needs them retrieved on behalf of Brother Paxton, though why a kobold would covet human tomes remains a mystery.",al={"why did kobold steal books","what did kobold take","sack of books"}},{q="Who reported the theft?",a="Kelsey Fargo is the new mining apprentice who works right outside the Northshire mine. She first noticed the unusual sight of a kobold departing the tunnels with a sack stuffed with books.",al={"who is kelsey","kelsey northshire"}},{q="Who is Brother Paxton?",a="Brother Paxton is a cleric residing at Northshire Abbey. The stolen library materials belong to him or his sacred duties, prompting the call for an experienced tracker to get them back.",al={"who is brother paxton","paxton books"}},{q="How do you track the thieving kobold?",a="Rather than charging blindly into the woods, adventurers are sent to consult Tordrin Sternblade behind the abbey, who prepares tracking techniques to follow the thief's trail.",al={"how do i track the kobold","tracking the thief"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This task introduces Tordrin Sternblade, a veteran hunter trainer newly arrived from Ironforge to assist Northshire Abbey.",["race:Dwarf"]="Tordrin Sternblade brings traditional dwarven tracking expertise straight from Ironforge to help the humans of Northshire.",["faction:Alliance"]="Cooperation between Stormwind's holy abbey and an Ironforge mountaineer highlights the strength of the Alliance's mutual support."},kw={"brother paxton","follow that kobold","hunter trainer","ironforge","kelsey","kobold","northshire","tordrin sternblade"},rel={"topic:hunter","topic:kobold","zone:ironforge","quest:344"},m={id=91758},src={"In-game quest text: Follow That Kobold!"},fv=true}
E["quest:91772"] = {t="quest",n="Shhh! We're Hunting Kobolds",z="general",s="'Shhh! We're Hunting Kobolds' is a quest that leads adventurers out of Northshire Valley to track wandering kobolds into Elwynn Forest. Using a tracking kit, players discover the trail leads toward the infested Fargodeep Mine and report the findings to Marshal Dughan in Goldshire.",h="'Shhh!",sec={{t="Tracking the Vermin",b="As the kobold infestation in Northshire Valley escalates, signs indicate that the vermin are organizing beyond the isolated abbey grounds. Equipped with a tracking kit, scouts are dispatched to follow the trail of the creatures to determine where they are concentrating their efforts. This reconnaissance serves as a crucial bridge between Northshire's local troubles and the broader defense of Elwynn Forest.",sp=0},{t="The Fargodeep Connection",b="The trail leads southwest out of Northshire and across Elwynn Forest toward the Fargodeep Mine. Marshal Dughan, commanding the garrison at Goldshire, is unsurprised by the report, noting that Fargodeep has already fallen to a heavy kobold infestation. The discovery sets the stage for military action to reclaim the vital mine.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the goal of this task?",a="The quest tasks an adventurer with using a specialized tracking kit to follow kobolds moving outside Northshire Valley. The goal is to discover where the creatures are gathering and deliver the intelligence to the Stormwind authorities in Goldshire.",al={"quest purpose","what is this quest","why hunt kobolds"}},{q="Who is concerned about the kobold movements?",a="Marshal McBride, the commander of Northshire Valley, is concerned with the movements of the kobolds. A local guide supplies the tracking kit to follow their trail out of the valley and toward the wider Elwynn Forest.",al={"who gives tracking kit","who is concerned about kobolds"}},{q="Where do the kobold tracks lead?",a="The tracks lead out of Northshire and directly toward the Fargodeep Mine in central Elwynn Forest, a site that has become heavily overrun with kobold infestations.",al={"where do tracks go","kobold destination","where are kobolds heading"}},{q="Who is Marshal Dughan?",a="Marshal Dughan is the commanding officer of the Stormwind guard garrison stationed in Goldshire. He coordinates local defense efforts across Elwynn Forest and receives reports on encroaching threats.",al={"who is marshal dughan","where is dughan","report to dughan"}},{q="Why are the kobolds heading to Fargodeep Mine?",a="Fargodeep Mine is an established mining site in Elwynn Forest that is already infested with kobolds. The movement of more vermin toward the mine signals worsening trouble for local workers and nearby settlements.",al={"why fargodeep mine","fargodeep kobolds"}},{q="What happens once you reach Goldshire?",a="Marshal Dughan confirms that Fargodeep Mine is infested with kobolds upon receiving your report. Rather than being surprised, he prepares to recruit capable fighters to deal directly with the mine's infestation.",al={"what happens after reporting","dughan reaction"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers departing Northshire help secure the supply lines and settlements of Elwynn by keeping Goldshire's garrison informed of gathering threats.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters skilled in tracking quarry can appreciate the practical application of tracking kits to follow vermin trails through the woods."},kw={"elwynn forest","fargodeep mine","goldshire","kobold tracking kit","kobolds","marshal dughan","marshal mcbride","northshire","shhh we're hunting kobolds"},rel={"subzone:fargodeep-mine","quest:62","npc:marshal-dughan","subzone:goldshire","npc:marshal-mcbride","topic:kobold"},m={id=91772},src={"In-game quest text: Shhh! We're Hunting Kobolds"},fv=true}
E["quest:91775"] = {t="quest",n="Book Return",z="general",s="Book Return is an Elwynn Forest quest directing adventurers to recover Brother Paxton's lost reading materials from the infested depths of Fargodeep Mine. Chief among the missing volumes is 'Fun with Elementals,' a beginner's guide that risks granting the mine's kobolds dangerous conjuration abilities if left in their clutches.",h="Book Return is an Elwynn Forest quest directing adventurers to recover Brother Paxton's lost reading...",sec={{t="Lost Pages in the Dark",b="While investigating the growing kobold menace within Fargodeep Mine, Marshal Dughan tasks adventurers with recovering missing volumes belonging to Brother Paxton of Northshire Abbey. Among the scattered texts lost in the dim subterranean tunnels is a particularly volatile primer titled 'Fun with Elementals.' Paxton fears that the instructions within are so accessible that even the mine's crude kobold squatters could decipher them and conjure an elemental creature in the depths.",sp=0},{t="Knowledge in Peril",b="Fargodeep Mine was once a prosperous gold venture for the kingdom of Stormwind, but recent troubles have allowed vermin to overrun its lower levels. As Elwynn's defenders stretch thin, recovered texts must be stored safely in a protective book bag to survive the damp, treacherous caverns. Retrieving Paxton's lost library ensures that mundane records are preserved and hazardous arcane lessons stay out of primitive hands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Book Return?",a="Book Return is a task assigned by Marshal Dughan in Goldshire to recover books belonging to Brother Paxton that went missing in the Fargodeep Mine. Along with scattered general reading, Paxton is especially anxious to recover a magical primer titled 'Fun with Elementals.'",al={"what is book return","book return quest","who gives book return"}},{q="Why are Brother Paxton's books inside Fargodeep Mine?",a="The kobolds stealing Brother Paxton's books from Northshire were tracked to the Fargodeep Mine, which has recently fallen overrun by kobolds. Paxton urgently wants his books gathered before they are ruined or misused.",al={"why paxton books in mine","how did books get into fargodeep","why are books in fargodeep mine"}},{q="What is the book 'Fun with Elementals'?",a="'Fun with Elementals' is an introductory conjuration text Brother Paxton is desperate to recover. He fears the instructions are so elementary and simple that even a dim-witted kobold could accidentally follow them to summon an elemental servant deep within the mine.",al={"fun with elementals","what is fun with elementals","paxton elemental book"}},{q="Who is Brother Paxton?",a="Brother Paxton is a cleric of Northshire Abbey known for his devotion to study, records, and literature. His concern over lost reading material extends beyond mere lost property, as some texts contain potent rudimentary magic.",al={"who is brother paxton","paxton lore","librarian paxton"}},{q="Why is Marshal Dughan involved in retrieving books?",a="Marshal Dughan oversees the defense of Elwynn Forest from Goldshire. While dispatching adventurers to investigate the kobold infestation threatening Fargodeep Mine, he uses the opportunity to help Brother Paxton retrieve his missing volumes before the kobolds pose an even greater magical threat.",al={"why dughan care about books","marshal dughan books"}},{q="Could a kobold really summon an elemental?",a="Kobolds are generally crude tunnel-dwellers known for geomancy and fire, but Brother Paxton worries that a simple instructional guide could grant them the means to conjure elemental forces, making an already dangerous infestation far worse.",al={"can kobolds use magic","can kobolds summon elementals"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The careless handling of arcane primers like 'Fun with Elementals' poses a serious hazard when left within reach of simple subterranean creatures like kobolds.",["faction:Alliance"]="Northshire's Abbey libraries and Goldshire's garrison work together to safeguard civilian literature and dangerous magical texts from Elwynn's pests."},kw={"book return","brother paxton","elwynn forest","fargodeep mine","fun with elementals","goldshire","kobold","marshal dughan"},rel={"quest:62","quest:344","subzone:fargodeep-mine","npc:marshal-dughan","topic:kobold"},m={id=91775},src={"In-game quest text: Book Return"},fv=true}
E["quest:91777"] = {t="quest",n="Rare Books",z="general",s="Rare Books is an Alliance quest in Northshire Valley focused on recovering two stolen magical primers for Brother Paxton. Kobolds have hauled the missing texts into the Jasperlode Mine, where they appear to be studying elementary magic and summoning rituals.",h="Rare Books is an Alliance quest in Northshire Valley focused on recovering two stolen magical primers for...",sec={{t="Missing from the Abbey",b="Brother Paxton of Northshire Abbey faces a crisis in his library: several volumes have been carried off by vermin infesting the valley. Only two books remain missing from his catalog: 'Geomancy for Curious Young Wizards' and 'Arcane Explainer: Magical Stuff in Simple Words'. These educational primers were carted away into the depths of the Jasperlode Mine by the occupying kobolds.",sp=0},{t="Arcane Kobolds in Jasperlode",b="The recovery of these simple texts carries unexpected urgency. While kobolds are typically known for petty thievery and mining picks, the kobolds within the Jasperlode Mine have begun meddling with the manuals' teachings. An apprentice kobold was already seen attempting to summon an elemental, prompting fears over what a more experienced geomancer or master might conjure if left with the arcane literature.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest Rare Books?",a="Rare Books is a quest in Northshire where an adventurer is tasked with recovering the final two stolen volumes from Brother Paxton's catalog, which have fallen into the hands of kobolds deep within the Jasperlode Mine.",al={"rare books quest","what is rare books","what is this quest"}},{q="Who is Brother Paxton?",a="Brother Paxton is a cleric residing inside Northshire Abbey who tends to the abbey's collection of books and documents. He relies on adventurers to recover library volumes stolen by invading kobolds.",al={"who is paxton","brother paxton lore","abbey librarian"}},{q="Which books need to be recovered?",a="The two missing volumes are 'Geomancy for Curious Young Wizards' and 'Arcane Explainer: Magical Stuff in Simple Words'. Both are introductory texts on arcane and magical theory.",al={"which books are missing","stolen book titles","missing library books"}},{q="Where are the missing books located?",a="The stolen books are located in the Jasperlode Mine, an infested gold mine situated in the Northshire Valley that has been overrun by kobold diggers and geomancers.",al={"where are the books","book locations","where to find the books"}},{q="Why did the kobolds take books on magic?",a="Kobolds in Jasperlode Mine appear to be using the stolen magical texts to experiment with geomancy and spellcasting. An apprentice was even reported attempting to summon an elemental using the stolen knowledge.",al={"why do kobolds have books","can kobolds read","kobold wizards"}},{q="Why does Brother Paxton reward weapons intended for the militia?",a="Brother Paxton rewards helpers with spare weapons originally stockpiled for the local Northshire militia, feeling they are better served in the hands of adventurers actively dealing with threats in the valley.",al={"paxton reward weapons","why militia weapons","abbey weapons"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Introductory primers like Geomancy for Curious Young Wizards and Arcane Explainer make for peculiar reading among subterranean kobolds, though novice arcanists will recognize the fundamental principles being studied.",["faction:Alliance"]="Recovering Northshire Abbey's stolen intellectual property from the Jasperlode Mine helps secure the valley's cultural heritage while assisting the local militia."},kw={"arcane explainer","brother paxton","geomancy","jasperlode mine","kobold","northshire abbey","northshire valley","rare books"},rel={"subzone:jasperlode-mine","quest:344","quest:76","topic:kobold"},m={id=91777},src={"In-game quest text: Rare Books"},fv=true}
E["quest:91899"] = {t="quest",n="A Sealed Crate",z="general",s="Trade handlers seek lost cargo as bandits disrupt the roads. You can recover sealed crates from the wilderness and outlaw camps to help keep supplies moving.",h="Trade handlers seek lost cargo as bandits disrupt the roads.",sec={{t="Endangered Trade Routes",b="Highway crime has risen of late. Organized bandits and rogue thieves prey on trade routes, ambushing wagons, pack trains, and lone couriers. Their cargo is scattered across wilderness roads or carried off to outlaw camps. These raids threaten the supply lines between settlements.",sp=0},{t="Reclaiming Lost Cargo",b="Traders and cargo handlers rely on independent adventurers to recover lost freight and keep supplies moving. Sealed crates rescued from bandits or found abandoned in the brush can be brought to trade handlers. There, the goods help secure supplies, curb illicit trade, and restore safety to regional commerce.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is A Sealed Crate?",a="A Sealed Crate holds lost or intercepted merchant goods recovered from wilderness routes. Handing it in keeps cargo from thieves and helps restore secure commerce.",al={"sealed crate quest","what is a sealed crate","crate turn in"}},{q="Why are trade routes being raided by bandits?",a="Bandits and outlaws have recently made travel dangerous on roads and trade routes. They target caravans and couriers, leaving cargo scattered in the wilds.",al={"why are bandits stealing crates","trade route bandits","who is attacking caravans"}},{q="Who collects these recovered crates?",a="Trade handlers and quartermasters accept recovered crates to catalog merchant property and build stockpiles safe from roaming thieves. They compensate adventurers and scavengers who retrieve them.",al={"who takes the sealed crates","where do sealed crates go","crate delivery"}},{q="Can you bring in multiple sealed crates?",a="Yes. Trade handlers encourage travelers to bring in any additional crates found on roads or in the wilds while bandits continue to disrupt deliveries.",al={"can you repeat sealed crate","more crates","are there more crates"}},{q="What is inside a sealed crate?",a="The crates hold recovered trade supplies and commercial cargo intercepted in transit. The courier delivering one has not opened it; it returns to trade stockpiles to limit losses to highwaymen.",al={"whats inside sealed crate","inside a sealed crate","what is in the crates"}},{q="Where do these recovered crates come from?",a="Crates may be found abandoned along dangerous trade routes, near bandit camps, or taken from outlaws who intercepted shipments.",al={"where to find sealed crates","how to get sealed crates","bandit crates"}}},kw={"bandits","caravan","cargo","delivery","sealed crate","supplies","thieves","trade routes"},m={id=91899},src={"In-game quest text: A Sealed Crate"},fv=true}
E["quest:91900"] = {t="quest",n="A Sealed Crate",z="general",s="A Sealed Crate represents trade cargo recovered from disrupted commercial routes. With bandits and thieves increasingly raiding roadways, local merchants rely on traveling adventurers to return abandoned shipments in exchange for payment.",h="A Sealed Crate represents trade cargo recovered from disrupted commercial routes.",sec={{t="Disrupted Trade Routes",b="Trade across the lands has grown increasingly dangerous due to a surge in banditry and roadside thefts. Caravans and couriers regularly face ambushes along established commercial highways, resulting in abandoned wagons and lost shipments scattered across the wilderness. To prevent these valuable goods from vanishing entirely, merchants and depot keepers rely on traveling adventurers to recover waylaid crates and return them safely.",sp=0},{t="Cargo Recovery",b="Recovering a sealed crate and bringing it to designated receiving depots earns an immediate bounty. Handlers consolidate the retrieved freight and handle proper sorting and redistribution, keeping commerce flowing despite the constant threat posed by local brigands and thieves. As long as the roads remain contested, returning lost cargo remains vital work for any traveler passing through.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is inside a sealed crate?",a="The crate contains recovered trade goods that were intercepted or lost along trade routes. Merchants and recipients pay couriers and adventurers to retrieve and return them unopened rather than letting the contents fall into criminal hands.",al={"what is in the crate","crate contents","whats inside a sealed crate"}},{q="Why are people asking for sealed crates to be delivered?",a="A recent uprising of bandits and thieves has disrupted active trade routes, leaving cargo scattered or stolen. Traders and local organizers offer bounties and compensation for anyone willing to recover and return these lost shipments.",al={"why return crates","who wants sealed crates","crate delivery reason"}},{q="Can you turn in multiple sealed crates?",a="Yes. Due to ongoing bandit raids on commercial routes, abandoned and stolen crates continue to turn up across troubled paths. Couriers are encouraged to bring any additional crates they discover back for further compensation.",al={"can i find more crates","where to get crates","more sealed crates"}},{q="Who is intercepting the trade routes?",a="Local thieves and bandits have increasingly targeted transport routes, ambushing caravans and intercepting commerce. While many different rogue outfits operate across the region, their common effect is leaving shipments discarded or seized along roads.",al={"who stole the crates","bandit uprising trade routes","who is attacking shipments"}},{q="Can you open a sealed crate yourself?",a="Whether you can open the crate yourself is unclear. Return recovered crates to the recipient to receive payment for the delivery.",al={"can i open the crate","open sealed crate","why keep crate sealed"}},{q="Who pays for recovering these shipments?",a="Local trade handlers and depot keepers receive the recovered cargo and provide compensation on the spot, managing the inventory and directing further retrieval efforts.",al={"who pays for the crates","who gives crate reward","crate compensation"}}},kw={"bandits","cargo","compensation","delivery","sealed crate","shipment","thieves","trade routes"},m={id=91900},src={"In-game quest text: A Sealed Crate"},fv=true}
E["quest:91904"] = {t="quest",n="A Sealed Crate",z="general",s="A Sealed Crate is a turn-in task involving recovered trade goods that have fallen into the hands of highwaymen or were lost along commercial routes. Adventurers return intercepted cargo to trade handlers to secure regional supply lines and receive payment.",h="A Sealed Crate is a turn-in task involving recovered trade goods that have fallen into the hands of...",sec={{t="Stolen Cargo and Imperiled Roads",b="Wagon trails and shipping lanes have become treacherous due to a sharp rise in ambushes by bandits and thieves targeting trade caravans. Unsuspecting haulers and couriers frequently lose their shipments when caught unawares, leaving heavy sealed crates abandoned along wild roads and wilderness paths until someone recovers them.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Supply Chain",b="Merchants and supply coordinators maintain standing bounties for the safe return of missing merchandise. Adventurers who retrieve displaced crates and haul them back to regional collection points help restore compromised distribution lines, earning direct monetary compensation for safeguarding commerce.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a sealed crate?",a="Sealed crates represent trade cargo that was intercepted or displaced along commercial paths. Adventurers who recover these abandoned goods can turn them in to merchants and local recipients to secure the freight and receive financial compensation for their salvage efforts.",al={"sealed crate purpose","what is a sealed crate","what are sealed crates for"}},{q="Why are these crates lying around?",a="A recent surge in banditry and organized thievery has plagued regional trade routes. Highwaymen and rogue opportunists regularly ambush supply caravans and haulers, resulting in shipments being stolen, abandoned, or dropped along the road.",al={"who lost the crates","why are crates lost","why crates on the road"}},{q="Who wants these crates returned?",a="Caravan coordinators, quartermasters, and trade merchants accept recovered crates to assemble consolidated freight piles and sort the cargo back into regular supply lines, compensating anyone who rescues their missing stock.",al={"who buys sealed crates","who wants sealed crates","crate turn in"}},{q="Can you look inside a sealed crate?",a="The crates remain locked and sealed for transit to ensure the security of their trade merchandise. Handing them over intact allows logistical handlers to process the goods and distribute payment to recoverers without tampering.",al={"can i open a sealed crate","whats inside the crate","open sealed crate"}},{q="Can you return more sealed crates in the future?",a="Yes. With highway robbery still widespread along major thoroughfares, merchants actively seek additional recovered freight and encourage wanderers to bring in any further displaced crates they locate across the wild.",al={"more crates","can i turn in more crates","are crates repeatable"}},{q="Who is intercepting the trade routes?",a="Thieves and cutpurses have grown increasingly bold across trade territories, targeting merchant shipments to pilfer valuable wares and disrupting commercial distribution between settlements.",al={"bandit attacks trade routes","thieves stealing goods","who is raiding the roads"}}},kw={"a sealed crate","bandits","caravan","cargo","crate","delivery","merchants","sealed crate","thieves","trade routes"},m={id=91904},src={"In-game quest text: A Sealed Crate"},fv=true}
E["quest:91905"] = {t="quest",n="A Sealed Crate",z="general",s="Trade recipients seek A Sealed Crate after bandits disrupt the roads. Recovered cargo helps keep local stores supplied and out of outlaw hands.",h="Trade recipients seek A Sealed Crate after bandits disrupt the roads.",sec={{t="Disrupted Trade Routes",b="Bandits and thieves have struck established trade routes, leaving merchant cargo scattered. Their ambushes put caravans and supply lines at risk. Across contested lands, lost shipments are a common sight. Recovering the crates helps local trade endure the lawlessness.",sp=0},{t="Recovered Cargo",b="Merchants and handlers rely on couriers to bring lost goods back to storage depots. Each returned crate adds to local stores and denies outlaws the gains of highway robbery. In return, salvage workers receive fair pay for facing dangerous roads.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is inside the sealed crate?",a="The crate holds commercial cargo, though its exact contents are not given. Merchants and handlers want the goods secured away from thieves.",al={"what is inside the sealed crate","crate contents","whats in the crate"}},{q="Why are these crates lying around?",a="Bandits and thieves have struck trade routes, scattering shipments and leaving crates abandoned or stolen across the region.",al={"why are crates lost","who is stealing crates","bandits stealing trade goods"}},{q="Who wants these crates returned?",a="Local merchants and trade coordinators receive the recovered crates. They add the goods to secure stores and pay adventurers who salvage them from danger.",al={"who takes the crates","where do the crates go","turn in crates"}},{q="Can more sealed crates be recovered?",a="Yes. Bandits continue to disrupt overland travel, so local handlers pay adventurers to recover other crates scattered along trade routes.",al={"can i find more crates","repeatable crates","more sealed crates"}},{q="What is threatening the trade routes?",a="Bandits and outlaws prey on unguarded caravans, threatening trade routes. Merchants pay for cargo recovered from the roads.",al={"bandit trade routes","trade disruptions","why are roads unsafe"}},{q="Why is the crate sealed?",a="The reason for the seal is not given. You return the lost cargo to the handlers, who add it to their pile and pay you.",al={"sealed crate purpose","why seal the crate"}}},kw={"bandits","cargo","crate","delivery","merchants","salvage","sealed crate","thieves","trade routes"},m={id=91905},src={"In-game quest text: A Sealed Crate"},fv=true}
E["quest:91920"] = {t="quest",n="Wild Eyes",z="general",s="In Silverpine Forest, Wild Eyes brings an ailing Forsaken scout to a desperate choice. Apothecary Renferrel's heart-based draughts have failed, and the scout seeks another way to stave off a second death.",h="In Silverpine Forest, Wild Eyes brings an ailing Forsaken scout to a desperate choice.",sec={{t="Desperate Measures",b="In Silverpine Forest, a Forsaken Deathstalker feels their body failing and faces a second death. Apothecary Renferrel's draughts have brought no relief. So the scout risks Renferrel's wrath and seeks another cure along the creature-ridden shores of Lake Lordamere.",sp=0},{t="New Reagents at the Sepulcher",b="The Deathstalker suspects Renferrel's focus on hearts has kept him from trying other reagents. So the scout asks you to gather murloc eyes and bring them to Renferrel at the Sepulcher. The strange organs broaden the apothecary's experiments, and he restores the fading scout.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who asks you to gather murloc eyes?",a="An ailing Forsaken Deathstalker in Silverpine Forest asks you to help restore their strength. With a second death looming and Renferrel's draughts failing, the scout gambles on different ingredients.",al={"who gives wild eyes","wild eyes quest giver","who asks for murloc eyes"}},{q="Why does the quest require murloc eyes?",a="The Deathstalker sees murlocs infesting Lake Lordamere's shores and proposes using their eyes. The scout hopes this new ingredient will draw Renferrel beyond his fixation on hearts.",al={"why murloc eyes","what are murloc eyes for","murloc eyes potion"}},{q="How does Apothecary Renferrel react to the delivery?",a="Renferrel first scoffs and blames the potion's failure on too few hearts. Yet he admits he had not considered murloc anatomy and agrees to use it to restore the Deathstalker.",al={"renferrel reaction","what does renferrel say","does renferrel like the eyes"}},{q="Where is Apothecary Renferrel located?",a="Apothecary Renferrel conducts his research at the Sepulcher, the main Forsaken outpost in Silverpine Forest.",al={"where is apothecary renferrel","renferrel location","where to turn in wild eyes"}},{q="Why did Renferrel's original potion fail to work?",a="Renferrel says the potion failed because he received only three hearts, though the recipe calls for six. The ailing Deathstalker noticed no improvement from the formula.",al={"why did potion fail","why did renferrels potion not work","deathstalker potion failure"}},{q="What is Apothecary Renferrel obsessed with?",a="Renferrel is fixated on animal hearts in his restorative mixtures. That focus keeps him from considering other specimens until the Deathstalker brings murloc eyes.",al={"renferrel heart obsession","why does renferrel want hearts"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Forsaken soldiers contend with the bodily decay of undeath. They rely on the Royal Apothecary Society's grim concoctions to sustain their failing strength.",["faction:Horde"]="The Forsaken war effort in Silverpine Forest relies on Apothecary Renferrel's work at the Sepulcher. His research sustains frontline scouts and Deathstalkers.",["class:Rogue"]="Deathstalkers are the Forsaken's elite scouts and assassins. Restoring their strength is a priority in the region."},kw={"apothecary renferrel","deathstalker","forsaken alchemy","lake lordamere","murloc eyes","sepulcher","silverpine forest","wild eyes"},rel={"npc:apothecary-renferrel","subzone:sepulcher","topic:murloc"},m={id=91920},src={"In-game quest text: Wild Eyes"},fv=true}
E["quest:91921"] = {t="quest",n="Return to Quinn (Again)",z="general",s="At the Ivar Patch, Quinn Yorick needs a remedy before his affliction claims his mind and body. A courier brings him a mixture brewed from six hearts, restoring his strength for the travels ahead.",h="At the Ivar Patch, Quinn Yorick needs a remedy before his affliction claims his mind and body.",sec={{t="A Desperate Remedy",b="North of the Sepulcher, Quinn Yorick rests at the Ivar Patch in Silverpine Forest. His affliction threatens both mind and body, so a special alchemical mixture brewed from six hearts is sent to his camp. For the Forsaken and their allies, bringing remedies to wandering kin can hold back rot and madness.",sp=0},{t="Averting Degeneration",b="Quinn Yorick feared he was nearing a grim end, becoming a mindless ghoul. He drinks the freshly brewed potion, and its effects come at once. His strength returns, and he can prepare to travel across the plagued land.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Quinn Yorick?",a="Quinn Yorick is an afflicted traveler at the Ivar Patch, north of the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. He needs special alchemical mixtures to stave off physical decline and regain enough strength to travel.",al={"who is quinn","quinn yorick lore","who is quinn yorick"}},{q="What is Quinn's Potion and why does he need it?",a="Quinn's Potion is a special mixture made to aid his recovery and keep his condition from worsening. Without it, he feared he would deteriorate into a mindless ghoul.",al={"what does the potion do","quinns potion purpose","why does quinn need a potion"}},{q="Where is the Ivar Patch located?",a="The Ivar Patch lies directly north of the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. It is Quinn Yorick's temporary shelter while he recovers.",al={"where is ivar patch","ivar patch location"}},{q="What are the ingredients in Quinn's potion?",a="Quinn's remedy is an alchemical mixture brewed from extracts of six whole hearts. It is meant to halt his bodily decay.",al={"potion ingredients","what is quinn potion made of","hearts potion"}},{q="Does the potion successfully cure Quinn Yorick?",a="The potion takes effect as soon as Quinn drinks it. He says he will soon be strong enough to travel again instead of becoming a ghoul.",al={"what happens to quinn","does quinn turn into a ghoul","quinn recovery"}},{q="Who brews the potion for Quinn?",a="Apothecary Renferrel at the Sepulcher brews Quinn's potion from the extract of six whole hearts. Impatient with paperwork, he hands it over and is eager to dismiss the courier.",al={"who makes quinn potion","apothecary quinn"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Quinn Yorick fears becoming a mindless ghoul. His plight shows how the Forsaken struggle to preserve their free will and physical faculties.",["faction:Horde"]="Helping distressed travelers and outcasts in Silverpine Forest strengthens Horde influence in the plagued lands north of the Sepulcher."},kw={"ghoul","ivar patch","quinn yorick","quinn's potion","return to quinn","silverpine forest","the sepulcher"},rel={"npc:quinn-yorick","item:quinn-s-potion","subzone:ivar-patch","subzone:sepulcher"},m={id=91921},src={"In-game quest text: Return to Quinn (Again)"},fv=true}
E["quest:92110"] = {t="quest",n="My First Real Potion",z="general",s="A child asks you to bring murloc eyes for My First Real Potion. She is eager to mix it herself.",h="A child asks you to bring murloc eyes for My First Real Potion.",sec={{t="A First Attempt",b="The child is glad to see the murloc eyes. While you were away, she gathered more herbs and found empty vials downstairs. Now she asks you to mix the ingredients as she watches. The potion turns sickly orange when the eyes go in.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the child want?",a="The child wants murloc eyes for her first real potion, along with herbs and empty vials.",al={"what she wants","child potion","potion ingredients"}},{q="Who mixes the potion?",a="You mix the potion while the child watches.",al={"who does the mixing","mix potion","potion maker"}},{q="What color does the potion turn?",a="The mixture turns sickly orange within seconds of adding the murloc eyes.",al={"potion color","orange potion","what does it look like"}},{q="Does the child drink it?",a="No. The child says she would not drink the potion, but gives you her recipe.",al={"drink potion","would she drink it","is it safe"}}},kw={"child","herbs","murloc eyes","my first real potion","orange potion","potion","vials"},rel={"topic:murloc"},m={id=92110},src={"In-game quest text: My First Real Potion"},fv=true}
E["quest:92401"] = {t="quest",n="A Frightened Request",z="general",s="Edward Heartweaver’s wife waits in fear after he vanishes in the Ruins of Lordaeron. Since their son was lost when the capital fell, Edward has gone each week to search the graveyard for his remains. Now his usual journey has lasted several days.",h="Edward Heartweaver’s wife waits in fear after he vanishes in the Ruins of Lordaeron.",sec={{t="A Father's Grief",b="When the Scourge destroyed Lordaeron during the Third War, the Heartweavers lost their young son. His body was never found in the ruin. So Edward Heartweaver went each week to the capital’s graveyard, still hoping to find some trace of his child.\n\nGrief and guilt kept Edward from letting go. The graveyard lay within the ruined capital, where the search was perilous.",sp=0},{t="The Perils of the Ruins",b="The surface ruins of Lordaeron are overrun by hostile creatures and lurking horrors. They are no place for ordinary citizens. Edward’s frightened wife warned him and begged him to stop going, but he kept to his weekly search.\n\nThen Edward failed to return for several days. His wife turned to capable adventurers and asked them to brave the ruins and learn what had become of him.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Edward Heartweaver?",a="Edward Heartweaver is a grieving father whose son was lost when the Scourge destroyed Lordaeron. He searched the ruined capital’s graveyard each week for the child’s remains, then vanished.",al={"who is edward","edward heartweaver lore","husband in ruins"}},{q="Why did Edward go to the Ruins of Lordaeron?",a="Edward went to the Ruins of Lordaeron to search for his son’s remains. The boy was lost when Prince Arthas and the Scourge destroyed the capital, and Edward kept searching despite his wife’s warnings.",al={"why did edward go to lordaeron","edward heartweaver graveyard","why search lordaeron ruins"}},{q="Why are the Ruins of Lordaeron so dangerous?",a="The surface ruins of Lordaeron are overrun by hostile creatures and dangerous horrors. Ordinary citizens cannot safely travel there, making a long search perilous.",al={"why are lordaeron ruins dangerous","what is in lordaeron ruins","ruins of lordaeron threats"}},{q="Who offers the quest A Frightened Request?",a="Edward Heartweaver’s frightened wife asks for help after he fails to return from his weekly search. She turns to capable adventurers to find out what happened to him.",al={"who gives a frightened request","a frightened request questgiver","edward wife quest"}},{q="What happened to Edward's son?",a="The Heartweavers’ son was lost when Prince Arthas and the Scourge destroyed Lordaeron. His body was never found, leaving Edward grieving and searching for his remains.",al={"what happened to the son","edward heartweaver son","lost son in lordaeron"}},{q="What is the objective of A Frightened Request?",a="Edward’s wife asks you to search the dangerous Ruins of Lordaeron and discover what happened to Edward Heartweaver.",al={"what do i do in a frightened request","a frightened request goal","how to complete a frightened request"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="The fall of Lordaeron remains a deep tragedy in human memory. As a human, you can see Edward’s desperate search as a reminder of the families torn apart when Prince Arthas destroyed his own people.",["race:Undead"]="The ruins above the Undercity are held by the Forsaken, who rose from the kingdom’s ashes. As an undead, you know living humans still wander the shattered capital’s graveyard, in lands the Forsaken now hold.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you can understand the grief that drives a father to search a graveyard for his lost son."},kw={"a frightened request","arthas","edward heartweaver","heartweaver","lordaeron graveyard","missing husband","ruins of lordaeron","scourge"},rel={"zone:ruinsoflordaeron","topic:lordaeron"},m={id=92401},src={"In-game quest text: A Frightened Request"},fv=true}
E["quest:92421"] = {t="quest",n="Light's Justice",z="general",s="In the Undercity, Morbin Lightbane sends warriors above to gather what the Forsaken need. Intact limbs from the Scourge in the Ruins of Lordaeron help repair wounded Forsaken and sustain their ranks.",h="In the Undercity, Morbin Lightbane sends warriors above to gather what the Forsaken need.",sec={{t="The Scourge Above",b="Above the Undercity, Scourge remnants still prowl the ruined streets of Lordaeron's former capital. Though the Dark Lady has defeated the dreadlords and founded the Forsaken kingdom, clearing the surface remains costly work. The undead threaten the gateway below and drain the forces sent to reclaim the fallen kingdom.",sp=0},{t="Flesh and Bone",b="Forsaken bodies do not mend on their own. Rotting tissue and shattered sinew cannot knit again, so Morbin Lightbane sends warriors to gather intact limbs from destroyed Scourge in the ruins above. Those parts can repair wounded Forsaken and help them endure and fight again.",sp=0},{t="A Captain's Legacy",b="Morbin Lightbane honors his late commander, Captain Garek Bandarion, by carrying on this work. Bandarion pledged to heal their people and free Lordaeron from Scourge occupation. To Morbin, destroying their mindless enemies and restoring their kin is justice in the service of the Banshee Queen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Morbin Lightbane?",a="Morbin Lightbane is a Forsaken paladin who lives in the Undercity. He carries out Lady Sylvanas's will by tending to his kin's physical survival and honoring his fallen captain, Garek Bandarion.",al={"who gives lights justice","who is morbin lightbane","quest giver"}},{q="Why are intact limbs needed?",a="Forsaken flesh decays and cannot heal or knit itself together. Intact limbs gathered from fallen undead in the ruins above can repair damaged bodies and help keep their fighting forces active.",al={"why intact limbs","why do forsaken need limbs","why harvest body parts"}},{q="Who was Captain Garek Bandarion?",a="Captain Garek Bandarion was a commander devoted to cleansing Lordaeron of Scourge remnants and healing his Forsaken kin. His followers carry on that work in his memory.",al={"who was captain bandarion","captain garek bandarion lore","garek bandarion"}},{q="Where are the Scourge forces located?",a="Scourge forces wander the Ruins of Lordaeron, the surface city directly above the Undercity. Forsaken efforts to purge them continue, but the invaders remain dangerous in number.",al={"where do scourge roam","where are intact limbs found","ruins of lordaeron scourge"}},{q="Are there paladins among the Forsaken?",a="Morbin Lightbane calls himself a paladin of the Forsaken. He directs his martial devotion toward serving Sylvanas Windrunner and protecting his people.",al={"can forsaken be paladins","undead paladins lore","forsaken paladin"}},{q="What does 'Light's Justice' signify for the Forsaken?",a="For Morbin Lightbane, justice means driving the mindless Scourge from Lordaeron's capital and using their remains to save the Forsaken.",al={"what does lights justice mean","why is it called lights justice","quest name meaning"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Forsaken bodies cannot regrow decaying flesh on their own. Gathering limbs from Scourge remnants is a grim necessity to keep wounded Forsaken and soldiers whole.",["class:Paladin"]="Morbin Lightbane calls himself a paladin of the Forsaken. His example shows holy warfare and martial duty enduring in undeath under the Dark Lady's service.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing the Ruins of Lordaeron above the Undercity protects the Horde's vital northern capital and strengthens its fragile hold in the Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"forsaken paladin","garek bandarion","intact limbs","light's justice","morbin lightbane","ruins of lordaeron","scourge","undercity"},rel={"zone:ruinsoflordaeron","zone:undercity","topic:forsaken","topic:paladin","topic:scourge","topic:lordaeron","topic:light"},m={id=92421},src={"In-game quest text: Light's Justice"},fv=true}
E["quest:92422"] = {t="quest",n="The Wrath of Rath'mael",z="general",s="In the Ruins of Lordaeron, a fog hides Scourge movements above the Undercity. Rath'mael, a Scourge necromancer, has shrouded the ruined streets and graveyard. Deathguard Kristof in Brill calls for you to strike him down.",h="In the Ruins of Lordaeron, a fog hides Scourge movements above the Undercity.",sec={{t="A Shroud Over the Ruins",b="Scout reports from Lordamere Overlook tell of a sinister presence in the Ruins of Lordaeron. An unholy fog now covers the ruined streets and nearby graveyard, hiding lingering Scourge forces. Rath'mael cast the shroud. His spells conceal the undead above the heart of Forsaken territory.",sp=0},{t="The Defense of Tirisfal",b="Deathguard Kristof in Brill sends you to rally your allies and strike Rath'mael down. The Forsaken cannot let this menace take root unchecked. The Scourge may replace him, but his fall will disrupt their coordination, disperse the fog, and give Tirisfal Glades room to breathe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Rath'mael?",a="Rath'mael is a Scourge necromancer who has risen among the undead in the Ruins of Lordaeron. His dark sorcery conjures an unholy fog that conceals Scourge movements in the streets and graveyard near Lordamere Overlook.",al={"who is rathmael","who is rath'mael","rathmael lore"}},{q="What is the unholy fog in the Ruins of Lordaeron?",a="The unholy fog over the ruined capital's streets and graveyard is conjured by Rath'mael. It conceals lingering Scourge forces from Forsaken patrols.",al={"what is the fog in lordaeron","why is there fog in ruins of lordaeron","unholy fog ruins"}},{q="Who offers this mission?",a="Deathguard Kristof, a Forsaken soldier, offers the call from Brill in Tirisfal Glades. He watches the approaches to the Capital City ruins and calls for Rath'mael's death before the Scourge threat grows too great.",al={"who gives wrath of rathmael","where is deathguard kristof","kristof brill"}},{q="Where is Rath'mael located?",a="Rath'mael operates in the Ruins of Lordaeron, around its streets and graveyard and near Lordamere Overlook above the lake.",al={"where is rathmael found","where to find rath'mael","rathmael location"}},{q="Why do the Forsaken want Rath'mael destroyed?",a="The Forsaken fear the Scourge gathering forces and hiding their movements above the Undercity. Rath'mael's fog threatens Forsaken defenses and travelers throughout Tirisfal.",al={"why kill rathmael","why fight rathmael"}},{q="Does Rath'mael's death permanently secure the ruins?",a="No. The Scourge will replace fallen lieutenants with another dark spellcaster. Rath'mael's death slows their immediate advance, thins their numbers, and lifts the fog for a time.",al={"does killing rathmael end the scourge","will scourge be gone after rathmael"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As Forsaken, you live in the Undercity beneath the ruined capital. Scourge forces in the courtyards and graveyards above threaten your doorstep.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you have a foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms. Securing Lordaeron's surrounding lands helps preserve it against the relentless undead."},kw={"brill","deathguard kristof","lordamere overlook","rath'mael","rathmael","ruins of lordaeron","scourge necromancer","the wrath of rath'mael","tirisfal glades","unholy fog"},rel={"zone:ruinsoflordaeron","subzone:brill","topic:scourge","topic:forsaken","topic:lordaeron"},m={id=92422},src={"In-game quest text: The Wrath of Rath'mael"},fv=true}
E["quest:92456"] = {t="quest",n="A Green Sample",z="general",s="A Green Sample is an errand offered by Shylamiir in Stormwind City, directing adventurers to infiltrate the secluded magical city of Dalaran. Expecting a pristine specimen of mana-infused vegetation from the central garden, the expedition uncovers unexpected demonic taint among the Kirin Tor's flora.",h="A Green Sample is an errand offered by Shylamiir in Stormwind City, directing adventurers to infiltrate the...",sec={{t="An Errand to Dalaran",b="In Stormwind City, the enigmatic Shylamiir seeks an adventurous traveler willing to run a discreet errand into restricted lands. Intrigued by the Kirin Tor's unusual pursuit of botany, Shylamiir desires a living specimen of the mana-infused vegetation cultivated within Dalaran's central garden. Because the secretive sorcerers keep their borders closed to outsiders, obtaining such flora requires someone adept at slipping into places uninvited.",sp=0},{t="Tainted Roots",b="Infiltrating the magocracy's central garden yields a shocking discovery: the flora has not merely absorbed pure arcane energy, but has mutated into a Fel Infused Blossom. When presented with the tainted cutting, Shylamiir is caught off guard, recognizing that the secretive Kirin Tor are contending with darker turmoil than outward appearances suggest. Nonetheless, the unexpected fel taint proves even more captivating to study than ordinary arcane herbs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest A Green Sample?",a="The quest is offered by Shylamiir in Stormwind City, who seeks an outsider daring enough to infiltrate Dalaran and gather botanic specimens.",al={"who gives a green sample","quest giver green sample","shylamiir location"}},{q="What does Shylamiir ask you to retrieve?",a="Shylamiir tasks the player with finding a way into Dalaran to retrieve vegetation growing in the city's central garden, expecting a sample of mana-infused plant life.",al={"what does shylamiir want","a green sample objective","green sample item"}},{q="Where is the requested sample found?",a="The sample comes from the central garden located within Dalaran, a secluded magocracy that strictly guards its borders from outside intrusion.",al={"where is central garden","how to get fel infused blossom","dalaran garden location"}},{q="Why is the blossom fel-infused instead of mana-infused?",a="Instead of healthy mana-infused vegetation, you discover a Fel Infused Blossom. Upon receiving it, Shylamiir realizes the Kirin Tor are dealing with far darker and more turbulent forces than expected.",al={"why fel infused","is dalaran fel corrupted","dalaran plant fel"}},{q="Why is Shylamiir interested in Dalaran's flora?",a="Shylamiir notes that despite being a magocracy, Dalaran maintains a surprising interest in botany, and seeks to study how the Kirin Tor nurture their mana-infused flora.",al={"why does shylamiir want dalaran plants","dalaran botany reason"}},{q="What is Shylamiir's reaction to the delivered blossom?",a="Though startled that the sample is corrupted by fel energy rather than arcane mana, Shylamiir is intrigued by the revelation and praises the adventurer for completing the dangerous errand.",al={"how does shylamiir react","green sample turn in"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The Kirin Tor's secluded magical experiments and Dalaran's botany have long intrigued scholars, though unexpected corruption hints at hidden troubles within the mage enclave.",["class:Rogue"]="Shylamiir specifically enlists someone comfortable trespassing where they are not welcome to slip past the Kirin Tor's defenses.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Gathering exotic flora from Dalaran's central garden reveals that unnatural energies are corrupting ordinary vegetation into fel-infused blossoms."},kw={"a green sample","botany","central garden","dalaran","fel infused blossom","kirin tor","shylamiir","stormwind city"},rel={"subzone:dalaran","zone:stormwind","topic:mage","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=92456},src={"In-game quest text: A Green Sample"},fv=true}
E["quest:92457"] = {t="quest",n="Starving Arcane",z="general",s="Archmage Modera asks you to clear rogue elementals from the City of Dalaran. A breach has unsettled the city's enchantments and defenses, and the Kirin Tor needs room to tend its wards.",h="Archmage Modera asks you to clear rogue elementals from the City of Dalaran.",sec={{t="Arcane Fragments",b="The breach has disturbed factotums and enchantments across Dalaran. Some of those enchantments protect the city's defenses. Fragmented arcane energy has drawn Seeking Remnants, Saturated Remnants, and Mana Echoes. They gather to fill themselves with its free-flowing magic. Archmage Modera asks you to thin their numbers while the Kirin Tor tends the wards and other incidents.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are elementals gathering in Dalaran?",a="Fragmented arcane energy from the breach has drawn rogue elementals seeking to fill themselves with its free-flowing magic.",al={"why elementals here","dalaran elementals","arcane elementals"}},{q="Who asks me to clear the elementals?",a="Archmage Modera asks you to thin the elementals inside the city. She is outside Dalaran.",al={"who gave this","Modera task","archmage Modera"}},{q="What did the breach affect?",a="The breach disturbed factotums and enchantments throughout Dalaran, including some tied to the city's defenses.",al={"breach damage","what broke","dalaran wards"}},{q="Which elementals are involved?",a="Seeking Remnants, Saturated Remnants, and Mana Echoes make up the elemental throng.",al={"which remnants","elemental types","what am I clearing"}}},kw={"arcane energy","archmage modera","breach","dalaran","elementals","kirin tor","mana echoes","saturated remnants","seeking remnants","starving arcane"},rel={"subzone:dalaran"},m={id=92457},src={"In-game quest text: Starving Arcane"},fv=true}
E["quest:92458"] = {t="quest",n="Heart of Disruption",z="general",s="The Image of Archmage Modera needs help containing an arcane disruption in Dalaran. The Violet Citadel’s wards are under strain, and the Kirin Tor need time to bring the crisis under control.",h="The Image of Archmage Modera needs help containing an arcane disruption in Dalaran.",sec={{t="A Way Inside",b="At the top of the Violet Citadel, a thunderous crash sounded from the parlors. The disturbance began there, and rampant arcane power now threatens the wards. The Kirin Tor are occupied inside, so you receive a key and are asked to find a less public way into the city before the disruption worsens.",sp=0},{t="The Mote",b="Modera asks you to collect an Arcane Mote. She does not ask what you saw in the citadel. Her concern is containing the mote and ending the disruption. The Kirin Tor must now take up that work while the wards hold.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="The Image of Archmage Modera asks you to help contain the arcane disruption in Dalaran.",al={"who needs help","modera help","who gave this"}},{q="What caused the disruption?",a="The disturbance began at the top of the Violet Citadel after a thunderous crash sounded from the parlors above.",al={"what happened in the citadel","why are the wards strained","arcane disturbance"}},{q="Why are the wards in danger?",a="Rampant arcane power has overrun the citadel, leaving the Kirin Tor struggling to keep the wards in place.",al={"what's wrong with the wards","why is dalaran overrun","rampant arcane power"}},{q="What does Modera ask me to collect?",a="Modera wants you to gather an Arcane Mote.",al={"what am i collecting","arcane mote","mote task"}},{q="Why do I need a key?",a="Modera gives you a key so you can take a less public approach to entering the city.",al={"what is the key for","how do i get inside","citadel key"}},{q="What happens after I bring back the mote?",a="Modera says the Kirin Tor must contain the mote and end the disruption.",al={"what does modera do with it","mote ending","what happens next"}}},kw={"arcane mote","archmage modera","dalaran","heart of disruption","kirin tor","leylines","violet citadel","wards"},rel={"subzone:dalaran"},m={id=92458},src={"In-game quest text: Heart of Disruption"},fv=true}
E["quest:92466"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="The Call of Earth is an elemental trial undertaken by spiritual initiates to prove their steadfastness. By retrieving requested materials, the petitioner demonstrates their grit and determination to their instructor. Successfully enduring the task confirms their readiness to proceed to the next stage of their training.",h="The Call of Earth is an elemental trial undertaken by spiritual initiates to prove their steadfastness.",sec={{t="A Test of Resolve",b="The Call of Earth is a foundational trial for aspiring practitioners of the elements. Initiates are tasked with fulfilling specific demands to test their patience, resilience, and connection to the stubborn, enduring nature of the earth. Fulfilling the request demonstrates that an adventurer possesses the inner fortitude required to commune with primeval forces.",sp=0},{t="The Next Step",b="Upon returning with the requested offerings, the mentor recognizes the petitioner's unquestionable grit and determination. Having proven steadfast in the face of elemental demands, the initiate earns recognition and is deemed prepared to undertake the next step of their spiritual journey.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Earth?",a="Call of Earth is a rite of passage for spiritual initiates, requiring the petitioner to gather requested items to prove their perseverance and steadfastness before moving on to the next stage of training.",al={"what is call of earth","call of earth quest"}},{q="What does finishing this trial demonstrate?",a="Completing this task demonstrates your grit and unwavering determination, showing your mentor that your focus is firm enough to shoulder greater spiritual responsibilities.",al={"what did i prove","grit and determination"}},{q="What happens after completing the Call of Earth?",a="Having proven your resolve, your instructor deems you ready for the next step along your path of elemental communion.",al={"whats next","next quest"}},{q="Why must an initiate commune with earth?",a="Earth represents stability, steadfast resolve, and unyielding strength. An initiate must prove their own endurance before they can truly commune with the earth spirits.",al={"why earth","earth spirits meaning"}},{q="Who oversees the Call of Earth?",a="A spiritual teacher or shaman mentor guides initiates through the trial to test their discipline and readiness to bond with the spirits.",al={"who gives this quest","call of earth trainer"}},{q="Who is eligible to undertake this trial?",a="The Call of Earth is tied directly to elemental attunement, serving as a formative test for those walking the path of the elements.",al={"can anyone do call of earth","is call of earth for shamans"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="The Call of Earth marks an initiate's journey into communion with the spirits of stone and soil, demanding proof of resolve before granting deeper insight.",["faction:Horde"]="Shamanistic trials like the Call of Earth form a foundational rite for spiritual practitioners among the peoples of the Horde."},kw={"call of earth","determination","earth","elements","shaman","spirits","trial"},rel={"quest:92472"},m={id=92466},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:92467"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="Call of Earth is a sacred trial in which an elemental initiate drinks an Earth Sapta at the Rise of Spirits to pierce the veil between worlds. Guided by an elemental spirit, the seeker learns the endurance and steadfast resolve of the earth as the foundation for rekindling their lost bond with the winds.",h="Call of Earth is a sacred trial in which an elemental initiate drinks an Earth Sapta at the Rise of Spirits...",sec={{t="The Nature of the Sapta",b="To perceive the unseen elemental world, shamans utilize saptas—potent magical brews specifically formulated to align a mortal spirit with a particular elemental realm. When consumed by those possessing sufficient resolve, a sapta opens one's spiritual vision to elemental manifestations that are otherwise hidden from mortal sight. Because of their profound nature, these potions are closely guarded secrets known only to select practitioners.",sp=0},{t="The Rise of Spirits",b="Ritual tradition strictly demands that saptas be consumed solely within sacred sites. For communion with the earth, initiates are directed northeast through a pass to the Rise of Spirits. Drinking the draught upon these consecrated grounds allows the seeker to pierce the veil and behold what the spirits wish to reveal.",sp=0},{t="Strength of the Earth",b="Upon drinking the Earth Sapta, an initiate meets an elemental guide who recognizes them as a child of the four winds seeking to understand why the winds have departed. While the earth spirit cannot explain the enigmatic motives of the wind spirits, it offers teachings on the steadfast resolve and strength of the earth. Even high within an endless sea of clouds, anchoring oneself to the earth is the necessary first step to speaking with the winds once more.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is an Earth Sapta?",a="A sapta is a special magical potion brewed to bind a shaman's spirit to a specific element. When ingested by someone with sufficient willpower, it unveils hidden elemental spirits that ordinary senses cannot perceive.",al={"what is earth sapta","sapta explanation","what does sapta do"}},{q="Where must the Earth Sapta be consumed?",a="The Earth Sapta must be consumed at the Rise of Spirits, a sacred site located up the pass northeast of the quest giver. Saptas must always be consumed in sacred grounds rather than ordinary locations.",al={"where to drink earth sapta","rise of spirits location","where is rise of spirits"}},{q="Why must saptas only be drunk in sacred places?",a="Saptas are potent ritual concoctions designed to link mortal spirits directly to the elements. According to shamanic teachings, they must strictly be drunk within designated sacred places to properly commune with the spirits.",al={"why drink sapta at sacred place","can i drink sapta anywhere"}},{q="Why does the spirit address the seeker as a 'child of the four winds'?",a="The elemental spirit at the Rise of Spirits greets the initiate as a child of the four winds who is searching for answers about why the winds have departed and how their favor might be restored.",al={"who is child of the four winds","why child of four winds"}},{q="Why have the spirits of the winds left?",a="The spirits of the winds have grown distant, but their exact motives and wanderings remain unknown even to the earth spirit at the Rise of Spirits.",al={"why did the winds leave","where did winds go"}},{q="How does communing with the earth help someone reconnect with the winds?",a="The earth represents unyielding strength and resolve. Even amid an endless sea of clouds, all creatures remain tethered to the earth, and mastering its stability is the first step toward regaining communion with the four winds.",al={"why earth first","how does earth help with winds"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Communing with the elements begins with potent elixirs like saptas, grounding the shaman's spirit to commune directly with elemental spirits.",["race:Skyborne"]="Even while adrift high in the endless sea of clouds and estranged from the spirits of the four winds, the earth offers the first steady foundation for understanding."},kw={"call of earth","earth sapta","elements","four winds","rise of spirits","shaman","spirits"},rel={"quest:1462"},m={id=92467},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:92468"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="Call of Earth is a sacred trial of communion where an initiate obtains a piece of Rough Quartz from an earth spirit. Brought to Windshaper Boros in Thendal Grove, this mineral shard becomes the core of the practitioner's first Earth Totem, grounding their bond with the elemental forces.",h="Call of Earth is a sacred trial of communion where an initiate obtains a piece of Rough Quartz from an earth...",sec={{t="The Earth's Gift",b="The quest begins when an earth spirit grants an initiate a shard of Rough Quartz as living proof of their communion. The spirit instructs the bearer to take this token to Windshaper Boros in Thendal Grove, explaining that even the smallest fragment carries enduring strength. The spirits demand patience, resilience, and wisdom before granting their favor and respect.",sp=0},{t="The Four Elements and the Totem",b="Windshaper Boros uses the Rough Quartz as the core to fashion an Earth Totem. He reminds the initiate that the elemental path is multifaceted: although their ultimate desire may be to hear and sing the songs of the wind, true mastery requires understanding all four elemental forces, starting with the unyielding ground beneath their feet.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Windshaper Boros?",a="Windshaper Boros (referred to as Boro) resides in Thendal Grove. As an elder practitioner and crafter, he guides initiates in understanding the elemental spirits and shapes their focal totems from elemental gifts.",al={"who is boro","windshaper boros location","where is thendal grove"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Rough Quartz?",a="The Rough Quartz is an elemental shard granted directly by an earth spirit as proof of your meeting and communion. Windshaper Boros uses it as the foundational core and heart of your crafted Earth Totem.",al={"what is rough quartz","shard of earth","quartz use"}},{q="Why is an Earth Totem necessary?",a="An Earth Totem acts as a personal focal implement required to commune with the spirits of the earth and channel earth-based spells. Beyond signifying status as an initiate, it anchors the practitioner to the world's elemental foundations.",al={"what does earth totem do","totem purpose","why need totem"}},{q="Why must one commune with earth before speaking with the winds?",a="While practitioners may strive to hear and sing the songs of the winds, the winds represent only one facet of the world's elemental balance. One must first study and master all four elements, beginning with the stability, patience, and strength of the earth.",al={"why start with earth","seeking the winds","all four elements"}},{q="Where is Thendal Grove located?",a="Thendal Grove is the sanctuary where Windshaper Boros tends to elemental matters and instructs novices embarking upon their shamanic journey.",al={"where is thendal grove","thendal grove zone"}},{q="What wisdom does the earth spirit impart?",a="The earth spirit teaches that size does not matter, for even the smallest stone can endure long after greater things crumble. It demands patience, inner strength, and the pursuit of wisdom in exchange for mutual respect and harmony.",al={"what elements ask","earth philosophy"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Obtaining your first totem marks the beginning of communion with the elemental forces. It serves as an indispensable physical focus to channel earth-aligned spells and rites.",["race:Skyborne"]="For the Skyborne seeking to reconnect with the winds, mastering the earth is an essential first step. The path to hearing the wind begins by grounding oneself in the solid foundations of the earth."},kw={"call of earth","earth totem","elements","rough quartz","shaman","thendal grove","windshaper boro","windshaper boros"},rel={"item:rough-quartz"},m={id=92468},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:92479"] = {t="quest",n="A Scribbled Letter",z="general",s="A Scribbled Letter is a summons delivered to adventurers at Northshire Abbey following their early clashes with nearby kobolds. Sent under the seal of Tordrin Stemblade, it directs promising recruits to seek out his training and guidance. The letter marks a crucial step in preparing defenders to face the escalating perils threatening the kingdom of Stormwind.",h="A Scribbled Letter is a summons delivered to adventurers at Northshire Abbey following their early clashes...",sec={{t="A Summons in Northshire",b="Upon returning from skirmishes with the kobold camps infesting the valley, adventurers at Northshire Abbey receive a hasty missive. Marked with the seal of Tordrin Stemblade, the note requests their prompt attention before attending to other business. The letter serves as an urgent summons to connect budding champions with formal mentorship within the abbey's protective grounds.",sp=0},{t="Guidance in Perilous Times",b="When presented with the letter, Tordrin Stemblade remarks on how times have changed, observing how unfamiliar folk have taken up weapons in defense of the realm. Acknowledging that the surrounding wilderness grows more hazardous by the day, he invites travelers to seek out his counsel day or night as they master their skills and assist the beleaguered people of Stormwind.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is A Scribbled Letter?",a="A Scribbled Letter is a missive delivered to adventurers in Northshire Abbey upon returning from the kobold camps. Bearing the seal of Tordrin Stemblade, it directs the recipient to seek him out for mentorship and training in the valley.",al={"what is the scribbled letter","scribbled letter quest","letter from tordrin"}},{q="Who sent the letter?",a="The letter was penned and sealed by Tordrin Stemblade, a local trainer stationed at Northshire Abbey who offers instruction to fledgling adventurers learning their craft.",al={"who sent the letter","who wrote the letter","author scribbled letter"}},{q="Where can Tordrin Stemblade be found?",a="Tordrin Stemblade is located at Northshire Abbey in the Northshire Valley. He remains stationed there day and night, ready to provide instruction and advice to travelers.",al={"where is tordrin stemblade","tordrin location","find tordrin stemblade"}},{q="Why did Tordrin Stemblade send the letter?",a="After an adventurer proves their capability against the local kobolds, Tordrin sends word to ensure they receive proper training. He aims to prepare them for the escalating dangers threatening the residents of Northshire and the wider realm of Stormwind.",al={"why did tordrin send a letter","tordrin stemblade purpose","why summons"}},{q="What threats are currently affecting Northshire?",a="Northshire Valley faces incursions from aggressive kobold camps and dangerous man-eating beasts that roam the surrounding woods, prompting local mentors to muster and drill capable fighters.",al={"what threats are in northshire","dangers in northshire abbey","kobolds northshire"}},{q="What does Tordrin Stemblade offer after receiving the letter?",a="Upon meeting with him, Tordrin offers continuous training and guidance, urging adventurers to learn the lay of the land and assist the local folk with the myriad troubles facing the kingdom.",al={"what does tordrin offer","tordrin training","what happens after meeting tordrin"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Tordrin Stemblade mentors hunters in Northshire Abbey, noting how times have changed as new races take up the bow to defend Stormwind from beasts and vermin.",["faction:Alliance"]="Training skilled defenders in Northshire Abbey helps secure Elwynn Forest against encroaching kobolds and other growing threats to the kingdom of Stormwind."},kw={"a scribbled letter","hunter trainer","kobold camps","northshire abbey","northshire valley","scribbled letter","tordrin stemblade"},rel={"subzone:northshire-abbey","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:kobold"},m={id=92479},src={"In-game quest text: A Scribbled Letter"},fv=true}
E["quest:92489"] = {t="quest",n="Power Overwhelming",z="general",s="Power Overwhelming is a quest issued by High Sorcerer Andromath in Stormwind City to assist the secluded Kirin Tor. Players are dispatched to the ruined city of Dalaran to quell a dangerous outbreak of wayward arcane energy manifested as a Mana Elemental.",h="Power Overwhelming is a quest issued by High Sorcerer Andromath in Stormwind City to assist the secluded...",sec={{t="A City in Seclusion",b="Following the devastating destruction of Dalaran at the hands of the arch-demon Archimonde during the Third War, the Kirin Tor retreated inward. Once welcoming scholars and sorcerers from across the world, the surviving magi have grown secretive, reclusive, and deeply distrustful of outsiders as they work tirelessly behind closed borders to rebuild their ruined city.",sp=0},{t="Wayward Arcane Forces",b="Recent reconstruction efforts to restore Dalaran to its former glory have met severe complications. Dangerous leaks of volatile arcane energies have surged out of control, coalescing into beings of pure mana. Fearing that Dalaran might fall to internal turmoil or abandon its historic ties to the Alliance, High Sorcerer Andromath dispatched champions from Stormwind to quell the Mana Elemental and remind the Kirin Tor of their enduring allies.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Power Overwhelming?",a="Power Overwhelming is an assignment from High Sorcerer Andromath in Stormwind City to assist the rebuilding Kirin Tor in Dalaran by slaying an unruly Mana Elemental.",al={"what is power overwhelming","power overwhelming quest","dalaran mana elemental quest"}},{q="Who gives this quest?",a="High Sorcerer Andromath, a prominent wizard of Stormwind City, offers the quest to champions willing to journey to Dalaran and assist the Kirin Tor.",al={"who gives power overwhelming","andromath quest","where to start power overwhelming"}},{q="Why is Dalaran reclusive and distrusting?",a="Dalaran was devastated by the arch-demon Archimonde during the Third War. Although the Kirin Tor are working to reconstruct their home, the catastrophe has left the surviving magi reclusive and wary of outsiders.",al={"why dalaran secluded","what happened to dalaran","why are dalaran mages distrustful"}},{q="What is causing the disturbance in Dalaran?",a="During ongoing reconstruction efforts to restore the city, the Kirin Tor began struggling to contain an outbreak of wayward arcane energies, which coalesced into hostile mana elementals.",al={"dalaran arcane outbreak","why are there mana elementals in dalaran","what is going on in dalaran"}},{q="What is the Mana Elemental?",a="The Mana Elemental is a manifestation of pure, uncontained arcane power. Its appearance indicates that the underlying arcane disturbance threatening Dalaran is far stronger than initial reports suggested.",al={"what is the mana elemental","dalaran elemental lore","creature of pure mana"}},{q="Why is Stormwind sending help to Dalaran?",a="The Kirin Tor have long been vital allies of the Alliance. High Sorcerer Andromath wants to ensure Dalaran does not fall to chaos or slip away from the Alliance during its time of vulnerability.",al={"why stormwind helping dalaran","why does alliance care about dalaran"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Kirin Tor are historic allies of the Alliance, and Stormwind is eager to reinforce those bonds by aiding Dalaran during its vulnerable rebuilding phase.",["class:Mage"]="Mages share a kinship with the Kirin Tor and will recognize the grave threat posed by unstable arcane manifestations run amok in Dalaran."},kw={"arcane energy","archimonde","dalaran","high sorcerer andromath","kirin tor","mana elemental","power overwhelming","stormwind"},rel={"quest:1939","subzone:dalaran","zone:stormwind","topic:alliance","topic:mage","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=92489},src={"In-game quest text: Power Overwhelming"},fv=true}
E["quest:92514"] = {t="quest",n="Welcome to Shen'dar Village",z="general",s="Welcome to Shen'dar Village introduces travelers to the key figures and local dynamics of Shen'dar Village. Upon arrival, newcomers are directed to consult the local Windshaper elder and the village innkeeper before assisting with pressing threats like regional bandits.",h="Welcome to Shen'dar Village introduces travelers to the key figures and local dynamics of Shen'dar Village.",sec={{t="First Steps in Shen'dar",b="Arriving in Shen'dar Village, travelers are encouraged to familiarize themselves with the settlement's key residents before taking on local tasks. Newcomers exhibiting spirit-touched qualities are directed to meet with Elder Illaya Amberwind near the village square, as well as Coriella Calmbreeze at the village inn for lodging and supplies. This greeting ensures visitors understand the settlement's delicate social dynamics before taking up arms.",sp=0},{t="Factions and Frontier Trouble",b="Beneath the quiet exterior of Shen'dar Village lies political friction between the spirit-attuned Windshapers and the High Order. Although ideological disputes between the two factions run hot across the region, residents in Shen'dar keep their disagreements civil. Both groups recognize that petty squabbles must take a backseat to pressing survival matters, not least of which is an encroaching bandit menace threatening the area.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the quest Welcome to Shen'dar Village?",a="Welcome to Shen'dar Village is an introductory quest that acquaints new arrivals with key community figures in Shen'dar Village. Before setting travelers to work handling local issues like bandit troubles, local leadership directs them to meet the resident Windshaper elder and the village innkeeper.",al={"what is shen'dar village quest","welcome to shendar quest overview","what to do in shendar village"}},{q="Who is Illaya Amberwind?",a="Illaya Amberwind is the resident Windshaper elder in Shen'dar Village. She can be found situated near the center of town, offering guidance to those with spirit-touched sensibilities amidst broader political friction.",al={"who is illaya amberwind","illaya amberwind location","windshaper elder shendar"}},{q="Who is Coriella Calmbreeze?",a="Coriella Calmbreeze runs the local inn within Shen'dar Village. She provides travelers, adventurers, and locals with necessary lodging, rest, and provisions.",al={"who is coriella calmbreeze","shendar village innkeeper","where is the inn in shendar village"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="Windshapers are spirit-touched practitioners characterized by a palpable charge of static in the air around them. While tensions simmer between the Windshapers and the High Order, both factions maintain a civil coexistence within Shen'dar Village in light of larger threats.",al={"what are windshapers","spirit touched windshaper lore","high order vs windshapers"}},{q="What is the tension between the High Order and the Windshapers?",a="The High Order is a local power or faction currently experiencing political friction with the Windshapers. Despite their ideological quibbles, representatives and residents in Shen'dar Village keep relations peaceful to deal with more pressing mutual concerns.",al={"what is the high order","conflict in shendar village","high order vs windshapers tension"}},{q="What problems is Shen'dar Village currently facing?",a="Beyond the uneasy philosophical dispute between the Windshapers and the High Order, the settlement faces immediate, practical danger from local bandits operating in the surrounding territory.",al={"shendar village troubles","what threat faces shendar village","bandits in shendar village"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As someone attuned to the spirits and elements, you will quickly notice the subtle static air that marks spirit-touched Windshapers like Illaya Amberwind in Shen'dar Village."},kw={"bandits","coriella calmbreeze","high order","illaya amberwind","shen'dar village","spirit-touched","welcome to shen'dar village","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=92514},src={"In-game quest text: Welcome to Shen'dar Village"},fv=true}
E["quest:92515"] = {t="quest",n="The Problem With Prideclaws",z="general",s="The Problem With Prideclaws addresses the encroaching threat of wild prideclaw predators in the Shen'dar Highlands. As local settlements already grapple with bandits and cultists, culling the encroaching beasts protects vulnerable villagers while yielding useful pelts for the community.",h="The Problem With Prideclaws addresses the encroaching threat of wild prideclaw predators in the Shen'dar...",sec={{t="A Growing Wild Menace",b="The hills surrounding the Shen'dar Highlands are fraught with peril, pressed not only by roving bandits and unhinged cultists but also by an encroaching population of prideclaws. These fierce beasts have begun pressing inward toward the village perimeter, growing bolder as their territory expands and creating an imminent hazard for the local inhabitants.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Borders",b="To avert disaster before pets or children are snatched by the wandering predators, villagers have organized a culling effort. Taking down the prideclaws serves a dual purpose: it drives the predators back from the settlement borders while providing sturdy pelts that craftsmen can fashion into essential goods for the village.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are prideclaws?",a="Prideclaws are predatory beasts inhabiting the hills and highlands of Shen'dar. Known for their fierce temperament and valuable pelts, they pose an escalating threat to nearby settlements when their numbers swell unchecked.",al={"what are prideclaws","prideclaw lore","prideclaws"}},{q="Where are the prideclaws located?",a="Prideclaws roam the hills of the Shen'dar Highlands. Their growing population has begun encroaching on the borders of the local village, bringing them dangerously close to inhabited areas.",al={"where is this quest","where do prideclaws live","prideclaw location"}},{q="Why does the village need the prideclaws culled?",a="With bandits and mad cultists already menacing the region, an overpopulation of prideclaws threatens the village borders. The culling is intended as a preventative measure before children or domestic animals fall victim to the encroaching predators.",al={"why kill prideclaws","why cull prideclaws","village danger"}},{q="What will be done with the prideclaw pelts?",a="The gathered pelts are repurposed by the village crafters into practical goods and supplies, turning the necessary culling into useful resources for the settlement.",al={"what happens to pelts","prideclaw pelt use","pelts purpose"}},{q="What other threats plague the surrounding hills?",a="Beyond the wild prideclaws prowling the hills, the local settlement contends with roaming bandits and unstable cultists, stretching local safety and defenses thin.",al={"other threats shendar","what else threatens the village","local threats"}},{q="How do the locals view the prideclaws?",a="The villager overseeing the task finds prideclaws somewhat cute from a safe vantage behind armed guards, but recognizes the harsh necessity of maintaining village safety before tragedy strikes.",al={"who gives the problem with prideclaws","quest giver thoughts"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Tracking and culling predatory beasts encroaching on settlements is bread-and-butter work for seasoned hunters, though prideclaws in these numbers demand respect."},kw={"cull","prideclaw pelts","prideclaws","shen'dar","shen'dar highlands","the problem with prideclaws","village borders"},m={id=92515},src={"In-game quest text: The Problem With Prideclaws"},fv=true}
E["quest:92516"] = {t="quest",n="Hippogryph Harassment",z="general",s="Hippogryph Harassment is a local task concerning an aggressive flock of wild hippogryphs nesting southeast of Shen'dar Village. Travelers passing along the nearby road have come under frequent attack from the territorial beasts inhabiting the Shen'dar Highlands. To secure the passage, adventurers are called upon to cull the flock's youths, protectors, and their commanding matriarch.",h="Hippogryph Harassment is a local task concerning an aggressive flock of wild hippogryphs nesting southeast of...",sec={{t="Peril on the Road",b="Travelers navigating the roads near Shen'dar Village face a rising danger from the skies. A wild flock of hippogryphs has recently claimed the rugged territory southeast of the settlement, carving out active nesting grounds in the Shen'dar Highlands. While hippogryphs are known across Kalimdor as noble beasts occasionally bonded to elven riders, untamed flocks guard their territory with fierce ferocity. Any wagon or lone traveler traversing the paths below has become a target for aerial ambushes, disrupting vital passage and leaving the village vulnerable to isolation if the creatures are left unchecked.",sp=0},{t="Culling the Flock",b="To eliminate the immediate threat, the nesting grounds in the Shen'dar Highlands must be directly confronted. Restoring peace to the high roads requires curbing the flock's rapid expansion by taking down their younger members before they reach maturity, as well as breaking through the vigilant hippogryph protectors guarding the perimeter. The true disruption, however, stems from the flock's matriarch, whose leadership directs the attacks on passersby. Only by striking at the nest and felling the matriarch can the roads be made safe once more for travelers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the hippogryphs attacking travelers near Shen'dar Village?",a="The hippogryphs have recently established active nesting grounds southeast of Shen'dar Village in the Shen'dar Highlands. Because the flock settled directly adjacent to the main travel route, the territorial creatures aggressively harass anyone venturing nearby to defend their newly claimed brood and hunting grounds.",al={"why hippogryphs attacking","why are hippogryphs hostile","hippogryph harassment cause"}},{q="Where is the hippogryph flock located?",a="The flock can be found nesting southeast of Shen'dar Village within the Shen'dar Highlands. Their roosts and territorial hunting grounds overlook the road passing through the region, where youths, protectors, and the flock's matriarch gather among the cliffs and ridges.",al={"where are the hippogryphs","hippogryph location","shen'dar highlands location"}},{q="Which hippogryphs must be slain to complete the task?",a="To quell the flock's aggressive behavior and restore safe travel along the roadway, adventurers must thin out eight hippogryph youths, six sturdy hippogryph protectors, and the formidable hippogryph matriarch who directs the roost within the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"which hippogryphs do i kill","hippogryph harassment targets","hippogryph objectives"}},{q="Who is the leader of the hippogryph flock?",a="The flock is led by a powerful Hippogryph Matriarch residing in the Shen'dar Highlands. She directs the roost and oversees the defense of their nesting grounds southeast of Shen'dar Village, making her demise essential to breaking the flock's hold on the roadway.",al={"who leads the hippogryphs","hippogryph matriarch","boss hippogryph"}},{q="Why must the hippogryph youths be culled?",a="The hippogryph youths represent the future growth and continued expansion of the newly settled flock. By thinning out their numbers alongside the mature protectors, adventurers ensure that the roost cannot rapidly replenish its strength or establish a permanent reign of terror over the local highways.",al={"why kill hippogryph youths","why target young hippogryphs","culling hippogryph young"}},{q="Are these hippogryphs tame or allied with anyone?",a="No, these are completely wild, untamed creatures that have established a roost in the Shen'dar Highlands on their own. Unlike the disciplined mounts carefully raised by the night elves, this feral flock treats any passing humanoid as an intruder or prey encroaching upon their territory.",al={"are the hippogryphs tamed","are these night elf hippogryphs","friendly hippogryphs"}},{q="What danger do these hippogryphs pose to the local settlement?",a="The flock has established nests immediately overlooking the road leading southeast from Shen'dar Village. By swooping down and terrorizing supply carts, messengers, and wandering travelers, the aggressive beasts threaten to cut off safe overland travel and isolate the village from the surrounding lands.",al={"what threat do hippogryphs pose","danger to shendar village","why are hippogryphs a problem"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Night elves hold a deep spiritual reverence for hippogryphs as sacred companions of Elune, making the culling of even an aggressive wild flock a somber task when wilderness clashes with traveler safety.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters understand the predatory instincts of aerial beasts and the hierarchy of a wild roost, recognizing that eliminating the matriarch is the most effective way to shatter the flock's aggression.",["class:Druid"]="Druids strive to maintain harmony with the wild, viewing this culling not as malice but as an unfortunate necessity to restore balance after the beasts began preying upon travelers."},kw={"hippogryph","hippogryph harassment","hippogryph matriarch","hippogryph protector","hippogryph youth","kalimdor","matriarch","shen'dar highlands","shen'dar village"},m={id=92516},src={"In-game quest text: Hippogryph Harassment"},fv=true}
E["quest:92517"] = {t="quest",n="The Criminal Element",z="general",s="The Criminal Element involves striking against the Highlands Bandits and their leader, \"Badwind\" Bennic, who operate out of a concealed cave behind a waterfall in the Shen'dar Highlands. With local forces overwhelmed by the recent arrival of a menacing cult, outside help is required to eliminate Bennic and disrupt the outlaw gang.",h="The Criminal Element involves striking against the Highlands Bandits and their leader, \"Badwind\" Bennic, who...",sec={{t="Outlaws Behind the Falls",b="In the Shen'dar Highlands, a den of outlaws known as the Highlands Bandits has entrenched itself inside a hidden cave concealed behind a northern waterfall. Led by the notorious \"Badwind\" Bennic, the cutthroats were already a persistent nuisance to locals before an enigmatic cult arrived in the highlands, after which their lawlessness escalated into an outright terror. With local defenders stretched thin and unable to spare forces, adventurers are tasked with infiltrating the waterfall redoubt, breaking the gang's ranks, and eliminating Bennic.",sp=0},{t="A Shift in the Shadows",b="Although eliminating \"Badwind\" Bennic successfully shatters the bandit enclave and brings temporary relief, it introduces a fresh strategic complication. Local authorities worry that without their commander, the surviving outlaws will seek shelter with the nearby cult. Absorbing desperate, seasoned highwaymen into the cult's ranks threatens to further swell the numbers of an already alarming regional threat.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is \"Badwind\" Bennic?",a="\"Badwind\" Bennic is the leader of the Highlands Bandits operating out of a hidden cave behind a waterfall in the Shen'dar Highlands. His gang has long plagued the area, turning into an even greater terror amid recent regional turmoil.",al={"who is badwind bennic","bennic","badwind"}},{q="Where are the Highlands Bandits hiding?",a="The bandits make their lair in a secluded cave tucked behind a waterfall located just to the north in the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"where are the bandits","bandit cave location","waterfall cave"}},{q="What is the cult mentioned in the area?",a="The cult is a newer, alarming faction whose followers gather at Falaath Village to the south, their numbers growing. While the bandits operated long before the cult's arrival, its presence has exacerbated local danger. There are lingering concerns that surviving bandits may seek refuge and bolster the cult's ranks once their leadership is broken.",al={"what cult","cult connection","who is the cult"}},{q="Why can't local forces handle the bandits themselves?",a="Local forces lack the manpower to clear the bandits themselves while dealing with the emerging cult. Eliminating Bennic and dispersing his gang removes an active threat preying on the region.",al={"why kill the bandits","bandit problem"}},{q="What happens to the bandits after Bennic is defeated?",a="Though Bennic falls and his hideout is scattered, concerns remain that surviving outlaws will turn to the nearby cult for protection, swelling the cultists' already alarming numbers.",al={"what happens to surviving bandits","did all bandits die"}},{q="Where does this conflict take place?",a="The Shen'dar Highlands is the rugged region where the Highlands Bandits roam and maintain their hidden waterfall redoubt.",al={"where is shendar highlands","shen'dar highlands"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Operating out of a hidden waterfall cave is classic bandit tradecraft, but \"Badwind\" Bennic and his crew lack the finesse or discretion to avoid drawing local retribution."},kw={"badwind bennic","bennic","cult","highlands bandits","shen'dar highlands","the criminal element","waterfall cave"},m={id=92517},src={"In-game quest text: The Criminal Element"},fv=true}
E["quest:92519"] = {t="quest",n="Ward Restocking",z="general",s="Ward Restocking is a task in the Arathi Highlands where travelers assist Doctor Gregory Victor in Hammerfall. With the outpost's infirmary running short on vital elixirs, couriers are dispatched to retrieve neglected supplies from Apothecary Jorell's lab at Go'Shek Farm.",h="Ward Restocking is a task in the Arathi Highlands where travelers assist Doctor Gregory Victor in Hammerfall.",sec={{t="A Shortage in the Ward",b="At the Horde settlement of Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands, Doctor Gregory Victor tends to the wounded and ailing in the outpost's medical ward. Keeping the infirmary functional demands a regular supply of remedies, antidotes, and elixirs, which are usually provided by the Forsaken apothecary stationed nearby. When these deliveries stall, Victor must enlist capable travelers to step in and keep the clinic stocked.",sp=0},{t="The Recluse of Go'Shek Farm",b="Apothecary Jorell conducts his alchemical experiments out of a cramped, poorly-ventilated shack at Go'Shek Farm, south and west of Hammerfall. According to Doctor Victor, Jorell's absence is nothing out of the ordinary; the eccentric Forsaken chemist is notorious for either ignoring his official duties or accidentally succumbing to his own noxious concoctions. Couriers sent to retrieve the packaged tonics are expected to find the medicine waiting, regardless of whether Jorell is present.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Ward Restocking?",a="Doctor Gregory Victor, the trauma surgeon overseeing the trauma ward and infirmary in Hammerfall, gives this task to dependable travelers. He needs reliable couriers to retrieve medical supplies when regular channels fail.",al={"who gives ward restocking","quest giver ward restocking","doctor gregory victor quest"}},{q="Where is Apothecary Jorell located?",a="Apothecary Jorell operates out of a small shack at Go'Shek Farm, located south and slightly west of Hammerfall along the road in the Arathi Highlands.",al={"where is apothecary jorell","jorell lab location","where to find tonics"}},{q="Why does Doctor Gregory Victor send you to Go'Shek Farm?",a="Doctor Gregory Victor sends travelers to Go'Shek Farm because Apothecary Jorell failed to deliver an urgently needed crate of antidotes and elixirs to the Hammerfall infirmary.",al={"why go to goshek farm","what is at goshek farm"}},{q="What supplies are needed for the ward?",a="You must collect a case of Packaged Tonics containing antidotes and elixirs prepared by Apothecary Jorell to replenish the dwindling stocks of the Hammerfall medical ward.",al={"what are packaged tonics","what items do you pick up"}},{q="Why hasn't Apothecary Jorell delivered the supplies himself?",a="Doctor Gregory Victor suspects that Jorell either poisoned himself again by concocting brews in his poorly-ventilated workshop shack or simply neglected his obligations to the ward.",al={"what happened to jorell","is jorell dead","why didn't jorell deliver"}},{q="Where must the supplies be delivered?",a="Once gathered from Go'Shek Farm, the case of tonics must be brought back to Doctor Gregory Victor at the medical ward in Hammerfall.",al={"where do tonics go","who to turn in ward restocking"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The outpost of Hammerfall relies on steady shipments of tonics and medicine to keep its soldiers, defenders, and travelers standing against threats in the Arathi Highlands.",["race:Undead"]="Apothecary Jorell represents the Royal Apothecary Society's far-flung presence in Arathi, though his erratic behavior and toxic experiments at Go'Shek Farm frustrate even his allies."},kw={"apothecary jorell","arathi highlands","doctor gregory victor","go'shek farm","hammerfall","packaged tonics","ward restocking"},rel={"subzone:hammerfall","subzone:go-shek-farm","npc:doctor-gregory-victor"},m={id=92519},src={"In-game quest text: Ward Restocking"},fv=true}
E["quest:92528"] = {t="quest",n="Among the Faithful",z="general",s="Among the Faithful follows an undercover investigation into the Faithful of Al'Akir stationed at Falaath Village in the Shen'dar Highlands. By blending in as a prospective recruit, an adventurer learns that the cult's supreme leadership is secretly gathering to launch an imminent offensive against local settlements and outposts.",h="Among the Faithful follows an undercover investigation into the Faithful of Al'Akir stationed at Falaath...",sec={{t="Infiltrating Falaath Village",b="Posing as an eager recruit among the Faithful of Al'Akir, an infiltrator enters Falaath Village in the Shen'dar Highlands. The cultists openly celebrate their growing ranks, boasting that their collective devotion lends shape and fury to the storm itself. New arrivals are typically sent directly to Skypriest Aanders for scrutiny, but an unexpected meeting at the local inn disrupts normal protocol.",sp=0},{t="The Secret Guest",b="Aanders is occupied on the north side of town hosting a distinguished guest, leaving the new recruit free to wander the grounds unmonitored. By keeping a low profile and eavesdropping on the settlement's leadership, the scout uncovers the true scope of the cult's immediate ambition: a coordinated military push across the Shen'dar Highlands.",sp=1},{t="Lorthuna's Scheme",b="The mysterious guest at the inn is revealed to be Lorthuna herself, the supreme commander of the entire cult. Her direct presence signals an aggressive strike aimed not only at Shen'dar Village, but also at the western watchtower. While the village is a clear population center, targeting the remote lookout tower baffles local scouts, suggesting the cult harbors hidden motives beneath their storm-driven offensive.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who are the Faithful of Al'Akir?",a="The Faithful of Al'Akir are a cult dedicated to the Windlord, Al'Akir. Based around Falaath Village in the Shen'dar Highlands, their members harness storm magic and work under the ultimate leadership of a figure named Lorthuna.",al={"who are the faithful of alakir","what cult is this","alakir cult"}},{q="Who is Skypriest Aanders?",a="Skypriest Aanders is a ranking officer within the cult at Falaath Village who handles the assessment and induction of new recruits. During the infiltration, he was occupied receiving an important guest at the village inn.",al={"who is skypriest aanders","aanders","cult assessment guy"}},{q="Who is Lorthuna?",a="Lorthuna is the supreme leader of the entire cult of Al'Akir. Her direct, covert presence at the Falaath Village inn reveals that the cult is orchestrating an imminent, high-level offensive in the highlands.",al={"who is lorthuna","cult leader lorthuna","guest at the inn"}},{q="Why is the cult targeting the western watchtower?",a="The cult is plotting a simultaneous strike against both Shen'dar Village and the western watchtower. While striking the village makes strategic sense, targeting the minor lookout post at the watchtower remains a puzzling mystery to highland defenders.",al={"why attack the watchtower","western watchtower cult","cult targets"}},{q="Where does this take place?",a="Falaath Village is a settlement located in the Shen'dar Highlands currently functioning as a major base and staging ground for the Faithful of Al'Akir and their new recruits.",al={"where is falaath village","falaath village location"}},{q="What are the cult's intentions in the Shen'dar Highlands?",a="The cult seeks to expand its influence and unleash storm magic across the Shen'dar Highlands. Under Lorthuna's leadership, their immediate objective is an assault aimed at both Shen'dar Village and the western watchtower.",al={"what are the cults goals in shendar highlands","cult plans shendar"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="While holy and shadow priests follow familiar faiths, the cultists in Falaath Village are led by Skypriests dedicated to Al'Akir the Windlord, channeling the primal strength of the storm.",["class:Shaman"]="The worship of Al'Akir and the cult's mastery over storm energies directly intersects with the elemental forces revered and negotiated with by shamans."},kw={"al'akir","among the faithful","falaath village","lorthuna","shen'dar highlands","skypriest aanders","western watchtower","windlord"},rel={"quest:92529"},m={id=92528},src={"In-game quest text: Among the Faithful"},fv=true}
E["quest:92529"] = {t="quest",n="Falaath Village",z="general",s="Falaath Village is a gathering place for penitent shen'dorei seeking to win back the favor of the Windlord, Al'Akir. Marked recruits journey from Shen'dar Village to report to Missionary Jasaan and join their fellow faithful.",h="Falaath Village is a gathering place for penitent shen'dorei seeking to win back the favor of the Windlord...",sec={{t="Contrition for the Windlord",b="In Shen'dar Village, Sania Silverstream laments that her people grew prideful, taking the bounty of the air for granted until the spirits of the wind abandoned them into stagnation. To remedy this fall from grace, penitent recruits are marked and sent to Falaath Village, seeking fellowship with fellow shen'dorei who believe that only acts of contrition can restore the wind's favor.",sp=0},{t="The Mission at Falaath",b="Upon reaching the outskirts of Falaath Village bearing the Mark of Akir, initiates are greeted by Missionary Jasaan. Viewing the arrival of fresh acolytes as a sign of blessing from the four winds, Jasaan welcomes all who arrive seeking to bask in the elemental grace of Al'Akir.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Falaath Village?",a="Falaath Village is an introductory quest where recruits marked by Sania Silverstream travel from Shen'dar Village to report to Missionary Jasaan, seeking fellowship with faithful worshippers of the Windlord.",al={"what is falaath village quest","falaath village overview","falaath village quest"}},{q="Who is Missionary Jasaan?",a="Missionary Jasaan is an acolyte stationed near the entrance to Falaath Village who welcomes new recruits sent by sister Sania Silverstream to bask in the blessings of Al'Akir.",al={"who is missionary jasaan","jasaan lore","falaath missionary"}},{q="Who is Sania Silverstream?",a="Sania Silverstream is a member of the faithful in Shen'dar Village who directs prospective followers to Falaath Village and bestows the Mark of Akir upon new recruits.",al={"who is sania silverstream","sania lore","sister sania"}},{q="Why are the shen'dorei worshipping Al'Akir?",a="The followers believe their people grew complacent and prideful, taking the bounty of the winds for granted until the spirits abandoned them. They worship the Windlord Al'Akir through acts of contrition to regain the wind's blessing.",al={"why worship alakir","windlord alakir worship","who is the windlord"}},{q="What is the Mark of Akir?",a="The Mark of Akir is a sign bestowed by Sania Silverstream in Shen'dar Village to identify an initiate as a recruit to the faithful before they enter Falaath Village.",al={"what is mark of akir","lost mark of akir","recruits mark"}},{q="Who are the shen'dorei mentioned in the quest?",a="The shen'dorei referenced by Sania are elves contending with spiritual stagnation after the spirits of the wind withdrew their favor, prompting some to seek atonement through elemental devotion.",al={"who are the shendorei","shen dorei lore"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="For the shen'dorei seeking to restore their bond with the winds, joining the faithful at Falaath Village represents an act of collective contrition to regain Al'Akir's lost favor.",["class:Shaman"]="While shaman seek harmony with elemental spirits, the cultists devoted to the Windlord approach Al'Akir through penitence and religious devotion to reclaim the abandoned winds."},kw={"al'akir","falaath village","mark of akir","missionary jasaan","sania silverstream","shen'dar village","shen'dorei","windlord"},m={id=92529},src={"In-game quest text: Falaath Village"},fv=true}
E["quest:92550"] = {t="quest",n="Havoc in the Highlands",z="general",s="Havoc in the Highlands is a preemptive strike undertaken on behalf of Shen'dar Village to disrupt an impending assault by Al'Aketh zealots. By striking their vanguard at the ruins of Falaath Village and eliminating Commander Cyclas, defenders turn the tables on the cultists before they can overrun the highlands.",h="Havoc in the Highlands is a preemptive strike undertaken on behalf of Shen'dar Village to disrupt an...",sec={{t="A Preemptive Strike",b="Facing an imminent invasion from zealous cultists, the leadership of Shen'dar Village decides that a swift preemptive offensive offers the best chance of survival. Rather than awaiting an attack behind fragile defenses, an adventurer is dispatched into the ruins of Falaath Village to catch the hostile Al'Aketh forces off guard before their assault can launch.",sp=0},{t="Assault on Falaath Village",b="The staging grounds at Falaath Village crawl with dangerous zealots wielding chaotic elemental power. Striking at the gathering forces requires dispatching Al'Aketh Stormcallers, shattering conjured Living Lightning, and tracking down Commander Cyclas himself to behead the expedition's leadership.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Highlands",b="With Commander Cyclas slain and his vanguard devastated, Shen'dar Village secures a vital reprieve. However, amidst the turmoil of arming the citizens, an individual named Sania Silverstream slips out of town unnoticed, fleeing the village just as the local defense consolidates.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Havoc in the Highlands?",a="Havoc in the Highlands is a preemptive strike launched by the defenders of Shen'dar Village against Al'Aketh cultists gathering in the ruins of Falaath Village before they can mount an assault.",al={"havoc in the highlands quest","what is havoc in the highlands","highlands quest"}},{q="Who is threatening Shen'dar Village?",a="The settlement faces danger from the Al'Aketh, a zealous cult of elemental storm worshippers fielding Stormcallers, bound Living Lightning, and their leader, Commander Cyclas.",al={"who are the enemies","what cult is this","who attacks shendar"}},{q="Where are the cultists massing?",a="The strike takes place in the ruins of Falaath Village, where Commander Cyclas and his Al'Aketh followers have set up their staging ground.",al={"where is commander cyclas","falaath village ruins","where to go"}},{q="Who is Commander Cyclas?",a="Commander Cyclas is the military commander leading the Al'Aketh forces assembled in the ruins of Falaath Village to march upon Shen'dar.",al={"who is commander cyclas","cyclas lore","cyclas cult"}},{q="Why did Sania Silverstream flee Shen'dar Village?",a="Sania Silverstream snuck out of Shen'dar Village right before the village's defenders rallied, likely fleeing once she realized the local populace was taking up arms and organizing a defense.",al={"sania silverstream fleeing","why did sania leave","who is sania silverstream"}},{q="Does the strike successfully secure Shen'dar Village?",a="By breaking the cultists' forward ranks and taking Commander Cyclas's head, Shen'dar Village repels the immediate threat and proves to any returning zealots that the highlands will not fall easily.",al={"did shendar survive","aftermath of the attack","is shendar safe"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="The Al'Aketh cultists command the elements with their Stormcallers and summoned Living Lightning, twisting wind and storm into tools of fanatical aggression."},kw={"al'aketh","commander cyclas","falaath village","havoc in the highlands","living lightning","sania silverstream","shen'dar village","stormcallers"},rel={"quest:92529"},m={id=92550},src={"In-game quest text: Havoc in the Highlands"},fv=true}
E["quest:92551"] = {t="quest",n="Stolen Supplies",z="general",s="Stolen Supplies tasks adventurers with recovering plundered highland goods from the Al'Aketh in Falaath Village. Caravan ambushes have cut off vital shipments of food and windstones between the lowlands and the Shen'dar Highlands, leaving local skyborne communities on the brink of starvation.",h="Stolen Supplies tasks adventurers with recovering plundered highland goods from the Al'Aketh in Falaath...",sec={{t="Raids on the Highland Lifeline",b="For months, caravans traveling between the lowlands and the Shen'dar Highlands have faced persistent attacks along their trade routes. These shipments transport vital necessities, including food and windstones, to sustain the highland settlements. Without steady deliveries, local townsfolk have been pushed to the brink of starvation, struggling to squeak by as supplies dwindle across the region.",sp=0},{t="The Falaath Cache",b="While the Al'Aketh publicly point fingers at common bandits for the caravan ambushes, the missing cargo tells a different story. The stolen packs of Shen'dar supplies consistently make their way into Falaath Village, where the Al'Aketh hoard the caches. Recovering these goods allows local authorities and the constable to redistribute much-needed sustenance directly to the most vulnerable skyborne.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What supplies were stolen from the caravans?",a="Caravans traveling between the highlands and the lowlands were carrying vital goods, specifically food supplies and windstones. These regular deliveries keep the local townsfolk fed and supplied.",al={"what was stolen","supplies taken","what goods were stolen"}},{q="Who is responsible for raiding the caravans?",a="The Al'Aketh have been raiding the supply caravans for months. Although they attempt to blame ordinary bandits for the missing shipments, the stolen caches are known to end up directly in their territory at Falaath Village.",al={"who stole the supplies","who raided the caravans","caravan attackers"}},{q="Where are the stolen supply caches being held?",a="The stolen supply packs are stored away in Falaath Village, an Al'Aketh settlement situated within the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"where are the supplies","where to find supplies","stolen caches location"}},{q="Why are these supply caravans being targeted?",a="The supplies include essential sustenance like food as well as windstones moving to and from the lowlands. Raiding these caravans deprives the local skyborne populace while stocking Falaath Village with critical highland resources.",al={"why steal windstones and food","why caravan raids"}},{q="What happens to the supplies once they are recovered?",a="Once recovered from Falaath Village, the supplies are coordinated with the local constable to be redistributed directly to the townsfolk who are in the most desperate need of food and relief.",al={"who gets the supplies back","supplies redistribution","what happens after recovery"}},{q="Why do the Al'Aketh blame bandits for the raids?",a="The Al'Aketh blame roving bandits to deflect responsibility for the missing trade goods, even though the stolen cargo consistently ends up stockpiled right inside their settlement at Falaath Village.",al={"why blame bandits","al aketh bandits excuse"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="The raids strike directly at the lifeline connecting the highlands to the lowlands, leaving skyborne townsfolk hungry and desperate without their regular deliveries of food and windstones."},kw={"al'aketh","caravans","constable","falaath village","shen'dar highlands","skyborne","stolen supplies","windstones"},rel={"quest:92529","topic:skyborne"},m={id=92551},src={"In-game quest text: Stolen Supplies"},fv=true}
E["quest:92553"] = {t="quest",n="Restocking the Larders",z="general",s="Restocking the Larders is a provisioning quest in the Shen'dar Highlands of Zephras. With escalating wilderness threats keeping villagers from foraging, an adventurer is enlisted to gather eggs and strider meat to sustain the settlement.",h="Restocking the Larders is a provisioning quest in the Shen'dar Highlands of Zephras.",sec={{t="Dangers in the Highlands",b="As unrest and rising dangers spread across the Shen'dar Highlands, normal daily life on Zephras has grown increasingly fraught. Everyday villagers can no longer safely venture into the wilderness to forage or hunt, leaving communal food stores severely diminished. To keep the settlement fed, local cooks must turn to traveling adventurers with the strength and skill to brave the wild trails.",sp=0},{t="Sustenance and Comfort",b="The task calls for tracking down native wildlife across the highlands to secure strider meat and fresh small eggs. For those who supply the provisions, the cook is eager to share culinary knowledge, teaching recipes and culinary techniques refined around local game, including eggs fried in rich ursera tallow alongside a quiet pipe of dried peacebloom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What supplies are needed for Restocking the Larders?",a="A local cook needs 3 Small Eggs and 8 cuts of Strider Meat to help feed the village while danger keeps ordinary foragers indoors.",al={"restocking larders objective","what to collect restocking larders"}},{q="Where can the ingredients be found?",a="The strider meat and small eggs come from wild creatures roaming throughout the Shen'dar Highlands on the island of Zephras.",al={"where get strider meat zephras","egg locations shendar"}},{q="Why is the village running low on food?",a="Escalating dangers across the region have made it too perilous for common villagers to venture out hunting or foraging, leaving food stores depleted.",al={"why need food zephras","why village hungry shendar"}},{q="What reward does the cook offer for the food?",a="In exchange for bringing in fresh provisions, the quest giver offers cooking tips, culinary tricks, and training in local recipes.",al={"what does quest giver offer","cooking training quest"}},{q="What are the Shen'dar Highlands and Zephras?",a="The Shen'dar Highlands are situated on the land of Zephras, where locals contend with mounting threats while trying to sustain their everyday lives.",al={"what is zephras","where is shendar highlands"}},{q="How does the cook prepare the eggs?",a="The cook swears by frying up freshly gathered small eggs in ursera tallow, calling the combination an unmatched comfort on Zephras.",al={"tallow cooking secret","how are eggs cooked zephras"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Gathering local ingredients like strider meat and small eggs offers practical experience and culinary recipes adapted to Zephras wildlife."},kw={"cooking","restocking the larders","shendar highlands","small eggs","strider meat","ursera tallow","zephras"},m={id=92553},src={"In-game quest text: Restocking the Larders"},fv=true}
E["quest:92579"] = {t="quest",n="To Valanaar",z="general",s="\"To Valanaar\" is an island quest in which adventurers carry an urgent military report from Aonda in Shen'dar to Peacekeeper leader Valennia Stormfist. The message alerts the settlement at the southeastern edge of the island that an aggressive cult is expanding rapidly and that direct conflict can no longer be avoided.",h="\"To Valanaar\" is an island quest in which adventurers carry an urgent military report from Aonda in Shen'dar...",sec={{t="An Urgent Dispatch",b="Following the defense of Shen'dar and its pylon, the highlander Aonda dispatches a trusted traveler to carry urgent word across the island to Valanaar. Situated at the southeastern edge of the land, Valanaar houses the headquarters of the island's peacekeepers under the command of Valennia Stormfist. The mission carries a vital warning: the peaceful days on the island are ending, and Valennia must prepare her people for an inevitable clash with an aggressive, expanding cult.",sp=0},{t="The Gathering Storm",b="Up until this warning, the local peacekeepers managed to avoid direct conflict with the mysterious cult. However, the cult's aggressive incursions have pushed tensions to a breaking point. Upon reviewing Aonda's written report, Valennia recognizes that the peacekeepers must immediately gather their remaining forces and formulate a defensive strategy before the cult directs its attention toward Valanaar itself.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who sends you to Valanaar?",a="The quest is given by Aonda, a highlander based in Shen'dar, after you help secure the local village and pylon from a looming cultist threat. Impressed by your assistance, she entrusts you with an urgent written report to deliver across the island.",al={"who gives to valanaar","quest giver to valanaar","who sends me to valanaar"}},{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist leads the peacekeepers stationed on the island, headquartered in the settlement of Valanaar. Responsible for local defense and order, she is tasked with coordinating responses to regional threats like the aggressive expansion of the cult.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","valennia leader","valennia"}},{q="What is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is a settlement located at the southeastern edge of the island. It serves as a base for the island's peacekeepers, led by Valennia Stormfist, and acts as a refuge and community hub for townsfolk.",al={"what is valanaar","where is valanaar","valanaar location"}},{q="What is the purpose of Aonda's report?",a="Aonda's report details the escalating threat of the cult and the events surrounding the safety of Shen'dar and its pylon. While the peacekeepers have historically avoided direct conflict, the report warns that the cult's rapid expansion makes an eventual clash inevitable, requiring Valanaar to prepare for defense.",al={"why deliver report","what is aonda report","what is in the letter"}},{q="What threat does the cult pose to the island?",a="The report warns of an unnamed cult that is expanding rapidly across the island. Though the peacekeepers previously kept their distance to avoid open warfare, the cult's recent aggressive moves against places like Shen'dar indicate that conflict can no longer be avoided.",al={"what cult to valanaar","who is the cult","cult threat island"}},{q="What happens once you reach Valanaar?",a="Upon receiving the grim news, Valennia must confer with her remaining peacekeepers to devise a defensive strategy against future cult attacks. While she coordinates their plans, she invites travelers to assist the local townsfolk around Valanaar who need a hand.",al={"what to do after delivering report","what next to valanaar"}}},kw={"aonda","cult","peacekeepers","shen'dar","to valanaar","valanaar","valennia stormfist"},m={id=92579},src={"In-game quest text: To Valanaar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92595"] = {t="quest",n="The Windshapers",z="general",s="\"The Windshapers\" is a quest in which Illaya Amberwind introduces the player to the Windshapers and their spiritual mission. Tasked with reconnecting with the wind spirits, the order strives to protect Zephras and ensure the survival of the Shen'dorei amidst threats within Skywall.",h="\"The Windshapers\" is a quest in which Illaya Amberwind introduces the player to the Windshapers and their...",sec={{t="Mandate of the Windshapers",b="The Windshapers are an order dedicated to communing with the wind spirits and protecting the Shen'dorei. Welcoming those with innate spiritual perception, they seek to bridge the growing rift between their people and the elemental forces that sustain their aerial realm. Illaya Amberwind guides new recruits into understanding the heavy burdens the order carries.",sp=0},{t="Trials on Zephras",b="Survival in Skywall is far from assured. The Windshapers must navigate treacherous internal turmoil caused by the Al'Aketh while enduring the wider perils of the elemental plane. Re-establishing dialogue with the spirits of the winds is deemed vital if Zephras is to endure and the Shen'dorei are to weather the storm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a group dedicated to communing with the spirits of the winds and safeguarding their people, the Shen'dorei. Guided by leaders like Illaya Amberwind, they seek out individuals gifted with skysight to restore dialogue with the wind spirits and preserve their imperiled homeland.",al={"who are windshapers","windshapers faction","what do windshapers do"}},{q="What is skysight?",a="Skysight is an innate spiritual sensitivity or gift that allows individuals to connect with the aerial elements. As Illaya explains, a person does not need formal training as a shaman to possess skysight, though commanding it is essential for reaching the spirits of the winds.",al={"what is skysight","skysight lore"}},{q="Who is Illaya Amberwind?",a="Illaya Amberwind is a Windshaper mentor who recognizes spiritual gifts in travelers. She welcomes those possessing skysight, explaining the mandate of the Windshapers, the peril facing Zephras, and the trials confronting the Shen'dorei.",al={"who is illaya","illaya amberwind"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is an aerial land or settlement enduring severe hardships and internal unrest. The Windshapers are actively struggling to protect Zephras and preserve a future for their people there.",al={"what is zephras","zephras lore"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are an internal hostile faction or threat troubling the Windshapers and the Shen'dorei, compounding the dangers of surviving within Skywall.",al={"who are the al'aketh","alaketh threat"}},{q="Who are the Shen'dorei?",a="The Shen'dorei are the people represented by the Windshapers. Facing internal threats like the Al'Aketh and precarious conditions in Skywall, their survival hinges on the Windshapers restoring balance with the winds.",al={"who are shendorei","shen'dorei lore"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Illaya notes that while trained shamans naturally commune with the elements, the gift of skysight can manifest in others as well, highlighting the Windshapers' shared spiritual duty with elemental callers.",["race:Skyborne"]="The Windshapers fight for the very survival and home of the Shen'dorei amidst the precarious heights of Skywall and Zephras."},kw={"al'aketh","illaya amberwind","shen'dorei","skysight","skywall","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"topic:skywall","topic:shaman"},m={id=92595},src={"In-game quest text: The Windshapers"},fv=true}
E["quest:92596"] = {t="quest",n="The High Order",z="general",s="On Zephras, Rathiril Sunlance tells newcomers why the shen'dorei must now stand alone. Their elemental benefactors are gone, so these Highborne descendants of Eldre'Thalas turn to ancient arcane magic to protect their people.",h="On Zephras, Rathiril Sunlance tells newcomers why the shen'dorei must now stand alone.",sec={{t="A People on Their Own",b="The shen'dorei on Zephras are working to reclaim their strength and choose their own path. For a long time, elemental benefactors protected them. Now those patrons are gone, leaving the people exposed to strife within and dangers around them. Rathiril Sunlance seeks those gifted in arcane magic to help preserve their people and build their strength on their own.",sp=0},{t="Legacy of Eldre'Thalas",b="The shen'dorei carry the legacy of Eldre'Thalas and an innate wellspring of arcane energy. Even those never formally trained in wizardry share that power. The High Order sees it as the destiny of Highborne descendants to wield these ancient gifts. With them, the shen'dorei hope to resist the Al'Aketh within and face the many threats to their home on Zephras.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the High Order?",a="Among the shen'dorei on Zephras, the High Order works for their people's survival. Founded by descendants of the Highborne of Eldre'Thalas, it seeks to wield their ancient arcane gifts so their society can defend itself and endure on its own.",al={"who are the high order","what is high order","high order purpose"}},{q="Who is Rathiril Sunlance?",a="Rathiril Sunlance is an elf associated with the High Order on Zephras. He teaches newcomers about local arcane energies, the shen'dorei's plight, and their resolve to survive without their former elemental patrons.",al={"who is rathiril","rathiril sunlance lore","sunlance"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is a floating isle in Skywall where Rathiril Sunlance and his kin now live. Arcane energies fill the isle, and the shen'dorei face dangers within their people and uncertainty about their security from outside threats.",al={"where is the high order","what is zephras","zephras lore"}},{q="Who are the shen'dorei?",a="The shen'dorei are elf descendants of the Highborne from Eldre'Thalas. Now living on Zephras, they have an innate bond with arcane magic and seek to stand on their own.",al={"who are the shendorei","shen dorei","shendorei lore"}},{q="Who were the elemental benefactors?",a="The elemental benefactors were powerful elemental beings who once aided and protected the shen'dorei. They are gone, so the people of Zephras must rely on their own magic and resourcefulness.",al={"who are the elementals","elemental patrons","elemental benefactors"}},{q="What are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are an internal faction or threat that endangers the shen'dorei on Zephras. The High Order organizes defenses in part to face such threats from within.",al={"what is alaketh","al aketh threat","internal threats zephras"}},{q="What is the connection between the High Order and Eldre'Thalas?",a="The shen'dorei's ancestors came from Eldre'Thalas, the ancient Highborne city in Kalimdor known to many in the years after the Third War as Dire Maul. The High Order honors this heritage by preserving and wielding their ancient arcane gifts.",al={"eldrethalas connection","are they from dire maul","highborne origins"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Rathiril says that even adventurers without formal mage training carry strong arcane auras. Their shared magic is an inborn heritage, not only a learned discipline.",["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you descend from the ancient Highborne of Eldre'Thalas. You must master your arcane gifts to stand on your own now that your elemental patrons are gone."},kw={"al'aketh","arcane","eldre'thalas","highborne","rathiril sunlance","shen'dorei","the high order","zephras"},rel={"topic:highborne","topic:mage"},m={id=92596},src={"In-game quest text: The High Order"},fv=true}
E["quest:92640"] = {t="quest",n="Desperate Times",z="general",s="Valennia Stormfist waits in Valanaar with a plan to strike the cult at its shrine. Lorthuna must be stopped there, but the Windshapers and the High Order must first set aside their deep tensions.",h="Valennia Stormfist waits in Valanaar with a plan to strike the cult at its shrine.",sec={{t="A Direct Strike",b="The cult cannot be safely faced in the open, so the defenders need a bolder plan. They will strike at the cult's shrine instead of fighting a long battle on unfavorable ground. If they eliminate Lorthuna before she completes her part in the cult's grand scheme, they can cripple the threat at its source.",sp=0},{t="Uniting Divided Factions",b="In Valanaar, Valennia Stormfist has spent time planning an assault on the shrine. The attack needs substantial reinforcements and strength united across the factions. Yet the Windshapers and the High Order remain divided by severe political tensions, putting their alliance at risk.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist is in Valanaar, where she has been planning an assault on the cult's shrine. The attack depends on cooperation between rival factions.",al={"who is valennia","valennia stormfist","where is valennia"}},{q="Who is Lorthuna?",a="Lorthuna is a key figure in the hostile cult. She is directing a critical scheme at the shrine, making her the target of the planned strike.",al={"who is lorthuna","lorthuna lore","cult leader lorthuna"}},{q="Why must the shrine be attacked directly?",a="The cult cannot be safely confronted in the open. The plan is to strike at the shrine and stop Lorthuna before her scheme comes to fruition.",al={"why attack shrine","cult attack reason"}},{q="What is the issue between the Windshapers and the High Order?",a="Tensions run high between the Windshapers and the High Order. Neither faction can carry out the assault on the shrine alone, so both must cooperate.",al={"high order windshapers tension","why are factions fighting","windshaper dispute"}},{q="Where is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is where Valennia Stormfist is coordinating the plan and rallying allies for a strike against the cult's shrine.",al={"where is valanaar","valanaar location"}},{q="What is the overall goal of this mission?",a="The goal is to find Valennia Stormfist in Valanaar, learn the details of her planned strike, and help secure cooperation between the divided factions.",al={"desperate times objective","what is the plan desperate times"}}},kw={"cult","desperate times","high order","lorthuna","shrine","valanaar","valennia stormfist","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=92640},src={"In-game quest text: Desperate Times"},fv=true}
E["quest:92642"] = {t="quest",n="Disrupting Logistics",z="general",s="At the Gustberry Lowlands, Al'Aketh cultists are pressing a full-scale assault. Their healers keep the brawlers fighting, and the defenders need help before their lines give way.",h="At the Gustberry Lowlands, Al'Aketh cultists are pressing a full-scale assault.",sec={{t="A Desperate Defense",b="A fierce assault is bearing down on the defenders above the Gustberry Lowlands. Enemy forces are gathering, and the local lines are near breaking. The commanders expected substantial reinforcements from Valennia, but they must rely on whatever capable hands arrive. Strikes against the enemy's support may be the only way to hold the defense together.",sp=0},{t="Breaking the Cultist Line",b="Al'Aketh brawlers press forward in ranks, sustained by healers who mend their wounds. In the Gustberry Lowlands, taking out those healers can break the cultists' push. That gives the defenders a chance to rally and drive back the invasion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Disrupting Logistics?",a="Disrupting Logistics is a desperate defense against the Al'Aketh assault in the Gustberry Lowlands. A lone defender needs help taking out the enemy's front-line support before the defense collapses.",al={"what is disrupting logistics","disrupting logistics quest","gustberry lowlands quest"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh cultists attacking the lowlands?",a="Al'Aketh cultists are attacking the Gustberry Lowlands with brawlers and healers. Their organized front depends on healing support to keep pressure on the local defenders.",al={"who are alaketh","who is attacking gustberry lowlands","who are the cultists"}},{q="Why are the Al'Aketh healers specifically targeted?",a="The Al'Aketh healers keep the vanguard fighters pressing forward. Taking them out may break the assault's momentum and give the defenders a chance to rally and push the cultists back.",al={"why kill healers","why target alaketh healers"}},{q="Where does this assault take place?",a="The assault is taking place in the Gustberry Lowlands, directly down the hill from the defensive outpost. Al'Aketh brawlers and healers have overrun the lowlands and are pressing their attack.",al={"where is gustberry lowlands","gustberry lowlands location"}},{q="Who is Valennia?",a="Valennia appears to organize aid and reinforcements. The defender is exasperated that Valennia sent only one adventurer against a massive assault.",al={"who is valennia","valennia reinforcements"}},{q="Who commands the defense against the Al'Aketh?",a="A beleaguered defender offers the call for aid as their people near defeat before the Al'Aketh. The defender reveres 'the winds' and needs any fighting force that can turn the battle.",al={"who gives disrupting logistics","who are my people holding out"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you know how healing support can sustain an offensive line. The Al'Aketh healers are the linchpin of the cultist assault.",["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you know the value of slipping behind enemy lines to eliminate healers and disrupt reinforcements."},kw={"al'aketh","brawlers","cultists","disrupting logistics","gustberry lowlands","healers","valennia"},m={id=92642},src={"In-game quest text: Disrupting Logistics"},fv=true}
E["quest:92643"] = {t="quest",n="The Turncoat",z="general",s="A cultist of the Al'Aketh offers to defect and reveal what the cult intends for the island. You are sent to bring him from a secluded highland retreat northwest of Valanaar, where the meeting may be a trap.",h="A cultist of the Al'Aketh offers to defect and reveal what the cult intends for the island.",sec={{t="A Broken Oath",b="A cultist within the Al'Aketh ranks claims to know the cult's true intentions for the island. He offers to defect, but demands firm assurances of protection. The cult guards its plans closely, so reaching out may be worth the danger.",sp=0},{t="The Highland Hideout",b="The defector waits alone in a secluded residence in the highlands northwest of Valanaar. You must reach him with care. The meeting may be an Al'Aketh ambush meant to draw out those who pry into the cult's affairs.",sp=0},{t="Silence in the Highlands",b="The defector lies dead inside the secluded highland house. Assassins tracked him down and struck before an escort could arrive. Outside, fallen assassins mark the aftermath. A gleam on the dead turncoat's body hints that he left an answer behind.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is the turncoat?",a="The turncoat was a former member of the Al'Aketh cult who claimed to know the cult's true intentions for the island. He offered to defect and share what he knew in exchange for protection.",al={"who is the turncoat","who was the defector","turncoat identity"}},{q="What is the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are an enigmatic cult operating in the region. Their plans for the island remain hidden behind secrecy and zealotry, so local authorities risk much to reach deserters willing to speak.",al={"who are the alaketh","alaketh cult","what is alaketh"}},{q="Where is the defector hiding?",a="The defector hid in a secluded residence in the highlands northwest of Valanaar. He waited there for an escort, trusting in promises of protection.",al={"where is the turncoat hiding","turncoat location","where to find the turncoat"}},{q="What happened to the defector?",a="By the time anyone reached the secluded highland residence, assassins had tracked down and slain the defector. He was found dead inside the house, beyond questioning.",al={"what happened to the turncoat","did the defector die","who killed the turncoat"}},{q="What did the turncoat leave behind?",a="After the assassins outside were dealt with, a glint on the dead defector's body caught the eye. The shiny item remained, and may hold a clue to the secrets he carried.",al={"what shiny object","turncoat shiny item","shiny reflection body"}},{q="Why did the cultist decide to defect?",a="The cultist claims to know the Al'Aketh's true intentions for the island and will defect only with assurances of protection. What drove him to leave the cult is not known.",al={"why did the cultist defect","turncoat motive","why betray alaketh"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Infiltration, defection, and ambush are familiar to a Rogue. Cultists who break ranks risk silence or a blade. The assassins outside show how secrecy can cut both ways in the highlands."},kw={"al'aketh","cultist","defector","highlands","the turncoat","turncoat","valanaar"},m={id=92643},src={"In-game quest text: The Turncoat"},fv=true}
E["quest:92644"] = {t="quest",n="Unfortunate News",z="general",s="You find the turncoat dead before they can speak. A damaged blue crystal remains beneath the body, and you carry it to High Elder Talanaanis Shadowsong in Valanaar.",h="You find the turncoat dead before they can speak.",sec={{t="A Broken Rendezvous",b="You reach the rendezvous and find the defecting cultist already slain. Their body is still warm. Beneath it lies a small, glowing blue crystal, damaged but still humming. You carry it to High Elder Talanaanis Shadowsong in Valanaar.",sp=0},{t="Shadowsong's Investigation",b="You return to Valanaar alone and tell High Elder Talanaanis Shadowsong that the turncoat died before giving testimony. The loss of a living contact is a serious setback. Yet the damaged blue crystal catches the High Elder's eye. He finds it interesting and takes it from you.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest Unfortunate News?",a="You find the defecting cultist dead before they can speak. Beneath the body lies a damaged, glowing blue crystal, which you carry to High Elder Talanaanis Shadowsong.",al={"what is unfortunate news","unfortunate news quest","unfortunate news wow forever"}},{q="Who was the turncoat cultist?",a="The turncoat was a defecting cultist you were sent to meet. They were killed before they could speak or escape, leaving a still-warm body and a damaged crystal.",al={"who is the dead turncoat","who died in unfortunate news","turncoat cultist"}},{q="Who is High Elder Shadowsong?",a="Talanaanis Shadowsong is an elf and the High Elder in Valanaar. He directs investigations into the cultists and receives the damaged crystal from the turncoat's body.",al={"who is shadowsong in valanaar","where is talanaanis shadowsong","talanaanis shadowsong"}},{q="What is the damaged blue crystal?",a="The damaged blue crystal is a small, glowing crystal found beneath the fallen turncoat. High Elder Shadowsong finds it interesting and takes it from you.",al={"what is the glowing crystal","blue crystal from cultist","damaged crystal unfortunate news"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="Valanaar is where High Elder Talanaanis Shadowsong resides. You return there with your report and the recovered crystal.",al={"where is valanaar","valanaar location","turn in unfortunate news"}},{q="How does this quest connect to The Turncoat?",a="This follows The Turncoat, in which you set out to find the defecting cultist. You arrive too late to save them.",al={"what quest comes before unfortunate news","unfortunate news prequel","the turncoat quest"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, you may recognize the damaged, glowing blue crystal as an artifact. High Elder Shadowsong investigates the cultists and takes the crystal for study."},kw={"cultist","glowing crystal","shadowsong","talanaanis shadowsong","turncoat","unfortunate news","valanaar"},rel={"quest:92643"},m={id=92644},src={"In-game quest text: Unfortunate News"},fv=true}
E["quest:92645"] = {t="quest",n="Breaking the Breaker",z="general",s="In the Gustberry Lowlands, cultists press the defenders hard. Commander Belguilos leads the assault from her hillside villa, and striking her there may be the defenders’ only chance to survive.",h="In the Gustberry Lowlands, cultists press the defenders hard.",sec={{t="A Desperate Defense",b="Cultists assault the Gustberry Lowlands, driving the local defenders to the brink. Repelling them seems nearly impossible while Commander Belguilos directs the attack. All fear her as “the Breaker.”\n\nHer villa is the operation’s base. Slaying her there is perilous, near-suicidal, yet passivity leaves the defenders doomed.",sp=0},{t="The Fallen Peacekeeper",b="Before she commanded the cultists, Belguilos kept order as a peacekeeper. Even then, those around her found something deeply unsettling in her manner. When the cult’s influence spread beyond control, she embraced its extremism. Now she commands the cultists and is a terror.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Belguilos the Breaker?",a="Belguilos, called “the Breaker,” commands the cultist attack. Before joining the cult, she served as a peacekeeper, though those around her found her unsettling even then.",al={"who is belguilos","who is the breaker","cult commander"}},{q="Where can Commander Belguilos be found?",a="Commander Belguilos is at the villa at the base of the hill in the Gustberry Lowlands. From there, she coordinates the cultist assault on the local defenders.",al={"where is belguilos","belguilos location","where to find the breaker"}},{q="Why must Belguilos be eliminated?",a="The cultists’ onslaught is nearly impossible to repel while Belguilos directs it. Eliminating her breaks the cult’s leadership and gives the defenders a real chance to survive.",al={"why kill belguilos","cult attack reason","why is breaker dangerous"}},{q="What was Belguilos's past before joining the cult?",a="Before the cult’s influence spread beyond control, Belguilos served as a peacekeeper. Those who knew her found something deeply unsettling in her even then.",al={"what was belguilos before","was belguilos good","peacekeeper cultist"}},{q="What is the significance of the blessing of the four winds?",a="The blessing of the four winds is an expression of profound relief and gratitude among the defenders after the terrifying cult commander has been permanently stopped.",al={"four winds meaning","who says four winds bless you"}},{q="What are the Gustberry Lowlands?",a="The Gustberry Lowlands are contested ground where the cult assault unfolds. Commander Belguilos can be found at a villa at the base of the hill.",al={"what is gustberry lowlands","where is breaker quest"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="The defenders invoke the four winds to bless the hero after the cultists are stopped.",["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, you face Commander Belguilos, a former peacekeeper feared as “the Breaker.”"},kw={"belguilos","breaking the breaker","commander belguilos","cult commander","gustberry lowlands","peacekeeper","the breaker","villa"},m={id=92645},src={"In-game quest text: Breaking the Breaker"},fv=true}
E["quest:92646"] = {t="quest",n="Confront Lorthuna",z="general",s="Elaadrin Evengale and Ayessa Dawnsinger pursue High Priestess Lorthuna through a portal into the Rohashi Spires above Zephras. They call for your aid before she can escape justice or strengthen her power.",h="Elaadrin Evengale and Ayessa Dawnsinger pursue High Priestess Lorthuna through a portal into the Rohashi...",sec={{t="Pursuit into the Spires",b="High Priestess Lorthuna flees through a mystic gateway into the Rohashi Spires above Zephras. An injured ally remains behind at the portal's threshold. Elaadrin Evengale and Ayessa Dawnsinger follow her into the heights and call for your help before she escapes justice or strengthens her power.",sp=0},{t="The Cost of the Battle",b="After the clash in the spires, Valennia and her surviving forces need a healer's care. They are escorted to recover within the High Elder's residence. Lorthuna has met them in combat, and the coalition is battered with its resources stretched thin.",sp=1},{t="An Island Left Vulnerable",b="After the battle, Muln confers on the Windshapers and their continuing mission, then departs for mainland Azeroth. Zephras remains critically vulnerable. The leaders left behind must urgently plan defenses against the dangers that still threaten the island.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is High Priestess Lorthuna?",a="High Priestess Lorthuna is a powerful adversary who flees through a portal to the Rohashi Spires above Zephras. Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale pursue her there.",al={"who is lorthuna","what is lorthuna","high priestess lorthuna lore"}},{q="What are the Rohashi Spires?",a="The Rohashi Spires are heights above the island of Zephras. High Priestess Lorthuna flees there through a portal, and her pursuers follow.",al={"what are rohashi spires","where is the portal going","rohashi spires location"}},{q="Who pursued Lorthuna into the portal?",a="Elaadrin Evengale and Ayessa Dawnsinger pursue High Priestess Lorthuna through the portal as she flees to the spires. They call for your aid.",al={"who went after lorthuna","ayessa and elaadrin","who enters the portal"}},{q="What happened to Valennia and her forces?",a="Valennia and her surviving forces need immediate care after the fighting. Healers tend them, and they are escorted to recover in the High Elder's residence.",al={"who is valennia","what happened to valennia","valennia forces"}},{q="What is Muln's role in these events?",a="Muln is an ally associated with the Windshapers who departs for mainland Azeroth after discussing their future and continuing mission.",al={"who is muln","muln leaving zephras","where did muln go"}},{q="Is the island of Zephras safe after Lorthuna is confronted?",a="Zephras remains critically vulnerable after the confrontation. Its leaders must urgently plan defenses against the dangers threatening the island.",al={"what happens to zephras","is zephras safe now","state of zephras island"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you face High Priestess Lorthuna, a corrupted spiritual leader among the spires. The battle against her carries weight for those who defend against dark doctrine.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you share a spiritual stake in the Windshapers' work to guide elemental balance on Zephras. You can help ensure that harmony survives Lorthuna's threat."},kw={"ayessa dawnsinger","confront lorthuna","elaadrin evengale","high priestess lorthuna","lorthuna","muln","rohashi spires","valennia","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","quest:92595"},m={id=92646},src={"In-game quest text: Confront Lorthuna"},fv=true}
E["quest:92679"] = {t="quest",n="Blood Tithe",z="general",s="Blood Tithe is a quest initiated by Alvarion Windfield, an ailing Skyborne farmer seeking aid in the settlement of Valanaar. Desperate to protect his livelihood, he recruits adventurers to travel west to his lowland orchard and assist his wife, Aamelia.",h="Blood Tithe is a quest initiated by Alvarion Windfield, an ailing Skyborne farmer seeking aid in the...",sec={{t="A Plea in Valanaar",b="In the settlement of Valanaar, an injured Skyborne man named Alvarion Windfield desperately searches for capable adventurers. Wracked with visible pain, Alvarion has traveled from his homestead into town to hire fighters who can help save his livelihood. Rather than tasking travelers on the spot, he implores those willing to help to journey westward into the lowlands, where his wife Aamelia maintains their family orchard.",sp=0},{t="Trouble in the Lowlands",b="The Windfield Orchard lies situated in the lowlands to the west of Valanaar. With Alvarion incapacitated by intense suffering, the burden of holding the farm falls upon Aamelia Windfield. Adventurers taking up Alvarion's offer must make their way into the western valley to discover what threatens the orchard and lend their strength to the embattled family.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Alvarion Windfield?",a="Alvarion Windfield is a Skyborne farmer who has come to Valanaar seeking capable fighters to help protect his home. Despite suffering from severe physical pain, he manages to direct adventurers to his wife, Aamelia, at their orchard.",al={"who is alvarion","alvarion windfield lore","who gives blood tithe"}},{q="Where is the Windfield Orchard located?",a="Windfield Orchard is situated to the west of Valanaar, down in the lowlands. Alvarion sends adventurers there to meet his wife, Aamelia Windfield, who oversees the farm's immediate defense.",al={"where is windfield orchard","aamelia windfield location","how to find the orchard"}},{q="Why is Alvarion in pain?",a="While the exact affliction is unstated when meeting him, Alvarion is visibly suffering from intense, debilitating pain as he speaks, adding urgency to his plea for help.",al={"why is alvarion hurting","what is wrong with alvarion","alvarion pain"}},{q="Who is Aamelia Windfield?",a="Aamelia Windfield is Alvarion's wife, stationed at the family orchard west of Valanaar. Alvarion directs willing adventurers to her to organize the defense of their land.",al={"who is aamelia","aamelia windfield lore","alvarion wife"}},{q="What is the task in Blood Tithe?",a="Blood Tithe begins with Alvarion Windfield recruiting travelers in Valanaar to assist his struggling farm. Adventurers must listen to his request and seek out his wife Aamelia at their lowland orchard.",al={"blood tithe quest summary","what is blood tithe about","farm under attack valanaar"}},{q="What is Valanaar's significance here?",a="Valanaar is a settlement where travelers and Skyborne gather. Alvarion visits this hub specifically to find hired help and adventurers capable of rescuing his orchard from peril.",al={"valanaar lore","where does blood tithe take place","what is valanaar"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="Alvarion Windfield is a Skyborne farmer whose physical suffering and desperate call for aid highlight the struggles everyday Skyborne face beyond the settlement of Valanaar."},kw={"aamelia windfield","alvarion windfield","blood tithe","skyborne","valanaar","windfield orchard"},rel={"topic:skyborne","quest:92579"},m={id=92679},src={"In-game quest text: Blood Tithe"},fv=true}
E["quest:92682"] = {t="quest",n="Make Yourself Useful",z="general",s="Make Yourself Useful is an assignment at Windfield Orchard where adventurers help secure the stormapple crop against undisciplined bandits. An extortionist named Ferauu is due to collect his regular share of the harvest, but hungry outlaws have broken ranks to pilfer the trees ahead of time. Dealing with these rogue thieves earns the respect of the watchful orchard keeper.",h="Make Yourself Useful is an assignment at Windfield Orchard where adventurers help secure the stormapple crop...",sec={{t="Trouble at Windfield Orchard",b="At Windfield Orchard, farming is complicated by extortion. A bandit leader named Ferauu and his crew have established an arrangement to collect their regular cut of the fruit harvest. However, lack of discipline within the bandit ranks means that petty outlaws cannot always wait for their boss's scheduled visit, sneaking in early to pick over the trees.",sp=0},{t="Thieves Among Thieves",b="Rather than enduring undisciplined thieves among the stormapple trees, the orchard caretaker puts ambitious newcomers to work before Ferauu's scheduled arrival. Adventurers are tasked with securing the ripe stormapple yield and dispatching the hungry bandits trespassing among the trees. The keeper notes that Ferauu would likely execute these rogues himself for going behind his back and tampering with the cut.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ferauu?",a="Ferauu is a bandit leader who extorts the Windfield Orchard, demanding a regular tribute or 'take' of the harvest. He maintains ruthless discipline over his crew, though evidently not enough to stop some hungry lackeys from sneaking in early behind his back.",al={"who is ferauu","ferauu lore","ferauu bandit leader"}},{q="What is Windfield Orchard?",a="Windfield Orchard is a local farming ground known for producing stormapples. The orchard operates under duress, forced to pay off Ferauu's bandit gang with portions of its fruit harvest to keep the peace.",al={"what is windfield orchard","windfield orchard lore","stormapple orchard"}},{q="Why are hungry bandits prowling the stormapple trees?",a="Hungry Bandits affiliated with Ferauu have jumped the gun, sneaking into the orchard ahead of the scheduled harvest pickup to steal stormapples for themselves. Because they broke ranks and are tampering with the crop, the orchard keeper puts adventurers to work clearing them out.",al={"why kill hungry bandits","why are bandits at the orchard","hungry bandits lore"}},{q="What are stormapples?",a="Stormapples are a variety of fruit cultivated at Windfield Orchard. They are prized enough to serve both as everyday sustenance and as the extortion payment demanded by local criminal gangs like Ferauu's.",al={"what are stormapples","ripe stormapples"}},{q="Does Ferauu know his bandits are sneaking into the orchard?",a="No, Ferauu is unaware that these specific bandits came early. As noted by the orchard keeper, these outlaws went rogue and would likely be executed by Ferauu himself if he caught them pocketing the harvest before his arrival.",al={"does ferauu know about the bandits","ferauu double cross"}},{q="Who tasks you with Make Yourself Useful?",a="The quest is offered by the caretaker at Windfield Orchard, who watches the groves from a nearby window. Faced with bold new arrivals looking for work or food, the keeper tests their mettle by sending them against the intruding bandits.",al={"who gives make yourself useful","make yourself useful quest giver"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="While rogue bandits are busy betraying their own crew for an early bite of stormapples, seasoned rogues will recognize the classic lack of discipline among amateur thieves."},kw={"bandits","ferauu","hungry bandits","make yourself useful","orchard","ripe stormapples","stormapples","windfield orchard"},rel={"topic:rogue"},m={id=92682},src={"In-game quest text: Make Yourself Useful"},fv=true}
E["quest:92683"] = {t="quest",n="Flutterfly Dust",z="general",s="Flutterfly Dust is a local task in the Gustberry Lowlands involving the harvesting of powder from giant, winged flutterflies. While the insects are usually docile, isolated homesteaders rely on travelers to gather the messy substance due to the constant threat of roving bandits.",h="Flutterfly Dust is a local task in the Gustberry Lowlands involving the harvesting of powder from giant...",sec={{t="The Giants of Gustberry",b="The outer fields and orchards of the Gustberry Lowlands are home to giant flutterflies, picturesque winged insects that drift lazily through the rural landscape. While their gentle appearance lends a pastoral charm to the area, their fluttering wings shed a fine, pervasive dust that can be gathered in bulk. Though primarily wild insects, their presence draws the attention of nearby homesteaders who seek to harvest this airborne byproduct for practical domestic needs.",sp=0},{t="Swatters and Shakes",b="Harvesting flutterfly dust requires a surprisingly blunt approach: striking the large insects with a Flutterfly Swatter. Most flutterflies simply panic and take to the air when struck, shaking their rumps and dropping piles of loose powder behind them. Occasionally, however, an agitated flutterfly will turn and strike back when provoked, proving that even docile garden bugs have their limits when swatted.",sp=0},{t="Unspoken Applications",b="Collecting the dust is a messy, clinging endeavor that tends to coat clothing and gear for weeks. While the local requester eagerly awaits the delivery due to lurking bandits making solo trips into the fields too dangerous, the final destination of the dust is kept tightly under wraps. Pressed on how it will be used, the homesteader only offers evasive dismissals about general 'stuff and things.'",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is flutterfly dust used for?",a="According to the local resident gathering it, the wing dust dropped by giant flutterflies has practical household applications when collected in sufficient quantities. However, the exact purpose remains deliberately vague, as the quest giver deflects questions with claims that it is for general 'stuff and things.'",al={"what is flutterfly dust","flutterfly dust use","what is the dust for"}},{q="Where are flutterflies found?",a="Giant flutterflies can be found fluttering across the outer fields near the local orchard, particularly roaming the Gustberry Lowlands. They share these fields with nearby bandit threats that make gathering dangerous for isolated residents.",al={"where are flutterflies","flutterfly location","gustberry lowlands bugs"}},{q="Are flutterflies aggressive?",a="Flutterflies are generally docile, calm creatures that usually flee when disturbed. However, if swatted or provoked, some will take offense and surprise adventurers by defending themselves before releasing their wing dust.",al={"are flutterflies dangerous","do flutterflies attack","flutterfly behavior"}},{q="How is flutterfly dust collected?",a="Adventurers use a specialized Flutterfly Swatter to deliver a firm swat to the oversized insects. The startled or agitated creatures shake their rumps as they react, scattering a gatherable pile of wing dust onto the ground.",al={"how to get dust","flutterfly swatter","collect flutterfly dust"}},{q="Why does the resident need someone else to gather the dust?",a="The homestead resident who needs the dust lives alone and cannot easily leave the house to harvest it, as bandits lurking throughout the surrounding lowlands pose too severe a threat to risk venturing far into the fields.",al={"why need help","bandit threat gustberry","why quest giver asks"}},{q="Why is the resident evasive about the dust's true purpose?",a="The quest giver refuses to divulge the exact household use for the messy dust, nervously waving off inquiries with 'things and stuff.' Whether it is an embarrassing personal chore, an unorthodox craft, or simply private business remains unexplained.",al={"why so secret","secret dust recipe"}}},kw={"bandits","flutterfly","flutterfly dust","flutterfly swatter","gustberry lowlands","orchard","wing dust"},m={id=92683},src={"In-game quest text: Flutterfly Dust"},fv=true}
E["quest:92684"] = {t="quest",n="Ornery Ornery Galestriders",z="general",s="Ornery Ornery Galestriders concerns an orchard farmer in the Gustberry Lowlands struggling with a meager seasonal harvest. The local lowlands galestriders—larger and more vicious than their highland kin—have been raiding the orchard for stormapples and threatening the farm's livelihood. Culling the aggressive birds secures the remaining fruit while providing much-needed fresh tenderloins for dinner.",h="Ornery Ornery Galestriders concerns an orchard farmer in the Gustberry Lowlands struggling with a meager...",sec={{t="Defending the Orchard",b="In the Gustberry Lowlands, local farmers face tough times following an already meager seasonal harvest. To make matters worse, wild galestriders have invaded the orchards, stripping branches of ripe stormapples and jeopardizing what little fruit remains. Keeping the farm viable means driving off or thinning out the boldest birds before the entire crop is lost.",sp=0},{t="Lowland Terrors",b="Unlike their smaller and comparatively calmer cousins in the highlands, the galestriders of the lowlands are towering, bad-tempered beasts. Travelers in the lowlands must approach them with extreme care, as a single well-aimed kick from their powerful legs is enough to incapacitate or kill an inattentive fighter. Taking them down yields massive tenderloins that supply hungry locals with rare, substantial meals.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the galestriders causing problems for the farmer?",a="The local galestriders have been raiding the orchard to eat the farmer's stormapples. With this season's harvest already remarkably light, the fruit theft poses a direct threat to the local food supply.",al={"what are galestriders stealing","why kill galestriders","stormapple orchard"}},{q="How dangerous are lowlands galestriders?",a="Lowlands galestriders are substantially larger and more aggressive than those found in the highlands. Their powerful, well-timed kicks can easily knock a traveler down for good if they are caught off guard.",al={"how dangerous are galestriders","galestrider attacks","are galestriders strong"}},{q="What does the farmer want from the galestriders?",a="The farmer asks travelers to cull the aggressive lowlands galestriders and collect tenderloins from the fallen beasts. The meat provides a much-needed hot meal after going without fresh meat for a long time.",al={"galestrider meat","what do galestriders drop","tenderloin quest"}},{q="Where are these galestriders found?",a="Ornery lowlands galestriders roam across the Gustberry Lowlands, particularly near the farmsteads and orchards where they forage for stormapples.",al={"where are galestriders found","galestrider location","gustberry lowlands"}},{q="How do lowlands galestriders compare to highlands galestriders?",a="Lowlands galestriders differ from their highland counterparts primarily in size and temperament: the lowlands variety grows notably larger, displays a far nastier disposition, and delivers punishing kicks.",al={"highlands vs lowlands galestriders","galestrider varieties"}},{q="What are stormapples?",a="Stormapples are an orchard crop cultivated in the region. Because the current season's yield is already scarce, protecting the remaining trees from foraging galestriders is critical.",al={"what are stormapples","orchard harvest"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Lowlands galestriders provide hearty cuts of tenderloin that make a substantial hot meal, offering a welcome change of pace from lean harvests and fruit.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters tracking wildlife in the Gustberry Lowlands should note the powerful kicks and aggressive temper of the local lowlands galestriders compared to their highland kin."},kw={"galestrider","gustberry lowlands","lowlands galestrider","orchard","ornery galestriders","stormapples","tenderloin"},m={id=92684},src={"In-game quest text: Ornery Ornery Galestriders"},fv=true}
E["quest:92685"] = {t="quest",n="The Hills Have Eyes",z="general",s="\"The Hills Have Eyes\" is a quest in the Gustberry Lowlands where the beleaguered farmer Aamelia takes a stand against highwayman bandits. Adventurers launch a preemptive strike on an encampment in the northern hills to diminish the bandit forces before their leader, Ferauu, launches a raid.",h="\"The Hills Have Eyes\" is a quest in the Gustberry Lowlands where the beleaguered farmer Aamelia takes a stand...",sec={{t="A Preemptive Strike",b="In the Gustberry Lowlands, a farmer named Aamelia faces an impending assault from a ring of outlaws led by Ferauu. Rather than waiting passively for doom to arrive at her doorstep, she urges travelers to launch a preemptive strike against a bandit encampment in the hills to the north. By taking out the highwaymen before they muster their attack, she aims to thin their ranks and buy precious time.",sp=0},{t="Renewed Hope and Retribution",b="As proof of the ambush, Aamelia asks for blood-stained masks stripped from the fallen highwaymen. She jokes grimly about stitching them into a blanket to present to Ferauu, reveling in the knowledge that her tormentors were met with fierce resistance. Thinning the northern camp shatters weeks of hopeless waiting, giving her the courage to make a stand when Ferauu and his main band finally arrive.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is The Hills Have Eyes?",a="\"The Hills Have Eyes\" is a defense quest given by Aamelia at her farm. Facing an imminent raid by Ferauu's gang of highwaymen, she directs adventurers into the northern hills to ambush the bandits and retrieve their blood-stained masks before they can strike.",al={"hills have eyes quest","what is this quest","what is the hills have eyes"}},{q="Who gives the quest The Hills Have Eyes?",a="Aamelia is a farmstead defender who has spent weeks in dread waiting for bandits to overrun her homestead. Striking back at the highwaymen gives her renewed hope to face Ferauu's looming raid.",al={"who gives the hills have eyes","who is aamelia","quest giver aamelia"}},{q="Who is Ferauu?",a="Ferauu is the leader of the highwayman bandits operating around the Gustberry Lowlands. He and his thugs have been threatening Aamelia's farm and are poised to launch an attack.",al={"who is ferauu","ferauu boss","bandit leader ferauu"}},{q="Where are the highwayman bandits located?",a="The Highwayman Bandits inhabit camps throughout the Gustberry Lowlands, including a small encampment perched in the hills directly north of Aamelia's farm.",al={"gustberry lowlands bandits","where are the bandits","bandit camp hills"}},{q="Why does Aamelia want blood-stained bandit masks?",a="Aamelia demands blood-stained masks as proof that the bandits were made to suffer and caught off guard. Taking them down beforehand helps even the odds against Ferauu's impending raid.",al={"why collect masks","why blood stained masks","what are the masks for"}},{q="What happens after completing the quest?",a="After decimating the hill camp, Aamelia feels hope returning to her farm. However, Ferauu and his remaining thugs are due to arrive at any moment, setting the stage for a final stand.",al={"what happens after the hills have eyes","next quest aamelia","ferauu attack"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="Aamelia jokes darkly about stitching a blanket out of blood-stained bandit masks for Ferauu, showing a rather gruesome appreciation for needlecraft."},kw={"aamelia","bandit masks","blood-stained bandit masks","ferauu","gustberry lowlands","highwayman bandits","the hills have eyes"},m={id=92685},src={"In-game quest text: The Hills Have Eyes"},fv=true}
E["quest:92693"] = {t="quest",n="Standing Our Ground",z="general",s="Standing Our Ground is a decisive quest where an adventurer stands alongside Aamelia Windfield in a desperate confrontation against a menacing bandit leader. Having prepared the defenders for battle, you face what seemed like a lost cause to secure safety from the brigands.",h="Standing Our Ground is a decisive quest where an adventurer stands alongside Aamelia Windfield in a desperate...",sec={{t="A Desperate Last Stand",b="Standing Our Ground marks the climax of a desperate struggle against a gang of bandits. Having helped prepare the local defenders for an inevitable clash, you are given one final opportunity to walk away and return safely to Valanaar rather than throw yourself into what appears to be a doomed last stand.",sp=0},{t="Confronting the Bandit Leader",b="When you signal that you are ready, you step forward alongside Aamelia Windfield to directly confront the bandit leader. Defeating the brigand ends the imminent threat, leaving Aamelia breathless with shock and relief that their desperate gamble actually succeeded.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Standing Our Ground?",a="The quest is given by Aamelia Windfield, who prepares to face a looming bandit assault and offers you a final chance to back out before confronting their leader.",al={"who gives standing our ground","quest giver standing our ground","standing our ground npc"}},{q="Who do you fight in Standing Our Ground?",a="The confrontation is against an encroaching bandit leader threatening the local defenders. Aamelia Windfield rallies her remaining resolve to march out and face this leader directly.",al={"who is the bandit leader standing our ground","standing our ground boss","bandit leader standing our ground"}},{q="What is Valanaar in relation to this quest?",a="Valanaar is the nearby settlement mentioned by Aamelia Windfield as a place of retreat. She explicitly gives you the option to fall back to Valanaar if you do not wish to risk your life in the fight.",al={"where is valanaar standing our ground","standing our ground retreat town","valanaar quest"}},{q="Why does Aamelia describe the battle as a lost cause?",a="Aamelia and her companions believe the odds are heavily stacked against them, viewing the standoff as a desperate last stand where surviving another day seems almost impossible without outside help.",al={"why is standing our ground a lost cause","standing our ground story","aamelia windfield struggle"}},{q="What happens once the confrontation is over?",a="Against the odds, the bandit leader is defeated. Upon completion, Aamelia Windfield is left in shock and relief that the ordeal is finally over, struggling to steady her racing heart.",al={"what happens after beating bandit leader standing our ground","standing our ground turn in","does aamelia survive"}},{q="What leads up to this final stand?",a="Before this quest, you aided the defenders with preparations that gave them a fighting chance against the bandit threat, paving the way for this decisive confrontation.",al={"standing our ground prerequisites","quests before standing our ground"}}},kw={"aamelia windfield","bandit leader","bandits","lost cause","standing our ground","valanaar"},rel={"quest:92579"},m={id=92693},src={"In-game quest text: Standing Our Ground"},fv=true}
E["quest:92698"] = {t="quest",n="What Is My Purpose?",z="general",s="\"What Is My Purpose?\" is a quest centered on a giant Malfunctioning Cyclone Construct wandering aimlessly through agricultural fields. Repeating broken fragments of speech beneath its armor plating, the ancient machine has forgotten its original duties after decades of neglect following the departure of the wind elementals. Investigating the construct leads adventurers to consult local orchardist Aamelia Windfield to learn about its mysterious past and former caretakers.",h="\"What Is My Purpose?\" is a quest centered on a giant Malfunctioning Cyclone Construct wandering aimlessly...",sec={{t="The Wandering Construct",b="Deep within the fields stands a towering Cyclone Construct, aimlessly pacing across the land while trapped in a broken operational loop. Those who approach the armored giant can hear muffled, fragmented speech vibrating from beneath its heavy plates, endlessly repeating errors and questioning its own fundamental function. Decades of isolation have left the machine hollow of purpose, its internal directives corrupted or forgotten. While towering and formidable in appearance, the automaton poses no active threat to passersby, existing instead as a melancholic relic of an older era whose voice has been reduced to static queries echoing over the soil.",sp=0},{t="Forgotten Labor and the Windshapers",b="When questioned about the machine, the local resident Aamelia Windfield reveals that the automaton has wandered the surrounding fields for decades, seemingly ever since the wind elementals departed. A local named Alvarion speculated that the machine once hauled bountiful harvests to distant markets or tended livestock centuries ago, long before the land was transformed into an orchard. In bygone ages, a group known as the Windshapers built and maintained these cyclone constructs. Although the orchard workers have occasionally discussed petitioning the Windshapers to repair the machine, the matter has never been urgent, leaving the construct to roam unbothered and largely ignored.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Malfunctioning Cyclone Construct?",a="The Malfunctioning Cyclone Construct is an ancient armored automaton that wanders aimlessly through local fields. Once maintained by the Windshapers, it has fallen into disrepair over decades of neglect. It endlessly repeats broken vocal fragments questioning its purpose and reporting operational errors from beneath its armored plating.",al={"what is the construct","cyclone construct","giant construct in fields"}},{q="Who is Aamelia Windfield?",a="Aamelia Windfield is a local resident living near the orchard where the malfunctioning construct wanders. She possesses knowledge regarding the history of the machine, explaining that it has paced the fields for decades since the wind elementals departed, and that its creators, the Windshapers, used to care for such devices.",al={"aamelia windfield","who is aamelia","orchard npc"}},{q="What was the construct's original purpose?",a="The exact original purpose of the construct has been lost to time. According to Aamelia Windfield, a resident named Alvarion believed it may have hauled harvests to market or tended livestock centuries ago before the current orchard existed, potentially dating back as far as a thousand years.",al={"construct purpose","what did the construct do","construct job"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are an ancient group known for creating and maintaining cyclone constructs. In earlier ages, they took responsibility for servicing these automated machines, though the local farmhands have not petitioned them for assistance in modern times due to the lack of any pressing danger or necessity.",al={"windshapers","who made the construct","who maintains cyclone constructs"}},{q="Why is the construct repeating fragmented words?",a="The construct suffers from severe mechanical or operational malfunction, likely caused by decades without maintenance following the departure of the wind elementals. When approached, it emits disjointed phrases such as 'function error' and 'what is function', suggesting its directive processing is trapped in an unresolved questioning loop.",al={"why is construct talking","function error construct","what is it saying"}},{q="Why haven't the locals repaired or dismantled the construct?",a="The construct poses no threat to the inhabitants or their orchards, merely roaming peacefully among the trees and crops. While residents like Aamelia Windfield have spoken about petitioning the Windshapers for maintenance, the issue has never been urgent enough to warrant action, leading locals to simply ignore it.",al={"why ignore the construct","why not fix construct","is the construct dangerous"}},{q="Who is Alvarion?",a="Alvarion is a local mentioned by Aamelia Windfield who investigated the history of the wandering construct. He theorized that the machine originally performed agrarian labor, either hauling gathered harvests to market or herding livestock long before the surrounding terrain was converted into an orchard.",al={"alvarion","who is alvarion","alvarion theory"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Engineers will find the Malfunctioning Cyclone Construct an intriguing case of ancient automation, noting how its internal logic loops persist despite centuries of wear and complete lack of technical maintenance.",["class:Shaman"]="Shamans may take particular interest in the construct's operational decline, as local accounts link its abandonment and failure directly to the departure of the wind elementals from the region."},kw={"aamelia windfield","alvarion","construct","cyclone construct","malfunctioning cyclone construct","orchard","what is my purpose","wind elementals","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=92698},src={"In-game quest text: What Is My Purpose?"},fv=true}
E["quest:92699"] = {t="quest",n="The Supreme Magister",z="general",s="In Valanaar, Elaadrin Evengale draws new arrivals toward the High Order. From a lodge in the settlement’s southeast, the order seeks allies as it faces hostile Windshapers and a nearby cult.",h="In Valanaar, Elaadrin Evengale draws new arrivals toward the High Order.",sec={{t="The High Order of Valanaar",b="New arrivals of promise in Valanaar are directed to the High Order. Supreme Magister Elaadrin Evengale leads it from a lodge in the settlement’s far southeast. He watches newcomers closely, seeking the strength, resolve, and arcane aura to face the dangers threatening their home.",sp=0},{t="Rival Factions and Looming Cults",b="Peace in Valanaar is fragile. The High Order and the Windshapers are bitter enemies, so newcomers are warned to keep their distance from the Windshapers lest violence break out in the streets. A dangerous cult lurks nearby. With threats outside the town, its leaders cannot afford bloodshed within.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elaadrin Evengale?",a="Elaadrin Evengale leads the High Order as its Supreme Magister. From a lodge in Valanaar’s southeast, he seeks people with the arcane aptitude and resolve to defend their home.",al={"who is elaadrin evengale","elaadrin evengale","who is the supreme magister"}},{q="Where can Elaadrin Evengale be found?",a="Elaadrin Evengale and his followers work from a lodge in Valanaar’s far southeast.",al={"where is supreme magister","where to find elaadrin evengale","valanaar lodge location"}},{q="What is the High Order?",a="The High Order is an arcane organization in Valanaar, led by Supreme Magister Elaadrin Evengale. Its members wield arcane power to defend their home, though their numbers are dwindling and tensions in the settlement are rising.",al={"what is high order","high order lore"}},{q="Why is there conflict between the High Order and the Windshapers?",a="The High Order and the Windshapers are bitter rivals in Valanaar. Local leaders urge them to keep apart and prevent violence in the streets, especially with cult activity threatening the settlement.",al={"why do high order and windshapers fight","windshapers conflict","valanaar tensions"}},{q="Who is Rathiril?",a="Rathiril directs promising newcomers to the High Order in Valanaar through magical missives. These messages alert Elaadrin Evengale to people of notable potential.",al={"who is rathiril","rathiril missive"}},{q="What cult threatens Valanaar?",a="A mysterious cult active around Valanaar threatens the settlement. Local authorities work to quell conflict between groups such as the High Order and the Windshapers to keep Valanaar secure.",al={"what cult in valanaar","cult threat valanaar"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you carry an arcane aura that Elaadrin recognizes. He values the rare aptitude and resolve needed to master the arcane and preserve your homeland."},kw={"arcane","cult","elaadrin evengale","high order","rathiril","the supreme magister","valanaar","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92596","quest:92595"},m={id=92699},src={"In-game quest text: The Supreme Magister"},fv=true}
E["quest:92700"] = {t="quest",n="The Grand Skyseer",z="general",s="The Grand Skyseer is an introductory journey into the heart of Valanaar, directing travelers to seek an audience with Ayessa Dawnsinger at the Windshaper lodge. As tensions simmer between local factions and a shadowy cult threatens the region, the Windshapers urgently seek individuals gifted with spiritual sight.",h="The Grand Skyseer is an introductory journey into the heart of Valanaar, directing travelers to seek an...",sec={{t="Arrival in Valanaar",b="Upon arriving in the town of Valanaar, travelers are directed toward the central lodge where the Windshapers convene under the leadership of Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger. Having caught the attention of Elder Amberwind through notable deeds in Shen'dar Village, new arrivals are welcomed into the fold of those attuned to the spirits. Yet Valanaar remains fraught with external and internal dangers, requiring newcomers to navigate fragile local politics carefully.",sp=0},{t="Rivalries and Looming Cults",b="Peace in Valanaar is fragile. The Windshapers maintain an open hostility with the High Order, and street skirmishes between the factions threaten to destabilize the settlement. With a shadowy cult actively menacing the region, local leaders have mandated that both factions keep their distance, prioritizing survival against common adversaries over bitter territorial feuds.",sp=0},{t="The Fading Skysight",b="Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger observes that the spiritual perception known as skysight is becoming increasingly rare among the people of Zephras. Those who arrive displaying a strong aura and spiritual harmony are treated as essential allies, urgently needed to help the Windshapers steer through the trying times confronting their land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Grand Skyseer?",a="The Grand Skyseer is Ayessa Dawnsinger, a prominent spiritual leader among the Windshapers in Valanaar. She operates from the lodge situated near the settlement's center, receiving travelers who display a resonance with the spirits and possess the rare gift known as skysight.",al={"who is grand skyseer","ayessa dawnsinger who is she","who is ayessa"}},{q="Where can Ayessa Dawnsinger be found?",a="Ayessa Dawnsinger can be found residing in the Windshaper lodge located near the center of the settlement of Valanaar. She awaits travelers arriving from Shen'dar Village who carry word of recent deeds from Elder Amberwind.",al={"where find ayessa dawnsinger","where is grand skyseer lodge","ayessa location valanaar"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a spiritual collective present in Valanaar, led locally by Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger. Their members study the skies and commune with the spirits, though they currently find themselves in sharp tension with another local group known as the High Order.",al={"what are windshapers","windshaper faction valanaar"}},{q="Why do the Windshapers clash with the High Order?",a="The two orders are split over the wind spirits. The High Order has grown wary of the spirits' motives and wants to understand the powers of Skywall for itself, while the Windshapers trust the elementals and see such questioning as an affront. The friction runs deep enough that faction violence in the streets of Valanaar will not be tolerated, especially with the cult threatening the region.",al={"windshapers high order conflict","why high order mad at windshapers","high order valanaar"}},{q="What is the gift of skysight?",a="Skysight is a rare spiritual perception possessed by few individuals in Zephras, allowing them to sense spiritual auras and commune with unseen forces. Ayessa Dawnsinger notes that fewer people possess this gift lately, making those with skysight vital for enduring current hardships.",al={"what is skysight","skysight gift zephras"}},{q="Who is Elder Amberwind?",a="Elder Amberwind is a respected elder stationed in Shen'dar Village who evaluates travelers and sends word ahead to Valanaar regarding their deeds and spiritual resonance, directing worthy individuals to Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger.",al={"who is elder amberwind","amberwind shendar village"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Ayessa Dawnsinger immediately recognizes travelers who resonate with the spirits and possess spiritual gifts, making the Windshapers an important contact for shamans attuned to elemental forces.",["class:Druid"]="Those sensitive to spiritual auras and nature's unseen flow will find kindred philosophies among the Windshapers, who watch the subtle shifts in the skies."},kw={"ayessa dawnsinger","elder amberwind","high order","shen'dar village","skysight","the grand skyseer","valanaar","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","quest:92595"},m={id=92700},src={"In-game quest text: The Grand Skyseer"},fv=true}
E["quest:92701"] = {t="quest",n="To Valanaar",z="general",s="At Valanaar, Valennia Stormfist must face the cult's growing threat. Aonda sends urgent news from Shen'dar, where the village and its pylon were defended.",h="At Valanaar, Valennia Stormfist must face the cult's growing threat.",sec={{t="Dispatch from Shen'dar",b="After Shen'dar and its pylon were defended, Aonda sends a trusted messenger across the island. The message is bound for Valanaar on the island's southeastern edge. There, the commander of the peacekeepers must hear how quickly the cult is advancing.",sp=0},{t="The Gathering Storm",b="Valennia Stormfist and her peacekeepers have long tried to avoid open conflict with the cult spreading across the island. Yet the events at Shen'dar mark a change. The cult's influence is growing, and Valennia can no longer hold back from a clash. Her dwindling force must ready Valanaar for an assault.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist commands the peacekeepers stationed at Valanaar. She is charged with protecting the island's people, while her small garrison faces growing cult activity.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","valennia","leader of peacekeepers"}},{q="Who is Aonda?",a="Aonda is a highlander stationed in Shen'dar. After securing a pylon and defending the village from cultists, she entrusts a traveler with her written report for Valennia.",al={"who sent the report","crafty highlander","aonda"}},{q="What is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar lies at the southeastern edge of the island. It is the base of Valennia Stormfist and the peacekeepers who defend the island's people.",al={"what is valanaar","where is valanaar","valanaar location"}},{q="What is the cult threatening the island?",a="A rapidly growing cult is spreading across the island and pressing into settlements and sacred pylons. The peacekeepers once avoided direct clashes, but now Valanaar's defenders must prepare to fight.",al={"what cult","who is attacking","cult expansion"}},{q="What is Shen'dar?",a="Shen'dar is a village near a vital pylon on the island. Its people recently repelled cultists to protect the village and pylon, and its leaders keep close ties with the peacekeepers at Valanaar.",al={"what is shendar","shen'dar","pylon village"}},{q="What is contained in Aonda's report?",a="Aonda's written report tells of the defense of Shen'dar and its pylon. It warns Valennia that the cult is spreading too quickly for direct conflict to be avoided.",al={"what is in aondas report","written report contents","what does letter say"}}},kw={"aonda","cult","peacekeepers","pylon","shen'dar","to valanaar","valanaar","valennia stormfist"},m={id=92701},src={"In-game quest text: To Valanaar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92703"] = {t="quest",n="Deliver the News",z="general",s="Deliver the News is a quest concluding the plight of the Windfield family orchard in Skywall. After helping Aamelia secure the farm, the adventurer travels to Valanaar to deliver tidings of their safety to an anxious Alvarion Windfield.",h="Deliver the News is a quest concluding the plight of the Windfield family orchard in Skywall.",sec={{t="Relief at the Orchard",b="Deliver the News marks the resolution of an ordeal at the Windfield family orchard. Having stepped in to save the farm from an impending crisis, the adventurer receives heartfelt thanks from Aamelia, who is ready to put the danger behind them. With the orchard secured, the immediate priority shifts to reassuring Alvarion Windfield, who fled to the settlement of Valanaar to plead for assistance.",sp=0},{t="A Secret for the Future",b="Before sending the traveler off to Valanaar, Aamelia asks that Alvarion recruit a skilled windshaper upon his recovery. A construct remains at the orchard, and she wants an expert to examine it thoroughly to guarantee it poses no threat. She also shares a tender secret: she is expecting a child, a revelation she wishes to hold back from Alvarion until he returns home safely in person.",sp=1},{t="A Hero in Valanaar",b="In Valanaar, Alvarion Windfield is overcome with emotion upon learning that his family and home are safe. Having left for the city believing his search for help was a desperate, almost vain hope, the traveler's success evokes for him the legendary heroes remembered from before his people arrived in Skywall. With their home saved, the Windfield family extends an enduring welcome to their savior.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Deliver the News?",a="Deliver the News is a quest given by Aamelia after resolving a crisis at the Windfield orchard. She asks the adventurer to travel to the city of Valanaar to inform her partner, Alvarion Windfield, that the danger has passed and that she is safe.",al={"deliver the news quest","what is deliver the news","who gives deliver the news"}},{q="Who is Alvarion Windfield?",a="Alvarion Windfield is a Skywall resident currently recovering in Valanaar. He left for the city in search of aid for the trouble back at his family's orchard, fearing all hope was lost before encountering the player.",al={"who is alvarion windfield","where is alvarion","alvarion location"}},{q="Who is Aamelia?",a="Aamelia is a resident of the Windfield homestead who tends to the orchard alongside Alvarion. Grateful for the player's intervention, she tasks them with bringing word of their success to Alvarion in Valanaar.",al={"who is aamelia","quest giver aamelia"}},{q="What does Aamelia keep secret from Alvarion?",a="Aamelia reveals quietly to the adventurer that she is expecting a child, gently touching her belly and asking to keep the news secret from Alvarion until he is back on his feet and returns home.",al={"what is aamelias secret","aamelia touches belly","is aamelia pregnant"}},{q="Why does Aamelia want a windshaper brought to the orchard?",a="Aamelia wants Alvarion to bring a windshaper back to the orchard once he has recovered. She wants the specialist to inspect a construct on the property to ensure it poses no danger to their growing family.",al={"why windshaper construct","what construct does aamelia mention","windfield construct"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The quest connects the Windfield orchard with the settlement of Valanaar, located within the elemental realm of Skywall where these settlers have made their home.",al={"where does deliver the news take place","valanaar skywall quest"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="Alvarion speaks of real heroes from stories before the people arrived in Skywall, reminding Skyborne adventurers of their roots and cultural heritage before their migration."},kw={"aamelia","alvarion windfield","construct","deliver the news","orchard","skywall","valanaar","windfield","windshaper"},rel={"quest:92579","topic:skywall"},m={id=92703},src={"In-game quest text: Deliver the News"},fv=true}
E["quest:92708"] = {t="quest",n="A Grand Adventure",z="general",s="A Grand Adventure marks a turning point for the crisis on Zephras Isle within Skywall. Led by Ayessa Dawnsinger and the Windshapers, it outlines a bold plan to seek salvation for their home by journeying down to the mortal realm of Azeroth.",h="A Grand Adventure marks a turning point for the crisis on Zephras Isle within Skywall.",sec={{t="The Crisis on Zephras Isle",b="Zephras Isle faces an escalating crisis as wind spirits have vanished without explanation. In response, the Windshapers have been investigating these disturbances, uncovering unexpected revelations about their missing kin and the fate of their elemental home. Ayessa Dawnsinger seeks capable adventurers to heed their findings and prepare for an unprecedented undertaking.",sp=0},{t="A Venture Beyond Skywall",b="Upon confiding her discoveries, Ayessa Dawnsinger reveals a bold and unconventional conclusion: Skywall alone does not hold the power or knowledge required to save Zephras Isle. Instead, salvation lies below in the mortal world of Azeroth. Venturing beyond their homeland represents a daring departure from tradition, asking adventurers to step into the unknown to secure the island's future.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest A Grand Adventure?",a="A Grand Adventure is a pivotal quest given by Ayessa Dawnsinger on Zephras Isle. In it, she shares the Windshapers' conclusion that saving their home requires venturing outside Skywall into the world of Azeroth.",al={"what is this quest","grand adventure quest","what is a grand adventure"}},{q="Who is Ayessa Dawnsinger?",a="Ayessa Dawnsinger is a key member of the Windshapers on Zephras Isle who investigates the crisis facing the island and proposes a plan to seek help and power from Azeroth.",al={"who is ayessa","dawnsinger","who is ayessa dawnsinger"}},{q="What crisis is taking place on Zephras Isle?",a="Zephras Isle is suffering from a major crisis involving missing wind spirits. The Windshapers have been investigating their disappearance to find a way to save their home.",al={"what is zephras isle crisis","what happened on zephras isle","zephras problem"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a group working on Zephras Isle dedicated to investigating and resolving the elemental disturbances and missing wind spirits threatening the island.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshapers zephras"}},{q="Why do the Windshapers need to travel to Azeroth?",a="Ayessa Dawnsinger believes that the knowledge, untapped potential, and power needed to resolve the crisis on Zephras Isle cannot be found within Skywall alone, but must be sought in the world below on Azeroth.",al={"why go to azeroth","why leave skywall","path to salvation"}},{q="What is Skywall?",a="Skywall is the elemental home realm of the Windshapers and Zephras Isle, situated above the mortal world of Azeroth.",al={"what is skywall","where is skywall"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="For the Skyborne of Skywall, this represents a monumental shift in perspective: leaving the elemental realm of their home to venture down to Azeroth in search of salvation for Zephras Isle."},kw={"a grand adventure","ayessa dawnsinger","azeroth","skywall","wind spirits","windshapers","zephras isle"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","zone:zephras","quest:92595","topic:skywall"},m={id=92708},src={"In-game quest text: A Grand Adventure"},fv=true}
E["quest:92709"] = {t="quest",n="A Grand Adventure",z="general",s="In Valanaar, Elaadrin Evengale asks you to hear the High Order’s plans. Their search for ancient magic may shape Zephras Isle’s future.",h="In Valanaar, Elaadrin Evengale asks you to hear the High Order’s plans.",sec={{t="A Lost Heritage",b="Elaadrin speaks of your Highborne lineage and the young people who may help save their home. The High Order seeks the ancient magic of its forebears, and what has been lost over many millennia.",sp=0},{t="The Way Ahead",b="Elaadrin says Azeroth is key to the search. The Kirin Tor will show the High Order the way. As its members prepare to leave, you can speak with others in Valanaar. Many need help with their work.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to listen?",a="Elaadrin Evengale in Valanaar asks you to hear the High Order’s plans.",al={"who is Elaadrin","Elaadrin Evengale","who wants to talk"}},{q="What is the High Order trying to rediscover?",a="The High Order seeks its forebears’ ancient magic and what was lost over many millennia.",al={"what are they looking for","ancient magic","high order plans"}},{q="Why does the High Order seek ancient magic?",a="Elaadrin says rediscovering ancient magic is key to unlocking Zephras Isle’s secrets and securing its future.",al={"why ancient magic","save Zephras","isle future"}},{q="Who will show them the way?",a="Elaadrin says the Kirin Tor will show the High Order the way.",al={"Kirin Tor","who guides them","azeroth plan"}},{q="What can I do while they prepare to leave?",a="While they prepare to leave, you can speak with other members of the High Order in Valanaar. Most need help with their work.",al={"help in Valanaar","other High Order members","what next"}}},kw={"a grand adventure","ancient magic","elaadrin evengale","high order","highborne","kirin tor","valanaar","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","topic:highborne","quest:92596"},m={id=92709},src={"In-game quest text: A Grand Adventure"},fv=true}
E["quest:92727"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Scholar",z="general",s="Fillion Flamebreeze is missing after setting out to study atmospheric magic on Zephras. The High Order sent the scholar toward the Shen'dar Highlands, and concern grows as the road from Valanaar yields no word of his return.",h="Fillion Flamebreeze is missing after setting out to study atmospheric magic on Zephras.",sec={{t="The High Order's Mission",b="The High Order gathers and preserves knowledge across Zephras. Yet field research on the island has grown perilous. Fillion Flamebreeze set out for the Shen'dar Highlands to study atmospheric arcane phenomena, but never reported back. His peers now fear the dangers along the frontier roads have claimed him.",sp=0},{t="Traces on the Western Road",b="Fillion Flamebreeze left Valanaar and traveled west toward the Shen'dar Highlands. There, he meant to study windborne magic pooling among the peaks. He vanished before completing the journey. Along the route, a search finds only his personal satchel in the dirt. Dried blood stains it, and his survival is in grave doubt.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Fillion Flamebreeze?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze is a scholar of the High Order on Zephras. He set out to study windborne magic in the Shen'dar Highlands, then disappeared along the road.",al={"who is fillion flamebreeze","fillion flamebreeze lore","who is the missing scholar"}},{q="What happened to Fillion Flamebreeze?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze never reached his destination. His discarded satchel lies along the western road toward the Shen'dar Highlands, marked with his embroidered name and specks of dried blood that suggest an attack.",al={"what happened to fillion","did fillion die","fillion flamebreeze fate"}},{q="What is the High Order?",a="The High Order gathers and preserves knowledge across Zephras. Its work has grown more perilous amid wild magical phenomena and threats along the roads.",al={"high order mandate","what does high order do","high order zephras"}},{q="Why was Fillion travelling to the Shen'dar Highlands?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze traveled to the Shen'dar Highlands to record unusual windborne magic pooling and flowing across parts of the highlands.",al={"where was fillion going","wind magic shendar","why did he go to the highlands"}},{q="Where was Fillion Flamebreeze last seen?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze's bloodstained satchel lies along the westward road from Valanaar as it climbs toward the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"where is the satchel","fillion satchel location","road from valanaar"}},{q="What is the windborne magic of the Shen'dar Highlands?",a="Windborne magic is made up of atmospheric arcane currents that gather naturally in certain high places on Zephras. The Shen'dar Highlands are a chief subject of arcane research.",al={"what is windborne magic zephras","wind magic highlands"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, you share Flamebreeze's curiosity about the windborne arcane currents gathering in Zephras's highlands.",["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you know Zephras's winds and atmospheric magic hold great interest. Hidden dangers make the terrain perilous even for scholars."},kw={"bloodstained satchel","fillion flamebreeze","high order","shen'dar highlands","the missing scholar","valanaar","windborne magic","zephras"},rel={"quest:92596"},m={id=92727},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Scholar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92741"] = {t="quest",n="Unwelcome Visitors",z="general",s="In Shadowgale Forest, shrieklings are pressing toward the woods' edge. Iaadaria Bitterwind in Valanaar asks travelers to gather their talons for the High Order.",h="In Shadowgale Forest, shrieklings are pressing toward the woods' edge.",sec={{t="The Shriekling Surge",b="Shadowgale Forest is a dangerous woodland that ordinary adventurers are forbidden to enter. Now its shrieklings have multiplied in numbers never seen before. They are pressing toward the forest's edge and extending their hunting grounds beyond the canopy. If they keep spreading, settled lands and travel routes will face a growing danger.",sp=0},{t="Scholarly Culling",b="In Valanaar, Iaadaria Bitterwind of the High Order sees both danger and opportunity in the shrieklings' growing numbers. The Order usually leaves pest control and trapping to others. Yet shriekling talons have rare alchemical properties, making an exception worthwhile. Travelers are asked to enter Shadowgale Forest and bring back eight talons. The High Order can then help protect the region and study the reagents.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Unwelcome Visitors?",a="Iaadaria Bitterwind gives the request in Valanaar. She is a researcher with the High Order and asks travelers to hunt shrieklings nearby, gathering materials for her alchemical studies.",al={"who gives unwelcome visitors","unwelcome visitors quest giver","where to pick up unwelcome visitors"}},{q="What is the High Order's interest in the shrieklings?",a="The High Order is interested in the shrieklings' talons, which have distinct alchemical properties. Though the Order usually leaves trapping and pest control to others, Iaadaria Bitterwind sees a chance to study valuable reagents while helping protect the region.",al={"why does high order want talons","high order shrieklings","iaadaria bitterwind motive"}},{q="Why must the shriekling population be culled?",a="The shrieklings in Shadowgale Forest have multiplied greatly in recent months. They are moving beyond their usual woodland territory, threatening neighboring areas if their spread continues.",al={"why kill shrieklings","shriekling threat","why are shrieklings a problem"}},{q="Where is Shadowgale Forest located?",a="Shadowgale Forest lies near Valanaar. Its dangerous woods are usually forbidden to ordinary travelers and inexperienced adventurers because of the violent wildlife within.",al={"where is shadowgale forest","shadowgale forest location","how to get to shadowgale forest"}},{q="What are shrieklings?",a="Shrieklings are vicious predatory beasts native to Shadowgale Forest. Their talons have alchemical properties, and in recent months their numbers have grown so greatly that they are pushing beyond the forest's edge.",al={"what is a shriekling","shriekling lore","what kind of beasts are shrieklings"}},{q="What items are required to complete Unwelcome Visitors?",a="Travelers must bring eight Shriekling Talons from Shadowgale Forest to Iaadaria Bitterwind in Valanaar. The talons will aid her scholarly and alchemical research.",al={"unwelcome visitors objectives","what do i need for unwelcome visitors","shriekling talons needed"}},{q="Why is Shadowgale Forest off-limits to normal adventurers?",a="Shadowgale Forest is forbidden to ordinary adventurers because its wildlife is deadly and the woods are treacherous. Those who enter, including travelers asked to cull the shrieklings, must be ready for severe danger.",al={"why is shadowgale off limits","shadowgale forest danger","can anyone enter shadowgale forest"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you can track and thin the overpopulated shrieklings in Shadowgale Forest. You take up the work of wardens and trappers, a duty the High Order disdains.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you can see the shrieklings' sudden growth and spread as a disturbance in Shadowgale Forest. Swift action is needed to restore the forest's natural balance."},kw={"alchemical properties","iaadaria bitterwind","shadowgale forest","shriekling","shriekling talons","the high order","unwelcome visitors","valanaar"},rel={"quest:92596"},m={id=92741},src={"In-game quest text: Unwelcome Visitors"},fv=true}
E["quest:92834"] = {t="quest",n="Avenged Tenfold",z="general",s="Elaadrin turns from diplomacy to retribution after the Al'Aketh cult answers his appeal with violence. The High Order scholar had sent his dear friend Belathaan Brightwish to seek peace at the Shrine of Akir, north of Valanaar.",h="Elaadrin turns from diplomacy to retribution after the Al'Aketh cult answers his appeal with violence.",sec={{t="The Diplomatic Overture",b="The Al'Aketh cult's violence brought armed posturing from factions like the Peacekeepers and Windshapers. Elaadrin spoke for the High Order and saw such displays as a foolish escalation. His order prizes pragmatism and avoids conflict, so he still believed reasoned dialogue could work. He sent his close friend, Belathaan Brightwish, to the Shrine of Akir to negotiate peace.",sp=0},{t="A Ghoulish Rejection",b="The Al'Aketh cult answered with a package bearing Belathaan's severed tongue, coated in silver pigment. Elaadrin was horrified and burdened by guilt. He saw that taking the high road had cost his dear friend's life, and admitted the Windshapers were right to reject negotiations. He abandoned diplomacy and called on travelers to hunt the cultists across the Gustberry Fields and the Shrine of Akir, collecting their Al'Aketh Windstone Charms.",sp=1},{t="Prudence and Arcane Wrath",b="The High Order puts logic before sentiment and holds that avoiding bloodshed is usually the prudent path. Yet Elaadrin shows how cold logic cuts both ways when foes mistake restraint for helplessness. Crackling arcane energy gathers around him. The High Order favors peace, but those who butcher its emissaries face overwhelming retribution.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elaadrin?",a="Elaadrin represents the High Order and first sought peace with the Al'Aketh cult. After they murdered his envoy and close friend Belathaan Brightwish, he abandoned diplomacy and ordered the cultists destroyed.",al={"who is elaadrin","quest giver avenged tenfold","who gives this quest"}},{q="What happened to Belathaan Brightwish?",a="Belathaan Brightwish was Elaadrin's close friend and envoy to the Shrine of Akir, sent to open peaceful dialogue with the Al'Aketh cult. The cult murdered him and returned a box to Elaadrin containing his severed tongue, painted with silver pigment.",al={"what happened to belathaan","who is belathaan brightwish","why did belathaan die"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh cultists?",a="The Al'Aketh are a violent cult active north of Valanaar, around the Gustberry Fields and Shrine of Akir. They carry prayer charms called Al'Aketh Windstone Charms and reject peaceful overtures from outsiders with violence.",al={"who are alaketh","who are the cultists","al'aketh cult"}},{q="What is the High Order?",a="The High Order prizes pragmatism, intellect, and logic. Its members generally favor avoiding conflict and preserving life, but can answer a grave offense with fierce magical retribution.",al={"what is the high order","high order beliefs"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers and Peacekeepers are factions that first answered the Al'Aketh cult's violence with forceful displays and posturing. Elaadrin called their response reckless, but later conceded they were right to dismiss diplomatic appeals.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshapers peacekeepers"}},{q="Where are the Al'Aketh cultists found?",a="The Al'Aketh cultists gather north of Valanaar, in the Gustberry Fields and around the Shrine of Akir. Belathaan Brightwish was sent to the Shrine before his death.",al={"where is shrine of akir","where are gustberry fields","where are the cultists"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you can see Elaadrin channel crackling arcane power when provoked. The High Order's devotion to logic rests alongside formidable magical force."},kw={"al'aketh","avenged tenfold","belathaan brightwish","elaadrin","gustberry fields","high order","shrine of akir","windshapers","windstone charms"},rel={"quest:92596","quest:92595"},m={id=92834},src={"In-game quest text: Avenged Tenfold"},fv=true}
E["quest:92840"] = {t="quest",n="Catching Wind",z="general",s="At the Overlook Standing Stones, the High Order seeks readings that may help save Zephras. Its expedition faces fierce opposition from the Windshapers in the Shen'dar Highlands.",h="At the Overlook Standing Stones, the High Order seeks readings that may help save Zephras.",sec={{t="Measuring the Gales",b="The High Order studies the wild elemental flows of the Shen'dar Highlands in hopes of saving Zephras. At the Overlook Standing Stones, its researchers use an Index Esoteria, a tool made to draw data from the great windstone formations. The Windshapers see these probes as profane trespass against the wind.",sp=0},{t="Clash of Dogma and Inquiry",b="The High Order's expedition and the Windshapers soon clash at the stones. The Windshapers call the study and taking of elemental energy a desecration of their sacred ways. The High Order sees them as narrow in their beliefs, much like the violent Al'Aketh. It will not let their resistance halt its work to save the realm.",sp=0},{t="The Standing Stone Massacre",b="At the Overlook Standing Stones, the High Order meets hostile resistance, and the clash leaves blood among the windstones. The High Order secures its readings with the Index Esoteria. The Windshapers call the fighting a massacre, and the feud casts a shadow over Valanaar.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest Catching Wind about?",a="The High Order sends an operative to study elemental energy at the Overlook Standing Stones in the Shen'dar Highlands. The operative carries an Index Esoteria to gather data from the windstones despite opposition from the Windshapers.",al={"what is catching wind","catching wind quest"}},{q="What is an Index Esoteria?",a="The Index Esoteria is a High Order instrument made to extract, measure, and record elemental energy from the great windstone formations at the Overlook Standing Stones.",al={"what is the index esoteria","index esoteria device"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers defend their sacred sites and traditional beliefs about elemental magic. They see the High Order's research and devices as desecration and fight to keep outsiders from the windstones.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshapers lore"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a violent group the High Order compares with the Windshapers. The High Order considers both groups dangerous zealots bound by inflexible beliefs, though it calls the Al'Aketh more violent.",al={"who are al'aketh","al aketh"}},{q="Where are the Overlook Standing Stones located?",a="The Overlook Standing Stones lie in the Shen'dar Highlands. Their great windstone formations channel strong elemental currents, making the site important to the High Order's research.",al={"where are overlook standing stones","overlook standing stones location"}},{q="Who is Fillion?",a="Fillion is a High Order operative or researcher sent to study elemental energy at the Overlook Standing Stones. After confrontation with the Windshapers, the High Order sent an adventurer with the Index Esoteria to secure the data.",al={"what happened to fillion","who is fillion"}},{q="Why does the High Order need the windstone data?",a="The High Order believes knowledge of the realm's elemental forces is vital to saving Zephras. It sees research as necessary to overcome threats that traditional beliefs cannot resolve.",al={"why does high order want data","saving zephras"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you may feel torn between the Windshapers' reverence for elemental currents and the High Order's measured taking of elemental power.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you may value the Index Esoteria's careful measurements and see elemental forces as a field of study."},kw={"al'aketh","catching wind","fillion","high order","index esoteria","overlook standing stones","shen'dar highlands","valanaar","windshapers","windstone formations","zephras"},rel={"quest:92596","quest:92595"},m={id=92840},src={"In-game quest text: Catching Wind"},fv=true}
E["quest:92849"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Scholar",z="general",s="You follow the trail of Fillion Flamebreeze, missing after an assignment at the Overlook Standing Stones. The search leads north to a cave, where he was dragged by a winged beast.",h="You follow the trail of Fillion Flamebreeze, missing after an assignment at the Overlook Standing Stones.",sec={{t="The Vanished Researcher",b="You find signs of trouble along the trail Fillion Flamebreeze took on his travels. They point to foul play. Following them leads north to a dark cave. The missing scholar may still be alive inside, waiting for help.",sp=0},{t="Ambush by the Windshapers",b="Fillion Flamebreeze was returning from an assignment at the Overlook Standing Stones when Windshapers ambushed him. They battered him, dragged him to the cave entrance, and left him bleeding. Helpless, he faced whatever waited within.",sp=1},{t="Lair of the Winged Beast",b="As Fillion lay fading at the cavern’s threshold, a massive winged beast emerged from the dark and dragged him inside. It carried him deep into its lair as prey for its younglings. Timely rescue spared him that fate.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Fillion Flamebreeze?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze is a scholar who was returning from an assignment at the Overlook Standing Stones when Windshapers ambushed him. Left bleeding and unconscious outside a nearby cave, he was dragged inside by a massive winged predator.",al={"who is fillion","fillion flamebreeze","missing scholar npc"}},{q="What was Fillion doing at the Overlook Standing Stones?",a="The Overlook Standing Stones were the site of Fillion Flamebreeze’s scholarly assignment. His journey back from the stones led to an ambush by Windshapers. The precise nature of his research is unclear.",al={"what are overlook standing stones","overlook stones assignment","fillion mission"}},{q="Who attacked Fillion Flamebreeze?",a="Windshapers ambushed and battered Fillion Flamebreeze. They forced him to the mouth of a nearby cave and left him bleeding and barely conscious.",al={"who attacked fillion","what are windshapers","windshaper ambush"}},{q="What creature took Fillion into the cave?",a="A massive winged beast in a cave north of the ambush site took Fillion from the cave entrance. It dragged him into its lair to feed itself and its hungry younglings.",al={"what cave beast","winged monster fillion","what took fillion"}},{q="Where can Fillion Flamebreeze be found?",a="Clues at the site of Fillion Flamebreeze’s misfortune point to a cave just to the north. Inside, he is trapped in the beast’s nesting grounds and awaits rescue before he is devoured.",al={"how to find fillion","where is the missing scholar","fillion cave location"}},{q="What was the fate of the missing scholar?",a="Fillion survives his ordeal because someone intervenes in the cave in time. Without an adventurer finding and helping him, he would have become dinner for the winged beast and its offspring.",al={"did fillion survive","what happens to fillion flamebreeze","save missing scholar"}}},kw={"fillion","fillion flamebreeze","overlook standing stones","the missing scholar","windshapers","winged beast"},m={id=92849},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Scholar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92850"] = {t="quest",n="The Missing Scholar",z="general",s="Fillion Flamebreeze is stranded near a dangerous den, and Valanaar needs the threat ended. Once he can travel, he asks you to slay the Shriekling Matriarch nearby.",h="Fillion Flamebreeze is stranded near a dangerous den, and Valanaar needs the threat ended.",sec={{t="A Perilous Nesting Ground",b="After his ordeal, Fillion Flamebreeze can travel back toward the safety of Valanaar. Yet a brood of shrieklings has nested close to the settlement. Fillion asks you to enter the Shriekling Den and slay the Shriekling Matriarch before the beasts threaten the town.",sp=0},{t="Reporting to the Magister",b="When you bring the Matriarch’s head to Fillion in Valanaar, he thanks you. Yet the shrieklings are only the least of their troubles. Fillion prepares to report on his mission and the surrounding area to the Supreme Magister.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Fillion Flamebreeze?",a="Fillion Flamebreeze is a scholar skilled in the arcane arts. He was on an assignment near Valanaar before becoming stranded near a nest of dangerous beasts.",al={"who is fillion flamebreeze","who is the scholar","who gives the missing scholar"}},{q="What are shrieklings?",a="Shrieklings are hostile beasts nesting in a nearby den close to Valanaar. Their presence threatens anyone traveling in the area.",al={"what are shrieklings","where do shrieklings nest","shriekling threat"}},{q="Who is the Supreme Magister?",a="The Supreme Magister is Elaadrin Evengale, who leads the High Order in Valanaar. Fillion reports the events at the den and the status of his mission to them.",al={"who is the supreme magister","fillion report","who does fillion answer to"}},{q="What is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is the nearby settlement where Fillion is stationed. He returns there once he has recovered enough to travel.",al={"what is valanaar","where is valanaar"}},{q="What is the Shriekling Matriarch?",a="The Shriekling Matriarch leads the shrieklings nesting in the Shriekling Den. Fillion asks you to slay her and bring back her head to end the immediate danger to Valanaar.",al={"shriekling matriarch target","who to kill in shriekling den"}},{q="Why does Fillion consider the shrieklings the least of their troubles?",a="The shrieklings threaten Valanaar because they nest so close to the settlement. When you return, Fillion says they are only the least of their troubles.",al={"why least of our troubles","greater danger valanaar"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you share Fillion Flamebreeze’s training in the arcane arts. He relies on that training to recover and travel safely."},kw={"fillion flamebreeze","shriekling","shriekling den","shriekling matriarch","supreme magister","the missing scholar","valanaar"},rel={"quest:92699","quest:92579"},m={id=92850},src={"In-game quest text: The Missing Scholar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92860"] = {t="quest",n="In Service of Zephras",z="general",s="The High Order now offers Valanaar its full military and diplomatic support against the Al'Aketh cult. Hostile movements across the island have ended its policy of avoiding direct confrontation.",h="The High Order now offers Valanaar its full military and diplomatic support against the Al'Aketh cult.",sec={{t="The Gathering Threat",b="The High Order once tried to avoid open conflict with the secretive Al'Aketh cult. Reports of large troop movements and clear hostility have ended that effort. The cultists are mobilizing, and every settlement on the island faces a threat to its peace and safety. So the High Order has chosen to act. It sends an emissary to join with local defenders before the Al'Aketh strike.",sp=0},{t="Alliance with Valanaar",b="Valennia Stormfist receives the High Order's offer of unconditional aid for Valanaar's resistance against the Al'Aketh. Together, the two sides can form a united defense and prepare joint military efforts across the region. The emissary also brings word of Belathaan's death. Valennia's cousin was a devoted servant of Zephras. She mourns him and vows vengeance, then turns to preparations for the campaign ahead.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest In Service of Zephras about?",a="The High Order sends an emissary to Valennia Stormfist to pledge support for Valanaar. It had tried to avoid direct violence with the Al'Aketh, but hostility and reports of large troop movements across the island forced a change. The offer brings the High Order into Valanaar's defense and carries news of Belathaan's death.",al={"in service of zephras overview","what happens in service of zephras","in service of zephras quest"}},{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist leads defensive efforts against the Al'Aketh in Valanaar. She receives the High Order's offer of mutual aid. Belathaan, her cousin and a servant of Zephras, has died, and Valennia vows vengeance against those threatening her people.",al={"valennia stormfist lore","who is valennia","valennia stormfist"}},{q="Who was Belathaan?",a="Belathaan was Valennia Stormfist's cousin and a devoted servant of Zephras. His death came before the emissary brought word of it to Valennia. The news deepens the conflict's personal cost and strengthens her vow to avenge him.",al={"who is belathaan","belathaan lore","what happened to belathaan"}},{q="What is the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh is a hostile cult active on the island. Its hostile acts and troop movements threaten the people of Zephras. The High Order's effort to avoid direct conflict has given way to an armed alliance against the cult.",al={"who are the al'aketh","al'aketh cult","what is alaketh"}},{q="What is the High Order's role in this conflict?",a="The High Order first sought to avoid direct war with the Al'Aketh. Their hostile actions and reports of large troop movements convinced its leaders to act. The High Order now sends a courier to Valennia Stormfist, pledging its full strength to Valanaar's resistance.",al={"high order involvement","why is the high order fighting","high order zephras"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is the island, also called Zephras Isle, that the High Order, Valanaar, and its defenders seek to protect. The Al'Aketh's growing movements threaten its people, and Belathaan is remembered as a faithful servant of Zephras. Their safety is why the High Order joins the defense.",al={"where is zephras","who is zephras","zephras lore"}}},kw={"al'aketh","belathaan","cult of al'aketh","high order","in service of zephras","stormfist","valanaar","valennia stormfist","zephras"},rel={"quest:92596"},m={id=92860},src={"In-game quest text: In Service of Zephras"},fv=true}
E["quest:92871"] = {t="quest",n="In Service of Zephras",z="general",s="Across Zephras, the Al'Aketh cult is gathering for a wider conflict. The Windshapers send an emissary to assure Valennia Stormfist that they will aid her peacekeepers.",h="Across Zephras, the Al'Aketh cult is gathering for a wider conflict.",sec={{t="Rumors on the Island",b="Reports of Al'Aketh activity are spreading across Zephras. Valennia Stormfist's peacekeepers have guarded the island for a long while, but the cult now seems to be preparing for a larger conflict. So the Windshapers pledge to stand with Valanaar's forces if open fighting begins.",sp=0},{t="Pledging Support",b="You are sent to Valennia Stormfist to assure her that the Windshapers are ready to aid her peacekeepers. Valennia welcomes their swift pledge. She is ready to speak with you about the cult's worsening threat.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist leads the peacekeepers who protect Zephras. She directs the island's defense, including Valanaar's campaign against the Al'Aketh.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","valennia stormfist"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are allies on Zephras who aid Valennia Stormfist and her peacekeepers. They offer full support to Valanaar's forces against the growing threat of the Al'Aketh.",al={"what are the windshapers","windshapers lore"}},{q="What are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a cult active across Zephras. Reports say they are making larger moves and preparing for conflict.",al={"who are alaketh","what is alaketh","al aketh cult"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is an island defended by Valennia Stormfist and her peacekeepers. The Windshapers aid them against the Al'Aketh cult.",al={"what is zephras","zephras island"}},{q="What is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is a settlement on Zephras and home to Valennia Stormfist's peacekeepers. The Windshapers fully support its fight against the Al'Aketh.",al={"what is valanaar","who is valanaar"}}},kw={"al'aketh","in service of zephras","valanaar","valennia stormfist","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=92871},src={"In-game quest text: In Service of Zephras"},fv=true}
E["quest:92880"] = {t="quest",n="Return to Valanaar",z="general",s="Yorna's report brings word to Valanaar that the eastern watchtower has held. Its perimeter is stable, and the remaining attackers are being cleared away.",h="Yorna's report brings word to Valanaar that the eastern watchtower has held.",sec={{t="Word from the Outpost",b="After the attack on the outpost, Yorna sends word to Valanaar. The attackers have been broken, and the defenders are clearing away those who remain. You carry Yorna's Report to ease the leaders waiting in town for news.",sp=0},{t="Relief in Valanaar",b="You bring the eastern watchtower's report to Valennia Stormfist in Valanaar. The tower has held, and its perimeter is stable. Valennia gives thanks to the winds, relieved that the frontier remains secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends you to Valanaar?",a="Yorna sends you to Valanaar after the outpost's hard-fought defense. She asks you to carry a report to the settlement's leaders.",al={"who gives return to valanaar","who sends you back to valanaar","yorna quest giver"}},{q="Who do you report to in Valanaar?",a="You report to Valennia Stormfist in Valanaar. She awaits news of the outlying defenders and their safety.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","where is valennia stormfist","hand in return to valanaar"}},{q="What is Yorna's Report?",a="Yorna's Report says the defenders have stabilized their position and are clearing away the attackers who remain.",al={"what is yornas report","what does yorna report say"}},{q="Which watchtower was under attack?",a="Yorna and her forces defended the eastern watchtower, a key position watching the approaches to Valanaar.",al={"what tower was attacked","which watchtower is yorna at"}},{q="What is the status of the eastern watchtower?",a="The defenders have secured the eastern watchtower and stabilized the situation. They are still clearing away attackers who remain.",al={"is the watchtower safe","did the defenders win"}},{q="Why do the defenders speak of the four winds?",a="The defenders of Valanaar honor the four winds and the elements. Their blessing and Valennia's thanks to the winds show that reverence.",al={"four winds meaning","may the four winds bless you"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you share the defenders' and leaders' ties to the four winds, reflected in their blessing."},kw={"eastern watchtower","four winds","return to valanaar","valanaar","valennia stormfist","yorna","yorna's report"},rel={"quest:92579"},m={id=92880},src={"In-game quest text: Return to Valanaar"},fv=true}
E["quest:92881"] = {t="quest",n="The High Elder's Request",z="general",s="In Valanaar, High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong calls for help after coordinated assaults strike the region's watchtowers. Reports from the western watchtower point to a hostile cult targeting the local anchor pylons.",h="In Valanaar, High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong calls for help after coordinated assaults strike the region's...",sec={{t="Trouble at the Watchtowers",b="Coordinated assaults on the western and eastern watchtowers reveal a troubling pattern. What seemed like scattered skirmishes now points to a planned attack on the local anchor pylons. A report from the western watchtower tells how a skypriest revealed the cult's interest in these structures. Now the towers' defense is an urgent concern.",sp=0},{t="Audience in Valanaar",b="The threat to the anchor pylons is growing, so leaders send you to speak with High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong in Valanaar. He has a promising lead on the cult's movements and seeks experienced allies to track the enemy. The cult may be preparing a ritual or sabotage.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is High Elder Shadowsong?",a="High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong is a leader in Valanaar. Coordinated cult activity has struck the region's watchtowers, and he seeks capable allies to pursue leads on the cult's intentions.",al={"who is talaanis shadowsong","high elder shadowsong","where is shadowsong"}},{q="Where is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is where High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong is stationed. He coordinates defenses and investigates cult incursions against local anchor pylons and towers.",al={"what is valanaar","valanaar location"}},{q="Why were the watchtowers attacked?",a="Coordinated attacks struck both the western and eastern watchtowers. Defenders suspect deliberate maneuvers aimed at the nearby anchor pylons, not random raids.",al={"why were watchtowers attacked","western and eastern watchtowers","watchtower attacks"}},{q="What are the anchor pylons?",a="Anchor pylons are important defensive or regional structures. Reports from the western watchtower suggest a hostile cult means to interfere with them or subvert them.",al={"what are anchor pylons","cult anchor pylons","why anchor pylons"}},{q="Who was the skypriest at the western watchtower?",a="A skypriest was involved in the incident at the western watchtower. Reports from that encounter led leaders to suspect the cult has a broader scheme involving the anchor pylons.",al={"who was the skypriest","western watchtower skypriest","skypriest role"}},{q="What cult is targeting the watchtowers?",a="A hostile cult carried out simultaneous strikes on the region's watchtowers. Its nature and motives remain under investigation as High Elder Shadowsong works to uncover its plans.",al={"what cult is this","who attacked the towers"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="For priests studying divine or ritual manipulation, the cult's interest in anchor pylons and the skypriest's involvement at the western watchtower are troubling signs."},kw={"anchor pylons","eastern watchtower","shadowsong","skypriest","talaanis shadowsong","the high elder's request","valanaar","western watchtower"},rel={"quest:93926"},m={id=92881},src={"In-game quest text: The High Elder's Request"},fv=true}
E["quest:92909"] = {t="quest",n="Harvesting the Harvesters",z="general",s="Harvesting the Harvesters is a local quest in Westfall where players assist Ozwin Ironsprocket, a visiting gnome tinkerer trapped inside a stable at Saldean's Farm. After underestimating the ferocity of the region's haywire harvest golems, Ozwin requires salvaged mechanical components to construct an escape contraption.",h="Harvesting the Harvesters is a local quest in Westfall where players assist Ozwin Ironsprocket, a visiting...",sec={{t="A Miscalculated Endeavor",b="The agricultural flatlands of Westfall have descended into chaos, overrun not only by outlaws but by rampaging mechanical harvesters. Word of these haywire automatons reached the technological workshops of Ironforge, inspiring the gnomish engineer Ozwin Ironsprocket to journey south. Confident in his technical acumen, Ozwin believed he could diagnose and recalibrate the wayward farm machinery to restore peace to the region's beleaguered farmlands.",sp=0},{t="Pinned in the Stable",b="Upon arriving in the fields surrounding Saldean's Farm, Ozwin quickly discovered that the harvest golems were far more volatile and violent than theoretical schematics suggested. The razor-sharp blades and erratic swings of the machines sent the gnome fleeing for his life into a nearby stable. Trapped and unable to venture out into the fields, Ozwin relies on daring adventurers to salvage salvageable isosprings and gyrostabilizers from the mechanical terror so he can assemble a device to safely escape.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ozwin Ironsprocket?",a="Ozwin Ironsprocket is a gnome tinkerer who traveled from Ironforge to Westfall with the ambitious goal of recalibrating the rampaging harvest golems. However, their extreme hostility forced him to take refuge inside a stable at Saldean's Farm.",al={"who is ozwin ironsprocket","ozwin","quest giver"}},{q="Why are the harvest golems so aggressive?",a="The mechanical harvest golems wandering Westfall's abandoned fields have become wildly aggressive and hostile to passersby. While Ozwin hoped their malfunction could be fixed with routine recalibration, their ferocity proved far worse than his calculations predicted.",al={"why are harvesters attacking","why are golems aggressive","what is wrong with harvesters"}},{q="Where can Ozwin Ironsprocket be found?",a="Ozwin Ironsprocket is hiding inside a stable at Saldean's Farm in Westfall, taking cover from the dangerous harvest golems prowling nearby fields.",al={"where is ozwin ironsprocket","where to turn in harvesting the harvesters","ozwin location"}},{q="What does Ozwin intend to build with the collected parts?",a="Ozwin needs golem isosprings and harvester gyrostabilizers to construct an emergency mechanical contraption that will provide enough cover for him to safely flee the stable.",al={"what does ozwin need parts for","why collect isosprings","escape device parts"}},{q="Why did Ozwin travel from Ironforge to Westfall?",a="Drawn by news of rogue machinery terrorizing the Westfall countryside, Ozwin journeyed all the way from the dwarven capital of Ironforge believing his tinkering expertise could successfully recalibrate the rogue automatons.",al={"why did ozwin come to westfall","why leave ironforge"}},{q="Where do the required golem parts come from?",a="The isosprings and gyrostabilizers are salvaged directly from the hostile harvest golems and harvesters prowling the agricultural fields of Westfall around Saldean's Farm.",al={"where do parts drop","how to get isosprings and gyrostabilizers"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Ozwin Ironsprocket exemplifies the quintessential gnomish ambition to tinker with complex machinery, even if underestimating mechanical aggression led to getting cornered in a farm stable.",["profession:Engineering"]="Harvesting complex parts like isosprings and gyrostabilizers from rogue harvest golems provides the critical raw components needed to invent a functional escape device.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance travelers visiting Saldean's Farm can aid a visiting gnome scholar whose miscalculated attempt to fix Westfall's mechanical plague left him stranded."},kw={"golem isosprings","harvest golems","harvester gyrostabilizers","harvesting the harvesters","ironforge","ozwin ironsprocket","saldean's farm","westfall"},rel={"subzone:saldean-s-farm","zone:westfall","quest:897","zone:ironforge"},m={id=92909},src={"In-game quest text: Harvesting the Harvesters"},fv=true}
E["quest:92947"] = {t="quest",n="Making Our Move",z="general",s="At the Shrine of Akir, a strike force moves against the Al'Aketh and their High Priestess. A frontal charge will draw the defenders while the main force approaches from the flank.",h="At the Shrine of Akir, a strike force moves against the Al'Aketh and their High Priestess.",sec={{t="A Coordinated Pincer",b="At the Shrine of Akir, the assault depends on timing and divided forces. A small band of elite fighters charges the front gates to raise the alarm and draw out the defenders. Meanwhile, the commander leads the main strike team in from the flank. Their push divides the defenders' attention and opens a path toward the shrine's leadership.",sp=0},{t="Breaking the Al'Aketh Lines",b="The Al'Aketh defend the Shrine of Akir with armored Guardians, mystical Spiritcallers and martial Blademasters. The strike force must break through their front lines to reach the inner sanctum, where the High Priestess directs her followers. Once the outer defenders are beaten, you meet the force's second-in-command, Hyusaa Quickbreeze, at the shrine steps.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the objective of the assault in Making Our Move?",a="The strike force aims to breach the Shrine of Akir and reach the High Priestess. Fighters charge the front to draw the defenders while the main team flanks their position.",al={"making our move","quest making our move","shrine of akir quest"}},{q="Who is Hyusaa Quickbreeze?",a="Hyusaa Quickbreeze is the strike force's second-in-command. After you thin the Al'Aketh defenders at the shrine, you are to meet Quickbreeze near its steps.",al={"who is hyusaa","hyusaa quickbreeze","where is hyusaa"}},{q="Who is the High Priestess targeted in the attack?",a="The High Priestess commands the Al'Aketh within the Shrine of Akir. The strike force flanks the enemy lines to reach and defeat her.",al={"who is high priestess akir","high priestess alaketh","target high priestess"}},{q="What is the Shrine of Akir?",a="The Shrine of Akir is a fortified religious site held by the Al'Aketh. Guardians, Spiritcallers and Blademasters defend its central sanctuary, where their leadership resides.",al={"what is shrine of akir","shrine of akir lore","where is shrine of akir"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh forces holding the shrine?",a="The Al'Aketh are the hostile defenders of the Shrine of Akir. Their ranks include frontline Guardians, spellcasting Spiritcallers and agile Blademasters who guard the way to their High Priestess.",al={"who are alaketh","al'aketh defenders","alaketh mobs"}},{q="Why is a frontal distraction used in the assault?",a="A handful of seasoned fighters enters through the shrine's front entrance to draw the defenders and cause chaos. This gives the main force a chance to flank the compound and push toward the inner shrine.",al={"why diversion","shrine attack plan","attack strategy making our move"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="The assault targets the Al'Aketh leadership at the Shrine of Akir, including the hostile High Priestess and her Spiritcallers."},kw={"al'aketh","al'aketh blademaster","al'aketh guardian","al'aketh spiritcaller","high priestess","hyusaa quickbreeze","making our move","shrine of akir"},m={id=92947},src={"In-game quest text: Making Our Move"},fv=true}
E["quest:93036"] = {t="quest",n="Infiltrating the Cult",z="general",s="Infiltrating the Cult is a quest in Shen'dar Village where an unfamiliar adventurer is tasked with approaching a local missionary named Sania Silverstream. Beneath her benign religious exterior, Sania recruits followers for the Al'Aketh, a cult dedicated to the Windlord Al'Akir. The mission seeks to uncover the cult's true designs for the settlement before an uneasy peace shatters.",h="Infiltrating the Cult is a quest in Shen'dar Village where an unfamiliar adventurer is tasked with...",sec={{t="A Precarious Peace",b="In Shen'dar Village, an uneasy truce is beginning to unravel. For some time, local authorities have tolerated the presence of Sania Silverstream, a self-proclaimed missionary living among them. She represents the Order of the Al'Aketh, an organization that dresses itself in the vestments of a benign religious movement while deploying gentle recruiters to attract unsuspecting followers. Village leaders permitted her recruitment efforts solely to avoid provoking the cult into outright conflict, but the pretense of mutual coexistence is wearing thin.",sp=0},{t="Beneath the Missionary's Veil",b="Beneath the diplomatic posturing and mild missionary work lies devotion to an ancient and dangerous power: Al'Akir the Windlord, ruler of the air elementals. When approached, Sania openly introduces herself as a junior priestess in the Order of the Al'Aketh, inviting prospective initiates to learn of their sovereign and promising a place at his table. Because existing residents are too familiar to investigate her safely, newcomers to the village are tasked with gaining her confidence to uncover what the cult truly plans for Shen'dar.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Sania Silverstream?",a="Sania Silverstream is a junior priestess residing in Shen'dar Village who claims to be a missionary for a religious order called the Al'Aketh. In truth, she acts as a friendly, non-threatening recruiter drawing locals into worship of the Windlord Al'Akir.",al={"who is sania","sania silverstream lore","priestess sania"}},{q="What is the Order of the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh is a cult masquerading as a conventional religious movement. Beneath their outward missionary activities, they revere Al'Akir the Windlord, the elemental lord of air, and seek to expand their influence by quietly recruiting converts from nearby settlements.",al={"who are the alaketh","what is alaketh","order of alaketh"}},{q="Why has Shen'dar Village tolerated Sania Silverstream?",a="The village leaders have tolerated Sania Silverstream's missionary presence to preserve a fragile illusion of peace and avoid triggering open hostilities with the cult. However, that uneasy truce is rapidly fraying, prompting the decision to investigate her covertly.",al={"why let sania stay","why not kill sania","why is sania in the village"}},{q="Who is the Windlord Al'Akir?",a="Al'Akir the Windlord is the Elemental Lord of the skies, storms, and air. The Order of the Al'Aketh venerates him as their divine sovereign, promising a place at the Windlord's table to any who repent and join their cause.",al={"who is the windlord","alakir lore","who is alakir"}},{q="Why are you asked to infiltrate the cult instead of a local?",a="Because you are an unfamiliar newcomer to Shen'dar Village, Sania and the Al'Aketh will not suspect you of working on behalf of the village leadership. Your anonymity allows you to approach her under the guise of an interested recruit without raising suspicion.",al={"why me","why send an adventurer","infiltrating quest reason"}},{q="What are the Al'Aketh planning for Shen'dar Village?",a="The local leadership suspects that the Al'Aketh's recruiting efforts conceal broader, aggressive ambitions for Shen'dar Village. The primary goal of infiltrating the cult is to uncover these clandestine plans before the village's fragile peace collapses entirely.",al={"cult plan for village","what do the alaketh want","alaketh intentions"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Sania Silverstream presents herself as a junior priestess, but her faith is pledged to an Elemental Lord rather than the Holy Light or Elune. True priests will recognize her pleasant religious veneer as cover for elemental worship.",["class:Rogue"]="Infiltrating a hostile cult through an unsuspecting recruiter plays directly to a rogue's talents for espionage, deception, and reconnaissance behind enemy lines."},kw={"al'aketh","al'akir","alaketh","alakir","infiltrating the cult","sania silverstream","shen'dar village","windlord"},m={id=93036},src={"In-game quest text: Infiltrating the Cult"},fv=true}
E["quest:93065"] = {t="quest",n="Prepare for Battle",z="general",s="The Peacekeepers are gathering in town to march into the surrounding territory. You are called to make your final preparations and meet Valennia Stormfist in the Gustberry Lowlands before the company departs.",h="The Peacekeepers are gathering in town to march into the surrounding territory.",sec={{t="A Call to Muster",b="The Peacekeepers, the local defense forces, have called their troops and volunteers to arms as tensions rise. Secure your equipment, ready your weapons, and join the company as it forms for an organized march into the surrounding territory.",sp=0},{t="The Gathering at Gustberry Lowlands",b="The company gathers along the eastern road leading north out of town into the Gustberry Lowlands. There, organizer Valennia Stormfist inspects the fighters and calls the roll. Then the company marches into danger.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist organizes the Peacekeepers' muster in the Gustberry Lowlands. She oversees final preparations along the eastern road leading north out of town before the forces depart.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","valennia stormfist","stormfist"}},{q="Who are the Peacekeepers?",a="The Peacekeepers are an organized local defense force. They are gathering to face a looming threat outside of town.",al={"who are the peacekeepers","peacekeepers","peacekeeper force"}},{q="Where are the Gustberry Lowlands?",a="The Gustberry Lowlands is where local defense forces gather. Their staging ground lies along the road leading north out of town.",al={"where is gustberry lowlands","gustberry lowlands location","gustberry lowlands"}},{q="What are the Peacekeepers preparing for?",a="The Peacekeepers are preparing to march out in force. They call on capable allies to check their gear and gather at the edge of town before the company departs.",al={"what are they preparing for","why prepare for battle","what is the battle"}},{q="Where is the rendezvous point for the march?",a="The company meets along the eastern road leading north out of town. There, its members can gather in formation before marching into the field.",al={"where is the muster point","where to meet valennia","rendezvous point"}},{q="What happens once everyone is assembled?",a="Valennia Stormfist inspects the assembled ranks and calls the roll. Then the forces march into danger.",al={"how does the muster finish","what does valennia say","we are as ready as we will ever be"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="As a front-line fighter, you know this gathering comes before combat. Check your gear and form ranks beside your allies as the company prepares to march."},kw={"eastern road","gustberry lowlands","mobilization","peacekeepers","prepare for battle","valennia stormfist"},m={id=93065},src={"In-game quest text: Prepare for Battle"},fv=true}
E["quest:93089"] = {t="quest",n="What Comes Next",z="general",s="On Zephras Isle, the High Order seeks your blessing before departing with its Kirin Tor allies. Their expedition depends on finding an arcane answer to the island’s instability.",h="On Zephras Isle, the High Order seeks your blessing before departing with its Kirin Tor allies.",sec={{t="The Departure",b="High Elder Shadowsong has blessed the expedition, and its preparations are ready. Ansirem has worked to establish skycutter travel between Dalaran and Zephras Isle. Now the vanguard is preparing to depart. Speak with Elaadrin Evengale when you are ready to leave the island.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who do I speak with before leaving Zephras Isle?",a="Speak with Elaadrin Evengale when you are ready to leave Zephras Isle.",al={"who do i talk to","leave zephras isle","elaadrin departure"}},{q="Where is the High Order going?",a="The High Order is preparing an expedition, with skycutter transport linking Dalaran and Zephras Isle.",al={"where are they going","high order destination","next stop"}},{q="Who is helping the High Order?",a="The Kirin Tor are helping the High Order. Ansirem has worked on skycutter transport between Dalaran and Zephras Isle.",al={"who are the allies","kirin tor help","high order friends"}},{q="What is the expedition seeking?",a="The expedition hopes to find an arcane solution to bring stability to Zephras Isle.",al={"what are they looking for","arcane solution","why leave the island"}},{q="What happened to the cult?",a="Elaadrin says the cult has been defeated, and preparations for the expedition are complete.",al={"cult status","is the cult defeated","what about the cult"}}},kw={"ansirem","dalaran","elaadrin evengale","high order","kirin tor","skycutter","what comes next","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","subzone:dalaran","quest:92596"},m={id=93089},src={"In-game quest text: What Comes Next"},fv=true}
E["quest:93090"] = {t="quest",n="What Comes Next",z="general",s="On Zephras Isle, the expedition prepares to cross the skies for Kalimdor. The local cult is defeated, and High Elder Shadowsong has given his blessing. Skycutters now carry travelers between the island and Mulgore.",h="On Zephras Isle, the expedition prepares to cross the skies for Kalimdor.",sec={{t="Departure from Zephras Isle",b="High Elder Shadowsong has blessed the expedition's onward journey from Zephras Isle, where the local cult has been defeated and safety restored. The expedition now looks beyond the island, with its focus set on travel across the open skies toward Kalimdor. Ayessa Dawnsinger coordinates the final departure for those ready to leave.",sp=0},{t="Skycutters to Mulgore",b="Muln worked to secure steady skycutter travel between Zephras Isle and Mulgore. Test voyages succeeded, and an advance party has already crossed over. They wait on the other side to make contact with the shaman of the Earthen Ring.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest What Comes Next?",a="The expedition is preparing to leave Zephras Isle after earning High Elder Shadowsong's blessing and defeating the local cult. Travelers are ready to cross the skies by skycutter to Kalimdor.",al={"what is what comes next","what comes next quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Where does the expedition travel?",a="Skycutters connect Zephras Isle with Mulgore, a grassy mesa region in central Kalimdor. The Earthen Ring makes its home there, and advance members of the expedition are already waiting.",al={"where are we going","skycutter destination","where does the skycutter fly"}},{q="Who is Ayessa Dawnsinger?",a="Ayessa Dawnsinger coordinates the departure from Zephras Isle. Preparations are in place, test flights have succeeded, and High Elder Shadowsong has blessed the expedition's departure.",al={"who is ayessa dawnsinger","ayessa dawnsinger","ayessa"}},{q="Who is Muln?",a="Muln worked to establish steady skycutter travel between Zephras Isle and Mulgore. He conducted test voyages to ensure safe passage across the skies.",al={"who is muln","muln skycutter","muln zephras"}},{q="What is the Earthen Ring?",a="The Earthen Ring is an organization of shamans devoted to keeping harmony among the elemental spirits. Its members are the expedition's contacts and hosts in Mulgore.",al={"what is the earthen ring","earthen ring mulgore","who are the earthen ring"}},{q="Why is the expedition leaving Zephras Isle?",a="The local cult is defeated, and High Elder Shadowsong has blessed the expedition. With those tasks complete, the expedition can leave Zephras Isle and continue to the mainland.",al={"why leave zephras isle","is zephras finished","why go to mulgore"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you travel to Mulgore to meet the Earthen Ring, a revered shamanic organization devoted to soothing Azeroth's elemental spirits.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you travel to Mulgore, your people's ancestral homeland. The Earthen Ring keeps its operations there."},kw={"ayessa dawnsinger","earthen ring","high elder shadowsong","mulgore","muln","skycutter","what comes next","zephras isle"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","zone:zephras","zone:mulgore","quest:95349"},m={id=93090},src={"In-game quest text: What Comes Next"},fv=true}
E["quest:93159"] = {t="quest",n="The Strange Hermit",z="general",s="The Strange Hermit is an introductory quest in Shadowgale Forest focusing on a shy, hooded recluse living away from civilization. Seeking conversation after long isolation, the hermit shares glimpses of his simple handcrafted life and his bond with the local singing forest spirits.",h="The Strange Hermit is an introductory quest in Shadowgale Forest focusing on a shy, hooded recluse living...",sec={{t="A Recluse in the Woods",b="Deep within the secluded canopy of Shadowgale Forest lives an enigmatic, hooded recluse known simply as the Strange Hermit. Far removed from settled towns and standard trade routes, he spends his days in quiet isolation among the trees. When approached, his awkward speech and nervous demeanor show how long he has lived without ordinary companionship, making even a basic greeting an unfamiliar challenge.",sp=0},{t="Spirits and Simple Things",b="Though solitary, the hermit insists he has all the company and provisions he needs. He confides that he once lived somewhere else before settling here, choosing to make a life surrounded by handmade trinkets and the local forest spirits. While the wild spirits frequently sing to him, he admits that melodic choruses cannot entirely replace speaking with someone who can truly answer back.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the strange hermit?",a="The strange hermit is an eccentric, hooded individual living in isolation within Shadowgale Forest. Unaccustomed to regular social interaction, he spends his time tending to handmade possessions and communing with local woodland spirits rather than other people.",al={"strange hermit","hermit identity","who is the hermit"}},{q="Where does the hermit live?",a="The hermit makes his home secluded deep among the trees of Shadowgale Forest, living well off the beaten path where few travelers ever wander.",al={"where is the hermit","shadowgale forest location","hermit zone"}},{q="Why does the hermit live by himself?",a="He admits he lived somewhere else entirely before retreating to the forest. While he remains quite vague about his past, he chose a quiet life surrounded only by things he built and the wild spirits of the woods.",al={"why is the hermit alone","hermit isolation","hermit backstory"}},{q="Can the hermit speak with forest spirits?",a="He considers the local forest spirits his companions, although they do not carry on normal speech. Instead, they sing to him, which he finds pleasant even if it fails to provide the conversational depth of a fellow mortal.",al={"hermit talking to spirits","forest spirits shadowgale","hermit friends"}},{q="What has the hermit built in the forest?",a="The hermit mentions having several handcrafted items and structures that keep him occupied, finding comfort and contentment in simple handmade belongings.",al={"hermit crafts","what did hermit build"}},{q="What happens during the quest 'The Strange Hermit'?",a="The quest involves approaching the nervous hermit in Shadowgale Forest to strike up a friendly conversation and hear what little he is willing to share about his reclusive existence.",al={"strange hermit quest","speak to hermit"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Druids attuned to nature may appreciate the hermit's interaction with the local forest spirits, though his inability to truly converse with them contrasts with a druid's deeper communion.",["profession:Engineering"]="Tinkers and craftsmen may relate to the hermit finding solace in building things by hand to furnish his lonely dwelling."},kw={"forest spirits","handcrafted","hermit","hooded figure","loner","shadowgale forest","strange hermit"},m={id=93159},src={"In-game quest text: The Strange Hermit"},fv=true}
E["quest:93160"] = {t="quest",n="The Forest's Bounty",z="general",s="The Forest's Bounty is a local quest in Shadowgale Forest where a timid forager asks for help collecting fallen acorns. Fierce predatory raptor-birds prowl the deeper woods, making harvesting too perilous without an adventurer's protection. The gathered acorns are then roasted into a tasty forest meal.",h="The Forest's Bounty is a local quest in Shadowgale Forest where a timid forager asks for help collecting...",sec={{t="A Perilous Foraging Run",b="A cautious forager seeking food in Shadowgale Forest finds themselves in a bind. While acorns lie scattered across the woodland floor, the surrounding trees are prowled by fierce, raptor-like creatures. Having gathered whatever fell near safety, the forager depends on passing travelers to venture into the wilder groves so that neither goes hungry.",sp=0},{t="Roasting the Harvest",b="Shadowgale Acorns provide hearty sustenance for those who know how to prepare them. Though bitter or tough in the raw, roasting them over a crackling fire transforms the wild harvest into a rich, roasted treat. Braving the predatory birds among the timber turns a simple woodland scavenge into a warm meal shared in the wilderness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who asks you to gather the acorns?",a="A nervous forager resting near Shadowgale Forest requests assistance. Having collected all the safe acorns nearby, they need a hardy adventurer to venture deeper into the woods where dangerous wildlife roams.",al={"who gives the forest bounty quest","who wants acorns","quest giver forests bounty"}},{q="Where are the acorns found?",a="Shadowgale Acorns are scattered across the ground beneath the canopy of Shadowgale Forest. Foragers must search the forest floor while staying watchful for hostile predators lurking among the trees.",al={"where are shadowgale acorns","acorn location","shadowgale forest acorns"}},{q="Why is collecting acorns dangerous here?",a="The forest floor is prowled by predatory raptor-like birds. These aggressive creatures make straying far from camp extremely hazardous for ordinary folk who lack martial training to defend themselves.",al={"why dangerous shadowgale forest","what raptor birds","why is gathering acorns dangerous"}},{q="What are the acorns used for?",a="The acorns serve as a hearty wild food source. Once collected, they are meant to be roasted over a fire into a warm, nutty snack to share with those willing to brave the forest beasts.",al={"what are shadowgale acorns for","roasted acorns","why gather acorns"}},{q="What are the raptor-bird creatures mentioned in the forest?",a="The quest giver describes them as dangerous 'raptor-bird things' hunting in Shadowgale Forest. These avian or reptilian predators are large enough and vicious enough to view an unwary traveler as their next meal.",al={"what raptor bird thing","raptor birds in shadowgale","forest raptors"}},{q="How are the acorns prepared for eating?",a="While tough or bitter off the ground, the forager specifically notes that Shadowgale Acorns become a delicious treat once properly roasted over heat.",al={"are shadowgale acorns edible raw","how to cook shadowgale acorns","eating forest acorns"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Gathering Shadowgale acorns showcases how local wild nuts can be roasted into simple forest fare, making it of keen interest to culinary foragers."},kw={"acorns","cooking","foraging","raptor-bird","shadowgale acorns","shadowgale forest","the forest's bounty"},m={id=93160},src={"In-game quest text: The Forest's Bounty"},fv=true}
E["quest:93165"] = {t="quest",n="Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears",z="general",s="Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears is a bounty offered by a grieving enclave seeking violent vengeance against the Al'Aketh cult. After suffering a terrible tragedy at the cultists' hands, the enclave promises coin and access to the Nightclaw's hidden lore in exchange for trophies harvested from their slain enemies. Taking up this task plunges travelers directly into the conflict spreading across Shadowgale Forest and surrounding territories.",h="Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears is a bounty offered by a grieving enclave seeking violent vengeance against the...",sec={{t="Blood for Blood",b="The feud between the Nightclaw enclave and the Al'Aketh cultists is bitter and merciless. Following an unspecified but devastating tragedy inflicted upon the enclave, the survivors have abandoned any hope of peaceful reconciliation. To avenge their fallen kin, they turn to wandering adventurers and mercenaries, demanding violent retribution. In exchange for cutting down the cultists and taking their ears as grim proof of the deed, the enclave offers cold coin and the prospect of deeper trust.",sp=0},{t="The Al'Aketh Menace",b="The Al'Aketh operate across several wilderness areas, spreading their influence throughout the Gustberry Lowlands, the darkened woods of Shadowgale Forest, and the ancient Shrine of Akir. Their dark practices and fanatical nature have earned them the hatred of local inhabitants. By targeting their encampments across these territories, travelers not only weaken the cult's presence but also earn favor with the secretive Nightclaw, who guard their own esoteric mysteries closely until an outsider proves their loyalty.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears?",a="Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears is a quest where an adventurer is hired to retaliate against the Al'Aketh cultists following a disastrous attack on a local enclave. The petitioner seeks retribution, requiring adventurers to slay cultists and return their severed ears as undeniable proof of the kill in exchange for coin and trust.",al={"mercy falls on deaf ears quest","what is this quest","quest overview"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a sect of accursed cultists known for bringing ruin and tragedy to neighboring settlements. They maintain a hostile presence across the Gustberry Lowlands, Shadowgale Forest, and the Shrine of Akir, provoking open blood feuds with those who reside in the surrounding territories.",al={"who are the alaketh","alaketh cultists lore","who are al'aketh"}},{q="Where can Al'Aketh cultists be found?",a="Al'Aketh cultists can be encountered in three distinct areas: the dense canopies of Shadowgale Forest, the sacred grounds around the Shrine of Akir, and throughout the Gustberry Lowlands. Slaying cultists in any of these territories yields the proof demanded by the grieving enclave.",al={"where are alaketh cultists","alaketh locations","where to find cultists"}},{q="Why does the quest giver collect cultist ears?",a="Severed ears serve as tangible, undeniable proof of death in a brutal wilderness conflict where blood must be repaid with blood. Because the enclave harbors deep hatred toward the Al'Aketh following a severe tragedy, they demand physical trophies to ensure every bounty paid corresponds to a dead cultist.",al={"why ears","why do they want cultist ears","ears proof"}},{q="Who are the Nightclaw?",a="The Nightclaw represent a secretive enclave that has suffered heavy losses due to the Al'Aketh cult's depredations. While wary of strangers, they hold closely guarded secrets and lore, offering outsiders eventual access to their inner mysteries if they prove their loyalty by destroying their cultist enemies.",al={"nightclaw faction","who are nightclaw","nightclaw secrets lore"}},{q="What tragedy did the Al'Aketh cause?",a="The quest giver speaks of a great tragedy visited directly upon the Nightclaw enclave by the Al'Aketh, which sparked an uncompromising blood feud. While specific casualties and details are not enumerated in the bounty itself, the incident was horrific enough that the survivors demand total vengeance with no mercy.",al={"what did the alaketh do","what happened to the enclave","alaketh tragedy"}},{q="What are the Nightclaw's secrets?",a="The exact nature of the Nightclaw's secrets is guarded within their enclave and not disclosed immediately to outsiders. The quest giver promises that adventurers who continually aid their fight against the Al'Aketh and prove themselves true friends will eventually be granted access to this hidden knowledge.",al={"nightclaw secrets","what secrets does nightclaw have","what does nightclaw reveal"}}},kw={"al'aketh","blood feud","cultists","ears","gustberry lowlands","mercy falls on deaf ears","nightclaw","shadowgale forest","shrine of akir"},m={id=93165},src={"In-game quest text: Mercy Falls on Deaf Ears"},fv=true}
E["quest:93172"] = {t="quest",n="Free the Hollows",z="general",s="Free the Hollows is a quest in Shadowgale Forest, where ghost-like spirits known as wind hollows linger among ancient ruins. Trapped by an ancient tragedy, their sorrowful singing haunts the surrounding woods until someone grants them release.",h="Free the Hollows is a quest in Shadowgale Forest, where ghost-like spirits known as wind hollows linger among...",sec={{t="Hauntings of Shadowgale",b="Elves dwelling across the island have long regarded Shadowgale Forest as a haunted grove, and their superstitions are grounded in truth. Deep within the woods to the northeast lie old stone ruins where phantom apparitions known as wind hollows drift endlessly among the overgrowth. Rather than hostile malice, these spirits project an air of deep sorrow, trapped by the weight of whatever disaster befell the ruins in ages past.",sp=0},{t="The Mournful Song",b="On quiet nights, travelers near the ruins report hearing a strange, ethereal singing drifting on the breeze. It does not resemble any known mortal song, carrying an intense melancholy that unsettles any who hear it. Believing that leaving these shades trapped in their eternal lament is a torment, compassionate travelers seek out the wind hollows to destroy their spectral bindings and grant them peace.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the wind hollows?",a="Wind hollows are sorrowful, ghost-like spirits that haunt old elven ruins to the northeast within Shadowgale Forest. Trapped by some ancient tragedy, they produce an eerie, mournful singing that echoes through the trees at night.",al={"what are wind hollows","hollows lore","what are the hollows"}},{q="Why do the wind hollows need to be freed?",a="The quest giver believes the spirits are trapped in perpetual sorrow following a terrible catastrophe. Destroying the wind hollows is viewed as an act of release and mercy, freeing their lingering forms from being bound to the ruins.",al={"why free hollows","why kill hollows","free the hollows purpose"}},{q="Where are the spirits found?",a="Shadowgale Forest is an island woodland inhabited by elves, featuring ancient ruins to the northeast where the ghost-like hollows congregate.",al={"where is shadowgale forest","shadowgale ruins","ruins location"}},{q="What is the strange singing heard in the woods?",a="The singing of the wind hollows is described as an extraordinarily sorrowful melody unlike any ordinary tune, thought to be born of their tragic demise and endless confinement to the island's ruins.",al={"hollow singing","ghost song","why are ghosts singing"}},{q="Who asks you to free the hollows?",a="The task is offered by an observer on the island who takes pity on the trapped spirits and notices that local elves are wary of the haunted woods.",al={"who gives free the hollows","questgiver free the hollows"}},{q="What happened at the ruins to create the hollows?",a="While the exact details remain lost to time, locals know that a terrible event unfolded at the northeastern ruins, binding these spirits to the site in an endless, mournful loop.",al={"what happened at shadowgale ruins","why are the ruins haunted"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The local elves of the island have long sensed that Shadowgale Forest is haunted, disturbed by the tragic lingering of spirits bound to their ancient ruins.",["class:Priest"]="The wind hollows are sorrowful apparitions trapped by tragedy, making their destruction an act of mercy to release souls bound in eternal anguish.",["class:Druid"]="Unquiet spirits lingering within Shadowgale Forest represent an unnatural disturbance in the wilderness that calls for balance and release."},kw={"free the hollows","ghosts","hollows","ruins","shadowgale","shadowgale forest","wind hollows"},m={id=93172},src={"In-game quest text: Free the Hollows"},fv=true}
E["quest:93317"] = {t="quest",n="Crab Season",z="general",s="Crab Season is an urgent foraging task on Zephras centered on harvesting rare Windsong crawlers during their brief annual window. Because recent cultist activity has made the shores of Windsong Lake perilous, adventurers are called upon to brave both hostile interlopers and aggressive crustaceans.",h="Crab Season is an urgent foraging task on Zephras centered on harvesting rare Windsong crawlers during their...",sec={{t="A Fleeting Harvest",b="On the shores of Windsong Lake across Zephras, a brief seasonal harvesting window opens for only a few weeks each year. The native Windsong crawlers emerge in numbers along the freshwater banks and tributary rivers, offering a rare harvest of meat to anyone bold enough to gather them before the annual opportunity slips away.",sp=0},{t="Peril by the Waters",b="Recent surges in cultist activity around Zephras have disrupted daily life and endangered local foragers. With hostile cultists patrolling the wilderness, traveling to Windsong Lake has become far too hazardous for solitary gatherers, threatening to waste the fleeting crawler season unless capable adventurers step in to brave the shores and the ill-tempered crabs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Windsong crawlers?",a="Windsong crawlers are a species of aggressive crab native exclusively to Windsong Lake on the island or land of Zephras. They are notable for being harvestable only during a brief seasonal window of a few weeks each year.",al={"what are windsong crawlers","windsong crabs","lake crabs zephras"}},{q="Why do the crabs need to be harvested right now?",a="The crawlers can only be gathered during a small seasonal window lasting just a few weeks annually. If they are not harvested before this narrow timeframe closes, the opportunity to gather their meat is lost for the rest of the year.",al={"why harvest crawlers now","crawler season window","why limited time crabs"}},{q="Why can't the local harvester gather the crabs themselves?",a="Recent cultist activity in the surrounding area has made traveling out to Windsong Lake too hazardous for regular foraging, leaving local gatherers unable to reach the shores before the short harvesting window shuts.",al={"why cant locals gather crabs","cultists zephras","windsong cult activity"}},{q="Where are Windsong crawlers found?",a="Windsong crawlers dwell along the shorelines surrounding Windsong Lake on Zephras, as well as along the nearby connecting riverbanks.",al={"windsong crawler locations","where are crabs on zephras","windsong lake shores"}},{q="Are Windsong crawlers dangerous to hunt?",a="Despite their humble appearance as simple freshwater crustaceans, Windsong crawlers are notably feisty and far meaner than they look, readily snapping at anyone trying to gather their meat.",al={"are windsong crawlers dangerous","crab temperament","mean crabs"}},{q="What is Windsong crawler meat used for?",a="The harvested crawler meat is prized seasonal food gathered from the lake during the few weeks the crabs are available, prized enough that locals hire adventurers when cultists block the paths.",al={"windsong crawler meat purpose","why collect crab meat","what is crawler meat for"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="While harvesting seasonal Windsong crawler meat is vital for local larders on Zephras, preparing fresh lake catches requires careful handling given the creatures' aggressive nature."},kw={"crab season","crabs","cult activity","windsong crawler meat","windsong crawlers","windsong lake","zephras"},m={id=93317},src={"In-game quest text: Crab Season"},fv=true}
E["quest:93318"] = {t="quest",n="WANTED: Vulgara the Insatiable",z="general",s="WANTED: Vulgara the Insatiable is a Peacekeeper bounty targeting a monstrous winged beast terrorizing the Shen'dar Highlands. Issued by Constable Aonda, the contract calls for adventurers to slay the ravenous vuldren and present her head to Danarii Bellowveil.",h="WANTED: Vulgara the Insatiable is a Peacekeeper bounty targeting a monstrous winged beast terrorizing the...",sec={{t="The Highland Scourge",b="A formal bounty posted by Constable Aonda of the Peacekeepers marks Vulgara the Insatiable as an immediate danger to the highlands. The massive winged vuldren has terrorized travelers and local settlements from her roost southwest of Shen'dar Village, prompting local authorities to call on adventurous bounty hunters to bring her reign to an end.",sp=0},{t="Pressured Protectors",b="The Peacekeepers already find themselves besieged on multiple fronts, struggling to manage threats from regional bandits and a shadowy cult operating in the area. Eliminating Vulgara relieves critical pressure on the local populace, ensuring the skies over Shen'dar remain safe while defenders focus on civil defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Vulgara the Insatiable?",a="Vulgara the Insatiable is a massive, winged vuldren terrorizing the Shen'dar Highlands. Deemed the scourge of the highlands by local authorities, her ravenous appetite and violent presence pose a severe danger to travelers and nearby settlements.",al={"who is vulgara","vulgara lore","what kind of monster is vulgara"}},{q="Who placed the bounty on Vulgara?",a="Constable Aonda of the local Peacekeepers issued the official bounty on Vulgara to protect local citizens, directing hunters to bring proof of the beast's death to Danarii Bellowveil.",al={"who posted vulgara bounty","who wants vulgara dead","constable aonda"}},{q="Where can Vulgara the Insatiable be found?",a="Vulgara is known to prowl a hilltop rise situated just southwest of Shen'dar Village in the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"vulgara location","where does vulgara spawn","find vulgara"}},{q="Who is Danarii Bellowveil?",a="Danarii Bellowveil is the advisor handling the bounty's fulfillment. Strained by surrounding bandits and cultists, Danarii inspects Vulgara's severed head and dispenses the promised reward.",al={"who is danarii bellowveil","danarii turn in"}},{q="What kind of creature is a vuldren?",a="A vuldren is a dangerous, predatory winged beast found in the highlands. Vulgara is a particularly massive and vicious specimen whose insatiable predation threatens local settlements.",al={"what is a vuldren","vuldren beast"}},{q="What other threats plague the Shen'dar Highlands?",a="Beyond predatory beasts like Vulgara, the Peacekeepers in the Shen'dar Highlands are stretched thin dealing with active threats from an entrenched cult and highland bandits preying on local citizens.",al={"what threats are in shendar highlands","shendar cult and bandits"}}},kw={"bounty","constable aonda","danarii bellowveil","peacekeepers","shen'dar highlands","shen'dar village","vuldren","vulgara","vulgara the insatiable"},m={id=93318},src={"In-game quest text: WANTED: Vulgara the Insatiable"},fv=true}
E["quest:93319"] = {t="quest",n="Pilfered Windstones",z="general",s="Pilfered Windstones is a local quest on Zephras where players recover vital stolen resources from the Highlands Bandits. Stored in reserve for emergencies, these windstones are crucial for daily survival, but local peacekeepers are too stretched by cultist threats to clear out the thieves themselves.",h="Pilfered Windstones is a local quest on Zephras where players recover vital stolen resources from the...",sec={{t="The Stolen Caches of Zephras",b="On the island of Zephras, windstones are not merely curious artifacts—they are vital resources necessary for day-to-day living. Settlements maintain an emergency cache to ensure survival during crises. However, village stores began suffering steady shortages, which were only unraveled when captured bandits from the northern Shen'dar Highlands were discovered carrying the stones. These sneakthieves had apparently been slipping into local stockpiles for months, exploiting thin security to snatch whatever supplies they could carry.",sp=0},{t="Stretched Defenses and Looming Threats",b="Although clearing out the northern cave seems like an obvious solution, the settlement's leadership faces difficult choices. Aonda, responsible for village defense, declined to sanction a counter-raid because the community's few peacekeepers cannot be spared. To the south, cultists at Falaath Village are multiplying rapidly, presenting a grave danger that forces the guards to hold their posts. With the peacekeepers pinned down by the cultist threat, recovering the pilfered windstones falls to independent adventurers willing to face the bandits directly.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are windstones used for on Zephras?",a="On Zephras, residents rely on windstones for their day-to-day living and survival. Because they are so essential to daily life, local settlements attempt to keep a reserve cache stored away for emergencies.",al={"what are windstones used for","why need windstones","windstone purpose"}},{q="Who stole the windstones?",a="The windstones were stolen by the Highlands Bandits operating out of a cave in the Shen'dar Highlands. After captured bandits were found carrying them, locals realized the thieves had likely been breaking into the village storehouses for months.",al={"who stole the windstones","who took windstones","windstone thieves"}},{q="Where are the Highlands Bandits located?",a="The Highlands Bandits make their camp in a cave situated to the north of the settlement within the Shen'dar Highlands, where they stockpile stolen goods pilfered from village stores.",al={"where is the bandit cave","highlands bandits location","shendar bandits"}},{q="Why hasn't the village cleared out the bandit cave?",a="Aonda, overseeing the settlement's defense, chose not to organize a raid against the northern cave because the village has very few peacekeepers. Pulling them away would leave the settlement vulnerable to the growing number of cultists at Falaath Village to the south.",al={"why not attack the bandit cave","why aonda wont attack","village defense bandits"}},{q="What is the threat at Falaath Village?",a="Falaath Village lies to the south and is currently seeing a concerning surge in cultist numbers. This growing presence poses an immediate threat that keeps local peacekeepers tied down defending the home settlement rather than chasing bandits.",al={"what is falaath village threat","cultists falaath","falaath cultists"}},{q="Does recovering the windstones solve the settlement's shortage?",a="Recovering the pilfered stones provides temporary relief for emergency stores, but local residents note that broader supply route disruptions remain an urgent issue. If supply lines are not secured soon, shortages will continue to threaten the settlement.",al={"are windstones secure now","supply routes zephras","windstone shortage fixed"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="While Highlands Bandits rely on crude sneakery to pilfer stored windstones, skilled rogues will recognize the opportunism of thieves exploiting a settlement stretched thin by cultist threats.",["race:Skyborne"]="Life on Zephras is deeply intertwined with windstones for everyday survival, making the defense and recovery of these caches critical to maintaining local settlements."},kw={"aonda","bandit cave","falaath village","highlands bandits","pilfered windstones","shen'dar highlands","windstones","zephras"},rel={"quest:92529"},m={id=93319},src={"In-game quest text: Pilfered Windstones"},fv=true}
E["quest:93320"] = {t="quest",n="Tower Defense",z="general",s="At the fortified position, Tower Defense holds against overwhelming numbers. The defenders have kept the line for now, but exhaustion threatens to break it.",h="At the fortified position, Tower Defense holds against overwhelming numbers.",sec={{t="A Beleaguered Defense",b="The garrison holds back the first assault at a fortified position. Yet the attackers outnumber them and keep pressing forward. The surviving fighters are growing tired, and the line between survival and defeat grows thin.",sp=0},{t="The Threat of Attrition",b="The garrison keeps its perimeter, but time favors the attackers. Prolonged skirmishes against superior numbers drain the defenders’ strength. They need a decisive move to break the stalemate before their lines buckle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Tower Defense about?",a="Tower Defense is a desperate defense against overwhelming numbers. The line still holds, but exhaustion threatens to break it unless the stalemate ends.",al={"what is tower defense","tower defense quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Who are the defenders fighting?",a="The attackers outnumber the defenders and press hard enough to wear down the garrison. The cult is attacking the pylon watchtower outside town.",al={"who attacks in tower defense","who are we fighting","tower defense enemy"}},{q="What is the current status of the defenders?",a="The defenders are tiring from prolonged combat under relentless assault. Without relief, the garrison risks losing ground unless the stalemate breaks soon.",al={"why are defenders tired","stalemate at the tower","status of the fight"}},{q="Does holding the line resolve the battle?",a="Holding off the assault buys only a little time. Fatigue will wear down the defenders unless they find a way to break the deadlock.",al={"did we win tower defense","how does tower defense end","what happens after holding them off"}},{q="Where does this defense take place?",a="The defense takes place at a fortified outpost under siege. Constant pressure from numerous attackers has stretched the defenders thin.",al={"where is tower defense located","tower defense zone","what tower is this"}},{q="What is needed to turn the tide?",a="The defenders need a swift plan to break the siege before their exhausted frontline gives way.",al={"what needs to be done next","how to win the siege","breaking the stalemate"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, you know a hold against superior numbers tests stamina as much as skill. Discipline on the frontline can keep the defense from collapsing."},kw={"defenders","exhaustion","fortification","outpost","siege","stalemate","tower defense"},m={id=93320},src={"In-game quest text: Tower Defense"},fv=true}
E["quest:93461"] = {t="quest",n="Welcome to Shen'dar Village",z="general",s="Welcome to Shen'dar Village introduces travelers to a remote haven shaped by ancient Highborne bloodlines and simmering factional rivalries. While the High Order and the Windshapers maintain an outward truce within the settlement, bitter tensions linger just beyond the village borders.",h="Welcome to Shen'dar Village introduces travelers to a remote haven shaped by ancient Highborne bloodlines and...",sec={{t="A Fragile Hearth",b="Shen'dar Village serves as a delicate haven where differing elven factions coexist under a tense truce. Newcomers who show traces of Highborne blood are promptly sent to pay respects to the elder of the High Order, Rathiril Sunlance, inside the settlement's main lodge, and to secure lodging with the local innkeeper, Coriella Calmbreeze.",sp=0},{t="Rivalries of the Wilds",b="Beneath the mundane routine of lodgings and odd jobs lies simmering friction between two prominent groups: the High Order and the Windshapers. Although civility is strictly observed within the safety of the village square, animosity runs unchecked in the surrounding wilderness, where old rivalries are settled away from prying eyes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the quest Welcome to Shen'dar Village?",a="Welcome to Shen'dar Village is an introductory quest that acquaints newly arrived adventurers with the key figures and fragile peace of the settlement. You are directed to meet Rathiril Sunlance, the resident High Order elder, and Coriella Calmbreeze at the village inn before taking on local tasks.",al={"what is this quest","welcome to shendar village info","shendar village intro"}},{q="Who is Rathiril Sunlance?",a="Rathiril Sunlance is an elder of the High Order residing in Shen'dar Village. He is stationed in the main village lodge, just around the corner from the initial questgiver, and serves as a primary contact for those bearing Highborne lineage or dealing with the High Order.",al={"who is rathiril sunlance","high order elder","where is rathiril"}},{q="Who is Coriella Calmbreeze?",a="Coriella Calmbreeze runs the local inn in Shen'dar Village. She provides essential hospitality to travelers, offering food, lodging, and supplies for those preparing to venture into the surrounding wilds.",al={"who is coriella calmbreeze","shendar innkeeper","where to get lodging shendar"}},{q="What is the tension between the High Order and the Windshapers?",a="Tensions run high between the High Order and the Windshapers due to differing traditions and ambitions. While both factions manage to maintain a civil coexistence within the borders of Shen'dar Village, that restraint ends at the village perimeter, where conflicts often spill into violence in the wilds.",al={"high order vs windshapers","why is there tension","village conflict"}},{q="What is the High Order look mentioned by the questgiver?",a="Those descended from the Highborne are noted for a distinct twinkle in their eyes, an arcane inheritance that marks them out immediately to observant locals as having the look of the High Order.",al={"high order look","highborne eyes","highborne twinkle"}},{q="Is Shen'dar Village safe from conflict?",a="Inside Shen'dar Village itself, civility is strictly enforced between rival factions like the High Order and Windshapers. Outside the settlement, however, no such peace holds, and open hostility often flares away from watchful eyes.",al={"is shendar safe","violence outside shendar","wilds danger"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Shen'dar Village is steeped in the legacy and bloodlines of the ancient Highborne, making it an uneasy reminder of the pride and arcane heritage that splintered elven society.",["class:Mage"]="Mages with ties to Highborne traditions will find familiar lineage in the High Order, whose elders like Rathiril Sunlance command respect in Shen'dar."},kw={"coriella calmbreeze","high order","highborne","main lodge","rathiril sunlance","shendar village","windshapers"},rel={"topic:highborne","quest:92595"},m={id=93461},src={"In-game quest text: Welcome to Shen'dar Village"},fv=true}
E["quest:93735"] = {t="quest",n="The Broken Construct",z="general",s="The Broken Construct involves aiding an artisan in Valanaar who is struggling to repair a rare, damaged automaton. These machines were originally gifted by the departed spirits of the wind and preserved by the Windshapers, making every remaining functional construct an invaluable relic of the island's past.",h="The Broken Construct involves aiding an artisan in Valanaar who is struggling to repair a rare, damaged...",sec={{t="Gifts of the Wind Spirits",b="Long ago, the spirits of the wind bestowed magical instruments, devices, and animated constructs upon the people of the island. The Windshapers were tasked with their upkeep, preserving these elemental gifts across generations. However, after the spirits left, the flow of their ancient patronage ceased, causing most of the wondrous creations to fall into disrepair until only a handful remained operational.",sp=0},{t="A Crisis in Valanaar",b="Within the city of Valanaar, one of the last functioning constructs recently suffered damage, threatening to reduce the island's dwindling heritage even further. An artisan named Riaani Nightwind was sent to the western edge of the city to examine the machine and attempt repairs, though the fragile nature of these ancient devices makes additional aid essential.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are local caretakers historically entrusted with tending the magical instruments, devices, and constructs gifted by the spirits of the wind. With the departure of those spirits, their role has shifted to carefully preserving the few remaining relics that still function.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshapers lore","what do windshapers do"}},{q="Where did the magical constructs come from?",a="The constructs were gifted to the island's inhabitants by the spirits of the wind, along with various other magical instruments and devices. Following the departure of the wind spirits, very few of these creations have remained functional.",al={"who built constructs","construct origins","where did the constructs come from"}},{q="Who is Riaani Nightwind?",a="Riaani Nightwind is an artisan dispatched to the west side of Valanaar to inspect and repair one of the city's few remaining working constructs after it sustained damage.",al={"who is riaani nightwind","riaani location","artisan in valanaar"}},{q="Why are most of the island's constructs broken?",a="The constructs rely on gifts and enchantment bestowed by the spirits of the wind. Once those spirits departed the island, the power and care needed to keep them operational dwindled, leaving only a scant few functional today.",al={"why are constructs broken","why did constructs fail","wind spirits leaving"}},{q="What is Valanaar's connection to the constructs?",a="Valanaar is a settlement home to some of the last operational wind-gifted constructs. One of these remaining machines recently sustained damage on the western end of the city, prompting urgent repairs.",al={"what is valanaar","valanaar city","where is the construct"}},{q="What needs to be done for The Broken Construct?",a="You are asked to travel to the west side of Valanaar to find the artisan Riaani Nightwind and provide any assistance needed to help repair the damaged construct.",al={"broken construct quest goal","what do i do broken construct","where to go broken construct"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The constructs and instruments maintained by the Windshapers run on delicate magical devices, making their maintenance of particular interest to practitioners of the arcane.",["class:Shaman"]="The constructs were originally gifted by the spirits of the wind, highlighting a deep historical bond between the local inhabitants and elemental forces before the spirits departed.",["profession:Engineering"]="Artisans working on ancient enchanted constructs face mechanical and magical challenges in keeping these relics operating without their original spirit patrons."},kw={"artisan","broken construct","construct","riaani nightwind","valanaar","wind spirits","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=93735},src={"In-game quest text: The Broken Construct"},fv=true}
E["quest:93736"] = {t="quest",n="Unwelcome Spirits",z="general",s="The Windshapers seek the spirits of the wind, gone from their reach. Now hollows appear in Shadowgale Forest, and the order gathers their essences to learn what drove the true spirits away.",h="The Windshapers seek the spirits of the wind, gone from their reach.",sec={{t="The Vanished Spirits",b="The Windshapers have lost communion with the spirits of the wind. They seek to learn why the spirits left and what might bring them back. Their departure has left a void in the local elemental atmosphere.",sp=0},{t="Shadows in Shadowgale",b="After the wind spirits departed, strange sprites called hollows appeared in Shadowgale Forest to the north. The Windshapers had not known of them before the spirits vanished. The hollows seem tied to the spirits’ absence, and their essences may help reveal what they are.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are an order seeking to learn why the spirits of the wind abandoned them and how to bring them back. Their inquiries now focus on strange occurrences that began after the spirits disappeared.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshaper faction","what do windshapers want"}},{q="What are the hollows?",a="Hollows are strange wind sprites that appeared in Shadowgale Forest after the original wind spirits departed. Their origins and nature remain unknown, so scholars gather their essences for study.",al={"what are hollows","wind hollow lore","hollow sprites"}},{q="Where is Shadowgale Forest?",a="Shadowgale Forest lies to the north. Strange wind hollows have appeared there since the area's elemental spirits departed.",al={"where is shadowgale forest","shadowgale location"}},{q="Why did the wind spirits leave?",a="No one knows why the spirits of the wind vanished. The Windshapers seek the cause and hope to restore communion with them.",al={"why did wind spirits leave","where did the spirits go"}},{q="Why are Wind Hollow Essences being gathered?",a="The Windshapers gather essences from dispersed hollows for study. They hope these remains will reveal what brought the beings into existence after the spirits departed.",al={"why collect hollow essences","wind hollow essence purpose"}},{q="Are the hollows considered hostile?",a="The hollows are unwelcome intruders that appeared when the natural spirits were gone. Their spread through Shadowgale Forest calls for dispersal so the region is protected and specimens can be gathered.",al={"are hollows dangerous","hollow sprite threat"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="For those who commune with the elements, the natural wind spirits' departure and the hollows' arrival point to a deep disturbance in the local balance of air."},kw={"hollows","shadowgale forest","sprites","unwelcome spirits","wind hollow essence","wind hollows","wind spirits","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=93736},src={"In-game quest text: Unwelcome Spirits"},fv=true}
E["quest:93737"] = {t="quest",n="The Broken Construct",z="general",s="The Broken Construct involves lending aid to Riaani Nightwind, who is shoulder-deep inside the innards of an inoperable machine. Sent as an assistant by Ayessa, travelers must listen to Riaani's plans to discover how they can help restore the device.",h="The Broken Construct involves lending aid to Riaani Nightwind, who is shoulder-deep inside the innards of an...",sec={{t="Assisting the Artisan",b="The Broken Construct begins when an adventurer is dispatched by Ayessa to aid Riaani Nightwind. Riaani is hard at work attempting to diagnose a damaged construct, his attention completely absorbed by the device's inner components. Though he is surprised to receive outside help and doubts whether an untrained traveler can handle delicate mechanical work, he agrees to outline a few necessary tasks once he pulls himself out of the machinery.",sp=0},{t="Troubleshooting Machinery",b="Constructs require precise assembly and careful calibration, making any breakdown a serious setback for an engineer. Riaani Nightwind finds himself shoulder-deep in mechanical parts, seeking an extra set of hands to gather materials or perform field tests. Listening carefully to Riaani's briefing marks the first step toward getting the inert machine functioning once again.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Riaani Nightwind?",a="Riaani Nightwind is a tinkerer working diligently on a disabled mechanical construct. Though initially skeptical of newcomers sent his way, he is in need of assistance to get the machine operational again.",al={"who is riaani nightwind","riaani","construct mechanic"}},{q="Who is Ayessa?",a="Ayessa is an associate who directs adventurers to assist Riaani Nightwind with his ongoing mechanical troubles. Her referral is what convinces Riaani to consider putting a newcomer to work.",al={"who sent me to riaani","ayessa","who is ayessa"}},{q="What is the broken construct?",a="The construct is a mechanical creation currently suffering from mechanical failures. Riaani Nightwind is found buried deep in its innards trying to determine what parts and tasks are required to repair it.",al={"what is the construct","what machine is riaani fixing","broken machine"}},{q="What does Riaani need help with?",a="Upon arriving from Ayessa, you must first give Riaani a moment to step away from the construct's inner workings and explain the situation so you can receive instructions on how best to assist.",al={"what does riaani want me to do","broken construct task","how to help riaani"}},{q="Why is Riaani skeptical of your help?",a="Riaani is completely engrossed in technical labor and somewhat impatient when interrupted. He views most adventurers as untrained novices until they prove capable of following mechanical instructions.",al={"why does riaani call me a novice","is riaani rude","riaani dialogue"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Tinkers and engineers will recognize the frustration of dealing with a broken construct whose mechanical inner workings require tedious troubleshooting."},kw={"ayessa","broken construct","construct","engineering","riaani nightwind","the broken construct"},m={id=93737},src={"In-game quest text: The Broken Construct"},fv=true}
E["quest:93738"] = {t="quest",n="The Broken Construct",z="general",s="A Windshaper asks you to tell Ayessa Dawnsinger what you learned about the broken construct. The High Order’s work with wind spirit magic has drawn concern.",h="A Windshaper asks you to tell Ayessa Dawnsinger what you learned about the broken construct.",sec={{t="Return to Ayessa",b="Take what you learned to Ayessa Dawnsinger in Valanaar. The High Order has tested the Windshapers’ patience with its experiments in wind spirit magic. It has also touched knowledge under Windshaper purview. Ayessa can decide what should happen next.",sp=0},{t="A Troubling Name",b="Ayessa believes the High Order was responsible for the construct breaking down. She knows Belathaan as Valennia Stormfist’s cousin, and says his mother was a Windshaper. His involvement leaves her disappointed. She needs time to reflect.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who should I report to?",a="Report what you learned to Ayessa Dawnsinger in Valanaar.",al={"report to ayessa","where report","ayessa daw sinsinger"}},{q="Why are the Windshapers concerned about the High Order?",a="The High Order has experimented with wind spirit magic and meddled with knowledge under Windshaper purview, though it is forbidden to do so.",al={"high order trouble","why windshapers mad","high order wind magic"}},{q="What happened to the construct?",a="Ayessa believes the High Order was responsible for the construct breaking down.",al={"broken construct","construct broke","why construct broken"}},{q="Who is Belathaan?",a="Belathaan is Valennia Stormfist’s cousin, and his mother was a Windshaper.",al={"belathaan","belathaan family","belathaan cousin"}}},kw={"ayessa dawnsinger","belathaan","broken construct","high order","valanaar","valennia stormfist","wind spirits","windshaper"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","quest:92596"},m={id=93738},src={"In-game quest text: The Broken Construct"},fv=true}
E["quest:93739"] = {t="quest",n="Exploring the Horde",z="general",s="From Grommash Hold, you travel to meet the leaders who guide the Horde. The journey carries you from Orgrimmar to the Darkspear trolls, the tauren and the Forsaken.",h="From Grommash Hold, you travel to meet the leaders who guide the Horde.",sec={{t="Orders from Grommash Hold",b="Nazgrel sends you from Grommash Hold into the wider Horde lands. The fortress stands at the heart of Orgrimmar, where Nazgrel and the Warchief's council oversee military affairs. You leave Durotar's red canyons behind to learn how far the peoples beneath the red banner are joined.",sp=0},{t="An Alliance of Survival",b="You meet the leaders of the Horde's allied peoples: Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear trolls, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren, and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, queen of the Forsaken. Speaking with each in person lets you see the ties between Kalimdor and Lordaeron, across the Great Sea, where plague has scarred the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Exploring the Horde?",a="You report to Nazgrel in Grommash Hold, then travel across Horde lands to meet Vol'jin, Cairne Bloodhoof and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"what is exploring the horde","exploring the horde quest","what do you do in this quest"}},{q="Who is Nazgrel and where is he located?",a="Nazgrel is an orc warrior and trusted lieutenant in Grommash Hold, within Orgrimmar. He advises the Horde and directs aspiring champions to commanders and allies across its lands.",al={"who is nazgrel","where is nazgrel","nazgrel role"}},{q="Which leaders are you sent to meet?",a="You meet Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear trolls, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren in Mulgore, and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen of the Forsaken in the Undercity.",al={"which leaders do i talk to","leaders to visit","who to speak with"}},{q="Why are adventurers instructed to visit each leader?",a="The Horde's peoples are bound by shared survival and pacts of brotherhood. Meeting Vol'jin, Cairne and Sylvanas shows you the alliance's reach, from Durotar and Mulgore to Lordaeron.",al={"why visit all leaders","purpose of visiting horde leaders","diplomacy reason"}},{q="Where can each of the leaders be found?",a="Vol'jin is among the Darkspear trolls in Orgrimmar's Valley of Spirits. Cairne Bloodhoof holds court atop the mesas of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner resides in the Undercity's Royal Quarter beneath the ruins of Lordaeron.",al={"where are the leaders located","leader locations","how to find voljin cairne sylvanas"}},{q="What is Grommash Hold?",a="Grommash Hold is the central fortress in Orgrimmar's Valley of Strength. Named for Grom Hellscream, it is where Warchief Thrall and his advisors, including Nazgrel, oversee Horde affairs.",al={"what is grommash hold","where is grommash hold","orgrimmar hold"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="You meet the leaders guiding the Horde across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["race:Orc"]="Nazgrel advises Warchief Thrall in Orgrimmar and guides young orc warriors making their mark.",["race:Troll"]="Speaking with Shadow Hunter Vol'jin strengthens the bond between the Darkspear tribe and their orc allies in the Valley of Spirits.",["race:Tauren"]="Meeting High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff brings you to the spiritual heart and strength of the tauren.",["race:Undead"]="Traveling to the Undercity to meet the Dark Lady Sylvanas Windrunner shows the Forsaken's strategic, if tense, alliance with Kalimdor's Horde."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","darkspear","exploring the horde","forsaken","grommash hold","nazgrel","orgrimmar","sylvanas windrunner","tauren","thunder bluff","undercity","vol'jin"},rel={"subzone:grommash-hold","npc:vol-jin","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:sylvanas-windrunner"},m={id=93739},src={"In-game quest text: Exploring the Horde"},fv=true}
E["quest:93740"] = {t="quest",n="Blood for Blood",z="general",s="Several Windshaper novices lie dead in the Highlands, and the Windshapers suspect the Al'Aketh. Ayessa sends you north to the Gustberry Lowlands to answer the killings and bring back the fallen cultists' windstone prayer charms.",h="Several Windshaper novices lie dead in the Highlands, and the Windshapers suspect the Al'Aketh.",sec={{t="A Grim Discovery",b="Several Windshaper novices have been found slain in the Highlands. Proof is scarce, yet the Windshapers suspect the Al'Aketh cultists. Their zealotry makes them the chief suspects. The Windshapers' creed demands an eye for an eye, so they will not let the killings go unanswered.",sp=0},{t="Demands of Retribution",b="Ayessa calls for you to strike at any Al'Aketh you find north in the Gustberry Lowlands. She asks you to bring back the windstone prayer charms carried by the fallen cultists, as proof of their deaths. The Windshapers see this strike as an answer to the cultists' long fanaticism.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are cultists whom the Windshapers regard as bloody zealots. The Windshapers suspect them of slaying novices in the Highlands.",al={"who are the alaketh","alaketh cultists","who are we fighting"}},{q="Why are the Windshapers seeking retribution?",a="Several Windshaper novices were found dead in the Highlands, and the Windshapers suspect the Al'Aketh. Their creed calls for vengeance in return.",al={"why kill alaketh","windshaper novices dead","why revenge"}},{q="Who is Ayessa?",a="Ayessa represents the Windshapers. She directs their retribution against the Al'Aketh and collects the prayer charms taken from fallen cultists.",al={"who is ayessa","quest giver","ayessa windshaper"}},{q="What are the windstone prayer charms?",a="Al'Aketh cultists carry windstone prayer charms. Ayessa asks for them as proof that the cultists responsible for the killings have been slain.",al={"what are windstone charms","prayer charms","proof of the deed"}},{q="Where can the Al'Aketh be found?",a="The Al'Aketh are to the north in the Gustberry Lowlands, where they carry out their practices.",al={"where are the alaketh","where are cultists","gustberry lowlands location"}}},kw={"al'aketh","ayessa","blood for blood","cultists","gustberry lowlands","highlands","windshapers","windstone prayer charms"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=93740},src={"In-game quest text: Blood for Blood"},fv=true}
E["quest:93791"] = {t="quest",n="Speak with Belann",z="general",s="In Valanaar, aspiring spellcasters are sent to meet Belann Windwood. The High Order honors the making of a personal spell focus as a long-held tradition.",h="In Valanaar, aspiring spellcasters are sent to meet Belann Windwood.",sec={{t="A Rite of Growing Power",b="As students grow in command of magic, the High Order turns to an ancient custom to test and strengthen their talents. Making a spell focus of their own, whether a wand or staff, is one of the order’s most enduring traditions. It is for students who wish to bind their growing power to an implement they have made themselves.",sp=0},{t="The Craftsman of Valanaar",b="In Valanaar, initiates seek Belann Windwood for help with this craft. He has spent more than a century forging staves and wands for the High Order. A trusted mentor, Belann welcomes dedicated students and guides them through the craft and history of their magical tools.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Belann Windwood?",a="Belann Windwood is a master craftsman who lives in Valanaar. For more than a century, he has made staves and wands for members of the High Order.",al={"who is belann","belann windwood","belann"}},{q="What is the High Order?",a="The High Order is an ancient organization of practitioners. Its traditions include guiding young spellcasters as they make and master their own spell focuses.",al={"what is the high order","high order lore"}},{q="What is a spell focus?",a="A spell focus, such as a wand or staff, channels magical energies. For developing spellcasters in the High Order, making one is among the oldest rites of passage.",al={"what is a spell focus","purpose of spell focus"}},{q="Where can Valanaar be found?",a="Valanaar is the settlement where Belann Windwood keeps his workshop and helps High Order students craft focuses.",al={"where is valanaar","valanaar location"}},{q="Why are you sent to speak with Belann?",a="As an aspiring wielder of magic, you are sent to study under Belann, whose expertise spans more than a century, and begin shaping your own magical implement.",al={"why visit belann","why craft a focus"}},{q="What happens after you meet Belann?",a="When you introduce yourself to Belann in Valanaar, he welcomes you and prepares to explain the next stages of assembling your spell focus.",al={"what happens next","after speaking with belann"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, making a personal spell focus marks a vital rite of passage and honors the ancient traditions of the High Order."},kw={"belann","belann windwood","high order","spell focus","staves","valanaar","wands"},rel={"quest:92472","quest:92596"},m={id=93791},src={"In-game quest text: Speak with Belann"},fv=true}
E["quest:93797"] = {t="quest",n="Boughs in the Wind",z="general",s="In Valanaar, Belann Windwood guides apprentices through the old rite of shaping a spell focus. They seek a wind-touched branch near Falaath Village, as fewer people in Zephras are born with a gift for magic.",h="In Valanaar, Belann Windwood guides apprentices through the old rite of shaping a spell focus.",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage",b="Young spellcasters in Zephras begin this old rite when they show a gift for magic. In Valanaar, Belann Windwood guides apprentices as they gather a fallen branch from the region’s oldest trees. The boughs keep traces of wind, making them fit to channel spells.",sp=0},{t="Whispers at the Wind Shrine",b="The strongest boughs lie near the wind shrine just south of Falaath Village, where elemental currents gather. Tradition says a branch may choose its spellcaster, and some say it sings softly to those able to wield its power. For elders like Belann, each apprentice who completes the rite helps carry their culture forward. Fewer people in Zephras are born with a gift for magic each passing year.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Boughs in the Wind?",a="Apprentices gather a wind-touched fallen branch to shape a spell focus. This old rite helps preserve Zephras’s magical heritage.",al={"what is boughs in the wind","boughs in the wind quest","spell focus quest"}},{q="Who is Belann Windwood?",a="Belann Windwood is a mentor in Valanaar who guides young spellcasters as they shape their spell focuses. He worries that fewer people show a gift for magic each passing year.",al={"who is belann windwood","belann questgiver","belann valanaar"}},{q="Where do you find the Wind-Infused Bough?",a="The boughs fall from the oldest trees near the wind shrine just south of Falaath Village. Belann directs apprentices there because ancient wood retains traces of elemental currents.",al={"where to find wind bough","where is wind-infused bough","bough location"}},{q="Why do young spellcasters create a spell focus?",a="Apprentices shape their own spell focus after showing a gift for magic. It channels elemental energies and marks their passage into an old cultural rite.",al={"why make spell focus","purpose of spell focus","spell focus lore"}},{q="Why does Belann ask if the branch sang to you?",a="Belann Windwood says tradition holds that a branch often chooses its spellcaster. A fitting bough may seem to sing to someone sensitive to elemental winds.",al={"branch picks the caster","singing branch","does the branch choose you"}},{q="What is happening to magic in Zephras?",a="Fewer people in Zephras are born with a natural gift for magic each passing year. Belann believes keeping the old rites alive matters all the more.",al={"where is zephras","magic fading zephras","zephras magic lore"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As fewer youths show a gift for magic each passing year, shaping a traditional spell focus helps preserve your people’s ancestral culture in Zephras.",["class:Mage"]="Shaping a spell focus from a wind-touched bough marks an early rite for spellcasters learning to channel elemental resonance."},kw={"belann windwood","boughs in the wind","falaath village","spell focus","valanaar","wind shrine","wind-infused bough","zephras"},rel={"quest:92529"},m={id=93797},src={"In-game quest text: Boughs in the Wind"},fv=true}
E["quest:93835"] = {t="quest",n="Confront Lorthuna",z="general",s="An injured mage needs you to follow Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale through a portal to the Rohashi Spires. There, they face High Priestess Lorthuna and need your help.",h="An injured mage needs you to follow Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale through a portal to the Rohashi...",sec={{t="Through the portal",b="Lorthuna fled through a portal to the spires above. Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale followed moments ago, but their companion is too badly hurt to go with them. Join the two mages through the portal as they face the High Priestess.",sp=0},{t="After the clash",b="The remaining mages were sent to help Valennia and her forces escape. Valennia is resting at High Elder Shadowsong's residence. Ansirem has returned to Azeroth after discussing the High Order's next steps. Zephras survived the day, but remains dangerously vulnerable, and plans must be made for what comes next.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to go after Lorthuna?",a="An injured mage asks you to follow Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale through the portal and help them face High Priestess Lorthuna.",al={"who sent me","injured mage","who needs help"}},{q="Where did Lorthuna go?",a="Lorthuna fled through a portal to the Rohashi Spires overhead.",al={"where is lorthuna","lorthuna portal","where'd she flee"}},{q="Who went after Lorthuna?",a="Ayessa Dawnsinger and Elaadrin Evengale went through the portal after Lorthuna.",al={"ayessa and elaadrin","who followed her","who's up there"}},{q="Why can't the injured mage go with them?",a="The mage took too many hits and must stay behind.",al={"why am i going alone","mage too hurt","why stay behind"}},{q="Where is Valennia?",a="Valennia is resting at High Elder Shadowsong's residence after the remaining mages helped her and her forces escape.",al={"valennia location","where did she go","valennia safe"}},{q="What is at risk now?",a="Zephras is dangerously vulnerable, and the High Order must plan for what comes next.",al={"zephras vulnerable","what happened to zephras","high order next"}}},kw={"ayessa dawnsinger","elaadrin evengale","high order","lorthuna","portal","rohashi spires","valennia","zephras"},rel={"npc:ayessa-dawnsinger","quest:92596"},m={id=93835},src={"In-game quest text: Confront Lorthuna"},fv=true}
E["quest:93836"] = {t="quest",n="The Fate of Zephras",z="general",s="After Lorthuna falls, word of danger reaches Valanaar. Zephras Isle's wards have failed, and Ayessa proposes a journey to Azeroth. High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong must hear what has happened.",h="After Lorthuna falls, word of danger reaches Valanaar.",sec={{t="A Precarious Victory",b="Lorthuna has fallen after the battle above the Shrine of Akir. Yet Zephras Isle has paid dearly for the victory. Its ancient wards, which hid it from Al'Akir, have collapsed. The floating sanctuary now lies exposed in the Windlord's domain. A messenger crosses the isle with word for Valanaar.",sp=0},{t="The Word to Valanaar",b="High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong hears that Lorthuna is dead and the wards have broken. He also learns that Ayessa means to lead an expedition to Azeroth. With their magical veil gone, the islanders look beyond their home for a way to protect it. Shadowsong weighs fear against hope. Al'Akir is a terrible menace, yet elemental gods move across vast stretches of time. That may leave the islanders precious time to act.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest The Fate of Zephras about?",a="Lorthuna has fallen at the Shrine of Akir, and word of the danger goes to Valanaar. The island's protective wards have failed, and Ayessa proposes an expedition to Azeroth. High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong receives the news.",al={"fate of zephras quest","what is the fate of zephras","what happens in the fate of zephras"}},{q="Why is Zephras Isle in danger from Al'Akir?",a="Zephras Isle lies within the elemental domain of Al'Akir, the Windlord. Ancient wards once hid the sanctuary from his sight. Now they are gone, leaving the island exposed should Al'Akir turn his wrath upon it.",al={"why is zephras in danger","why attack zephras","zephras elemental threat"}},{q="Who is Talaanis Shadowsong?",a="Talaanis Shadowsong is the High Elder of the Skyborne in Valanaar on Zephras Isle. He oversees his people's rule and survival, and receives reports of threats and choices facing their home.",al={"who is talaanis shadowsong","high elder shadowsong","where is talaanis shadowsong"}},{q="What is Ayessa's plan?",a="Ayessa plans to lead an expedition from Zephras Isle to Azeroth. She seeks answers and allies beyond their domain to help protect her people from Al'Akir.",al={"who is ayessa","ayessa plan","what is ayessa doing"}},{q="Who was Lorthuna and what happened to her?",a="Lorthuna led the cult on Zephras Isle. She was slain above the Shrine of Akir, breaking the cult's leadership as the island's protective wards fell.",al={"who is lorthuna","cult leader lorthuna","what happened to lorthuna"}},{q="Who is Muln?",a="Muln is an advisor or scholar who knows about the elemental lords. He warned Zephras's defenders of Al'Akir's great power and danger, and confirmed their vulnerability without magical concealment.",al={"who is muln","muln lore"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne of Zephras Isle, you face the loss of the wards and the threat of Al'Akir. Your people now look beyond their floating home for a way to protect it.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you know the Windlord Al'Akir's elemental domain surrounds Zephras. With the ancient wards gone, the island lies exposed."},kw={"al'akir","ayessa","fate of zephras","lorthuna","muln","shrine of akir","talaanis shadowsong","valanaar","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras"},m={id=93836},src={"In-game quest text: The Fate of Zephras"},fv=true}
E["quest:93926"] = {t="quest",n="The Western Watch",z="general",s="The Western Watch is an urgent mission to recall Peacekeeper Vaaniel and the garrison from an isolated southwest outpost under siege by cultists. Recognizing that holding the fortification is not worth the lives it demands, local leadership seeks to pull defenders back to safeguard the main settlement.",h="The Western Watch is an urgent mission to recall Peacekeeper Vaaniel and the garrison from an isolated...",sec={{t="A Costly Outpost",b="The western watchtower stands southwest of the settlement as an outpost guarding the frontier. Facing renewed pressure from hostile cultists, local leaders judged the isolated structure too costly to defend. Rather than sacrifice more lives trying to maintain an exposed position, orders were drafted to recall the peacekeepers and concentrate all available military forces on shielding the village and its citizens.",sp=0},{t="Too Late to Retreat",b="Peacekeeper Vaaniel led the garrison assigned to hold the western watch against incoming threats. Unfortunately, the withdrawal directive arrived too late. By the time messengers reached the fortification, the cult's storm-wielding spellcasters had already overrun the tower, leaving Peacekeeper Vaaniel and his cohort dead amid scorched stone and lightning burns.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Peacekeeper Vaaniel?",a="Peacekeeper Vaaniel is the officer in charge of the peacekeepers holding the western watchtower. With cultists attacking the tower, you are sent to find him and tell him to bring his garrison back to the village as quickly as possible.",al={"who is vaaniel","vaaniel lore","peacekeeper vaaniel"}},{q="Why were the peacekeepers ordered to abandon the watchtower?",a="Local leadership recognized that holding the outlying western watchtower was costing too many lives against relentless cult assaults. The decision was made to withdraw the garrison so defenders could consolidate at the village and protect its civilian population.",al={"why abandon tower","why leave watchtower","western watch order"}},{q="What killed Peacekeeper Vaaniel?",a="Peacekeeper Vaaniel was slain by storm-wielding cult spellcasters. Fresh burn and scorch marks left across his body reveal that lightning and raw elemental magic were used to strike down the tower's defenders.",al={"how did vaaniel die","who killed vaaniel","peacekeeper killed by what"}},{q="Who attacked the western watchtower?",a="The assault was mounted by a hostile cult employing destructive storm magic. Their exact motives for taking the watchtower remain unclear to local commanders, but their aggressive offensive posed an immediate danger to nearby settlements.",al={"who is attacking the tower","cult attack western tower","what cult is this"}},{q="Where is the western watchtower located?",a="The watchtower stands to the southwest of the local settlement. It serves as an outpost guarding the western approaches, though its isolated position made it vulnerable to coordinated cult assaults.",al={"where is the watchtower","western watchtower location"}},{q="What happened to the garrison at the tower?",a="By the time runners arrived with orders to evacuate back to the settlement, the watchtower had already fallen. Peacekeeper Vaaniel and his outpost defenders were overwhelmed and killed by the cult's spellcasters shortly before aid could arrive.",al={"what happens at the tower","watchtower outcome","did any peacekeepers survive"}}},kw={"cult","peacekeeper vaaniel","peacekeepers","storm spellcasters","the western watch","watchtower","western watchtower"},m={id=93926},src={"In-game quest text: The Western Watch"},fv=true}
E["quest:93927"] = {t="quest",n="A Last Request",z="general",s="A Last Request begins upon discovering the corpse of a fallen peacekeeper in the field. Clutched in the dead guard's hands is a bloodstained note containing vital clues about his final moments. Reading his dying words offers the only way to carry out his unfinished duty and bring closure to his fate.",h="A Last Request begins upon discovering the corpse of a fallen peacekeeper in the field.",sec={{t="A Grim Discovery",b="A Last Request begins with a grim discovery in the field: the lifeless body of a fallen peacekeeper. Slumped in death, the guardian still clutches a bloodstained note that slips free upon inspection. In these turbulent days on Zephras, peacekeepers often pay the ultimate price trying to maintain order, and recovering their dying words is frequently the only way to uncover what befell them.",sp=0},{t="The Final Message",b="The bloodied parchment pried from the peacekeeper's hands contains his final plea or instructions. Whoever ended the guard's life left his duty unfinished, leaving it to whoever finds the body to interpret the clues and see his final mission completed. Reading the note provides the critical direction needed to proceed and answer the fallen sentinel's dying call.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the dead peacekeeper?",a="The fallen peacekeeper's exact identity remains unstated in the initial discovery. What is evident is his role as a keeper of order whose duty ended violently, leaving behind only the urgent message clutched in his grip.",al={"who is the dead peacekeeper","dead peacekeeper","peacekeeper identity"}},{q="What is written on the bloody note?",a="The bloody note is the peacekeeper's last account. The cult struck his post by surprise: Raani never saw it coming, Arvensus took a sword to the gut, and he himself was felled by a lightning bolt and left for dead, expecting his wounds to finish what the cult began.",al={"what is the bloody note","what does the note say","peacekeeper note"}},{q="How is this request discovered?",a="This task begins directly upon discovering and examining the lifeless body of a peacekeeper. Searching the fallen defender reveals the bloody note clutched in his lifeless hands, outlining his final plea.",al={"how to start a last request","where is a last request found"}},{q="What happened to the peacekeeper?",a="The cult ambushed his post without warning. His fellow peacekeepers fell, and he was struck by a lightning bolt and left for dead. He lived just long enough to set down what happened in the note found clutched in his hands.",al={"why was peacekeeper killed","who killed peacekeeper"}},{q="What is the peacekeeper's last request?",a="The peacekeeper died before he could finish his mission or deliver his message himself. By retrieving and inspecting his bloodstained note, an adventurer can decipher his final instructions and see his dying wish fulfilled.",al={"what is the peacekeeper's request","last request purpose"}},{q="What role do peacekeepers serve?",a="Peacekeepers serve as protectors tasked with maintaining order and defending settlements and roads from danger. Falling in the field leaves their duties unfinished and their allies unwarned unless someone recovers their final reports.",al={"who are peacekeepers","peacekeeper lore"}}},kw={"a last request","bloody note","dead peacekeeper","peacekeeper","peacekeeper note"},m={id=93927},src={"In-game quest text: A Last Request"},fv=true}
E["quest:93948"] = {t="quest",n="Deliver the Signet",z="general",s="Deliver the Signet is a quest in which a traveler carries a recovered family ring to High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong in Valanaar. The delivery brings tragic confirmation of his nephew's demise on Zephras Isle, while opening new opportunities to assist the local peacekeepers.",h="Deliver the Signet is a quest in which a traveler carries a recovered family ring to High Elder Talaanis...",sec={{t="A Somber Errand",b="When an adventurer journeys toward the settlement of Valanaar, they carry with them the Shadowsong Family Signet recovered from an isolated tower. Entrusted with this grim token, travelers seek out Talaanis Shadowsong, the High Elder of Zephras Isle. Beloved by his people despite widespread local hardships, Talaanis must face the painful confirmation that his nephew has perished, receiving condolences alongside the reclaimed heirloom.",sp=0},{t="Grief and Duty in Valanaar",b="Upon receiving the signet ring in Valanaar, High Elder Talaanis expresses heartfelt gratitude despite his profound grief. Recognizing the skill of the traveler who retrieved the relic, Talaanis directs them to Captain Valennia Stormfist, leader of the Valanaar peacekeepers. Facing strained circumstances and mounting pressures, the settlement welcomes any aid capable protectors can offer.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Talaanis Shadowsong?",a="Talaanis Shadowsong serves as the High Elder of Zephras Isle. Widely respected and loved by his community, he guides his people through turbulent times and endures personal tragedy when presented with the signet ring confirming his nephew's death.",al={"who is talaanis","high elder talaanis","talaanis shadowsong lore"}},{q="What is the Shadowsong Family Signet?",a="The signet is the Shadowsong Family Signet, recovered from a tower. It serves as grim confirmation that Talaanis Shadowsong's nephew has perished, returning a family heirloom to the grieving High Elder.",al={"what is the signet ring","nephews ring","shadowsong signet"}},{q="Where is Valanaar located?",a="Valanaar is a settlement located on Zephras Isle where High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong resides and where Captain Valennia Stormfist oversees the local peacekeepers.",al={"where is valanaar","valanaar settlement","talaanis location"}},{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist is the captain of the peacekeepers stationed in Valanaar. High Elder Talaanis recommends speaking to her for work, noting that the settlement is in dire need of capable hands.",al={"who is valennia stormfist","captain valennia","valanaar peacekeeper captain"}},{q="Who are the shen'dorei mentioned by Talaanis?",a="Talaanis refers to capable young travelers coming down from the highlands as shen'dorei, highlighting the elven presence and heritage enduring on Zephras Isle.",al={"who are the shendorei","shendorei highlands"}},{q="What happened to Talaanis Shadowsong's nephew?",a="Talaanis's nephew died at a tower where his family signet was lost. Bringing the signet to Valanaar informs the High Elder of his nephew's demise, bringing closure to a sorrowful loss.",al={"what happened to talaanis nephew","how did nephew die"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The prominent Shadowsong family name evokes deep history among elven lineages, though here on Zephras Isle, the High Elder Talaanis and the local shen'dorei navigate their own distinct struggles."},kw={"deliver the signet","shadowsong family signet","shen'dorei","talaanis shadowsong","valanaar","valennia stormfist","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","quest:92579"},m={id=93948},src={"In-game quest text: Deliver the Signet"},fv=true}
E["quest:93949"] = {t="quest",n="Bugged",z="general",s="Bugged is a quest in Valanaar where an influx of seasonal lowland insects turns out to be an espionage plot. High Order mages discovered that local cultists are weaving listening charms onto skyhoppers to spy on the settlement, requiring an extermination effort to weed out the enchanted pests.",h="Bugged is a quest in Valanaar where an influx of seasonal lowland insects turns out to be an espionage plot.",sec={{t="Eavesdroppers in the Swarm",b="During skyhopper season in the lowlands, swarms of native insects surge around the city of Valanaar. While usually regarded merely as a seasonal annoyance, an inspection by High Order mages revealed that several slain specimens carried sophisticated listening enchantments. A local cult has weaponized the insect swarm, transforming common lowland pests into inconspicuous surveillance tools to eavesdrop on the city.",sp=0},{t="Clearing the Air",b="Because enchanted skyhoppers appear indistinguishable from their mundane brethren, protectors of Valanaar are forced to cull the swarms indiscriminately. Eradicating these bugs denies the hostile cult access to critical intelligence, cutting off magical surveillance and reinforcing the city's defenses against internal subversion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are skyhoppers?",a="Skyhoppers are winged pests native to the lowlands surrounding Valanaar that swarm in great numbers during their seasonal surge.",al={"what are skyhoppers","skyhopper bug"}},{q="Who enchanted the insects?",a="Local cultists are suspected of capturing skyhoppers and placing magical listening spells on them to eavesdrop on the settlement.",al={"who enchanted the bugs","cult listening bugs","who is spying in valanaar"}},{q="How do you tell which skyhoppers are enchanted?",a="Enchanted skyhoppers look identical to ordinary insects to the naked eye. Only by exterminating the swarms can the magically imbued spies be uncovered and eliminated.",al={"how to tell enchanted skyhoppers","which bugs are enchanted"}},{q="Where does this take place?",a="The infestation and espionage occur around Valanaar, an aerial city where seasonal swarms rise from the adjacent lowlands.",al={"where is bugged quest","valanaar bugs"}},{q="What kind of enchantment was placed on the skyhoppers?",a="High Order mages inspected slain insects and detected a specialized listening spell bound to them, effectively converting the creatures into roving surveillance tools.",al={"what spell is on the bugs","skyhopper enchantment"}},{q="Why do all the skyhoppers need to be exterminated?",a="Because enchanted skyhoppers cannot be distinguished visually from ordinary pests, clearing out the swarm is necessary to purge the cult's network of magical listening posts and protect Valanaar.",al={"why kill skyhoppers","bugged quest goal"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="High Order mages uncovered the magical eavesdropping wards woven onto ordinary vermin, turning arcane analysis into counter-espionage."},kw={"bugged","cult","enchanted skyhoppers","high order","listening spell","skyhoppers","valanaar"},rel={"topic:mage"},m={id=93949},src={"In-game quest text: Bugged"},fv=true}
E["quest:93951"] = {t="quest",n="A Little Beauty",z="general",s="A Little Beauty is a local task in the Shen'dar Highlands where travelers gather soft hippogryph down feathers near Zephras. The colorful plumes are used by the Skyborne to adorn their hair, bringing a touch of simple joy and beauty to the village during challenging times.",h="A Little Beauty is a local task in the Shen'dar Highlands where travelers gather soft hippogryph down...",sec={{t="Plumes of the Highlands",b="On Zephras Isle, southwest of the village, wild hippogryphs have established nesting grounds in the Shen'dar Highlands. While these territorial beasts can be an unruly nuisance to nearby travelers, they shed exceptionally vibrant and silky soft down feathers. For the local Skyborne, collecting these discarded plumes presents an opportunity to craft exquisite ornaments woven directly into their hair.",sp=0},{t="Finding Joy in Small Things",b="Though collecting decorative feathers for hair might appear to be nothing more than vanity, the artisan in Zephras views it as an essential pursuit. Even when circumstances are harsh, finding and sharing small everyday joys helps sustain the spirit of the community. Bringing beauty into the lives of the villagers ensures that each day holds something worth celebrating.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are hippogryph feathers being collected?",a="The down feathers of wild hippogryphs are collected to create decorative hair adornments for the local Skyborne. Although hippogryphs near the settlement can be a nuisance, their fallen plumes are prized for being remarkably silky soft and vibrant.",al={"why gather feathers","what are feathers for","feather purpose"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is Zephras Isle, the island home of the Skyborne where this quest takes place. The local artisan crafts hair adornments from the gathered plumage in an effort to bring happiness and lighthearted moments to the residents living there.",al={"who is Zephras","what town is this","where does this happen"}},{q="Where do the hippogryphs nest?",a="The wild hippogryphs nest southwest of the village in the Shen'dar Highlands. Because wild hippogryphs are extremely territorial and do not tolerate unwanted guests, gathering fallen feathers around their nesting grounds requires caution.",al={"where are the nests","where are hippogryphs","shendar highlands location"}},{q="Why are small luxuries like hair adornments important in times like these?",a="While adorning hair with feathers might seem like a vain triviality, the questgiver explains that embracing small joys and beauty is vital, especially during difficult times. Bringing a moment of happiness to others makes the effort worthwhile.",al={"are feathers important","why hair decorations","vain triviality"}},{q="Are the hippogryphs hostile to visitors?",a="The hippogryphs living southwest of the village are wild creatures. They consider their nesting grounds their territory and will not tolerate intruders, making collecting their fallen down feathers hazardous for unwary travelers.",al={"why hippogryphs dangerous","are hippogryphs wild"}},{q="Is decorating hair with feathers common among the Skyborne?",a="Yes, many Skyborne enjoy weaving vibrant plumes into their hair as personal adornments. The exceptionally soft down feathers of hippogryphs make ideal materials for these decorative ornaments.",al={"skyborne feathers","do skyborne wear feathers"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="Gathering vibrant hippogryph down feathers allows the Skyborne of Zephras to adorn their hair with soft plumes, maintaining their cultural customs and finding small joys despite hardship."},kw={"a little beauty","down feathers","feathers","hippogryph","plumes","shen'dar highlands","skyborne","zephras"},rel={"topic:skyborne"},m={id=93951},src={"In-game quest text: A Little Beauty"},fv=true}
E["quest:93958"] = {t="quest",n="The Inner Sanctum",z="general",s="At the Shrine of Akir, Valennia Stormfist's assault has gone silent. Her force breached the deepest chamber amid fierce fighting, and now allies outside fear for those within.",h="At the Shrine of Akir, Valennia Stormfist's assault has gone silent.",sec={{t="A Sudden Silence",b="Valennia Stormfist led her detachment into the inner sanctum of the Shrine of Akir after a hard assault. Fighting sounded from deep within the chambers, fierce and desperate. Then the noise stopped without warning. Allies waiting outside can only fear the worst.",sp=0},{t="The Cost of the Breach",b="Valennia Stormfist is found gravely wounded inside the quiet sanctum. Her vanguard's fate remains uncertain in the aftermath of the battle.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Valennia Stormfist?",a="Valennia Stormfist commands the assault into the depths of the Shrine of Akir. She led her forces through the breach into the inner sanctum, where the fighting soon fell silent.",al={"who is valennia","who is valennia stormfist","valennia"}},{q="What is the Shrine of Akir?",a="The Shrine of Akir holds an inner sanctum now under assault. Valennia's troops broke through its defenses amid heavy fighting, making the shrine the heart of a deadly clash.",al={"what is shrine of akir","shrine of akir lore","akir shrine"}},{q="What happened to Valennia's forces inside?",a="Heavy fighting rang out inside after the breach, then stopped all at once. The silence leaves allies outside fearing Valennia's vanguard may have been overwhelmed or killed.",al={"why is it quiet","what happened to valennias forces","valennia soldiers"}},{q="Is Valennia Stormfist still alive?",a="Valennia Stormfist survives the assault, gravely wounded inside the Shrine of Akir's inner sanctum after the fighting ends.",al={"is valennia alive","did valennia die","valennia fate"}},{q="What is the objective inside the inner sanctum?",a="The inner sanctum is the deepest chamber in the Shrine of Akir. You are sent in alone to assess the aftermath and find Valennia and any fighters who survived.",al={"what is the inner sanctum","inner sanctum quest purpose"}},{q="Who sends you into the inner sanctum?",a="An ally keeping watch at the rear outside the inner sanctum sends you in. They hold the perimeter and watch your back as you go to join Valennia's vanguard.",al={"who sends you to valennia","quest giver inner sanctum"}}},kw={"inner sanctum","shrine of akir","stormfist","the inner sanctum","valennia","valennia stormfist"},m={id=93958},src={"In-game quest text: The Inner Sanctum"},fv=true}
E["quest:94003"] = {t="quest",n="The Skybreaker Bulwark",z="general",s="At the Shrine of Akir, Zaal Stormshield holds the Skybreaker Bulwark. The last of the Skybreakers seeks its return from the cultist who took it.",h="At the Shrine of Akir, Zaal Stormshield holds the Skybreaker Bulwark.",sec={{t="A Fallen Lineage",b="On Zephras Isle, the Al'Aketh have slain the Skybreakers, leaving one survivor. The last of the family bears deep sorrow and bitter shame over the loss of their greatest heirloom. The cultist Zaal Stormshield took the Skybreaker Bulwark and now lurks at the Shrine of Akir.",sp=0},{t="The Sacred Shield",b="By family tradition, the Skybreaker Bulwark protects anyone truly worthy to wield it. The last Skybreaker believes Zaal unworthy of its divine favor. So the survivor seeks an adventurer to strike down the thief at the Shrine of Akir and reclaim the ancestral shield. In return, the Skybreaker offers training in the Skybreaker Defense technique.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Skybreakers?",a="The Skybreakers are an ancient lineage on Zephras Isle, nearly wiped out by the Al'Aketh. The one who asks for the shield is the sole survivor.",al={"who are the skybreakers","skybreaker family"}},{q="What is the Skybreaker Bulwark?",a="The Skybreaker Bulwark is an ancestral shield of the Skybreaker line on Zephras Isle. Family tradition says no harm can befall anyone worthy to wield it.",al={"what is the skybreaker bulwark","the shield","skybreaker shield lore"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh slew the Skybreaker family on Zephras Isle, leaving one survivor to mourn their loss.",al={"who killed the skybreakers","what are the alaketh","alaketh"}},{q="Who is Zaal Stormshield?",a="Zaal Stormshield is a cultist who took the Skybreaker Bulwark after the Skybreaker family was slain. He lurks with the heirloom at the Shrine of Akir on Zephras Isle.",al={"who has the shield","zaal","stormshield"}},{q="Where is the Shrine of Akir?",a="The Shrine of Akir is on Zephras Isle. Zaal Stormshield lurks there with the stolen Skybreaker Bulwark.",al={"where is zaal","shrine of akir location","where to find shield"}},{q="Why must the Bulwark be reclaimed?",a="The last Skybreaker seeks the Bulwark's return to right the family's shame and take the sacred relic from unworthy hands. In return, the survivor teaches the Skybreaker Defense technique.",al={"why recover shield","what is skybreaker defense","shield reward"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, you can learn the Skybreaker Defense technique by recovering the ancestral shield.",["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you may value the work of reclaiming a blessed heirloom from a corrupt cultist."},kw={"al'aketh","shrine of akir","skybreaker","skybreaker bulwark","skybreaker defense","the skybreaker bulwark","zaal stormshield","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras"},m={id=94003},src={"In-game quest text: The Skybreaker Bulwark"},fv=true}
E["quest:94007"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="In Valanaar, Quel'ana Quickgale can teach you to tame a beast. Your time in the highlands has prepared you to seek an animal companion.",h="In Valanaar, Quel'ana Quickgale can teach you to tame a beast.",sec={{t="A New Companion",b="Your host believes you are ready to seek an animal companion after your time in the highlands. Go to Valanaar and speak with Quel'ana Quickgale. He will teach you the art of beast taming.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches beast taming?",a="Quel'ana Quickgale teaches you to tame beasts in Valanaar.",al={"who is Quel'ana Quickgale","beast taming teacher"}},{q="Where do I find Quel'ana Quickgale?",a="Quel'ana Quickgale is in Valanaar, the port to the southeast.",al={"where is Quel'ana","Valanaar taming"}},{q="Why am I going to Valanaar?",a="You are sent to Valanaar to speak with Quel'ana Quickgale and begin learning to tame beasts.",al={"why Valanaar","taming beast quest"}},{q="What am I learning?",a="Quel'ana Quickgale will teach you to tame a beast so you can seek an animal companion.",al={"what is beast taming","animal companion"}}},kw={"animal companion","beast taming","highlands","quel'ana quickgale","taming the beast","valanaar"},m={id=94007},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94013"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="Quel'ana Quickgale gives you one last trial in taming a Vuldren Alpha. Mastering it prepares you for the deeper teachings of your class.",h="Quel'ana Quickgale gives you one last trial in taming a Vuldren Alpha.",sec={{t="The Final Trial",b="Quel'ana gives you a Taming Rod and sends you to the Gustberry Lowlands. There, you are to tame a Vuldren Alpha. Then return the rod to him in Valanaar. This trial proves your mastery and prepares you for deeper class teachings.",sp=0},{t="A New Lesson",b="After you return, Quel'ana says you have learned the basics. He offers to teach you to form a lasting bond with an animal companion. He urges you to carry that knowledge forward for the good of the class Zephras Isle needs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What does Quel'ana ask me to tame?",a="Quel'ana asks you to tame a Vuldren Alpha with the Taming Rod in the Gustberry Lowlands.",al={"vuldren alpha taming","what beast","tame alpha"}},{q="Where do I find the Vuldren Alpha?",a="The Vuldren Alpha is in the Gustberry Lowlands.",al={"alpha location","gustberry lowlands","where tame beast"}},{q="What is the Taming Rod for?",a="Quel'ana gives you the Taming Rod to tame a Vuldren Alpha.",al={"rod purpose","taming rod quest","how use rod"}},{q="Where do I return the Taming Rod?",a="Return the Taming Rod to Quel'ana Quickgale in Valanaar.",al={"return rod","quelana location","where return taming rod"}},{q="What will I learn after this?",a="Quel'ana says he can teach you to form a lasting bond with an animal companion.",al={"next lesson","animal companion bond","what comes after"}}},kw={"animal companion","gustberry lowlands","quel'ana quickgale","taming rod","taming the beast","valanaar","vuldren alpha","zephras isle"},rel={"item:taming-rod","zone:zephras"},m={id=94013},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94050"] = {t="quest",n="Training the Beast",z="general",s="In Valanaar, you meet Quel'dora Quickgale to learn how to train your pet. Her teaching helps you care for and guide it.",h="In Valanaar, you meet Quel'dora Quickgale to learn how to train your pet.",sec={{t="Quel'dora's Teaching",b="Your fellow hunter sends you to his sister, Quel'dora Quickgale, in Valanaar. You already know how to tame a pet. Now Quel'dora can teach you to train your companion.",sp=0},{t="A Hunter's Charge",b="Quel'dora teaches you to guide your pet, teach it new abilities, feed it, and revive it if it falls in battle. She urges you to respect the balance and remain in awe of the nature around you.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Quel'dora Quickgale?",a="Quel'dora Quickgale is the sister of the hunter who sends you to learn pet training.",al={"who is queldora","quel'dora","quickgale"}},{q="Where can I find Quel'dora?",a="Quel'dora Quickgale is in Valanaar.",al={"where queldora","quel'dora location","valanaar trainer"}},{q="What does Quel'dora teach?",a="Quel'dora teaches you to train and guide your pet, teach it abilities, feed it, and revive it if it falls in battle.",al={"pet training","train my pet","what can pet trainer teach"}},{q="Can I already tame a pet?",a="The hunter who sends you to Quel'dora says you already have the power to tame a pet.",al={"tame pet","pet taming","can hunter tame"}},{q="What does the hunter ask me to do?",a="The hunter asks you to speak with Quel'dora Quickgale in Valanaar.",al={"training the beast","pet trainer quest","speak to queldora"}},{q="What does Quel'dora say about nature?",a="Quel'dora urges you to respect the balance and live in awe of the nature around you.",al={"respect nature","balance","quel'dora advice"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you are learning to train and guide a pet you can already tame."},kw={"feed pet","hunter","pet training","quel'dora quickgale","revive pet","tame pet","training the beast","valanaar"},m={id=94050},src={"In-game quest text: Training the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94222"] = {t="quest",n="Craftsman's Writ: Iridescent Hammer",z="general",s="A customer awaits one Iridescent Hammer, with delivery and payment details set down in a writ. Its transferable mark lets the holder complete the exchange.",h="A customer awaits one Iridescent Hammer, with delivery and payment details set down in a writ.",sec={{t="The Writ",b="The customer asks for one Iridescent Hammer. Bring the writ to complete the exchange at the marked location.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the customer want?",a="One Iridescent Hammer is what the customer wants.",al={"iridescent hammer writ","what item is requested"}},{q="Where do I deliver it?",a="The writ gives the delivery details, and the location is marked on your map.",al={"writ delivery place","where to take hammer"}},{q="Does the writ need to be with me?",a="Yes. Keep the writ with you to complete the exchange.",al={"need writ to deliver","writ required"}},{q="Can someone else use the writ?",a="Yes. The writ is marked as transferable.",al={"transferable writ","pass writ along"}},{q="Does the writ say how payment works?",a="Yes. The writ includes payment information.",al={"hammer payment","how much payment"}}},kw={"craftsman's writ","delivery","hammer","iridescent hammer","payment","transferable","writ"},m={id=94222},src={"In-game quest text: Craftsman's Writ: Iridescent Hammer"},fv=true}
E["quest:94243"] = {t="quest",n="Craftsman's Writ: Heavy Quiver",z="general",s="A faded Craftsman's Writ: Heavy Quiver asks you to bring one Heavy Quiver to a marked location and gives payment details.",h="A faded Craftsman's Writ: Heavy Quiver asks you to bring one Heavy Quiver to a marked location and gives...",sec={{t="The Writ",b="The note asks you to acquire one Heavy Quiver and bring it to the place marked on your map. Keep the writ with you to complete the exchange.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the writ request?",a="The customer requests one Heavy Quiver.",al={"heavy quiver request","what item is wanted"}},{q="Where do I deliver it?",a="The delivery place is marked on your map.",al={"delivery location","where to take quiver"}},{q="Do I need to carry the writ?",a="Yes. Carry the writ with you to complete the exchange.",al={"need the writ","writ required"}},{q="Can I give this writ to someone else?",a="Yes. The writ can be transferred to someone else.",al={"transfer writ","can someone else use it"}},{q="Does the writ mention payment?",a="The writ gives payment details, but no amount is stated.",al={"how much payment","quiver payment"}}},kw={"craftsman's writ","delivery","heavy quiver","payment","quiver","transferable"},m={id=94243},src={"In-game quest text: Craftsman's Writ: Heavy Quiver"},fv=true}
E["quest:94253"] = {t="quest",n="Craftsman's Writ: Colorful Kilt",z="general",s="A customer seeks a disguise for slipping into Zul'Gurub, and Craftsman's Writ: Colorful Kilt names the garment they want.",h="A customer seeks a disguise for slipping into Zul'Gurub, and Craftsman's Writ: Colorful Kilt names the...",sec={{t="A disguise",b="The transferable writ asks you to bring one Colorful Kilt to the marked location. The customer plans to pass through Zul'Gurub's gates dressed as one of its ritualists, and says the kilt suits the disguise.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the writ ask for?",a="The writ requests one Colorful Kilt, to be delivered to the location marked on your map.",al={"colorful kilt writ","what item is needed"}},{q="Why does the customer want a colorful kilt?",a="The customer plans to sneak into Zul'Gurub dressed as one of its ritualists and says the kilt will help.",al={"why kilt","kilt disguise"}},{q="Can I transfer this writ?",a="Yes. The writ says it is transferable.",al={"transfer writ","give writ away"}},{q="Where should I deliver the kilt?",a="The writ directs you to the location marked on your map.",al={"delivery place","where to bring it"}}},kw={"colorful kilt","craftsman's writ","delivery","disguise","ritualist","zul'gurub"},rel={"subzone:zul-gurub"},m={id=94253},src={"In-game quest text: Craftsman's Writ: Colorful Kilt"},fv=true}
E["quest:94369"] = {t="quest",n="The Fate of Zephras",z="general",s="In Valanaar, you bring Talaanis Shadowsong word of the danger facing Zephras. The fallen wards leave the isle exposed to Al'Akir, and its people must decide how to protect their home.",h="In Valanaar, you bring Talaanis Shadowsong word of the danger facing Zephras.",sec={{t="A warning for Talaanis",b="You tell Talaanis what happened above the Shrine of Akir. You share Elaadrin's plan to leave Zephras Isle. High Elder Shadowsong needs to understand the danger and why you were sent to Azeroth. The people of Zephras must find a way to save their home.",sp=0},{t="What remains",b="Talaanis weighs the news. Lorthuna is gone, and the cult has lost its leader. Zephras has not been invaded. Yet Al'Akir may turn his attention to the isle in years or decades, and fear now stands beside cause for celebration.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who do I speak with?",a="You speak with Talaanis Shadowsong in Valanaar.",al={"who to talk to","talaanis","where do i report"}},{q="What do I tell Talaanis?",a="You tell Talaanis what happened above the Shrine of Akir and of Elaadrin's plan to leave Zephras Isle.",al={"what happened at shrine","what do i report","shrine of akir news"}},{q="Why are the people of Zephras afraid of Al'Akir?",a="The wards that hid Zephras have fallen. Al'Akir may choose to attack while the people are in his domain.",al={"why fear al'akir","windlord danger","al akir threat"}},{q="What does High Elder Shadowsong need to know?",a="High Elder Shadowsong needs to know of the danger and what the people of Zephras intend to do on their mission to Azeroth.",al={"what talaanis needs","high elder news","intentions for azeroth"}}},kw={"al'akir","elaadrin","lorthuna","shrine of akir","talaanis shadowsong","valanaar","wards","windlord","zephras"},rel={"zone:zephras"},m={id=94369},src={"In-game quest text: The Fate of Zephras"},fv=true}
E["quest:94373"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="The Call of Earth is a sacred trial overseen by Teo Hammerstorm in Anvilmar, testing initiates in the patience and resilience of stone. Aspirants must confront the Frostmane trolls to the south to claim their bear pendants before being granted an earthen sapta.",h="The Call of Earth is a sacred trial overseen by Teo Hammerstorm in Anvilmar, testing initiates in the...",sec={{t="The Wisdom of Stone",b="At Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley, the mentor Teo Hammerstorm watches over initiates ready to walk the rugged path of the earth. Viewing the land as a stout and unyielding foundation, Teo guides young wanderers to heed the wisdom of the mountains. He warns that while stone serves as an enduring shield, its favor is not given lightly, requiring true grit and deep respect before any secrets of the soil will be shared.",sp=0},{t="Trial in the Frostmane Den",b="To demonstrate readiness, an initiate must face the Frostmane trolls lurking in a cave to the south. These trolls fashion coarse pendants from the ferocious Iceclaw bears that roam the frozen heights. Reclaiming two of these bear pendants from the troll den serves as tangible proof that the petitioner possesses the stubborn resolve and fortitude expected by the elemental spirits.",sp=0},{t="The Earthen Sapta",b="Upon presenting the pendants to Teo Hammerstorm, the elder commends the initiate's patience—the earth's finest virtue, which outlasts crumbling peaks and carved river valleys. In recognition of this proven respect, Teo sets to work preparing an earthen sapta, an essential catalyst enabling the initiate to perceive and walk the true path of the elements.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who offers the Call of Earth quest?",a="Teo Hammerstorm, an elder guide located in Anvilmar within Coldridge Valley, offers the trial. He instructs young initiates in the ways of stone and charges them to prove their resolve before the spirits of the earth.",al={"who gives call of earth","quest giver call of earth","teo hammerstorm quest"}},{q="What does Teo Hammerstorm ask you to do?",a="You must venture south of Anvilmar to the cave inhabited by Frostmane trolls and recover two Frostmane Bear Pendants, which the trolls craft from Iceclaw bears. Delivering these to Teo Hammerstorm fulfills the trial.",al={"call of earth objectives","what do i need for call of earth","frostmane pendants quest"}},{q="Where do you find the Frostmane Bear Pendants?",a="The pendants are carried by the Frostmane trolls living in a cave situated to the south of Anvilmar. These talismans are crafted from local Iceclaw bears and worn by the trolls dwelling within the den.",al={"where are frostmane bear pendants","how to get frostmane bear pendants","frostmane cave anvilmar"}},{q="What reward does Teo Hammerstorm prepare upon completion?",a="A sapta is a ceremonial mixture or draught prepared by spiritual mentors to attune a practitioner to the elemental spirits. Upon completing this trial, Teo crafts an earthen sapta to allow you to walk the true path of the earth.",al={"what is a sapta","earth sapta dwarf","what does teo hammerstorm craft"}},{q="Why must initiates face the Frostmane trolls for this trial?",a="Confronting the Frostmane trolls demonstrates the physical grit and spiritual resilience required to commune with the earth. By claiming the bear pendants from hostile trolls in their den, the initiate proves worthy of the mountain's enduring favor.",al={"why kill frostmane trolls","why frostmane pendants"}},{q="What does Teo Hammerstorm teach about the nature of earth?",a="Teo teaches that patience is the earth's greatest virtue, noting how it endures while mountains crumble and rivers slowly carve valleys. He emphasizes that stone shields those who respect it, but warns that even bedrock can shift if slighted.",al={"earth virtues teo hammerstorm","wisdom of the mountain"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="The Call of Earth marks an early initiation into communion with the spirits of stone and soil, culminating in the preparation of an earthen sapta.",["race:Dwarf"]="Offered in Coldridge Valley near Anvilmar, this trial connects dwarven fortitude and mountain heritage with the enduring patience of the earth itself.",["faction:Alliance"]="This rite tests fledgling defenders against the encroaching Frostmane trolls dwelling in the caves south of Anvilmar."},kw={"anvilmar","call of earth","coldridge valley","earth totem","frostmane","frostmane bear pendant","iceclaw bear","sapta","shaman","teo hammerstorm"},rel={"subzone:anvilmar","topic:troll"},m={id=94373},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:94374"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="The Call of Earth is a sacred shamanic rite that binds an initiate's mortal spirit to the enduring foundations of the world. By imbibing the visionary brew known as Earth Sapta at a secluded mountain Spirit Stone, the seeker learns humility, patience, and elemental awareness amid the snow and rock.",h="The Call of Earth is a sacred shamanic rite that binds an initiate's mortal spirit to the enduring...",sec={{t="The Draught of Vision",b="The Call of Earth is a sacred trial of spiritual attunement, requiring an initiate to drink the enchanted brew known as Earth Sapta at an isolated mountain shrine. Forged through ancient elemental magic, sapta binds the drinker's spirit to the bedrock of the world, granting mortal eyes the rare power to witness elemental spirits and subtle currents invisible to ordinary sight. Because of its potency, the draught must only be consumed in a designated holy site where the bond between spirit and stone can safely take hold.",sp=0},{t="The Spirit Stone in the Snow",b="High up a snow-swept mountain, tucked away at the terminus of a secluded hidden path, rests the Spirit Stone. Initiates journey into this bitter, frozen landscape to stand humbly before the ancient rock. By presenting themselves without pride and imbibing the sapta, they discover that even in the harshest winter crags, the living earth extends its welcome. The mountains become a foundation of enduring physical strength, while the vast, steady plains teach the quiet power of patience.",sp=0},{t="Strength and Stillness",b="Communing with stone serves as a foundational rite for spiritual pathfinders. Unlike the restless fury of wind, fire, or crashing water, the earth teaches unmoving discipline and quiet resilience. To pass the rite, the seeker must absorb the earth's quiet essence, preparing their mind and body for the conflicting, chaotic dualities of the more volatile elements that roar across the world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Earth Sapta?",a="Earth Sapta is a potent mystical brew crafted with magic to bind a practitioner's spirit directly to the elements. It is not intended for the weak-willed, as drinking it pierces ordinary mortal sight and reveals spirits and elemental truths that others cannot perceive.",al={"what is sapta","earth sapta lore","what does sapta do"}},{q="Where must the sapta be consumed?",a="The brew must be consumed only at a dedicated sacred site. In this rite, the practitioner is directed north up the mountain along a hidden path to locate the ancient Spirit Stone tucked away in the crags.",al={"where do i drink sapta","spirit stone location","where is the spirit stone"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Call of Earth rite?",a="The Call of Earth is a foundational trial of communion and humility. By drinking the sapta at the Spirit Stone, the initiate learns to draw strength from the mountains, patience from the plains, and attune their mortal spirit to the living world before facing more turbulent elements.",al={"why do this quest","meaning of call of earth","call of earth lore"}},{q="Who knows how to brew Earth Sapta?",a="Only a select few initiated shamans possess the knowledge and spiritual preparation required to brew sapta. Its recipe is guarded and treated with great reverence, as the draught connects mortal awareness directly to primal forces.",al={"who knows sapta recipe","can anyone make sapta"}},{q="How does the element of earth differ from the other elements?",a="While other elemental forces clash, burn, and roar with violent unrest, the earth element represents steadfast stillness, patience, and unyielding foundation. Initiates must first learn humility before the stone before confronting the volatile dual nature of the other elements.",al={"earth vs other elements","how does earth compare to fire water air"}},{q="What setting is this rite performed in?",a="The quest takes place high in a cold, snowbound mountain range, where initiates follow an secluded path winding through the snow and stone to commune with the spirits of the land despite the biting frost.",al={"where is call of earth set","frozen land earth quest"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite represents an essential communion for shamans, binding spirit to stone and granting vision beyond mortal perception through the ritual draught of sapta.",["race:Dwarf"]="Spoken in the rough cadence of dwarven speech amid frozen mountain peaks, this sacred communion ties communion with the earth directly to Dun Morogh's snowy heights.",["faction:Alliance"]="The presence of an elemental rite in the snowy mountain crags shows shamanic traditions taking root within Alliance lands."},kw={"call of earth","dun morogh","earth sapta","elemental rite","hidden path","sapta","shaman","spirit stone"},rel={"quest:1462","subzone:hidden-path","quest:99141"},m={id=94374},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:94375"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="Call of Earth is an elemental rite of passage for young shaman in the snowy reaches of Dun Morogh. By communing with the local earth spirits, initiates receive a rough quartz to bring to Teo Hammerstorm in Anvilmar. This simple stone proves their communion and serves as the core of their Earth Totem.",h="Call of Earth is an elemental rite of passage for young shaman in the snowy reaches of Dun Morogh.",sec={{t="The Whispering Stone",b="The Call of Earth marks an initiate's first steps onto the spiritual path of the shaman in Dun Morogh. Deep within the frozen peaks, an aspirant must seek out the spirit of the earth and listen to its teachings. The trial demands humility: the spirit gifts only a rough quartz, a simple pebble intended to become the beating heart of an Earth Totem. It reminds the seeker that true endurance is not measured by sheer scale, but by patience, strength, and unwavering resolve.",sp=0},{t="The Mountain's Favor",b="Upon returning to Anvilmar with the stone in hand, Teo Hammerstorm judges the initiate's worthiness. As a craftsman and guide, Teo requires unmistakable evidence that the mountain spirits have acknowledged the seeker. Without the rough quartz, the initiate is turned away without ceremony; with it, Teo shapes the raw stone into an Earth Totem, binding the young shaman to the timeless strength of the world's roots.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Rough Quartz for?",a="The rough quartz is given by an earth elemental spirit as proof of communion. Though it appears to be a modest pebble, it serves as the essential heart for crafting your Earth Totem.",al={"rough quartz purpose","what is rough quartz","why do i need rough quartz"}},{q="Who is Teo Hammerstorm?",a="Teo Hammerstorm is a mentor located in Anvilmar within Dun Morogh. He tests aspiring shaman, requiring proof of elemental communion before fashioning their sacred totems.",al={"who is teo hammerstorm","teo hammerstorm location","where is teo"}},{q="What is the Call of Earth?",a="Call of Earth is a sacred trial undertaken by young shaman to seek communion with the earth spirits, demonstrate patience and strength, and earn their first Earth Totem.",al={"call of earth questline","what is call of earth","earth totem quest"}},{q="What does the earth spirit ask of the initiate?",a="The earth spirit asks the initiate to remain strong, stay patient, and seek wisdom. It teaches that even the smallest things can outlast others, and that true power comes from mutual respect with the earth.",al={"earth spirit lesson","what does earth ask","shaman earth philosophy"}},{q="Where is Anvilmar located?",a="Anvilmar is the dwarven settlement nestled in Coldridge Valley within Dun Morogh. It serves as the staging ground where young dwarven initiates train and commune with the surrounding mountains.",al={"what is anvilmar","anvilmar lore","where does call of earth take place"}},{q="Why is the Earth Totem important?",a="A totem acts as a sacred conduit between a shaman and the elemental spirits. The Earth Totem binds the bearer's will to the steadfast power and resilience of the mountains.",al={"why totem matters","shaman totem lore","what does an earth totem do"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Communing with the spirits of the earth and earning an Earth Totem is a foundational rite of passage for every aspiring shaman.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven ties to stone and mountain run deep, and communing directly with earth spirits in Dun Morogh bridges their ancestral stonecraft with the shamanic path.",["faction:Alliance"]="Shamanic rites taking place around Anvilmar signify the growing presence and guidance of elemental spiritualists within Ironforge lands."},kw={"anvilmar","call of earth","coldridge valley","dun morogh","earth totem","rough quartz","shaman","teo hammerstorm","totem"},rel={"item:rough-quartz","subzone:anvilmar"},m={id=94375},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:94411"] = {t="quest",n="Meddlesome Mages",z="general",s="Meddlesome Mages centers on rising friction in the Shen'dar Highlands, where apprentices of the High Order trespass near sacred standing stones. Local guardians must drive them away to protect the delicate mystical forces that hold their island together from reckless arcane tampering.",h="Meddlesome Mages centers on rising friction in the Shen'dar Highlands, where apprentices of the High Order...",sec={{t="Encroachment at the Stones",b="Tensions have flared north of the village in the Shen'dar Highlands, where apprentices of the High Order have gathered around ancient standing stones. Despite repeated warnings to leave these sacred sites undisturbed, the mages have pressed forward with their arcane interests. The local leadership, though hesitant to shed the blood of their shen'dorei kin, has called for outside intervention to disperse the encroaching apprentices before their interference causes permanent harm.",sp=0},{t="The Fragile Island",b="The urgency behind driving off the High Order stems from the delicate balance that sustains the island. The mystical forces binding the land together are fragile, leaving the homeland vulnerable to reckless spellcraft. To the defenders of the standing stones, the High Order demonstrates complete disregard for the dangers of their meddling, meaning even a single careless spell could rupture the island's bindings and bring ruin upon all who dwell there.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Meddlesome Mages about?",a="Meddlesome Mages is a task asking adventurers to confront and disperse High Order Apprentices who have encroached upon sacred standing stones north of the village in the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"what is meddlesome mages","meddlesome mages quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Who are the High Order Apprentices?",a="The High Order is a faction of mages, described as shen'dorei brothers and sisters to the locals, whose apprentices are intruding upon sacred grounds and experimenting with magic despite warnings to stay away.",al={"who are the high order apprentices","what is high order","high order mages"}},{q="Why must the High Order Apprentices be dispersed?",a="The High Order has repeatedly ignored warnings to avoid the standing stones. Their reckless magical tampering threatens the fragile arcane forces binding the island, risking catastrophic destruction to the local homeland.",al={"why disperse high order","why fight the apprentices","standing stones problem"}},{q="Where are the High Order Apprentices gathering?",a="The confrontation takes place at the standing stones located north of the local village within the Shen'dar Highlands.",al={"where is this quest","shen'dar highlands location","standing stones north of village"}},{q="Who are the shen'dorei mentioned in the quest?",a="The shen'dorei are the kin of the quest giver's people. Although internal conflict weakens their shared community, local leaders feel forced to take decisive action against the High Order to avert disaster.",al={"who are shendorei","shendorei brothers and sisters","what are shen'dorei"}},{q="What danger do the mages pose to the island?",a="The forces holding the island together are delicate and vulnerable. Unchecked meddling by arcane practitioners could destabilize these bindings, potentially destroying the island entirely.",al={"what is the danger","island fragile forces","what could happen to the island"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Mages will note the sharp critique of reckless spellcasting; the High Order's apprentices disregard the delicate environmental balance holding the island together, showing the hazards of unrestrained arcane manipulation."},kw={"arcane meddling","high order","high order apprentices","meddlesome mages","shen'dar highlands","shen'dorei","standing stones"},rel={"topic:mage"},m={id=94411},src={"In-game quest text: Meddlesome Mages"},fv=true}
E["quest:94413"] = {t="quest",n="A Magical Affront",z="general",s="On Zephras, A Magical Affront sets the local order against the Windshapers. The order seeks to harness and study the surfaced leylines, while the Windshapers see them as scars to heal. Their conflict has reached the southwestern forest.",h="On Zephras, A Magical Affront sets the local order against the Windshapers.",sec={{t="Unveiled Currents",b="Ancient conduits of raw magic have recently broken through the earth on Zephras. For scholars and practitioners of the local order, this is a rare boon. Direct contact with the leylines gives them new insight into the arcane currents across the region and deepens their mastery of magic.",sp=0},{t="Scars and Strife",b="The Windshapers see the exposed energies as open wounds and scars across Zephras. They gather at the rupture sites to suppress or mend the leylines and heal the earth.\n\nThe order depends on that raw power, so it sees their efforts as a threat. It sends you southwest into the forest to drive the Windshapers back.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a group on Zephras who see the surfaced leylines as unnatural wounds in the land. They seek to heal them, putting them at odds with orders that study and channel the leylines.",al={"who are the windshapers","what are windshapers","windshapers zephras"}},{q="Why does the order want the Windshapers driven off?",a="The order needs access to the surfaced leylines to deepen its understanding of magic. The Windshapers try to suppress or mend them, so the order sees their efforts as a threat and sends you to drive them off.",al={"why kill windshapers","why fight the windshapers","windshaper conflict"}},{q="What are the leylines on Zephras?",a="Leylines are conduits of mystical energy that traverse the world. On Zephras, they have recently broken through the earth, exposing raw magic and giving arcane practitioners direct access to study it.",al={"what are leylines zephras","zephras leylines","surface leylines"}},{q="Where is the contested leyline located?",a="The contested leyline lies in the forest southwest on Zephras. Windshapers have gathered there to close or mend the exposed surge of magic.",al={"where is the leyline forest","leyline location","forest to southwest"}},{q="Why do the Windshapers consider the leylines scars?",a="The Windshapers see the sudden rupture of raw subterranean energy through the earth as violent scars across Zephras. They believe mending those tears will restore natural balance, which puts them at odds with spellcasters who want the leylines left open.",al={"why do windshapers want to heal leylines","are leylines harmful zephras","healing the scars"}},{q="What is the significance of Zephras in this conflict?",a="Zephras is where the leylines have recently emerged at the surface. Their raw magic has made the land a flashpoint between those who seek to master its arcane power and those who see the breaches as wounds to heal.",al={"what is zephras","where does a magical affront take place","zephras lore"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, you may see the surfaced leylines on Zephras as a rare chance to study raw mystical energy directly. Their preservation matters to your arcane craft.",["class:Druid"]="As a Druid, you may feel the tension in the Windshapers’ view of the exposed leylines as wounds across the natural land, in need of healing.",["class:Shaman"]="As a Shaman, you may share the Windshapers’ elemental and natural stewardship as they seek to calm and heal disturbances across Zephras. That puts their aims at odds with orders harnessing raw magical flows."},kw={"a magical affront","arcane","leyline","leylines","scars","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=94413},src={"In-game quest text: A Magical Affront"},fv=true}
E["quest:94427"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="Your order sends you to Tanis Alderwood in Undercity’s Trade Quarter. There, a chance to show grace comes through good deeds.",h="Your order sends you to Tanis Alderwood in Undercity’s Trade Quarter.",sec={{t="A Call for Grace",b="Your order teaches that divinity means doing good for its own sake, not seeking godhood. It says tragedy brought you here. Now it asks you to find Tanis Alderwood in Undercity’s Trade Quarter. Tanis is in need and may benefit from your grace.",sp=0},{t="Tanis’s Plea",b="When you approach Tanis Alderwood, they fear you may hurt them. They insist they have no quarrel with you or your order. Then they beg you to leave them be.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Tanis Alderwood?",a="Tanis Alderwood is someone in need in Undercity’s Trade Quarter.",al={"Tanis","who's Tanis","Trade Quarter person"}},{q="Why am I sent to Tanis?",a="Your order says Tanis Alderwood may benefit from your grace.",al={"why Tanis","help Tanis","what's the point"}},{q="What does divinity mean to my order?",a="Your order defines divinity as doing good for its own sake, rather than seeking godhood.",al={"divinity meaning","good for its own sake","what is divinity"}},{q="Where can I find Tanis Alderwood?",a="You can find Tanis Alderwood in Undercity’s Trade Quarter.",al={"Tanis location","where's Tanis","Trade Quarter"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, your order defines divinity as doing good for its own sake. It says tragedy can threaten the grace that defined you in life."},kw={"a lesson in divinity","divinity","grace","panhandling","tanis alderwood","trade quarter","undercity"},rel={"zone:undercity","subzone:trade-quarter"},m={id=94427},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94434"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="In the Undercity, Tanis Alderwood asks you for fresh cloth. His wife is falling apart, and he hopes to wrap her until an apothecary is free.",h="In the Undercity, Tanis Alderwood asks you for fresh cloth.",sec={{t="A Beggar's Plea",b="The apothecaries are occupied with the Dark Lady's business. So Tanis asks you to bring linen for his wife. He hopes the cloth will hold her together while they wait for help.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Tanis Alderwood, a beggar in the Undercity, asks you to help his wife.",al={"who is Tanis","Tanis Alderwood","beggar guy"}},{q="What's wrong with Tanis's wife?",a="Tanis says she is literally falling apart and needs a wrapping to hold her together.",al={"wife falling apart","what happened to his wife","Tanis wife"}},{q="Why can't the apothecaries help?",a="Tanis says the apothecaries are occupied with the Dark Lady's business.",al={"apothecaries busy","why no apothecary","Dark Lady business"}},{q="What does Tanis need?",a="Tanis asks you to bring fresh cloth so he can wrap his wife while they wait for the apothecaries.",al={"what cloth does he need","linen for Tanis","help beggar"}}},kw={"a lesson in divinity","apothecaries","beggar","dark lady","linen cloth","tanis alderwood","undercity","wife"},rel={"zone:undercity"},m={id=94434},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94435"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="Tanis Alderwood thanks you for your help and calls you a friend. Danitha Morr sees this small act of service as a way to strengthen the Forsaken’s connection to the Light.",h="Tanis Alderwood thanks you for your help and calls you a friend.",sec={{t="A Small Service",b="Danitha Morr says your help may strengthen the Forsaken’s connection to the Light. Mistrust and conspiracies linger among the Dark Lady’s followers. The Forsaken must meet that fear with courage and righteousness. Danitha gives you a symbol for what comes next.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is thanking me?",a="Tanis Alderwood thanks you for helping him and says you will always have a friend in him.",al={"tanis alderwood","who is tanis","tanis thanks"}},{q="What does Danitha say my help means?",a="Danitha says even a small act of service may strengthen the Forsaken’s connection to the Light.",al={"why help danitha","small act meaning","light connection"}},{q="Why does Danitha mention mistrust?",a="Danitha says the Dark Lady and some in her service mistrust them, and that some even conspire against them.",al={"forsaken mistrust","dark lady fear","why are they mistrusted"}},{q="What does Danitha give me?",a="A symbol from Danitha, for what comes next.",al={"what symbol","danitha item","symbol for next"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, you hear Danitha describe the Forsaken’s effort to help their people prosper again.",["race:Undead"]="As Forsaken, you hear Danitha call for courage and righteousness in helping your people prosper again."},kw={"a lesson in divinity","danitha morr","dark lady","forsaken","light","mistrust","symbol","tanis alderwood"},rel={"topic:light","topic:forsaken","subzone:bandarion-keep"},m={id=94435},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94436"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="In A Lesson in Divinity, Danitha Morr asks you to show what a priest can do with the Symbol of Life. Deathguard Billmuth at Bandarion Keep has come seeking aid for a friend.",h="In A Lesson in Divinity, Danitha Morr asks you to show what a priest can do with the Symbol of Life.",sec={{t="A Plea for Aid",b="Billmuth says the priests turned him away, and his friend may have no one else to turn to. He is not proud to ask you, but he needs aid for the Forsaken. The Symbol of Life is meant to show your ability to channel the Light. Handling it may cause discomfort. Its power can still be your ally and guide, even to the Forsaken, whose creation is tied to a force opposed to the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Deathguard Billmuth at Bandarion Keep has come seeking help for a friend.",al={"billmuth help","who needs aid"}},{q="Why does Billmuth ask a priest?",a="Billmuth says the priests turned him away, and you may be his friend's only chance.",al={"why priest","priests turned him away"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life?",a="The Symbol of Life is a talisman of the Light that Danitha Morr gives you to show your ability to channel the Light.",al={"symbol of life","light talisman"}},{q="Why might the Symbol of Life hurt to handle?",a="Danitha warns that the talisman may cause discomfort because the Light is opposed to the force that created the Forsaken.",al={"symbol pain","light hurts"}},{q="Where can I find Deathguard Billmuth?",a="Deathguard Billmuth is downstairs at Bandarion Keep.",al={"billmuth location","bandarion keep"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, Danitha asks you to show your ability to channel the Light with the Symbol of Life."},kw={"a lesson in divinity","bandarion keep","danitha morr","deathguard billmuth","forsaken","light","priest","symbol of life"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:light","topic:forsaken","topic:priest"},m={id=94436},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94438"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="Danitha Morr sends you to Venomweb Vale to find Deathguard Falgan. He and his companion were separated after learning the Scarlet Crusade plans an attack on Brill.",h="Danitha Morr sends you to Venomweb Vale to find Deathguard Falgan.",sec={{t="Falgan's Return",b="Deathguard Falgan went with Billmuth to scout Scarlet activity in Venomweb Vale. They learned the Scarlet Crusade plans some kind of attack. Now Falgan asks whether you will help the Forsaken further.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to find Falgan?",a="Danitha Morr asks you to find Deathguard Falgan and help him if you can.",al={"who sent me","Danitha Morr","quest giver"}},{q="Where is Falgan?",a="Falgan is at Venomweb Vale.",al={"Falgan location","where falgan","find deathguard"}},{q="What happened to Falgan?",a="Falgan stayed behind after a noise alerted Scarlet forces to him and his companion. His companion reached Brill without him.",al={"Falgan missing","what happened","save falgan"}},{q="What did Falgan and Billmuth discover?",a="Falgan and Billmuth learned that the Scarlet Crusade plans some kind of attack.",al={"scarlet orders","what did they find","attack plans"}},{q="What is the Symbol of Life for?",a="The Symbol of Life is used to bring Deathguard Falgan back to life.",al={"resurrect falgan","symbol life","how save falgan"}},{q="What does Falgan ask after returning?",a="After returning, Falgan asks whether you are willing to help the Forsaken further.",al={"help forsaken","what next","Falgan asks"}}},kw={"a lesson in divinity","billmuth","brill","danitha morr","deathguard falgan","scarlet crusade","symbol of life","venomweb vale"},rel={"subzone:venomweb-vale","subzone:brill","topic:forsaken"},m={id=94438},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94440"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="Deathguard Billmuth needs you to recover Scarlet Crusade attack plans from Venomweb Vale. The Deathguard expects an assault and hopes the plans will help prepare an ambush.",h="Deathguard Billmuth needs you to recover Scarlet Crusade attack plans from Venomweb Vale.",sec={{t="Orders for the Keep",b="Scarlet Crusaders carry deployment orders in their satchels, and the Deathguard expects an attack. Bring a copy of the plans to Deathguard Billmuth at Bandarion Keep. Then the Deathguard can prepare an ambush.",sp=0},{t="A Friend Saved",b="Billmuth thanks you for saving his friend and says he will take the plans to his commander. Your actions have shown him you are Forsaken through and through, despite the mistrust some of his people hold toward your class.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What does Billmuth need?",a="Deathguard Billmuth asks you to recover the Scarlet Crusade's attack plans from Venomweb Vale.",al={"what does billmuth want","billmuth plans","plans for billmuth"}},{q="Where are the attack plans?",a="The Scarlet Crusaders at Venomweb Vale carry the attack plans.",al={"where plans","scarlet plans location","find attack plans"}},{q="Why does the Deathguard want the plans?",a="The Deathguard expects an attack and wants the plans so it can prepare an ambush.",al={"why get plans","what are plans for","deathguard ambush"}},{q="Who should receive the plans?",a="Bring the plans to Deathguard Billmuth at Bandarion Keep.",al={"who gets plans","return plans to","billmuth where"}}},kw={"a lesson in divinity","attack plans","bandarion keep","deathguard billmuth","forsaken","scarlet crusade","venomweb vale"},rel={"quest:381","subzone:bandarion-keep","subzone:venomweb-vale","topic:forsaken","topic:scarlet-crusade"},m={id=94440},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94441"] = {t="quest",n="A Lesson in Divinity",z="general",s="A fellow Forsaken asks you to thank Danitha Morr for the paladins’ service at Bandarion Keep. Your work has earned the Deathguard’s gratitude and brings a gift of redemption.",h="A fellow Forsaken asks you to thank Danitha Morr for the paladins’ service at Bandarion Keep.",sec={{t="Thanks at the Keep",b="A fellow Forsaken asks you to carry their thanks to Danitha Morr at Bandarion Keep. The rest of the Deathguard will hear of the service you and the other paladins performed. They are proud to count you among the Forsaken and trust the Dark Lady watches over you.",sp=0},{t="A Gift of Light",b="Danitha says a paladin ready to receive one of the Light’s greatest blessings can use the Symbol of Life. She bestows redemption upon you, letting you resurrect fallen companions. Your service matters to the Forsaken and the paladins of Bandarion Keep. The Deathguard hopes such acts will win the hearts and minds of fellow Forsaken.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who do I thank?",a="You are asked to thank Danitha Morr for the paladins’ service at Bandarion Keep.",al={"who is Danitha","thank Danitha","Danitha Morr"}},{q="Who will hear about the service?",a="The rest of the Deathguard will hear of the paladins’ service at Bandarion Keep.",al={"Deathguard thanks","who gets told","keep service"}},{q="What does the Symbol of Life do?",a="The Symbol of Life can be used by a paladin ready to receive one of the Light’s greatest blessings.",al={"Symbol of Life","life symbol","what does it do"}},{q="Why is the Deathguard proud of me?",a="The Deathguard is proud to count you among the Forsaken and values the service you performed with the paladins of Bandarion Keep.",al={"Forsaken proud","why proud","Deathguard proud"}}},kw={"a lesson in divinity","bandarion keep","danitha morr","deathguard","forsaken","light","redemption","symbol of life"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:light","topic:forsaken"},m={id=94441},src={"In-game quest text: A Lesson in Divinity"},fv=true}
E["quest:94449"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="In Dun Morogh, an elder sends shaman initiates to Bruegs Kindleborn to test their readiness for the fire sapta. They have already learned patience and grounding through earth.",h="In Dun Morogh, an elder sends shaman initiates to Bruegs Kindleborn to test their readiness for the fire...",sec={{t="The Burning Trial",b="After proving patience and grounding through earth, shamans face a more volatile force: the fire elemental spirit. Stone holds steady, but fire promises chaos, ruin, and fierce energy. So an elder sends the initiate across Dun Morogh’s snow-laden peaks to seek guidance before trying to harness its wild heat.",sp=0},{t="The Recluse of the Peaks",b="Bruegs Kindleborn keeps watch in a secluded cave high in the mountains above South Gate Outpost. When an initiate arrives with an elder’s recommendation, Bruegs prepares them for the sapta of fire. He warns that flame’s hunger to burn and consume is easily stirred. To master it, a shaman must know when to hold back.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Bruegs Kindleborn?",a="Bruegs Kindleborn mentors shamans in a mountain cave high above South Gate Outpost in Dun Morogh. Elders send initiates to him, and he judges whether they have the maturity and discipline to receive the fire sapta.",al={"who is bruegs kindleborn","bruegs kindleborn lore","where is bruegs"}},{q="What is the sapta of fire?",a="A sapta is an elemental draught or preparation that lets a shaman commune with spirits of that element. The sapta of fire grants communion with flame and its raw power, and calls for mental focus and prior grounding in stone.",al={"what is sapta of fire","fire sapta","sapta meaning"}},{q="Where is Bruegs Kindleborn located?",a="Bruegs Kindleborn lives far east in the snowy mountains of Dun Morogh, sheltered in a cave overlooking South Gate Outpost.",al={"where to find bruegs kindleborn","south gate outpost cave","where is call of fire turn in"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Call of Fire?",a="Call of Fire brings shamans who have shown patience and mastered earth before the chaotic power of destruction. Bruegs Kindleborn watches over their trial.",al={"what is call of fire","fire totem quest dun morogh"}},{q="Why must a shaman learn the lessons of stone before fire?",a="Earth teaches steadfast patience, stability, and control. Without stone’s grounding, a shaman risks being overwhelmed by fire’s volatile hunger and destructive passions.",al={"why stone first","remember what stone taught you"}},{q="What prerequisites are required before undertaking the Call of Fire?",a="Before this rite, an initiate has shown patience and grounding in earth through earlier trials. The elder sends a student to Bruegs Kindleborn only when their discipline is proven solid.",al={"call of fire prereq","patience quest connection"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you face a spiritual trial that carries you from stone’s endurance to fire’s fierce, destructive power.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance shaman, you follow your elders’ guidance in the mountains of Dun Morogh to awaken communion with the elements of fire.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you face this trial high above South Gate Outpost in Dun Morogh, where your discipline is tested against flame’s burning hunger."},kw={"bruegs kindleborn","call of fire","dun morogh","earth sapta","fire totem","sapta of fire","shaman","south gate outpost"},rel={"zone:dunmorogh","subzone:south-gate-outpost"},m={id=94449},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:94465"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="You carry the Torch of the Dormant Flame through Loch Modan to seek Braldir Ashmantle. The trial tests whether an initiate is ready to wield elemental flame.",h="You carry the Torch of the Dormant Flame through Loch Modan to seek Braldir Ashmantle.",sec={{t="The Path Beyond the Valley of Kings",b="To earn the totem of fire, you carry the unlit Torch of the Dormant Flame into the crags of Loch Modan. Beyond the stone carvings of the Valley of Kings, a steep path climbs to an isolated mountain summit. There the elements hold sway, far from the roads travelers know.",sp=0},{t="The Shrine of the Dormant Flame",b="Braldir Ashmantle watches over the Shrine of the Dormant Flame on a high, windswept peak. Unprepared wanderers and fortune hunters find only silence and bare stone. But when you arrive as a true initiate bearing the sacred torch, Braldir reveals the elemental power sleeping within the shrine's brazier, waiting to be rekindled.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Fire quest?",a="The Call of Fire is a trial for an initiate seeking to earn a fire totem. You travel into the crags of Loch Modan and bring the Torch of the Dormant Flame to Braldir Ashmantle at a sacred mountain peak.",al={"what is call of fire","shaman fire quest loch modan","fire totem quest"}},{q="Who is Braldir Ashmantle?",a="Braldir Ashmantle guards the Shrine of the Dormant Flame high in the crags of Loch Modan. He turns away ordinary travelers and treasure seekers, and welcomes those who bear the Torch of the Dormant Flame.",al={"who is braldir ashmantle","shrine keeper loch modan"}},{q="Where is the Shrine of the Dormant Flame located?",a="The Shrine of the Dormant Flame rests high in the crags of Loch Modan. A hidden path winds there beyond the Valley of Kings.",al={"where is shrine of the dormant flame","dormant flame location"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Torch of the Dormant Flame?",a="Initiates carry the Torch of the Dormant Flame to show their earnest intent and readiness to commune with elemental fire. Braldir Ashmantle requires you to present it before granting an audience.",al={"what is the torch of the dormant flame","torch of the dormant flame purpose"}},{q="Why must the petitioner travel to Loch Modan?",a="You travel to Loch Modan because the secluded elemental peak that holds the Shrine of the Dormant Flame rises beyond the Valley of Kings, away from casual travelers.",al={"why go to loch modan","why is the fire shrine in loch modan"}},{q="How is the fire totem earned through this trial?",a="After you reach Braldir Ashmantle and prove your intent with the torch, you kindle the shrine's brazier. You return with the flame burning and earn the totem of fire.",al={"what happens after finding braldir","how to get fire totem"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As an aspiring shaman, you prove your dedication to the elemental spirits of fire by carrying a sacred torch to an isolated peak in Loch Modan.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you hear dwarven speech as the path leads beyond the Valley of Kings into the rugged crags of Khaz Modan.",["faction:Alliance"]="Across Alliance lands, common travelers see only barren mountain stone. Keepers of the shrine guard ancient elemental secrets from ordinary eyes."},kw={"braldir ashmantle","call of fire","fire totem","loch modan","shaman","shrine of the dormant flame","torch of the dormant flame","valley of kings"},rel={"item:torch-of-the-dormant-flame","zone:lochmodan","topic:alliance"},m={id=94465},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:94466"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="In Loch Modan, initiates seek the Eternal Flame and the fire totem it grants. Braldir Ashmantle guides them through a trial that calls for reagents from kobolds and troggs. They prepare a fire sapta and learn that flame can forge and give life as well as destroy.",h="In Loch Modan, initiates seek the Eternal Flame and the fire totem it grants.",sec={{t="The Path to the Flame",b="In Loch Modan, Braldir Ashmantle guides initiates who seek the power of flame. Before they can commune with fire spirits, they must attune their mortal spirits with a sacred draught called a fire sapta. Without it, the Eternal Flame would consume them instead of enlightening them. Braldir asks each petitioner to gather two reagents from hostile valley dwellers and prove their resolve.",sp=0},{t="Reagents of the Earth and Spark",b="A fire sapta needs substances rich in elemental power. In the north, Tunnel Rat Geomancers gather sticky fire tar in their warrens to fuel their subterranean magic. In the south, Stonesplinter Seers carry reagent pouches filled with primitive elemental charms across the hills. Claiming these items means facing two of Loch Modan’s most entrenched pests, and gaining what is needed to bind an initiate’s soul to flame.",sp=0},{t="The Tempering of Wisdom",b="Once the reagents are gathered, Braldir Ashmantle teaches patience alongside the power to destroy. To him, fire means more than fury and ash. It is life, the craft of the forge, and the breath of creation. Mastery calls for balance: shadow gives meaning to light, and flame keeps stone from being only a cold memory. To master fire, an initiate must learn when to blend rather than burn.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Call of Fire?",a="Aspiring shaman undertake Call of Fire to seek communion with the Eternal Flame. First, they prepare a fire sapta to bind their mortal spirit to the element.",al={"what is call of fire","what is this quest","call of fire purpose"}},{q="Who is Braldir Ashmantle?",a="Braldir Ashmantle guides initiates in Loch Modan. He teaches the balance of fire and prepares a fire sapta once they gather the needed reagents.",al={"who gives call of fire","who is braldir ashmantle","braldir ashmantle location"}},{q="What is a fire sapta?",a="A fire sapta is a preparation made from reagents bound to flame. It attunes and binds a shaman’s spirit to fire, making communion with the Eternal Flame possible.",al={"what is a fire sapta","what does a sapta do","why need sapta"}},{q="Where is the fire tar found?",a="Tunnel Rat Geomancers carry fire tar in their caves in northern Loch Modan. They use it in their primitive fire crafts.",al={"where do i get fire tar","fire tar location","kobold caves fire tar"}},{q="Where is the Stonesplinter reagent pouch found?",a="Stonesplinter Seers carry reagent pouches in the southern hills of Loch Modan. The trogg mystics use them to hold reagents tied to elemental magic.",al={"where do i get reagent pouch","stonesplinter seer pouch","stonesplinter pouch location"}},{q="How does Braldir Ashmantle view the element of fire?",a="Braldir teaches that fire is more than destruction: it is forge, life, and the breath of creation. In balance with other forces, it tempers cold stone and gives warmth and purpose to existence.",al={"braldir view on fire","meaning of fire loch modan","dwarven fire philosophy"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="To commune with fire spirits and seek the Eternal Flame, you forge an elemental bond with a fire sapta. This path leads toward the fire totem.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you find Braldir Ashmantle guiding initiates in Loch Modan. He sees fire as the breath of the forge and creation, not mere destruction.",["faction:Alliance"]="In Alliance lands, you face local trogg and kobold threats as you take part in shamanic rites in Loch Modan."},kw={"braldir ashmantle","call of fire","eternal flame","fire sapta","fire tar","fire totem","loch modan","stonesplinter seer","tunnel rat geomancer"},rel={"zone:lochmodan","item:fire-tar","item:reagent-pouch-shaman","topic:kobold"},m={id=94466},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:94467"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="At the shrine, an initiate drinks fire sapta to face the trial called Call of Fire. The rite asks them to subdue a spirit and offer its embers to the sacred brazier.",h="At the shrine, an initiate drinks fire sapta to face the trial called Call of Fire.",sec={{t="The Rite of Flame",b="An initiate receives prepared fire sapta, guided by elemental lore. The draught awakens their senses to the unseen world. After drinking at the shrine, they climb toward the peak, where mortal sight gives way to primal forces. There, a dormant manifestation of fire awaits their challenge.",sp=0},{t="Facing the Manifestation",b="The Minor Manifestation of Fire tests an initiate's resolve and endurance. It does not attack until challenged, then grows fiercer with each passing moment. When subdued, it yields glowing embers. Carry them to the brazier atop the Shrine of Eternal Flame to kindle the torch and win the spirit's lasting favor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Fire?",a="Call of Fire is a sacred rite in which an initiate drinks prepared fire sapta, faces a spirit of flame, and offers its ember to a brazier to earn fire's favor.",al={"what is call of fire","call of fire quest","fire totem trial"}},{q="What is the purpose of the fire sapta?",a="Fire sapta is a prepared draught that opens an initiate's spiritual sight, revealing the elemental spirit waiting at the mountain peak.",al={"fire sapta purpose","why drink sapta","what does sapta do"}},{q="What spirit appears during the trial?",a="A Minor Manifestation of Fire awaits the initiate atop the peak. It does not fight until struck.",al={"what spirit do i fight","call of fire mob","fire manifestation"}},{q="Why must the fire spirit be defeated quickly?",a="The elemental spirit grows fiercer and hotter as the battle wears on. A swift, decisive strike can subdue it before its flames overwhelm the initiate.",al={"why hit it quick","call of fire fight","fire spirit timer"}},{q="What is done with the glowing embers?",a="After the Minor Manifestation of Fire is defeated, its glowing embers are carried to the brazier atop the Shrine of Eternal Flame to kindle the ritual torch.",al={"where do embers go","call of fire brazier","shrine of eternal flame brazier"}},{q="What does earning the fire's favor mean?",a="Kindling the torch earns the direct favor and communion of the fire elementals. The rite establishes a bond with the flames.",al={"what is the reward","what does the ritual give","fire favor"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you undertake a foundational trial in communion with fire. Sacred sapta lets you perceive and confront the spirits of flame."},kw={"brazier","call of fire","fire sapta","glowing ember","minor manifestation of fire","shaman trial","shrine of eternal flame"},rel={"item:glowing-ember"},m={id=94467},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:94468"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="Above Loch Modan, an initiate kindles the flame that must reach Dun Morogh. Bruegs Kindleborn awaits its return and judges the traveler's command of the elements.",h="Above Loch Modan, an initiate kindles the flame that must reach Dun Morogh.",sec={{t="The Flame Over the Loch",b="An initiate lights the Torch of Eternal Flame on a mountain ridge above Loch Modan. The flame marks the beginning of a deeper bond with the elements. From there, the initiate carries it down the trails and into the snowy vales of Dun Morogh.",sp=0},{t="The Flameborn's Reward",b="In Dun Morogh, Bruegs Kindleborn awaits the traveler. He is an elemental mentor who judges whether the traveler's inner fire has taken hold. The blazing torch proves the initiate's will and reverence for fire. Bruegs then grants the Fire Totem, welcoming the flameborn into a lineage of elemental guidance for their kin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Fire?",a="Call of Fire is a spiritual trial for shamans in Khaz Modan. The initiate lights the Torch of Eternal Flame at a brazier overlooking Loch Modan, then carries it through the mountains to Bruegs Kindleborn in Dun Morogh.",al={"what is call of fire","call of fire quest","what do you do in call of fire"}},{q="Who is Bruegs Kindleborn?",a="Bruegs Kindleborn is an elemental mentor who lives in Dun Morogh. He oversees the rites of flame and gives initiates who complete the trek their Fire Totem.",al={"who is bruegs kindleborn","where is bruegs kindleborn","bruegs kindleborn location"}},{q="What is the Torch of Eternal Flame?",a="The Torch of Eternal Flame is a ceremonial torch lit at a brazier overlooking Loch Modan. It marks an initiate's bond with elemental fire and must be carried safely to Bruegs Kindleborn in Dun Morogh.",al={"what is torch of eternal flame","torch of eternal flame","where do you get the torch"}},{q="What do you receive upon completing this trial?",a="When you deliver the Torch of Eternal Flame safely to Bruegs Kindleborn, he acknowledges your blazing spirit and gives you your Fire Totem. The totem marks you as flameborn.",al={"what reward for call of fire","do you get fire totem","call of fire totem"}},{q="Where does this journey take place?",a="The journey begins at an overlook above Loch Modan, where the torch is lit. The mountain path leads into Dun Morogh, where Bruegs Kindleborn awaits.",al={"where does call of fire take place","call of fire path","where to take the torch"}},{q="Why does Bruegs mention Madoran?",a="Bruegs mentions Madoran in an oath, referring to Madoran Bronzebeard. He was the revered dwarven leader who led the Bronzebeard clan during the War of the Three Hammers.",al={"why by madorans beard","madoran meaning","who is madoran bruegs mentions"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you complete the trial of fire in Khaz Modan and earn the Fire Totem. It lets you channel flame and commune with the fire spirits.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you carry the flame from the heights above Loch Modan into Dun Morogh. The journey joins your spiritual seeking to the elemental forces of your mountain home.",["faction:Alliance"]="Within Alliance lands in Khaz Modan, spiritual callings take root. Bruegs Kindleborn guides new initiates as they learn the elemental arts."},kw={"bruegs kindleborn","call of fire","dun morogh","fire totem","flameborn","loch modan","shaman","torch of eternal flame"},rel={"zone:dunmorogh","zone:lochmodan"},m={id=94468},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:94484"] = {t="quest",n="Unnerving Silence",z="general",s="Unnerving Silence concerns an abrupt loss of communication from a druid enclave nestled inside a cave in northeastern Shadowgale Forest. In the wake of Ban'aethall's ruin and growing regional unrest, an investigator is dispatched to locate Elegael Thornpaw and assess the conclave's fate.",h="Unnerving Silence concerns an abrupt loss of communication from a druid enclave nestled inside a cave in...",sec={{t="Silence in the Canopy",b="A quiet unease hangs over the wilderness following the fall of Ban'aethall. With that village reduced to ruins and mysterious unrest stirring through the territory, established lines of contact have begun to fray. In particular, regular dispatches from a secluded druid conclave dwelling deep inside a cave in northeastern Shadowgale Forest have abruptly ceased, leaving their allies on edge.",sp=0},{t="Seeking the Enclave",b="Travelers heading toward Shadowgale Forest are tasked with tracking down Elegael Thornpaw, a prominent figure among the cave-dwelling druids. Venturing into the shadowed northeast, an investigator must discover whether the enclave has simply withdrawn into deeper communion with nature or succumbed to the same dangers that tore through Ban'aethall.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Unnerving Silence about?",a="Unnerving Silence is a mission to investigate a sudden lapse in communication from a druid conclave based in Shadowgale Forest. A concerned party asks travelers venturing that way to search a northeastern cave and discover what has befallen the secluded order.",al={"unnerving silence quest","what is unnerving silence"}},{q="Where can Elegael Thornpaw be found?",a="Elegael Thornpaw resides within a cave situated in the northeastern reaches of Shadowgale Forest. She serves as the point of contact for the secluded druid conclave located there.",al={"where is elegael thornpaw","elegael thornpaw location","find thornpaw"}},{q="Who is Elegael Thornpaw?",a="Elegael Thornpaw is a member of the druid conclave dwelling within a cave in northeastern Shadowgale Forest. Her prolonged silence and failure to send scheduled correspondence have sparked grave worries about the safety of her enclave.",al={"who is elegael thornpaw","thornpaw lore"}},{q="What is Shadowgale Forest?",a="Shadowgale Forest is a wooded expanse housing a hidden druidic cave conclave in its northeastern region. Following widespread regional turmoil and the fall of Ban'aethall, communication with the forest's dwellers has grown perilously uncertain.",al={"what is shadowgale forest","shadowgale forest conclave"}},{q="What is Ban'aethall?",a="Ban'aethall was a village deep in Shadowgale Forest that has recently fallen into ruin. Its destruction, alongside broader unrest across the territory, has heightened fears that nearby encampments like the Shadowgale conclave may also be in danger.",al={"what happened to banaethall","banaethall ruins lore","ban'aethall fallen"}},{q="Why has the druid conclave gone silent?",a="Scheduled messages and reports from the druids in Shadowgale Forest have ceased without explanation. Given the destruction of Ban'aethall and escalating regional tension, there is serious concern that catastrophe or hostile forces have overtaken them.",al={"why did druids stop sending messages","why are druids quiet"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The loss of contact with a secluded enclave in Shadowgale Forest raises immediate concern for druids attuned to the balance of the wild. Investigating Elegael Thornpaw's silence is a matter of safeguarding brethren and untangling what has disrupted their communion."},kw={"ban'aethall","druid","druid conclave","elegael thornpaw","shadowgale forest","unnerving silence"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94484},src={"In-game quest text: Unnerving Silence"},fv=true}
E["quest:94485"] = {t="quest",n="Tears of the Lady",z="general",s="Tears of the Lady is an urgent mercy task undertaken for Elegael in the forest of Shadowgale. To treat wounded comrades left stranded in the Nightclaw Den, travelers must scour local tree trunks for Lady's Tear, an antiseptic moss vital for binding severe wounds.",h="Tears of the Lady is an urgent mercy task undertaken for Elegael in the forest of Shadowgale.",sec={{t="Desperate Remedies in Shadowgale",b="In the shadowed depths of Shadowgale, Elegael urgently seeks aid to treat companions trapped within the Nightclaw Den. With critically injured allies awaiting rescue, conventional supplies are lacking, requiring field medicine scavenged directly from the surrounding wilderness. The effort hinges on securing natural remedies before time runs out for those left behind.",sp=0},{t="The Healing Moss",b="The vital component of Elegael's planned poultice is Lady's Tear, a spindly moss clinging to tree bases across Shadowgale. The plant produces a viscous, sticky secretion with natural antiseptic properties, capable of sealing open gashes and shielding traumatic wounds from worsening in the damp wild. Delivering enough moss allows Elegael to prepare the dressings necessary to stabilize the fallen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Tears of the Lady about?",a="Tears of the Lady is a recovery effort directed by Elegael in Shadowgale. Wounded comrades remain trapped in Nightclaw Den, and Elegael requires local flora to brew an urgent poultice to keep them alive.",al={"what is tears of the lady","tears of the lady quest info"}},{q="What is Lady's Tear?",a="Lady's Tear is a spindly moss that grows near the bases of trees throughout the Shadowgale woods. It secretes a naturally sticky sap that serves as a powerful antiseptic, making it ideal for dressing and binding open wounds.",al={"what is ladys tear","ladys tear moss","what is lady's tear moss"}},{q="Who is Elegael?",a="Elegael is the survivor who organizes the medical effort in Shadowgale. Having left wounded companions behind in the Nightclaw Den, Elegael gathers medicinal supplies to prepare treatments before returning to help them.",al={"who is elegael","quest giver tears of the lady"}},{q="What is the Nightclaw Den?",a="Nightclaw Den is a location near Shadowgale where several of Elegael's injured allies are stranded. Their condition is precarious, prompting the desperate rush to gather healing ingredients.",al={"what is nightclaw den","where is nightclaw den"}},{q="Why does Elegael need Lady's Tear moss?",a="The moss provides a crucial antiseptic secretion needed to compound a medical poultice. This sticky substance seals open gashes and fights off infection for the survivors left behind in the den.",al={"why need ladys tear","what does the moss do"}},{q="Where is Lady's Tear moss found?",a="Lady's Tear moss typically clings to the base of the trees throughout the Shadowgale forest. While Elegael gathered what was immediately reachable, more must be scavenged from the surrounding woods.",al={"where does ladys tear grow","finding ladys tear"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Herbalists and field healers value Lady's Tear for its sticky antiseptic secretions, which can quickly bind open flesh and prevent infection in wilderness emergencies.",["class:Priest"]="While priests channel the Light to mend mortal injuries, natural poultices like Lady's Tear serve as crucial physical dressings for wounded survivors when magical relief is stretched thin."},kw={"elegael","lady's tear","moss","nightclaw den","poultice","shadowgale","tears of the lady"},m={id=94485},src={"In-game quest text: Tears of the Lady"},fv=true}
E["quest:94486"] = {t="quest",n="Feathers for Binding",z="general",s="Feathers for Binding is a quest in Shadowgale Forest centered on gathering insulating plumage from young shrieklings to craft a protective medicinal poultice. While these normally docile creatures have grown dangerously aggressive, their feathers remain essential for preserving antiseptic salves against the elements. Adventurers must brave the hostile fledglings to secure the materials needed for regional care.",h="Feathers for Binding is a quest in Shadowgale Forest centered on gathering insulating plumage from young...",sec={{t="Remedies of the Forest",b="In the depths of Shadowgale Forest, preparing field medicine often demands resourcefulness from the land. When treating grave injuries, herbalists and healers turn to a specialized poultice built on moss and antiseptic tree sap. To ensure the sap does not rapidly dry out or lose its potency to harsh weather, durable insulative bindings are required. The juvenile feathers of Shadowgale shrieklings provide an ideal natural barrier, shielding the wound while sealing in the medicinal treatment.",sp=0},{t="Uncharacteristic Hostility",b="Procuring these downy feathers has become unexpectedly perilous. Under ordinary circumstances, young Shadowgale shrieklings are docile woodland dwellers. In recent times, however, an inexplicable spike in aggression has swept through their ranks. Wielding formidable talons capable of inflicting lethal wounds, these young predators now strike aggressively at passersby. Harvesting their plumage requires adventurers to face a creature transformed from a harmless fledgling into a genuine hazard.",sp=0},{t="A Regrettable Necessity",b="Asking travelers to hunt juvenile creatures weighs heavily on the local caretakers, who openly mourn the necessity of the cull. Yet with multiple emergencies threatening the region and the beasts growing increasingly dangerous, thinning their numbers serves a dual purpose: gathering vital curative materials while suppressing a burgeoning threat to the forest's paths. Every feather gathered is treated with respect, ensuring their sacrifice is not wasted.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are Shadowgale shriekling feathers needed for the poultice?",a="The feathers of juvenile Shadowgale shrieklings possess natural insulative qualities. Placed over a layer of moss, they serve as a protective binding that prevents antiseptic sap from drying out while shielding wounds against harsh environmental elements.",al={"purpose of feathers","why shriekling feathers","poultice feathers"}},{q="How dangerous are the juvenile shrieklings?",a="Although Shadowgale shrieklings are normally docile creatures, they have recently become unusually hostile. Despite their young age, they possess large, vicious talons capable of disemboweling an unwary traveler.",al={"shriekling danger","are shrieklings dangerous","shriekling aggression"}},{q="Why must juvenile shrieklings be hunted instead of adults?",a="The questgiver expresses genuine remorse over culling young beasts. However, because of their sudden spike in hostility and the need to manage competing wilderness emergencies, thinning their numbers while harvesting needed medicine is deemed a painful necessity.",al={"why kill young shrieklings","killing young shrieklings"}},{q="Where are the Shadowgale shrieklings found?",a="Shadowgale shrieklings roam the canopies and underbrush of Shadowgale Forest, where travelers must venture carefully through the woods to track them down.",al={"where is shadowgale forest","shriekling location","where are shrieklings"}},{q="What other ingredients are being used alongside these feathers?",a="The poultice consists of local moss layered with antiseptic sap, which is then bound and insulated using the downy feathers harvested from juvenile shrieklings.",al={"poultice ingredients","what is in the poultice"}},{q="Why have the shrieklings become so hostile lately?",a="The exact cause of their sudden hostility is unclear, but the normally peaceful young creatures have become uncharacteristically aggressive, adding to the growing list of crises confronting local caretakers.",al={"why are shrieklings angry","what caused shriekling aggression"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Herbalists and natural healers can appreciate the careful balance of combining moss, antiseptic tree sap, and insulating feathers to craft an effective field dressing or medicinal poultice.",["class:Druid"]="Druids may share the questgiver's sorrow over slaying young woodland beasts, even when rising aggression and urgent medical necessity leave few other alternatives."},kw={"antiseptic sap","feathers for binding","poultice","shadowgale forest","shadowgale shriekling","shriekling","shrieklings"},m={id=94486},src={"In-game quest text: Feathers for Binding"},fv=true}
E["quest:94487"] = {t="quest",n="Unwanted and Unworthy",z="general",s="Unwanted and Unworthy is a quest on Zephras where adventurers aid Elegael in retaliating against the Al'Aketh Cultists after a bloody ambush in Shadowgale Forest. By slaying the cultists and recovering stolen heirlooms, players avenge fallen druids and reclaim sacred ancestral relics for the High Elder.",h="Unwanted and Unworthy is a quest on Zephras where adventurers aid Elegael in retaliating against the Al'Aketh...",sec={{t="Ambush in Shadowgale Forest",b="The peaceful balance of Zephras was abruptly shattered when the Al'Aketh Cultists launched a surprise ambush against the local druids in Shadowgale Forest. Elegael, once dismissive of these zealots as harmless dreamers with 'clouds between their ears,' was caught off guard by the sheer brutality of their assault. The attack left druid blood spilled across the forest floor, prompting a demand for swift retribution against the Footsoldiers and Stormchasers responsible.",sp=0},{t="Tainted Heirlooms and Lost Youth",b="To Elegael, the cultists' betrayal stripped them of their heritage: they no longer deserve to be called shen'dorei, nor do their remains deserve the winds of their homeland. Many of the cultists were young individuals with promising futures cut short by fanaticism. By reclaiming their bloodied heirlooms, adventurers help ensure these sacred items are returned to the High Elder, awaiting a wiser and more worthy generation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Al'Aketh Cultists?",a="The Al'Aketh Cultists are storm-obsessed cultists operating on the island of Zephras. According to Elegael, they were once regarded as merely foolish or having 'clouds between their ears,' but they abruptly turned to brutal violence against the local druids.",al={"who are alaketh","alaketh cult","who are the cultists"}},{q="Who is Elegael?",a="Elegael is a shen'dorei protector or druid affiliated with Zephras who was ambushed by the Al'Aketh Cultists. Distressed by the betrayal and bloodshed of young lives, Elegael seeks retribution and the recovery of stolen ancestral heirlooms.",al={"who is elegael","quest giver elegael"}},{q="What are the bloodied heirlooms?",a="The bloodied heirlooms belonged to young shen'dorei who were drawn into the Al'Aketh Cult. Elegael gathers them so they can eventually be returned to the High Elder's collection and passed down to a more worthy future generation.",al={"bloodied heirlooms","why collect heirlooms","what are the heirlooms"}},{q="What are the shen'dorei?",a="The shen'dorei are the people inhabiting Zephras. Elegael views the treacherous Al'Aketh Cultists as having forfeited their right to be called shen'dorei due to their violent betrayal and desecration of the island's harmony.",al={"shendorei meaning","who are shendorei"}},{q="Where is Shadowgale Forest?",a="Shadowgale Forest is a wooded region located on Zephras where Al'Aketh Footsoldiers and Stormchasers operate and where the unprovoked ambush against the druids took place.",al={"shadowgale forest location","where is shadowgale"}},{q="Why did the Al'Aketh Cultists ambush the druids?",a="The cultists' exact motives remain an unsettling mystery to Elegael. Although long dismissed as harmless zealots, their sudden, vicious uprising shattered the delicate balance maintained on the island.",al={"why did cultists attack","cult motives","why ambush druids"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Al'Aketh Cultists spilled the blood of local druids during their unprovoked assault on Zephras, making this strike a direct act of vengeance and defense of nature's balance."},kw={"al'aketh","bloodied heirlooms","elegael","footsoldiers","heirlooms","shadowgale forest","shen'dorei","stormchasers","zephras"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94487},src={"In-game quest text: Unwanted and Unworthy"},fv=true}
E["quest:94488"] = {t="quest",n="The Ties That Bind",z="general",s="The Ties That Bind is a quest in the Shadowgale Forest centering on a bitter betrayal within the druidic circle. Elegael Thornpaw discovers that a missing circle druid, Haalien, has joined the attacking cultists as a commander and orders his execution. The task reveals the painful personal cost of duty when family turns against their own kind.",h="The Ties That Bind is a quest in the Shadowgale Forest centering on a bitter betrayal within the druidic...",sec={{t="Betrayal in the Forest",b="In the depths of Shadowgale Forest, druids encounter a vicious cultist presence sowing terror and bloodshed. For Elegael Thornpaw, the attack is compounded by a bitter discovery: the scent of a former circle druid named Haalien hangs over the slaughter. Having vanished months earlier, Haalien did not perish in the wilds, but instead threw his lot in with the invading cultists, taking command of their violent campaign against his former kin.",sp=0},{t="Blood and Kinship",b="Haalien's betrayal goes deeper than broken vows to the Cenarion Circle. Upon the presentation of the fallen commander's severed head, Elegael reveals that Haalien was her own cousin. Though filled with fury at his atrocities, striking down her own kin proved too heavy a burden to bear alone. His death puts a bloody end to a renegade whose malice cost numerous innocent lives.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest The Ties That Bind?",a="Elegael Thornpaw is a druid stationed in the Shadowgale Forest. Grappling with an incursion of cultists, she directs adventurers to hunt down Commander Haalien after discovering his hand in the bloodshed.",al={"who gives ties that bind","quest giver shadowgale","who is elegael thornpaw"}},{q="Who is Commander Haalien?",a="Commander Haalien was formerly a druid of the Cenarion Circle who vanished months prior to the cultist attacks. Rather than falling victim to the encroaching darkness, he willingly joined the hostile cultists and rose to command their forces against his kin.",al={"who is haalien","commander haalien lore","haalien background"}},{q="Why does Elegael want Commander Haalien dead?",a="Haalien abandoned his druidic circle to command cultists who slaughtered innocent lives and spilled the blood of his brethren. Because of his actions and the danger he poses to the forest, Elegael orders his execution.",al={"why kill haalien","haalien crime","what did haalien do"}},{q="What is the connection between Elegael Thornpaw and Haalien?",a="Commander Haalien is Elegael Thornpaw's cousin. While deeply enraged by his crimes, she admits that executing her own flesh and blood directly would have been a bitter burden, prompting her to enlist an adventurer's help.",al={"elegael haalien related","how do elegael and haalien know each other"}},{q="Why are cultists present in the Shadowgale Forest?",a="The exact motives behind the cultists' presence in Shadowgale Forest remain baffling even to the local druids. However, their ranks are spearheaded by former circle members like Haalien, who directed violence and bloodshed against the forest's defenders.",al={"shadowgale forest cultists","why are cultists in shadowgale"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The events of the quest unfold within the Shadowgale Forest, where Elegael Thornpaw investigates the cultist invasion and confronts the grim reality of her cousin's betrayal.",al={"where does ties that bind happen","shadowgale forest location"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Cenarion Circle holds the preservation of balance and kinship sacred, making Haalien's betrayal and bloodspill among fellow circle druids an especially grave offense against the order."},kw={"cenarion circle","commander haalien","cultists","druid","elegael thornpaw","haalien","shadowgale forest","the ties that bind"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94488},src={"In-game quest text: The Ties That Bind"},fv=true}
E["quest:94489"] = {t="quest",n="The Wounds of Betrayal",z="general",s="The Wounds of Betrayal is a quest in Shadowgale Forest centering on the aftermath of a tragic assault against the rare shen'dorei druids. Players are sent by Elegael to deliver urgent poultices to her wounded brethren in Nightfang Den and search for her missing mate, Jorel Windsinger. The mission quickly exposes the bitter costs of betrayal and fuels a vengeful conflict against the Al'Aketh.",h="The Wounds of Betrayal is a quest in Shadowgale Forest centering on the aftermath of a tragic assault against...",sec={{t="Aid for the Shen'dorei",b="The Wounds of Betrayal begins with Elegael, a shen'dorei druid tending to the aftermath of a catastrophic assault in Shadowgale Forest. Having hastily prepared medicinal poultices, she urgently seeks an adventurer to deliver aid to her brethren taking shelter in the nearby Nightfang Den, just north across the clearing. The shen'dorei druids are exceedingly few in number, making the survival of every single member vital to their fragile enclave.",sp=0},{t="The Search for Jorel Windsinger",b="Beyond delivering emergency care to the seven battered druids in the cave, Elegael carries a personal plea close to her heart: finding her mate, Jorel Windsinger. When she fled the fighting to prepare medicines, Jorel was already clinging to life. Venturing into the dark recesses of Nightfang Den reveals the true devastation wrought upon the sanctuary and the grim fate that befell Jorel.",sp=1},{t="A Vow of Retribution",b="Upon hearing of Jorel's tragic end and the horrific toll on her brethren, Elegael's composure shatters into grief and fury. Gritting her teeth with a pained snarl, she renounces despair in favor of outright retribution, swearing that anyone allied with the shadowy Al'Aketh will suffer her unrelenting wrath for the slaughter of her people.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest The Wounds of Betrayal?",a="The quest is given by Elegael, a shen'dorei druid desperately trying to tend to her wounded brethren following a violent attack in Shadowgale Forest.",al={"who gives the wounds of betrayal","quest giver the wounds of betrayal","who is elegael"}},{q="Where is Nightfang Den located?",a="Nightfang Den is a cave situated north just across the clearing in Shadowgale Forest, where injured shen'dorei druids have retreated seeking refuge.",al={"where is nightfang den","nightfang den location","where are the wounded druids"}},{q="Who is Jorel Windsinger?",a="Jorel Windsinger is the mate of Elegael. He was severely injured in the attack and remained inside Nightfang Den, where the player discovers his tragic fate.",al={"who is jorel windsinger","jorel windsinger fate","where is jorel"}},{q="What are the shen'dorei druids?",a="The shen'dorei are a rare group with very few druidic practitioners among them. Because their numbers are so small, Elegael emphasizes that every single surviving life among them is precious.",al={"what are the shendorei","who are shendorei druids","shendorei druid lore"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are enemies responsible for or allied with those who attacked the druids. Upon learning of Jorel's fate and the devastation of her order, Elegael vows vengeance against the Al'Aketh and anyone allied with them.",al={"who are the alaketh","al aketh","why does elegael hate al aketh"}},{q="What happens to Jorel Windsinger in the quest?",a="When you locate Jorel Windsinger in the den, you discover that his condition took a tragic turn. Returning to Elegael with news of his fate and the dire state of the surviving druids drives her to grief and a furious vow of revenge.",al={"what happens to jorel windsinger","does jorel live","jorel outcome"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As druidic caretakers of the wilds, seeing fellow practitioners—specifically the scarce shen'dorei druids—succumb to betrayal and grievous injury carries a heavy, personal weight.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elves revere the druidic traditions, making the suffering of kindred druids in Shadowgale Forest and the struggle against treachery deeply resonant."},kw={"al'aketh","druid","elegael","jorel windsinger","nightfang den","shadowgale forest","shen'dorei","the wounds of betrayal"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94489},src={"In-game quest text: The Wounds of Betrayal"},fv=true}
E["quest:94490"] = {t="quest",n="Ripped Missive",z="general",s="The Ripped Missive is a blood-spattered letter recovered from Haalien that sheds light on a brutal cultist assault against a druid den in Shadowgale Forest. Its contents expose the callous motives behind the slaughter and hint at dangerous subversion originating from within Valanaar.",h="The Ripped Missive is a blood-spattered letter recovered from Haalien that sheds light on a brutal cultist...",sec={{t="Bloodstained Orders",b="Tucked behind the bracer of Haalien, this torn and bloodstained document holds grim intelligence concerning the recent violence in Shadowgale Forest. Recovered after a fierce skirmish, the scrap of writing sheds light on the dark intentions of the cultists who fell upon the local druid den, offering vital clues for those seeking justice for the fallen.",sp=0},{t="Senseless Slaughter",b="Upon presenting the parchment to Elegael Thornpaw in the Shadowgale Forest, the horrifying truth of the raid comes to light. The cultists did not strike out of a specific feud with the druids; they came seeking a mysterious pylon, murdering the den's inhabitants simply because they were an inconvenience in their path. Enraged by the senseless massacre, Elegael vows vengeance against any cultists remaining in the forest.",sp=1},{t="Treachery in Valanaar",b="Beyond revealing the cultists' focus on the pylon, the missive bears a far more alarming revelation: the directive appears to have originated from Valanaar itself. This grim realization points to a traitor operating from within the settlement. To uncover the conspiracy before more lives are lost, the missive must be carried without delay to Lotheluum in Valanaar.",sp=2}},faq={{q="What is the Ripped Missive?",a="The Ripped Missive is a bloodstained, torn letter discovered tucked behind the bracer of Haalien following a battle. It contains critical intelligence exposing the motives behind a cultist assault on a druid den in Shadowgale Forest.",al={"what is ripped missive","ripped missive quest","bloodstained note haalien"}},{q="Where is the missive recovered from?",a="The missive is recovered directly from Haalien, found slipped behind one of their bracers after a violent confrontation. The letter was damaged and stained with blood during the combat.",al={"who dropped the ripped missive","where did ripped missive come from","who is haalien"}},{q="Who should receive the Ripped Missive?",a="The missive is initially brought to Elegael Thornpaw in Shadowgale Forest. Upon reading the document, Elegael directs that it be delivered swiftly to Lotheluum in Valanaar to warn of potential treachery.",al={"who wants the ripped missive","who do i give ripped missive to","elegael thornpaw missive"}},{q="Why did the cultists attack the druid den?",a="The letter reveals that the cultists did not target the druid den out of hatred for the druids themselves, but rather to secure a pylon situated there. The druids residing within were mercilessly slaughtered merely because they stood in the cultists' way.",al={"why did cultists attack the druid den","cultist motive druid den","why did haalien attack"}},{q="Where did the attack orders originate?",a="The missive indicates that the cultists' orders originated from Valanaar, suggesting that a traitor is actively hiding and orchestrating operations from within the settlement.",al={"where did the orders come from","who sent haalien","valanaar traitor"}},{q="Who is Lotheluum and why must they be notified?",a="Lotheluum is a contact located in Valanaar. Elegael Thornpaw orders the adventurer to take the missive to Lotheluum to alert them that the cultists' deadly orders trace back to someone hidden in Valanaar.",al={"who is lotheluum","why take missive to lotheluum","lotheluum valanaar"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Druids will find bitter tragedy in this letter, which reveals that the slaughtered druids of the den were simply in the way of cultists seeking an ancient pylon."},kw={"cultist","druid den","elegael thornpaw","haalien","lotheluum","pylon","ripped missive","shadowgale forest","valanaar"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94490},src={"In-game quest text: Ripped Missive"},fv=true}
E["quest:94491"] = {t="quest",n="The Fate of the Den",z="general",s="The Fate of the Den recounts the grim aftermath of a cultist assault upon a secluded druid den. An adventurer is dispatched to carry a recovered missive to Lotheluum in Valanaar, delivering news of severe casualties. The dispatch uncovers a deeply troubling reality: someone within Valanaar has been feeding vital information to the enemy.",h="The Fate of the Den recounts the grim aftermath of a cultist assault upon a secluded druid den.",sec={{t="Tragedy at the Den",b="Following a violent incursion by cultists at the druid den, the surviving defenders find themselves battered and mourning the loss of their comrades. While the immediate crisis was stabilized with outside aid, the physical and emotional devastation is immense. Those who fell cannot be restored, and the wounded survivors remain in no condition to undertake a long trek while lingering cultists still roam nearby.",sp=0},{t="Treachery in Valanaar",b="A recovered missive confirms that the cult did not act at random; someone concealed within Valanaar has been actively leaking intelligence to them. When presented with the dispatch, Lotheluum expresses utter horror over the slaughter of his fellows and the terrifying implication that an accomplice or traitor walks free in their settlement. Determined that their sacrifice will not be forgotten, he brings the evidence straight to the High Elder.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Lotheluum?",a="Lotheluum is a figure residing in Valanaar who receives reports concerning the druid den. Upon learning of the assault and reviewing the recovered missive, he expresses profound grief for his fallen fellows and vows to bring the evidence of betrayal directly to the High Elder.",al={"lotheluum","who is lotheluum","lotheluum npc"}},{q="What happened at the druid den?",a="The druid den suffered a devastating strike by hostile cultists, leaving many defenders dead. Although the immediate threat was suppressed with the aid of an adventurer, the surviving protectors were badly battered and discovered evidence that the attack was guided by an insider.",al={"what happened at the den","druid den attack","fate of the den backstory"}},{q="What does the recovered missive reveal?",a="The missive reveals that a correspondent concealed within the settlement of Valanaar has been secretly feeding intelligence to the cult. This intelligence suggests that the deadly raid on the den was made possible through local sabotage and espionage.",al={"what does the missive say","den missive","traitor missive"}},{q="Who is the High Elder mentioned by Lotheluum?",a="The High Elder is a prominent authority in Valanaar to whom Lotheluum answers. Due to the severe nature of the casualties and the threat of an active traitor, Lotheluum immediately escalates the missive and news of the tragedy to the High Elder.",al={"who is the high elder","valanaar high elder","high elder"}},{q="Who was responsible for attacking the druid den?",a="The assault was carried out by members of a cult operating in the region. The cultists managed to inflict catastrophic losses on the den's inhabitants thanks to guidance from an informant sheltered in Valanaar.",al={"who attacked the den","who attacked druid den","den cultists"}},{q="Why could the survivor at the den not deliver the missive personally?",a="The survivor at the den was too wounded to endure the trek to Valanaar after the attack. In addition to being physically unfit for the journey, the survivor stayed behind to eliminate remaining cultists while sending an adventurer to sound the alarm.",al={"why do we deliver the missive","quest giver injured","why cant the survivor go to valanaar"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Druids feel the weight of this tragedy acutely, as the fallen at the den were their fellows whose secluded refuge was betrayed to hostile cultists."},kw={"cultists","druid den","high elder","lotheluum","missive","the fate of the den","traitor","valanaar"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=94491},src={"In-game quest text: The Fate of the Den"},fv=true}
E["quest:94493"] = {t="quest",n="A Sacrifice in Vain",z="general",s="Elegael Thornpaw fears the Al'Aketh Cultists will overwhelm the wounded in her den. A message meant for Valanaar has not reached them, leaving her to ask for help.",h="Elegael Thornpaw fears the Al'Aketh Cultists will overwhelm the wounded in her den.",sec={{t="A Plea for Aid",b="The Al'Aketh Cultists fell upon Elegael Thornpaw's fellows with almost no warning. Many in her den are wounded or dying, and she does not know how long they can survive the attack. She had tried to send word to Valanaar, but the message did not get through. With no reinforcements in sight, Elegael asks you to help.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Elegael Thornpaw asks for help as the Al'Aketh Cultists attack her fellows.",al={"who needs help","Elegael Thornpaw","Elegael"}},{q="What happened to the people in the den?",a="The Al'Aketh Cultists ambushed them with little warning. Many are wounded or dying.",al={"den wounded","what happened here","cultist attack"}},{q="Why did Valanaar not send reinforcements?",a="Elegael's message did not reach Valanaar, so reinforcements have not arrived.",al={"where are reinforcements","Valanaar message","message to Valanaar"}},{q="What does Elegael need?",a="Elegael asks you to help her wounded fellows survive the Al'Aketh Cultists' attack.",al={"how can I help","help Elegael","what does she ask"}}},kw={"a sacrifice in vain","al'aketh cultists","den","elegael thornpaw","reinforcements","valanaar","wounded"},m={id=94493},src={"In-game quest text: A Sacrifice in Vain"},fv=true}
E["quest:94494"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="The Call of Water is a spiritual quest for shamans ready to commune with the purifying nature of the water element. Having established harmony with wind, earth, and flame, the initiate reaches Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan, who urges them to take the first step toward learning water's purity.",h="The Call of Water is a spiritual quest for shamans ready to commune with the purifying nature of the water...",sec={{t="The Path of Purity",b="The Call of Water marks a vital stage in a shaman's journey into elemental balance. With earth grounding every step, the rush of the wind at one's back, and the inner fire burning bright, the traveler is called to grasp the deeper nature of water. It is not merely an external force, but a spiritual reflection of purity and resilience.",sp=0},{t="Ascent into the Elements",b="Guided by a seasoned mentor speaking in the sturdy cadence of the peaks, the initiate is urged forward without hesitation. Shamanic communion is never handed over lightly; it demands physical effort, enduring spirit, and an ascent into rugged terrain. As the mentor warns, the mountain waits for no one, and the journey toward true understanding must begin with that decisive first step.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Water?",a="Call of Water represents a spiritual rite of passage for shamans seeking communion with the element of water. Having already learned the steadfastness of earth, the rush of wind, and the spark of fire, the petitioner is summoned to learn water's deeper truth.",al={"what is call of water","call of water quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Who guides this step of the journey?",a="Eldrun Stormbreaker, the shaman trainer in Ironforge, sends the initiate to Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan. Norric speaks these words, urging decisive action and reminding the traveler that the elements and the heights do not wait for the hesitant.",al={"who gives call of water","who says the mountain waits for no one","call of water mentor"}},{q="What does it mean that the mountain waits for no one?",a="It is a call to take the first step of a demanding ascent. Shamanic journeys often lead high into peaks and remote wilderness where the spirits dwell, demanding resolve rather than lingering doubt.",al={"what does mountain waits for no one mean","the mountain waits for no one","mountain meaning"}},{q="What is the truth of water's purity?",a="In shamanic practice, each element offers distinct teachings. While stone provides stability and fire fuels resolve, water embodies cleansing, fluidity, and purity—essential wisdom for balancing the spirits within oneself.",al={"why water purity","truth of waters purity","water element meaning"}},{q="How does water relate to the other elements here?",a="The journey acknowledges earth, wind, and fire as foundations already felt in boot, breath, and belly before approaching water. Harmony with the elements requires mastering balance rather than pursuing a single power in isolation.",al={"which elements come first","prerequisites for water","call of water elements"}},{q="Where does the initiate go after hearing these words?",a="The initiate must take the first step. Norric Lochthane sends them on to the Wetlands to seek the water sapta from Hervdana Saegrund, who dwells in a cave beneath the southern falls.",al={"what happens next call of water","where do i go now","next step call of water"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This trial marks a fundamental step in attuning to the elemental spirits, progressing from earth and fire toward understanding the fluidity and purity of water.",["race:Dwarf"]="The gruff dwarven cadence of the mentor reflects how dwarven shamans in Forever approach communion with nature through rugged mountain paths."},kw={"call of water","elements","mountain","purity","shaman","totem trial","water totem"},m={id=94494},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94495"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water sends spiritual seekers into the Wetlands to learn the cleansing power of water and seek the water sapta. In a secluded cave beneath the southern falls, the dwarf Hervdana Saegrund greets travelers braving the wilderness.",h="Call of Water sends spiritual seekers into the Wetlands to learn the cleansing power of water and seek the...",sec={{t="The Nature of Water",b="The journey to understand water begins with a simple lesson on its dual nature: water embodies rebirth, carving stone and cleansing impurities when pure, but threatening ruin when tainted. Initiates seeking communion are sent into the wild to obtain a water sapta from those who dwell beside the element.",sp=0},{t="Shelter Beneath the Falls",b="Deep in the damp marshes of the Wetlands, Hervdana Saegrund and her family make their home in a cave hidden directly beneath the southern waterfalls. Far removed from the protection of Ironforge guards, they endure the threat of Dark Iron dwarves lurking in the surrounding swamps to maintain their isolated life by the rushing falls.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Hervdana Saegrund?",a="Hervdana Saegrund is a dwarf who lives with her family in a cave beneath the southern falls of the Wetlands. She aids shamans seeking communion with water and the secrets of the water sapta.",al={"who is hervdana","hervdana saegrund","where is hervdana"}},{q="What is the water sapta?",a="A water sapta is a ceremonial elixir used by shamans to commune with the spirits of water. To acquire one in the eastern realms, seekers are directed to Hervdana Saegrund near the southern falls.",al={"what is water sapta","sapta meaning","why get water sapta"}},{q="Where can Hervdana Saegrund be found?",a="Hervdana and her family dwell secluded inside a cave located directly beneath the southern waterfalls in the Wetlands.",al={"where does hervdana live","where is the cave","southern falls location"}},{q="Why does Hervdana live in the dangerous Wetlands?",a="Despite the constant danger of Dark Iron dwarves lurking throughout the marshes and the total absence of city guards, Hervdana and her kin have chosen the seclusion of the waterfall cave for their own reasons.",al={"why live in wetlands","wetlands dangers","dark irons wetlands"}},{q="What is the lesson of water taught here?",a="Water represents rebirth, carving stone, washing away filth, and soothing the weary when pure. However, if corrupted or tainted, it ruins everything it touches.",al={"what does water mean","philosophy of water","water lesson"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Call of Water quest?",a="Call of Water is a shaman rite of passage guiding initiates to understand the nature of the element and seek out the water sapta from isolated guides.",al={"what kind of quest is this","who is this quest for","dwarf shaman quest"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="The quest marks a vital step in learning the nature of water and obtaining a water sapta to commune with the spirits.",["race:Dwarf"]="Hervdana recognizes the seeker as kin braving the dangerous marshlands and Dark Iron dwarves lurking nearby.",["faction:Alliance"]="The journey takes travelers deep into the perilous Wetlands, far away from any dwarven or Alliance guard patrols."},kw={"call of water","dwarf","hervdana saegrund","shaman","southern falls","water sapta","wetlands"},rel={"quest:972","zone:wetlands","topic:dwarf"},m={id=94495},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94497"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water is a spiritual quest in the Wetlands guiding an initiate on the path to earning the sapta. By gathering brackish estuary water where mountain falls meet sea tides, the seeker learns the vital energies of water while navigating the hazards of the marsh.",h="Call of Water is a spiritual quest in the Wetlands guiding an initiate on the path to earning the sapta.",sec={{t="The Way of the Sapta",b="To earn the sapta and commune with the spirits, an initiate must seek water in its various forms. Hervdana guides seekers to observe how water moves, changes lives, and sustains the land. The journey begins with understanding estuary waters where ocean tide meets fresh highland runoff.",sp=0},{t="Where Fresh Meets Brackish",b="At the base of the waterfalls beneath Hervdana's cave, highland springwater descends into the marsh to collide with saltwater brought inland by coastal tides. Ocean tides carry sediment, minerals, and energies vastly different from clear mountain snowmelt, making the estuary a wellspring of natural wisdom.",sp=0},{t="Shadows in the Marsh",b="Gathering water in the Wetlands is not without peril. In addition to the dangerous marsh fauna, Dark Iron dwarves have pushed uncomfortably far north. Hervdana observes with regret the ongoing hostility among the dwarven clans, noting how much dwarfkind could achieve if they ever set aside their feuds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the sapta?",a="The sapta is a sacred draught used to commune with the elemental spirits. To earn it, an initiate must seek out water in its various states and understand how it shapes the land and nourishes living things.",al={"what is the sapta","sapta meaning","why seek sapta"}},{q="Who is Hervdana?",a="Hervdana gives the task from her cave situated above the waterfalls in the Wetlands. She acts as a guide in gathering water samples to attune an initiate to the element.",al={"who gives call of water","where is hervdana","hervdana cave"}},{q="Why gather water where the fresh meets the brackish?",a="Estuary water represents a special confluence where highland snowmelt and fresh springs mix with ocean tides. This meeting of fresh and mineral-rich saltwater carries unique sediment and spiritual wisdom.",al={"why estuary water","brackish water wetlands","fresh meets salt"}},{q="What dangers lurk near the waterfalls?",a="Dark Iron dwarves have crept northward into the Wetlands, posing an active hazard to travelers and elemental seekers trying to reach the marshes and waterfalls.",al={"dark iron in wetlands","why are dark iron dwarves here","dark iron threat"}},{q="What does it mean to become a vessel?",a="A vessel is an initiate who has begun actively seeking the waters' wisdom, carrying offerings of nature's waters to develop deeper elemental harmony.",al={"what is a vessel","vessel of water"}},{q="What is the Empty Brown Waterskin used for?",a="The Empty Brown Waterskin is the vessel provided by Hervdana to collect the brackish sample from the base of the marsh waterfalls.",al={"empty brown waterskin use","what skin is used"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Seeking the sapta requires understanding the nature of water across different lands, attuning the spirit to its various manifestations.",["race:Dwarf"]="Hervdana laments the lingering division between the dwarf clans, especially as Dark Iron dwarves encroach as far north as the Wetlands."},kw={"call of water","dark iron","estuary","hervdana","sapta","waterskin","wetlands"},rel={"zone:wetlands","item:empty-brown-waterskin"},m={id=94497},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94499"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water is a quest tasking travelers with journeying to Stonewatch Falls in Redridge to investigate suspected corruption in the runoff from Lake Everstill. By filling an Empty Red Waterskin and returning it to Hervdana Saegrund in the Wetlands, shamans uncover the true source behind mutated local murlocs while securing a key reagent for their spiritual trials.",h="Call of Water is a quest tasking travelers with journeying to Stonewatch Falls in Redridge to investigate...",sec={{t="A Suspect Cascade",b="Hervdana Saegrund in the Wetlands dispatches aspiring shamans south to Redridge to investigate the waterways tumbling down Stonewatch Falls. While Lake Everstill nourishes the settlement of Lakeshire with pristine water, the murlocs lurking at the base of the cascade have grown into unusually massive, twisted beasts. Suspecting venom, foul sorcery, or seepage from the old fortress above, Hervdana tasks travelers with gathering a sample in an Empty Red Waterskin.",sp=0},{t="The True Source of Corruption",b="Upon inspecting the filled waterskin, Hervdana discovers that the water cascading from Everstill is entirely clean and untouched by pollution. The physical distortion observed among the local Nightcrawler murlocs is not caused by foul drinking water, but rather by the dark sorcery wielded by shadowseers lurking around Stonewatch. Proving both the purity of the falls and the dedication of the shaman, the sample serves as a vital reagent needed to brew a spiritual sapta.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="Hervdana Saegrund, located in the Wetlands, assigns this task. She studies water sources and reagents required to craft a sapta for an aspiring shaman's spiritual trials.",al={"who gives call of water","quest giver hervdana"}},{q="Why are you sent to collect water from Stonewatch Falls?",a="Hervdana suspects that strange venom, dark magic, or corruption might be seeping over Stonewatch Falls from the old fort above, leading to deformed murlocs at the base. She needs a fresh sample to inspect.",al={"why fill waterskin redridge","stonewatch falls purpose"}},{q="Is the water at Stonewatch Falls actually tainted?",a="No, the water itself proves completely pure and uncontaminated. Hervdana determines that the twisted state of the local murlocs stems instead from dark magic cast by regional shadowseers rather than fouled runoff.",al={"are stonewatch waters tainted","is lake everstill corrupted"}},{q="Why are the murlocs around Stonewatch Falls so large and twisted?",a="The Nightcrawler murlocs near the base of the falls are large and twisted compared to those found in the Loch or Wetlands. Hervdana concludes this change is wrought by the foul magic of local shadowseers.",al={"why are redridge murlocs mutated","stonewatch murlocs difference"}},{q="What is the water sample used for?",a="A sapta is a ceremonial mixture that allows a shaman to pierce the veil and perceive elemental spirits. Hervdana collects pure samples from various waters to brew the reagent required for yours.",al={"what is sapta","call of water reward purpose"}},{q="Who is Norric?",a="Norric is an associate or mentor involved in the shamanic trials whom Hervdana mentions will be pleased with your successful retrieval of the pure water sample.",al={"who is norric","norric call of water"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This quest is an essential step in brewing a water sapta, testing a shaman's devotion and perception across distant waterways before communing with the spirits of water."},kw={"call of water","hervdana saegrund","lake everstill","nightcrawler murlocs","redridge","sapta","shadowseers","stonewatch falls","wetlands"},rel={"subzone:stonewatch-falls","subzone:lake-everstill","subzone:stonewatch","subzone:lakeshire","zone:wetlands","topic:murloc","item:empty-red-waterskin","subzone:the-loch"},m={id=94499},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94500"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water tasks an adventurer with journeying into the emerald canopy of Ashenvale to collect pure, ancient water from the lake cradling Astranaar. It marks the final gathering step before the traveler is sent back to Norric.",h="Call of Water tasks an adventurer with journeying into the emerald canopy of Ashenvale to collect pure...",sec={{t="The Search for Pure Water",b="Call of Water charges an adventurer with filling a waterskin from the uncorrupted waters of Ashenvale. The seeker must venture to Astranaar, a haven nestled amidst clean, ancient waters that lap against sunken ruins from a bygone era. This pristine sample serves as the final liquid needed by the questgiver before the traveler is sent on to Norric.",sp=0},{t="The Road to Astranaar",b="Reaching the depths of Ashenvale requires crossing the sea from the Eastern Kingdoms. The recommended path takes travelers aboard the ship sailing out of Menethil Harbor toward Auberdine, making port at Southshore along the journey. From the docks of Auberdine, the road leads inland into the emerald boughs of Ashenvale, where travelers are warned to stay alert among the watchful shadows of the trees.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Call of Water?",a="Call of Water is a quest requiring the traveler to take an empty waterskin deep into the ancient forest of Ashenvale. The goal is to collect a sample of pure, ancient, and potentially enchanted water from the lake cradling the settlement of Astranaar before returning it to the questgiver.",al={"what is call of water","call of water quest","call of water objective"}},{q="Where do I collect the water for this quest?",a="The water must be gathered from the lake surrounding Astranaar, located deep within Ashenvale. The waters there remain uncorrupted, flowing amidst ancient sunken ruins that date back to a bygone era.",al={"where to find pure water","where to fill waterskin","call of water location"}},{q="How do I travel to Ashenvale for this quest?",a="Travelers are advised to board the boat from Menethil Harbor to Auberdine, which makes a stop at Southshore along the way. From Auberdine on the Darkshore coast, one travels south into the deep emerald woods of Ashenvale to reach Astranaar.",al={"how to get to astranaar","travel to ashenvale","boat to astranaar"}},{q="What makes the water at Astranaar special?",a="The lake cradling Astranaar is noted for holding waters untouched by corruption. Beneath its surface lie submerged stone ruins hinting at an ancient, bygone era of elven civilization, lending the water its pristine and potentially enchanted qualities.",al={"what lake in astranaar","astranaar water ruins","ruins in astranaar lake"}},{q="Who is Norric?",a="Norric is the individual awaiting the traveler after this final liquid sample is gathered. The questgiver collects this pure Ashenvale water as the last ingredient before sending the adventurer back to Norric.",al={"who is norric","return to norric"}},{q="What dangers should I expect in Ashenvale?",a="Ashenvale is an ancient, dense forest filled with perils. The questgiver warns travelers to be constantly alert, cautioning that the forest has watchful eyes that do not blink as one ventures beneath its emerald canopy.",al={"danger in ashenvale","ashenvale threats"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="This quest guides travelers along the Alliance maritime route from the Eastern Kingdoms out to Kalimdor, connecting Menethil Harbor to Auberdine and the Night Elf settlement of Astranaar.",["race:NightElf"]="The quest directs travelers to the pristine waters surrounding Astranaar, one of the heartlands of the Kaldorei in Ashenvale, where ancient ruins still lie undisturbed."},kw={"ashenvale","astranaar","auberdine","call of water","menethil","norric","southshore","waterskin"},rel={"zone:ashenvale","subzone:astranaar","subzone:auberdine","subzone:southshore"},m={id=94500},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94501"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="The Call of Water is a spiritual trial for shamans seeking the sacred water totem rites. By delivering pure water blessed by Hervdana to Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan, seekers prepare the elemental sapta necessary to commune with spirits at Stendel's Pond.",h="The Call of Water is a spiritual trial for shamans seeking the sacred water totem rites.",sec={{t="The Path to the Water Totem",b="The Call of Water represents an essential trial of spiritual attunement for aspiring shamans seeking harmony with the element of water. Undertaking this path requires gathering water blessed by Hervdana herself, proving both patience and reverence for the spirits before bringing the vessel to Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan.",sp=0},{t="Birthing the Sapta",b="Upon receiving the blessed vessel, Norric reveals that the sacred liquid is destined to brew a water sapta. This draught allows the seeker to pierce the unseen and witness the water spirits dwelling at Stendel's Pond near Moonbrook. Every single drop is deemed precious, as even a dampened stopper holds purpose in the sacred rites.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Norric Lochthane?",a="Norric Lochthane is a mentor residing in Loch Modan who guides candidates through their elemental communion. He oversees the preparations and rites needed to earn the water totem.",al={"who is norric","norric loch modan","who is norric lochthane"}},{q="Who is Hervdana?",a="Hervdana Saegrund is the guide in the Wetlands, living with her family in a cave by the southern falls, who blesses sacred water gathered by aspiring shamans, imbuing it with the spiritual purity required to craft water sapta.",al={"who is hervdana","blessed by hervdana","hervdana water"}},{q="What is a sapta used for?",a="A sapta is an elemental draught prepared using sacred water. Drinking it allows a shaman to pierce the veil and perceive elusive water spirits that dwell within natural pools.",al={"what is water sapta","sapta purpose","what does sapta do"}},{q="Where is Stendel's Pond located?",a="Stendel's Pond is located in Westfall near Moonbrook. Its waters harbor elemental water spirits that a shaman must seek out after consuming their prepared sapta.",al={"where is stendels pond","stendel pond location","water spirits stendel"}},{q="Why is the Vial of Purest Water important?",a="The Vial of Purest Water contains blessed water essential for brewing the water sapta and performing the sacred rites required to earn a water totem. Norric cautions that not even a single drop may be wasted.",al={"why need pure water","vial of purest water purpose"}},{q="What are the water totem rites?",a="The water totem rites are spiritual trials undertaken by shamans to attune themselves to the element of water, demonstrating respect and balance before being granted a water totem.",al={"what are water totem rites","how to get water totem"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This trial is a crucial step in communing with the elements to earn the water totem rites and craft the sapta needed to see elemental spirits.",["race:Dwarf"]="The journey connects dwarven shamanic candidates with elemental mentors like Norric Lochthane across Khaz Modan.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance shamans seek communion with water spirits across eastern lands, bridging Loch Modan and Westfall."},kw={"call of water","hervdana","loch modan","moonbrook","norric lochthane","shaman","stendel's pond","vial of purest water","water sapta","water totem"},rel={"item:vial-of-purest-water","zone:lochmodan","subzone:stendel-s-pond","subzone:moonbrook"},m={id=94501},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94502"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water is an elemental quest requiring a journey from Loch Modan to Westfall to restore balance to a corrupted spring. By purging the tainted entity at Stendel's Pond and performing an ancient ritual at the forgotten shrine, petitioners earn the favor of the water spirits and their Water Totem.",h="Call of Water is an elemental quest requiring a journey from Loch Modan to Westfall to restore balance to a...",sec={{t="A Spring Tainted by Shadow",b="In the shadow of Moonbrook lies Stendel's Pond, once celebrated as a clear and pure spring. Dark influences sweeping across Westfall have fouled its tranquil waters, driving the local water spirits into profound silence. An aged shrine stands along the shore, neglected and draped in dust and cobwebs, waiting for someone to awaken the dormant powers within.",sp=0},{t="The Rite of Cleansing",b="Norric in Loch Modan guides petitioners through the ritual required to restore balance. The rite demands facing the foul entity polluting Stendel's Pond, securing its bracers, and offering them upon the stone shrine along with drops of an alchemical draught. When the incantation is spoken, the vapors summon forth a cleansed spirit, imparting wisdom and proving the bearer worthy of the Water Totem.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Water quest?",a="Call of Water is a spiritual trial for shamans seeking to commune with the elements of water. The quest tasks the petitioner with cleansing the corruption at Stendel's Pond in Westfall, using an old shrine there to summon a cleansed water spirit and earn their Water Totem.",al={"what is call of water","water totem quest","water quest westfall"}},{q="Who gives this quest?",a="The quest is given by Norric, a mentor situated in Loch Modan. He instructs petitioners in elemental rites, provides special potions, and guides them in cleansing corrupted elemental waters.",al={"who gives call of water","norric loch modan","quest giver"}},{q="Where is Stendel's Pond?",a="Stendel's Pond lies just outside Moonbrook in southwestern Westfall. Once a pure spring, it has fallen still and murky under the influence of dark, corrupting forces.",al={"where is stendels pond","stendel pond location","pond near moonbrook"}},{q="How do you cleanse the corruption at Stendel's Pond?",a="To cleanse the waters, you must defeat the corrupting presence at Stendel's Pond, take its bracers, and place them on the ancient shrine alongside the remaining drops of potion while incanting the words taught by Norric.",al={"how to cleanse pond","cleanse stendels pond","shrine ritual"}},{q="What do you earn for completing this quest?",a="Completing the trial and returning proof of the cleansing to Norric in Loch Modan rewards the petitioner with their Water Totem, granting command over the elemental powers of water.",al={"what reward call of water","water totem reward"}},{q="Why is Stendel's Pond corrupted?",a="The source of the darkness is uncertain. The pool lies unnaturally stagnant, surrounded by the bones of beasts, prompting concern over whether it was poisoned by Westfall's spreading malaise or acts as a fountainhead of corruption itself.",al={"why is stendels pond corrupted","what corrupted the pond"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This trial represents a crucial rite of passage to attune with the water spirits and earn the Water Totem, demonstrating harmony and mastery over the elemental forces.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance shamans must travel across their territories, venturing from Loch Modan into the troubled lands of Westfall to purify corrupted waters."},kw={"call of water","loch modan","moonbrook","norric","shaman","stendel's pond","water totem","westfall"},rel={"subzone:stendel-s-pond","zone:westfall","zone:lochmodan","subzone:moonbrook"},m={id=94502},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94503"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="Call of Water is a quest centered in Westfall that tasks adventurers with purifying corrupted elemental forces. By unraveling restless spirits with radiant light, players commune with a Minor Manifestation of Water to safeguard the region's dwindling vitality.",h="Call of Water is a quest centered in Westfall that tasks adventurers with purifying corrupted elemental...",sec={{t="Cleansing the Tainted Waters",b="In the windswept lands of Westfall, the natural balance of the waters faces contamination by corrupt spirits. The rite known as the Call of Water requires confronting this malevolence, invoking sacred words that conjure radiant light to disperse the restless, tainted essences into harmless ash and quiet mist.",sp=0},{t="The Spirit's Warning",b="With the corrupt entities driven back, a Minor Manifestation of Water can safely emerge to commune. While the immediate corruption ebbs, the spirit cautions that the vital energies of the land continue to wane, reminding champions that preserving life is an unending vigil.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Call of Water?",a="Call of Water is a spiritual trial in Westfall where corrupt spirits are banished by a ritual invocation that erupts in light, freeing the troubled waters and allowing communion with a Minor Manifestation of Water.",al={"what is call of water","call of water westfall","westfall water quest"}},{q="Where can the Minor Manifestation of Water be found?",a="The Minor Manifestation of Water is located in Westfall. It appears once the surrounding corrupt spirits are subdued and dispelled, waiting to speak with those who aided the waters.",al={"where is the minor manifestation of water","manifestation location","water spirit westfall"}},{q="What happens when the corrupted spirits are cleansed?",a="As the incantation concludes, brilliant light erupts to disperse the corrupt spirits, causing their tainted essence to unravel and scatter like ash while water hisses into gentle mist.",al={"what happens during ritual","corrupt spirits call of water"}},{q="Is the land fully restored after completing the task?",a="The immediate corruption is beaten back and ebbs away, but the elemental warns that vitality continues to wane, leaving the enduring protection of life in mortal hands.",al={"is corruption gone","did we save the water"}},{q="Who is called upon to assist the manifestation?",a="The quest recognizes the player's calling, giving thanks to their class for stepping forward to cleanse the spirits and shoulder the responsibility of protecting dwindling life.",al={"who can do call of water","class requirement water quest"}},{q="Why were the water spirits corrupted in Westfall?",a="Corrupt spirits have disturbed the natural waters along Westfall. Although the precise origin of the taint remains subtle, their malevolent presence directly saps the living vitality of the area.",al={"why is water corrupted westfall","westfall water problem"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Communing with primal elements and cleansing tainted water spirits is a foundational rite of the elements, reminding shamans of their eternal duty to balance nature.",["faction:Alliance"]="The manifestation resides in Westfall, an Alliance territory where local spirits endure the creeping blight touching the land."},kw={"call of water","cleansing","corruption","elemental","minor manifestation of water","water spirit","westfall"},rel={"npc:minor-manifestation-of-water","zone:westfall"},m={id=94503},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94505"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Water",z="general",s="The Call of Water is an elemental rite of passage where shamans learn to commune with water spirits and wield their restorative powers. By retrieving a Shard of Water from Westfall for Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan, initiates prove their dedication and receive their water totem.",h="The Call of Water is an elemental rite of passage where shamans learn to commune with water spirits and wield...",sec={{t="The Path of Water",b="The Call of Water is a vital spiritual trial undertaken by shamans to forge a bond with the element of water. Initiates are tasked with journeying to Westfall and returning to Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan with the Shard of Water. By demonstrating their understanding of water's life-giving essence and its role in purging corruption, shamans earn their water totem and take an essential step on their elemental journey.",sp=0},{t="Giver of Life",b="The trial imparts a foundational elemental truth: water is life, and corruption brings an end to life. Shamans are charged with wielding water's restorative strength to protect the vulnerable and preserve living things. As mentor Norric Lochthane observes upon receiving the Shard of Water, the pure gifts of water reach far beyond simple physical mending, touching upon endurance, renewal, and spiritual balance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Norric Lochthane?",a="Norric Lochthane is a shaman mentor stationed in Loch Modan. He guides initiates through their trials to understand water spirits, tests their resolve with a journey to Westfall, and presents them with their water totem upon delivering proof of their communion.",al={"who is norric lochthane","who gives water totem","norric loch modan"}},{q="What is the Shard of Water?",a="The Shard of Water is the physical proof brought back from Westfall to Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan. It signifies that the initiate has communed with water, grasped its life-giving nature, and learned to combat corruption.",al={"what is shard of water","shard of water use","shard of water quest item"}},{q="Why does the trial lead to Westfall?",a="Initiates are sent to Westfall to understand water's life-giving power and how corruption threatens it. Even modest victories in that troubled land carry weight in demonstrating the initiate's ability to protect life.",al={"why go to westfall","westfall water quest","journey to westfall call of water"}},{q="What is the significance of the water totem?",a="The water totem embodies a shaman's bond with the element of water. While primarily known for soothing and mending wounds through pure water, its spiritual gifts extend far beyond basic healing to encompassing preservation and vitality.",al={"what does water totem do","why do shamans need water totem","water totem lore"}},{q="What lesson does the trial teach about water?",a="The elemental philosophy taught during the trial emphasizes that water gives life, whereas corruption halts it. A shaman is charged with using water's elemental power to nurture, protect, and restore balance wherever corruption threatens living things.",al={"what is water is life","call of water philosophy","shaman water teachings"}},{q="What comes after completing the Call of Water?",a="Norric Lochthane notes that an initiate's next elemental trial will not come right away. He encourages shamans to take time to practice and explore water's deeper mysteries before undertaking further trials.",al={"what trial comes next","next shaman trial","what happens after call of water"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This trial marks a fundamental rite of passage, granting a shaman communion with water spirits and their water totem to heal and sustain life.",["faction:Alliance"]="Unlike the Horde shamans of Kalimdor, Alliance-aligned initiates report to mentors like Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan and face trials in places like Westfall."},kw={"call of water","loch modan","norric lochthane","shaman","shard of water","water totem","westfall"},rel={"item:shard-of-water","zone:lochmodan","zone:westfall"},m={id=94505},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Water"},fv=true}
E["quest:94568"] = {t="quest",n="The Cult's True Plans",z="general",s="The cult's informant has died before sharing what he knew. Talaanis Shadowsong must help investigators find another way to oppose the cult.",h="The cult's informant has died before sharing what he knew.",sec={{t="A Silenced Defector",b="A frightened insider left the cult and hoped to seek help beyond it. Before he could reveal what he knew, he was killed. His warnings remain, but the cult's plans do not.",sp=0},{t="Rethinking the Approach",b="The informant's death leaves the leadership without its chief source of knowledge. They consult Talaanis Shadowsong and reconsider how to confront the cult. An open attack with peacekeepers is judged a losing battle, so the investigators must find another way forward.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was the turncoat mentioned in this quest?",a="The turncoat was a cult insider who sought help after becoming terrified of the cult's schemes. He was killed before he could reveal what he knew.",al={"who died in the cults true plans","who is the dead turncoat","turncoat cult secrets"}},{q="Who is Talaanis Shadowsong?",a="Talaanis Shadowsong is the contact consulted after the informant's death. You observe a strategic discussion with Talaanis about how to proceed against the cult.",al={"talaanis shadowsong role","who is talaanis shadowsong","speak with talaanis"}},{q="Why can't peacekeepers simply attack the cult directly?",a="Peacekeepers are not expected to fare well in an open clash with the cult. The investigators judge that a direct attack would be a losing battle.",al={"why not fight the cult","peacekeepers vs cult","direct assault cult"}},{q="What are the cult's actual plans?",a="The cult's plans remain unknown because the informant died before sharing what he knew. Whatever they intend was frightening enough to drive a member to seek help.",al={"what is the cult planning","cult scheme","cult true plans meaning"}},{q="What is the next step after speaking with Talaanis?",a="After the informant's death and the decision against open battle, the leadership must find another way to uncover and thwart the cult's plans.",al={"what happens after the cults true plans","next steps cult quest"}},{q="What happens during The Cult's True Plans?",a="You speak with Talaanis Shadowsong and observe a council about the informant's death. The discussion considers why the peacekeepers cannot be relied on to stop the cult by force.",al={"the cults true plans quest summary","what do you do in the cults true plans"}}},kw={"cult","defector","peacekeepers","talaanis shadowsong","the cult's true plans","turncoat"},rel={"quest:92643"},m={id=94568},src={"In-game quest text: The Cult's True Plans"},fv=true}
E["quest:94616"] = {t="quest",n="Water Sapta",z="general",s="The Water Sapta is a ceremonial draught used to attune one's spiritual sight to the hidden spirits and corruptions of water. Entrusted with this preparation, the bearer is sent to locate tainted pond water within the fields of Westfall to investigate an elemental imbalance.",h="The Water Sapta is a ceremonial draught used to attune one's spiritual sight to the hidden spirits and...",sec={{t="The Sight of the Waters",b="In the elemental traditions of shamans, a sapta is a ceremonial concoction prepared to pierce the veil between the physical realm and the spirits. The Water Sapta attunes the senses specifically to the element of water, granting the spiritual vision necessary to perceive elemental disturbances and hidden spirits. Without such an attunement, ordinary travelers could look directly upon an elemental manifestation and see nothing more than ordinary ripples.",sp=0},{t="Disturbance in Westfall",b="The directive behind the Water Sapta sends the bearer to Westfall, an arid Alliance realm troubled by unrest and neglect. Within this windswept land lies a tainted pond whose waters suffer from spiritual pollution. Guided by the sapta's perception, the bearer must seek out these corrupted waters to uncover the disturbance and restore harmony to the local water spirits.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Water Sapta?",a="A water sapta is a specialized spiritual brew created for shamans to commune with the elements. Drinking or using a sapta temporarily opens one's perception to elemental entities and spiritual imbalances that are otherwise hidden from mortal eyes.",al={"what is a water sapta","water sapta purpose","what does water sapta do"}},{q="Where is the tainted pond in Westfall?",a="The tainted pond water is located within the lands of Westfall. Shamans are directed there while holding the water sapta to locate corrupted water sources and address whatever spiritual disturbance festers within the region.",al={"where is the tainted pond","tainted pond westfall","tainted pond location"}},{q="Why does the water sapta lead to Westfall?",a="Even in agrarian and drought-stricken human lands like Westfall, elemental waters can fall prey to pollution or dark corruptions. The quest tasks you with seeking out tainted pond water to investigate and restore balance to the troubled spirits residing there.",al={"why go to westfall","westfall water corruption","why is westfall water tainted"}},{q="Who uses the water sapta?",a="Water saptas are entrusted to shamans undergoing trials of elemental communion. By partaking in the sapta, the practitioner attunes their senses to water spirits, allowing them to perceive hidden elementals or inspect tainted pools.",al={"who drinks water sapta","who uses water sapta","shaman sapta drink"}},{q="What happens when you bring the sapta to the tainted pond?",a="Upon using the water sapta near the tainted pond in Westfall, elemental sight is granted, revealing the spiritual state of the water or summoning hidden spirits tied to the local water trial.",al={"what happens after using sapta","sapta elemental sight"}},{q="How do elemental saptas work in shamanic tradition?",a="Saptas combine earthy reagents and elemental blessings into a ceremonial draught. Shamans rely on them during their trials to attune to specific elements—earth, fire, water, or air—bridging the gap between the physical world and the spirit realm.",al={"how do saptas work","sapta lore"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="A sapta is an alchemical or ritual draught used by shamans to attune their spiritual senses to elemental spirits that remain invisible to ordinary sight.",["faction:Alliance"]="Investigating corrupted waters within Westfall brings elemental cleansing rites into the heart of an Alliance realm."},kw={"elemental attunement","sapta","shaman trial","tainted pond","water sapta","water spirits","westfall"},rel={"quest:972","zone:westfall"},m={id=94616},src={"In-game quest text: Water Sapta"},fv=true}
E["quest:94792"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="Taming the Beast is an introductory trial where aspiring adventurers learn the art of pacifying and bonding with wild animal companions. Guided by Josephine Carson, initiates take a Taming Rod into the forest to tame a Rockhide Boar and practice essential survival skills. The task demonstrates that surviving harsh frontiers is far easier alongside a loyal beast, provided one honors and preserves the delicate balance of nature.",h="Taming the Beast is an introductory trial where aspiring adventurers learn the art of pacifying and bonding...",sec={{t="Bonding with Beasts",b="For many wanderers of the wilds, venturing alone through untamed territories is an invitation to danger. An animal companion provides not only fierce protection in battle, but steadfast companionship through grueling journeys. Learning how to form such a bond is a vital milestone for any aspiring hunter or tracker. Through the guidance of Josephine Carson, novices are entrusted with a specialized Taming Rod and instructed to seek out a Rockhide Boar within the forest. Calming and directing such a creature serves as a foundational trial, proving whether a traveler possesses the focus and composure required to guide a beast without succumbing to fear or provoking needless bloodshed.",sp=0},{t="The Balance of Nature",b="Mastering wild animals carries profound ethical and ecological responsibilities. As Josephine Carson reminds her pupils, drawing beasts into service must never degenerate into reckless exploitation, as the balance of nature remains fragile and must always be upheld. The taming process is not about cruelty or dominance, but about establishing harmony and discipline between beast and master. Using the enchanted rod allows a novice to safely practice handling a formidable creature like a Rockhide Boar without permanently depleting the local wildlife. Once the fundamentals of commanding and soothing the beast are achieved, the rod is returned, demonstrating that respect for the natural order comes before pride.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the quest Taming the Beast?",a="This quest serves as essential training for adventurers seeking to bond with wild creatures. Under the instruction of Josephine Carson, novices learn how to approach and pacify a wild Rockhide Boar using a specialized Taming Rod. The exercise teaches aspiring hunters how an animal companion can help them survive the wilderness while underscoring the importance of respecting the balance of nature.",al={"taming the beast quest","what is taming the beast","hunter pet quest"}},{q="Who is Josephine Carson?",a="Josephine Carson is a mentor who instructs adventurers on the delicate art of managing animal companions. She provides the Taming Rod and assigns students the task of practicing their abilities on a Rockhide Boar in the nearby woods. Josephine emphasizes that learning to work alongside beasts requires patience, practice, and a mindful awareness of nature's delicate balance.",al={"josephine carson","who gives taming the beast","taming rod quest giver"}},{q="What is the Taming Rod used for?",a="The Taming Rod is a specialized implement provided by Josephine Carson to facilitate animal training. Adventurers take the rod into the forest to pacify and tame a wild Rockhide Boar. It enables novices to safely practice commanding a beast without causing lasting harm to the local fauna, and it must be returned to Josephine once the trial is complete.",al={"taming rod","what does the taming rod do","how to use taming rod"}},{q="Why do hunters need animal companions?",a="Navigating the perils of the wild alone exposes travelers to severe hazards and ambush. An animal companion offers dependable protection, shared vigilance, and formidable aid in combat, making dangerous circumstances far easier to endure. A loyal beast acts as both a fierce frontline guardian and a trusted companion during long and hazardous expeditions across untamed lands.",al={"why get a pet","beast companion benefits","why tame beasts"}},{q="What creature must be tamed during this trial?",a="The trial requires adventurers to venture into the forest and tame a Rockhide Boar using the provided Taming Rod. These sturdy beasts serve as an ideal test for practicing basic animal handling skills, teaching travelers how to maintain composure and discipline when facing aggressive wildlife before returning to report their progress to Josephine Carson.",al={"which boar do i tame","rockhide boar target","taming the beast mob"}},{q="Why does Josephine Carson emphasize the balance of nature?",a="Josephine Carson warns that subduing wild beasts must always be approached with caution and respect rather than greed or force. The natural order is delicate, and recklessly disrupting local wildlife or abusing creatures upsets the environmental equilibrium. True mastery over beasts requires understanding and honoring their place in the ecosystem rather than treating them merely as disposable tools.",al={"balance of nature meaning","nature balance taming","why respect nature"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For hunters, mastering the art of taming wild beasts is a defining rite of passage that fundamentally alters how they survive the wilderness. A loyal companion provides essential combat protection and vigilance, forging an enduring partnership between tracker and beast.",["class:Druid"]="While druids do not keep animal companions in the manner of hunters, the quest's emphasis on preserving the delicate balance of nature reflects core druidic teachings. Interacting with forest wildlife with care and restraint honors the natural equilibrium they are sworn to protect."},kw={"animal companion","balance of nature","beast taming","hunter pet","josephine carson","rockhide boar","taming rod","taming the beast"},rel={"item:taming-rod","quest:456"},m={id=94792},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94793"] = {t="quest",n="Training the Beast",z="general",s="Training the Beast is a foundational quest for young hunters, directing them to seek out the veteran woodsman Isaac Chan in Goldshire. By completing this step, hunters learn to properly train, nourish, and revive their animal companions, transforming a freshly tamed beast into a loyal combat partner.",h="Training the Beast is a foundational quest for young hunters, directing them to seek out the veteran woodsman...",sec={{t="The Responsibilities of the Hunt",b="Learning to tame a wild creature is only the initial step for an aspiring hunter; maintaining a lasting partnership demands discipline and knowledge. The quest directs fledgling hunters to seek out Isaac Chan, an experienced woodsman stationed in Goldshire, to acquire the essential craft of beast training. Without proper guidance, a tamed beast remains an unruly wild animal rather than a steadfast ally in the wilderness.",sp=0},{t="A Woodsman's Guidance",b="Upon arriving in Goldshire, Isaac Chan bestows the skills necessary to care for an animal companion. This instruction goes beyond simple combat commands, encompassing the vital duties of feeding the companion to keep it satisfied and learning the rites to revive it should it fall in battle. Chan reminds his pupils that wielding power over wildlife comes with the duty to respect natural balance and live in awe of the surrounding world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Isaac Chan?",a="Isaac Chan is a seasoned, lifelong woodsman residing in Goldshire within Elwynn Forest. Respected for his deep understanding of wildlife and wilderness survival, he teaches novice hunters how to properly guide, feed, revive, and teach abilities to their animal companions.",al={"who is isaac chan","goldshire beast trainer","isaac chan lore"}},{q="What is the purpose of Training the Beast?",a="While a hunter may already know how to tame a beast, this quest provides the essential knowledge to maintain that bond. It instructs the hunter in feeding their companion, reviving it if it falls in battle, and teaching it new combat abilities.",al={"what does this quest do","why train beast","purpose of training the beast"}},{q="Where can Isaac Chan be found?",a="Isaac Chan is located in Goldshire, the central township of Elwynn Forest just south of Northshire Valley and southwest of Stormwind City.",al={"where is isaac chan","where do i turn this in","isaac chan location"}},{q="What skills does Isaac Chan teach?",a="Isaac Chan imparts the core principles of pet care and training. This encompasses feeding your beast to sustain its wellbeing, reviving it should it perish in combat, and teaching it new abilities as it grows alongside you.",al={"what skills do you get","pet abilities learned","what does isaac chan teach"}},{q="Why does Isaac Chan remind hunters to respect nature?",a="Isaac Chan emphasizes that mastering wild beasts requires humility rather than domination. He urges hunters to honor the balance of the natural world and live in awe of surrounding nature, ensuring that the partnership between hunter and beast is built on stewardship rather than exploitation.",al={"why respect nature","respect the balance hunter"}},{q="Who undertakes this quest?",a="Training the Beast is tailored specifically for hunters who have acquired the initial ability to tame beasts and need the woodland discipline required to sustain and train an animal companion.",al={"who can do this quest","is training the beast hunter only"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This quest marks a vital rite of passage for developing hunters, transitioning from merely capturing beasts to forging an enduring, disciplined bond through feeding, reviving, and training them.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance hunters operating in Elwynn Forest rely on veteran woodsmen like Isaac Chan in Goldshire to master the fundamentals of beast handling."},kw={"elwynn forest","feed pet","goldshire","hunter pet","isaac chan","pet training","revive pet","training the beast","woodsman"},rel={"subzone:goldshire"},m={id=94793},src={"In-game quest text: Training the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94863"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="Taming the Beast is a hunter training lesson overseen by Josephine Carson. After learning the fundamentals of beast handling with a boar, the aspiring hunter must use a Taming Rod to earn the trust of a fiercer Gray Forest Wolf.",h="Taming the Beast is a hunter training lesson overseen by Josephine Carson.",sec={{t="The Path of the Hunter",b="A hunter's connection with the wild requires more than mere tracking and marksmanship; it demands the respect and patience needed to earn a wild creature's trust. After proving their foundational skills on simple boars, aspiring hunters are presented with a far fiercer challenge: venturing into the forest to tame a Gray Forest Wolf. Under the watchful eye of trainer Josephine Carson, the hunter is entrusted with an enchanted rod to calm the predator and learn the delicate balance between hunter and beast.",sp=0},{t="A Test of Instinct and Trust",b="Josephine Carson provides the hunter with a specialized Taming Rod, designed to channel a calming influence that allows the hunter to approach dangerous wildlife. The task requires patience and precision, as the wolf must be approached without lethal force while its natural aggression is gently overcome. Once the bond is practiced and the hunter proves capable of harmonizing with the beast's spirit, the rod is returned to Josephine as proof of growing mastery over the wild.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Taming the Beast?",a="Taming the Beast is a hunter training trial where a trainer tasks an aspiring hunter with tracking down and taming a Gray Forest Wolf using an enchanted Taming Rod.",al={"taming the beast quest","gray forest wolf quest","tame wolf lesson"}},{q="Who gives and completes this quest?",a="Josephine Carson serves as the instructor for this trial. She provides the Taming Rod and evaluates your bond with the forest's beasts before taking the rod back upon completion.",al={"who gives taming the beast","who is josephine carson","where to turn in taming the beast"}},{q="What creature do you have to tame?",a="You are instructed to tame a Gray Forest Wolf, moving up from your previous practice with boars to a fiercer forest predator.",al={"what do i tame","which beast","target of taming the beast"}},{q="What is the Taming Rod used for?",a="The Taming Rod is an instructional tool provided by Josephine Carson to help an apprentice hunter soothe and form a temporary bond with a wild Gray Forest Wolf without causing permanent harm.",al={"what does taming rod do","why use taming rod","taming rod lore"}},{q="What did the hunter tame before this trial?",a="Before facing the Gray Forest Wolf, you demonstrated your initial command of beast handling by mastering how to tame a forest boar.",al={"taming previous lesson","what was tamed before wolf"}},{q="Do you keep the Gray Forest Wolf as a permanent companion?",a="No, the trial is designed as a focused lesson in mastering the technique of beast bonding. Once you have practiced your skills, you return the Taming Rod to Josephine Carson.",al={"do you keep wolf","is wolf permanent pet"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This training marks an essential rite of passage for hunters, teaching the patience and respect required to commune with fierce predators of the wild."},kw={"beast taming","gray forest wolf","hunter","hunter training","josephine carson","taming rod","taming the beast"},rel={"item:taming-rod"},m={id=94863},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94864"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="Taming the Beast is the culmination of a hunter's early training in Elwynn Forest, overseen by Josephine Carson. After demonstrating mastery over boars and wolves, hunters are tasked with using a Taming Rod to pacify and gain the trust of a Young Forest Bear. This final trial deepens a hunter's attunement to beasts and maintains the woodland's natural balance through respect and skill.",h="Taming the Beast is the culmination of a hunter's early training in Elwynn Forest, overseen by Josephine...",sec={{t="The Final Trial of the Hunt",b="Aspiring hunters in Elwynn Forest undergo rigorous training to understand the wild creatures sharing their lands. Having already demonstrated command over the cunning wolf and the hardy boar, hunters face a final test of courage and patience. Josephine Carson directs them to confront the Young Forest Bear, the burliest and mightiest of the forest's common predators. This rite tests whether a hunter can withstand the bear's raw strength and earn its trust rather than simply subjugating it.",sp=0},{t="Respect and Balance in Elwynn",b="The lesson behind taming Elwynn's wildlife is rooted in respect rather than dominion. Forest bears represent the brawn and vitality of the woodland, playing a vital role in maintaining the natural order. By using a specially prepared Taming Rod, hunters learn to quiet the beast's aggression and establish a genuine bond of mutual understanding. Successfully calming such a formidable animal deepens the hunter's spiritual attunement to the wild, completing their foundational lessons under Carson's guidance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who assigns this trial to hunters?",a="Josephine Carson oversees this trial for hunters training in Elwynn Forest. She evaluates the hunter's progress after they have successfully tamed lesser beasts, providing the necessary guidance and the Taming Rod needed to tame the forest bear. Once the trial is complete, hunters return the rod directly to her to mark the completion of their training.",al={"who gives taming the beast","taming the beast quest giver","josephine carson quest"}},{q="Which beasts must be tamed before this challenge?",a="Before undertaking the challenge of taming a Young Forest Bear, hunters must first prove their skill and patience by taming a boar and a wolf. These preliminary trials demonstrate that the hunter can handle both aggressive and pack-oriented beasts before attempting to confront the sheer strength and brawn of Elwynn's bears.",al={"prerequisite beasts for taming","what did i tame before bear","beasts tamed before this"}},{q="Why are hunters sent after a Young Forest Bear?",a="The Young Forest Bear represents the brawn and might of Elwynn Forest's wildlife. While boars and wolves test a hunter's ability to handle quick or resilient beasts, the bear serves as the ultimate test of endurance, respect, and physical courage. Earning the trust of such a powerful predator demonstrates complete mastery over basic beast handling.",al={"why tame young forest bear","young forest bear purpose","why bears in elwynn"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Taming Rod?",a="The Taming Rod is a specialized training implement provided by Josephine Carson to assist hunters in calming wild animals. It channels the focus required to soothe an aggressive Young Forest Bear without slaying it, allowing the hunter to forge a peaceful bond. Once the trial concludes, the rod is returned to Carson as training is finalized.",al={"taming rod purpose","what does taming rod do","why use taming rod"}},{q="How does taming beasts help Elwynn Forest?",a="Taming wildlife is seen as an essential practice for maintaining the natural balance of Elwynn Forest. By approaching predators with respect and restraint rather than hunting them down, hunters help preserve harmony between the local ecosystem and the humanoid settlements throughout the region, ensuring dangerous beasts are guided peacefully rather than destroyed.",al={"why does elwynn balance matter","elwynn forest balance","taming and forest balance"}},{q="What does completing this lesson signify for a hunter?",a="Completing this final task deepens a hunter's intrinsic attunement to beasts of the wild. By earning the trust of Elwynn's mightiest creature, the hunter moves beyond simple hunting techniques and masters the spiritual connection necessary to form lifelong partnerships with animal companions across Azeroth.",al={"what happens after taming the bear","hunter training completion","attunement to beasts meaning"}},{q="Where are Young Forest Bears found?",a="Young Forest Bears roam the wooded areas of Elwynn Forest, living among the dense trees alongside wolves and spiders. Hunters venturing out from nearby settlements must track these burly creatures through the forest undergrowth to administer the taming rod and fulfill Carson's final directive.",al={"young forest bear location","where to find bears in elwynn","forest bear elwynn"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This trial marks a defining rite of passage for apprentice hunters, transforming simple tracking skills into a deep, empathetic attunement with beasts of the wild.",["race:Human"]="For human hunters training in Elwynn Forest, taming native wildlife teaches the importance of coexisting with the kingdom's natural predators rather than merely eradicating them.",["faction:Alliance"]="Skilled beast masters bolster the Alliance's wilderness scouts, providing disciplined companions capable of defending homesteads and patrolling trade roads across Stormwind's territories."},kw={"attunement to beasts","beast training","elwynn forest","hunter quest","josephine carson","taming rod","taming the beast","young forest bear"},rel={"item:taming-rod"},m={id=94864},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94896"] = {t="quest",n="Aid For The Refugees",z="general",s="Aid For The Refugees centers on the displaced survivors of Ban'aethal, a settlement deep within Shadowgale Forest that was suddenly evacuated in the middle of the night. With their home falling into the sky and the surrounding wilderness grown far too perilous to enter, the refugees rely on brave travelers to recover the belongings they had to leave behind.",h="Aid For The Refugees centers on the displaced survivors of Ban'aethal, a settlement deep within Shadowgale...",sec={{t="A Desperate Plea",b="Aid for the Refugees is an appeal for assistance from Ealaane and her fellow displaced villagers. Having narrowly fled their homes deep in Shadowgale Forest, the survivors have gathered together with almost nothing to their names, hoping a passing traveler will brave the dangers they left behind.",sp=0},{t="The Calamity of Ban'aethal",b="The settlement of Ban'aethal met an unthinkable fate in the dead of night when the village suddenly began to fall into the sky. The bizarre and terrifying calamity gave the inhabitants mere moments to flee, forcing them to abandon their personal possessions as the surrounding woods turned hostile and perilous.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ealaane?",a="Ealaane is a refugee from Ban'aethal who escaped with others when their settlement began to collapse. She asks passing adventurers traveling toward Shadowgale Forest to recover personal belongings left behind in the ruins.",al={"who gave aid for the refugees","quest giver aid for the refugees","ealaane"}},{q="What happened to Ban'aethal?",a="Ban'aethal was a settlement situated deep within Shadowgale Forest. Its residents were recently forced to evacuate in the middle of the night due to a sudden and bizarre catastrophe.",al={"what is banaethal","where is banaethal","ban aethal village"}},{q="Why did the villagers have to evacuate Ban'aethal?",a="The evacuation was triggered suddenly in the middle of the night when Ban'aethal began to fall into the sky. The village and surrounding forest became far too perilous for the residents to stay or return.",al={"why evacuate banaethal","falling into sky","ban aethal disaster"}},{q="Why are the villagers asking for their belongings to be retrieved?",a="The catastrophe struck suddenly in the middle of the night, forcing the villagers to flee for their lives with virtually nothing. With the forest and ruins now overrun with danger, they rely on capable adventurers to retrieve what was left behind.",al={"why need belongings","abandoned belongings","why recover belongings"}},{q="What is Shadowgale Forest?",a="Shadowgale Forest is the wooded region holding the settlement of Ban'aethal. Following the bizarre disaster that tore the village apart, the forest has grown extremely hazardous for ordinary folk.",al={"where is shadowgale forest","shadowgale forest lore"}},{q="Can the refugees return to their homes?",a="No. According to Ealaane, both Shadowgale Forest and the ruins of Ban'aethal are far too dangerous for the displaced residents to ever return on their own.",al={"can refugees go back","is banaethal safe"}}},kw={"aid for the refugees","ban'aethal","banaethal","belongings","ealaane","refugees","shadowgale forest"},rel={"topic:refugee"},m={id=94896},src={"In-game quest text: Aid For The Refugees"},fv=true}
E["quest:94897"] = {t="quest",n="The Fate of a Loved One",z="general",s="The Fate of a Loved One is a quest concerning Resaan, who ventured into the dangerous Ruins of Ban'aethal to scavenge supplies and never returned. Over a week later, his grieving partner asks travelers bound for the ruins to discover his fate and bring him home or recover his cherished heirloom.",h="The Fate of a Loved One is a quest concerning Resaan, who ventured into the dangerous Ruins of Ban'aethal to...",sec={{t="A Desperate Plea",b="The Fate of a Loved One centers on a desperate plea from someone whose partner, Resaan, ventured back into the Ruins of Ban'aethal. Despite earnest pleas for him to stay, Resaan felt compelled to brave the ruins in search of supplies, leaving behind someone who now waits in agonizing uncertainty.",sp=0},{t="Vanished in the Ruins",b="More than a week has passed since Resaan set out, far exceeding any reasonable expectation for a swift return. Travelers bound for Ban'aethal are begged to search for him among the ruins—to guide him back if life remains, or to recover his family heirloom if only tragedy is left to find.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Resaan?",a="Resaan is an individual who ventured back into the Ruins of Ban'aethal to gather supplies despite being begged to stay. After more than a week without word, his loved one fears he has met a tragic end among the ruins.",al={"who is resaan","where is resaan","find resaan"}},{q="What are the Ruins of Ban'aethal?",a="The Ruins of Ban'aethal are a dangerous site where Resaan ventured in search of necessary supplies. His loved one considers the journey hazardous enough that surviving a week there alone is unlikely.",al={"what is banaethal","ruins of banaethal lore","where is banaethal"}},{q="Why did Resaan return to Ban'aethal?",a="Resaan returned to Ban'aethal specifically to retrieve supplies. He chose to make the perilous journey even though his loved one pleaded with him not to go.",al={"why did resaan go back","why resaan in banaethal"}},{q="What does the questgiver ask you to bring back?",a="If Resaan is still alive, the questgiver asks that he be brought safely home. If he has perished in the ruins, the questgiver begs for the recovery of his personal heirloom as a final keepsake.",al={"what does questgiver want","what happens if resaan dead"}},{q="How long has Resaan been missing?",a="Resaan has been missing for over a week since setting off into the Ruins of Ban'aethal. Given the dangers of the area, this prolonged silence leads his loved one to fear the worst.",al={"how long resaan missing","how long has resaan been gone"}},{q="Who offers this quest?",a="The quest is offered by Resaan's grieving partner or lover, who remains desperate for closure and willing to ask passing travelers heading toward Ban'aethal for aid.",al={"who gives fate of a loved one","fate of a loved one questgiver"}}},kw={"ban'aethal","heirloom","resaan","ruins of ban'aethal","supplies","the fate of a loved one"},m={id=94897},src={"In-game quest text: The Fate of a Loved One"},fv=true}
E["quest:94911"] = {t="quest",n="Child of Nature",z="general",s="In Thunder Bluff, a fellow Skyborne sees promise in your druidic arts and points you toward Turak Runetotem. He can teach you how to reach the Cenarion Circle’s stronghold in Moonglade.",h="In Thunder Bluff, a fellow Skyborne sees promise in your druidic arts and points you toward Turak Runetotem.",sec={{t="A Druid’s Welcome",b="Your teacher believes the druids of the Cenarion Circle will welcome you and share their knowledge. Turak Runetotem in Thunder Bluff can show you how to reach their stronghold in Moonglade.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who should I speak with?",a="Speak with Turak Runetotem in Thunder Bluff. He can teach you how to reach Moonglade.",al={"who do I talk to","Turak Runetotem","who is Turak"}},{q="Where is the Cenarion Circle’s stronghold?",a="The Cenarion Circle’s stronghold is in Moonglade on Kalimdor.",al={"where is the circle","Cenarion Circle location","Moonglade"}},{q="Why am I being sent to Moonglade?",a="The druids of the Cenarion Circle can share their knowledge with you.",al={"why Moonglade","learn druid","druid training"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you are told the Cenarion Circle’s druids will welcome you and share their knowledge.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you are directed to Turak Runetotem in Thunder Bluff, who can teach you how to reach the Cenarion Circle in Moonglade."},kw={"cenarion circle","child of nature","druid","moonglade","skyborne","thunder bluff","turak runetotem"},rel={"quest:5921","zone:thunderbluff","npc:turak-runetotem","topic:cenarion-circle","topic:druid","topic:skyborne"},m={id=94911},src={"In-game quest text: Child of Nature"},fv=true}
E["quest:94912"] = {t="quest",n="Child of Nature",z="general",s="In Stormwind, Sheldras Moontree can show you the way to Moonglade. There, the Cenarion Circle’s druids have wisdom to share with a child of nature.",h="In Stormwind, Sheldras Moontree can show you the way to Moonglade.",sec={{t="A Far Stronghold",b="Sheldras Moontree, a night elf in Stormwind, can show you how to reach Moonglade on Kalimdor. The Cenarion Circle keeps its stronghold there. It is an ancient order of naturalists and druids, with much to teach you.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sheldras Moontree?",a="Sheldras Moontree is a night elf in Stormwind who can show you how to reach Moonglade.",al={"sheldras","night elf in stormwind","who teaches me moonglade"}},{q="What is the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient order of naturalists and practitioners of the druidic arts.",al={"cenarion circle","druid order","nature circle"}},{q="Where is the Cenarion Circle's stronghold?",a="The Cenarion Circle keeps its stronghold in Moonglade on Kalimdor.",al={"cenarion stronghold","where is moonglade","moonglade"}},{q="What can I learn from the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle’s druids have much wisdom to share with you.",al={"cenarion teachings","what can they teach","druid wisdom"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you are among the first of your people to arrive in Azeroth, and you are welcomed.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you are directed toward the Cenarion Circle, an ancient order whose members practice the druidic arts."},kw={"cenarion circle","child of nature","druid","kalimdor","moonglade","sheldras moontree","skyborne","stormwind"},rel={"quest:5921","topic:cenarion-circle","quest:94911"},m={id=94912},src={"In-game quest text: Child of Nature"},fv=true}
E["quest:94914"] = {t="quest",n="Moonglade",z="general",s="Your teacher opens a way to Moonglade, where Dendrite Starblaze awaits. There, you can speak with him about the path you have begun.",h="Your teacher opens a way to Moonglade, where Dendrite Starblaze awaits.",sec={{t="A First Lesson",b="Your teacher welcomes druids to Moonglade. Keeper Remulos watches over those who rest there. You receive Teleport: Moonglade, a spell that carries you there when you seek knowledge or solace. Your first lesson is to speak with Dendrite Starblaze in Nighthaven about the path you now follow.",sp=0},{t="The Bear Form",b="Dendrite Starblaze recognizes that you have already mastered the form of the Bear. He sees you well on your way.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who should I speak to in Moonglade?",a="Speak with Dendrite Starblaze in Nighthaven about the path you are on.",al={"who do i talk to","dendrite starblaze","nighthaven druid"}},{q="How do I get to Moonglade?",a="Your teacher gives you Teleport: Moonglade, a spell that carries you there.",al={"moonglade teleport","how reach moonglade","teleport spell"}},{q="Who watches over druids in Moonglade?",a="Keeper Remulos watches over druids who rest in Moonglade.",al={"keeper remulos","moonglade guardian","who guards moonglade"}},{q="What does Dendrite say about my progress?",a="Dendrite recognizes that you have already mastered the form of the Bear.",al={"bear form","did i master bear","dendrite response"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, your teacher welcomes you to Moonglade and teaches you a spell to travel there."},kw={"bear form","dendrite starblaze","druid","keeper remulos","moonglade","nighthaven","teleport"},rel={"quest:5921","subzone:nighthaven","npc:dendrite-starblaze","npc:remulos"},m={id=94914},src={"In-game quest text: Moonglade"},fv=true}
E["quest:94946"] = {t="quest",n="The Magical City of Dalaran",z="general",s="A mage in Valanaar sends you from Zephras Isle to Dalaran City, where several mages await your people's arrival. The city offers a place to begin exploring Azeroth, but the lands beyond can be dangerous without preparation and experience.",h="A mage in Valanaar sends you from Zephras Isle to Dalaran City, where several mages await your people's...",sec={{t="Arrival in Dalaran",b="Recent events with the cult keep the speaker in Valanaar for now. Several mages wait in Dalaran for your people, so you are sent to the dock outside the lodge. There, you board a skycutter bound for Dalaran City.\n\nWhen you arrive, speak with Danaaris Stargale. Your people are still making their home in the city, and the country beyond its safety can prove dangerous.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is waiting in Dalaran?",a="Several mages await your people's arrival in Dalaran. When you reach the city, you are asked to speak with Danaaris Stargale.",al={"mages in dalaran","who's in dalaran","dalaran mages"}},{q="Where do I travel from?",a="You travel from Zephras Isle to Dalaran City aboard the skycutter at the dock outside the lodge.",al={"how get to dalaran","from zephras","skycutter"}},{q="Why is the speaker staying in Valanaar?",a="Recent events with the cult changed the speaker's plans, so they intend to remain in Valanaar for a time.",al={"why stay valanaar","cult changed plans","valanaar mage"}},{q="Is it safe outside Dalaran?",a="The land beyond Dalaran can be dangerous without proper preparation and experience.",al={"outside dalaran safe","danger near dalaran","dalaran outskirts"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you travel from Zephras Isle to join the mages awaiting your people's arrival in Dalaran."},kw={"cult","dalaran","dalaran city","danaaris stargale","mages","skycutter","valanaar","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","subzone:dalaran"},m={id=94946},src={"In-game quest text: The Magical City of Dalaran"},fv=true}
E["quest:94947"] = {t="quest",n="Welcome to Azeroth",z="general",s="You arrive in Stormwind through the Skyborne Portal and are welcomed by its regent. Highlord Bolvar Fordragon grants you leave to travel across Alliance lands.",h="You arrive in Stormwind through the Skyborne Portal and are welcomed by its regent.",sec={{t="Arrival in the Capital",b="You step through the Skyborne Portal into Stormwind, the human capital. From there, you make your way along the city's grand thoroughfares to Stormwind Keep, where the regency awaits your arrival.",sp=0},{t="An Audience with the Regent",b="Highlord Bolvar Fordragon receives the arriving members of the High Order in the throne room. A Kirin Tor courier had warned him they were coming. It has been some time since high elves walked the halls of Stormwind Keep, yet Bolvar welcomes them as guests and grants them free passage across Alliance territories on behalf of the King.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is this quest about?",a="You arrive in Stormwind through the Skyborne Portal and present yourself to Highlord Bolvar Fordragon at Stormwind Keep.",al={"what is welcome to azeroth","welcome to azeroth quest"}},{q="Who is Bolvar Fordragon?",a="Highlord Bolvar Fordragon governs Stormwind as regent from Stormwind Keep. He rules on behalf of the young King Anduin Wrynn while King Varian remains missing.",al={"who is bolvar fordragon","regent of stormwind"}},{q="Why is Bolvar receiving the Skyborne?",a="A Kirin Tor messenger told Bolvar Fordragon that members of the High Order would arrive. He welcomes them as guests of the crown and grants them leave to travel freely across Alliance lands.",al={"why are skyborne in stormwind","high elves stormwind"}},{q="What is the High Order?",a="The High Order is the group associated with the Skyborne high elves. The Kirin Tor announced their arrival to Stormwind's leaders.",al={"who is high order","high order lore"}},{q="Where does the Skyborne Portal lead?",a="The Skyborne Portal brings travelers directly into Stormwind, the human capital. From there, newcomers can go to Stormwind Keep to seek an audience with the crown.",al={"skyborne portal destination","where does portal go"}},{q="Are the Skyborne allowed throughout Alliance territory?",a="Bolvar Fordragon welcomes the Skyborne as the King's guests and grants them leave to travel unhindered throughout Alliance lands.",al={"are skyborne alliance","skyborne welcome alliance"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you are welcomed into Stormwind and granted passage across Alliance territory when you arrive through the portal.",["faction:Alliance"]="Highlord Bolvar Fordragon officially welcomes the High Order on behalf of King Anduin Wrynn, granting them leave to freely traverse Alliance lands."},kw={"bolvar fordragon","high elf","high order","kirin tor","portal","skyborne","stormwind keep","welcome to azeroth"},rel={"npc:bolvar-fordragon","subzone:stormwind-keep","topic:skyborne","quest:92596","topic:alliance","topic:stormwind-kingdom","quest:95350"},m={id=94947},src={"In-game quest text: Welcome to Azeroth"},fv=true}
E["quest:94978"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="Quel'ana Quickgale teaches you the bond between beast and master. You begin by taming a Windsong Crawler near the waters of Zephras.",h="Quel'ana Quickgale teaches you the bond between beast and master.",sec={{t="First Bond",b="You have the Taming Rod and a Windsong Crawler to tame. Find one near a body of water and practice your skills. Then return the rod to Quel'ana Quickgale in Valanaar. This creature is your first step toward mastery.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Quel'ana ask me to tame?",a="Quel'ana asks you to tame a Windsong Crawler near a body of water on Zephras.",al={"what beast","taming target","crawler quest"}},{q="What is the Taming Rod for?",a="You use the Taming Rod to tame a Windsong Crawler.",al={"how rod works","taming tool","rod quest"}},{q="Where do I find a Windsong Crawler?",a="Windsong Crawlers live near bodies of water on Zephras.",al={"crawler location","where crawler","zephras beast"}},{q="Who gave me the Taming Rod?",a="Quel'ana Quickgale gives you the Taming Rod in Valanaar.",al={"quest giver","who is quelana","quelana quickgale"}},{q="Why am I learning to tame a beast?",a="Learning the bond between beast and master is the first step toward mastery of your class.",al={"why tame crawler","beast mastery","first taming"}}},kw={"beast taming","quel'ana quickgale","taming rod","taming the beast","valanaar","windsong crawler","zephras"},rel={"item:taming-rod"},m={id=94978},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:94979"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="general",s="In the Gustberry Lowlands, Quel'ana Quickgale asks you to tame a galestrider. Your skill on open ground will prepare you for trials ahead.",h="In the Gustberry Lowlands, Quel'ana Quickgale asks you to tame a galestrider.",sec={{t="Across the Plains",b="You've already shown you can tame the river's crawler. Now Quel'ana Quickgale gives you a Taming Rod and sends you to the lowlands of Zephras. There, a proud, winged galestrider runs swiftly across the plains. Earn its trust and practice your skill. Then bring the rod back to Quel'ana in Valanaar.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Quel'ana want me to tame?",a="Quel'ana asks you to tame an Ornery Galestrider in the Gustberry Lowlands.",al={"tame what","galestrider tame","what beast"}},{q="Where do I find the galestrider?",a="You can find the Ornery Galestrider in the Gustberry Lowlands, in the plains of Zephras.",al={"where galestrider","galestrider location","lowlands where"}},{q="What is the Taming Rod for?",a="Quel'ana gives you the Taming Rod so you can tame an Ornery Galestrider.",al={"taming rod use","rod for what","how tame beast"}},{q="Who gave me this assignment?",a="Quel'ana Quickgale in Valanaar asks you to tame an Ornery Galestrider and bring back the Taming Rod.",al={"who sent me","quelana who","quickgale"}}},kw={"galestrider","gustberry lowlands","ornery galestrider","quel'ana quickgale","taming rod","taming the beast","valanaar","zephras"},rel={"item:taming-rod"},m={id=94979},src={"In-game quest text: Taming the Beast"},fv=true}
E["quest:95036"] = {t="quest",n="A Moon-Kissed Blade",z="general",s="Trevan warns you that worgen threaten Silverpine Forest. Old Fire-Eye resists death through unknown magic, and Trevan asks you to learn what a special blade will require.",h="Trevan warns you that worgen threaten Silverpine Forest.",sec={{t="A smith's list",b="Old Fire-Eye is the worgen who attacked Trevan and his companions. Trevan will give you a list of materials for a weapon against him. The materials are gathered, and Trevan believes he knows a smith who can craft the blade.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Old Fire-Eye?",a="Old Fire-Eye is the largest and meanest of the worgen. He attacked Trevan and his companions.",al={"old fire-eye","fire eye worgen","big worgen"}},{q="Why does Old Fire-Eye keep coming back?",a="Unknown magic keeps Old Fire-Eye from staying dead.",al={"why wont fire-eye die","old fire eye magic","worgen that wont stay dead"}},{q="What does Trevan need from me?",a="Trevan will give you a list of materials needed to forge a special weapon.",al={"what does Trevan want","Trevan list","blade materials"}},{q="Why is a special blade needed?",a="Old Fire-Eye resists death through unknown magic. Trevan says a special weapon is needed to put him down.",al={"moon kissed blade why","weapon for Old Fire-Eye","kill fire eye"}},{q="Who can craft the blade?",a="Trevan thinks he knows a smith able to craft the blade.",al={"smith for the blade","who makes moon blade","blade blacksmith"}}},kw={"a moon-kissed blade","blade materials","old fire-eye","silverpine forest","special weapon","trevan","worgen"},rel={"zone:silverpine","topic:worgen","quest:95140","topic:light"},m={id=95036},src={"In-game quest text: A Moon-Kissed Blade"},fv=true}
E["quest:95042"] = {t="quest",n="Seeking the Kor Gem",z="general",s="In Ashenvale, Ulric Frostveil sees your search for a Kor Gem as familiar work for Alliance spellcasters. He points you north to Blackfathom Deeps, where naga still use the gems.",h="In Ashenvale, Ulric Frostveil sees your search for a Kor Gem as familiar work for Alliance spellcasters.",sec={{t="A Gem in Ashenvale",b="Ulric asks you to bring him a Kor Gem from the ruins of Blackfathom Deeps, north of Ashenvale. The naga below still use the gems. They can empower magical weapons and implements, but Ulric warns you not to go alone.",sp=0},{t="Ulric's Interest",b="Ulric Frostveil recognizes corruption in the gem. He says he can dispel the evil infused into it by the naga witches. Then he asks whether you would sell it to him for his academic pursuits.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Ulric Frostveil?",a="Ulric Frostveil is a Forsaken in Ashenvale who asks you to bring him a Kor Gem.",al={"ulric frostveil","who is ulric","forsaken in ashenvale"}},{q="Where can I find a Kor Gem?",a="Ulric directs you north to Blackfathom Deeps, where naga still use Kor Gems.",al={"kor gem location","where gem","blackfathom gem"}},{q="What are Kor Gems used for?",a="Kor Gems can empower magical weapons and implements.",al={"kor gem use","gem magic","what does kor gem do"}},{q="Why does Ulric want a Kor Gem?",a="Ulric asks you to bring him a Kor Gem. He says he may value it for his academic pursuits.",al={"ulric wants gem","why gem","frostveil gem"}},{q="Who still uses Kor Gems?",a="The naga in Blackfathom Deeps still use Kor Gems.",al={"naga gems","who uses kor gems","blackfathom naga"}}},kw={"ashenvale","blackfathom deeps","forsaken","kor gem","naga","ulric frostveil"},rel={"zone:ashenvale","zone:blackfathom","topic:naga","topic:forsaken","topic:alliance"},m={id=95042},src={"In-game quest text: Seeking the Kor Gem"},fv=true}
E["quest:95111"] = {t="quest",n="An Underrated Talent",z="general",s="In Silverpine Forest, Trevan Rol gives Ott a chance to make a blade to your design. Ott’s simple weapons have earned praise from smiths in the Undercity.",h="In Silverpine Forest, Trevan Rol gives Ott a chance to make a blade to your design.",sec={{t="A Smith's Chance",b="Trevan Rol asks you to carry materials to Ott in Tarren Mill and offer him a commission. Ott sells simple blades to travelers. Trevan bought one and later took it to the Undercity for maintenance. There, local smiths praised its quality and mistook it for the work of a famous master. Now Ott has the chance to forge a blade to your specifications.",sp=0},{t="Ott's Answer",b="Ott says no one has asked him to make a custom weapon before. He usually crafts humble, functional weapons in his own style and seeks no acclaim. Still, he is willing to try. He wonders why you chose him when smiths in the Undercity also take custom work.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Ott?",a="Ott is a weaponsmith in Tarren Mill who sells simple blades to travelers.",al={"ott smith","who's ott","tarren mill smith"}},{q="Why does the Undercity smith want me to find Ott?",a="The smith bought one of Ott’s blades and was surprised when Undercity smiths praised its quality. The smith wants Ott to have a chance to make something special.",al={"why ott","undercity smith order","ott commission"}},{q="What am I bringing to Ott?",a="You are taking materials to Ott in Tarren Mill and offering him a commission to forge your blade.",al={"materials for ott","what do i take","ott materials"}},{q="Does Ott make custom weapons?",a="Ott says no one has asked him to make a custom weapon before, but he is willing to try.",al={"ott custom blade","can ott forge for me","ott commissions"}},{q="Why does Ott ask why I chose him?",a="Ott says smiths in the Undercity take custom work, and wonders why you sought him instead.",al={"why choose ott","ott wonders why","why not undercity smith"}}},kw={"commission","custom blade","ott","smith","tarren mill","undercity","weaponsmith"},rel={"subzone:tarren-mill","zone:undercity"},m={id=95111},src={"In-game quest text: An Underrated Talent"},fv=true}
E["quest:95125"] = {t="quest",n="Ott's Masterwork",z="general",s="Ott is ready to forge a blade for you from fine materials. You may watch him at work.",h="Ott is ready to forge a blade for you from fine materials.",sec={{t="At the Forge",b="Ott values the fine materials and hopes his work will meet your expectations. He forges a blade for you, then offers it for your travels.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is making my blade?",a="Ott is forging your blade from the fine materials you provided.",al={"who forges it","otts blade","ott smith"}},{q="Can I watch Ott forge it?",a="Ott asks you to stand back as he begins forging. You are asked to watch him work.",al={"watch ott forge","see blade made","forge process"}},{q="What does Ott say when the blade is finished?",a="Ott calls the blade his finest work and hopes it serves you well in your travels.",al={"ott completion","what does ott say","blade done"}}},kw={"blacksmith","blade","forging","masterwork","materials","ott"},m={id=95125},src={"In-game quest text: Ott's Masterwork"},fv=true}
E["quest:95126"] = {t="quest",n="The Moonsilver Blade",z="general",s="In Silverpine Forest, Trevan Rol awaits the blade that will complete his work before he faces a beast.",h="In Silverpine Forest, Trevan Rol awaits the blade that will complete his work before he faces a beast.",sec={{t="The Blade",b="Ott thanks you for the chance to help and asks you to carry his gratitude to Trevan. You bring Ott's Masterwork back to Trevan Rol in Silverpine Forest.",sp=0},{t="Trevan's Approval",b="Trevan Rol examines the blade and approves of Ott's work. Only the beast remains. When you are ready, he directs you to speak with Lumina.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who made Ott's Masterwork?",a="Ott made Ott's Masterwork and sent it with you to Trevan Rol.",al={"who made the blade","ott blade","masterwork maker"}},{q="Who is Trevan Rol?",a="Trevan Rol receives and inspects Ott's Masterwork in Silverpine Forest.",al={"who is trevan","trevan blade","trevan rol"}},{q="Where do I bring the blade?",a="Bring Ott's Masterwork to Trevan Rol in Silverpine Forest.",al={"where blade go","take ott's masterwork where","blade destination"}},{q="What does Trevan think of the blade?",a="Trevan Rol inspects the blade and says it will do nicely.",al={"trevan like blade","is ott's blade good","trevan approval"}},{q="Who should I speak with after Trevan?",a="When you are ready, Trevan Rol directs you to speak with Lumina.",al={"what next","who is lumina","speak to lumina"}}},kw={"beast","lumina","moonsilver blade","ott","ott's masterwork","silverpine forest","trevan rol"},rel={"quest:95125","zone:silverpine"},m={id=95126},src={"In-game quest text: The Moonsilver Blade"},fv=true}
E["quest:95140"] = {t="quest",n="Old Fire-Eye",z="general",s="Lumina Windsinger sends you after Old Fire-Eye in Silverpine Forest with the Moonsilver Blade. The Windshapers await his death, and Lumina hopes the blade will guide you well.",h="Lumina Windsinger sends you after Old Fire-Eye in Silverpine Forest with the Moonsilver Blade.",sec={{t="A Blade's Purpose",b="The Windshapers have given you the Moonsilver Blade. Locals say Old Fire-Eye hunts across a wide stretch south of here, usually near the gates to Gilneas. Lumina asks you to find him and use the blade to end him. The Windshapers' gratitude depends on his death.",sp=0},{t="After the Hunt",b="When you return, Lumina thanks you and says Banon may rest in peace. Then she lays a small measure of her people's magic upon the bloodstained blade, hoping the winds will guide it well.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to hunt Old Fire-Eye?",a="Lumina Windsinger asks you to hunt Old Fire-Eye with the Moonsilver Blade.",al={"who sent me","lumina task","windsinger"}},{q="Where can I find Old Fire-Eye?",a="Locals say Old Fire-Eye hunts across a wide area south of Lumina, usually near the gates to Gilneas.",al={"fire eye location","where is old fire-eye","gilneas gates"}},{q="What should I use against Old Fire-Eye?",a="Lumina asks you to use the Moonsilver Blade to end Old Fire-Eye.",al={"blade for fire-eye","moonsilver weapon","what weapon"}},{q="Why does Lumina want Old Fire-Eye slain?",a="Lumina says the Windshapers will be grateful when Old Fire-Eye is dead.",al={"why kill fire-eye","why hunt him","fire-eye reason"}},{q="Who is Banon?",a="Lumina says Banon may rest in peace after Old Fire-Eye is slain.",al={"banon who","banon peace","who is banon"}},{q="What does Lumina do with the blade?",a="Lumina lays a small measure of her people's magic upon the bloodstained Moonsilver Blade.",al={"blade magic","lumina enchants blade","wind magic"}}},kw={"banon","gilneas","lumina windsinger","moonsilver blade","old fire-eye","silverpine forest","windshapers"},rel={"quest:95126","quest:92595","zone:silverpine"},m={id=95140},src={"In-game quest text: Old Fire-Eye"},fv=true}
E["quest:95204"] = {t="quest",n="Crest of Lordaeron",z="general",s="The Forsaken preserve what little remains of Lordaeron in Undercity. A weathered crest bears the fallen kingdom’s emblem, a rare link to its past.",h="The Forsaken preserve what little remains of Lordaeron in Undercity.",sec={{t="A Surviving Symbol of the Fallen Kingdom",b="The Third War shattered Lordaeron. Ash, ruins, and Scourge desolation swallowed much of the kingdom, leaving few emblems behind. To the Forsaken beneath the ruins of the Capital City, those that survive are precious links to the civilization they once belonged to.",sp=0},{t="Preserving Forsaken Heritage",b="The weathered crest is brought from the wider world to Oran Snakewrithe in Undercity. Oran tests the metal with a bite, then confirms it is genuine and mostly intact. For Oran and the Forsaken archivists, each recovered piece helps restore a history the Scourge sought to erase.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Crest of Lordaeron?",a="The Crest of Lordaeron is a weathered emblem of the fallen human kingdom. Few physical relics survived the Scourge’s destruction, so it preserves a rare piece of Lordaeron’s past.",al={"what is crest of lordaeron","lordaeron crest quest","crest item"}},{q="Who wants the crest in Undercity?",a="Oran Snakewrithe in Undercity receives the crest. Oran inspects and safeguards artifacts tied to the old kingdom.",al={"who gets the crest","where to turn in crest","crest turn in undercity"}},{q="Why does Oran Snakewrithe bite the crest?",a="Oran Snakewrithe bites the crest to test its metal and verify that it is genuine. Oran confirms it is a mostly intact relic of Lordaeron.",al={"why bite the crest","oran bites crest"}},{q="Why do the Forsaken value relics of Lordaeron?",a="The Forsaken are the undead remnants of Lordaeron’s former people. Relics of their ruined homeland remain tangible links to their history and identity.",al={"why do forsaken care about lordaeron","forsaken relics"}},{q="What will happen to the crest after it is turned in?",a="Oran Snakewrithe takes custody of the crest and keeps it safe. It helps the Forsaken preserve and remember their kingdom’s heritage.",al={"what happens to the crest","crest preservation"}},{q="How rare are surviving relics from Lordaeron?",a="Surviving emblems and relics from before the Third War are exceptionally scarce. The Scourge’s invasion left few physical signs of Lordaeron behind.",al={"is the crest rare","how many crests left"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As a former citizen of Lordaeron, you treasure relics of the fallen kingdom as rare remnants of your past and heritage.",["faction:Horde"]="You help Forsaken archivists in Undercity preserve Lordaeron’s legacy amid the ruins left by the Scourge."},kw={"crest of lordaeron","forsaken","lordaeron","oran snakewrithe","relic","undercity"},rel={"zone:undercity","topic:lordaeron","quest:95189"},m={id=95204},src={"In-game quest text: Crest of Lordaeron"},fv=true}
E["quest:95212"] = {t="quest",n="Never Saddle on Quality",z="general",s="At Amberstill Ranch, handlers prepare rams for the Alliance expedition in Alterac. They need pristine mountain leopard pelts to make warm saddles for the beasts, so mounts and riders can face the northern mountains' biting frost.",h="At Amberstill Ranch, handlers prepare rams for the Alliance expedition in Alterac.",sec={{t="Mounting the Expedition",b="At Amberstill Ranch in Dun Morogh, handlers are preparing sturdy mountain rams to reinforce the Alliance expedition in Alterac. But the work has stalled: they lack the material needed to make durable, cold-weather saddles for the rams.",sp=0},{t="Warmth for the High Passes",b="The expedition's mounts need thick insulation to endure the freeze of the Alterac mountains. Hunters are sent to the eastern crags to track the largest, fiercest mountain leopards in Dun Morogh and bring back pristine pelts. The Alliance forces need gear that can withstand the high passes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Never Saddle on Quality?",a="At Amberstill Ranch in Dun Morogh, a breeder needs high-quality leopard pelts for warm, sturdy saddles. The saddles are for a team of rams being readied as reinforcements for the Alterac expedition.",al={"what is this quest","never saddle on quality quest","what is never saddle on quality"}},{q="Why are leopard pelts needed?",a="Leopard pelts provide the thick fur needed to make durable, warm saddles for the expedition's mounts. The insulation protects riders and beasts from Alterac's biting mountain chill.",al={"why leopard pelts","what are pelts for","why pristine pelts"}},{q="Where are the rams being sent?",a="The mounts are bound for Alliance forces in the Alterac expedition. Well-equipped riding rams are vital support in the harsh weather and ongoing operations in the high passes.",al={"where are the rams going","who gets the rams","alterac reinforcements"}},{q="What is Amberstill Ranch's role here?",a="Amberstill Ranch lies in Dun Morogh, east of Kharanos. It is the main breeding and training ground for dwarven mountain rams.",al={"where is amberstill ranch","amberstill ranch location"}},{q="Where do the leopards roam?",a="The leopards roam the snowy ridges and eastern crags of Dun Morogh, east of Amberstill Ranch. Hunters seek the largest beasts there for their pristine hides.",al={"where find leopards","where are leopards dun morogh"}},{q="Why is cold weather protection so important for the expedition?",a="Alterac's kingdom and peaks are freezing and windswept. Without thick, high-quality saddle material, mounts and riders risk frostbite and exhaustion in the mountains.",al={"is alterac cold","why warm saddles"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Amberstill Ranch breeds rams to reinforce Alliance forces in Alterac's freezing heights.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dun Morogh's hardy mountain rams are iconic dwarven mounts. Their breeders at Amberstill Ranch refuse to equip them with substandard gear.",["class:Hunter"]="Skilled trackers and hunters are needed to seek out Dun Morogh's fiercest mountain leopards and secure pristine pelts."},kw={"alterac","alterac expedition","amberstill ranch","dun morogh","leopard pelts","never saddle on quality","pristine leopard pelts","rams","saddles"},rel={"subzone:amberstill-ranch","topic:alterac-kingdom","topic:alliance"},m={id=95212},src={"In-game quest text: Never Saddle on Quality"},fv=true}
E["quest:95213"] = {t="quest",n="Stolen Blasting Powder",z="general",s="Near Gol'Bolar Quarry, an empty keg of hazardous blasting powder has turned up among troggs. Its return to Quarrymaster Thesten reveals a breach in the quarry's security and explosive stores.",h="Near Gol'Bolar Quarry, an empty keg of hazardous blasting powder has turned up among troggs.",sec={{t="A Dangerous Discovery",b="At Gol'Bolar Quarry, a warning stamped on an empty keg shows that hazardous blasting powder has slipped out of authorized hands. The explosives are reserved for controlled quarrying, with the quarrymaster's direct approval. The keg was found among hostile troggs, and that points to a threat beyond ordinary trouble for the miners.",sp=0},{t="The Quarrymaster's Alarm",b="When the empty keg reaches Quarrymaster Thesten, the dwarven overseers' worst fears are confirmed. Thesten turns pale: the blasting powder is gone, and subterranean troggs swarming the area have handled it. Now the quarry leaders must learn what the creatures mean to blast next.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Stolen Blasting Powder?",a="Stolen Blasting Powder concerns an empty keg stamped with hazardous warnings, found among troggs near Gol'Bolar Quarry. It alerts Quarrymaster Thesten to the theft of volatile mining supplies.",al={"what is stolen blasting powder","powder keg quest","empty powder keg lore"}},{q="Who should receive the empty powder keg?",a="Bring the empty powder keg directly to Quarrymaster Thesten, who oversees operations at Gol'Bolar Quarry in Dun Morogh.",al={"where to bring the keg","who is thesten","quarrymaster location"}},{q="Why is the stolen blasting powder dangerous?",a="The keg bears quarrymaster authorization warnings and held hazardous blasting powder. Troggs with heavy explosives threaten the quarry excavation and nearby settlements.",al={"why is powder dangerous","troggs with explosives","why do troggs have powder"}},{q="What is Gol'Bolar Quarry?",a="Gol'Bolar Quarry is a dwarven excavation site in Dun Morogh that quarries stone and minerals for Ironforge.",al={"where is gol bolar","quarry location","golbolar dun morogh"}},{q="Why does Quarrymaster Thesten turn pale?",a="Quarrymaster Thesten turns pale at the sight of the empty keg and asks where it was found. He knows its dangerous contents have already fallen into hostile trogg hands.",al={"why thesten pale","thesten reaction","what does thesten say"}},{q="What are troggs doing near Gol'Bolar Quarry?",a="Troggs have infested the lower reaches and outskirts of Gol'Bolar Quarry. They disrupt dwarven miners and scavenge equipment and explosive powder from the dig site.",al={"are troggs in the quarry","trogg infestation dun morogh","why are troggs taking powder"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Gol'Bolar Quarry provides stone and resources vital to Ironforge. For a dwarf, the security of its mining explosives is an urgent concern.",["profession:Engineering"]="Blasting powder is volatile and dangerous. As an engineer, you know the havoc troggs could cause with unauthorized explosives.",["profession:Mining"]="Quarry operations depend on controlled demolitions to extract stone. Missing powder kegs signal grave danger inside the excavation pits.",["faction:Alliance"]="For the Alliance, securing Dun Morogh's supply lines and mining outposts from trogg incursions is essential to operations across Khaz Modan."},kw={"blasting powder","dun morogh","empty powder keg","gol'bolar quarry","powder keg","quarrymaster thesten","stolen blasting powder","thesten","troggs"},rel={"subzone:gol-bolar-quarry","topic:trogg"},m={id=95213},src={"In-game quest text: Stolen Blasting Powder"},fv=true}
E["quest:95214"] = {t="quest",n="Stolen Blasting Powder",z="general",s="At Gol'Bolar Quarry, Stolen Blasting Powder has gone east with the troggs. Quarrymaster Thesten needs help recovering the missing munitions before the brutes set them off by accident.",h="At Gol'Bolar Quarry, Stolen Blasting Powder has gone east with the troggs.",sec={{t="A Missing Stockpile",b="At Gol'Bolar Quarry in Dun Morogh, dwarves use heavy blasting charges to break stone and open deep veins. But primitive troggs from the nearby crags stole a bulk shipment of high-grade powder. Quarrymaster Thesten needs capable hands before the pack causes an accidental detonation.",sp=0},{t="A Powder Keg in the Crags",b="The missing keg holds enough force to reduce King Magni Bronzebeard's grand throne room to an open-air courtyard. The troggs carried the powder east to their rough foothill camps. Left alone, they may soon discover fire or strike stone against spark.",sp=0},{t="Quiet Resolution",b="After the stolen charges are recovered from the troggs and returned to the quarry depot, Quarrymaster Thesten can breathe easy. He rewards you, then quietly asks that you tell no one about the incident.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Quarrymaster Thesten?",a="Quarrymaster Thesten oversees excavation at Gol'Bolar Quarry in Dun Morogh. After a great stockpile of industrial blasting powder goes missing under his watch, he needs help recovering it.",al={"who is thesten","quarrymaster thesten","who gives this quest"}},{q="Where can the stolen blasting powder be found?",a="The troggs carried the powder to the crags and dens immediately east of Gol'Bolar Quarry. You are sent to recover the volatile kegs and sacks before disaster strikes.",al={"where is stolen blasting powder","trogg location","powder location"}},{q="Why did the troggs steal the blasting powder?",a="The entry gives no reason for the troggs' theft. They scavenged the powder kegs from quarry workers, and Quarrymaster Thesten fears they may ignite the explosives through clumsy experimentation.",al={"why troggs steal powder","what do troggs want with powder"}},{q="How dangerous is the stolen blasting powder?",a="Quarrymaster Thesten says the missing keg could blast King Magni's throne room into an open-air courtyard. If it detonates in the enclosed quarry or caverns, the force would be devastating.",al={"how powerful is powder","magni throne room powder","is the powder dangerous"}},{q="Why does Quarrymaster Thesten want the incident kept quiet?",a="When you return the powder, Quarrymaster Thesten pays you and asks you to keep the incident quiet. He gives no reason, but losing so much explosive to the troggs is something he would rather conceal.",al={"why keep it secret","thesten secret","dont mention incident"}},{q="What is Gol'Bolar Quarry?",a="Gol'Bolar Quarry is a dwarven excavation site in eastern Dun Morogh. It quarries stone for construction and engineering across the realm, while troggs emerge from the rock into its deep pits.",al={"what is gol bolar","gol bolar quarry","where is golbolar"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Gol'Bolar Quarry supports Ironforge's stonecraft and excavation. Letting troggs weaponize mining explosives so near King Magni's doorstep is a humiliating failure for a dwarven supervisor.",["faction:Alliance"]="Dun Morogh's supply lines and stone works matter to the Alliance's mountain stronghold. Guarding dangerous munitions helps keep the nearby High Seat in Ironforge safe.",["profession:Mining"]="Blasting powder clears rock and opens veins in deep digs, but it takes discipline to handle. Losing bulk kegs to wild cave-dwellers is every quarrymaster's nightmare."},kw={"blasting powder","dun morogh","explosives","gol'bolar quarry","ironforge","king magni","quarrymaster thesten","thesten","troggs"},rel={"subzone:gol-bolar-quarry","topic:trogg","quest:95213"},m={id=95214},src={"In-game quest text: Stolen Blasting Powder"},fv=true}
E["quest:95216"] = {t="quest",n="The New Plague",z="general",s="In the Undercity, Theodore Griffs studies reports of a lethal creature in the ruins above. Its venom consumes even undead flesh, and he seeks to learn how its toxins work.",h="In the Undercity, Theodore Griffs studies reports of a lethal creature in the ruins above.",sec={{t="The Apothecary's Ambition",b="The Royal Apothecary Society gathers dangerous substances from across Azeroth. Theodore Griffs is one of its researchers. He tracks strange beasts and deadly contaminants to advance Forsaken alchemy. Now rumors tell of a venomous creature in the shattered stone above, so Griffs sends you to bring back a sample before the chance is lost.",sp=0},{t="A Flesh-Dissolving Toxin",b="Witherfang is the venomous arthropod stalking the surface ruins of Lordaeron. Its venom breaks down living tissue and dead, preserved flesh alike. Griffs wants to learn how it overcomes the resistance of dead flesh, knowledge that could help refine biological weapons and protect the Forsaken kingdom.",sp=0},{t="Reverse Engineering the Ichor",b="Theodore Griffs is frustrated that Witherfang was killed instead of captured alive. Still, the gathered ichor gives him enough to study. By breaking down the creature’s bodily fluids, he hopes to learn how its venom works and add to the Undercity’s growing chemical knowledge.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Theodore Griffs?",a="Theodore Griffs is a researcher with the Royal Apothecary Society in the Undercity’s Apothecarium. He studies potent toxins and strange specimens to make compounds for the Forsaken, and has little patience for those who fail to value careful alchemical work.",al={"who gives the new plague","theodore griffs","who is theodore griffs"}},{q="What is Witherfang?",a="Witherfang is a venomous arthropod found in the Ruins of Lordaeron above the Undercity. Its toxin can dissolve even resilient undead flesh.",al={"what is witherfang","where is witherfang","ruins of lordaeron arthropod"}},{q="Why does Theodore Griffs want Witherfang's venom?",a="Theodore Griffs wants to learn how Witherfang’s venom eats through undead flesh. He hopes to recreate its effects for the Forsaken’s alchemical arsenal.",al={"what does theodore want with witherfang","why collect the highly toxic strain"}},{q="Why is Theodore Griffs upset when you return?",a="Theodore Griffs hoped you would keep Witherfang intact or gather its venom carefully. He is annoyed that it was slain, but recovered enough ichor from its remains to study.",al={"why was theodore angry","did i ruin the sample"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Apothecarium?",a="The Apothecarium is the Royal Apothecary Society’s research center beneath Lordaeron. There, its members test dangerous strains, make alchemical weapons, and experiment on strange creatures. It is the Undercity’s most vital quarter.",al={"apothecarium purpose","why is the apothecarium important"}},{q="How does Theodore believe virulent strains are best transmitted?",a="Theodore Griffs believes the surest way to spread a potent strain is to put it directly into the bloodstream.",al={"how is the venom transmitted","transmission method"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Royal Apothecary Society seeks toxins that can harm the living and the dead. It pursues Forsaken supremacy and protection for its young nation.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde accepts the Forsaken as allies in the Eastern Kingdoms, yet the Apothecarium’s grim experiments often trouble its more honorable members."},kw={"apothecarium","ichor","ruins of lordaeron","the new plague","theodore griffs","toxic strain","undercity","witherfang"},rel={"subzone:apothecarium","zone:undercity","zone:ruinsoflordaeron","topic:lordaeron"},m={id=95216},src={"In-game quest text: The New Plague"},fv=true}
E["quest:95217"] = {t="quest",n="The Quarry's Smith",z="general",s="At the quarry, The Quarry's Smith, Frast Dokner, needs metal and leather for camp repairs. With a supply shipment late, he asks travelers to bring the materials. In return, Dokner offers practical smithing techniques.",h="At the quarry, The Quarry's Smith, Frast Dokner, needs metal and leather for camp repairs.",sec={{t="Shortages at the Quarry",b="An excavation needs steady care. Pickaxes must be sharpened, worn brackets replaced, and camp gear reinforced. When a vital shipment misses its schedule, work begins to grind to a halt. Without fresh stock from the major trade routes, the craftsmen must improvise and look to passing travelers for help.\n\nFrast Dokner works the forge through these delays. He asks anyone willing to gather basic metals and hides to help keep the camp running, rather than wait for transport wagons that may be far behind.",sp=0},{t="An Apprentice's Bargain",b="Dwarven blacksmithing guilds prize discipline and guard their methods, rarely sharing trade secrets with casual wanderers. Dokner weighs that custom against the camp's immediate needs. He lacks workable copper and durable leather for patching tools and camp gear.\n\nBring the raw materials to his anvil, and he will teach you his personal smithing techniques. The bargain trades useful craft knowledge for materials the camp needs to endure and keep its gear in repair.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Frast Dokner?",a="Frast Dokner is an experienced blacksmith at a quarry encampment. He maintains tools, structural fittings, and equipment for the workers there. When delays cut off his regular supply line, he asks passing travelers to gather the raw materials he needs.",al={"frast dokner lore","who is the quarry smith","quarry blacksmith"}},{q="Why does the quarry need copper bars and boar hides?",a="Quarry work and camp upkeep use great amounts of sturdy materials. Copper is an accessible, workable metal for replacement fittings, nails, fasteners, and bands that reinforce broken tools. Boar hides provide tough leather for bellows, tool wraps, straps, and protective aprons against sparks and the grinding work.",al={"why copper bars and boar hides","materials needed quarry smith","quarry repair materials"}},{q="Why is the supply shipment late?",a="Frast Dokner does not explain why the shipment is late. Overland routes across snowy crags and mountain passes often face blizzards, roaming wildlife, and hostile trogg or bandit ambushes. Remote mining camps and quarries feel the strain first when transport wagons fall behind.",al={"late supply shipment","what happened to the supplies","why are supplies delayed"}},{q="What does Frast Dokner offer in exchange for the materials?",a="Frast Dokner offers more than coin: he promises to share his personal blacksmithing techniques with whoever gathers the needed supplies. An aspiring artisan can learn practical metalworking and equipment care from a seasoned field craftsman, lessons city guildhalls rarely teach novices.",al={"quarry smith reward lore","what does frast teach","blacksmithing techniques frast"}},{q="Where is this quarry located?",a="Frast Dokner works at Gol'Bolar Quarry in Dun Morogh, south of Kharanos. The dwarven excavation lies in snowy foothills and faces harsh winter weather and aggressive subterranean troggs, who harass miners and disrupt trade convoys from Ironforge.",al={"which quarry is this","where is frast dokner","quarry smith location"}},{q="Is Frast Dokner affiliated with Ironforge?",a="Frast Dokner and the quarry camp are under the Bronzebeard clan and the Kingdom of Ironforge. Quarrying stone and mining ore in Dun Morogh support the kingdom's construction projects and defenses. Blacksmiths such as Dokner help sustain Ironforge's industry.",al={"frast dokner ironforge","is the quarry part of ironforge","quarry dwarf allegiance"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="As a blacksmith, you can learn from Frast Dokner, a field mentor for apprentice smiths. He trades his hard-won forging techniques to anyone who supplies the raw metal and leather that keep his anvil hot.",["profession:Mining"]="As a miner working the surrounding deposits, you have a buyer close at hand for your smelted copper bars. They go toward practical training and camp repairs.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you can see Bronzebeard industry at Gol'Bolar Quarry, where dwarven resolve keeps the forges burning when supply caravans run late.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you can help keep the remote dwarven quarry running and its equipment ready in the snowy high country."},kw={"blacksmithing","copper bars","dun morogh","dwarf","frast dokner","gol'bolar quarry","ironforge","the quarry's smith","toughened boar hides"},m={id=95217},src={"In-game quest text: The Quarry's Smith"},fv=true}
E["quest:95250"] = {t="quest",n="Abominable Creatures",z="general",s="Abominable Creatures is a quest given by Captain Truman, a human loyalist desperately awaiting reinforcements on the outskirts of Lordaeron's fallen capital. Believing his commander—a revered Grand Marshal—is still valiantly fighting in the nearby Ruins of Lordaeron, Truman implores a capable adventurer to track him down. The mission exposes the grim fate of the kingdom's lost defenders and the bitter hostility smoldering among surviving loyalists.",h="Abominable Creatures is a quest given by Captain Truman, a human loyalist desperately awaiting reinforcements...",sec={{t="A Desperate Vigil",b="Captain Truman stands vigilant at his post, anxiously awaiting long-overdue reinforcements to support the defense of Lordaeron. Confident in the martial prowess of his superior—a renowned Grand Marshal whom he describes as a tall, statuesque hero—Truman remains bound by duty after being commanded to hold the line while his commander charged into the ongoing fray. Harboring deep suspicion toward outsiders and openly scorning those he considers lesser breeds, the captain refuses to trust local irregulars. When a capable traveler approaches, Truman seizes the opportunity to enlist aid, pleading for someone to venture into the ruined territory to check on his master's well-being and assess the battle.",sp=0},{t="The Ruins of Lordaeron",b="The search directs adventurers straight into the perilous Ruins of Lordaeron, the shattered capital that fell to the Scourge during the Third War. Although Captain Truman expects his glorious leader to be standing triumphant over the fallen, the reality within the blighted city is far grimmer. The ruins are overrun with horrors, undead remnants, and abominations wandering the broken stone plazas. Navigating these devastated streets requires confronting the brutal truth of what happened to the defenders who ventured into the city. Instead of finding an immaculate knight holding the line, travelers discover that the commander has met a tragic, twisted end among the monstrosities occupying the courtyards.",sp=0},{t="A Horrifying Revelation",b="Upon locating the missing commander, designated as the Baron, adventurers make a horrifying discovery: the once-statuesque hero has been transformed into a grotesque undead monstrosity. Fulfilling the grim task requires slaying the creature and taking the Head of the Baron back to Captain Truman. When presented with the severed head of his former lord, Truman reacts with shock, horror, and profound revulsion. Unable to accept the dreadful fate of his commander, the captain lashes out in bitter outrage, accusing the adventurer of being in league with monsters and vowing that the true sons of Lordaeron will one day reclaim their land and purge all beasts.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Abominable Creatures?",a="The quest is offered by Captain Truman, a human loyalist stationed in the region surrounding the fallen kingdom. Anxiously holding his post while awaiting reinforcements, Truman desperately seeks news of his commander, a revered Grand Marshal who charged into the nearby Ruins of Lordaeron.",al={"who gives abominable creatures","abominable creatures quest giver","where to start abominable creatures"}},{q="Where does the quest Abominable Creatures take place?",a="The quest directs adventurers into the Ruins of Lordaeron, the shattered capital city overrun by Scourge horrors and undead remnants. Captain Truman maintains a post outside the blighted capital, asking brave travelers to venture directly into the dangerous ruins to locate his missing superior.",al={"abominable creatures location","where is abominable creatures completed","abominable creatures zone"}},{q="Who is Captain Truman's master?",a="Captain Truman refers to his master as the Grand Marshal, praising him as a tall, statuesque military hero. However, the quest objectives refer to this missing noble as the Baron, indicating that he was a high-ranking aristocrat and military commander leading the defense of the realm.",al={"who is truman's commander","grand marshal identity truman","who is the baron"}},{q="What happened to the Grand Marshal in the Ruins of Lordaeron?",a="Rather than holding out victoriously, the Grand Marshal was slain and transformed into a monstrous undead creature within the Ruins of Lordaeron. When adventurers locate him, they find an abomination designated as the Baron, forcing them to destroy him and claim his severed head.",al={"what happened to the baron","grand marshal fate","is the grand marshal dead"}},{q="Why does Captain Truman get angry when you turn in the quest?",a="Captain Truman expected his statuesque commander to be alive and victorious. When presented with the grotesque, mutated head of the Baron, Truman is overcome with horror and revulsion, furiously accusing the adventurer of playing a sick joke and consorting with vile beasts.",al={"why is truman mad","truman reaction abominable creatures","why does truman yell at you"}},{q="What does Captain Truman mean by the 'true sons of Lordaeron'?",a="Captain Truman uses the term to describe zealous human patriots dedicated to reclaiming their shattered homeland. His words express a fierce, intolerant vision where the rightful human survivors will purge the kingdom of all undead, beasts, and anyone perceived as consorting with them.",al={"true sons of lordaeron meaning","who are the true sons of lordaeron","truman true sons"}},{q="Why does Captain Truman refuse to trust 'half breeds'?",a="Captain Truman displays deep disdain for non-human races and irregular allies, dismissing them as untrustworthy half-breeds and filth. His intense xenophobia prevents him from confiding in anyone other than those he deems worthy representatives of the realm.",al={"truman half breeds","why is captain truman racist","truman xenophobia"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="Human adventurers witness the heartbreaking fate of Lordaeron's fallen leadership, as even an esteemed Grand Marshal was transformed into a grotesque undead horror. Captain Truman's bitter grief and nationalistic zeal reflect the raw trauma carried by human survivors desperate to reclaim their ancestral home.",["faction:Alliance"]="The quest highlights the perilous, isolated plight of Alliance-aligned remnants trying to operate within the blighted ruins of northern Lordaeron. It emphasizes how lingering loyalist detachments struggle with dwindling hope and the psychological toll of fighting overwhelming monstrosities.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the Baron's grotesque transformation is a grim reminder of how the Scourge mutilated Lordaeron's finest defenders into monstrous abominations. Captain Truman's vow to purge the ruins of all beasts and undead reflects the relentless hostility living loyalists hold against the Undercity's people."},kw={"abominable creatures","captain truman","grand marshal","head of the baron","lordaeron","ruins of lordaeron","the baron","true sons of lordaeron"},rel={"zone:ruinsoflordaeron","topic:lordaeron"},m={id=95250},src={"In-game quest text: Abominable Creatures"},fv=true}
E["quest:95328"] = {t="quest",n="Whispering Horror Residue",z="general",s="Whispering Horror Residue is the squirming remnant of a strange beast recovered from beneath the Whispering Forest. Gathered by intrepid adventurers, these void-touched remains are brought to Father Lankester in Undercity's War Quarter for shadow research.",h="Whispering Horror Residue is the squirming remnant of a strange beast recovered from beneath the Whispering...",sec={{t="An Unsettling Harvest",b="Whispering Horror Residue represents the bizarre, squirming remains scraped up from a defeated beast dwelling beneath the Whispering Forest. Rather than abandoning such an unnatural curiosity, resourceful adventurers gather the twitching material in hopes that researchers within the Undercity can turn it to good use.",sp=0},{t="Studies in Shadow",b="The creature's remains find an eager recipient in Father Lankester, located within the War Quarter of Undercity. Lankester notes that the Whispering Forest has long been touched by the void, welcoming the chance to examine physical remnants of that dark corruption as he delves into the mysteries of shadow.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Whispering Horror Residue?",a="Whispering Horror Residue consists of the squirming bodily remains harvested from a strange beast defeated beneath the Whispering Forest. Despite its unsettling nature, scholars of shadow magic find it valuable for arcane and occult research.",al={"what is the residue","what is whispering horror residue","residue item"}},{q="Who wants the Whispering Horror Residue?",a="The residue is delivered to Father Lankester, a Forsaken figure located in the War Quarter of Undercity. He welcomes the opportunity to explore the mysteries of shadow and void-touched phenomena.",al={"who takes the residue","who is father lankester","where to turn in residue"}},{q="Where did this specimen come from?",a="The specimen originates from beneath the Whispering Forest, an ominous woodland located west of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. As Father Lankester observes, the foul area is known to be touched by the void.",al={"where does horror come from","whispering forest lore","under whispering forest"}},{q="Why does Father Lankester want the residue?",a="Father Lankester seeks to study the mysteries of the shadow and the void. To Forsaken practitioners probing forbidden arts, physical remnants from void-tainted creatures offer rare insight and research material.",al={"why lankester want this","undercity shadow research","why study residue"}},{q="Where can Father Lankester be found?",a="Father Lankester resides in the War Quarter of Undercity. The War Quarter serves as a bustling hub for Forsaken warriors, scholars, and military planners beneath the ruins of Lordaeron's capital.",al={"where is war quarter","where is lankester located","undercity lankester location"}},{q="Is the Whispering Forest touched by the void?",a="The Whispering Forest has long held a sinister reputation among the locals of Tirisfal. Father Lankester notes it is no secret that the foul woods and the subterranean spaces beneath them are touched by the void.",al={"is whispering forest cursed","void corruption whispering forest","what touches whispering forest"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Forsaken shadow priests like Father Lankester maintain an intense scholarly and theological interest in void specimens, viewing strange planar remains as objects of study rather than mere abominations.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken of Undercity actively study forbidden magic and unnatural corruptions across Lordaeron, seeking to harness dark phenomena to strengthen their precarious hold on the kingdom.",["faction:Horde"]="Adventurers serving the Horde recover unsettling specimens from the hidden wilds of Tirisfal Glades to fuel research efforts beneath the Capital City ruins."},kw={"father lankester","shadow","tirisfal","undercity","void","war quarter","whispering forest","whispering horror residue"},rel={"subzone:whispering-forest","subzone:war-quarter","zone:undercity"},m={id=95328},src={"In-game quest text: Whispering Horror Residue"},fv=true}
E["quest:95349"] = {t="quest",n="The Earthen Ring",z="general",s="From Zephras, the Windshapers of The Earthen Ring reach Azeroth as an advance vanguard. After cult activity delays the rest in Valanaar, they board a skycutter outside the lodge and make landfall in Mulgore to meet Alaana Stormwalker and adjust to a vast, dangerous world.",h="From Zephras, the Windshapers of The Earthen Ring reach Azeroth as an advance vanguard.",sec={{t="Departure from Valanaar",b="Cult activity in Valanaar has disrupted the Windshapers’ plans to travel to Azeroth. Their senior leaders stay behind at the lodge to finish urgent business and quell the threat, while a small vanguard sets out ahead. At the docks just outside the lodge, the pioneers board a skycutter bound across the skies.\n\nTheir commanders promise to join them soon. Until then, the advance group must establish a presence before the rest can follow.",sp=0},{t="Arrival in Mulgore",b="In Mulgore, Alaana Stormwalker welcomes the arriving adventurers from Zephras. Establishing a foothold in Azeroth will take time, so newcomers are encouraged to scout the surrounding territory and grow accustomed to it.\n\nThe winds favor the expedition, but Azeroth is unfamiliar and perilous. Travelers should learn the local terrain, wildlife, and neighboring inhabitants before those still in Valanaar arrive to join them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the Windshapers traveling to Azeroth?",a="The Windshapers send a vanguard ahead to establish a presence on Azeroth while their leaders remain in Valanaar. Cult activity there disrupted the original plans and delayed the rest of the group, so the advance party travels alone until reinforcements can follow.",al={"why go to azeroth","windshaper mission","why are windshapers in mulgore"}},{q="Who is Alaana Stormwalker?",a="Alaana Stormwalker welcomes Windshapers arriving from Zephras in Mulgore. She says establishing a secure presence in these new lands will take time and advises newcomers to explore the surrounding region and prepare for Azeroth’s dangers.",al={"who is alaana","alaana stormwalker role","where is alaana stormwalker"}},{q="Why was the main departure delayed?",a="Cult activity in Valanaar delayed the main departure. Expedition leaders stay behind at the lodge to settle the trouble, while a small vanguard boards a skycutter and travels ahead to Azeroth.",al={"why the delay","what cult delayed travel","why stay in valanaar"}},{q="How do travelers journey from Valanaar to Mulgore?",a="Travelers board a skycutter at the docks just outside the lodge in Valanaar. It carries the vanguard across the sky to Mulgore, connecting Zephras with Azeroth during the initial expedition.",al={"how to reach mulgore","where is the ship","how do you travel to azeroth"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is the home of the adventurers arriving aboard the skycutter. In Mulgore, Alaana Stormwalker welcomes newcomers from Zephras and says favorable winds guided their crossing into unfamiliar, dangerous Azeroth.",al={"where is zephras","what is zephras","zephras lore"}},{q="What should adventurers do upon arriving in Mulgore?",a="New arrivals are encouraged to scout the surrounding lands of Mulgore. Learning the nearby terrain, creatures, and local threats will help them as they establish a presence in this unfamiliar, hazardous world.",al={"what to do in mulgore","next steps after arriving","what to do next"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne adventurer leaving Valanaar, you make the historic journey to Azeroth as part of its advance vanguard. You leave the cult troubles behind and set foot on Mulgore’s plains.",["race:Tauren"]="As a Tauren native to Mulgore, you witness travelers from Zephras descending upon your ancestral plains. Welcoming these visitors offers an early connection to allies seeking a foothold in your lands.",["class:Shaman"]="As a Shaman, you share the Windshapers’ reverence for the elements and their reliance on the winds to guide their crossing. Arriving under The Earthen Ring connects you to elemental traditions taking root in Mulgore."},kw={"alaana stormwalker","banu-alah","cult","mulgore","skycutter","the earthen ring","valanaar","windshapers","zephras"},rel={"zone:mulgore"},m={id=95349},src={"In-game quest text: The Earthen Ring"},fv=true}
E["quest:95350"] = {t="quest",n="Welcome to Azeroth",z="general",s="'Welcome to Azeroth' is an introductory quest that sends a traveler from Thunder Bluff to Orgrimmar on behalf of the Windshapers. Guided by the spiritual ties of the Earthen Ring, the emissary meets with Warchief Thrall to forge bonds of survival and alliance with the Horde.",h="'Welcome to Azeroth' is an introductory quest that sends a traveler from Thunder Bluff to Orgrimmar on behalf...",sec={{t="Allies in a Broader World",b="Venturing into the sprawling expanse of Kalimdor, newcomers quickly discover that survival hinges on brotherhood. The Windshapers look beyond their isolated origins to the peoples walking below, recognizing that isolation offers no defense against the threats of Azeroth. By extending a hand to the neighboring tauren city of Thunder Bluff and looking toward the orcish bastions to the east, they seek steadfast companions to brave the wilderness together.",sp=0},{t="An Audience with the Warchief",b="The journey carries envoys to the soaring towers of Thunder Bluff before directing them across the barrens to Orgrimmar, the mighty capital of the Horde. Waiting in the tranquil Valley of Wisdom is Warchief Thrall, the guiding heart of the coalition. Standing before Thrall marks the official entry of the Windshapers into the political and spiritual fabric of Kalimdor, bound by common purpose and shared respect for the elements.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the quest Welcome to Azeroth?",a="'Welcome to Azeroth' is a diplomatic introductory quest dispatching an emissary on behalf of the Windshapers to meet Warchief Thrall in Orgrimmar. It serves as an official introduction to the wider Horde and the surface world of Kalimdor.",al={"what is this quest","welcome to azeroth quest","purpose of welcome to azeroth"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a group reaching out to surface allies from above, seeking survival through fellowship with the Earthen Ring and the Horde. They send travelers to speak directly with Horde leadership in Durotar.",al={"who are windshapers","what are windshapers"}},{q="Why are you sent to speak with Thrall?",a="As the Warchief of the Horde and a revered shamanic leader, Thrall oversees the coalition's major alliances from the Valley of Wisdom. Making contact with him formalizes relations between the Windshapers and the Horde.",al={"why visit thrall","speak with thrall orgrimmar","why go to orgrimmar"}},{q="How do you travel from Thunder Bluff to Orgrimmar?",a="Travelers take flight from the flight master located at the central roost of Thunder Bluff, soaring across the plains of Kalimdor eastward into the canyon fortress of Orgrimmar.",al={"how to reach orgrimmar","thunder bluff flight master","getting to orgrimmar from thunder bluff"}},{q="How does the Earthen Ring fit into this mission?",a="In this encounter, the Earthen Ring is described as aligned with the Horde's coalition of like-minded peoples, helping facilitate bonds between shamanic factions and the nations of Kalimdor.",al={"earthen ring horde role","is earthen ring horde"}},{q="Where is Thrall located in Orgrimmar?",a="Warchief Thrall resides within the Valley of Wisdom, a quiet spiritual sanctuary in the western reaches of Orgrimmar where shamans and tribal advisors convene.",al={"where is thrall","valley of wisdom thrall location"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="This diplomatic mission formalizes ties with the Horde, introducing newcomers directly to Warchief Thrall as key allies for survival in Kalimdor.",["race:Skyborne"]="Leaving the high heights for the surface world below, the Skyborne Windshapers reach out to Thunder Bluff and Orgrimmar to secure their place among new allies.",["class:Shaman"]="With guidance invoking the Earthen Ring and the blessing of the winds, this journey directly links spiritual kin with the heart of shamanic leadership in the Valley of Wisdom."},kw={"earthen ring","horde","orgrimmar","thrall","thunder bluff","valley of wisdom","welcome to azeroth","windshapers"},rel={"npc:thrall","zone:orgrimmar","zone:thunderbluff","topic:horde","quest:92595"},m={id=95350},src={"In-game quest text: Welcome to Azeroth"},fv=true}
E["quest:95494"] = {t="quest",n="Bruised Pride and Lion Hides",z="general",s="An injury leaves a hunter or tradesperson unable to gather lion hides for a local tanner. They turn to you to keep their trade agreement and their professional record intact.",h="An injury leaves a hunter or tradesperson unable to gather lion hides for a local tanner.",sec={{t="A Hunter's Dilemma",b="A hunter or tradesperson has been hurt and cannot safely track wild beasts. Their agreement with a neighborhood craftsman is still due, so they hire you to hunt wild lions and gather their pelts.",sp=0},{t="Honoring the Arrangement",b="The raw hides go to a local tanner, who exchanges them for finished leather goods the giver needs for their livelihood. The giver’s pride is stung by needing help, but honoring the bargain keeps their professional reputation unbroken.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is this quest about?",a="A wounded huntsman or trader needs lion hides to supply a local tanner. In exchange, the giver receives finished leather goods essential to their livelihood.",al={"what is bruised pride and lion hides","quest goal","what are the lion hides for"}},{q="Why does the quest giver need help gathering hides?",a="An injury keeps the giver from hunting lions themselves. They rely on you to uphold a long-standing agreement with a local tanner.",al={"why need help","why can't they hunt"}},{q="Who receives the lion hides?",a="A local tanner receives the raw hides and processes them into finished leather goods the giver needs for regular trade and work.",al={"who gets the lion hides","where do hides go"}},{q="What does the name 'Bruised Pride and Lion Hides' mean?",a="The name refers to both a pride of lions to hunt and the injured hunter’s pride. The hunter has never failed to deliver on a contract, but their injury now limits them.",al={"bruised pride meaning","why is it called bruised pride"}},{q="Was the arrangement with the tanner at risk?",a="Yes. The injury puts the giver’s next delivery at risk. Gathering the lion hides lets them keep their bargain without coming up short.",al={"does tanner arrangement hold","trade deal broken"}},{q="How was the quest giver injured?",a="The giver suffered an injury that prevents them from hunting lions in the wild. Its exact cause is not given, and delivering the hides lets them preserve their reputation.",al={"quest giver injury","what happened to hunter"}}},kw={"bruised pride and lion hides","hunting","leather goods","lion hides","tanner","trade"},m={id=95494},src={"In-game quest text: Bruised Pride and Lion Hides"},fv=true}
E["quest:95495"] = {t="quest",n="The Hermit Tanner",z="general",s="You carry raw hides across the Barrens to The Hermit Tanner, who lives above the Dry Hills. Vrang cannot make the climb, so he sends you to his secluded trading partner.",h="You carry raw hides across the Barrens to The Hermit Tanner, who lives above the Dry Hills.",sec={{t="A Ridge in the Barrens",b="Across the Barrens, Vrang trades with a tanner who lives on a ridge above the Dry Hills. The steep, rocky climb is too hard for Vrang, so he asks passing travelers to carry raw hides to his partner's homestead.",sp=0},{t="An Unconventional Craftsman",b="Vrang warns you that the solitary tanner's unusual appearance may startle you, but says he is no danger to visitors. When you bring the hides to the ridge, the hermit welcomes you warmly. He regrets that Vrang could not come himself, for there is something he hoped to discuss with the trader.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends you on this delivery?",a="Vrang sends you with a bundle of raw hides for his trading partner on the ridge above the Dry Hills.",al={"who gives hermit tanner","where start quest","vrang delivery"}},{q="Where does the hermit tanner live?",a="The hermit tanner lives alone on a secluded homestead atop a ridge overlooking the Dry Hills in the Barrens.",al={"hermit tanner location","where is hermit tanner","ridge hermit"}},{q="Why does Vrang warn you about the tanner's appearance?",a="Vrang warns you that the tanner's unusual appearance may startle you, but assures you that he poses no danger to visitors.",al={"hermit looks","why hermit scary","hermit appearance"}},{q="Why couldn't Vrang make the climb himself?",a="The steep, rocky climb is too taxing for Vrang to manage, so he asks a passing traveler to carry the hides.",al={"why need delivery","why vrang cant go"}},{q="What items are being delivered to the ridge?",a="You carry a bundle of raw hides for the hermit's tanning trade, which he and Vrang share.",al={"what is bundle of hides","quest items delivered"}},{q="What does the hermit tanner wish to discuss with Vrang?",a="The hermit regrets that Vrang could not come in person and says he has something to discuss with him.",al={"what does tanner want with vrang","tanner business with vrang"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Across the Barrens, you can help traders like Vrang carry goods over the rugged savanna."},kw={"barrens","bundle of hides","dry hills","hermit tanner","ridge","tanner","vrang"},rel={"subzone:dry-hills","zone:barrens"},m={id=95495},src={"In-game quest text: The Hermit Tanner"},fv=true}
E["quest:95507"] = {t="quest",n="Vrang's Game",z="general",s="Vrang sends you across a valley in the Barrens to check his traps. The small game they catch can help him, though other wildlife makes the area dangerous.",h="Vrang sends you across a valley in the Barrens to check his traps.",sec={{t="Vrang's Traps",b="Vrang has traps scattered across the valley, and he wants you to bring back any small game they have caught. Keep your attention on the wildlife too, for it can make the area dangerous.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Vrang want?",a="Vrang wants you to check his traps and bring back any small game they have caught.",al={"vrang traps","vrang game","what does vrang need"}},{q="Where are Vrang's traps?",a="Vrang's traps are scattered across a valley in the Barrens.",al={"where traps","trap location","barrens traps"}},{q="What is dangerous around the traps?",a="Other wildlife around the traps can be dangerous if you are not paying attention.",al={"danger nearby","other animals","wildlife danger"}}},kw={"barrens","small game","trapped game","traps","valley","vrang","wildlife"},m={id=95507},src={"In-game quest text: Vrang's Game"},fv=true}
E["quest:95508"] = {t="quest",n="Unwelcome Guests",z="general",s="You helped someone survive an encounter with unwelcome guests, and they are grateful. What brought you together remains unclear.",h="You helped someone survive an encounter with unwelcome guests, and they are grateful.",sec={},faq={{q="What happened here?",a="You helped the speaker survive an encounter, though they give no details about it.",al={"what happened","unwelcome guests","guest trouble"}},{q="Who is thanking me?",a="The speaker who thanks you is not named.",al={"who is this","who thanked me","quest giver"}},{q="Why are they in my debt?",a="The speaker says you helped them survive, but does not explain what happened.",al={"why thank me","why do they owe me","helped them"}}},kw={"debt","help","survive","thanks","unwelcome guests"},m={id=95508},src={"In-game quest text: Unwelcome Guests"},fv=true}
E["quest:95621"] = {t="quest",n="Trouble in the Valley",z="general",s="A squad of Kul Tiran soldiers is searching the valley, and their purpose is unknown. They have camped near a wrecked caravan to the south, drawing the attention of local scouts.",h="A squad of Kul Tiran soldiers is searching the valley, and their purpose is unknown.",sec={{t="Suspicious Activity in the Valley",b="A rifleman watching the lowlands spotted human soldiers searching the valley floor. Through his scope, he recognized the heraldry and livery of Kul Tiras. Armed foreigners rarely work so openly here, and their presence has raised alarm.",sp=0},{t="The Wrecked Caravan",b="The soldiers have camped near a ruined caravan to the south. What they seek among the wreckage remains unknown. Local observers consider their armed presence an immediate concern, so investigators are sent to eliminate them and uncover the purpose of their search.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Kul Tiran soldiers doing in the valley?",a="A sharpshooter spotted Kul Tiran soldiers searching the valley below. They camped near a wrecked caravan to the south, and local scouts are sent to investigate their purpose.",al={"why kul tirans here","kul tiras soldiers in valley","what are kul tirans doing"}},{q="Where is the Kul Tiran camp located?",a="The Kul Tiran soldiers camp near a ruined caravan south of the vantage point overlooking the valley.",al={"wrecked caravan location","where is kul tiras camp","caravan site"}},{q="How was the Kul Tiran presence discovered?",a="A scout watching the valley inspected the human group through a rifle scope and recognized Kul Tiras by its uniforms and insignia.",al={"how were they identified","kul tiras livery"}},{q="What is the objective of investigating the camp?",a="You are sent south to the wrecked caravan to confront the Kul Tiran soldiers and discover what brought them to search the area.",al={"what is the goal","trouble in the valley objectives"}},{q="Why are the Kul Tiran soldiers treated as enemies?",a="The soldiers are armed foreigners searching the land around a destroyed caravan without explanation. Scouts see them as a threat to be subdued so their intentions can be uncovered.",al={"why fight kul tiras","are kul tirans hostile"}},{q="Who detected the soldiers in the valley?",a="Walton, the hermit tanner watching the valley, spotted the soldiers through his rifle scope. He hired an adventurer to eliminate them and learn why they were there.",al={"who spotted soldiers","who gave quest"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="As a human, you may recognize the sea green and anchor colors of Kul Tiras. Soldiers from this reclusive seafaring nation are a rare and unexpected sight in the valley."},kw={"caravan","kul tiras","soldiers","trouble in the valley","valley","wrecked caravan"},rel={"topic:kul-tiras","topic:human"},m={id=95621},src={"In-game quest text: Trouble in the Valley"},fv=true}
E["quest:95646"] = {t="quest",n="Horrors in the Highland",z="general",s="Rethiel the Greenwarden senses a foul corruption seeping through a mountain gully into the unsalted waters above the Wetlands. Adventurers must ascend to the excavation sites to slay a Highland Horror and recover its root core. Rethiel discovers that an ancient energy is stirring, warning that ongoing digging threatens the safety of everyone in the region.",h="Rethiel the Greenwarden senses a foul corruption seeping through a mountain gully into the unsalted waters...",sec={{t="Taint in the Gully",b="Deep in the Wetlands, Rethiel the Greenwarden senses a disturbing shift in the land. A foul taint seeps through a mountain gully, infecting the freshwater creatures inhabiting the highlands above. Unlike the hardy life shaped by the brackish lowland waters, these freshwater beings have never known the sea's protective salt, leaving them entirely defenseless against spreading evil. Rethiel calls upon adventurers to venture up to the excavation sites, slay a Highland Horror, and retrieve its root core.",sp=0},{t="An Ancient Stirring",b="When the Highland Horror's root core is brought before Rethiel, the guardian's fears are confirmed: an ancient energy has begun to stir. The affliction plaguing the highland creatures is no ordinary sickness, but the consequence of recent delving. Rethiel urgently warns that the eager excavators working the mountain sites must cease their digging immediately before their unthinking curiosity unleashes something far worse upon the Wetlands.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Horrors in the Highland?",a="Rethiel the Greenwarden, an ancient protector watching over the Wetlands, offers this quest after sensing a foul corruption seeping through a nearby mountain gully.",al={"who gives horrors in the highland","horrors in the highland questgiver","rethiel quest"}},{q="Where can Highland Horrors be found?",a="Highland Horrors dwell within the excavation sites situated in the higher ground above the lowlands, near the mountain gully where the corruption seeps.",al={"where are highland horrors","highland horror location","where to find highland horror"}},{q="Why are the highland creatures so susceptible to corruption?",a="The freshwater creatures of the highlands lack the hardening brine and salt of the sea found in the lower wetlands, leaving them fragile and especially bitter in their reaction to corrupting taint.",al={"why are the creatures tainted","why highland creatures corrupted","unsalted water corruption"}},{q="What is the root core of a Highland Horror?",a="The root core is the heart of the corrupted Highland Horror. Rethiel requires it to inspect the unnatural power spreading through the waterways and confirm the nature of the corruption.",al={"what is the root core","highland horror core","why take the root core"}},{q="What does Rethiel discover when you return the root core?",a="Upon examining the core, Rethiel realizes that an ancient energy has begun to stir from deep within the earth, likely disturbed by reckless digging at the excavation sites.",al={"what does rethiel discover","ancient energy wetlands","excavation danger"}},{q="Why does Rethiel want the excavators to stop digging?",a="Rethiel warns that the eager excavators working in the highlands should immediately cease their digging for their own safety, as their delving risks releasing dangerous ancient forces.",al={"stop digging rethiel","what should excavators do","dwarf dig site warning"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Rethiel senses the natural balance shifting through his roots and the waterways; druids attuned to the land will recognize the sudden vulnerability of freshwater life to ancient corruption.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven dig sites in the highlands have unearthed more than ancient relics, prompting Rethiel to urge excavators to halt their digging before worse evils awaken.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance explorers and Prospectors operating excavation sites in the mountains risk disturbing ancient forces buried beneath the earth."},kw={"corruption","excavation sites","highland horror","horrors in the highland","rethiel","rethiel the greenwarden","root core","wetlands"},rel={"npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden","quest:463","zone:wetlands"},m={id=95646},src={"In-game quest text: Horrors in the Highland"},fv=true}
E["quest:95647"] = {t="quest",n="Lost in the Thicket Things",z="general",s="A concerned alchemist asks travelers to track down her missing husband, Ardin Grassman, who vanished after pursuing rumors of towering, unknown grasses. Ardin's botanical curiosity led him straight toward a nearby excavation site, where dangerous wildlife and spider attacks have besieged the crew.",h="A concerned alchemist asks travelers to track down her missing husband, Ardin Grassman, who vanished after...",sec={{t="A Forager's Calling",b="In 'Lost in the Thicket Things', an alchemist seeks help tracking down her missing husband, Ardin Grassman. Ardin possesses an uncanny, instinctual affinity for nature, routinely braving steep cliffs and distant wilderness to gather rare herbs and botanical specimens to stock her alchemy supplies. When a passing excavation crew brought word of bizarre, towering grasses unlike anything seen before, Ardin's curiosity got the better of him, and he set out to inspect the flora for himself.",sp=0},{t="Ambush at the Dig Site",b="Ardin tracked the expedition toward their excavation site, only to walk straight into peril. Something in the surrounding land agitated the local fauna, causing aggressive wildlife—most notably swarms of hostile spiders—to lash out and overrun the dig team. Trapped by the encroaching beasts, Ardin and the crew found themselves besieged until travelers intervened to rescue the stranded botanist.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Ardin Grassman?",a="Ardin Grassman is a gatherer with a rare natural gift for sensing plants and herbs. He regularly scours cliffs and peaks to supply his wife with wild flora for her alchemy craft, but his curiosity about strange, towering grasses drew him away toward an excavation site.",al={"who is ardin","ardin grassman lore","who is ardin grassman"}},{q="Why did Ardin wander off into the wild?",a="Ardin overheard an excavation crew describing massive, towering grasses unlike anything they had encountered before. Drawn by his affinity for unusual plants and flora, he ventured out to see the strange vegetation with his own eyes and failed to return home.",al={"why did ardin leave","why is ardin missing","what was ardin looking for"}},{q="Where did Ardin go?",a="Ardin ventured toward the nearby Excavation Sites after hearing reports from a passing expedition crew. Travelers searching for him must scour the dig sites where the crew set up operations.",al={"where is ardin","ardin location","where to find ardin grassman"}},{q="What happened to Ardin at the excavation site?",a="Ardin found the excavation camp overwhelmed when local wildlife became agitated and aggressive. Giant spiders and other stirred beasts ambushed the expedition, trapping Ardin and the crew until outside assistance arrived.",al={"what happened to ardin","is ardin dead","what attacked ardin"}},{q="Who is looking for Ardin?",a="The search begins with Ardin's wife, an alchemist who relies on his herb-gathering expeditions to stock her shelves. When he failed to return from investigating the rumors of strange grass, she sought capable travelers to bring him back.",al={"who gives lost in the thicket things","who started this quest","ardin wife"}},{q="Why did the wildlife attack the excavation crew?",a="The wildlife surrounding the excavation sites became suddenly and violently agitated, though the exact root cause of their distress remains uncertain. The disturbance drove aggressive creatures, particularly spiders, to swarm and overwhelm the dig team.",al={"why are spiders attacking","what upset the animals","why are beasts angry"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Ardin Grassman possesses an innate gift for sensing plants and herbs, often scaling treacherous terrain to gather rare flora for alchemy.",["class:Druid"]="Druids attuned to the balance of the wilds may find interest in Ardin's natural gift for sensing flora, as well as the unnatural agitation upsetting the wildlife near the dig sites."},kw={"alchemy","ardin","ardin grassman","excavation sites","herbalism","lost in the thicket things","spiders","towering grasses"},m={id=95647},src={"In-game quest text: Lost in the Thicket Things"},fv=true}
E["quest:95697"] = {t="quest",n="Changing Tastes",z="general",s="At Borstan's Firepit, a new dish may bring travelers to Orgrimmar. Zamja's advice and tales of giant raptor talons lead Borstan to seek thicket raptor meat from the Wetlands across the sea.",h="At Borstan's Firepit, a new dish may bring travelers to Orgrimmar.",sec={{t="Elevating Capital Cuisine",b="Borstan's Firepit in Orgrimmar hopes to widen its menu. Zamja suggests homestyle raptor cooking for travelers who miss the familiar meals of their birth villages. Borstan likes the thought of drawing new patrons with raptor, but simple village fare seems too modest for the Horde's great capital. He wants a bolder version of the old staple.",sp=0},{t="The Hunt in the Wetlands",b="A hunter returns to Orgrimmar with a massive talon from a thicket raptor of the Wetlands excavation sites. The beast's size and quality catch Borstan's interest. He arranges for adventurers to cross the sea to the Eastern Kingdoms, brave the excavation grounds, and bring back prime thicket raptor meat for his fires.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Changing Tastes?",a="Changing Tastes is a culinary task in Orgrimmar. Borstan, owner of Borstan's Firepit, seeks thicket raptor meat from the distant Wetlands to raise his menu above basic village cooking.",al={"what is changing tastes","changing tastes quest"}},{q="Who is Zamja?",a="Zamja is an associate and fellow culinary worker who suggests homestyle raptor dishes for Borstan's Firepit, hoping to attract customers who do not usually visit.",al={"who is zamja","zamja cooking"}},{q="What is Borstan's Firepit?",a="Borstan's Firepit is an eatery in Orgrimmar, the Horde capital. It serves hearty cooked meats to locals and travelers alike.",al={"borstans firepit","where is borstan"}},{q="Why does Borstan want thicket raptor meat?",a="Borstan wants a grander take on familiar flavors than Zamja's homestyle raptor cooking. A hunter's tale of giant talons inspires him to seek thicket raptor meat from the Wetlands.",al={"why raptor meat","why thicket raptor"}},{q="Where are the thicket raptors located?",a="Thicket raptors live at excavation sites in the Wetlands, an Alliance-leaning region across the Great Sea in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"where do i find thicket raptors","wetlands excavation sites raptor"}},{q="Who gives the quest Changing Tastes?",a="Borstan, proprietor of Borstan's Firepit in Orgrimmar, offers Changing Tastes after Zamja's advice and a trophy brought back by an adventurous hunter inspire him.",al={"who gives changing tastes","borstan questgiver"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Borstan hopes to raise rustic meals to a grander height for the Horde capital, as tastes and tavern menus evolve beyond village staples.",["faction:Horde"]="Supplying Borstan's Firepit means crossing the sea into contested Wetlands to bring exotic meat back to Orgrimmar."},kw={"borstan","borstan's firepit","changing tastes","excavation sites","orgrimmar","thicket raptor","thicket raptor meat","wetlands","zamja"},rel={"npc:zamja","zone:orgrimmar","zone:wetlands","topic:hunter"},m={id=95697},src={"In-game quest text: Changing Tastes"},fv=true}
E["quest:95737"] = {t="quest",n="Seeking Caitlin",z="general",s="In Menethil Harbor, an alchemist waits for word about a rare grass. She asks you to find Caitlin Grassman, whose husband has not reported back from an excavation.",h="In Menethil Harbor, an alchemist waits for word about a rare grass.",sec={{t="A Missing Report",b="The alchemist heard from Caitlin that her husband's excavation team found signs of a primordial grass. Caitlin does not know how much they found or whether it can be reached.\n\nNow the alchemist asks you to speak with Caitlin in Menethil Harbor. Both women are waiting to learn what happened to the promised seeds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Caitlin Grassman?",a="Caitlin Grassman is an alchemist in Menethil Harbor. Her husband joined an excavation team that found signs of a primordial grass.",al={"Caitlin","who is Caitlin","Caitlin Grassman"}},{q="What does the alchemist want me to do?",a="The alchemist asks you to speak with Caitlin Grassman in Menethil Harbor.",al={"find Caitlin","talk to Caitlin","Caitlin quest"}},{q="What did the excavation team find?",a="The team found signs of a long-lost primordial grass. How much there is and whether it can be reached remain unknown.",al={"primordial grass","rare grass","excavation find"}},{q="Why are they concerned?",a="Caitlin promised the alchemist that her husband would bring back grass seeds, but none have arrived. Both women suspect trouble at the excavation site.",al={"where are the seeds","missing grass seeds","why worried"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="As an herbalist, you are asked to check on Caitlin after her husband's team found signs of a rare primordial grass."},kw={"alchemist","caitlin grassman","excavation","grass seeds","menethil harbor","primordial grass","seeking caitlin","wetlands"},rel={"zone:wetlands","subzone:menethil-harbor"},m={id=95737},src={"In-game quest text: Seeking Caitlin"},fv=true}
E["quest:95772"] = {t="quest",n="Songblade Search",z="general",s="Songblade Search is a dwarven errand in which Dorin Songblade asks travelers to check on his brother Daewyn in the Wetlands. Daewyn set off with an archaeological crew from Loch Modan to dig in unexplored hills, but his uncharacteristic silence left his brother fearing the worst.",h="Songblade Search is a dwarven errand in which Dorin Songblade asks travelers to check on his brother Daewyn...",sec={{t="A Brother's Unease",b="Dorin Songblade remained behind at his workshop, inundated with orders to refit girdles, while his brother Daewyn joined a dwarven excavation crew setting out from Loch Modan. Daewyn ventured north into the rugged hills of the Wetlands, eager to explore virgin ground untouched by dwarven pickaxes. However, Daewyn customarily sends letters immediately after arriving, and his silence left Dorin deeply unsettled.",sp=0},{t="The Dig in the Wetlands",b="Travelers journeying into the Wetlands can locate Daewyn Songblade among the diggers at Whelgar's Excavation Site. Resembling his brother Dorin closely, Daewyn carries a distinctive rifle marked with the initials 'DLS' carved into the wooden stock, proving that he did reach the remote excavation camp in the marshlands.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Songblade Search?",a="Dorin Songblade gives the quest. He is an artisan currently overwhelmed with work refitting a pile of girdles, which kept him from accompanying his brother on an archaeological dig.",al={"who gives songblade search","songblade search quest giver","dorin songblade"}},{q="Who is Daewyn Songblade?",a="Daewyn Songblade is Dorin Songblade's brother. He departed alongside an excavation crew out of Loch Modan bound for the untamed hills of the Wetlands, but failed to send word back as he typically does.",al={"who is dorin brother","missing songblade dwarf","daewyn songblade"}},{q="Where can Daewyn be found?",a="Daewyn is located at Whelgar's Excavation Site in the Wetlands. He can be identified by his strong familial resemblance to Dorin and his firearm, which has 'DLS' carved into its wooden stock.",al={"where is daewyn","where to find daewyn songblade","songblade search location"}},{q="Why did Daewyn travel to the Wetlands?",a="Daewyn joined a dwarven digging expedition out of Loch Modan seeking uncharted hills in the Wetlands to conduct an archaeological excavation.",al={"why did daewyn go to wetlands","whelgars excavation purpose","why did the dwarfs go to wetlands"}},{q="What do the initials carved on the gun mean?",a="The initials 'DLS' carved into the firearm stock belong to Daewyn Songblade, confirming the dwarf found at the excavation site is indeed Dorin's missing brother.",al={"what does dls stand for","dls gun carving","dls initials"}},{q="Why was Dorin worried about his brother?",a="Dorin grew concerned because Daewyn usually writes back promptly after setting off on a journey. Having received no letters following the departure to the Wetlands, Dorin felt an uneasy premonition that something had gone wrong.",al={"why is dorin worried","why look for daewyn","songblade search reason"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven expedition crews and archaeological digs are common across Khaz Modan, but venturing beyond the safety of Loch Modan into the dangerous Wetlands often carries severe risks for kin.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance travelers venturing between Loch Modan and Menethil Harbor often pass through the excavation sites, lending aid to stranded dwarven prospectors and artisans."},kw={"daewyn songblade","dls","dorin songblade","loch modan","songblade search","wetlands","whelgar's excavation site"},rel={"subzone:whelgar-s-excavation-site","zone:wetlands","zone:lochmodan","topic:dwarf"},m={id=95772},src={"In-game quest text: Songblade Search"},fv=true}
E["quest:95774"] = {t="quest",n="Her Name Is Olgra",z="general",s="Mankrik mourns Olgra and prepares a monument to keep her name alive. He asks you to seek her missing bone adornments among the nearby Razormane raiders.",h="Mankrik mourns Olgra and prepares a monument to keep her name alive.",sec={{t="Honoring the Fallen",b="A healer saved Mankrik, but Olgra died from her wounds on the battlefield. Grieving, Mankrik prepares a lasting tribute so her name and sacrifice will be remembered.",sp=0},{t="Lost Keepsakes",b="Olgra wore cherished ornaments made of polished bone, but they are missing from her resting place. Mankrik asks you to recover them from the nearby Razormane raiders, so they can rest beside the monument he is raising for her.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Olgra?",a="Olgra fell in battle against the quilboar. The healer who saved Mankrik could not save her, and he intends to honor her memory forever.",al={"who is olgra","mankrik wife","who died"}},{q="What are Olgra's adornments?",a="Olgra's adornments are cherished pieces of jewelry made from polished bone. She wore them before she was slain.",al={"what are olgras adornments","olgra jewels","what are the adornments made of"}},{q="Why are the Razormane raiders being hunted?",a="The Razormane raiders have Olgra's ornaments. Mankrik sends you to recover them from the raiders near the place where she died.",al={"why kill razormane raiders","who stole olgras adornments","where are the adornments"}},{q="What will Mankrik do with the recovered adornments?",a="Mankrik plans to place the recovered bone jewelry beside the monument he is building for Olgra, so her memory will endure.",al={"mankrik memorial","what will mankrik do with the adornments","why does mankrik want the adornments"}}},kw={"adornments","mankrik","memorial","olgra","polished bone","quilboar","razormane raiders"},rel={"npc:mankrik","topic:quilboar"},m={id=95774},src={"In-game quest text: Her Name Is Olgra"},fv=true}
E["quest:95795"] = {t="quest",n="Fallen in the Fen",z="general",s="Fallen in the Fen is a somber journey begun by uncovering a fallen dwarf amidst an excavation site. Delivering the tragic news to his brother, Dorin Songblade in Lakeshire, brings closure to a heartbroken craftsman who had been forging gifts for his departed kin.",h="Fallen in the Fen is a somber journey begun by uncovering a fallen dwarf amidst an excavation site.",sec={{t="Tragedy at the Dig",b="A quiet tragedy unfolds at the excavation sites, where the lifeless body of an adventurous dwarf is discovered. After covering the remains with the dwarf's own cloak, an adventurer carries the grim news back to Lakeshire to find his kin.",sp=0},{t="A Brother's Grief",b="In Lakeshire, the dwarven craftsman Dorin Songblade breaks down upon learning his brother's fate. Dorin had spent weeks working at his forge, crafting a surprise set of gear for his brother that was not ready in time for the expedition. Stricken with regret for not going along, Dorin can neither bear to reforge nor sell what he crafted with his own hands, choosing instead to bestow the finished pieces upon the one who brought him closure.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Fallen in the Fen?",a="Fallen in the Fen is a somber task begun upon discovering a fallen dwarf's remains within an excavation site. After covering the body with his cloak, you journey to Lakeshire to personally inform the dwarf's grieving brother, Dorin Songblade, of his fate.",al={"what is fallen in the fen","fallen in the fen quest"}},{q="Who is Dorin Songblade?",a="Dorin Songblade is a dwarven smith residing in Lakeshire. He had been forging gear as a surprise gift for his brother before learning of the tragic death at the excavation sites.",al={"who is dorin songblade","dorin in lakeshire","dorin songblade role"}},{q="Who is the fallen dwarf found at the excavation?",a="The fallen dwarf discovered at the excavation sites was the brother of Dorin Songblade. He had recently passed through Lakeshire gathering supplies for his expedition before meeting his demise in the field.",al={"who died in the fen","dead dwarf excavation","whose body is in the excavation"}},{q="Where can Dorin Songblade be found?",a="Dorin Songblade can be found in Lakeshire, the primary settlement in Redridge Mountains, where he works his craft at the forge.",al={"where is dorin songblade","dorin songblade location"}},{q="What was Dorin forging for his brother?",a="Dorin was busy forging equipment intended as a surprise gift for his brother. Regrettably, the work was not finished before his brother set out on his expedition, leaving Dorin devastated with gear he cannot bear to keep or sell.",al={"what was dorin forging","dorin surprise gift"}},{q="Why does Dorin give away the items he crafted?",a="Overcome with grief and regret that he did not accompany his brother, Dorin has no desire to reforge or sell the arms he labored over. He parts with the finished work as gratitude to the traveler who brought him news of his kin.",al={"why did dorin give away the gear","why does dorin give you equipment"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven kin understand the bitter grief of losing a brother to ancient digs and untamed wilderness, especially when crafted metal outlives the hand meant to hold it.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dorin poured his craftsmanship into armaments intended as a surprise for his kin, crafting gear that now serves only as a reminder of his brother's untimely demise.",["faction:Alliance"]="Bringing closure to an artisan in Redridge provides solace to a grieving town resident during troubled times for the kingdom."},kw={"blacksmith","dorin","dorin songblade","dwarf","excavation","excavation sites","fallen in the fen","lakeshire","songblade"},rel={"subzone:lakeshire","topic:dwarf"},m={id=95795},src={"In-game quest text: Fallen in the Fen"},fv=true}
E["quest:95803"] = {t="quest",n="A Token of Good Faith",z="general",s="Danitha sends you to carry Rudolph Gelhardt’s head to Sylvanas Windrunner in the Undercity. The delivery is meant to show the Forsaken that your people are devoted to their prosperity.",h="Danitha sends you to carry Rudolph Gelhardt’s head to Sylvanas Windrunner in the Undercity.",sec={{t="A Show of Loyalty",b="Danitha says Sylvanas distrusts your people and openly disparages them. She asks you to bring Rudolph Gelhardt’s head as proof that you tend to your own house and stand with the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="The Dark Lady’s Answer",b="Sylvanas doubts Danitha’s order can stop more of its members from turning the Light against their own people. She warns that she may no longer tolerate the order if it stops serving the Forsaken’s interests. The order will be watched.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who asks me to bring Rudolph Gelhardt’s head to Sylvanas?",a="Danitha asks you to carry Rudolph Gelhardt’s head to Sylvanas Windrunner in the Undercity.",al={"who sent me to Sylvanas","Danitha head delivery","head to the Dark Lady"}},{q="Why does Danitha want me to bring the head to Sylvanas?",a="Danitha wants the delivery to show Sylvanas that your people tend to their own house and are devoted to the prosperity of the Forsaken.",al={"why bring Rudolph's head","what is this delivery for","prove loyalty"}},{q="Why does Sylvanas mistrust Danitha’s people?",a="Danitha says Sylvanas greatly mistrusts your people and nearly openly disparages them.",al={"why Sylvanas distrusts us","Dark Lady mistrust","why does she disparage us"}},{q="Where do I take Rudolph Gelhardt’s head?",a="You are asked to bring Rudolph Gelhardt’s head to Sylvanas Windrunner in the Undercity.",al={"where is Sylvanas","head delivery location","take it to Undercity"}}},kw={"a token of good faith","danitha","dark lady","forsaken","light","rudolph gelhardt","sylvanas windrunner","undercity"},rel={"quest:91317","zone:undercity","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:forsaken","topic:light"},m={id=95803},src={"In-game quest text: A Token of Good Faith"},fv=true}
E["quest:95805"] = {t="quest",n="Grace of An'she and Mu'sha",z="general",s="Grace of An'she and Mu'sha is a sacred tauren pilgrimage undertaken on Red Cloud Mesa to honor the sun and moon as the celestial eyes of the Earthmother. By carrying fragile, smoldering incense to an elevated shrine in the southeastern hills, travelers show respect to the heavenly lights that bring warmth, life, and direction to the plains.",h="Grace of An'she and Mu'sha is a sacred tauren pilgrimage undertaken on Red Cloud Mesa to honor the sun and...",sec={{t="The Eyes of the Earthmother",b="In tauren myth, the sun and the moon are known as An'she and Mu'sha, revered as the twin eyes of the Earthmother. While the elemental spirits dwell directly within the stones, winds, and waters of Azeroth, these celestial twins watch over the world from the heights of heaven. Their constant journey across the sky marks the eternal rhythm of day and night, granting warmth, guidance, and hope to all living creatures. Honoring the twins is an essential spiritual duty, teaching that balance requires reverence not only for the land beneath one's feet, but also for the light shining down from the sky.",sp=0},{t="An Offering on the Heights",b="To honor the celestial twins, a pilgrim must carry smoldering incense into the southeastern hills overlooking the mesa, nestled just south of the prickly quilboar grounds. The journey is an exercise in attentiveness and speed: the fragrant embers must be delivered before they burn away to cold ash, and travelers must take care to keep them away from water along the trail. Upon reaching the elevated shrine, the pilgrim casts the embers into the sacred fire, letting the aromatic smoke carry their respect up into the sky toward the sun and moon.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are An'she and Mu'sha?",a="An'she and Mu'sha are the sun and the moon in tauren mythology, revered as the twin eyes of the Earthmother. Unlike the spirits of earth, air, fire, and water, they do not dwell directly within the soil of Azeroth. Instead, they illuminate the world from the firmament above, with An'she offering warmth and life, while Mu'sha provides nocturnal guidance and solace.",al={"who are anshe and musha","anshe and musha lore","tauren sun and moon gods"}},{q="Why do the tauren make offerings to the sun and moon?",a="Tauren philosophy teaches that balance is essential to all existence. While earthly spirits govern the immediate landscape, the light of An'she and Mu'sha gives vital energy, vision, and hope to all living creatures. Offering smoldering incense at high places is an act of reciprocal respect, acknowledging that mortal life cannot thrive without the continuous benevolence of both celestial bodies.",al={"why revere the sun and moon","tauren celestial worship","why honor anshe and musha"}},{q="Where is the shrine of An'she and Mu'sha located?",a="The shrine of An'she and Mu'sha is situated in the high hills to the southeast of the mesa. It overlooks the broad plains below and lies just south of the thorny quilboar territories. Its high elevation provides a clear, unobstructed view of the open sky, making it an ideal place to lift smoke toward the heavens.",al={"where is the anshe shrine","shrine location mulgore","how to find the incense shrine"}},{q="What is the significance of the smoldering incense?",a="The smoldering incense acts as a physical conduit of prayer and respect. As its fragrant embers burn, the rising smoke carries the traveler's devotion directly upward into the sky toward the sun and moon. Casting the embers into the shrine fire concludes the ritual, rewarding the pilgrim with a tranquil sense of spiritual fulfillment.",al={"what does the incense do","meaning of the incense offering","why burn smoldering incense"}},{q="Why must the incense be delivered quickly and kept dry?",a="The ritual demands vigilance, speed, and care from the pilgrim. The incense must still be smoldering when cast into the sacred fire at the shrine, meaning the journey must be completed before the embers burn out into cold ash. Furthermore, immersion in water will immediately extinguish the embers, spoiling the offering before it can reach the heights.",al={"why is the incense timed","dont get it wet","incense burning down"}},{q="Do the quilboar threaten the pilgrimage to the shrine?",a="Quilboar tribes occupy heavily defended thorn dens in the hills just north of the shrine's location. While the shrine itself rests in a quiet overlook south of their grounds, travelers must be careful not to stumble into quilboar brambles, as hostile attacks could easily delay or disrupt the delicate journey.",al={"quilboar near the shrine","are quilboar a threat to pilgrims","quilboar south of the mesa"}},{q="How does Mu'sha compare to other cultures' views of the moon?",a="Tauren myth recognizes the moon as Mu'sha, the loving left eye of the Earthmother who gazes down upon mortal life. Other races on Azeroth, most notably the night elves, revere the same luminous white moon as the goddess Elune, but tauren tradition views her as inextricably bound to her solar twin, An'she.",al={"is musha elune","tauren moon versus elune","musha and the night elves"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Reverence for An'she and Mu'sha lies at the core of tauren spiritual identity, reminding young braves that life requires honoring the twin celestial lights as faithfully as the Earthmother herself.",["class:Druid"]="Tauren druids look to the eternal dance of sun and moon as the primal embodiment of balance, guiding their understanding of nature's cosmic rhythms.",["class:Shaman"]="While shaman speak directly with the worldly spirits of earth, fire, water, and air, they pay equal homage to the celestial twins whose light sustains all living energy.",["faction:Horde"]="The sacred rites performed on the mesa showcase the deep, nature-revering culture that the nomadic tauren bring to their allies within the Horde."},kw={"an'she","earthmother","moon","mu'sha","mulgore","red cloud mesa","shrine","smoldering incense","sun","tauren"},rel={"topic:light","topic:quilboar"},m={id=95805},src={"In-game quest text: Grace of An'she and Mu'sha"},fv=true}
E["quest:95809"] = {t="quest",n="Heartwoven",z="general",s="Heartwoven is an Alliance quest in the Wetlands centered on a simple message of survival and affection. After clearing an escape route for a stranded gatherer in the marshes, adventurers carry a hand-braided reed heart back to Caitlin Grassman in Menethil Harbor.",h="Heartwoven is an Alliance quest in the Wetlands centered on a simple message of survival and affection.",sec={{t="A Signal from the Marshes",b="Out in the treacherous marshes of the Wetlands, life and labor carry constant peril. Ardin Grassman, who went out to see the strange grasses at the excavation sites, found himself stranded amidst the swamp's dangers until a passing traveler cleared a path through the wilderness. Exhausted but determined, he pauses to catch his breath and secure his harvest before heading back toward the safety of the port.",sp=0},{t="Tokens of Menethil",b="Before making the trek home, the gatherer takes marsh reeds from his pack and braids them into a woven heart. He entrusts this token to the adventurer to carry ahead to Caitlin Grassman in Menethil Harbor. In the bustling coastal settlement, Caitlin waits in deep worry, clutching the woven reed heart to her chest in relief upon hearing that he survived and will soon return for a warm meal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Heartwoven?",a="Heartwoven is an Alliance quest based in the Wetlands. An adventurer aids Ardin Grassman, stranded at the excavation sites out in the marshes, who crafts a woven token of reeds as a message of survival and love to deliver to Caitlin Grassman in Menethil Harbor.",al={"heartwoven quest","what is heartwoven","wetlands heartwoven"}},{q="Who is Caitlin Grassman?",a="Caitlin Grassman is an Alliance resident waiting anxiously in Menethil Harbor. She fears for the life of her husband, Ardin, who went out to the excavation sites amid the hazards of the Wetlands, and is profoundly relieved when you bring her word of his survival.",al={"who is caitlin grassman","caitlin menethil harbor","caitlin grassman"}},{q="What is the reed-woven heart?",a="The reed-woven heart is a handcrafted token fashioned by the stranded gatherer from the marsh reeds in his pack. He braids it quickly to serve as proof and reassurance that he is alive and returning home soon.",al={"what is the reed heart","heart woven from reeds","reed woven heart"}},{q="Where do you turn in Heartwoven?",a="The quest concludes with Caitlin Grassman, who is waiting in the Alliance port town of Menethil Harbor on the western coast of the Wetlands.",al={"where to turn in heartwoven","heartwoven turn in location","where is caitlin grassman"}},{q="Who gives you the quest Heartwoven?",a="The quest is offered by Ardin Grassman, Caitlin's husband, who survived danger at the excavation sites out in the Wetlands. After an adventurer clears a safe path for him, he rests, gathers his bushels, and sends the adventurer ahead with a handmade token for Caitlin.",al={"who gives heartwoven","who crafts the heart","the survivor heartwoven"}},{q="What happened to the stranded gatherer?",a="Ardin went to the excavation sites to see strange towering grasses and was trapped when agitated wildlife, spiders above all, overwhelmed the excavation team. Thanks to an adventurer clearing a path through the surrounding threats, he survived and was able to safely gather his bushels and head back to Menethil Harbor.",al={"what happened to the gatherer","is he alive","heartwoven survivor"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Menethil Harbor serves as a critical Alliance port in the Wetlands, where everyday civilians rely on travelers to keep the perilous marsh routes safe.",["profession:Cooking"]="Upon receiving the reed heart and learning her partner survived, Caitlin immediately sets out to prepare a warm, hearty meal to welcome him home."},kw={"caitlin grassman","grassman","heartwoven","menethil harbor","reed heart","reeds","wetlands"},rel={"subzone:menethil-harbor","topic:light"},m={id=95809},src={"In-game quest text: Heartwoven"},fv=true}
E["quest:95810"] = {t="quest",n="Lost Relic Carry",z="general",s="Lost Relic Carry involves recovering a fractured piece of an ancient rock-mech automaton and delivering it to Prospector Whelgar in the Wetlands. The relic's unknown composite material hints at the sophisticated engineering of the Titans, earning serious reverence from dwarven researchers.",h="Lost Relic Carry involves recovering a fractured piece of an ancient rock-mech automaton and delivering it to...",sec={{t="A Fragment of Ancient Craft",b="Deep within ancient grounds, adventurers can uncover a fractured component of a rock-mech automaton. Far from being simple rubble, its unusual composite material reveals the craftsmanship of an advanced, ancient mechanism tied to the Titans.",sp=0},{t="Delivery to the Excavation",b="Prospector Whelgar, stationed at the Wetlands excavation site, studies remnants of antiquity. Carrying the relic out of danger and delivering it to him provides valuable physical evidence for dwarven scholars probing into their ancient heritage.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Titan Relic?",a="The relic is a fractured piece of an ancient rock-mech automaton. Rather than ordinary stone debris, it consists of an unknown composite material that clearly functioned as part of an intricate mechanical device.",al={"what is the relic","what is the item","rock mech piece"}},{q="Who is Prospector Whelgar?",a="Prospector Whelgar is a dwarven researcher overseeing the excavation site in the Wetlands. He studies ancient earthen remains and relics uncovered from the dig sites across the region.",al={"who is whelgar","whelgar lore","excavation lead"}},{q="Where does the relic need to be delivered?",a="The relic is brought to Prospector Whelgar at his excavation site located in the Wetlands, where dwarven excavators investigate ancient ruins.",al={"where do i bring it","where is the turn in","whelgar location"}},{q="What created the rock-mech automaton?",a="The automaton fragment is identified as a Titan relic. In dwarven lore, ancient mechanical constructs and stone automatons are tied to the enigmatic Titans who shaped the world.",al={"who made the rock mech","automaton origins","titan connection"}},{q="Why does Prospector Whelgar treat the relic with reverence?",a="Venturing into the depths where these automatons reside is extremely hazardous. Whelgar notes that surviving the delve and returning with such a piece intact commands genuine respect.",al={"why is whelgar impressed","perilous delve"}},{q="What is unusual about the automaton's material?",a="The material is a unique composite with an unfamiliar texture unlike typical stone or forged metal, demonstrating the sophisticated and mysterious craft of ancient Titan constructs.",al={"what is the material","strange composite"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven explorers of the Explorers' League are obsessed with unearthing ancient relics of their makers, making this automaton fragment a prized find for dwarven heritage.",["profession:Engineering"]="The composite material and intricate design of the rock-mech automaton hint at advanced mechanical principles far beyond standard mortal engineering."},kw={"automaton","excavation site","explorers league","lost relic carry","prospector whelgar","rock-mech","titan relic","wetlands"},rel={"npc:prospector-whelgar","zone:wetlands","topic:titan","subzone:whelgar-s-excavation-site"},m={id=95810},src={"In-game quest text: Lost Relic Carry"},fv=true}
E["quest:95883"] = {t="quest",n="The Tortured Soul",z="general",s="At Beren's Peril in Silverpine Forest, a dark rite waits unfinished. An abandoned note names the last offerings still needed: blood and a soul marked by suffering.",h="At Beren's Peril in Silverpine Forest, a dark rite waits unfinished.",sec={{t="A Hasty Rite",b="At Beren's Peril in Silverpine Forest, an altar bears the remains of a hastily assembled dark rite. A scrawled note lies among the scattered materials. Most of the ingredients are already gathered, but the rite still calls for blood and a Tortured Soul.",sp=0},{t="Foul Energies Unleashed",b="The creatures dwelling among Beren's Peril's crags endure great agony, making them fit sources for the rite's needed essence. Bring that soul to the ominous offering stone, and foul magical energy surges from it. The rite moves closer to fulfillment.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is The Tortured Soul?",a="The Tortured Soul tells of an unfinished dark rite at Beren's Peril in Silverpine Forest. An offering stone awaits blood and the soul of a tormented creature.",al={"what is the tortured soul quest","tortured soul silverpine","beren peril quest"}},{q="Where does this ritual take place?",a="The ritual altar and its offering stone are at Beren's Peril, a perilous mountainous enclave in Silverpine Forest.",al={"where is the ritual","where is beren's peril","offering stone location"}},{q="What components are required for the ritual?",a="A scrap of note at the altar says that nearly all the materials are gathered. The two still needed are blood and a Tortured Soul.",al={"what is needed for ritual","ritual components berens peril"}},{q="Where do you find the Tortured Soul?",a="Nearby creatures dwelling among Beren's Peril's crags are tortured enough to yield the essence the offering stone needs.",al={"where to get tortured soul","who has the soul"}},{q="What happens when the Tortured Soul is brought to the altar?",a="Bringing the tortured soul to the offering stone releases foul energy and brings the dark rite closer to completion.",al={"what happens when you bring soul","turn in tortured soul"}},{q="Who created the ritual at Beren's Peril?",a="No creator is named. The note and hurriedly gathered materials suggest someone assembled the altar in haste among Beren's Peril's hostile denizens.",al={"who made the ritual","who wrote the note"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="Channeling tormented souls and preparing dark rites at ancient offering stones reflect the grim, manipulative nature of demonic and shadow magic.",["race:Undead"]="Silverpine Forest is Forsaken territory, where dark magic and lingering suffering threaten the borders of Lordaeron.",["class:Priest"]="The rite draws foul energy from suffering spirits, in stark opposition to the restorative principles of the Holy Light."},kw={"beren's peril","foul energy","offering of blood","offering stone","ritual","silverpine forest","the tortured soul"},rel={"quest:95884","quest:95885","quest:516","zone:silverpine"},m={id=95883},src={"In-game quest text: The Tortured Soul"},fv=true}
E["quest:95884"] = {t="quest",n="The Offering Stone",z="general",s="In Beren's Peril, a ritual offering is being prepared at the Offering Stone. Fragments of a weathered note point travelers to the altar in Silverpine Forest.",h="In Beren's Peril, a ritual offering is being prepared at the Offering Stone.",sec={{t="A Tattered Note",b="A weathered scrap found in Silverpine Forest holds fragments about a ritual and an offering stone nearby. Time and moisture have worn away most of its words. Follow the clues into Beren's Peril, and you can find the crude altar in the wilderness.",sp=0},{t="The Ritual Site",b="At Beren's Peril, strange implements and bizarre items lie scattered across the rough stone. Someone has been using the site to prepare an offering or dark rite, far from prying eyes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Offering Stone?",a="The Offering Stone is a rough altar in Beren's Peril, Silverpine Forest. Strange implements and items scattered across it point to preparations for an offering or dark rite.",al={"what is offering stone","offering stone silverpine","what is this stone"}},{q="Where can the Offering Stone be found?",a="You can find the Offering Stone in Beren's Peril, in southern Silverpine Forest.",al={"where is offering stone","offering stone location","find offering stone"}},{q="Who created or uses the Offering Stone?",a="The damaged note does not name the ritualists. Tools and items left on the stone show that someone was preparing an offering or dark rite nearby.",al={"who made the offering stone","who uses offering stone","who is performing the ritual"}},{q="What does the mysterious note say?",a="Most of the note is weathered and illegible. The surviving fragments mention preparations for a ritual and point to an offering stone nearby.",al={"what note","scrap of note silverpine","what does the note say"}},{q="What is found on the Offering Stone?",a="Strange tools, ritual implements, and other items lie scattered across the rough stone, meant for an offering.",al={"what items are on the stone","what implements on stone","offering stone items"}},{q="What kind of ritual is being performed here?",a="The note does not fully describe the rite. The stone is prepared as an altar where items or sacrifices are laid out for a ritual.",al={"what ritual offering stone","why an offering stone","what offering silverpine"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde adventurer in Silverpine Forest, keep watch for suspicious rituals and cultist activity in the shadowed crags of Beren's Peril.",["class:Warlock"]="As a warlock, you may read the dark implements on the offering stone as signs of ritual practice, worth close study."},kw={"beren's peril","implements","offering stone","ritual","scrap of note","silverpine forest","the offering stone"},rel={"quest:516","zone:silverpine"},m={id=95884},src={"In-game quest text: The Offering Stone"},fv=true}
E["quest:95885"] = {t="quest",n="The Offering of Blood",z="general",s="At Beren's Peril, the scrap of note calls for blood at the Offering Stone. You bring a Tortured Soul to answer it.",h="At Beren's Peril, the scrap of note calls for blood at the Offering Stone.",sec={{t="The Final Rite",b="Deep in Beren's Peril, the Offering Stone waits for a final offering. Scraps of note call for a willing sacrifice, and lead you to bring blood to the altar. As you approach with the restless essence, dread closes in.",sp=0},{t="Cracking the Stone",b="The Tortured Soul draws the stone's lingering power away. The rite drains the ancient monument instead of waking a greater terror. Its rough surface cracks, then collapses into rubble. Something glittering lies among the shattered fragments.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where does this ritual take place?",a="The rite takes place at the Offering Stone in Beren's Peril, within Silverpine Forest. Peril and shadowed magic haunt the area.",al={"where is this quest","where is offering of blood","beren's peril quest"}},{q="What is the Offering Stone?",a="The Offering Stone is an ancient monument in Beren's Peril. It calls for blood from a willing sacrifice to unlock or drain the power within.",al={"what is the offering stone","offering stone berens peril","altar in berens peril"}},{q="What is the Tortured Soul used for?",a="A Tortured Soul is given at the Offering Stone to answer the scrap of note's call for a willing sacrifice.",al={"who is the tortured soul","what is tortured soul","what sacrifice is needed"}},{q="What happens when the offering is completed?",a="The rite drains the power that animates the Offering Stone. The rough stone cracks and crumbles, leaving something glittering among its remains.",al={"what happens to the stone","does the stone break","what happens after the ritual"}},{q="Who created the stone and why?",a="No one knows who made the Offering Stone or why. The rite drains its power and destroys it, but its maker's purpose remains unknown.",al={"who built the offering stone","purpose of offering of blood","why was stone made"}},{q="What is Beren's Peril?",a="Beren's Peril is a rocky landmark in Silverpine Forest, where wild beasts and hidden dangers lurk. Dark rites and restless souls have also left their mark there.",al={"what is berens peril","berens peril lore","why is it called berens peril"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="As a warlock, you know dark rites and bargains with souls. Even a seasoned practitioner of the fel may feel a chill at offering blood to an ancient, unknown stone.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you may find the use of a tortured soul in an ominous blood rite troubling, whatever its fate."},kw={"beren's peril","blood ritual","offering of blood","offering stone","sacrifice","silverpine forest","the offering of blood","tortured soul"},rel={"quest:95884","quest:95883","quest:516"},m={id=95885},src={"In-game quest text: The Offering of Blood"},fv=true}
E["quest:95981"] = {t="quest",n="Watching the Roads",z="general",s="Shadow Priest Allister sends the Forsaken against Kirin Tor patrols near Ambermill. With Dalaran's barrier down, their wizards and watchers guard the roads into Silverpine Forest while carrying magical goods into Forsaken lands.",h="Shadow Priest Allister sends the Forsaken against Kirin Tor patrols near Ambermill.",sec={{t="Tensions Along the Ambermill Road",b="Near Ambermill, Kirin Tor wizards and sentries watch the roads into Silverpine Forest. Since the barrier around Dalaran fell, they have tightened their hold on travel across the border. From the Sepulcher, the Forsaken see the human magi using these roads to carry dangerous relics and enchanted pendants into contested lands.",sp=0},{t="Severing the Supply Line",b="Shadow Priest Allister wants to stop the flow of Dalaran's magical goods at its source. He sends agents after the Kirin Tor escorts on the roads near Ambermill. By striking Dalaran Wizards and Dalaran Watchers, the Forsaken aim to break the Kirin Tor's hold on regional travel and guard their eastern frontier from outside arcane meddling.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Watching the Roads?",a="Shadow Priest Allister gives the order from the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. He sends champions against Kirin Tor patrols on the local roads.",al={"who gives watching the roads","quest giver watching the roads","where to turn in watching the roads"}},{q="Why are the Forsaken attacking Dalaran patrols near Ambermill?",a="Shadow Priest Allister wants to stop magical relics and pendants from reaching Silverpine Forest from Dalaran. Striking the wizards and watchers near Ambermill cuts off their passage across Forsaken borders.",al={"why kill dalaran wizards","why attack ambermill roads","kirin tor silverpine purpose"}},{q="What is the situation with the barrier around Dalaran?",a="The barrier around Dalaran has fallen. The Kirin Tor have tightened their watch on nearby roads and patrol the Ambermill region with suspicion.",al={"what happened to dalaran barrier","dalaran dome down","barrier down dalaran"}},{q="Where can the Dalaran Wizards and Watchers be found?",a="Dalaran Wizards and Dalaran Watchers patrol the roads around Ambermill in Silverpine Forest. They watch travel and guard Kirin Tor personnel and enchanted items.",al={"where are dalaran watchers","ambermill patrol locations","where to find dalaran wizards"}},{q="What kind of items is Dalaran shipping along these roads?",a="The Kirin Tor carry enchanted pendants and other magical relics from Dalaran into Silverpine Forest. Allister wants to stop them before the artifacts spread farther through the territory.",al={"what relics dalaran shipping","what are the pendants","ambermill pendants"}},{q="Why are the Kirin Tor so vigilant along the Ambermill roads?",a="The Kirin Tor watch the roads into Silverpine Forest closely since Dalaran's magical barrier fell. Allister sees their suspicion and vigilance, but the Forsaken at the Sepulcher do not know their exact motives.",al={"why is kirin tor paranoid","why are dalaran guards patrolling","kirin tor road control"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As an Undead, you help secure Silverpine's roads and defend Forsaken borders from Dalaran's magical incursions.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you help disrupt Dalaran's supply lines near Ambermill and hold the region against hostile human enclaves.",["class:Priest"]="As a Priest, you see Shadow Priest Allister direct the offensive from the Sepulcher, where Forsaken clergy take an active part in border defense.",["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, you face Dalaran's wizards and watchers, who use magical relics and patrols to guard passage. Their work makes them targets for rival spellcasters."},kw={"ambermill","dalaran watcher","dalaran wizard","kirin tor","pendants","relics","sepulcher","shadow priest allister","silverpine forest","watching the roads"},rel={"npc:shadow-priest-allister","subzone:ambermill","subzone:sepulcher","zone:silverpine","subzone:dalaran","topic:priest"},m={id=95981},src={"In-game quest text: Watching the Roads"},fv=true}
E["quest:95998"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival quest where the traveler Sam teaches fledgling adventurers the art of roughing it in the wild. By pausing to rest beside a basic campfire, travelers learn how to sustain themselves on the road before pursuing specialized tradeskills.",h="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival quest where the traveler Sam teaches fledgling...",sec={{t="Lessons by the Fire",b="The wide lands of Azeroth call to countless restless souls seeking fortune, danger, and renown. Yet raw ambition alone cannot shield a fledgling adventurer from the biting cold or exhaustion of the open road. Mastering basic wilderness craft—knowing when to stop, build a fire, and gather one's strength—is the quiet foundation upon which every legendary journey is built. Sam's invitation offers newcomers a chance to pause and learn these essential outdoor survival habits.",sp=0},{t="Fieldcraft and Trade",b="Taking shelter beside a warm campfire does more than stave off the night chill; it provides a vital moment of recuperation known as boosted rest. Seasoned survivalists recognize that endurance in the wild depends on resting properly between expeditions. Once these survival basics are understood, craftsmen and gatherers can turn their attention to tradeskills, learning how to assemble dedicated camp fixtures tailored to their specific work out in the field.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sam?",a="Sam is an experienced outdoorsman and seasoned traveler who takes it upon himself to teach fledgling adventurers the fundamentals of surviving in the wilds. He welcomes wandering souls to share the warmth of his fire while imparting practical outdoor wisdom.",al={"who is sam","sam campfire quest","quest giver"}},{q="What is the purpose of sitting by the campfire?",a="Boosted Rest represents the rejuvenating effect of resting beside a proper campfire out in the wilderness. Taking time to sit in warmth and comfort allows weary adventurers to recuperate their vitality and stay refreshed for the road ahead.",al={"what is boosted rest","boosted rest buff","resting by fire"}},{q="Why are wilderness skills important for adventurers?",a="The world outside settlement walls is vast and perilous, demanding that any wanderer seeking fortune and glory learn how to sustain themselves. Proper fieldcraft ensures travelers can endure long journeys without succumbing to fatigue.",al={"why learn outdoorsman skills","why thrive in wilds","survival skills"}},{q="How do tradeskills relate to wilderness camping?",a="After learning how to establish a basic camp, tradeskill masters can teach you how to craft specialized camp fixtures tailored to your chosen profession, enhancing your capabilities while stationed out in the field.",al={"tradeskills camping","camp fixtures","professions after this"}},{q="What should an adventurer do after completing this lesson?",a="Once you have rested by Sam's fire and grasped basic campcraft, Sam urges you to seek out profession trainers. Advancing in a practical craft enables you to build customized field stations suited to your trade.",al={"next steps","what after great outdoors","after campfire quest"}},{q="Who can undertake The Great Outdoors?",a="The quest is open to any restless soul setting out into the wider world, regardless of background or allegiance, serving as an introductory lesson in surviving life on the road.",al={"can anyone do this","who can take great outdoors","is this quest general"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Campfires are a staple of field cooking, and learning proper wilderness campcraft provides the foundation for preparing meals under the open sky.",["class:Hunter"]="Living off the land and resting beside an open flame is second nature to wilderness stalkers, but even seasoned scouts benefit from mastering campcraft."},kw={"basic campfire","boosted rest","camp fixture","campcraft","outdoorsman","sam","the great outdoors","tradeskill"},m={id=95998},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96031"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Leatherworking",z="general",s="At a camp in the wilderness, leatherworkers put cured hides to use and make shelter of their own. A sound campsite needs a pitched tent and a fire, whose kindling is taught by cooking trainers.",h="At a camp in the wilderness, leatherworkers put cured hides to use and make shelter of their own.",sec={{t="Pitching Shelter in the Wild",b="For tradespeople who work with hides and leather, making a shelter of their own is an essential milestone. A sturdy leather tent shields travelers from harsh weather, wind and the chill of night on open ground. Townsfolk frown on tents pitched in crowded streets or market squares, so you need to pass beyond the city gates and into the wilderness to make camp.",sp=0},{t="The Warmth of a Campfire",b="A pitched tent is only half a campsite. A proper fire keeps back darkness and cold winds, and gives you a hearth in dangerous lands. Since cooking in the field begins with building and tending a fire, seek instruction from culinary masters and cooking trainers. They teach you to kindle a campfire before you establish camp.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can you pitch a camp tent?",a="Townsfolk frown on tents pitched in streets or city squares, so take your tent beyond the city gates and into the wilderness.",al={"camp tent use","where pitch tent","can i camp in town"}},{q="What else is required besides a tent to pitch a camp?",a="A tent alone is not a full camp. You also need a campfire burning beside it.",al={"need fire for camp","camp requirements","how to camp"}},{q="Where do adventurers learn to build a campfire?",a="Culinary masters and cooking trainers teach you to kindle a campfire as part of preparing food. Seek a cooking trainer to learn the basics.",al={"learn campfire","who teaches campfire","cooking fire"}},{q="How does leatherworking relate to crafting camp tents?",a="Leatherworkers use cured hides and pelts, joined with sturdy stitching, to make tent coverings that can withstand rough wilderness elements. This craft gives wanderers a reliable shelter from animal hides.",al={"camping 101 leatherworking","leather camp tent","tent crafting leatherworking"}},{q="Why do adventurers establish camps in the wilderness?",a="Wilderness camps give travelers a secure haven to rest and recuperate when they are far from settled outposts, inns and settlements. Portable shelter and a warm fire help against the elements and perilous terrain.",al={"why camp out","camping purpose","wilderness survival camp"}},{q="Why do townsfolk object to setting up tents inside town?",a="Townsfolk object because tents across public streets clutter thoroughfares and cause disturbances. Settlements have inns and private lodgings for travelers, so locals expect camps to remain in the wild.",al={"camping town rules","no tents in town","why cant i pitch tent in city"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="A campfire is the heart of a wilderness campsite. Basic cooking teaches you to kindle a flame and sustain a camp in the wild."},kw={"camp tent","campfire","camping","cooking","leatherworking","shelter","tent","wilderness"},m={id=96031},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Leatherworking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96044"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Blacksmithing",z="general",s="Smiths bound for the wilderness are sent to Kharanos to learn from Tognus Flintfire. He teaches them to make a portable sharpening wheel for keeping weapons keen at camp.",h="Smiths bound for the wilderness are sent to Kharanos to learn from Tognus Flintfire.",sec={{t="Field Maintenance for the Open Road",b="You are sent to Kharanos to learn how a smith can serve far from a town forge. There, skilled hands turn raw metal into tools for camp. A blade kept keen is ready for the long road.",sp=0},{t="The Master of Kharanos",b="In Kharanos, Tognus Flintfire teaches capable smiths to make a sharpening wheel. Set one beside your campsite and warriors and rogues can grind nicks from their weapons and hone dull edges before the next march.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tognus Flintfire?",a="Tognus Flintfire teaches blacksmithing in Kharanos, in Dun Morogh. He trains smiths who have shown skill in their trade, teaching them to make gear for campsites.",al={"who is tognus flintfire","where is tognus","tognus flintfire location"}},{q="What is a Sharpening Wheel?",a="A sharpening wheel is a portable tool made by blacksmiths. At camp, it gives warriors, rogues and other weapon bearers a place to hone their blades in the field.",al={"what does sharpening wheel do","sharpening wheel camp","sharpening wheel purpose"}},{q="Why do warriors and rogues care about the Sharpening Wheel?",a="Warriors and rogues depend on sharp weapons in battle. A sharpening wheel lets them tend their blades, axes and daggers while traveling far from town forges.",al={"who needs sharpening wheel","warriors sharpening wheel","rogues sharpening wheel"}},{q="Where is Kharanos?",a="Kharanos lies southwest of Ironforge in the snowy peaks of Dun Morogh. Travelers, miners and craftsmen use the settlement as a stopping place while crossing the mountain passes.",al={"where is kharanos","kharanos location","finding kharanos"}},{q="What is required to learn this camping item?",a="Show basic skill at the anvil, and Tognus Flintfire will teach you to make campsite gear. Then you can learn to forge the portable wheel.",al={"camping 101 blacksmithing requirement","when can i learn sharpening wheel","smithing camp training"}},{q="Why do blacksmiths craft camping items?",a="Blacksmiths make camping tools to help adventuring parties beyond settled towns. A portable sharpening wheel lets companions maintain their weapons under the open sky, without returning to a city forge.",al={"why craft camp items","blacksmithing camping items","smithing utility in wild"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="You can learn to make field tools such as the sharpening wheel, taking your craft beyond city forges to help allies in the wild.",["class:Warrior"]="Keen weapons matter in battle, and a sharpening wheel at camp helps you keep your edge on an expedition.",["class:Rogue"]="A sharp dagger is essential to your trade, so a camp sharpening wheel is welcome between field contracts.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you can find Kharanos in the snowy foothills of Dun Morogh. Its smiths keep cold steel sharp for the road."},kw={"blacksmithing","blades","camp","camping 101","dun morogh","kharanos","sharpening wheel","tognus flintfire","weapons"},rel={"subzone:kharanos","topic:warrior","topic:rogue"},m={id=96044},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Blacksmithing"},fv=true}
E["quest:96045"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Alchemy",z="general",s="In Ironforge, Vosur Brakthel shows alchemists how to prepare for the road. With a Mana Well, they can give spellcasters and healers a place to recover while traveling.",h="In Ironforge, Vosur Brakthel shows alchemists how to prepare for the road.",sec={{t="Brewing for the Road",b="Vosur Brakthel trains alchemists in Ironforge to carry their craft beyond the city. Brewing vials in a quiet laboratory is only part of the work. Out in Azeroth's wild places, alchemists can use what they know to make a Mana Well for a camp, helping companions endure the journey.",sp=0},{t="Wilderness Campsites",b="A Mana Well can refresh healers and spellcasters, but it belongs at a wilderness camp, not on an Ironforge street. City folk frown on tents pitched within their walls. A proper camp also needs a fire, so seek a cooking trainer's guidance before heading into the wild.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Vosur Brakthel?",a="Vosur Brakthel is an alchemy trainer in Ironforge. He teaches alchemists to use their craft to make a Mana Well for companions in the field.",al={"who teaches camping 101 alchemy","vosur brakthel role","who is vosur brakthel"}},{q="What is a Mana Well?",a="A Mana Well is an alchemical fixture for a wilderness camp. It helps spellcasters and healers recover as they travel.",al={"what does a mana well do","mana well purpose","why make a mana well"}},{q="Can you set up a camp inside Ironforge?",a="No. City folk frown on camps pitched in Ironforge's streets, so take your Mana Well beyond the city to the wilderness.",al={"can i camp in ironforge","camping in town","where to place mana well"}},{q="What else is needed to set up a camp?",a="A camp also needs a campfire. If you do not know how to make one, seek a cooking trainer before heading out.",al={"campfire requirement","do i need cooking for camping","how to start a camp"}},{q="When can an alchemist learn to make a Mana Well?",a="Vosur Brakthel teaches alchemists who have taken up the profession and shown some skill in it. With that experience, they can learn to make a Mana Well.",al={"when can i learn mana well","mana well requirements","camping 101 requirements"}},{q="Who benefits most from a Mana Well at camp?",a="Spellcasters and healers benefit most from a Mana Well. It helps them replenish their mana while traveling far from town taverns and hearthstones.",al={"who benefits from mana well","mana well classes","who uses mana well"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="A campsite needs a campfire, a practical skill taught by cooking trainers across the realms.",["faction:Alliance"]="In Ironforge, alchemists can seek Vosur Brakthel's guidance on preparing restorative concoctions for their journeys."},kw={"alchemy","campfire","camping","camping 101: alchemy","ironforge","mana well","vosur brakthel"},rel={"zone:ironforge"},m={id=96045},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Alchemy"},fv=true}
E["quest:96046"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="Out beyond the guarded roads, miners learn to make a lodestone for a wilderness camp. The work pairs knowledge of stone with the firecraft needed to live outdoors.",h="Out beyond the guarded roads, miners learn to make a lodestone for a wilderness camp.",sec={{t="Fieldcraft for Miners",b="Beyond guarded roads, a miner must rely on fieldcraft. Mineral knowledge gives you the means to shape a lodestone, the anchor for a campsite in the wild.",sp=0},{t="Rules of the Wild",b="A campsite belongs beyond town limits, where travelers can pitch their shelters without troubling settled folk. Choose a place in the open wilds. A camp also needs a fire to ward off the night chill. Learning to build one is part of the same fieldcraft as working stone.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a lodestone used for?",a="A lodestone anchors a campsite in the wilderness.",al={"lodestone purpose","what is lodestone for","why craft lodestone"}},{q="Can I set up camp inside a town or city?",a="Townspeople and settlement authorities frown on tents in public squares and streets. Set up camp beyond the town limits.",al={"can i camp in town","camping in city","camp inside settlement"}},{q="What is required before setting up a camp?",a="A camp needs a warm fire burning nearby.",al={"do i need campfire","how to make camp","camp prerequisites"}},{q="Where do I learn how to build a campfire?",a="Cooking instructors and chefs across Azeroth teach the basic fire-starting skills used in cooking.",al={"where learn campfire","who teaches campfire","campfire trainer"}},{q="How does mining connect to crafting a lodestone?",a="Mining teaches you to work the minerals and stone needed to make a lodestone.",al={"why miners camp","mining lodestone connection","lodestone craft"}},{q="Where should I go to test my new camp?",a="Go beyond the city gates into the open wilderness, where you can find room to camp away from townsfolk.",al={"where to test camp","testing lodestone","camp outdoors"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="As a miner, you learn to shape earth and ore into a lodestone that anchors a wilderness camp.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you learn the firecraft a camp needs from cooking trainers."},kw={"campfire","camping","camping 101: mining","campsite","cooking","lodestone","mining","wilderness"},m={id=96046},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:96050"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="Camping 101: Fishing guides outdoorsmen in the essential crafts and etiquette of wilderness survival. It teaches adventurers how to create a decorative Fish Bowl for their campsite and emphasizes the necessity of learning campfire craft from a cooking trainer before heading into the wild.",h="Camping 101: Fishing guides outdoorsmen in the essential crafts and etiquette of wilderness survival.",sec={{t="Wilderness Etiquette",b="Life on the road across Azeroth demands practical knowledge of the frontier. For aspiring anglers and wanderers, setting up an orderly site requires understanding basic outdoor etiquette. Townsfolk and guards frown upon travelers pitching tents and scattering gear in the center of settlements, so anyone wishing to rest must take their supplies out beyond city borders and into the open wilderness.",sp=0},{t="The Comforts of Camp",b="A proper expedition site requires several essential amenities. Above all, no campsite is considered functional without a warm campfire, a skill traditionally imparted by culinary instructors. Once a fire is sparked, seasoned travelers can lay out specialty accoutrements such as a handcrafted Fish Bowl, transforming a simple rest stop into a fully outfitted angler's haven.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do you learn from Camping 101: Fishing?",a="This lesson teaches adventurers how to craft a Fish Bowl to furnish their campsite. It covers the fundamentals of setting up a wilderness retreat suited for fishing and relaxing away from civilized settlements.",al={"what does this quest teach","fish bowl craft","camping fishing reward"}},{q="Where can you set up a camp?",a="Camps cannot be pitched in the middle of town, as townsfolk take issue with travelers sprawling out in public thoroughfares. You must venture out into the wilderness to set up your site.",al={"where pitch camp","can i camp in town","camping location rules"}},{q="What do you need before pitching a camp?",a="No wilderness camp is considered complete without a campfire. Before you begin pitching your camp, you must have a fire ready to anchor the site and provide warmth.",al={"how to start camp","do i need a campfire","camp requirements"}},{q="How do you learn to build a campfire?",a="Culinary instructors and cooking trainers teach the basic art of kindling a campfire. Those who have not yet learned the basics of cooking should seek out a cook to learn how to make one.",al={"how make campfire","where learn campfire","who teaches fire"}},{q="What is the purpose of the Fish Bowl?",a="The Fish Bowl is a decorative addition crafted for an outdoor campsite, providing an appropriate touch for any traveling angler looking to set up an outpost by the water.",al={"what is fish bowl for","camping fish bowl use","fish bowl lore"}},{q="Why are camping lessons taught to adventurers?",a="Wilderness mentors offer practical guidance on frontier living, ensuring wanderers know how to properly sustain themselves, build fires, and respect settlement boundaries before heading into the wild.",al={"who offers camping 101","why camping lessons","camping survival guide"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Learning the basics of cooking is essential for setting up camp, as culinary trainers teach travelers how to build the required campfire."},kw={"camp","campfire","camping","cooking","fish bowl","fishing","wilderness"},m={id=96050},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Fishing"},fv=true}
E["quest:96055"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="general",s="In Ironforge, herbalists can learn to make Incense Candles for life in camp. Reyna Stonebranch teaches the craft to gatherers ready to put wild herbs to practical use.",h="In Ironforge, herbalists can learn to make Incense Candles for life in camp.",sec={{t="Practical Field Botany",b="Gathering wild herbs can serve a camp as well as a traveling party. With skill, herbalists turn what they gather into useful aids for life outdoors.",sp=0},{t="Aromatics for the Hearth",b="In Ironforge, proficient herbalists can seek instruction from Reyna Stonebranch. Once they prove their skill, she teaches them to make an Incense Candle. Its soothing aroma lifts the spirits at camp and brings calm to spellcasters and healers on long expeditions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Herbalism?",a="Herbalists learn to put wild plants to practical use in camp. Reyna Stonebranch teaches the craft in Ironforge.",al={"what is camping 101 herbalism","herbalism camping quest","herbalism camp guide"}},{q="Who is Reyna Stonebranch?",a="Reyna Stonebranch teaches proficient herbalists in Ironforge how to make Incense Candles for camp.",al={"who teaches incense candle","where is reyna stonebranch","reyna stonebranch location"}},{q="What does an Incense Candle do?",a="An Incense Candle brings a soothing aroma to camp and lifts the spirits of travelers.",al={"what does incense candle do","incense candle camping","incense candle purpose"}},{q="Why are Incense Candles useful to spellcasters?",a="Its soothing aroma helps spellcasters and healers find a calmer place during long expeditions.",al={"who benefits from incense candle","is incense candle for casters"}},{q="Where must herbalists travel for this instruction?",a="Herbalists travel to Ironforge to meet Reyna Stonebranch after proving their skill in gathering herbs.",al={"where do i go for camping herbalism","which city for camping 101 herbalism"}},{q="What is required before learning to craft the candle?",a="Herbalists must prove their skill in gathering herbs before Reyna Stonebranch teaches them to make Incense Candles.",al={"when can i learn incense candle","herbalism camping requirement"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="As an herbalist, you can make Incense Candles from gathered wild herbs to aid spellcasters at camp.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance herbalist, you can seek Reyna Stonebranch in Ironforge for instruction in fieldcraft.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you can find herbalism instructor Reyna Stonebranch in Ironforge, where she teaches how to make Incense Candles."},kw={"camp","camping 101","herbalism","herbs","incense candle","ironforge","reyna stonebranch"},rel={"zone:ironforge"},m={id=96055},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:96056"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers in the art of fashioning wilderness furnishings from harvested animal pelts. It directs skilled skinners to Balthus Stoneflayer in Ironforge to learn how to craft a comfortable Camp Chair for their campsite.",h="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers in the art of fashioning wilderness...",sec={{t="Harvesting the Wilds",b="Skinning fallen beasts is among the most practical disciplines an adventurer can cultivate while traversing the wilds of Azeroth. Beyond supplying raw leather for armorers and crafters, the hides gathered from predatory cats, bears, and beasts can be repurposed directly in the field. This introductory task directs skinners to transform raw materials into practical wilderness amenities rather than simply selling them off.",sp=0},{t="The Artisan of Ironforge",b="The quest sends skinning practitioners to seek out Balthus Stoneflayer in the dwarven capital of Ironforge. Balthus knows the secrets of turning prepared animal pelts into a functional Camp Chair. However, he demands that an adventurer first demonstrate clear proficiency in the art of skinning before he agrees to impart his crafting techniques.",sp=0},{t="Rest and Readiness",b="Life on the road takes a heavy physical toll on travelers and hunters alike. A sturdy Camp Chair offers an adventurer a chance to sit down and unwind by the fire, regaining their energy and sharp senses. Taking a moment to recuperate ensures that when the next dangerous dust-up begins, the adventurer is fighting at peak condition and can strike where it counts.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Skinning?",a="Camping 101: Skinning is an instructional quest that directs skilled skinners to seek out specialized camp crafting training in Ironforge. It teaches adventurers how to turn field-harvested animal hides into a comfortable Camp Chair for their personal campsites.",al={"what is camping 101 skinning","quest purpose","camping 101 skinning quest"}},{q="Who teaches you to craft the camping item?",a="The quest directs you to speak with Balthus Stoneflayer, a master craftsman residing in the mountain city of Ironforge. Once you have demonstrated sufficient proficiency in your gathering trade, he teaches you how to fashion specialized camp amenities.",al={"who to talk to","who teaches camp chair","balthus stoneflayer who"}},{q="Where do you need to go to complete this quest?",a="You must travel to Ironforge, the dwarven capital nestled in the snowy peaks of Dun Morogh. Balthus Stoneflayer can be found within the city, ready to instruct qualified skinners.",al={"where to go","balthus location","ironforge trainer"}},{q="What item do skinners learn to craft for their camp?",a="You learn to craft a Camp Chair out of harvested hides. Setting up a comfortable chair allows an adventurer to rest their legs in the field, helping them recover their focus and maintain peak physical condition between dangerous encounters.",al={"what do you make","camp chair purpose","skinning camp craft"}},{q="Why is crafting a camp chair useful for an adventurer?",a="Resting in a comfortable camp chair provides a vital respite during long treks across the wilderness. Taking time to relax ensures an adventurer remains in peak fighting shape and can strike with precision when combat inevitably breaks out.",al={"why make a chair","what does camp chair do","camp chair benefit"}},{q="What proficiency is required before Balthus will teach you?",a="Balthus Stoneflayer will only share his crafting knowledge once an adventurer has proven their proficiency in the skinning trade. You must demonstrate solid skill in skinning beasts before he will teach you the pattern.",al={"requirements","when can i learn","proficiency needed"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The trail to craft this field seat leads straight into the dwarven mountain city of Ironforge, making the Alliance capital a key stop for leather gatherers.",["race:Dwarf"]="Balthus Stoneflayer makes his home in Ironforge, offering skilled skinners the dwarven secret of turning raw hides into sturdy camp comforts.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters who live off the land and skin their prey in the wild benefit from setting up a proper campsite to rest between long tracks."},kw={"balthus stoneflayer","camp chair","camping","camping 101: skinning","hides","ironforge","skinning"},rel={"zone:ironforge"},m={id=96056},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:96101"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors is an introductory quest offered by the outdoorsman Raan, who teaches novice adventurers the fundamentals of wilderness survival. By resting beside a basic campfire to gain Boosted Rest, wanderers learn essential campcraft before venturing toward nearby trade trainers to learn practical professions.",h="The Great Outdoors is an introductory quest offered by the outdoorsman Raan, who teaches novice adventurers...",sec={{t="Lessons in the Wild",b="The Great Outdoors serves as an introductory lesson in surviving beyond the safety of settlement walls. Offered by an experienced wilderness traveler named Raan, the task reminds wandering adventurers that raw ambition and steel are useless without the practical campcraft needed to endure harsh elements. Raan welcomes aspiring wanderers into the ranks of restless souls seeking fortune and glory, offering practical guidance to those willing to learn.",sp=0},{t="The Warmth of the Hearth",b="The core requirement of the lesson is deceptively simple: taking a seat by Raan's basic campfire until the warmth grants the Boosted Rest effect. Out in the vast, untamed territories of Azeroth, knowing how to pause, set up camp, and recuperate can mean the difference between life and death. Once the basics of camping are mastered, Raan directs novices toward local village trainers to take up a trade and learn to craft specialized campsite fixtures.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest The Great Outdoors?",a="The quest is offered by Raan, an experienced traveler who recognizes potential in wandering adventurers and teaches them the fundamentals of surviving in the open wilderness.",al={"who offers the great outdoors","quest giver the great outdoors","raan campfire quest"}},{q="What is the purpose of The Great Outdoors?",a="It introduces adventurers to survival fundamentals in the wild, demonstrating how resting beside a basic campfire provides comfort, recuperation, and the boosted rest needed before undertaking perilous journeys.",al={"what does the great outdoors teach","the great outdoors purpose","what do i learn from raan"}},{q="How is the quest completed?",a="To complete the lesson, take a seat beside Raan's basic campfire and rest there until you receive the Boosted Rest buff, showing you understand how to recuperate in the field.",al={"how to complete the great outdoors","raan campfire objective","boosted rest objective"}},{q="What does Raan suggest doing after completing the quest?",a="Upon finishing the campfire lesson, Raan encourages you to visit trainers in the nearby village to learn a tradeskill, noting that craftsmen can create camp fixtures tailored to their craft.",al={"what does raan advise next","where to go after the great outdoors","after completing the great outdoors"}},{q="What is Boosted Rest?",a="Boosted Rest is the restorative effect gained from taking respite beside an established campfire, granting traveling adventurers enhanced recovery while braving the elements.",al={"what is boosted rest","campfire boosted rest buff"}},{q="Do tradeskills allow adventurers to craft campsite fixtures?",a="Yes, local trade trainers can instruct artisans on how to craft functional camp fixtures appropriate for their chosen craft, allowing travelers to maintain specialized campsite setups outdoors.",al={"can professions make campfires","profession camp fixtures","trade camp items"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Gathering around a basic campfire to rest and recover is second nature to camp cooks, who rely on open flames to sustain traveling parties in the wild.",["class:Hunter"]="Survival in the wilderness begins with the fundamentals of campcraft, an essential discipline for trackers and rangers accustomed to living under open skies."},kw={"basic campfire","boosted rest","camp fixture","campfire","raan","the great outdoors","tradeskill","wilderness survival"},m={id=96101},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96102"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Tailoring",z="general",s="Tailoring training teaches you to make a Rallying Banner. Its test belongs in the wilderness, where a camp needs a campfire.",h="Tailoring training teaches you to make a Rallying Banner.",sec={{t="A Campfire First",b="You can now craft a Rallying Banner, and its completion note calls for a wilderness test. A camp needs a campfire before you set it up. Most cooks can teach you to make one, so ask a cooking trainer for the basics if you have not learned them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can I make after this?",a="Tailoring training teaches you to craft your own Rallying Banner.",al={"what do i craft","banner recipe","tailoring banner"}},{q="Where should I test the banner?",a="Test your Rallying Banner in the wilderness. The completion note says townsfolk generally do not care for camps in the center of town.",al={"where to test it","camping where","test banner"}},{q="What does a camp need?",a="Before you set up a camp, you need a campfire.",al={"camp needs what","campfire needed","camping basics"}},{q="Who can teach me to make a campfire?",a="Most cooks can teach you to make a fire. A cooking trainer can teach you the basics.",al={"campfire trainer","who teaches fire","cooking fire"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="As a tailor, you can craft a Rallying Banner for a camp.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you can teach others to make a campfire."},kw={"campfire","camping","cooking","rallying banner","tailoring","wilderness"},m={id=96102},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Tailoring"},fv=true}
E["quest:96130"] = {t="quest",n="Chakuyak",z="general",s="Near the tree stands west of Stonebull Lake, Chakuyak threatens travelers crossing Mulgore. Local tribesfolk have marked the fierce wolf for hunting, and her pelt will be honored.",h="Near the tree stands west of Stonebull Lake, Chakuyak threatens travelers crossing Mulgore.",sec={{t="The Shadow of Stonebull Lake",b="Chakuyak prowls the tree stands west of Stonebull Lake, where prairie wolves roam Mulgore’s grassy plateaus and rolling hills. This fierce alpha female has become a danger to travelers arriving from abroad, so local hunters have marked her for removal before more wanderers are harmed.",sp=0},{t="Reverence and Necessity",b="For the local tribes, killing Chakuyak calls for solemn respect, not boasting. They see it as necessary to protect travelers and villagers. Her thick pelt is cured and put to use, honoring the wolf and ensuring her sacrifice is not wasted.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who or what is Chakuyak?",a="Chakuyak is a large, unusually aggressive prairie wolf who threatens travelers in Mulgore.",al={"what is chakuyak","who is chakuyak","is chakuyak a wolf"}},{q="Where is Chakuyak located?",a="Chakuyak prowls the tree stands west of Stonebull Lake, among the wooded hills.",al={"where to find chakuyak","chakuyak spot","where does chakuyak roam"}},{q="Why must Chakuyak be hunted down?",a="Chakuyak has grown bold and aggressive, making her a danger to travelers arriving from abroad.",al={"why kill chakuyak","chakuyak threat"}},{q="What happens to Chakuyak's pelt after the hunt?",a="Her pelt is preserved and put to use, so nothing is wasted.",al={"what happens to chakuyak pelt","chakuyak fur"}},{q="Do I need a group to hunt Chakuyak?",a="Chakuyak is exceptionally fierce. Travelers often join small hunting parties to track and bring her down safely.",al={"should i group for chakuyak","is chakuyak hard to solo"}},{q="Who acknowledges your victory over Chakuyak?",a="The hunter who set the hunt promises to praise your deed to the tribe’s chief when you return with Chakuyak’s pelt.",al={"who rewards chakuyak hunt","chakuyak chief"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know the balance between reverence for nature and the duty to protect your tribe. Using a slain beast’s pelt honors it.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you need patience and keen senses to track a fierce, solitary alpha wolf through wooded hills. Treat the predator with respect."},kw={"chakuyak","mulgore","prairie wolf","stonebull lake","tauren hunt","travelers","wolf pelt"},rel={"subzone:stonebull-lake"},m={id=96130},src={"In-game quest text: Chakuyak"},fv=true}
E["quest:96243"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="general",s="Call of Earth is an introductory summons calling an initiate to attune with the spirits of the stone. Directed to Coldridge Valley, the traveler must seek out Teo Hammerstorm within the dwarven holdfast of Anvilmar.",h="Call of Earth is an introductory summons calling an initiate to attune with the spirits of the stone.",sec={{t="The Voice of Stone",b="For an initiate walking the path of the elements, the call of earth is the vital first communion. While elemental energies may already stir across the skin, learning to speak directly with stone establishes a steadfast balance. It teaches the spirit how to find immovable strength, setting firm footing beneath one's boots and within the spirit itself.",sp=0},{t="The Holdfast of Anvilmar",b="Initiates in the snowy reaches of Dun Morogh are directed into the shelter of Anvilmar, a massive holdfast forged by hammer, fire, and will. Within this venerable redoubt in Coldridge Valley waits Teo Hammerstorm, who instructs fledgling shamans on how to heed the rumbling earth and begin their elemental duties.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Call of Earth?",a="Call of Earth is an introductory summons directing an aspiring shaman to begin communion with the earth spirits. It instructs the initiate to seek guidance and ground themselves in the power of the living stone.",al={"what is call of earth","call of earth quest","what is this quest"}},{q="Who is Teo Hammerstorm?",a="Teo Hammerstorm is a mentor located inside the dwarven stronghold of Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley. He guides initiates in communing with the earth and understanding its steady strength.",al={"who is teo hammerstorm","where is teo hammerstorm","teo hammerstorm location"}},{q="What is Anvilmar?",a="Anvilmar is a sturdy holdfast forged by hammer, fire, and will, situated in the snowy expanse of Coldridge Valley within Dun Morogh. It serves as a central redoubt and training ground for young heroes.",al={"where is anvilmar","what is anvilmar","anvilmar location"}},{q="Why must a shaman heed the call of earth?",a="Communing with the earth allows a shaman to ground their spiritual essence, setting firm footing both beneath their soles and within their soul so they can channel the steady power of stone.",al={"why speak to earth","purpose of call of earth","shaman earth connection"}},{q="Who undertakes this summons?",a="The quest is aimed at developing shamans starting out in Dun Morogh, directing them to seek out experienced elemental trainers who can guide their connection to the spirits.",al={"who sends call of earth","who takes call of earth","who does this quest"}},{q="Where does this quest lead?",a="The journey leads to Anvilmar, nestled within Coldridge Valley in the snowy realm of Dun Morogh. Initiates must venture inside the holdfast to find their mentor.",al={"where does call of earth take place","call of earth zone","where to go"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Heeding the call of earth is a foundational step in walking the shamanic path, binding the initiate to the deep stone and steadfast spirits of the land.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarves share an inherent bond with stone and earth, making communion with these terrestrial spirits feel natural amid the deep vaults of Dun Morogh.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance shamans in Dun Morogh begin their elemental journey right from the ancestral dwarven stronghold of Anvilmar."},kw={"anvilmar","call of earth","coldridge valley","dun morogh","earth totem","elements","shaman","teo hammerstorm"},rel={"subzone:anvilmar","subzone:coldridge-valley","zone:dunmorogh"},m={id=96243},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:96339"] = {t="quest",n="Legacy Reward: Replica Ironforge Air Rifle",z="general",s="Legacy Reward: Replica Ironforge Air Rifle marks the collection of a mysterious package held on your behalf. Left behind by an unidentified messenger, the parcel was narrowly saved from being dumped into the sea before reaching your hands.",h="Legacy Reward: Replica Ironforge Air Rifle marks the collection of a mysterious package held on your behalf.",sec={{t="A Package Awaiting Collection",b="A mysterious parcel addressed specifically to you was dropped off with an unwilling caretaker. The unknown courier insisted you would arrive to collect it, leaving so swiftly that the receiver could not even refuse delivery. Fortunately, you arrived just before the unclaimed box was tossed into the ocean alongside the evening's refuse.",sp=0},{t="Dwarven Engineering in Miniature",b="Inside the delivered parcel is a Replica Ironforge Air Rifle, a souvenir honoring the renowned firearm traditions of the dwarven capital. While dwarven mountaineers and marksmen carry deadly firepower through Dun Morogh, replicas like this celebrate the intricate barrelwork and pneumatic mechanisms perfected by Ironforge craftsmen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sent this package?",a="The courier or original sender never left a name or explanation. According to the recipient holding it, someone simply dropped off the package addressed specifically to you, insisted you would eventually arrive to claim it, and vanished before any questions could be asked.",al={"who sent the package","sender of air rifle","who left the rifle"}},{q="What was almost done to the package?",a="If you had not arrived in time, the package would have been thrown into the sea along with the nightly garbage. The caretaker admitted they do not run a storage service and routinely dispose of unwanted clutter into the ocean.",al={"what was going to happen to the package","garbage in sea","package thrown away"}},{q="What is inside the parcel?",a="The package contains a Replica Ironforge Air Rifle, delivered as a legacy item meant specifically for you. It serves as a commemorative piece representing dwarven firearm design.",al={"what is inside the package","replica rifle contents","package reward"}},{q="Why was the caretaker holding the package?",a="The holder made it clear they do not offer mail-holding or storage services. The parcel was only kept because the mystery courier left in such a hurry that there was no opportunity to refuse delivery before they disappeared.",al={"why did they hold it","holding mail","does this place hold packages"}},{q="Why is this considered a legacy reward?",a="Legacy rewards in WoW Forever represent special recognitions and parcels left behind by mysterious benefactors for seasoned adventurers, waiting at designated outposts to be retrieved upon your arrival.",al={"why is it called legacy reward","legacy reward lore","what does legacy mean here"}},{q="Is the replica rifle a functional weapon?",a="It is specifically noted as a replica of an Ironforge air rifle, fashioned after the finely engineered dwarven ranged weapons rather than being a frontline battle rifle.",al={"is the rifle a real weapon","can you shoot it","replica function"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="The parcel holds a replica of an Ironforge air rifle, a commemorative nod to the famed firearms and mechanical craftsmanship of Khaz Modan.",["profession:Engineering"]="Tinkers and gunsmiths will appreciate receiving an intricate firearm model, even if it arrived via an indifferent courier and nearly ended up at the bottom of the ocean."},kw={"air rifle","ironforge","ironforge air rifle","legacy reward","mysterious package","replica ironforge air rifle"},m={id=96339},src={"In-game quest text: Legacy Reward: Replica Ironforge Air Rifle"},fv=true}
E["quest:96394"] = {t="quest",n="The Restless Dead",z="general",s="The Restless Dead addresses an alarming disturbance within the crypts of the Hall of Thanes, where ancient dwarven spirits have been roused from their rest. With the caretakers terrified to enter the vaults, an adventurer must descend into the catacombs to put the angry phantoms and the spirit of Anvilmar back to sleep.",h="The Restless Dead addresses an alarming disturbance within the crypts of the Hall of Thanes, where ancient...",sec={{t="Disturbance in the Deep Vaults",b="Deep beneath the surface in the Hall of Thanes, ancestral crypts hold generations of honored dead. Normally resting in quiet slumber, the spirits have been violently agitated by an unseen disturbance in the vaults. Strange noises echoing up from below have spooked the site's caretakers, who refuse to enter the lower chambers to perform their duties until order is restored. Adventurers are called upon to descend into the shadowed stone halls and quiet the agitated souls.",sp=0},{t="Quieting the Ancestors",b="The spirits resting within the Hall of Thanes are notoriously light sleepers. Whatever event or presence shook the lower tombs stirred up waves of Enraged Apparitions and Tormented Souls, turning sacred ground into a perilous haunt. Among those awakened is the revered spirit of Anvilmar, requiring a respectful yet firm hand to quell the fury of the awakened ancestors and return peace to the catacombs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is The Restless Dead quest about?",a="The Restless Dead is a task given to investigate and quell an outbreak of ghostly disturbances beneath the Hall of Thanes. Someone or something startled the entombed spirits, leaving keepers terrified to enter the vaults until the apparitions are dealt with.",al={"what is the restless dead","what is this quest","restless dead quest overview"}},{q="Why have the spirits awakened?",a="An unknown disturbance deep within the vaults woke the entombed dead. As the spirits in the Hall of Thanes are described as light sleepers, whatever transpired down in the tombs startled them into a frenzy of rage and torment.",al={"why are the ghosts angry","who disturbed the tombs","why are apparitions awake"}},{q="Where does this disturbance take place?",a="The disturbance is centered down in the crypts and vaults of the Hall of Thanes, an ancestral resting place beneath Ironforge where ancient dwarven spirits lie entombed.",al={"where is hall of thanes","where do restless dead spawn","hall of thanes location"}},{q="Who is the spirit of Anvilmar?",a="The spirit of Anvilmar is an ancient and prominent soul resting in the deep tombs of the Hall of Thanes. Alongside lesser apparitions, this great spirit was roused from slumber and must be put back to peaceful rest.",al={"who is spirit of anvilmar","spirit of anvilmar lore","who is anvilmar ghost"}},{q="Why do the keepers need an adventurer's help?",a="The keepers are caretakers assigned to tend to the crypts and perform their regular duties in the Hall of Thanes. With angry spirits roaming the halls, they refuse to descend into the lower vaults until the threat is eliminated.",al={"who are the keepers","why do keepers need help","keepers hall of thanes"}},{q="What spirits haunt the Hall of Thanes?",a="The disturbed vaults are haunted by Enraged Apparitions, Tormented Souls, and the venerable spirit of Anvilmar itself, all startled into an aggressive vigil within the burial chambers.",al={"what kinds of ghosts are down there","apparitions in hall of thanes","types of restless dead"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="The Hall of Thanes holds the honored dead and ancestors of the dwarven people, making their sudden, violent stirring an unsettling disturbance right at the heart of their heritage.",["class:Priest"]="Spirits lingering in torment or anger are a direct call to duty for those who commune with souls and guide troubled dead toward peaceful rest.",["faction:Alliance"]="Securing the ancestral crypts beneath Ironforge ensures that the keepers can maintain their sacred duties without fear of being overrun by vengeful apparitions."},kw={"anvilmar","dwarf tombs","dwarven spirits","enraged apparitions","ghosts","hall of thanes","spirit of anvilmar","the restless dead","tormented souls"},rel={"zone:hallofthanes","subzone:anvilmar"},m={id=96394},src={"In-game quest text: The Restless Dead"},fv=true}
E["quest:96403"] = {t="quest",n="Important Heirlooms",z="general",s="Important Heirlooms is a quest where an adventurer aids a nervous gnome named Thom, who was caught lurking in the Hall of Thanes when Dark Iron dwarves breached the vaults in subterranean mole machines. The task involves retrieving scattered dwarven relics under the dubious guise of historic preservation.",h="Important Heirlooms is a quest where an adventurer aids a nervous gnome named Thom, who was caught lurking in...",sec={{t="Trouble in the Vaults",b="While exploring the subterranean vaults of the Hall of Thanes, an enterprising gnome named Thom was interrupted by the sudden arrival of Dark Iron dwarves tunneling upward in mole machines. Unable to secure the area himself, Thom recruits passing adventurers to brave the hostile dig site and retrieve valuable dwarven relics scattered across the chambers before the invaders seize or destroy them.",sp=0},{t="Cultural Preservation or Lucrative Plunder?",b="Despite Thom's insistence that he was merely on patrol and wishes to catalogue the relics for preservation, his constant slips about 'priceless loot' and 'appraising the haul' suggest far more self-serving motives. Adventurers are handsomely compensated for their bravery, though whether these ancient dwarven treasures will ever see a museum or an auction block remains an open question.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Thom?",a="The quest is given by Thom, an enterprising gnome found near the vaults. His nervous slips of the tongue suggest he is far more interested in lucrative plunder than legitimate vault security.",al={"who is quest giver","who is thom","quest giver"}},{q="What is the Hall of Thanes?",a="The Hall of Thanes is an underground dwarven vault containing ancient cultural relics and heirlooms, which has recently been breached by Dark Iron subterranean forces.",al={"what is hall of thanes","where is hall of thanes"}},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves in the vaults?",a="A strike force of Dark Iron dwarves breached the vaults directly from the depths using tunneling mole machines, throwing the area into chaos for unknown purposes.",al={"why dark iron there","dark iron mole machines"}},{q="Is Thom really returning the heirlooms?",a="Thom constantly slips up, referring to sneaking as 'patrolling,' heirlooms as 'loot' and 'haul,' and cataloging as 'appraising.' While he pays his promised reward, his claims of returning the relics to places of honor are highly suspect.",al={"is thom lying","does thom steal","thom trustworthy"}},{q="What are the Dwarven Heirlooms?",a="They are ancient dwarven cultural relics housed within the Hall of Thanes, considered priceless treasures by historians—and lucrative loot by opportunists like Thom.",al={"what are the heirlooms","dwarven heirlooms lore"}},{q="Why can't Thom retrieve the heirlooms himself?",a="Thom was caught off guard by the sudden arrival of Dark Iron mole machines while lurking in the vaults, leaving him unable to safely scavenge the relics himself without an adventurer's protection.",al={"why does thom need help","why help thom"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Thom's opportunistic nature and clumsy cover stories highlight a less-than-scholarly side of gnome curiosity when valuable relics are on the line.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven adventurers may view Thom's sudden interest in salvaging ancestral relics with deep skepticism, especially given his slips of the tongue regarding loot and appraisal.",["class:Rogue"]="A rogue easily sees right through Thom's flimsy excuse of 'patrolling' the vaults, recognizing a fellow scavenger caught off guard by an underground invasion."},kw={"dark iron","dwarven heirlooms","gnome","hall of thanes","important heirlooms","mole machine","thom","vaults"},rel={"zone:hallofthanes","topic:gnome"},m={id=96403},src={"In-game quest text: Important Heirlooms"},fv=true}
E["quest:96604"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival quest offered by Brakk to aspiring adventurers. By learning to rest beside a basic campfire, travelers discover the fundamentals of sustaining themselves in the wild before pursuing practical tradeskills.",h="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival quest offered by Brakk to aspiring adventurers.",sec={{t="Lessons in the Wild",b="Venturing into the untamed reaches of Azeroth requires more than martial grit; it demands the basic knowledge to endure the elements. Brakk greets aspiring adventurers with a hearty 'Lok-tar', recognizing the restless spirit of those seeking fortune and glory. To survive the long road ahead, every traveler must first master the art of making camp and recovering their strength amidst the wilderness.",sp=0},{t="From Hearth to Craft",b="Taking a seat beside Brakk's Basic Campfire imparts the essentials of recuperation, granting a restorative rest in the open air. Once these survival fundamentals are understood, novice adventurers are directed toward local village craftsmen. There, they can learn tradeskills and discover how to build functional camp fixtures suited to their chosen craft.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Great Outdoors?",a="The Great Outdoors is an introductory survival quest offered by Brakk, aimed at teaching new adventurers how to thrive in the wilderness by resting near a campfire before taking up a profession.",al={"what is the great outdoors","the great outdoors quest"}},{q="Who is Brakk?",a="Brakk is an experienced outdoor survivalist who welcomes restless travelers, offering practical lessons on wilderness survival and resting by a campfire.",al={"who is brakk","quest giver"}},{q="What is the Boosted Rest buff?",a="Resting near Brakk's Basic Campfire grants the Boosted Rest buff, demonstrating how taking shelter and recovering by an open flame revitalizes an adventurer in the wild.",al={"boosted rest buff","campfire buff"}},{q="What should you do after completing the quest?",a="Upon learning the basics of wilderness rest, Brakk advises adventurers to visit trainers in the nearby village to study a tradeskill and learn how to construct camp fixtures tailored to their craft.",al={"next step after quest","what to do after campfire"}},{q="What are camp fixtures?",a="Camp fixtures are specialized campsite installations taught by profession trainers in nearby villages, allowing travelers to adapt their camps to support their chosen tradeskills.",al={"camp fixtures","what are camp fixtures"}},{q="Who is this training intended for?",a="Any restless soul seeking fortune and glory in the wild benefits from this lesson, particularly those just beginning their journey and preparing to select a trade.",al={"who should do this quest","is this for horde"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Greeted with a traditional Orcish 'Lok-tar', this survival lesson offers nascent Horde adventurers fundamental grounding before setting out across treacherous territories."},kw={"boosted rest","brakk","camp fixture","campfire","the great outdoors","tradeskill","wilderness survival"},m={id=96604},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96605"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors is an introductory quest that instructs aspiring adventurers in the fundamental arts of wilderness survival. Guided by the traveler Kaga, newcomers learn how to recuperate beside a basic campfire before advancing to learn tradeskills and craft specialized camp fixtures.",h="The Great Outdoors is an introductory quest that instructs aspiring adventurers in the fundamental arts of...",sec={{t="Lessons of the Wild",b="Every adventurer setting foot beyond the safety of civilization quickly discovers that raw courage is not enough to survive the frontier. The Great Outdoors introduces newcomers to the vital necessity of wilderness acclimation. Taught by the experienced traveler Kaga, this brief lesson reminds fledgling heroes that thriving in unexplored territories begins with knowing how to establish a camp and endure the open elements.",sp=0},{t="The Hearth and the Craft",b="A simple campfire is often all that stands between an explorer and the exhaustion of the untamed wilderness. By taking a seat beside Kaga's hearth and gaining the Boosted Rest buff, adventurers discover the recuperative power of camp life. Once the fundamentals of wilderness rest are understood, Kaga urges travelers to visit nearby settlement artisans to learn tradeskills and master the crafting of professional camp fixtures.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Great Outdoors?",a="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival quest offered by Kaga. It introduces fledgling adventurers to the fundamentals of surviving in the wilds, resting by a campfire, and preparing for professional trades.",al={"what is the great outdoors","the great outdoors quest"}},{q="Who offers this quest?",a="Kaga is a seasoned traveler and outdoor mentor who guides aspiring adventurers in the basics of wilderness survival. He maintains a basic campfire where newcomers can learn to rest and recover.",al={"who is kaga","kaga questgiver"}},{q="What is the Boosted Rest buff?",a="Boosted Rest is the beneficial state of relaxation gained by sitting peacefully beside Kaga's Basic Campfire. It represents the rejuvenating effect of resting properly at a wilderness campsite.",al={"boosted rest","what is boosted rest buff"}},{q="What are camp fixtures?",a="After mastering basic wilderness resting, tradeskill trainers in nearby settlements can teach adventurers how to construct specialized camp fixtures tailored to their chosen crafts.",al={"camp fixtures lore","trade camp fixtures"}},{q="Why does Kaga have you sit by the campfire?",a="Surviving outside civilized towns demands learning how to make camp and recover stamina. Sitting by a campfire allows adventurers to gain warmth, focus, and rested recovery between perilous journeys.",al={"why rest at campfire","campfire resting"}},{q="What should you do after completing this lesson?",a="Upon learning how to properly rest in the wilds, Kaga advises seeking out profession trainers in the nearby village to learn a trade and acquire recipes for trade-specific camp fixtures.",al={"what next after great outdoors","after kaga quest"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Campfires and wilderness survival form the foundation of cooking in the field, making camp craft a natural complement to preparing meals under the open sky."},kw={"boosted rest","camp fixture","campfire","kaga","survival","the great outdoors","tradeskill","wilderness"},m={id=96605},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96606"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival lesson offered to hunters by an experienced scout named Lyreena. It emphasizes the importance of resting beside a campfire to recover and gain the Boosted Rest buff while journeying through the untamed wilds.",h="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival lesson offered to hunters by an experienced scout...",sec={{t="Lessons of the Wild",b="Venturing beyond civilization's borders demands more than sharp aim and keen senses; it requires respect for the raw untamed elements. An experienced scout named Lyreena offers guidance to wandering hunters, welcoming them to pause their trek and take comfort by her campfire. In the perilous wilds of Azeroth, knowing how to establish a hearth and replenish one's strength between hunts is just as critical as knowing how to track quarry.",sp=0},{t="Campfire Respite",b="A roaring campfire provides comfort against the nocturnal cold, offering a moment of calm where weary travellers can shake off fatigue. Sitting close to the blaze allows adventurers to gain Boosted Rest, revitalizing their energy and preparing them for the rigors of exploration. Taking time to sit by the embers teaches young adventurers that patience and rest are enduring pillars of wilderness survival.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Lyreena?",a="Lyreena is an experienced wilderness mentor who teaches aspiring adventurers and hunters how to properly rest and survive out in the elements. She offers practical advice around her campfire.",al={"who is lyreena","lyreena campfire","quest giver"}},{q="What is The Great Outdoors?",a="The Great Outdoors is an introductory wilderness survival task where a seasoned outdoorswoman named Lyreena invites hunters to sit by her campfire and learn how to sustain themselves during long expeditions.",al={"what is the great outdoors","what is this quest","the great outdoors quest"}},{q="What is Boosted Rest?",a="Sitting down in close proximity to a basic campfire allows weary wanderers to recover from travel fatigue and gain the Boosted Rest effect, revitalizing their spirits for upcoming journeys.",al={"boosted rest buff","boosted rest campfire","how to get boosted rest"}},{q="Why do you need to rest by a campfire?",a="Campfires provide vital warmth and respite in the harsh wilderness. Resting near one allows an adventurer to catch their breath, clear their mind, and prepare for dangerous treks across untamed lands.",al={"campfire purpose","why sit by fire","resting at campfire"}},{q="Why is outdoor survival crucial for hunters?",a="Hunters spend the bulk of their lives traversing isolated territories without taverns or safe towns nearby. Mastering basic campcraft ensures a hunter can endure harsh environments without succumbing to exhaustion.",al={"hunter survival skills","outdoor survival","why hunter quest"}},{q="What must you do to complete the task?",a="To complete the task, you simply need to take a seat near Lyreena's basic campfire and wait a short while until the campfire's warmth imparts the Boosted Rest buff.",al={"quest goal","how to finish the great outdoors","great outdoors objectives"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Offered directly to hunters, this lesson reinforces the necessity of wilderness survival and campcraft when tracking through the untamed wilds."},kw={"adventurer","boosted rest","campfire","hunter","lyreena","survival","the great outdoors","wilds"},rel={"topic:hunter"},m={id=96606},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96608"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Outdoors",z="general",s="The Great Outdoors introduces wandering adventurers to the essentials of wilderness survival and campcraft. By taking a seat at Eric's campfire, travelers learn how to recuperate properly in the wild before taking up tradeskills to support their journeys.",h="The Great Outdoors introduces wandering adventurers to the essentials of wilderness survival and campcraft.",sec={{t="Lessons of the Wild",b="Stepping out into the wider world requires more than ambition and a weapon or holy symbol. For fledgling travelers, survival depends on knowing how to rest properly amidst the elements. At his basic campfire, the seasoned traveler Eric welcomes wandering adventurers to take a seat, warm themselves by the embers, and discover how vital proper rest is before tackling the untamed wilderness.",sp=0},{t="From Campfire to Craft",b="A simple fire is only the beginning of maintaining an effective campsite on long journeys. Upon learning the fundamentals of wilderness recovery, travelers are encouraged to visit tradeskill trainers in neighboring settlements. There, artisans and crafters teach how to assemble specialized camp fixtures, allowing adventurers to practice their professions and support their expeditions wherever the road leads.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Eric?",a="Eric is an experienced outdoorsman and fellow adventurer who offers to teach fledgling travelers the essentials of surviving in the wilderness. He welcomes travelers to rest by his campfire and shares practical advice on thriving far from city walls.",al={"who gives this quest","who is eric","who teaches outdoors"}},{q="What is the Boosted Rest buff?",a="Resting beside Eric's campfire provides the Boosted Rest effect, helping weary travelers recover and prepare their spirits for the trials ahead. It represents taking time out of the elements to recuperate properly in the field.",al={"what is boosted rest","how does the campfire help","rest buff at camp"}},{q="What are camp fixtures?",a="Camp fixtures are practical structures or tools tailored to specific trade skills that adventurers can set up in the wild. As tradeskill trainers in nearby settlements explain, learning a profession allows craftspeople to establish working setups at their campsites.",al={"what are camp fixtures","profession fixtures","tradeskill camp item"}},{q="Why do you need to sit near Eric's campfire?",a="Taking a seat beside the campfire allows adventurers to pause, warm up, and listen to Eric's guidance on wilderness survival. It gives the body time to relax and gain the full benefit of restful shelter outdoors.",al={"why sit at fire","sit near campfire","campfire objective"}},{q="Where should an adventurer go after completing this lesson?",a="After mastering the basics of resting at camp, Eric suggests heading to the nearby village to study a tradeskill under local craft trainers. Once trained, you can begin learning how to construct camp fixtures suited to your craft.",al={"what to do after","where next","after the great outdoors"}},{q="Who is this survival lesson intended for?",a="Eric calls out to passing adventurers, noting their restless spirit and readiness to seek fortune and glory across the world. His wilderness guidance helps anyone stepping away from safety and venturing out into the open wilds.",al={"who can do this quest","priest only","is this for adventurers"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="While priests often find their calling in sacred sanctuaries and chapels, life on the road demands practical survival skills beyond prayers and blessings."},kw={"boosted rest","camp fixture","campfire","eric","survival","the great outdoors","tradeskill","wilderness"},rel={"topic:priest"},m={id=96608},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Outdoors"},fv=true}
E["quest:96626"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking teaches budding adventurers that cooking is the fundamental cornerstone of establishing a camp in the wild. By learning the craft from Cook Tomas in Goldshire, travelers acquire the ability to build campfires and pitch their own wilderness camps.",h="Camping 101: Cooking teaches budding adventurers that cooking is the fundamental cornerstone of establishing...",sec={{t="The Hearth in the Wild",b="For any adventurer braving the wilds of Azeroth, setting up camp begins with a reliable blaze. The quest Camping 101: Cooking instructs novice wanderers that culinary training is not merely about preparing hot meals; it is the vital foundation for survival in the wilderness. By mastering the cooking profession, travelers learn how to strike a proper campfire, turning an untamed clearing into a safe refuge.",sp=0},{t="Instruction in Goldshire",b="New travelers in Elwynn Forest are sent to speak with Cook Tomas in Goldshire to learn the fundamentals of the craft. Upon receiving his instruction, adventurers gain the ability to establish basic wilderness camps. While initial camps are simple and hold limited furnishings, honing one's cooking proficiency unlocks larger fires, while other crafting disciplines offer further options to furnish and customize a campsite.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Camping 101: Cooking?",a="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers to the fundamentals of surviving in the wilds. It guides you to take up the cooking profession, teaching you how to spark a basic campfire that serves as the centerpiece for your campsite out in the wilderness.",al={"what is camping 101 cooking","what does camping 101 teach","purpose of camping 101"}},{q="Who is Tomas and where can he be found?",a="Tomas is the resident culinary trainer located in Goldshire within Elwynn Forest. Based out of the Lion's Pride Inn, he instructs aspiring adventurers in basic cooking and the art of lighting a campfire.",al={"who is tomas","where is tomas","tomas goldshire"}},{q="Why is cooking required to establish a campsite?",a="A campfire is the foundation of any wilderness encampment. Learning cooking provides the essential knowledge required to build a fire, around which an adventurer can rest and arrange their campsite furnishings.",al={"why do i need cooking for camping","is cooking required to camp","cooking campfire relationship"}},{q="Can you make larger campfires as your skill improves?",a="Advancing your culinary expertise allows you to construct larger and sturdier fires. As your cooking skill develops, your campfire becomes a more substantial base for wilderness exploration.",al={"how to upgrade campfire","can i build bigger campfires","bigger fires cooking"}},{q="How do you acquire furnishings for your campsite?",a="Beyond cooking, additional campsite furnishings can be learned from other tradeskill trainers across the world. Adventurers skilled in various crafting professions can create items to decorate and enhance their wilderness camps.",al={"how to add camp furnishings","where do camp furnishings come from","crafting camp items"}},{q="Where can an adventurer set up camp?",a="Once you have mastered the basics of cooking and campfire creation, you gain the ability to pitch a camp in the open wilderness. However, initial campsites are modest and support only a minimal number of furnishings until you expand your skills.",al={"can i camp anywhere","wilderness camping rules","camp placement limits"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Culinary training serves as the essential bedrock for wilderness survival, granting adventurers the skill to build basic campfires that anchor their campsites.",["faction:Alliance"]="Novice Alliance adventurers in Elwynn Forest are directed to Cook Tomas at the Lion's Pride Inn in Goldshire to master culinary fundamentals and campfire crafting."},kw={"campfire","camping","camping 101: cooking","campsite","cooking","furnishings","goldshire","tomas"},rel={"subzone:goldshire"},m={id=96626},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96629"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers in Dun Morogh to the essential survival skill of cooking. Beyond preparing provisions, learning this trade enables travelers to light campfires that serve as the foundation for wilderness campsites.",h="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers in Dun Morogh to the essential survival skill of cooking.",sec={{t="The Hearth in the Wild",b="For any traveler braving the wilds, establishing a reliable campsite begins with a flame. In Dun Morogh, novice adventurers are directed to Kharanos to learn the fundamentals of cooking under Gremlock Pilsnor. Cooking is not simply about sustaining oneself with hot meals on the road; a sturdy campfire serves as the literal and practical anchor for any outdoor camp.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Campsite",b="A newly learned campfire allows an adventurer to pitch a humble camp anywhere in the open wilderness. While rudimentary fires support only minimal furnishings, improving one's cooking proficiency unlocks the ability to build larger blazes. Furthermore, artisans skilled in other crafting trades can fashion additional furnishings to transform a simple rest stop into a fully outfitted encampment.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Camping 101: Cooking?",a="Camping 101: Cooking is an instructional quest that introduces adventurers to the essential craft of cooking as the cornerstone of wilderness survival and camp setup.",al={"what is camping 101 cooking","quest purpose","camping basics cooking"}},{q="Who is Gremlock Pilsnor?",a="Gremlock Pilsnor is the local cooking trainer located in Kharanos in Dun Morogh, who teaches novices how to prepare food and build campfires.",al={"who teaches cooking in kharanos","gremlock pilsnor location","cooking trainer dun morogh"}},{q="Why is cooking necessary for camping?",a="Cooking allows adventurers to build a basic campfire, which serves as the core foundation for setting up a wilderness camp and placing furnishings around it.",al={"why do i need cooking for camping","campfire purpose","cooking and camps"}},{q="Where can a campsite be established?",a="Once you know how to build a fire, you can pitch a basic campsite out in the wilderness, providing a resting place during your journeys.",al={"where can i make a camp","campsite rules","setting up camp"}},{q="How do you improve or expand a campsite?",a="Advancing your cooking skill allows you to build larger campfires, and learning other crafting trades enables you to craft and place additional campsite furnishings.",al={"how to upgrade campsite","bigger fires","more camp decorations"}},{q="Can other professions contribute to camping?",a="Other tradeskill trainers can teach the basics of crafting furnishings for your campsite if you are skilled in their respective professions.",al={"who offers camping furnishings","tradeskill camp items","other camp trainers"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking provides the foundational campfire required to establish wilderness campsites and place camp furnishings.",["class:Priest"]="Even spiritual practitioners devoted to the Light must master basic survival skills like fire-building when traveling far from temples and settlements.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers starting near Dun Morogh learn the fundamentals of bushcraft and cooking from the dwarven instructors of Kharanos."},kw={"campfire","camping","cooking","dun morogh","furnishings","gremlock pilsnor","kharanos","wilderness"},rel={"subzone:kharanos","topic:priest"},m={id=96629},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96634"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers on the essentials of wilderness camping through the art of cooking. By learning to start a campfire from local trainers like Zarrin in Dolanaar, travelers establish the necessary anchor for setting up wilderness camps. It marks the first step toward mastering outdoor survival and outfitting personal campsites.",h="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers on the essentials of wilderness...",sec={{t="The Hearth of the Wild",b="Every long journey across the wilderness begins with the ability to build a warm fire. In Camping 101: Cooking, fledgling adventurers are directed to seek out culinary training as their initial lesson in bushcraft. Learning to cook is more than preparing meals from wild game; the essential skill of lighting a basic campfire serves as the physical and practical anchor for any outdoor camp. Without a fire to ward off the chill and keep the dark at bay, establishing a reliable campsite in the wilds is impossible.",sp=0},{t="Expanding the Campsite",b="Learning to kindle a basic campfire grants the ability to establish modest camps throughout the wilderness. Although an entry-level camp can support only a few rudimentary furnishings, dedicated adventurers can expand their capabilities. As an adventurer hones their cooking proficiency, they unlock methods to kindle larger, sturdier blazes. Furthermore, artisans versed in other tradeskills can consult their respective trainers to learn how to craft furnishings and improvements to enrich their camps.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Camping 101: Cooking?",a="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers to the fundamentals of wilderness survival by directing them to learn the cooking trade. Establishing a campfire serves as the core foundation for setting up a campsite anywhere in the wild.",al={"what does this quest do","camping 101 cooking purpose","why do camping 101"}},{q="Who is Zarrin?",a="Zarrin is a cooking trainer located in Dolanaar on Teldrassil. He teaches novice adventurers the basics of the culinary arts and how to build a basic campfire.",al={"who is zarrin","zarrin dolanaar","where is cooking trainer dolanaar"}},{q="How do you establish a campsite in the wild?",a="Setting up a campsite starts with building a basic campfire learned through the cooking profession. While basic training allows you to pitch camp in the wilderness with modest furnishings, advancing your skills allows for larger fires and additional campsite improvements.",al={"how to build camp","how does camping work","making a campsite"}},{q="Can you add furnishings to your campsite?",a="While cooking provides the campfire that anchors your campsite, other tradeskill trainers across Azeroth can teach skilled craftsmen how to furnish and upgrade their campsites with specialized gear and decorations.",al={"furnishing campsite","camp decorations","campsite furnishings other professions"}},{q="Why is cooking required for camping?",a="Cooking teaches adventurers how to build and maintain an open campfire out in the wild. Because a fire is the essential centerpiece of any outdoor camp, learning to cook is the first necessary step for proper wilderness camping.",al={"why need cooking for camping","cooking connection to camping"}},{q="Can you make larger campfires?",a="Yes. As you practice your culinary craft and raise your cooking skill, you can learn how to construct larger fires to better support your wilderness camps.",al={"can i make bigger campfires","how to upgrade campfire"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking is the foundational discipline of survival in the wilderness, providing the campfire necessary to anchor any outdoor campsite. Advancing in the culinary craft allows adventurers to build larger, more resilient campfires.",["race:NightElf"]="Novice night elf adventurers passing through Dolanaar often learn the fundamentals of bushcraft and firemaking from Zarrin before venturing deeper into the wilds of Teldrassil."},kw={"campfire","camping 101","campsite","cooking","dolanaar","furnishings","wilderness survival","zarrin"},rel={"npc:zarrin","subzone:dolanaar"},m={id=96634},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96646"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that teaches adventurers the culinary arts as the vital foundation for wilderness survival. By learning to start a campfire from Zerril Softbreeze in Shen'dar Village, travelers unlock the ability to pitch their own campsites in the wild.",h="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that teaches adventurers the culinary arts as the vital...",sec={{t="The Hearth of the Wild",b="For an adventurer traveling across untamed lands, understanding survival begins with the hearth. Camping 101: Cooking introduces wanderers to the art of culinary craft, not merely for preparing meals, but because mastering a basic fire is the essential first step to establishing a safe resting spot in the wild.",sp=0},{t="Foundations of the Campsite",b="Aspiring campers are directed to speak with Zerril Softbreeze in Shen'dar Village to learn the fundamentals of the cooking trade. Once an adventurer understands how to kindle a campfire, they gain the ability to establish a small campsite anywhere across the wilderness. Although rudimentary camps support only minimal furnishings, improving one's craft allows for larger fires and richer arrangements crafted with aid from other tradeskills.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Cooking?",a="It is an introductory wilderness instruction that teaches adventurers how the art of cooking and lighting basic campfires serves as the foundation for setting up a campsite.",al={"what is camping 101 cooking","camping cooking quest"}},{q="Who is Zerril Softbreeze?",a="Zerril Softbreeze is a cooking trainer situated in Shen'dar Village who instructs novices in the basic culinary arts and how to start a campfire.",al={"who is zerril softbreeze","cooking trainer shen'dar"}},{q="Why is cooking required to set up a camp?",a="Cooking teaches adventurers how to build and maintain a campfire, which acts as the core foundation around which any wilderness campsite is established.",al={"why learn cooking for camping","why need cooking for camp"}},{q="Where is Zerril Softbreeze located?",a="Zerril Softbreeze can be found teaching tradeskills within Shen'dar Village.",al={"where is zerril softbreeze","shen'dar village location"}},{q="How can campsites be furnished?",a="Novices can place basic furnishings around their campfires, but learning trade skills from other craft trainers allows adventurers to craft and set up additional campsite furnishings.",al={"campsite furnishings","how to furnish camp"}},{q="Can campfires be upgraded?",a="By advancing your cooking skill, you can learn how to kindle even larger campfires to improve your wilderness camps.",al={"bigger campfires","upgrade camp"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Learning basic cooking provides the fundamental knowledge needed to build a campfire, establishing the central anchor for any wilderness camp."},kw={"campfire","camping","campsite","cooking","furnishings","shen'dar village","zerril softbreeze"},m={id=96646},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96655"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers to the essential craft of survival in the wilderness. By directing travelers to Cook Torka in Razor Hill, it teaches the fundamentals of cooking and campfire creation necessary to establish personal campsites in the wild.",h="Camping 101: Cooking introduces adventurers to the essential craft of survival in the wilderness.",sec={{t="The Hearth of the Wild",b="For any wanderer setting foot beyond settlement gates, surviving the wilderness requires more than sharp steel and sturdy armor. The quest Camping 101: Cooking directs budding adventurers to Cook Torka in Razor Hill to learn the culinary arts. In the open wastes of Durotar and beyond, cooking represents far more than preparing a hot meal—it is the foundational discipline required to light a reliable campfire, which serves as the heart of any campsite.",sp=0},{t="Building and Furnishing a Camp",b="Mastering the basics allows travelers to pitch a modest camp nearly anywhere across the frontier. While a novice campfire supports only a handful of essential furnishings, further dedication to the craft opens new possibilities. Deepening one's cooking expertise allows the construction of larger blazes, while pursuing other trade skills provides the craftsmanship needed to furnish and enhance camps into reliable bastions against the untamed wild.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Camping 101: Cooking?",a="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that teaches adventurers the fundamentals of outdoor survival. By learning cooking and campfire creation, adventurers gain the ability to establish their own campsites anywhere in the wild.",al={"what is camping 101 cooking","camping 101 cooking quest","what does this quest do"}},{q="Who is Cook Torka?",a="Cook Torka is the Horde cooking trainer located in Razor Hill in Durotar. He teaches aspiring adventurers the basics of the cooking profession and how to light a basic campfire.",al={"who teaches cooking razor hill","who is cook torka","where is cook torka"}},{q="Why is cooking required for camping?",a="Cooking is not just for preparing food; the campfire built through cooking serves as the foundational anchor for pitching a camp. Without a fire, a proper campsite cannot be maintained out in the wilderness.",al={"why do i need cooking for camping","cooking campfire connection","does camping require cooking"}},{q="Where can an adventurer set up a campsite?",a="Once you learn the basics of cooking and campfire creation, you are able to set up a small campsite almost anywhere out in the open wilderness. Initial campsites are basic and cannot support many furnishings.",al={"where can i camp","how does camping work","campsite placement"}},{q="How do you upgrade your campfire?",a="As you raise your proficiency in the cooking profession, you can learn to construct larger, more enduring campfires, allowing for greater campsite capabilities.",al={"bigger campfires","upgrade campfire","how to improve campsite"}},{q="How do you obtain furnishings for a campsite?",a="While cooking provides the campfire foundation, other tradeskill trainers across the world can teach adventurers how to craft and place additional furnishings to customize and expand their campsites.",al={"campsite decorations","furnishing camp","camp furnishings trainers"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking is the core requirement for building a basic campfire, forming the indispensable anchor for wilderness survival and campsite setup.",["faction:Horde"]="Adventurers starting out across Durotar can report to Cook Torka in Razor Hill to learn the foundational trade of cooking."},kw={"campfire","camping 101","camping 101: cooking","campsite","cook torka","cooking","durotar","furnishings","razor hill"},rel={"subzone:razor-hill"},m={id=96655},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96661"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Cooking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers on how basic culinary knowledge forms the foundation of establishing a campsite in the wilderness. By seeking out Pyall Silentstride in Bloodhoof Village, travelers learn to build a campfire to anchor their camp and prepare for life on the frontier.",h="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that instructs adventurers on how basic culinary knowledge...",sec={{t="The Hearth of the Wild",b="Every long journey through the untamed wilds demands basic wilderness survival, and every true camp begins with fire. For wanderers stepping across Kalimdor, the quest Camping 101: Cooking introduces the essential relationship between culinary training and frontier survival. Cooking is not merely about preparing nourishing meals from hunted game; it provides the fundamental knowledge needed to build a working campfire out in the elements. Without a fire, an adventurer has no camp to return to when night falls.",sp=0},{t="Building the Camp",b="In the rolling plains of Mulgore, adventurers are directed to Pyall Silentstride in Bloodhoof Village to learn the basics of cooking. With this foundational training, an adventurer gains the ability to strike a campfire anywhere in the wilderness and pitch a basic camp. While initial camps are simple and allow few furnishings, advancing one's cooking proficiency unlocks the secrets of kindling larger fires. In turn, craftsmen versed in other trades can craft furnishings to flesh out their frontier outposts.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Cooking?",a="Camping 101: Cooking is an introductory quest that teaches adventurers how cooking and fire-making form the literal foundation of surviving in the wild. By learning cooking, an adventurer gains the ability to create campfires and establish campsites in the field.",al={"what is camping 101 cooking","camping cooking quest","camping basics cooking"}},{q="Who is Pyall Silentstride?",a="Pyall Silentstride is a cooking trainer situated in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He instructs novice adventurers in the culinary arts and teaches them how to build basic campfires.",al={"who is pyall silentstride","pyall silentstride location","bloodhoof cooking trainer"}},{q="Why is cooking necessary to learn camping?",a="In the wilderness, cooking is about more than preparing meals; learning to cook teaches an adventurer how to kindle a campfire, which acts as the physical and practical anchor for any campsite.",al={"why need cooking for camping","why learn cooking to camp","campfire camping connection"}},{q="Can you make bigger campfires as your skill improves?",a="As you advance and raise your cooking skill, you develop the knowledge required to build larger, sturdier fires, allowing for more substantial campsite setups in the wilderness.",al={"can i upgrade campfires","bigger campfires","how to make larger fires"}},{q="How do you furnish a campsite?",a="While a campfire provides the central anchor of a campsite, additional furnishings and accessories can be learned from other tradeskill trainers across the world if you are trained in their crafts.",al={"how do campsite furnishings work","campsite decorations","furnishing campsites tradeskills"}},{q="Where can you set up a camp?",a="Once you understand how to kindle a campfire through basic cooking training, you are able to establish a humble camp out in the open wilderness wherever your travels take you.",al={"where to place camp","can i camp anywhere","campsite restrictions"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking serves as the core cornerstone of survival in the wilds, providing not only sustenance but the essential skill to spark a campfire around which an entire campsite is built.",["faction:Horde"]="Adventurers traveling through Mulgore begin their frontier survival training in Bloodhoof Village under the guidance of local tauren craftsmen and cooks."},kw={"bloodhoof village","campfire","camping 101","campsite","cooking","furnishings","pyall silentstride","wilderness"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village"},m={id=96661},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Cooking"},fv=true}
E["quest:96800"] = {t="quest",n="Past Due",z="general",s="A scholar needs a missing tome from the Scarlet Monastery to study Grim Batol’s ancient Wildhammer runemasters.",h="A scholar needs a missing tome from the Scarlet Monastery to study Grim Batol’s ancient Wildhammer...",sec={{t="An overdue book",b="A scholar is writing a thesis on the ancient Wildhammer runemasters of Grim Batol. The old Dalaran library’s ledgers say Doan borrowed the tome before the Third War and never returned it. Now in the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades, Doan still has the book, waiting to be recovered.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who needs the book?",a="A scholar writing a thesis on the ancient Wildhammer runemasters of Grim Batol needs the book for that research.",al={"who wants the tome","scholar research"}},{q="What book was borrowed?",a="The borrowed book is called The Lost Magic of the Runemasters.",al={"which tome","lost runemasters book"}},{q="Who borrowed the book?",a="The old Dalaran library’s ledgers say a mage named Doan borrowed the book before the Third War.",al={"who checked it out","Doan book"}},{q="Where is the book?",a="The book is in the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades, where Arcanist Doan is now.",al={"where's the tome","book location"}},{q="What is the research about?",a="The scholar is researching the ancient Wildhammer runemasters of Grim Batol.",al={"wildhammer runemasters","Grim Batol thesis"}}},kw={"arcanist doan","dalaran library","grim batol","past due","scarlet monastery","the lost magic of the runemasters","wildhammer runemasters"},rel={"subzone:grim-batol","zone:scarlet","zone:tirisfal","subzone:dalaran","topic:mage"},m={id=96800},src={"In-game quest text: Past Due"},fv=true}
E["quest:96821"] = {t="quest",n="Legging It",z="general",s="Legging It is a quest focused on clearing a dangerous southern path to protect working peons. Threats along the roadway had frightened the labor force into halting their travels, stalling essential duties. By driving back the dangers, adventurers restore safe passage and enable the workforce to return to their assignments.",h="Legging It is a quest focused on clearing a dangerous southern path to protect working peons.",sec={{t="Securing the Line",b="Legging It addresses a disruption in local labor operations where peons became too frightened to journey southward. Rather than performing their assigned tasks, the workers refused to risk travel along the road due to looming perils. By intervening to push back threats along the path, travelers restore enough security for overseers to send their crews back to work.",sp=0},{t="Labor and Route Security",b="Peons represent the backbone of Horde infrastructure, responsible for timber harvesting, quarrying, and building outposts. Because peons possess little martial prowess, even minor roadside predators or hostile scouts can freeze supply chains. Ensuring that transit corridors remain clear is essential for keeping settlements supplied and productive.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Legging It?",a="Legging It is a task undertaken to secure a southern transit route for labor crews. Threats along the road had intimidated local peons, halting their daily duties until an adventurer intervened to clear the way.",al={"what is legging it","legging it quest info","legging it quest"}},{q="Why were the peons afraid to travel south?",a="The peons feared venturing southward due to active dangers along the road. Without protection or a cleared pathway, the workers refused to risk traveling to their assignments, bringing local operations to a standstill.",al={"why were peons scared","peon fear","why peons stopped working"}},{q="What is accomplished by completing the task?",a="With the southern route cleared and immediate dangers suppressed, the overseers are able to safely send the peon workforce back into the field to resume labor and resource gathering.",al={"what happens after legging it","legging it outcome","clearing path result"}},{q="Who are the peons mentioned in the task?",a="Peons are the basic labor force of the Horde, responsible for heavy lifting, lumber harvesting, mining, and constructing fortifications. Though essential to settlement operations, they are ill-suited for combat and readily retreat from danger.",al={"who are the peons","peon role","what do peons do"}},{q="Where were the peons traveling?",a="The path leads southward toward outlying work areas and resource deposits that require regular peon maintenance, though the journey had grown perilous enough to stop all traffic before the route was cleared.",al={"where does the path lead","southern path","heading south"}},{q="Why do peons require adventurers to clear their routes?",a="Peons lack formal military training and combat gear, making them vulnerable to wilderness predators and hostile forces. Overseers must rely on seasoned adventurers to eliminate perimeter threats and maintain safe working corridors.",al={"why do peons need protection","peon safety","guarding peons"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Orcish labor and supply lines depend heavily on peons, making route security vital for Horde construction and resource gathering.",["race:Orc"]="Peons form the backbone of orcish settlements, and overseers rely on warriors to keep pathways clear of threats so work can continue."},kw={"cleared the path","horde labor","legging it","overseer","peon labor","peons","southern path"},m={id=96821},src={"In-game quest text: Legging It"},fv=true}
E["quest:96822"] = {t="quest",n="For Honor",z="general",s="For Honor is a quest dedicated to commemorating fallen warriors so their deeds are not forgotten. By completing the task, an adventurer ensures that the sacrifices of these combatants endure in memory.",h="For Honor is a quest dedicated to commemorating fallen warriors so their deeds are not forgotten.",sec={{t="A Duty of Remembrance",b="The quest For Honor is a solemn mission centered on memorializing fallen combatants. In a world continuously tested by conflict, ensuring that soldiers who gave everything are not forgotten stands as a vital duty for those who survive them.",sp=0},{t="Preserving the Legacy",b="Upon fulfilling the task, the quest giver confirms that the fallen fighters will endure in memory thanks to your efforts. The completed deed ensures their sacrifices remain recognized and respected rather than fading into obscurity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest For Honor about?",a="For Honor is a quest focused on commemorating fallen fighters. By undertaking and completing the task, you help ensure that the sacrifices of these warriors are recognized and not lost to history.",al={"what is for honor","for honor quest overview"}},{q="Who are the warriors mentioned upon completion?",a="The quest text specifically honors fallen warriors whose deeds and memory are preserved through your actions. Their service and sacrifice are kept alive by those who see the task through.",al={"who are the warriors","who is remembered"}},{q="Why is honoring these warriors important?",a="The purpose of the task is remembrance. In a world fraught with ongoing war and conflict, honoring the memory of those who fell in battle provides dignity and ensures their courage is acknowledged.",al={"why do this quest","for honor meaning"}},{q="Who asks for this task to be done?",a="The task is directed by someone seeking to memorialize fallen comrades. Upon return, they verify whether the deed has been performed and acknowledge that the warriors will forever be remembered.",al={"who gives for honor","quest giver for honor"}},{q="Does this quest lead to other tasks?",a="Nothing in the quest points onward to a further task. It stands on its own as an act of commemoration for fallen fighters, cementing their legacy so their sacrifices are never forgotten.",al={"is for honor part of a chain","what follows for honor"}},{q="What does the title For Honor signify?",a="The name reflects the central theme of honoring fallen soldiers. It highlights that acknowledging sacrifice and keeping battlefield memory alive is among the most solemn duties a fighter can carry out.",al={"what makes it honorable","meaning of for honor"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="This task centers directly on honoring fallen combatants, resonating strongly with warriors who value battlefield sacrifice and enduring remembrance."},kw={"fallen warriors","for honor","honor","memorial","quest","remembrance","warrior"},rel={"topic:warrior"},m={id=96822},src={"In-game quest text: For Honor"},fv=true}
E["quest:96825"] = {t="quest",n="This Fruit Could Bite Back",z="general",s="This Fruit Could Bite Back is a foraging quest focused on collecting wild prickly pears in the arid lands near Orgrimmar. The fruits are prepared into food while their seeds are saved for future planting, with the aim of cultivating a resilient desert crop to nourish the Horde capital.",h="This Fruit Could Bite Back is a foraging quest focused on collecting wild prickly pears in the arid lands...",sec={{t="Harvesting the Arid Wastes",b="Durotar is a rugged, sun-baked landscape where traditional farming is nearly impossible. To support the burgeoning capital of Orgrimmar, resourceful inhabitants turn to native desert flora. The prickly pear, a hardy cactus fruit equipped with protective spines, thrives in the arid soil and provides much-needed nourishment when gathered with care.",sp=0},{t="Cultivation and Sustenance",b="Gathering prickly pears is not merely about providing an immediate meal. While the flesh of the fruit is prepared into tasty dishes, the harvested seeds are carefully preserved for cultivation. The ambition is to plant these resilient crops all around Orgrimmar, establishing an accessible and enduring food source right outside the fortress walls.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is This Fruit Could Bite Back?",a="This Fruit Could Bite Back is a task focused on gathering wild prickly pears in Durotar. The collected fruit is intended both for immediate cooking and for saving seeds to propagate across the dry terrain surrounding Orgrimmar.",al={"what is this fruit could bite back","this fruit could bite back quest","bite back fruit quest"}},{q="Why are prickly pears being gathered?",a="The gathered prickly pears serve a dual purpose: preparing delicious meals for local consumption and harvesting viable seeds. The long-term goal is cultivating prickly pears widely throughout the arid land around Orgrimmar.",al={"why gather prickly pears","prickly pear purpose","what are prickly pears for"}},{q="Where will the seeds be planted?",a="The seeds salvaged from the gathered harvest are slated for planting throughout the outskirts and lands surrounding Orgrimmar, helping establish a resilient, self-sustaining food supply in the harsh desert soil.",al={"where grow prickly pears","prickly pear planting orgrimmar"}},{q="Who wants these fruits gathered?",a="A local resident seeking to improve local food stores and cultivate crops in the harsh desert climate of Durotar requests the harvest to feed travelers and cultivate future crops.",al={"who wants the prickly pears","quest giver bite back"}},{q="Why does the fruit 'bite back'?",a="Prickly pears are covered in sharp spines and needles typical of hardy desert cacti, making harvesting them hazardous without proper care.",al={"why bite back","why does fruit bite back"}},{q="Why is planting fruit around Orgrimmar important?",a="Durotar is predominantly arid and rocky, making conventional agriculture difficult. Establishing drought-tolerant crops like prickly pears helps provide sustainable sustenance for the capital city.",al={"does orgrimmar lack food","orgrimmar agriculture","durotar farming"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Securing sustainable food sources like hearty desert fruits is vital for sustaining the Orcish capital and nourishing the Horde's growing population.",["profession:Cooking"]="Prickly pears offer culinary potential in the arid climate of Durotar, serving as ingredients for tasty dishes and supplies for city residents.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Harvesters and botanists understand the hardiness of desert flora and the effort required to gather spiny wild fruits for cultivation."},kw={"cactus fruit","durotar","fruit","orgrimmar","prickly pear","prickly pears","seeds","this fruit could bite back"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=96825},src={"In-game quest text: This Fruit Could Bite Back"},fv=true}
E["quest:96874"] = {t="quest",n="This Is Spinal Axe",z="general",s="Ug’thok in Orgrimmar asks you to gather spines from the huge beasts of Thunder Ridge. He hopes to shape them into an axe head for your smithing lessons.",h="Ug’thok in Orgrimmar asks you to gather spines from the huge beasts of Thunder Ridge.",sec={{t="A smith’s idea",b="Ug’thok asks you to bring five Weathered Spines and five Rough Grinding Stones. He believes the spines could make a fine axe head, though Saru laughed at the idea.",sp=0},{t="Tough material",b="The spines are tougher than Ug’thok expected, which may make them difficult to work. He plans to keep shaping the design.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is asking for the spines?",a="Ug’thok in Orgrimmar asks you to gather Weathered Spines and Rough Grinding Stones.",al={"who wants spines","ugthok axe","smithing lesson"}},{q="What does Ug’thok want to make?",a="Ug’thok hopes to make an axe head from the beasts’ spines.",al={"spine axe","what is he crafting","axe head idea"}},{q="Where do the spines come from?",a="The Weathered Spines come from the huge beasts of Thunder Ridge.",al={"where are spines","thunder ridge beasts","spine location"}},{q="What else does Ug’thok need?",a="Ug’thok asks for Rough Grinding Stones along with the spines.",al={"grinding stones needed","what materials","rough stones"}},{q="Why does Ug’thok want the materials?",a="Ug’thok wants to try making an axe head from the spines as part of your smithing lessons.",al={"why spines","what are they for","ugthok materials"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="As a smithing student, you help Ug’thok gather materials for an axe head he hopes to make."},kw={"axe head","orgrimmar","rough grinding stones","saru","smithing","thunder ridge","ug’thok","weathered spines"},rel={"subzone:thunder-ridge","zone:orgrimmar","quest:96875"},m={id=96874},src={"In-game quest text: This Is Spinal Axe"},fv=true}
E["quest:96875"] = {t="quest",n="Beasts of Thunder Ridge",z="general",s="Across Durotar, Orgrimmar's builders need hides to keep the growing capital strong. Kamari sends you to Thunder Ridge for lizard hides and cured light hides.",h="Across Durotar, Orgrimmar's builders need hides to keep the growing capital strong.",sec={{t="A Growing Capital",b="Orgrimmar keeps growing as the Horde makes a home in Durotar. New homes, workshops, and defenses need lumber, stone, and animal skins. Timber comes from farther away, but Durotar's beasts provide sturdy hides for the peons and builders.",sp=0},{t="Beasts of the Canyon",b="The lizards of Thunder Ridge have tough hides, too thick and stiff for wearable armor. Properly harvested, their pelts line the walls and ceilings of Orgrimmar's timber and stone buildings. There, they shield the interiors from the harsh sun and sudden canyon winds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Orgrimmar need lizard hides from Thunder Ridge?",a="Thunder Ridge lizard hides are too thick and coarse for wearable armor, but their toughness suits them for building. Orgrimmar's peons line new walls and roofs with the hides to guard against the elements.",al={"what are the hides for","why rough lizard hide","what are thunder ridge hides used for"}},{q="Who is Kamari?",a="Kamari is a leatherworker in Orgrimmar who supplies sturdy pelts and hides to the city's builders and peons. The hides are needed for ongoing construction.",al={"who is kamari","kamari orgrimmar","who gives beasts of thunder ridge"}},{q="Where is Thunder Ridge located?",a="Thunder Ridge is a rugged canyon in Durotar, where lizards have thick hides Kamari wants for Orgrimmar's new buildings.",al={"where is thunder ridge","thunder ridge location","thunder ridge durotar"}},{q="Why are cured light hides requested along with lizard hides?",a="Kamari requests cured light hides alongside the heavy lizard hides to complement those materials. The request also tests your broader familiarity and skill with leather processing.",al={"why cured light hides","what are the cured hides for"}},{q="Why is Orgrimmar expanding so rapidly?",a="Orgrimmar is the newly established Horde capital, and its ongoing construction must house growing numbers of orcs and their allies. That growth creates a constant need for raw materials.",al={"why is orgrimmar building","orgrimmar expansion"}},{q="Can the lizard hides from Thunder Ridge be made into armor?",a="Thunder Ridge lizard hides are generally too thick and rigid for flexible armor. Their chief practical use is in construction.",al={"can you wear lizard hides","is rough lizard hide armor"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As the Horde settles in Durotar, supplies from the wilderness help fortify and grow Orgrimmar.",["race:Orc"]="As an orc, you know that Durotar's elements call for rugged materials gathered by hunters and laborers."},kw={"beasts of thunder ridge","cured light hide","durotar","kamari","leatherworking","orgrimmar","peons","rough lizard hide","thunder ridge"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar","subzone:thunder-ridge"},m={id=96875},src={"In-game quest text: Beasts of Thunder Ridge"},fv=true}
E["quest:96876"] = {t="quest",n="Ukor's Lost Pack",z="general",s="In southern Durotar, Ukor's Lost Pack lies abandoned in the wilds. Its owner fled a huge spider and left his supplies behind.",h="In southern Durotar, Ukor's Lost Pack lies abandoned in the wilds.",sec={{t="A Lost Belonging",b="In southern Durotar, you find a traveling bag with Ukor's name crudely stitched across its flap. In these harsh lands, discarded gear may point to trouble nearby.",sp=0},{t="Fleeing the Wilds",b="Ukor receives his pack in southern Durotar and tells how he lost it. A huge spider ambushed him, and he fled in panic, certain his supplies were gone. Finding the bag intact earns you Ukor's heartfelt gratitude and praise.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ukor?",a="Ukor lives in southern Durotar. When a huge spider confronted him in the wilds, he abandoned his belongings to escape.",al={"who is ukor","ukor durotar"}},{q="Why did Ukor lose his pack?",a="Ukor dropped his pack while fleeing a huge spider in Durotar. He thought his belongings were gone for good until you found the bag.",al={"why was pack lost","where did ukor lose pack"}},{q="Where does the pack need to be taken?",a="Take the recovered pack to Ukor in southern Durotar, where he waits to greet returning travelers.",al={"where to return pack","ukor turn in"}},{q="How is the pack identified?",a="Ukor's name is crudely stitched across the bag's flap.",al={"how is pack identified","pack stitching"}},{q="What kind of creature chased Ukor?",a="A huge spider ambushed Ukor in southern Durotar. Terrified, he dropped his possessions and ran for safety.",al={"spider in durotar","what spider attacked ukor"}},{q="What happens when you return the pack to Ukor?",a="Ukor is astonished and deeply grateful to see his belongings again. He calls you a true hero for retrieving the bag.",al={"ukor reward","how does ukor react"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a young Horde adventurer in southern Durotar, you can help locals like Ukor by recovering belongings lost to dangerous wildlife."},kw={"durotar","horde","lost pack","southern durotar","spider","ukor","ukor's lost pack"},rel={"npc:ukor","zone:durotar"},m={id=96876},src={"In-game quest text: Ukor's Lost Pack"},fv=true}
E["quest:96877"] = {t="quest",n="Halikor's Hoof",z="general",s="In the wilds, Halikor's Hoof is carried to Orgrimmar as proof of a hunter's triumph. The enormous severed hoof honors the hunter's victory over the formidable lizard.",h="In the wilds, Halikor's Hoof is carried to Orgrimmar as proof of a hunter's triumph.",sec={{t="Trophy of a Mighty Beast",b="Halikor's vast size is plain in the hoof taken from its remains. Hunters and warriors in the wilds have long claimed a piece of a formidable beast to prove the kill. The hoof leaves no doubt of the lizard's great size and strength.",sp=0},{t="Proof for the Capital",b="The heavy hoof is carried to Orgrimmar to announce the beast's defeat to the Horde. Strength and survival against the land's harsh predators matter in this warrior society. Presenting the trophy honors the hunter and assures the capital that a dangerous creature has been slain.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Halikor?",a="Halikor is a formidable lizard of immense size. Hunters claim its severed hoof as proof that the beast was slain.",al={"what is halikor","who is halikor","halikor lizard"}},{q="Why must the hoof be delivered to Orgrimmar?",a="The hoof is carried to Orgrimmar as proof of the beast's defeat and the hunter's triumph.",al={"why bring hoof to orgrimmar","halikor hoof deliver","where does the hoof go"}},{q="How is Halikor's Hoof obtained?",a="You claim Halikor's Hoof from the lizard's body after tracking down and slaying it in combat.",al={"how to get halikors hoof","halikor drop"}},{q="Why does a lizard have a hoof?",a="Halikor is a mighty beast of the wilds, and its enormous hoof proves you brought it down. No one has said why it bears a hoof.",al={"does halikor have hooves","why does a lizard have hooves"}},{q="Who in Orgrimmar wants Halikor's Hoof?",a="You carry the hoof to Orgrimmar as proof of your victory. No particular hunter or official is named to receive it.",al={"who accepts halikors hoof in orgrimmar","turn in halikor hoof orgrimmar"}},{q="What is the story behind the quest Halikor's Hoof?",a="The mighty lizard Halikor is slain, and its enormous severed hoof is carried to Orgrimmar. The trophy shows the hunter's prowess and brings the Horde word of the beast's death.",al={"what is halikors hoof quest","halikors hoof story"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Carrying proof of a mighty beast's defeat to Orgrimmar honors Horde traditions of strength and hunting prowess.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, bringing down the enormous Halikor shows the skill of the tracker and the thrill of the hunt."},kw={"halikor","halikor's hoof","horde","hunt","lizard","orgrimmar","trophy"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=96877},src={"In-game quest text: Halikor's Hoof"},fv=true}
E["quest:96895"] = {t="quest",n="The Argent Emissary",z="general",s="The Argent Emissary marks the arrival of two Forsaken representatives of the Argent Dawn tracking Scourge cultists near the Undercity. Their presence underscores both the shared fight against the Scourge and the simmering tension between the neutral order and the Forsaken authorities.",h="The Argent Emissary marks the arrival of two Forsaken representatives of the Argent Dawn tracking Scourge...",sec={{t="Wary Welcome",b="Two Forsaken emissaries from the Argent Dawn have recently arrived in Tirisfal Glades, setting local authorities on edge. Although the Argent Dawn opposes the Scourge, the local Forsaken remain suspicious of any armed, neutral order operating unmonitored within their sovereign territory. Even the shared heritage of the emissaries does little to soothe their vigilance.",sp=0},{t="Cultists at the Gates",b="The emissaries did not come to admire the ruins of Lordaeron; they are pursuing a group of Scourge cultists discovered lurking southeast around the outskirts of the Undercity. Determined to root out the agents of the Lich King before they establish a foothold, one of the emissaries has moved into position near the city gates to monitor the enemy's movements.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Argent emissaries?",a="Two emissaries representing the Argent Dawn recently journeyed into Tirisfal Glades. Notably, both of these emissaries are Forsaken who have aligned themselves with the holy and neutral order rather than solely serving the Undercity.",al={"who are the emissaries","argent dawn visitors","who came to tirisfal"}},{q="Why is the Argent Dawn operating near the Undercity?",a="They are tracking Scourge cultists spotted snooping around the outskirts southeast of the Undercity. The Argent Dawn is committed to hunting down Scourge remnants wherever they appear, even near major Horde settlements.",al={"why are they near undercity","argent dawn mission tirisfal","what are emissaries doing"}},{q="Why are local Forsaken wary of the emissaries?",a="Even though the Argent Dawn fights the common enemy in the Scourge, local Forsaken do not like neutral armed organizations operating unsupervised in Tirisfal Glades, regardless of whether those emissaries share their undead condition.",al={"why nervous about argent dawn","can we trust argent dawn","forsaken view of argent dawn"}},{q="What is the threat posed by the cultists?",a="A cell of Scourge cultists has been spotted lurking southeast near the city's outskirts. Because of the lingering threat the Lich King poses to free undead, their presence immediately drew the attention of both local scouts and the Argent Dawn.",al={"cultists outside undercity","scourge in tirisfal","who are the cultists"}},{q="Where did the emissary go?",a="One of the emissaries moved southeast toward the entrance of the Undercity, shadowing the movements of the Scourge cultists operating just outside the city walls.",al={"where to find emissary","emissary location","find argent dawn member"}},{q="Do the Forsaken serve in the Argent Dawn?",a="Yes. Unlike the Scarlet Crusade, the Argent Dawn welcomes all who are willing to fight against the Scourge, including free-willed undead who dedicate themselves to the cause.",al={"are forsaken allowed in argent dawn","argent dawn undead","can undead join argent dawn"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="While the Argent Dawn is neutral, Horde authorities keep a wary eye on their independent operations near the Undercity.",["race:Undead"]="Seeing fellow Forsaken choose the Argent Dawn over the Undercity serves as a stark reminder of divided loyalties among the free undead."},kw={"argent dawn","argent emissary","cultists","forsaken","scourge cultists","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"topic:argent-dawn","zone:undercity","topic:scourge","topic:forsaken"},m={id=96895},src={"In-game quest text: The Argent Emissary"},fv=true}
E["quest:96896"] = {t="quest",n="A Righteous Cause",z="general",s="A Righteous Cause witnesses a conversation between Leonid Barthalomew the Revered and Danitha Morr as they reflect on the defense of the Undercity against Naxxramas. Their exchange highlights the memory of Garek Bandarion, an undead hero who rediscovered how to wield the Holy Light as a paladin. The quest illuminates the rare Forsaken who strive to maintain their connection to the Light despite the skepticism of their kin.",h="A Righteous Cause witnesses a conversation between Leonid Barthalomew the Revered and Danitha Morr as they...",sec={{t="Reunion of Veterans",b="A Righteous Cause centers on a poignant reunion between two veteran defenders of Lordaeron: Leonid Barthalomew the Revered and Danitha Morr. Having survived the harrowing assault when Naxxramas unleashed the Scourge upon the Undercity, the two reflect upon their fallen comrades and the difficult path walked by Forsaken who still revere the Holy Light.",sp=0},{t="The Legacy of Garek Bandarion",b="The focal point of their shared memory is Garek Bandarion, the fallen hero after whom the keep was named. According to Leonid, Garek may have been the very first of the Forsaken to rediscover how to wield the Holy Light as a true paladin. While most Forsaken have turned away from the Light entirely, Danitha and her cohort dedicate themselves to preserving Garek's legacy and exploring the potential of holy unlife.",sp=0},{t="Return to the Plaguelands",b="Following his discussion with Danitha, Leonid prepares to depart the keep. His loyalties lie with the ongoing fight against the Scourge in the ravaged east, where he makes his station at Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands, welcoming any able-bodied adventurers willing to take up arms for the cause.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Leonid Barthalomew the Revered?",a="Leonid Barthalomew the Revered is a prominent undead member of the Argent Dawn. Though he operates out of Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands, he maintains ties with former comrades in Lordaeron who fought alongside him against the Scourge.",al={"who is leonid barthalomew","leonid the revered","leonid barthalomew lore"}},{q="Who is Danitha Morr?",a="Danitha Morr is a Forsaken leader who fought alongside Leonid Barthalomew during the Scourge attack on the Undercity. She now leads a cohort dedicated to carrying forward the teachings and holy legacy of Garek Bandarion.",al={"who is danitha morr","danitha","danitha morr lore"}},{q="Who was Garek Bandarion?",a="Garek Bandarion was a heroic Forsaken fighter who fell during the defense of the Undercity when Naxxramas attacked. Leonid notes he may have been the very first Forsaken to rediscover how to wield the Holy Light as a paladin.",al={"who is garek bandarion","garek bandarion lore","keep named after"}},{q="What happened during the attack on the Undercity?",a="When the necropolis Naxxramas launched an assault against the Undercity, Forsaken champions including Danitha Morr, Leonid Barthalomew, and Garek Bandarion banded together to defend the city against the Scourge onslaught, suffering heavy losses in the struggle.",al={"undercity naxxramas attack","defense of undercity","scourge attack undercity"}},{q="Can the Forsaken wield the Light as paladins?",a="While channeling the Light causes excruciating pain to undead flesh and is rejected by most of the Forsaken, Garek Bandarion demonstrated that it is possible for an undead warrior to wield the Holy Light as a paladin, a practice Danitha and her cohort continue to study.",al={"can forsaken be paladins","undead paladin lore","forsaken light wielders"}},{q="Where does Leonid Barthalomew go after this?",a="After visiting his friends and speaking with Danitha Morr, Leonid plans to return to Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands, where the Argent Dawn coordinates their crusade against the Scourge.",al={"where is leonid going","find leonid later","lights hope chapel leonid"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Garek Bandarion and Danitha's cohort represent an extraordinary development: Forsaken rediscovering how to wield the Holy Light as paladins despite their undead state.",["race:Undead"]="While Leonid and Danitha strive to reconnect with the Light, most of the Forsaken remain distant or hostile to the holy path in their unlife.",["faction:Horde"]="Leonid Barthalomew's presence highlights ties between Undercity's defenders and the Argent Dawn based at Light's Hope Chapel in the Plaguelands."},kw={"danitha morr","forsaken","garek bandarion","leonid barthalomew","light's hope chapel","naxxramas","paladin","righteous cause","scourge","undercity"},rel={"topic:paladin","topic:light","topic:forsaken","zone:undercity","subzone:light-s-hope-chapel","zone:easternplaguelands","topic:scourge"},m={id=96896},src={"In-game quest text: A Righteous Cause"},fv=true}
E["quest:96897"] = {t="quest",n="The Cult of the Damned",z="general",s="The Cult of the Damned is a task confronting mortal servants of the Scourge active within Tirisfal Glades. By culling Dark Neophytes and Dark Enforcers, defenders prevent the Lich King's insidious living agents from repeating the betrayals that originally shattered Lordaeron.",h="The Cult of the Damned is a task confronting mortal servants of the Scourge active within Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Mortal Agents of the Scourge",b="The Cult of the Damned represents the mortal face of the Scourge. Composed of living men and women who swore fealty to the Lich King, these cultists act where mindless dead cannot. In Tirisfal Glades, where inhabitants already contend with roaming undead and relentless zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, newly discovered cells of Dark Neophytes and Dark Enforcers signal an insidious attempt by the Scourge to re-establish a foothold.",sp=0},{t="Lessons of the Fall",b="Lordaeron was brought to ruin not merely by marching legions of the dead, but by neighbors who betrayed neighbors. The Cult of the Damned originally infiltrated villages, poisoned granaries, and orchestrated the kingdom's collapse from the shadows. Standing vigilant against fresh Cult activity in Tirisfal is essential to ensure that history does not repeat itself in the scarred lands of the north.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Cult of the Damned?",a="The Cult of the Damned is an organization of mortals who willingly serve the Lich King and the Scourge. Rather than being raised as mindless undead, these living acolytes deliberately spread plague, sabotage defenses, and practice necromancy in service to death.",al={"what is cult of the damned","who are the cultists","who are dark neophytes"}},{q="Why are members of the Cult operating in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Tirisfal Glades remains a contested borderland haunted by mindless undead, zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, and surviving pockets of Scourge influence. The Cult operates covertly in the region to undermine local resistance and advance the Scourge's dark designs.",al={"why are cultists in tirisfal","cult in tirisfal glades"}},{q="Who are the Dark Neophytes and Dark Enforcers?",a="Dark Neophytes are fresh initiates learning dark arts and rituals, while Dark Enforcers act as the muscle guarding cult operations and enforcing discipline among their ranks. Together they form active operational cells on the ground.",al={"who are dark enforcers","dark neophytes role"}},{q="Why are living servants considered worse than mindless undead?",a="Mindless undead act purely on instinct or direct command, but living cultists have chosen treachery with full awareness. Their ability to blend into mortal societies and infiltrate settlements makes them far more insidious than roaming ghouls or skeletons.",al={"why living cultists worse","living servants scourge"}},{q="What does the quest mean by not letting Lordaeron's history repeat itself?",a="Lordaeron fell not from an overt external invasion alone, but because the Cult of the Damned quietly poisoned grain supplies and betrayed their fellow citizens from within. Allowing cultists to gain a foothold invites the same catastrophe.",al={"lordaeron history repeat","fall of lordaeron cult"}},{q="How does the Cult relate to the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal?",a="The Scarlet Crusade fanatically hunts anything tainted by undeath, making both the Scourge and the Cult primary targets. This ongoing conflict leaves local inhabitants trapped between mindless horrors, zealous crusaders, and covert cultists.",al={"scarlet crusade relation","cult vs scarlet crusade"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Forsaken know all too well how the living cultists laid waste to Lordaeron and brought about their undeath. Stopping fresh Cult cells in Tirisfal is vital to securing the new kingdom.",["class:Priest"]="Priests of all disciplines recognize the perversion of faith represented by the Cult of the Damned, who trade sacred devotion for dark necromancy.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins swore oaths to eradicate the shadow that broke Lordaeron, making living cultists dedicated to the Scourge sworn enemies of the Light."},kw={"cult of the damned","dark enforcers","dark neophytes","living servants","lordaeron","necromancy","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal","topic:lordaeron","topic:scarlet-crusade","quest:381"},m={id=96897},src={"In-game quest text: The Cult of the Damned"},fv=true}
E["quest:96898"] = {t="quest",n="Remnants of War",z="general",s="Remnants of War tasks adventurers with recovering shattered shards of the necrotic focusing crystal blown off Naxxramas during its assault on the Undercity. Both the Forsaken and the Argent Dawn suffered heavy losses repelling the citadel, and gathering these dangerous fragments prevents Scourge cultists from reclaiming their dark magic.",h="Remnants of War tasks adventurers with recovering shattered shards of the necrotic focusing crystal blown off...",sec={{t="The Shadow Over the City",b="Not long ago, the dread citadel Naxxramas loomed directly over Tirisfal Glades, seeking to wipe out the Undercity and erase the Forsaken entirely. In a desperate defense, the Forsaken and the Argent Dawn fought back the floating fortress at great cost. During the clash, the immense necrotic focusing crystal mounted atop Naxxramas was destroyed, blasting fragments across the glades below.",sp=0},{t="Scattered Necromancy",b="Though the citadel was driven off, the fallout remains embedded in the soil. Scourge cultists have moved in to retrieve the necrotic crystal fragments, hoping to harness the dark magic still lingering inside them. Collecting these shards deprives the Scourge of raw unholy power, though clearing every piece from Tirisfal will take years.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the necrotic crystal fragments?",a="They are shattered pieces of the massive necrotic focusing crystal once mounted atop the floating dread citadel Naxxramas. When the crystal was destroyed during the assault on the Undercity, shards rained down across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what are necrotic crystal fragments","what are the crystals","necrotic crystals"}},{q="Why did Naxxramas attack the Undercity?",a="Naxxramas descended upon Tirisfal Glades with the direct aim of obliterating the Undercity and wiping the Forsaken off the map. A combined defense by the Forsaken and the Argent Dawn repelled the citadel, though both factions sustained heavy losses.",al={"why naxxramas attack","naxxramas undercity attack","what did naxxramas do here"}},{q="Why are Scourge cultists collecting the crystal fragments?",a="Scourge cultists are scouring Tirisfal Glades to recover the shattered fragments. Even broken and spent, the crystals hold lingering necrotic magic that the Scourge could salvage or repurpose for their unholy rituals.",al={"why do cultists want the crystals","what do cultists want with shards","scourge cultists crystals"}},{q="Is handling the necrotic crystals dangerous?",a="Yes. Despite being broken and largely drained of their primary charge, the fragments still harbor potent and volatile necrotic energy, making direct physical contact hazardous.",al={"are the crystals dangerous to touch","crystal physical contact","can necrotic crystals hurt you"}},{q="Who helped the Forsaken fight off Naxxramas?",a="The battle to defend the Undercity saw the Forsaken fighting side-by-side with the Argent Dawn against the forces of Naxxramas, suffering heavy casualties to secure the survival of the Forsaken.",al={"who fought at naxxramas","who defended undercity","argent dawn undercity defense"}},{q="Will all the necrotic crystals ever be cleared from Tirisfal?",a="It is unlikely that the area will be cleared quickly. Because the massive crystal shattered high above the glades, fragments scattered everywhere, meaning residents will likely be pulling shards from the earth for years to come.",al={"will tirisfal be clean of crystals","can they clear all the fragments","crystals left in tirisfal"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The assault by Naxxramas threatened the very existence of the Undercity. Recovering these fragments protects your people from a Scourge resurgence on your own doorstep.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Tirisfal Glades from lingering Scourge magic helps safeguard the Undercity and secures the Horde's vital foothold in northern Lordaeron.",["class:Mage"]="Even broken and largely spent, the necrotic focusing crystals of Naxxramas retain unstable magical energy that demands extreme caution.",["class:Priest"]="The lingering unholy corruption residing within the necrotic shards represents a dangerous blight that must be contained before it further defiles the land."},kw={"argent dawn","cultists","forsaken","naxxramas","necrotic crystal fragments","remnants of war","scourge","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:argent-dawn"},m={id=96898},src={"In-game quest text: Remnants of War"},fv=true}
E["quest:96899"] = {t="quest",n="Bandarion Keep",z="general",s="Bandarion Keep is a quest in Tirisfal Glades directing adventurers to meet Argent Dawn Commander Leonid Barthalomew at a secluded fortress. Once an Argent Dawn staging base against the Scourge, the stronghold is now maintained by Danitha and her order of paladins.",h="Bandarion Keep is a quest in Tirisfal Glades directing adventurers to meet Argent Dawn Commander Leonid...",sec={{t="A Summons to the Keep",b="Bandarion Keep is an Argent Dawn quest that directs adventurers across Tirisfal Glades to meet Commander Leonid Barthalomew. After aiding the Dawn against Cult of the Damned activity, travelers are pointed northwest toward the fortress to rendezvous with the commander and learn more about the order's ongoing crusade against darkness.",sp=0},{t="History and Stewardship",b="The stronghold once served as a vital staging post for the Argent Dawn as they combated Scourge incursions throughout Tirisfal Glades. When the Dawn shifted its focus toward the Eastern and Western Plaguelands, the fort was left behind and threatened to decay into ruin, until the paladin Danitha and her companions assumed stewardship over its walls.",sp=0},{t="Memories of a Mutual Friend",b="Upon reuniting with Leonid Barthalomew at the fortress, the commander reflects on the memory of the battles waged from its ramparts. Though relieved that the stronghold has not fallen into neglect, he notes that Danitha bestowed its name in tribute to a mutual friend.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Leonid Barthalomew?",a="Leonid Barthalomew is a commander of the Argent Dawn. He previously used Bandarion Keep as a base during earlier Scourge incursions into Tirisfal Glades, and he has returned there to meet with Danitha and her paladins.",al={"who is leonid barthalomew","where is leonid barthalomew","leonid bandarion keep"}},{q="What is Bandarion Keep?",a="Bandarion Keep is a fortress located in western Tirisfal Glades. Originally an Argent Dawn outpost during Scourge assaults, it fell into disrepair when the order pulled back to the Plaguelands before being restored and held by Danitha's paladins.",al={"what is bandarion keep","bandarion keep history","who holds bandarion keep"}},{q="Who is Danitha?",a="Danitha is a paladin who took over stewardship of Bandarion Keep alongside her order after the Argent Dawn withdrew east to the Plaguelands. She also named the keep in tribute to a mutual friend shared with Leonid Barthalomew.",al={"who is danitha","danitha paladin","who runs bandarion keep"}},{q="Why is it called Bandarion Keep?",a="The fortress was named by Danitha in honor of a mutual friend known to both her and Commander Leonid Barthalomew.",al={"why was it named bandarion keep","who is bandarion","bandarion name meaning"}},{q="Why is the Argent Dawn interested in Bandarion Keep?",a="Members of the Argent Dawn journey to Bandarion Keep to coordinate with Danitha's paladins and maintain vigilance against Scourge forces and Cult of the Damned activity across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"why go to bandarion keep","argent dawn tirisfal glades","bandarion keep argent dawn"}},{q="Why did the Argent Dawn previously abandon the keep?",a="The Argent Dawn withdrew from the fortress when they shifted their main forces eastward to battle the Scourge in the Plaguelands, leaving the structure empty until Danitha and her paladins stepped in to maintain it.",al={"what happened to the keep after the war","why did argent dawn leave bandarion keep"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Bandarion Keep serves as a stronghold stewarded by Danitha and her fellow paladins, maintaining a holy bastion against dark forces in Tirisfal Glades."},kw={"argent dawn","bandarion keep","cult of the damned","danitha","leonid barthalomew","paladin","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:argent-dawn","zone:tirisfal","topic:paladin","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:scourge","quest:96897","quest:376"},m={id=96899},src={"In-game quest text: Bandarion Keep"},fv=true}
E["quest:96986"] = {t="quest",n="The Grave Knight",z="general",s="In Tarren Mill, Melisara sends you after Atrexis, who raises the dead beneath Dalaran. She sees his work as an affront to the Forsaken.",h="In Tarren Mill, Melisara sends you after Atrexis, who raises the dead beneath Dalaran.",sec={{t="Melisara's Charge",b="Melisara calls Atrexis a traitor to the Forsaken. Beneath Dalaran, he raises an undead army. She asks you to defeat him, though she cares little for the mage city's fate. No one is beyond the Dark Lady's reach.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Grave Knight?",a="Atrexis is a necromancer in Dalaran, whom Melisara calls the Grave Knight.",al={"atrexis","atrexis grave knight","grave knight who"}},{q="Who sent me after Atrexis?",a="Melisara in Tarren Mill asks you to defeat Atrexis.",al={"who gave grave knight quest","melisara atrexis","who wants atrexis dead"}},{q="Why does Melisara want Atrexis defeated?",a="Melisara calls Atrexis a traitor to the Forsaken because he raises an undead army beneath Dalaran.",al={"why kill atrexis","why grave knight","forsaken reason atrexis"}},{q="Where is Atrexis?",a="Atrexis is in the City of Dalaran.",al={"atrexis location","grave knight location","where grave knight"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Melisara cares little for Dalaran's fate as she sends you after Atrexis.",["race:Undead"]="As an undead, you know Melisara calls Atrexis a traitor to the Forsaken. She says his undead army defies what the Forsaken stand for."},kw={"atrexis","dalaran","forsaken","grave knight","melisara","necromancer","tarren mill","undead army"},rel={"subzone:tarren-mill","subzone:dalaran","topic:forsaken","topic:mage"},m={id=96986},src={"In-game quest text: The Grave Knight"},fv=true}
E["quest:96988"] = {t="quest",n="Source of Power",z="general",s="In Dalaran, rogue constructs have turned on their makers, and Doctor Martin Felben sees a chance for the Forsaken. He wants their arcane cores for his work in Undercity and hopes to catch the Dark Lady’s eye.",h="In Dalaran, rogue constructs have turned on their makers, and Doctor Martin Felben sees a chance for the...",sec={{t="An Opportunistic Harvest",b="Dalaran’s rogue arcane constructs are laying siege to the magi, and the Forsaken are eager to exploit the city’s turmoil. Doctor Martin Felben, an apothecary in Undercity, seeks to scavenge the cores that power the sentries rather than aid the besieged sorcerers. Dalaran has long guarded its magical research, but its vulnerable sentries now offer an opening to opportunistic researchers.",sp=0},{t="Ambition in the Apothecary Society",b="Doctor Felben wants the cracked sentry cores to advance his standing within the Royal Apothecary Society. He hopes to infuse Forsaken abominations with volatile Dalaran construct magic and raise a devastating army of arcane-powered flesh beasts. Such a breakthrough, he believes, would win Lady Sylvanas Windrunner’s undivided attention and show his alchemical genius. He hopes to rise in the Society, where ruthless cunning and potent results dictate survival and rank.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Source of Power?",a="Doctor Martin Felben gives it. He is an apothecary residing in Undercity, the Forsaken capital, and seeks to prove his alchemical talents to the Royal Apothecary Society and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner by creating new military monstrosities.",al={"who gives source of power","source of power quest giver","martin felben quest"}},{q="What is happening in Dalaran during this quest?",a="Dalaran is under siege by its own arcane constructs, which have turned against their creators. The fighting creates an opening for outsiders to enter the city and salvage the magical components powering the rogue sentries.",al={"what happened in dalaran","dalaran under siege","why are constructs attacking dalaran"}},{q="Why does Doctor Felben want the construct cores?",a="Doctor Felben wants to use the arcane energy in Dalaran’s sentry cores to augment Forsaken abominations. By synthesizing arcane machinery with fleshcraft, he hopes to create an army of arcane-powered abominations and prove his alchemical mastery to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"why collect sentry cores","what does felben need cores for","felben sentry core plan"}},{q="What are the objectives for Source of Power?",a="You are sent into Dalaran to confront the rogue constructs and gather six Cracked Sentry Cores. Then you bring them to Doctor Martin Felben in Undercity for his ongoing experiments.",al={"source of power objectives","how many cores do i need","what to collect for source of power"}},{q="What is Doctor Felben's goal within the Royal Apothecary Society?",a="Doctor Martin Felben seeks recognition and rapid advancement among the Forsaken. He hopes a formidable force of arcane-infused abominations will impress Lady Sylvanas Windrunner enough for her to name him head of the Royal Apothecary Society.",al={"felben apothecaries goal","who leads the royal apothecary society","felben dark lady recognition"}},{q="Why are the Forsaken scavenging Dalaran's technology?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society and the Forsaken seek unconventional weapons and power sources to ensure their survival in Lordaeron. While Dalaran’s defenses collapse from within, Forsaken apothecaries can take its sophisticated arcane components without formally engaging the magi in open warfare.",al={"why forsaken want dalaran magic","undercity dalaran constructs","forsaken interest in dalaran"}},{q="Where are the Cracked Sentry Cores found?",a="Cracked Sentry Cores come from rogue constructs laying siege to Dalaran. You gather them from the hostile enchanted guardians across the city for Doctor Felben’s research in Undercity.",al={"where to find cracked sentry cores","cracked sentry core location","dalaran sentry core drops"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken rely on the Royal Apothecary Society’s ruthless innovations to survive in Lordaeron. Helping Doctor Felben advances the faction’s arcane fleshcraft research under the Dark Lady’s watch.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers in Lordaeron support the Forsaken’s efforts to secure advanced military assets against their rivals while Dalaran is crippled by its rogue creations.",["class:Mage"]="Mages may find grim irony in Dalaran’s constructs turning on their masters, while their arcane power sources are plundered for gruesome experiments in Undercity.",["profession:Engineering"]="Engineers can appreciate the craft behind Dalaran’s autonomous sentries, even as their delicate arcane cores are torn out to fuel horrific fleshcraft abominations."},kw={"abominations","arcane constructs","cracked sentry core","dalaran","doctor martin felben","royal apothecary society","source of power","sylvanas windrunner","undercity"},rel={"topic:royal-apothecary-society","subzone:dalaran","zone:undercity"},m={id=96988},src={"In-game quest text: Source of Power"},fv=true}
E["quest:97003"] = {t="quest",n="Chol'aruk the Ravener",z="general",s="In the Barrens, Gur'ak sends travelers after a colossal quilboar called Chol'aruk the Ravener. He believes the tales and asks them to return with its head.",h="In the Barrens, Gur'ak sends travelers after a colossal quilboar called Chol'aruk the Ravener.",sec={{t="The Legend of the Ravener",b="Razormane quilboar whisper of Chol'aruk, an enormous terror they call The Ravener. They say it lairs deep in a cavern at Thorn Hill, northeast of the local trails. By their account, it is roughly five times the size of an ordinary quilboar and driven by an unquenchable thirst for blood. Many dismiss the tales as campfire exaggeration. Gur'ak knows better from past battles with similar beasts.",sp=0},{t="Gur'ak's Warning",b="Gur'ak knows a beast of such size poses a real danger. He directs capable travelers to track Chol'aruk to its lair in Thorn Hill, end its threat, and bring back its head as proof. The Ravener is said to be ferocious and colossal. Gur'ak warns that only well-prepared groups should enter the cave to face it.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chol'aruk the Ravener?",a="The Razormane call Chol'aruk an enormous quilboar known as The Ravener. They say it is roughly five times the size of an ordinary quilboar, thirsts endlessly for blood, and lairs in a deep cave in the Barrens.",al={"who is cholaruk","what is cholaruk","the ravener"}},{q="Where can Chol'aruk be found?",a="Chol'aruk lairs deep in a cave at Thorn Hill, northeast of Gur'ak's encampment in the Barrens. Travelers must enter Thorn Hill's caverns to reach it.",al={"where is cholaruk","cholaruk location","where is thorn hill cave"}},{q="Who offers the hunt for Chol'aruk?",a="Gur'ak, an orc in the Barrens, sends travelers after Chol'aruk. He believes the rumors because he has faced similar beasts before.",al={"who gives cholaruk quest","who is gurak","gurak barrens"}},{q="Why is Gur'ak hunting Chol'aruk?",a="Gur'ak wants Chol'aruk destroyed because it terrorizes the region. He asks for the beast's severed head as proof that the Ravener has fallen.",al={"why kill cholaruk","cholaruk bounty reason"}},{q="Should I fight Chol'aruk alone?",a="Gur'ak warns travelers not to face Chol'aruk alone. Its reported size and bloodlust make it a formidable foe, so he calls for a prepared hunting party.",al={"can i solo cholaruk","is cholaruk hard","bring group for cholaruk"}},{q="What is the connection between Chol'aruk and the Razormane quilboar?",a="The Razormane quilboar speak of Chol'aruk with dread and call it The Ravener. The Razormane are a quilboar tribe living in parts of the Barrens, and quilboar are boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor.",al={"what are quilboar","razormane cholaruk","why razormane fear him"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Gur'ak, an orc, calls Horde travelers to hunt a dangerous menace in the Barrens with the battle cry Blood and thunder.",["class:Hunter"]="As a Hunter, tracking a beast said to be five times larger than an ordinary quilboar calls for keen instincts and preparation for formidable prey.",["class:Warrior"]="As a Warrior, you face a brutal creature with an unquenchable bloodlust. Be ready to brace against its overwhelming physical fury."},kw={"barrens","chol'aruk","cholaruk","gur'ak","gurak","quilboar","razormane","the ravener","thorn hill"},rel={"zone:barrens","subzone:thorn-hill","topic:quilboar"},m={id=97003},src={"In-game quest text: Chol'aruk the Ravener"},fv=true}
E["quest:97220"] = {t="quest",n="Philmor's Favor",z="general",s="Philmor's Favor is a delivery task in Stormwind prompted by an ongoing shortage of local dockhands. Couriers are asked to transport a delicate Gatehouse shipment to a secluded stockroom entrance situated on the second floor of Trias' Cheese shop in the Trade District.",h="Philmor's Favor is a delivery task in Stormwind prompted by an ongoing shortage of local dockhands.",sec={{t="Strained Logistics in Stormwind",b="A persistent shortage of dockhands across Stormwind has strained the city's routine freight operations. To keep local cargo schedules from falling behind, handlers look for reliable travelers willing to haul sensitive crates directly into the commercial quarter.",sp=0},{t="Discreet Deliveries at Trias' Cheese",b="The shipment is marked for the Gatehouse, but its drop-off is anything but conventional. Rather than passing through the main guardhouse entryway, the crate is carried up to the second floor of Trias' Cheese shop in the Trade District. The shop conceals a stockroom entrance used to quietly move supplies and ceremonial gifts intended for the guard's officers away from prying eyes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who needs help with this delivery?",a="The quest is offered to help relieve the cargo schedule around Stormwind when local operations run short on dockhands.",al={"who gives philmor's favor","philmor quest giver"}},{q="Where is the delivery destination?",a="The shipment must be brought to the stockroom entrance of the Gatehouse, which is reached through the second floor of Trias' Cheese shop in Stormwind's Trade District.",al={"where do i take the package","trias cheese delivery","where is the gatehouse entrance"}},{q="Who receives the shipment?",a="Elaine is the mistress at Trias' Cheese shop who is accustomed to handling discreet cargo shipments headed toward the adjacent Gatehouse stockroom.",al={"who is elaine","elaine trias cheese"}},{q="What is inside the Gatehouse shipment?",a="The shipment contains delicate goods bound for the Gatehouse. According to local handlers, deliveries often arrive with special discretion to facilitate ceremonial surprises arranged for guard officers.",al={"what is in the package","gatehouse delivery contents"}},{q="Why is a military stockroom accessed through a cheese shop?",a="The upper floor of Trias' Cheese shop houses an access point to the Gatehouse stockroom, allowing discreet passage and cargo drop-offs without parading goods through the main military entrance.",al={"why trias cheese gatehouse","secret entrance trias cheese"}},{q="Why are dockhands in short supply?",a="Staffing shortages have left dockside and cargo handlers short-handed throughout the week, forcing logistics coordinators to rely on passing travelers to keep deliveries on schedule.",al={"why short dockhands","stormwind dockhands shortage"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Assisting with deliveries throughout Stormwind's Trade District keeps city logistics moving smoothly while dockhands remain in short supply."},kw={"elaine","gatehouse","gatehouse shipment","philmor","philmor's favor","stormwind","trade district","trias' cheese"},rel={"subzone:trade-district"},m={id=97220},src={"In-game quest text: Philmor's Favor"},fv=true}
E["quest:97222"] = {t="quest",n="Gatehouse Goods",z="general",s="Gatehouse Goods is a local Stormwind task involving the transport of a shipment up the stairs within Trias' Cheese. Because the cheese shop shares a floor with the adjacent gatehouse, goods can be quietly dropped outside the stockroom door for guards to collect. The arrangement highlights the tight, interconnected urban design of Stormwind's commercial and defensive quarters.",h="Gatehouse Goods is a local Stormwind task involving the transport of a shipment up the stairs within Trias'...",sec={{t="Shared Quarters and Shared Floors",b="Gatehouse Goods entails a straightforward delivery task nestled within the bustling streets of Stormwind. The job requires carrying a gatehouse shipment up the interior stairs of Trias' Cheese. While the famed cheese shop is an entirely distinct business with its own separate inventory, its physical structure shares a floor with the neighboring gatehouse facilities. This architectural quirk makes the shop's upper landing a practical passage for moving supplies between neighboring quarters without disturbing the front avenues.",sp=0},{t="A Practical Drop-off",b="The delivery protocol is precise: couriers must leave the crate directly outside the stockroom door rather than carrying it inside. Regular gatehouse guards keep an eye on incoming traffic and assign personnel to retrieve packages as they return to their posts. Furthermore, the stockroom's door is deliberately built with inward-swinging hinges, ensuring that goods stacked outside will never block the entryway or hinder those inside from moving freely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Gatehouse Goods?",a="Gatehouse Goods is a local delivery errand in Stormwind involving Trias' Cheese and the adjacent gatehouse. A courier is asked to haul a shipment up the stairs and drop it off outside the gatehouse stockroom door.",al={"what is gatehouse goods","gatehouse goods quest"}},{q="Where is the shipment supposed to be delivered?",a="The delivery goes up the stairs inside Trias' Cheese shop. Although Trias' Cheese is an independent business, its building shares a floor with the gatehouse, allowing direct drop-offs right outside their stockroom.",al={"trias cheese gatehouse","where is delivery going"}},{q="Why is the crate left outside the door instead of brought inside?",a="The recipient specifically instructs the courier to leave the crate outside the stockroom door rather than bringing it inside. The gatehouse guards notice the arrival and send someone on regular pickup duty to carry it in as they return to post.",al={"why not deliver inside","who picks up the crate"}},{q="Why does the stockroom door open inward?",a="The stockroom hinge opens inward specifically so packages and goods dropped outside the doorway will not block the door or trap people inside.",al={"inward door trias","stockroom door hinge"}},{q="Is Trias' Cheese affiliated with the gatehouse?",a="No, Trias' Cheese is not part of the gatehouse and does not share stocking inventory. However, the two establishments share a building floor, making the upper stairs a convenient transfer point.",al={"is trias cheese part of gatehouse","trias cheese stock"}},{q="Who oversees the delivery of the goods?",a="The task is arranged by local operators who expect professional respect and mutual discretion, acknowledging reliable couriers once the drop-off is successfully carried out without obstructing the threshold.",al={"who gives gatehouse goods","who accepts the delivery"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Helping arrange discreet deliveries through Trias' Cheese keeps goods flowing smoothly between local Stormwind shopkeepers and adjacent gatehouse personnel.",["class:Rogue"]="Operating quietly around Trias' Cheese and minding subtle architectural details like inward-swinging doors is second nature to those who appreciate discretion."},kw={"delivery","elling trias","gatehouse goods","gatehouse shipment","stockroom","stormwind","trias cheese"},m={id=97222},src={"In-game quest text: Gatehouse Goods"},fv=true}
E["quest:97223"] = {t="quest",n="Bloodtalon Matriarch",z="general",s="The Bloodtalon Matriarch is the formidable leader of a swift and cunning raptor pack near Sen'jin Village. After proving too dangerous and stubborn to tame as an adult, she must be confronted so her offspring can be raised from the egg as loyal companions for the Darkspear tribe.",h="The Bloodtalon Matriarch is the formidable leader of a swift and cunning raptor pack near Sen'jin Village.",sec={{t="The Bloodtalon Lineage",b="The Bloodtalon pack is renowned along the coast of Durotar for its remarkable speed and keen cunning. Handlers in Sen'jin Village prize these traits above all else when seeking dependable mounts and companions for the Darkspear tribe. However, full-grown raptors possess stubborn and violent instincts, making adults almost impossible to domesticate once they have adapted to life in the wild.",sp=0},{t="Preserving the Bloodline",b="Despite multiple attempts to capture and train the Matriarch directly, her escalating aggression turned her into an outright threat to the surrounding territory. To safeguard the tribe while ensuring her exceptional bloodline was not lost, the trolls resolved to quell the dangerous mother and salvage her clutch, ensuring the next generation can be raised as loyal allies from the egg.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Bloodtalon Matriarch?",a="The Bloodtalon Matriarch is a fierce, adult raptor whose pack is renowned for speed and cunning. While earlier efforts were made to capture and train her, she eventually grew far too dangerous to handle, prompting the Darkspear trolls to secure her eggs instead.",al={"who is bloodtalon matriarch","bloodtalon raptor matriarch","matriarch raptor"}},{q="Why must the Bloodtalon Matriarch be dealt with?",a="Adult raptors are notoriously difficult to tame, and the Matriarch became too great a hazard to the local settlement. By subduing her and gathering her clutch, the handlers in Sen'jin Village can neutralize the danger while preserving her bloodline.",al={"why kill matriarch","why capture eggs","why hunt the matriarch"}},{q="Why not tame the Matriarch instead?",a="Adult raptors possess fierce, untamable instincts that make them nearly impossible to train once full-grown. Handlers achieve far better success by taking eggs and raising the hatchlings directly alongside their future companions.",al={"can you tame adult raptors","train adult raptor"}},{q="What will happen to the Matriarch's offspring?",a="The captured clutch is brought back to Sen'jin Village to be incubated and raised. The trolls intend to train the young raptors from infancy so they grow into reliable allies and mounts for the tribe.",al={"what happens to the eggs","matriarch children","raptors in senjin"}},{q="Where is this quest centered?",a="The quest centers around Sen'jin Village, the coastal haven of the Darkspear trolls in southern Durotar, where handlers work to breed and raise loyal raptors for their people.",al={"where is this quest handed in","senjin village raptor quest"}},{q="What makes the Bloodtalon pack special?",a="The Bloodtalon raptors are prized specifically for their swiftness and intelligence. These traits make their lineage exceptionally desirable as companions and mounts, provided they are trained before their wild instincts take hold.",al={"bloodtalon raptor traits","why bloodtalon raptors"}}},ang={["race:Troll"]="The Darkspear trolls of Sen'jin Village prize raptors as agile companions and war mounts, valuing the Bloodtalon lineage for its cunning and swiftness.",["faction:Horde"]="Raising local predators into loyal allies helps secure the Darkspear settlement and bolsters the Horde's presence on the Durotar coast.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters understand the difficulty of taming seasoned adult beasts and the necessity of raising predators from birth to forge a true bond."},kw={"bloodtalon","darkspear","matriarch","raptor","raptor eggs","raptors","sen'jin village","senjin","troll mounts"},rel={"subzone:sen-jin-village"},m={id=97223},src={"In-game quest text: Bloodtalon Matriarch"},fv=true}
E["quest:97225"] = {t="quest",n="Forgotten Loa Idols",z="general",s="Forgotten Loa Idols centers on recovering sacred religious effigies for the Darkspear trolls. These lost relics are urgently needed to resume vital spiritual ceremonies and preserve the tribe's enduring bond of reverence with their patron wild spirits.",h="Forgotten Loa Idols centers on recovering sacred religious effigies for the Darkspear trolls.",sec={{t="Sacred Remnants of the Loa",b="For the Darkspear trolls, spiritual balance is inextricably bound to their connection with the loa—the primal animal spirits and wild gods of their ancestral heritage. Forgotten Loa Idols involves recovering displaced religious relics essential for conducting tribal ceremonies. Without these sacred objects, spiritual leaders cannot perform the rites required to maintain harmony with their divine guardians.",sp=0},{t="The Darkspear Bond",b="While several hostile troll factions across Azeroth practice blood sacrifices or seek to compel spirits by force, the Darkspear pride themselves on maintaining a bond of mutual respect and humble reverence. To the Darkspear, falling out of favor with their patrons is a grave catastrophe, making the restoration of these lost idols a vital act of tribal preservation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Forgotten Loa Idols?",a="Forgotten Loa Idols is a task undertaken for the Darkspear trolls to recover missing sacred effigies. The idols are needed to resume critical tribal rituals and maintain honor with their revered wild spirits.",al={"forgotten loa idols","what is this quest","what is forgotten loa idols"}},{q="What are loa?",a="The loa are ancient, powerful primal spirits and animal deities revered by trolls. Troll culture honors them through offerings, prayer, and ritual ceremonies in exchange for protection, favor, and spiritual guidance.",al={"what are loa","troll gods","who are the loa"}},{q="How do the Darkspear view their loa compared to other troll tribes?",a="Unlike more brutal troll tribes who attempt to subordinate or sacrifice to dark deities, the Darkspear view their kinship with the loa as one of deep reverence, mutual respect, and spiritual duty. They believe their survival hinges on remaining in the spirits' favor.",al={"darkspear loa relationship","why revere loa","other tribes loa"}},{q="Why are the idols needed for tribal rituals?",a="The idols serve as consecrated foci for tribal rituals. Without them, the elders and spiritual leaders cannot properly conduct the rites required to commune with and honor their patron spirits.",al={"why need idols","loa idols purpose","why are idols important"}},{q="Why is keeping the loa's favor so vital to the Darkspear?",a="Darkspear elders fear that without proper rites and offerings, their tribe could lose the favor and protective blessings of the loa, leaving the tribe vulnerable in an already perilous world.",al={"what happens if not returned","falling out of favor","darkspear loa favor"}},{q="Who seeks the return of the Forgotten Loa Idols?",a="The quest is granted by a Darkspear troll spiritual guide who is anxious to resume communal ceremonies and secure the blessings of the tribe's ancestral patrons.",al={"who gives forgotten loa idols","quest giver","who asks for idols"}}},ang={["race:Troll"]="For Darkspear trolls, communion with the loa is central to spiritual life. Recovering lost idols ensures vital rituals can continue and the tribe remains in the spirits' good graces.",["class:Priest"]="Troll priests and witch doctors rely directly on rituals and ancestral sacred items to channel spiritual power and honor the loa.",["class:Shaman"]="While shamanic practices invoke elemental forces, troll spiritualists hold deep reverence for the wild gods and ancestral loa tied to their tribe's survival.",["faction:Horde"]="The Darkspear are foundational members of the Horde, and assisting their holy elders in preserving vital religious artifacts strengthens tribal unity within the faction."},kw={"darkspear","forgotten loa idols","idols","loa","reverence","rituals","spirits","troll"},m={id=97225},src={"In-game quest text: Forgotten Loa Idols"},fv=true}
E["quest:97234"] = {t="quest",n="Reading Room",z="general",s="At Roy's Book Club, members trade older literature for newer works from the Stormwind library. Wrapped books carry the exchange between the club and the librarian.",h="At Roy's Book Club, members trade older literature for newer works from the Stormwind library.",sec={{t="Literary Pursuits in Stormwind",b="Roy's Book Club gathers readers in Stormwind to discuss classical legends and provocative writings. To keep those talks fresh, members rotate their reading. They trade older or less popular works for newer volumes from the city's library.",sp=0},{t="The Library Exchange",b="Roy's Book Club keeps an established lending arrangement with the Stormwind library. Roy wraps the club's returned books in a distinctive way, and you carry them to the librarian. In return, the club receives a new list of modern tomes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Reading Room quest?",a="Roy's Book Club trades older books for modern tomes from the Stormwind library. You carry a wrapped bundle of less popular works to the library for the exchange.",al={"reading room quest","what is reading room","reading room story"}},{q="Who is Roy?",a="Roy runs a reading club where members discuss legends and literature. He arranges with the Stormwind library to rotate reading material and returns borrowed books in pristine condition.",al={"who is roy","roys book club","book club leader"}},{q="Where are the books being taken?",a="The books go to the library in Stormwind. The librarian recognizes Roy's club's distinctive wrapping and accepts the books in exchange for modern writing.",al={"where do books go","stormwind library drop off","wrapped reading material delivery"}},{q="Why are the books being exchanged?",a="The club trades less popular books for more modern and thought-provoking works through its borrowing arrangement with the city library.",al={"why trade books","why return tomes","exchange reading material"}},{q="How does the Stormwind librarian view the book club?",a="The Stormwind librarian is pleased with the arrangement. Roy's club returns loaned books in excellent condition and treats every text with care, even when its subject is provocative.",al={"what does librarian think","is book club allowed","library arrangement"}},{q="What are the club refreshments mentioned?",a="The club offers free refreshments to patrons and helpers who assist with book exchanges between the reading venue and the city archives.",al={"complimentary club refreshments","club perks","what refreshments"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, you can visit the Royal Library in Stormwind Keep. Civic and scholarly exchanges help sustain the city's intellectual life."},kw={"books","legends","librarian","reading room","roy's book club","stormwind library","tomes"},rel={"quest:579","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=97234},src={"In-game quest text: Reading Room"},fv=true}
E["quest:97236"] = {t="quest",n="Fang of Githyiss",z="general",s="The Fang of Githyiss is a rare and formidable specimen taken from a giant arachnid stalking the wilds near Aldrassil. Delivering the fang to a naturalist in the sanctuary allows scholars to study the monstrous creature and monitor the predatory threats nesting within Shadowglen.",h="The Fang of Githyiss is a rare and formidable specimen taken from a giant arachnid stalking the wilds near...",sec={{t="A Monstrous Specimen",b="The Fang of Githyiss is a trophy and biological sample harvested from the overgrown spider Githyiss, who lurks in the wilds of Shadowglen. A beast of such notable bulk poses a clear threat to young travelers venturing out from Aldrassil, making the recovery of its fang both a matter of safety and scientific interest. Rather than letting the creature's remains rot in the underbrush, resourceful scouts take the specimen back to the sanctuary of the great tree.",sp=0},{t="Research in Aldrassil",b="Within Aldrassil, night elf naturalists and scholars keep close track of the wildlife roaming the forest floor. Presenting a fresh, intact fang from a creature of Githyiss's magnitude provides crucial insight into the arachnid populations flourishing beneath Teldrassil's canopy. The local researchers welcome such unexpected discoveries, utilizing the massive fang to further their ongoing studies into the aggressive fauna of the glade.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Fang of Githyiss?",a="The Fang of Githyiss is a fresh specimen taken from the great spider Githyiss. Recovering such a massive fang provides rare research material regarding the arachnid predators dwelling in the wilds surrounding Aldrassil.",al={"what is fang of githyiss","githyiss fang quest","fang of githyiss item"}},{q="Who or what is Githyiss?",a="Githyiss is a notably large and dangerous spider prowling the forests near Aldrassil in Shadowglen. Its size makes it a menace to travelers and an intriguing subject for local naturalists.",al={"who is githyiss","githyiss spider","where is githyiss"}},{q="Who wants the Fang of Githyiss?",a="The fang is brought to a scholar in Aldrassil who specializes in the study of spiders. Such researchers welcome fresh specimens to advance their understanding of local arachnid biology.",al={"who turns in fang of githyiss","where do i bring the fang","spider researcher aldrassil"}},{q="Where is Aldrassil?",a="Aldrassil is the great bough and central sanctuary of Shadowglen, located within the crown of the world tree Teldrassil. It serves as a training ground and haven for young night elves.",al={"where is aldrassil","aldrassil location"}},{q="Why are scholars in Aldrassil studying spider fangs?",a="Scholars and naturalists in Aldrassil study local arachnids to better understand the ecology of Shadowglen and monitor the aggressive, oversized beasts dwelling beneath the canopy of Teldrassil.",al={"why do they study spiders in aldrassil","why study spider fangs"}},{q="Can Horde players turn in the Fang of Githyiss?",a="No. The quest takes place within Shadowglen, the night elf starting enclave atop Teldrassil, making it accessible solely to Alliance adventurers beginning their journey in Aldrassil.",al={"is this quest alliance only","can horde do fang of githyiss"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As defenders of Teldrassil, young night elf adventurers in Shadowglen help safeguard the boughs of Aldrassil by eliminating dangerous monstrosities and aiding local researchers.",["faction:Alliance"]="The study of dangerous wildlife across Teldrassil aids the Alliance by ensuring young recruits and researchers understand the corrupting threats nesting near Aldrassil.",["class:Druid"]="Druids keep close watch over the natural balance within Teldrassil, studying unusual predators and overgrown arachnids to monitor the health of the surrounding wilds."},kw={"aldrassil","fang of githyiss","githyiss","shadowglen","spider","spider study","teldrassil"},rel={"subzone:aldrassil"},m={id=97236},src={"In-game quest text: Fang of Githyiss"},fv=true}
E["quest:97237"] = {t="quest",n="Shelf Picked",z="general",s="In Stormwind, Roy Lewells sends a runner to gather books for a reading group in the Park.",h="In Stormwind, Roy Lewells sends a runner to gather books for a reading group in the Park.",sec={{t="An Errand for the Stacks",b="Roy Lewells has sent a note asking for several books from the Stormwind library. The librarian checks the list and confirms every title is in stock. The books are there for you to find and pull from the shelves. The librarian stays at the desk to keep the inventory records.",sp=0},{t="The Cellar Reading Club",b="Roy Lewells's note makes you an official courier, so you are not responsible for returning the borrowed books. That duty belongs to Lewells's establishment. Once you bring the volumes to the Park, Roy prepares them for lively after-hours discussions in a cellar. The books have an exotic smell.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Shelf Picked?",a="A librarian overseeing the Stormwind library gives you the errand after receiving Roy Lewells's note. The librarian confirms the requested books are in stock and asks you to pull them from the shelves.",al={"who gives shelf picked","shelf picked quest giver","where does shelf picked start"}},{q="Who is Roy Lewells?",a="Roy Lewells organizes a local reading group based in Stormwind's Park. He sends couriers to collect books from the library for the group's after-hours discussions in a cellar.",al={"who is roy lewells","roy lewells park","who is lewells"}},{q="Where does the book collection take place?",a="The books are collected at a Stormwind library. The librarian keeps the inventory records while you search the shelves for Roy Lewells's requested titles.",al={"what library is this","where are the books picked","which library shelf picked"}},{q="Why does Roy Lewells need these books?",a="Roy Lewells needs the books for an informal book club whose members gather for lively discussions after hours in a cellar.",al={"why retrieve books for lewells","what is lewells book club","cellar after hours book club"}},{q="Are you responsible for returning the borrowed books?",a="No. With Roy Lewells's note, you act as an official courier. His establishment is responsible for returning the borrowed books.",al={"am i responsible for returning books","who returns shelf picked books","library book return accountability"}},{q="Where do you bring the books once retrieved?",a="You bring the collected books to Roy Lewells in Stormwind's Park.",al={"where do you turn in shelf picked","where to find roy lewells","shelf picked turn in"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Courier errands between Stormwind's libraries and local gathering places are part of everyday life in the capital, even amid wider tensions in the kingdom."},kw={"book club","courier","library","park","roy lewells","shelf picked","stormwind"},rel={"subzone:park","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=97237},src={"In-game quest text: Shelf Picked"},fv=true}
E["quest:97242"] = {t="quest",n="Yelmak's Medley",z="general",s="Tensions rise in Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions, in Orgrimmar's Valley of Honor. Master alchemist Yelmak hopes a calming mixture made with local water plants will quiet his apprentice Whuut's clumsy frustration and stubborn pride.",h="Tensions rise in Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions, in Orgrimmar's Valley of Honor.",sec={{t="Trouble in the Laboratory",b="At Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions, young apprentice Whuut's pride is making trouble. Rather than ask for help when he is out of his depth, he breaks the shop's equipment in frustration. Master alchemist Yelmak seeks to calm the youth without bruising his fragile ego, so he devises a soothing concoction.",sp=0},{t="Soothing Local Flora",b="The recipe calls for fresh cattails and speargrass from the watercourses winding through the Valley of Honor. Kor'geld cannot leave the shop without alerting the suspicious apprentice, so someone discreet must gather the plants. The botanical mixture is meant to temper Whuut's attitude and restore peace to the alchemy shop.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Yelmak's Medley?",a="Kor'geld offers the task on behalf of the shop's master alchemist. He is found in Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions in Orgrimmar's Valley of Honor.",al={"who gives yelmaks medley","quest giver yelmak medley"}},{q="Who is Whuut?",a="Whuut is an apprentice mixer training under Yelmak in the Valley of Honor. His stubborn pride leads him to break equipment rather than admit he needs guidance or help.",al={"who is whuut","yelmak apprentice","whuut orgrimmar"}},{q="What is the purpose of the home-brewed medley?",a="The home-brewed medley is meant to soothe Yelmak's proud apprentice and temper his sour mood. It uses fresh cattail and speargrass cuttings.",al={"what is yelmak's medley recipe","what does yelmak want to brew"}},{q="Where are the ingredients gathered?",a="Wild cattails and speargrass grow along the freshwater streams and pools flowing through Orgrimmar's Valley of Honor.",al={"where to find cattails and speargrass","valley of honor cuttings"}},{q="Why doesn't Kor'geld gather the cuttings personally?",a="Kor'geld cannot leave Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions without Whuut realizing the shop staff is catering to his difficult temperament.",al={"why does korgeld need help","why cant korgeld pick plants"}},{q="Where is Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions located?",a="Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions is in the Valley of Honor, an eastern district of Orgrimmar. Its waterways run among warrior barracks and artisan shops.",al={"where is yelmak alchemy and potions","yelmak shop location"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you help smooth tensions inside Orgrimmar's Valley of Honor and its alchemical trade.",["profession:Herbalism"]="As a herbalist, you gather fresh cattails and speargrass along Orgrimmar's watercourses. Local plants can be used in concoctions and herbal brews."},kw={"alchemy","cattail","kor'geld","orgrimmar","speargrass","valley of honor","whuut","yelmak","yelmak's medley"},rel={"subzone:valley-of-honor"},m={id=97242},src={"In-game quest text: Yelmak's Medley"},fv=true}
E["quest:97243"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="Call of Fire sends an initiate through the winds of Skywall to seek out Olariaan Swiftburn, a shen'dorei Windshaper attuned to the element of fire. By bringing the Torch of Dormant Flame to the Shrine of Flames in the Gustberry Lowlands, the traveler begins learning to heed the call of the flame even within the domain of air.",h="Call of Fire sends an initiate through the winds of Skywall to seek out Olariaan Swiftburn, a shen'dorei...",sec={{t="Voices Across the Elemental Plane",b="Even within Skywall, the domain ruled by the air elementals, the broader currents of the Elemental Plane remain connected. The spirits of earth, water, and fire still speak to those with the sensitivity to hear them. The Windshapers count among their fellowship individuals uniquely receptive to these other forces, including Olariaan Swiftburn, a shen'dorei who fosters a deep attunement with the element of fire.",sp=0},{t="The Shrine of Flames",b="Initiates are entrusted with the Torch of Dormant Flame and directed toward the Gustberry Lowlands. Far out along the island rim, tucked west of Valanaar, stands the Shrine of Flames. There, Olariaan Swiftburn receives the dormant flame, offering to instruct aspiring students in answering the demanding call of fire amidst the churning currents of the air realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Olariaan Swiftburn?",a="Olariaan Swiftburn is a shen'dorei member of the Windshapers who possesses an innate attunement to fire. He resides at the Shrine of Flames tucked along the edge of Gustberry Lowlands, west of Valanaar.",al={"who gives call of fire","who do i take the torch to","olariaan swiftburn"}},{q="Where is the Shrine of Flames?",a="The Shrine of Flames is located in the Gustberry Lowlands within Skywall. It sits secluded along the edge of the floating island expanse, directly west of Valanaar.",al={"where is shrine of flames","gustberry lowlands location","how to find olariaan"}},{q="Why is fire being studied in Skywall?",a="Although Skywall is predominantly the elemental domain of air, it remains an integral part of the broader Elemental Plane. The spirits of all four elements still resonate across its reaches for those attuned enough to listen.",al={"why fire in skywall","elemental plane fire","isnt skywall just air"}},{q="What is the Torch of Dormant Flame?",a="The Torch of Dormant Flame is an elemental focus carried to Olariaan Swiftburn, serving as the conduit for an initiate seeking instruction in communing with fire.",al={"what is the dormant flame","torch of dormant flame","what item do i carry"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are practitioners within Skywall who navigate and shape elemental forces, counting among their ranks individuals of varied origins such as the shen'dorei.",al={"who are windshapers","windshaper faction"}},{q="What does this quest lead to?",a="Olariaan Swiftburn instructs disciples on how to heed the call of fire, emphasizing that mastering primal forces within the demanding domain of Skywall requires great perseverance.",al={"what does olariaan teach","fire training skywall"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Heeding the call of the elements is fundamental to shamanic training, and even in Skywall, communing with fire requires dedicated guidance and ancestral reverence.",["race:Skyborne"]="Though native to the winds of Skywall, Skyborne travelers and Windshapers recognize that balance requires acknowledging all four primal elements."},kw={"call of fire","elemental plane","gustberry lowlands","olariaan swiftburn","shen'dorei","shrine of flames","skywall","torch of dormant flame","valanaar","windshapers"},rel={"quest:1524","topic:skywall","topic:elemental-plane"},m={id=97243},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:97244"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="Call of Fire is a quest from the Windshapers commanding the execution of Skypriest Faladiel, a former brother who betrayed his order for power. Adventurers must track the rogue skypriest to the Gustberry Lowlands and retrieve his heart to fuel a powerful elemental ritual.",h="Call of Fire is a quest from the Windshapers commanding the execution of Skypriest Faladiel, a former brother...",sec={{t="A Traitor Among the Flame",b="Call of Fire tasks adventurers with hunting down Skypriest Faladiel, a former Windshaper who abandoned his loyalties to join the Al'Aketh. Once a dedicated caller of flame, Faladiel chose power over allegiance, turning on his fellow order members and leaving their blood upon his hands. The Windshapers dispatch an agent to deliver their uncompromising judgment upon him.",sp=0},{t="Reckoning in the Lowlands",b="Faladiel has secluded himself at a farm within the Gustberry Lowlands, situated northeast past Valanaar and just south of the Shrine of Akir. Slaying the rogue skypriest serves more than mere vengeance; the Windshapers require his heart as a grisly component for an upcoming ritual, believing that consuming the heart of a betrayer allows their sacred flames to burn with unmatched intensity.",sp=0},{t="Blood Paid with Blood",b="Upon receiving the heart, the Windshapers reaffirm their creed that blood must answer for blood. Yet Faladiel's demise marks only a single phase of their work, as the heart is merely one part of what must feed their ceremonial fire before their rites can be concluded.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Skypriest Faladiel?",a="Skypriest Faladiel was once a devoted caller of the flame and a stalwart member of the Windshapers. Like many among the Al'Aketh, he bartered his loyalties away in pursuit of power, turning on his kin and staining his hands with their blood.",al={"who is faladiel","faladiel backstory","skypriest faladiel lore"}},{q="Why do the Windshapers want Faladiel killed?",a="The Windshapers seek retribution because Faladiel betrayed them for power and murdered his former brothers and sisters. Under Windshaper creed, blood is paid with blood, making his execution both justice and retribution.",al={"why kill faladiel","why do windshapers want faladiel dead","faladiel betrayal"}},{q="Why do the Windshapers need Faladiel's heart?",a="Faladiel's heart serves as a potent ritual reagent. The Windshapers believe that the flames eagerly devour the offering of a traitor's heart, growing hotter and burning far more intensely when fed upon it.",al={"why faladiel heart","what is heart for","ritual reagent heart"}},{q="Where can Skypriest Faladiel be found?",a="Skypriest Faladiel has taken refuge at a farm in the Gustberry Lowlands, located to the northeast past Valanaar, just south of the Shrine of Akir.",al={"where is faladiel","skypriest faladiel location","find faladiel"}},{q="What is the creed of the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are an order bound by a strict and unyielding creed where treachery is never forgotten and blood is always repaid in blood. They practice flame-calling and sacred elemental rituals.",al={"who are windshapers","windshaper creed","windshapers faction"}},{q="What is the Al'Aketh's connection to this mission?",a="The Al'Aketh are a faction or group known to have members who traded away their former allegiances and honor in exchange for personal power, with Faladiel being a prominent example among their skypriests.",al={"what are alaketh","faladiel alaketh","alaketh traitors"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Priests may find Skypriest Faladiel's corruption and fall from grace notable, as his sacred calling was twisted from calling the flame into betrayal and murder of his own order.",["class:Shaman"]="Shamans attuned to fire and elemental rites will recognize the severe, primal nature of feeding a traitor's essence to the flames to intensify their sacred power."},kw={"al'aketh","call of fire","faladiel","faladiel's heart","gustberry lowlands","shrine of akir","skypriest faladiel","valanaar","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92595"},m={id=97244},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:97245"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="Call of Fire is a quest in which adventurers are tasked by Olariaan Swiftburn to locate and confront the ancient forest sprite Kuramaa in the Shen'dar Highlands. By invoking the sprite from its stump and defeating it with flame, travelers obtain its powerful wooden mask. The relic is then brought to the Gustberry Lowlands to ignite an eternal flame and commune with the spirits of fire.",h="Call of Fire is a quest in which adventurers are tasked by Olariaan Swiftburn to locate and confront the...",sec={{t="The Sprite of the Highlands",b="In the Shen'dar Highlands, local residents whisper tales of Kuramaa, an ancient forest sprite that resides inside a twisted tree stump at the summit of a hill near the regional Anchor Pylon. To draw the reclusive entity into the open, a challenger must strike the bark of its wooden home with a bare fist, demanding its presence and testing their mettle in combat.",sp=0},{t="Fire and Wood",b="Overcoming Kuramaa is no simple task for wielders of the arcane or divine, as the sprite possesses deep natural wards against most forms of magic. Only searing flame can pierce its ancient bark and bring it low. Defeating the creature yields Kuramaa's wooden mask, an artifact imbued with great power. Even so, Kuramaa is eternal, bound to reform and manifest again when called upon.",sp=0},{t="The Eternal Flame",b="Down in the Gustberry Lowlands, Olariaan Swiftburn awaits the retrieval of Kuramaa's mask. The enchanted wooden relic serves as the catalyst for an esoteric ceremony: igniting an eternal flame to commune directly with the spirits of fire, unlocking ancient elemental communion through the defeat of highland nature spirits.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Kuramaa?",a="Kuramaa is an ancient forest sprite dwelling within the Shen'dar Highlands. According to local highland residents, it makes its home inside a twisted tree stump perched at the summit of a hill near the Highlands Anchor Pylon.",al={"who is kuramaa","kuramaa lore","what is kuramaa"}},{q="Who is Olariaan Swiftburn?",a="Olariaan Swiftburn is the ritualist who tasks adventurers with confronting Kuramaa. Stationed in the Gustberry Lowlands, Olariaan requires Kuramaa's wooden mask to conduct a sacred rite of fire.",al={"who gives call of fire","who is olariaan swiftburn","where turn in call of fire"}},{q="How do you summon Kuramaa?",a="Kuramaa lives inside a twisted tree stump atop a hill near the Anchor Pylon in the Shen'dar Highlands. To summon the spirit out for combat, one must rap a fist firmly upon the bark of its stump.",al={"how to summon kuramaa","where is kuramaa stump","summon forest sprite"}},{q="What is Kuramaa vulnerable to in combat?",a="Kuramaa possesses an exceptional natural resistance to most forms of magic. Only the touch of flame can overcome its defenses and lay the ancient forest sprite low in battle.",al={"kuramaa weaknesses","how to beat kuramaa","kuramaa magic immune"}},{q="Why does Olariaan Swiftburn need Kuramaa's mask?",a="Kuramaa's wooden mask is recognized as a vessel of substantial power. Olariaan Swiftburn utilizes this potent relic as an essential component to light an eternal flame and commune directly with fire spirits.",al={"why collect kuramaa mask","what does mask do","kuramaa mask purpose"}},{q="Is Kuramaa permanently destroyed when defeated?",a="Kuramaa is an eternal woodland spirit rather than a mortal creature. Although its physical manifestation is defeated to claim its mask, the spirit endures and will manifest again when needed.",al={"is kuramaa dead","did i kill kuramaa","kuramaa respawn lore"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="While sharing a name with traditional shamanic trials of fire, this rite focuses on subduing a resilient highland spirit with flame to gather power for communion with fire spirits.",["class:Mage"]="Kuramaa is heavily resistant to most magical disciplines, forcing spellcasters to rely strictly upon fire magic to overcome the sprite."},kw={"anchor pylon","call of fire","forest sprite","gustberry lowlands","kuramaa","kuramaa's mask","olariaan swiftburn","shen'dar highlands"},m={id=97245},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:97246"] = {t="quest",n="Meal Appeal",z="general",s="Meal Appeal is an Orgrimmar errand given by Thatog, an orc tied down by his daily duties who worries his young son Migi is skipping meals to play. Travelers are asked to pick up a prepared lunch from Borstan's Firepit and track down Migi playing behind Grommash Hold. It offers a grounded look at domestic family life within the Horde's capital.",h="Meal Appeal is an Orgrimmar errand given by Thatog, an orc tied down by his daily duties who worries his...",sec={{t="A Parent's Duty in Orgrimmar",b="In the bustling red-rock avenues of Orgrimmar, everyday domestic life continues alongside the drills of grunt warriors and the machinations of the Horde. Thatog, bound to his daily post by duty, finds himself unable to keep a watchful eye over his energetic young son, Migi. Left to his own devices, Migi spends from morning till dusk racing through the city streets with his friends, consistently neglecting his stomach until returning home famished.",sp=0},{t="Delivery Behind the Hold",b="Rather than let the boy go hungry yet another afternoon, Thatog arranges for a hot meal at Borstan's Firepit. Because Migi will not bother to stop his games to pick up lunch himself, Thatog asks passing adventurers to act as a personal courier. The errand takes travelers from Borstan's cookfire to the open yard behind Grommash Hold, where Migi and the rest of the neighborhood youths can almost always be found caught up in their childhood games.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Thatog?",a="Thatog is a busy orc parent stationed in Orgrimmar whose daily responsibilities prevent him from stepping away to supervise his young son, Migi. Concerned that Migi is skipping his meals while playing with friends, Thatog enlists a willing adventurer to pick up and deliver food for the boy.",al={"who is thatog","quest giver thatog","thatog orgrimmar"}},{q="Where can Migi be found?",a="Migi spends his days playing games with his friends in the capital city. Thatog notes that the children can almost certainly be found roughhousing and playing behind Grommash Hold in Orgrimmar.",al={"where is migi","migi location","find migi"}},{q="What is Borstan's Firepit?",a="Borstan runs Borstan's Firepit, a local food establishment in Orgrimmar. Thatog asks travelers to stop there first and inform Borstan that they are picking up a pre-arranged lunch to deliver to Migi personally.",al={"where is borstan firepit","borstans firepit location","borstan lunch"}},{q="Why does Migi forget to eat?",a="Like many energetic young orcs, Migi gets so absorbed in playing all day with his friends behind Grommash Hold that he forgets or chooses not to stop by Borstan's Firepit for food, frequently arriving home at the end of the day completely starved.",al={"why does migi skip lunch","migi starving","why is migi not eating"}},{q="Who is Migi?",a="Migi is the young son of Thatog. He roams Orgrimmar playing games with a group of friends, prioritizing his playtime over returning home or picking up his prepared meals.",al={"who is migi","migi orc kid","thatogs son"}},{q="What is the objective of Meal Appeal?",a="The task requires stopping by Borstan's Firepit to let Borstan know Thatog sent you for Migi's lunch, and then delivering the food directly to Migi behind Grommash Hold so the boy does not go hungry during his games.",al={"meal appeal quest goal","what to do meal appeal","meal appeal task"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Life inside the walls of Orgrimmar is not solely about warfare and defense; everyday matters like feeding children running through the Valley of Strength keep the city's community alive.",["race:Orc"]="Even in the harsh culture of the orcs, parents like Thatog worry about their children neglecting meals for play in the streets of Durotar's capital."},kw={"borstan","borstans firepit","grommash hold","lunch delivery","meal appeal","migi","orgrimmar","thatog"},rel={"subzone:grommash-hold"},m={id=97246},src={"In-game quest text: Meal Appeal"},fv=true}
E["quest:97249"] = {t="quest",n="Favorite Food",z="general",s="In Orgrimmar, Migi's parents worry that play keeps him from eating enough. Borstan of Borstan's Meats prepares varied lunches with a taste Migi loves, so he and his friends stop to eat.",h="In Orgrimmar, Migi's parents worry that play keeps him from eating enough.",sec={{t="An Appetite for Play",b="Behind Grommash Hold, young Migi and his friends spend their afternoons playing along Orgrimmar's dusty paths. His parents worry that he often neglects his meals and does not eat enough. So they ask Borstan's Meats to prepare daily packed lunches. They insist that Borstan vary the meals, hoping Migi will learn to enjoy more than one favorite dish.",sp=0},{t="The Sweetglaze Compromise",b="Migi loves sweetglazed hog belly sandwiches and comes for lunch when the smokehouse fills with their sweet aroma. Borstan finds a way to honor his parents' request while keeping Migi fed. He crumbles the leftover crispy glazed cracklings into a savory seasoning that can be sprinkled over any dish. Behind Grommash Hold, the topping persuades Migi and his companions to pause their play and share lunch.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Migi?",a="Migi is a young resident of Orgrimmar who plays with friends behind Grommash Hold. His parents worry that he gets so caught up in play that he does not eat enough, so they arrange daily lunches from Borstan's Meats.",al={"migi orgrimmar","who is the kid behind grommash hold","migi lunch"}},{q="Who gives the quest Favorite Food?",a="Borstan of Borstan's Meats gives it in Orgrimmar. He prepares a varied meal after learning that Migi's parents worry about the boy's eating habits.",al={"favorite food quest giver","who starts favorite food","borstan favorite food"}},{q="Where can Migi be found?",a="Migi can be found behind Grommash Hold in Orgrimmar, playing with other local children. They often pass along the road to the stronghold and recognize Borstan's daily meals by their scent.",al={"migi location","where is migi","where to deliver migis lunch","find migi orgrimmar"}},{q="What is Migi's favorite food?",a="Migi loves the sweetglazed hog belly sandwich made in the smoker at Borstan's Meats. When the smoker is running, its aroma draws him toward lunch behind Grommash Hold.",al={"what does migi like to eat","migis favorite meal","sweetglazed hog belly migi"}},{q="Why do Migi's parents request different meals?",a="Migi's parents ask Borstan to vary his meals so their son will learn to enjoy more than one dish. They want him to eat enough without relying only on sweetglazed pork.",al={"why change migis lunch","migis parents request","why not sweetglazed sandwiches every day"}},{q="How does Borstan get Migi to eat different foods?",a="Borstan crumbles leftover crispy glazed cracklings into a topping for different dishes. Its familiar sweetglaze flavor helps Migi enjoy the varied meals.",al={"borstans trick","how does borstan get migi to eat","sweetglaze crumbs"}},{q="What is Borstan's Meats?",a="Borstan's Meats is a smokehouse and butchery in Orgrimmar. Borstan smokes hog belly sandwiches and prepares packed meals for city residents, including neighborhood youths.",al={"borstans meats orgrimmar","where is borstans meats","what is borstan"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="This shows everyday life in Orgrimmar, where tradesfolk and families look after the capital's children and make sure they are fed.",["profession:Cooking"]="In Orgrimmar, Borstan turns crispy smoked hog belly cracklings into a flavorful seasoning for daily meals."},kw={"borstan","borstan's meats","favorite food","grommash hold","hog belly","lunch","migi","orgrimmar","sweetglaze"},rel={"subzone:grommash-hold"},m={id=97249},src={"In-game quest text: Favorite Food"},fv=true}
E["quest:97250"] = {t="quest",n="Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected",z="general",s="Along the eastern coast, Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected follows rising Horde and Alliance tensions after a roadside tavern is destroyed. The incident warns Alliance forces, while the question of who threatened a vital civilian trade route remains open.",h="Along the eastern coast, Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected follows rising Horde and Alliance tensions after a...",sec={{t="Friction on the Coast",b="Along the eastern coast, Horde and Alliance skirmishes are reaching contested ground. Then a tavern on a major thoroughfare is destroyed. Ordinary travelers now face danger, and the region's stability is at risk.",sp=0},{t="Doubt and Disruption",b="Local leaders doubt Horde forces destroyed the tavern, though suspicion first fell on the Horde. Civilians and merchants rely on that road to move goods, so the Horde has reason to keep it open. They call for a closer look at who ruined the tavern.",sp=1},{t="A Warning to the Alliance",b="The affair ends in victory for Horde forces in the area, though questions about the tavern remain. The result warns the Alliance that any move into Horde territory along the coast will meet immediate resistance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected about?",a="Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected follows rising Horde and Alliance tensions along the eastern coast after a roadside tavern is destroyed on a vital trade route.",al={"what is this quest","wrongly blamed justly corrected lore","what happened in wrongly blamed"}},{q="Did the Horde destroy the tavern?",a="Horde leaders doubt their forces destroyed the tavern. Horde civilians depend on the route for trade and everyday travel, so its destruction works against their interests.",al={"did horde destroy tavern","who burned the tavern","who destroyed the tavern"}},{q="Why does the destroyed tavern matter to the Horde?",a="The tavern lies along a major road used by civilian travelers and merchants. Its destruction disrupts trade and movement, making the road a concern for the Horde.",al={"why is tavern route important","why does the tavern matter"}},{q="What caused tensions on the eastern coast?",a="The cause of the tensions is unclear. Friction between the Horde and Alliance has been mounting along the eastern coast, and conflict has reached Horde territory.",al={"why fighting on eastern coast","eastern coast conflict"}},{q="What message does this outcome send to the Alliance?",a="The outcome warns the Alliance that incursions into Horde territory along the coast will meet resistance.",al={"what did horde achieve","why warning to alliance"}},{q="Is there further investigation into the tavern's destruction?",a="Horde leaders seek a closer look at who destroyed the tavern. Its ruin threatens civilian passage, and the circumstances remain suspicious.",al={"what to investigate","will the tavern be investigated"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The outcome is a Horde victory and a warning against Alliance incursions. It also raises concern about protecting the trade routes and civilian roads the Horde depends on.",["faction:Alliance"]="The outcome warns the Alliance against further presence and provocations along the eastern coast."},kw={"alliance warning","civilian route","eastern coast","horde trade","tavern","territorial conflict","wrongly blamed, justly corrected"},rel={"topic:horde","topic:alliance"},m={id=97250},src={"In-game quest text: Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected"},fv=true}
E["quest:97253"] = {t="quest",n="Parts and Pieces",z="general",s="In Ratchet, Wrenix's machine lies in pieces after the pirates dismantled it. He sent it to face the raiders after five or six mercenaries failed to return alive. Now its scattered parts lie in the pirates' upper camp.",h="In Ratchet, Wrenix's machine lies in pieces after the pirates dismantled it.",sec={{t="A Mechanical Solution",b="Pirates along the coast south of Ratchet trouble the town's residents and tradesfolk. Wrenix the Wretched wanted to stop them without risking more coin on hired hands. Five or six mercenaries had already gone after the pirates and failed to return alive.\n\nSo Wrenix sent an automated machine to deal with the encampment on its own. He hoped the contraption could face the marauders without putting another living soul at risk.",sp=0},{t="Scimitars and Screwdrivers",b="The pirates south of Ratchet proved resourceful. They used their curved scimitars as makeshift screwdrivers and pried Wrenix's machine apart before he could send instructions to retreat toward town.\n\nIts delicate components now lie scattered in their upper camp. Wrenix needs someone to slip inside and gather five handfuls of the complicated parts from the dirt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Wrenix the Wretched?",a="Wrenix the Wretched is an eccentric tinkerer in the port town of Ratchet. After five or six mercenaries failed to return alive from the pirates south of town, he sent an automated machine to face them. The pirates dismantled it, and its parts lie scattered in their upper camp.",al={"who is wrenix","wrenix the wretched lore","ratchet wrenix"}},{q="What happened to the earlier mercenaries Wrenix hired?",a="Wrenix hired five or six mercenaries to deal with the pirates south of Ratchet. None returned alive, so he turned to a machine instead.",al={"wrenix previous mercenaries","what happened to the other adventurers","who did wrenix send first"}},{q="Why did Wrenix send a machine to fight the pirates?",a="Wrenix sent a machine because five or six mercenaries he hired failed to return alive. He hoped an automated contraption could face the pirates without risking another living soul.",al={"why send a machine","wrenix robot plan","why did wrenix use a contraption"}},{q="How did the pirates dismantle Wrenix's machine?",a="The pirates used their curved scimitars as makeshift screwdrivers to pry Wrenix's machine apart. They scattered its components through their camp and dismantled the contraption down to its frame before Wrenix could send it instructions to retreat toward Ratchet.",al={"how was the machine destroyed","how did pirates dismantle the robot","pirates scimitar screwdrivers"}},{q="Where are the complicated parts located?",a="The complicated parts lie scattered on the ground in the pirates' upper camp along the coast south of Ratchet. They left the pieces there after taking Wrenix's machine apart. Five handfuls must be gathered.",al={"where are the parts","upper pirate camp location","handfuls of complicated parts location"}},{q="Why does Wrenix believe this retrieval mission is safer?",a="Wrenix believes gathering scattered parts is safer than sending mercenaries to eliminate the pirates. The earlier mercenaries did not return alive; this work calls for slipping into the upper camp and collecting five handfuls of components from the dirt.",al={"is parts and pieces safe","why does wrenix think you will survive","wrenix survival odds"}},{q="What are the Complicated Parts needed for?",a="The complicated parts are pieces of Wrenix's automated machine. He needs them recovered so his engineering work and materials do not go entirely to waste.",al={"what are complicated parts","why does wrenix need the parts","what does wrenix do with the parts"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Engineers may see the irony in pirates taking apart a sophisticated machine. Wrenix's scattered parts show the danger of sending an automated prototype into a hostile camp without physical safeguards."},kw={"complicated parts","machine","parts and pieces","pirate","pirates","ratchet","scimitars","upper pirate camp","wrenix the wretched"},rel={"npc:wrenix-the-wretched","subzone:ratchet","topic:pirate"},m={id=97253},src={"In-game quest text: Parts and Pieces"},fv=true}
E["quest:97257"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Fire",z="general",s="Call of Fire is a shamanic rite of passage overseen by the Windshapers within Skywall. Initiates must aid in invoking spirits of flame, capture the Eternal Flame, and race against time to kindle the brazier at Valanaar. Successfully completing the trial proves one's respect for the elements and earns the initiate their Fire Totem.",h="Call of Fire is a shamanic rite of passage overseen by the Windshapers within Skywall.",sec={{t="The Ritual of Flame",b="The Call of Fire is a sacred trial of elemental attunement conducted within Skywall. Led by Olariaan of the Windshapers, initiates gather at the Brazier of Offering to invoke the spirits of flame from the Firelands. If the offerings are deemed worthy, the spirits ignite the altar with the sacred Eternal Flame, ready to be harnessed by a worthy aspirant.",sp=0},{t="The Journey to Valanaar",b="Once lit, the Eternal Flame must be captured onto a torch and carried swiftly across Skywall to Valanaar. Because the energies of the Firelands naturally weaken and disperse in the swirling atmosphere of the elemental air realm, the initiate faces a race against time to reach the Brazier of Eternal Flames before the flame dies out.",sp=0},{t="The Fire Totem",b="Rekindling the brazier at Valanaar proves the initiate's discipline and reverence toward the elements. In recognition of this achievement, the Windshapers bestow the initiate with their Fire Totem. As taught by the elders, elemental power readily serves those who respect it, but will mercilessly consume those who do not.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Call of Fire?",a="Call of Fire is a sacred rite of passage where an aspiring shaman assists in summoning spirits of flame and capturing the Eternal Flame. Carrying this sacred fire to Valanaar proves the initiate's respect for the elemental plane and grants them their Fire Totem.",al={"what is call of fire","call of fire quest","what is this quest about"}},{q="Who is Olariaan?",a="Olariaan is the ritual leader who directs the ceremony at the Brazier of Offering. He places the necessary materials upon the altar and calls forth the spirits of flame to ignite the Eternal Flame.",al={"who is olariaan","olariaan role","who starts the ritual"}},{q="What is the Eternal Flame?",a="The Eternal Flame is a manifestation of the spirits of flame called forth from the Firelands during the Windshaper ritual. Initiates must capture it on a torch and kindle the Brazier of Eternal Flames in Valanaar.",al={"what is the eternal flame","eternal flame skywall"}},{q="Why must the flame be carried quickly to Valanaar?",a="The raw elemental fire summoned originates from the Firelands, and its intense heat quickly dissipates within the air-dominated realm of Skywall. Initiates must move swiftly to ensure the torch does not burn out before reaching Valanaar.",al={"why does eternal flame dissipate","why rush to valanaar","flames of firelands skywall"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are an order residing in Skywall who guide initiates through elemental trials. They honor the balance and power of the elemental planes and oversee the granting of totems.",al={"who are the windshapers","windshaper faction"}},{q="What is the reward for completing the trial?",a="Completing the ritual and successfully relighting the brazier in Valanaar earns the initiate their Fire Totem, a vital tool and focus for channeling fiery elemental powers.",al={"call of fire reward","fire totem reward","what do you get from call of fire"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite marks a pivotal initiation for shamans, granting them mastery over fire and awarding them their Fire Totem upon completion.",["race:Skyborne"]="Administered by the Windshapers in Skywall, this trial reflects Skyborne shamanic traditions honoring the elemental planes."},kw={"call of fire","eternal flame","fire totem","firelands","olariaan","skywall","valanaar","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92579","quest:92595","topic:skywall","topic:elemental-plane"},m={id=97257},src={"In-game quest text: Call of Fire"},fv=true}
E["quest:97263"] = {t="quest",n="Your Package Has Arrived",z="general",s="\"Your Package Has Arrived\" is an urgent courier task concerning misplaced mail intended for delivery in Ironforge. A courier crate sent all the way from the Wildhammer stronghold of Aerie Peak was left abandoned beside a mailbox, addressed specifically to Eldrun Stormbreaker. Travelers who discover the parcel are tasked with completing its journey and seeing that it reaches its rightful owner.",h="\"Your Package Has Arrived\" is an urgent courier task concerning misplaced mail intended for delivery in...",sec={{t="Misplaced Freight",b="A postal mishap leaves several stray parcels abandoned beside a city mailbox, left out in the open rather than carried safely to their destinations. Among this jumbled heap of neglected freight sits an urgent parcel marked for delivery all the way from Aerie Peak, the mountain redoubt of the Wildhammer dwarves in the Hinterlands. Transporting goods between distant outposts across Khaz Modan and Lordaeron can be perilous and chaotic, meaning mislaid parcels occasionally require an alert passerby to step in and finish the delivery route properly.",sp=0},{t="A Parcel for Eldrun Stormbreaker",b="The misplaced package bears clear labeling designating Ironforge as its destination and Eldrun Stormbreaker as its final recipient. Marked explicitly with an urgent notice, whatever cargo the bundle holds was deemed important enough to be expedited from the rustic peaks of the Hinterlands to the subterranean halls of the dwarven capital. Taking up the crate means taking on the responsibility of the postal service, ensuring that Eldrun receives what was sent across mountain ridges and wild hills before it can be stolen or forgotten completely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the package found by the mailbox?",a="The parcel is an urgent delivery sent from Aerie Peak in the Hinterlands, addressed to a recipient named Eldrun Stormbreaker in Ironforge. It appears to have been misplaced alongside several other packages near a mailbox instead of reaching its final destination through normal courier handling.",al={"what is the package","misplaced package","what was left by the mailbox","mailbox package"}},{q="Who is Eldrun Stormbreaker?",a="Eldrun Stormbreaker is the designated recipient of the misplaced parcel within the dwarven capital of Ironforge. While the package itself is clearly marked with their name and noted as urgent, little else is disclosed on the shipping label until the delivery is safely placed in their hands.",al={"who is eldrun stormbreaker","eldrun stormbreaker","who gets the package","package recipient"}},{q="Where was the parcel sent from?",a="The shipment originated in Aerie Peak, the prominent settlement and fortress of the Wildhammer clan situated high in the mountains of the Hinterlands. The label shows it traveled across regions toward Ironforge before being misplaced near a local mailbox along the route.",al={"where did the package come from","package origin","where was it mailed from","aerie peak delivery"}},{q="Why was the parcel marked urgent?",a="The shipping label bears an urgent tag because whoever sent the crate from Aerie Peak considered its contents or arrival time of high importance. Given the distance across territories between the Wildhammer highlands and the city of Ironforge, delayed cargo could cause significant trouble for Eldrun Stormbreaker.",al={"why is it urgent","urgent delivery","what is inside the package","why urgent"}},{q="Why was the package left sitting by a mailbox?",a="Several parcels were inexplicably left abandoned by the mailbox rather than being sorted and distributed by local couriers. Heavy courier traffic, logistical confusion, or simple negligence often lead to freight being temporarily mislaid in transit, leaving it up to helpful adventurers to resolve the oversight.",al={"why was it abandoned","why was package dropped","lost mail","misplaced packages"}},{q="Where does this package need to be delivered?",a="The package is marked for delivery to Ironforge, directed specifically into the hands of Eldrun Stormbreaker. Anyone who discovers the misplaced freight must take on the courier's role and carry the urgent bundle directly to Eldrun inside the subterranean dwarven fortress city.",al={"where do i deliver it","package destination","delivery location","who do i bring the package to"}},{q="What connection exists between Aerie Peak and Ironforge?",a="Aerie Peak serves as the heart of the Wildhammer dwarves, while Ironforge is the grand seat of the Bronzebeard clan. Although the clans maintain distinct customs and homes, regular correspondence, trade, and kinship ties flow between the crags of the Hinterlands and the Great Forge under the mountain.",al={"aerie peak and ironforge","wildhammer and bronzebeard trade","ironforge aerie peak connection","why send mail from aerie peak"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarves will recognize the strong ties between the Bronzebeards of Ironforge and their Wildhammer cousins in Aerie Peak, making the safe arrival of this parcel a matter of clan respect.",["faction:Alliance"]="Keeping open and reliable communication routes between the Hinterlands and Ironforge is vital for Alliance interests across Khaz Modan and northern territories."},kw={"aerie peak","eldrun stormbreaker","ironforge","mailbox","misplaced package","urgent delivery","wildhammer","your package has arrived"},rel={"zone:ironforge"},m={id=97263},src={"In-game quest text: Your Package Has Arrived"},fv=true}
E["quest:97275"] = {t="quest",n="Whuut's the Rush",z="general",s="Yelmak sends you to carry a tonic to Whuut, whose eagerness has outrun his care. His apprentice reaches for rare herbs before learning what common plants can do.",h="Yelmak sends you to carry a tonic to Whuut, whose eagerness has outrun his care.",sec={{t="The Eager Prodigy",b="Whuut has a gift for alchemy, and the stirring rod comes naturally to him. Yet impatience leads him to spend the shop’s rarest reagents before he has mastered the basics. He reaches for legendary plants before learning how common herbs must be balanced, treating alchemy as a race.",sp=0},{t="A Lesson in a Vial",b="Yelmak answers his apprentice’s haste with a lesson in a vial. He brews a refined tonic from common speargrass and cattail using a quick method. Whuut is daydreaming about Black Lotus and Mountain Silversage when the tonic reaches his hands. Its simple ingredients show how much skill, patience, and respect for everyday plants can matter.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Whuut?",a="Whuut is Yelmak’s young apprentice and a gifted alchemist. Eager to experiment with rare herbs, he rushes past the trade’s fundamentals.",al={"who is whuut","whuut backstory","about whuut"}},{q="Who is Yelmak?",a="Yelmak is an experienced alchemist who mentors Whuut in the shop. He worries that the young alchemist is wasting precious supplies.",al={"who gives this quest","yelmak role","who is yelmak"}},{q="Why does Yelmak send you with a potion for Whuut?",a="Yelmak sends you with a tonic to teach Whuut patience and respect for the basics. He made it with speargrass and cattail, ordinary plants Whuut has been overlooking.",al={"what is yelmak's lesson","why does yelmak send the potion","point of the quest"}},{q="What ingredients did Yelmak use in his potion?",a="Yelmak used speargrass and cattail to brew the tonic. His careful method makes these common plants useful.",al={"what is in the potion","yelmak potion reagents","quick tonic ingredients"}},{q="Which rare reagents was Whuut trying to work with?",a="Whuut was reaching for Black Lotus and Mountain Silversage. He had passed over everyday ingredients in favor of these rare plants.",al={"what was whuut brewing","rare herbs whuut wants","what reagents did whuut use"}},{q="Does Whuut learn his lesson?",a="After tasting the tonic and hearing its recipe, Whuut is surprised that common plants could make such a brew. He admits Yelmak is right and resolves to pay closer attention to the everyday plants he had dismissed.",al={"does whuut change his mind","whuut reaction to potion","outcome of whuut's the rush"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="As an herbalist, you know that learning the properties of everyday plants such as speargrass and cattail matters as much as seeking rare blooms.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you can learn from Yelmak’s patience and resourcefulness with local Kalimdor ingredients."},kw={"alchemy","black lotus","cattail","herbalism","mountain silversage","potion","speargrass","whuut","yelmak"},m={id=97275},src={"In-game quest text: Whuut's the Rush"},fv=true}
E["quest:97277"] = {t="quest",n="Grund and Gozwin",z="general",s="Grund and Gozwin recounts an ambush at a campsite in the snowy northwestern hills, where a dwarf and a gnome were attacked by a predatory snow leopard. Although the gnome survived thanks to his partner's shotgun, his prized mechanic's log was left behind in the commotion. Adventurers are sent into the hills to recover the delicate notes and slay the stalking beast.",h="Grund and Gozwin recounts an ambush at a campsite in the snowy northwestern hills, where a dwarf and a gnome...",sec={{t="An Ambush by the Fire",b="While traveling together in the snowy hills, the dwarf Grund and his gnomish companion Gozwin set up an encampment for the evening. As Grund turned his back to prep a boar over the fire, a snow leopard prowler lunged from the brush and grabbed Gozwin in its jaws. Reacting instantly, Grund grabbed his trusty shotgun and blasted a round over the beast's head, startling the predator into dropping the gnome and fleeing into the cold crags.",sp=0},{t="A Tinkerer's Lost Work",b="Although Gozwin escaped the jaws of the cat with deep scratches rather than fatal injuries, their camp was thoroughly disrupted. In the chaos of the retreat, Gozwin dropped his prized Mechanic's Log—a technical notebook containing critical schematics for his mecha-walker. Terribly anxious that someone might discover his engineering secrets, the battered gnome could not rest until an adventurer agreed to venture northwest into the hills to reclaim his book and slay the prowling predator.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are Grund and Gozwin?",a="Grund is a shotgun-wielding dwarf camper, and Gozwin is his gnomish engineer companion. While setting up camp in the snowy hills to roast a boar, they were ambushed by a prowling snow leopard, leaving Gozwin wounded and their gear scattered.",al={"who are grund and gozwin","grund gozwin"}},{q="What attacked Gozwin at camp?",a="A fierce snow leopard prowler ambushed Gozwin from behind while Grund was preparing dinner. The beast sank its teeth into the gnome and attempted to drag him off before Grund fired a warning shot that scared the cat away.",al={"what attacked gozwin","who attacked gozwin"}},{q="Why is Gozwin's Mechanic's Log so important to him?",a="Gozwin's Mechanic's Log contains vital technical schematics and notes regarding his mecha-walker. Gozwin feared that if anyone else got their hands on his personal engineering notes, his project would be completely ruined.",al={"why is the log important","mechanic log gozwin"}},{q="How badly hurt was Gozwin in the attack?",a="Yes, Gozwin survived the ambush thanks to Grund's quick shotgun shot. He escaped with deep scratches and severe shock, though he is recovering safely while Grund tends to him.",al={"is gozwin okay","did gozwin survive"}},{q="Where is Grund and Gozwin's camp located?",a="The pair's campsite lies nestled in the hills to the northwest. Adventurers are dispatched to the camp to retrieve the lost logbook and eliminate the leopard prowler that stalks the area.",al={"where is the camp","grund camp location"}},{q="What happened to the meal Grund was preparing?",a="After being scared off Gozwin by Grund's gunfire, the predatory snow leopard returned to the abandoned campsite and scavenged the roast boar that Grund had been preparing for dinner.",al={"what happened to the boar roast","leopard eating boar"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Gozwin embodies the quintessential gnome tinkerer whose first instinct after surviving a vicious predator attack is worrying about the safety of his mechano-strider schematics.",["race:Dwarf"]="Grund's quick reaction with a shotgun and stubborn campfire camaraderie reflect the steady loyalty dwarves bring when traveling alongside their gnomish allies.",["profession:Engineering"]="Gozwin's anxiety over losing his mechanic's log shows how guarded tinkerers are regarding their mecha-walker designs and proprietary notes."},kw={"dwarf","gnome engineer","gozwin","grund","mecha-walker","mechanic's log","snow leopard prowler"},m={id=97277},src={"In-game quest text: Grund and Gozwin"},fv=true}
E["quest:97281"] = {t="quest",n="A Simmering Storm",z="general",s="A Simmering Storm begins when an adventurer recovers a strange, spark-emitting relic known as the Dull Stormy Orb from the harpies of Drygulch Ravine. Bearing the swirling miniature storm to the goblin tinker Rezlak provides insight into the harpies' odd trinkets and earns the interest of Ratchet's enterprising salvagers.",h="A Simmering Storm begins when an adventurer recovers a strange, spark-emitting relic known as the Dull Stormy...",sec={{t="A Spark in the Dust",b="While contending with the aggressive Dustwind harpies nesting throughout Drygulch Ravine, travelers may come across an unusual curiosity: the Dull Stormy Orb. Though its outer surface appears muted and worn, holding the object reveals a steady vibration of raw electrical current. Inside the sphere, dark mist churns endlessly, illuminated at random intervals by sharp internal sparks. It is unmistakable proof that the harpies were manipulating storm magic through strange relics.",sp=0},{t="Goblin Curiosity",b="Rather than leaving such volatile magic in the talons of wild harpies, the orb can be brought directly to Rezlak. Stationed at the entrance of Drygulch Ravine, the pragmatic goblin tinker takes an immediate interest in the strange relic, noting that its discovery recalls old advice from his employer in Ratchet, Chief Engineer Gazlowe. Whatever the harpies intended to brew with the miniature storm, it now sits in goblin hands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Dull Stormy Orb?",a="The Dull Stormy Orb is a curious magical curio recovered from a harpy in Durotar. Despite its dull exterior, it buzzes with a faint electrical current, swirling dark mist, and periodic sparks contained within.",al={"what is dull stormy orb","stormy orb","orb from harpies"}},{q="Who wants the Dull Stormy Orb?",a="The orb is brought to Rezlak, a goblin scout stationed just outside Drygulch Ravine in Durotar, who has a keen eye for peculiar curiosities and valuable salvage.",al={"who takes dull stormy orb","turn in stormy orb","where to bring orb"}},{q="Why did the harpies have this orb?",a="The precise purpose remains unknown, but the harpies of Drygulch Ravine were clearly employing the orb to channel or contain electrical and stormy elemental energies.",al={"what were harpies doing with orb","why do harpies have storm orb","harpy orb purpose"}},{q="Who is Rezlak?",a="Rezlak is a goblin operative working in northern Durotar near Drygulch Ravine, affiliated with the Steamwheedle Cartel and Chief Engineer Gazlowe of Ratchet.",al={"who is rezlak","rezlak durotar","goblin near drygulch"}},{q="Where is Drygulch Ravine?",a="Drygulch Ravine is a rugged canyon system in northern Durotar, infested with hostile Dustwind harpies that harass travelers and disrupt local trade and settlements.",al={"where is drygulch ravine","drygulch location","harpy canyon durotar"}},{q="What does Rezlak think of the orb?",a="Upon seeing the orb, Rezlak is immediately reminded of lessons and remarks from his boss, Chief Engineer Gazlowe, regarding peculiar and potentially profitable oddities.",al={"what does rezlak say about gazlowe","rezlak gazlowe connection"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers aiding the goblin Rezlak near Drygulch Ravine help secure mysterious magical artifacts recovered from hostile harpies threatening Durotar's canyons.",["class:Shaman"]="The miniature electrical storm contained within the orb mirrors primal elemental forces, though harnessing it in this crude vessel raises questions about the harpies' methods.",["profession:Engineering"]="Tinkers and engineers like Rezlak are naturally drawn to odd curiosities that spark with erratic electrical charges and swirling internal mists."},kw={"a simmering storm","drygulch ravine","dull stormy orb","durotar","dustwind","gazlowe","harpy","rezlak"},rel={"subzone:drygulch-ravine","npc:rezlak","topic:harpy"},m={id=97281},src={"In-game quest text: A Simmering Storm"},fv=true}
E["quest:97282"] = {t="quest",n="Stormy Potential",z="general",s="Stormy Potential is a quest in Durotar given by the goblin Rezlak near Drygulch Ravine. Hoping to protect his trade caravan against marauding harpies, Rezlak seeks to supercharge a depleted orb using the potent electrical organs of native thunder lizards.",h="Stormy Potential is a quest in Durotar given by the goblin Rezlak near Drygulch Ravine.",sec={{t="Seizing Opportunity",b="Near the shadow of Drygulch Ravine in Durotar, the goblin Rezlak found himself facing two problems: a caravan under constant threat from local harpies, and an intact magical orb completely depleted of power. Remembering the pragmatic advice of Ratchet's chief engineer Gazlowe—to seize opportunity whenever it knocks—Rezlak hatched an ambitious plan to solve both problems at once by supercharging the dormant device.",sp=0},{t="Harnessing the Storm",b="To restore the drained orb, Rezlak required a potent, immediate source of raw electricity. The gorge to the west of Drygulch Ravine is home to thunder lizards—creatures known for internal organs capable of generating and conducting lethal jolts of lightning. By sending an adventurer to harvest these charged organs, Rezlak hoped to safely channel their volatile current directly into the device, giving his vulnerable supply wagons a formidable defense against future harpy ambushes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Stormy Potential?",a="Stormy Potential is offered by Rezlak, a goblin merchant and tinkerer stationed near Drygulch Ravine in Durotar. Taking advice from Gazlowe to seize opportunity when it knocks, he seeks to restore an unpowered orb using the natural electrical charge of local beasts.",al={"who gives stormy potential","where is rezlak","rezlak quest giver"}},{q="What is Rezlak trying to charge with the organs?",a="Rezlak possesses an orb that is intact but completely drained of power. He hopes to supercharge it back to full strength using the electrical organs of thunder lizards so he can defend his caravan from the harpies harassing the area.",al={"what is rezlak charging","why need thunder lizard organs","rezlak orb purpose"}},{q="Where are the thunder lizards found?",a="Thunder lizards inhabit a gorge to the west of Rezlak's position near Drygulch Ravine in Durotar. Hunters must harvest their electrical organs to bring back to Rezlak.",al={"where find thunder lizards","thunder lizard location durotar","where are the organs"}},{q="Why are thunder lizards dangerous to harvest?",a="Despite their thunderous name, thunder lizards emit dangerous bolts of lightning rather than just roaring. As Rezlak warns, their electrical blasts are a painful lesson most travelers only get to learn once.",al={"why thunder lizards lightning","do thunder lizards use lightning"}},{q="Why does Rezlak fear the harpies?",a="Rezlak's caravan was ambushed by the hostile harpies infesting Drygulch Ravine. He intends to use the recharged orb as a deterrent or weapon to make the harpies think twice before attacking his shipments again.",al={"why rezlak need defense","who attacked rezlak","harpy caravan attack"}},{q="What is Rezlak's connection to Gazlowe?",a="Rezlak views Gazlowe, the chief engineer of Ratchet, as an experienced mentor and business inspiration. Rezlak explicitly quotes Gazlowe's business philosophy about seizing opportunity when formulating his plan to supercharge the orb.",al={"who is gazlowe to rezlak","rezlak gazlowe advice"}}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Harnessing volatile biological energy from thunder lizards to jump-start dormant devices is classic goblin ingenuity, even if the safety margins are essentially nonexistent.",["faction:Horde"]="Rezlak's operations support the Horde's foothold and transit across Durotar, where marauding harpies from Drygulch Ravine threaten travelers and supply caravans."},kw={"charged organs","drygulch ravine","durotar","gazlowe","harpies","orb","rezlak","stormy potential","thunder lizard"},rel={"npc:rezlak","subzone:drygulch-ravine"},m={id=97282},src={"In-game quest text: Stormy Potential"},fv=true}
E["quest:97286"] = {t="quest",n="Research Access",z="general",s="Your actions have earned you a place in the library. Its doors are open for your study.",h="Your actions have earned you a place in the library.",sec={{t="Library Access",b="You have earned access to the library for your studies. Its doors are open to you.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why can I enter the library?",a="Your actions showed respect for knowledge, so the library is open to you for study.",al={"library access","why library open","study library"}},{q="What does the library offer me?",a="The library is open for your study.",al={"library for what","study access","what can i do"}},{q="What did my actions show?",a="Your actions showed respect for knowledge.",al={"respect knowledge","why accepted","what did i prove"}}},kw={"access","knowledge","library","research access","respect","study"},m={id=97286},src={"In-game quest text: Research Access"},fv=true}
E["quest:97288"] = {t="quest",n="Unending Torment",z="general",s="The head of a fallen abomination sets Unending Torment in motion. Its repeated stitching points toward deliberate dark experimentation in the Forsaken capital, and you bring it to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity.",h="The head of a fallen abomination sets Unending Torment in motion.",sec={{t="A Loose Stitch",b="When the towering abomination falls in battle, its head topples from its shoulders. The neck was severed, stitched, torn away, and reattached many times. Each repair left the seam more fragile, until the head barely clung to its shoulders.\n\nSuch persistent patchwork is unusual even for undead constructs. The repeated destruction and reassembly suggest this abomination was rebuilt again and again. Ordinary scavengers could not explain its craftsmanship. Only the skilled flesh-crafters of the Undercity could identify the creature's origins.",sp=0},{t="The Dark Lady's Jest",b="At the Apothecarium, Master Apothecary Faranell, leader of the Royal Apothecary Society, recognizes the severed head at once. He sees its repeated reassembly as an intentional, cruel joke devised by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, not an accident of necromancy.\n\nOnly the Dark Lady and her inner circle know what offense earned the creature this degrading punishment. Faranell gleefully accepts the battered head, confident the Royal Apothecary Society can put it to use in their ongoing alchemical and necromantic endeavors.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Abominable Head?",a="The Abominable Head is the severed head of a unique construct, loosened when the creature is defeated. Its neck bears the scars of repeated reattachment, and each repair weakened the flesh and stitches. You bring it to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity for examination.",al={"abominable head lore","what is abominable head","abomination head quest item"}},{q="Why has the head been reattached so many times?",a="The head was stitched back after each fall or loss so the construct could be pieced together to continue serving or suffering. Its increasingly fragile neck eventually gave way again.",al={"why reattached head","why was the head sewn back on","unending torment head stitches"}},{q="Who is Master Apothecary Faranell?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell leads the Royal Apothecary Society in the Apothecarium of the Undercity. Under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner's authority, he oversees Forsaken apothecaries researching poisons, plagues, and anatomical alterations. His familiarity with the Dark Lady's projects lets him identify the stitched head's origins at once.",al={"who is faranell","master apothecary faranell lore","faranell undercity"}},{q="Why does Sylvanas consider this a cruel joke?",a="The cruelty lies in the endless cycle itself. Each time the creature falls, it is sloppily stitched back together to serve or suffer once more, and each repair leaves it weaker. This is how the Dark Lady punishes her enemies. Faranell does not say who the creature once was, or what it did to earn this.",al={"sylvanas joke","why dark lady joke","cruel joke by dark lady"}},{q="What will Faranell do with the head?",a="Faranell regularly repurposes necromantic remnants, flesh, and biological oddities to develop concoctions, plagues, and construct reinforcements for the Forsaken's ongoing conflicts. He accepts the head for the society's alchemical and necromantic work.",al={"what happens to the head","faranell use for head","what will the apothecaries do with it"}},{q="Where do you turn in the quest Unending Torment?",a="Bring the Abominable Head to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Apothecarium, in the Undercity's southern quadrant. He is surrounded by bubbling vats, assistants, and test subjects as he oversees the society's research.",al={"where to turn in unending torment","unending torment hand in","who takes abominable head"}},{q="Can Alliance players turn in this quest?",a="The delivery brings you to Master Apothecary Faranell inside the Apothecarium of the Undercity, so it is intended for Horde adventurers. Alliance adventurers who approach him would face hostile Undercity guards and death at the hands of the Forsaken upon entering their stronghold.",al={"is unending torment horde only","alliance unending torment","can alliance do unending torment"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the head shows how repeated repairs weaken an undead construct. It is a grim reminder of their queen's absolute authority and the Royal Apothecary Society's macabre ingenuity.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers who see the head glimpse the Forsaken's practice of repeated reanimation and flesh-crafting. These methods work, but they can unnerve peers who are more bound to honor."},kw={"abominable head","abomination","apothecarium","dark lady","master apothecary faranell","royal apothecary society","sylvanas windrunner","undercity","unending torment"},rel={"npc:master-apothecary-faranell","zone:undercity"},m={id=97288},src={"In-game quest text: Unending Torment"},fv=true}
E["quest:97289"] = {t="quest",n="Unending Torment",z="general",s="In the Undercity, the Forsaken prepare to question a fallen Alliance commander. They mean to give Grand Marshal Othmar Garithos a new body, and learn what he carried to his grave.",h="In the Undercity, the Forsaken prepare to question a fallen Alliance commander.",sec={{t="A Trophy of Conquest",b="Deep in the Undercity, Forsaken practitioners stitch abominations from discarded pieces of the dead. Among the preserved skulls lies one with a bitter history: the severed head of Grand Marshal Othmar Garithos. He led the surviving Alliance forces across Lordaeron, until the Dark Lady killed him.",sp=0},{t="Secrets of the Grave",b="The Forsaken plan to question Garithos, not keep his head as a trophy. Their necromancers prepare a cobbled abomination body as his new host, hoping to uncover Alliance secrets locked in his skull. When they bring the head to the lifeless construct, the abomination wakes in agony and relives its gruesome final moments.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Othmar?",a="Othmar was Grand Marshal Othmar Garithos, a notorious commander who led Alliance remnants in Lordaeron after the Third War. Non-humans despised him. Master Apothecary Faranell says the Dark Lady had the pleasure of dispatching him herself.",al={"who is othmar","othmar identity","who was othmar"}},{q="Why are the Forsaken reanimating Othmar's remains?",a="The Forsaken hope to pry secrets from Garithos that he took to his grave. They place his preserved head near a prepared abomination host to draw information from his revived remains.",al={"why reanimate othmar","purpose of reviving head","what secrets othmar"}},{q="How did Othmar die?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell says Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Dark Lady, had the pleasure of killing Grand Marshal Garithos herself.",al={"who killed othmar","how did othmar die","othmar death"}},{q="Why is the quest item called the Head of the Baron?",a="The Baron is the name of the towering abomination in the Ruins of Lordaeron. Its head once belonged to Grand Marshal Garithos, Faranell reveals.",al={"head of the baron othmar","why called head of the baron"}},{q="Where is Othmar's new body located?",a="The headless abomination meant to receive Othmar's head is kept in an adjoining chamber within the Undercity. Forsaken practitioners await its reanimation.",al={"where is othmars body","abomination location undercity"}},{q="What happens when the construct awakens?",a="When the head is placed near the assembled construct, its stitched flesh twitches violently to life. The remains panic as they relive the terror of Garithos's final moments alive.",al={"what happens when body wakes","completion reaction othmar"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As Forsaken, you see the remains of Grand Marshal Garithos used in experiments that recall Lady Sylvanas's conquest of Lordaeron.",["faction:Alliance"]="As Alliance, you see the gruesome fate of Grand Marshal Garithos, an Alliance leader who crossed the Banshee Queen in Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="As Horde, you see the Royal Apothecary Society conduct reanimation experiments in the Undercity. The work shows the Forsaken's dark craft and vengeance against former oppressors."},kw={"abomination","forsaken","garithos","grand marshal","head of the baron","othmar","sylvanas","undercity","unending torment"},rel={"zone:undercity","topic:alliance"},m={id=97289},src={"In-game quest text: Unending Torment"},fv=true}
E["quest:97290"] = {t="quest",n="Unending Torment",z="general",s="At the Undercity, a defiant subject refuses the Royal Apothecary Society's demands. Master Apothecary Faranell welcomes the news, eager to break a difficult captive.",h="At the Undercity, a defiant subject refuses the Royal Apothecary Society's demands.",sec={{t="Defiance in the Shadows",b="A subject under the Royal Apothecary Society's scrutiny despises you. The captive calls you an abomination and refuses to help. Such hatred is familiar to the Forsaken, for many living beings and captives recoil from Lordaeron's undead.",sp=0},{t="Faranell's Ambition",b="You bring the subject's refusal to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. The head of the Royal Apothecary Society greets the news with wicked delight. To him, resistance makes the subject a challenge, fit for the cruel experiments of the Apothecarium.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Unending Torment?",a="Unending Torment sends you to face a defiant subject tied to the Royal Apothecary Society. The prisoner or hostile being calls you an abomination and refuses to help, so you report this failure to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity.",al={"unending torment quest","what is unending torment","unending torment lore"}},{q="Who gives the quest Unending Torment?",a="A defiant subject rejects your approach and calls you an abomination. The captive remains nameless in the exchange and sends you back to the Royal Apothecary Society's leadership with your findings.",al={"who starts unending torment","unending torment quest giver","where to get unending torment"}},{q="Who is Master Apothecary Faranell?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell leads the Royal Apothecary Society in the Apothecarium, beneath the ruins of Lordaeron in the Undercity. He oversees Forsaken alchemists and researchers who develop plagues, concoctions, and poisons to defend the Forsaken and destroy their enemies across Azeroth.",al={"who is faranell","master apothecary faranell","apothecary faranell role"}},{q="Why does the speaker call the player an abomination?",a="The captive calls you an abomination out of disgust for the undead and their agents. Many living beings and enemies of the Forsaken share this horror of the animated dead in the Undercity.",al={"why abomination","who calls you abomination","why is the prisoner angry"}},{q="Where must you report after completing the task?",a="Report the subject's refusal directly to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. Deep in the Apothecarium, fellow researchers surround him as they make lethal concoctions for Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken.",al={"where to turn in unending torment","unending torment turn in","who to report to unending torment"}},{q="How does Master Apothecary Faranell react to the subject's defiance?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell is pleased by the subject's stubborn refusal. He welcomes the impasse as an entertaining challenge and relishes testing difficult subjects with the Royal Apothecary Society's ruthless methods.",al={"faranell reaction","what does faranell say","does faranell get mad"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As a Forsaken, you know the living may call you an abomination. Helping Master Apothecary Faranell supports the Royal Apothecary Society's grim work.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde champion in Lordaeron, you see the ruthlessness of your Forsaken allies. Faranell's delight in breaking stubborn captives reveals the dark pragmatism of the Undercity."},kw={"abomination","apothecarium","faranell","forsaken","master apothecary faranell","royal apothecary society","undercity","unending torment"},rel={"npc:master-apothecary-faranell","zone:undercity"},m={id=97290},src={"In-game quest text: Unending Torment"},fv=true}
E["quest:97291"] = {t="quest",n="Unending Torment",z="general",s="In the Undercity, Master Apothecary Faranell prepares a brew to bind a captive to the Dark Lady's will. He needs lethal reagents from across the Forsaken capital to finish it.",h="In the Undercity, Master Apothecary Faranell prepares a brew to bind a captive to the Dark Lady's will.",sec={{t="Binding the Will",b="Deep in the Undercity's Apothecarium, Master Apothecary Faranell conducts experiments to strengthen the Forsaken. He has reattached the severed head of a battered captive. Now he needs a special alchemical mixture to crush the creature's remaining resistance and bind its will to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner.",sp=0},{t="Apothecary Reagents",b="Faranell sends his agent across the Undercity to gather potent ingredients for his brew. A Toxic Skullcap comes from Tawny Grisette in the Trade Quarter. Blisterweed is held by nearby herbalist trainers who owe him a debt, and a poison vendor in the Rogues' Quarter sells Essence of Agony.",sp=0},{t="The Masterpiece",b="With the ingredients in hand, Faranell is grimly satisfied with the masterpiece he is about to make. He looks forward to testing the concoction on their 'honored guest.' He trusts the agonizing draught to strip away the captive's remaining bravado and return the stray creature obediently to the Dark Lady's service.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Unending Torment?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell offers it. He leads the Royal Apothecary Society and can be found in the Apothecarium within the Undercity.",al={"who gives unending torment","quest giver unending torment","unending torment start"}},{q="What reagents are needed for Faranell's concoction?",a="Faranell needs three ingredients: a Toxic Skullcap from Tawny Grisette in the Trade Quarter, Blisterweed from near the herbalism trainers in the Apothecarium, and Essence of Agony from a poison vendor in the Rogues' Quarter.",al={"what are the ingredients","unending torment items","what reagents faranell need"}},{q="Who is Master Apothecary Faranell experimenting on?",a="Faranell is experimenting on an unwilling captive whose severed head has just been reattached. He mockingly calls him a 'lost sheep' and an 'honored guest,' and seeks to crush the defiant bravado he clings to after severe beatings.",al={"who is the beast","who is the lost sheep faranell mentions","who is being tortured"}},{q="What is the goal of Faranell's potion?",a="Faranell's potion is meant to mentally break the subject and bind it to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's will. She is the Dark Lady of the Forsaken.",al={"why make this potion","purpose of unending torment potion","what does the concoction do"}},{q="Where can players find Tawny Grisette?",a="Tawny Grisette is in the Undercity's Trade Quarter, where she supplies specialty goods including the Toxic Skullcap the apothecaries need.",al={"where is tawny grisette","toxic skullcap vendor","find miss grisette"}},{q="Where is the Blisterweed located?",a="Blisterweed grows near the Undercity's herbalism trainers, the Alliestars, in the Apothecarium. Faranell says they owe him a favor, so adventurers may take what is needed.",al={"where to find blisterweed","alliestaers herbalism","herbalist trainer undrecity blisterweed"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, alchemical torment to bind reanimated remains to the Dark Lady's service is part of daily life in the Apothecarium.",["faction:Horde"]="The Royal Apothecary Society continues brewing sinister concoctions deep beneath Lordaeron's ruins and testing the limits of alchemical subjugation.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Gathering Blisterweed from the Apothecarium's herbalist trainers shows how ordinary botanical study overlaps with the Society's darker alchemical work.",["class:Rogue"]="The work takes you into the Rogues' Quarter to buy Essence of Agony, a potent reagent favored by poison crafters and assassins."},kw={"apothecarium","blisterweed","essence of agony","faranell","master apothecary faranell","tawny grisette","toxic skullcap","undercity","unending torment"},rel={"npc:master-apothecary-faranell","zone:undercity","subzone:trade-quarter","subzone:rogues-quarter","topic:rogue"},m={id=97291},src={"In-game quest text: Unending Torment"},fv=true}
E["quest:97292"] = {t="quest",n="Unending Torment",z="general",s="Master Apothecary Faranell sets a punishment in motion beneath the Undercity. In the Apothecarium, the Royal Apothecary Society turns Hissing Serum against Othmar’s remains, seeking lasting retribution for crimes against the Dark Lady.",h="Master Apothecary Faranell sets a punishment in motion beneath the Undercity.",sec={{t="Apothecary Retribution",b="In the Undercity’s Apothecarium, Master Apothecary Faranell conducts harsh experiments against the Forsaken’s enemies. He has prepared Hissing Serum, a caustic substance meant to drip without end onto a captive beneath an alchemical apparatus.",sp=0},{t="The Fate of Garithos",b="Grand Marshal Garithos lies beneath Faranell’s apparatus as Othmar, his remains held in a suspended framework. Faranell judges chemical torment fitting for crimes against the Dark Lady. The serum runs down over Othmar’s body, simmering his flesh as punishment and study.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Othmar?",a="Othmar is Grand Marshal Othmar Garithos, the former human commander who made an uneasy alliance with Sylvanas Windrunner during Lordaeron’s reclamation. The Forsaken betrayed and killed him. Master Apothecary Faranell now holds his remains for continued torment and experiments.",al={"who is othmar","what othmar","othmar garithos"}},{q="Why is Othmar being tormented?",a="Othmar Garithos aided Sylvanas Windrunner in taking Capital City back from the dreadlords. Yet he despised non-humans and sought to claim all Lordaeron for humanity. The Forsaken hold him guilty of crimes against the Dark Lady and judge chemical torment fitting for his arrogance and deeds.",al={"why torturing othmar","what did othmar do","crimes against sylvanas"}},{q="Who is Master Apothecary Faranell?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell heads the Royal Apothecary Society in the Undercity’s Apothecarium. He oversees the making of serums, toxins, and plagues, and directs the operation against Othmar.",al={"faranell role","who gives unending torment","apothecary faranell"}},{q="What does the Hissing Serum do?",a="Hissing Serum is an alchemical brew by Master Apothecary Faranell. It simmers flesh and brings excruciating pain as it flows from an apparatus above Othmar’s suspended body.",al={"what is hissing serum","serum effects","faranell serum"}},{q="Is Othmar alive or undead?",a="Othmar is undead. The Dark Lady slew Garithos herself, and his head has been joined to an abomination’s body that twitches to life. Faranell calls him their “new guest” and intends to keep him suffering beneath the dripping serum.",al={"othmar dead or alive","is garithos alive","state of othmar"}},{q="What does Faranell plan to do with Othmar next?",a="Master Apothecary Faranell plans to keep Othmar’s flesh simmering beneath the dripping apparatus and watch him closely. He says he has extensive plans for Othmar’s future, with further experiments and retribution in store.",al={"faranell future plans","what happens to othmar next","othmar future"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Master Apothecary Faranell leads the Royal Apothecary Society in the Undercity. For the Forsaken, punishing enemies of the Dark Lady like Garithos is central to their retribution.",["faction:Horde"]="By helping Faranell in the Undercity, you take part in the severe methods the Forsaken use against those who have crossed them and the Banshee Queen."},kw={"apothecarium","dark lady","garithos","hissing serum","master apothecary faranell","othmar","undercity","unending torment"},rel={"npc:master-apothecary-faranell"},m={id=97292},src={"In-game quest text: Unending Torment"},fv=true}
E["quest:97326"] = {t="quest",n="Rocks to Rests",z="general",s="Behind Grommash Hold, Thra and friends make room for games in Orgrimmar. They need smooth boulders to use as chairs and tables.",h="Behind Grommash Hold, Thra and friends make room for games in Orgrimmar.",sec={{t="A Quiet Corner of Orgrimmar",b="Young orcs gather in a dirt clearing behind Grommash Hold in Orgrimmar. There they can play away from the fortress guards. The open ground suits their miniature figures, but with no furniture, they sit in the dust.",sp=0},{t="Makeshift Furniture",b="Thra spots you passing and asks you to carry eight medium-sized, smooth boulders to the clearing. Their flat sides will make sturdy chairs and low tables. Then the youths can arrange their games without sitting in the dirt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Rocks to Rests?",a="Thra is a young orc playing games with friends in the clearing behind Grommash Hold in Orgrimmar. Thra is observant and playful, and asks passersby to help improve the play area.",al={"who gives rocks to rests","thra orgrimmar","who is thra"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The clearing lies directly behind Grommash Hold, Warchief Thrall's seat in Orgrimmar's Valley of Wisdom. The children gather in this relatively clear patch of dirt, away from the fortress's heavy traffic.",al={"where is rocks to rests","thra location","where behind grommash hold"}},{q="Why does Thra need smooth boulders?",a="Thra and the other youths need smooth, medium-sized rocks for chairs and tables. Without furniture, they sit in the dirt while arranging and playing their miniature games.",al={"why rocks","what are smooth boulders for","why boulders"}},{q="What game is being played behind Grommash Hold?",a="Thra and the other youths play an informal tabletop game with small figures spread across the ground. The smooth boulders will give them steady places for their figures and somewhere comfortable to sit.",al={"what game are they playing","thra game orgrimmar","figures behind grommash hold"}},{q="Does Rocks to Rests involve fighting?",a="No. This is a peaceful undertaking, with no fighting or military trial. You use your strength to haul heavy boulders to the children's game spot.",al={"is rocks to rests dangerous","any combat rocks to rests"}},{q="What do the children do with the boulders once delivered?",a="After you deliver the eight smooth boulders, Thra and the children move them around the clearing. Their flat sides make seats and tables for the figures in their tabletop game.",al={"what happens after rocks to rests","what does thra do with rocks"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Helping young orcs behind the Warchief's stronghold shows how community and everyday life matter in Orgrimmar, even in a warrior society.",["race:Orc"]="Orc youths making games behind Grommash Hold show how the next generation grows up in the heart of Durotar."},kw={"durotar","grommash hold","orcs","orgrimmar","rocks to rests","smooth boulders","tabletop","thra"},rel={"subzone:grommash-hold"},m={id=97326},src={"In-game quest text: Rocks to Rests"},fv=true}
E["quest:97538"] = {t="quest",n="Pigments for Paints",z="general",s="Tah Winterhoof needs red pigment for his paints, and you can help him replenish it. The color comes from mirkweed pods gathered in Mirkfallon Lake in the Stonetalon Mountains.",h="Tah Winterhoof needs red pigment for his paints, and you can help him replenish it.",sec={{t="An Artisan's Need",b="Tah Winterhoof has run out of red pigment and cannot continue his work. He lives in Thunder Bluff, and his traveling days are behind him. So he asks you to make the long journey north into the Stonetalon Mountains and gather more coloring.",sp=0},{t="The Waters of Mirkfallon",b="Mirkfallon Lake lies in a mountain pass in the Stonetalon Mountains. Mirkweed grows beneath its waters, and its seed pods turn red when harvested and dried. Gather thirty pods, and Tah will have the raw material he needs to return to his work.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Pigments for Paints?",a="Tah Winterhoof asks for help in Thunder Bluff. His traveling days are behind him, so he needs someone younger to gather materials for his craft.",al={"who gives pigments for paints","pigments quest giver","tah winterhoof location"}},{q="Where are Mirkweed Pods found?",a="Mirkweed grows in the waters of Mirkfallon Lake, a large, round lake in a mountain pass in the Stonetalon Mountains.",al={"where to find mirkweed pods","mirkweed location","where is mirkfallon lake"}},{q="Why does Tah Winterhoof need mirkweed?",a="Tah Winterhoof has run out of red pigment for his artwork. Dried mirkweed pods turn red and provide dye for his paints.",al={"why collect mirkweed","what is mirkweed used for","why red pigment"}},{q="How many Mirkweed Pods are needed?",a="Tah Winterhoof needs thirty Mirkweed Pods from Mirkfallon Lake to replenish his red pigment.",al={"how many pods needed","mirkweed count"}},{q="What is mirkweed?",a="Mirkweed is an aquatic plant that grows in Mirkfallon Lake in the Stonetalon Mountains. Its pods dry into red pigment.",al={"what kind of plant is mirkweed","mirkweed details"}},{q="Where is Tah Winterhoof located?",a="Tah Winterhoof lives atop the mesa city of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore, where he practices his craft and prepares traditional paints.",al={"where is tah winterhoof","tah winterhoof thunder bluff"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can see how Thunder Bluff artisans use natural plants to make traditional pigments and dyes.",["profession:Herbalism"]="As a herbalist, you know that aquatic plants such as mirkweed can provide useful pigments beyond medicinal herbs. Mirkfallon Lake's waters are dangerous.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you know the ties between Thunder Bluff and the rugged passes of the Stonetalon Mountains."},kw={"mirkfallon lake","mirkweed","mirkweed pods","pigments for paints","red pigment","stonetalon mountains","tah winterhoof","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:mirkfallon-lake","zone:stonetalon","zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=97538},src={"In-game quest text: Pigments for Paints"},fv=true}
E["quest:97539"] = {t="quest",n="Silky Sutures",z="general",s="Silky Sutures is a Horde quest in the Arathi Highlands offered by Doctor Gregory Victor in the outpost of Hammerfall. Frustrated by unrepaired siege damage and dwindling medical supplies, the camp's resident physician recruits adventurers to gather venomous highland spider silk for use as emergency field sutures. The task highlights the desperate, resource-starved conditions facing the defenders of Hammerfall as they struggle to keep their guards and laborers on their feet.",h="Silky Sutures is a Horde quest in the Arathi Highlands offered by Doctor Gregory Victor in the outpost of...",sec={{t="The Crumbling Outpost",b="Hammerfall endures in the rugged Arathi Highlands, but the encampment shows visible signs of prolonged strain. Unrepaired siege damage marks the perimeter, leading the local physician, Doctor Gregory Victor, to bitterly observe that neglect seems to be official policy. Despite the crumbling fortifications and lack of broader support, Victor refuses to let the decay claim his infirmary. Maintaining a working clinic is essential for keeping the laborers and sentinel guards upright and ready for conflict.",sp=0},{t="Makeshift Medical Supplies",b="Dwindling supplies have left the Hammerfall medical ward on the brink of exhaustion, forcing Doctor Victor to turn to battlefield improvisation. When conventional bandages and stitches run scarce, the tough, tensile silk harvested from highland spiders can be twisted into surprisingly resilient field sutures. Though Victor dryly quips about the risk of infection being the least of a wounded soldier's concerns, harvesting the silk provides the infirmary with the vital equipment needed to patch up wounded defenders in a hostile territory.",sp=0},{t="Gallows Humor in Hammerfall",b="Operating a frontline clinic under siege conditions requires a hardened temperament, which Doctor Gregory Victor displays in abundance. Greeting travelers with mordant jokes about grave prognoses and spider-string stitches, the physician masks the grim reality of Hammerfall's isolation. Regular aid remains rare in the highlands, making any assistance from visiting champions crucial to sustaining the clinic. Through improvised remedies and stubborn defiance, Victor ensures that Hammerfall's wounded receive care even when the outpost around them seems abandoned to rot.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Doctor Gregory Victor?",a="Doctor Gregory Victor is the resident medical officer and trauma surgeon overseeing the infirmary at Hammerfall in the Arathi Highlands. Possessing a mordant wit and a no-nonsense approach to battlefield triage, he works tirelessly to treat wounded guards and laborers. Despite severe supply shortages and unrepaired siege damage across the outpost, Victor remains determined to prevent the medical ward from succumbing to neglect.",al={"who is victor","gregory victor lore","hammerfall doctor"}},{q="Why does Doctor Victor need spider silk?",a="The infirmary in Hammerfall is almost entirely out of standard medical supplies and equipment. Under extreme battlefield pressure, Doctor Victor relies on improvisation, twisting strong highland spider silk into effective surgical sutures to sew up lacerations and wounds. The spider silk provides durable thread capable of holding torn flesh together when proper clinical sutures are unavailable.",al={"why spider silk","surgical spidersilk use","spider silk sutures"}},{q="What condition is Hammerfall in during this quest?",a="Hammerfall is in a state of visible neglect and physical distress. Old siege damage remains completely unrepaired along the encampment's defenses, giving Doctor Victor the impression that administrative neglect has become official policy. The base suffers from a lack of consistent outside aid and supplies, leaving its stationed guards, laborers, and medical staff forced to fend for themselves.",al={"hammerfall status","why is hammerfall damaged","state of hammerfall"}},{q="Where do the spiders for this quest come from?",a="The spiders stalk the wilderness of the Arathi Highlands surrounding the Hammerfall encampment. Adventurers venturing outside the outpost's walls must hunt these aggressive arachnids across the highland plains to harvest the surgical spidersilk needed for Doctor Victor's field sutures before returning safely to the clinic.",al={"arathi spiders","where to find spidersilk","surgical spidersilk location"}},{q="Why is Doctor Victor sarcastic about infection?",a="When presented with the gathered spider silk, Doctor Victor jokes that a little infection is the least of a wounded soldier's concerns when being stitched back together. This dark humor reflects the grim reality of frontline triage in Hammerfall, where preventing severe blood loss and fatal trauma on the battlefield takes precedence over sterile clinical luxury.",al={"doctor humor","spider silk infection","victor jokes"}},{q="Why are supplies so scarce at Hammerfall?",a="Hammerfall sits in a contested and isolated position within the Arathi Highlands, surrounded by hostile threats and cut off from steady supply lines. Outside reinforcement and logistical deliveries arrive infrequently, leaving the outpost's garrison and medical ward to endure chronic shortages of raw materials, tools, and basic healing supplies.",al={"why no supplies","hammerfall supply shortage","lack of help hammerfall"}},{q="Who does the infirmary in Hammerfall treat?",a="The infirmary answers directly to Doctor Gregory Victor and is responsible for treating the laborers and guards stationed at Hammerfall. With the outpost under constant strain from highland dangers and structural damage, maintaining the physical health of these defenders is essential to keeping the settlement fortified and functional.",al={"who does victor treat","hammerfall patients","clinic patients"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Hammerfall represents a vital Horde stronghold in the Arathi Highlands, making Doctor Victor's efforts to keep its defenders healthy and battle-ready critical to the faction's regional survival.",["race:Undead"]="Doctor Gregory Victor brings the pragmatic, unsentimental medical expertise characteristic of the Forsaken to Hammerfall, utilizing improvised highland resources to keep the clinic functional."},kw={"arathi highlands","doctor gregory victor","field sutures","hammerfall","infirmary","silky sutures","spider silk","surgical spidersilk"},rel={"npc:doctor-gregory-victor","subzone:hammerfall","zone:arathi"},m={id=97539},src={"In-game quest text: Silky Sutures"},fv=true}
E["quest:97583"] = {t="quest",n="WRIGGLE.",z="general",s="WRIGGLE. is an encounter with an excitable young slime that loves to dance until it runs out of energy. To revive and befriend the creature, an adventurer must fetch a special batch of nutritious sludge from the canals of the Undercity.",h="WRIGGLE.",sec={{t="A Dancing Slime",b="WRIGGLE. involves interacting with a lively, excitable little slime that delights in mirroring the dance movements of friendly travelers. Despite its enthusiasm, dancing quickly exhausts the small ooze, leaving it weary and in need of sustenance to restore its strength.",sp=0},{t="Sustenance from the Canals",b="To revitalize the tuckered-out creature, adventurers must seek out Nutritious Slime Sludge, a peculiar mixture sold within the rancid canals of the Undercity. Upon absorbing this favored foul treat, the energized slime eagerly bonds with its provider, ready to follow its new friend.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is WRIGGLE.?",a="WRIGGLE. is an encounter where an adventurer befriends an excitable little slime through dancing, only to discover the creature requires specialized sustenance to keep up its energy.",al={"wriggle quest","what is wriggle","slime dance quest"}},{q="Where do you find Nutritious Slime Sludge?",a="Nutritious Slime Sludge is sold by a vendor residing within the canals of the Undercity, where alchemical waste and runoff are commonplace.",al={"where to get sludge","nutritious slime sludge vendor","feed slime undrecity"}},{q="How do you catch the slime's attention if it seems tired?",a="If the excitable slime becomes tired or distracted, dancing with it catches its attention and mirrors its playful movements.",al={"how to get slime attention","tired slime","wake slime wriggle"}},{q="Why does the slime feed on sludge?",a="Slimes absorb ambient alchemical ooze and sludge to sustain themselves and regain vitality, rejuvenating after exerting energy.",al={"why slime eat sludge","what do slimes eat"}},{q="What happens once the slime is fed?",a="After being nourished with its preferred sludge, the revitalized slime bonds with the adventurer and seeks to follow them around.",al={"does slime follow you","slime companion","wriggle reward"}},{q="Can Alliance players easily obtain the sludge?",a="Because obtaining the necessary Nutritious Slime Sludge requires visiting the vendors in the canals of the Undercity, Alliance travelers will find entering the Forsaken capital extremely perilous.",al={"can alliance do wriggle","alliance slime sludge"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken are well-accustomed to the toxic runoff and animated slimes dwelling within the Undercity's canal networks, making a slime companion a natural fit.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing the special sludge requires venturing into the canals of the Forsaken capital of Undercity to trade with local vendors."},kw={"canals","excitable slime","nutritious slime sludge","slime","sludge","undercity","wriggle"},rel={"zone:undercity"},m={id=97583},src={"In-game quest text: WRIGGLE."},fv=true}
E["quest:97891"] = {t="quest",n="Prompt Potion Runner",z="general",s="Prompt Potion Runner is an urgent Undercity delivery quest where players must race an unstable concoction across the slime canal to the Apothecarium. The quest highlights the physical infirmities of undeath and the volatile nature of Forsaken alchemical research.",h="Prompt Potion Runner is an urgent Undercity delivery quest where players must race an unstable concoction...",sec={{t="A Race Against Decay",b="In the subterranean corridors of the Undercity, the Forsaken alchemist Alessandro urgently needs a swift courier. Having prepared a highly volatile concoction with an extremely short shelf life, he cannot risk transporting it himself across the city's toxic slime canal due to the fragile state of his undead joints. An adventurer must sprint against the clock to ensure the brew arrives intact before it degrades into useless sludge.",sp=0},{t="The Apothecarium's Demands",b="The destination is the Apothecarium, where Doctor Martin Felben eagerly awaits the delivery for his ongoing research. Felben reveals that the previous six couriers or attempts failed because the potions destabilized mid-transit. A timely arrival allows the doctor to continue his shadowy experiments uninterrupted, underscoring both the volatility of Royal Apothecary concoctions and the physical indignities of undeath.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="The quest is given by Alessandro in the Undercity. He needs an adventurer to sprint across the slime canal because his own decaying undead knees pop out and rattle if he tries to run.",al={"who gives prompt potion runner","who starts prompt potion runner","who is alessandro"}},{q="Where does the potion need to go?",a="The potion must be delivered to Doctor Martin Felben, who conducts alchemical experiments in the Apothecarium of the Undercity.",al={"where do you take the potion","who is doctor martin felben","prompt potion runner turn in"}},{q="Why is the delivery timed?",a="The concoction is an unstable potion with an exceptionally short shelf life. If it is not delivered quickly across the canal, the mixture destabilizes and becomes useless.",al={"why timed","unstable potion shelf life","why hurry"}},{q="Have other delivery attempts failed?",a="Doctor Martin Felben notes that the previous six potions Alessandro attempted to send destabilized along the way before reaching the Apothecarium, making your successful delivery a rare triumph.",al={"did other couriers fail","alessandro past attempts","how many potions failed"}},{q="Why can't Alessandro deliver the potion himself?",a="Alessandro explains that his knees rattle and pop out whenever he tries to run, calling it another unfortunate physical side effect of undeath that prevents him from rushing the delivery himself.",al={"why cant alessandro deliver it","alessandro knees"}},{q="What are Doctor Felben's experiments?",a="Doctor Martin Felben is an apothecary conducting experiments in the Apothecarium. While the exact formula is kept obscure, he immediately resumes his research once the stable mixture arrives.",al={"what does doctor felben do","what is the potion used for"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The delivery showcases the fragile physical reality of Forsaken unlife, where brittle joints and decaying knees can easily derail vital Royal Apothecary Society laboratory work.",["faction:Horde"]="Undercity serves as a crucial hub for the Horde, though travelers must navigate its toxic slime canals to assist the Forsaken with their perilous alchemical endeavors."},kw={"alessandro","apothecarium","doctor martin felben","prompt potion runner","royal apothecary society","slime canal","undercity","unstable potion"},rel={"subzone:apothecarium"},m={id=97891},src={"In-game quest text: Prompt Potion Runner"},fv=true}
E["quest:97900"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Blacksmithing",z="general",s="In Razor Hill, Dwukk can teach you a camp craft suited to your blacksmithing skill. A Sharpening Wheel gives nearby fighters a way to hone their weapons.",h="In Razor Hill, Dwukk can teach you a camp craft suited to your blacksmithing skill.",sec={{t="A camp craft",b="Dwukk teaches you to craft a Sharpening Wheel once you show skill in blacksmithing. Set it up at a wilderness camp, where nearby fighters and rogues can hone their weapons.\n\nYou need a campfire to start your camp. Cooks can teach you how to make one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches me to make a Sharpening Wheel?",a="Dwukk in Razor Hill teaches you to craft a Sharpening Wheel once you show skill in blacksmithing.",al={"sharpening wheel trainer","dwukk blacksmithing","who teaches camping blacksmithing"}},{q="What does the Sharpening Wheel do?",a="Nearby fighters and rogues can use a Sharpening Wheel at your camp to hone their weapons.",al={"sharpening wheel use","what's the wheel for","hone weapons camp"}},{q="Where can I try out my camp?",a="Try out your camp in the wilderness. Camps should not be set up in the center of town.",al={"where to set up camp","camping location","camp in town"}},{q="Do I need a campfire?",a="Yes. You need a campfire to start your camp, and cooks can teach you how to make one.",al={"campfire needed","how start camp","fire for camping"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="As a blacksmith, you can learn from Dwukk to craft a Sharpening Wheel for a wilderness camp.",["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you can hone your weapons at a nearby Sharpening Wheel set up at camp."},kw={"blacksmithing","campfire","camping","dwukk","razor hill","rogues","sharpening wheel","weapons"},rel={"subzone:razor-hill","topic:rogue"},m={id=97900},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Blacksmithing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97901"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Enchanting",z="general",s="You can make an Enchanted Lute, but townsfolk dislike camps in town centers. To set up camp in the wilderness, you need a fire.",h="You can make an Enchanted Lute, but townsfolk dislike camps in town centers.",sec={{t="A Campfire First",b="A campfire gives you the basis for your camp. Most cooks can show you how to make one, so seek a cooking trainer if you have not learned the basics.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can I craft?",a="You can craft an Enchanted Lute.",al={"enchanted lute","make a lute","camping lute"}},{q="Where can I try it out?",a="You need to head into the wilderness to try it out. Most folks dislike camps in town centers.",al={"where to camp","camping spot","try the lute"}},{q="Why do I need a campfire?",a="Your camp begins with a campfire.",al={"need campfire","camp fire","camping fire"}},{q="Who can teach me to make a fire?",a="Most cooks can teach you to make a fire. Ask a cooking trainer if you have not learned the basics.",al={"learn fire","cooking trainer","make campfire"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you can teach others to make a fire for the basis of a camp."},kw={"campfire","camping","cooking","enchanted lute","fire","wilderness"},m={id=97901},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Enchanting"},fv=true}
E["quest:97903"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: First Aid",z="general",s="Camping 101: First Aid is an instructional quest that sends skilled travelers to Rawrk in Razor Hill, who teaches them how to craft their own First Aid Kit for wilderness survival. It emphasizes the necessities of self-reliance on the trail, reminding adventurers to master campfire creation and respect local settlements by camping out in the wild.",h="Camping 101: First Aid is an instructional quest that sends skilled travelers to Rawrk in Razor Hill, who...",sec={{t="Survival on the Frontier",b="Survival in the untamed expanses of Durotar and beyond requires more than just raw martial strength; it demands self-sufficiency on the trail. In Razor Hill, Rawrk offers instruction to capable wanderers seeking to fashion essential wilderness gear. Through Camping 101: First Aid, practitioners who have proven their basic proficiency learn to assemble a dedicated First Aid Kit, giving them the means to mend wounds and tend to injuries far from the safety of an apothecary or infirmary.",sp=0},{t="The Art of the Campfire",b="Setting up camp is an art governed by practical frontier etiquette and necessity. Rawrk cautions adventurers that pitching tents in the center of settlements like Razor Hill is unwelcome, meaning these wilderness stations must be tested out in the open wilds. Furthermore, no campsite is functional without a roaring campfire to stave off the night, making a passing familiarity with the culinary arts—where firemaking is first mastered—a vital asset for any prospective camper.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Rawrk and where is he found?",a="Rawrk is located in Razor Hill in Durotar. He provides instruction in survival campcraft, teaching demonstrated practitioners how to assemble their own First Aid Kits for backcountry travel.",al={"who teaches camping first aid","where is rawrk","camping 101 trainer"}},{q="What is required before setting up a wilderness camp?",a="Setting up a camp requires both an open wilderness setting and a proper campfire. Rawrk warns that setting up camp within town limits is frowned upon and reminds travelers to learn basic firemaking from a cooking instructor before heading out.",al={"what do i need to camp","how to make a camp","campfire requirement camping"}},{q="Can camps be pitched inside towns or settlements?",a="Camps cannot be placed within settlements or town centers. Local residents object to travelers pitching tents in the middle of streets, requiring adventurers to venture out into the open wilds to set up their campsite.",al={"can i camp in razor hill","camping inside cities","where can i place my camp"}},{q="What does this training teach adventurers to craft?",a="Completing this training instructs adventurers in crafting a personal First Aid Kit, an essential field accessory used to maintain health and support sustained excursions in the wilderness.",al={"camping 101 first aid reward","what item does this teach","first aid kit crafting"}},{q="Why does Rawrk recommend learning cooking?",a="A proper camp requires a warm campfire to anchor it. Since the fundamentals of kindling and tending a basic fire are taught by cooking trainers, Rawrk advises campers to study the culinary arts beforehand.",al={"why do i need cooking for camping","cooking campfire camping"}},{q="What prerequisites are needed before learning this craft?",a="Travelers must display foundational proficiency in their medical and survival skills before Rawrk will teach them how to fashion a specialized field First Aid Kit.",al={"prerequisites for camping 101 first aid","skill needed for camping first aid"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Based out of Razor Hill in Durotar, this training is directed at Horde travelers looking to sustain themselves across the rugged frontiers of Kalimdor.",["profession:Cooking"]="Campcraft and culinary skill go hand in hand; Rawrk notes that pitching a proper camp requires a roaring campfire, an essential technique taught by culinary trainers."},kw={"campfire","camping 101","durotar","first aid kit","rawrk","razor hill","survival","wilderness camp"},rel={"subzone:razor-hill"},m={id=97903},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: First Aid"},fv=true}
E["quest:97904"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="At your camp, fishing can bring comfort to you and your companions. Near Sen'jin Village, Lau'Tiki can teach you to make a Fish Bowl.",h="At your camp, fishing can bring comfort to you and your companions.",sec={{t="A Camp Comfort",b="Once you have shown skill in fishing, speak with Lau'Tiki near Sen'jin Village. Lau'Tiki can teach you to make a Fish Bowl, a fixture for camp meant to bring joy to you and your fellow adventurers. A happier camp, the giver hopes, makes for stronger fighters.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can fishing do for my camp?",a="Your fishing skill can help you learn to make a Fish Bowl for your camp.",al={"fishing camp item","camping fishing"}},{q="Who teaches me to make a Fish Bowl?",a="Lau'Tiki near Sen'jin Village can teach you to make a Fish Bowl once you have shown skill in fishing.",al={"fish bowl teacher","Lau'Tiki fishing"}},{q="What is the Fish Bowl for?",a="The Fish Bowl is a camping fixture meant to bring joy to you and your fellow adventurers.",al={"fish bowl use","camp fish fixture"}},{q="Where can I find Lau'Tiki?",a="Lau'Tiki is near Sen'jin Village.",al={"Lau'Tiki location","Sen'jin fishing"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="As a fisher, you can learn to make a Fish Bowl for your camp once you have shown skill in fishing."},kw={"camp fixture","camping","fish bowl","fisherman","fishing","lau'tiki","sen'jin village"},rel={"subzone:sen-jin-village"},m={id=97904},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Fishing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97905"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="general",s="You learn to make incense candles from wild plants in Camping 101: Herbalism. First, though, a proper camp needs a fire, and your campsite belongs in the wilderness beyond town.",h="You learn to make incense candles from wild plants in Camping 101: Herbalism.",sec={{t="Aromatic Craft of the Wild",b="Herbalism lets you turn gathered plants into incense candles for comfort on the trail. Townsfolk frown on tents pitched in city streets, so these candles are meant for camps beyond guarded settlements.",sp=0},{t="The Heart of the Campsite",b="A campfire belongs at the heart of every campsite. Set one before you use an incense candle. Herbalists provide the aromatic burn, while cooking trainers across the land teach travelers the basics of starting a fire.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an incense candle?",a="Herbalists craft incense candles from wild plants. Burn one at a wilderness campsite beside a campfire.",al={"what is incense candle","incense candle use","camping herbalism item"}},{q="Where can you set up a camp?",a="You can set up camp in the open wilderness. Townsfolk object to tents spread across town thoroughfares.",al={"where to make camp","can i camp in town","camping in cities"}},{q="Why do you need a campfire to set up a camp?",a="A camp needs a campfire at its heart. Cooking trainers across Azeroth teach basic fire-building techniques.",al={"how to make campfire","campfire needed for camp","cook campfire camping"}},{q="Who teaches how to make a campfire?",a="Cooking trainers teach you to kindle a simple campfire. Learn the basics of cooking before you head into the wild.",al={"who teaches campfires","learn to build fire","cooking trainer fire"}},{q="How does herbalism tie into camping?",a="Herbalism provides the plants and resins for incense candles. Gathered from the wilds, they bring soothing aromas to your campsite.",al={"herbalism connection to camping","why herbalism camping","what does herbalism give for camps"}},{q="Do you need a campfire to use an incense candle?",a="A camp needs a proper campfire. Set one before you settle in to use an incense candle.",al={"can i light incense candle without fire","incense candle campfire requirement"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="As an herbalist, you learn to make incense candles from gathered plants for use at wilderness camps.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you learn to kindle the campfire at the heart of every camp."},kw={"camp","campfire","camping","cooking","herbalism","incense candle","wilderness"},m={id=97905},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:97907"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="Camping 101: Mining is an instructional trade quest directing budding miners to Krunn in Razor Hill. By demonstrating proficiency in their trade, miners learn to forge a Lodestone, a specialized camp fixture that enhances the combat prowess of warriors gathered around a wilderness fire.",h="Camping 101: Mining is an instructional trade quest directing budding miners to Krunn in Razor Hill.",sec={{t="The Miner's Camp",b="Mining offers an honest living that provides practical materials for exploration and survival in the wild. As miners develop their trade and demonstrate proficiency with pick and ore, they can learn to craft specialized campsite equipment. In Durotar, skilled miners are pointed toward Krunn in the settlement of Razor Hill to discover how to forge a Lodestone for their travels.",sp=0},{t="Campfire Camaraderie",b="A Lodestone serves as an attractive centerpiece for adventurers resting in the wilderness. When set up beside a camp, it provides martial fighters gathering by the fire with an added edge to their combat prowess. However, proper camp etiquette requires leaving civilized settlements behind: townsfolk frown upon camps being erected in city squares, meaning travelers must seek the open wild to test their craftsmanship.",sp=0},{t="Fire and Fieldcraft",b="Even the sturdiest lodestone is useless without a proper fire to anchor the camp. A basic campfire is necessary before establishing any resting spot, and those unfamiliar with building fires are encouraged to seek out a culinary instructor. Training in the cooking arts ensures that wilderness expeditions have the warmth and basic sustenance required to survive outside town walls.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Mining?",a="Camping 101: Mining is an introductory training quest directed at miners. It directs miners who have proven their proficiency to seek out Krunn in Razor Hill to learn how to craft a campsite utility known as a Lodestone.",al={"what is camping 101 mining","camping mining quest","mining lodestone quest"}},{q="Who is Krunn?",a="Krunn is a trainer located in Razor Hill within Durotar. He instructs skilled miners in how to fashion camp items such as the Lodestone once they display sufficient trade proficiency.",al={"who is krunn","where is krunn","razor hill trainer"}},{q="What is a Lodestone used for?",a="A Lodestone is a craftable camp fixture created by miners. When placed at a wilderness campfire, it grants physical combatants who gather around the fire extra martial prowess to bolster their fighting strength.",al={"what does a lodestone do","lodestone benefit","why craft lodestone"}},{q="Can you set up camp inside Razor Hill?",a="Townspeople and settlement authorities do not take kindly to campsites pitched in the middle of populated streets. You must venture out into the surrounding wilderness to pitch a camp and place your lodestone.",al={"can i camp in town","why cant i camp in razor hill","camping inside cities"}},{q="Why do you need a campfire before setting up camp?",a="Every functional campsite requires a campfire as its center. Basic campfire construction is taught by culinary trainers, making cooking an essential complementary skill for campers setting out into the wilds.",al={"do i need a campfire","how to start camp","fire requirement camping"}},{q="Who can learn to craft a Lodestone?",a="Crafting a Lodestone requires training in the mining profession. Miners who demonstrate sufficient skill in their craft can learn the technique directly from Krunn in Razor Hill.",al={"who can make lodestones","can anyone make a lodestone"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="Mining allows you to unearth raw minerals and craft specialized field utilities like a Lodestone to support your allies at camp.",["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking instructors provide the foundational knowledge needed to build a basic campfire, the centerpiece of any functional wilderness campsite.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde miners travelling through Durotar can report to Krunn in Razor Hill to learn the craft of lodging lodestones for their expeditions."},kw={"camp","campfire","camping 101: mining","cooking","durotar","krunn","lodestone","mining","razor hill"},rel={"subzone:razor-hill"},m={id=97907},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:97908"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory wilderness quest directing skinners in Orgrimmar to Thuwd to learn how to craft a Camp Chair. By putting harvested hides to use for field comfort, adventurers can establish proper campsites out in the wilds of Kalimdor.",h="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory wilderness quest directing skinners in Orgrimmar to Thuwd to learn...",sec={{t="Comfort in the Wild",b="Harvesting the pelts and hides of fallen beasts is one of the most practical survival skills an adventurer can cultivate in the wild. While raw leathers usually feed armorcraft, skilled skinners can also turn their harvested pelts into field comforts. In Orgrimmar, instructors direct capable skinners to Thuwd to learn the craft of assembling a sturdy Camp Chair from cured hides.",sp=0},{t="Rules of the Trail",b="Townsfolk and guards frown upon travelers pitching temporary camps along busy city thoroughfares. As Thuwd reminds new craftsmen, a camp chair is meant for open territory away from settlement walls. Furthermore, no wilderness camp is complete with seating alone; a proper site requires a warm campfire, a basic technique taught by cooks across Kalimdor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches skinners how to make camp items?",a="The quest directs skinner initiates to speak with Thuwd, a leatherworking and skinning trainer located in the Horde capital of Orgrimmar, once they have demonstrated basic proficiency in their gathering craft.",al={"who gives camping 101 skinning","who to speak to in orgrimmar","who is thuwd"}},{q="What camping item can skinners craft?",a="Skinners learn how to fashion a Camp Chair using the hides and pelts harvested from fallen beasts. It provides a comfortable seat to rest and recover while out in the wild.",al={"what do skinners make for camp","skinning camping craft","camp chair skinning"}},{q="Can you set up a camp chair inside a city?",a="City residents and guards frown upon setting up campsite equipment in the middle of town. Adventurers must venture out into the wilderness beyond settlement gates to pitch a camp and set out their chair.",al={"can i camp in orgrimmar","camps in city","where to pitch camp"}},{q="What else is required to complete a camp?",a="A campsite is incomplete without a fire for warmth, visibility, and cooking. Adventurers are advised to consult a cooking trainer to learn how to build a simple campfire before pitching camp.",al={"why need campfire","camp prerequisites","how to set up camp"}},{q="Why should an adventurer craft a camp chair?",a="Taking a moment to rest in a comfortable chair between hunts helps adventurers recover from the road and ensures they stay in peak condition before jumping into their next skirmish.",al={"skinning benefit camping","why rest in camp chair","camp chair benefits"}},{q="When does Thuwd teach the Camp Chair pattern?",a="Skinners must prove their basic proficiency in harvesting skins and hides from fallen beasts before Thuwd will share the secrets of crafting wilderness camp furniture.",al={"when can i learn camp chair","camping 101 requirements","skinning skill needed"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Thuwd trains skinners in Orgrimmar, making this introduction to wilderness craft uniquely accessible to Horde adventurers operating out of Durotar.",["profession:Cooking"]="A proper campsite demands both seating and heat; novice skinners are encouraged to study cooking basics to learn how to build a basic campfire."},kw={"camp chair","campfire","camping","camping 101: skinning","orgrimmar","skinning","thuwd"},rel={"zone:orgrimmar"},m={id=97908},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:97916"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Blacksmithing",z="general",s="In Goldshire, Smith Argus teaches skilled metalworkers to prepare for work in the wild. They learn to forge a portable Sharpening Wheel and to make camp in open country with a proper fire.",h="In Goldshire, Smith Argus teaches skilled metalworkers to prepare for work in the wild.",sec={{t="Forging for the Trail",b="Smith Argus of Goldshire shows skilled metalworkers how to carry their craft beyond the town anvils. Those ready for the work can learn to forge a Sharpening Wheel, keeping blades keen on long travels.",sp=0},{t="Life in the Field",b="Field gear alone does not make a proper camp. Goldshire townsfolk frown on camps within settlements, so crafters must head into the open frontier to use their equipment. A wilderness camp also needs a warm fire. Visit a local cook to learn the basics of making one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Blacksmithing?",a="Smith Argus teaches skilled blacksmiths to forge a portable Sharpening Wheel for use in the wilderness.",al={"what does this quest do","camping 101 blacksmithing purpose","what do i learn"}},{q="Who is Smith Argus?",a="Smith Argus is the blacksmith and trainer in Goldshire, within Elwynn Forest. He teaches promising smiths basic metalworking and camping craft.",al={"who is smith argus","goldshire blacksmith trainer","argus goldshire"}},{q="What is a Sharpening Wheel?",a="A Sharpening Wheel is camping equipment that blacksmiths can forge to hone tools and weapons away from town forges.",al={"what is a sharpening wheel","camping sharpening wheel","blacksmith camp item"}},{q="Can you set up camp inside town?",a="Townsfolk frown on camps in settled places like Goldshire. Smiths need to head into the wilderness to set up their gear.",al={"can i camp in goldshire","pitch tent in town","where can i camp"}},{q="Why do you need a campfire to make camp?",a="A campfire completes a wilderness camp. Blacksmiths can visit a cooking trainer to learn basic fire-making before heading out.",al={"do i need cooking for camping","campfire for camp","how to make a camp"}},{q="When can you learn this camping craft?",a="Smith Argus teaches this camping craft to blacksmiths with sufficient skill.",al={"camping 101 requirements","blacksmith proficiency requirement"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Blacksmiths learning their trade in Elwynn Forest can forge a Sharpening Wheel to maintain equipment and hone blades in the field.",["profession:Cooking"]="A proper campsite needs a reliable fire, so basic cooking training pairs well with metalworking in the wilderness.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance artisans starting out in Goldshire can ask Smith Argus how to prepare for long journeys beyond town."},kw={"blacksmithing","campfire","camping","camping 101","elwynn forest","goldshire","sharpening wheel","smith argus"},rel={"npc:smith-argus","subzone:goldshire"},m={id=97916},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Blacksmithing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97917"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Enchanting",z="general",s="In Stormwind, Betty Quin teaches enchanters to bring a little magic to the campfire. With an Enchanted Lute, they can lift spirits and help travelers rest along the trail.",h="In Stormwind, Betty Quin teaches enchanters to bring a little magic to the campfire.",sec={{t="Magic on the Trail",b="Long roads across Azeroth call for more than steel and rations. Comfort and good spirits matter in the wild, too. Enchanters put their craft to work at camp, turning quiet hours into music and rest before the next stretch of trail.",sp=0},{t="Seeking Betty Quin",b="Betty Quin in Stormwind teaches enchanters to make special items for the campsite. Once they have shown skill in enchanting, she can teach them to craft an Enchanted Lute. Its charms lift spirits around the campfire.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Enchanting?",a="Enchanters can learn to make magical supplies for camp from Betty Quin in Stormwind. She teaches those who have honed their craft.",al={"what is camping 101 enchanting","camping 101 enchanters","enchanting camp quest"}},{q="Who is Betty Quin?",a="Betty Quin teaches skilled enchanters in Stormwind how to make magical items for the road and for leisure.",al={"who is betty quin","stormwind enchanting trainer betty","where is betty quin"}},{q="What is an Enchanted Lute?",a="An Enchanted Lute is a magical instrument that brings music and lifts spirits during camp. It helps travelers face the hardships of the trail.",al={"what does enchanted lute do","why craft enchanted lute","enchanted lute purpose"}},{q="Why are enchanters valued at a campsite?",a="Enchanters bring magic to camp, making downtime a chance to rest, prepare, and enjoy some music before a hard journey.",al={"why enchanters in camp","enchanter camp usefulness"}},{q="Where do I go to complete this task?",a="Find Betty Quin in Stormwind, the capital of the kingdom of Stormwind.",al={"where do i turn in camping 101 enchanting","betty quin location"}},{q="What skill is needed to learn camping enchanting recipes?",a="Betty Quin teaches campcraft to enchanters who have shown solid skill in enchanting, including how to make an Enchanted Lute.",al={"camping 101 enchanting requirements","when can i learn enchanted lute"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance enchanters traveling through their lands can find Betty Quin in Stormwind for lessons in campcraft."},kw={"betty quin","camp","camping 101","camping 101: enchanting","enchanted lute","enchanting","stormwind"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=97917},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Enchanting"},fv=true}
E["quest:97921"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="general",s="Camping 101: Herbalism is a training quest that directs skilled gatherers to seek specialized wilderness instruction in Elwynn Forest. Upon demonstrating proficiency in harvesting flora, herbalists are pointed toward Herbalist Pomeroy to learn the art of crafting campsite utilities. Completing this training unlocks the ability to create Incense Candles, botanical aids designed to bolster the focus and spirits of spellcasters resting in the field.",h="Camping 101: Herbalism is a training quest that directs skilled gatherers to seek specialized wilderness...",sec={{t="Botanical Utilities for the Road",b="For wandering herbalists, harvesting herbs across the wilds of Azeroth serves purposes well beyond brewing basic restorative draughts or selling bundles of flowers to city merchants. As practitioners hone their gathering craft, opportunities arise to apply their floral knowledge toward wilderness survival. Field camps require more than just a roaring fire and dry bedroll; maintaining the endurance and mental acuity of a traveling party demands specialized comforts. Experienced foragers recognize that certain plants hold aromatic properties capable of invigorating weary minds. By directing promising herbalists toward established mentors, this task bridges simple gathering with practical fieldcraft, proving that a keen eye for nature's bounty can sustain an entire expedition far from civilization.",sp=0},{t="The Guidance of Herbalist Pomeroy",b="Aspiring herbalists seeking to expand their campsite craft are sent into the heart of Elwynn Forest to consult with Herbalist Pomeroy. Well-versed in the botanical varieties of the surrounding woodlands, Pomeroy understands how dried petals, aromatic stems, and natural resins can be blended into functional camp gear. Rather than using herbs solely for alchemy flasks, she instructs gatherers in the construction of an Incense Candle. When burned at a campsite, this fragrant creation soothes exhausted travelers and lifts the spirits of mana-reliant companions. Finding Pomeroy and proving one's competence with the sickle ensures that an herbalist becomes an indispensable asset to any adventuring party venturing into the untamed wild.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Herbalism?",a="Camping 101: Herbalism is an introductory quest that instructs experienced herbalists on how to expand their gathering trade into camping utility. It directs players to travel to Elwynn Forest to meet Herbalist Pomeroy, who teaches how to craft specialized Incense Candles for field campsites.",al={"camping 101 herbalism quest","herbalism camping quest","what is camping 101 herbalism"}},{q="Who teaches the camping recipe for herbalists?",a="Herbalist Pomeroy teaches the herbalism camping recipe. She can be found in Elwynn Forest, where she shares her knowledge of local plants and guides skilled gatherers on how to craft aromatic Incense Candles to enhance their campsite rests.",al={"who teaches herbalism camping","camping 101 trainer","where is herbalist pomeroy","herbalist pomeroy location"}},{q="What item do herbalists learn to craft from this quest?",a="Completing this training allows herbalists to craft an Incense Candle for their campsite. Built using gathered herbs and botanical materials, the candle releases soothing aromas that raise spirits and provide a welcome benefit for spellcasters and healers resting in the wild.",al={"what do herbalists make for camping","herbalism camp item","incense candle herbalism","what does camping 101 teach"}},{q="What are the requirements to start Camping 101: Herbalism?",a="You must practice the Herbalism profession and demonstrate sufficient gathering proficiency before receiving this task. Once your herbalism skill has advanced beyond the novice level, instructors consider you experienced enough to learn how to prepare botanical camp utilities from Herbalist Pomeroy.",al={"camping 101 herbalism requirements","when can i get camping 101 herbalism","herbalism skill needed for camping 101"}},{q="Why are Incense Candles useful for adventuring parties?",a="Incense Candles release soothing herbal fragrances that uplift morale and restore mental focus. In the field, spellcasters and healers expend tremendous energy channeling magical powers, making the spiritual and magical boost granted by these scented candles particularly valuable during wilderness recoveries.",al={"what does incense candle do","why make incense candle","incense candle benefits","herbalism incense candle use"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The objective leads adventurers to Elwynn Forest, the lush woodland territory surrounding the city of Stormwind. Players journey into the Elwynn countryside to consult Herbalist Pomeroy, completing the assignment and unlocking their ability to create botanical camping supplies.",al={"camping 101 herbalism zone","where to turn in camping 101 herbalism","elwynn forest herbalism quest"}},{q="Can non-herbalists craft the Incense Candle?",a="No, crafting the Incense Candle is tied directly to the Herbalism profession. The recipe relies on specialized understanding of wild plants, floral preservation, and aromatic essences taught specifically by Herbalist Pomeroy to adventurers who gather botanicals.",al={"can anyone make incense candle","is incense candle herbalism only","do i need herbalism for incense candle"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="This quest elevates herbalism from a simple gathering trade into an active camp-support role, enabling botanists to craft Incense Candles that assist traveling companions.",["class:Priest"]="Priests and other devoted healers benefit greatly from resting near Incense Candles, which lift spirits and help restore vital energy during wilderness expeditions.",["class:Mage"]="Mages in the field value the soothing botanical properties of Incense Candles, making an herbalist companion especially desirable for long wilderness travels.",["faction:Alliance"]="With Herbalist Pomeroy residing in Elwynn Forest, Alliance adventurers enjoy easy proximity to this specialized campsite instruction."},kw={"camp","camping 101","campsite","elwynn forest","healers","herbalism","herbalist pomeroy","incense candle","spellcasters"},rel={"zone:elwynn"},m={id=97921},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:97922"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Leatherworking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Leatherworking directs apprentice leatherworkers to Adele Fielder in Goldshire to learn the craft of making a travel tent. Pitching a tent in the wild offers shelter and rest against harsh weather, providing adventurers a vital survival tool outside settled towns.",h="Camping 101: Leatherworking directs apprentice leatherworkers to Adele Fielder in Goldshire to learn the...",sec={{t="Shelter for the Open Road",b="For an adventurer wandering far beyond town gates, shelter is as vital as armor. Leatherworkers in Elwynn Forest are directed to Adele Fielder in Goldshire to expand their craft beyond simple belts and tunics. Once a crafter demonstrates proficiency with hide and needle, Adele shares the practical art of assembling a durable camp tent designed to withstand harsh weather on long overland expeditions.",sp=0},{t="The Essentials of Campcraft",b="A finished tent provides more than just aesthetic cover; it keeps weary travelers cool under scorching suns, dry during driving rainstorms, and well-rested between perilous encounters. However, wilderness etiquette and civic ordinances matter: Goldshire locals and city guards do not tolerate campsites pitched in the middle of town streets. Crafters must head into the open wilderness to set up camp, and Adele reminds all wanderers that no campsite is complete without a proper campfire learned from a culinary trainer.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches leatherworkers how to craft tents?",a="Adele Fielder can be found in the town of Goldshire within Elwynn Forest. She teaches qualified leatherworkers how to fashion sturdy survival tents once they have demonstrated proficiency in their craft.",al={"who gives tent training","adele fielder location","leatherworking tent teacher"}},{q="What purpose does a crafted tent serve?",a="A well-crafted tent shelters travelers from extreme weather, keeping them cool on sweltering days and dry during heavy rain. More importantly, it provides adventurers with the rest they need to remain sharp while traveling through the wilderness.",al={"why make tent","what does tent do","tent benefits"}},{q="Can you pitch a tent inside a town?",a="Tents cannot simply be pitched anywhere; townsfolk frown upon travelers cluttering streets or town centers with campsites. Adventurers are expected to take their gear out into the wilderness to set up camp properly.",al={"can i pitch tent in town","where can i use tent","tent in stormwind or goldshire"}},{q="What else is needed alongside a tent to complete a camp?",a="A tent alone does not make a functional campsite. Adele Fielder emphasizes that every proper camp requires a campfire, which provides warmth and practical utility in the wild.",al={"what else do i need for camp","tent campfire requirement","how to complete campsite"}},{q="Where can a traveler learn to build a campfire?",a="Culinary trainers and local cooks teach the fundamentals of lighting a campfire. Adventurers preparing to camp are directed to study the culinary arts so they know how to build a reliable fire wherever they roam.",al={"who teaches campfire","how to learn campfire","fire for camp"}},{q="When can a leatherworker learn to craft a camping tent?",a="Aspiring leatherworkers must first demonstrate proficiency in leatherworking before Adele Fielder will instruct them in crafting a tent. Once sufficient craft mastery is shown, the technique can be learned.",al={"tent skill requirements","how to get tent recipe","when can i craft tent"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Adele Fielder notes that no wilderness campsite is complete without a campfire, urging travelers to train in cooking to learn how to kindle a proper blaze.",["faction:Alliance"]="Based out of Goldshire in Elwynn Forest, this training guides Alliance leatherworkers toward practical survival crafts for their journeys across Azeroth."},kw={"adele fielder","campfire","camping","elwynn forest","goldshire","leatherworking","tent","wilderness survival"},rel={"subzone:goldshire"},m={id=97922},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Leatherworking"},fv=true}
E["quest:97923"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="Camping 101: Mining is an introductory quest that teaches miners how to craft a lodestone to enhance wilderness campsites. Instructed by Gelman Stonehand in Stormwind, prospectors learn to channel resonant earthcraft to bolster their companions' combat prowess around the campfire.",h="Camping 101: Mining is an introductory quest that teaches miners how to craft a lodestone to enhance...",sec={{t="The Earth's Resonant Power",b="Mining is tough, honest labor, but its rewards go far beyond smelting raw ingots and shaping armor. Dedicated miners in Stormwind can learn to harness the natural magnetism of the earth into a functional lodestone. When placed at an expedition's campsite, this stone bolsters the combat vigor of any fighter gathered around the hearth fire. Gelman Stonehand, the seasoned mining instructor in the kingdom's capital, passes down the craft to prospectors who demonstrate proficiency with a pickaxe.",sp=0},{t="Wilderness Camps and Campfires",b="A lodestone cannot simply be dropped onto the cobblestones of Cathedral Square or the Trade District. Townsfolk take a dim view of travelers pitching temporary encampments within city limits, meaning pioneers must head out into the untamed wilds to establish camp. Furthermore, specialized gear like a lodestone is useless without the warm foundation of a campfire, a fundamental wilderness survival skill taught by cooks across the realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Mining?",a="This quest introduces miners to the art of crafting specialized campsite enhancements. Once you prove your proficiency in mining, you speak with Gelman Stonehand in Stormwind to learn how to forge a lodestone.",al={"what is camping 101 mining","quest objective","what do i do"}},{q="Who teaches the Lodestone schematic?",a="Gelman Stonehand is a mining trainer situated within Stormwind City. He teaches qualified miners how to craft a lodestone to bolster their expeditions in the wild.",al={"who teaches lodestone","where is gelman stonehand","stonehand location"}},{q="What does a Lodestone do?",a="A lodestone is an item set down at a wilderness campsite. It emits an empowering presence around the campfire that enhances the combat prowess of warriors and other martial fighters who gather to rest there.",al={"what does lodestone do","lodestone buff","why make lodestone"}},{q="Can I set up camp inside Stormwind?",a="City authorities and common folk do not appreciate camps pitched in town squares or along city streets. You must journey out into the wilderness beyond settlement gates to set up camp and deploy your lodestone.",al={"can i camp in town","where can i place camp","camping restrictions"}},{q="Why do I need a campfire to set up camp?",a="Every campsite requires a basic campfire as its core foundation before any specialized camp equipment can be used. Cooks commonly teach travelers how to start fires with simple wood and flint.",al={"do i need cooking for camping","how to make camp","campfire requirement"}},{q="Who benefits most from gathering around a Lodestone?",a="Anyone who swings a blade, axe, or hammer benefits greatly from the lodestone's power. It radiates an extra martial edge that fighters seek out while resting by the flames before a dangerous battle.",al={"who benefits from lodestone","is lodestone for melee","lodestone classes"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="Miners who hone their craft can shape lodestones to enhance their wilderness campsites, offering martial benefits to allies resting beside them.",["profession:Cooking"]="Camps rely fundamentally on a well-tended campfire, making basic culinary training an essential partner to specialized camp accessories.",["faction:Alliance"]="Gelman Stonehand operates out of Stormwind City, guiding Alliance tradesfolk in turning raw earthcraft into practical field utilities."},kw={"campfire","camping 101: mining","campsite","gelman stonehand","lodestone","mining","stormwind","wilderness"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=97923},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:97924"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory instruction task for proficient skinners traveling through Elwynn Forest. It directs adventurers to Helene Peltskinner in Goldshire to learn how to fashion a Camp Chair out of harvested hides. Learning to craft personal camp furnishings allows wilderness travelers to rest and recuperate far beyond the safety of settled towns.",h="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory instruction task for proficient skinners traveling through Elwynn...",sec={{t="Creature Comforts in the Wild",b="For wilderness hunters and scouts, the art of harvesting beast hides provides more than trade goods and armor scraps. Seasoned skinners can fashion field amenities like a Camp Chair, allowing travelers to sit, rest their limbs, and maintain peak physical condition during long treks through dangerous lands. Helene Peltskinner in Goldshire instructs proficient skinners on how to turn raw beast pelts into comfortable campsite seating.",sp=0},{t="Rules of the Campsite",b="Setting up a field rest requires respect for local settlements and proper outdoor preparation. Most townspeople frown upon adventurers pitching personal camps in the town square, requiring travelers to head into the wilderness before unrolling their gear. Furthermore, no field site is truly viable without a campfire, leading many skinners to study basic culinary arts to master fire-building.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Skinning?",a="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory training task that directs proficient skinners to learn how to craft a Camp Chair from harvested beast hides, expanding their wilderness survival capabilities.",al={"what is camping 101 skinning","what does this quest do","skinning camp chair quest"}},{q="Who teaches skinners how to craft a Camp Chair?",a="Helene Peltskinner, a leatherworking and skinning trainer located in Goldshire within Elwynn Forest, teaches skilled skinners how to craft the chair.",al={"who teaches camp chair","helene peltskinner location","where to turn in camping 101 skinning"}},{q="What is the purpose of crafting a Camp Chair?",a="A Camp Chair provides an adventurer with a comfortable seat at their campsite to rest and recuperate, ensuring they stay in peak condition for travel and combat in the wild.",al={"what does a camp chair do","why make a camp chair","camp chair purpose"}},{q="Where can adventurers set up camp?",a="Town residents generally dislike campsites being pitched in the middle of settlements. Adventurers are expected to venture out into the wilderness before setting up their chairs and camps.",al={"can i camp in town","where can i place camp chair","camping in goldshire"}},{q="What else is required to build a complete campsite?",a="Every proper campsite requires a basic campfire before setting up gear. Adventurers without fire-making knowledge are encouraged to learn the culinary arts from a local cook.",al={"what do i need for a camp","do i need a fire to camp","campfire requirement"}},{q="Who is eligible to learn this camping craft?",a="This particular camping craft is tailored for skinners who have demonstrated proficiency in harvesting hides from fallen beasts, putting their leather gathered in the field to practical survival use.",al={"who can learn camp chair","can non skinners do this","skinning requirement camping"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Skinners learning to build a field camp are advised to consult a cook, as no campsite is complete without a basic campfire to keep warm and prepare meals.",["faction:Alliance"]="Novice Alliance skinners visiting Elwynn Forest are directed to Helene Peltskinner in Goldshire to expand their wilderness survival craft."},kw={"camp chair","campfire","camping 101: skinning","goldshire","helene peltskinner","skinning","wilderness camping"},rel={"subzone:goldshire"},m={id=97924},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:97925"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Tailoring",z="general",s="In Elwynn Forest, skilled clothworkers are sent to Eldrin at the Eastvale Logging Camp. He teaches them to weave a Faction Banner that inspires travelers and raises morale at wilderness camps.",h="In Elwynn Forest, skilled clothworkers are sent to Eldrin at the Eastvale Logging Camp.",sec={{t="A Weaver's Purpose in the Wild",b="Eldrin, an artisan at the Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest, guides skilled needleworkers. Tailoring makes garments and bags, but it can also sustain a journey. Those who have proven their skill with thread and loom can learn to make camp accessories for travel through the wilderness.",sp=0},{t="Banners of Morale",b="The Faction Banner is the chief craft Eldrin teaches. It stands beside a traveler's fire, where its emblem can hearten those on dangerous journeys and in battle. A banner at camp gives weary travelers a familiar sign of resolve and helps them press on through hostile lands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Camping 101: Tailoring?",a="Eldrin at the Eastvale Logging Camp in Elwynn Forest teaches skilled tailors to make a Faction Banner. The banner inspires travelers and raises morale at camp.",al={"what is camping 101 tailoring","tailoring camp item quest","how to get camp banner"}},{q="Who is Eldrin and where can he be found?",a="Eldrin is an artisan at the Eastvale Logging Camp in eastern Elwynn Forest. He teaches skilled tailors to craft faction banners for camps.",al={"who is eldrin","where is eldrin","eldrin location"}},{q="What does the Faction Banner do for a camp?",a="A Faction Banner inspires travelers and raises morale at camp. Its emblem offers weary travelers resolve as they journey through hostile lands.",al={"what does the tailoring banner do","faction banner camp","why craft a faction banner"}},{q="What requirements are needed to learn the craft from Eldrin?",a="Tailors must first prove their skill before Eldrin teaches them the craft. They need honed needlework to weave and assemble the banner.",al={"what skill do i need","camping banner requirements","can any tailor make a banner"}},{q="Where is the Eastvale Logging Camp located?",a="The Eastvale Logging Camp lies in the eastern woods of Elwynn Forest, along the road toward Redridge Mountains. Local craftspeople and workers gather at this logging hub.",al={"where is eastvale logging camp","eastvale logging camp elwynn"}},{q="Is this task exclusive to Alliance tailors?",a="Eldrin is in the heart of Elwynn Forest, within Alliance territory. The instruction there is for Alliance-aligned craftsmen from the eastern settlements.",al={"can horde do this quest","is this alliance only"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="Tailors can weave a Faction Banner to inspire travelers and raise morale at camp during long expeditions.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers are directed to Eldrin at the Eastvale Logging Camp in Elwynn Forest to learn to stitch a faction banner."},kw={"banner","camping","camping 101","eastvale logging camp","eldrin","elwynn forest","faction banner","tailoring"},rel={"subzone:eastvale-logging-camp","zone:elwynn"},m={id=97925},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Tailoring"},fv=true}
E["quest:97928"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Blacksmithing",z="general",s="You’re ready to make a Sharpening Wheel and try it in the wilderness. First, you’ll need a campfire to set up camp.",h="You’re ready to make a Sharpening Wheel and try it in the wilderness.",sec={{t="Ready to Camp",b="You can now craft a Sharpening Wheel. Take it into the wilderness to try it out, where you can set up camp away from the center of town. Bring a campfire along. A cook can teach you how to make one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can I craft now?",a="You can now craft your own Sharpening Wheel.",al={"sharpening wheel","blacksmithing craft","what do i make"}},{q="Where can I try the Sharpening Wheel?",a="You can try it in the wilderness, where camps can be set up away from the center of town.",al={"where to test it","sharpening wheel location","camping where"}},{q="What do I need before setting up camp?",a="You need a campfire before you can set up camp.",al={"campfire needed","how to start a camp","camping basics"}},{q="Who can teach me to make a campfire?",a="A cook can teach you to make a campfire.",al={"learn campfire","who teaches fire","cooking fire"}}},ang={["profession:Blacksmithing"]="You can now craft a Sharpening Wheel to use in the wilderness.",["profession:Cooking"]="A cook can teach you how to make a campfire."},kw={"blacksmithing","campfire","camping","cook","sharpening wheel","wilderness"},m={id=97928},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Blacksmithing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97931"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: First Aid",z="general",s="You are ready to make a bandage pouch, and now you need a place to try it.",h="You are ready to make a bandage pouch, and now you need a place to try it.",sec={{t="A Place to Camp",b="You can make the pouch, but most people would rather not camp in the middle of town. So take it out into the wilderness. Before you set up camp, you need a campfire. Most cooks can teach you how to make one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I try the bandage pouch?",a="Try the bandage pouch in the wilderness, where people are less likely to object to a camp.",al={"where try pouch","bandage pouch place"}},{q="What do I need before setting up camp?",a="You need a campfire before you can set up camp.",al={"campfire needed","what for camping"}},{q="Who can teach me to make a campfire?",a="Most cooks can teach you how to make a campfire.",al={"make campfire","fire teacher"}}},kw={"bandage pouch","campfire","camping","cooking","first aid","wilderness"},m={id=97931},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: First Aid"},fv=true}
E["quest:97932"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="In Mulgore, anglers can turn fishing into a comfort for camps in the wild. Show Uthan Stillwater in Bloodhoof Village your skill with rod and reel, and he teaches you to make a Fish Bowl for travelers resting outdoors.",h="In Mulgore, anglers can turn fishing into a comfort for camps in the wild.",sec={{t="Angling in the Wild",b="Anglers passing through Mulgore can put their quiet pastime to use around camp. If you have proved your skill with rod and reel, seek out Uthan Stillwater in Bloodhoof Village. He teaches skilled fishermen to make a Fish Bowl, a homely touch for a campsite.",sp=0},{t="Comforts of Camp",b="A Fish Bowl brings a little cheer to a hard journey. Its calm presence makes a resting place in the wild more pleasant and lifts the spirits of those gathered there. Better spirits mean sharper instincts and greater resilience in battle.",sp=0},{t="Campfire and Open Country",b="A campsite belongs in the open country, not in a settlement like Bloodhoof Village. The Fish Bowl can only be tried in the wilderness. A camp also needs a proper fire, so seek out a culinary trainer to learn basic fire-making before you set one up.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the Fish Bowl do for a campsite?",a="A Fish Bowl is a camp fixture made by skilled fishermen. It brings comfort and cheer to travelers resting in the wilderness, lifting their spirits and helping them stay sharp in battle.",al={"fish bowl camp","fish bowl what do","fish bowl benefit"}},{q="Who teaches the Fish Bowl recipe?",a="Uthan Stillwater teaches the craft in Bloodhoof Village, in Mulgore. He teaches anglers who have already shown skill at fishing.",al={"where learn fish bowl","who teaches fish bowl","uthan stillwater location"}},{q="Why can't you pitch camp inside a settlement?",a="Settlements like Bloodhoof Village do not allow travelers to set up camps in town. Pitch your camp in the open wild instead.",al={"camp in town","why cant i camp in bloodhoof village","camping inside cities"}},{q="What else is required to set up a camp?",a="A proper campsite needs a campfire. If you do not know how to kindle one, seek out a cooking trainer to learn basic fire-making.",al={"campfire camping requirement","need fire for camp","camping basics fire"}},{q="What requirements are needed before learning the Fish Bowl?",a="You need to show fishing skill before Uthan Stillwater will teach you to make camp fixtures. If you are a novice angler, spend time honing your craft by the water first.",al={"fishing proficiency camping","when can i get fish bowl","camping 101 fishing requirements"}},{q="Why would an adventurer want a Fish Bowl while camping?",a="A Fish Bowl brings a touch of home to a hard, tiring life on the road. Watching a fish by the fire can bring joy and lift an adventurer's spirits.",al={"why bring fish bowl camping","camp fish bowl lore"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="A proper campsite needs a campfire. Basic cooking training pairs naturally with a fisherman's camp in the wild.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde angler traveling through Mulgore, you can learn to make camp fixtures from Uthan Stillwater in Bloodhoof Village."},kw={"bloodhoof village","campfire","camping","camping 101: fishing","fish bowl","fishing","mulgore","uthan stillwater"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village"},m={id=97932},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Fishing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97934"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Leatherworking",z="general",s="In Bloodhoof Village, leatherworkers can learn to make a tent for shelter on the road. Chaw Stronghide teaches the craft to those ready to handle heavier hides and rugged stitching.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, leatherworkers can learn to make a tent for shelter on the road.",sec={{t="Shelters for the Open Plains",b="Across Kalimdor, leatherworkers who know how to stitch a sturdy tent can shelter from the midday sun and prairie rain. A tent also gives weary travelers a place to rest and recover before crossing the open plains again.",sp=0},{t="The Craft of Chaw Stronghide",b="At Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, Chaw Stronghide teaches leatherworkers who can handle heavier hides and rugged stitching. Once they prove their skill, he shows them how to make a durable tent for journeys far from civilization.",sp=0},{t="Camps in the Wild",b="Choose a camp with care and respect for local settlements. Village folk frown on travelers setting up along central paths and thoroughfares, so take your tent into the wild frontier. A proper camp also needs a fire. Ask a skilled cook how to kindle one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does this quest teach leatherworkers?",a="Leatherworkers learn to make a tent for their travels. It shelters them from harsh weather and gives them a place to rest in the wilderness.",al={"leatherworking tent quest","what does camping 101 teach","leatherworking camp item"}},{q="Who teaches leatherworkers how to craft camp tents?",a="Chaw Stronghide teaches tent making in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He shares the craft once a leatherworker proves able to handle heavier hides and rugged stitching.",al={"who teaches tent crafting","chaw stronghide tent","leatherworking tent trainer"}},{q="Where can travelers set up their camp tents?",a="Pitch camp in the open wilderness, away from populated settlements. Town residents and village elders do not want private shelters in town squares or along roads.",al={"where can i pitch tent","can i camp in town","tent placement"}},{q="Why is a campfire needed alongside a tent?",a="A campfire brings warmth and protection to a wilderness camp. Visit a skilled cook to learn how to kindle one before traveling into the wild.",al={"why do i need a campfire","cooking campfire tent","tent and fire"}},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village lies in central Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff along the shores of Stonebull Lake. The tauren settlement serves hunters, gatherers and artisans, and offers training, trade supplies and guidance to travelers exploring the plains.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof village location","how to find chaw stronghide"}},{q="Do you need cooking skill to maintain a proper campsite?",a="Cooking is not needed to stitch a leather tent, but cooks teach travelers how to kindle campfires. A fire completes an outdoor camp, warms the night air and lets travelers prepare food on long journeys.",al={"is cooking needed for camping","do i need cooking","campfire cooking basics"}},{q="What benefits does a camp tent provide to a traveler?",a="A tent shelters travelers from intense heat and heavy rain. Resting inside helps them stay refreshed and alert as they explore distant territories.",al={"benefits of camping tent","why make a tent","what does tent do"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Making portable leather shelters suits the tauren tribes' nomadic traditions and wilderness survival heritage across Mulgore's plains.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde travelers crossing Kalimdor's wide, arid lands rely on rugged camping gear far from fortified strongholds.",["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking trainers teach travelers to build a proper campfire, completing a leather tent's warm campsite."},kw={"bloodhoof village","campfire","camping 101","chaw stronghide","leatherworking","mulgore","shelter","tent"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village"},m={id=97934},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Leatherworking"},fv=true}
E["quest:97935"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="In Thunder Bluff, miners can learn to carry their craft into the wild. Brek Stonehoof teaches them to shape a lodestone that gives strength to those resting by a campfire.",h="In Thunder Bluff, miners can learn to carry their craft into the wild.",sec={{t="Honest Labor in the Earth",b="Mining may lack the glamour of high arcana, but it is honest work found across Azeroth. Miners draw useful minerals and dense stone from deep rock faces. Those materials make weapons and armor, and now they serve a purpose in the field. A miner can shape stone into sturdy tools for a wilderness camp.",sp=0},{t="The Lodestone of Thunder Bluff",b="Brek Stonehoof in Thunder Bluff teaches miners to put their trade to use in the wild. Once they show skill with a pick, he teaches them to shape a camp lodestone. Set beside an open blaze, the stone lends vigor and an edge in battle to those preparing to wield a blade.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the lodestone do for a campsite?",a="A lodestone set beside a campfire lends vigor and combat strength to those gathered there.",al={"lodestone camp benefit","lodestone bonus","what does lodestone do"}},{q="Who is Brek Stonehoof?",a="Brek Stonehoof lives in Thunder Bluff, the tauren capital. He teaches skilled miners to make camping tools from stone and ore.",al={"brek stonehoof location","where is brek stonehoof","who teaches mining camp item"}},{q="When can a miner learn how to craft a lodestone?",a="A miner must first show skill in the trade before Brek Stonehoof teaches the craft of shaping a lodestone.",al={"when can i learn lodestone","mining skill requirement lodestone"}},{q="Who benefits most from a miner's lodestone?",a="Anyone preparing to wield a weapon can benefit from the lodestone's strength while resting beside it.",al={"who benefits lodestone","is lodestone for melee"}},{q="How does mining tie into establishing a campsite?",a="Mining provides metal for smelters and blacksmiths, and lets travelers shape stone tools like lodestones to strengthen a wilderness camp.",al={"why mine for camping","how does mining help camp"}},{q="What is the purpose of Camping 101: Mining?",a="In Thunder Bluff, aspiring miners can learn from Brek Stonehoof to make field tools for survival in the wild.",al={"camping 101 mining overview","what is camping 101 mining"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="As a miner, you can put your gathering craft to work in the wild by preparing a lodestone for an expedition camp.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can learn from Brek Stonehoof atop Thunder Bluff's mesas, drawing on tauren knowledge of stone and earthcraft.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you can give those resting by your campfire a lodestone's strength for battles ahead."},kw={"brek stonehoof","camp","campfire","camping 101: mining","lodestone","mining","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=97935},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:97936"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning guides skilled hide-gatherers to Bloodhoof Village to expand their practical field craft. Under the instruction of Yonn Deepcut, skinners learn to fashion beast pelts into comfortable Camp Chairs for wilderness survival. This training emphasizes the importance of rest and preparation when journeying far beyond the safety of civilization.",h="Camping 101: Skinning guides skilled hide-gatherers to Bloodhoof Village to expand their practical field...",sec={{t="Hides of the Wild",b="Harvesting the hides and pelts of fallen beasts is one of the most practical survival skills on Azeroth. Beyond tanning leather for armor and gear, a resourceful skinner can turn rough animal skins into wilderness comforts. For travelers who spend long weeks traversing wild territories, learning how to work hides into resting equipment makes the difference between exhaustion and staying sharp in combat.",sp=0},{t="Lessons in Bloodhoof Village",b="In Mulgore, skinners are directed to Bloodhoof Village to speak with Yonn Deepcut. Once a skinner demonstrates basic proficiency in harvesting beast hides, Yonn imparts the craft of stitching together a Camp Chair. Settlers in towns and villages do not welcome travelers pitching tents in the middle of streets, so these chairs are designed specifically for wilderness use, paired with a crackling campfire taught by local cooks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Skinning?",a="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory task directing proficient skinners to seek out a specialized instructor in Mulgore. Upon demonstrating skill in harvesting hides, skinners learn how to fashion a Camp Chair to furnish their wilderness camps.",al={"what is camping 101 skinning","camping skinning quest","what does this quest do"}},{q="Who teaches the Camp Chair recipe?",a="Yonn Deepcut, a skinner stationed in Bloodhoof Village within Mulgore, teaches the technique for crafting a Camp Chair once an adventurer demonstrates proficiency in harvesting beast hides.",al={"who teaches camp chair","who to talk to for camping skinning","where is yonn deepcut"}},{q="What is a Camp Chair used for?",a="A Camp Chair provides a comfortable place to rest when pitching camp out in the wild. Taking time to sit and relax helps adventurers recover their strength and stay in peak condition for upcoming battles.",al={"what does a camp chair do","camp chair use","why make a camp chair"}},{q="Where can you set up a camp?",a="Townspeople frown upon travelers setting up camps in the middle of settlements. Adventurers must head out into the wilderness to set up their camps and test out their crafted gear.",al={"where can i make a camp","can i camp in town","camp chair in city"}},{q="What else is required to set up a camp?",a="A camp is not complete without a roaring campfire. Adventurers must build a fire before establishing their camp, a fundamental skill commonly taught by culinary trainers and cooks.",al={"what else do i need for camp","campfire needed","do i need a fire to camp"}},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a major tauren settlement situated in the windswept plains of Mulgore, home to various tradespeople including skinning trainers.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof village location"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Horde skinners operating in Mulgore can visit Bloodhoof Village to learn how to make functional camp gear from their gathered hides.",["race:Tauren"]="Yonn Deepcut teaches skinners in the heart of Mulgore how to craft comfortable chairs out of beast hides for wilderness rest.",["profession:Cooking"]="A proper camp requires a warm campfire to accompany seating, which culinary trainers throughout the world can teach an adventurer to start."},kw={"bloodhoof village","camp chair","campfire","camping 101","camping 101: skinning","mulgore","skinning","yonn deepcut"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village"},m={id=97936},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:97938"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Alchemy",z="general",s="In Dolanaar, Cyndra Kindwhisper teaches alchemists how to tend a campsite. Her lesson joins potion craft with wilderness survival and shows how to make a Mana Well for traveling healers and casters.",h="In Dolanaar, Cyndra Kindwhisper teaches alchemists how to tend a campsite.",sec={{t="Field Craft for Alchemists",b="Alchemists learn to carry their craft beyond the workbench. Cyndra Kindwhisper shows them how to make useful camp fittings that can sustain a traveling party on long journeys through the wild.",sp=0},{t="The Mana Well of Dolanaar",b="In Dolanaar, alchemists who know the basics can seek Cyndra Kindwhisper. She teaches them to set up a Mana Well at camp. Its refreshing magic helps healers and spellcasters replenish their mana far from taverns and settlements.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Alchemy?",a="Camping 101: Alchemy teaches novice alchemists in Dolanaar to use their craft at camp. They learn to make useful wilderness fittings with their potion-making skills.",al={"what is camping 101 alchemy","camping 101 alchemy quest","alchemy camp quest"}},{q="Who teaches this camping skill?",a="Cyndra Kindwhisper teaches alchemists in Dolanaar, the night elf settlement on Teldrassil. She instructs those who have shown skill in brewing and crafting specialized camping items.",al={"who is cyndra kindwhisper","cyndra kindwhisper location","where is cyndra kindwhisper"}},{q="What is a Mana Well?",a="A Mana Well is an alchemist's camp installation. It offers arcane refreshment to spellcasters and healers who need to replenish their reserves on the road.",al={"what does a mana well do","mana well camp item","mana well lore"}},{q="Where does this quest take you?",a="You travel to Dolanaar, a night elf village in central Teldrassil. It is a hub for young adventurers and tradecraft trainers leaving Shadowglen.",al={"where is dolanaar","how to get to dolanaar","dolanaar location"}},{q="Who benefits from an alchemist's camp installation?",a="Traveling healers and spellcasters benefit most from Mana Wells. The refreshing fonts help replenish them on taxing journeys far from towns.",al={"who benefits from mana wells","who uses mana wells","mana well spellcasters"}},{q="What is required before learning to craft a Mana Well?",a="Alchemists must first show skill in basic brewing and potion craft. Then Cyndra Kindwhisper teaches them to assemble camping fittings such as a Mana Well.",al={"when can i learn the mana well","camping 101 requirements","skill proficiency needed"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance alchemists traveling across Teldrassil are directed to Dolanaar to learn magical camp craft for wilderness survival.",["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf herbalist or alchemist in Dolanaar, you can join natural botany with camp craft to support spellcasters in the wild."},kw={"alchemy","camp","camping 101","camping 101: alchemy","cyndra kindwhisper","dolanaar","mana well","teldrassil"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar"},m={id=97938},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Alchemy"},fv=true}
E["quest:97942"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: First Aid",z="general",s="Camping 101: First Aid introduces adventurers to wilderness survival by teaching them how to craft a field medical kit for their campsite. Instructed by Byancie in Dolanaar, it emphasizes self-reliance and practical triage far away from town clinics.",h="Camping 101: First Aid introduces adventurers to wilderness survival by teaching them how to craft a field...",sec={{t="Field Medicine in the Wilds",b="Venturing deep into the wilds carries constant hazard, from beast bites to jagged terrain. For rangers and field travelers, medical supplies cannot always wait for a return to town. Learning to assemble a dedicated First Aid Kit turns a simple roadside rest into a functional field station, ensuring that minor scrapes do not turn fatal under the open sky.",sp=0},{t="Lessons in Dolanaar",b="Byancie, found among the shaded bowers of Dolanaar, instructs travelers skilled in triage on how to package medical supplies suitable for rough trail life. Setting up such gear requires respect for local settlements; townsfolk do not tolerate pitched tents and messy supplies in town centers, requiring campers to strike out into the forest before establishing their camp.",sp=0},{t="The Hearth of the Camp",b="Every functional encampment begins with an open flame. Before setting down medical kits or bedding, adventurers must lay down a campfire for warmth and protection. Because building and tending a fire is foundational to field survival, trainers recommend consulting a cook to master the art of kindling before attempting to maintain a wilderness camp.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Byancie?",a="Byancie is a teacher located in the settlement of Dolanaar on Teldrassil. She instructs adventurers who have shown proficiency in their medical skills on how to assemble specialized First Aid Kits for wilderness camping.",al={"who is byancie","byancie location","where is byancie"}},{q="What is a camping First Aid Kit?",a="A First Aid Kit is a specialized camping utility crafted by adventurers trained in medicine. When placed at an established campsite, it provides a dedicated station to treat wounds and offer vital healing out in the field.",al={"what is first aid kit camp","how does first aid kit work","camping first aid kit"}},{q="Where can you set up a camp?",a="Settlement residents and guards frown upon travelers pitching temporary campsites within city squares or settlement centers. Camps must be established out in the open wilderness away from town borders.",al={"can i camp in town","why camp outside town","camp rules"}},{q="Why is a campfire required for a camp?",a="A campfire is the core foundation of any wilderness encampment, providing heat, light, and safety. Adventurers typically learn how to kindle a basic campfire from culinary trainers and cooks.",al={"why do i need campfire","cooking campfire camp","camp fire requirement"}},{q="Why is First Aid considered pragmatic for hunters?",a="Hunters routinely operate alone in remote reaches of the wilderness, far from established healers or templars. Understanding field triage and bandage craft ensures self-reliance when tracking game or surviving ambushes.",al={"why first aid for hunters","hunters healing"}},{q="How do you learn to craft a camping First Aid Kit?",a="Adventurers must first demonstrate basic proficiency in the First Aid discipline, after which Byancie in Dolanaar can teach them the specific techniques needed to assemble a camping First Aid Kit.",al={"how to get first aid kit","learn first aid kit craft"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For hunters spending long periods tracking beasts far from civilization, pairing wilderness survival with First Aid allows them to tend to injuries without relying on town healers.",["profession:Cooking"]="Camp life demands both sustenance and warmth, making basic culinary training essential for learning how to build the required campfire."},kw={"byancie","campfire","camping","dolanaar","field medic","first aid","first aid kit","hunter"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar","topic:hunter"},m={id=97942},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: First Aid"},fv=true}
E["quest:97943"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="In Darnassus, anglers are directed to Astaia to learn how a Fish Bowl can brighten a wilderness camp. The craft rewards skilled fishermen with a touch of comfort for life outdoors.",h="In Darnassus, anglers are directed to Astaia to learn how a Fish Bowl can brighten a wilderness camp.",sec={{t="Comfort at the Campsite",b="Anglers crossing Kalimdor can put quiet hours by the water to use. In Darnassus, Astaia teaches experienced fishermen to turn their catches and skill into campsite comforts. One of these is a Fish Bowl, made to display at camp and bring cheer to weary travelers.",sp=0},{t="Wilderness Necessities",b="A wilderness camp needs more than fishing gear. You cannot pitch one in a crowded city center or urban district, so you must find open ground. Every camp also needs a warm fire. Visit a local cooking trainer to learn basic fire-making before settling down for the night.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches you how to craft camping items for fishing?",a="Astaia in Darnassus teaches experienced fishermen to craft a decorative Fish Bowl for camp once they show proficiency in fishing.",al={"who teaches camping fishing","where is astaia","fish bowl trainer"}},{q="What purpose does a Fish Bowl serve at camp?",a="A Fish Bowl brings comfort and joy to resting adventurers. A happy, well-rested camper fights with renewed vigor on outdoor journeys.",al={"what does fish bowl do","camp fish bowl use","fish bowl camp fixture"}},{q="Can you set up a camp inside Darnassus or other cities?",a="You cannot pitch a camp in the heart of a settlement or town center. Head into the wilderness to set up camp and place fixtures such as a Fish Bowl.",al={"can you camp in darnassus","where to make camp","camp in cities"}},{q="What is required before setting up a camp?",a="A basic campfire is required for a campsite. Visit a cooking trainer to learn how to kindle one before establishing a wilderness camp.",al={"what do you need for a camp","how to start camp","campfire required"}},{q="Do you need fishing skill to learn how to craft a Fish Bowl?",a="Yes. An adventurer must first demonstrate proficiency in fishing to learn the craft from Astaia.",al={"fishing proficiency camping","when can i learn fish bowl"}},{q="Why do adventurers set up camps in the wild?",a="A camp gives outdoor travelers a place to rest and find morale-boosting comforts such as a Fish Bowl, helping them stay rested and capable in the wilderness.",al={"why make camp wow forever","camping benefits"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance angler, you can visit Astaia in Darnassus to learn campsite craft.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, your basic training helps you make the campfire every camp needs."},kw={"astaia","campfire","camping","camping 101: fishing","campsite","darnassus","fish bowl","fishing"},rel={"zone:darnassus"},m={id=97943},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Fishing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97944"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="general",s="In Dolanaar, herbalists learn to make camp goods from the plants they gather. Malorne Bladeleaf teaches skilled gatherers to craft Incense Candles, which bolster healers and spellcasters by the campfire.",h="In Dolanaar, herbalists learn to make camp goods from the plants they gather.",sec={{t="More Than Picking Flowers",b="Herbalism can serve a traveler beyond gathering flowers for sale or for alchemists. In the wilderness, harvested plants can become useful camp equipment. Preparing them for survival lets your gathered plants support longer journeys in the field.",sp=0},{t="The Soothing Flame of Dolanaar",b="At Dolanaar, Malorne Bladeleaf teaches skilled herbalists to make an Incense Candle. Its soothing flame creates a focused, high-spirited refuge around the campfire. Healers and spellcasters value its revitalizing presence as they restore their magical energies between perils.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="Skilled herbalists travel to Malorne Bladeleaf in Dolanaar to learn how to make camp goods from gathered plants.",al={"what is camping 101 herbalism","camping herbalism quest"}},{q="What does an Incense Candle do for a camp?",a="An Incense Candle lifts spirits and creates a soothing, focused atmosphere around a campfire.",al={"what does incense candle do","incense candle benefit"}},{q="Who is Malorne Bladeleaf?",a="Malorne Bladeleaf teaches skilled herbalists in Dolanaar to make camp supplies, including Incense Candles.",al={"who teaches incense candle","who is malorne bladeleaf","bladeleaf dolanaar"}},{q="Where does this quest take you?",a="You travel to Dolanaar, a night elf settlement on Teldrassil and a hub for travelers, trainers, and woodland crafters.",al={"where is dolanaar","dolanaar location"}},{q="Why are Incense Candles useful to magic users?",a="An Incense Candle creates a serene, focused place where healers and spellcasters can restore their spirits and magical strength during wilderness journeys.",al={"who benefits from incense candle","is incense candle for casters"}},{q="What is required before Malorne Bladeleaf will teach you?",a="You need enough practical skill in herbalism before Malorne Bladeleaf will teach you to make camp supplies.",al={"when can i learn incense candle","herbalism skill requirement camping"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="As an herbalist, you can use gathered plants for wilderness survival as well as alchemy, making camp supplies such as Incense Candles.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance herbalist near Teldrassil, you can learn fieldcraft from local night elf practitioners in Dolanaar.",["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, you share a tradition of understanding the restorative properties of nature's herbs, taught in settlements such as Dolanaar."},kw={"camping","camping 101: herbalism","dolanaar","herbalism","incense candle","malorne bladeleaf","teldrassil"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar"},m={id=97944},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:97946"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Leatherworking",z="general",s="On Teldrassil, leatherworkers can learn to make a shelter for the trail. Find Nadyia Maneweaver on the road between Dolanaar and Darnassus, where she teaches the craft of tents that keep travelers dry and cool.",h="On Teldrassil, leatherworkers can learn to make a shelter for the trail.",sec={{t="Shelter in the Wilderness",b="Across Kalimdor, an open sky can bring hard weather. Skilled leatherworkers learn to shape hides and pelts into sturdy shelters, giving travelers a safe place to rest wherever the road leads. A tent lets wanderers find the rest they need far from the inns.",sp=0},{t="The Road from Dolanaar",b="Nadyia Maneweaver teaches proficient leatherworkers along the forest road between Dolanaar and Darnassus. She shows them how to stitch a sturdy tent for the trail. A tent needs a campfire to make a proper camp, and cooks teach that craft. Set the shelter in the open wilds, away from town streets.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches this camping skill?",a="Nadyia Maneweaver teaches the camping skill along the road from Dolanaar toward Darnassus on Teldrassil.",al={"who gives camping 101 leatherworking","who teaches camping 101","nadyia maneweaver"}},{q="What do leatherworkers learn from this quest?",a="Leatherworkers learn to make a tent that keeps travelers cool in hot weather and dry in the rain.",al={"what does camping 101 teach","tent recipe","what do i learn from camping 101"}},{q="Where can you pitch a camp tent?",a="Pitch tents in the open wilderness, outside settlements and city centers.",al={"where can i pitch my tent","can i pitch a tent in town","camping in cities"}},{q="What else is required to set up a camp?",a="A camp needs a lit campfire as well as a tent. Cooking trainers teach the craft of making one.",al={"what do i need for a campsite","campfire requirement","do i need a campfire for tent"}},{q="Why is having a tent useful for travelers?",a="A tent shelters travelers from the weather and gives them rest, helping them stay alert far from inns.",al={"why build a tent","tent rest benefit","why camp"}},{q="Where is Nadyia Maneweaver located?",a="Nadyia Maneweaver is along the main road from Dolanaar toward Darnassus, the night elf capital.",al={"where is nadyia maneweaver","nadyia location","road to darnassus trainer"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="As a rogue, you rely on rest and shelter in the wilds to stay sharp. A leather tent can help you find both.",["profession:Cooking"]="To make a proper camp, you need a warm campfire. Cooking trainers across the land teach you how to make one."},kw={"campfire","camping","darnassus","dolanaar","leatherworking","nadyia maneweaver","tent","wilderness"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar","zone:darnassus","quest:487","topic:rogue"},m={id=97946},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Leatherworking"},fv=true}
E["quest:97948"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="You’re sent to Auberdine to learn a new use for mining. There, Kurdram Stonehammer teaches skilled miners to craft a lodestone that strengthens allies gathered around a campfire.",h="You’re sent to Auberdine to learn a new use for mining.",sec={{t="The Practical Art of Earth and Stone",b="Mining has uses beyond smelting ingots and hammering armor. Experienced prospectors shape raw earth and magnetic ore into camping gear, so travelers can make well-fortified hearths in the wilderness. This introduction shows miners a practical use for their craft.",sp=0},{t="A Mentor in Auberdine",b="In Auberdine, the coastal town of Darkshore, promising miners seek out Kurdram Stonehammer. Once a miner proves dedication and skill, Kurdram teaches the method for making a camp lodestone. Set beside a fire, the stone strengthens the martial companions gathered nearby.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Mining?",a="You’re sent to Auberdine to find Kurdram Stonehammer. Once you show your mining skill, he teaches you to make a lodestone that strengthens a camp.",al={"what is this quest","camping 101 mining objective","what do i do camping mining"}},{q="Who is Kurdram Stonehammer?",a="Kurdram Stonehammer is a craftsman in Auberdine on the coast of Darkshore. He teaches qualified miners to make camping gear, including lodestones.",al={"who is kurdram stonehammer","auberdine mining trainer","kurdram stonehammer location"}},{q="What is a camp lodestone?",a="Miners craft a camp lodestone to set beside a campfire. It strengthens physical fighters gathered nearby.",al={"what does lodestone do","camp lodestone effect","camping oomph buff"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="Auberdine is a coastal port in Darkshore, an Alliance harbor and crossroads in northern Kalimdor. Kurdram Stonehammer lives there and teaches miners campcraft.",al={"where is auberdine","auberdine location","how to get to auberdine"}},{q="Who benefits from a camp lodestone?",a="Close-quarters fighters especially value a camp lodestone. Its power gives travelers gathered around the fire greater vigor in combat.",al={"who benefits from lodestone","is lodestone good for warriors","camp lodestone buff target"}},{q="When can you learn to craft the lodestone?",a="Kurdram Stonehammer teaches lodestone crafting once you show sufficient skill in mining.",al={"when can i learn lodestone","mining level for camp lodestone","proficiency requirement"}}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="As a miner, you can shape rough stone into a camp lodestone that aids travelers on the road and strengthens frontline fighters.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, you can find Kurdram Stonehammer in Auberdine on the shores of Darkshore. He teaches campcraft to those passing through northern Kalimdor."},kw={"auberdine","campcraft","campfire","camping 101: mining","darkshore","kurdram stonehammer","lodestone","mining"},rel={"subzone:auberdine"},m={id=97948},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:97949"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning instructs wilderness travelers on how to craft their own portable Camp Chair. It emphasizes the fundamentals of outdoor survival, reminding adventurers that a proper campsite requires venturing away from town and starting a campfire.",h="Camping 101: Skinning instructs wilderness travelers on how to craft their own portable Camp Chair.",sec={{t="Comforts of the Trail",b="Camping 101: Skinning introduces outdoorsmen and skinners to the craft of wilderness survival. By putting harvested hides and materials to practical use, travelers learn to construct a portable Camp Chair for their journeys. Rather than cluttering bustling city centers where locals object to pitched camps, adventurers are expected to take their craft out into the open wilds of Azeroth.",sp=0},{t="The Essential Campfire",b="No campsite in the wild is functional without a warming campfire. Establishing a camp requires a fire to be laid first, offering heat and shelter against the elements. Novice survivalists who have not yet picked up basic culinary skills are encouraged to visit a cooking trainer, who can instruct them in the fundamental craft of kindling a fire anywhere in the field.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do you learn from Camping 101: Skinning?",a="Completing this task teaches you how to fashion your own Camp Chair, an essential comfort for resting out in the wilderness.",al={"what do i get","what does camping 101 give","camp chair craft"}},{q="Can you set up a camp in town?",a="Townsfolk generally frown upon travelers pitching camps in town squares or city streets. You will need to travel out into the wilds to pitch your camp properly.",al={"can i camp in town","pitch tent in stormwind","camping inside cities"}},{q="Do you need a campfire to make camp?",a="A proper campsite requires a lit campfire before it can be established. Basic fire-starting techniques can be learned from most cooking trainers throughout Azeroth.",al={"need fire for camp","how to make campfire","fire for camp chair"}},{q="Where can you learn how to make a campfire?",a="Most culinary instructors and cooking trainers can teach you the basics of preparing food, which includes how to start a simple campfire with basic flint and wood.",al={"who teaches campfire","cooking fire trainer","where to learn cooking fire"}},{q="Why must you head into the wilderness to set up camp?",a="The wilderness provides the open space needed to pitch camp without disturbing town residents or obstructing local roads and settlements.",al={"why need wilderness","where can i use camp chair","where to pitch camp"}},{q="How does skinning relate to making a camp chair?",a="Skinners work with leathers and hides harvested from beasts, materials suited for stretching and stitching into portable outdoor seating such as a Camp Chair.",al={"skinning camping relation","why skinning for chair","skinning camp chair"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Campers are advised to learn basic cooking from a culinary trainer, as knowing how to build a basic campfire is essential to setting up a proper campsite in the wild."},kw={"camp chair","campfire","camping 101","camping 101: skinning","cooking","skinning","wilderness"},m={id=97949},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:97951"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Alchemy",z="general",s="At your camp, alchemists can learn to craft a Mana Well. Carolai Anise in Brill teaches the craft once you have shown skill in alchemy.",h="At your camp, alchemists can learn to craft a Mana Well.",sec={{t="A camp for brews",b="Carolai Anise in Brill teaches alchemists to craft a Mana Well for their camp. Once you know the craft, you can make camp in the wilderness. First, you need a campfire, which cooks can teach you to make.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches me to craft a Mana Well?",a="Carolai Anise in Brill can teach you to craft a Mana Well once you have shown skill in alchemy.",al={"mana well trainer","who teaches mana well","carolai anise alchemy"}},{q="What is a Mana Well for?",a="A Mana Well is a camping item you can craft as an alchemist. Healers and spellcasters are drawn to its refreshing taste.",al={"mana well use","what does mana well do","mana camp item"}},{q="Where can I learn the Mana Well craft?",a="Carolai Anise in Brill can teach you the Mana Well craft.",al={"mana well location","brill alchemy trainer","where is carolai"}},{q="What do I need before starting a camp?",a="You need a campfire before you start your camp. Most cooks can teach you to make one.",al={"campfire needed","how start camp","camp basics"}}},kw={"alchemist","alchemy","brill","camp","campfire","camping 101","carolai anise","mana well"},rel={"npc:carolai-anise","subzone:brill"},m={id=97951},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Alchemy"},fv=true}
E["quest:97953"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Enchanting",z="general",s="At Brill, Vance Undergloom teaches enchanters to make an Enchanted Lute for camp. First, show proficiency in your skill.",h="At Brill, Vance Undergloom teaches enchanters to make an Enchanted Lute for camp.",sec={{t="A Camp Companion",b="At a camp in the wilds, enchanters can learn to make an Enchanted Lute. Bring it along to lend some entertainment to your time away from the trail.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can I learn to craft?",a="You can learn to craft an Enchanted Lute for your camp.",al={"enchanted lute","camping enchanting item","what does vance teach"}},{q="Who teaches me about the lute?",a="Vance Undergloom in Brill can teach you to craft an Enchanted Lute.",al={"who is vance","vance undergloom","lute teacher"}},{q="Where do I find Vance Undergloom?",a="Vance Undergloom is in Brill.",al={"where is vance","vance location","brill enchanter"}},{q="What do I need before speaking with Vance?",a="Show proficiency in your skill before you learn to craft the camping item.",al={"skill requirement","show proficiency","enchanting requirement"}},{q="Why bring an Enchanted Lute to camp?",a="The Enchanted Lute brings entertainment to your time at camp.",al={"lute purpose","camp entertainment","why enchanted lute"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="As an enchanter, you can learn to craft an Enchanted Lute for camp after showing proficiency in your skill."},kw={"brill","camp","camping 101","enchanted lute","enchanter","enchanting","vance undergloom"},rel={"subzone:brill"},m={id=97953},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Enchanting"},fv=true}
E["quest:97955"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: First Aid",z="general",s="Camping 101: First Aid directs adventurers with medical training to seek out Nurse Neela in Brill. By learning to construct a First Aid Kit for their campsites, travelers can provide life-saving treatment and recuperation while surviving deep in the wilderness.",h="Camping 101: First Aid directs adventurers with medical training to seek out Nurse Neela in Brill.",sec={{t="Medicine in the Field",b="Venturing beyond fortified settlements carries constant physical danger, from hostile beasts to opposing factions. For traveling practitioners of First Aid, learning to assemble a dedicated First Aid Kit allows an ordinary wilderness resting stop to serve as a mobile field clinic, patching up wounds and restoring vitality far from civil infrastructure.",sp=0},{t="Instruction in Brill",b="In the Forsaken town of Brill, Nurse Neela provides instruction on turning basic medical expertise into practical camp equipment. Once a traveler demonstrates sufficient skill in bandaging and treatment, she shares the techniques required to construct a portable kit. Setting up such equipment requires a proper wilderness site centered around a basic campfire, which standard cooking instructors can teach travelers to build.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Nurse Neela?",a="Nurse Neela is a medical practitioner located in the Forsaken settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. She instructs skilled First Aid practitioners on how to craft First Aid Kits designed for field campsites.",al={"who is nurse neela","where is nurse neela","nurse neela location"}},{q="What does a First Aid Kit do at a camp?",a="A First Aid Kit is a specialized camping accessory crafted by practitioners of First Aid. When set up at a wilderness campsite, it provides essential healing resources to treat injuries sustained while adventuring.",al={"what is a first aid kit","first aid kit purpose","camp first aid kit"}},{q="Why do you need a campfire to set up camp?",a="A campfire is the foundation of any wilderness encampment, required before setting up additional amenities like medical kits. Basic campfire construction is commonly taught by culinary trainers and cooks.",al={"why need campfire","how to make campfire","cooking campfire camp"}},{q="Can you set up a camp inside a town?",a="Town residents and local authorities generally object to travelers pitching tents and building fires in the middle of town streets. Campsites must be established out in the open wilderness.",al={"can i camp in town","camping in brill","why camp in wilderness"}},{q="Who teaches how to make the First Aid Kit?",a="Nurse Neela in Brill teaches eligible adventurers how to craft the kit once they demonstrate adequate proficiency in the First Aid profession.",al={"who teaches camping first aid","camping first aid trainer","first aid kit trainer"}},{q="What is the goal of Camping 101: First Aid?",a="Camping 101: First Aid is an introductory task directing aspiring medics to Nurse Neela in Brill. It teaches adventurers how to apply their field medicine skills toward constructing medical supplies for outdoor camps.",al={"what is camping 101 first aid","camping 101 quest"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking instructors teach the art of lighting a basic campfire, which is required to establish any wilderness campsite before adding medical amenities.",["faction:Horde"]="Nurse Neela operates out of the Forsaken settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, making this instruction a key local resource for Horde adventurers.",["race:Undead"]="Based in Brill, Nurse Neela aids the Forsaken and their allies in adapting medicinal skills for survival in the ruined landscapes of Lordaeron."},kw={"brill","campfire","camping","camping 101","first aid","first aid kit","nurse neela","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill"},m={id=97955},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: First Aid"},fv=true}
E["quest:97956"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="In Brill, someone hopes your fishing skill will bring cheer to camp. Clyde Kellen can teach you to make a fishbowl for it.",h="In Brill, someone hopes your fishing skill will bring cheer to camp.",sec={{t="A Camp Keepsake",b="Your fishing may brighten the camp. Once you've proved your skill, speak with Clyde Kellen in Brill. He can teach you to make a fishbowl for the camp.\n\nThe giver believes cheerful adventurers fight more strongly. They hope the fishbowl will bring joy to you and the others.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What am I being asked to do?",a="Prove your fishing skill, then speak with Clyde Kellen in Brill. He can teach you to make a fishbowl for your camp.",al={"what do i do","fishing camp task"}},{q="Who is Clyde Kellen?",a="Clyde Kellen is in Brill and can teach you to make a fishbowl for your camp.",al={"who's clyde","clyde kellen"}},{q="What is the fishbowl for?",a="The fishbowl is meant to bring joy to you and the other adventurers at camp.",al={"fishbowl purpose","camp fish glass"}},{q="Why would fishing help the camp?",a="Your fishing skill can help you make a fishbowl. The giver hopes it will cheer the adventurers.",al={"fishing useful how","why fishing camp"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="As an angler, you can use your fishing skill to make a fishbowl for your camp."},kw={"brill","camp","camping basics","clyde kellen","fishbowl","fishing"},rel={"subzone:brill"},m={id=97956},src={"In-game quest text: Grundlagen des Zeltens: Angeln"},fv=true}
E["quest:97959"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Mining",z="general",s="You can now make a Lodestone and prepare to camp in the wilderness.",h="You can now make a Lodestone and prepare to camp in the wilderness.",sec={{t="A Place to Camp",b="You can now craft your own Lodestone. Try it in the wilderness, where folk are less likely to mind a camp. Before you start, you will also need a campfire. A cook can teach you how to make one.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What can I make now?",a="From now on you can craft a Lodestone yourself.",al={"lodestone recipe","make lodestone"}},{q="Where should I try the Lodestone?",a="Head into the wilderness to try it; most folk do not want camps in the center of town.",al={"where to camp","test lodestone"}},{q="What do I need before starting a camp?",a="You need a campfire before starting your camp.",al={"campfire needed","camp setup"}},{q="Who can teach me to make a campfire?",a="A cook can teach you to make a campfire.",al={"learn campfire","who teaches fire"}}},kw={"campfire","camping","cook","lodestone","mining","wilderness"},m={id=97959},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Mining"},fv=true}
E["quest:97965"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: First Aid",z="general",s="Camping 101: First Aid directs travelers to Shen'dar Village to learn how to fashion essential medical supplies for a wilderness camp. By seeking out Naleeia Tattermend, skilled practitioners can create camp kits capable of treating injuries and saving lives far from civilization.",h="Camping 101: First Aid directs travelers to Shen'dar Village to learn how to fashion essential medical...",sec={{t="Healing in the Wilderness",b="Life on the road across Azeroth is fraught with violence, wild beasts, and environmental hazards. For any traveler, learning First Aid is as practical as it is honorable, ensuring that bloodied cuts and broken bones can be bound without relying purely on spellcraft. Establishing a dedicated First Aid Kit at a wilderness camp provides a reliable center for healing, allowing exhausted adventurers to recover and tend to their companions before moving on.",sp=0},{t="The Mentor of Shen'dar Village",b="Not every field medic knows how to properly assemble durable camp medical supplies. In Shen'dar Village, a specialist named Naleeia Tattermend instructs dedicated practitioners in the art of fashioning campsite First Aid Kits. Those seeking her guidance must first demonstrate their own proficiency in basic medical care before she reveals her techniques.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who do I need to speak with for this quest?",a="You must seek out Naleeia Tattermend in Shen'dar Village. She possesses the knowledge required to craft specialized medical gear for wilderness camps.",al={"who to talk to camping first aid","where is naleeia tattermend","camping 101 first aid turn in"}},{q="Where is Shen'dar Village located?",a="Shen'dar Village is a village on Zephras Isle, where the medic Naleeia Tattermend resides. Practitioners seeking advanced wilderness first aid training travel there once they have honed their skills.",al={"where is shendar village","shen'dar village location","shendar village first aid"}},{q="What is the purpose of crafting a First Aid Kit for camp?",a="The training allows you to craft a First Aid Kit tailored for an adventurer's campsite. In the wilds, having medical supplies readily available at camp provides vital healing between journeys and can mean the difference between life and death.",al={"what does camping first aid do","first aid kit camp","why craft first aid kit"}},{q="When will Naleeia Tattermend teach me to craft the kit?",a="Naleeia Tattermend requires that you demonstrate sufficient proficiency in First Aid before she agrees to share her crafting methods. You must practice the basic arts of bandaging and treatment before assembling field kits.",al={"when can i learn the first aid kit","naleeia tattermend requirement","camping 101 first aid requirement"}},{q="Why is First Aid considered both honorable and pragmatic?",a="Wounds and physical trauma are an inevitable hazard of adventuring across Azeroth. Bandages and medical treatments allow travelers of every discipline to tend to their own wounds and stabilize companions without solely relying on magical aid.",al={"why is first aid important","first aid lore","is first aid useful"}},{q="Can any adventurer craft a camping First Aid Kit?",a="Any adventurer who commits to the study of First Aid and proves their competence can learn to fashion a First Aid Kit for their camp from Naleeia Tattermend in Shen'dar Village.",al={"can anyone learn this kit","who can make first aid kit camp"}}},kw={"bandages","camping","camping 101: first aid","first aid","first aid kit","naleeia tattermend","shen'dar village","wilderness survival"},m={id=97965},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: First Aid"},fv=true}
E["quest:97967"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Fishing",z="general",s="Camping 101: Fishing directs aspiring anglers to discover how their patience with a rod and line can elevate wilderness expeditions. By seeking out Fenn Fairweather in Shen'dar Village, proficient fishermen can learn to craft a decorative Fish Bowl fixture to bolster morale and camp comfort.",h="Camping 101: Fishing directs aspiring anglers to discover how their patience with a rod and line can elevate...",sec={{t="Comforts of the Hearth",b="Life in the wilderness demands more than sharp blades and sturdy armor; keeping morale high in the field can mean the difference between triumph and exhaustion. While fishing is often treated purely as a quiet pastime or a means to harvest provisions, skilled anglers can turn their craft toward improving campsite comfort. By capturing live fish and preparing dedicated fixtures, a traveler can transform a bleak encampment into a welcoming sanctuary.",sp=0},{t="The Artisan of Shen'dar",b="In Shen'dar Village, the artisan Fenn Fairweather shares trade secrets with seasoned fishermen who understand the patience of the rod and reel. Those who demonstrate sufficient skill can learn to fashion a Fish Bowl—a simple camp fixture designed to bring cheer and relaxation to weary companions. Maintaining high spirits out in the wilds helps keep party members focused and resolute when facing looming dangers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches the Fish Bowl camping craft?",a="Fenn Fairweather resides in Shen'dar Village. Once you have demonstrated adequate proficiency in your fishing trade, Fenn can teach you how to craft a Fish Bowl to furnish and brighten your campsite.",al={"who gives fish bowl","where is fenn fairweather","fish bowl trainer"}},{q="What purpose does a Fish Bowl serve at camp?",a="A Fish Bowl serves as a decorative fixture for your camp that brings a touch of comfort and cheer to you and your companions. A well-maintained camp and high spirits keep adventurers sharp and bolster their readiness for dangerous trials in the field.",al={"what does fish bowl do","fish bowl buff","camping fish bowl effect"}},{q="Where is Shen'dar Village?",a="Shen'dar Village is a settlement that serves as a gathering place for tradesfolk and instructors, including Fenn Fairweather. Practitioners of fishing travel there to learn practical camping crafts suited to their skills.",al={"where is shendar village","shen'dar village location"}},{q="What skill is required to learn this camping craft?",a="You must first demonstrate solid basic proficiency as an angler before you can take up the craft. Once you have honed your fishing skill, instructors in Shen'dar Village will share the methods for assembling camp fixtures like the Fish Bowl.",al={"camping fishing requirements","when can i learn fish bowl","how to start camping fishing"}},{q="Why does fishing matter for wilderness camping?",a="Beyond simply hauling in catches for sustenance, an angler's keen eye and patience can contribute to camp life in unexpected ways. Displaying a fresh catch in a crafted bowl offers tranquil familiarity and uplifts morale during long wilderness expeditions.",al={"why fishing for camping","fishing usefulness in camp"}},{q="Who is Fenn Fairweather?",a="Fenn Fairweather is an artisan and instructor located in Shen'dar Village. Fenn recognizes the practical comforts of wilderness life and shares specialized knowledge of camp fixtures with skilled fishermen.",al={"who is fenn fairweather","fenn fairweather lore"}}},kw={"camping","camping 101: fishing","fenn fairweather","fish bowl","fishing","shen'dar village"},m={id=97967},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Fishing"},fv=true}
E["quest:97968"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="general",s="At camps along the trail, Camping 101: Herbalism points gatherers toward instructors who teach them to make Incense Candles. Herbalists use gathered plants to bring comfort to spellcasters and healers on long journeys.",h="At camps along the trail, Camping 101: Herbalism points gatherers toward instructors who teach them to make...",sec={{t="Wild Flora on the Trail",b="Travelers can put roots and petals to use beyond selling fresh stems or brewing draughts. Gatherers meet instructors who teach them to make Incense Candles from wild aromatic plants. These candles support companions on the trail.",sp=0},{t="Soothing the Mind",b="Long journeys through hostile country wear down healers and spellcasters. At an active campsite, an Incense Candle gives off a soothing, revitalizing scent that lifts their spirits and helps them recover clarity and strength before danger calls again. Those who wield magic or tend the wounded value that comfort most.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Herbalism?",a="Camping 101: Herbalism connects skilled herb gatherers with instructors who teach them to make Incense Candles for wilderness camps.",al={"what is camping 101 herbalism","camping 101 herbalism quest","herbalism camping quest"}},{q="What is an Incense Candle used for?",a="An Incense Candle is made from gathered plants and burned at a campsite. Its soothing, revitalizing scent lifts resting companions’ spirits and gives healers and spellcasters a beneficial boost.",al={"what does incense candle do","incense candle camp effect","why make incense candle"}},{q="Who teaches how to make an Incense Candle?",a="Your instructor depends on where you begin: Reyna Stonebranch in Ironforge, Malorne Bladeleaf in Dolanaar, Herbalist Pomeroy in Elwynn Forest, Jandi in Orgrimmar, Faruza in Brill, Komin Winterhoof in Thunder Bluff, or Syriel Nightrain in Valanaar.",al={"who teaches incense candle","incense candle trainer","where to learn incense candle"}},{q="Why are Incense Candles especially useful for magic users?",a="Casting spells and tending wounds drain physical energy and mental focus. The soothing smoke of an Incense Candle helps replenish both, keeping spirits high and casters ready for battle.",al={"why do spellcasters need incense","incense candle healers","incense candle magic users"}},{q="Do you need to be an herbalist to take this quest?",a="This instruction is for those skilled in gathering plants and herbs. It teaches how to find and use aromatic wild plants.",al={"can anyone learn incense candle","do i need herbalism for camping 101 herbalism","herbalism requirement camping candle"}},{q="How does Herbalism tie into camping?",a="Gathered plants have uses around camp beyond brewing concoctions and salves. They can be made into incense that refreshes weary companions.",al={"is herbalism just for potions","uses for herbs camping","herbalism camp items"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Gathering plants in the wilderness provides the aromatic herbs needed for camp incense. That makes the work useful for survival as well as alchemy.",["class:Priest"]="Incense Candles lit at camp lift the spirits and replenish the mental reserves of holy healers between skirmishes.",["class:Mage"]="Spellcasters who spend their focus in battle value camp incense, whose soothing scent helps restore their minds and support arcane recovery."},kw={"camp","camping","camping 101: herbalism","healers","herbalism","herbs","incense candle","spellcasters"},m={id=97968},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:97969"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Leatherworking",z="general",s="Camping 101: Leatherworking directs fledgling leatherworkers to seek advanced instruction on crafting durable wilderness shelters. Guided to Indari Sunseam in Shen'dar Village, craftsmen learn to stitch protective tents that offer essential rest across long expeditions into the wilds.",h="Camping 101: Leatherworking directs fledgling leatherworkers to seek advanced instruction on crafting durable...",sec={{t="Shelter on the Road",b="For traveling leatherworkers, the ability to work hide and pelt into durable wilderness gear is vital. Upon learning the craft, tradesfolk are directed to seek out Indari Sunseam in Shen'dar Village to learn how to fashion a proper camping tent. Such shelters keep adventurers dry during relentless downpours and cool beneath blazing suns, ensuring they stay well-rested throughout long journeys.",sp=0},{t="Wilderness Campsites",b="Pitching a tent requires respect for civil boundaries, as townsfolk take issue with crude campsites cluttering municipal streets. Camps must be pitched out in the wild. Furthermore, a sturdy shelter is only half the equation: no campsite is complete without a warming campfire, prompting adventurers to visit a local cook to pick up basic firemaking skills before striking out into the wilds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who teaches leatherworkers how to craft a tent?",a="Indari Sunseam resides in Shen'dar Village and instructs proficient leatherworkers on how to craft their own camping tents.",al={"who teaches leatherworking tents","camp tent trainer","where is indari sunseam"}},{q="What is the purpose of crafting a tent?",a="A well-made tent protects travelers from harsh elements like rain and blistering heat, allowing adventurers to get the rest they need to remain alert on the road.",al={"what does a tent do","tent purpose","tent benefits"}},{q="Where can a camping tent be pitched?",a="Tents cannot be pitched in town centers where locals object to them. Travelers must journey out into the open wilderness before setting up camp.",al={"can i pitch tent in city","where to use tent","camp rules"}},{q="What else is needed to pitch a proper camp?",a="Every complete campsite requires a roaring campfire alongside the shelter. Adventurers are encouraged to seek out a cooking trainer to learn how to build a basic fire.",al={"do i need a fire","camp requirements","what else for camp"}},{q="When does Indari Sunseam teach you the tent pattern?",a="You must practice leatherworking and demonstrate adequate proficiency in your trade before Indari Sunseam will instruct you in crafting a camping item.",al={"when can i learn tent","tent requirements","leatherworking tent req"}},{q="What is Shen'dar Village?",a="Shen'dar Village is the settlement where Indari Sunseam lives and works, offering trade instruction in crafting camping supplies to travelers.",al={"where is shen'dar village","shendar village location","indari sunseam town"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="A tent alone does not make a functional camp; travelers must also know basic campfire craft, typically learned from cooking trainers."},kw={"camp tent","campfire","camping","cooking","indari sunseam","leatherworking","shen'dar village","tent","wilderness"},m={id=97969},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Leatherworking"},fv=true}
E["quest:97971"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="general",s="Camping 101: Skinning instructs adventurers in turning harvested pelts into a functional Camp Chair for field camps. Guided by Mendalass Tattermend in Shen'dar Village, skinners learn to combine their harvesting craft with wilderness survival and campfire relaxation.",h="Camping 101: Skinning instructs adventurers in turning harvested pelts into a functional Camp Chair for field...",sec={{t="Comfort in the Wilds",b="Harvesting hides from fallen beasts is an essential survival skill across Azeroth, but its utility extends far beyond simple armor crafting. Through Camping 101: Skinning, gatherers learn to direct their trade toward wilderness survival by crafting a portable Camp Chair. Taking time to rest upon a comfortable seat out in the wilds allows weary adventurers to regain their strength and remain in peak fighting condition before their next battle.",sp=0},{t="The Teachings of Shen'dar Village",b="To master this pattern, adventurers are sent to Shen'dar Village to meet with Mendalass Tattermend once they have proven their proficiency with a skinning knife. Mendalass instructs travelers on assembling durable seating from collected pelts. However, establishing a campsite requires more than just furniture: settlements will not tolerate impromptu camps within town limits, and no site is truly finished without a crackling campfire, a skill traditionally picked up from local culinary instructors.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Skinning about?",a="Camping 101: Skinning directs skilled skinners to Mendalass Tattermend in Shen'dar Village. By completing this training, adventurers learn how to repurpose harvested hides into a practical Camp Chair for field rest and recuperation.",al={"what is this quest","camping 101 purpose","why do this quest"}},{q="Who is Mendalass Tattermend?",a="Mendalass Tattermend is a skinning trainer located in Shen'dar Village. He teaches proficient skinners how to stitch harvested hides into comfortable field seating for wilderness camps.",al={"who is mendalass","who teaches camp chair","mendalass tattermend"}},{q="Where can Mendalass Tattermend be found?",a="Mendalass Tattermend resides in Shen'dar Village, where adventurers must travel to demonstrate their skill and learn the art of crafting camp gear.",al={"where is mendalass","where is shendar village","where do i turn this in"}},{q="What item does this quest teach you to make?",a="Skinners learn the pattern to fashion a Camp Chair using the hides and pelts gathered from fallen beasts, providing a comfortable seat to rest upon while out in the wild.",al={"what do skinners make","camp chair recipe","what item do i get"}},{q="Can you set up camp inside a town?",a="Townsfolk frown upon setting up camp in the middle of settlements. Camps and camp chairs are meant to be pitched out in the wilderness away from city centers.",al={"can i camp in town","where can i pitch camp","where to use camp chair"}},{q="What else is required to make a proper camp?",a="Every proper campsite requires a crackling campfire before it is complete. Adventurers who have not yet mastered starting fires are advised to consult a cooking trainer to learn the basics.",al={"what else do i need for camp","campfire needed","camp requirements"}}},ang={["profession:Cooking"]="Cooking and wilderness survival go hand in hand; as Mendalass notes, setting up a proper campsite requires a basic campfire, a skill traditionally taught by culinary trainers."},kw={"camp chair","campfire","camping 101","hides","mendalass tattermend","shen'dar village","skinning","wilderness"},m={id=97971},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:97973"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Tailoring",z="general",s="At Shen'dar Village, tailors can learn to make a Faction Banner for wilderness camps. The banner lifts spirits and helps sustain morale.",h="At Shen'dar Village, tailors can learn to make a Faction Banner for wilderness camps.",sec={{t="The Weaver's Contribution to Camp",b="A campsite in the wild calls for more than warm clothes. A well-made banner can give a camp a shared purpose, so clothworkers are urged to carry their craft beyond garments and bags. With further instruction, bolts of woven fabric become a sign of unity and fortitude.",sp=0},{t="Instruction in Shen'dar Village",b="In Shen'dar Village, Taleen Shimmerthread teaches camping crafts to tailors with enough skill. She shows them how to fashion a Faction Banner. Stitched with care and planted at camp, it rallies allies and helps keep their spirits up through the trials ahead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camping 101: Tailoring?",a="At Shen'dar Village, promising tailors can seek instruction in field craft. Show enough skill in tailoring and you can learn to make a Faction Banner for a wilderness campsite.",al={"what is this quest","camping 101 tailoring quest","tailoring camping banner"}},{q="Who is Taleen Shimmerthread?",a="Taleen Shimmerthread is a skilled tailor in Shen'dar Village. She teaches qualified crafters to make camping items, including banners that lift spirits at camp.",al={"taleen shimmerthread","who teaches tailoring banner","where is taleen shimmerthread"}},{q="Where is Shen'dar Village?",a="Shen'dar Village is where Taleen Shimmerthread lives. Tailors go there to learn how to use their cloth-working craft to make camping gear.",al={"where is shendar village","shendar village location"}},{q="What camping item can tailors learn to craft?",a="Tailors can learn to make a Faction Banner for camp. Planting one gives those resting there an inspiring presence.",al={"faction banner camping","what does tailoring make for camp","tailoring camp item"}},{q="What are the requirements to learn the camping banner?",a="You need to show skill in tailoring before Taleen Shimmerthread in Shen'dar Village will teach you to make a Faction Banner for camp.",al={"how to learn faction banner","tailoring requirements camping banner"}},{q="Why are faction banners valuable at camp?",a="Faction banners lift spirits and sustain morale on a campaign. Tailors stitch them as an energizing focal point for adventurers on grueling journeys.",al={"why make a faction banner","camping banner morale"}}},ang={["profession:Tailoring"]="As a tailor, you can make Faction Banners that lift camp morale and energize allies in the field."},kw={"camp banner","camping","camping 101: tailoring","faction banner","shen'dar village","tailoring","taleen shimmerthread","tradeskill"},m={id=97973},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Tailoring"},fv=true}
E["quest:97977"] = {t="quest",n="Nature's Call",z="general",s="Nature's Call is a Shadowglen quest where the dryad Tarindrella seeks to uncover why the local Gnarlpine furbolgs suddenly turned against their long-standing night elf allies. By gathering carved totems from their abandoned camps, adventurers help reveal the distressing spiritual corruption spreading through Teldrassil.",h="Nature's Call is a Shadowglen quest where the dryad Tarindrella seeks to uncover why the local Gnarlpine...",sec={{t="Investigating the Glen",b="Nature's Call is an investigative mission in Shadowglen given by the dryad Tarindrella. Refusing to believe that the Gnarlpine furbolg would abandon centuries of friendship with the night elves without reason, she asks travelers to comb through the furbolgs' hastily abandoned camps along the western border of the glen to recover intact engraved totems.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow and the Nightmare",b="Deciphering the recovered totems reveals a tragic descent into madness. The Gnarlpine were plagued by recurring nightmares of an encroaching shadow destined to wipe them out. Over time, sheer terror curdled into blind rage, warping their minds until they viewed their longtime elven neighbors as the very shadow they feared. Tarindrella hopes these findings will shed light on the deeper malaise creeping through Teldrassil.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Nature's Call?",a="Tarindrella is a dryad found watching over Shadowglen in Teldrassil. Concerned by the sudden hostility of the local Gnarlpine furbolg, she directs adventurers to recover relics from their abandoned camps to investigate the source of their madness.",al={"who gives nature's call","who is tarindrella","tarindrella location"}},{q="Why did the Gnarlpine furbolgs turn hostile?",a="Engravings recovered from Gnarlpine totems reveal that the furbolg began suffering terrible nightmares of a looming shadow threatening to destroy them. That creeping dread warped into uncontrollable paranoia and fury, leading them to see their ancient night elf allies as enemies.",al={"why did gnarlpine attack","why are gnarlpine mad","what happened to gnarlpine furbolgs"}},{q="Where are the Gnarlpine totems located?",a="The smaller totems can be found within the abandoned Gnarlpine furbolg camps situated along the western edge of the glen, though grell and grellkin scavenging through the ruins have scattered some across the surrounding area.",al={"where are the gnarlpine totems","find gnarlpine totems","gnarlpine camp location"}},{q="Why can't players collect the larger totems in the camps?",a="Tarindrella specifically advises against attempting to retrieve the larger totems found in the camps. As a dryad, she notes that she would have no idea what to do with such cumbersome carvings, preferring smaller, transportable engraved totems instead.",al={"why large totems ignored","can i loot big totems","big gnarlpine totem"}},{q="Who are the Gnarlpine furbolg?",a="The Gnarlpine are a tribe of furbolg native to Teldrassil who lived alongside the night elves as peaceful allies. Their recent transformation into aggressive foes is an unnatural development caused by creeping nightmares rather than ancient malice.",al={"who are the gnarlpine","gnarlpine lore","are gnarlpine evil"}},{q="What is the great shadow mentioned in the totems?",a="The engravings speak of an unseen Great Shadow haunting the furbolg's dreams. While the exact origin of this dark influence remains unknown, Tarindrella hopes deciphering their fate will help unravel the broader corruption afflicting Teldrassil.",al={"what shadow gnarlpine","what is the shadow in teldrassil","nightmares in teldrassil"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Gnarlpine were long-standing companions to the night elves, making their descent into hostility and madness a deeply personal tragedy for the defenders of Teldrassil.",["class:Druid"]="Druids are attuned to the natural balance and spiritual corruption affecting the creatures of Kalimdor, making the nightmare-fueled corruption of furbolg totems an alarming development.",["faction:Alliance"]="Securing the boughs of Teldrassil is essential for protecting the fledgling night elven realm and ensuring their newfound Alliance allies remain anchored on Kalimdor."},kw={"furbolg","gnarlpine","grell","grellkin","nature's call","nightmares","shadowglen","tarindrella","teldrassil","totem"},rel={"npc:tarindrella","topic:furbolg","quest:940"},m={id=97977},src={"In-game quest text: Nature's Call"},fv=true}
E["quest:97979"] = {t="quest",n="The Goddess Provides",z="general",s="The Goddess Provides is an introductory kaldorei quest centered on recognizing Elune's divine gifts. Set in the boughs of Teldrassil following the end of the Long Vigil, it teaches young night elves to embrace the lunar guidance and cloaking shadows bestowed by Mother Moon.",h="The Goddess Provides is an introductory kaldorei quest centered on recognizing Elune's divine gifts.",sec={{t="A Vigil Concluded",b="For millennia following the Sundering, the kaldorei maintained their solitary Long Vigil, watching over Kalimdor and guarding the sacred peaks of Mount Hyjal. The devastation of the Third War and the climactic defense of the World Tree forced an end to that ancient seclusion. Now relocated to the sheltering boughs of Teldrassil, the night elves face an unfamiliar world, stepping onto the broader stage of Azeroth by aligning themselves with distant allies across the ocean.",sp=0},{t="Blessings of Mother Moon",b="Even amidst shifting allegiances and new dwellings, the foundation of kaldorei identity remains rooted in the worship of Elune, the Mother Moon. Night elves are taught from their earliest days to call upon her dual aspects: radiance to guide martial strikes and clarify purpose, and shadow to cloak one's presence from enemy sight. Calling upon these hereditary gifts serves as both spiritual communion and practical preparation for the perils beyond the Great Tree.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Goddess Provides?",a="The Goddess Provides is an introductory rite for young night elves nestled within the boughs of Teldrassil. It instructs kaldorei in recognizing and demonstrating the innate blessings granted to them by the moon goddess Elune.",al={"what is the goddess provides","the goddess provides quest"}},{q="Who is the goddess referenced in this quest?",a="The goddess is Elune, also revered by the kaldorei as Mother Moon. She is the central deity of night elf culture and spiritual life, believed to bestow lunar blessings of protection, clarity, and stealth upon her children.",al={"who is the goddess","who is mother moon","night elf goddess"}},{q="What gifts does Elune provide to the kaldorei?",a="Elune grants grace that can illuminate a warrior's strikes to help them hit true, as well as the ability to melt into the surrounding shadows just as the moon conceals the sun during an eclipse.",al={"what gifts does elune give","elune racial abilities","elune powers"}},{q="What was the Long Vigil?",a="The Long Vigil was the thousands of years of guarded isolation maintained by the night elves to watch over Kalimdor. It concluded after the climactic defense of Mount Hyjal against the Burning Legion, leading the kaldorei to finally open their borders.",al={"what was the long vigil","why did the long vigil end"}},{q="Why have the night elves joined the Alliance?",a="After the defense of Mount Hyjal brought an end to their solitary isolation, the kaldorei chose to forge ties with mortal races across the sea, entering into the Grand Alliance for mutual survival and camaraderie.",al={"why did night elves join alliance","kaldorei alliance"}},{q="Where does this instruction take place?",a="Teldrassil is the massive Great Tree situated off the northern coast of Kalimdor. Its high, sheltered boughs serve as the new home and sanctuary for the night elven people following the Third War.",al={"where is teldrassil","what is teldrassil"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As children of the stars, kaldorei inherit the direct blessings of Elune, utilizing moonlight to reveal unseen paths and concealment to blend into shadows.",["faction:Alliance"]="Following the battle at Mount Hyjal, the night elves ended their long isolation and joined the Alliance, offering their sacred lunar powers in service of mutual defense.",["class:Priest"]="Priesthood among the kaldorei is devoted to Mother Moon, channeling Elune's grace directly as guiding radiance and divine protection."},kw={"elune","goddess provides","hyjal","kaldorei","long vigil","mother moon","shadowmeld","teldrassil"},rel={"topic:elune","quest:940","zone:hyjal","topic:alliance","quest:5672"},m={id=97979},src={"In-game quest text: The Goddess Provides"},fv=true}
E["quest:97985"] = {t="quest",n="Thundering Hearts",z="general",s="In Thousand Needles, the researcher seeks Stormheart’s massive heart for work on an abomination.",h="In Thousand Needles, the researcher seeks Stormheart’s massive heart for work on an abomination.",sec={{t="A Large Heart",b="A researcher studying abomination creation believes the work calls for a massive heart. Stormheart, a thunder lizard said to patrol the canyon edge in Thousand Needles, seems large enough. But Stormheart is not docile.",sp=0},{t="The Heart",b="Stormheart’s heart still carries electrical pulses. The researcher says it appears to match the size specified in the notes and will be added to the abomination.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who wants Stormheart’s heart?",a="A researcher studying abomination creation wants Stormheart’s heart for an abomination.",al={"who needs the heart","abomination researcher","who sent me"}},{q="Where is Stormheart?",a="Stormheart patrols along the canyon edge in Thousand Needles.",al={"stormheart location","where's the thunder lizard","stormheart where"}},{q="What is Stormheart?",a="Stormheart is a thunder lizard of impressive size.",al={"stormheart creature","big lizard","thunder lizard"}},{q="Why is a heart needed?",a="The researcher’s notes say abomination creation requires a massive heart.",al={"why heart","abomination heart","what's the heart for"}},{q="What does the researcher know about the heart?",a="The researcher says the heart still carries electrical pulses and appears to match the size specified in the notes.",al={"heart details","electrical heart","what's special about it"}}},kw={"abomination","creation","heart","stormheart","thousand needles","thunder lizard","thundering hearts"},rel={"zone:thousandneedles"},m={id=97985},src={"In-game quest text: Thundering Hearts"},fv=true}
E["quest:98013"] = {t="quest",n="Swelling Forces",z="general",s="At the Grove of the Ancients, Arbal sends defenders against the Stormscale naga in the northeast ruins. These cursed former Highborne threaten the surrounding woodlands as their numbers grow.",h="At the Grove of the Ancients, Arbal sends defenders against the Stormscale naga in the northeast ruins.",sec={{t="Serpents in the Sunken City",b="Arbal watches the northeast ruins from the Grove of the Ancients with grave concern. Cursed Highborne have become the serpent-like naga of the Stormscale tribe. They now occupy the remains of their old civilization, and their growing army could spread beyond the ruins into the wider forest. Arbal sends defenders there to cut down their numbers before that happens.",sp=0},{t="An Unholy Alliance",b="Arbal warns that the Stormscale naga aid the Twilight's Hammer cult. These former Highborne now desecrate and pillage the forests that once sheltered them, serving sinister masters. To Arbal, this is unforgivable. Their numbers must be curbed before the cult and its serpentine allies tighten their hold on the land.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Swelling Forces?",a="Arbal gives the order from the Grove of the Ancients, directing defenders against the hostile naga gathering in the northeast ruins.",al={"who gives swelling forces","arbal quest giver","where is arbal"}},{q="Why are the naga being targeted in this quest?",a="The Stormscale naga have occupied the nearby ruins and threaten the surrounding woodlands. They also aid the Twilight's Hammer cult, bringing violence to lands they once called home.",al={"why kill naga","naga in the ruins","stormscale threat"}},{q="What is the connection between the naga and the ruins?",a="The naga were once Highborne elves of ancient Kalimdor, changed by a terrible curse. They once lived in these cities and have returned as serpents seeking conquest and devastation.",al={"who are the stormscale naga","naga origins","cursed highborne"}},{q="What is the naga's connection to the Twilight's Hammer?",a="The Stormscale naga are assisting the Twilight's Hammer cult. Arbal warns that their alliance threatens further ruin for the ancient forests.",al={"twilights hammer naga","why naga working with cult"}},{q="Where do the Stormscale forces gather?",a="The Stormscale forces gather in ruins northeast of the Grove of the Ancients. Defenders are sent to curb their growing numbers there.",al={"where are the ruins","where to find stormscale"}},{q="Why does Arbal consider the naga's actions unforgivable?",a="Arbal considers it unforgivable that the naga pillage their ancient homeland at a dark master's command. Their former Highborne nobility has given way to cruelty and dark alliances.",al={"why does arbal hate naga","unforgivable arbal"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The naga in these ruins were once Highborne kindred of the night elves. Their return to desecrate and pillage the ancient forests alongside dark cultists is a bitter betrayal.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance forces defending the sacred forests around Darkshore and the Grove of the Ancients face a coordinated front of naga and Twilight's Hammer."},kw={"arbal","grove of the ancients","highborne","myrmidon","naga","sorceress","stormscale","swelling forces","twilight's hammer"},rel={"quest:952","topic:naga","topic:highborne","topic:twilight-s-hammer"},m={id=98013},src={"In-game quest text: Swelling Forces"},fv=true}
E["quest:98021"] = {t="quest",n="Journey to Sentinel Hill",z="general",s="At Sentinel Hill, Journey to Sentinel Hill brings Alliance travelers into Westfall’s troubled farmlands. The local commanders have pleaded with Stormwind for months without answer, and now put any willing traveler to work.",h="At Sentinel Hill, Journey to Sentinel Hill brings Alliance travelers into Westfall’s troubled farmlands.",sec={{t="Arrival on the Frontier",b="In Westfall, you arrive at Sentinel Hill, the outpost guarding the surrounding farmlands. Its garrison is short of soldiers and overlooked by the royal court. When travelers offer help, the local commander asks little about their standing. Any willing hands are welcome.",sp=0},{t="Pleas to Stormwind",b="For months, Sentinel Hill’s leaders have asked Stormwind for soldiers to defend Westfall from threats drawing near. With no reinforcements, local commanders accept whoever arrives. One commander expected an army and got a skinny elf instead. In Westfall, beggars cannot be choosers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Journey to Sentinel Hill?",a="Journey to Sentinel Hill brings Alliance travelers into Westfall, where defenders at Sentinel Hill have asked Stormwind for help without receiving enough soldiers.",al={"what is journey to sentinel hill","journey to sentinel hill quest","sentinel hill quest"}},{q="Why is Sentinel Hill asking for help?",a="Sentinel Hill’s leaders have asked Stormwind for soldiers for months to meet the troubles in Westfall. With no reinforcements, they rely on whichever travelers arrive.",al={"who is begging stormwind for aid","sentinel hill garrison","why sentinel hill needs help"}},{q="Why does the quest text mention a skinny elf?",a="The commander expected help from Stormwind after months of pleas, but got a skinny elf instead. Beggars cannot be choosers, so the commander puts the newcomer to work.",al={"skinny elf quote","why skinny elf","sentinel hill elf comment"}},{q="Why hasn't Stormwind sent proper reinforcements?",a="Sentinel Hill has appealed to Stormwind for months, but royal support has not arrived. The local commanders depend on volunteers.",al={"why won't stormwind help westfall","stormwind neglecting westfall","stormwind westfall aid"}},{q="What is Sentinel Hill?",a="Sentinel Hill is a military outpost guarding Westfall’s farmlands. It is the main defense against the region’s lawlessness, Defias bandits, and gnolls.",al={"where is sentinel hill","what is sentinel hill","sentinel hill location"}},{q="What happens after reaching Sentinel Hill?",a="When you report to Sentinel Hill, the local defenders put you to work at once. You help face the many threats across Westfall’s plains and farmlands.",al={"what to do in westfall","westfall next quests","what happens after arriving in westfall"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Westfall needs help, but Stormwind has left its commanders waiting for soldiers. They rely on whatever volunteers arrive.",["race:NightElf"]="At Sentinel Hill, the frontier commander may grumble that Stormwind sent a skinny elf instead of seasoned infantry. The outpost needs all the help it can get."},kw={"journey to sentinel hill","peoples militia","sentinel hill","skinny elf","stormwind","westfall"},rel={"zone:westfall","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=98021},src={"In-game quest text: Journey to Sentinel Hill"},fv=true}
E["quest:98024"] = {t="quest",n="Journey to the Crossroads",z="general",s="A newly arrived traveler from the elemental plane is sent to the Crossroads in the Barrens. There, Thork calls for aid as the Horde faces the dangers of Kalimdor's frontier.",h="A newly arrived traveler from the elemental plane is sent to the Crossroads in the Barrens.",sec={{t="A Call to the Frontier",b="You have come to Azeroth from the elemental plane. Mortal conflicts may seem small beside what you have known, but the Horde faces dangers of its own. Service on the frontier gives you a chance to grow formidable while aiding the realm.",sp=0},{t="The Crossroads of Kalimdor",b="The Barrens lie southwest, and the Crossroads stands among its broad, sun-baked plains. This Horde outpost is a vital junction, though dangers press its defenders from every side. Report to Thork and join the settlement's defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Thork?",a="Thork is a Horde authority stationed at the Crossroads in the Barrens. He oversees the local defense and directs newcomers to help against threats to the settlement and nearby lands.",al={"who is thork","crossroads leader","where is thork"}},{q="What is the Crossroads?",a="The Crossroads is a Horde outpost and trading hub where major trails meet in the Barrens. Hostile wildlife, centaur tribes, and quilboar raiders threaten the settlement, so its defenders need help keeping supply lines secure.",al={"what is the crossroads","crossroads barrens","why go to crossroads"}},{q="Where are the Barrens located?",a="The Barrens is a vast, arid savannah in central Kalimdor, southwest of Durotar. It connects Horde territories across the continent.",al={"where are the barrens","barrens location"}},{q="Why does the quest mention the elemental plane?",a="The traveler has recently arrived in Azeroth from the elemental plane. Conflicts there may seem vast, but dangers to the mortal realm and the Horde are immediate and severe.",al={"elemental plane arrival","why elemental plane","journey from elemental plane"}},{q="What is the purpose of Journey to the Crossroads?",a="You are sent to report to Thork at the Crossroads. There, you can join the Horde's frontline efforts and grow formidable through honorable service.",al={"crossroads quest purpose","why send to crossroads"}},{q="What dangers do the citizens of the Crossroads face?",a="The Barrens faces continual aggression from native predators, roaming centaur, quilboar warbands, and Alliance encroachment. Its outposts need capable defenders to hold their ground.",al={"what dangers in barrens","barrens threats"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Your journey brings you to the Horde's rugged frontier in Kalimdor, where you are called to prove yourself in its service.",["race:Skyborne"]="You have arrived in Azeroth from the elemental plane. Mortal conflicts may seem small beside what you have known, but the Horde's dangers are real, and you are urged to prove your mettle in its service."},kw={"barrens","crossroads","elemental plane","horde","journey to the crossroads","the barrens","thork"},rel={"subzone:crossroads","npc:thork","zone:barrens","topic:horde","topic:elemental-plane"},m={id=98024},src={"In-game quest text: Journey to the Crossroads"},fv=true}
E["quest:98025"] = {t="quest",n="WANTED: Jai'vhanel",z="general",s="WANTED: Jai'vhanel is an Alliance bounty posted by the Auberdine Village Council in Darkshore. The notice calls for the elimination of Jai'vhanel, a maddened black owl known as 'Swordfeather' that has begun ambushing travelers along the road near Auberdine. Adventurers must hunt the creature near the ruins of Ameth'Aran and bring proof of her death to Sentinel Glynda Nal'Shea.",h="WANTED: Jai'vhanel is an Alliance bounty posted by the Auberdine Village Council in Darkshore.",sec={{t="A Threat on the Road",b="A posted notice from the Auberdine Village Council alerts travelers along the coastal roads of Darkshore to a dangerous menace. A prominent local owl known as Jai'vhanel, nicknamed 'Swordfeather', has succumbed to a sudden madness. Rather than keeping to the dense wilderness, she has begun ambushing travelers and couriers journeying near Auberdine, transforming an ordinary woodland predator into a critical hazard for the settlement.",sp=0},{t="The Council's Bounty",b="To secure the safety of the roadways, the council has placed an open bounty on Jai'vhanel's head. Slayers are directed to track the black-feathered raptor to her hunting grounds north of the ruins of Ameth'Aran, along the mountain base. Hunters must bring back one of her distinctive jet black feathers to Sentinel Glynda Nal'Shea in Auberdine to verify the kill and collect their reward.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Jai'vhanel?",a="Jai'vhanel, also known by the moniker 'Swordfeather', is a large owl distinguished by her jet black feathers. Normally a wild creature native to the forests of Darkshore, she has succumbed to madness and turned aggressively hostile toward travelers.",al={"who is jaivhanel","what is jaivhanel","who is swordfeather"}},{q="Why is there a bounty on Jai'vhanel?",a="The Auberdine Village Council issued a formal bounty on Jai'vhanel because she has gone mad and started attacking travelers walking along the main road near Auberdine, posing a severe danger to local transit.",al={"why kill jaivhanel","why is there a bounty on jaivhanel","why is the owl attacked"}},{q="Where can Jai'vhanel be found?",a="Jai'vhanel was last spotted north of the ancient Ameth'Aran ruins in Darkshore, prowling close to the base of the eastern mountains.",al={"where is jaivhanel","jaivhanel location","where to find swordfeather"}},{q="Who posted the bounty on Jai'vhanel?",a="The bounty was posted by the Auberdine Village Council, who oversee civil security and local welfare for the coastal settlement and surrounding roads.",al={"who posted the bounty","who wants jaivhanel dead"}},{q="Who collects the reward for Jai'vhanel?",a="Bounty hunters must present one of Jai'vhanel's jet black feathers as proof of her demise to Sentinel Glynda Nal'Shea, stationed in the town of Auberdine.",al={"who takes proof of death","where to turn in jaivhanel feather","glynda nalshea bounty"}},{q="What proof is needed to claim the bounty?",a="Anyone claiming the council's bounty must retrieve and present one of Jai'vhanel's distinct jet black feathers to prove the mad bird has been slain.",al={"what is proof of death for jaivhanel","what item proves jaivhanel died"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Auberdine Village Council relies on Alliance adventurers and night elf sentinels to keep Darkshore's vital coastal highways safe from maddened predators.",["class:Druid"]="Night elf druids closely observe the wildlife of Darkshore, making the sudden madness and aggression of a noble bird like Jai'vhanel an alarming ecological disturbance.",["class:Hunter"]="Known as Swordfeather, this fierce jet-black raptor is an aggressive stalker near the mountains of Darkshore whose hunting habits turned lethally erratic."},kw={"ameth'aran","auberdine","black owl","darkshore","glynda nal'shea","jai'vhanel","swordfeather","wanted: jai'vhanel"},rel={"subzone:auberdine","subzone:ameth-aran"},m={id=98025},src={"In-game quest text: WANTED: Jai'vhanel"},fv=true}
E["quest:98042"] = {t="quest",n="It's All Fun and Games Until...",z="general",s="Thundris hears a troubled report and examines the discovery brought before him. Its mark sends the relic to Darnassus for study.",h="Thundris hears a troubled report and examines the discovery brought before him.",sec={{t="A Troubling Report",b="You bring a troubling discovery to Thundris and seek his counsel. He listens to your report and examines what you have brought. Then he falls quiet, for the signs before him are too dark to dismiss.",sp=0},{t="The Mark of Ancient Evil",b="Thundris sees the mark of an ancient evil on the discovery. He knows the danger reaches beyond a local mishap, so he sends the relic at once to Darnassus. The capital's scholars and priests will study it and keep it contained.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is involved in completing this quest?",a="Thundris hears the adventurer's troubling report and examines the item or evidence presented. He prepares it for transport.",al={"who gives its all fun and games until","thundris quest","who turns in its all fun and games until"}},{q="Why is the object being sent to Darnassus?",a="Thundris sees the mark of an ancient evil and says the finding will be taken to Darnassus at once for study.",al={"why send to darnassus","darnassus study","ancient evil darnassus"}},{q="What is the ancient evil referenced by Thundris?",a="Thundris names the mark of an ancient evil, but gives no details about its origins before closer study in the night elven capital.",al={"what is the ancient evil","mark of ancient evil meaning"}},{q="What does 'Ande'thoras-ethil' mean?",a="“Ande'thoras-ethil” is a Darnassian phrase Thundris speaks as he finishes talking with you, after saying the find will be taken to Darnassus.",al={"meaning of ande'thoras-ethil","andethoras ethil night elf phrase","darnassian farewell thundris"}},{q="What happens after completing the quest?",a="After recognizing the mark of ancient corruption, Thundris prepares the relic for immediate transport to Darnassus, where scholars can examine it.",al={"what happens after its all fun and games until","next quest after fun and games","to darnassus followup"}},{q="Which faction handles this investigation?",a="Darnassian leadership and night elf figures like Thundris handle the matter, bringing threats before the night elven authorities.",al={"is its all fun and games alliance only","who can do its all fun and games until"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, you see Darnassus respond at once to a discovery marked by ancient corruption.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance adventurer, you can bring troubling discoveries to night elf authorities so Darnassian scholars can examine and contain dangerous relics."},kw={"ancient evil","ande'thoras-ethil","darnassus","mark of an ancient evil","night elf","thundris"},rel={"zone:darnassus"},m={id=98042},src={"In-game quest text: It's All Fun and Games Until..."},fv=true}
E["quest:98046"] = {t="quest",n="Crown of the Earth",z="general",s="In Darnassus, Fandral Staghelm turns from a failed rite and sends its vessel away. He asks you to carry it to Priestess Lariia at the Temple of the Moon for disposal.",h="In Darnassus, Fandral Staghelm turns from a failed rite and sends its vessel away.",sec={{t="Fandral's Frustration",b="Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm guides the night elves while Malfurion Stormrage is absent. Seeking answers, he performs an incantation with sacred water held in a phial. The rite brings no answer, and Fandral's temper rises. Certain the water is useless, he dismisses you with sharp words and sends you south to the Temple of the Moon, where the priestesses can dispose of the vessel.",sp=0},{t="The Remaining Drops",b="Priestess Lariia asks you to make allowance for Fandral's harsh manner and the burden he bears. As she examines the phial, she finds several drops of water still inside. Perhaps Fandral's magic did not fail; perhaps the vessel was never emptied.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Crown of the Earth?",a="Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm gives you the task in Darnassus. With Malfurion Stormrage absent, Fandral oversees the Cenarion Circle. After an incantation with sacred water brings no answers, he sends you away in frustration.",al={"who starts crown of the earth","crown of the earth quest giver","fandral quest"}},{q="Where do you take the Drained Vessel?",a="You take the Drained Vessel to Priestess Lariia at the Temple of the Moon in southern Darnassus. Fandral expects the priestesses of Elune to dispose of the vessel, which he believes has no use left.",al={"where to take drained vessel","drained vessel turn in","where is lariia"}},{q="Why is Fandral Staghelm so frustrated?",a="Fandral Staghelm bears the burden of guiding the night elves and leading the Cenarion Circle while Malfurion Stormrage is away. When his incantation yields nothing, frustration and pride make him lash out and dismiss you before examining the failure more closely.",al={"why is fandral mad","fandral attitude","why is fandral impatient"}},{q="Why did Fandral's incantation fail?",a="Fandral believes the water had no useful power, but Priestess Lariia finds several drops still clinging inside the vessel. The rite may have failed because the vessel was not fully emptied.",al={"why did the incantation fail","did fandral ritual work","failed incantation"}},{q="Where is Malfurion during this quest?",a="Malfurion Stormrage is absent from Darnassus. Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm guides the night elves and leads the Cenarion Circle in his absence, a heavy burden that feeds Fandral's temper and impatience with setbacks.",al={"where is malfurion","why is malfurion absent","malfurion status"}},{q="Who is Priestess Lariia?",a="Priestess Lariia serves Elune at the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus. She receives the vessel with compassion for Fandral's burdens and notices that water remains inside.",al={"who is lariia","priestess lariia lore","lariia temple of the moon"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Fandral addresses you as a fellow druid. He meets the ritual's failure with curt impatience, then dismisses you.",["race:NightElf"]="In Darnassus, Fandral Staghelm's prickly authority meets the patient devotion of the Sisterhood of Elune at the Temple of the Moon.",["faction:Alliance"]="Darnassus is the Alliance's chief city on Kalimdor. With Malfurion Stormrage absent, quiet tensions mark the city's leadership."},kw={"cenarion circle","crown of the earth","darnassus","drained vessel","elune","fandral staghelm","lariia","malfurion","temple of the moon"},rel={"npc:fandral-staghelm","subzone:temple-of-the-moon","zone:darnassus","topic:druid","quest:2519"},m={id=98046},src={"In-game quest text: Crown of the Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:98065"] = {t="quest",n="Crown of the Earth",z="general",s="Within Darnassus, purified Moonwell Remnants are brought to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind. The meeting takes place in the Temple of the Moon, where the waters’ sacred meaning meets the strain between Tyrande and Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm.",h="Within Darnassus, purified Moonwell Remnants are brought to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind.",sec={{t="Audience with the High Priestess",b="Once the sacred Moonwell Remnants are collected, you pour the blessed water into a ceremonial vessel and carry it to Tyrande Whisperwind. The High Priestess of Elune keeps audience high within the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus. She watches over the city and offers her goddess’s light to all who enter. To place sacred water in her care is an honor and an act of devotion to Elune.",sp=0},{t="Tensions in Leadership",b="Tyrande gives deep thanks for saving the blessed water. Then her thoughts turn to the strains gripping Darnassus. With Malfurion Stormrage absent, Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm has taken charge of druidic affairs. Yet Tyrande makes clear that governing the kaldorei is her burden alone. She urges respect for Staghelm’s station, but the exchange shows sharp friction between the Sisterhood of Elune and the Cenarion Circle.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Crown of the Earth?",a="In Darnassus, you carry Moonwell Remnants in a ceremonial vessel to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind inside the Temple of the Moon. The meeting brings you before the night elves’ supreme leader.",al={"what is crown of the earth","crown of the earth quest","quest crown of the earth"}},{q="Who is Tyrande Whisperwind?",a="Tyrande Whisperwind is the High Priestess of Elune and the night elves’ political leader. From the upper tier of the Temple of the Moon, she oversees Darnassus and bears the burden of guiding her people through troubled times.",al={"who is tyrande whisperwind","where is tyrande","high priestess tyrande"}},{q="Why are the Moonwell Remnants brought to Tyrande?",a="The Moonwell Remnants are sacred water blessed by Elune. You place them in a ceremonial vessel and bring them to Tyrande, who receives and safeguards them on the goddess’s behalf.",al={"why bring moonwell remnants to tyrande","what are moonwell remnants","sacred water darnassus"}},{q="What is the dispute between Tyrande and Fandral Staghelm?",a="With Malfurion Stormrage absent, Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm leads the druids. Tyrande maintains that governing Darnassus and leading the kaldorei remain her responsibility alone, and will not let druidic authority eclipse Elune’s priesthood.",al={"tyrande fandral tension","why does tyrande dislike staghelm","fandral vs tyrande"}},{q="Where is Malfurion during this quest?",a="Malfurion Stormrage is absent from Darnassus, leaving a leadership vacuum among the druids. Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm has stepped in to fill it, heightening the political strain with Tyrande.",al={"where is malfurion","what happened to malfurion","malfurion absence"}},{q="Where is the Temple of the Moon?",a="The Temple of the Moon stands in the heart of Darnassus. It is the Sisterhood of Elune’s chief sanctuary, and Tyrande holds audience on the upper level above the main sanctuary floor.",al={"temple of the moon location","where is temple of the moon","where to find tyrande"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, you carry sacred moonwell water into a meeting that shows the kaldorei’s divided leadership between Tyrande’s priesthood and Fandral’s druids.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you serve High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, whose clergy helps guide and govern night elf society.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you hear Tyrande’s sharp words about Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm, which show the growing friction within the Cenarion Circle in Malfurion’s absence.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an ally, you meet the leader of Darnassus and glimpse the political strains within one of the Alliance’s key capitals."},kw={"crown of the earth","darnassus","elune","fandral staghelm","malfurion","moonwell remnants","temple of the moon","tyrande whisperwind"},rel={"npc:tyrande-whisperwind","subzone:temple-of-the-moon","zone:darnassus","topic:elune","topic:druid","quest:2519"},m={id=98065},src={"In-game quest text: Crown of the Earth"},fv=true}
E["quest:98067"] = {t="quest",n="Eyes of the Sentinels",z="general",s="Eyes of the Sentinels is a Darnassus quest where players assist Sentinel Dalia Sunblade in stationing magical scout owls at critical posts throughout the night elven capital. Facing growing unease across Teldrassil, this aerial surveillance bolsters the city's defenses while allowing the Sentinels time for prayer to Elune.",h="Eyes of the Sentinels is a Darnassus quest where players assist Sentinel Dalia Sunblade in stationing magical...",sec={{t="Devotion and Defense",b="In the sacred sanctuary of the Temple of the Moon, Sentinel Dalia Sunblade maintains the vigil over Darnassus. Guided by General Shandris Feathermoon's decree that every warrior must balance martial prowess with spiritual devotion, Dalia arranges the deployment of watchful sentinel owls across key city hubs. Taking over these daily duties affords the sentinel valuable moments of prayer and reflection before the Moon Goddess, while ensuring the kaldorei capital remains tightly guarded.",sp=0},{t="Watch Over Darnassus",b="Unease hangs in the air above Teldrassil, prompting the Sentinels to double their vigilance. To guard against infiltration and unrest, sentinel owls are stationed at pivotal junctions: the depths beneath Cenarion Hold to the north, the bustling bank in the town square, the inn within Craftsman's Terrace, and the main city gates. With the local city guards assisting in their placement, these avian sentries act as silent guardians watching over all who walk the streets of the Great Tree.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Eyes of the Sentinels?",a="Eyes of the Sentinels is a task in Darnassus where an adventurer aids Sentinel Dalia Sunblade by stationing sentinel owls at key locations across the city to maintain vigilance against looming threats.",al={"eyes of the sentinels quest","what is eyes of the sentinels","eyes of the sentinels overview"}},{q="Who is Sentinel Dalia Sunblade?",a="Sentinel Dalia Sunblade is a kaldorei guardian stationed in the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus who oversees local defensive routines and ensures watchful owls guard the city's key thoroughfares.",al={"who is dalia sunblade","dalia sunblade lore","sentinel dalia sunblade"}},{q="Why are Sentinel Owls being placed around Darnassus?",a="Sentinel owls serve as vigilant aerial lookouts and divine scouts granted by Elune, allowing the Sentinels to survey critical gathering points and transit chokepoints throughout Darnassus.",al={"why place sentinel owls","what do sentinel owls do","sentinel owl purpose"}},{q="Where are the Sentinel Owls placed?",a="The sentinel owls are posted at the entrance to the depths beneath Cenarion Hold to the north, the Darnassus Bank in the center of town, the inn at Craftsman's Terrace, and the city gates.",al={"owl placement spots","where do owls go","eyes of the sentinels locations"}},{q="Why are the Sentinels on high alert in Teldrassil?",a="Sentinel Dalia Sunblade notes that something feels amiss across the World Tree of Teldrassil, prompting the Sentinels to step up surveillance and maintain constant readiness against unseen dangers.",al={"why are sentinels worried","what is wrong with teldrassil","teldrassil amiss"}},{q="What role does General Feathermoon have in the Sentinels' daily duties?",a="General Shandris Feathermoon commands the Sentinels and ensures that her huntresses balance their martial responsibilities with daily devotion and prayer to the goddess Elune.",al={"general feathermoon role","shandris feathermoon sentinels","feathermoon prayer rule"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Sentinels are the traditional standing army and guardians of the night elf people, blending martial prowess with the divine blessings and watchful eyes of Elune.",["faction:Alliance"]="Ensuring the security of Darnassus safeguards the capital of the night elves and reinforces the Alliance's primary stronghold in northern Kalimdor.",["class:Hunter"]="The deployment of scout owls reflects classic night elf reconnaissance, where trained beasts and celestial avian watchers serve as natural extensions of a huntress's senses.",["class:Priest"]="The Sentinels emphasize that weapons alone do not protect the kaldorei; regular prayer and the spiritual gifts of Elune are vital to their guardianship."},kw={"cenarion hold","craftsman terrace","dalia sunblade","darnassus","elune","eyes of the sentinels","feathermoon","sentinel owls","teldrassil","temple of the moon"},rel={"zone:darnassus","subzone:temple-of-the-moon","topic:sentinels","topic:elune","quest:940","subzone:cenarion-hold"},m={id=98067},src={"In-game quest text: Eyes of the Sentinels"},fv=true}
E["quest:98069"] = {t="quest",n="Stolen Freewind Supplies",z="general",s="At Freewind Post, Jandia needs the harpies' stolen supplies back. The post has enough for now, but it will soon run short without another shipment.",h="At Freewind Post, Jandia needs the harpies' stolen supplies back.",sec={{t="The Missing Shipment",b="The harpies struck the last caravan bound for Freewind Post. Jandia says the post can manage for now, though its stores will not last without more supplies. The harpies' lair lies west of the post, along the cliff base and in a cave above.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who needs the supplies?",a="Jandia in Freewind Post needs the stolen supplies recovered.",al={"who asked for supplies","Jandia supplies","freewind supplies"}},{q="Who took the caravan's supplies?",a="Harpies attacked the last caravan carrying supplies to Freewind Post.",al={"who stole them","harpy supplies","harpies stole what"}},{q="Where is the harpy lair?",a="The harpies' lair lies west of Freewind Post, along the cliff base and in a cave above.",al={"where are harpies","harpy cave","harpies west"}},{q="Why does Jandia need them back?",a="Freewind Post has enough supplies for now, but it will soon run short without another shipment.",al={"why recover supplies","freewind running short","why supplies matter"}},{q="What does Jandia ask you to recover?",a="Jandia asks you to recover eight stolen supplies for Freewind Post.",al={"how many supplies","stolen goods","what do i bring"}}},kw={"caravan","cave","cliff","freewind post","harpies","jandia","stolen freewind supplies","supplies"},rel={"subzone:freewind-post","topic:harpy"},m={id=98069},src={"In-game quest text: Stolen Freewind Supplies"},fv=true}
E["quest:98070"] = {t="quest",n="Stop the Screeching",z="general",s="In Screeching Canyon, harpies ambush supply caravans bound for Freewind Post. Their attacks may keep those caravans from traveling there.",h="In Screeching Canyon, harpies ambush supply caravans bound for Freewind Post.",sec={{t="Caravans at Risk",b="A supplier fears the harpy attacks will stop caravans from traveling to Freewind Post. You are asked to slay Screeching Harpies, Screeching Roguefeather Harpies, and Screeching Windcallers. Until then, caravans that brave the canyon remain in danger.",sp=0},{t="Word to Suppliers",b="After the harpies are dealt with, the supplier will notify those who provide goods to Freewind Post. Then the caravans will face one less threat on their journeys.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are the caravans being attacked?",a="Harpies in Screeching Canyon ambushed the last few supply caravans that traveled through.",al={"harpies attacking caravans","why harpies attack","caravan trouble"}},{q="What is at risk if the attacks continue?",a="The supplier fears no caravans will dare travel to Freewind Post if the attacks continue.",al={"why protect caravans","freewind post supplies","caravans stop"}},{q="Which harpies are involved?",a="The harpies are Screeching Harpies, Screeching Roguefeather Harpies, and Screeching Windcallers.",al={"harpy types","screeching harpies","roguefeather windcaller"}},{q="Where are the harpies?",a="The harpies live in Screeching Canyon.",al={"harpy location","screeching canyon harpies","where is screeching canyon"}}},kw={"caravan","freewind post","harpy","roguefeather","screeching canyon","screeching harpy","stop the screeching","supplies","windcaller"},rel={"subzone:freewind-post","topic:harpy"},m={id=98070},src={"In-game quest text: Stop the Screeching"},fv=true}
E["quest:98072"] = {t="quest",n="Crocs of the Sky",z="general",s="James asks if you have the scales, then praises your skill. He offers you work if you leave adventuring behind.",h="James asks if you have the scales, then praises your skill.",sec={{t="James's Offer",b="James calls you a natural talent and invites you back if you take a break from adventuring. He jokes that you may be older by then.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking about the scales?",a="James asks whether you have the scales.",al={"who wants the scales","scales for james"}},{q="What does James think of me?",a="James thinks you are a natural talent.",al={"does james like me","james opinion"}},{q="What does James offer?",a="James offers you work if you take a break from adventuring.",al={"james job","work offer"}}},kw={"adventuring","crocs of the sky","james","scales","work"},m={id=98072},src={"In-game quest text: Crocs of the Sky"},fv=true}
E["quest:98094"] = {t="quest",n="Scout Support",z="general",s="South of Tarren Mill, you carry new orders from High Executor Darthalia to Deathstalker Masoj. The Forsaken press on with their plan to take control of these lands.",h="South of Tarren Mill, you carry new orders from High Executor Darthalia to Deathstalker Masoj.",sec={{t="Orders for Masoj",b="High Executor Darthalia trusts you with word for the forward spies. She gives you updated orders for Deathstalker Masoj, who waits on high ground north of Nethander Stead and outside Dun Garok. The Forsaken plan to take control of these lands continues, and Masoj is waiting for your delivery.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gave me the orders?",a="High Executor Darthalia gave you updated orders for Deathstalker Masoj.",al={"who sent me","Darthalia orders","giver"}},{q="Who is Deathstalker Masoj?",a="Deathstalker Masoj is a forward spy posted outside Dun Garok, north of Nethander Stead.",al={"Masoj who","who's Masoj","Masoj"}},{q="Where do I find Masoj?",a="Masoj waits south of Tarren Mill, on high ground north of Nethander Stead and outside Dun Garok.",al={"Masoj location","where is Masoj","south of Tarren Mill"}},{q="What am I carrying to Masoj?",a="You carry updated orders for Deathstalker Masoj.",al={"what to deliver","orders for Masoj","the package"}},{q="Why am I delivering orders?",a="Your delivery supports the Forsaken plan to take control of these lands.",al={"why Masoj","what is the plan","Forsaken plan"}}},kw={"deathstalker masoj","dun garok","forsaken","forward spies","high executor darthalia","nethander stead","scout support","tarren mill","updated orders"},rel={"npc:high-executor-darthalia","subzone:tarren-mill","subzone:nethander-stead","subzone:dun-garok"},m={id=98094},src={"In-game quest text: Scout Support"},fv=true}
E["quest:98095"] = {t="quest",n="Valuable Vantages",z="general",s="Around Hillsbrad Foothills, Deathstalker Masoj needs you to restore three signaling marks. Bat riders use those points to guide strategic troops to their drop-off sites.",h="Around Hillsbrad Foothills, Deathstalker Masoj needs you to restore three signaling marks.",sec={{t="Three Peaks",b="You carry Apothecary Vials to three high places: the hills beside Dun Garok, the plateau above Dunholde Keep, and the Headland ridge above Hillsbrad. Their vapors draw bats back to the marks. Then Masoj's high command can guide its troops through the foothills.",sp=0},{t="Masoj's Orders",b="Masoj asks you to search for crushed, empty vials and renew the marks at all three places. He says this work serves plans advancing under the Deathstalkers' banner.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking me to place the vials?",a="Deathstalker Masoj asks you to restore the signaling marks around Hillsbrad Foothills.",al={"who gave valuable vantages","masoj vials","who is Masoj"}},{q="Why are the signaling marks needed?",a="Bat riders use the signaling points to guide strategic troops to drop-off sites.",al={"why bats need marks","bat signals","what are the beacons for"}},{q="Where do I place the vials?",a="Place the vials on the hills beside Dun Garok, the plateau above Dunholde Keep, and the Headland ridge above Hillsbrad.",al={"vial locations","where are the peaks","three vial spots"}},{q="What do the vial vapors do?",a="The vapors draw bats back to the desired drop-off points.",al={"what do vials do","bat scent","vial purpose"}}},kw={"apothecary vials","bat riders","deathstalker masoj","dun garok","dunholde keep","headland","hillsbrad foothills","signaling beacons","valuable vantages"},rel={"zone:hillsbrad","subzone:dun-garok"},m={id=98095},src={"In-game quest text: Valuable Vantages"},fv=true}
E["quest:98155"] = {t="quest",n="Pristine Pesterhide Pelts",z="general",s="Pristine Pesterhide Pelts is a task undertaken on behalf of Kristy Grant, a traveler seeking the radiant coats of the Pesterhide hyenas in Thousand Needles. Lacking combat training herself, Kristy relies on capable adventurers to hunt the beasts and harvest their pelts. Her goal is to send these unique hides back across the sea to her sisters in Stormwind, where they can be fashioned into fine garments and goods.",h="Pristine Pesterhide Pelts is a task undertaken on behalf of Kristy Grant, a traveler seeking the radiant...",sec={{t="A Sisterly Ambition",b="Far from the bustling cobblestone avenues of Stormwind, Kristy Grant finds herself traveling through the arid, sun-baked canyon floor of Thousand Needles. While observing the local wildlife, she is struck by the striking hue and quality of the coats worn by the native Pesterhide hyenas. Recognizing that such distinct pelts are virtually unknown in the Eastern Kingdoms, Kristy envisions sending a shipment of hides back home to her sisters. In her eyes, the pelts represent not only a piece of the Kalimdor frontier, but raw material that skilled family hands in the kingdom can transform into something truly extraordinary.",sp=0},{t="Hunters of the Salt Flats and Canyons",b="Despite her eye for craft and trade, Kristy Grant is no warrior and possesses no combat experience to face the ferocious predators of the canyon herself. The Pesterhide hyenas are vicious pack scavengers that roam the dusty expanses beneath the towering mesas, making the harvesting of pristine pelts dangerous for any lone traveler. To secure five unblemished hides without risking her own neck, Kristy enlists the aid of seasoned adventurers passing through the needles. The task requires venturing out into the wilderness, overcoming the territorial beasts, and carefully collecting the hides needed for her family's crafting ventures back home.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Kristy Grant?",a="Kristy Grant is a traveler from the kingdom of Stormwind currently venturing through Kalimdor. Unlike the hardened soldiers and mercenaries traversing Thousand Needles, she admits to having no fighting experience of her own. Instead, she relies on keen observation and an eye for valuable crafting materials, contracting capable adventurers to gather goods like hyena pelts on her behalf.",al={"kristy grant lore","who gives pristine pesterhide pelts","who is kristy grant"}},{q="What are Pesterhide hyenas?",a="Pesterhide hyenas are wild canine predators native to the arid canyon floors and expanses of Thousand Needles. They roam the harsh terrain hunting and scavenging in packs. Beyond their vicious nature, they are notable for their brilliant, beautifully hued fur coats, which stand out sharply against the desert rock and catch the attention of traveling tailors and furriers.",al={"pesterhide hyena","thousand needles hyenas","what are pesterhides"}},{q="Why does Kristy Grant want the pelts?",a="Kristy Grant wants to collect pristine hyena pelts to send them back across the sea to her sisters in the kingdom of Stormwind. She is thoroughly impressed by the brilliant hue of their coats and believes her sisters possess the craftsmanship to weave or tailor the exotic furs into incredible finished wares.",al={"why collect pesterhide pelts","pesterhide pelt purpose","what does kristy do with the pelts"}},{q="Where do the pelts end up?",a="The pelts gathered in Thousand Needles are destined for the Eastern Kingdoms, specifically the city-kingdom of Stormwind. Kristy intends to ship the harvested hides back home across the Great Sea so her sisters can work with exotic materials that are practically unobtainable within the familiar forests of Elwynn or Redridge.",al={"where are the pelts sent","pelts stormwind","who gets the hyena pelts"}},{q="Why can't Kristy hunt the hyenas herself?",a="Kristy Grant openly admits that she has no fighting experience whatsoever. The wild beasts of Thousand Needles are aggressive and perilous to encounter, especially for an unarmed artisan or traveler. Rather than risking injury or death against the prowling packs of hyenas, she hires skilled wanderers to hunt the animals safely.",al={"why can't kristy fight","kristy grant combat","why does kristy need help"}},{q="What makes these pelts pristine?",a="Because the pelts are intended for fine crafting and fashion by Kristy's sisters in Stormwind, ordinary or mangled furs will not suffice. Only undamaged, pristine hides preserve the vibrant hue and texture required to fashion high-quality garments, making it necessary to hunt carefully and salvage only the best specimens from the beasts.",al={"pristine pelts meaning","why pristine hides","pristine pesterhide pelt quality"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="As an endeavor by a Stormwind traveler to supply her artisan family in the capital, this task highlights how Alliance subjects venture deep into the Kalimdor frontier to source exotic materials.",["race:Human"]="Kristy Grant exemplifies the enterprising spirit of Stormwind humans traveling far across the sea, keeping close ties with kin back in the kingdom.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Crafters who work with cloth and decorative furs can appreciate the ambition of Kristy's sisters, who seek to turn wild Kalimdor hides into refined garments."},kw={"fur","hunting","hyena","kristy grant","pelts","pesterhide","stormwind","thousand needles"},rel={"zone:thousandneedles","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},m={id=98155},src={"In-game quest text: Pristine Pesterhide Pelts"},fv=true}
E["quest:98189"] = {t="quest",n="Return the Statuette",z="general",s="A statuette's delivery to Menethil Harbor was interrupted by tragedy. You are asked to bring it to Captain Stoutfist at Menethil Keep.",h="A statuette's delivery to Menethil Harbor was interrupted by tragedy.",sec={{t="An Unfinished Delivery",b="The statuette was meant for Menethil Harbor, where Captain Stoutfist at Menethil Keep was expected to sign for it. Now you are asked to finish the delivery.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is meant to receive the statuette?",a="Captain Stoutfist at Menethil Keep is expected to sign for the Menethil Statuette.",al={"who gets the statuette","statuette recipient","captain stoutfist delivery"}},{q="Where was the statuette supposed to go?",a="The statuette was meant to be delivered to Menethil Harbor.",al={"where does it go","statuette destination","menethil statuette"}},{q="Who was carrying it before?",a="The Blind Princess was supposed to deliver the statuette before tragedy struck.",al={"who had the statuette","blind princess statuette","statuette delivery"}},{q="What is being asked of me?",a="You are asked to bring the Menethil Statuette to Captain Stoutfist at Menethil Keep.",al={"what do i do","return statuette","statuette quest"}}},kw={"blind princess","captain stoutfist","menethil harbor","menethil keep","menethil statuette","statuette"},rel={"item:menethil-statuette","npc:captain-stoutfist","subzone:menethil-keep","subzone:menethil-harbor"},m={id=98189},src={"In-game quest text: Return the Statuette"},fv=true}
E["quest:98248"] = {t="quest",n="Shipping Label",z="general",s="A torn manifest points couriers toward Durotar Supply and Logistics in the Barrens. The company depends on travelers to recover lost crates and return paperwork to its post west of the Crossroads, keeping supplies and trade moving across central Kalimdor.",h="A torn manifest points couriers toward Durotar Supply and Logistics in the Barrens.",sec={{t="A Wayward Manifest",b="A torn manifest is fixed to a freshly restocked cargo crate. Its recipient's name has worn away, but the heading names Durotar Supply and Logistics, a freight company in Horde territory. Beside the writing, a rough map points couriers toward the company's staging grounds in the central plains.",sp=0},{t="Frontier Commerce",b="The notice welcomes travelers bringing stray containers, damaged goods, or proof of salvage to the trade outpost west of the Crossroads. Durotar Supply and Logistics depends on independent haulers to recover scattered freight across the Barrens. Those who help keep regional supplies moving receive compensation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Shipping Label?",a="The Shipping Label is a torn document fixed to a freshly restocked crate, with return instructions for Durotar Supply and Logistics and directions back to its post near the Crossroads.",al={"what is the shipping label","shipping label quest","torn shipping label"}},{q="Where should the label be delivered?",a="The label directs couriers to the Durotar Supply and Logistics trade hub west of the Crossroads in the Barrens.",al={"where do i take the shipping label","who wants the shipping label","shipping label turn in"}},{q="What is Durotar Supply and Logistics?",a="Durotar Supply and Logistics is a freight company in Horde territory. It handles cargo transport, crate collection, and logistical support across Durotar and the Barrens.",al={"what is durotar supply and logistics","durotar supply outpost"}},{q="Who was the shipment originally intended for?",a="The recipient's name has been torn away, leaving the return instructions and the company's origin information on the crate.",al={"who was the crate meant for","crate recipient"}},{q="Where is the Durotar Supply and Logistics trade outpost located?",a="The label's directions and rough map place the outpost west of the Crossroads in the Barrens.",al={"where is the trade outpost","durotar supply location crossroads"}},{q="Does Durotar Supply and Logistics compensate travelers for returning crates?",a="Yes. The notice promises compensation for travelers who bring damaged shipments, labels, or empty crates to the hub.",al={"do they pay for returned crates","durotar supply compensation"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you may encounter Durotar Supply and Logistics while traveling through the Barrens. Its work helps keep trade and supplies moving between Durotar and central Kalimdor."},kw={"crate","crossroads","durotar supply and logistics","shipping label","the barrens","trade outpost"},rel={"subzone:crossroads","zone:barrens","zone:durotar","quest:98247"},m={id=98248},src={"In-game quest text: Shipping Label"},fv=true}
E["quest:98286"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Enchanting",z="general",s="At Shen'dar Village, skilled enchanters can learn to make an Enchanted Lute for the campfire. Nasalanna Windsinger teaches them how it entertains and bolsters companions.",h="At Shen'dar Village, skilled enchanters can learn to make an Enchanted Lute for the campfire.",sec={{t="Comforts of the Hearth",b="On the open road, sharpened blades and rations are not enough. Travelers need rest and good spirits to endure long expeditions. Enchanters bring magical comforts to camps across Azeroth, helping weary travelers recover their strength and spirits before they return to perilous trails.",sp=0},{t="The Song of Shen'dar",b="Aspiring enchanters seeking to learn campsite magic travel to Shen'dar Village. There, Nasalanna Windsinger teaches capable spell-weavers to make an Enchanted Lute. First, they must prove their dedication and skill in enchanting. Then Nasalanna shares how to fashion an instrument whose melodies restore spirits beneath the stars.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Nasalanna Windsinger?",a="Nasalanna Windsinger lives in Shen'dar Village and teaches skilled enchanters to craft special items for wilderness camps. She teaches those who prove their skill to make the Enchanted Lute.",al={"who teaches enchanted lute","who is nasalanna windsinger","where is nasalanna windsinger"}},{q="Where is Shen'dar Village?",a="Shen'dar Village is where Nasalanna Windsinger lives. Enchanters travel there for instruction in making campsite gear like the Enchanted Lute once they have developed their core skills.",al={"where is shendar village","how to find shendar village"}},{q="What is an Enchanted Lute?",a="Enchanters make the Enchanted Lute as a magical instrument for camps. It entertains companions around the campfire and restores their morale, helping travelers steel themselves for the wilderness ahead.",al={"what does enchanted lute do","why craft enchanted lute","camping lute"}},{q="What are the requirements to learn the recipe?",a="To learn how to make an Enchanted Lute, you must first prove your skill in enchanting to Nasalanna Windsinger. She trusts seasoned practitioners with this camp enchantment.",al={"requirements for camping 101 enchanting","when can i learn enchanted lute"}},{q="Why are enchanters valuable at wilderness campsites?",a="At wilderness camps, enchanters bring magical comforts when ordinary supplies fall short. Their enchantments help travelers recover, stay entertained, and prepare for hazards on the trail.",al={"why do campers want enchanters","enchanters camping benefits"}},{q="Can other professions craft camping items?",a="Yes. Craftsmen and gatherers in different disciplines learn to make tools for campsites. Enchanters learn to make the Enchanted Lute from Nasalanna Windsinger in Shen'dar Village.",al={"are there other camping items","other profession camp crafts"}}},kw={"campfire","camping","camping 101","enchanted lute","enchanting","lute","nasalanna windsinger","shen'dar village"},m={id=98286},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Enchanting"},fv=true}
E["quest:98293"] = {t="quest",n="Forced Disarmament",z="general",s="Forced Disarmament is an Alliance quest in the Wetlands tasking adventurers with seizing weapon caches from the Dragonmaw clan at Dragonmaw Retreat. By collecting thirty bundles of armaments, players prevent the hostile orcs from equipping additional soldiers for future raids. The confiscated gear is delivered to Captain Stoutfist at Menethil Keep to safeguard the region's vital harbor.",h="Forced Disarmament is an Alliance quest in the Wetlands tasking adventurers with seizing weapon caches from...",sec={{t="An Armory in the Marshes",b="In the marshlands of the Wetlands, the hostile Dragonmaw clan has begun gathering an alarming stockpile of weapons within the Dragonmaw Retreat. Left unchecked, these supplies would allow the renegade orcs to equip fresh waves of warriors and launch devastating raids against neighboring settlements. Disarming their encampments strikes directly at their logistical capability, blunting any planned offensive before their warbands can fully mobilize and take the field.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Coastal Port",b="Menethil Harbor serves as the primary gateway connecting Khaz Modan and Stormwind to the sea, making any nearby military build-up a severe peril. Captain Stoutfist coordinates defensive measures from Menethil Keep, keeping vigilant watch over the hostile clans and beasts encroaching upon the town. Recovering thirty sets of Dragonmaw armaments deprives the orcs of essential munitions while providing the dwarven garrison with vital intelligence regarding the enemy's readiness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Captain Stoutfist?",a="Captain Stoutfist is a dwarven military officer stationed inside Menethil Keep, responsible for the defense of Menethil Harbor and the surrounding Wetlands. Facing constant pressure from hostile beasts, Dark Iron saboteurs, and the Dragonmaw clan, Stoutfist actively directs soldiers and adventurers to suppress nearby threats before the port's vital supply lines and harbor facilities are overrun.",al={"stoutfist","who is stoutfist","menethil keep captain"}},{q="Why do the Dragonmaw need to be disarmed?",a="The Dragonmaw clan is actively amassing weapons within their retreat to equip new fighters for battle. If allowed to hoard munitions uncontested, their warbands could overwhelm local Alliance defenses and imperil Menethil Harbor. Seizing thirty bundles of weaponry directly impedes their logistical preparations and prevents more orc soldiers from taking the field.",al={"why take dragonmaw weapons","dragonmaw armaments purpose","disarming dragonmaw"}},{q="Where is Dragonmaw Retreat located?",a="Dragonmaw Retreat is an encampment established by the Dragonmaw orcs in the Wetlands, situated eastward toward the mountains beyond Menethil Harbor. The clan uses this secluded outpost to muster forces, store weaponry, and stage attacks against travelers along the marshes. Adventurers must venture into the heart of the camp to recover their stockpiled weapons.",al={"dragonmaw retreat location","where is dragonmaw retreat","dragonmaw retreat wetlands"}},{q="What are Dragonmaw Armaments?",a="Dragonmaw Armaments are caches of martial gear, blades, and equipment forged or stolen by the Dragonmaw clan to supply their warriors. These weapons represent the clan's immediate military capacity in the region. Captain Stoutfist requests that thirty bundles of these munitions be confiscated and brought back to Menethil Keep for evaluation and disposal.",al={"dragonmaw weapons","what are armaments","dragonmaw gear"}},{q="Is the Dragonmaw clan part of the Horde in 25 ADP?",a="No, the Dragonmaw clan in the Wetlands operates as an independent, hostile faction that does not answer to Warchief Thrall. Remaining loyal to their old ways following the Second War, these orcs harbor deep hatred for the Alliance and refuse any peaceful overtures. As a result, both Alliance defenders and independent travelers treat them as lethal adversaries.",al={"are dragonmaw horde","dragonmaw clan faction","dragonmaw thrall"}},{q="Why is Menethil Keep concerned with the Dragonmaw?",a="Menethil Keep is the administrative and military fortress safeguarding Menethil Harbor, the principal maritime hub for the Alliance in Khaz Modan. Because the wetlands surrounding the harbor are plagued by marsh beasts and aggressive orcs, Captain Stoutfist cannot allow a heavily armed encampment like Dragonmaw Retreat to flourish so close to the kingdom's docks and trade routes.",al={"menethil keep dragonmaw threat","why stoutfist cares","menethil harbor defense"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance forces rely on Menethil Harbor as a vital lifeline across the Great Sea, making the disarmament of nearby hostile clans an urgent strategic necessity.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven defenders from Ironforge maintain the garrison at Menethil Keep, striving to secure the perimeter of Khaz Modan from old enemies entrenched in the marshes.",["race:Orc"]="The Dragonmaw represent a splintered, unrepentant remnant of the Old Horde whose violent warmongering isolates them from the new Horde leadership in Kalimdor."},kw={"captain stoutfist","dragonmaw armaments","dragonmaw clan","dragonmaw retreat","forced disarmament","menethil harbor","menethil keep","wetlands"},rel={"npc:captain-stoutfist","subzone:menethil-keep"},m={id=98293},src={"In-game quest text: Forced Disarmament"},fv=true}
E["quest:98297"] = {t="quest",n="Stopping the Cycle",z="general",s="At the Dragonmaw Gates, warlocks are trying to enslave a red dragonspawn. Howin Kindfeather returns from a test flight with troubling news, and the Wildhammer will not let it happen again.",h="At the Dragonmaw Gates, warlocks are trying to enslave a red dragonspawn.",sec={{t="At the Gates",b="Howin Kindfeather asks you to face the subdued dragonspawn at the Dragonmaw Gates. First, kill the warlocks holding it down. That should free it. Then put the dragonspawn out of its misery. Howin expects you to bring friends, for the Wildhammer will not let the Dragonmaw stand in their way.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is asking for help?",a="Howin Kindfeather asks you to stop the Dragonmaw from enslaving a red dragonspawn.",al={"who wants this","howin task"}},{q="What are the Dragonmaw doing?",a="A group of Dragonmaw warlocks is holding down a red dragonspawn and trying to enslave it.",al={"dragonmaw doing what","why dragonspawn subdued"}},{q="Where is the dragonspawn?",a="The subdued dragonspawn is at the Dragonmaw Gates.",al={"dragonspawn location","where subdued dragonspawn"}},{q="What does Howin ask me to do?",a="Howin asks you to kill the warlocks holding the dragonspawn down, then put it out of its misery.",al={"what do i do","stop the cycle"}},{q="Why does Howin say the Wildhammer cannot allow this?",a="Howin says the Dragonmaw are enslaving a red dragonspawn, and insists the Wildhammer will not let them stand in their way.",al={"why not again","wildhammer reason"}}},kw={"dragonmaw","dragonmaw gates","howin kindfeather","red dragonspawn","stopping the cycle","subdued dragonspawn","warlocks","wildhammer"},rel={"subzone:dragonmaw-gates","topic:warlock"},m={id=98297},src={"In-game quest text: Stopping the Cycle"},fv=true}
E["quest:98298"] = {t="quest",n="Arugal's Folly",z="general",s="At the Sepulcher, Dalar Dawnweaver seeks the truth behind the worgen near the Greymane Wall. He hopes their remains will prove that Archmage Arugal bound the beasts but did not bring their curse into being.",h="At the Sepulcher, Dalar Dawnweaver seeks the truth behind the worgen near the Greymane Wall.",sec={{t="Exposing the Archmage",b="At the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest, Forsaken mage Dalar Dawnweaver means to strip away the mystery around his rival, Archmage Arugal. Enchanted shackles show that Arugal lacked the power to summon or create the worgen packs terrorizing the region. His magic could only hold the beasts for a time. How the affliction began remains unknown.",sp=0},{t="The Beastly Incursion",b="Dalar sends you south to the Greymane Wall for proof. Feral worgen attack and infect nearby human populations in the wilderness around its imposing fortifications. You can bring Dalar physical evidence by killing the beasts and gathering pieces of their bodies. He needs those samples to study the affliction's nature.",sp=0},{t="A Bitter Vindication",b="Dalar Dawnweaver inspects the worgen remains and claims victory over Arugal's reputation. The archmage had no part in the affliction's origin. Yet this finding brings grim news to the Forsaken. If Arugal did not create the curse, then his supposed remedies and countermeasures are worthless. The worgen outbreak remains unexplained.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Arugal's Folly?",a="Dalar Dawnweaver gives and receives the work at the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. He is a Forsaken mage who studies the arcane.",al={"who gives arugals folly","where do i turn in arugals folly","quest giver arugals folly"}},{q="Why does Dalar Dawnweaver consider Arugal a fraud?",a="Dalar Dawnweaver believes Archmage Arugal lacked the power to create the worgen. He says Arugal could only bind them briefly, not bring them or their affliction into being.",al={"what does dalar think of arugal","did arugal make worgen","arugal fraud"}},{q="Where do you find the worgen bits for Dalar?",a="You find the worgen south of the Sepulcher near the Greymane Wall. They prowl by nearby human settlements, and their remains provide samples for Dalar's study.",al={"where are the worgen bits","greymane wall worgen","where to go arugals folly"}},{q="Does Dalar find a cure for the worgen curse?",a="Dalar discovers no cure for the worgen affliction. Since Arugal did not create it, his remedies and counter-spells cannot help, and the Forsaken must seek a true solution elsewhere.",al={"can worgen curse be cured","does arugal remedy work","is there a cure for worgen"}},{q="How is the worgen affliction spreading near the Greymane Wall?",a="Near the Greymane Wall, the affliction appears to spread to nearby humans through physical encounters and bites. Dalar finds no sign of an overarching ritual or spell cast by Arugal.",al={"how does the curse spread","worgen spreading to humans","who is getting infected"}},{q="What does Dalar conclude after inspecting the worgen bits?",a="The worgen remains convince Dalar Dawnweaver that Arugal's sorcery did not cause their curse. They confirm Arugal's limited magic, while the affliction remains active and unexplained, with no working antidote.",al={"what does dalar learn from the bits","conclusion arugals folly","arugals folly lore reveal"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As Forsaken of the Sepulcher, you help uncover the worgen outbreak's true nature in Silverpine. That knowledge matters as Lordaeron faces feral threats it cannot control.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde adventurer, you help Dalar Dawnweaver protect Silverpine Forest from feral predators and nearby human settlements suffering the affliction.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you can see the difference between Dalar's binding enchantments and the affliction spreading among the worgen."},kw={"arugal","arugal's folly","dalar dawnweaver","greymane wall","silverpine forest","the sepulcher","worgen bits","worgen curse"},rel={"npc:dalar-dawnweaver","npc:arugal","subzone:greymane-wall","subzone:sepulcher","topic:worgen","topic:human"},m={id=98298},src={"In-game quest text: Arugal's Folly"},fv=true}
E["quest:98299"] = {t="quest",n="Stop the Spread",z="general",s="Stop the Spread is a Silverpine Forest quest concerning the containment of the worgen curse linked to Archmage Arugal. While researchers seek a way to halt the affliction, preemptive measures are ordered against living human refugees at the Greymane Wall to prevent them from turning into Arugal's servants.",h="Stop the Spread is a Silverpine Forest quest concerning the containment of the worgen curse linked to...",sec={{t="The Silverpine Affliction",b="In Silverpine Forest, the scourge of the worgen spreads at the hands of the renegade Archmage Arugal. While the exact methods and mechanics behind the spread of this feral condition remain largely unknown, the danger it presents to the territory is undeniable. Researchers are actively working to understand the nature of the magic, but their immediate focus is preventing Arugal from amassing an unstoppable host of savage followers.",sp=0},{t="Containment at the Wall",b="Forsaken investigators have observed that their undead physiology makes them immune to the worgen infection. Living humans, however, possess no such natural defense. At the southern border, a desperate encampment of human refugees has clustered around the locked gates of the Greymane Wall. Fearful that these defenseless survivors will be bitten and transformed into loyal thralls for Arugal, preemptive and ruthless containment orders have been issued to purge the gathering before the affliction claims them.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest Stop the Spread about?",a="Stop the Spread is a mission focused on containing the worgen curse ravaging Silverpine Forest. To prevent Archmage Arugal from gaining new servants, forces are sent to eliminate human refugees near the Greymane Wall before they can succumb to the affliction.",al={"what is stop the spread","stop the spread quest lore"}},{q="Who is responsible for the worgen in Silverpine?",a="Archmage Arugal is held responsible for the presence of the worgen within Silverpine Forest, whether through his deliberate machinations or unforeseen consequences of his magic. His growing pack threatens the entire region.",al={"who spreads worgen curse","arugal worgen connection"}},{q="Can the Forsaken catch the worgen curse?",a="According to local Forsaken research, the worgen curse cannot infect the undead. This immunity allows Forsaken operatives to confront the threat without danger of turning into beastly servants of Arugal.",al={"can forsaken become worgen","are undead immune to worgen"}},{q="Why are human refugees being targeted at the Greymane Wall?",a="A large congregation of human refugees has gathered outside the sealed Greymane Wall. Because living humans are susceptible to Arugal's curse, they are considered an imminent threat of swelling the ranks of the worgen.",al={"why kill refugees","greymane wall refugees"}},{q="How does the worgen affliction spread?",a="While it is known that the worgen curse can be transmitted through bites, the exact magical and biological mechanics of its spread remain under active investigation by researchers in Silverpine Forest.",al={"how do worgen spread","worgen curse transmission"}},{q="Is there a way to stop or cure the worgen curse?",a="At present, there is no known cure for the worgen affliction. Researchers are attempting to discover a remedy to halt the curse, but their immediate priority remains aggressive containment.",al={"can the worgen curse be cured","cure for worgen"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="Skyborne adventurers are directly warned to avoid being bitten while carrying out the grim containment orders near the Greymane Wall.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken appear uniquely immune to the worgen affliction, making containment operations viable without risk of infection to undead forces.",["race:Human"]="Uninfected human refugees huddled at the sealed Greymane Wall are viewed by Forsaken researchers as prime vectors for Arugal's spreading curse."},kw={"arugal","curse","forsaken","greymane wall","refugees","silverpine","skyborne","stop the spread","worgen"},rel={"subzone:greymane-wall","npc:arugal","topic:worgen","topic:forsaken","topic:skyborne","topic:human","topic:refugee"},m={id=98299},src={"In-game quest text: Stop the Spread"},fv=true}
E["quest:98313"] = {t="quest",n="For Further Study",z="general",s="Historian Karnik awaits a package in Ironforge, tied to something epic. What has been seen so far makes further study worthwhile.",h="Historian Karnik awaits a package in Ironforge, tied to something epic.",sec={{t="Karnik's Library",b="You are carrying the Whelgar Relic Package to Historian Karnik in the Hall of Explorers. He is usually in the library, reading at the back.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is waiting for the package?",a="Historian Karnik awaits the Whelgar Relic Package in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers.",al={"who gets it","package for who"}},{q="Where can I find Karnik?",a="Historian Karnik is usually reading at the back of the library in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers.",al={"where's karnik","karnik location"}},{q="What package am I carrying?",a="You are carrying the Whelgar Relic Package for Historian Karnik.",al={"what package","relic package"}},{q="Why does this need further study?",a="The matter is called epic, and what has been seen so far makes further study seem worthwhile. No further details are given.",al={"why study it","what's the big deal"}}},kw={"for further study","hall of explorers","historian karnik","ironforge","library","whelgar relic package"},rel={"subzone:hall-of-explorers","subzone:library-ironforge","zone:ironforge"},m={id=98313},src={"In-game quest text: For Further Study"},fv=true}
E["quest:98319"] = {t="quest",n="Secure the Mountain",z="general",s="You face a growing danger in Dun Morogh: wendigos have overrun the Grizzled Den, and Whitebeard's report is missing. Cornelius entered the cavern while his partner waited outside.",h="You face a growing danger in Dun Morogh: wendigos have overrun the Grizzled Den, and Whitebeard's report is...",sec={{t="A Den Overrun",b="Wendigos are multiplying across the cold mountain slopes and crowding into the Grizzled Den to the north. Dwarven scouts already contend with Frostmane trolls. Now savage beasts make the crags perilous for patrols and couriers.",sp=0},{t="Reckless Valor",b="Cornelius charged into the Grizzled Den to secure Whitebeard's vital report. His partner waits outside, seeking help to find the scout and thin the ferocious wendigos in the cave.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Cornelius?",a="Cornelius is a scout whose partner waits outside the Grizzled Den. He charged into the wendigo-filled cavern to secure Whitebeard's report, and his partner needs help finding him.",al={"who is cornelius","cornelius dwarf","where is cornelius"}},{q="What is the Grizzled Den?",a="The Grizzled Den is a cavern north of the mountaineers' post. Wendigos that usually keep to deep mountain hollows have overrun it in large numbers.",al={"what is grizzled den","where are wendigos","wendigo cave dun morogh"}},{q="Why are the wendigos spreading?",a="Wendigos have begun multiplying and spreading rapidly across the snowy mountain crags. Their numbers add to the danger scouts already face from local Frostmane trolls.",al={"why wendigos spreading","wendigo problem dun morogh","wendigos like bunnies"}},{q="Who is Whitebeard and what is his report?",a="Whitebeard awaits an essential report on mountain conditions. Cornelius went into the Grizzled Den to secure it.",al={"who is whitebeard","whitebeard report","senir whitebeard"}},{q="What is the goal of Secure the Mountain?",a="You enter the Grizzled Den to find Cornelius and thin out the wendigos overrunning the cave.",al={"what is secure the mountain","secure the mountain objective"}},{q="How do the trolls factor into the mountain's troubles?",a="Frostmane trolls already threaten scouts in the peaks. The sudden surge in wendigos adds another danger for patrols on the mountain passes.",al={"trolls dun morogh","troll threat"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, reclaiming the Grizzled Den helps protect mountaineers and travelers from wendigos encroaching on Dun Morogh's paths.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you help get Whitebeard the intelligence he needs to maintain border security against threats in the mountains."},kw={"cornelius","dun morogh","grizzled den","mountaineer","secure the mountain","troll","wendigo","whitebeard"},rel={"subzone:grizzled-den","quest:313","topic:troll"},m={id=98319},src={"In-game quest text: Secure the Mountain"},fv=true}
E["quest:98321"] = {t="quest",n="Flintfire's Shipment",z="general",s="Tognus Flintfire in Kharanos is facing a severe supply shortage after savage wendigos overran his primary source of ore at the Grizzled Den. With the miners forced to abandon their crates during the evacuation, adventurers are tasked with recovering the lost shipments to keep the local forges running.",h="Tognus Flintfire in Kharanos is facing a severe supply shortage after savage wendigos overran his primary...",sec={{t="A Supply Crisis in Kharanos",b="In the snowy settlement of Kharanos, Tognus Flintfire faces a critical supply crisis at his forge. The region's most productive and dependable source of ore has suddenly fallen silent after savage wendigos invaded the dig site. Caught off guard by the ferocious predators, the local miners were forced to abandon their posts on a moment's notice, fleeing for their lives and leaving behind freshly packed crates of valuable ore scattered across the cavern floor.",sp=0},{t="Venturing into the Grizzled Den",b="The overrun mine, known as the Grizzled Den, lies to the west of Kharanos. Although Tognus is thankful that every miner managed to escape the ferocious beasts in one piece, the work stoppage threatens to halt local blacksmithing entirely. Rather than waiting for an armed expedition to clear the cave, Tognus seeks daring adventurers to slip into the wendigo lair, gather the stranded crates, and bring the vital ore back to the forge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tognus Flintfire?",a="Tognus Flintfire is a dwarf in Kharanos who relies on steady shipments of mined ore to keep his forge operational. Facing a severe supply shortage after wendigos overran his primary mining cave, he enlists travelers to brave the danger and retrieve the abandoned crates.",al={"who is tongus flintfire","tongus flintfire","quest giver"}},{q="Where is the Grizzled Den located?",a="The Grizzled Den is a cave system situated to the west of Kharanos in Dun Morogh. Formerly a reliable mining site, it now serves as a den for savage wendigos, forcing anyone looking for the lost ore to venture inside hostile territory.",al={"where is grizzled den","grizzled den location","where to find shipments"}},{q="Why was the mining cave abandoned?",a="The cave was the local miners' most reliable source of ore until hostile wendigos invaded and overran the site. The workers were forced to drop their equipment and stockpiled shipments in a desperate scramble to evacuate, miraculously making it out alive.",al={"why miners flee","what happened to the mine","wendigo attack"}},{q="What is inside the missing shipments?",a="The shipments consist of extracted ore packed into crates by the miners before they were chased out. Without these materials, local metalworking and forge operations in Kharanos are brought to a grinding halt.",al={"what are flintfire shipments","what is in the crates","shipment contents"}},{q="Did any miners get hurt during the attack?",a="Despite the terrifying ferocity of the wendigos, Tognus notes with relief that all of the miners managed to escape the Grizzled Den in one piece, leaving only their supplies and ore behind.",al={"did miners survive","miner casualties","wendigo victims"}},{q="What happens once the shipments are recovered?",a="Securing the recovered shipments provides Tognus Flintfire with the raw metal stock required to get the local forge fired up and operational again without having to clear the entire infested cavern first.",al={"why need ore","what happens after recovery","forge running"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Tognus Flintfire specifically welcomes gnome adventurers willing to do hard physical labor in Dun Morogh, showing genuine appreciation for their grit.",["profession:Mining"]="The lost shipments consist of vital ore left behind by miners who evacuated their primary dig site when wendigos attacked.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Recovering Flintfire's shipments is crucial to keeping the local forges in Kharanos supplied with workable metal."},kw={"dun morogh","flintfire","forge","grizzled den","kharanos","miners","ore","shipments","tongus flintfire","wendigo"},rel={"subzone:kharanos","subzone:grizzled-den","quest:313","topic:gnome"},m={id=98321},src={"In-game quest text: Flintfire's Shipment"},fv=true}
E["quest:98322"] = {t="quest",n="Secure the Mountain",z="general",s="Secure the Mountain is an Alliance quest originating in Kharanos, Dun Morogh. A local official compiling intelligence for Ironforge sends adventurers west of town to check on Mountaineer Gretchen after scouts investigating aggressive wendigos fail to report back.",h="Secure the Mountain is an Alliance quest originating in Kharanos, Dun Morogh.",sec={{t="An Overdue Report",b="Tasked with compiling local military intelligence for the high seat of Ironforge, an official in Kharanos finds his routine correspondence interrupted. A mountaineer team sent to assess nearby wendigo activity has failed to return with updates. Rather than spelunking through icy caverns himself, the bureaucrat enlists capable travelers to venture just west of town and determine the scouts' status.",sp=0},{t="Trouble on the Western Ridge",b="Upon tracking down Mountaineer Gretchen west of Kharanos, the quiet routine of regional surveillance gives way to alarm. Gretchen confirms that Whitebeard's inquiries reached her, but reveals that the wendigo disturbance in the surrounding mountain crags is far more volatile than initial dispatches suggested, setting the stage for deeper reconnaissance into the nearby wilds.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Secure the Mountain?",a="The quest is given by Senir Whitebeard in Kharanos, referred to by Mountaineer Gretchen as 'Ol' Whitebeard.' His duty involves compiling official reports on local wilderness threats and dispatching them to the dwarven leadership in Ironforge.",al={"who gives secure the mountain","secure the mountain questgiver","who starts secure the mountain"}},{q="Where can Mountaineer Gretchen be found?",a="Mountaineer Gretchen is stationed just west of the town of Kharanos in Dun Morogh, watching over the local wendigo threat near the mountainsides and nearby caves.",al={"where is gretchen","mountaineer gretchen location","find mountaineer gretchen"}},{q="Why is the questgiver looking for Mountaineer Gretchen?",a="The questgiver is responsible for sending intelligence back to Ironforge, but the scout dispatched to investigate local wendigo activity failed to report back in time. Running behind schedule and avoiding dangerous cave exploration, he asks a passing adventurer to check in on Mountaineer Gretchen.",al={"why send adventurer","why does whitebeard need help","why check on gretchen"}},{q="What wendigo problem is threatening the area?",a="Wendigos inhabit the rocky foothills and dark caves surrounding Kharanos. Their aggressive presence poses a direct threat to travelers and local outposts, prompting Ironforge scouts to monitor their movements closely.",al={"wendigo threat kharanos","what are wendigos doing","why wendigo problem"}},{q="What does Mountaineer Gretchen report upon arrival?",a="Upon reaching Mountaineer Gretchen west of town, she confirms receipt of Whitebeard's inquiries and grimly notes that the entire situation regarding the nearby wendigos has become a serious mess.",al={"what does gretchen say","secure the mountain turn in","gretchen report status"}},{q="What role do mountaineers play around Kharanos?",a="Mountaineers serve as the primary border guards, trackers, and scout forces of the Ironforge dwarves, surveying remote passes, dark caves, and snowy trails across Dun Morogh to protect towns like Kharanos.",al={"who are mountaineers","ironforge mountaineer patrol","what do mountaineers do"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dun Morogh's mountaineers maintain vigilant patrols across snowy ridges and perilous caves, making reliable reconnaissance vital to keeping dwarven settlements safe.",["faction:Alliance"]="Assisting local dwarven patrols around Kharanos reinforces defensive coordination throughout the snowy heart of Khaz Modan."},kw={"dun morogh","dwarf","ironforge","kharanos","mountaineer gretchen","secure the mountain","wendigo","whitebeard"},rel={"subzone:kharanos","zone:ironforge"},m={id=98322},src={"In-game quest text: Secure the Mountain"},fv=true}
E["quest:98323"] = {t="quest",n="Secure the Mountain",z="general",s="Secure the Mountain is a scouting report delivered on behalf of Mountaineer Gretchen to Senir Whitebeard in Kharanos. After an expedition into a nearby cave proves catastrophic, Gretchen determines the cavern is a lost cause and refuses to send any more scouts into the overrun death trap. The mission highlights the ongoing struggle of Ironforge's mountaineers to keep Dun Morogh safe amidst rising threats.",h="Secure the Mountain is a scouting report delivered on behalf of Mountaineer Gretchen to Senir Whitebeard in...",sec={{t="A Grim Report",b="Stationed out in the freezing peaks of Dun Morogh, Mountaineer Gretchen stands watch with orders to ensure the high passes remain safe. However, a recent foray into a nearby cavern reveals that the wilderness is far from pacified. Having assessed the situation, Gretchen concludes that the cave has become a complete death trap, overrun by hostile mongrels. Rather than risk further lives, she remains at her post on the slopes, determined to hold the line while dispatching an emissary to deliver her written assessment.",sp=0},{t="Losses in the Depths",b="The decision to abandon further incursions into the cavern follows a costly sacrifice. Among the casualties of the failed scouting effort was Cornelius, whose loss weighs heavily on Mountaineer Gretchen. Conceding that the cavern cannot be reclaimed under current conditions, she orders a halt to all further exploratory missions into the subterranean tunnels. Her priority shifts from offensive clearing to defensive containment, refusing to needlessly sacrifice any more scouts to the vicious creatures lurking beneath the snowy crags.",sp=1},{t="Senir Whitebeard's Dossier",b="In the bustling settlement of Kharanos, Senir Whitebeard tracks the numerous dangers encroaching on the dwarven heartland. Upon receiving Gretchen's grim assessment, Whitebeard shows little surprise at the news that the cavern is completely overrun. Resigned to the grim reality facing Dun Morogh, he quietly folds Gretchen's letter into his growing stack of field reports. The intelligence serves as another sobering piece of documentation in Ironforge's broader effort to catalog and manage the volatile frontier.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Mountaineer Gretchen?",a="Mountaineer Gretchen is an Ironforge sentry stationed out in the snowy wilderness of Dun Morogh. Dedicated to her duty, she refuses to abandon her post on the mountain until the surrounding territory is deemed secure. Following a deadly scouting mission into a nearby cave, she writes an official field report detailing the heavy enemy presence and dispatches it to Kharanos.",al={"gretchen","mountaineer gretchen lore","who is gretchen"}},{q="Who is Senir Whitebeard?",a="Senir Whitebeard is a dwarven authority stationed in the town of Kharanos. He acts as an intelligence gatherer and coordinator for local security operations across Dun Morogh, receiving dispatches and scouting assessments from mountaineers in the field. When informed of dangerous enemy incursions or lost caverns, he catalogs the reports into his official records to assess the regional threat level.",al={"senir whitebeard","whitebeard kharanos","who is senir"}},{q="Who was Cornelius?",a="Cornelius was an associate or fellow scout involved in the exploration of the infested cavern on the mountain. Mountaineer Gretchen mentions his tragic fate with regret, indicating that he was lost during the ill-fated reconnaissance effort. His demise directly influenced Gretchen's decision to deem the cavern a lethal death trap and forbid any further lives from being squandered inside.",al={"cornelius","who is cornelius","what happened to cornelius","cornelius secure the mountain"}},{q="What happened in the mountain cave?",a="An exploration of the cavern proved disastrous when scouts discovered that the tunnels were completely overrun by hostile mongrels. The mission resulted in the tragic loss of Cornelius. Following this grim discovery, Mountaineer Gretchen declared the subterranean passage to be a lost cause and determined that sending more personnel inside would be suicidal, opting instead to seal off or avoid the area.",al={"what is in the cave","why is the cave a death trap","cave lost cause"}},{q="Why does Gretchen refuse to leave her post?",a="Mountaineer Gretchen takes her oath to protect Dun Morogh seriously and firmly declares that she will not abandon her position until the mountain itself is secure. Although she recognizes that the nearby cavern is too dangerous to retake, she remains on the snowy heights to guard the approach and keep watch while sending word of the threat back to Kharanos.",al={"why is gretchen staying","gretchen post","why stay on the mountain"}},{q="How does Senir Whitebeard react to Gretchen's report?",a="Senir Whitebeard is hardly surprised to learn that the cavern is entirely overrun, suggesting that such hostile infestations have become an all too common problem across Dun Morogh. Rather than expressing shock, he stoically accepts the bad news and folds Gretchen's written dispatch into his growing pile of notes, keeping a meticulous record of the hazards menacing the realm.",al={"senir reaction","whitebeard report reaction","what does senir do"}},{q="What role do the Mountaineers play in Dun Morogh?",a="The Mountaineers serve as the vigilant frontier scouts and border guards of Ironforge, patrolling the treacherous snow-covered peaks, high trails, and mountain passes of Dun Morogh. Stationed far from the warmth of the capital, these hardy dwarves monitor hostile beast movements, scout out caverns, and report critical intelligence back to local hubs like Kharanos to protect travelers and settlements.",al={"ironforge mountaineers","what are mountaineers","dwarf mountaineers lore"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dun Morogh is the ancestral mountain home of the Bronzebeard dwarves, making Mountaineer Gretchen's stubborn vigil a vital effort to protect her kin from surrounding perils.",["faction:Alliance"]="Securing the high passes around Ironforge is essential for preserving safe travel and defense across the Alliance's snowy stronghold in Khaz Modan."},kw={"cave","cornelius","dun morogh","kharanos","mountaineer gretchen","mountaineer's report","secure the mountain","senir whitebeard"},rel={"npc:senir-whitebeard","subzone:kharanos"},m={id=98323},src={"In-game quest text: Secure the Mountain"},fv=true}
E["quest:98326"] = {t="quest",n="Frosthowl",z="general",s="Frosthowl is a quest in Dun Morogh tasking adventurers with recovering a stolen sack of fish from a thieving wendigo. The beast ambushed a local dwarven fisher and fled into the depths of the Grizzled Den.",h="Frosthowl is a quest in Dun Morogh tasking adventurers with recovering a stolen sack of fish from a thieving...",sec={{t="A Stolen Haul",b="Ice fishing across the freezing waters of Dun Morogh is grueling, bitter labor. A dwarven fisher and her husband, Paxton, spent days out on the frozen lake braving biting winds just to harvest a single sack of fish. Their hard work was abruptly undone when Frosthowl, a menacing and thunderous wendigo, ambushed the camp and made off with their entire haul into the snowy wilderness.",sp=0},{t="The Loudmouth of the Grizzled Den",b="Frosthowl is not just an ordinary scavenger; the beast is notorious for its booming roars that rattle the ice and scare off the lake's sparse fish. After snatching the fisher's supply sack, the drooling creature retreated deep into the dark tunnels of the Grizzled Den to the southeast, leaving the stranded fishers desperate for someone to confront the monster and retrieve their stolen goods.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Frosthowl?",a="Frosthowl is a loud, aggressive wendigo lurking in Dun Morogh. Known for roaring and terrorizing the frozen waterways, the beast recently attacked a local dwarven fisher and made off with her husband's hard-earned catch.",al={"who is frosthowl","what is frosthowl","frosthowl wendigo"}},{q="Where can Frosthowl be found?",a="Frosthowl has taken refuge deep within the Grizzled Den, a cave system located to the southeast of the frozen lake where Paxton fishes.",al={"where is frosthowl","frosthowl location","where is the wendigo hiding"}},{q="What did Frosthowl steal?",a="Frosthowl ambushed a dwarven fisher and stole a full sack of fish that her husband, Paxton, had spent days catching on the frozen lake. Adventurers are asked to hunt down the beast and recover the stolen haul.",al={"what did frosthowl steal","why kill frosthowl","what did the wendigo take"}},{q="Who is Paxton?",a="Paxton is a dwarven fisherman who spent days out on the frozen lake trying to bring in a modest catch. His wife tasks passing travelers with tracking down Frosthowl after the beast stole their only sack of fish.",al={"who is paxton","paxton dun morogh","paxton fisher"}},{q="What is the Grizzled Den?",a="The Grizzled Den is a known wendigo lair situated to the southeast of the frozen lake in Dun Morogh, serving as a dark retreat for beasts like Frosthowl to hoard scavenged meat and stolen supplies.",al={"what is the grizzled den","grizzled den wendigo cave","where do wendigos live in dun morogh"}},{q="Why is the beast named Frosthowl?",a="The wendigo is referred to as a loudmouth whose thunderous roars and howls echo across the snowy plains, threatening to scare away the fish and terrifying anyone working the frozen lakes.",al={"how did frosthowl get its name","why called frosthowl","loudmouth wendigo"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Hardy dwarven fishers endure biting cold across Dun Morogh's frozen lakes, making predatory wendigo attacks an ever-present hazard to local homesteaders and livelihoods.",["faction:Alliance"]="Keeping the icy paths and fishing spots near Brewnall and Kharanos safe from aggressive predators falls squarely on local Alliance adventurers."},kw={"dun morogh","frosthowl","frozen lake","grizzled den","ice fishing","paxton","stolen fish","wendigo"},rel={"subzone:grizzled-den","quest:313"},m={id=98326},src={"In-game quest text: Frosthowl"},fv=true}
E["quest:98341"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Windborne Cat Spirit",z="general",s="In Moonglade, the Avatar of Saeyleenan guides young shen'dorei druids through a spiritual trial. The spirit comes from Zephras and helps them embody the windborne feline form of their homeland.",h="In Moonglade, the Avatar of Saeyleenan guides young shen'dorei druids through a spiritual trial.",sec={{t="A Visitor from Zephras",b="South of Moonglade's twin bridges, a spirit from distant Zephras has appeared in the sacred sanctuary. Calling itself the Avatar of Saeyleenan, this windborne cat spirit has crossed into Azeroth to guide young shen'dorei druids ready to unlock their feline form.",sp=0},{t="The Nature of the Windborne Form",b="Druids seek wisdom in the feline form, but those from Zephras follow a different tradition from Azeroth's native peoples. The shen'dorei embody the windborne predator of their homeland instead of an Azerothian wildcat. So Saeyleenan oversees their trial in Moonglade, to teach them the spirit's nature.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Great Windborne Cat Spirit?",a="The windborne cat spirit appears as the Avatar of Saeyleenan, from distant Zephras. It has come to Moonglade to mentor and test young shen'dorei druids seeking its feline shape.",al={"who is the windborne cat spirit","what is avatar of saeyleenan","saeyleenan lore"}},{q="Where can the Avatar of Saeyleenan be found?",a="The Avatar of Saeyleenan appears in southern Moonglade, just south of the twin bridges.",al={"where to find cat spirit moonglade","avatar of saeyleenan location","windborne cat location"}},{q="Why do Zephras druids commune with a different cat spirit?",a="Shen'dorei druids come from Zephras, so their feline shape reflects the windborne spirits of their homeland rather than Azeroth's native big cats. The spirit oversees their trial to ensure they embody its essence.",al={"why does cat form look different","zephras cat form difference","why not azeroth cat spirit"}},{q="What does the trial of the windborne cat entail?",a="The Avatar of Saeyleenan has the initiate undertake the trial of Azeroth's Great Cat Spirit. The Avatar supervises and confirms that the druid understands the windborne spirit's unique nature.",al={"what trial does the cat spirit give","cat spirit trial zephras","how to get windborne cat form"}},{q="What does shen'dorei refer to?",a="The Avatar of Saeyleenan calls the initiate shen'dorei, marking their origin in Zephras and distinguishing their heritage and spiritual practices from Azeroth's native peoples.",al={"who are shendorei","shen dorei druids","what does shendorei mean"}},{q="What is Zephras?",a="Zephras is the distant homeland of the shen'dorei. Its native flora, fauna, and spirits differ from Azeroth's, giving rise to unique shapeshifting forms among its druids.",al={"what is zephras","where is zephras located","zephras homeland"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid from Zephras, you commune with the windborne cat spirit to master your feline form. The rite follows your heritage rather than Azeroth's traditional path.",["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, you are called shen'dorei. The spirits of your homeland, Zephras, guide your druidic path."},kw={"cat form","cat spirit","druid trial","moonglade","saeyleenan","shen'dorei","windborne cat","zephras"},rel={"quest:98393","quest:5921"},m={id=98341},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Windborne Cat Spirit"},fv=true}
E["quest:98372"] = {t="quest",n="An Unfortunate End",z="general",s="In Duskwood, a Night Watchman lies dead in the gloom. A beast mauled him, and the unlit brand in his hand may help you see what the darkness hides.",h="In Duskwood, a Night Watchman lies dead in the gloom.",sec={{t="A Grim Discovery",b="A Night Watch patrolman lies dead on Duskwood's dark soil. Deep gashes and cuts mark his body. A great beast caught him before he could escape.",sp=0},{t="Light in the Darkness",b="An unlit torch remains in the fallen guard's rigid hand. Take it, and its light can reveal what hides in Duskwood's gloom and along other dark paths.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Whose body is this?",a="The fallen body belongs to a Night Watch guard, who patrols Duskwood's dark roads and borders. He died alone in the shadows.",al={"who is the dead body","who died here","who is the dead night watchman"}},{q="What killed the watchman?",a="A large beast mauled the watchman, leaving deep gashes and savage cuts. It caught him before he could defend himself or light his way.",al={"what killed the night watchman","how did the watchman die","what attacked him"}},{q="Why is the torch important?",a="The unlit torch from the guard's hand can light the gloom. Its glow may reveal what the darkness hides in Duskwood and beyond.",al={"what is the torch for","what do i do with the torch","torch uses duskwood"}}},kw={"an unfortunate end","beast","duskwood","mauled","night watchman","torch"},rel={"zone:duskwood"},m={id=98372},src={"In-game quest text: An Unfortunate End"},fv=true}
E["quest:98391"] = {t="quest",n="The Sisterhood of Elune",z="general",s="The Sisterhood of Elune is an introductory quest directing adventurers to Darnassus to meet Sister Aquinne. Serving as the primary spiritual authority among the night elves, the Sisterhood requires swift outside aid to confront brewing challenges in their lands.",h="The Sisterhood of Elune is an introductory quest directing adventurers to Darnassus to meet Sister Aquinne.",sec={{t="A Call to the Capital",b="\"The Sisterhood of Elune\" is a summons directing adventurers to seek out Sister Aquinne in the night elf capital of Darnassus. As representatives of Elune's faithful, the priestesses often dispatch calls for reliable champions to assist with urgent matters affecting their people and sacred lands.",sp=0},{t="Reporting to Sister Aquinne",b="Upon arriving in Darnassus and locating Sister Aquinne, she expresses profound relief at your prompt arrival. Her welcome marks the start of direct service to the Sisterhood, signaling that the priestesses face mounting demands requiring trustworthy hands from outside their cloistered ranks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sister Aquinne?",a="Sister Aquinne is an emissary and priestess serving the Sisterhood of Elune within the capital city of Darnassus. She receives travelers sent to assist the priesthood with urgent matters confronting the night elves.",al={"who is sister aquinne","aquinne darnassus","where is aquinne"}},{q="What is the Sisterhood of Elune?",a="The Sisterhood of Elune is the ancient order of night elf priestesses devoted to the moon goddess Elune. Revered as spiritual guides, healers, and leaders of kaldorei culture, they oversee the spiritual welfare and defense of their people.",al={"what is sisterhood of elune","who are the sisters of elune","night elf priestesses"}},{q="Where do I need to go to complete this quest?",a="You must travel to Darnassus, the great capital of the night elves atop Teldrassil. Sister Aquinne awaits your arrival within the city to deliver her briefing on behalf of the Sisterhood.",al={"where to turn in sisterhood of elune","sisterhood quest location","where do i go for sisterhood of elune"}},{q="Why does the Sisterhood require aid?",a="Sister Aquinne indicates that the Sisterhood requires prompt outside assistance to handle pressing troubles confronting Darnassus and the surrounding night elf lands. Speaking with her unlocks further duties on their behalf.",al={"why does the sisterhood need help","sisterhood trouble","what aid does sisterhood require"}},{q="Can Horde characters undertake this quest?",a="No. The Sisterhood of Elune is central to night elf leadership and aligned strictly with the Alliance. Darnassus is an Alliance capital, making this quest exclusive to Alliance adventurers.",al={"do horde get this quest","can horde do sisterhood of elune"}},{q="Who leads the Sisterhood of Elune?",a="High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind stands as the spiritual head of the Sisterhood of Elune and the supreme leader of the kaldorei nation, guiding her sisters from the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus.",al={"who leads the sisterhood of elune","sisterhood leader","tyrande sisterhood"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Sisterhood of Elune is the spiritual heart of night elf society and leadership, making answering their summons a sacred duty for any child of the stars.",["class:Priest"]="Priesthood in kaldorei culture is synonymous with devotion to Elune, and answering Sister Aquinne connects directly to the high traditions of the temple.",["faction:Alliance"]="With Darnassus standing as a vital capital of the Alliance in Kalimdor, assisting the Sisterhood ensures the stability of night elf spiritual and civic governance."},kw={"aquinne","darnassus","elune","priestess","sister aquinne","sisterhood of elune","teldrassil","the sisterhood of elune"},rel={"npc:sister-aquinne","zone:darnassus"},m={id=98391},src={"In-game quest text: The Sisterhood of Elune"},fv=true}
E["quest:98392"] = {t="quest",n="Darkness in the Glade",z="general",s="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak investigates tainted harpy belts in the Oracle Glade. Their amulets may show what is spreading through Teldrassil.",h="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak investigates tainted harpy belts in the Oracle Glade.",sec={{t="Tainted Fibers",b="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak finds something wrong among the harpies of the Oracle Glade. Their leather belts carry an unnatural taint woven through the fibers. She suspects demonic corruption, not ordinary aggression. Before she warns Darnassus, she needs proof.",sp=0},{t="A Blight Confirmed",b="Arynia sends you to the western nests to face three harpy matriarchs: Hatescreech, Windmistress Gaedress, and Witchmother Arysa. Each carries an amulet marked by her power. The gems hold foul green swirls. They confirm fel moss is warping the harpies and seeping into Teldrassil's sacred boughs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Darkness in the Glade?",a="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak investigates strange taint in harpy belts in the Oracle Glade. She sends you to recover amulets from the harpy leaders and learn what is behind the affliction.",al={"what is darkness in the glade","darkness in the glade quest"}},{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak asks for your help. She watches over Teldrassil's boughs from the Oracle Glade and investigates strange changes among its harpies.",al={"who gives darkness in the glade","quest giver oracle glade"}},{q="Why does Arynia want amulets from the harpy leaders?",a="Arynia found tainted fibers in belts taken from common harpies and needs more proof. She suspects the leaders' amulets may reveal the blight's nature and origin.",al={"why kill harpy leaders","why get amulets","harpy amulets reason"}},{q="Which harpy leaders hold the amulets?",a="The amulets belong to three leaders in western nests: Hatescreech, Windmistress Gaedress, and Witchmother Arysa.",al={"which harpies have amulets","harpy leaders oracle glade"}},{q="What is causing the corruption in the glade?",a="Fel moss is causing the corruption. Arynia finds foul green swirls in the recovered amulets' gemstones, proof that fel moss is tainting the region.",al={"what is corrupting the harpies","what is the green swirl","origin of glade darkness"}},{q="Has this corruption appeared anywhere else?",a="Yes. Arynia has heard reports of fel moss turning the land against the night elves in Shadowglen and other boughs across the world tree.",al={"fel moss elsewhere","is shadowglen corrupted"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Fel corruption in sacred boughs like the Oracle Glade threatens the kaldorei effort to nurture and protect Teldrassil.",["faction:Alliance"]="Sentinel Cloudsbreak serves Darnassus through its vigilance against demonic corruption threatening Alliance lands.",["class:Druid"]="Fel moss among wildlife and leather fibers points to an unnatural blight upsetting the World Tree's natural balance."},kw={"arynia cloudsbreak","arysa","darkness in the glade","fel moss","gaedress","harpies","hatescreech","oracle glade","teldrassil"},rel={"npc:sentinel-arynia-cloudsbreak","subzone:oracle-glade","item:fel-moss","topic:harpy","subzone:shadowglen"},m={id=98392},src={"In-game quest text: Darkness in the Glade"},fv=true}
E["quest:98393"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Cat Spirit",z="general",s="In Nighthaven, druids gather at Dendrite Starblaze's call to learn the cat's ways. They have mastered the bear and now turn to another aspect of the wild under Father Cenarius's blessing.",h="In Nighthaven, druids gather at Dendrite Starblaze's call to learn the cat's ways.",sec={{t="The Summons to Moonglade",b="Druids who have mastered the enduring bear are summoned to Moonglade again. Elder Dendrite Starblaze calls them to Nighthaven, where they can deepen their communion with nature. Their spiritual gifts carry them across the distance to this haven of Cenarion wisdom.",sp=0},{t="The Perspective of the Cat",b="When you reach Nighthaven, Dendrite Starblaze invokes Father Cenarius and greets you with a faint aura of power. He says it is time to embrace the cat's perspective: swift, agile, and predatory. As before, Dendrite opens the path to understanding, while the work of embodying and spreading the feline spirit rests with you.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends for you in The Great Cat Spirit?",a="Your druid trainers send you on behalf of Dendrite Starblaze, an elder mentor in Nighthaven, Moonglade's secluded haven.",al={"who gives the great cat spirit quest","dendrite starblaze summons"}},{q="Where can Dendrite Starblaze be found?",a="Dendrite Starblaze lives in Nighthaven, Moonglade's central settlement. Druids can reach it by teleportation.",al={"where is dendrite starblaze","dendrite starblaze location"}},{q="What is the purpose of meeting Dendrite Starblaze?",a="Dendrite prepares you to understand and commune with nature's feline aspect, after you have learned the bear's ways.",al={"what does dendrite teach","cat form training quest"}},{q="Why does Dendrite invoke Father Cenarius?",a="Dendrite invokes Father Cenarius to honor the demigod and patron of druidism, who taught mortals the secrets of nature and shapeshifting.",al={"why father cenarius","cenarius mention dendrite"}},{q="How do druids travel to Moonglade for this summons?",a="Druids use their spiritual bond with nature to teleport to Moonglade when their mentors call.",al={"how to get to moonglade","teleport to moonglade druid"}},{q="How does this aspect relate to the bear aspect?",a="Druidic teaching approaches nature through different aspects. You first master the bear's enduring strength, then advance to the cat's swift predator's instincts.",al={"cat form vs bear form lore","bear form first"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you take this rite of passage to extend your mastery of the wild beyond the bear.",["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, you answer Cenarius's ancient teachings on Moonglade, ancestral ground for kaldorei druids.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you travel freely to Nighthaven with your night elf brethren under the Cenarion Circle's shared guidance."},kw={"cat aspect","cenarius","dendrite starblaze","druid training","moonglade","nighthaven","the great cat spirit"},rel={"npc:dendrite-starblaze","subzone:nighthaven","quest:5921","topic:druid","topic:cenarius"},m={id=98393},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Cat Spirit"},fv=true}
E["quest:98398"] = {t="quest",n="The Oracle Tree",z="general",s="A fallen piece of bark draws travelers to the Oracle Tree, where an ancient voice reaches the mind. Its message calls those who have finished their first duties toward greater work.",h="A fallen piece of bark draws travelers to the Oracle Tree, where an ancient voice reaches the mind.",sec={{t="A Call to Greater Duties",b="The Oracle Tree watches its surroundings and guides travelers who have proved their mettle. When it needs an agent in the world, it deliberately sheds a piece of bark nearby. Those who notice are called to step forward, for their first duties are finished and greater responsibilities await.",sp=0},{t="Communion Through the Bark",b="The Oracle Tree reaches the mind through its fallen bark rather than speaking aloud. Examine the piece, and its calm presence washes over you. Its thoughts and desires come like a soft breeze through a canopy of leaves.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Oracle Tree?",a="The Oracle Tree is an ancient, sentient tree that can communicate telepathically with those nearby. When it has a message or duty to give, it sheds a piece of bark to draw worthy travelers’ attention.",al={"what is oracle tree","oracle tree lore"}},{q="How does the Oracle Tree communicate?",a="The Oracle Tree sends its thoughts telepathically instead of speaking aloud. Inspect the fallen bark, and its calm voice enters your mind like a gentle breeze through leaves.",al={"how does oracle tree speak","oracle tree communication"}},{q="Why do you need to find the Oracle Tree's bark?",a="The Oracle Tree sheds bark when it has a duty that needs tending. Inspecting the piece lets the tree send its guidance directly to your mind.",al={"why find bark","fallen bark purpose"}},{q="Who directs you to the Oracle Tree?",a="A local overseer or caretaker directs you to the Oracle Tree. They have watched your accomplishments, see that greater duties await, and notice the freshly shed bark.",al={"who sends you to oracle tree","quest giver oracle tree"}},{q="What is the purpose of the quest The Oracle Tree?",a="You make contact with the Oracle Tree by seeking out its bark. Inspecting it opens your mind to the tree’s message and the greater duty it needs completed.",al={"oracle tree quest goal","what does oracle tree want"}},{q="Where is the Oracle Tree bark found?",a="The Oracle Tree’s shed bark lies on the ground nearby, waiting for someone attuned enough to hear its message.",al={"where find oracle bark","bark location"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you are attuned to sentient plant life and the voices of ancient trees. Communion with the Oracle Tree extends your reverence for nature."},kw={"oracle tree","oracle tree bark","sentient tree","shed bark","telepathy","the oracle tree"},m={id=98398},src={"In-game quest text: The Oracle Tree"},fv=true}
E["quest:98403"] = {t="quest",n="Twisted Hatred",z="general",s="Twisted Hatred is a quest in Teldrassil uncovering the puppeteer behind Lord Melenas's actions. Adventurers journey into the Cleft northwest of Dolanaar to confront Xethorr the Wicked and eradicate his corrupted spiders before his vile schemes can spread across the boughs.",h="Twisted Hatred is a quest in Teldrassil uncovering the puppeteer behind Lord Melenas's actions.",sec={{t="A Shadowed Handler",b="The discovery that Lord Melenas did not operate in isolation reveals a darker undercurrent running through Teldrassil's wilderness. Behind Melenas stood Xethorr the Wicked, an entrenched satyr pulling strings from the shadows of the forest to undermine the tranquil boughs of the World Tree.",sp=0},{t="The Befouled Brood of the Cleft",b="Taking refuge northwest of Dolanaar in the secluded enclave called the Cleft, Xethorr turned his attention to Teldrassil's native arachnids. By twisting local Webwood spiders into befouled beasts, the satyr sought to weaponize their venom for insidious demonic plots. Tallonkai Swiftroot dispatched adventurers to cull the brood and seize Xethorr's Mature Fel Moss before the corruption could spread further.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Xethorr the Wicked?",a="Xethorr the Wicked is a malevolent satyr orchestrating corruption within Teldrassil. He directed Lord Melenas and made his lair in the Cleft, twisting native spiders and utilizing Mature Fel Moss to harbor his sinister schemes.",al={"who is xethorr","xethorr the wicked","who is the satyr"}},{q="Where is the Cleft located?",a="The Cleft is a secluded area located deep in the forest to the northwest of Dolanaar on Teldrassil, where Xethorr the Wicked and his befouled brood have taken residence.",al={"where is the cleft","cleft location","where do i find xethorr"}},{q="Why are the spiders of Teldrassil befouled?",a="The Befouled Webwoods are native Teldrassil spiders corrupted by Xethorr the Wicked. The satyr twisted their minds and physiology, intending to harvest their venom for a foul demonic plan.",al={"why did spiders change","what are befouled webwoods","befouled webwood lore"}},{q="Who is Tallonkai Swiftroot?",a="Tallonkai Swiftroot is a night elf guardian stationed in the village of Dolanaar on Teldrassil who tasks adventurers with rooting out demonic subversion in the surrounding woods.",al={"who gave this quest","where is tallonkai","tallonkai swiftroot role"}},{q="What is the Mature Fel Moss?",a="Mature Fel Moss is a corrupting botanical specimen carried by Xethorr the Wicked, sought by Tallonkai Swiftroot to uncover more about the satyr's true motives and ongoing work within Teldrassil.",al={"mature fel moss","why collect fel moss","what is fel moss"}},{q="How was Lord Melenas involved?",a="Lord Melenas was acting not out of isolated malice, but at the explicit behest of Xethorr the Wicked, revealing a coordinated satyr conspiracy operating within Teldrassil.",al={"melenas connection","who is lord melenas","melenas and xethorr"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Teldrassil is the fresh heart of Kaldorei resurgence, making demonic corruption festering among the native wildlife in the Cleft a direct insult and threat to your people.",["class:Druid"]="The perversion of Teldrassil's natural arachnids into befouled brood strikes against the balance of nature, demanding cleansing at the hands of Cenarion defenders.",["faction:Alliance"]="Aiding Tallonkai Swiftroot protects the settlement of Dolanaar from spreading satyr machinations within the Alliance's primary sanctuary on Kalimdor."},kw={"befouled webwood","cleft","dolanaar","lord melenas","mature fel moss","satyr","tallonkai swiftroot","teldrassil","twisted hatred","xethorr the wicked"},rel={"subzone:cleft","subzone:dolanaar","npc:tallonkai-swiftroot","topic:satyr","item:fel-moss","quest:940"},m={id=98403},src={"In-game quest text: Twisted Hatred"},fv=true}
E["quest:98404"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Windborne Cat Spirit",z="general",s="At the Avatar of Saeyleenan, young druids face a trial of cunning and stealth. They enter the nearby Stormrage Barrow Den to recover three relics while avoiding enraged spirits. Passing the rite earns them the blessing to wield cat form.",h="At the Avatar of Saeyleenan, young druids face a trial of cunning and stealth.",sec={{t="The Trial of Cunning and Stealth",b="A seeker must show the nature of a predator before drawing upon the Great Windborne Cat Spirit. The cat calls for cunning and patience, then fierce action when cornered. The spirit tests those who seek its power, asking them to think, move, and strike like wild feline hunters. Only then may an initiate earn the right to take the cat's shape.",sp=0},{t="The Stormrage Barrow Den",b="The trial leads into the Stormrage Barrow Den, just east of the spirit's avatar. Three sacred tokens lie in its halls: the Relic of the Fang, the Relic of the Claw, and the Relic of the Silent Shadow.\n\nEnraged spirits have overrun the den and attack intruders on sight. A seeker cannot rely on brute force alone, but must tread carefully through the darkness to reach the relics.",sp=0},{t="The Avatar of Saeyleenan",b="The Avatar of Saeyleenan is the physical manifestation through which the Great Windborne Cat Spirit speaks to young initiates. Untutored seekers lack a seasoned predator's stealth, so a statue at the barrow den's entrance grants them temporary concealment.\n\nThose who slip past the frenzied spirits and return all three relics show they have learned the predator's ways. The Avatar recognizes their worthiness and grants the blessing to wield cat form.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Great Windborne Cat Spirit?",a="The Great Windborne Cat Spirit guides young druids in mastering feline shapeshifting. Through the Avatar of Saeyleenan, it embodies cunning, stealth, and ferocity. It tests aspirants before granting its form, to ensure they respect the cat's instincts and nature.",al={"windborne cat spirit","who is saeyleenan","cat spirit lore"}},{q="What is the Avatar of Saeyleenan?",a="The Avatar of Saeyleenan speaks for the Great Windborne Cat Spirit and stands west of the Stormrage Barrow Den. It assigns seekers to recover the stolen or hidden relics, then evaluates whether each initiate has shown enough stealth and cunning to earn the feline blessing.",al={"avatar of saeyleenan","who is avatar saeyleenan","saeyleenan npc"}},{q="What relics are required for the trial?",a="The trial calls for the Relic of the Fang, the Relic of the Claw, and the Relic of the Silent Shadow, all found in the Stormrage Barrow Den. Together, they symbolize the cat's physical weapons and elusive stealth, and prove an initiate's reverence for every aspect of the predator.",al={"three relics","relic of the claw","stormrage barrow den relics"}},{q="Where is the Stormrage Barrow Den located?",a="The Stormrage Barrow Den lies just east of the Avatar of Saeyleenan. Its subterranean chambers hold the three sacred relics and test the druids. Initiates must avoid the enraged spirits wandering its dark tunnels.",al={"stormrage barrow den location","where is barrow den","where to find relics"}},{q="Why are enraged spirits guarding the barrow den?",a="The Stormrage Barrow Den is overrun by powerful enraged spirits that attack living intruders on sight. They are hostile threats, not willing guardians. Their presence forces aspiring druids to use cunning and stealth to recover the relics instead of fighting them directly.",al={"why are spirits enraged","enraged spirits barrow den","barrow den guardians"}},{q="How do seekers sneak past the enraged spirits?",a="A stone statue at the Stormrage Barrow Den's entrance grants seekers a temporary blessing of stealth. Young initiates have not yet earned their own cat form or prowling abilities, so the enchantment helps them pass the hostile spirits unseen and secure the relics.",al={"how to stealth barrow den","statue stealth buff","how to avoid enraged spirits"}},{q="What reward or blessing is granted upon completing the trial?",a="When the seeker returns the Relic of the Fang, the Relic of the Claw, and the Relic of the Silent Shadow, the Avatar of Saeyleenan deems them worthy of the Great Windborne Cat Spirit's blessing. The rite shows their understanding of the cat's nature and grants the spiritual approval needed to take the predator's shape.",al={"trial reward","what do you get from cat spirit","cat spirit blessing"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you must show cunning and ferocity before the spirit grants its shapeshifting blessing. The rite teaches you to channel the wild cat's agility, stealth, and predatory instinct.",["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, you can trace the Stormrage Barrow Den's name to one of the most prominent night elven druidic lineages. Its depths connect Kaldorei practitioners to their people's ancient barrow heritage and ancestral spirits."},kw={"avatar of saeyleenan","cat form","druid trial","enraged spirits","relic of the claw","relic of the fang","relic of the silent shadow","saeyleenan","stormrage barrow den","the great windborne cat spirit"},rel={"topic:druid"},m={id=98404},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Windborne Cat Spirit"},fv=true}
E["quest:98423"] = {t="quest",n="The Treaty of Understanding",z="general",s="The Treaty of Understanding is an ancient dwarven tablet detailing the historic peace agreement between the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans following the War of the Three Hammers. Recovered from subterranean depths and brought before King Magni Bronzebeard, the artifact preserves a pivotal moment in dwarven history while highlighting the bitter rift that left the Dark Iron clan excluded from the peace.",h="The Treaty of Understanding is an ancient dwarven tablet detailing the historic peace agreement between the...",sec={{t="A Relic of Dwarven Reconciliation",b="Recovered from deep within an ancient vault, the Treaty of Understanding is a historic stone tablet documenting the formal cessation of hostilities between dwarven clans. Dating to the turbulent aftermath of the War of the Three Hammers, it represents the moment peace was forged between the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans after the Bronzebeards secured control of Ironforge mountain.",sp=0},{t="The Unsigned Peace",b="When presented with the recovered tablet, King Magni Bronzebeard recognizes it as the work of his ancestor, Madoran Bronzebeard, the first Bronzebeard king of Ironforge. While the document bears the signatures of both the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer leaders, it conspicuously lacks the mark of the Dark Iron clan, serving as a lasting testament to the enduring rift between the Dark Irons and their mountain kin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Treaty of Understanding?",a="The Treaty of Understanding is an ancient dwarven stone tablet recovered from deep vaults. It records the formal peace agreement between the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans established directly following the conclusion of the War of the Three Hammers.",al={"what is treaty of understanding","treaty tablet","what is this treaty"}},{q="Who created the treaty?",a="The treaty was drafted in the aftermath of the War of the Three Hammers during the reign of Madoran Bronzebeard, the first Bronzebeard king of Ironforge after the three clans fought for control of the mountain.",al={"who wrote treaty of understanding","who made treaty of understanding","who drafted the treaty"}},{q="Why did the Dark Iron clan not sign the treaty?",a="The Dark Iron clan refused to accept peace after the Bronzebeards took control of Ironforge. Consequently, only the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans signed the agreement, leaving the Dark Irons isolated and hostile.",al={"why didnt dark iron sign","did dark iron sign treaty","dark irons not on treaty"}},{q="Who is interested in the Treaty of Understanding?",a="The tablet is delivered to King Magni Bronzebeard in the High Seat of Ironforge, who values it as an invaluable relic of his ancestor Madoran and dwarven history.",al={"who wants the treaty","who do i give treaty of understanding to","where to turn in treaty"}},{q="What was the War of the Three Hammers?",a="The War of the Three Hammers was a bitter civil war fought between the Bronzebeard, Wildhammer, and Dark Iron dwarf clans for control of Ironforge mountain. The Bronzebeards emerged victorious, claiming the mountain while the other clans were driven out.",al={"what was the war of the three hammers","dwarf civil war","three hammers war"}},{q="Who was Madoran Bronzebeard?",a="Madoran Bronzebeard was the ancestor of King Magni Bronzebeard and the first Bronzebeard king to rule Ironforge following the civil war among the three dwarven clans.",al={"who is madoran bronzebeard","madoran bronzebeard","magni ancestor madoran"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="This stone tablet is a tangible record of dwarven reconciliation, showing how your ancestors put aside their feud after the mountain was contested.",["faction:Alliance"]="The historic peace between Bronzebeard and Wildhammer dwarves formed the foundation of their modern cooperation within the Grand Alliance."},kw={"dark iron","ironforge","madoran bronzebeard","magni bronzebeard","peace treaty","treaty of understanding","war of the three hammers","wildhammer"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","zone:ironforge","topic:dwarf","topic:dark-iron-clan"},m={id=98423},src={"In-game quest text: The Treaty of Understanding"},fv=true}
E["quest:98424"] = {t="quest",n="Fizsprockets Notizen",z="general",s="In the Venture Co. mine, Morin Cloudstalker seeks the notebook pages still missing. They hold important information about the company's schemes in the region.",h="In the Venture Co.",sec={{t="The Missing Pages",b="Morin Cloudstalker waits at Bloodhoof Village for the pages still missing from Fizsprocket’s notebook. You have found folded pages in Fizsprocket’s pocket, and the remaining notes lie in the Venture Co. mine. Their information can guide what comes next.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What pages am I looking for?",a="You are looking for the remaining pages of Overseer Fizsprocket’s notebook.",al={"fizsprocket notes","notebook pages","missing notes"}},{q="Where are the rest of the notes?",a="The remaining pages are in the Venture Co. mine.",al={"where pages","venture co mine","notebook location"}},{q="Who wants the pages?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is waiting for the pages at Bloodhoof Village.",al={"who needs notes","Morin request","bring pages to who"}},{q="What is in Fizsprocket’s notebook?",a="The notebook contains important information about the Venture Co. and its schemes in the region.",al={"what notes say","Fizsprocket notebook","venture co information"}}},kw={"bloodhoof village","fizsprocket","mine","morin cloudstalker","notebook","pages","venture co."},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker"},m={id=98424},src={"In-game quest text: Fizsprockets Notizen"},fv=true}
E["quest:98427"] = {t="quest",n="Ceasing Operations",z="general",s="Morin Cloudstalker calls on you to stop the shredder before it fells another tree. He asks you to gather allies, return to the mine in the northeast, and stop it from cutting more trees.",h="Morin Cloudstalker calls on you to stop the shredder before it fells another tree.",sec={{t="A Threat to Home",b="Morin has heard of the shredder described on a page. Such machines can bring down a forest far faster than axes and muscle. Groves that sheltered the children of the Earth Mother for ages can vanish in days. He asks you to gather allies, return to the mine in the northeast, and make sure the machine fells no more trees. Bring the Mechanoroderschlüssel to Morin at Bloodhoof Village.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Morin asking me to do?",a="Morin asks you to gather allies, return to the mine in the northeast, and stop the shredder from felling more trees.",al={"what does Morin want","Morin request","shredder task"}},{q="Why is the shredder a threat?",a="Morin says shredders can cut down a forest far faster than axes and muscle. They can destroy groves that sheltered the children of the Earth Mother.",al={"why stop shredder","shredder danger","what can it do"}},{q="Where does Morin want me to go?",a="Morin asks you to return to the mine in the northeast.",al={"where is the mine","northeast mine","where return"}},{q="What do I bring back to Morin?",a="Bring the Mechanoroderschlüssel to Morin Cloudstalker at Bloodhoof Village.",al={"what item Morin","Mechanoroderschlüssel","key for Morin"}},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker asks you to stop the shredder from cutting trees near the tauren home.",al={"who is Morin","Morin Cloudstalker","Morin guy"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you share Morin's concern for the groves that sheltered the children of the Earth Mother and for the safety of the young near home."},kw={"bloodhoof village","children of the earth mother","mechanoroderschlüssel","mine","morin cloudstalker","northeast","shredder","trees"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker"},m={id=98427},src={"In-game quest text: Ceasing Operations"},fv=true}
E["quest:98430"] = {t="quest",n="Die Langläufer",z="general",s="At Bleachmane Rock, Perith Stormhoof is trapped after a peace effort with the gnolls turned violent. Cairne Bloodhoof sent the scout, and his safe return matters to the Tauren people.",h="At Bleachmane Rock, Perith Stormhoof is trapped after a peace effort with the gnolls turned violent.",sec={{t="A Scout in Danger",b="Cairne Bloodhoof sent Perith Stormhoof to meet the gnolls settled here. Perith is one of Cairne’s Long Runners, so he cannot speak of the full mission. The attempt at peace turned violent, and he fears the gnolls will eat him if he stays through the night. You are asked to help him escape Bleachmane Rock and report his safety to Cairne in Thunder Bluff.",sp=0},{t="Cairne’s Thanks",b="Cairne is relieved that Perith is safe. He calls the scout his most trusted and says the mission is greatly important. Cairne thanks you for risking your life to save Perith. You have done the Tauren people a greater service than you know.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Perith Stormhoof?",a="Perith Stormhoof is one of Cairne Bloodhoof’s Long Runners, the chieftain’s scouts.",al={"perith who","stormhoof scout","long runner guy"}},{q="Why is Perith at Bleachmane Rock?",a="Cairne sent Perith to meet the gnolls settled there, though Perith cannot discuss the full mission.",al={"why perith there","bleachmane rock","perith mission"}},{q="What happened to the peace effort?",a="The gnolls answered the Long Runners’ attempt at peace with violence, leaving Perith in danger.",al={"gnolls peace","why is perith trapped","peace with gnolls"}},{q="What does Cairne ask me to do?",a="You are asked to help Perith escape Bleachmane Rock and tell Cairne in Thunder Bluff that he is safe.",al={"help perith","stormhoof task","get him out"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a Tauren, you are helping Cairne Bloodhoof’s trusted scout. His mission matters to the Tauren people."},kw={"bleachmane rock","cairne bloodhoof","gnolls","long runners","peace effort","perith stormhoof","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=98430},src={"In-game quest text: Die Langläufer"},fv=true}
E["quest:98435"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorns Bericht",z="general",s="Mull sends you to carry Thunderhorn’s report to Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem in Thunder Bluff. The elders need to hear what your help has accomplished.",h="Mull sends you to carry Thunderhorn’s report to Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem in Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="The Report",b="You bring Thunderhorn’s report to Hamuul on the Elder Rise in eastern Thunder Bluff. The report concerns Mulgore’s springs, which sustain the tauren and the animals they rely on for food, clothing, and companionship.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who needs Thunderhorn’s report?",a="Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem in Thunder Bluff needs Thunderhorn’s report.",al={"who gets the report","report for Hamuul","thunderhorn report"}},{q="Where is Hamuul Runetotem?",a="Hamuul is on the Elder Rise in eastern Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is Hamuul","Hamuul location","find Hamuul"}},{q="Why does the report matter?",a="Mulgore’s springs support the tauren and the animals they rely on for food, clothing, and companionship.",al={"why report matters","what are the springs for","Mulgore springs"}},{q="What does Hamuul say about the springs?",a="Hamuul says the springs sustain both the tauren and the animals they depend on.",al={"Hamuul says what","springs and animals","what did Hamuul say"}}},kw={"elder rise","hamuul runetotem","mulgore","report","springs","tauren","thunder bluff","thunderhorn"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff"},m={id=98435},src={"In-game quest text: Thunderhorns Bericht"},fv=true}
E["quest:98459"] = {t="quest",n="A Lack of Virtue",z="general",s="A Lack of Virtue is a delivery quest involving an enduring petty grudge between two card-playing sailors, Tom Half-fish and Bart Tidewater. Travelers carry a packaged gift across the sea to Menethil Harbor, stepping right into the middle of a mutual cheating accusation over how the Maiden's Virtue was won.",h="A Lack of Virtue is a delivery quest involving an enduring petty grudge between two card-playing sailors, Tom...",sec={{t="A Bitter Grudge",b="A Lack of Virtue centers on an acrimonious rivalry between two salty seafaring gamblers: Tom Half-fish and Bart Tidewater. Tom recruits passing travelers to deliver a packaged parcel across the water to Bart in Menethil Harbor, using the delivery as a petty spiteful jab against his former companion. Along the way, Tom warns that Bart is no true sea dog at all, but rather a former actor who cheated his way through card games to purchase his command.",sp=0},{t="Cards on the Table",b="Upon receiving Tom's Gift on the docks of Menethil Harbor, Bart Tidewater flies into a rage at the mere mention of his old associate. Bart immediately turns the accusation around, swearing that it was Tom who cheated at cards and that Bart only earned enough coin to purchase the Maiden's Virtue after their poker circle banished Half-fish. Despite the explosive temper flare, Bart quickly catches himself, apologizes for barking at the courier, and welcomes the traveler to the harbor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Bart Tidewater?",a="Bart Tidewater is the self-proclaimed captain of the Maiden's Virtue, docked in Menethil Harbor. According to his old associate Tom Half-fish, Bart was originally an actor who bought his ship using winnings from poker games—though both men accuse each other of cheating at cards.",al={"who is bart tidewater","bart tidewater lore","who is captain of the maidens virtue"}},{q="Who is Tom Half-fish?",a="Tom Half-fish is an old acquaintance of Bart Tidewater who gives travelers a gift to carry across the sea to Bart. He harbors a fierce grudge against the captain, alleging that Bart is a fraud, a cheapskate, and a cheater at cards.",al={"who is half-fish","who is tom half-fish","who gives a lack of virtue"}},{q="What is the Maiden's Virtue?",a="The Maiden's Virtue is a vessel docked in Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands. Bart Tidewater claims it is the finest ship in the Great Sea, though Tom Half-fish disparagingly claims the vessel has sailed as far as a rock in Tanaris.",al={"what is the maidens virtue","maidens virtue ship","where is the maidens virtue"}},{q="What is Tom's Gift?",a="Tom's Gift is a package sent by Tom Half-fish to Bart Tidewater in Menethil Harbor. While the exact contents remain unopened to the courier, receiving it immediately provokes an outburst of furious insults from Bart regarding Tom's own poker cheating.",al={"what is toms gift","what is inside toms gift","why does bart get mad at toms gift"}},{q="Who actually cheated at poker?",a="Both sailors vehemently accuse one another of cheating. Tom Half-fish insists Bart is an actor who only won enough poker hands to buy his ship by cheating, whereas Bart claims he legitimately won his vessel only after kicking Tom out of their poker circle for cheating.",al={"who cheated at poker","did bart cheat at poker","did tom cheat at poker"}},{q="How do you deliver the gift to Menethil Harbor?",a="Travelers can reach Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands by taking a ship across the Great Sea. The quest giver notes that a direct boat service stops at the harbor on its second stop.",al={"how do i get to menethil harbor","boat to menethil harbor"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance travelers passing through Menethil Harbor often run into Bart Tidewater barking orders at his crew, making this delivery a quick detour while waiting for transport across the Great Sea."},kw={"a lack of virtue","bart tidewater","maidens virtue","menethil harbor","poker","tanaris","tom half-fish","toms gift","wetlands"},rel={"subzone:menethil-harbor","zone:wetlands","zone:tanaris"},m={id=98459},src={"In-game quest text: A Lack of Virtue"},fv=true}
E["quest:98461"] = {t="quest",n="Unrequited Love",z="general",s="Unrequited Love is an Alliance delivery quest originating in Auberdine on the shores of Darkshore. Hollee asks travelers taking the vessel to Menethil Harbor to deliver a personal note to Tarrel Rockweaver, an excavation classmate for whom she still holds quiet affection.",h="Unrequited Love is an Alliance delivery quest originating in Auberdine on the shores of Darkshore.",sec={{t="A Message Across the Sea",b="In the port town of Auberdine along the misty coast of Darkshore, Hollee watches travelers boarding ships for the Eastern Kingdoms. She seeks aid from passersby to reach an old classmate, Tarrel Rockweaver, with whom she shared years studying the craft of excavation before graduation separated them across continents.",sp=0},{t="Unspoken Feelings",b="While Hollee fondly recalls Tarrel's sharp mind and skill with excavation, her composure falters at the mention of his wife, Merrin. Despite the distance and his marriage, Hollee entrusts an adventurer with a personal letter, asking them to seek out Tarrel near Menethil Harbor to ensure he is safe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the quest Unrequited Love?",a="Hollee gives the quest in the coastal settlement of Auberdine in Darkshore. She asks travelers boarding the boat to Menethil Harbor to deliver a personal note to an old schoolmate.",al={"who gives unrequited love","quest giver auberdine"}},{q="Who is Tarrel Rockweaver?",a="Tarrel Rockweaver is an excavator and former schoolmate of Hollee. He was dispatched to the Wetlands after their studies, where he now works near Menethil Harbor alongside his wife Merrin.",al={"who is tarrel rockweaver","tarrel lore"}},{q="Who is Merrin?",a="Merrin is Tarrel Rockweaver's wife. Hollee mentions her with visible hesitation and a furrowed brow, revealing her lingering, unreciprocated feelings for Tarrel despite his marriage.",al={"who is merrin","tarrels wife"}},{q="Why is the quest called Unrequited Love?",a="The title refers to Hollee's quiet feelings for Tarrel Rockweaver. Although they studied excavation together as close classmates, Tarrel married Merrin, leaving Hollee pining from across the sea in Auberdine.",al={"what is unrequited love quest about","why is it called unrequited love"}},{q="Where is Tarrel Rockweaver located?",a="Tarrel Rockweaver can be found near Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands, across the Great Sea from Darkshore. A passenger boat from Auberdine takes travelers directly to the harbor docks.",al={"where to find tarrel rockweaver","tarrel location wetlands"}},{q="What item is delivered in this quest?",a="You are asked to carry Hollee's Note to Tarrel Rockweaver to check on his well-being in the Wetlands and deliver her personal message.",al={"what do you deliver","hollees letter"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="This delivery connects the Alliance port of Auberdine in Darkshore directly with Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands, linking Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["race:Dwarf"]="Tarrel Rockweaver and Hollee share a background in excavation, a trade central to dwarven tradition and study across Azeroth."},kw={"auberdine","darkshore","excavation","hollee","hollee's note","menethil harbor","merrin","tarrel rockweaver","unrequited love","wetlands"},rel={"npc:tarrel-rockweaver","subzone:auberdine","subzone:menethil-harbor","zone:darkshore","zone:wetlands"},m={id=98461},src={"In-game quest text: Unrequited Love"},fv=true}
E["quest:98512"] = {t="quest",n="Al'Aketh Assassins",z="general",s="A scout asks you to end the Al'Aketh Assassins' deadly threat. Your survival and aid will be recorded for Valanaar.",h="A scout asks you to end the Al'Aketh Assassins' deadly threat.",sec={{t="A Deadly Threat",b="Scouts in the field call the Al'Aketh assassins stone-cold killers. They move together with ruthless purpose, putting nearby scouts and travelers in grave danger. You face a perilous fight before they can strike their targets.",sp=0},{t="Reporting to Valanaar",b="After the Al'Aketh force is defeated, the scout is relieved to find you alive. Your timely aid will go into an official report for Valanaar.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Al'Aketh Assassins?",a="You are asked to eliminate a lethal force of Al'Aketh operatives. A scout will record your aid in a report for Valanaar.",al={"what is alaketh assassins","what is this quest","alaketh assassins quest"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh assassins?",a="Scouts call the Al'Aketh assassins stone-cold killers. They work together as a dangerous strike force.",al={"who are alaketh","who are the alaketh assassins","alaketh killers"}},{q="Who is Valanaar?",a="Valanaar is a settlement at the island's southeastern edge and a base for its peacekeepers. The scout plans to report your aid there.",al={"who is valanaar","what is valanaar","reporting to valanaar"}},{q="Why must the Al'Aketh assassins be dealt with?",a="The Al'Aketh assassins threaten scouts and travelers in the area. Defeating them protects allied forces from their lethal attacks.",al={"why fight alaketh","why kill alaketh assassins","alaketh threat"}},{q="What happens after dealing with the assassins?",a="After the fight, the scout prepares to report to Valanaar and record your aid.",al={"what happens after alaketh assassins","next quest alaketh","report to valanaar quest"}},{q="Who acknowledges your aid in defeating the assassins?",a="A scout in the field acknowledges your help against the Al'Aketh assassins and notes your contribution for command.",al={"who gives alaketh assassins","who is the scout","scout report valanaar"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="As a Rogue, you know the focus and precision it takes to face lethal killers like the Al'Aketh."},kw={"al'aketh","assassins","killers","quest","report","valanaar"},rel={"quest:92880","quest:92579"},m={id=98512},src={"In-game quest text: Al'Aketh Assassins"},fv=true}
E["quest:98545"] = {t="quest",n="Leonid's Letter",z="general",s="Leonid's Letter is a delivery quest directing travelers into the depths of Undercity. Wary of entering the Forsaken capital himself due to a strained past with Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, Leonid hires an impartial courier to deliver a message to Glix Xizzix, an old goblin acquaintance stationed in the Trade Quarter.",h="Leonid's Letter is a delivery quest directing travelers into the depths of Undercity.",sec={{t="An Outsider's Errand",b="Leonid needs a courier to transport a sealed letter to an acquaintance in Undercity. Because of past friction and a fraught relationship with Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, Leonid chooses not to walk the halls of the Forsaken capital himself, entrusting a traveling neutral party to pass past the city's guards without raising alarm.",sp=0},{t="Old Comrades in the Trade Quarter",b="The recipient is Glix Xizzix, an opportunistic goblin doing business in the Trade Quarter of Undercity. Upon receiving the message, Glix reminisces about surviving alongside Leonid under the shadow of the Scourge citadel Naxxramas. Though surprised and annoyed by Leonid's promise of compensation, Glix reluctantly pays out from the assorted lost-and-found items left behind by careless undead clientele.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Leonid and why won't he enter Undercity?",a="Leonid is an experienced soldier who maintains delicate relations with various factions. Due to a complicated past with Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, he avoids entering Undercity directly and relies on wandering adventurers to carry messages to his acquaintances.",al={"who gives leonid letter","leonid barthalomew","leonid quest giver"}},{q="Who is Glix Xizzix?",a="Glix Xizzix is a goblin stationed within the bustling Trade Quarter of Undercity. He appears to operate near or around grooming services, keeping a lost-and-found assortment left behind by absent-minded undead patrons.",al={"where is glix xizzix","who is glix xizzix","undercity goblin barber"}},{q="What is Leonid's history with the Dark Lady?",a="Leonid acknowledges having a 'somewhat colorful history' with Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen of the Forsaken. Rather than risk provoking her or her guards, he employs neutral third parties to handle his business inside the city.",al={"leonid dark lady history","leonid and sylvanas"}},{q="How do Leonid and Glix know each other?",a="Glix remembers Leonid as a seasoned veteran from intense military struggles against the Scourge, recalling times when the dread citadel Naxxramas rained destruction upon them.",al={"glix xizzix leonid naxxramas","how do glix and leonid know each other"}},{q="Why does Glix reward you with lost-and-found items?",a="Leonid told the courier that Glix would gladly pay a tip, but Glix had made no such promise. Grudgingly complying, the goblin offers odd trinkets gathered from the salon's lost and found left behind by forgetful Forsaken customers.",al={"quest reward glix","why lost and found"}},{q="Where is the letter delivered?",a="The letter is brought down into the Forsaken capital of Undercity, directly into the Trade Quarter where merchants, artisans, and goblins conduct business under the watchful eyes of the Royal Apothecary Society and city guards.",al={"where to deliver leonid letter","trade quarter delivery"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Leonid's history with the Dark Lady prevents him from stepping into Undercity himself, highlighting the political tensions between independent undead and Sylvanas's loyalists.",["faction:Horde"]="Navigating into the Forsaken capital on behalf of an outsider allows Horde adventurers easy access to Undercity's Trade Quarter amenities."},kw={"dark lady","glix xizzix","leonid","lost and found","naxxramas","sealed letter","sylvanas","trade quarter","undercity"},rel={"zone:undercity","subzone:trade-quarter","topic:forsaken","topic:goblin"},m={id=98545},src={"In-game quest text: Leonid's Letter"},fv=true}
E["quest:98574"] = {t="quest",n="Hallowed Memorandum",z="general",s="The Hallowed Memorandum is a written summons directing fledgling gnome priests in Coldridge Valley to meet with Branstock Khalder inside Anvilmar. It marks the commencement of their spiritual training, framing the Holy Light as a philosophical riddle well-suited to gnomish intellect and curiosity.",h="The Hallowed Memorandum is a written summons directing fledgling gnome priests in Coldridge Valley to meet...",sec={{t="A Summons to Anvilmar",b="While assisting allies in Coldridge Valley, promising gnomish novitiates receive the Hallowed Memorandum, an introductory message directing them to seek out Branstock Khalder inside Anvilmar. Serving as a call to spiritual study, the note invites aspiring priests to begin their formal tutelage in Dun Morogh.",sp=0},{t="Puzzles of the Faith",b="Upon presenting the memorandum, Branstock Khalder welcomes the novice into the fold. He explains that delving into the Holy Light is not a rigid technical craft, but a profound riddle requiring curiosity, keen intelligence, and harmony between intellect and emotion. Through contemplation and practice, students learn to channel these holy mysteries into restorative mending and divine retribution.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sent the Hallowed Memorandum?",a="The memorandum was passed along by a local ally while you were assisting around Coldridge Valley, having originated from Branstock Khalder, the priest trainer residing inside Anvilmar.",al={"who gave me the memorandum","who sent hallowed memorandum","where did note come from"}},{q="Who is Branstock Khalder?",a="Branstock Khalder is a dwarven priest trainer stationed inside Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley. He mentors fledgling priests in understanding the Light and harnessing holy forces to mend wounds or smite evil.",al={"who is branstock","branstock khalder anvilmar"}},{q="How do gnomes approach the Holy Light?",a="Branstock explains that gnomish curiosity and intellect serve as natural avenues for understanding the Light. Rather than treating it as a strict technical craft, he encourages viewing it as a philosophical riddle that unites thought and feeling.",al={"can gnomes be priests","gnome connection to the light","how do gnomes view the light"}},{q="What does Branstock teach about the Light?",a="Branstock describes the Light not as an exacting technical discipline, but as a riddle that offers practical blessings and life lessons, discovered by uniting one's thoughts and inner emotions.",al={"what is the light according to branstock","light riddle branstock"}},{q="What abilities does Branstock offer to teach?",a="Once a priest begins to comprehend the Light, Branstock offers guidance on channeling that power directly into protective healing prayers and holy smiting against the wicked, in exchange for modest donations.",al={"what does branstock teach","what can priest trainers teach"}},{q="Where can Branstock Khalder be found?",a="Anvilmar is the central fortress and welcoming hall located in the heart of Coldridge Valley in Dun Morogh, housing trainers and refuge for dwarves and visiting gnomes.",al={"where is anvilmar","branstock location"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Gnomes approaching the Light bring sharp intellect and curiosity to spiritual study, treating holy mysteries as puzzles to ponder alongside faith.",["class:Priest"]="This memorandum marks the start of formal priestly instruction in Coldridge Valley, introducing nascent healers to the philosophy of the Holy Light."},kw={"anvilmar","branstock khalder","coldridge valley","dun morogh","gnome priest","hallowed memorandum","holy light"},rel={"npc:branstock-khalder","subzone:anvilmar","topic:light","topic:gnome","topic:priest"},m={id=98574},src={"In-game quest text: Hallowed Memorandum"},fv=true}
E["quest:98575"] = {t="quest",n="Tainted Tablet",z="general",s="In the Valley of Trials, Tainted Tablet calls new practitioners to Nartok inside the Den. He teaches warlocks to command the Nether and shape their own fate, though its powers must be kept guarded.",h="In the Valley of Trials, Tainted Tablet calls new practitioners to Nartok inside the Den.",sec={{t="A Dark Summons",b="A stone message passes through uneasy camp caretakers to newly arrived practitioners in the Valley of Trials. Nartok, a warlock trainer who makes his home inside the Den, calls them to him. He invites you to leave ordinary tribal duties and study deeper, forbidden magic.",sp=0},{t="Commanding the Nether",b="Nartok speaks plainly about the troubled history between orcs and the Nether. He says the strong should command these volatile powers instead of rejecting them from shame or fear. Where ordinary chieftains falter, he believes those who cunningly master Nether entities can rise to prominence. Yet fearful minds strike at what they cannot understand, so these powers must be guarded closely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Tainted Tablet?",a="Tainted Tablet is a written summons for an aspiring practitioner in the Valley of Trials. It directs you to Nartok, your trainer inside the Den.",al={"what is tainted tablet","tainted tablet quest","tablet purpose"}},{q="Who sent the Tainted Tablet?",a="Nartok sent Tainted Tablet. He is an orc warlock trainer inside the Den in the Valley of Trials, and calls newly arrived practitioners to instruction.",al={"who wrote the tablet","who is nartok","who sent the tablet"}},{q="Where can Nartok be found?",a="Nartok lives inside the Den, a cavern in the Valley of Trials where darker arts and specialized training take place out of sight.",al={"where is nartok","where is the den","how to find nartok"}},{q="Why does Nartok warn to guard your powers closely?",a="Nartok warns you to guard your powers because ordinary folk may lash out in fear at what they do not understand. Orcs also remember the deep harm demonic corruption caused in their past, so warlocks keep their arts guarded and tread carefully.",al={"why hide warlocks","why guard powers","why secrecy warlocks"}},{q="What is Nartok's view on practicing fel magic?",a="Nartok believes those who master Nether entities can forge their own path and shape their people's fate. Where conventional leaders falter, warlocks can seize power instead of being enslaved by it.",al={"what is nartoks goal","nartok philosophy","why use nether powers"}},{q="Who gives you the Tainted Tablet?",a="A local in the Valley of Trials passes you the tablet. They admit unease with fel spellcasters, but know Nartok's summons must be delivered.",al={"who delivered the tablet","who handed me the tablet"}}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="As a warlock, you receive your first call to Nartok, your trainer in Durotar. He urges you to master the Nether instead of fearing it.",["race:Orc"]="As an orc, you know demonic pacts brought catastrophe to Draenor. Fel magic remains controversial, so Durotar's warlocks counsel both secrecy and boldness.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you know Thrall's new people are wary of demonic corruption. Warlocks in places like the Den say they can command Nether powers safely and secure their people's future."},kw={"durotar","nartok","nether","tainted tablet","the den","valley of trials","warlock"},rel={"subzone:valley-of-trials"},m={id=98575},src={"In-game quest text: Tainted Tablet"},fv=true}
E["quest:98581"] = {t="quest",n="Archaic Rune",z="general",s="The Archaic Rune is an introductory quest given to fledgling dwarf Shamans in Dun Morogh. Imbued with elemental power from ancient Wildhammer inscriptions, this sacred tablet introduces young initiates to their elemental heritage. Delivering the stone to Teo Hammerstorm in Anvilmar marks the formal beginning of their training in the ways of earth, air, fire, and water.",h="The Archaic Rune is an introductory quest given to fledgling dwarf Shamans in Dun Morogh.",sec={{t="An Ancient Inscription",b="Fledgling adventurers in Coldridge Valley discover the Archaic Rune after assisting local allies near Anvilmar. Bearing distinctive script carved in the style of the Wildhammer clan, the stone is visibly and palpably charged with ancient elemental power. The rune serves not merely as a relic of stonecraft, but as an elemental test: only those who possess a nascent attunement to the primordial spirits can comprehend the markings etched upon its weathered surface. For those capable of reading the script, it provides clear guidance to seek out proper instruction within the nearby dwarven outpost.",sp=0},{t="Wildhammer Wisdom in Dun Morogh",b="Inside the great hall of Anvilmar, the Wildhammer shaman Teo Hammerstorm receives the inscribed stone. For generations, the spiritual arts of the elements remained largely preserved by the Wildhammer clan among their windswept peaks. Now, those traditions have begun to take root among the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge and Dun Morogh. Hammerstorm serves as a vital bridge between the two dwarven cultures, welcoming young initiates who show a calling to commune with the elements and offering formal instruction in the disciplines of earth, air, fire, and water.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Archaic Rune?",a="The Archaic Rune is an ancient stone tablet inscribed with old Wildhammer script and imbued with elemental power. It is handed to promising new adventurers in Coldridge Valley as an initial test of their spiritual potential. If an initiate possesses the inner sensitivity to read the rune, it confirms their calling to the shamanic path and directs them to speak with the trainer in Anvilmar.",al={"archaic rune quest","what is this rune","archaic rune item"}},{q="Who carved the Archaic Rune?",a="The inscriptions on the rune are crafted in the traditional style of the Wildhammer clan. It was sent by Teo Hammerstorm, a Wildhammer shaman residing inside Anvilmar, who uses the inscribed stone to identify and reach out to new initiates who possess an innate connection to the elemental spirits.",al={"who made the archaic rune","rune origin","wildhammer rune maker"}},{q="Who is Teo Hammerstorm?",a="Teo Hammerstorm is a Wildhammer dwarf and shaman trainer stationed inside Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley. He has traveled to Dun Morogh to help share the ancestral shamanic teachings of his clan with the Bronzebeard dwarves, guiding new practitioners in the ways of earth, fire, water, and air.",al={"who is teo hammerstorm","teo hammerstorm anvilmar","shaman trainer anvilmar"}},{q="Why are Wildhammer teachings being shared with Bronzebeard dwarves?",a="While the shamanic arts were traditionally preserved and guarded by the Wildhammer clan for many years, those elemental practices have recently passed into the care and practice of the Bronzebeard dwarves as well. Mentors like Teo Hammerstorm have stepped forward to teach Ironforge dwarves how to commune with and honor the elemental spirits.",al={"why are dwarves shamans","bronzebeard wildhammer shaman","dwarf shaman lore"}},{q="Where do I take the Archaic Rune?",a="The rune must be brought to Teo Hammerstorm, who is located inside the main lodge of Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley. Presenting the rune to him demonstrates your ability to channel the elements and unlocks further training in your fundamental shamanic spells.",al={"where to turn in archaic rune","archaic rune turn in","find teo hammerstorm"}},{q="What does reading the rune signify?",a="The Archaic Rune is imbued with the elemental energies of ancient dwarven script. Only an initiate with an innate attunement to the elemental forces can decipher its writing. Reading it successfully proves that an adventurer has the natural makings and spiritual readiness of a true shaman.",al={"why can i read the rune","rune meaning","reading the archaic rune"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="The Archaic Rune represents the very first step in a dwarven shaman's spiritual journey, validating their elemental attunement and introducing them to their primary mentor.",["race:Dwarf"]="This rune marks an important cultural exchange between dwarf clans, as the Wildhammer share their ancient spiritual traditions with their Bronzebeard kin in Coldridge Valley.",["faction:Alliance"]="Wildhammer mentors bringing shamanic arts to Dun Morogh expands the Alliance's spiritual practices, establishing elemental traditions alongside the Holy Light."},kw={"anvilmar","archaic rune","bronzebeard","coldridge valley","elements","shaman","teo hammerstorm","wildhammer clan"},rel={"topic:wildhammer-clan","subzone:anvilmar"},m={id=98581},src={"In-game quest text: Archaic Rune"},fv=true}
E["quest:98738"] = {t="quest",n="Blessings of the Great Windborne Cat Spirit",z="general",s="After meeting the Great Cat Spirit of Zephras, an initiate returns to Dendrite with a newly awakened aura. The blessing brings the wild cat's stealth, speed, and ferocity, and Dendrite prepares the initiate for further instruction.",h="After meeting the Great Cat Spirit of Zephras, an initiate returns to Dendrite with a newly awakened aura.",sec={{t="Communion with the Windborne Spirit",b="An initiate returns to Dendrite after seeking the Great Cat Spirit of Zephras and earning its favor. The communion has awakened the wild cat's cunning, predatory stealth, and ferocity within them.",sp=0},{t="Dendrite's Instruction",b="Dendrite reads the initiate's newly awakened aura with subtle gestures. Sensing feline instincts woven into their spirit, he sees that the initiate is ready to focus their mind and call upon the true aspect of the cat.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Blessings of the Great Windborne Cat Spirit?",a="An initiate who has received the Great Cat Spirit's blessing returns to Dendrite to continue their spiritual communion.",al={"what is this quest","blessings of the cat quest","cat spirit quest"}},{q="Who is Dendrite?",a="Dendrite mentors initiates and sends them to commune with the Great Cat Spirit. When they return, his ritual gestures let him sense whether they have absorbed the stealth, speed, and cunning needed to channel feline nature.",al={"who is dendrite","dendrite lore"}},{q="Who is the Great Cat Spirit?",a="The Great Cat Spirit is an ancient patron in Zephras. It grants worthy initiates spiritual favor, the speed of the winds, and predatory stealth before they return to their instruction.",al={"what is the cat spirit","great windborne cat spirit"}},{q="Where is Zephras located?",a="Zephras is the Great Cat Spirit's home. Promising initiates travel there to seek its counsel, receive its blessings of wind and shadow, and learn the foundations of the feline form.",al={"where is zephras","cat spirit location"}},{q="Why does the spirit address the initiate as shen'dorei?",a="The Great Cat Spirit addresses the initiate as young shen'dorei, recognizing the practitioner's ancestral heritage as they seek communion with nature spirits and mastery of feral shapeshifting.",al={"who are shendorei","young shendorei"}},{q="What does the blessing of the cat spirit grant?",a="The blessing grants the cunning, speed, and stealth of the feline form. It also earns the initiate Dendrite's recognition, so they can focus their inner ferocity and call upon the true nature of the cat.",al={"what does the blessing give","cat spirit blessing effect"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="For a druid, this rite marks a milestone in mastering feline predatory instinct. It grants the stealth, speed, and ferocity needed to commune with and assume the aspect of the cat.",["race:Skyborne"]="As a Skyborne, the Great Cat Spirit recognizes you as young shen'dorei. Its guidance from Zephras teaches you to harness feral power through wind and shadow."},kw={"blessings of the great windborne cat spirit","cat form","dendrite","druid","great cat spirit","shen'dorei","stealth","zephras"},rel={"quest:98393"},m={id=98738},src={"In-game quest text: Blessings of the Great Windborne Cat Spirit"},fv=true}
E["quest:98815"] = {t="quest",n="Highland Hides",z="general",s="Highland Hides is an expedition contract commissioned by James Halloran, a tanner based in Menethil Harbor. Seeking superior materials from the misty mountain marshes of the Highlands, Halloran trades crafted leather goods for the tough skins of predatory Thicket Raptors.",h="Highland Hides is an expedition contract commissioned by James Halloran, a tanner based in Menethil Harbor.",sec={{t="A Tanner's Opportunity",b="James Halloran, a skilled tanner working out of the port town of Menethil Harbor, constantly keeps an eye out for exotic crafting materials. When word arrives of an expedition exploring forgotten ruins in the Highlands, Halloran pays less mind to ancient history and more to the formidable beasts prowling the mountain marshland. Recognizing the commercial and craft value of the wildlife, he contracts bold adventurers to venture into the mist-shrouded peaks and bring back choice pelts.",sp=0},{t="Beasts of the Highlands",b="Among the fauna lurking within the Highlands, Thicket Raptors are notorious predators suited to the rugged marsh terrain. Their tough, textured skins are prized by craftsmen for specialized leatherworking. Halloran tasks travelers with tracking down these creatures and returning with four intact hides, which he uses to produce fine detailing and decorative trim for his workshop's creations.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is James Halloran?",a="James Halloran is a local tanner and craftsman stationed in Menethil Harbor. He works with leathers and oils, seeking high-quality skins from exotic beasts across the Wetlands and surrounding highlands to craft fine trim and armor.",al={"who gives highland hides","who is james halloran","tanner in menethil"}},{q="Why does James Halloran need these hides?",a="Halloran seeks the hides of Thicket Raptors because of their exceptional durability and texture. He considers them among the finest hides he has handled, using them for spectacular detailing on leathercraft trim.",al={"what are highland hides for","why does james want raptor hides","purpose of raptor hides"}},{q="Where can Thicket Raptors be found?",a="Thicket Raptors dwell within the mountainous marshlands of the Highlands, lurking amidst the rugged wilderness near ancient ruins currently being explored by an expedition.",al={"where are thicket raptors","thicket raptor location","where are the raptors"}},{q="What ruins is Halloran referring to?",a="The Highlands host forgotten ruins that are currently being investigated by an expedition team. While craftsmen like Halloran focus on the wildlife, scholars and explorers delve deeper into the area's ancient secrets.",al={"what ruins in highlands","highlands expedition ruins","who is exploring highlands"}},{q="Is this hunt intended for a group?",a="Halloran advises bringing well-equipped companions along. The mountain marshlands of the Highlands harbor fierce wildlife, making excursions against predators like Thicket Raptors treacherous for a solitary traveler.",al={"is highland hides dangerous","group needed highland hides","do i need friends for highland hides"}},{q="What does James Halloran offer in trade?",a="In exchange for four pristine Thicket Raptor hides, Halloran offers quality leatherwork pieces tailored from his workshop, allowing adventurers to choose a piece that suits their travels.",al={"what does halloran trade","highland hides reward lore"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Menethil Harbor serves as a key Alliance port in the Wetlands, where tradesmen like James Halloran equip travelers heading into dangerous surrounding marshlands.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters venturing into the mountain marshes of the Highlands will find formidable beasts like Thicket Raptors, whose tough hides require precise tracking and clean hunting."},kw={"highland hides","highlands","james halloran","menethil harbor","raptor hides","tanner","thicket raptor","wetlands"},rel={"npc:james-halloran","subzone:menethil-harbor"},m={id=98815},src={"In-game quest text: Highland Hides"},fv=true}
E["quest:98895"] = {t="quest",n="Darkspear Islands Clash",z="general",s="Darkspear Islands Clash is an Alliance military call to arms urging champions to defend vital ground against fierce Darkspear Raiders. Without immediate support on the front lines, the Alliance risks losing the contested archipelago entirely.",h="Darkspear Islands Clash is an Alliance military call to arms urging champions to defend vital ground against...",sec={{t="A Contested Front",b="The struggle for control of the Darkspear Islands has erupted into open war between the Alliance and aggressive warbands of Darkspear Raiders. Alliance commanders warning that without urgent reinforcements, their foothold in the archipelago will be overwhelmed, have sounded a call to arms for capable champions to stem the hostile tide.",sp=0},{t="Proof of Valor",b="Alliance leadership relies on proven combatants willing to step directly onto the front lines. To ensure discipline and recognize genuine heroism, commanders require warriors to bring back Darkspear Islands Marks of Honor as tangible proof of their combat service, honoring those who turn the tide of battle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Darkspear Islands Clash?",a="Darkspear Islands Clash is a military call to arms issued by Alliance officers overseeing the war effort on the front lines. Alliance champions are tasked with engaging the Darkspear Raiders in battle and proving their contributions by recovering marks of honor from the fighting.",al={"what is darkspear islands clash","clash quest","darkspear islands battle"}},{q="Who are the Darkspear Raiders?",a="The Darkspear Raiders are troll warbands contesting control of the Darkspear Islands. Their aggressive pushes threaten Alliance footholds in the area, prompting high command to issue urgent calls for reinforcements.",al={"who are darkspear raiders","darkspear raider enemy","who are we fighting in darkspear islands"}},{q="Why is the Alliance fighting on the Darkspear Islands?",a="The Alliance seeks to hold strategic territory in the Darkspear Islands and prevent the region from falling entirely under troll control. Losing ground would jeopardize Alliance influence and security across contested coastal waters.",al={"why alliance fighting darkspear islands","why fight here","alliance darkspear war"}},{q="Who issues and completes this mission?",a="An Alliance Brigadier General stationed outside the battlegrounds oversees troop mobilization and receives proof of battlefield service from returning champions.",al={"who gives darkspear islands clash","who takes darkspear marks of honor","alliance brigadier general location"}},{q="What are Darkspear Islands Marks of Honor?",a="Marks of Honor are military tokens awarded to combatants for valor and active participation on the frontline. Presenting three of these marks to an Alliance Brigadier General serves as definitive proof of your battlefield contributions.",al={"what are marks of honor","darkspear islands mark of honor","how to prove front line service"}},{q="What is at stake if the Alliance fails?",a="Without rapid reinforcements, Alliance positions on the Darkspear Islands face complete collapse under the relentless pressure of the Darkspear Raiders. Securing the islands prevents hostile forces from cementing unchallenged dominance over the archipelago.",al={"what happens if alliance loses darkspear islands","why urgent aid needed","losing darkspear islands"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance high command relies on champion volunteers to reinforce the front against the Darkspear Raiders and safeguard strategic positions in the islands.",["class:Warrior"]="Frontline combatants bear the brunt of the assault against fierce troll raiders, where martial discipline and battlefield valor turn the tide.",["class:Paladin"]="Holy warriors of the Alliance bring unwavering resolve to the violent clashes, answering the call to protect Alliance lives and repel hostile forces."},kw={"alliance","battleground","brigadier general","darkspear islands","darkspear islands clash","darkspear raiders","marks of honor"},rel={"zone:darkspearislands","topic:alliance"},m={id=98895},src={"In-game quest text: Darkspear Islands Clash"},fv=true}
E["quest:98896"] = {t="quest",n="Darkspear Islands Clash",z="general",s="Darkspear Islands Clash calls upon fighters to rally their companions and defeat the hostile Darkspear Raiders contesting the archipelago. The ongoing battle demands coordinated action to stem enemy incursions across the contested island chain.",h="Darkspear Islands Clash calls upon fighters to rally their companions and defeat the hostile Darkspear...",sec={{t="The Archipelago at War",b="The Darkspear Islands Clash represents an active military push within the contested Darkspear Islands. Forces deployed to the theater are urged to stand fast against waves of Darkspear Raiders who continue to wage skirmishes across the beaches and interior grounds. Rather than an isolated incident, the clash forms part of a sustained campaign to secure foothold territory in the archipelago.",sp=0},{t="Rallying the Front Line",b="Commanders on the front line emphasize the necessity of organization and numbers to overcome the resilient raiders. Survival and victory depend upon rallying nearby allies into a cohesive force capable of withstanding counterattacks, routing fortified raiding bands, and breaking their grip over the disputed island strongholds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Darkspear Islands Clash?",a="Darkspear Islands Clash is a task calling upon adventurers to rally their allies and press the attack against hostile Darkspear Raiders amid ongoing conflict across the Darkspear Islands.",al={"what is this quest","darkspear islands clash summary","clash quest info"}},{q="Where does this fight take place?",a="The skirmish takes place across the Darkspear Islands, an archipelago marked by fierce territorial struggles and ongoing maritime clashes.",al={"where does this happen","where are darkspear islands","clash location"}},{q="Who are the Darkspear Raiders?",a="The Darkspear Raiders are the Horde's troll defenders of the Darkspear Islands, the sacred isles of Vol'jin's tribe. The directive orders combatants to defeat them as they contest territory across the islands.",al={"who are the enemies","who do we fight","darkspear raiders target"}},{q="Why must forces be rallied for this clash?",a="The conflict requires gathering and rallying allied forces to withstand sustained counterattacks and break the lines of the opposing raiders.",al={"why rally forces","what is the goal","how to complete clash"}},{q="Why does the fight in the Darkspear Islands continue?",a="The Horde and the Alliance are locked in an unresolved struggle for the islands' strongpoints: the Horde guards them as sacred troll ground, while the Alliance seeks a foothold off Kalimdor's shore.",al={"why is there fighting","why conflict in islands","island battle cause"}},{q="Who directs the forces in this engagement?",a="The directive is issued by the Alliance Brigadier General in the Alterac Mountains, urging soldiers on the ground to push forward without wavering.",al={"who gives this quest","quest giver clash","clash commander"}}},ang={["race:Troll"]="For troll adventurers, the Darkspear Raiders this Alliance order targets are Vol'jin's own people, defending the sacred isles of their tribe.",["faction:Horde"]="This is the Alliance side of the fight for the Darkspear Islands, the sacred troll isles off Kalimdor's coast that the Horde holds against all outsiders."},kw={"clash","darkspear islands","darkspear raiders","islands conflict","rally","skirmish","troll raiders"},rel={"zone:darkspearislands"},m={id=98896},src={"In-game quest text: Darkspear Islands Clash"},fv=true}
E["quest:99046"] = {t="quest",n="The Lost Runner",z="general",s="The Lost Runner is a task to locate Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow, an initiate courier overdue from the Oracle Tree. It matters because communications between the sacred tree and the elders in Darnassus are vital to the night elf leadership.",h="The Lost Runner is a task to locate Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow, an initiate courier overdue from the Oracle...",sec={{t="Messengers of the Oracle",b="The Council of the Forest maintains a contingent of Sentinels stationed at the revered Oracle Tree within Teldrassil. The tree conveys sacred messages to the night elf elders, relying on dedicated runners to carry these dispatches back to Darnassus along the winding forest roads. Ensuring these missives arrive safely is essential for the guidance and governance of the kaldorei.",sp=0},{t="An Overdue Courier",b="Communication from the Oracle Tree varies in timing, but an unusually long silence raises alarm when a courier fails to appear. The latest duty fell to Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow, an initiate still undergoing her training. Fearful that misfortune has met her on the trail, her superiors direct travelers to search the westward road toward the Oracle Glade to determine her fate.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the lost runner?",a="The missing runner is Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow, a night elf sentinel still in training who was tasked with delivering a message from the Oracle Tree to Darnassus.",al={"who is the lost runner","missing sentinel","who is eralya"}},{q="What is the Oracle Tree?",a="The Oracle Tree is an ancient tree located in the Oracle Glade that periodically conveys messages to the night elf elders in Darnassus, requiring stationed Sentinels to deliver them safely.",al={"what is the oracle tree","oracle glade purpose","why messages from oracle tree"}},{q="Where was Eralya Leafshadow heading?",a="Eralya was expected along the westward road leading between Darnassus and the Oracle Glade, where runners regularly travel to transport communion from the tree.",al={"where was eralya going","eralya route","lost runner location"}},{q="Who stationed the Sentinels at the Oracle Tree?",a="The Council of the Forest assigned a detachment of Sentinels to the Oracle Tree to ensure messages are safely and swiftly relayed to the elders in Darnassus.",al={"who sent sentinels to oracle tree","what is council of the forest"}},{q="Why is her absence a concern?",a="While communications from the Oracle Tree vary in frequency, her absence has grown abnormally long, prompting concern from her superiors given her trainee status.",al={"why is her delay unusual","how often does oracle send messages"}},{q="What is the objective of this search?",a="You are asked to head west along the path toward the Oracle Glade to search for Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow and discover what has delayed her.",al={"what do you do in the lost runner","lost runner quest goal"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Ensuring the safe communication between the ancient Oracle Tree and the leadership in Darnassus is vital to the security and spiritual guidance of the kaldorei.",["faction:Alliance"]="Maintaining secure roads and communication lines across Teldrassil supports the stability of Darnassus and Alliance territory."},kw={"council of the forest","darnassus","eralya leafshadow","oracle glade","oracle tree","sentinels","the lost runner"},rel={"zone:darnassus","subzone:oracle-glade","topic:sentinels","topic:elune","quest:98397"},m={id=99046},src={"In-game quest text: The Lost Runner"},fv=true}
E["quest:99047"] = {t="quest",n="Not Dead Yet",z="general",s="'Not Dead Yet' is an urgent plea from Eralya, a mortally wounded night elf discovered after a disastrous mission toward the Oracle Tree. Overwhelmed by vicious harpies, she begs travelers to deliver word of her patrol's fate to Byancie in Dolanaar. It highlights the deadly threats encroaching upon the woodland paths of Teldrassil.",h="'Not Dead Yet' is an urgent plea from Eralya, a mortally wounded night elf discovered after a disastrous...",sec={{t="An Ambushed Patrol",b="A desperate plea in the wilderness of Teldrassil, 'Not Dead Yet' centers on Eralya, a wounded night elf messenger found bleeding and clinging to life. Sent on a crucial mission toward the sacred Oracle Tree alongside her sisters, the patrol was violently ambushed and cut down by encroaching harpies. With no help coming and her own strength fading rapidly, Eralya entrusts passing travelers with carrying urgent news back to safety.",sp=0},{t="A Cry for Dolanaar",b="Through ragged breaths and coughing up blood, Eralya begs the player to carry her report to Byancie in the nearby village of Dolanaar. The harpies that infest the great boughs of Teldrassil have grown increasingly bold and aggressive, posing a deadly threat to lone messengers and sentinels trying to navigate the forest paths. Delivering Eralya's plea ensures that the settlement of Dolanaar learns of the ambush at the Oracle Tree before more lives are lost.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Eralya?",a="Eralya is a night elf sentinel or messenger who was ambushed while on a mission to the Oracle Tree. Clinging to life and coughing up blood, she implores passing adventurers to deliver word of her unit's fate before her time runs out.",al={"who is eralya","eralya quest","dying night elf"}},{q="What is the Oracle Tree?",a="The Oracle Tree is an ancient landmark located in Teldrassil's Oracle Glade. Eralya and her sisters were dispatched there with an urgent message before being overwhelmed along the way.",al={"what is oracle tree","oracle glade tree","where was eralya going"}},{q="What happened to Eralya and her patrol?",a="Eralya and her sisters were ambushed and overrun by harpies infesting the boughs of Teldrassil. The creatures swarmed in great numbers, cutting down the patrol and leaving Eralya mortally wounded.",al={"who attacked eralya","who ambushed her","why is eralya bleeding"}},{q="Who is Byancie?",a="Byancie is a contact stationed in the night elf village of Dolanaar. Eralya urgently asks that word of the harpies' ambush and the patrol's plight be brought directly to Byancie.",al={"who is byancie","where is byancie","byancie dolanaar"}},{q="Where must Eralya's message be delivered?",a="Dolanaar is the primary night elf village situated along the roadway south of the Shadowglen on Teldrassil. Byancie resides there to receive reports and coordinate local defense efforts.",al={"where to bring message","eralya delivery","where is dolanaar"}},{q="What happened to the other sisters on the mission?",a="Eralya's fellow night elf sentinels were separated or slain during the vicious harpy assault. With no reinforcements in sight, Eralya feared that none of her sisters escaped the onslaught.",al={"what happened to eralyas sisters","sentinel patrol sisters"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The fallen messenger Eralya and her sisters are Sentinels serving in Teldrassil. Seeing night elf defenders ambushed by harpies near the sacred Oracle Tree cuts straight to the heart of the bough's escalating perils.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers exploring Teldrassil are called upon to carry urgent word back to settlement outposts like Dolanaar when field scouts are cut off and overwhelmed."},kw={"byancie","dolanaar","eralya","harpies","not dead yet","oracle tree","teldrassil"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar"},m={id=99047},src={"In-game quest text: Not Dead Yet"},fv=true}
E["quest:99048"] = {t="quest",n="A Missing Hand",z="general",s="A Missing Hand is a quest in Durotar concerning the sudden disappearance of Heglan Shadeeye, a Shattered Hand scout tasked with keeping watch on Tiragarde Keep. When her regular intelligence dispatches abruptly cease, an investigator is sent to find her post and uncover what silenced her.",h="A Missing Hand is a quest in Durotar concerning the sudden disappearance of Heglan Shadeeye, a Shattered Hand...",sec={{t="Silent Reports",b="A Missing Hand begins with growing concern over Heglan Shadeeye, a scout belonging to the Shattered Hand. Stationed in Durotar to monitor the hostile human garrison occupying Tiragarde Keep from afar, her steady stream of intelligence reports suddenly vanished without explanation. Her superiors doubt the human soldiers managed to detect and capture her, raising fears that an unexpected and more dangerous hazard intervened. Adventurers are dispatched north of the keep to locate her post and determine whether the disruption is merely a delay in communication or something far grimmer.",sp=0},{t="The Cold Hut",b="Upon arriving at the designated observation post north of Tiragarde Keep, the search ends in tragedy. Heglan's cold body lies among the splintered wreckage of a ruined hut. The physical evidence left behind indicates that her attacker was not human, leaving open the unsettling question of what creature or entity struck down a trained operative so close to Horde lands. Clues scattered near the wreckage hint at an unfamiliar threat that must now be uncovered.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the quest A Missing Hand?",a="A Missing Hand is a quest in Durotar where an adventurer is tasked with locating Heglan Shadeeye, a Shattered Hand surveillance operative stationed north of Tiragarde Keep whose regular intelligence reports suddenly ceased.",al={"what is a missing hand","missing hand quest overview"}},{q="Who is Heglan Shadeeye?",a="Heglan Shadeeye is an operative of the Shattered Hand tasked with covertly observing Tiragarde Keep from a nearby vantage point. Her surveillance reports abruptly stopped, prompting an investigation into her whereabouts.",al={"who is heglan","where is heglan shadeeye","missing scout"}},{q="What happened to Heglan Shadeeye?",a="That is what this quest sets out to learn. Her reports from her post north of Tiragarde Keep stopped without warning, and the one who sends you doubts the humans caught her, fearing something more dangerous is at play.",al={"what happened to heglan shadeeye","is heglan dead","heglan fate"}},{q="What is the Shattered Hand?",a="In the modern Horde, the Shattered Hand serves as an organization of subtle operatives, assassins, and spies. They carry out covert surveillance and intelligence operations to protect Horde territories.",al={"what is shattered hand","who are the shattered hand"}},{q="Did the humans at Tiragarde Keep kill Heglan?",a="Her superior doubts it. Although Heglan was monitoring the hostile Kul Tiras humans occupying Tiragarde Keep, the one who sends you does not believe they could have caught her, and fears that something more dangerous is at play.",al={"did humans kill heglan","did tiragarde soldiers kill her"}},{q="Why was Tiragarde Keep being watched?",a="Tiragarde Keep is an outpost held by hostile Kul Tiras sailors and soldiers threatening the coast of Durotar. Heglan was stationed nearby to keep eyes on their activities and relay intelligence back to the Horde.",al={"why was heglan watching tiragarde keep","tiragarde keep surveillance"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="The Shattered Hand operates as the covert backbone and intelligence guild of the Horde. Investigating a vanished operative shows just how perilous their field surveillance missions can be.",["faction:Horde"]="Keeping watch over Kul Tiras remnants at Tiragarde Keep is vital for Durotar's security, and the unexplained loss of a scout signals a potential unseen threat right on Orgrimmar's doorstep.",["race:Orc"]="Durotar is newly secured orcish territory, and having an operative fall silent within sight of the nearby human squatters raises immediate alarm for the safety of the realm."},kw={"a missing hand","durotar","heglan shadeeye","rogue","scout","shattered hand","tiragarde keep"},rel={"quest:1858","subzone:tiragarde-keep","topic:human"},m={id=99048},src={"In-game quest text: A Missing Hand"},fv=true}
E["quest:99049"] = {t="quest",n="Threat from Below",z="general",s="Threat from Below is a Horde quest centered on investigating a violent ambush along the eastern shores of Durotar. After scout Heglan Shadeeye is ambushed at her post in a fierce skirmish, adventurers must inspect the devastated area for clues regarding the attackers. Uncovering physical evidence reveals an encroaching threat that directly endangers Razor Hill's defensive lines.",h="Threat from Below is a Horde quest centered on investigating a violent ambush along the eastern shores of...",sec={{t="Ambush on the Durotar Coast",b="A violent skirmish along the Durotar coast leaves behind shattered earth and immediate cause for alarm among the Horde's defenders. Heglan Shadeeye, a Shattered Hand scout keeping watch from her post, was ambushed by an unseen adversary. The skirmish site clearly demonstrates that Heglan fought back fiercely and injured her attackers. With hostile forces moving unchecked along the eastern shoreline, Horde adventurers are called upon to survey the devastated campsite, comb the surrounding ground for clues, and determine what manner of enemy struck the scout.",sp=0},{t="The Serpent Threat",b="Searching the wreckage uncovers a torn banner and the fragmented edge of an exotic blade, both belonging to the serpent-like naga. These sinister aquatic creatures appear to have crept southward down the Durotar coastline from Azshara to establish a predatory foothold. For the garrison stationed at Razor Hill, this discovery signals impending crisis. Beset by human invaders from Tiragarde Keep to the south and quilboar along the western canyons, the settlement cannot endure a new maritime offensive pressing down from the north without risking total collapse.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Threat from Below?",a="Threat from Below is a quest in Durotar wherein Horde adventurers investigate the ambush on the scout Heglan Shadeeye. Following evidence of a violent battle along the coastline, players search the disturbed terrain for clues left behind by the unknown assailants. Recovered artifacts are then brought directly to Orgnil Soulscar in Razor Hill to assess the military peril facing the region.",al={"threat from below quest","what is threat from below","threat from below overview"}},{q="Who is Heglan Shadeeye?",a="Heglan Shadeeye is a Shattered Hand scout who kept watch over Tiragarde Keep from a hut north of it, guarding the Horde's heartland. While on assignment, she was ambushed at her coastal vantage point. The severe damage at the campsite indicates she put up a fierce fight against her assailants, prompting urgent inquiries from the settlement leadership in Razor Hill.",al={"heglan shadeeye","who is heglan","heglan shadeeye lore"}},{q="Who attacked Heglan Shadeeye?",a="Heglan Shadeeye was ambushed by naga moving southward along the Durotar shoreline. Physical evidence recovered from the skirmish, specifically a distinct banner and a fractured blade piece, confirms that the serpent beings carried out the raid. The ferocity of the encounter left the attackers wounded as well.",al={"who ambushed heglan","heglan attackers","what attacked heglan shadeeye"}},{q="Who is Orgnil Soulscar?",a="Orgnil Soulscar is a prominent orc authority stationed at Razor Hill in central Durotar. Tasked with organizing the defense of the region and coordinating Horde scouts, Orgnil oversees intelligence regarding hostile incursions. When Heglan Shadeeye fails to report in, Orgnil relies on returning scouts and adventurers to identify the attackers and safeguard the settlement.",al={"who is orgnil","orgnil soulscar","orgnil in razor hill"}},{q="Why are the naga a danger to Razor Hill?",a="Razor Hill already faces intense military pressure from Kul Tiras human marines entrenched to the south and hostile quilboar in the west. If the naga establish a permanent beachhead along the eastern coast to the north, the Horde outpost risks being caught in an inescapable pincer maneuver, overextending its limited garrison and cutting off reinforcement routes across Durotar.",al={"why are naga dangerous here","naga threat razor hill","razor hill naga danger"}},{q="Where did the attacking naga come from?",a="The recovered military remnants suggest that the naga traveled south along the coast from northern waters, likely originating from Azshara or the Great Sea shelf. By using the waterways to bypass inland patrols, the aquatic invaders struck quickly against isolated coastal observation posts before Durotar's defenders could marshal an organized perimeter.",al={"where did naga come from","naga origin durotar","how did naga get to durotar"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Investigating the coastline is vital to the security of Durotar, safeguarding the young Horde nation from being flanked along its maritime borders.",["race:Orc"]="Orc defenders regard the ambush of scout Heglan Shadeeye as an intolerable intrusion upon their settled homeland, demanding immediate investigation and retaliation.",["race:Troll"]="Darkspear trolls understand the treacherous nature of the naga all too well, having previously faced sea witches and aquatic horrors in the Great Sea."},kw={"durotar","heglan shadeeye","horde","naga","orgnil soulscar","razor hill","scout","threat from below"},rel={"topic:naga","subzone:razor-hill","npc:orgnil-soulscar"},m={id=99049},src={"In-game quest text: Threat from Below"},fv=true}
E["quest:99050"] = {t="quest",n="The Great Tree Provides",z="general",s="Byancie works in Dolanaar to save Eralya from a dangerous affliction. Dewy lasher fronds from Teldrassil's lakes and a few simple supplies can make a salve to keep the illness at bay.",h="Byancie works in Dolanaar to save Eralya from a dangerous affliction.",sec={{t="A Race Against Time",b="Byancie tends to the night elves of Dolanaar. When she learns that Eralya has fallen ill in the wilds of Teldrassil, she begins preparing an urgent treatment. Without a salve to slow the affliction, Eralya may not survive the journey back to Dolanaar for proper care.",sp=0},{t="The Bountiful World Tree",b="Byancie seeks a remedy in Teldrassil's living bounty. Dewy lasher fronds grow near Lake Al'Ameth to the south and Wellspring Lake to the northwest. Blended with clean spring water in a glass vial, the leaves make medicine that can hold the affliction at bay.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is The Great Tree Provides?",a="Byancie asks for help in Dolanaar because Eralya suffers from an affliction. A restorative salve can keep it at bay until further treatment is possible.",al={"what is the great tree provides","great tree provides quest","byancie quest"}},{q="Who is Byancie?",a="Byancie is a night elf healer or apothecary in Dolanaar on Teldrassil. She tends the sick and prepares natural remedies from local plants.",al={"who is byancie","byancie dolanaar","quest giver byancie"}},{q="Who is Eralya and what happened to her?",a="Eralya is afflicted and outside Dolanaar. Her condition is worsening, so Byancie needs a salve to stabilize her for the journey into town and full treatment.",al={"who is eralya","what happened to eralya","eralya affliction"}},{q="Where are dewy lasher fronds found?",a="Dewy lasher fronds grow on plant lashers around Lake Al'Ameth, south of Dolanaar, and along Wellspring Lake and its river to the northwest.",al={"where to find dewy lasher fronds","dewy lasher fronds location","lasher fronds teldrassil"}},{q="What ingredients are required to prepare the salve?",a="Byancie needs six dewy lasher fronds, an empty vial, and refreshing spring water to prepare the salve.",al={"what ingredients does byancie need","salve ingredients dolanaar","great tree provides items"}},{q="Where can you obtain an empty vial and refreshing spring water?",a="General or trade supplies vendors in Dolanaar sell empty vials and refreshing spring water.",al={"where do you get vial and spring water","empty vial dolanaar","refreshing spring water dolanaar"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Teldrassil's herbal remedies reflect the kaldorei belief that their world tree sustains and protects them. Its living plants can help against sudden afflictions.",["class:Druid"]="Druids value the restorative properties of Teldrassil's plants. They use native growth such as lasher fronds rather than artificial concoctions.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Practiced herbalists know how to gather native plants like dewy lasher fronds and brew them into healing salves."},kw={"affliction","byancie","dewy lasher fronds","dolanaar","eralya","healing salve","lake al'ameth","teldrassil","the great tree provides","wellspring lake"},rel={"subzone:dolanaar","subzone:lake-al-ameth","subzone:wellspring-lake"},m={id=99050},src={"In-game quest text: The Great Tree Provides"},fv=true}
E["quest:99051"] = {t="quest",n="Threat from Below",z="general",s="Threat from Below is a defensive mission to purge aggressive naga scouts who are setting up camp nearby. By striking the scout forces and taking their spinefins, adventurers aim to drive the serpentine invaders back north and safeguard the area.",h="Threat from Below is a defensive mission to purge aggressive naga scouts who are setting up camp nearby.",sec={{t="Serpentine Incursion",b="Threat from Below addresses a mounting border disturbance caused by encroaching naga scouts. The serpentine raiders have begun setting up advance camps nearby, prompting local defenders to strike before the intruders can secure a permanent foothold. Adventurers are dispatched to cull the scout forces and return with naga spinefins as proof that the encampment has been crushed.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Borders",b="This is not the first time the serpentfolk have troubled the area, and local command makes it clear that their presence will not be tolerated. Following the strike against their scout camps, the goal is to drive any surviving naga back to the north, leaving vigilant Horde grunts posted to guard against stragglers or sudden ambushes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Threat from Below?",a="Threat from Below is a Horde quest tasking adventurers with eliminating naga scouts who have begun establishing encampments nearby. To ensure the incursions are halted, players must slay the encroaching serpentfolk and gather naga spinefins as proof of the deed.",al={"what is threat from below","threat from below quest"}},{q="Who are the snakes mentioned in the orders?",a="The 'snakes' or 'snake scum' refer to the naga, amphibious serpentine humanoids who have emerged from coastal or aquatic depths to scout out local positions.",al={"who are the snakes","snakes in threat from below"}},{q="Why must the naga scouts be eliminated?",a="The encampments suggest an organized scouting effort rather than wandering creatures. The local commander demands swift retaliation to send the invaders packing before they can mount a larger assault or entrench themselves further.",al={"why kill naga scouts","naga motive"}},{q="Why are naga spinefins collected?",a="Severed spinefins serve as undeniable physical proof that the scout camps were discovered and dismantled, confirming that the immediate pressure along the border has been broken.",al={"why spinefins","naga proof"}},{q="Where are the naga expected to retreat?",a="Upon turning in the fins, the commander notes hopes that the strike will send the naga packing back to the north, assigning grunts to watch for stragglers or suspicious movements.",al={"where do naga go","naga north"}},{q="Who keeps watch after the scouts are slain?",a="Horde grunts are instructed to keep watch over the area to ensure no stragglers remain and that the naga do not attempt any further incursions.",al={"who watches for naga","grunts naga watch"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The quest giver mobilizes Horde grunts to watch for stragglers, making this a proactive defense of Horde-held territory against encroaching naga scouts."},kw={"grunts","naga","naga spinefins","scouts","snake scum","spinefins","threat from below"},rel={"topic:naga"},m={id=99051},src={"In-game quest text: Threat from Below"},fv=true}
E["quest:99052"] = {t="quest",n="Threat from Below",z="general",s="Threat from Below is a Horde bounty tasking adventurers with hunting down a dangerous sea giant prowling Durotar's north coast. Overcoming such an enormous coastal titan protects the nearby shores and earns the respect of the warriors of Durotar.",h="Threat from Below is a Horde bounty tasking adventurers with hunting down a dangerous sea giant prowling...",sec={{t="The Northern Coastline",b="Along the northern coastline of Durotar, the waters of the Great Sea wash against jagged sands and rocky shores. While typically frequented by simple scavengers and crabs, the northern coast harbors a far deadlier menace: a hulking sea giant roaming the shallows, threatening anyone attempting to travel or secure the waters bordering the Horde's arid new homeland.",sp=0},{t="A Titan of the Waves",b="Sea giants are towering brutes capable of shattering longboats and crushing warriors beneath massive fists. Slaying one is no ordinary feat for young adventurers, and bringing down this behemoth of the waves earns deep respect among the orcs, celebrated with the traditional war cry of 'blood and thunder' echoing across the dry red bluffs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Threat from Below?",a="Threat from Below is a task given to Horde champions to hunt down and defeat a colossal sea giant prowling along Durotar's northern coast and return with proof of the deed.",al={"what is threat from below","threat from below quest","sea giant quest durotar"}},{q="Where is the sea giant found?",a="The sea giant is encountered roaming the waters and shores along the north coast of Durotar.",al={"where is sea giant","durotar sea giant location","threat from below location"}},{q="What kind of creature is a sea giant?",a="Sea giants are massive, ancient coastal behemoths created to dredge the oceans and shape the coastlines of Azeroth. Few wanderers survive a direct confrontation with one.",al={"what is a sea giant","sea giant lore","who are sea giants"}},{q="Why does the sea giant need to be eliminated?",a="A creature of such enormous scale prowling so close to Durotar poses a severe hazard to coastal travelers, fishermen, and Horde encampments along the shore.",al={"why kill sea giant","is sea giant dangerous","threat to durotar"}},{q="What does the quest giver mean by 'blood and thunder'?",a="'Blood and thunder' is a traditional orcish battle rallying cry honoring martial strength, ferocity, and glory in victory against dangerous adversaries.",al={"blood and thunder meaning","orc greeting blood and thunder"}},{q="What proof is required to complete the task?",a="The quest requires bringing back tangible physical proof of the sea giant's fall from the north coast to verify that the massive beast has been slain.",al={"threat from below proof","proof of accomplishment"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Taking down a sea giant along Durotar's coastline removes a dangerous colossus lurking right on the doorstep of the Horde's heartland.",["race:Orc"]="Orcish honor values facing down titanic adversaries, answering the battle cry of 'blood and thunder' against overwhelming physical might.",["class:Warrior"]="Facing a hulking sea giant in direct combat is a fierce trial of martial endurance, proving oneself against one of the coast's heaviest brutes."},kw={"blood and thunder","coastal monster","durotar","horde quest","north coast","sea giant","threat from below"},rel={"zone:durotar"},m={id=99052},src={"In-game quest text: Threat from Below"},fv=true}
E["quest:99053"] = {t="quest",n="Escaping Ban'ethil",z="general",s="Escaping Ban'ethil is a rescue mission within the subterranean barrow den on Teldrassil. Sentinel Lynessa Duskblossom, captured by aggressive Gnarlpine furbolg while delivering a message to slumbering druids, must be escorted safely back to the surface and reported safe to Dolanaar.",h="Escaping Ban'ethil is a rescue mission within the subterranean barrow den on Teldrassil.",sec={{t="Ambushed Beneath the Roots",b="Sentinel Lynessa Duskblossom was dispatched on an urgent errand by the Oracle Tree to convey a message to the druids resting within Ban'ethil Barrow Den. What was meant to be a routine courier duty turned disastrous when she discovered the subterranean chambers thoroughly overrun by aggressive Gnarlpine furbolg. Cut off and ambushed before she could retreat, she was taken prisoner in the dark tunnels, fearing a grim demise at the hands of the tribe until an adventurer intervened to fight her to the surface.",sp=0},{t="Safe Return to Dolanaar",b="For the kaldorei Sentinels, the subterranean gloom of a barrow den is an unnatural and abhorrent place to be trapped, far removed from the soothing starlight of Elune and the open air of Teldrassil. Following her rescue from the depths, Lynessa makes her escape back toward civilization while word of her survival is carried to Dolanaar. There, Sentinel Kyra Starsong receives the news of her friend's survival with profound relief, rewarding those who aided her comrade in defending the kaldorei people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sentinel Lynessa Duskblossom?",a="Sentinel Lynessa Duskblossom is a night elf sentinel stationed on Teldrassil. She was dispatched by the Oracle Tree to deliver a vital message to the druids resting within Ban'ethil Barrow Den, but she was ambushed and captured by Gnarlpine furbolg deep underground.",al={"who is lynessa duskblossom","lynessa duskblossom","who is trapped in the barrow den"}},{q="Why did Sentinel Lynessa enter Ban'ethil Barrow Den?",a="She was sent on an official mission by the Oracle Tree to carry a message to the druids slumbering within Ban'ethil Barrow Den. Upon reaching the subterranean den, she discovered it had fallen to hostile furbolg before she could deliver her missive.",al={"why did lynessa go to banethil","what was lynessa's mission","oracle tree message"}},{q="Who captured Sentinel Lynessa?",a="She was taken captive by the Gnarlpine furbolg inhabiting the barrow den. Hostile and aggressive, the tribe ambushed her before she could escape and dragged her down into the depths, leaving her fearing she would become their next meal.",al={"who captured lynessa","what furbolg are in banethil","gnarlpine furbolg"}},{q="What is Ban'ethil Barrow Den?",a="Ban'ethil Barrow Den is a subterranean sanctuary located on Teldrassil, west of Dolanaar. Traditionally carved beneath the earth as a resting place for night elf druids entering deep communion, it has become overrun by corrupt Gnarlpine furbolg.",al={"where is banethil barrow den","what is banethil barrow den","barrow den teldrassil"}},{q="Who is Sentinel Kyra Starsong?",a="Sentinel Kyra Starsong is a night elf sentinel posted in the village of Dolanaar. She is a close personal friend of Lynessa Duskblossom and eagerly awaits news of Lynessa's escape and return to safety.",al={"who is kyra starsong","where do i turn in escaping banethil","dolanaar sentinel"}},{q="Why does Lynessa loathe being underground?",a="While druids utilize barrow dens for sacred slumber, many night elf sentinels and warriors strongly dislike being trapped underground. Kaldorei culture cherishes the open night sky, the starlight of Elune, and the sheltering branches of the forest canopy over subterranean tunnels.",al={"why do night elves dislike barrow dens","under the earth is no place for kaldorei"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Sentinel Lynessa Duskblossom is a sister-in-arms whose entrapment beneath the roots cuts against the night elves' sacred bond with the open stars and canopy of Teldrassil.",["class:Druid"]="Ban'ethil Barrow Den is intended as a sanctuary for slumbering druids communing with the Emerald Dream, making its fall to feral furbolg an urgent crisis for Cenarion protectors.",["faction:Alliance"]="Safeguarding Darnassus and Teldrassil requires aiding the Sentinels against the mounting hostility of corrupted native wildlife and tribes."},kw={"ban'ethil barrow den","dolanaar","escaping ban'ethil","furbolg","gnarlpine","kaldorei","kyra starsong","lynessa duskblossom","oracle tree","teldrassil"},rel={"subzone:ban-ethil-barrow-den","subzone:dolanaar","topic:furbolg","topic:druid","topic:elune"},m={id=99053},src={"In-game quest text: Escaping Ban'ethil"},fv=true}
E["quest:99073"] = {t="quest",n="Easing Suffering",z="general",s="Near the Oracle Tree, Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow lies stricken by a spreading rot. You carry Lashwater Salve to ease her pain and calm her failing breath.",h="Near the Oracle Tree, Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow lies stricken by a spreading rot.",sec={{t="A Race Against the Rot",b="Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow lies on the road to the Oracle Tree, stricken by a rot spreading fast. To ease her agony, you bring her freshly made Lashwater Salve before the affliction takes firmer hold.",sp=0},{t="Peace in the Shadows",b="Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow receives the salve, though her healers doubt it can drive out the blight. It quickly calms her labored breath. Whether she will survive remains uncertain, but she finds peace in her darkest hour.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow?",a="Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow is a night elf guardian found ailing on the road to the Oracle Tree. A fast spreading rot has left her helpless and near death.",al={"who is eralya","eralya leafshadow","who is sentinel eralya"}},{q="What is Lashwater Salve?",a="Lashwater Salve is a prepared remedy brought to ease the pain of Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow's virulent rot. It may not cure her, but it soothes her distress.",al={"what is lashwater salve","lashwater salve","salve cure rot"}},{q="Does Sentinel Eralya survive the affliction?",a="The salve eases Sentinel Eralya's breathing and brings her peace, but whether she will recover or succumb to the rot remains uncertain.",al={"does eralya survive","is eralya cured","did eralya die"}},{q="Where can Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow be found?",a="Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow lies on the road leading toward the Oracle Tree in Teldrassil.",al={"where is oracle tree","road to oracle tree","eralya location"}},{q="What affliction struck Sentinel Eralya?",a="Eralya suffers from a fast spreading rot that festers through her body. Its source is unknown, but the affliction grows more virulent with time.",al={"what caused eralyas sickness","what is the rot","eralya affliction"}},{q="Why is haste required to deliver the salve?",a="The rot spreads and strengthens quickly through Eralya's body. Haste matters because each passing moment brings her closer to succumbing to its agony and corruption.",al={"why rush salve","why haste eralya"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Sentinel Eralya Leafshadow is one of your own, stricken by a virulent corruption in Teldrassil near the Oracle Tree. Bringing her comfort honors the duty Sentinels bear toward their kin.",["faction:Alliance"]="Aiding fallen night elf Sentinels on the roads of Darnassus's homeland helps preserve the fragile frontline against growing corruption in Teldrassil's boughs."},kw={"easing suffering","eralya leafshadow","lashwater salve","oracle tree","rot","sentinel eralya","teldrassil"},rel={"quest:98398"},m={id=99073},src={"In-game quest text: Easing Suffering"},fv=true}
E["quest:99079"] = {t="quest",n="Longwalker Malah",z="general",s="Longwalker Malah is a missing tauren scout dispatched to survey the eastern borderlands of Mulgore on behalf of Bloodhoof Village. As an elite Longwalker answering directly to the High Chieftain, her sudden failure to return after three days prompts an urgent search to ensure both her survival and the safety of the valley.",h="Longwalker Malah is a missing tauren scout dispatched to survey the eastern borderlands of Mulgore on behalf...",sec={{t="The High Chieftain's Eyes",b="The Longwalkers serve as the eyes and ears of the shu'halo, renowned across the plains for their tireless stride and survival prowess. Pledged directly to High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, these elite scouts are tasked with traversing vast territories to gather intelligence and deliver vital messages. Though their ultimate loyalty lies with the High Chieftain alone, young Baine Bloodhoof occasionally calls upon their aid to safeguard the settlement of Bloodhoof Village and its surrounding pastures.",sp=0},{t="Vanished on the Borderlands",b="Dispatched to monitor Mulgore's eastern borders, Longwalker Malah Longwind ventured out on a routine three-day reconnaissance run. When she failed to return to Bloodhoof Village on schedule, concern spread among the village leadership. Given the vital intelligence Longwalkers carry and the ever-present dangers lurking along Mulgore's frontier cliffs, scouts and willing travelers are called upon to seek out her trail and confirm her safety. Malah can be recognized by the traditional markings of her order: a ceremonial headband and a feathered staff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Longwalker Malah?",a="Longwalker Malah Longwind is an esteemed tauren scout serving the High Chieftain. She was dispatched on a reconnaissance mission near the eastern border of Mulgore, carrying the traditional headband and feathered staff emblematic of her order.",al={"who is malah","malah lore","longwalker malah identity"}},{q="What are the Longwalkers?",a="The Longwalkers are the tauren people's most trusted elite scouts and messengers. Their primary loyalty is pledged directly to the High Chieftain, though other tribal leaders such as young Baine Bloodhoof occasionally request their aid for crucial border intelligence.",al={"what are longwalkers","who are longwalkers","tauren scouts"}},{q="Why is Longwalker Malah being searched for?",a="Malah was sent to survey the eastern edges of Mulgore and was expected back within three days. When she failed to return, concerns arose in Bloodhoof Village that she may have met with trouble while patrolling the rugged frontier.",al={"why is malah missing","malah quest why","where did malah go"}},{q="Where can Malah be found?",a="Longwalker Malah was dispatched toward the eastern frontier of Mulgore, past Bloodhoof Village toward the boundary ridge. Travelers seeking her must search the borderlands east of the village.",al={"where is malah","malah location","find malah"}},{q="How do you recognize Longwalker Malah?",a="Malah can be identified by the signature garments of the Longwalkers: a distinctive headband and a tall feathered staff carried by scouts of her esteemed order.",al={"malah appearance","how to recognize malah","longwalker gear"}},{q="Why is Baine Bloodhoof involved with the Longwalkers?",a="Although the Longwalkers report strictly to High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, his son Baine oversees local matters in Bloodhoof Village and occasionally requests their tracking expertise to monitor border activity and ensure the safety of the valley.",al={"baine role","why baine involved","baine bloodhoof scout"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Longwalkers are revered scouts answering directly to High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, making their safety of paramount cultural and tactical concern to all tauren.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the leadership of Bloodhoof Village in locating missing border scouts helps secure Mulgore against external threats encroaching on Horde lands.",["class:Hunter"]="Hunters respect the wilderness tracking skills and endurance required of the Longwalkers, who patrol the far edges of Mulgore on foot."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","feathered staff","longwalker malah","longwalkers","malah longwind","mulgore","tauren scout"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={id=99079},src={"In-game quest text: Longwalker Malah"},fv=true}
E["quest:99080"] = {t="quest",n="Vertreibt sie",z="general",s="Baine Bloodhoof asks you to clear the Galak centaurs from the village’s path. Their defeat could give the children a measure of safety.",h="Baine Bloodhoof asks you to clear the Galak centaurs from the village’s path.",sec={{t="The Galak",b="Baine asks you to kill six Galak centaurs and four Galak scouts. Then bring him Herak the Plunderer’s head. The village’s safety is at stake.",sp=0},{t="A Village’s Relief",b="Baine hears the last centaur fall and the stampede cease. He calls it a great victory for the village. Its children need that feeling of safety more than ever.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who asks me to drive off the Galak?",a="Baine Bloodhoof asks you to confront the Galak centaurs and scouts. He also asks you to bring him Herak the Plunderer’s head.",al={"who gave this","Baine task"}},{q="What does Baine want from me?",a="Kill six Galak centaurs and four Galak scouts for Baine, then bring him Herak the Plunderer’s head.",al={"what do I do","Galak job"}},{q="Who is Herak the Plunderer?",a="Herak the Plunderer is a Galak whose head Baine asks you to bring.",al={"Herak who","plunderer head"}},{q="Why does this matter to the village?",a="Baine says victory gives the village’s children a feeling of safety they need more than ever.",al={"why help village","kids safe"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, Baine calls the bloodshed a unique honor among your people."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","centaurs","children","galak","herak the plunderer","scouts","village"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof"},m={id=99080},src={"In-game quest text: Vertreibt sie"},fv=true}
E["quest:99081"] = {t="quest",n="Schwieriges Unterfangen",z="general",s="Langläuferin Malah’s report carries word of a new centaur camp in Mulgore. Kriegerheld Wildrunner needs to know what happened to her, who killed her, and why the camp points to a small raiding party entering the land.",h="Langläuferin Malah’s report carries word of a new centaur camp in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Malah's Report",b="You bring Langläuferin Malah’s report to Kriegerheld Wildrunner in Bloodhoof Village. He needs to know what happened to Malah and who killed her. Her report brings troubling news, and Baine must hear it at once.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who wants Malah's report?",a="Kriegerheld Wildrunner in Bloodhoof Village wants Langläuferin Malah’s report.",al={"who needs the report","wildrunner report","who do i take it to"}},{q="What does the report concern?",a="The report concerns what happened to Langläuferin Malah and who killed her.",al={"what happened to malah","malah report","why is malah dead"}},{q="What is near the arrows' direction?",a="A new centaur camp lies in the direction from which the arrows that struck Malah seem to have come.",al={"new centaur camp","centaur camp","where did the raiders come from"}},{q="What does the camp suggest?",a="Activity at the camp suggests a small raiding party has entered Mulgore.",al={"centaur raid","raiders in mulgore","what does the camp mean"}},{q="Who needs to hear the news?",a="Kriegerheld Wildrunner says Baine must hear the news at once.",al={"tell baine","wildrunner tells baine","who gets informed"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you carry news of a centaur raiding party in Mulgore to Kriegerheld Wildrunner."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof village","centaur camp","kriegerheld wildrunner","langläuferin malah","malah","mulgore","schwieriges unterfangen"},rel={"zone:mulgore"},m={id=99081},src={"In-game quest text: Schwieriges Unterfangen"},fv=true}
E["quest:99082"] = {t="quest",n="The High Chieftain",z="general",s="Cairne Bloodhoof’s son sends you to Thunder Bluff to seek his father’s audience. Your defense of your people has earned recognition.",h="Cairne Bloodhoof’s son sends you to Thunder Bluff to seek his father’s audience.",sec={{t="A Chieftain’s Deed",b="Cairne Bloodhoof and the young Warchief Thrall led the effort to reclaim these plains from the centaurs. As High Chieftain, Cairne can recognize your part in defending your people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who am I being sent to see?",a="You are being sent to seek an audience with Cairne Bloodhoof, the High Chieftain of Thunder Bluff.",al={"who is the high chieftain","cairne audience","meet cairne"}},{q="Why am I going to Thunder Bluff?",a="Your courage in defending your people has earned recognition. So you are asked to seek an audience with Cairne Bloodhoof.",al={"why thunder bluff","what does cairne want","cairne meeting"}},{q="Who helped reclaim the plains?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof and the young Warchief Thrall led the effort to reclaim the plains from the centaurs.",al={"who fought centaurs","thrall and cairne","reclaim plains"}},{q="Where is Cairne?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is on Thunder Bluff’s central rise.",al={"where is high chieftain","cairne location","find cairne"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you are sent to Cairne Bloodhoof after defending your people. He and Thrall led the effort to reclaim the plains."},kw={"audience","cairne bloodhoof","centaurs","high chieftain","tauren","thrall","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:thrall"},m={id=99082},src={"In-game quest text: The High Chieftain"},fv=true}
E["quest:99101"] = {t="quest",n="Our Ancient Enemy",z="general",s="Malah's report has reached Baine Bloodhoof, and his answer is grim. The matter will not go unanswered.",h="Malah's report has reached Baine Bloodhoof, and his answer is grim.",sec={},faq={{q="Who receives Malah's report?",a="Baine Bloodhoof receives Malah's report.",al={"who gets the report","Baine report","Malah's report"}},{q="What does Baine say about the report?",a="After reading Malah's report, Baine says the matter will not go unanswered.",al={"Baine reaction","what does Baine say","report response"}},{q="Who brought the report to Baine?",a="You bring Malah's report to Baine Bloodhoof.",al={"bring Malah report","who delivers it","report to Baine"}}},kw={"ancient enemy","baine bloodhoof","malah","our ancient enemy","report"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof"},m={id=99101},src={"In-game quest text: Our Ancient Enemy"},fv=true}
E["quest:99108"] = {t="quest",n="Sparring Match",z="general",s="Sparring Match is an introductory trial overseen by the martial instructor Tang Stonehoof. Designed to test newcomers regardless of their class or background, the trial challenges adventurers to defeat three arena novices or fellow duelists to prove their mettle in direct combat.",h="Sparring Match is an introductory trial overseen by the martial instructor Tang Stonehoof.",sec={{t="The Sparring Grounds",b="Overseen by the veteran instructor Tang Stonehoof, the sparring match invites adventurers of all backgrounds into the ring to test their mettle. Though Tang specifically acknowledges those who walk paths outside the warrior tradition, he maintains that martial discipline and direct battle offer universal wisdom to any traveler in a dangerous world.",sp=0},{t="Sharpening the Horns",b="Within Tang Stonehoof's arena, distinctions of lineage and preferred weaponry fall away. Whether wielding sword, bow, or spellcraft, challengers are tasked with proving their combat readiness by defeating three willing opponents—either the training Novice Warriors or fellow adventurers in duels. Overcoming these bouts earns Tang's respect and bandages to dress the wounds of honest combat.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Tang Stonehoof?",a="Tang Stonehoof is a combat instructor who oversees the local sparring arena. Even for those walking paths other than that of a warrior, Tang offers combat tutelage and invites promising adventurers to test their mettle in direct battle against novices or willing peers.",al={"who is tang stonehoof","tang stonehoof lore","quest giver"}},{q="What does Tang Stonehoof require for the sparring match?",a="The trial requires defeating three opponents in the arena—either other willing adventurers in duels or Novice Warriors stationed there under Tang Stonehoof's guidance. The purpose is to demonstrate readiness and martial ability in direct combat.",al={"sparring match goal","what to do sparring match","who to fight"}},{q="Why does a warrior instructor train other classes?",a="Tang Stonehoof believes that every adventurer, regardless of whether they wield blade, bow, or magic, benefits from facing direct combat. In his arena, lineage and specific class disciplines matter far less than the will to test oneself and hone martial resolve.",al={"can non warriors do this","why sparring for other classes","not a warrior"}},{q="Where does the sparring match take place?",a="The contest takes place right before Tang Stonehoof in the open sparring arena, where novice combatants train and willing challengers can face each other on equal footing.",al={"where fight novices","arena location","where is the ring"}},{q="Can you duel other players instead of the novice warriors?",a="Yes. Tang Stonehoof accepts victories over either Novice Warriors training in the arena or other adventurers defeated in formal duels, caring only that you test your abilities against willing adversaries.",al={"can i fight players","duel players for quest","novice warriors or players"}},{q="What does Tang Stonehoof say when you complete the match?",a="Upon proving yourself against three adversaries, Tang acknowledges your skill with hope for the new generation, offering medical supplies to tend your wounds in preparation for the harsh road ahead.",al={"sparring match reward","what does tang give you"}}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="While Tang Stonehoof cannot train non-warriors in their specific disciplines, his sparring ring emphasizes the warrior philosophy: that combat fundamentals, courage, and sharpening one's martial resolve benefit any adventurer.",["race:Tauren"]="Tang Stonehoof uses traditional tauren turns of phrase like 'sharpen their horns' to describe testing one's mettle, welcoming any willing adversary into the sparring arena regardless of their origins."},kw={"arena","combat training","duel","novice warrior","sparring","sparring match","tang stonehoof"},rel={"topic:warrior"},m={id=99108},src={"In-game quest text: Sparring Match"},fv=true}
E["quest:99123"] = {t="quest",n="Lost in the Shadows",z="general",s="'Lost in the Shadows' is a quest involving the rescue of Pal'juh, an impetuous hunter who ventured into danger alone. Her rescue highlights the delicate balance between raw aggression and patient stalking required of wilderness trackers.",h="'Lost in the Shadows' is a quest involving the rescue of Pal'juh, an impetuous hunter who ventured into...",sec={{t="A Reckless Scout",b="'Lost in the Shadows' involves locating and rescuing Pal'juh, a talented but hotheaded hunter who ventured out alone into hostile territory. Unwilling to wait for support, she found herself in over her head, requiring outside assistance to ensure her safe return.",sp=0},{t="Patience of the Hunt",b="Upon Pal'juh's rescue, her overseers note that while her potential as a scout and tracker is undeniable, she charges headlong like a raptor rather than stalking with the patience of a tiger. Surviving the ordeal serves as a stark reminder of the wilderness's unforgiving nature.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Pal'juh?",a="Pal'juh is a promising young hunter known for heading out into danger on her own. While talented, she has a reputation for acting rashly and finding herself trapped or overwhelmed by beasts and the wilds.",al={"who is paljuh","paljuh lore","pal'juh"}},{q="What happens during the quest 'Lost in the Shadows'?",a="In 'Lost in the Shadows', Pal'juh ventured out alone and got into trouble yet again. An adventurer was needed to track her down and assist her before she came to serious harm.",al={"what happened in lost in the shadows","lost in the shadows quest lore","lost in the shadows story"}},{q="Why does the quest giver say Pal'juh acts more like a raptor than a tiger?",a="The comparison refers to hunting technique and discipline. A raptor lunges aggressively without caution or forethought, whereas a tiger waits, stalks patiently, and strikes with precision and calculated cunning.",al={"why raptor tiger","raptor than a tiger meaning","hunter raptor tiger"}},{q="Who receives the report for Lost in the Shadows?",a="The quest concludes with a mentor or peer who knows Pal'juh well enough to anticipate her recklessness, expressing relief that someone stepped in to rescue the headstrong tracker.",al={"who turns in lost in the shadows","lost in the shadows questgiver"}},{q="Does Pal'juh learn her lesson from this misadventure?",a="Pal'juh made it through the ordeal thanks to outside intervention, though those who oversee her remain watchful over whether she will truly learn the danger of wandering off solo.",al={"did paljuh learn her lesson","is paljuh okay"}},{q="Is Pal'juh considered a skilled hunter?",a="She is considered a promising hunter with plenty of raw talent and bravery, but her mentors caution that instinct alone cannot replace tactical patience when tracking dangerous prey.",al={"is paljuh a good hunter","paljuh skill"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="The quest highlights a fundamental hunter discipline: patience and stalking like a tiger rather than charging recklessly like a hungry raptor.",["race:Troll"]="Pal'juh's name and temperament reflect young Darkspear hunters eager to prove their prowess in the wilderness before mastering restraint."},kw={"hunter","lost in the shadows","pal'juh","paljuh","raptor","tiger"},rel={"topic:hunter"},m={id=99123},src={"In-game quest text: Lost in the Shadows"},fv=true}
E["quest:99127"] = {t="quest",n="A Net Disaster",z="general",s="A Net Disaster is an Elwynn Forest quest centered on an unusual disruption at Crystal Lake. With hook-and-line fishing yielding nothing and even minnows disappearing from the shallows, adventurers are dispatched to check overnight traps. The ruined traps reveal that hostile neighboring intruders are actively wrecking local fishing gear.",h="A Net Disaster is an Elwynn Forest quest centered on an unusual disruption at Crystal Lake.",sec={{t="Troubled Waters at Crystal Lake",b="At Crystal Lake in Elwynn Forest, local fishermen face an alarming drought in their daily catch. Empty hooks are common enough, but when not even minnows appear in the shallows, everyday angling turns into a serious mystery. To salvage what they can, overnight traps are set in hopes of pulling in basic stock.",sp=0},{t="Wrecked Traps and Neighboring Nuisances",b="Pulling the submerged traps reveals that the problem is not poor luck, but outright sabotage and destruction. Hostile neighboring nuisances along the lake's shores have ruined the traps, threatening to drive the local fishermen completely out of business unless something is done.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is A Net Disaster?",a="A Net Disaster is a quest set at Crystal Lake in Elwynn Forest. Local fishermen have noticed that the fish have stopped biting and even the minnows have vanished, prompting an investigation into the overnight traps.",al={"what is a net disaster","net disaster quest","crystal lake net disaster"}},{q="Where do you inspect the fishing traps?",a="The fishing traps are located submerged in Crystal Lake, placed overnight by Lee to maintain fish stock when regular hook-and-line fishing failed.",al={"where are the traps","net disaster location","crystal lake traps"}},{q="Who is Lee?",a="Lee is a local fisherman at Crystal Lake who set traps overnight to help secure stock when fish stopped biting normally on line and hook.",al={"who is lee","lee fisherman"}},{q="What happened to the fish in Crystal Lake?",a="The fish have completely vanished from the surface waters, with not even a single minnow visible. An inspection reveals that the overnight traps have been wrecked by new neighboring nuisances.",al={"what happened to crystal lake fish","why no fish crystal lake","crystal lake empty"}},{q="Who are the neighboring nuisances mentioned at the end?",a="The quest giver refers to new neighboring nuisances encroaching upon Crystal Lake. In the waters and shores of Crystal Lake, aggressive murlocs have established camps and caused havoc among the local wildlife and fishing equipment.",al={"who are the neighboring nuisances","net disaster nuisances","who broke the traps"}},{q="Why is the lack of fish such a big problem for the fishermen?",a="The quest giver relies directly on fish stocks from Crystal Lake for their livelihood. With damaged traps and empty hauls, the threat of financial ruin looms unless the disruption is stopped.",al={"why does the quest giver care","crystal lake fishing business"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Crystal Lake sits squarely in Elwynn Forest, making local disruptions directly threaten the livelihoods and supplies supporting the heart of Alliance territory."},kw={"a net disaster","crystal lake","elwynn forest","fish stock","fishing traps","lee","murlocs","nuisances"},rel={"subzone:crystal-lake"},m={id=99127},src={"In-game quest text: A Net Disaster"},fv=true}
E["quest:99128"] = {t="quest",n="Slimy Menace",z="general",s="Slimy Menace addresses a growing murloc infestation along the shores of Crystal Lake in Elwynn Forest. These aggressive creatures threaten to deplete the lake's fish supply and endanger nearby locals, requiring swift action to keep their numbers in check.",h="Slimy Menace addresses a growing murloc infestation along the shores of Crystal Lake in Elwynn Forest.",sec={{t="Trouble at Crystal Lake",b="Along the eastern shore of Crystal Lake, an aggressive enclave of murlocs has established a foothold. These savage, semi-aquatic creatures possess sharp teeth and fierce tempers, turning a once-peaceful body of water into a dangerous feeding ground. Local residents rely heavily on the lake for their daily livelihoods, but the murloc presence now threatens anyone daring to fish or approach the water's edge.",sp=0},{t="An Endless Infestation",b="Beyond the physical danger they present to travelers, the murlocs threaten to devour every fish in the lake if allowed to multiply unchecked. Thinning their numbers provides a much-needed respite for the local waters, though their eerie, echoing warbles remain a grim reminder of their tenacity. Even when scattered, murlocs replenish their ranks swiftly, ensuring that vigilant watch over the shoreline remains an ongoing necessity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Slimy Menace about?",a="Slimy Menace is a local task concerning the aggressive murloc population dwelling along the far shores of Crystal Lake. Left unchecked, these amphibious creatures devour the local fish and menace anyone attempting to work or travel nearby.",al={"what is slimy menace","slimy menace quest info","slimy menace summary"}},{q="Where do the events of this quest take place?",a="The disturbance takes place across Crystal Lake in Elwynn Forest, where a band of murlocs has gathered on the opposite shoreline. Their presence disrupts the lake's natural wildlife and poses a hazard to local residents.",al={"where are murlocs crystal lake","crystal lake location","where does this happen"}},{q="Why must the murlocs at Crystal Lake be culled?",a="The murlocs are rapidly depleting the lake's fish population while terrorizing the surrounding area with their vicious tempers and sharp teeth. Clearing their ranks helps protect the lake's fishing and temporarily halts their aggressive expansion.",al={"why kill crystal lake murlocs","murlocs eating fish","what is the problem with murlocs"}},{q="Does clearing the murlocs permanently resolve the problem?",a="Culling the murlocs provides only a temporary reprieve. As noted upon completing the task, murlocs reproduce and regroup at an alarming rate, ensuring their numbers will quickly recover despite any initial losses.",al={"are murlocs gone forever","did we wipe out murlocs","will crystal lake murlocs return"}},{q="Who asks for help with the slimy menace?",a="The task is given by an Elwynn local concerned with the state of Crystal Lake. Distressed by the murlocs' unsettling warbling and voracious appetite, they ask adventurers to thin the creatures' numbers to safeguard the fishery.",al={"who gives slimy menace","slimy menace quest giver"}},{q="What makes the murlocs such a threat?",a="Murlocs are described as vicious, temperamental amphibious creatures with sharp teeth and an infamous, guttural warble. They gather along shores, aggressively defending their territory and attacking anything that ventures too close to their feeding grounds.",al={"what are murlocs like","murloc behavior crystal lake"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Securing the shores of Crystal Lake protects local Elwynn fishermen and travelers near Goldshire from aggressive murloc raids.",["race:Human"]="For the people of Elwynn Forest, murloc encroachment directly threatens their waterways, food supply, and the safety of nearby settlements."},kw={"crystal lake","elwynn forest","fish","goldshire","murloc","murlocs","slimy menace"},rel={"subzone:crystal-lake","topic:murloc"},m={id=99128},src={"In-game quest text: Slimy Menace"},fv=true}
E["quest:99129"] = {t="quest",n="A Man About a Murloc",z="general",s="A Man About a Murloc is an errand undertaken on behalf of a fisherman named Jason who is troubled by local murlocs raiding his fish. Jason sends adventurers to consult Remy \"Two Times\", a local eccentric harboring a deep grudge and schemes of his own against the creatures.",h="A Man About a Murloc is an errand undertaken on behalf of a fisherman named Jason who is troubled by local...",sec={{t="Trouble on the Water",b="A local fisherman named Jason has grown fed up with murlocs raiding his catches. Knowing that Remy \"Two Times\" harbors a mutual hatred for the amphibians, Jason directs travelers to confer with Remy to see if he has devised any practical ways to drive them away from the waters.",sp=0},{t="Remy's Retaliation",b="Upon being approached, Remy vents his frustration that the local guards refuse to heed his persistent warnings about the murloc threat. Rather than waiting on official help, Remy prepares his own retaliatory measures against the creatures, enlisting able travelers to assist in his plans.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest A Man About a Murloc?",a="A local fisherman named Jason sends adventurers on this errand, seeking advice on how to stop murlocs from raiding his catches.",al={"who gives a man about a murloc","who starts a man about a murloc","jason murloc quest"}},{q="Who is Remy \"Two Times\"?",a="Remy \"Two Times\" is a resident known for his persistent complaints and warnings about murlocs, whom Jason consults for practical deterrence schemes.",al={"who is remy two times","remy two times elwynn","where is remy"}},{q="Why does Jason want to consult Remy?",a="Murlocs have been encroaching on Jason's fishing spots and stealing his fish, prompting him to seek outside help to protect his livelihood.",al={"why does jason need help","jason fish murlocs","murloc problem jason"}},{q="What does Remy plan to do about the murlocs?",a="Remy claims the town guards ignore his warnings, so he devises his own retaliatory schemes to deal with the creatures, requiring an adventurer's assistance to gather materials.",al={"remy two times ideas","what is remy plan","remy murlocs plan"}},{q="Why haven't the guards dealt with the murlocs?",a="According to Remy, he has repeatedly tried warning the local guards about the rising murloc nuisance, but they have refused to take his warnings seriously.",al={"guards murlocs elwynn","why dont guards help remy","remy guards warning"}},{q="What happens after speaking to Remy?",a="Speaking with Remy leads directly into his personal schemes to strike back at the local murlocs, prompting further tasks on his behalf.",al={"where does a man about a murloc lead","after a man about a murloc","next quest after remy"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Local settlements like lakeshores and docks frequently suffer from murloc incursions, leaving residents and guards strained across Elwynn and nearby Alliance territories."},kw={"a man about a murloc","elwynn","fish","jason","murloc","remy two times"},rel={"npc:remy-two-times","topic:murloc"},m={id=99129},src={"In-game quest text: A Man About a Murloc"},fv=true}
E["quest:99130"] = {t="quest",n="An Enticing Offer",z="general",s="'An Enticing Offer' is a local initiative aimed at repelling encroaching murlocs using bait concocted from toxic duskweed. By harvesting the noxious weed directly from nearby farmland, the plan simultaneously assists local growers and establishes a deterrent against shoreline pests.",h="'An Enticing Offer' is a local initiative aimed at repelling encroaching murlocs using bait concocted from...",sec={{t="A Twofold Solution",b="Dealing with pervasive murloc infestations often calls for inventive pest control rather than endless frontline skirmishes. 'An Enticing Offer' proposes a clever solution to address two local nuisances in a single stroke: crafting a specialized bait laced with duskweed to deter murlocs from encroaching onto settled lands, while clearing encroaching weeds from neighboring crop fields in the process.",sp=0},{t="The Nature of Duskweed",b="The core ingredient of the concoction is duskweed, a persistent wild weed that plagues nearby agricultural plots. In substantial quantities, the petals of duskweed contain potent toxins harmful to most beasts. By gathering the weed directly from troubled farmers, the fields are purged of an aggressive pest plant, while its toxic harvest provides the necessary base to train murloc packs to steer clear.",sp=0},{t="Baiting the Waters",b="While the resulting concoction is relatively simple to prepare, duskweed's indiscriminate toxicity makes handling it somewhat hazardous. Domestic animals and pets must be strictly kept away from the finished mixture, and the quest giver offers a parting caution to ensure that local acquaintances, namely someone named Jason, do not mistake the pungent bait for a meal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest An Enticing Offer?",a="An Enticing Offer is a task centered on dealing with local murloc trouble while simultaneously aiding nearby farmers. It involves gathering ingredients to concoct a special toxic bait designed to deter the creatures.",al={"what is an enticing offer","what is this quest"}},{q="What is duskweed?",a="Duskweed is a common weed that infests local farm fields. While generally considered an agricultural pest, its petals are toxic in large quantities to most animals, making it an effective deterrent against pests when concentrated into bait.",al={"what is duskweed","duskweed plant","duskweed effects"}},{q="How is the murloc bait intended to work?",a="The plan is to craft bait infused with toxic duskweed petals. Rather than standard extermination, the goal is to make the bait unpleasant or harmful enough to train local murloc populations to avoid the area.",al={"how does the bait work","murloc poison bait","why make bait"}},{q="Who is Jason?",a="Jason is the fisherman who sent you to Remy over his murloc trouble. Once the toxic duskweed bait is made, Remy says to tell Jason not to eat any of it.",al={"who is jason","dont let jason eat it","jason bait warning"}},{q="Is the murloc bait dangerous to other creatures?",a="Yes. While intended for murlocs, duskweed petals are toxic to most animals in substantial amounts. The quest giver specifically cautions that domestic pets and companions must be kept far away from the mixture.",al={"is duskweed bait dangerous","pet poison bait"}},{q="Why is gathering duskweed considered a win-win?",a="Duskweed aggressively invades cultivated farmland, causing headaches for local growers. Gathering the weed to brew bait clears the agricultural fields while simultaneously providing the materials needed to push back murlocs.",al={"why help farmers","win win situation"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Gatherers familiar with local flora will recognize the dual nature of duskweed, a nuisance weed whose toxic petals require careful handling when concocting potent baits.",["class:Hunter"]="Trackers and beast masters should take note of duskweed's toxic properties, as the resulting bait poses a severe hazard to domestic beasts and hunting companions if ingested."},kw={"an enticing offer","duskweed","jason","murloc bait","murlocs","toxic bait"},rel={"topic:murloc"},m={id=99130},src={"In-game quest text: An Enticing Offer"},fv=true}
E["quest:99131"] = {t="quest",n="Baited for Success",z="general",s="Baited for Success involves assisting local fisherman Jason Mathers in dealing with a disruptive murloc infestation. By coordinating with Remy to prepare a targeted poisoned bait, Mathers hopes to clear the waterways and safely resume selling his catch.",h="Baited for Success involves assisting local fisherman Jason Mathers in dealing with a disruptive murloc...",sec={{t="A Fisherman's Dilemma",b="Jason Mathers depends on open waters for his fishing trade, but an infestation of aggressive murlocs has disrupted his work. Unable to sell his fish safely, he seeks a solution to rid the area of the creatures. Remy steps in to craft a specialized poisoned bait designed to thin out the local murlocs so ordinary fishing can resume.",sp=0},{t="A Targeted Solution",b="Remy prepares the toxic concoction and promises a rundown on proper deployment so the poison targets only murlocs without contaminating the fish. Upon receiving word, Mathers plans to collect the mixture later that day, hoping it will finally clear the waters and restore his livelihood.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who prepared the bait for Jason Mathers?",a="Remy prepares the specialized bait for Jason Mathers, promising to explain its proper usage when Mathers comes by later in the day.",al={"who gives baited for success","remy bait quest","who made the bait"}},{q="Why does Jason Mathers need this bait?",a="Jason Mathers is a fisherman struggling to sell his catch due to local murlocs. The poisoned bait is intended to cull the murlocs without harming the fish he intends to catch and sell.",al={"why does jason mathers need bait","mathers fish business","why poisoned bait"}},{q="Will the poisoned bait harm the fish?",a="Mathers specifically notes his concern that fish might consume it. The plan relies on the poison targeting murlocs while leaving the fish unharmed so that Mathers can resume his livelihood.",al={"is the bait safe for fish","does poisoned bait kill fish"}},{q="Where is this task completed?",a="The task is completed by delivering the update directly back to Jason Mathers, letting him know Remy's preparation is underway.",al={"where do i turn in baited for success","baited for success quest end"}},{q="What threat do the murlocs pose to Mathers?",a="Murlocs disrupt local fishing waters, making it impossible or dangerous for fishermen like Mathers to make a living until their numbers are reduced.",al={"why kill murlocs here","murloc problem mathers"}},{q="What are Mathers's plans once he hears from Remy?",a="Once delivered, Mathers resolves to head over to Remy later in the day to pick up the poisoned bait and receive instructions on how to deploy it safely.",al={"what happens after delivering the message","does mathers get the bait"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Securing the waters and restoring local trades like fishing keeps settlements supplied while cutting down the aggressive murloc population.",["class:Rogue"]="Using custom-tailored poison as a pest control solution against troublesome murlocs reflects practical, understated problem solving."},kw={"baited for success","fishing","jason mathers","murloc","poisoned bait","remy"},rel={"topic:murloc"},m={id=99131},src={"In-game quest text: Baited for Success"},fv=true}
E["quest:99143"] = {t="quest",n="Bottles and Baubles",z="general",s="Bottles and Baubles is a quest built around the quirky logic that because murlocs look like fish, they must think like them. Adventurers are sent to rummage through a nearby murloc camp for glittering junk and discarded trinkets that an eager angler can fashion into fishing bait.",h="Bottles and Baubles is a quest built around the quirky logic that because murlocs look like fish, they must...",sec={{t="Fish Logic and Shoreline Scavenging",b="Bottles and Baubles begins with a rather unusual theory of aquatic biology: that because murlocs are fish-like beings, their minds must function just like the fish swimming in the deep. Driven by this logic, an eccentric angler posits that whatever curious oddities catch a murloc's wandering eye will surely entice a hungry fish on a hook. Scavengers are tasked with venturing into a nearby murloc camp to rummage through their hoard piles and gather shiny discarded junk to test the hypothesis.",sp=0},{t="Gleaming Baubles as Bait",b="Murlocs along coastal waterways and riverbanks are notorious scavengers, hoarding bright stones, shells, bits of polished glass, and metal scraps in messy mounds around their huts. Sifting through these muddy collections yields all manner of glittering refuse that an opportunistic fisherman can fashion into crude lures and flashers. Once retrieved and handed over, the odd assortment proves satisfactory enough to earn the adventurer a set of starter fishing supplies.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do you need to collect junk from murlocs?",a="The questgiver believes that because murlocs resemble fish, their minds must function like fish as well. Under the theory that anything attracting a murloc will also attract fish beneath the water, adventurers are sent to scavenge trinkets and shiny refuse from their camps to repurpose as fishing bait.",al={"why collect junk","what are bottles and baubles for","shiny junk murloc"}},{q="Where is the shiny junk found?",a="The shiny junk can be found scattered around the nearby murloc encampment. Murlocs are notorious scavengers who hoard glittering debris, discarded trinkets, and glass along shorelines and waterways, which can be retrieved directly from their territory.",al={"where find junk","murloc camp location","how to get shiny junk"}},{q="Who gives this task and what do they want?",a="The task is commissioned by a local angler looking to stock up on lures and bait. While their theories on murloc psychology are somewhat eccentric, they reward helpful scavengers with surplus angling gear to encourage beginners in the craft.",al={"who gives bottles and baubles","who is the questgiver","quest turn in"}},{q="Do murlocs actually think like fish?",a="Despite the questgiver's reasoning, murlocs are amphibious, tribal humanoids rather than simple mindless fish. However, like many creatures and predators, they are naturally drawn to gleaming items, polished shells, and bright trinkets, creating hoard piles that happen to hold glittering materials.",al={"do murlocs think like fish","murloc intellect","are murlocs actually fish"}},{q="How will the murloc junk be used as bait?",a="The collected junk consists of reflective glass, bits of metal, and polished odds and ends. The angler intends to fashion these reflective fragments into lures and improvised bait, hoping their sheen mimics small minnows or catches the curiosity of feeding fish.",al={"what is shiny junk used for","is shiny junk bait","does murloc junk work as bait"}},{q="What does the questgiver offer as a reward?",a="Upon bringing back enough suitable shiny scraps, the questgiver hands over older personal angling supplies. These basic tools and provisions are aimed at helping aspiring fishermen find their footing and try their hand at the waters.",al={"what reward bottles and baubles","what do you get","fishing supplies reward"}}},kw={"bait","bottles and baubles","fishing","lures","murloc","murloc camp","shiny junk"},rel={"topic:murloc"},m={id=99143},src={"In-game quest text: Bottles and Baubles"},fv=true}
E["quest:99144"] = {t="quest",n="Seeking Refuge",z="general",s="Seeking Refuge is a Tirisfal Glades quest involving the rescue of Bareth Dawnstone, an emissary from Bandarion Keep held captive at Crusader's Outpost. His struggle highlights the emergence of an unconventional order devoted to the Holy Light despite severe prejudice from local zealots. Safely returning news of his escape to Brill secures vital ties for this fledgling sanctuary.",h="Seeking Refuge is a Tirisfal Glades quest involving the rescue of Bareth Dawnstone, an emissary from...",sec={{t="A Perilous Journey",b="Seeking Refuge recounts the perilous journey of Bareth Dawnstone, a messenger dispatched from the secluded monastery of Bandarion Keep. While en route to confer with Shari Stilwell in Brill, Bareth was intercepted and captured by zealous warriors at Crusader's Outpost. Despite the hatred directed at his undead state, Bareth remains steadfast in his devotion to the Light, yearning only to escape his tormentors and ensure his message reaches its destination.",sp=0},{t="Vengeance Versus Devotion",b="Bareth's captivity highlights the bitter ideological divide haunting Tirisfal Glades. The zealots at Crusader's Outpost channel the Holy Light through vengeful fury and dogmatic righteousness, viewing Bareth's existence as an affront. In stark contrast, Dawnstone meditates on the Light as a source of protection and inner purity, lamenting how his captors' cruelty distorts the sacred teachings he cherishes.",sp=0},{t="Sanctuary on the Bluffs",b="Following his escape, Bareth's safety is confirmed with Shari Stilwell in Brill. Stilwell reveals that their monastic order is still establishing its roots in the bluffs northwest of Solliden Farmstead. Losing a practitioner as promising as Dawnstone would have dealt a tragic blow to their young fellowship, which opens its doors to any traveler seeking authentic spiritual guidance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Bareth Dawnstone?",a="Bareth Dawnstone is a devotee of the Holy Light based at Bandarion Keep. While journeying toward Brill to deliver a message, he was ambushed and imprisoned by zealous crusaders who revile his kind. Despite his undead state, he meditates upon the Light and strives to use it for protection.",al={"who is bareth dawnstone","bareth dawnstone lore","dawnstone"}},{q="Who captured Bareth Dawnstone, and why?",a="Zealots stationed at Crusader's Outpost ambushed Bareth Dawnstone on the road from Bandarion Keep. He laments that his captors wield the Holy Light with fierce hatred in their hearts, perverting the virtue of benevolence he meditates upon and persecuting those they deem abominations.",al={"who captured bareth dawnstone","crusaders outpost captors","why was he captured"}},{q="Who is Shari Stilwell?",a="Shari Stilwell is a representative of the monastery's order stationed in the Forsaken town of Brill. She receives travelers and coordinates with emissaries sent from Bandarion Keep, welcoming anyone seeking guidance in the ways of the Light.",al={"who is shari stilwell","stilwell brill","shari brill"}},{q="What is Bandarion Keep?",a="Bandarion Keep is a secluded monastery situated on the bluffs northwest of the Solliden Farmstead in Tirisfal Glades. It serves as a sanctuary and training ground for a burgeoning religious order dedicated to pure meditation and study of the Holy Light.",al={"what is bandarion keep","where is bandarion keep","monastery northwest solliden"}},{q="Why do Bareth's captors take issue with his kind?",a="Bareth Dawnstone speaks of 'what is left of my blood' and acknowledges prejudice against his kind, indicating he is an undead practitioner of the Holy Light. The fanatical crusaders of Tirisfal despise all undead, refusing to believe their intentions or devotion could ever be pure.",al={"why do they hate his kind","undead using light","bareth what is left of my blood"}},{q="What kind of order is based at Bandarion Keep?",a="The order based at Bandarion Keep is a young, developing monastic brotherhood taking root on the bluffs of Tirisfal. Unlike the hateful crusaders nearby, they preach peaceful meditation, protection, and open their doors to anyone seeking true guidance in the Light.",al={"what is the order at bandarion keep","bandarion keep monks","bandarion faith"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Priests will find Bandarion Keep's philosophy fascinating, as its members pursue pure meditation and guidance in the Holy Light despite the prejudice surrounding undead wielders.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins may take note of the contrast between the zealots at Crusader's Outpost, who wield the Light fueled by hatred, and Bareth Dawnstone's pursuit of benevolence and protection.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken who retain reverence for the Holy Light face hostility from both living zealots and skeptical kin, making the growing brotherhood at Bandarion Keep a rare sanctuary.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing couriers between Bandarion Keep and Brill ensures that emerging neutral or allied orders gain a foothold in the Tirisfal Glades against hostile crusaders."},kw={"bandarion keep","bareth dawnstone","brill","crusader's outpost","crusaders","holy light","seeking refuge","shari stilwell","solliden farmstead","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:shari-stilwell","subzone:bandarion-keep","subzone:crusader-s-outpost","subzone:brill","subzone:solliden-farmstead","topic:light"},m={id=99144},src={"In-game quest text: Seeking Refuge"},fv=true}
E["quest:99152"] = {t="quest",n="As Above, So Below",z="general",s="At Bandarion Keep, Hilda the Breaker sends you to investigate the hidden Scourge beneath Shadowvale. Bones still humming with reanimation magic may reveal how the undead gathered beneath the town unseen.",h="At Bandarion Keep, Hilda the Breaker sends you to investigate the hidden Scourge beneath Shadowvale.",sec={{t="The Hidden Threat",b="Hilda the Breaker prepares defenders at Bandarion Keep for the dangers around them. To the southwest, Shadowvale faces regular unrest from the Tarnished, a hostile faction. Yet beneath the town, the Scourge gathers strength in hidden cellars. If left unchecked, it may erupt in a sudden assault on the keep.",sp=0},{t="Magic in the Cellars",b="Hilda sends seasoned adventurers into the cellars beneath Shadowvale for a direct lesson in the danger below. She will not send green trainees blindly into subterranean crypts. The aim is to gather intelligence by recovering skeletal remains that still hold reanimation magic. Those bones may show defenders how dark necromancy worked beneath their feet without being noticed.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is As Above, So Below?",a="Hilda the Breaker sends an adventurer from Bandarion Keep into Shadowvale's cellars to investigate a hidden Scourge presence. The adventurer collects reanimated bone samples while trainees are drilled at the keep.",al={"what is as above so below","as above so below quest"}},{q="Who gives the quest As Above, So Below?",a="Hilda the Breaker instructs and prepares recruits at Bandarion Keep. She is tough and uncompromising, and demands results from those under her command. She would rather a recruit return on a stretcher than come back empty-handed.",al={"who gives as above so below","who is hilda the breaker","quest giver hilda"}},{q="Where is Shadowvale located?",a="Shadowvale lies southwest of Bandarion Keep. The Tarnished cause trouble on the surface, while the town's cellars hold a greater danger.",al={"where is shadowvale","shadowvale location"}},{q="What threat lurks beneath Shadowvale?",a="Reanimated undead gather in Shadowvale's cellars. If left unchecked, the hidden Scourge presence threatens Bandarion Keep with an uprising.",al={"what are the cellars below shadowvale","scourge in shadowvale cellars","threat beneath shadowvale"}},{q="Why does Hilda need faintly glowing bones?",a="Hilda needs bones that retain the magic of their reanimation. By studying them, defenders hope to learn how the necromancy went unnoticed beneath the town.",al={"why collect glowing bones","faintly glowing bones purpose","reanimation magic bones"}},{q="Who are the Tarnished in Shadowvale?",a="The Tarnished are a hostile faction active on Shadowvale's surface. Hilda sees the hidden Scourge beneath the town as the greater danger.",al={"who are the tarnished","tarnished shadowvale"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you see any hidden Scourge presence as an immediate peril. Investigating the reanimation magic beneath Shadowvale is a pressing duty.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest trained against the unholy, you have reason to fear dark reanimation arts beneath an inhabited settlement.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you can study the reanimation magic clinging to the glowing bones. It may reveal the necromantic methods used beneath the town."},kw={"as above so below","bandarion keep","glowing bones","hilda the breaker","reanimation magic","scourge","shadowvale","tarnished"},rel={"subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:scourge"},m={id=99152},src={"In-game quest text: As Above, So Below"},fv=true}
E["quest:99153"] = {t="quest",n="The One That Got Away",z="general",s="Near Naxxramas's remnants, Ephram Barbaro seeks the cultist who escaped with a dangerous crystal fragment. The fugitive fled beneath Shadowvale, and its power may raise or strengthen the dead.",h="Near Naxxramas's remnants, Ephram Barbaro seeks the cultist who escaped with a dangerous crystal fragment.",sec={{t="A Fugitive in the Shadows",b="After the clash with cultists near the Naxxramas battle remnants, word spreads among the ranks. Ephram Barbaro hears that one cultist slipped past the perimeter with an active piece of a Scourge focusing crystal. He seeks capable adventurers to find the fugitive.",sp=0},{t="The Threat Beneath Shadowvale",b="The escaped cultist fled into passages beneath Shadowvale. The fragment still holds necromantic power, and may raise the dead or strengthen hostile forces underground. Ephram Barbaro sends you to track it down and secure it before that power brings greater harm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest The One That Got Away?",a="Ephram Barbaro gives the request after hearing of your earlier efforts against cultists near the Naxxramas battle remnants.",al={"who gives the one that got away","who starts this quest","quest giver the one that got away"}},{q="What is the focusing crystal fragment?",a="The fragment is a piece of a Scourge focusing crystal carried off by an escaped cultist. Ephram Barbaro says it still holds power that may raise the dead or strengthen them beneath Shadowvale.",al={"what is the crystal fragment","what does the crystal do","why is the crystal dangerous"}},{q="Where is the crystal fragment located?",a="Ephram Barbaro's intelligence places the fragment beneath Shadowvale, where the fugitive hid it underground.",al={"where is the crystal hidden","crystal location shadowvale","where to find crystal fragment"}},{q="Who is 'the one that got away'?",a="The title refers to the cultist who escaped the clash near the Naxxramas battle remnants with an active shard of a focusing crystal.",al={"who escaped with the crystal","cultist with crystal","the one that got away meaning"}},{q="What happens when you return the crystal to Ephram Barbaro?",a="When you return the fragment, Ephram Barbaro commends your speed and says it is time for his favorite part of handling the situation.",al={"what happens when you return the crystal","turn in the one that got away","ephram barbaro favorite part"}},{q="How does Ephram Barbaro know about you?",a="Ephram Barbaro says word travels quickly among the ranks. He has heard of your earlier work against cultists near the Naxxramas battle remnants.",al={"naxxramas battle remnants cultists","how does ephram know you","prerequisites the one that got away"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you know the danger of an active Scourge focusing crystal that can animate or strengthen the dead beneath the ground.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you know unholy relics can corrupt the land around them if they are not recovered and cleansed promptly."},kw={"crystal fragment","cultist","ephram barbaro","focusing crystal","naxxramas","shadowvale","the one that got away"},m={id=99153},src={"In-game quest text: The One That Got Away"},fv=true}
E["quest:99158"] = {t="quest",n="Dawn in the Mountains",z="general",s="Dawn in the Mountains is a quest to deliver a shipment of holy candles to Father Gavin at Misty Pine Refuge. A gentle priest of the Argent Dawn, Gavin maintains this mountain shelter to heal and comfort weary travelers rather than wage war with weapons.",h="Dawn in the Mountains is a quest to deliver a shipment of holy candles to Father Gavin at Misty Pine Refuge.",sec={{t="A Sanctuary in the Peaks",b="While many within the Argent Dawn take up arms to purge unholy horrors from Azeroth, some choose the gentler calling of the Light. Father Gavin, a priest dwelling in the rugged mountain wilderness, has turned away from the blade to focus wholly on healing the afflicted and tending to the weary. Taking an abandoned structure, he restored it into Misty Pine Refuge, a safe stop for travelers facing the perils of the high passes.",sp=0},{t="Candles for the Light",b="Maintaining an isolated refuge in the mountains requires constant upkeep and practical devotion. To sustain his spiritual ministry and keep the lamps and altars burning, Father Gavin relies on the charity of distant allies. A delivery of consecrated candles ensures that the sanctuary remains a beacon of light, warmth, and hope amidst the unforgiving wilderness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Father Gavin?",a="Father Gavin is a priest affiliated with the Argent Dawn stationed up in the mountains. Unlike many in his holy order who take up arms against the unholy, Gavin is dedicated primarily to healing, comforting travelers, and providing shelter at Misty Pine Refuge.",al={"who is father gavin","father gavin lore","who is the priest at misty pine"}},{q="What is Misty Pine Refuge?",a="Misty Pine Refuge is a rest stop for mountain travelers established by Father Gavin. He renovated an old building in the wilderness to create a peaceful sanctuary where wanderers can recover, rest, and receive healing.",al={"what is misty pine refuge","where is misty pine refuge","misty pine refuge lore"}},{q="What is the Argent Dawn?",a="The Argent Dawn is a dedicated holy order committed to seeking out and destroying unholy and unnatural threats throughout Azeroth, while offering aid and solace to those struggling to survive in troubled lands.",al={"what is the argent dawn","argent dawn purpose","who are argent dawn"}},{q="Why does Father Gavin need a shipment of holy candles?",a="The crate of holy candles is meant to replenish Father Gavin's supplies at Misty Pine Refuge. In holy ministries and remote sanctuaries, consecrated candles are essential for religious rites, prayer, and lighting the refuge for weary travelers.",al={"why candles","holy candles purpose","crate of candles delivery"}},{q="Does Father Gavin take up arms like other Argent Dawn members?",a="Father Gavin prefers healing and sheltering travelers over taking up a weapon. While the Argent Dawn is known for martial crusade against darkness, Gavin serves the order through compassion, hospitality, and spiritual care.",al={"does father gavin fight","is father gavin a fighter"}},{q="What is the purpose of the quest Dawn in the Mountains?",a="The request asks a traveler heading into the mountains to carry a crate of holy candles directly to Father Gavin at Misty Pine Refuge, supporting his humble mountain sanctuary.",al={"who gave this quest","where does dawn in the mountains lead"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Father Gavin embodies the gentler, restorative path of the priesthood, choosing to minister to travelers and tend wounds rather than wield weapons on the front lines of the Argent Dawn.",["faction:Alliance"]="Providing supplies to an Argent Dawn shelter like Misty Pine Refuge aids neutral humanitarian work in contested and hazardous mountainous terrain.",["faction:Horde"]="The Argent Dawn accepts aid and offers sanctuary regardless of allegiance, making outposts like Father Gavin's a rare safe haven in the wilderness."},kw={"argent dawn","dawn in the mountains","father gavin","holy candles","misty pine refuge","priest","refuge"},rel={"subzone:misty-pine-refuge","topic:argent-dawn","topic:priest"},m={id=99158},src={"In-game quest text: Dawn in the Mountains"},fv=true}
E["quest:99159"] = {t="quest",n="Finding Warmth",z="general",s="Finding Warmth is a humble errand centered on keeping a welcoming hearth fueled with firewood. With the host occupied by other responsibilities, travelers are asked to gather logs so that any soul seeking warmth can find comfort. Completing the task keeps the fire burning and opens the way to further chores.",h="Finding Warmth is a humble errand centered on keeping a welcoming hearth fueled with firewood.",sec={{t="A Welcoming Hearth",b="Finding Warmth is a simple chore undertaken to sustain a welcoming fire. The questgiver keeps an open hearth ready to warm any wandering soul, but mounting obligations have left them unable to gather fuel on their own. By stepping in to collect firewood, travelers ensure the hearth remains alight for anyone seeking shelter and comfort.",sp=0},{t="Tending the Fire",b="Not all timber retrieved from the surroundings is ready for immediate burning. Upon delivery, pieces too damp or wet to catch fire are set aside to dry properly, while seasoned wood feeds the flames right away. Pleased with the timely assistance, the host quickly turns to other pressing matters that still demand attention.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the quest Finding Warmth about?",a="Finding Warmth is a local task where an adventurer is asked to gather firewood to keep a welcoming hearth burning. The questgiver normally tends to the fire personally, but has grown preoccupied with other pressing chores and asks for outside assistance.",al={"finding warmth quest","what is finding warmth","finding warmth firewood"}},{q="Why is the firewood needed?",a="The firewood is needed to fuel a hearth kept open to comfort and warm passing travelers and locals. Gathering wood is an ordinary chore that fell behind because the host became busy with other duties.",al={"why need wood","firewood finding warmth","hearth firewood"}},{q="What happens to wet firewood brought in?",a="Some gathered logs may be too damp or wet to burn immediately upon delivery. The questgiver sets the wet pieces aside to dry out over time while using the dry wood to stoke the hearth right away.",al={"finding warmth wet wood","damp firewood"}},{q="What kind of person offers this task?",a="While the host's specific identity is not detailed, their blessing of 'Light guide you on your tasks' indicates an individual rooted in faith or reverence toward the Holy Light, offering an open hearth to warm any soul.",al={"who gives finding warmth","finding warmth light guide you"}},{q="Are there more tasks after completing Finding Warmth?",a="Yes. Upon delivering the firewood, the host mentions having further tasks at hand that require an able pair of hands if the traveler is willing to help further.",al={"more quests after finding warmth","finding warmth followup"}},{q="Who is welcome at this hearth?",a="The host keeps their hearth burning to offer shelter, comfort, and warmth to anyone passing through who might need a reprieve from the cold or weary travels.",al={"hearth open soul","open hearth finding warmth"}}},kw={"finding warmth","firewood","hearth","light","warmth","wood"},rel={"topic:light"},m={id=99159},src={"In-game quest text: Finding Warmth"},fv=true}
E["quest:99160"] = {t="quest",n="Rime's Wrath",z="general",s="Rime's Wrath is a quest focused on quelling a hazardous outbreak of minor ice elementals. Because methods to peacefully soothe the aggressive frost spirits were unknown, eliminating them became necessary to keep the area safe.",h="Rime's Wrath is a quest focused on quelling a hazardous outbreak of minor ice elementals.",sec={{t="A Bitter Manifestation",b="Rime's Wrath involves dealing with a localized surge of minor ice elementals. These spirits of biting frost and cold have become an active danger in the area, necessitating swift intervention before their chill spreads further or harms passersby. Rather than a coordinated assault, the response is a containment measure against wild, hostile elemental manifestations.",sp=0},{t="Beyond Placation",b="While the preferred outcome was to soothe the agitated frost spirits, the knowledge required to placate them was out of reach. With no practical means of pacification available and the spirits growing increasingly perilous, the only remaining choice was to destroy them to protect the surrounding territory.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Rime's Wrath?",a="Rime's Wrath is a task requiring the destruction of minor ice elementals that pose an immediate threat. Because peaceful methods to pacify these agitated spirits were unknown or unavailable, direct force became the only option to maintain safety.",al={"what is rimes wrath","quest rimes wrath","rimes wrath overview"}},{q="Why do the minor ice elementals need to be destroyed?",a="The minor ice elementals grew too volatile and dangerous to leave unchecked. While peaceful pacification would have been preferred, the knowledge to calm them was lacking, leaving destruction as the necessary measure.",al={"why kill ice elementals","why fight them","cant we calm the elementals"}},{q="Who requests the destruction of the elementals?",a="The quest is directed by someone concerned with containment who hoped to placate the spirits peacefully. Lacking the arcane or spiritual knowledge required to pacify them, they instead turned to adventurers to neutralize the threat.",al={"who gives rimes wrath","who wants the elementals dead","who is the questgiver"}},{q="Could the elementals have been dealt with peacefully?",a="The questgiver notes that placating them was beyond their understanding. Elemental spirits can sometimes be balanced or appeased, but without the proper expertise, their aggression left no alternative but combat.",al={"can ice elementals be calmed","placate ice elementals","why not soothe elementals"}},{q="What kind of creatures are minor ice elementals?",a="Minor ice elementals are volatile manifestations of elemental frost. Though classified as minor spirits, their ambient cold and aggressive nature make them dangerous to travelers and local inhabitants if left roaming freely.",al={"what are minor ice elementals","ice elementals danger","how strong are minor ice elementals"}},{q="What is the outcome of dealing with the elementals?",a="Once ten minor ice elementals are defeated, the immediate danger is quelled. The questgiver laments that violence was necessary, acknowledging that leaving them unchecked was simply too risky.",al={"what happens after rimes wrath","rimes wrath conclusion"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Frost mages study the raw conjuration and shaping of ice magic, making wild minor ice elementals an intriguing yet unruly display of elemental forces.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman seek to understand and commune with elemental spirits, though these hostile ice entities proved beyond peaceful placation and had to be quelled by force."},kw={"elementals","frost","ice elementals","minor ice elementals","placate","rime's wrath","rimes wrath"},m={id=99160},src={"In-game quest text: Rime's Wrath"},fv=true}
E["quest:99161"] = {t="quest",n="Rime's Wrath",z="general",s="Following a rockslide, an unusually large elemental named Avala emerged and began triggering erratic weather swings and secondary landslides. An observer tasks adventurers with hunting down the elemental and claiming its core to secure the area. Defeating the creature restores stability to the mountain slopes and safeguards travelers from deadly winter perils.",h="Following a rockslide, an unusually large elemental named Avala emerged and began triggering erratic weather...",sec={{t="The Wake of the Rockslide",b="The disturbances began when a sudden rockslide destabilized the mountain pass, opening the way for elemental activity. Among the creatures that surfaced, one particular elemental stood out, towering over its kin in sheer size and raw power. Given the informal moniker \"Avala\" by the watchful questgiver, this potent entity is not merely an imposing presence—it directly fuels localized atmospheric turmoil and destabilizes the surrounding cliffs. Its presence poses an immediate peril to anyone attempting to navigate the area, as unstable earth and sudden winter chills make ordinary travel lethal.",sp=0},{t="Quelling Avala",b="Recognizing the escalating danger of Avala's influence over the terrain, a call was put forth to eliminate the beast before further collapses claimed lives. Slaying the creature requires facing both its elemental ferocity and the harsh conditions it generates, culminating in the recovery of its core as definitive proof of its demise. With Avala vanquished and its animating heart secured, the violent weather shifts subside, restoring a measure of safety to the surrounding mountain passes and safeguarding future travelers from needless catastrophe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Rime's Wrath?",a="Rime's Wrath is a quest tasking adventurers with eliminating Avala, a large and hazardous elemental that manifested following a sudden rockslide. By hunting down the elemental and recovering its core, adventurers put an end to the erratic weather disturbances and recurring slides threatening travelers across the mountain pass.",al={"rimes wrath","what is rimes wrath","rime's wrath quest"}},{q="Who or what is Avala?",a="Avala is a particularly large elemental that surfaced in the aftermath of a major rockslide. Larger than its elemental peers, Avala actively causes sudden weather changes, freezing chills, and dangerous secondary earth tremors in the area. The name 'Avala' was given to it by the observer monitoring the pass.",al={"what is avala","who is avala","avala elemental"}},{q="Why does Avala need to be destroyed?",a="Avala's continuous presence causes unpredictable weather disruptions and minor rockslides that destabilize the surrounding slopes. Left unchecked, the elemental's supernatural influence over the winter climate and terrain threatens to trigger further collapses and cause avoidable deaths among travelers traversing the mountain pass.",al={"why kill avala","reason to kill avala","why destroy avala"}},{q="What proof is needed to complete Rime's Wrath?",a="Adventurers must retrieve the core of Avala after defeating the creature in combat. Presenting this elemental core to the questgiver confirms that the threat has been thoroughly eliminated and the regional climate can return to normal.",al={"avala proof","what item drops","avala core proof"}},{q="How did Avala first appear?",a="Avala emerged following an initial rockslide that disrupted the region. It appeared alongside other elementals in the aftermath of the collapse, quickly establishing itself as the most prominent and dangerous creature among them due to its size and elemental power.",al={"where did avala come from","avala origin","how did avala spawn"}},{q="Who offers the quest Rime's Wrath?",a="The quest is offered by a local observer stationed near the hazardous mountain pass who closely monitored the rockslide and the emergence of the elementals. Invoking the blessing of the Holy Light upon completion, this watcher seeks to prevent unnecessary deaths by contracting capable champions to eliminate Avala.",al={"who gives rimes wrath","quest giver rimes wrath","rimes wrath questgiver"}},{q="What happens after Avala is defeated?",a="Once Avala is slain and its core returned, the severe weather swings and localized tremors triggered by the creature's presence diminish. The questgiver bestows a blessing of the Light and a reward, noting that the area has been made significantly safer for future travelers.",al={"what happens after avala dies","rimes wrath completion","after rimes wrath"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Shamans recognize the danger posed by unbridled elementals like Avala, whose turbulent presence warps the local weather and shakes the stone. Quelling such agitated beings is vital to restoring harmony between the elements.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins uphold the duty of protecting travelers and vulnerable commoners from sudden catastrophes. The questgiver invokes the Light's blessing upon the quest's completion in gratitude for averting unnecessary deaths."},kw={"avala","avala core","elemental","elemental core","rime's wrath","rockslide","weather","winter"},rel={"topic:light"},m={id=99161},src={"In-game quest text: Rime's Wrath"},fv=true}
E["quest:99162"] = {t="quest",n="Treacherous Cold",z="general",s="Treacherous Cold is a recovery effort in the treacherous high peaks of Dun Morogh following devastating snow and ice slides. Adventurers are tasked with retrieving the inscribed rifles of fallen Ironforge mountaineers to ensure their identities are confirmed and their families receive closure.",h="Treacherous Cold is a recovery effort in the treacherous high peaks of Dun Morogh following devastating snow...",sec={{t="Perils of the Peaks",b="The rugged, snowy crags of Dun Morogh pose a constant danger to travelers and soldiers alike. Even the hardened mountaineers of Ironforge can find themselves overwhelmed by sudden avalanches and sheets of falling ice, with novice recruits paying with their lives when winter strikes without warning.",sp=0},{t="Grim Recovery",b="When mountain slides bury patrols, searching the hazardous terrain becomes a grim race against time. Heightened elemental activity across the region makes thorough rescue expeditions perilous, forcing scouts to enlist brave wanderers to search the drifts for fallen mountaineers and recover their inscribed rifles before the snow conceals them forever.",sp=0},{t="Inscribed Stocks",b="Every Ironforge mountaineer engraves their name into the stock of their rifle, serving as a lasting mark of identification in the harshest conditions. By securing these firearms from the drifts, searchers ensure the fallen can be identified and their honored service recognized by their families back in Ironforge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Treacherous Cold?",a="Treacherous Cold is a task focused on searching the snow-choked passes of Dun Morogh for mountaineers caught in sudden slides and recovering their service rifles.",al={"what is treacherous cold","treacherous cold quest"}},{q="Why are the mountaineers' rifles collected?",a="Mountaineers engrave their names directly onto the stocks of their rifles. In the freezing drifts where bodies may be buried or unrecoverable, retrieving their weapons allows Ironforge to positively identify the fallen and notify their families.",al={"why collect rifles","mountaineer rifles","why do we need their guns"}},{q="Who is being lost in the snow?",a="Novice mountaineers patrolling Dun Morogh are particularly susceptible to sudden avalanches, falling ice, and extreme weather in the high mountain peaks.",al={"who are the victims","who gets lost in dun morogh"}},{q="Why hasn't the search been conducted already?",a="While the questgiver typically conducts rescue and recovery sweeps after a slide, recent elemental disturbances in Dun Morogh have made venturing deep into the mountain passes far too dangerous to handle alone.",al={"why cant the questgiver look","why need an adventurer"}},{q="What happens to the rifles once recovered?",a="Recovered rifles are gathered and sent back to the city of Ironforge, where authorities ensure the personal weapons are returned to the families of the fallen mountaineers.",al={"where do rifles go","what happens to recovered rifles"}},{q="What makes the high passes of Dun Morogh so dangerous?",a="Dun Morogh's heights are fraught with severe cold, blinding snowslides, and unstable cliffs. Coupled with restless elemental activity, even trained dwarven patrols can quickly succumb to the environment.",al={"how dangerous is dun morogh","dun morogh hazards"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Dun Morogh's peaks are the dwarven homeland, yet even seasoned mountaineers can be claimed by sudden avalanches and hostile elements in the high passes.",["class:Hunter"]="Mountaineers rely on their trusty rifles in the frozen wilderness, carving their names into the wooden stocks so their identities and deeds are never forgotten.",["faction:Alliance"]="Recovering fallen mountaineers' gear ensures that Ironforge can give closure to families who sacrifice their loved ones to keep the high roads protected."},kw={"avalanche","dun morogh","ironforge","mountaineers","rifles","snow slide","treacherous cold"},rel={"zone:dunmorogh","zone:ironforge","topic:light"},m={id=99162},src={"In-game quest text: Treacherous Cold"},fv=true}
E["quest:99191"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="The High Order is taking wool cloth from those who seek its full recognition. Sixty pieces meet its current need.",h="The High Order is taking wool cloth from those who seek its full recognition.",sec={{t="A Worthy Gift",b="The High Order is accepting wool cloth, and sixty pieces meet its current needs. The giver can take your donation now. In return, the order grants you full recognition.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the High Order need?",a="The High Order needs wool cloth, and sixty pieces meet its current need.",al={"high order wool","what to donate","wool donation"}},{q="How much wool cloth should I give?",a="Give sixty pieces of wool cloth to earn full recognition from the High Order.",al={"how much wool","sixty wool","wool amount"}},{q="Who can take the donation?",a="The giver can accept your sixty pieces of wool cloth.",al={"who takes wool","donation giver","give wool"}},{q="What do I gain by donating?",a="Giving sixty pieces of wool cloth earns you full recognition from the High Order.",al={"wool recognition","high order standing","donation benefit"}}},kw={"donation","high order","recognition","sixty pieces","wool","wool cloth"},rel={"quest:92596"},m={id=99191},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:99196"] = {t="quest",n="A Donation of Wool",z="general",s="The Windshapers are taking wool cloth donations, and sixty pieces will earn their full recognition.",h="The Windshapers are taking wool cloth donations, and sixty pieces will earn their full recognition.",sec={{t="A Gift of Wool",b="The Windshapers accept wool cloth from you. When you are ready to give, their representative can take sixty pieces.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do the Windshapers want?",a="The Windshapers are accepting wool cloth donations.",al={"windshapers donation","what wool do they take"}},{q="How much wool cloth should I give?",a="Give the Windshapers sixty pieces of wool cloth for full recognition.",al={"how much wool","sixty wool"}},{q="What do I get for donating the wool?",a="Sixty pieces of wool cloth earn the Windshapers’ full recognition for your generosity.",al={"wool donation result","windshapers recognition"}},{q="Can their representative take the wool now?",a="Their representative can take the sixty pieces when you have them with you and are ready to donate.",al={"give wool now","donate cloth"}}},kw={"donation","recognition","sixty pieces","windshapers","wool","wool cloth"},m={id=99196},src={"In-game quest text: A Donation of Wool"},fv=true}
E["quest:99260"] = {t="quest",n="Fillion's Mission",z="general",s="Fillion lies wounded after his survey at the Overlook Standing Stones. The readings meant to guide skycutters between Skywall and Azeroth are presumed lost.",h="Fillion lies wounded after his survey at the Overlook Standing Stones.",sec={{t="A Perilous Assignment",b="Fillion went to the Overlook Standing Stones to measure elemental currents. Those readings are needed to calculate safe courses for skycutter vessels between Skywall and Azeroth. After an ordeal that required his rescue, he is too wounded to finish the work. The vital data is presumed lost, so he sends his rescuer to report to the Supreme Magister.",sp=0},{t="Planar Strife in Valanaar",b="In Valanaar, Supreme Magister Elaadrin Evengale is relieved to hear that Fillion survived, though the research is lost. Without reliable readings, safe routes across the Elemental Plane will be hard to open. Reports say the rival Windshapers have found their own way through the planes. They refuse to share it, putting factional secrecy before the welfare of all.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Fillion?",a="Fillion is an emissary or scout sent by the Supreme Magister to measure elemental currents at the Overlook Standing Stones. After an adventurer rescues him, he is too weak to finish the work.",al={"who is fillion","fillion backstory","fillion wow"}},{q="Who is Supreme Magister Elaadrin Evengale?",a="Elaadrin Evengale is the Supreme Magister in Valanaar. He directs efforts to navigate the elemental plane safely and recover planar data for travel between Skywall and Azeroth.",al={"who is supreme magister","elaadrin evengale","who is elaadrin"}},{q="Why was Fillion's data so important?",a="The readings track elemental currents at the Overlook Standing Stones. They are needed to calculate safe courses for skycutters through the elemental plane to Azeroth.",al={"why is data important","what data fillion lost","elemental data skywall"}},{q="Who are the Windshapers?",a="The Windshapers are a faction reported to have found their own way through the planes. Supreme Magister Evengale says they guard it out of dogmatic hubris instead of sharing it for the wider good of their people.",al={"who are windshapers","windshapers lore","windshaper rivals"}},{q="What are skycutters?",a="Skycutters are vessels made to fly through the elemental planes. They need precise readings of elemental currents to travel between Skywall and Azeroth safely.",al={"what are skycutters","skycutter ship","sky cutter"}},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a faction Supreme Magister Evengale regards with disdain. He compares the Windshapers' selfish, dogmatic conduct to theirs.",al={"who are alaketh","al'aketh","alaketh lore"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a Mage, you can appreciate the delicate magical calculations needed to guide skycutter vessels through planar turbulence.",["race:Skyborne"]="As one of the Skyborne, you know the struggle to establish safe routes between Skywall and Azeroth amid factional discord."},kw={"elaadrin evengale","elemental plane","fillion","overlook standing stones","skycutter","skywall","valanaar","windshapers"},rel={"quest:92699","topic:skywall","topic:elemental-plane","quest:92595"},m={id=99260},src={"In-game quest text: Fillion's Mission"},fv=true}
E["quest:99411"] = {t="quest",n="Kyle's Gone Missing!",z="general",s="Kyle's Gone Missing! is a local quest in Bloodhoof Village where Ahab Wheathoof asks passing adventurers to find his wayward dog, Kyle the Frenzied. Feeding the hungry pup a cut of tender strider meat soothes his temper and ensures his safe return home.",h="Kyle's Gone Missing!",sec={{t="A Missing Hound",b="In Bloodhoof Village, elder Ahab Wheathoof worries over his wayward pet, Kyle the Frenzied. While the title might sound alarming to outsiders, Kyle is Ahab's prized puppy who has slipped away onto the grassy plains of Mulgore. Ahab asks travelers passing through the settlement to track down his unruly companion and offer him a hearty meal to calm his wild spirits.",sp=0},{t="A Simple Remedy",b="Pacifying Kyle requires a cut of tender strider meat, sourced from the swift plainstriders that roam Mulgore. Presenting the treat satisfies the hungry pup, soothing his frenzied mood and nudging him safely back toward Bloodhoof Village and his grateful owner.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ahab Wheathoof?",a="Ahab Wheathoof is an elder tauren living at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Caring deeply for his prized dog, Kyle, Ahab turns to traveling adventurers to help locate and feed the runaway canine when he wanders too far from home.",al={"who is ahab wheathoof","ahab mulgore","quest giver kyle"}},{q="Who is Kyle the Frenzied?",a="Kyle is Ahab Wheathoof's beloved canine pet, known as Kyle the Frenzied. Despite his ferocious title, he is Ahab's prized companion and can be pacified and lured back simply by feeding him tender strider meat.",al={"who is kyle","kyle dog","kyle the frenzied"}},{q="Where does this task take place?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement situated in the heart of Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff. It serves as a vital gathering hub for local tribes, traders, and herders.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof location"}},{q="Why does Kyle need tender strider meat?",a="Kyle has a particular appetite for fresh meat cut from the plainstriders wandering Mulgore. Offering tender strider meat pacifies the hungry hound and convinces him to return safely to Ahab.",al={"why tender strider meat","strider meat kyle","how to calm kyle"}},{q="Why is Kyle considered frenzied?",a="Kyle is known locally as 'the Frenzied,' roaming the plains when lost or famished. His frenzy subsides once he is properly fed by a patient traveler.",al={"why is kyle frenzied","kyle angry"}},{q="Where can Kyle be found?",a="Kyle wanders the open plains surrounding Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Travelers usually track him down by scanning the prairies nearby until they can offer him a meal.",al={"kyle location","where is kyle"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Ahab Wheathoof is a resident of Bloodhoof Village, making this personal errand a familiar gesture of community support across the pastures of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the local villagers of Bloodhoof with everyday matters strengthens ties with the Shu'halo as they settle and defend their homeland."},kw={"ahab wheathoof","bloodhoof village","kyle","kyle the frenzied","kyle's gone missing!","mulgore","plainstrider","tender strider meat"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={id=99411},src={"In-game quest text: Kyle's Gone Missing!"},fv=true}
E["topic:agamaggan"] = {t="topic",n="Agamaggan",z="general",s="Agamaggan the Great Boar was one of the colossal Ancient Guardians who walked Azeroth in its earliest days. He fell ten thousand years ago fighting the Burning Legion in the War of the Ancients, and his sacrifice helped save Azeroth from ruin. Where his blood and body crashed into the earth, vast networks of thorny vines arose, creating the Razorfen and granting life and purpose to his descendants, the quilboar.",h="Agamaggan the Great Boar was one of the colossal Ancient Guardians who walked Azeroth in its earliest days.",sec={{t="The War of the Ancients",b="Ten thousand years ago, during the War of the Ancients, the mighty demigod Agamaggan came forth to battle the Burning Legion. The colossal boar fought with terrifying ferocity, and though he ultimately fell in combat, his actions helped save Azeroth from ruin.",sp=0},{t="The Razorfen and Blood Shards",b="Where Agamaggan collapsed in the southern Barrens, his colossal corpse reshaped the land. His spilled blood soaked into the soil, causing enormous, impenetrable brambles and thorny vines to burst upward from the dry earth. These tangled thickets became known as the Razorfen. The quilboar revere the site as their holiest ground, viewing the labyrinth of Razorfen Kraul as his resting body and Razorfen Downs as his gaping skull. Scattered throughout the Barrens are crystallized Blood Shards, believed to be the physical remnants of the demigod's spilled blood, pulsing with lingering primal energy that quilboar geomancers and shamans harness for power.",sp=0},{t="Father of the Quilboar",b="Agamaggan is revered as the divine progenitor of all quilboar. They view the surrounding brambles not merely as homes, but as the living armor left behind by their creator to shelter them from hostile neighbors. Quilboar tribes believe their god's spirit watches over them, granting martial vigor to their warriors and earth magic to their geomancers. In turn, they aggressively defend the thorns against encroaching tauren, orcs, and centaur, convinced that their sacred duty is to guard the fallen body of the boar god who birthed them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan is an Ancient Guardian, a colossal boar wild god who existed in the earliest eras of Azeroth. He fought alongside Cenarius and the night elves against the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients, sacrificing himself to save the world.",al={"agamaggan lore","who is the great boar","agamaggan background"},it="identity"},{q="How did the Razorfen form?",a="When Agamaggan died in battle against the Burning Legion, his colossal body fell in what is now the southern Barrens. Wherever his divine blood touched the ground, massive thorny briars surged upward, creating the dense thickets known as Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs.",al={"how did razorfen get there","why are there huge thorns in the barrens","razorfen origin"},it="origin"},{q="What is the connection between Agamaggan and the quilboar?",a="The quilboar are the direct descendants of Agamaggan. They worship him fervently as their creator and god, building their settlements within his thorny remains and dedicating their shamanic rituals to his memory.",al={"are quilboar related to agamaggan","quilboar god","who made the quilboar"},it="ties"},{q="What are Blood Shards?",a="Blood Shards are red crystalline stones found in the Barrens, said to be formed from the dried, concentrated blood of Agamaggan. They hold residual primal magic, which quilboar geomancers and curious spellcasters use to empower their physical strength and rituals.",al={"what do blood shards do","blood shards lore","blood of agamaggan"},it="item"},{q="Did night elves worship Agamaggan?",a="Yes. Night elves revere him as one of the noble Ancients who gave his life defending Kalimdor from the Legion.",al={"night elves agamaggan"}},{q="Is Agamaggan's skull visible in the world?",a="Quilboar tradition holds that the great gaping entrance leading into Razorfen Downs is the hollowed skull and jaw of Agamaggan himself. Quilboar believe the maw will consume and destroy any trespassers who are not loyal to their god.",al={"razorfen downs skull","where is agamaggan head","agamaggan entrance"},it="place"},{q="What is the history of Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan was one of the primeval Ancient Guardians, revered as the Great Boar. Ten thousand years ago, during the War of the Ancients, he came forth to battle the Burning Legion. Though the colossal boar fell in combat, his actions helped save Azeroth from ruin, and where his blood soaked the earth, the great thorny vines of the Razorfen arose.",al={"agamaggan lore","story of agamaggan","how did agamaggan die","agamaggan war of the ancients"},it="history",add="f6e0fe38"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Druids hold Agamaggan in high esteem as one of the Ancient Guardians who fought alongside Cenarius, honoring his sacrifice during the War of the Ancients.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman who venture into the Barrens can sense the potent, lingering earth and spirit energies embedded within Agamaggan's blood and the Razorfen thorns.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elves remember Agamaggan as the fierce demigod who gave his life battling the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde contends daily with Agamaggan's quilboar children, whose fanatical devotion to their fallen god drives relentless attacks across the Barrens."},kw={"agamaggan","ancient guardian","barrens","blood shards","cenarius","great boar","quilboar","razorfen","war of the ancients"},rel={"topic:quilboar","topic:razorfen","zone:razorfenkraul","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:cenarius","quest:5052","quest:5044"},src={"Agamaggan"}}
E["topic:alliance"] = {t="topic",n="Alliance",z="general",s="Across Azeroth, the Alliance now gathers its peoples around Stormwind. Its roots lie in Lordaeron's ruin, and its heart is Stormwind City, beside Ironforge's dwarves, the exiled gnomes, and Kalimdor's night elves.",h="Across Azeroth, the Alliance now gathers its peoples around Stormwind.",sec={{t="From Lordaeron to Stormwind",b="The modern Alliance descends from the Alliance of Lordaeron. During the Second War, Sir Anduin Lothar led Stormwind refugees north and united the Seven Kingdoms, dwarves, and high elves against the Old Horde.\n\nThe Scourge and the Third War ravaged the north. Lordaeron lay in ruins, and survivors were driven into scattered resistance. The restored Kingdom of Stormwind filled the void. Today it is the foremost seat of human leadership, carrying honor and faith into a fractured world.",sp=0},{t="A Coalition of Necessity and Kinship",b="In the Eastern Kingdoms, the Alliance rests on the steadfast bond between humans and the dwarves of Ironforge, ruled by King Magni Bronzebeard. Gnomes also live among the dwarves, driven from Gnomeregan by a devastating trogg uprising.\n\nHigh Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque guides the exiled gnomes. They bring their brilliant engineering to the Alliance war effort, believing the coalition must survive if they are ever to reclaim their irradiated home.",sp=0},{t="The Kalimdor Front",b="Across the Great Sea, the reclusive night elves left millennia of isolation after the Battle of Mount Hyjal. They lost their immortality and now clash bitterly with the Horde over logging in Ashenvale. So the kaldorei allied with their newfound mortal companions.\n\nCultural friction remains. Some traditionalists condemn the presence of humans and dwarves in ancient forests. The Alliance offers vital military and diplomatic help to guard Kalimdor from demonic remnants and Horde expansion.",sp=0},{t="Uneasy Shadows",b="The Alliance faces strains within its own ranks. King Varian Wrynn vanished under mysterious circumstances while traveling to a diplomatic summit. His young son Anduin took the throne under Highlord Bolvar Fordragon's regency.\n\nRelations with former human allies have also frayed. The maritime kingdom of Kul Tiras keeps its distance, while Sylvanas Windrunner's undead Forsaken hold the ruins of Lordaeron.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What races make up the Alliance?",a="The Alliance's core peoples are the humans of Stormwind, the mountain dwarves of Ironforge, the exiled gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the night elves of Kalimdor.",al={"alliance races","who is in the alliance","playable alliance races"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the Alliance right now?",a="No single absolute monarch rules the whole Alliance. Stormwind is ruled by the boy king anduin wrynn under highlord bolvar fordragon, his regent, after King Varian Wrynn disappeared. The other member nations follow King Magni Bronzebeard of Ironforge, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque of the gnomes, and the night elf leadership on Kalimdor.",al={"alliance leader","who is high king","who runs stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Alliance of Lordaeron become the modern Alliance?",a="King Terenas Menethil II and Anduin Lothar established the original Alliance to repel the Old Horde during the Second War. After the Scourge destroyed Lordaeron in the Third War, leadership shifted south to the rebuilt Kingdom of Stormwind, which forged new covenants with the dwarves, gnomes, and night elves.",al={"alliance of lordaeron vs alliance","what happened to lordaeron","alliance origin"},it="history"},{q="Why did the night elves join the Alliance?",a="After the Third War and the Battle of Mount Hyjal, the night elves lost their immortality and could no longer remain secluded. The Orcish Horde was actively deforesting Warsong Gulch in Ashenvale, so the kaldorei made a military pact with the humans and dwarves to defend their ancestral lands.",al={"why are night elves alliance","night elf alliance lore","kaldorei alliance"},it="ties"},{q="Why is Kul Tiras not part of the Alliance right now?",a="Kul Tiras was a pillar of the old Alliance of Lordaeron, but it has kept to itself since that alliance splintered. Its Grand Admiral, Daelin Proudmoore, invaded Durotar and was slain by the Horde. The island kingdom has not joined the new Alliance led from Stormwind.",al={"where is kul tiras","is kul tiras alliance","daelin proudmoore kul tiras"}},{q="Why didn't the gnomes help during the Third War?",a="During the Third War, a subterranean trogg invasion struck Gnomeregan. Believing the Scourge threat to the surface was greater, the gnomes concealed their crisis from their allies and fought alone. Radiation and invasion brought down the city, forcing survivors into Ironforge.",al={"where were gnomes in warcraft 3","gnomes third war","gnomeregan fall"},it="history"},{q="What is the Alliance's stance toward the Forsaken?",a="Many in the Alliance regard the undead as abominations and tools of the Scourge that destroyed Lordaeron. The Alliance remains openly hostile toward the undead who are reclaiming the northern kingdom.",al={"alliance view of forsaken","why hate undead","forsaken alliance relations"},it="hostile"},{q="What is the Alliance?",a="The Alliance is a grand coalition of mortal races dedicated to nobility, honor, justice, and preserving order across Azeroth. Evolved from the remnants of the fallen Alliance of Lordaeron, it unites humans, Ironforge dwarves, gnomes, and night elves under a shared purpose. Rather than being bound purely by desperation, its members are tied together by common traditions, faith, and an abiding loathing of demonic corruption. Together, they pool their military might, technical skill, and ancient wisdom to protect their people and bring light to a war-torn world.",al={"what is the alliance","who are the alliance","grand alliance"},it="identity",add="90827062"},{q="Why does the Alliance fight the Horde?",a="The conflict runs deep, born from generations of bloodshed. The Alliance remembers the brutality of the First and Second Wars, when the original Horde destroyed the kingdom of Stormwind and devastated the Eastern Kingdoms. Even after uniting temporarily at Mount Hyjal, peace remains fragile and bitter. Ongoing resource skirmishes, such as the Warsong Clan's continued logging in the ancestral forests of Ashenvale, keep animosity boiling. To many across the Alliance, the Horde represents an untamed and dangerous threat that must constantly be guarded against.",al={"why fight horde","why hate horde","alliance horde conflict","why war with horde"},it="conflict",add="76fd3754"},{q="What does it mean to serve the Alliance?",a="To champion the Alliance is to stand as a bulwark of duty, justice, and order against the encroaching darkness. Whether you hail from the halls of Ironforge, the towers of Stormwind, the refugee workshops of Tinker Town, or the sacred boughs of Teldrassil, you are sworn to protect allied lands from monsters, invaders, and the savage reach of enemies. Serving means putting the greater good and noble ideals above individual gain, upholding sacred traditions, and ensuring the survival of free peoples across Azeroth.",al={"serving the alliance","alliance duty","what does alliance expect"},it="for_you",add="760e04fc"}},ang={["race:Human"]="As the chief architects of the faction's recovery, humans see the Alliance as civilization's rightful defender and a guardian of the Church of the Holy Light against wild and demonic forces.",["race:Dwarf"]="The dwarves of Ironforge are humanity's most steadfast and loyal allies. They lend the Alliance their formidable iron, mountain infantry, and deep friendship.",["race:Gnome"]="As refugees in Ironforge, gnomes bring their scientific ingenuity to Alliance armadas. They trust that loyalty between allies will one day provide the strength to liberate Gnomeregan.",["race:NightElf"]="Driven from isolation by the Third War, night elves must find a necessary kinship with the younger races to check Horde aggression in Ashenvale.",["faction:Alliance"]="Your sworn faction fights across two continents to preserve law, tradition, and mortal sovereignty against the Scourge, wild beasts, and the advancing Horde.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde sees the Alliance as an entrenched, expansionist power whose self-righteous ideals threaten its members' sovereignty and survival on their lands."},kw={"alliance","alliance of lordaeron","anduin wrynn","bolvar fordragon","darnassus","gnomeregan","grand alliance","ironforge","magni bronzebeard","mekkatorque","stormwind"},rel={"topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:horde","zone:stormwind","zone:ironforge","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:varian-wrynn","topic:second-war"},src={"Alliance"}}
E["topic:alliance-of-lordaeron"] = {t="topic",n="Alliance of Lordaeron",z="general",s="The Alliance of Lordaeron was the historic grand coalition of the seven human kingdoms, Khaz Modan dwarves, Wildhammer gryphon riders, gnomes, and high elves formed to resist the Old Horde in the Second War. Led militarily by Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and diplomatically by King Terenas Menethil II, it shattered the invading orcish forces and secured the Eastern Kingdoms. Though political fractures and the devastation of the Scourge tore it apart during the Third War, its legacy laid the foundation for the current Alliance.",h="The Alliance of Lordaeron was the historic grand coalition of the seven human kingdoms, Khaz Modan dwarves...",sec={{t="A Coalition Born in Ashes",b="Following the horrific sack of Stormwind in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar brought the battered southern refugees across the Great Sea to Capital City. Pleading their cause before King Terenas Menethil II, Lothar convinced him to gather the rulers of the human nations in council. Old rivalries and border tensions ran deep, but the threat of the Horde outweighed them. Choosing Lothar as their Supreme Commander, the seven human nations bound themselves together in a desperate pact.",sp=0},{t="The Second War and the New Vanguard",b="Facing brutal orcish warriors, dark warlocks, and monstrous red dragons, the Alliance forged entirely new doctrines of war. Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Lothar established the Knights of the Silver Hand, elevating holy paladins like Uther the Lightbringer to counter the Horde's dark magic. When the war raged across the Hinterlands and quelled the siege of Aerie Peak, Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his fierce gryphon riders to rule the skies. Shortly thereafter, the Bronzebeard dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes were liberated, joining high elven rangers under Alleria Windrunner. Despite the treacherous betrayal of King Perenolde of Alterac, the grand coalition drove the Horde back to Blackrock Spire and crushed their dominion.",sp=0},{t="Fracture and Ruin",b="Victory brought bitterness rather than enduring peace. Bitter disputes erupted over the high taxes required to maintain the orc internment camps and rebuild the blasted kingdom of Stormwind. Seeing little personal gain, Gilneas built the Greymane Wall and severed ties, Stromgarde departed over territorial and prisoner quarrels, and Quel'Thalas pulled back behind its enchanted runestones. The coalition that remained under King Terenas attempted to weather the political storm, only to be utterly dismantled during the Third War when the Cult of the Damned and the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron. Arthas Menethil's betrayal tore the heart out of the kingdom, ending the golden age of the northern realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What was the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="It was the military and political coalition formed between the Seven Kingdoms of humanity, the dwarves of Ironforge and Aerie Peak, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas to fight Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde during the Second War.",al={"what is lordaeron alliance","who was in the first alliance","grand alliance history"},it="identity"},{q="Why was the Alliance formed in Capital City instead of Stormwind?",a="Stormwind had been completely conquered and destroyed during the First War. Regent Lord Anduin Lothar led the surviving population north by ship to Lordaeron to warn King Terenas Menethil II of the alien threat.",al={"why lordaeron","stormwind in second war","why did lothar go north"},it="origin"},{q="Who led the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="King Terenas Menethil II hosted the coalition and guided its political diplomacy, while Sir Anduin Lothar was named Supreme Commander of all allied armed forces. Daelin Proudmoore served as Grand Admiral of the fleets.",al={"leader of lordaeron alliance","supreme commander","who was in charge"},it="leader"},{q="Why did Alterac betray the Alliance?",a="Fearing that the Orcish Horde was unstoppable, King Aiden Perenolde secretly struck a deal with Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. Alterac offered the Horde safe passage through its mountain passes in exchange for being spared from slaughter.",al={"perenolde betrayal","what did alterac do","alterac second war"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Alliance of Lordaeron fall apart?",a="After the Second War, disagreements over the cost of internment camps and rebuilding Stormwind caused Gilneas, Stromgarde, and Quel'Thalas to secede. The remnants were later destroyed during the Third War when Prince Arthas and the Scourge annihilated Lordaeron.",al={"why did alliance collapse","what happened to the old alliance","why did gilneas leave"},it="ruin"},{q="How is this different from the Alliance of today?",a="The Alliance of Lordaeron was centered entirely in the northern kingdoms around Capital City and Dalaran. Today's Alliance is centered in the rebuilt southern kingdom of Stormwind and includes the night elves of Kalimdor, though it draws inspiration from Lothar's original coalition.",al={"old alliance vs new alliance","difference between alliances","is this the current alliance"},it="today"},{q="Who was part of the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="The coalition united all seven human kingdoms: Lordaeron, Stormwind, Stromgarde, Kul Tiras, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. As the Second War escalated, they were joined by the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, the Wildhammer dwarves of Aerie Peak, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas, alongside the paladins of the Silver Hand and the Church of the Holy Light.",al={"alliance of lordaeron members","who joined alliance of lordaeron","alliance of lordaeron nations"},it="allies",add="dd426ccb"},{q="How was the Alliance of Lordaeron formed?",a="After the Orcish Horde destroyed Stormwind in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar fled north with the survivors to seek aid from King Terenas Menethil II. Terenas gathered the rulers of the human nations in Capital City to confront the threat. Setting aside their rivalries, the kingdoms united under Lothar's supreme command.",al={"alliance of lordaeron history","how did alliance of lordaeron start","creation of alliance of lordaeron"},it="history",add="62444fc6"},{q="Why was it called the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="It earned the name because the northern kingdom of Lordaeron served as the heart and rallying ground of the union. King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron called the Seven Kingdoms together in Capital City to answer the fall of Stormwind, and Lordaeron stood as the political anchor and primary military supplier through the Second War.",al={"why alliance of lordaeron","alliance of lordaeron name meaning","why called alliance of lordaeron"},it="name",add="913db280"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The memory of the Alliance of Lordaeron provides the philosophical foundation and heroic ideals for the modern Alliance centered in Stormwind.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were created specifically by Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Anduin Lothar to serve as the divine shock troops of the Alliance of Lordaeron.",["race:Human"]="The alliance brought all Seven Kingdoms together under one banner for the only time since the ancient Empire of Arathor, before regional pettiness and the Scourge scattered them.",["race:Dwarf"]="Both the Ironforge Bronzebeards and the Wildhammers of Aerie Peak forged their modern ties to human civilization during the battles of the Second War."},kw={"alliance of lordaeron","anduin lothar","capital city","grand alliance","second war","seven kingdoms","silver hand","terenas menethil"},rel={"topic:anduin-lothar","topic:terenas-menethil-ii","topic:second-war","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:lordaeron","topic:scourge","topic:alterac-kingdom"},src={"Alliance of Lordaeron"}}
E["topic:alterac-kingdom"] = {t="topic",n="Alterac (kingdom)",z="general",s="Alterac was one of the seven historic human kingdoms, nestled high in the rugged Alterac Mountains. During the Second War, its ruler, King Aiden Perenolde, made a treacherous pact with the Old Horde to spare his realm, leading to the kingdom's military downfall and total collapse. Today, its ruined capital lies abandoned in the snow, while dispossessed nobles and outlaws wage a guerrilla war under the banner of the Syndicate.",h="Alterac was one of the seven historic human kingdoms, nestled high in the rugged Alterac Mountains.",sec={{t="A Nation in the Crags",b="Alterac arose thousands of years ago as an Arathorian city-state following the Troll Wars. Settlers capitalized on strategic mountain passes and trading outposts, eventually establishing an independent realm when the empire fractured. Though rugged and mountainous, Alterac bordered the prosperous lands of Lordaeron and Stromgarde. Historically, it fielded tough mountain fighters, but enduring border friction with Thoras Trollbane's Stromgarde left the kingdom politically isolated from its neighbors long before the Dark Portal ever opened.",sp=0},{t="The Great Betrayal",b="When Sir Anduin Lothar forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, King Aiden Perenolde joined only with deep reluctance. As Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde swept north, Perenolde concluded humanity was doomed and secretly struck a deal with the Warchief. In exchange for the safety of Alterac, Perenolde opened fortified mountain passes, conspired against Alliance forces, and even dispatched soldiers and sailors to assist the Horde's siege of Lordaeron. The treachery allowed the orcs to reach Capital City far ahead of schedule, shaking the Alliance to its core when the truth was laid bare.",sp=0},{t="Ruin and the Syndicate",b="Retribution was swift. Stromgarde's forces marched into the mountains, exposing Perenolde's treason and breaking Alterac's military. King Terenas Menethil II placed the fallen nation under martial law, while arguments over who should inherit the throne paralyzed Alliance diplomats for years. Following the kingdom's collapse, King Perenolde died in disgrace. His son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, rallied the bitter, displaced nobility to form the Syndicate. Wearing the orange cloth of their fallen homeland, these bandits and mercenaries now fight ruthlessly to reclaim their ancestral holdings across Hillsbrad and Alterac.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to the Kingdom of Alterac?",a="Alterac collapsed during the Second War after its king, Aiden Perenolde, betrayed humanity to side with the Old Horde. Alliance armies, spearheaded by Stromgarde, marched in and dismantled the kingdom's military. The crown was dissolved, the realm was placed under martial law, and its people fragmented into ruin.",al={"alterac downfall","why is alterac destroyed","fate of alterac","who destroyed alterac"},it="ruin"},{q="Why did King Perenolde betray the Alliance?",a="Aiden Perenolde believed the Alliance could not defeat the invading Horde. Fearing the total destruction of his mountain realm, he secretly allied with Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. Doomhammer promised that Alterac would be left untouched and granted sovereign protection once Lordaeron fell, in exchange for open passage through the mountains.",al={"why did perenolde betray","perenolde motive","alterac betrayal reason"},it="motive"},{q="How does the Syndicate relate to Alterac?",a="The Syndicate is the criminal continuation of Alterac's nobility, founded by King Perenolde's son, Lord Aliden Perenolde. The organization seeks to take back their family estates and land across Hillsbrad and Alterac by force. Their signature orange masks match the historic national color of Alterac.",al={"syndicate connection alterac","who leads the syndicate","why orange masks"},it="ties"},{q="What are Alterac's traditional colors and symbols?",a="Alterac's national color is orange. This crest and color scheme were worn by its royal military during the Second War and are preserved today by the Syndicate, whose members wear orange masks and hoods.",al={"alterac flag","alterac color"},it="identity"},{q="Who controls Alterac City right now?",a="The ruined capital of Alterac City has no recognized monarch or lawful government. It is largely a shattered ruin overrun by Syndicate outlaws, ogres, and wild mountain beasts, with no formal Alliance garrison capable of holding the high passes.",al={"who rules alterac now","alterac city ruins","who lives in alterac city"},it="in_charge"},{q="What was the Kingdom of Alterac?",a="Alterac was one of the seven historic human nations, settled in the high, rugged peaks of the Alterac Mountains. Originating as a fortified city-state of the ancient empire of Arathor, it grew into an independent kingdom commanding strategic mountain passes and trading routes. Though smaller and militarily weaker than neighbors like Lordaeron and Stromgarde, Alterac held vital territory between the northern kingdoms until its catastrophic treason during the Second War shattered its standing forever.",al={"what is alterac","alterac kingdom overview","who are alterac"},it="identity",add="a8320e62"},{q="Where did the Kingdom of Alterac come from?",a="Alterac began as one of the city-states founded by the ancient human empire of Arathor. When Arathor fractured, Alterac became an independent kingdom, often clashing with neighboring Stromgarde, before joining the Alliance of Lordaeron against the Orcish Horde.",al={"alterac history","origin of alterac","how was alterac founded"},it="history",add="c0d5a913"},{q="Who leads the people of Alterac now?",a="The kingdom itself has no recognized sovereign or unified government following the imprisonment and death of King Aiden Perenolde. Its remnants are fractured: many dispossessed nobles and citizens rally under Aiden's son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, who commands the Syndicate in a lawless campaign to reclaim their ancestral lands. Meanwhile, their devastated capital remains contested by wild beasts and rival factions.",al={"who leads alterac","king of alterac","alterac leader"},it="leader",add="6b4d83cf"},{q="Who is Alterac allied with?",a="As a recognized nation, Alterac has no allies left. It broke its original pact with the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War to aid Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, earning the lasting enmity of the other human realms. Today, its noble remnants within the Syndicate operate as outlaws, working with hired cutthroats and rogues while fighting both the Alliance and the new Horde for survival.",al={"alterac allies","who does alterac fight for","who supports alterac"},it="allies",add="080e3676"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="To the Alliance, Alterac is a bitter reminder of treason that nearly cost humanity everything during the Second War. Its memory fuels ongoing hostility toward the Syndicate bandits attempting to reclaim their ancestral estates.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the northern kingdoms, Alterac's fall is a tragic lesson in cowardice and division, showing how easily fear can shatter the unity forged against foreign invaders.",["class:Rogue"]="The mountain valleys of former Alterac are a cutthroat haven for covert operatives, split between the highborn thugs of the Syndicate and the secretive assassins of Ravenholdt Manor."},kw={"aiden","aliden","alterac","betrayal","horde","orgrim doomhammer","perenolde","second war","seven kingdoms","syndicate","treason"},rel={"topic:syndicate","topic:second-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","zone:alterac","zone:hillsbrad","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:terenas-menethil-ii","subzone:ravenholdt-manor"},src={"Alterac (kingdom)"}}
E["topic:anduin-lothar"] = {t="topic",n="Anduin Lothar",z="general",s="Sir Anduin Lothar, known as the Lion of Stormwind, was the last descendant of the ancient Arathi bloodline and the Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron. A childhood companion of King Llane Wrynn and the Guardian Medivh, Lothar led humanity through the horrors of the First War and rallied the northern nations during the Second War before falling at Blackrock Mountain.",h="Sir Anduin Lothar, known as the Lion of Stormwind, was the last descendant of the ancient Arathi bloodline...",sec={{t="The Lion's Youth",b="Carrying the ancient bloodline of the Arathi kings, Lothar grew up within Stormwind's royal court alongside Prince Llane Wrynn and Medivh. The trio embarked on reckless excursions into the wilderness, skirmishing with Gurubashi trolls in Stranglethorn Vale. Lothar rose rapidly through Stormwind's martial ranks, joining the King's Honor Guard and earning renown as an Armsman of the Brotherhood of the Horse.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Stormwind",b="When mysterious green-skinned invaders poured from the Black Morass, King Llane named Lothar the King's Champion to mount the kingdom's defense. Lothar pioneered devastating ambushes against orcish raiding parties across Westfall, the Redridge Mountains, and Elwynn Forest, outmaneuvering Warchief Blackhand and breaking the first siege of Stormwind City. Yet betrayal doomed the kingdom from within: Medivh was revealed as the architect who opened the Dark Portal, compelling Lothar and the apprentice Khadgar to storm Karazhan and execute the corrupted Guardian. Returning to find King Llane assassinated and Stormwind burning, Lothar gathered the survivors—including young Prince Varian—and evacuated them north across the Great Sea.",sp=1},{t="Supreme Commander and Blackrock Mountain",b="Arriving on the shores of Lordaeron, Lothar presented the grim reality of the Horde to King Terenas Menethil II. Invoking his ancestral Arathi lineage and diplomatic gravitas, he unified the human realms, Ironforge dwarves, and high elves into the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Named Supreme Commander of the united forces, Lothar spearheaded the campaign that pushed the Horde back south to the foot of Blackrock Mountain. There, in a fateful clash near the end of the Second War, Lothar was slain by Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. His death did not shatter the Alliance; instead, it galvanized his lieutenant, Turalyon, to lead the charge that broke the Horde forever.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Anduin Lothar?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar was a knight of Stormwind and the last descendant of the Arathi bloodline. He served as King's Champion during the First War and later became the Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. He is remembered as one of the greatest heroes and tacticians in human history.",al={"who is lothar","who was lothar","lion of stormwind lore"},it="identity"},{q="How did Lothar die?",a="Lothar was slain during the climax of the Second War at the base of Blackrock Mountain. He fought Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer in single combat and fell just before Alliance forces under Turalyon routed the Horde and pursued them to the Dark Portal.",al={"lothar death","how lothar died","who killed lothar"},it="history"},{q="Why is the current prince named Anduin?",a="King Varian Wrynn named his young son, Prince Anduin Wrynn, in honor of Sir Anduin Lothar. Lothar rescued Varian as a child from the ruins of Stormwind, protected him in exile, and reclaimed their homeland through his sacrifice in the Second War.",al={"why is anduin named anduin","anduin wrynn name origin"}},{q="What was Lothar's connection to Medivh and King Llane?",a="Lothar, Prince Llane Wrynn, and the young mage Medivh were inseparable childhood friends in the royal court of Stormwind. They explored Azeroth together in their youth, fighting trolls and beasts. Tragically, Lothar was later forced to lead the strike against Medivh at Karazhan after discovering his friend had betrayed the realm and summoned the Horde.",al={"lothar medivh llane","lothar childhood friends"},it="allies"},{q="Why is Lothar called the 'Last of the Arathi'?",a="Lothar was the final direct scion of the ancient Arathi line, the dynasty that originally united humanity and founded the empire of Arathor millennia ago. This prestigious heritage gave him immense diplomatic standing when uniting the squabbling northern kings under the Alliance banner in Lordaeron.",al={"lothar arathi bloodline","arathi descendant"},it="name"},{q="What was Lothar's role in the Brotherhood of the Horse?",a="Lothar was knighted early in his career and rose to the position of Armsman within the Brotherhood of the Horse, Stormwind's elite order of mounted knights. He utilized their disciplined cavalry charges to repeatedly outflank and disrupt Horde formations throughout the First War.",al={"brotherhood of the horse lothar","knights of stormwind lothar"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="To humanity, Lothar is the ultimate paragon of sacrifice and honor. His Arathi blood brought the disparate human kingdoms together, and his tragic death at Blackrock Mountain sealed the kingdom's liberation.",["faction:Alliance"]="Lothar was the foundational architect and first Supreme Commander of the Alliance, uniting elves, dwarves, and humans to push the Horde out of the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Warrior"]="Regarded as one of the finest armsmen and battlefield commanders who ever lived, Lothar's mastery of mounted tactics and sheer martial resolve serve as an inspiration to warriors across Azeroth.",["class:Paladin"]="Lothar embraced the nascent Knights of the Silver Hand during the Second War, placing his trust in paladins like Turalyon, who took up Lothar's shattered sword after his fall to lead the Alliance to victory."},kw={"anduin lothar","arathi","blackrock mountain","brotherhood of the horse","lion of stormwind","llane wrynn","lothar","supreme commander"},rel={"topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:medivh","topic:varian-wrynn","subzone:blackrock-mountain","quest:2997","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},src={"Anduin Lothar"}}
E["topic:argent-dawn"] = {t="topic",n="Argent Dawn",z="general",s="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to stamping out malevolent threats across Azeroth, most notably the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Formed by survivors of the Knights of the Silver Hand who rejected the fanatical zealotry of the Scarlet Crusade, they welcome champions of any race or faction who honor the cause of the Light. From their fortified sanctuary at Light's Hope Chapel to far-flung outposts across Kalimdor, they stand on the front lines against darkness.",h="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to stamping out malevolent threats across Azeroth...",sec={{t="A Schism of Faith",b="When Highlord Alexandros Mograine fell and Lordaeron collapsed into ruin, the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand broke into bitter disagreement. Many survivors surrendered to grief and paranoia, morphing into the xenophobic Scarlet Crusade. Yet veterans like Lord Maxwell Tyrosus recognized that their former comrades had abandoned the true tenets of the Holy Light. Refusing to succumb to madness or abandon the dead kingdom, they split away to form the Argent Dawn—an order committed to defending Azeroth alongside anyone willing to fight, whether Alliance or Horde.",sp=0},{t="Front Lines of the Plaguelands",b="The Dawn's primary stronghold is Light's Hope Chapel, situated in the blighted expanse of the Eastern Plaguelands. From this consecrated bastion, the order coordinates an unending war against Kel'Thuzad's necromancers and skeletal legions. Operating forward camps at Chillwind Camp and outside the Bulwark in Tirisfal Glades, Argent officers issue enchanted commissions to adventurers, paying bounties in exchange for Scourgestones wrested from fallen undead. Despite lacking the raw manpower of the Scarlet Crusade, the Dawn holds the line through perseverance, unity, and unshakable discipline.",sp=0},{t="Vigilance Across Azeroth",b="While Lordaeron remains their principal battleground, the Argent Dawn maintains an active watch across Kalimdor and the capitals of the world. They keep diplomatic presences in Stormwind City, Darnassus, and the goblin trade town of Everlook. Farther afield, operatives like Delgren the Purifier contest demonic cults in Darkshore and Ashenvale, while scouts in Desolace and the flooded ruins of Blackfathom Deeps investigate sinister threats involving the Shadow Council and the Twilight's Hammer cult.",sp=0},{t="Uneasy Truces and Hidden Foes",b="Facing annihilation by the Scourge, a pragmatic sect within the order known as the Brotherhood of the Light strives to maintain a cold truce with the Scarlet Crusade. Yet relations remain deeply strained. High-ranking officers like Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff actively sanction covert strikes against Scarlet leadership in Stratholme to purge what they view as heretical madness. Compounding their perils, dark forces relentlessly seek to infiltrate the Dawn's ranks—including secret agents who siphon necromantic secrets from the front lines for treacherous masters elsewhere.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Argent Dawn and the Scarlet Crusade?",a="Both orders were born from the remnants of the Silver Hand after Lordaeron's fall. The Scarlet Crusade became fanatical and xenophobic, viewing all outsiders and non-humans as potential carriers of the plague. The Argent Dawn refused to abandon the true tenets of the Holy Light, opening their ranks to both Alliance and Horde races to fight evil together.",al={"argent dawn vs scarlet crusade","why did argent dawn split","difference between dawn and crusade"},it="conflict"},{q="What do I do with Scourgestones?",a="Argent Dawn officers accept Scourgestones as evidence of service against the undead. These grisly tokens represent victories over the Scourge and help the order recognize its allies.",al={"what are scourgestones for","argent dawn scourgestones","scourgestone turn in"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Can Horde players join or work with the Argent Dawn?",a="Yes. The Argent Dawn is strictly neutral and operates outside political allegiances. They actively recruit orcs, trolls, tauren, and even Forsaken who are committed to combating the Scourge and the Burning Legion, maintaining recruitment stations like the Bulwark on the Tirisfal border.",al={"can horde join argent dawn","is argent dawn alliance only","argent dawn horde friendly"},it="side"},{q="What are they doing in Kalimdor?",a="The Dawn views its mandate as the protection of all Azeroth, not just Lordaeron. Agents have traveled to Darkshore, Ashenvale, and Desolace to eliminate cults of the Shadow Council, confront Twilight's Hammer conspirators, and guard against demonic incursions.",al={"argent dawn in kalimdor","argent dawn ashenvale","why is argent dawn in darkshore"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Brotherhood of the Light?",a="The Brotherhood of the Light is a militant sub-faction of the Argent Dawn led by Commander Eligor Dawnbringer. They work to maintain a functional, if tense, working relationship with the Scarlet Crusade so both factions can direct their fury toward Kel'Thuzad rather than each other.",al={"brotherhood of the light","brotherhood of light argent dawn","eligor dawnbringer faction"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Argent Dawn?",a="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to defending Azeroth from destructive evils, chief among them the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Unlike political factions or insular sects, the Dawn welcomes members and champions from any race without distinction, united solely by their devotion to the Light and their refusal to let the world fall to darkness.",al={"who are argent dawn","argent dawn order","what does argent dawn do"},it="identity",add="a2a95d31"},{q="Who leads the Argent Dawn?",a="Lord Maxwell Tyrosus leads the Argent Dawn, directing their crusade against the undead from their primary sanctuary at Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands.",al={"argent dawn leader","who is in charge of argent dawn","lord tyrosus argent dawn"},it="leader",add="fb8338d1"},{q="Who are the Argent Dawn allied with?",a="The Argent Dawn maintains strict political neutrality, counting both the Alliance and the Horde as allies in the greater struggle against evil. Their officers recruit heroes from Stormwind to Orgrimmar alike. While they share common undead foes with the Scarlet Crusade and occasionally hold an uneasy truce through the Brotherhood of the Light, they view the Crusade's zealous fanaticism as dangerous heresy.",al={"argent dawn allies","does argent dawn work with horde","who works with argent dawn"},it="allies",add="7fcb74f5"},{q="How was the Argent Dawn founded?",a="Following the fall of Lordaeron and the murder of Highlord Alexandros Mograine, the remnants of the Silver Hand fractured. While many knights succumbed to zealous paranoia and formed the Scarlet Crusade, those disgusted by this rising extremism broke away. Determined to uphold the true tenets of the Light without prejudice, they formed the Argent Dawn to fight the Scourge with honor.",al={"argent dawn history","origin of argent dawn","how did argent dawn start"},it="history",add="7042b727"}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For holy warriors, the Argent Dawn represents the uncorrupted legacy of the Silver Hand, holding fast to compassion, sacrifice, and duty when the Scarlet Crusade succumbed to madness.",["faction:Horde"]="Though established by former knights of Lordaeron, the Dawn welcomes Horde champions without prejudice, making them one of the few avenues for Horde heroes to campaign directly in the Plaguelands.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken who despise the Scourge find a rare, pragmatic refuge in the Dawn's camps, where your actions against Kel'Thuzad matter far more than your rotting flesh.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Dawn maintains official embassies in Stormwind and Darnassus, offering Alliance soldiers a righteous path to reclaim Lordaeron from the grasp of the Lich King."},kw={"argent dawn","bulwark","chillwind camp","commission","light's hope chapel","maxwell tyrosus","plaguelands","scarlet crusade","scourge","scourgestone","silver hand"},rel={"subzone:light-s-hope-chapel","npc:lord-maxwell-tyrosus","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:scourge","zone:easternplaguelands","subzone:chillwind-camp","subzone:bulwark"},src={"Argent Dawn"}}
E["topic:black-dragonflight"] = {t="topic",n="Black dragonflight",z="general",s="Once the stalwart Earth-Warders charged by the titans to safeguard Azeroth's deep subterranean realms, the black dragonflight fell to madness and treachery. Corrupted by the insidious whispers of the imprisoned Old Gods, their leader Neltharion became Deathwing, turning his brood into one of the most destructive and hated forces on the planet. Today, their surviving scions weave plots from the shadows and wage covert wars against mortal kingdoms.",h="Once the stalwart Earth-Warders charged by the titans to safeguard Azeroth's deep subterranean realms, the...",sec={{t="The Fallen Protectors",b="Long ago, the Titan Khaz'goroth bestowed his blessing upon Neltharion, creating the black dragonflight to watch over the earth, raise mountains, and guard Azeroth's deep caverns. For ages, they stood alongside the other flights as patient, unyielding guardians. Yet their intimate bond with the world's subterranean foundations proved their undoing. Dwelling within the deepest hollows, they lay dangerously close to the sunken prisons of the Old Gods. Over centuries, dark whispers eroded the noble Earth-Warder's mind, driving him to madness and twisting his entire brood into vicious instruments of ruin.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal and Fire",b="During the ancient War of the Ancients, Neltharion shattered the unity of dragonkind, turning upon his kin as Deathwing the Destroyer. Ever since, the black flight has waged relentless, spiteful war against the red, blue, green, and bronze flights, who view their cruelty as an absolute profanation of life. Rather than tending the soil, black dragons now wield molten magma, black magic, and searing brimstone to scorch the lands they once nurtured, finding delight in sowing discord, misery, and endless strife across mortal realms.",sp=0},{t="Human Guises and Mortal Intrigues",b="Unlike many beasts that rely purely on claw and flame, the black dragons are masters of manipulation. They frequently adopt humanoid forms—most often cultured, dark-haired humans with piercing eyes—to infiltrate royal courts and council chambers. By steering leaders toward arrogance, sowing division between noble houses, and whispering poisonous council into vulnerable ears, the flight orchestrates the downfall of mortal civilizations from within, preferring that kingdoms tear themselves apart before their drakes arrive to burn the remnants.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are black dragons evil?",a="Their ancient duty tied them closely to the subterranean depths of Azeroth, directly where the ancient Old Gods were sealed away. Over millennia, the maddening whispers of these entities seeped into Neltharion's mind, twisting his protective instincts into paranoia and malice until his entire brood followed him into corruption.",al={"why did black dragons turn bad","who corrupted deathwing","black dragon madness"},it="hostile"},{q="What powers do black dragons wield?",a="Granted dominance over the earth by the Titan Khaz'goroth, they command stone, magma, and deep subterranean forces. In their corrupted state, they wield volatile earth elemental power known as black magic, exhaling searing fire and molten rock while their heavy scales continually radiate intense heat.",al={"black dragon magic","black dragon flight abilities","what can black dragons do"},it="power"},{q="Do black dragons use disguises?",a="Yes. Black dragons excel at infiltration and political manipulation. Their favored humanoid guise is typically an attractive, noble human with dark hair and dark eyes, allowing them to mingle freely in courts, manipulate monarchs, and spark civil conflicts without revealing their true draconic nature.",al={"do dragons take human form","black dragon mortal guises","black dragons disguised as humans"}},{q="How can you tell a black dragon's name?",a="Members of the black flight commonly follow specific naming traditions: male names frequently end in the suffix '-ion' (such as Neltharion), while female names typically end in '-ia' (such as Onyxia). Many also adopt words evoking ash, shadow, stone, or ruin.",al={"black dragon naming conventions","black dragon names","how do black dragons get named"},it="name"},{q="Why do the other dragonflights hate them?",a="During the War of the Ancients, the Earth-Warder betrayed the other flights, slaughtering thousands of his brethren. The red dragonflight in particular despises them, viewing their fiery malice and corrupting influence as an unnatural blasphemy against life itself.",al={"red dragonflight vs black dragonflight","why do dragons fight each other","dragonflight civil war"},it="conflict"},{q="Who leads the black dragonflight now?",a="Their ancient Aspect, Deathwing, has vanished from public view following past defeats, leaving his dangerous brood scattered into splintered cabals. Today, prominent offspring—such as Nefarian and Onyxia—pursue their own ruthless ambitions to enslave rivals, subvert kingdoms, and breed dark creations.",al={"who is the black dragon leader","where is deathwing","who rules the black dragons"},it="leader"},{q="What is the black dragonflight?",a="The black dragonflight is a brood of dragons, drakonid, and dragonspawn originally blessed by the titan Khaz'goroth to serve as the steadfast warders of the earth. Once revered as the noble defenders of Azeroth's deepest realms, mountains, and subterranean caverns, the entire flight fell into madness when their ancient leader succumbed to the dark whispers of the Old Gods.",al={"who are the black dragons","what are black dragons","what is black dragonflight"},it="identity",add="0af770e1"},{q="Where did the black dragonflight come from?",a="Countless millennia ago, the titans empowered the Great Aspect Neltharion the Earth-Warder and his black brood with dominion over stone and magma. For ages, they shaped mountains and valleys in harmony with the other flights. However, their intimate bond with the deep places of the earth left them exposed to the whispers of the imprisoned Old Gods. Driven utterly mad, Neltharion and his children betrayed their sacred charge, turning their elemental might toward ruin and terror.",al={"black dragonflight history","black dragon origin","history of black dragons"},it="history",add="146344cc"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance must remain constantly vigilant against political subversion, as the black dragonflight has repeatedly sought to corrupt human nobility and exploit aristocratic ambitions in human courts.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde holds an enduring grudge against the flight's machinations, having suffered under their manipulative meddling during previous wars and facing their subterranean broods throughout the crags of Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman view the black dragons with deep sorrow and revulsion, knowing they wield the sacred elemental power of earth not to maintain harmony, but to twist stone and magma into instruments of torment."},kw={"black dragonkin","black dragons","deathwing","dragonflight","earth-warder","khaz'goroth","nefarian","neltharion","onyxia"},rel={"npc:deathwing","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:dragonmaw-clan"},src={"Black dragonflight"}}
E["topic:blackrock-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Blackrock clan",z="general",s="The Blackrock clan is a martial orcish clan renowned for its strict military discipline, unmatched blacksmithing, and central role in the First and Second Wars. Today, the clan is deeply fractured: while some veterans follow Thrall in the New Horde, a vicious and renegade splinter group known as the Dark Horde occupies the upper peaks of Blackrock Mountain under Rend Blackhand.",h="The Blackrock clan is a martial orcish clan renowned for its strict military discipline, unmatched...",sec={{t="The Anvil of the Old Horde",b="Hailing from Draenor, the Blackrock clan made its name through rigid hierarchy, heavy armor, and mastery over the forge. Under Chieftain Blackhand, they served as the brutal core of the Old Horde during the invasion of Azeroth, laying siege to Stormwind. When Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand to cleanse the Horde of Gul'dan's direct puppet mastery, leadership of both the clan and the Horde remained firmly in Blackrock hands through the grueling campaigns of the Second War.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Legacy",b="Following the Horde's catastrophic defeat at Blackrock Mountain, the clan shattered. Many warriors were rounded up and placed in Alliance internment camps, later to be liberated by Thrall and inducted into his reformed New Horde. However, unrepentant elements rallied behind Blackhand's sons, Rend and Maim. This faction retreated into the heights of Blackrock Spire, forming the hostile Dark Horde alongside remnants of the Dragonmaw and other rogue clans.",sp=0},{t="The Threat in the Spire",b="Today, Rend Blackhand claims the mantle of true Warchief from his throne in Blackrock Spire. The Dark Horde maintains an uneasy and treacherous truce with the Dark Iron dwarves who lurk in the molten depths below, all while waging war against both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde. Uncompromising, demon-tainted remnants of the clan have also been spotted lingering in Lordaeron, seeking to revive dark sacrificial rituals.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Are the Blackrock orcs part of Thrall's Horde?",a="Some are, but most encountered in the Eastern Kingdoms are not. While venerable Blackrock veterans like Eitrigg serve Thrall in Kalimdor, the bulk of the clan entrenched at Blackrock Mountain follow Rend Blackhand and consider Thrall's New Horde to be weak traitors.",al={"is blackrock clan horde","are blackrocks friendly","thrall blackrock clan"},it="side"},{q="Who is currently leading the Blackrock clan?",a="In Blackrock Mountain, Dal'rend (Rend) Blackhand leads the Dark Horde splinter faction, claiming leadership over the clan his father once commanded. Those Blackrock orcs who integrated into the New Horde answer directly to Warchief Thrall.",al={"blackrock clan leader","who leads blackrock","rend blackhand leader"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Dark Horde?",a="The Dark Horde is a rogue faction occupying Upper Blackrock Spire, comprised of unrepentant Blackrock orcs, Black Tooth Grin veterans, and allied Dragonmaw orcs. Led by Rend Blackhand, they reject Thrall's authority and seek to conquer Azeroth under the banner of the Old Horde.",al={"what is dark horde","rend's horde","blackrock dark horde"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Blackhand?",a="During the First War, Orgrim Doomhammer challenged Warchief Blackhand to a mak'gora duel to strip the Shadow Council of its puppet ruler. Orgrim crushed Blackhand's skull with the Doomhammer, claiming both the title of Warchief and leadership of the Blackrock clan.",al={"how did blackhand die","who killed blackhand","orgrim kills blackhand"},it="history"},{q="Why are there Blackrock orcs in Lordaeron?",a="Scattered splinter forces of the clan fled north or remained hidden in Lordaeron after the Second War. Some of these warbands turned back toward demon worship and dark sacrifices, drawing the ire of the Alliance and later the Scourge during the Third War.",al={"blackrock orcs alterac","blackrocks in lordaeron","demon worshipping blackrocks"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Blackrock clan?",a="The Blackrock clan is one of the most renowned and militarily disciplined orcish clans from Draenor. Famed for their master blacksmithing and iron will, they formed the central backbone of the Old Horde during the First and Second Wars under Warchiefs Blackhand and Orgrim Doomhammer. While some members eventually followed Thrall into his new Horde, the majority remain rogue traditionalists entrenched in Blackrock Mountain.",al={"who are blackrock orcs","blackrock clan lore","what is blackrock clan"},it="identity",add="2b76c337"},{q="Why are the Blackrock orcs hostile to travelers?",a="The free Blackrock orcs still cling to the bloody conquests of the Old Horde and reject Thrall's peaceful ideals. Driven by martial pride and deep hatred for both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde, they treat any trespassers into their lands—particularly around Blackrock Mountain and Redridge—as invaders to be slaughtered or enslaved.",al={"why do blackrock orcs attack","blackrock clan hostile","why are blackrocks evil"},it="hostile",add="77427c0a"},{q="Who are the Blackrock clan allied with?",a="The Blackrocks who follow Rend Blackhand lead the Dark Horde from Blackrock Spire. To bolster their numbers against the Dark Iron dwarves and the Alliance, they have allied with remnants of the Dragonmaw clan, local forest trolls, ogres, and the black dragonflight.",al={"blackrock clan allies","who do blackrock orcs work with","dark horde allies"},it="allies",add="2a83fd4b"}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The Blackrocks produced two of the greatest Warchiefs in orcish history, yet their surviving remnants in the Eastern Kingdoms serve as a bitter reminder of the blood-fueled Old Horde.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="The Blackrock clan's legendary metalworking shaped modern orcish siegecraft and heavy plate armor, born from centuries of smelting unique ores in elemental-powered forges.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance forces have fought the Blackrock clan across three wars, from the burning of Stormwind to the bloody sieges of Blackrock Mountain and Khaz Modan.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall views Rend's Blackrock extremists as an existential threat to the peace and honor of the New Horde, branding them renegades who cling to demonic servitude."},kw={"blackhand","blackrock","dark horde","doomhammer","eitrigg","rend","spire"},rel={"subzone:blackrock-mountain","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","npc:eitrigg","topic:old-horde","topic:new-horde","topic:dragonmaw-clan","topic:second-war"},src={"Blackrock clan"}}
E["topic:bronzebeard-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Bronzebeard clan",z="general",s="The Bronzebeard clan is the ruling dwarven royal line and dominant population of Ironforge. Following their victory in the ancient War of the Three Hammers, they forged strong ties with humanity and the gnomes, standing today under King Magni Bronzebeard as a cornerstone of the Alliance.",h="The Bronzebeard clan is the ruling dwarven royal line and dominant population of Ironforge.",sec={{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Long ago, dwarven society in Ironforge flourished under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died of old age roughly 230 years ago, simmering rivalries erupted into civil war. Thane Madoran Bronzebeard commanded the traditional garrison and largest standing army within Ironforge, successfully driving both the Wildhammer and Dark Iron clans from under the mountain. When Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan mounted an assault to reconquer Khaz Modan, Madoran Bronzebeard led his clan in a decisive campaign that shattered the Dark Iron host, solidifying Bronzebeard supremacy over Ironforge.",sp=0},{t="A Pillar of the Alliance",b="Human kingdoms first encountered the Bronzebeards nearly two millennia ago, building mutual respect through shared mastery of metalcraft, trade, and a passion for battle. When the Orcish Horde swept through the continent in the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard brought his realm into the Alliance of Lordaeron. Despite withstanding brutal sieges within their subterranean fortress, Bronzebeard engineers, gunners, and demolitionists provided pivotal firepower and arms that helped turn the tide of the war.",sp=0},{t="Grief at the High Seat",b="Today, King Magni governs from the High Seat in the Great Forge, yet his reign is shadowed by loss. His younger brother, Muradin Bronzebeard, served as an Alliance emissary in Lordaeron and vanished in the frozen wastes of Northrend alongside Prince Arthas Menethil, presumed dead when the prince claimed the cursed blade Frostmourne. Compounding this grief, Magni's daughter, Princess Moira, has gone missing, leaving the lineage facing an uncertain succession while the clan searches ancient Titan excavation sites to divine their origins.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who rules the Bronzebeard clan?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard sits upon the High Seat within the Great Forge. A veteran of the Second War and master smith, he leads both his clan and the allied dwarves of Khaz Modan.",al={"bronzebeard leader","who is king of ironforge","current bronzebeard king"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Bronzebeard clan win control of Ironforge?",a="Following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar roughly 230 years ago, civil war broke out between the three major clans. Commanding Ironforge's central standing army, Thane Madoran Bronzebeard drove the Wildhammers and Dark Irons from the mountain and later crushed Thaurissan's invading Dark Iron army.",al={"war of the three hammers","dwarf civil war","why bronzebeards rule"},it="history"},{q="What is the difference between Bronzebeard and other dwarven clans?",a="The Bronzebeards inhabit Ironforge and represent the traditional mountain dwarves who excel in heavy plate, firearms, and subterranean architecture. In contrast, the Wildhammers dwell in the crags and skies as gryphon riders, while the hostile Dark Irons master dark sorcery and pyromancy beneath Blackrock Mountain.",al={"dwarf clans difference","ironforge vs wildhammer","types of dwarves"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Muradin Bronzebeard?",a="Muradin Bronzebeard traveled to Northrend as an Alliance emissary and explorer. He guided Prince Arthas Menethil in the hunt for the runeblade Frostmourne and was reported dead when the cursed weapon was freed from its pedestal.",al={"where is muradin","is muradin alive","muradin fate"}},{q="Why are the Bronzebeards so devoted to archaeological digs?",a="Bronzebeard scholars have uncovered relics linking their ancestry to the ancient, stone-hewn earthen. Driven to discover the roots of their race, the clan finances digs and expeditions across Azeroth.",al={"why dwarves dig","dwarven excavations","earthen origins"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Bronzebeard royal family's seat of power?",a="The royal throne, known as the High Seat, is situated in the heart of Ironforge along the Great Forge, where King Magni conducts state affairs surrounded by councilors and royal guards.",al={"where is the throne room","high seat location","bronzebeard throne"},it="place"},{q="Who are the Bronzebeard clan's allies?",a="The Bronzebeards are staunch, founding pillars of the Grand Alliance. Their closest bond lies with humanity, dating back roughly eighteen centuries when human explorers from Arathor first journeyed south into Khaz Modan and discovered Ironforge. The two races forged an enduring partnership over shared smithing, engineering, and storytelling. They also share strong ties with the gnomes of nearby Gnomeregan and have long reestablished friendly relations with their Wildhammer cousins in the Aerie Peak, despite past civil conflicts.",al={"bronzebeard allies","who do bronzebeards ally with","ironforge allies"},it="allies",add="177e0e99"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every playable dwarf hails from or answers to the Bronzebeard kingdom, serving King Magni as steadfast defenders of Khaz Modan.",["race:Gnome"]="The Bronzebeards offered unconditional refuge in Ironforge's Tinkertown when Gnomeregan fell to the trogg invasion.",["race:Human"]="Humanity and the Bronzebeards have maintained longstanding ties, bonded by shared martial traditions and mutual trade.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="The Bronzebeards are renowned across Azeroth for their unparalleled mastery of smelting, weapon crafting, and heavy armors."},kw={"bronzebeard","high seat","ironforge","khaz modan","madoran","magni","muradin","three hammers"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:dwarf","zone:ironforge","topic:muradin-bronzebeard","subzone:high-seat","topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","topic:alliance"},src={"Bronzebeard clan"}}
E["topic:brotherhood-of-northshire"] = {t="topic",n="Brotherhood of Northshire",z="general",s="The Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy human clerical order dedicated to the Church of the Holy Light, based out of Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest. Originally known as the Clerics of Northshire, the order suffered catastrophic losses during the First War before being reformed under Archbishop Alonsus Faol. Today, the Brotherhood serves as both a spiritual sanctuary and the primary training ground for fledgling Alliance defenders.",h="The Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy human clerical order dedicated to the Church of the Holy Light, based...",sec={{t="The Clerics of the First War",b="Centuries ago, pious missionaries traveled south from Lordaeron into the kingdom of Stormwind, establishing Northshire Abbey to minister to the people and soothe the realm through the Holy Light. Originally known as the Holy Order of Northshire Clerics, their compassion was legendary—they even tended to injured gnolls and jungle trolls during past border wars. When the Orcish Horde crossed through the Dark Portal, the clerics marched bravely to the front lines without armor or weapons to heal fallen soldiers. Because their healing sustained the Stormwind Army, the orcs ruthlessly targeted them. The order was decimated, the abbey was ransacked and burned, and the surviving clerics were forced to flee north across the sea to Southshore with Sir Anduin Lothar.",sp=0},{t="The Spark of the Paladins",b="The slaughter of the Northshire clerics sent shockwaves through the human kingdoms. Realizing that faith alone could not withstand an axe without armor and martial discipline, Archbishop Alonsus Faol resolved to create warriors who possessed both the sacred powers of the Light and the martial mastery of a knight. Drawing upon the surviving clerics and devout knights, Faol formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, giving rise to humanity's first paladins. In this way, the sacrifice of Northshire's fallen healers became the very foundation for the holy warriors who broke the Horde in the Second War.",sp=0},{t="Rebuilding and Present Duty",b="Following the reclamation of Stormwind and the destruction of the Dark Portal, Archbishop Faol oversaw the rebuilding of Northshire Abbey and reorganized the order as the Brotherhood of Northshire. Now in 25 ADP, Northshire Abbey stands as a quiet yet vital center where new recruits gather to begin their service to the realm. Under figures like Priestess Anetta, Brother Sammuel, and Brother Paxton, the Brotherhood attends to the spiritual health of the valley, preserves ancient texts in their library, and trains the next generation of defenders to confront brigands, wolves, and the gathering shadows across Elwynn Forest.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Brotherhood of Northshire?",a="They are a monastic order within the Church of the Holy Light based at Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest. Formerly known as the Clerics of Northshire, they provide spiritual guidance, heal the wounded, and train new Alliance adventurers starting their journey.",al={"who are the brotherhood of northshire","brotherhood of northshire lore","clerics of northshire"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to the original clerics during the First War?",a="The clerics entered the First War as unarmored battlefield healers. Recognizing that human soldiers could not easily be beaten while sustained by holy magic, the orcs prioritized butchering the clerics. The abbey was overrun and ruined, and only a remnant escaped across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",al={"northshire clerics first war","what happened to the abbey","destruction of northshire abbey"},it="history"},{q="How did the Northshire Clerics inspire the paladins?",a="Witnessing how easily unarmored clerics were slain by the Horde, Archbishop Alonsus Faol realized the Alliance needed holy warriors capable of surviving brutal combat. He combined the spiritual devotion of the clerics with the martial prowess of knights, forging the Knights of the Silver Hand.",al={"creation of paladins","silver hand origin","alonsus faol paladins"},it="origin"},{q="Who leads or trains people at Northshire Abbey today?",a="The abbey is home to several notable figures of the Brotherhood, including Brother Paxton, who manages provisions and copies texts for the library, Priestess Anetta, who instructs aspiring priests, and Brother Sammuel, who guides novice paladins.",al={"who is at northshire abbey","northshire abbey trainers","brother paxton"},it="leader"},{q="What was the expedition to the Deadmines during the First War?",a="An ogre chieftain named Turok stole the sacred Tome of Divinity from Northshire. Sir Anduin Lothar led an expedition into the Deadmines of Westfall to retrieve it, but was trapped until Stormwind forces rescued him and secured the book back to the abbey.",al={"tome of divinity deadmines","anduin lothar deadmines","turok deadmines"},it="history"},{q="Are they connected to the Defias Brotherhood?",a="No, they are entirely opposed. While both share the word 'brotherhood', the Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy religious order loyal to Stormwind, whereas the Defias Brotherhood is a criminal syndicate of outlaws and bandits plaguing the countryside.",al={"brotherhood of northshire defias","are they defias","northshire defias"},it="ties"},{q="Why is the Brotherhood based in Northshire Valley?",a="Centuries ago, clerics from the Church of the Holy Light traveled south from Lordaeron and established Northshire Abbey as their spiritual home in Stormwind. Though the original abbey was razed by the Orcish Horde during the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol oversaw its reconstruction after the war ended. The valley remains their ancestral sanctuary, now serving as a training and recruitment ground to prepare new defenders for the kingdom.",al={"why are they in northshire","why northshire valley","brotherhood of northshire location"},it="why_here",add="5eaf3832"},{q="Who is the Brotherhood of Northshire allied with?",a="The Brotherhood of Northshire is an order within the Church of the Holy Light and operates directly in service to the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Grand Alliance. Historically, its clerics tended to Stormwind's armies during major conflicts like the Gnoll War and the Gurubashi War, and their restored order now directly recruits and trains fledgling champions to safeguard the realm.",al={"brotherhood of northshire allies","who does the brotherhood work for","brotherhood allegiance"},it="allies",add="24abd8af"}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Northshire Abbey is the cradle of Stormwind's clerical tradition, where ancient disciplines of healing, confession, and the Holy Light were cultivated long before the First War.",["class:Paladin"]="The tragic butchery of Northshire's unarmored clerics during the First War was the direct catalyst for Archbishop Faol to create the Knights of the Silver Hand.",["race:Human"]="For generations of Stormwind citizens, the Brotherhood has offered spiritual comfort in hardship, and its abbey serves as the humble beginning for the kingdom's greatest champions."},kw={"alonsus faol","brother paxton","brotherhood of northshire","church of the holy light","clerics of northshire","elwynn forest","northshire abbey","priestess anetta"},rel={"subzone:northshire-abbey","subzone:northshire-valley","topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:anduin-lothar","zone:elwynn","quest:1641"},src={"Brotherhood of Northshire"}}
E["topic:burning-blade-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Burning Blade clan",z="general",s="Once an ancient orcish clan renowned for its deadly blademasters and consumed by chaotic frenzy during the early wars, the Burning Blade now largely persists as a sinister demon-worshiping cult. Infiltrating Durotar and the Barrens under the direction of the Shadow Council, its acolytes threaten the fragile peace of the region. Meanwhile, a handful of honorable blademasters have forsaken demonic corruption to serve Warchief Thrall in the New Horde.",h="Once an ancient orcish clan renowned for its deadly blademasters and consumed by chaotic frenzy during the...",sec={{t="From Clan to Elemental Fury",b="Originating in the lands of Draenor alongside clans like the Warsong, the Burning Blade were long respected for the lethal tradition of their blademasters. When they drank the blood of Mannoroth and marched through the Dark Portal into Azeroth during the First War, however, the clan fractured mentally. By the Second War, they were scarcely an organized society, behaving instead like a berserk force of nature. Nomadic and demented, they threw themselves into battle with total disregard for survival, requiring ogres to hold them in check until unleashed. Ultimately, their numbers were battered and routed near the Dark Portal by the Alliance.",sp=0},{t="The Threat in Durotar",b="The modern cult operates covens across Kalimdor, troubling newly arrived orcs and Darkspear trolls from their first days in the Valley of Trials. Burning Blade warlocks inhabit Thunder Ridge, and garrison a major hideout within Skull Rock near Orgrimmar's eastern gates. Their cultists rally around dark relics like the Demon Seed and harvest souls for demonic rituals, making them a primary internal menace against the fledgling Horde's stability in Durotar and the Barrens.",sp=0},{t="The Traitor in the Cleft",b="While Thrall works to establish the New Horde, the cult's master orchestrator sits directly under his nose. Neeru Fireblade, an ostensible Horde warlock dwelling in the Cleft of Shadow, secretly leads the Burning Blade as an agent of the Shadow Council. Neeru uses pawns, including the expendable Searing Blade of Ragefire Chasm, to maintain his disguise as a benign researcher helping the Warchief. Unbeknownst to Neeru, Thrall has already deduced his treacherous allegiance and watches him closely.",sp=1,bt={"q5726.2","q5726.3"}},{t="A Split Legacy",b="Following the Second War, the surviving remnants of the Burning Blade split dramatically. A disciplined contingent of blademasters swore solemn oaths to liberate their people from demonic enslavement, pledging their blades directly to Warchief Thrall as his personal honor guard. Yet a far larger portion of the scattered clan fell entirely under the sway of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, morphing into a clandestine cult of warlocks, fel acolytes, and conspirators lurking throughout Kalimdor.",sp=1,bt={"q5726.3"}}},faq={{q="What is the Burning Blade clan?",a="Originally an orcish clan from Draenor known for elite blademasters, the Burning Blade was consumed by bloodlust during the First and Second Wars. Today, the name primarily refers to a demon-worshiping cult operating across Kalimdor on behalf of the Shadow Council, although a small number of reformed blademasters serve Thrall's Horde.",al={"burning blade lore","who are burning blade","burning blade background"},sp=1,it="identity",bt={"q5726.3"}},{q="Who actually leads the Burning Blade?",a="The cult answers to the Shadow Council, and its covens across Durotar and the Barrens are run by warlocks and demon-summoners who keep their true master hidden. Whispers say the trail of command leads uncomfortably close to Orgrimmar itself.",al={"burning blade leader","who is the leader of burning blade"},sp=1,it="leader",bt={"q5726.3"}},{q="Why are they attacking new orcs in the Valley of Trials?",a="The Burning Blade established a coven in the northern caves of the Valley of Trials to disrupt young orcs and trolls undergoing their rites of passage, using vile familiars and dark medallions to cultivate fel corruption at the heart of Horde lands.",al={"valley of trials burning blade","why are cultists in valley of trials"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the difference between the Burning Blade and Searing Blade?",a="The Searing Blade is a sect based deep inside Ragefire Chasm beneath Orgrimmar. It is a smaller offshoot, while the Burning Blade is the broader cult operating across Durotar and the Barrens. Investigating the chasm reveals who directs the sect.",al={"searing blade vs burning blade","difference between burning and searing blade"},sp=1,it="conflict"},{q="Are all Burning Blade members demon-worshiping cultists?",a="No. While the cult constitutes the majority, proud blademasters of the original clan broke away by the Third War. They took vows to redeem their people from demonic enslavement and now fight loyally within the New Horde as Thrall's personal honor guard.",al={"are all burning blade bad","burning blade blademasters good"},it="residents"},{q="Why are the Burning Blade hostile to travelers?",a="The Burning Blade serves the demonic will of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council. Ever since their clan was consumed by demonic bloodlust during the early wars, they have abandoned any true allegiance or honor. Now reorganized as a secretive apocalyptic cult, they view outsiders, loyal Horde subjects, and curious travelers as direct threats to their covens or as fuel for demonic blood sacrifices.",al={"why do burning blade attack","burning blade hostile","why does the burning blade fight"},it="hostile",add="59650569"},{q="Who are the Burning Blade allied with?",a="The cult acts as a direct arm of the sinister Shadow Council, carrying out their dark designs across Kalimdor. They frequently cooperate with allied demon-worshiping splinter factions like the Searing Blade hiding beneath Orgrimmar and the Argus Wake in the Eastern Kingdoms. Above all, they are sworn in servitude to the Burning Legion, summoning demons and gathering corrupt artifacts to hasten their masters' designs.",al={"burning blade allies","who does the burning blade work for","burning blade shadow council"},it="allies",add="d02d80de"},{q="Where did the Burning Blade come from?",a="Originally an ancient orcish clan from Draenor known for their fearsome blademasters, the Burning Blade joined the Old Horde and crossed into Azeroth during the First War. Demonic corruption eroded their minds until they became a reckless, mad frenzy during the Second War, eventually breaking apart entirely. While honorable blademasters later swore fealty to Thrall's New Horde, the remaining remnants transformed into the shadowy demonic cult threatening Kalimdor today.",al={"burning blade origin","burning blade lore","history of burning blade clan"},it="history",add="7b15f473"}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The Burning Blade represents the persistent specter of the Horde's corrupt demonic past, directly threatening Durotar and the Barrens from within.",["class:Warlock"]="The cult exemplifies the reckless, demonic enslavement that forces Horde warlocks like those studying in the Cleft of Shadow under Neeru Fireblade into hiding and intense suspicion.",["race:Orc"]="The clan's fall is a bitter reminder of the blood curse, standing in direct contrast to the honor-bound blademasters who seek atonement in service to Thrall."},kw={"blademaster","burning blade","cleft of shadow","durotar","searing blade","shadow council","skull rock","zmodlor"},rel={"npc:thrall","quest:5726","quest:829","zone:durotar","zone:ragefire","topic:shadow-council","quest:827","npc:orgnil-soulscar"},src={"Burning Blade clan"}}
E["topic:burning-legion"] = {t="topic",n="Burning Legion",z="general",s="The Burning Legion is an infinite, apocalyptic host of demons led by the fallen titan Sargeras, dedicated to scouring all life from creation. Having already shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, the demons remain Azeroth's most relentless nemesis, orchestrating the corruption of the orcs and the horrors of the Third War.",h="The Burning Legion is an infinite, apocalyptic host of demons led by the fallen titan Sargeras, dedicated to...",sec={{t="Genesis of the Host",b="Long ago, Sargeras stood as the champion of the titans, tasked with hunting down and imprisoning the demons that plagued the cosmos. Over countless ages of battling their evil, he grew disillusioned and concluded that creation itself was fatally flawed and must be unmade in flame. To enact his crusade, he freed the demons he had imprisoned, bending them to his will through fel magic. He augmented their chaos with the intellect of the corrupted eredar—elevating Archimonde and Kil'jaeden as his chief lieutenants—and forged an unstoppable engine of cosmic annihilation.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Ancients",b="Ten millennia ago, the reckless sorceries of the Highborne drew the Legion's gaze to Azeroth. Whispering through the Well of Eternity, Sargeras seduced Queen Azshara and her councilor Xavius with promises of reshaping the world into paradise. Instead, the portal unleashed commanders like Mannoroth and Archimonde alongside swarms of doomguard, felhounds, and flaming infernals. The ensuing war reduced Kalimdor's civilization to ruin before an alliance of night elves, dragonflights, and demigods collapsed the gateway. The catastrophic implosion tore the supercontinent apart in the Great Sundering, casting the ruined lands across vast new seas.",sp=0},{t="Shadows over Azeroth",b="Though cast back into the Twisting Nether, the Legion never abandoned Azeroth. Deciding that direct invasion invited failure, Kil'jaeden orchestrated proxy cataclysms from afar. The deceiver bound the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood pacts, sending the Horde charging through the Dark Portal to weaken mortal defenses. When that failed, the Legion forged the Lich King and unleashed the undead Scourge across Lordaeron, paving the way for Archimonde's return in the Third War. Even after Archimonde fell at Mount Hyjal, remnants of the Legion linger across Felwood, the Blasted Lands, and hidden cults throughout the world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Burning Legion?",a="The Burning Legion is an infinite army of demons, corrupted races, and destructive horrors forged by the fallen titan Sargeras. Its singular objective is the Burning Crusade: scouring all life and ordered existence from the cosmos.",al={"who are the legion","what is the burning legion","legion demons","what are demons"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Burning Legion?",a="The Legion's ultimate master is the fallen titan Sargeras. Below him serve the demon lords of the eredar, most notably Archimonde the Defiler, who spearheaded military assaults until his demise at Mount Hyjal, and Kil'jaeden the Deceiver, who orchestrates insidious plots across worlds.",al={"who rules the legion","legion leader","who is the demon king","archimonde boss"},it="leader"},{q="Why does the Legion want to destroy Azeroth?",a="Azeroth has repeatedly defied the Legion, standing as the only world known to have repelled their assaults.",al={"why do demons attack","legion motive","why invade azeroth","sargeras goal"},it="purpose"},{q="How did the demons first arrive on Azeroth?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne channeled the vast energies of the Well of Eternity, inadvertently attracting Sargeras. Deceived by promises of limitless power, the Highborne opened a massive portal beneath the palace, allowing the Legion to invade Kalimdor.",al={"first demon invasion","how did legion get here","well of eternity portal","queen azshara demons"},it="history"},{q="What happened to the Legion during the Third War?",a="Summoned back to Azeroth using the book of Medivh, Archimonde marched across Lordaeron and sailed west to devour the World Tree, Nordrassil. At Mount Hyjal, mortal races bought time for Malfurion Stormrage to call upon the ancestral spirits, detonating the tree's power and obliterating Archimonde.",al={"third war demons","mount hyjal invasion","did legion lose third war","what happened to archimonde"},it="history"},{q="Are demons still a threat right now?",a="Yes. Though their main host fractured after Archimonde's fall, dangerous remnants remain entrenched across Azeroth. Dreadlords, infernals, and lingering fiends occupy corrupted zones like Felwood and the Blasted Lands, while mortal cultists such as the Shadow Council work in secret.",al={"are demons still around","legion remnants","demons in classic","where are demons now"},it="threat"},{q="Why are the Burning Legion hostile to everyone?",a="The Burning Legion's ultimate purpose is nothing less than the absolute annihilation of all mortal life and creation across the cosmos. Under the command of the fallen titan Sargeras, demons do not seek conquest, coexistence, or surrender; they view mortal worlds as flawed remnants to be purged in fel fire. Any traveler or soul they encounter on Azeroth is merely fuel for their magic or prey to be wiped out.",al={"why is the legion hostile","why does the burning legion attack","burning legion hostile reasons"},it="hostile",add="5561206b"},{q="Who serves alongside or works for the Burning Legion?",a="The Legion relies on an endless tide of demonic races, including monstrous pit lords, cunning dreadlords, ravenous felhounds, and towering infernals. Beyond pure demons, they corrupt mortal races to act as pawns and vanguards. In the past, they swayed Queen Azshara's Highborne sorcerers, corrupted the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood, and forged the Scourge to soften mortal resistance before invasions.",al={"burning legion allies","who works for the legion","who serves the burning legion"},it="allies",add="c3b2191d"},{q="Why are demonic remnants still found across Azeroth?",a="Following Archimonde's defeat at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, the centralized demonic assault shattered, scattering surviving fiends into hiding. Today, lingering demons and renegade dreadlords lurk in desolate reaches like Desolace, Felwood, and the Blasted Lands. Additionally, corrupt mortal cabals, such as the warlocks of the Shadow Council, secretly summon fresh reinforcements through hidden dark portals to carry on the Legion's work.",al={"why are demons still on azeroth","why are legion demons here","burning legion locations azeroth"},it="why_here",add="9252264e"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The night elves bear the deepest scars of the Legion's invasions; their ancient empire was shattered in the Sundering, and the recent sacrifice of their World Tree at Mount Hyjal cost them their very immortality.",["race:Orc"]="Orcs carry a bitter legacy with the Legion, having been enslaved through the demonic blood pact of Mannoroth before Grom Hellscream sacrificed himself to break the curse once and for all.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks walk a razor's edge, bending the raw fel magic and subservient demons of the Legion to their own mortal will despite constant suspicion and the ever-present threat of corruption.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins view the Burning Legion as the antithesis of the Holy Light, dedicating their martial vows and righteous power to purging demonic filth wherever it festers."},kw={"archimonde","burning legion","demons","eredar","fel","hyjal","kil'jaeden","mannoroth","sargeras","sundering","twisting nether","well of eternity"},rel={"topic:sargeras","topic:third-war","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:lich-king","npc:malfurion-stormrage"},src={"Burning Legion"}}
E["topic:cenarion-circle"] = {t="topic",n="Cenarion Circle",z="general",s="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient, independent order of druids founded by Malfurion Stormrage under the guidance of the demigod Cenarius. Operating from their sacred haven in Moonglade, they strive to maintain the balance of nature, safeguard the Emerald Dream, and mend Azeroth's blighted lands. Though historically composed of night elves, they have recently welcomed the tauren into their ranks, transcending factional divides to defend the living world.",h="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient, independent order of druids founded by Malfurion Stormrage under the...",sec={{t="Origins of the Circle",b="The roots of the Cenarion Circle go back thousands of years to the teachings of Cenarius, the Patron of Druids, who mentored the first mortal druid, Malfurion Stormrage. Following the Great Sundering, Malfurion bound together those who wished to study nature's ways. The order made its home in the sacred vale of Moonglade, and ever since, the Circle has held sacred vigil over Azeroth's wild places.",sp=0},{t="A Sacred Neutrality",b="While deeply intertwined with night elf history, the Cenarion Circle exists independently of Darnassus and the Sisterhood of Elune. Their heart remains the enchanted valley of Moonglade, centered around the peaceful town of Nighthaven, where weapons are sheathed and the wars of mortals hold no sway. This ancient neutrality was demonstrated anew following the Third War, when Malfurion befriended the tauren elder Hamuul Runetotem. Recognizing the tauren people's profound spiritual reverence for the Earth Mother, the Circle formally inducted Hamuul, reintroducing druidism to his race after thousands of years.",sp=0},{t="Tension and the Absent Archdruid",b="Following the devastation at Mount Hyjal, Malfurion fell into an unnatural, impenetrable coma deep within his barrow den, leaving the druids leaderless. In his absence, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm claimed practical leadership of the Circle. Fandral took advantage of Malfurion's silence to rally support for growing Teldrassil, hoping to restore the kaldorei's lost immortality. However, the Dragon Aspects refused to bless the new tree, and strange corruptions have begun taking root in its branches. Today, internal division simmers within the order as Fandral rules with a stern hand, while ancient guardians sense a creeping darkness spreading from both Silithus and the Emerald Dream.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient society of druids dedicated to preserving nature's balance, studying the wild ways, and tending to the Emerald Dream. Named after the demigod Cenarius and founded by Malfurion Stormrage, the order governs druidic teachings across Azeroth from its sanctuary in Moonglade.",al={"cenarion circle lore","who are the cenarion circle","what is the circle"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the headquarters of the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Circle is headquartered in the moonlit, secluded haven of Moonglade, specifically around the village of Nighthaven and the shores of Lake Elune'ara. They maintain smaller outposts and barrow dens across Kalimdor, including a major vigil in the desert wastes of Silithus at Cenarion Hold.",al={"cenarion circle base","where are cenarion druids based","nighthaven cenarion"},it="place"},{q="Why are tauren in the Cenarion Circle?",a="During the Third War, the tauren elder Hamuul Runetotem fought alongside Malfurion Stormrage at Mount Hyjal. The two developed mutual respect, and Malfurion shared deep druidic secrets with Hamuul. Hamuul became the first tauren druid in millennia and was warmly inducted into the Circle, bringing the tauren and night elf druids into rare partnership.",al={"why are tauren druids","hamuul cenarion circle","tauren night elf druids"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the Cenarion Circle?",a="Historically, Malfurion Stormrage led the order alongside elder archdruids. With Malfurion currently trapped in an unexplained barrow coma, leadership has effectively fallen to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm in Darnassus, though respected elders like Hamuul Runetotem and keepers in Moonglade guide their respective factions.",al={"leader of cenarion circle","who is the archdruid","archdruid leader"},it="leader"},{q="Is the Cenarion Circle part of the Alliance?",a="No. The Cenarion Circle is entirely neutral. While its membership was once strictly night elven, their primary oath is to nature, not mortal thrones. In Moonglade and other Cenarion encampments, Alliance and Horde druids walk side by side without bloodshed.",al={"is cenarion circle alliance","cenarion circle faction","are cenarion druids neutral"},it="side"},{q="What happened at the Scarab Wall in Silithus?",a="A thousand years ago, during the War of the Shifting Sands, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm led the night elves and the Circle against the insectoid qiraji swarm, aided by the dragonflights. In the end, the bronze dragon Anachronos and his allies raised the Scarab Wall to seal the hive away, and the Circle established Cenarion Hold to keep watch over that seal.",al={"cenarion hold silithus","war of the shifting sands","druids scarab wall"},it="conflict"},{q="Why is it called the Cenarion Circle?",a="The order was named after and blessed by Cenarius, the demigod Lord of the Forest. Cenarius was the patron of the wild who originally tutored Malfurion Stormrage, the first mortal druid of Azeroth. When Malfurion and his fellow practitioners gathered to organize their traditions and safeguard the balance of nature, they took the demigod's name to honor their divine teacher.",al={"cenarion circle name origin","why cenarion","who is cenarion circle named after"},it="name",add="93dd49b8"},{q="Where did the Cenarion Circle come from?",a="The Circle was founded in Moonglade by Malfurion Stormrage following the Great Sundering, when he brought the early night elf druids together to establish their guidelines and traditions. For thousands of years during the Long Vigil, the Circle maintained balance and healed demonic scars across Azeroth while walking the Emerald Dream alongside the green dragonflight.",al={"cenarion circle history","origin of the cenarion circle","how was cenarion circle founded"},it="history",add="975f0135"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Cenarion Circle is your order and spiritual authority. Whether you trace your tutelage to Darnassus or the plains of Mulgore, all druidic power, trials, and callings flow through the Circle's rites in Moonglade.",["race:NightElf"]="For ten millennia your people's men primarily slept in barrows serving the Circle while the Sentinels guarded your borders; the Circle's deep bond with the wild shaped every facet of kaldorei culture.",["race:Tauren"]="Through Hamuul Runetotem's fellowship with Malfurion Stormrage, your people have reclaimed ancient traditions tied directly to the Earth Mother, earning an honored place within the Circle.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The druids of the Cenarion Circle possess unmatched understanding of Azeroth's botanical life and the delicate ecosystems that nurture rare and magical flora."},kw={"cenarion circle","cenarion hold","cenarius","druid","emerald dream","fandral staghelm","hamuul runetotem","malfurion","moonglade","nighthaven"},rel={"quest:5921","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:cenarius","quest:1489","npc:fandral-staghelm","subzone:nighthaven","subzone:cenarion-hold","topic:druid"},src={"Cenarion Circle"}}
E["topic:centaur"] = {t="topic",n="Centaur",z="general",s="Centaur are a violent, horse-bodied humanoid race whose nomadic clans dominate the arid plains of central Kalimdor. Born from the tragic union between the demigod Zaetar and the earth princess Theradras, they spent centuries driving the tauren toward extinction before the newly arrived orcish Horde intervened.",h="Centaur are a violent, horse-bodied humanoid race whose nomadic clans dominate the arid plains of central...",sec={{t="A Cursed Lineage",b="Centaur legend traces the race to the caverns of Maraudon beneath Desolace. There, Zaetar, one of the immortal sons of the demigod Cenarius, and Princess Theradras, daughter of the elemental lord Therazane, sired the first centaur. Upon their emergence, the barbaric offspring turned on their father and killed him. Grief-stricken, Theradras trapped Zaetar's spirit within the winding caverns of Maraudon, yet she shielded her vicious children, who swiftly multiplied across Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Centuries of Slaughter",b="Following their emergence, the centaur expanded outward from Desolace, waging a brutal war of attrition against Kalimdor's native populations. The nomadic tauren suffered the most: driven from their fertile ancestral homeland of Mulgore, they were chased relentlessly across the Barrens. Centaur warbands treated the tauren as prey, burning settlements and hunting them for sport. By the time of the Third War, centuries of relentless pursuit had brought the tauren to the precipice of extinction, leaving them desperately fleeing endless marauders across the savannah.",sp=0},{t="The Tide Turns",b="Everything shifted when Warchief Thrall and the newly arrived orcish Horde landed upon Kalimdor. Thrall formed a pact with Cairne Bloodhoof, uniting orcish steel with tauren might. Together, they crushed the aggressive Kolkar warbands, broke their grip on the plains, and enabled the tauren to safely establish Thunder Bluff and reclaim Mulgore. This alliance ended the nomadic exile of the tauren and established a debt of blood and honor that permanently bound them into the Horde.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Menace",b="Despite their staggering defeats, centaur remain an active threat across central Kalimdor. Their society is divided into warring clans—primarily the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, and Maraudine—each led by a khan and locked in perpetual blood feuds over territory and water. While their internal squabbles keep them from uniting into an unstoppable horde, aggressive raiding parties continue to strike at trade caravans and settlements across Durotar, the Barrens, Thousand Needles, and the borders of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where did centaur come from?",a="They were born from the union between Zaetar, a son of Cenarius, and Princess Theradras, an elemental princess of earth. The barbaric first centaur turned on their father and killed him, and Theradras then trapped his spirit in the sacred caverns of Maraudon.",al={"centaur origin","who created the centaur","centaur creation lore"},it="origin"},{q="Why do centaur hate the tauren so much?",a="Centaur expanded aggressively from Desolace and spent generations raiding the indigenous peoples of the plains. They hunted the tauren for sport and drove them out of Mulgore into an endless nomadic exile across the Barrens, nearly exterminating the entire race before the orcs intervened.",al={"tauren centaur feud","why do centaur hunt tauren","centaur tauren war"},it="conflict"},{q="What clans do the centaur belong to?",a="The primary clans on Kalimdor are the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, Maraudine, and Galak. Each trace their leadership back to the original khans who founded them and frequently wage bloody feuds against one another.",al={"centaur tribes","names of centaur clans","centaur factions"},it="people"},{q="Where are centaur encountered today?",a="The heart of their territory is the wasteland of Desolace, where their sacred grounds of Maraudon lie. However, aggressive warbands and outposts roam across the Barrens, Durotar, and Thousand Needles.",al={"where are centaur found","centaur locations","centaur zones"},it="place"},{q="Can centaur be reasoned or allied with?",a="Generally, centaur view all other races as weak prey to be enslaved or destroyed. However, the fierce rivalry between rival clans can sometimes be manipulated; for instance, clans in Desolace like the Magram or Gelkis have occasionally accepted outsider assistance to annihilate their hated kin.",al={"are centaur friendly","can you ally with centaur","friendly centaur"},it="allies"},{q="Who is Princess Theradras to the centaur?",a="Theradras is their mother and revered patron. Deep within Maraudon, she protects the spirit of their murdered father, Zaetar, and has periodically infused her descendants with elemental fury to spur them into conquering new territory across Kalimdor.",al={"centaur mother","theradras and centaur","princess theradras lore"},it="ties"},{q="What are centaur?",a="Centaur are a warlike, primitive race possessing the torso and arms of a human joined to the lower body and four legs of a horse. Savage and nomadic by nature, they roam the arid plains and valleys of central Kalimdor. They are organized into fiercely territorial clans that value brute strength, raiding neighboring settlements, and driving off any outsiders who tread near their hunting grounds and watering holes.",al={"what is centaur","who are the centaur","centaur race"},it="identity",add="f5a4aa83"},{q="Why are centaur so hostile to travelers?",a="Hostility is deeply woven into centaur nature. Born cursed and filled with self-loathing and fury, they murdered their own father shortly after their creation. Ever since, they have viewed the world through violence and conquest. They see central Kalimdor as their rightful territory, treating any passing caravan, traveler, or settlement as trespassers to be slaughtered, enslaved, or driven out.",al={"why do centaur attack","why are centaur aggressive","centaur hostile"},it="hostile",add="5b0a3e11"},{q="Who leads the centaur?",a="The centaur have no single ruler. Instead, they are divided into independent, feuding clans—such as the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, Maraudine, and Galak—each governed by its own khan. These khans rule through ruthless physical dominance and cunning, and the clans spend nearly as much effort waging bloody territorial wars against one another as they do raiding foreign enemies.",al={"centaur leader","who rules the centaur","centaur khan"},it="leader",add="9cda8173"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For your people, centaur are not mere beasts; they are the ancient tormentors who nearly eradicated your ancestors and drove you across the Barrens for centuries until the Horde offered salvation.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending against centaur incursions in the Barrens and Durotar is a matter of survival, honoring the founding pact struck between Thrall and Cairne Bloodhoof.",["class:Druid"]="Centaur represent a tragic perversion of nature's balance, sharing blood with Cenarius through his fallen son Zaetar yet bringing only desolation and slaughter in their wake."},kw={"barrens","centaur","desolace","khan","kolkar","maraudon","theradras","zaetar"},rel={"topic:tauren","zone:desolace","zone:barrens","topic:kolkar-clan","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:thrall","topic:cenarius","quest:850"},src={"Centaur"}}
E["topic:church-of-the-holy-light"] = {t="topic",n="Church of the Holy Light",z="general",s="The Church of the Holy Light is the dominant religious institution of humanity, guiding mortal souls through the virtues of compassion, justice, and devotion. Originally centered in the lost northern kingdom of Lordaeron, its leadership now resides within the grand Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City following the devastation wrought by the Scourge.",h="The Church of the Holy Light is the dominant religious institution of humanity, guiding mortal souls through...",sec={{t="Origins and the Light's Creed",b="The Church's origins reach back over a millennium to the faith in the Holy Light that took root among the humans of Arathor, teaching them to channel sacred power to cure injuries and purge affliction. Driven by tenets of charity, righteousness, and the fundamental belief that the Light resides within all thinking beings, these teachings eventually coalesced into an organized faith. The kingdom of Lordaeron became the religious heartland of this doctrine, establishing revered sanctuaries throughout the north, including Stratholme, Andorhal, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel.",sp=0},{t="A Shield in the Great Wars",b="In the First War, the Brotherhood of Northshire answered the call to battle, healing fallen defenders against the invading Orcish Horde. Because they lacked weapons or heavy armor, these frontline clerics suffered appalling casualties at the hands of the orcs. Recognizing the need for holy warriors who could both channel holy wrath and survive physical combat, Archbishop Alonsus Faol convened with Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar during the Second War to establish the Knights of the Silver Hand. Paladins and priests alike became the moral and martial bulwark of the Alliance of Lordaeron, preserving humanity from total annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Fall of the North and the Southern Haven",b="The Third War brought immense sorrow upon the faithful. When the Plague of Undeath crept across Lordaeron, the Church worked tirelessly to alleviate the sick and stem the contamination, only to be overwhelmed when the kingdom collapsed from within. With Lordaeron transformed into a Scourge-haunted wilderness, the seat of the Church transferred southward. Under the stewardship of Archbishop Benedictus, the clergy reconstituted its hierarchy within the newly constructed Cathedral of Light in Stormwind, offering solace, spiritual guidance, and military purpose to the battered survivors of the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the core philosophy of the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The Church teaches that a unifying holy energy connects all living things. Practitioners cultivate personal righteousness, altruism, and compassion, seeking to improve both themselves and the world around them. Adherents believe that acting with virtue, justice, and self-sacrifice allows one to reflect the Light's purest essence.",al={"holy light beliefs","what does the church believe","tenets of the light","church philosophy"},it="purpose"},{q="Who leads the Church today?",a="Archbishop Benedictus serves as the spiritual leader of the Church of the Holy Light. Following the passing of Archbishop Alonsus Faol and the fall of the northern kingdom of Lordaeron, Benedictus organized the priesthood from the grand Cathedral of Light situated in the heart of Stormwind City.",al={"church leader","who is the archbishop","head of the church"},it="leader"},{q="How was the order of Paladins formed?",a="During the First War, unarmored clerics from Northshire were deliberately targeted and massacred by the Horde. To ensure holy spellcasters could survive front-line warfare, Archbishop Alonsus Faol founded the Knights of the Silver Hand prior to the Second War, imbuing knightly martial combatants with divine spellcasting abilities.",al={"origin of paladins","how were paladins created","alonsus faol paladins","formation of silver hand"}},{q="Where are the Church's most sacred places?",a="Historically, the Church's oldest holy sites rested in the Eastweald of Lordaeron, including Andorhal, Stratholme, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel. Following the Scourge's destruction of the north, Stormwind's Cathedral of Light became the faith's preeminent sanctuary and seat of authority.",al={"holy sites","sacred places","important shrines"},it="place"},{q="What role did the Church play against the Plague of Undeath?",a="During the Third War, priests and clerics threw themselves into field hospices across Lordaeron, attempting to cleanse the afflicted and halt the plague's spread. Tragically, holy magic proved unable to completely reverse the supernatural corruption, and many clergy fell when their patients succumbed and rose as Scourge monstrosities.",al={"church role in plague","holy light curing the plague","scourge and church"},it="conflict"},{q="Do races other than humans follow the Church?",a="Yes. Ironforge dwarves embraced the Church's teachings centuries ago, establishing their own devout orders of priests and paladins. The high elves of Quel'Thalas also adopted the philosophy, mastering restorative healing magic that they later shared back with human clerics.",al={"non human light worshipers","do dwarves follow the light","high elf priests"},it="people"},{q="What is the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The Church of the Holy Light is the central religious institution of humanity. Its clergy preach that a benevolent Holy Light dwells within all living beings, urging followers to practice compassion, honor, and justice to bring out the best in themselves and the world. Through their devotion, its priests heal the sick and wounded, while the paladins of the Silver Hand serve as the Church's martial arm to protect the weak and vanquish evil.",al={"what is the church of light","who are the church of holy light","church of holy light"},it="identity",add="1109d54d"},{q="Where did the Church of the Holy Light come from?",a="The Church traces its roots to the faith in the Holy Light that spread among the humans of Arathor. Centuries later, migrants founded the kingdom of Lordaeron, which became a heartland for holy ascetics and pilgrims. Lordaeron's leaders eventually organized these beliefs into a unified church hierarchy, establishing shrines, monasteries, and chapels across the human kingdoms.",al={"origins of the church of light","history of church of holy light","how was the church founded"},it="history",add="e7672a04"},{q="Who is the Church of the Holy Light allied with?",a="The Church is deeply intertwined with the Grand Alliance. During the Second War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol pledged the Church's spiritual and martial support to repel the Horde, establishing the Knights of the Silver Hand to fight on the front lines. Following the fall of Lordaeron, the Church reconstituted its seat at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, working directly alongside the Kingdom of Stormwind and its allies.",al={"church of light faction","who does the church of light support","church of light alliance"},it="allies",add="12379874"}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Your holy vows stem from Archbishop Faol's covenant during the Second War, marrying knightly chivalry to the restorative and retributive grace of the Light.",["class:Priest"]="Whether providing comfort to the wounded or studying the difficult balance between luminescence and shadow, your vocation belongs to a legacy spanning thousands of years of human devotion.",["race:Human"]="For generations, the Church has blessed human kingdoms through crisis and plague, and its presence in Stormwind remains the spiritual backbone of your civilization.",["race:Dwarf"]="Long allied with the human realms, many Ironforge dwarves find common ground and shared virtue within the sermons and sanctified halls of the Holy Light.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Church of the Holy Light provides the Alliance with moral unity and battlefield hospice care, standing as an enduring symbol of hope against darkness."},kw={"alonsus faol","benedictus","cathedral of light","church","holy light","lordaeron","northshire","paladin","priest","silver hand","stormwind"},rel={"subzone:cathedral-of-light","npc:archbishop-benedictus","topic:light","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:brotherhood-of-northshire","topic:lordaeron","zone:stormwind","topic:plague-of-undeath"},src={"Church of the Holy Light"}}
E["topic:cult-of-the-damned"] = {t="topic",n="Cult of the Damned",z="general",s="The Cult of the Damned is a secretive cabal of mortal zealots, necromancers, and aristocrats devoted to the Lich King. Formed in northern Lordaeron prior to the Third War, its members poisoned the grain supplies that spawned the Scourge and engineered the downfall of humanity's greatest kingdom. Today, the surviving cultists manage the plague cauldrons and necromantic academies across the Plaguelands while actively infiltrating the cities of the living.",h="The Cult of the Damned is a secretive cabal of mortal zealots, necromancers, and aristocrats devoted to the...",sec={{t="A Sickness in the Soul",b="The Cult of the Damned was forged by the exiled Archmage Kel'Thuzad after he answered the telepathic whispers of the Lich King emanating from the Frozen Throne. Returning to Lordaeron with vast wealth and forbidden dark arts, Kel'Thuzad offered two false gospels: to the desperate, impoverished lower classes, he promised social equality and liberation from misery; to the wealthy landowners, such as the noble House of Barov, he promised eternal life and an unshakeable grip upon their domains. In truth, both the destitute and the affluent were cattle intended for slaughter, bound into a death cult designed to betray their homeland from the inside.",sp=0},{t="The Poisoned Harvest",b="Operating in complete secrecy from the dungeons beneath Caer Darrow, the cultists honed their necrotic craft, built hulking abominations, and perfected the Plague of Undeath brought south from Northrend. They struck at Lordaeron's breadbasket, contaminating the grain stores at Andorhal. As tainted flour traveled through regular merchant routes to hearths across the Eastweald, commoners sickened and died within days, only to rise as shambling thralls under the Lich King's telepathic lash. When Prince Arthas slaughtered Kel'Thuzad in Andorhal, the cultist network was already so deeply rooted that the kingdom's doom was sealed.",sp=0},{t="The Present Threat",b="Though Arthas sailed to Northrend and ascended the Frozen Throne, the Cult of the Damned remains fully operational in northern Lordaeron under the command of the resurrected archlich Kel'Thuzad. From the shadows of Stratholme and the halls of Scholomance, living cultists perform dark rites, maintain the toxic plague cauldrons across the ruined farmsteads, and attempt to assassinate Scarlet Crusaders and Forsaken alike. Because they still breathe, these acolytes can walk unnoticed through living settlements, poisoning wells, sabotaging supply lines, and grooming new recruits to feed the Scourge machine.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why would living mortals join a cult that turns people undead?",a="Kel'Thuzad preyed upon mortal weakness. He offered the impoverished lower classes a new order of absolute equality and relief from their hardships, while promising wealthy nobles like Baron Rivendare and the Barov family immortality and everlasting power. Most initiates were kept ignorant of the true, horrific nature of the plague until it was too late to turn back.",al={"why join cult of the damned","cultist motives","why serve scourge"},it="motive"},{q="Why are cultists allowed to stay alive instead of being killed and raised?",a="Living agents possess immense practical value for the Lich King. They can pass through human settlements unnoticed, trade along standard merchant lines, purchase supplies, and subvert kingdoms from within. In cult doctrine, undeath is also treated as a glorious reward that must be earned through devoted service.",al={"why are cultists human","why aren't they undead","living cultists"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Cult of the Damned based?",a="Their primary scholastic and spiritual redoubt is Scholomance, located within the catacombs beneath Caer Darrow. Beyond this academy, large concentrations of cultists operate in the ruins of Stratholme, around the plague cauldrons of the Western and Eastern Plaguelands, and across secret cells hidden in living settlements.",al={"cult headquarters","where is the cult","cult main base"},it="place"},{q="Who founded the Cult of the Damned?",a="Archmage Kel'Thuzad, a former high-ranking member of Dalaran's Kirin Tor. After being censured for his forbidden necromantic studies, he traveled north to commune with the Lich King, who granted him immense power and sent him back to Lordaeron to organize the mortal vanguard of the Scourge.",al={"who made cult of damned","cult founder","kel'thuzad cult"},it="origin"},{q="How does the Cult of the Damned spread the plague today?",a="While they initially spread the plague through tainted grain shipments from Andorhal, they now primarily rely on massive plague cauldrons situated across Plaguelands farms. Cultist cauldron lords brew foul reagents to continuously release noxious clouds of blight that choke the land and raise fallen corpses.",al={"how plague spreads","plague cauldrons","how does cult attack"},it="threat"},{q="What is the cult's relationship to the Forsaken?",a="They are bitter enemies. The Forsaken broke free from the Lich King's telepathic command and seek vengeance against the Scourge. Cultists view the Forsaken as stolen property and heretics, constantly seeking to annihilate them or reclaim the ruins of Lordaeron for the Frozen Throne.",al={"cult vs forsaken","forsaken cult of the damned"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the Cult of the Damned?",a="The Cult of the Damned is a clandestine network of living mortals who serve the Lich King and act as the mortal foundation of the Scourge. Composed primarily of fanatical humans alongside members of other races, they infiltrate settlements, study dark necromancy, and work tirelessly to bring death and plague to the living kingdoms in exchange for promises of immortality and power.",al={"what is cult of the damned","who are the cult of the damned","cult of the damned identity"},it="identity",add="13254224"},{q="Who leads the Cult of the Damned?",a="The cult is commanded on Azeroth by the archlich Kel'Thuzad, the former Dalaran archmage who founded the order. Above him reigns the Lich King himself, bound to the Frozen Throne in Northrend, whom all cultists revere as their dark lord and god.",al={"who leads cult of the damned","cult of the damned leader","who is the leader of the cult"},it="leader",add="3f52b63c"},{q="Who are the Cult of the Damned allied with?",a="The cult works directly for the Lich King and serves alongside the legions of the undead Scourge. While they once facilitated the arrival of the Burning Legion during the Third War, their absolute devotion belongs to the throne of Northrend and its lich commander, Kel'Thuzad.",al={"cult of the damned allies","who does the cult work for","who is cult of the damned allied with"},it="allies",add="3a452ce2"},{q="Where did the Cult of the Damned come from?",a="Years before the Third War, the Lich King telepathically reached out from Northrend to Archmage Kel'Thuzad, tempting him with necromantic power. Kel'Thuzad returned to Lordaeron, using his fortune and demagoguery to recruit disillusioned commoners and power-hungry nobles alike. Operating in secret out of Caer Darrow's catacombs, the cult developed the plague of undeath, distributed tainted grain throughout Andorhal and northern Lordaeron, and paved the way for the kingdom's catastrophic downfall.",al={"cult of the damned history","history of cult of the damned","how did the cult start"},it="history",add="3250aa21"},{q="Why are the Cult of the Damned hostile to travelers?",a="The cultists view all living people outside their ranks as raw materials for their necromantic experiments or future soldiers for the Scourge. They attack outsiders on sight to guard their clandestine laboratories, protect their plague cauldrons, and eliminate anyone who could disrupt their dark rituals.",al={"why are cultists hostile","why does the cult attack you","cult of the damned hostile"},it="hostile",add="af150ded"}},ang={["race:Human"]="Every human remembers the collapse of Lordaeron; the Cult of the Damned serves as a bitter reminder that humanity was not conquered by demons alone, but by traitors from within their own cities and estates.",["race:Undead"]="To the Forsaken, cultists are pathetic lapdogs who willingly sacrifice their freedom to the master whose mental chains the Forsaken fought so hard to shatter.",["class:Paladin"]="The Cult of the Damned represents the ultimate blasphemy against the Holy Light, wielding foul necromancy to corrupt the living and disturb the eternal rest of the dead.",["class:Mage"]="The cult is a painful stain on the honor of the Kirin Tor, having been founded by one of Dalaran's own esteemed archmages, Kel'Thuzad.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance scouts and soldiers must remain constantly vigilant, as cult infiltrators still attempt to sow plague and chaos inside living cities like Stormwind."},kw={"andorhal","caer darrow","cauldron lord","cult of the damned","kel'thuzad","lich king","plague of undeath","plaguelands","scholomance","scourge"},rel={"subzone:scholomance","subzone:andorhal","subzone:caer-darrow","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:forsaken"},src={"Cult of the Damned"}}
E["topic:dark-iron-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Dark Iron clan",z="general",s="The Dark Iron clan is an ancient, ash-skinned dwarven faction ruling from the volcanic depths of Blackrock Mountain. Cast out of Ironforge during the War of the Three Hammers, they accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, who enslaved their empire. Today, under Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, they wage shadow wars against both the Alliance and the Horde while serving their fiery elemental master.",h="The Dark Iron clan is an ancient, ash-skinned dwarven faction ruling from the volcanic depths of Blackrock...",sec={{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Long ago, three dwarven factions lived together in Ironforge under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died, civil war erupted. The Bronzebeards defeated both rival factions, casting out the Wildhammers and Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan's Dark Irons. Vowing vengeance, Thaurissan established a settlement in the Redridge Mountains while his sorceress wife, Modgud, led an army against Grim Batol. When both assaults failed and the united Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies marched on his stronghold, Thaurissan attempted to unleash an apocalyptic subterranean entity to save himself. His ritual breached the core of the world and freed Ragnaros the Firelord.",sp=0},{t="Shattered Lands and Bondage",b="The rebirth of Ragnaros violently shattered the surrounding peaks, carving out the blazing caldera of Blackrock Mountain flanked by the Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes. The sorcerer-thane perished in the cataclysm, and the surviving Dark Irons found themselves bound into perpetual servitude to the Firelord. Over centuries within the lower chambers of the mountain, they constructed the sprawling subterranean capital of Shadowforge City. Their artisans and sorcerers became masters of stone golems, dark sorceries, and steam-driven siege machinery, weaponizing the molten elements around them under the gaze of flamewakers and elemental overlords.",sp=0},{t="Spreading Shadow",b="In 25 ADP, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan rules the clan from his Imperial Seat deep in Blackrock Depths. While secretly plotting to overthrow Ragnaros using the combined strength of all dwarven lands, Thaurissan obeys the Firelord's commands to prepare for total war. Dark Iron infiltrators and raiding parties strike far from their mountain, launching incursions into Loch Modan, Dun Morogh, the Badlands, and the Wetlands. Their forces and an ambassador can even be found inside Gnomeregan, though their precise role in its fall is unclear.",sp=1},{t="The Stolen Princess",b="Tensions reached a boiling point when Princess Moira Bronzebeard, daughter and sole heir of King Magni, was taken by Dark Iron agents while traveling to Lakeshire. Magni believes his daughter is an enchanted hostage held against her will in Blackrock Depths. Rumors filtering out of Shadowforge City paint a far more complex picture: the princess has become the emperor's bride, embracing a place of power among the very dwarves her father despises.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Dark Iron capital located?",a="Their capital is Shadowforge City, carved deep within the subterranean caverns of Blackrock Depths beneath Blackrock Mountain. It features enormous stonework, Senate chambers, barracks, and the emperor's Imperial Seat.",al={"where do dark iron dwarves live","dark iron home","dark iron capital name","shadowforge city location"},it="place"},{q="Why do the Dark Iron dwarves have dark skin and glowing eyes?",a="Dark Iron dwarves have ash-gray or dark skin and glowing red eyes. They live beneath Blackrock Mountain, but the precise cause of their appearance is not established.",al={"why do dark iron look different","dark iron dwarf appearance","why are they ashen"},it="origin"},{q="Who is Emperor Dagran Thaurissan?",a="Emperor Dagran Thaurissan is the direct descendant of the original Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan. He rules the Dark Iron clan from Blackrock Depths and answers directly to the elemental lord Ragnaros, though he privately dreams of breaking free from the Firelord's bondage.",al={"dagran thaurissan","who leads dark iron clan","dark iron king"},it="leader"},{q="Why did the Dark Iron clan summon Ragnaros?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan faced certain defeat when the combined armies of Ironforge and Grim Batol marched upon his city. Desperate for a weapon to destroy his enemies, he performed an ancient ritual that accidentally released the slumbering Firelord, shattering the mountains and enslaving his people.",al={"how was ragnaros summoned","thaurissan ragnaros summon","why summoned firelord"},it="motive"},{q="What is the Thorium Brotherhood?",a="The Thorium Brotherhood is a splinter faction of Dark Iron craftsmen who broke away from the clan and the Firelord's dominion. Based out of Thorium Point in Searing Gorge, they trade with outsiders and seek their own fortunes, earning the permanent enmity of their former kin.",al={"thorium brotherhood dark iron","are thorium brotherhood dark irons","friendly dark iron dwarves"}},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves fighting in Gnomeregan?",a="Dark Iron forces, including the Dark Iron Ambassador, can be found inside fallen Gnomeregan. Their presence suggests involvement with Mekgineer Thermaplugg, but their precise role in the disaster is unclear.",al={"dark iron in gnomeregan","dark iron ambassador gnomeregan","why are dark irons with troggs"},it="why_here"},{q="Are the Dark Irons allied with the Blackrock orcs?",a="No. While they once tolerated the Old Horde occupying the upper spire, the Dark Irons and the Blackrock orcs of the upper levels now fight a vicious turf war. The Dark Irons hold the depths, while Rend Blackhand's orcs control the upper citadel.",al={"dark iron orc alliance","dark iron vs blackrock clan","who owns blackrock mountain"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Dark Irons attack Grim Batol?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorceress Modgud led a Dark Iron army to destroy the Wildhammer clan at Grim Batol. Although Modgud was slain by Khardros Wildhammer, her foul sorceries cursed the fortress so deeply that the Wildhammers could never live there again.",al={"modgud grim batol","why grim batol abandoned","curse of grim batol"},it="history"},{q="What is the Dark Iron clan?",a="The Dark Iron clan is one of the three primary dwarven factions, alongside the Bronzebeards and Wildhammers. Renowned for their sorcery, ruthless ambition, and master stonework, they dwell deep within Blackrock Mountain under their capital of Shadowforge City. Bound in servitude to the Firelord Ragnaros, they wage war against both the Alliance and the Horde.",al={"who are dark irons","dark iron dwarves","what are dark irons"},it="identity",add="2c9aae62"},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves hostile to travelers?",a="Driven by centuries of bitter hatred toward Ironforge and ensnared by the fiery tyranny of Ragnaros, the Dark Irons see all outsiders as enemies or tools. Under the Firelord's commands, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan is preparing his armies for total conquest, launching aggressive raids into neighboring lands and slaughtering anyone who trespasses on their territory.",al={"why do dark irons attack","dark iron hostility","are dark irons evil"},it="hostile",add="afe1d736"},{q="Who are the Dark Iron clan allied with?",a="The Dark Iron clan serves Ragnaros the Firelord and his elemental armies, bound in harsh servitude ever since their sorcerers mistakenly summoned him. While they maintain a treacherous rivalry with the Blackrock orcs occupying the upper spire, their forces are also present inside fallen Gnomeregan, though their precise role there is unclear.",al={"dark iron allies","who do dark irons work for","dark iron masters"},it="allies",add="627d3f57"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For a Bronzebeard, the Dark Irons are ancestral traitors and sorcerers whose ancient grudges still threaten Ironforge's borders from Loch Modan to Dun Morogh.",["race:Gnome"]="The presence of a Dark Iron Ambassador deep inside fallen Gnomeregan raises suspicions about the clan’s involvement in its fall.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dark Iron smiths are unmatched masters of fiery metallurgy; their specialized Dark Iron anvils and forges deep in Blackrock Depths are required to craft legendary armaments.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance views the Dark Irons as an urgent existential threat, not only for their constant border raids, but for the kidnapping of Ironforge's crown princess Moira Bronzebeard.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde actively clashes with the Dark Iron armies across the Badlands and the Burning Steppes as both seek supremacy over the ruins surrounding Blackrock Spire."},kw={"blackrock","bronzebeard","dark iron","flamelash","modgud","moira","ragnaros","shadowforge","sorcerer-thane","thaurissan"},rel={"topic:dark-iron-dwarf","subzone:blackrock-mountain","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","zone:searinggorge","zone:burningsteppes","npc:magni-bronzebeard","npc:dark-iron-ambassador"},src={"Dark Iron clan"}}
E["topic:dark-iron-dwarf"] = {t="topic",n="Dark Iron dwarf",z="general",s="Dark Iron dwarves are a distinct dwarven sub-race distinguished by their ashen skin, blazing red eyes, and innate affinity for fire. Banished from Ironforge following the civil War of the Three Hammers, they dwell deep within Blackrock Mountain where their society has fallen under the tyrannical rule of the Firelord, Ragnaros.",h="Dark Iron dwarves are a distinct dwarven sub-race distinguished by their ashen skin, blazing red eyes, and...",sec={{t="Fiery Heritage and Exile",b="Unlike their Bronzebeard and Wildhammer cousins, Dark Iron dwarves possess dark gray or soot-black skin and eyes that burn like glowing embers. Their history fractured during the War of the Three Hammers, when their Sorcerer-thane Thaurissan sought to conquer Ironforge. Defeated and driven south into the Redridge Mountains, Thaurissan attempted to summon dark powers to avenge his losses, inadvertently releasing Ragnaros the Firelord from the Elemental Plane. The catastrophic summoning shattered the surrounding peaks into the volcanic waste of Blackrock Mountain and bound the Dark Iron race into fiery servitude.",sp=0},{t="Under the Firelord's Shadow",b="Life in the deep subterranean city of Shadowforge is brutal and unforgiving. While the Dark Irons were once merely ambitious and cunning, generations beneath Blackrock Mountain under the oppressive aura of Ragnaros have warped much of their culture into cruelty, paranoia, and tyranny. Dark Iron law knows only one sentence for infractions: trial by combat in the brutal Ring of the Law. Poisoning and political intrigue are so prevalent that Dark Iron travelers customarily pack their own rations rather than trust food from strangers.",sp=0},{t="Splinters and Outcasts",b="Not all Dark Iron dwarves serve Emperor Dagran Thaurissan and the cult of Ragnaros. Dissident factions have broken away to pursue their own ends. The most prominent among them is the Thorium Brotherhood, a guild of master smiths based out of Thorium Point in Searing Gorge who have defected from the main clan to hire outside adventurers against their former masters. Others operate as mercenaries, spies, or trade engineers alongside neutral organizations like the Venture Company.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do Dark Iron dwarves have gray skin and red eyes?",a="Their dark gray skin and fiery, glowing red eyes are natural traits that distinguish them from mountain and hill dwarves. Their deep subterranean lifestyle and centuries living in proximity to the elemental fires of Ragnaros have further emphasized their volcanic nature.",al={"dark iron appearance","why do dark irons look like that","red eyes dark iron"},it="origin"},{q="Are all Dark Iron dwarves evil?",a="Not necessarily. Although imperial Dark Iron society is treacherous and ruled by the will of Ragnaros, historical figures like the legendary architect Franclorn Forgewright and groups like the rebel Thorium Brotherhood demonstrate honor, valor, and independent will apart from the Firelord's corruption.",al={"are all dark irons bad","good dark iron dwarves","can dark irons be good"},it="people"},{q="Where is the capital of the Dark Iron dwarves?",a="Their civilization is centered in Shadowforge City, deep inside the subterranean caverns of Blackrock Depths within Blackrock Mountain. From there, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan oversees his empire under the shadow of Molten Core.",al={"dark iron capital","where do dark irons live","dark iron city"},it="place"},{q="What is the Ring of the Law?",a="The Ring of the Law is the arena where Dark Iron justice is meted out. In Dark Iron society, the penalty for crimes against the clan is death. Accused individuals are forced to pay the blood price by fighting waves of vicious beasts and gladiators, a trial virtually no prisoner ever survives.",al={"ring of the law","dark iron arena","shadowforge trial"}},{q="What is the Thorium Brotherhood?",a="The Thorium Brotherhood is a rogue faction of Dark Iron craftsmen who broke away from the service of Ragnaros. Camped at Thorium Point in Searing Gorge, they trade their unrivaled knowledge of fiery metallurgy and Dark Iron smithing secrets to adventurers willing to undermine the imperial clan.",al={"thorium brotherhood lore","who are the thorium brotherhood","thorium point dwarves"}},{q="What are Dark Iron dwarves?",a="The Dark Irons are a distinct breed of dwarves, predominantly hailing from the Dark Iron clan. Known for their ashen skin, fiery red eyes, and mastery over sorcery and craft, they possess fierce determination and notorious tempers. While most dwell in their subterranean domain beneath Blackrock Mountain, some belong to independent factions like the Thorium Brotherhood or mercenary outfits.",al={"who are dark irons","dark iron dwarves","what is dark iron dwarf"},it="identity",add="95842395"},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves hostile to travelers?",a="Centuries of isolation, internal treachery, and the corrupting presence of the Firelord, Ragnaros, have left the Dark Iron clan manipulative, cruel, and deeply suspicious of outsiders. They view transgressions against their clan with extreme severity, punishing trespassers and criminals with death or bloody combat in the Ring of the Law.",al={"why do dark irons attack","dark iron dwarves aggressive","why are dark irons hostile"},it="hostile",add="25c6bc58"},{q="Who leads the Dark Iron dwarves?",a="The Dark Iron clan is ruled by Emperor Dagran Thaurissan from his throne in Shadowforge City, deep within Blackrock Depths.",al={"dark iron dwarf leader","who is emperor of dark irons","dark iron ruler"},sp=1,it="leader",add="606deda9"},{q="Who are the Dark Iron dwarves allied with or serving?",a="Most of the Dark Iron clan is bound in servitude to the elemental Firelord, Ragnaros, whose fiery influence permeates their society. Many also operate alongside elemental forces and dark cults, while independent splinter groups like the Thorium Brotherhood work against the clan's leadership.",al={"who do dark irons work for","dark iron allies","dark irons ragnaros"},it="allies",add="d8b686f4"},{q="Where did the Dark Iron dwarves come from?",a="The Dark Irons were once one of the three ruling dwarven clans of Ironforge. Following the War of the Three Hammers, their sorcerer-thane Thaurissan fled south and inadvertently summoned Ragnaros the Firelord into Azeroth. The cataclysm shattered the Redridge Mountains, creating Blackrock Mountain and chaining the Dark Iron clan to the Firelord's will.",al={"dark iron dwarf origin","history of dark irons","war of the three hammers"},it="history",add="7edffefb"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, the Dark Irons represent an enduring family feud turned existential threat, dating back to their bloody rebellion during the War of the Three Hammers.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dark Iron artisans are legendary smiths who hold exclusive mastery over Dark Iron ore, requiring the searing heat of Blackrock Mountain's Black Forge to melt and shape.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance scouts watch the Dark Irons warily, recognizing their imperial armies as an aggressive, expansionist menace pressing right at the southern gates of Ironforge's territory.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde views the Dark Irons as dangerous zealots occupying Blackrock Mountain, directly threatening Horde supply routes through the Badlands and adjacent passes."},kw={"blackrock depths","dark iron","dwarf","ironfoe","ragnaros","shadowforge","thaurissan","thorium brotherhood"},rel={"topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:dwarf","subzone:blackrock-mountain","subzone:thorium-point","zone:searinggorge","subzone:grim-batol","zone:ironforge","topic:venture-company"},src={"Dark Iron dwarf"}}
E["topic:darkspear-tribe"] = {t="topic",n="Darkspear tribe",z="multi",s="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who joined Thrall's Horde after being driven from their ancestral home in Stranglethorn Vale and rescued from a sea witch in the Great Sea. Led by Shadow Hunter Vol'jin from their temporary refuge at Sen'jin Village, they now serve as core members of the Horde across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. Despite their alliance, they currently struggle to reclaim their new home, the Echo Isles, from the traitorous witch doctor Zalazane.",h="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who joined Thrall's Horde after being driven from their ancestral...",sec={{t="Exile and Salvation",b="Originally a smaller, less ruthless tribe within the broken Gurubashi Empire of Stranglethorn Vale, the Darkspear endured centuries of warfare and famine against brutal neighbors like the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter tribes. Driven out to sea, they settled on remote islands, only to face aquatic murlocs, Kul Tiran sailors, and a naga sea witch. Their elder chieftain, Sen'jin, was captured and slain in dark sacrificial rites beneath the sea, but before dying he foretold that the orc Warchief Thrall would lead the Darkspear to a new destiny. Rescued by Thrall's passing fleet, the survivors pledged their undying loyalty to the new Horde and sailed westward to Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the Echo Isles",b="Upon reaching Durotar, the Darkspear established a home on the tropical Echo Isles just off the mainland coast. That peace was short-lived. First, Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet drove them temporarily into the swamps of Dustwallow Marsh before Horde forces destroyed the invaders. Worse betrayal followed from within. A prominent witch doctor named Zalazane embraced twisted voodoo, seizing control of the minds of his kin. To preserve his free people, Sen'jin's son and successor, Vol'jin, ordered another evacuation. Today, the Darkspear dwell in the coastal encampment of Sen'jin Village, sending champions across the water to slay Zalazane and release their thrall-bound brothers.",sp=1},{t="Voodoo and the New Horde",b="Unlike their cannibalistic jungle kin, the Darkspear have adapted rapidly to the Horde's values of honor, establishing enclaves like Malaka'jin in the Stonetalon Mountains, Shadowprey Village in Desolace, and Zoram'gar Outpost in Ashenvale. In Orgrimmar, they make their spiritual home within the Valley of Spirits, where shadow hunters and witch doctors commune with the loa. Though respected for their scouts, bat riders, and powerful hexes, old traditions run deep; some ancient rituals frowned upon by their allies are still practiced quietly in the dark.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the Darkspear trolls in the Horde?",a="During the Third War, Warchief Thrall's fleet took shelter on the Darkspear islands during a violent storm. When a naga sea witch and her murloc minions captured the trolls and orcs alike, Thrall freed them. Though Chieftain Sen'jin died in the escape, his dying vision foresaw Thrall guiding the Darkspear to safety. In gratitude, Sen'jin's son Vol'jin swore the tribe's allegiance to the Horde.",al={"why trolls horde","darkspear thrall origin","how did trolls join horde"},it="allies"},{q="Who leads the Darkspear tribe right now?",a="The tribe is led by Vol'jin, the son of the revered fallen chieftain Sen'jin. Vol'jin is a cunning shadow hunter and one of Warchief Thrall's closest, most trusted confidants, advising the Horde from the Valley of Spirits in Orgrimmar and overseeing the struggle to reclaim the Echo Isles.",al={"darkspear leader","who is troll leader","troll chieftain"},it="leader"},{q="Why do the Darkspear live in Sen'jin Village instead of the Echo Isles?",a="The Echo Isles were meant to be the tribe's permanent sanctuary in Durotar, but they were betrayed from within. A power-mad witch doctor named Zalazane used dark magic to enslave the minds of his fellow trolls. Rather than slaughter their own enchanted kin, Vol'jin ordered the free Darkspear to retreat to the mainland shore, founding Sen'jin Village as a base to fight back.",al={"why senjin village","what happened to echo isles","why are echo isles hostile"},it="why_here"},{q="Where did the Darkspear originally come from?",a="The Darkspear originated in the jungles of Stranglethorn Vale as part of the fractured Gurubashi Empire. Being smaller and less merciless than rival tribes like the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter, they suffered constant warfare and starvation until they were driven out of the Eastern Kingdoms entirely.",al={"darkspear original home","darkspear stranglethorn","where are darkspear from"},it="history"},{q="Do Darkspear trolls still practice cannibalism?",a="Officially, no. To live in harmony with Thrall's Horde and honor their alliance, the Darkspear renounced cannibalism and headhunting as tribal policy. However, centuries of jungle heritage do not vanish overnight, and whispers persist that darker traditional rites are still observed in secret far from prying eyes.",al={"are darkspear cannibals","troll cannibalism horde","do horde trolls eat people"}},{q="What deities do the Darkspear worship?",a="The Darkspear revere the loa, powerful primal spirits and animal gods. Their faith centers around a vast pantheon, honored through the voodoo rites of their witch doctors and shadow hunters.",al={"darkspear religion","darkspear gods","troll loa"}},{q="What is the Darkspear tribe?",a="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who stand as core members of Thrall's Horde. Once driven out of their ancient lands in Stranglethorn Vale, they survived exile, a perilous sea voyage, and betrayal at home. Known for their adaptability and cunning, they have embraced the Horde's code of honor while maintaining their traditions of shadow magic, voodoo, and loa worship.",al={"who are darkspear trolls","what are the darkspear","darkspear tribe"},it="identity",add="f751a05c"}},ang={["race:Troll"]="As a Darkspear troll, you carry the heavy weight of an exiled people rebuilding on foreign shores. Every blow struck against Zalazane on the Echo Isles or against your brutal Gurubashi cousins in Stranglethorn Vale helps secure your tribe's survival.",["race:Orc"]="The bond between orc and troll was forged in the blood of the Third War. Thrall considers Vol'jin his closest brother-in-arms, and the Darkspear's scouts and witch doctors serve faithfully beside the warrior clans of Durotar.",["faction:Horde"]="From bat riders defending the skies to herbalists brewing draughts in Stonetalon, the Darkspear provide mystical cunning and stealth that balance the raw martial might of the Horde.",["class:Shaman"]="Darkspear shamans bridge the ancient worship of primal loa spirits with the elemental teachings learned from their orc and tauren allies, with figures like Je'neu Sancrea representing the tribe within the Earthen Ring."},kw={"darkspear","echo isles","gurubashi","loa","malaka'jin","sen'jin","shadowprey","vol'jin","zalazane"},rel={"npc:vol-jin","subzone:sen-jin-village","subzone:echo-isles","quest:826","subzone:valley-of-spirits","topic:troll","npc:thrall","topic:horde"},src={"Darkspear tribe"}}
E["topic:defias-brotherhood"] = {t="topic",n="Defias Brotherhood",z="general",s="The Defias Brotherhood is a widespread criminal network of bandits, brigands, and assassins waging an underground insurgency against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Their origins are a mystery to most, and they have grown into violent outlaws terrorizing the southern farmlands. From a hidden redoubt, they aim to overthrow the corrupt House of Nobles and lay waste to the kingdom.",h="The Defias Brotherhood is a widespread criminal network of bandits, brigands, and assassins waging an...",sec={{t="A Web of Conspiracies",b="The Brotherhood has long outgrown a simple band of displaced laborers. VanCleef recruited mercenary goblins to oversee armaments, struck alliances with Riverpaw gnolls and Elwynn kobolds, and traded with cutthroat pirates like the Bloodsail Buccaneers. Defias highwaymen raid gold shipments across Elwynn Forest, and their agents even established a foothold in Duskwood before unearthing a cursed artifact in Roland's Doom. Far worse, the Brotherhood's influence reaches into the very heart of the capital, enjoying covert support from treacherous aristocrats within the House of Nobles while orchestrating kidnappings that strike at the highest offices of the Alliance.",sp=1,bt={"unsent.lescovar"}},{t="The Juggernaught in the Deadmines",b="Beneath Moonbrook lies the Ironclad Cove, where VanCleef's goblin engineers are secretly assembling an enormous iron-plated warship known as the Defias Juggernaught. The guildmaster intends to sail this floating fortress out through the subterranean coastal waterways and bombard Stormwind City from the sea, reducing his own grand architecture to rubble. The conspiracy extends into the royal court itself: corrupt nobles like Lord Gregor Lescovar serve as eyes and ears for the uprising, while the court machinations of Lady Katrana Prestor deliberately keep the kingdom fractured and blind to the rising fleet beneath Westfall.",sp=2,bt={"defias.juggernaut","unsent.lescovar","prestor.onyxia"}},{t="From Masons to Marauders",b="Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild was hired to rebuild Stormwind from smoldering ruins into a grand stone capital. When their masterwork was finished, the House of Nobles refused to deliver the promised payment, citing crushing wartime debt. Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef spoke out on behalf of his workers, demanding fair restitution, but the nobles responded by ordering the Stonemasons Guild disbanded. The ensuing riots in the city streets ended with the laborers driven out of Stormwind into exile. Stripped of their livelihood and outlawed, VanCleef and the desperate workers banded together, binding their faces in red silk to represent the blood of the wronged, and turned to highway robbery to claim what they believed they were owed.",sp=1,bt={"defias.masons"}},{t="The Shadow Over Westfall",b="With the royal army overextended, VanCleef turned the neglected farmlands of Westfall into the Brotherhood's base of operations. The bandits overran the hamlet of Moonbrook and commandeered the abandoned subterranean tunnels of the Deadmines. Defias agents seized local mines, driven farmers from their homesteads with reprogrammed harvest golems, and salted the earth to starve the region. With the Crown unable or unwilling to send soldiers to protect rural citizens, local farmers formed The People's Militia out of Sentinel Hill to fight a desperate war of attrition against an enemy that now outmans and outguns them.",sp=1,bt={"defias.hideout"}}},faq={{q="Why do the Defias wear red bandanas?",a="The red silk bandana was adopted by Edwin VanCleef and the exiled stonemasons as a symbol of defiance. It represents the blood shed by the oppressed working class who rebuilt Stormwind and were met with broken promises, violence, and exile by the nobility.",al={"defias mask meaning","why red silk","red bandanas meaning"},sp=1,it="name",bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="Why is the Brotherhood attacking innocent farmers instead of nobles?",a="While the Brotherhood was founded on genuine grievances against the nobility, years of lawless exile transformed it into a brutal criminal empire. Desperation, greed, and the recruitment of ruthless cutthroats and goblins turned them toward pillaging local homesteads, burning crops, and seizing mines to fund their war machine.",al={"why defias attack farmers","why hurt westfall","defias motive"},it="hostile"},{q="Who is Edwin VanCleef?",a="Edwin VanCleef was the brilliant guildmaster of the Stonemasons Guild who oversaw the reconstruction of Stormwind City after the Second War. Trained in stealth and subterfuge, he founded the Defias Brotherhood after the House of Nobles refused to pay the workers. He now orchestrates their operations from deep within the Deadmines.",al={"who is vancleef","edwin vancleef lore","defias leader"},sp=1,it="leader",bt={"defias.masons","defias.hideout"}},{q="Why doesn't Stormwind send the army to deal with the Defias?",a="The kingdom's finances are heavily drained, and the Stormwind Army is stretched thin garrisoning outposts in distant territories like Stranglethorn. Political gridlock within the House of Nobles further paralyzes the throne, leaving rural Westfall and Duskwood to defend themselves with volunteer militias.",al={"where is the stormwind army","why no guards in westfall","why people's militia"},sp=2,it="conflict"},{q="What is the People's Militia?",a="The People's Militia is a volunteer defense force organized by Gryan Stoutmantle and the beleaguered farmers of Sentinel Hill. Because Stormwind has provided almost no soldiers to clear the Defias out of Westfall, local citizens took up arms to defend their own land.",al={"peoples militia westfall","gryan stoutmantle militia","sentinel hill army"},it="conflict"},{q="What are the Defias building inside the Deadmines?",a="Hidden within the subterranean cavern known as the Ironclad Cove, goblin engineers and rogue artisans are constructing the Defias Juggernaught, a massive iron-plated war galley. VanCleef intends to sail the warship into Stormwind Harbor and obliterate the capital.",al={"deadmines ship","what are they building deadmines","defias juggernaught"},sp=1,it="threat",bt={"defias.juggernaut"}},{q="Did the Defias have something to do with the King's disappearance?",a="What became of King Varian Wrynn remains a mystery. He vanished while traveling to a peace summit in Theramore, leaving the child Anduin on the throne under regent rule.",al={"did defias kidnap varian","missing king defias","missing diplomat defias"},sp=2,it="ties"},{q="What is the Defias Brotherhood?",a="The Defias Brotherhood is an extensive criminal syndicate and rebel order made up of former stonemasons, thieves, brigands, and mercenaries. Originally born out of a bitter dispute between the craftsmen who rebuilt Stormwind and the corrupt nobility who refused to pay them, the group has evolved into a violent underground army committed to overthrowing the Kingdom of Stormwind.",al={"what is the defias brotherhood","who are the defias","defias brotherhood overview"},it="identity",add="d3e0b685",nx={9}},{q="How did the Defias Brotherhood form?",a="After the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild restored Stormwind City from ruins. When the work was complete, the House of Nobles, burdened by military debt, refused to pay them. The guildmaster, Edwin VanCleef, led riots in protest, and the stonemasons were driven into exile. They took refuge in Westfall and turned to banditry to claim their promised wages, steadily swelling their ranks with criminals and outcasts.",al={"defias origin","history of defias","how did defias start","stonemasons guild defias"},it="history",add="5e60415a"},{q="Why are the Defias Brotherhood operating in Westfall and Elwynn?",a="The Defias base their primary operations beneath the town of Moonbrook in the Deadmines of Westfall, an impoverished region neglected by Stormwind's military. From there, they spread through Westfall, Elwynn Forest, and Duskwood to seize valuable mines, raid supply lines, and keep Stormwind's stretched defenses preoccupied while they secretly prepare a catastrophic strike against the capital.",al={"why are defias in westfall","why are defias in elwynn","where are defias located"},it="why_here",add="a3005855"},{q="Who are the Defias Brotherhood allied with?",a="The Brotherhood has forged ties across the southern lands. In Elwynn Forest, they employ kobolds to secure captured mines, while coordinating with Riverpaw gnolls in Westfall. They have engaged goblin engineers to build deadly harvest golems, dealt with the Bloodsail Buccaneers for maritime expertise, and covertly collaborated with treacherous figures inside the capital.",al={"defias allies","who works with defias","defias brotherhood partners"},sp=1,it="allies",add="b1c3d7cc",bt={"q65.7"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of the kingdom, the Defias are a bitter reminder of Stormwind's internal rot—a tragedy where honest city builders were turned into cutthroats due to aristocratic greed.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand and church leaders like Duthorian Rall call upon paladins to defend defenseless homesteaders like Daphne Stilwell from ruthless Defias brigands in Westfall.",["class:Rogue"]="Master Mathias Shaw and SI:7 keep close watch on the Defias, as VanCleef's operatives match royal scouts in lockpicking, stealth, and lethal subterranean ambushes.",["faction:Alliance"]="The uprising threatens the stability of the entire kingdom, choking grain supplies from Westfall, destabilizing gold mines, and shaking public faith in Stormwind's leadership."},kw={"bandanas","brotherhood","deadmines","defias","elwynn","juggernaught","moonbrook","stonemasons","vancleef","westfall"},rel={"npc:edwin-vancleef","zone:westfall","zone:deadmines","topic:stonemasons-guild","npc:gryan-stoutmantle","topic:house-of-nobles","subzone:moonbrook","quest:65"},src={"Defias Brotherhood"}}
E["topic:dragonmaw-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Dragonmaw clan",z="general",s="The Dragonmaw clan is an infamous orcish faction notorious for subduing and riding red dragons during the Second War. Guided by dark artifacts and foul magic, they imprisoned the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza beneath the cursed halls of Grim Batol until her liberation shattered their dominion. Today, their scattered remnants lurk in the wetlands and mountains of Khaz Modan, remaining hostile to both the Alliance and Thrall's New Horde.",h="The Dragonmaw clan is an infamous orcish faction notorious for subduing and riding red dragons during the...",sec={{t="From Draenor to Azeroth",b="The Dragonmaw clan hailed from Draenor and crossed the Dark Portal with the Old Horde. On Azeroth, they turned their talents to subjugating its winged terrors.",sp=0},{t="The Demon Soul and Grim Batol",b="Guided by whispers from the black dragon Deathwing, the clan's chieftain, Zuluhed the Whacked, recovered an ancient golden disk: the Dragon Soul. Zuluhed entrusted this relic to his greatest warlock, Nekros Skullcrusher, who tapped into its fel taint and christened it the Demon Soul. Using the artifact's agonizing power, Nekros ensnared Alexstrasza the Life-Binder herself. The Dragonmaw fortified the abandoned dwarven bastion of Grim Batol, keeping the Dragonqueen chained in darkness and forcing her red dragon brood to serve the Old Horde as aerial dreadnoughts during the Second War.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the Dragon Riders",b="The Dragonmaw reign ended when the Demon Soul was finally destroyed, freeing Alexstrasza and her flight from their psychic torment. Alexstrasza devoured Nekros Skullcrusher, and her vengeful flight laid waste to Grim Batol, breaking the clan's back. Routed and terrified, the surviving Dragonmaw scattered into the marshy bogs of the Wetlands and the jagged peaks around Grim Batol. The remnants on Azeroth live as desperate, feral renegades, fiercely guarding their dwindling encampments against dwarven patrols and vengeful dragons alike.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are they called the Dragonmaw if they are from Draenor?",a="The name reflects what the clan became infamous for on Azeroth: enslaving and riding red dragons for the Old Horde during the Second War.",al={"dragonmaw name origin","orcish name"},it="name"},{q="How did the clan manage to control giant red dragons?",a="They utilized the Demon Soul, an ancient artifact provided indirectly by the black Aspect Deathwing. The warlock Nekros Skullcrusher wielded it to inflict torturous agony on the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza, effectively forcing her and her entire flight to obey under threat of her destruction.",al={"how did they control dragons","demon soul enslavement","how was alexstrasza captured"},it="power"},{q="Are the Dragonmaw part of Thrall's Horde?",a="No. The Dragonmaw were loyal to the Old Horde of Blackhand and his sons. Thrall's New Horde has rejected demon worship and the enslavement of dragons, leaving the Dragonmaw as renegade outcasts hostile to both the Horde and the Alliance.",al={"are dragonmaw in horde","dragonmaw new horde","thrall and dragonmaw"},it="side"},{q="What happened to the clan after the Second War?",a="When the Demon Soul was destroyed, the liberated red dragons turned on the Dragonmaw and destroyed their stronghold at Grim Batol. Nekros was slain, and the surviving orcs fled into the Wetlands and the neighboring mountains, where they remain entrenched as aggressive raiders.",al={"where are the dragonmaw now","dragonmaw after second war","what happened to grim batol"},it="history"},{q="Who is Zuluhed the Whacked?",a="Zuluhed was the elder shaman and chieftain of the Dragonmaw clan who first received the visions of dragon riding from Deathwing.",al={"zuluhed","chieftain of dragonmaw","who leads dragonmaw"},it="leader"},{q="Where can Dragonmaw orcs be found right now?",a="On Azeroth, they are primarily encountered in the Wetlands, lurking around sites like Angerfang Encampment and the grim approaches to Grim Batol. They skirmish constantly with the dwarves of Menethil Harbor and the Wildhammer clan.",al={"dragonmaw locations","where to find dragonmaw","dragonmaw wetlands"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Dragonmaw clan?",a="The Dragonmaw clan is an orcish clan from Draenor. They gained infamy during the Second War after using the Demon Soul to enslave the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza and force the red dragonflight to fight for the Old Horde. Broken after losing control of their dragons at Grim Batol, they remain a dangerous, renegade remnant in the eastern lands.",al={"who are dragonmaw","dragonmaw clan info","what are dragonmaw"},it="identity",add="27e0e992"},{q="Why are the Dragonmaw hostile to travelers?",a="The Dragonmaw are unrepentant remnants of the Old Horde who reject Thrall's peaceful new path. Embittered by the loss of their enslaved dragons and driven from Grim Batol, they survive through violent raiding, clashing fiercely with local Wildhammer dwarves, Alliance travelers, and anyone else who ventures near their fortified encampments in the marshlands.",al={"why dragonmaw attack","are dragonmaw evil","why are dragonmaw hostile"},it="hostile",add="ef3e041c"},{q="Who are the Dragonmaw clan allied with?",a="The Dragonmaw remnants in the Wetlands operate outside Thrall's New Horde. Under Nek'rosh Skullcrusher, they defend their camps against nearby enemies and seek revenge against the dragons that destroyed their power at Grim Batol.",al={"dragonmaw allies","who do dragonmaw work for","dragonmaw faction"},it="allies",add="2417d206"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance remembers the terrifying rain of fire the Dragonmaw brought upon their fleets and armies during the Second War; any sighting of them in Khaz Modan is met with immediate military response.",["race:Dwarf"]="For dwarves, the Dragonmaw are a desecration: they fouled the ancient fortress of Grim Batol and continue to infest the mountain passes of Khaz Modan.",["race:Orc"]="To the orcs of Thrall's New Horde, the Dragonmaw embody the cruelty and fel corruption of the Old Horde that brought ruin upon their people.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks study the history of Nekros Skullcrusher, whose mastery over the Demon Soul proved how fel energies could subjugate even the grandest creatures of Azeroth."},kw={"alexstrasza","deathwing","demon soul","dragon soul","dragonmaw","grim batol","nekros skullcrusher","red dragonflight","wetlands","zuluhed the whacked"},rel={"subzone:grim-batol","zone:wetlands","topic:second-war","topic:old-horde","topic:new-horde","npc:deathwing","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Dragonmaw clan"}}
E["topic:druid"] = {t="topic",n="Druid",z="general",s="Druids are guardians of the natural world who draw power directly from the wild spirits, the celestial bodies, and the Emerald Dream. Master shapeshifters, they adopt the forms of primal beasts to protect the land or mend grievous wounds. Practiced primarily by the night elves, newly revived among the tauren and kept alive by the Skyborne, druids place the preservation of nature above all mortal allegiance.",h="Druids are guardians of the natural world who draw power directly from the wild spirits, the celestial...",sec={{t="Wardens of the Wilds",b="Druidism is far more than a martial art; it is an ancient way of life rooted in deep communion with nature and the demigod Cenarius. Druids wield raw natural energies, commanding roots to entangle their foes, directing the celestial brilliance of the moon and stars, and invoking restorative flora to knit bone and flesh. Their duty is to preserve the cosmic balance and protect the unfettered wild from exploitation and corruption.",sp=0},{t="The Gift of Shapeshifting",b="The defining hallmark of the druid is the supernatural art of shapeshifting. Through spiritual devotion, practitioners channel the primal aspects of Azeroth's beasts. They take on the thick hide of the bear to withstand crushing assaults, slip into the lithe form of the feline predator to strike from the shadows, or adopt aquatic and avian shapes to traverse sea and sky. While transformed, their animal mouths can rarely form mortal words, yet their physical prowess rivals the apex predators of the wild.",sp=0},{t="The Cenarion Circle",b="First taught to Malfurion Stormrage by Cenarius ten thousand years ago, mortal druidism was later formalized under the Cenarion Circle. Headquartered in sacred Moonglade, the Circle watches over druidic practices across Kalimdor, standing independent of mortal factions and political squabbles. While long dominated solely by night elf men who spent centuries slumbering in the Emerald Dream, the aftermath of the Third War saw both night elf women and the tauren welcomed into its fold.",sp=0},{t="Druids of the Skyborne",b="Druidism reached the Skyborne long ago. When the Shal'nan's rebellion rose against Prince Tortheldrin in Eldre'Thalas, the common folk who joined it took up nature magic, influenced by the Cenarion Circle to the north, while Tortheldrin scorned it as beneath the Highborne. Their descendants still practice it: the Nightclaw Druids of Shadowgale Forest on Zephras Isle keep largely to themselves, apart from the other Skyborne factions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was the first mortal druid?",a="Malfurion Stormrage was the first mortal to master the druidic arts under the direct tutelage of the demigod Cenarius, roughly ten thousand years ago prior to the War of the Ancients.",al={"first druid","who founded druidism","malfurion first druid"}},{q="How can tauren become druids?",a="Tauren druids learn the ways of the wild under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem of Thunder Bluff, and the tauren now stand within the Cenarion Circle alongside the night elves.",al={"tauren druid lore","why are tauren druids","hamuul runetotem druid"}},{q="What is the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle is the governing order of druids on Azeroth, founded to regulate wild practices and protect nature. Operating independently from both the Alliance and the Horde, its members answer to the Circle's archdruids and gather peacefully in sacred Moonglade.",al={"cenarion circle lore","what is moonglade order"}},{q="Can druids speak while in animal form?",a="Generally, no. When shapeshifted into a beast, a druid's vocal anatomy reflects the animal, making normal mortal speech impossible, although a handful of rare, exceptionally powerful individuals have found magical ways around this limitation.",al={"can druids talk in bear form","shapeshift talking lore"}},{q="What is the Emerald Dream?",a="The Emerald Dream is a verdant, pristine realm that mirrors Azeroth as if it had never been touched by civilization. Night elf druids long entered centuries of hibernation to walk its pathways and commune with the spirits of nature.",al={"emerald dream lore","where do druids sleep"}},{q="Why were druids originally sleeping for centuries?",a="Following the World Tree Nordrassil's blessing, night elf druids entered the Barrow Dens to spend centuries wandering the Emerald Dream, aiding Ysera's green dragonflight in nurturing Azeroth's spiritual ecosystem until the Third War forced them awake.",al={"druid hibernation","barrow dens sleeping","long slumber"}},{q="Which races can be druids?",a="Night elves and tauren, both bound to the Cenarion Circle, and the Skyborne, whose Nightclaw Druids keep their own traditions on Zephras Isle.",al={"who can be a druid","druid races","skyborne druid"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For ten millennia, kaldorei druids served as the quiet, sleeping guardians of Kalimdor, maintaining nature's balance under the guidance of Malfurion Stormrage and the demigod Cenarius.",["race:Tauren"]="Under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, the tauren have rekindled an ancient connection to the wilds, joining the Cenarion Circle in sacred Moonglade to walk the path of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="As a protector of balance, a druid walks the fine line between animal fury, restorative life magic, and celestial devastation, drawing wisdom directly from Cenarius and the Emerald Dream.",["race:Skyborne"]="Skyborne druids descend from the common folk of Eldre'Thalas who took up nature magic during the Shal'nan's rebellion; the Nightclaw Druids of Shadowgale Forest still keep that path."},kw={"bear form","cat form","cenarion circle","cenarius","druid","emerald dream","hamuul","malfurion","moonglade","shapeshifter"},rel={"topic:cenarion-circle","topic:cenarius","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:emerald-dream","quest:5921","quest:1489","npc:remulos","npc:fandral-staghelm","topic:skyborne"},src={"Druid"}}
E["topic:druids-of-the-fang"] = {t="topic",n="Druids of the Fang",z="general",s="The Druids of the Fang are a sinister sect entrenched deep within the labyrinthine Wailing Caverns. Severing all ties to the Cenarion Circle, these rogue night elves have adopted serpent-like forms under the command of the self-proclaimed Lord Serpentis. While the true origin of their dark communion remains a mystery to outside observers, they relentlessly pursue their grand design: to seize the subterranean waters and remake the Barrens into their own twisted paradise.",h="The Druids of the Fang are a sinister sect entrenched deep within the labyrinthine Wailing Caverns.",sec={{t="A Noble Vision Turned Poisonous",b="The Barrens was not always an arid wasteland, and Naralex sought to return it to the lush, verdant sanctuary it once was. Naralex led a dedicated order of young druids into the subterranean depths of the Wailing Caverns, tapping into the natural underground fissures and subterranean springs. By entering an induced slumber, Naralex hoped to channel the living energies of the Emerald Dream into the waterways, nursing the scorched savannah above back to vibrant life.",sp=0},{t="Descent into Nightmare",b="Something went horribly awry inside Naralex's trance. Instead of channeling the pristine serenity of the Emerald Dream, Naralex’s slumber was infiltrated by a malevolent, twisting nightmare. This darkness seeped outwards through the caverns, rapidly warping the resident wildlife and poisoning the underground springs. Rather than fleeing to seek aid from the Cenarion Circle, Naralex's acolytes were overwhelmed by the nightmare's influence, their minds and spirits mutating alongside the subterranean ecosystem.",sp=1,bt={"q886.5"}},{t="The Fang Ascendant",b="Naralex's prize pupil, once a dedicated student, cast aside his master's original teachings and embraced the corruption under the title of Lord Serpentis. Rallying the fallen acolytes to form the Druids of the Fang, Serpentis adopted the primal, venomous aspects of vipers and serpents rather than the traditional bear and cat forms sanctioned by Cenarius. The cult effectively imprisoned their slumbering master, intent on feeding on his nightmare to violently remold the Barrens into their own serpent-ruled paradise.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who are the Druids of the Fang?",a="They are a corrupted order of night elf druids who entered the Wailing Caverns under Naralex. Instead of healing the Barrens, they were driven mad when Naralex's trance tapped into a dark nightmare, transforming them into fanatical snake-worshiping renegades.",al={"druids of the fang lore","who are the fang druids","wailing caverns druids"},sp=1,it="identity",bt={"q886.5"}},{q="Why did they enter the Wailing Caverns originally?",a="Naralex and his acolytes sought to tap into the cavern's rich subterranean springs. By channeling the lush vitality of the Emerald Dream into the water system, they hoped to turn the arid Barrens back into a thriving, green forest.",al={"why are they in wailing caverns","naralex goal","why heal the barrens"},it="why_here"},{q="Who leads the Druids of the Fang?",a="They are led by Lord Serpentis. Once the foremost and brightest student of Naralex, Serpentis fell wholly into madness when the nightmare struck, declaring himself the leader of the newly formed Druids of the Fang.",al={"leader of the fang","lord serpentis lore","who leads them"},sp=1,it="leader"},{q="What animal forms do they take?",a="Unlike traditional druids of the Cenarion Circle who adopt the forms of bears, cats, or stormcrows, the Druids of the Fang draw on venomous serpents and vipers, embracing serpentine magic and vicious snake transformations.",al={"snake form druids","fang druid shapeshift","serpent form"}},{q="How does the Cenarion Circle view them?",a="The Cenarion Circle views the Druids of the Fang as a dangerous aberration and a cult. Elders like Nara Wildmane of Thunder Bluff and Naralex's remaining loyalists work urgently to eliminate the leaders of the Fang and cleanse the corruption before it destroys the land.",al={"cenarion circle opinion","are they part of the cenarion circle","hamuul on the fang"}},{q="What happened to Naralex?",a="Naralex remains trapped in a deep, agonizing slumber within the heart of the Wailing Caverns. His disciples keep him confined, using his ongoing nightmare as the power source to warp the caverns and their own magic.",al={"is naralex dead","naralex status","where is naralex"},sp=1},{q="Why are the Druids of the Fang hostile?",a="When Naralex tapped into the Emerald Dream to heal the Barrens, his vision decayed into an encroaching nightmare. That nightmare warped the minds and spirits of his disciples, driving them mad. Now twisted into the Druids of the Fang, they violently attack any intruders to protect their corrupted domain and force the surrounding world to reflect their nightmarish visions.",al={"why do druids of the fang attack","why are druids of the fang aggressive","druids of the fang enemies"},it="hostile",add="f17ecb7b"},{q="How did the Druids of the Fang come to exist?",a="They were originally a noble order of night elf druids who followed Naralex into the subterranean springs of the Wailing Caverns. Their mission was benevolent: channel the Emerald Dream to restore lush life to the arid Barrens. However, the Dream twisted into madness around Naralex, corrupting his followers and turning his finest student, Lord Serpentis, toward dark fanatical devotion.",al={"druids of the fang origin","how did druids of the fang start","history of druids of the fang"},it="history",add="1c4e8a0d"},{q="Why are they called the Druids of the Fang?",a="Rather than embracing traditional druidic forms like bears or cats, these fallen druids embraced the aspect of the serpent. Under the guidance of Lord Serpentis and their serpentine leaders, they shapeshift into vipers, employ deadly venom, and keep lethal reptiles as companions, abandoning traditional Cenarion paths to embody the venomous fang.",al={"why druids of the fang","druids of the fang name meaning","why the fang name"},it="name",add="d5ccf0b1"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Druids of the Fang represent the grave peril of the Emerald Dream being corrupted, demonstrating what happens when a druid abandons Cenarion harmony for twisted, unorthodox animal forms.",["race:Tauren"]="Nara Wildmane and the tauren of Thunder Bluff are deeply invested in ending the Fang's threat, viewing their defilement of the Barrens' water as an affront to the Earth Mother.",["race:NightElf"]="Seeing kaldorei brethren degenerate into a feral, nightmare-ridden snake cult in the heart of Kalimdor is a bitter sorrow and embarrassment for night elf traditions."},kw={"barrens","druids of the fang","emerald dream","nara wildmane","naralex","nightmare","serpentis","snake form","wailing caverns"},rel={"npc:lord-serpentis","npc:naralex","zone:wailing","quest:914","topic:emerald-dream","topic:druid","quest:1489","zone:barrens"},src={"Druids of the Fang"}}
E["topic:dwarf"] = {t="topic",n="Dwarf",z="general",s="Dwarves are a stout, resilient race carved from the living stone of ancient titan-forged earthen, now inhabiting the snow-capped mountain kingdoms of Khaz Modan and beyond. Known for their mastery of stonework, metallurgy, and devastating frontline combat, they stand as one of the Alliance's fiercest and most dependable pillars. Under the reign of King Magni Bronzebeard, their sprawling subterranean capital of Ironforge serves as the beating heart of dwarven society.",h="Dwarves are a stout, resilient race carved from the living stone of ancient titan-forged earthen, now...",sec={{t="Sons of the Mountain",b="Long ago, the subterranean titan-forged known as earthen awoke from an eons-long slumber within the vaults of Uldaman, transformed by the Curse of Flesh into beings of blood and bone. Taking the name dwarves, they reclaimed the high crags of Khaz Modan—named in honor of the titan Khaz'goroth—and carved the majestic subterranean stronghold of Ironforge beneath the peaks of Dun Morogh. Endowed with incredible natural fortitude, a sharp eye for stone and ore, and a penchant for roaring hearths and heady ale, dwarves approach craftsmanship and warfare with identical tenacity.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Dwarven unity shattered centuries ago following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar. Civil war erupted across Ironforge among three rival factions: the Bronzebeard clan, the Wildhammer clan, and the Dark Iron clan. After the Bronzebeards secured the mountain and drove out their kin, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan of the Dark Irons launched a disastrous war of vengeance against both rivals. Driven to desperation beneath the Redridge Mountains, Thaurissan accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, immolating his own realm into the volcanic wastes of Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes. The surviving Dark Irons were enslaved beneath Blackrock Mountain, while the devastated Wildhammers migrated north to Aerie Peak in the Hinterlands.",sp=0},{t="Steadfast Shield of the Alliance",b="When the orcish Horde surged across the continent during the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard sealed Ironforge's gates against brutal sieges while dwarven forces took up arms alongside the Alliance of Lordaeron. Renowned for heavy plate armor, deadly muskets, and fearless gryphon riders, dwarven warriors played a vital role in shattering the Horde. Today, the Kingdom of Ironforge remains one of the Alliance's most stalwart defenders, welcoming their displaced gnome cousins into their halls while actively unearthing the titan ruins of Khaz Modan to unravel the mysteries of their ancient origins.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where did the dwarves come from originally?",a="Dwarves are descended from the earthen, an ancient race of living stone beings created by the titan keepers. Sealed away inside the subterranean vaults of Uldaman, the earthen succumbed to the Curse of Flesh over millennia, awakening as mortal dwarves who then ventured out to settle the mountains of Khaz Modan.",al={"dwarf origin","dwarf lore","how were dwarves made","earthen"}},{q="Who rules the dwarves right now?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard rules the Kingdom of Ironforge and the Bronzebeard clan from the Great Forge. He has led his people through the Second and Third Wars and remains one of the core founding pillars of the Grand Alliance.",al={"who is dwarf king","dwarf leader","king of ironforge","magni bronzebeard"}},{q="What are the three main dwarven clans?",a="The three major clans are the Bronzebeards, who rule Ironforge and Dun Morogh; the Wildhammers, who dwell in the open air of the Hinterlands and ride gryphons; and the Dark Irons, who dwell deep beneath Blackrock Mountain under the fiery dominion of Ragnaros.",al={"dwarf clans","three hammers","bronzebeard wildhammer dark iron","clans of ironforge"}},{q="Why are the Dark Iron dwarves enemies?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Irons attempted to annihilate both the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans. Facing defeat, their sorcerer-thane summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, which shattered their homeland and bound the clan in servitude to elemental fire and shadow beneath Blackrock Mountain.",al={"why are dark irons evil","dark iron clan lore","shadowforge city dwarves"}},{q="Why are gnomes living inside Ironforge?",a="The dwarves and gnomes have shared a close bond since their first meeting centuries ago, exchanging stoneworking secrets for mechanical engineering. When Gnomeregan fell to a trogg invasion and toxic radiation, King Magni gave High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and his refugees asylum in Tinker Town.",al={"why are gnomes in ironforge","ironforge gnomes","what happened to gnomeregan"}},{q="Do dwarves worship the Holy Light?",a="Yes. Many Ironforge dwarves follow the Holy Light, which the humans introduced to them. Dwarven priests keep their own teachings of the Light, and a number of stout-hearted dwarves have recently been welcomed into the Order of the Silver Hand as paladins.",al={"dwarf religion","dwarves holy light","do dwarves believe in the light"}},{q="Which classes can dwarves be?",a="Dwarves train as warriors, paladins, hunters, rogues, priests and shaman.",al={"dwarf classes","what classes can dwarves be","can dwarves be shaman"}}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every dwarf carries the weight of a stoneborn legacy, navigating ancient blood feuds between clans while excavating titan secrets that define your very existence.",["race:Gnome"]="The dwarves are your oldest and truest allies, having helped build Gnomeregan's initial stonework and now offering your people shelter in Tinker Town.",["faction:Alliance"]="Ironforge's vast mineral wealth, unmatched siege engineering, and unyielding infantry form the northern bulwark of the Alliance.",["class:Paladin"]="Dwarven paladins carry the Holy Light with mountain-hewn resolve, blending human theology with the unbreakable martial discipline of Khaz Modan.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dwarves are renowned as the finest smiths in Azeroth, utilizing the volcanic heat of the Great Forge to temper legendary arms and armor.",["profession:Mining"]="Born from living stone, dwarves possess an innate eye for veins of rich ore and ancient gemstones buried beneath the subterranean deeps."},kw={"bronzebeard","dark iron","dun morogh","dwarf","dwarves","earthen","ironforge","khaz modan","magni","uldaman","wildhammer"},rel={"zone:ironforge","zone:dunmorogh","npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","topic:dark-iron-clan","subzone:uldaman","topic:alliance"},src={"Dwarf"}}
E["topic:earth-mother"] = {t="topic",n="Earth Mother",z="general",s="The Earth Mother is the supreme, benevolent creator deity worshipped by the tauren, revered as the living embodiment and architect of Azeroth's land and elements. In tauren myth, she shaped the mountains, plains, and seasons and set the sun and moon in the sky as her watchful eyes. Her presence permeates everyday tauren culture, guiding their reverence for nature, duty to the elements, and hopes for the afterlife.",h="The Earth Mother is the supreme, benevolent creator deity worshipped by the tauren, revered as the living...",sec={{t="The Dawn of Creation",b="Tauren creation myths tell that before day and night existed, the Earth Mother set about shaping Azeroth as a haven for her coming children. Scraping her nails across newly raised mountains sparked the world's first fires, while her hands carved rivers and ocean beds. Her laughter gave breath to the wind, and by lifting the primordial fogs into the sky, she brought the first rains, which coaxed grass, forests, and grain from the soil.",sp=0},{t="Sun, Moon, and the Shu'halo",b="Aided by the friendly elements, the Earth Mother brought forth twins: An'she, the sun, and Mu'sha, the moon. To rest while keeping them safe, she placed the radiant twins into her eyes, creating day, night, and the cycle of the seasons as she opened and closed each lid. Later, she pressed her heart against the fertile earth, breathing the song of life into the land to create the shu'halo, the first tauren. She charged the twins and the elements with teaching the young mortals how to hunt, build, dance, and live in harmony with the wilds.",sp=0},{t="Eyes in the Heavens",b="In time, the Earth Mother set An'she and Mu'sha in the sky, where the sun and moon keep watch over the land by day and by night. Tauren say that her spirit now dwells in the rivers, trees, plains, and mountains of the world, and that every lesser nature spirit springs from her and returns to her in the end.",sp=0},{t="Living Legacy",b="Tauren believe her spirit cradles Azeroth still. Her ear is attuned to the winds, hearing the prayers of her children, and her heart beats within the deep stone. Tauren hunters, shamans, and druids direct their thanks to her for every kill and harvest, believing that all life originates from her embrace and that every tauren's spirit returns to her soil upon death.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Earth Mother?",a="The Earth Mother is the benevolent creator deity at the heart of tauren religion. According to their myths, she crafted the physical landscape of Azeroth, birthed the sun and the moon, and created the tauren race from the soil.",al={"what is the earth mother","earthmother lore","tauren god"},it="identity"},{q="What are her eyes in the sky?",a="Her right eye is An'she, the sun, who brings light and warmth to the world. Her left eye is Mu'sha, the White Lady moon, who brings peace and slumber. Tauren myth states she placed them in the heavens to watch over the land.",al={"anshe and musha","tauren sun and moon","earth mother eyes"},it="power"},{q="Is the Earth Mother an actual living entity?",a="To the tauren, she is an active, loving spiritual reality whose essence permeates the natural world, stone, and winds. Shamans and druids channel nature and elemental spirits through her blessings, though whether she was a primordial giant, a world-soul, or an allegory remains a matter of faith.",al={"is earth mother real","does earth mother exist","who is the earthmother"}},{q="Do other races worship the Earth Mother?",a="While the tauren are her primary worshippers, other peoples who commune with the land acknowledge her. Some orc shamans and centaur invoke her name respectfully, and her blessings are frequently exchanged throughout the Horde.",al={"orcs earth mother","who worships earth mother","do trolls believe in earth mother"},it="ties"},{q="What do tauren believe happens after death?",a="Tauren believe that because all living things were born from the Earth Mother's loving touch, every soul returns to her upon death. Traditional rites commit the deceased back to the land, trusting their spirits to rest peacefully in her eternal embrace.",al={"tauren afterlife","tauren burial beliefs","returning to the earth mother"},it="history"},{q="How did the Earth Mother create the world and the tauren?",a="According to tauren creation myths, the Earth Mother shaped the mountains, seas, and skies before the dawn of memory. After birthing her twin children, An'she and Mu'sha, the sun and moon, she gave life to the plains and fashioned the first tauren, whom she loved dearly. Tauren believe her spirit still dwells in every river, tree, plain, and mountain of the world.",al={"earth mother creation story","how did earth mother make world","earth mother history"},it="history",add="dbad9a55"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every aspect of shu'halo society—from the nomad's respect for prey to the council of chieftains—is rooted in the law, sacrifice, and enduring love of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="Tauren druids view their sacred bond with nature as a direct communion with the Earth Mother's living body, answering her ancient call to preserve the land's balance.",["class:Shaman"]="Tauren shamans honor the Earth Mother alongside the elemental spirits, remembering that the wind, water, fire, and stone were her first companions.",["faction:Horde"]="Through their alliance with Mulgore, orcs and jungle trolls have grown familiar with tauren reverence for the Earth Mother, often invoking her name as a respectful blessing."},kw={"an'she","creation","earth mother","earthmother","mu'sha","mulgore","shu'halo","tauren"},rel={"topic:tauren","topic:horde","topic:centaur","zone:feralas","quest:860","quest:6381","topic:light"},src={"Earth Mother"}}
E["topic:elemental-plane"] = {t="topic",n="Elemental Plane",z="general",s="The Elemental Plane is an interconnected pocket dimension forged by the titans to imprison Azeroth's chaotic elementals. Divided into four distinct realms—Skywall, Deepholm, the Abyssal Maw, and the Firelands—it confines the primordial spirits away from the mortal world. Though sealed long ago, cracks in its barriers allow violent elemental incursions and catastrophic summonings into Azeroth.",h="The Elemental Plane is an interconnected pocket dimension forged by the titans to imprison Azeroth's chaotic...",sec={{t="A Prison for Primordial Chaos",b="In the ancient days, the elementals served as lieutenants of the Old Gods. When the titans triumphed, they banished the elemental lords and their myriad servants into an isolated realm known as the Elemental Plane, divided into four domains tailored to each primal force: Skywall for air, Deepholm for earth, the Abyssal Maw for water, and the Firelands for fire. With the elementals' departure, nature calmed, and Azeroth's surface finally knew peace.",sp=0},{t="Endless Strife",b="Banishment did not temper the fury of the elementals. Confined within their prison, the primordial powers wage endless wars against one another across the boundaries of their domains.",sp=0},{t="Breaching the Wards",b="The barriers separating Azeroth from the Elemental Plane are formidable, but not unbreakable. Two centuries ago, during the desperate climax of the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan pierced the veil to summon Ragnaros the Firelord from the Firelands, an apocalyptic event that ruptured the Redridge Mountains and forged Blackrock Mountain. In recent days, volatile cracks have opened across Kalimdor. Dimensional rifts repeatedly manifest in Silithus, Un'Goro Crater, Azshara, and Winterspring, disgorging waves of hostile elementals eager to test the defenses of the mortal world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Elemental Plane?",a="The Elemental Plane is an artificial pocket dimension created by the titans to contain the chaotic elementals of Azeroth. It is composed of four distinct domains corresponding to the four primary elements: Skywall for air, Deepholm for earth, the Abyssal Maw for water, and the Firelands for fire.",al={"what is elemental plane","elemental plane explained","elemental realms"},it="identity"},{q="Who created the Elemental Plane and why?",a="The titans banished the elementals into this realm after defeating the Old Gods, whom the elemental lords had served. Sealing them away allowed nature on the surface to calm and flourish in peace.",al={"who made elemental plane","why was elemental plane made","how was elemental plane made"},it="origin"},{q="What are the four realms within the plane?",a="The four interlinked domains are Skywall (the realm of air), Deepholm (the realm of earth), the Abyssal Maw (the realm of water), and the Firelands (the realm of fire). Each realm is shaped as an ideal home and prison for its respective elemental spirits.",al={"four elemental planes","elemental domains","names of elemental realms"},it="place"},{q="Can creatures travel between Azeroth and the Elemental Plane?",a="Travel between realms is strictly barred by ancient wards, but powerful sorcery and dimensional instability can create breaches. Mortal spellcasters have summoned entities across the veil, such as Thaurissan summoning Ragnaros, and volatile rifts periodically tear open in remote regions of Kalimdor.",al={"portal to elemental plane","visiting elemental plane","how to get to elemental plane"},it="power"},{q="Why are elementals invading Kalimdor right now?",a="Periodic incursions have begun erupting across Silithus, Un'Goro Crater, Azshara, and Winterspring. Portals tearing through the veil allow waves of invaders from the Elemental Plane to push into mortal territory until beaten back by the defenders of the Horde and Alliance.",al={"elemental invasions","why are elementals attacking","elemental rifts kalimdor"},it="conflict"},{q="How was Blackrock Mountain created by the plane?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan attempted to conjure a fiery power to save his clan. He inadvertently tore open a gateway into the Firelands, wrenching Ragnaros into Azeroth. The Firelord's explosive arrival shattered the surrounding mountains, carving out the Burning Steppes, Searing Gorge, and Blackrock Mountain.",al={"thaurissan ragnaros","creation of blackrock mountain","how did ragnaros get here"},it="history"},{q="Who rules the Elemental Plane?",a="The plane has no single ruler, as its four domains are governed independently by the four mighty Elemental Lords: Ragnaros the Firelord commands the Firelands, Therazane the Stonemother rules Deepholm, Al'Akir the Windlord holds Skywall, and Neptulon the Tidehunter oversees the Abyssal Maw. Rather than cooperating, these entities and their armies have spent millenia locked in endless war against one another across the boundaries of their respective realms.",al={"elemental plane leaders","who leads the elemental plane","elemental plane lords"},it="leader",add="4757a26f"},{q="Who are the elementals allied with?",a="In primordial times, the elementals were bound into service by the Old Gods, warring against the titans and their forged armies on their masters' behalf. In the current age, however, most elementals fight strictly for their respective Elemental Lords. They remain intensely hostile to nearly all mortals, though specific factions or summoners—such as the Dark Iron dwarves who accidentally bound themselves to Ragnaros—occasionally serve or bargain with them.",al={"elemental plane allies","who do elementals serve","elemental allegiances"},it="allies",add="a219565c"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Shaman seek harmony with spirits whose ancient rulers remain imprisoned across the veil. Understanding the division between Azeroth and the Elemental Plane is central to grasping why chaotic elements lash out when summoned.",["class:Mage"]="Conjuring elemental familiars requires tapping into the fringe energies of these distant realms, demanding strict control to avoid tearing wider breaches into the material world.",["race:Dwarf"]="The dark history of the Dark Iron clan serves as a grim warning of what happens when mortal ambition pierces the Elemental Plane to summon beings like Ragnaros.",["race:Skyborne"]="Zephras Isle floats within the towering expanse of Skywall, serving as a sanctuary within the windy domain of the Elemental Plane."},kw={"abyssal maw","deepholm","elemental plane","firelands","invasions","ragnaros","skywall"},rel={"topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:skywall","topic:skyborne","subzone:blackrock-mountain","zone:burningsteppes","zone:searinggorge","npc:highlord-demitrian"},src={"Elemental Plane"}}
E["topic:elune"] = {t="topic",n="Elune",z="general",s="Across Azeroth, Elune is chiefly worshipped by the night elves as their protector and mother of Cenarius. The larger moon, the White Lady, is hers, and her gifts reach from healing and peace to lunar fury. Tauren know her as Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother.",h="Across Azeroth, Elune is chiefly worshipped by the night elves as their protector and mother of Cenarius.",sec={{t="The White Lady",b="Elune is one of the few true deities known across Azeroth, bound to the night sky and the larger moon, the White Lady. Though she has never appeared in bodily form, night elven artists portray her as a radiant kaldorei with alabaster skin, silver hair, and eyes of pure moonlight. Ancient kaldorei legends say she rested in the depths of the Well of Eternity by day, whispering wisdom to the first night elves.",sp=0},{t="The Sisters and the Forest",b="Night elven life, both sacred and civic, centers on devotion to Elune through the Sisterhood of Elune. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind leads this ancient order. The Sisters call on Elune to mend even lethal wounds, and turn her radiant wrath upon their foes with searing starlight.\n\nElune and the Great White Stag, Malorne, brought forth Cenarius. Through him, Elune's heavenly mysteries are bound to the druidic traditions that tend Kalimdor's wildlands.",sp=0},{t="Aspects and Names",b="Elune is known for peace, harmony, and restoration, yet battle has its place in her worship. As the Night Warrior, she is a fierce patron of battle. She gathers the spirits of fallen heroes and sets them in the heavens, where they ride as stars.\n\nThe nomadic tauren call Elune Mu'sha, the radiant left eye of the Earth Mother. She lights the night and guides wandering hunters beside her fiery twin, An'she the sun.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elune?",a="Elune is the moon goddess chiefly worshipped by the night elves and one of Azeroth's few true deities. She is associated with healing, nature, peace, and the White Lady, the world's larger moon.",al={"what is elune","elune lore","who is the moon goddess"},it="identity"},{q="Has Elune ever appeared in person?",a="Elune has never appeared in physical form on Azeroth. Kaldorei artists depict her as a radiant night elf maiden crowned in platinum, with long silver hair and glowing moonlight eyes. Her true cosmic shape remains a mystery.",al={"has elune taken physical form","what does elune look like"}},{q="What is the Night Warrior?",a="The Night Warrior is Elune's fiercest aspect and her patronage of battle. In this form, her wrath rises in combat, and she lifts the spirits of courageous fallen warriors into the night sky to ride among the stars.",al={"elune night warrior","wrath of elune"},it="power"},{q="How is Elune related to Cenarius?",a="Elune and the wild stag demigod Malorne brought forth Cenarius. He inherited his mother's deep love for living creatures and his father's bond with the natural wild and the Emerald Dream.",al={"is elune cenarius mother","cenarius parents"},it="ties"},{q="Do the tauren worship Elune?",a="Yes. Tauren call Elune Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother. In their myths, she watches over the world by night alongside her solar brother, An'she.",al={"musha","tauren moon goddess","who is musha"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the worship of Elune in Kalimdor?",a="High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind leads the Sisterhood of Elune in Kalimdor. The Sisterhood is the chief spiritual and civic authority among the night elves.",al={"priestesses of elune leader","sisters of elune"},it="leader"},{q="Where did Elune come from?",a="Elune's true origins remain shrouded in myth, as she is one of the few true deities worshipped across Azeroth. According to night elven belief, the early kaldorei first sensed her presence after being transformed by the Well of Eternity, where they believed she rested during the day. Ancient legends tell of her taking the Great White Stag, Malorne, as her consort to bring forth Cenarius.",al={"elune origins","where did elune come from","history of elune"},it="history",add="33d35cb4"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf, your civilization looks to the Mother Moon, from the ruling Sisterhood to the moonlight bathing your woodland settlements.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, your ancestral myths praise Mu'sha, the left eye of the Earth Mother, for watching over the tribes and creatures of the dark.",["class:Priest"]="As a night elf priest, you draw divine spells and celestial fury from Elune's guidance. You wield radiant moonlight rather than the Holy Light worshipped in the eastern kingdoms.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you are kin to the living wild, and Elune's celestial influence is woven into your astral powers as the divine mother of Lord Cenarius."},kw={"cenarius","elune","mother moon","mu'sha","night warrior","sisterhood of elune","tyrande","white lady"},rel={"npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius","topic:earth-mother","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:druid","topic:tauren","topic:emerald-dream"},src={"Elune"}}
E["topic:emerald-dream"] = {t="topic",n="Emerald Dream",z="general",s="The Emerald Dream is a vast, ever-shifting spirit realm representing Azeroth in its primordial state, completely untouched by civilization. Watched over by the Dragon Aspect Ysera and her green flight, it serves as the spiritual blueprint of nature and the sacred domain where druids commune with the cycle of life.",h="The Emerald Dream is a vast, ever-shifting spirit realm representing Azeroth in its primordial state...",sec={{t="A Mirror of Primordial Azeroth",b="Existing outside the physical plane yet tethered to its heartbeat, the Emerald Dream reflects what Azeroth would look like had intelligent beings never cleared its forests or built cities upon its stone. Sculpted during the ancient ordering of the world, it is an ever-shifting wilderness of pristine forests, boundless plains, and primordial rivers. Time and distance hold little meaning within the Dream; days in the physical world can feel like decades in the spirit, and landmarks change shape like mist before mortal eyes.",sp=0},{t="The Realm of the Dreamer",b="Following the ordering of the world, the titans entrusted this verdant plane to Ysera the Dreamer, the great Aspect of the green dragonflight. Charged with guiding the evolution and natural rhythm of Azeroth, Ysera dwells in an unending trance, gazing through enchanted dreamsight that perceives both the waking world and its mirrored spirit plane. The green dragons guard the rare, natural portals linking the physical plane to the Dream, maintaining eternal vigilance over the boundary between sleep and waking.",sp=0},{t="Druids and the Dreaming",b="To mortal druids, the Emerald Dream is the wellspring of their craft. By channeling nature magic, druids can project their spirits into the Dream, leaving their corporeal bodies resting safely in barrow dens for years or even centuries. In this untamed realm, they commune with ancient nature spirits and the Wild Gods, gaining profound insight into the Great Cycle of life and death. Skilled walkers of the Dream learn to transcend physical laws while in their dreamforms, traversing vast distances or slipping through solid barriers at will.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Emerald Dream?",a="The Emerald Dream is an ethereal, spiritual reflection of Azeroth. It preserves a pristine, untamed blueprint of the world as it existed before civilized races altered the landscape, serving as a sanctuary for nature spirits and the green dragonflight.",al={"what is emerald dream","emerald dream lore","the dream explained"},it="identity"},{q="Who rules the Emerald Dream?",a="The Dragon Aspect Ysera watches over the Dream alongside the green dragonflight. However, she does not absolute control it. The Dream's wild, underlying structure operates on its own ancient rhythm, guiding Ysera's stewardship rather than submitting to her will.",al={"who controls emerald dream","emerald dream ruler","ysera dream"},it="leader"},{q="How do mortals enter the Emerald Dream?",a="Most mortals enter through deep, druidic slumber, projecting their spirit into a dreamform while leaving their physical bodies protected in barrows. Rare, physical portals guarded by the green dragonflight also exist across Azeroth, though stepping through them is perilous.",al={"visiting emerald dream","how to enter the dream","dreamform"}},{q="Can you change things inside the Dream?",a="Dreamers can shape and alter the local environment temporarily with their thoughts and magic, but these changes never last. The Dream's primordial blueprint inevitably reasserts itself, erasing any permanent mortal imprint.",al={"changing emerald dream","can you alter the dream"}},{q="Why do druids sleep for centuries?",a="Druids enter prolonged hibernation to wander the Emerald Dream, communing with nature spirits, learning from the Wild Gods, and helping the green dragonflight maintain the balance of Azeroth's natural cycles across generations.",al={"why druids hibernate","druid slumber","barrow dens sleeping"}},{q="Where do animal spirits go when they die?",a="No one can say for certain. Druids honor the spirits of beasts as part of the natural cycle of life and death, but what becomes of those spirits after death remains a mystery.",al={"animal afterlife","wild god spirits","beast spirits"}},{q="Where did the Emerald Dream come from?",a="Its origins are shrouded in mystery. The Dream is said to have been shaped during the titans' ordering of Azeroth as a living blueprint of the natural world, and it was entrusted to Ysera and her green dragonflight to watch over.",al={"emerald dream origin","who made emerald dream","how was emerald dream created"},it="history",add="8b438d74"},{q="Why is it called the Emerald Dream?",a="It is named for its lush, pristine verdure and its spiritual nature. Charged to Ysera and the green dragonflight, this verdant realm mirrors an untouched, flourishing Azeroth that mortals often enter through dreams and spirit forms, though physical portals also exist.",al={"why emerald dream","emerald dream name meaning"},it="name",add="68609b47"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Emerald Dream is the sacred source of your power, where your order communes with the Wild Gods and studies the delicate balance of the Great Cycle.",["race:NightElf"]="For ten thousand years, your male druids slept in sacred barrows to walk the Dream alongside Ysera, leaving the Sentinels and Priestesses to guard the waking world.",["race:Tauren"]="Your druids walk the land of dreams to commune with the spirits of the earth, seeing it as the untamed cradle of the Earth Mother's creation."},kw={"cenarius","dreaming","druid slumber","emerald dream","green dragonflight","wild gods","ysera"},rel={"topic:druid","topic:titan","topic:night-elf","quest:1490","quest:480","quest:1599","quest:6381"},src={"Emerald Dream"}}
E["topic:explorers-league"] = {t="topic",n="Explorers' League",z="general",s="The Explorers' League is a renowned dwarven archaeological guild headquartered in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. Founded under King Magni Bronzebeard and led by his wandering brother Brann, the League seeks to uncover the forgotten origins of the dwarven race. Their expansive digs span across Azeroth, uncovering ancient titan ruins while frequently igniting regional conflicts.",h="The Explorers' League is a renowned dwarven archaeological guild headquartered in Ironforge's Hall of...",sec={{t="A Shift in Dwarven Purpose",b="For centuries, the dwarves of Khaz Modan focused their formidable energies on mining, stonework, and engineering. That focus changed drastically when ancient ruins were unearthed, hinting at a forgotten legacy connecting dwarves to mythical titan creators. Eager to solve the mystery of his people's beginnings, King Magni Bronzebeard decreed that dwarven industry pivot heavily toward archaeology. Magni, alongside his brother Brann, established the Explorers' Guild—now widely known as the Explorers' League—to scour the world for relics, forgotten cities, and traces of their ancient ancestry.",sp=0},{t="The Hall and the Digs",b="The League's operations are directed from the cavernous Hall of Explorers in Ironforge, oversaw on a day-to-day administrative level by High Explorer Muninn Magellas while Brann Bronzebeard explores distant reaches. Across Khaz Modan and beyond, prospectors manage extensive digsites. Sites like Ironband's Excavation in Loch Modan and Whelgar's Excavation in the Wetlands uncover immense fossil records and ancient architecture. However, prospectors often find themselves under siege by displaced wildlife, troggs, and rival factions interested in subterranean relics.",sp=0},{t="Frontier Clashes",b="The League's aggressive expeditions have sparked fierce resistance outside dwarven homelands. In Kalimdor, League operations such as the Bael Modan dig in the southern Barrens drove out the native Stonespire tauren through heavy industrial blasting, stirring vengeance and angering local earth spirits. In the arid Badlands, League prospectors clash openly with the Dark Iron clan over the vast titan vaults of Uldaman.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Explorers' League?",a="The Explorers' League is an Ironforge-based organization dedicated to surveying, archaeology, and researching the origins of the dwarven race. They maintain excavation sites across the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor to unearth relics of the ancient world.",al={"explorers guild","dwarf explorers","who are the explorers league","league of explorers"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Explorers' League?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard helped found the guild, but his youngest brother, the renowned wanderer Brann Bronzebeard, serves as its official leader. In Brann's frequent absences across the globe, High Explorer Muninn Magellas oversees business from the Hall of Explorers in Ironforge.",al={"leader of explorers league","brann bronzebeard role","muninn magellas"},it="leader"},{q="Why did King Magni start the League?",a="After strange subterranean ruins revealed evidence that the dwarves were not merely born of ordinary mountain rock, King Magni shifted a massive portion of dwarven industry toward archaeology to discover the true, mythical heritage of his people.",al={"why do dwarves dig","dwarven origins","why archaeology"},it="history"},{q="Can non-dwarves join the League?",a="Yes. While the League remains predominantly dwarven in membership, leadership, and aesthetic, individuals of other Alliance races do join, including adventurous gnomes like Clopper Wizbang, along with trusted field assistants across the continents.",al={"gnomes in explorers league","other races in league","who can join"},it="people"},{q="Why are the tauren fighting the Explorers' League?",a="In their race to excavate Kalimdor, dwarven prospectors at Bael Modan used heavy explosives and displaced the native Stonespire tauren tribe, decimating their settlement and outraging the local earth spirits through wanton destruction of sacred ground.",al={"bael modan tauren","dwarves vs tauren","stonespire feud"},it="hostile"},{q="What is Uldaman and why does the League care about it?",a="Uldaman is a massive underground titan facility buried beneath the Badlands. The League views it as the crown jewel of their research, believing its sealed chambers hold definitive proof regarding the transformation and ancient creation of the dwarven race.",al={"uldaman excavation","league badlands","titan ruins uldaman"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are Explorers' League camps found across the world?",a="King Magni turned dwarven industry toward archaeology after discovering ancient ruins tied to their heritage. Since then, the League has dispatched prospectors and excavators to anywhere ancient titan relics, mysterious ruins, or historical secrets might lie buried. You will find their dig sites scattered across Khaz Modan, deep in Kalimdor's wilderness like Darkshore and the Barrens, and throughout ancient subterranean complexes like Uldaman.",al={"why is explorers league everywhere","explorers league dig sites locations","why is the league in kalimdor"},it="why_here",add="f9af476b"},{q="Who is the Explorers' League allied with?",a="The League is an official arm of the dwarven kingdom of Ironforge, placing them firmly within the Grand Alliance. Dwarven and Alliance troops guard their digs, and prospectors across Azeroth's remote frontiers rely on that protection against troggs, rival factions, and hostile locals.",al={"explorers league alliance ties","who does the explorers league work for","explorers league si7"},it="allies",add="5a1072b2"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every digsite and titan artifact brings the dwarven race closer to understanding their ancient creator roots and their departure from stone.",["race:Tauren"]="The League's careless use of dynamite in Kalimdor desecrated Stonespire ancestral lands, proving their relentless digging often comes at the expense of the earth.",["faction:Alliance"]="The League's digs carry the Alliance banner into remote territories, where prospectors often need protection from troggs, rival factions, and angry locals.",["profession:Mining"]="Dwarven prospectors rely heavily on mining traditions to shore up tunnels, survey strata, and extract delicate titan machinery from solid rock."},kw={"archaeology","brann bronzebeard","excavation","explorers guild","explorers' league","hall of explorers","magni bronzebeard","muninn magellas","prospector","uldaman"},rel={"subzone:hall-of-explorers","npc:brann-bronzebeard","npc:magni-bronzebeard","subzone:uldaman","subzone:ironband-s-excavation-site","subzone:whelgar-s-excavation-site","subzone:bael-modan","quest:2300"},src={"Explorers' League"}}
E["topic:first-war"] = {t="topic",n="First War",z="general",s="The First War was the devastating five-year conflict in which the Orcish Horde emerged through the Dark Portal and completely destroyed the human Kingdom of Stormwind. Orchestrated behind the scenes by the demon-possessed Guardian Medivh and the warlock Gul'dan, it concluded with the assassination of King Llane Wrynn I and the flight of human survivors north to Lordaeron.",h="The First War was the devastating five-year conflict in which the Orcish Horde emerged through the Dark...",sec={{t="Opening of the Portal",b="Before the war, the Kingdom of Stormwind enjoyed long years of peace following the Gnoll War, guarded by its Knights and the Clerics of Northshire Abbey. That stability shattered when the Guardian Medivh, secretly possessed by the demon lord Sargeras, contacted the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Together, they forged the Dark Portal in the swampy Black Morass. Driven by demon blood and the slow death of their homeworld, the Orcish Horde crossed into Azeroth in force. Early rumors dismissed the invaders as vengeful spirits or strange offshoots of trolls, but Sir Anduin Lothar's scouting patrols quickly confirmed the arrival of a disciplined, bloodthirsty army.",sp=0},{t="The War for Stormwind",b="Led openly by Warchief Blackhand and manipulated from the shadows by Gul'dan's Shadow Council, the Horde overran the southern reaches of the kingdom. While an attempt by the orcs to push into Stranglethorn Vale against the Gurubashi trolls failed, their campaign across human territory proved catastrophic. The Horde conquered outward until only Elwynn Forest remained under human control. Blackhand launched a first direct assault on Stormwind City, but Sir Anduin Lothar repelled the invasion with a brilliant, surprise flanking counterattack by sea, temporarily saving the capital from annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Treachery and the Fall",b="The war turned when Lothar and the young mage Khadgar uncovered Medivh's betrayal, invading Karazhan and slaying the Guardian. Medivh's demise cast Gul'dan into a coma, creating an opportunity for Orgrim Doomhammer to challenge and kill Blackhand in a mak'gora duel. Taking absolute command, Doomhammer crushed the Shadow Council and marched on Stormwind for a second siege. During the onslaught, the half-orc assassin Garona murdered King Llane Wrynn I within his keep. Stormwind was sacked and burned to ash, forcing Lothar to gather the surviving populace and flee north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",sp=1}},faq={{q="How did the orcs get to Azeroth in the first place?",a="The warlock Gul'dan on Draenor and the Guardian Medivh on Azeroth collaborated using dark fel magic to breach the dimensional fabric between worlds, erecting the Dark Portal in the Black Morass.",al={"how did orcs get here","dark portal origin","first war start"},it="origin"},{q="Why didn't the other human kingdoms help Stormwind?",a="Stormwind stood alone because the distant northern kingdoms viewed rumors of green-skinned invaders as far-fetched exaggerations or local squabbles. It was only after Stormwind fell and Lothar arrived in Lordaeron with boats full of refugees that the northern kings united.",al={"why no alliance","did lordaeron help","reinforcements for stormwind"},it="conflict"},{q="Who killed King Llane?",a="King Llane Wrynn I was assassinated inside his own keep by Garona Halforcen, an emissary whom the king had trusted, striking a fatal blow to human morale during the final siege.",al={"who murdered llane","king llane death","garona killed king"},it="history"},{q="Did the orcs fight anyone besides humans in the First War?",a="Yes. Early in the conflict, the Horde pushed southward into Stranglethorn Vale, but they were repelled in a fierce clash with the Gurubashi jungle trolls.",al={"did orcs fight trolls","gurubashi in first war","other races first war"},it="history"},{q="Who won the First War?",a="The Orcish Horde won a total victory. The Kingdom of Stormwind was razed, King Llane was slain, and the surviving human population was forced into a massive sea evacuation north to Lordaeron.",al={"first war victor","who won wc1","outcome of first war"},it="history"},{q="What happened to Warchief Blackhand?",a="When Gul'dan was incapacitated following Medivh's death, Orgrim Doomhammer challenged Blackhand to mak'gora—a duel of honor—slew him, and seized leadership of the Horde.",al={"how did blackhand die","orgrim doomhammer challenge","warchief killed"},it="leader"},{q="What was the First War?",a="The First War, also known as the first Great War, was the devastating five-year conflict fought between the Orcish Horde and the human Kingdom of Stormwind. Orchestrated in secret by the warlock Gul'dan and the corrupted Guardian Medivh, the orcs crossed from their dying homeworld of Draenor through the Dark Portal into the Black Morass. What began as scattered frontier clashes escalated into an all-out invasion that destroyed the kingdom's towns, culminated in the assassination of King Llane Wrynn, and ended with the total sack of Stormwind City.",al={"what is first war","first war summary","the first great war"},it="identity",add="a8364e3f"},{q="Why is it called the First War?",a="It is named the First War because it marked the very first clash between humanity and the Orcish Horde on Azeroth. Before the Dark Portal opened, humans had never seen orcs and had enjoyed generations of relative peace following the Gnoll War. The conflict shattered that isolation, and because a second grand confrontation soon followed when the Horde invaded the northern human nations, historians and survivors refer to this initial struggle for Stormwind as the First War.",al={"why first war","first war name meaning","why is it called the first war"},it="name",add="bf3015cc"}},ang={["race:Human"]="The First War represents the darkest hour of Stormwind's history, when the kingdom fell, its king was murdered, and its survivors fled across the sea as penniless refugees.",["race:Orc"]="For the orcs, the First War marked their initial bloody conquest on Azeroth, tainted by the blood-curse of Mannoroth and the hidden machinations of Gul'dan's Shadow Council.",["class:Paladin"]="The slaughter of the Clerics of Northshire Abbey during this conflict demonstrated that holy faith alone was not enough to survive brutal melee combat, directly inspiring the founding of the Knights of the Silver Hand.",["faction:Alliance"]="The total annihilation of Stormwind served as the grim warning that convinced the northern nations to abandon their isolation and unite into the Alliance of Lordaeron.",["class:Warlock"]="Gul'dan and his Shadow Council directed the Horde's conquests through fel sorceries, demonstrating the immense, world-breaking devastation demonic pacts can unleash."},kw={"anduin lothar","black morass","blackhand","dark portal","first war","gul'dan","llane wrynn","medivh","northshire abbey","orgrim doomhammer"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:llane-wrynn-i","topic:medivh","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","subzone:dark-portal","topic:second-war","subzone:northshire-abbey"},src={"First War"}}
E["topic:forsaken"] = {t="topic",n="Forsaken",z="general",s="The Forsaken are a free-willed faction of intelligent undead who broke away from the Scourge following the Third War. Led by the Banshee Queen Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, they dwell within the subterranean Undercity carved beneath the ruins of Lordaeron's capital. Though deeply distrusted across Azeroth, they maintain a tenuous alliance of convenience with the Horde.",h="The Forsaken are a free-willed faction of intelligent undead who broke away from the Scourge following the...",sec={{t="Broken Shackles",b="When the Lich King's grip over his northern armies slipped after the Third War, thousands of undead in the blighted ruins of Lordaeron suddenly regained their sapience and memories. Horrified by the butchery they had been forced to carry out while enslaved to his will, these former citizens found themselves reviled and hunted by the living. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the fallen ranger-general of Quel'Thalas who had been transformed into an unholy banshee, rallied these adrift spirits, claimed the catacombs beneath Capital City, and dubbed her new nation the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="An Alliance of Convenience",b="Hemmed in by relentless enemies—chief among them the fanatic Scarlet Crusade and the Scourge—Sylvanas dispatched emissaries seeking allies. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, tauren elders counseled compassion, believing the cursed people might yet find redemption or a cure through the earth. Despite Warchief Thrall's misgivings, the Horde admitted the Forsaken, gaining a vital northern foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",sp=0},{t="Dark Laboratories",b="While presenting a cooperative front to their Kalimdor allies, the Forsaken pursue their own grim survival. Beneath the Undercity in the Apothecarium, the Royal Apothecary Society works incessantly to concoct a 'New Plague' potent enough to extinguish both the Scourge and living threats alike. Meanwhile, Forsaken champions like Nathanos Blightcaller wage bitter wars across the Plaguelands, Hillsbrad Foothills, and the Scarlet Monastery to secure their sovereign borders.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Are the Forsaken part of the Scourge?",a="No. The Forsaken broke free from the Lich King's mental domination after the Third War. They loathe the Scourge and consider the Lich King Arthas their absolute mortal enemy, fighting bitterly against his minions throughout the Plaguelands.",al={"scourge difference","are forsaken scourge","are undead evil"}},{q="Where is the Forsaken capital?",a="Their capital is the Undercity, a vast subterranean sprawl built beneath the ruins of Capital City in Tirisfal Glades. Sylvanas's forces expanded the royal crypts and sewers into a fortified sanctuary safe from the light and outdoor threats.",al={"undead capital","forsaken city","where do undead live"}},{q="Why did the Horde let the Forsaken join?",a="The tauren advocated strongly for them, seeing potential for redemption and hoping the earth's powers might cure their affliction. Magatha Grimtotem also supported the pact. Warchief Thrall accepted because the Horde urgently needed a strategic military foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why are undead horde","how did forsaken join horde","thrall forsaken alliance"}},{q="Who rules the Forsaken?",a="Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen. Formerly the high elven ranger-general who fell defending Silvermoon, she liberated free-willed undead and rules from the Undercity alongside her dreadlord advisor, Varimathras.",al={"undead leader","forsaken queen","who leads forsaken"}},{q="Are the Forsaken all former humans?",a="The vast majority are former humans from the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron who were slain and raised during the Scourge invasion. However, their leadership includes former elves like Sylvanas, and anyone who bends the knee to the Dark Lady is welcomed into their ranks.",al={"undead races","are forsaken only humans","what race are forsaken"}},{q="Why does the Scarlet Crusade hate the Forsaken so much?",a="The Scarlet Crusade makes zero distinction between mindful Forsaken and mindless Scourge. Their zealots view all undead as abominations that must be purified through fire and holy wrath, regardless of free will or allegiances.",al={"scarlet crusade undead","crusade vs forsaken","why scarlet crusade attacks"}},{q="Which classes can the Forsaken be?",a="The Forsaken train as warriors, paladins, rogues, priests, mages and warlocks. Their paladins still wield the Holy Light, though it burns their dead flesh.",al={"undead classes","forsaken classes","what classes can undead be","can undead be paladins","undead paladin"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This is your people: cursed to walk the earth in rotting flesh, spurned by former kin, yet bound together under the Dark Lady to carve out an empire of your own.",["race:Tauren"]="Your elders championed the Forsaken's entry into the Horde out of sympathy, hoping your shamanic and druidic rites might one day soothe or heal their tragic condition.",["faction:Alliance"]="To the Alliance, the Forsaken are nightmarish parodies of their fallen friends and family from Lordaeron, holding former imperial lands and answering overtures with poison and steel.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall forged an alliance of convenience with the Undercity for territory in the East, but many orcs and trolls view their grim apothecary experiments and necromantic culture with deep suspicion."},kw={"apothecary","forsaken","lordaeron","plaguelands","sylvanas","tirisfal","undead","undercity"},rel={"npc:sylvanas-windrunner","zone:undercity","topic:scourge","topic:horde","topic:scarlet-crusade","quest:1489","npc:varimathras","npc:nathanos-blightcaller"},src={"Forsaken"}}
E["topic:furbolg"] = {t="topic",n="Furbolg",z="general",s="Furbolgs are a hulking, reclusive race of bear-men native to northern Kalimdor who lived in harmony with nature and the night elves for millennia. In the wake of the Third War, demonic corruption and lingering fel magic have driven nearly every tribe into a frenzied madness. Today, former friends have become vicious territorial predators, with only a tiny handful of tribes clinging to their sanity.",h="Furbolgs are a hulking, reclusive race of bear-men native to northern Kalimdor who lived in harmony with...",sec={{t="Ancient Kinship",b="Furbolgs are an ancient people of Kalimdor who have long revered the wild god Ursoc. During the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago, tribal packs united under chieftain Unng Ak to fight alongside night elves and tauren against the first demonic invasion. Following the Great Sundering, they chose seclusion deep in the northern woodlands, maintaining peaceful ties with night elf druids and forest wardens while living in simple harmony with the wild.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge of Madness",b="The Third War shattered the furbolgs' tranquil existence. The passage of the Burning Legion through Kalimdor left lingering fel taint across the soil and canopy of Ashenvale and Felwood. Simple-minded and deeply attuned to the land, the bear-men absorbed this corruption directly. Their wizened shamans were overthrown or silenced by bloodthirsty firebrands who channeled fel moss, dark talismans, and corrupt alchemy, transforming peaceful gatherers into rabid raiders who now attack travelers and night elf settlements on sight.",sp=0},{t="The Unbroken Line",b="Across northern Kalimdor, nearly every major tribe has succumbed: the Gnarlpine across Teldrassil, the Blackwood of Darkshore, the Thistlefur and Foulweald in Ashenvale, and the Deadwood and Winterfall of the northern highlands. Yet a few hold the line. Deep within the fortified tunnels connecting Felwood, Winterspring, and Moonglade, the Timbermaw tribe fiercely guards its sanity. Wary and isolated, the Timbermaw defend their hold against their crazed northern kin, desperate to preserve what remains of their uncorrupted culture.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the furbolgs attacking everyone?",a="The aftermath of the Third War left lingering demonic corruption throughout Kalimdor's northern forests. Because furbolgs are deeply attuned to nature, this taint seeped into their minds, driving their tribes into paranoid, violent frenzy and replacing their peaceful elders with aggressive chieftains.",al={"why are furbolgs hostile","furbolg madness","why furbolgs went crazy"},it="hostile"},{q="Were furbolgs always enemies of the night elves?",a="No. For thousands of years, furbolgs and night elves were peaceful neighbors and close allies. They fought shoulder to shoulder against the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients, and night elf druids maintained gentle relations with tribal settlements until the recent wave of madness took hold.",al={"furbolg night elf history","did furbolgs like night elves"},it="ties"},{q="Are there any furbolgs who are still friendly?",a="Yes. The Timbermaw tribe maintains an uncorrupted enclave inside Timbermaw Hold, the crossroads tunnel system between Felwood, Moonglade, and Winterspring. Though deeply suspicious of outsiders, they will tolerate travelers who prove their enmity toward the tainted Deadwood and Winterfall tribes.",al={"friendly furbolgs","good furbolg tribe","uncorrupted furbolgs"},it="residents"},{q="What is wrong with the furbolgs on Teldrassil?",a="The Gnarlpine tribe on Teldrassil has fallen under the sway of fel moss, an insidious corruption spreading through the world tree's boughs. Under Chieftain Ursal the Mauler, the corrupted Gnarlpine abandoned their traditional villages and overran sacred sites like the Ban'ethil Barrow Den and Starbreeze Village.",al={"teldrassil furbolgs","gnarlpine corruption"},it="ruin"},{q="Who is behind the corruption of the Winterfall in Winterspring?",a="The Winterfall are secretly manipulated by a satyr named Xandivious, who lurks within their chieftain's den. The tribe's frenzy is sustained by Winterfall Firewater, brewed from tainted materials inside Felwood's Deadwood Cauldron and ferried north through the mountain passes.",al={"winterfall tribe lore","who corrupted winterfall","winterfall firewater origin"},sp=1},{q="Can corrupted furbolgs ever be cured?",a="Night elf sentinels and druids have attempted to cleanse individual tribes using moonwell waters and sacred relics, but broad recovery has proven nearly impossible. With satyrs and dark relics actively driving the corruption deeper, defenders often have no choice but to cull hostile packs to protect settlements.",al={"cure furbolgs","can furbolgs be saved"},it="threat"},{q="What are furbolgs?",a="Furbolgs are a race of hulking, bear-like humanoids native to the ancient forests of northern Kalimdor. Traditionally simple-minded, deeply spiritual, and reclusive, they lived peacefully alongside nature for millennia. Though powerful in stature and capable of fierce combat, they possessed no natural appetite for war or cruelty until recent tragedies twisted their minds.",al={"what is a furbolg","who are furbolgs","furbolg race"},it="identity",add="ec2e8979"},{q="Where did the furbolgs come from?",a="Furbolgs are an ancient people of Kalimdor. During the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago, furbolg packs led by Unng Ak stood shoulder to shoulder with night elves, tauren, and earthen against the Burning Legion, cementing an alliance that lasted peacefully for generations.",al={"furbolg origin","history of furbolgs","where do furbolgs come from"},it="history",add="195c4c39"},{q="Who leads the furbolgs?",a="Furbolgs do not have a single overarching leader or ruler. Instead, they are divided into independent tribes, each led by its own chieftains, shamans, and elders. In recent times, many of these tribes' wise elders have been overthrown, slain, or replaced by aggressive firebrands and corrupt chieftains who lead their crazed kin into violence.",al={"furbolg leader","who rules the furbolgs","furbolg king"},it="leader",add="11e5052c"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For night elves, the fall of the furbolgs is a heartbreaking tragedy. Tribes who were trusted woodland allies for ten thousand years have become feral predators threatening Astranaar and the sacred barrows of Teldrassil.",["class:Druid"]="Druids mourn the bear-men as kindred spirits of Ursoc whose connection to nature has been weaponized by fel taint, making tribal unrest a direct symptom of the land's broader sickness.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde views the hostile tribes of Ashenvale as territorial roadblocks, clearing aggressive packs like the Foulweald and Thistlefur to secure supply routes and outposts along the forest fringe."},kw={"ashenvale","corruption","deadwood","foulweald","furbolg","gnarlpine","thistlefur","timbermaw","ursoc","winterfall"},rel={"subzone:timbermaw-hold","zone:ashenvale","zone:felwood","zone:winterspring","topic:night-elf","topic:war-of-the-ancients","quest:486","quest:991"},src={"Furbolg"}}
E["topic:gnoll"] = {t="topic",n="Gnoll",z="general",s="Gnolls are a savage, hyena-like race of humanoid scavengers and raiders found scattered across both the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. Renowned for their vicious infighting, brutal strength, and animal cunning, they survive by pillaging settlements, butchering wildlife, and ambushing unwary travelers. Left unchecked, their sprawling packs and warlords pose a constant threat to frontier hamlets and trade roads alike.",h="Gnolls are a savage, hyena-like race of humanoid scavengers and raiders found scattered across both the...",sec={{t="Hyenas of the Wilds",b="Standing slightly shorter than the average human but possessing explosive physical power, gnolls are bipedal hyena-kin known for their spiky teeth, thick pelts, and distinct barking laugh. While fully capable of walking upright, they often drop to all fours to lope swiftly through brush or track prey by scent. Gnoll culture values raw physical dominance above all else. They organize into fractious packs led by the strongest, cruelest alpha, only yielding when battered into submission by a superior force.",sp=0},{t="The Gnoll War",b="Though usually fractured by petty squabbles, united gnolls once waged open war on the kingdom of Stormwind. Stormwind's armies prevailed in that Gnoll War, and the kingdom enjoyed a lasting peace afterward until the orcs came through the Dark Portal.",sp=0},{t="Raiders of the Southern Kingdoms",b="In the south, gnoll packs run rampant in the absence of Stormwind's full military might. In Elwynn Forest and Westfall, the Riverpaw pack—marshaled by the notorious Hogger—raids homesteads and forces subjugated kobolds to dig for gold, secretly colluding with the Defias Brotherhood. Further east in the Redridge Mountains, the Redridge pack snatches horses outside Lakeshire and sabotages bridge repairs, while the Shadowhide clan in Galardell Valley serves the dark will of the rogue wizard Morganth.",sp=1},{t="Pestilence and Territory",b="Gnoll menace is not limited to the human south. In northern Lordaeron, the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw raises undead Rot Hide gnolls to dig up corpses across Tirisfal and Silverpine. Near Hillsbrad, the Mudsnout harvest foul, toxic blossoms among ruined farmsteads. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, the Palemane pack butchers game across Mulgore despite tauren efforts to make peace, while the Woodpaw of Feralas wage a desperate, frenzied war against encroaching silithid swarms.",sp=1,bt={"q437.6"}}},faq={{q="What exactly is a gnoll?",a="Gnolls are hyena-like humanoid carnivores known for their violent nature, physical endurance, and low cunning. They live in nomadic or semi-permanent camps, surviving mostly on plunder, hunting, and carrion.",al={"what are gnolls","gnoll race","are gnolls hyenas"},it="identity"},{q="Why don't gnolls form a proper kingdom?",a="Gnolls are fiercely belligerent and driven by immediate hierarchy. Without an exceptional and tyrannical warlord beating them into line, packs inevitably splinter into bloody territorial feuds, preventing long-term organization or civil progress.",al={"why are gnolls scattered","gnoll empire","gnoll unity"},it="conflict"},{q="What was the Gnoll War?",a="Long before the First War, united gnoll packs waged war on the kingdom of Stormwind. The kingdom's armies prevailed, and Stormwind enjoyed a lasting peace afterward until the orcs came through the Dark Portal.",al={"stormwind gnoll war"},it="history"},{q="Why are gnolls working with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="The Riverpaw pack in Westfall and Elwynn collaborates with the Defias Brotherhood in exchange for arms, coordination, and shared spoils. The bandits use the gnolls as brute muscle to overrun farmlands and distract Stormwind's dwindling garrisons.",al={"defias gnoll alliance","riverpaw defias","hogger defias"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="Are the Rot Hide gnolls alive?",a="No. The Rot Hide gnolls prowling Tirisfal Glades and Silverpine Forest are undead thralls. They answer to Thule Ravenclaw, an exiled human necromancer using Fenris Isle as a redoubt to gather corpses for the Scourge.",al={"rot hide undead","silverpine gnolls","fenris isle gnolls"},sp=1,bt={"q437.6"}},{q="Why do tauren fight the Palemane gnolls?",a="When the tauren settled Mulgore, they attempted to coexist and communicate with the indigenous Palemane pack. The gnolls rebuffed all peaceful gestures, slaughtering the plains wildlife without restraint and threatening tauren villages.",al={"palemane mulgore","tauren and gnolls","why kill palemane"},it="motive"},{q="Why are gnolls hostile to travelers?",a="Gnolls are brutish, predatory scavengers who view other races primarily as prey, trespassers, or sources of plunder. Belligerent and driven by basic animal cunning, they value physical strength above all else and respect little else. Because they rely heavily on raiding, kidnapping, and stealing livestock from nearby settlements, gnoll packs reflexively attack anyone who wanders into their territory or crosses their hunting grounds.",al={"why do gnolls attack","are gnolls hostile","gnolls attack on sight"},it="hostile",add="06f85769"},{q="Who leads the gnolls?",a="Gnolls have no unified leader or central king. Instead, they are splintered into scattered, squabbling packs and tribes across both continents. In each clan, leadership falls to the alpha or packlord—the largest, most brutal brute capable of beating rivals into submission. Notable warlords include Hogger of the Riverpaw in Elwynn Forest, Packlord Yowler in Redridge, and Lieutenant Fangore among the Shadowhide.",al={"who is gnoll leader","gnoll king","gnoll pack leader"},it="leader",add="15503234"},{q="Where did gnolls come from?",a="Gnolls are an ancient race of hyena-like humanoids whose origins are lost to history. They have inhabited both Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms for millennia, long before the rise of modern nations. Early human settlers encountered vicious gnoll packs in Tirisfal Glades thousands of years ago, and troll tribes fought them in southern forests. Wherever wild lands exist, gnolls have carved out a savage existence.",al={"gnoll origin","where do gnolls come from","history of gnolls"},it="origin",add="1c9639a8"}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, gnolls are an ancient enemy that once burned the kingdom's heartlands; today, packs like the Riverpaw and Shadowhide threaten the kingdom's vulnerable frontier roads.",["race:Tauren"]="The Palemane tribe in Mulgore treats the land with careless cruelty, forcing young tauren braves to drive them back to preserve the balance of the plains.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance marshals across Elwynn, Westfall, Redridge, and the Wetlands rely heavily on freelance champions to thin out encroaching gnoll populations.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken view gnolls either as an annoyance or a resource: the Mudsnout cultivate restorative fungi in Hillsbrad, while Sylvanas previously enslaved northern packs through banshee possession."},kw={"gnoll","hogger","mosshide","mudsnout","palemane","riverpaw","rot hide","shadowhide","woodpaw"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:defias-brotherhood","zone:elwynn","zone:redridge","zone:westfall","zone:silverpine","zone:mulgore","quest:124"},src={"Gnoll"}}
E["topic:gnome"] = {t="topic",n="Gnome",z="general",s="Gnomes are a diminutive, eccentric, and astonishingly intelligent race renowned across Azeroth for their inventive engineering and mastery of the arcane. Despite their cheerful demeanor, they recently suffered a catastrophic tragedy when a subterranean trogg invasion and a disastrous defense plan irradiated their capital of Gnomeregan. Now living as refugees in Ironforge's Tinker Town, the gnomes remain staunch Alliance allies as they fight to reclaim their lost home.",h="Gnomes are a diminutive, eccentric, and astonishingly intelligent race renowned across Azeroth for their...",sec={{t="Ingenuity and Optimism",b="Small in stature but towering in intellect, gnomes approach the world with boundless curiosity and scientific ambition. While other races focus on recording histories and preserving traditions, gnomes look relentlessly forward, dedicating their lives to mechanical engineering, arcane studies, and technological innovation. Their inventions—ranging from clockwork devices and flying machines to complex subterranean trains—have shaped the modern world. Even after devastating hardships, gnomefolk maintain an irrepressible cheerfulness and an unshakable drive to rebuild and solve the impossible.",sp=0},{t="Forged in the Mountains",b="The gnomes made their home in the frozen crags of Dun Morogh, and when they met the dwarves of Ironforge, the two peoples discovered an immediate kinship. Dwarven brawn and gnomish ingenuity united to carve out the marvelous subterranean techno-city of Gnomeregan in western Dun Morogh, where the gnomes are led by visionary leaders known as High Tinkers.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Gnomeregan",b="During the Third War, the gnomes sent armaments to Lordaeron but withheld troops, drawing confusion from their allies. In truth, an apocalyptic catastrophe had struck their doorstep: hordes of savage troggs, stirred by excavations near Uldaman, surged into Gnomeregan's lower sectors. Desperate to purge the invaders without burdening an embattled Alliance, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque followed the council of his chief advisor and vented radioactive waste into the lower city. The toxic gambit failed utterly. The troggs survived mutated and frenzied, while nearly eighty percent of the gnomish population perished.",sp=1},{t="Exile in Tinker Town",b="The surviving gnomes fled their poisoned home, finding sanctuary among their dwarven cousins in Ironforge. High Tinker Mekkatorque and his people established a bustling enclave in Ironforge's Tinker Town, continuing to support the Alliance and oversee the Deeprun Tram linking Ironforge to Stormwind. From this adopted quarter, gnomish commandos and brave volunteers constantly mount expeditions into Gnomeregan's irradiated depths, striving to clear out the troggs, deal with mechanical abominations, and someday reclaim their grand city.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are gnomes living in Ironforge instead of their own city?",a="Gnomes are currently refugees residing in Tinker Town within Ironforge. Their ancestral home of Gnomeregan was overrun by a sudden invasion of subterranean troggs. During the crisis, a desperate plan to irradiate the troggs went wrong, bathing the city in lethal fallout and killing the vast majority of its inhabitants. King Magni Bronzebeard offered the survivors shelter in the dwarven capital.",al={"why in ironforge","where do gnomes live","gnome refugee city"}},{q="Who leads the gnomes?",a="The gnomes are led by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque. Rather than maintaining a hereditary royal line, gnomes elect their leaders based on merit, engineering prowess, and visionary thinking. Mekkatorque carries the heavy burden of having ordered the irradiation of Gnomeregan to stop the troggs, and now directs the war effort to take back their home.",al={"gnome leader","gnome king","who rules gnomes","high tinker"}},{q="Why didn't the gnomes help fight the Scourge in Lordaeron?",a="While the gnomes provided siege weapons and technology to the Alliance of Lordaeron, they could not spare soldiers because Gnomeregan was fighting for its survival against troggs at the exact same time. The gnomes chose not to ask their allies for help, believing the Alliance had enough to handle against the undead Scourge.",al={"gnomes in third war","why gnomes skipped third war","gnomes third war help"}},{q="What is the Deeprun Tram?",a="The Deeprun Tram is an extraordinary subterranean railway designed by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and built by gnomish engineers. It connects Tinker Town in Ironforge directly to the dwarven district of Stormwind City, allowing rapid transport of troops, supplies, and travelers between the two allied capitals.",al={"who built the tram","stormwind ironforge train","deeprun tram origin"}},{q="What is the rivalry between gnomes and goblins?",a="Gnomes and goblins possess an intense cultural and technological rivalry that flared during the Second War. Gnomish engineering focuses on precision, stability, and intricate, safe mechanisms. In contrast, goblin engineering prioritizes raw explosive power, cheap production, and swift results regardless of volatility. Both races constantly attempt to out-invent and mock one another.",al={"gnomes vs goblins","gnome goblin engineering","why do gnomes hate goblins"}},{q="Which classes can gnomes be?",a="Gnomes train as warriors, rogues, priests, mages and warlocks.",al={"gnome classes","what classes can gnomes be","can gnomes be priests"}}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Your people carry the tragic loss of Gnomeregan with quiet resolve, using brilliance, optimism, and explosive firepower to fight for your stolen home from exile in Tinker Town.",["race:Dwarf"]="The gnomes are your oldest and dearest mountain neighbors; opening Ironforge's gates to their survivors in Tinker Town was an easy choice after generations of shared brotherhood.",["profession:Engineering"]="Gnomish engineering is world-renowned for sophisticated contraptions, ray devices, and mind-boggling precision that values meticulous design over volatile goblin detonations.",["class:Mage"]="Despite their diminutive size, gnomes are exceptionally gifted arcanists.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance owes immense strategic mobility and siege technology to the gnomes, who faithfully supply machines and maintain the Deeprun Tram despite their own displacement."},kw={"deeprun tram","gnomanity","gnome","gnomeregan","gnomes","high tinker","mekkatorque","tinker town","trogg"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","subzone:tinker-town","zone:ironforge","subzone:deeprun-tram","topic:trogg","topic:goblin","subzone:uldaman"},src={"Gnome"}}
E["topic:goblin"] = {t="topic",n="Goblin",z="general",s="Goblins are diminutive, cunning humanoids originating from the Isle of Kezan, renowned across Azeroth for their relentless pursuit of profit, explosive engineering, and aggressive trade cartels. While most maintain a stance of strict neutrality to maximize commerce between the Alliance and Horde, mercenary and cutthroat outfits like the Venture Company and Steamwheedle Cartel leave an indelible mark on every corner of the world.",h="Goblins are diminutive, cunning humanoids originating from the Isle of Kezan, renowned across Azeroth for...",sec={{t="Cartels of Kezan",b="Goblins hail from the Isle of Kezan in the South Seas, where their society long ago divided into competing economic syndicates known as cartels. Rather than building kingdoms or armies of their own, goblin leaders channeled their ambitions into cutthroat mercantilism, volatile engineering, and industrial ventures that now reach every corner of the world.",sp=0},{t="Mercenaries and Mercantilism",b="During the Second War, Trade Prince Steamwheedle allied his cartel with Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, supplying maps, siege craft, and fleet-building expertise in exchange for Stormwind's captured plunder. Following the Horde's eventual defeat, the cartels learned that open allegiance cost them lucrative markets. In the modern era, the major cartels claim official neutrality, maintaining bustling trade havens like Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert. Here, Alliance and Horde travelers cross paths without blades drawn—so long as port bruisers keep the peace.",sp=0},{t="Hired Hands and Industry",b="A goblin's primary loyalty belongs to gold, and many operate outside the neutral cartels as hired specialists or ruthless corporate barons. The Venture Company pursues unchecked deforestation and resource stripping across continents, heedless of local populations. Elsewhere, skilled artificers sell their talents directly to criminal enterprises: deep inside the Deadmines, goblin engineers like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin oversee smelters and shipwrights constructing a massive ironclad juggernaut for the Defias Brotherhood.",sp=1,bt={"defias.hideout"}}},faq={{q="Are goblins aligned with the Alliance or the Horde?",a="Neither. The primary trade syndicates, including the Steamwheedle Cartel, adhere to strict neutrality to trade freely with both sides. Individual goblins and private enterprises happily contract out their services to the Horde, Alliance, or third parties like the Defias Brotherhood for the right price.",al={"are goblins horde","goblin faction","who do goblins fight for"},it="side"},{q="Why are goblin inventions so prone to exploding?",a="Goblin engineering prizes raw power, haste, cheap production, and high explosive yields over structural safety, so their devices deliver spectacular results and spectacular explosions in roughly equal measure.",al={"goblin engineering dangerous","why do goblin machines blow up","goblin vs gnome tech"}},{q="What is the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the most influential mercantile cartel operating on the mainland. They manage vital neutral trading ports and cross-continental transit hubs, including Booty Bay in southern Stranglethorn and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert.",al={"steamwheedle cartel","who rules booty bay","goblin ports"}},{q="What is the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a ruthless goblin-run industrial conglomerate notorious for aggressive deforestation, strip-mining, and oil extraction. Unlike the Steamwheedle Cartel, they operate with little regard for local law or environmental balance, often clashing violently with native populations.",al={"venture co","who are venture company","what is venture co"}},{q="Why are there goblins working with the Defias in the Deadmines?",a="The Defias Brotherhood possessed abundant plundered coin and raw ore from the Moonbrook mines, allowing them to hire top goblin specialists. Craftsmen like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin were contracted to oversee heavy smelting and build Edwin VanCleef's ironclad juggernaut.",al={"deadmines goblins","why are goblins in deadmines","gilnid sneed deadmines"},sp=1,it="why_here",bt={"defias.hideout"}},{q="Where is the goblin homeland?",a="Goblins hail from the tropical Isle of Kezan in the South Seas. It was there that their great trading cartels first rose, building an empire of commerce and industrial smog.",al={"where do goblins come from","goblin home island","kezan"},it="origin"},{q="What are goblins?",a="Goblins are small, green-skinned humanoids renowned across Azeroth for their shrewd mercantile cunning, mastery of volatile technology, and love of explosives. Driven by an insatiable appetite for profit, they view almost every endeavor through the lens of trade, mining, and industry. While physically small, their ingenuity and willingness to weaponize raw engineering make them both formidable and unpredictable.",al={"what is a goblin","who are goblins","goblin race"},it="identity",add="d8aa07c2"},{q="Who leads the goblins?",a="Goblins have no single ruler. Instead, their society is divided into competing commercial cartels and syndicates, each commanded by a wealthy Trade Prince or executive mogul. Prominent figures like Trade Prince Steamwheedle lead massive trading syndicates, while local barons and overseers—such as Baron Revilgaz in Booty Bay—direct operations and enforce order across individual ports and settlements.",al={"goblin leader","who is in charge of goblins","goblin ruler"},it="leader",add="d5109913"},{q="Why are some goblins hostile to travelers?",a="While major ports like Booty Bay and Gadgetzan maintain strict armed neutrality to protect trade, many goblins belong to cutthroat organizations that view travelers as obstacles or competition. Entities like the Venture Company aggressively defend their illicit logging and mining claims with brute force, while other goblins readily hire themselves out as mercenaries, technicians, or pirates to criminal factions.",al={"hostile goblins","why do goblins attack","are goblins enemies"},it="hostile",add="68045a57"},{q="Where did the goblins come from, and what is their history?",a="Goblins hail from the Isle of Kezan in the South Seas, where their society divided into rival trading cartels. Rather than building kingdoms or armies of their own, the cartels turned their energy toward economic warfare, trade, and worldwide technological enterprise.",al={"goblin history","origin of goblins","where did goblins come from"},it="history",add="a6996125"}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Goblin engineering prioritizes extreme explosive force, offensive gadgetry, and high-risk mechanical contraptions, standing in sharp contrast to the precise, defensive designs favored by gnomes.",["profession:Mining"]="Mining fuels the goblin cartels' industry and trade, and they remain fiercely protective of valuable mineral claims across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance authorities remain deeply suspicious of goblin neutrality, remembering well their mercenary service providing warships and sappers to the Old Horde during the Second War.",["faction:Horde"]="While Thrall's Horde utilizes goblin mechanics and trades with neutral hubs, memory remains fresh of the mercenary contracts forged between Orgrim Doomhammer and Trade Prince Steamwheedle."},kw={"booty bay","cartel","deadmines","engineering","gadgetzan","goblin","kezan","steamwheedle","venture company"},rel={"topic:steamwheedle-cartel","topic:venture-company","subzone:booty-bay","subzone:gadgetzan","npc:baron-revilgaz","npc:captain-greenskin","topic:second-war","zone:deadmines"},src={"Goblin"}}
E["topic:grimtotem-tribe"] = {t="topic",n="Grimtotem tribe",z="general",s="The Grimtotem tribe is an aggressive, fiercely traditionalist tauren clan that refuses to formally swear allegiance to Thrall's Horde. Distinguishable by their dark hides and fierce warpaint, they seek to purge foreign races from Kalimdor and retake ancestral lands by any means necessary. Even as their matriarch, the Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem, resides atop Thunder Bluff playing regional politics, her kin actively raid settlements across Kalimdor.",h="The Grimtotem tribe is an aggressive, fiercely traditionalist tauren clan that refuses to formally swear...",sec={{t="A Tribe Apart",b="Unlike their peaceful cousins who honor the Earth Mother through reverence, the Grimtotem believe Kalimdor belongs exclusively to the tauren. From youth, they are trained not in the patient harmony of the Great Hunt, but in ruthlessness, stealth, and lethal combat. They harbor immense disdain for the other races that have settled upon their ancient soils. Though Cairne Bloodhoof has welcomed them into Thunder Bluff in an effort to unite all tauren people, the Grimtotem clan stands alone as the only major tribe that has never formally joined Thrall's Horde, maintaining their own insular and violent agenda.",sp=0},{t="Wildland Aggressors",b="In the wilderness far from Thunder Bluff's watchful eyes, Grimtotem hostility flares openly into violence. In Stonetalon Mountains, they butcher local settlements and threaten Horde supply outposts. Across the towering mesas of Thousand Needles, centered around Darkcloud Pinnacle, they constantly menace Freewind Post and plot to seize the canyon. Encounters in Feralas prove equally perilous: the Grimtotem attack Horde travelers, Alliance caravans, and fellow tauren without hesitation. Horde garrisons at Sun Rock Retreat and Camp Mojache regularly post bounties and dispatch warriors to cut down these violent outcasts.",sp=0},{t="Shadows and Apothecaries",b="Beneath Magatha's veneer of spiritual counsel sits an intricate web of intrigue. The Elder Crone is an ardent sponsor of the tauren's controversial ties with the Forsaken, publicly claiming she wishes to heal the undead, while secretly seeking out toxic plagues and Highborne artifacts. In Thousand Needles, scouts from Freewind Post intercept guarded correspondence between cloaked couriers and Grimtotem chieftains in Darkcloud Pinnacle.",sp=1,bt={"q4542.4"}}},faq={{q="Why are the Grimtotem hostile if their leader lives in Thunder Bluff?",a="The Grimtotem tribe never formally joined the Horde. While Cairne Bloodhoof tolerates Magatha Grimtotem on the high mesas of Thunder Bluff in hopes of keeping peace among all tauren, the tribe's roaming warriors reject outside diplomacy. In Stonetalon, Thousand Needles, and Feralas, they attack anyone trespassing on ancestral territory, including other tauren.",al={"why grimtotem red","why do grimtotem attack horde","are grimtotem friendly"},it="hostile"},{q="How can you tell a Grimtotem apart from other tauren?",a="Grimtotem tauren almost universally possess pitch-black fur. They are also known for painting their bodies and faces with distinctive, sharp warpaint in stark white, deep red, or blue.",al={"grimtotem appearance","grimtotem fur color"}},{q="Who leads the Grimtotem?",a="They are ruled by the Elder Crone, Magatha Grimtotem, a powerful shaman residing on the Elder Rise in Thunder Bluff. She attained rule after marrying the previous chieftain, who perished shortly afterward in an unexplained climbing mishap.",al={"grimtotem leader","elder crone"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Grimtotem goal?",a="The Grimtotem aim to purge Kalimdor of what they view as lesser foreign races—including orcs, humans, and night elves—and reclaim all ancestral tauren territories. Unlike Cairne's people, they believe this land should be seized purely through force, deception, and elemental dominion.",al={"what do grimtotem want","grimtotem motive"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Magatha Grimtotem work with the Forsaken?",a="Publicly, Magatha claims she champions the Forsaken because tauren shamanism and earth wisdom can help cure their affliction and return their humanity. Privately, she seeks their expertise in virulent poisons, rot, and dark magic to eliminate her enemies and consolidate power across Kalimdor.",al={"magatha and undead","grimtotem forsaken"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="What is the Grimtotem tribe?",a="The Grimtotem are one of the most powerful and aggressive tauren tribes in Kalimdor. Unlike the generally peaceful and spiritual clans united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, the Grimtotem are militaristic extremists. From childhood, their warriors are trained in ruthless combat, eschewing traditional honorable hunting rites in favor of learning to kill swiftly with any weapon at hand.",al={"who are the grimtotem","grimtotem clan","what is grimtotem"},it="identity",add="855936f4"},{q="Why are the Grimtotem found throughout Kalimdor?",a="You will encounter Grimtotem outposts and warbands across the Stonetalon Mountains, Thousand Needles, and Feralas. They have established fortified positions in these regions to assert dominance over ancestral lands, actively raiding rival settlements and encroaching on both Horde and Alliance territories.",al={"where are grimtotem found","why are grimtotem in stonetalon","grimtotem locations"},it="why_here",add="04251e4b"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Grimtotem are an open shame to the Bloodhoof and other tribes; though Cairne offers them shelter in Thunder Bluff in the name of tauren unity, their warmongering threatens to tear that very unity apart.",["faction:Horde"]="Do not mistake their black hides for allies: out in the barrens, mountains, and marshes of Kalimdor, Grimtotem warriors will ambush Horde scouts on sight.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Grimtotem despise all who cross into Kalimdor, attacking Alliance traders and night elf enclaves indiscriminately.",["race:Undead"]="Magatha Grimtotem is one of the few tauren leaders in Thunder Bluff who openly courts the Forsaken, brokering research into exotic plagues alongside Royal Apothecary Society specialists."},kw={"darkcloud pinnacle","elder crone","freewind post","grimtotem","magatha","tauren tribe","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:magatha-grimtotem","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle","zone:thousandneedles","zone:stonetalon","quest:5064","quest:6548"},src={"Grimtotem tribe"}}
E["topic:harpy"] = {t="topic",n="Harpy",z="general",s="Harpies are predatory, avian humanoids resembling winged night elves who dwell in foul-smelling roosts across Kalimdor and the high mountain passes of Alterac. Infamous for their shrill shrieks and tempestuous elemental magic, they raid unwary travelers and blight the environments they inhabit. Night elf folklore links them to cursed betrayers of Queen Azshara, though their true origins remain uncertain.",h="Harpies are predatory, avian humanoids resembling winged night elves who dwell in foul-smelling roosts across...",sec={{t="Winged Scourge of Kalimdor",b="Harpies bear the upper torsos and heads of night elf women, joined to broad feathered wings and wicked talons. Despite their delicate, elven visage—which ironically grows more striking as they age—their nature is thoroughly savage. Originally roosting among the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains, they have aggressively migrated across Kalimdor, terrorizing the arid canyons of Durotar, the sacred boughs of Teldrassil, and the heights of Mulgore. Unlike the wild creatures they hunt, harpies do not live in harmony with nature; they despoil surrounding flora and fauna, turning their nesting grounds into foul, fetid middens littered with the picked-clean bones of their prey.",sp=0},{t="Avian Lineage and Dark Nature",b="Night elf myth often claims harpies originated as treacherous handmaidens cursed by Queen Azshara before the Great Sundering, though no one can say for certain where they truly came from. Whatever their origins, harpies today operate as ruthless matriarchies, where aspiring queens must betray, slaughter, and torment rivals to maintain command.",sp=0},{t="Tempest Magic and Wicked Habits",b="Beyond rending flesh with razor-sharp claws, harpies command dangerous wind and lightning magic, channeling shrieking gales to batter foes from above. They are deeply cunning hunters capable of spoken language, occasionally trading scavenged oddities with subterranean creatures like kobolds in exchange for bright gems and shiny trinkets. Unwary explorers are sometimes beguiled by their elven forms, but physical contact carries grave danger: a harpy's kiss is venomous, rapidly inflicting a violent, wasting fever upon those foolish enough to let their guard down.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where do harpies originally come from?",a="Harpies originally hailed from the jagged peaks of the Stonetalon Mountains in central Kalimdor. From these ancestral heights, their populations expanded rapidly, establishing nests in Teldrassil, the Barrens, Durotar, Mulgore, Feralas, the Thousand Needles, and even across the Great Sea in the snowy crags of Alterac Valley.",al={"harpy origins","harpy homeland","where do harpies live"},it="origin"},{q="Are harpies actually related to night elves?",a="Kalimdor folklore claims they were once night elf women cursed for betraying Queen Azshara, which would explain their elven features. Whether the tale is true remains unknown.",al={"are harpies night elves","harpy night elf connection","curse of azshara"},it="ties"},{q="Why are harpy nests so infamous?",a="Unlike wild birds, harpies defile the land around them. Their nests are filthy, stench-ridden middens composed of trampled foliage, noxious waste, and the butchered bones and remnants of unwary travelers and beasts they drag from nearby trails.",al={"harpy smell","what are harpy nests like","harpy roosts"},it="danger"},{q="How do harpy covens choose their leaders?",a="Harpy leadership is fiercely contested. To claim and hold the title of queen or matriarch, a harpy must prove herself far more ruthless than her roost-mates, employing betrayal, murder, and relentless cruelty against enemies and rivals alike.",al={"harpy matriarch","harpy queen","who leads harpies"},it="leader"},{q="What powers and combat tactics do harpies use?",a="Common harpies dive from above using crude weapons or razor-sharp talons. Their spellcasters, however, wield tempestuous storm magic, striking foes with crackling lightning, swirling wind gusts, and shrill, disorienting shrieks.",al={"harpy abilities","harpy magic","how do harpies fight"},it="power"},{q="Is it true that a harpy's kiss is dangerous?",a="Yes. Beyond their claws and storm magic, harpies carry a toxic kiss. Travelers who are lured by their elven features into letting one close risk suffering from a potent poison that induces a sudden, debilitating fever.",al={"harpy kiss","harpy poison","harpy fever"},it="threat"},{q="What are harpies?",a="Harpies are vicious, intelligent avian creatures resembling a hybrid of a female night elf and a predatory bird, possessing graceful wings for arms and wicked talons for feet. Rather than harmonizing with the wilderness, they actively despoil their surroundings, slaughtering local wildlife and building foul, noxious nests. They wield primal storm magic alongside their talons and crude weaponry, causing misery wherever they roost.",al={"what is a harpy","who are harpies","harpy lore"},it="identity",add="fb0f8e51"},{q="Why are harpies hostile to travelers?",a="Harpies attack on sight because they are territorial, predatory, and thrive on causing havoc and sorrow. They expand aggressively from their roosts, ambushing travelers, raiding supply caravans, and killing nearly every creature that wanders into their lands to litter their nests with the grisly remains of their victims.",al={"why do harpies attack","are harpies friendly","why are harpies aggressive"},it="hostile",add="6541e8e0"},{q="Do harpies have any allies?",a="Harpies rarely tolerate outsiders, but they are known to maintain loose ties with kobolds. Because harpies covet shiny gems and ores, kobolds trade these scavenged treasures in exchange for items like harpy earwax, and in some regions, kobolds offer tribute to harpies in exchange for protection.",al={"who do harpies work with","harpy allies","who aligns with harpies"},it="allies",add="b7cf3fa6"},{q="Why are harpies found all across Kalimdor?",a="Harpies were once confined to their ancestral roosts in the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains. Over time, their growing numbers and aggressive nature drove covens to migrate outward across the continent. You can now encounter their foul roosts anywhere from the forests of Teldrassil to the mesas of Mulgore, Durotar, and the Barrens.",al={"where do harpies live","why are harpies in kalimdor","harpy territory"},it="why_here",add="518dbd10"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Though they bear an uncanny resemblance to night elf women, harpies represent a perversion of the natural skies, despoiling sacred boughs around Teldrassil's Oracle Glade.",["race:Tauren"]="The Windfury harpies of Mulgore and the Screeching brood of Thousand Needles have plagued tauren caravans and blighted the plains for generations, making them reviled enemies of the tribes.",["race:Orc"]="The Dustwind harpies roosting in Drygulch Ravine and Razorwind Canyon persistently menace supply routes between Sen'jin Village, Razor Hill, and Orgrimmar, requiring constant armed patrols.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold particular anger toward harpies, who scorn nature's balance and pollute the wilderness with tempest magic and foul nests."},kw={"bloodfeather","dustwind","harpy","kalimdor","stonetalon","talons","windfury","witchwing"},rel={"zone:stonetalon","quest:876","zone:durotar","zone:mulgore","zone:barrens","subzone:oracle-glade","topic:night-elf","topic:tauren"},src={"Harpy"}}
E["topic:highborne"] = {t="topic",n="Highborne",z="general",s="The Highborne, or quel'dorei, were the aristocratic sorcerer caste of the ancient Kaldorei Empire who served Queen Azshara. Their reckless manipulation of the Well of Eternity brought the Burning Legion to Azeroth, leading to the devastating War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering.",h="The Highborne, or quel'dorei, were the aristocratic sorcerer caste of the ancient Kaldorei Empire who served...",sec={{t="Aristocrats of the Well",b="Before the Sundering, the Highborne arose as the favored upper class surrounding Queen Azshara. Claiming superiority over common kaldorei through selective bloodlines, intelligence, and opulent privilege, they strictly controlled access to the Well of Eternity. They discovered how to tap and shape its primal arcane energies, erecting magnificent cities across ancient Kalimdor. Their endless thirst for greater sorcery led them to draw deeper from the Well, turning its calm waters turbulent and dark.",sp=0},{t="The Betrayal and the Sundering",b="The ripples of arcane power created by Highborne spellcraft caught the attention of the dark titan Sargeras and the Burning Legion. Convinced by Azshara and her counselor Xavius that the demons would remake the world into an untainted paradise, the Highborne opened a massive gateway within the Well itself. This unleashed the catastrophic War of the Ancients. As demonic hordes slaughtered the populace, rebel forces rallied by Malfurion Stormrage fought to close the portal, causing the Well of Eternity to implode and tear the supercontinent apart.",sp=0},{t="Scattered Heirs and Remnants",b="The Highborne fate splintered after the Sundering. Those dragged beneath the waves alongside Azshara were twisted into serpentine naga, while others fell to demonic curses and became satyrs. A faction under Dath'Remar Sunstrider aided the resistance but refused to abandon arcane magic, ultimately suffering exile across the sea to found the high elven kingdom of Quel'Thalas. Today, isolated enclaves like the Shen'dralar remain cloistered in Eldre'Thalas, shunned relics of an empire that nearly destroyed Azeroth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does 'Highborne' mean?",a="In Darnassian, they are known as the quel'dorei, which translates directly to 'children of noble birth.' They chose the name to distinguish their aristocratic bloodlines and magical aptitude from the rest of kaldorei society.",al={"queldorei meaning","what does queldorei mean","highborne translation"},it="name"},{q="Are modern night elves related to the Highborne?",a="Yes. The Highborne were merely the noble, magic-wielding ruling caste of the ancient night elves. Aside from being slightly paler and prone to flamboyant dress, they were physically the same race. After the Sundering, modern night elf society banned arcane magic and abolished the caste entirely.",al={"are night elves highborne","difference between night elves and highborne","highborne night elf connection"},it="people"},{q="How did the Highborne cause the War of the Ancients?",a="Their excessive and unchecked use of the Well of Eternity sent magical ripples across the cosmos, attracting the Burning Legion. Enticed by Sargeras's promises of unmatched power and an idealized world, Azshara and her nobles channeled the Well to create a portal, inviting the demon army directly into Kalimdor.",al={"why did burning legion come","how did highborne summon demons","highborne war of ancients role"},it="history"},{q="Did all Highborne side with Queen Azshara?",a="No. Several factions broke away when they saw the demonic devastation. Sorcerers led by Dath'Remar Sunstrider defected to join Malfurion's Kaldorei Resistance, while groups like the Shen'dralar in Eldre'Thalas focused on surviving and defending their own regional holdings.",al={"good highborne","did any highborne fight azshara","highborne resistance"},it="side"},{q="What happened to the surviving Highborne after the war?",a="Their lineage scattered across several destinies. Many who sank with Zin-Azshari became the naga, others were twisted into satyrs, and those who followed Dath'Remar were exiled to the Eastern Kingdoms, evolving into high elves. A few, like the Shen'dralar in Dire Maul, remained cloistered in ancient ruins.",al={"what became of the highborne","where are the highborne now","highborne descendants"},it="origin"},{q="Who are the Shen'dralar?",a="The Shen'dralar are an ancient sect of Highborne scholars and arcanists who successfully defended the southern city of Eldre'Thalas during the Great Sundering. Cut off from Queen Azshara and mainstream kaldorei society, they retreated into the city—now called Dire Maul—relying on dark sorceries to preserve their immortality.",al={"dire maul highborne","eldre thalas elves","who lives in dire maul"},it="residents"},{q="Who were the Highborne?",a="The Highborne—or quel'dorei—were the wealthy aristocratic caste of the ancient night elven empire, serving as the favored courtiers and sorcerers of Queen Azshara. Distinguishing themselves through hereditary privilege and relentless study of arcane magic from the Well of Eternity, they grew arrogant and detached from ordinary kaldorei. Their reckless magical experimentation and pact with Sargeras brought about the Burning Legion's first invasion ten thousand years ago.",al={"what are highborne","who are the highborne","quel'dorei night elves"},it="identity",add="798aa346"},{q="Who leads the Highborne?",a="In ancient times, the Highborne answered directly to Queen Azshara, their beloved and tyrannical monarch. Following the Great Sundering and the collapse of the Kaldorei Empire, the caste fragmented into scattered remnants. Surviving enclaves like the Shen'dralar in Dire Maul follow their own local rulers, while the Highborne who fled across the sea formed new lineages, leaving no single leader over the ancient caste today.",al={"highborne leader","who rules the highborne","queen azshara highborne"},it="leader",add="de607c9c"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The memory of the Highborne caste remains a deep scar on night elven culture, serving as the sole reason arcane magic is outlawed under pain of exile or death across Kalimdor.",["class:Mage"]="The Highborne were the first mortal practitioners of mortal arcane magic on Azeroth, whose breathtaking achievements and catastrophic mistakes established the dangerous rules of modern spellcasting.",["class:Druid"]="Druidism arose as the natural philosophy that opposed the Highborne's reckless exploitation of the Well of Eternity, providing the foundation for healing Kalimdor after the empire fell.",["class:Warlock"]="The Highborne were the first mortals on Azeroth to treat with the Burning Legion, bartering cosmic secrets and demonic pacts that paved the way for modern fel studies."},kw={"azshara","dath'remar","dire maul","eldre'thalas","highborne","quel'dorei","sargeras","shen'dralar","well of eternity","zin-azshari"},rel={"topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:night-elf","subzone:dire-maul","topic:burning-legion","topic:naga","topic:satyr","npc:malfurion-stormrage","zone:azshara"},src={"Highborne"}}
E["topic:horde"] = {t="topic",n="Horde",z="general",s="The Horde is a coalition of outcast nations united under Warchief Thrall to secure their mutual survival and freedom. Anchored by the shamanic bond of the orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren across Kalimdor, the faction also maintains an uneasy alliance of convenience with the Forsaken in Lordaeron. Beset by historical grievances and hostile neighbors, the Horde values strength, action, and honor over political maneuverings.",h="The Horde is a coalition of outcast nations united under Warchief Thrall to secure their mutual survival and...",sec={{t="A Reborn Union",b="The modern Horde bears little resemblance to the demonic army that marched through the Dark Portal in the First and Second Wars. After shattering the Burning Legion's grasp upon his people, Warchief Thrall led the orcs across the Great Sea to Kalimdor to build a spiritual homeland founded on ancestral traditions and honor. Along the way, Thrall forged lasting ties with Vol'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls and Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren tribes, fighting side by side to drive off centaur and demonic incursions during the Third War.",sp=0},{t="The Kalimdor Core",b="In Kalimdor, the orcs carved out the nation of Durotar and built the fortress capital of Orgrimmar, offering the Darkspear refuge upon the nearby Echo Isles. Just south, in the lush plains of Mulgore, the nomadic tauren unified around Thunder Bluff under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Sharing shamanic reverence, a deep bond with nature, and mutual survival needs, these three races view each other not as tactical assets, but as an enduring family bound by spilled blood and spiritual respect.",sp=0},{t="An Alliance of Convenience",b="Across the sea in the ruined lands of Lordaeron, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner led the free-willed Forsaken out of the Scourge's grasp. Desperate for allies against surviving human factions and remnants of the Scourge, Sylvanas sought a place within the Horde. Tauren elders argued the undead might yet be redeemed, and Thrall admitted the Forsaken into the Horde, establishing a vital military outpost in the Eastern Kingdoms despite persistent misgivings among Kalimdor's ranks.",sp=0},{t="A Cold War",b="Despite Thrall's hopes to build an enduring peace with the Alliance, the legacy of the Old Horde casts an inescapable shadow. To human and dwarven eyes, orcs remain bloodthirsty invaders, and undead can never be trusted. Clashes over vital lumber in Ashenvale and skirmishes along territorial borders have plunged both factions into a tense, fragile cold war. Thrall actively directs campaigns against demon cults like the Burning Blade to prove the Horde has severed ties with the Legion, but deep-seated hatred threatens to reignite all-out war at any spark.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Old Horde and the New Horde?",a="The Old Horde was a blood-crazed conqueror host manipulated by the Burning Legion into invading Azeroth during the First and Second Wars. The New Horde, founded by Warchief Thrall, rejected demonic corruption, returned to ancestral shamanism, and reorganized as a defensive alliance of outcasts seeking survival, land, and spiritual renewal.",al={"old horde vs new horde","how is this horde different","is this the same horde"},it="history"},{q="Who leads the Horde?",a="The faction is led supreme by Warchief Thrall, based in the Valley of Wisdom within Orgrimmar. While Thrall directs the military and diplomatic affairs of the entire Horde, he relies on the counsel and leadership of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren, Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear, and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner of the Forsaken.",al={"who is the horde leader","horde warchief","who rules the horde"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Forsaken part of the Horde?",a="Surrounded by hostile Alliance remnants and the Scourge in Lordaeron, Sylvanas Windrunner sought allies to survive. Tauren elders argued that the Forsaken might yet be redeemed, and Thrall, though reluctant, accepted them, gaining the Horde a critical defensive foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why are undead in horde","how did forsaken join horde","tauren helped undead join"},it="ties"},{q="Where is the capital of the Horde?",a="The fortified city of Orgrimmar, built into the crags of northern Durotar in Kalimdor, serves as the Horde's administrative and military center. It houses Thrall's throne as well as enclaves for troll, tauren, and Forsaken delegates.",al={"horde capital","what is the horde main city","where does thrall rule"},it="place"},{q="Are the Horde and Alliance actively at war right now?",a="Formally, the factions exist under an uneasy truce negotiated following the Battle of Mount Hyjal. However, border conflicts over lumber in Ashenvale, territorial friction in the Alterac Mountains, and deep-seated historical hatred keep both sides locked in a simmering cold war marked by localized skirmishes.",al={"are we at war with alliance","horde alliance war classic","is it peace or war"}},{q="Do all trolls belong to the Horde?",a="No. The Darkspear tribe is the primary troll member of the Horde, having sailed to Kalimdor with Thrall. Most other troll tribes across Azeroth are hostile, though certain groups, like the Revantusk tribe of the Hinterlands, maintain a separate pact of friendship and mutual defense without formally joining.",al={"which trolls are horde","are forest trolls horde","trolls in horde"},it="people"},{q="What is the Horde?",a="The Horde is a coalition of four disparate races—orcs, tauren, Darkspear trolls, and the Forsaken—banded together for mutual survival in an unforgiving world that would see them destroyed. Reborn under Warchief Thrall after breaking free from demonic corruption, this New Horde values strength, honor, and freedom. Rather than conquering, these outcasts and spiritual allies stand united to protect their newfound homes in Kalimdor and Lordaeron from enemies on all sides.",al={"what is horde","who are the horde","horde faction explained"},it="identity",add="34e0453c"},{q="Why does the Horde fight the Alliance?",a="Deep-seated mistrust and painful history keep both sides locked in a tense cold war. The Alliance remembers the brutality of the demonic Old Horde and struggles to believe the orcs have genuinely changed, while also viewing the undead Forsaken as monstrous abominations. Territorial skirmishes, scarce resources, and old grudges flare up constantly across the world. Even though leaders like Thrall seek peace and distance from the past, the Alliance's lingering suspicion makes open conflict an ever-present reality.",al={"why hate alliance","horde alliance war reason","why fight the alliance"},it="conflict",add="b47060cc"},{q="What does it mean to serve the Horde?",a="Serving the Horde means fighting for the survival, freedom, and honor of peoples cast aside by the rest of Azeroth. You are expected to stand beside your comrades as family and answer threats with action and strength rather than empty diplomacy. Whether securing vital timber in contested forests, hunting alongside tauren braves, or defending new settlements from hostile neighbors, your blade protects an alliance of outcasts striving to carve out a permanent place in the world.",al={"serving the horde","horde duty","what does the horde expect"},it="for_you",add="93715b4a"}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The New Horde is your people's path to redemption, casting off the legacy of the demon blood to reclaim ancestral shamanic honor under Thrall's banner.",["race:Tauren"]="By standing shoulder-to-shoulder with the orcs during the centaur wars, the tribes preserved their sacred lands in Mulgore and found steadfast spiritual brothers.",["race:Troll"]="Rescued from destruction on their island home by Thrall's fleet, the Darkspear tribe pledged their spears and shadows to the survival of the Horde.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken membership within the Horde is a vital shield against the Living who would purge Lordaeron, though many in Kalimdor eye your methods with quiet caution.",["faction:Alliance"]="To human, dwarven, and elven eyes, the Horde remains an assembly of monstrous savages and treacherous corpses that cannot be trusted to keep any peace."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","darkspear","durotar","forsaken","horde","kalimdor","new horde","orgrimmar","tauren","thrall","vol'jin","warchief"},rel={"npc:thrall","zone:orgrimmar","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:vol-jin","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:old-horde","topic:alliance","quest:787"},src={"Horde"}}
E["topic:house-of-nobles"] = {t="topic",n="House of Nobles",z="general",s="The House of Nobles is the hereditary aristocratic council that helps administer the kingdom of Stormwind from Stormwind Keep. Holding immense leverage over regional land, taxation, and trade, their refusal to pay the rebuilt city's laborers ignited the bloody riots that ultimately shook the realm.",h="The House of Nobles is the hereditary aristocratic council that helps administer the kingdom of Stormwind...",sec={{t="Aristocrats of the Realm",b="Composed of Stormwind's most powerful patrician bloodlines, the House of Nobles serves as the primary advisory body to the Crown. Because the aristocracy owns the majority of the arable land throughout Elwynn, Westfall, and Duskwood, they wield outsized authority over commerce, regional magistrates, and royal decrees. While the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon officially rule the kingdom, the council routinely ties the throne's hands in red tape, asserting the power to stall trials, block executions, and meddle in provincial administration.",sp=0},{t="Bureaucratic Paralysis",b="With King Varian missing, the nobles have largely abandoned the kingdom's outlying territories to fend for themselves while exploiting royal authority for petty squabbles. Officials in Darkshire seethe because the House of Nobles countermanded their death sentence for the grave robber Dextren Ward, ordering him spared and transferred to the Stormwind Stockade instead. Similarly, the execution of the Blackrock Orc chieftain Targorr the Dread has been indefinitely delayed simply because a scheming noble wishes to use the monster as a political pawn, leaving local sentries to watch Stormwind rot from within.",sp=1},{t="Whispers of Treason",b="The council's obstinacy is far from simple greed. In truth, Lady Katrana Prestor—secretly the black dragon Onyxia—has systematically manipulated the House of Nobles to paralyze the kingdom. By stoking the aristocracy's arrogance during the stonemason negotiations and encouraging them to hoard royal funds, she orchestrated the realm's internal paralysis. High-ranking aristocrats like Lord Gregor Lescovar actively conspire alongside the Defias Brotherhood in the shadow of the throne, ensuring no imperial troops march to save the outlying towns.",sp=1,bt={"q65.7"}},{t="The Labor Revolt",b="Following the Second War, the House of Nobles contracted Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef and the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild Stormwind City from its ashes. However, the nobility simultaneously incurred staggering debts while expanding the kingdom's military frontier south into Stranglethorn. When the gleaming capital was finished, the nobles balked at the invoice and refused to pay the workers their full wages. Enraged by the betrayal, the masons rioted in the streets, prompting the nobility to banish the guild, driving the ruined workers into Westfall to form the Defias Brotherhood.",sp=1,bt={"defias.masons"}}},faq={{q="What is the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles is Stormwind's ruling aristocratic assembly, composed of prominent landowning families whose titles and seats are passed down through inheritance. They serve as advisors to the monarch and control much of the kingdom's domestic policy, taxes, and labor laws.",al={"what is the house of nobles","who are the nobles in stormwind","house of nobles lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the House of Nobles refuse to pay the Stonemasons?",a="The nobility heavily overextended Stormwind's treasury by financing costly military expeditions southward into Elwynn and Stranglethorn. When Edwin VanCleef presented the bill for rebuilding Stormwind City, the nobles could not pay, offering a fraction of what was promised and eventually ordering the guild disbanded.",al={"why didn't stonemasons get paid","why did nobles stiff vancleef","defias unpaid"},it="conflict"},{q="What happened when the Stonemasons rioted?",a="When the House of Nobles refused to pay, the stonemasons rioted in the streets of Stormwind. The nobility banished the guild from the city, and the ruined workers fled into Westfall, where they became the Defias Brotherhood.",al={"stonemason riots","what happened in the riots","why were the stonemasons banished"},sp=1,bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="Why won't Stormwind send troops to help Westfall or Duskwood?",a="The House of Nobles controls the realm's purse strings and administrative authorizations. The nobility continuously blocks funding, military deployments, and municipal aid to outlying territories, leaving local militias like the People's Militia and the Night Watch entirely isolated.",al={"why no help for duskwood","why does stormwind ignore westfall","why won't stormwind send army"},sp=1,it="motive"},{q="Who was Lord Baurles K. Wishock?",a="Lord Baurles K. Wishock is an influential noble lobbying for an investigation of the warlocks beneath The Slaughtered Lamb. The consequences of his inquiry are uncovered in the warlock quest chain.",al={"lord wishock","baurles wishock","noble murdered by warlocks"},sp=1},{q="Did all the nobles turn against the Stonemasons?",a="No. Count S. J. Erlgadin supported the masons and lobbied fiercely to improve their wages and working conditions. Erlgadin grew so disgusted by his peers' betrayal of the guild that he renounced his title and seat entirely, fleeing society to join Hemet Nesingwary's expedition in Stranglethorn.",al={"did any nobles help stonemasons","count erlgadin","nobles who supported masons"}},{q="What is the history of the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles predates the First War, representing the kingdom's wealthiest aristocratic landholders. Most of its bloodlines were nearly wiped out when the Old Horde sacked Stormwind City. After the Second War, Prince Varian Wrynn restored the assembly with ministers and advisors to oversee domestic recovery. The House oversaw the rebuilding of Stormwind by hiring the Stonemasons' Guild, but ran up heavy debts through military ventures, sparking bitter disputes, riots, and years of political paralysis.",al={"history of house of nobles","house of nobles origins","where did the nobles come from"},it="history",add="7a9e9579"},{q="Who leads the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles has no single presiding ruler; it is a hereditary council of powerful aristocrats that advises the throne. With King Varian missing, the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon hold formal executive authority, but court counselor Lady Katrana Prestor commands immense sway over the nobility. Her whispers guide much of their policy, keeping the assembly divided, fearful, and hesitant to commit royal troops to the outlying frontiers.",al={"leader of house of nobles","who controls the house of nobles","who is in charge of the nobles"},it="leader",add="f6b9ddca"}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Westfall and Duskwood, the House of Nobles represents arrogant highborn who sit comfortably in their marble palace while common folk bleed to defend their homesteads from bandits and monsters.",["class:Rogue"]="The nobility's bitter squabbles, corrupt dealings, and dirty secrets provide ample work for fixers, cutpurses, and the master cheesemonger Elling Trias.",["class:Warlock"]="Stormwind's practitioners of the dark arts beneath The Slaughtered Lamb face unwanted inquiries from influential nobles.",["faction:Alliance"]="While powerful within Stormwind City's court, the House of Nobles holds little sway over the wider Grand Alliance, though their stinginess constantly threatens the southern human territories' stability."},kw={"baurles wishock","defias","erlgadin","gregor lescovar","house of nobles","katrana prestor","nobles","stonemasons guild","vancleef"},rel={"topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:stonemasons-guild","topic:stormwind-kingdom","subzone:stormwind-keep","npc:edwin-vancleef","npc:onyxia","npc:lord-baurles-k-wishock","quest:434"},src={"House of Nobles"}}
E["topic:human"] = {t="topic",n="Human",z="general",s="Humans are an adaptable and resilient mortal race who have shaped much of the Eastern Kingdoms' history. Once unified under the ancient Empire of Arathor, their civilization fractured into the Seven Kingdoms, with Stormwind now standing as humanity's primary surviving bastion within the Alliance.",h="Humans are an adaptable and resilient mortal race who have shaped much of the Eastern Kingdoms' history.",sec={{t="The Empire of Arathor",b="Millennia ago, early humans lived as scattered, nomadic tribes constantly threatened by the Amani forest trolls. Recognizing that disunity would mean extinction, Warlord Thoradin of the Arathi tribe united the disparate clans through diplomacy and conquest, establishing the Empire of Arathor with its fortress capital at Strom. During the Troll Wars, the beleaguered high elves of Quel'Thalas allied with Arathor, teaching one hundred humans the arts of arcane magic. Together, they broke the troll armies, securing humanity's dominion over the central continents and laying the foundation for future mortal alliances.",sp=0},{t="Fracture into Seven Kingdoms",b="As Arathor grew secure and prosperous, its outlying settlements became powerful, self-sufficient city-states. Over time, the centralized empire splintered into seven distinct realms: Gilneas, Alterac, Dalaran, Lordaeron, Kul Tiras, Stormwind, and Stromgarde (the original home of Strom). While these nations traded vigorously with dwarves, gnomes, and elves, peace was fragile. Territorial disputes, border skirmishes—such as the perennial mountain feuds between Alterac and Stromgarde—and courtly intrigue consumed the kingdoms, creating deep rivalries that persisted until external catastrophes forced their hand.",sp=0},{t="Invasion and Ruin",b="The arrival of the orcish Horde through the Dark Portal shattered humanity's internal squabbles. Stormwind was razed during the First War, its refugees fleeing north to warn the other realms. In response, King Terenas Menethil II helped forge the Alliance of Lordaeron, combining the strength of mankind, dwarves, gnomes, and high elves to defeat the Horde in the Second War. Though Stormwind was painstakingly rebuilt, tragedy struck again when the Scourge plague ravaged Lordaeron and Dalaran fell during the Third War, leaving the northern human realms in smoking ruin.",sp=0},{t="A Precarious Present",b="Today, Stormwind stands as the strongest remaining human kingdom and the linchpin of the grand Alliance. However, peace remains elusive. King Varian Wrynn has mysteriously vanished while en route to a diplomatic summit, leaving his ten-year-old son, Anduin Wrynn, crowned king while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon serves as regent. To the north, the survivors of Lordaeron are scattered or dead, while Gilneas hides behind its colossal Greymane Wall, leaving Stormwind to bear the weight of mankind's defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What were the Seven Kingdoms of humanity?",a="The Seven Kingdoms arose when the ancient Empire of Arathor fractured into independent city-states: Lordaeron, Stormwind, Stromgarde, Dalaran, Kul Tiras, Gilneas, and Alterac. Each developed its own culture, from the seafaring fleets of Kul Tiras to the arcane colleges of Dalaran.",al={"seven human kingdoms","human nations","arathor kingdoms"}},{q="Where did humans originally come from?",a="Humans emerged thousands of years ago as nomadic tribes roaming the forests and highlands of the northern Eastern Kingdoms, particularly around Tirisfal Glades, before uniting into the first human nation under Warlord Thoradin.",al={"human origin","ancient humans","human ancestors"}},{q="What happened to the kingdom of Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was devastated during the Third War by the Scourge's plague of undeath and betrayed by its own prince, Arthas Menethil. Today, its ruined capital and surrounding lands are contested between the undead Forsaken, the Scourge, and remnants like the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"why is lordaeron destroyed","fall of lordaeron","fate of lordaeron"}},{q="Who currently rules humanity and Stormwind?",a="With King Varian Wrynn missing under suspicious circumstances, his young son Anduin Wrynn sits upon the throne as a figurehead, while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon rules as Regent alongside royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"who leads stormwind","king of stormwind","stormwind ruler"}},{q="Why did the high elves teach humans magic?",a="During the ancient Troll Wars, the high elves of Quel'Thalas faced annihilation by the Amani trolls. Desperate for military reinforcements, they made a treaty with Thoradin, ruler of Arathor, agreeing to teach one hundred humans the arcane in exchange for human armies.",al={"elves taught humans magic","first human mages","troll wars magic"}},{q="What role did humans play in forming the Alliance?",a="After Stormwind fell to the orcs in the First War, human refugees rallied the northern kingdoms. Under King Terenas of Lordaeron and Lord Lothar of Stormwind, the human realms united with Ironforge dwarves, gnomes, and high elves to create the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"formation of alliance","alliance of lordaeron origin","how did alliance start"}},{q="Which classes can humans be?",a="Humans train as warriors, paladins, hunters, rogues, priests, mages and warlocks.",al={"human classes","what classes can humans be","can humans be hunters"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="As a human, you inherit the legacy of fallen Arathor and ruined Lordaeron; the defense and survival of the mortal realms rests squarely on the shoulders of Stormwind.",["faction:Alliance"]="Human diplomacy and resolve forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, and Stormwind remains the faction's political and military heart.",["class:Mage"]="Human wizardry dates back to the Troll Wars, when the high elves taught the first One Hundred human mages, eventually leading to the founding of Dalaran.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were founded by human holy men and knights in Lordaeron to combat the horrors of the Second War.",["faction:Horde"]="Humans were the first enemies the orcs encountered upon crossing through the Dark Portal, instigating three brutal wars for survival on Azeroth."},kw={"alliance","arathor","human","lordaeron","mankind","stormwind","thoradin","troll wars","wrynn"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:alliance","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:third-war","topic:lordaeron","zone:stormwind"},src={"Human"}}
E["topic:hunter"] = {t="topic",n="Hunter",z="general",s="Masters of wilderness survival and deadly marksmanship, hunters roam the untamed reaches of Azeroth alongside fierce beast companions. Armed with bows, rifles, or crossbows, they excel at harassing enemies from a distance while relying on clever traps and primal aspects to control the flow of battle. Whether tracking elusive quarry across snowcapped peaks or stalking prey through dense forests, they live by their wits and connection to nature.",h="Masters of wilderness survival and deadly marksmanship, hunters roam the untamed reaches of Azeroth alongside...",sec={{t="Kinship with the Wild",b="At the heart of every hunter lies an instinctual empathy for the primal world. Unlike those who exploit nature or isolate themselves within stone bastions, hunters immerse themselves entirely in untamed lands. They study the habits, movements, and strengths of predatory beasts, earning their respect rather than breaking their spirits. Through patient bonding, a hunter tames dangerous creatures—such as fierce prowling cats, stubborn mountain bears, and sharp-eyed raptors—turning wild animals into steadfast allies that defend them to the death.",sp=0},{t="Master Marksmen and Trackers",b="On the battlefield, hunters dictate the terms of engagement from a safe distance. Armed with bows, heavy crossbows, or calibrated rifles, they unleash deadly volleys that pierce through heavy armor before an attacker can even draw near. Their finely honed senses make them peerless trackers, capable of tracing the faintest footsteps, scents, and disturbances left by quarry. When foes attempt to close the distance, hunters deploy frost and flame traps, disorienting ambushes, or agile evasive maneuvers to keep enemies right where they want them.",sp=0},{t="Traditions Across Cultures",b="The path of the hunter manifests differently among the various cultures of Azeroth. Night elf Sentinels embody ancient marksmanship and stealth beneath the canopy of Ashenvale, utilizing bows and shadowcraft in quiet harmony with the wilderness. Dwarven riflemen brave the freezing crags of Dun Morogh alongside hardy bears, mastering the engineering of gunpowder. Across Kalimdor, Tauren and Orc trackers honor ancient nomadic customs, running down their prey with sacred respect and calling upon bestial spirits to guide their shots. In Elwynn Forest, young humans learn the trade from seasoned trackers like the dwarf Tordrin Sternblade in Northshire Valley and Josephine Carson in Goldshire, and the Skyborne learn to track and tame the beasts of Zephras Isle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What weapons do hunters use?",a="Hunters specialize in ranged weapons, including bows, crossbows, and rifles. In close quarters, they are also skilled in wielding melee weapons such as polearms, staves, axes, and daggers, often engaging in dual-wielding or using traps to create space and resume firing from a distance.",al={"hunter weapons","what do hunters equip","hunter bows rifles"},gp=true},{q="How do hunters tame beasts?",a="Hunters rely on an innate empathy and deep understanding of wild creatures. By observing their behavior, approaching without malice, and demonstrating strength and respect, they can soothe dangerous predators and form a lifelong companion bond, training them to fight at their side.",al={"how to tame pets","beast taming","tame animal"},gp=true},{q="What armor do hunters wear?",a="Hunters begin their journeys wearing light, flexible leather armor to maintain their agility while traversing rugged terrain. As they grow in experience and face deadlier threats, they learn to wear durable mail armor, providing greater protection without severely impeding their movement.",al={"hunter armor type","can hunters wear mail","hunter gear"},gp=true},{q="What are hunter aspects?",a="Aspects are primal techniques where a hunter attunes their senses and physical reflexes to the innate strengths of wild creatures. By invoking these aspects, a hunter can run with the fleetness of a cheetah, focus their aim like a hawk, or adopt the defensive resilience of a monkey.",al={"hunter aspects","aspect of the cheetah","hunter animal aspects"},gp=true},{q="What happens if an enemy gets too close to a hunter?",a="Hunters are at a distinct disadvantage at close range, where firing a ranged weapon becomes difficult. To survive, they rely on frost and immolation traps, concussive tools, and melee strikes to slow, wing, or disorient attackers while they or their beasts break away to establish distance.",al={"hunter deadzone","close combat hunter","hunter melee"},gp=true},{q="Which races practice the ways of the hunter?",a="Hunting is practiced across nearly all cultures, though it takes varied forms. In the Alliance, night elves, dwarves and humans take up the bow and rifle. In the Horde, orcs, tauren and trolls revere tracking and beast mastery as ancestral traditions of survival. The Skyborne train hunters on both sides, while gnomes and the Forsaken do not walk the hunter's path.",al={"who can be a hunter","hunter races","playable hunter races","can humans be hunters","human hunter"},gp=true},{q="What is a Hunter in the lore?",a="Hunters are masters of the wilderness, expert marksmen, and rugged trackers who heed the call of the wild. Uniquely attuned to nature, they survive in uncharted territories by their wits and knowledge of the creatures they pursue. While deadly with bows, crossbows, rifles, and traps, a hunter is defined by a deep empathy for beasts, forging lifelong bonds with feral companions who fight and scout alongside them against the encroaching threats of the world.",al={"what is a hunter","hunter lore","hunter class lore"},it="identity",add="b60a9390"},{q="Where does a Hunter's power come from?",a="A hunter's prowess comes from an intimate connection to the natural world, rigorous martial training, and keen observation of wild quarry. Rather than drawing upon arcane study or divine worship, they channel strength through attuning themselves to the primal aspects of wild beasts. Combined with unmatched marksmanship, herbal venoms, and an innate kinship that commands loyalty from ferocious predators, their power lies in complete harmony with the wilderness.",al={"where do hunters get their power","hunter source of power","hunter magic source"},it="power",add="49aa042d"},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to become a Hunter?",a="Stepping out as a hunter means answering an inner call to leave civilized comforts behind for the untamed wilds. You rely entirely on your cunning, your keen senses, and your ranged weapons to survive alone in hostile lands. As you learn to read trails and set ambushes, you will earn the respect of a wild beast, gaining a loyal partner to guard your back as you defend the wilderness from corruption and exploitation.",al={"becoming a hunter","what does it mean to be a hunter","starting as a hunter"},it="for_you",add="fa19871b"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Your strength lies in your connection to the wilds and your companion beast. Master your ranged arsenal, maintain distance, and let your pet hold the enemy's attention.",["race:NightElf"]="For millennia, Kaldorei Sentinels have patrolled ancient forests with bows and trusted sabers, defending sacred groves from outside corruption.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven marksmen are renowned for their marksmanship with heavy rifles, braving the treacherous mountain passes of Khaz Modan alongside fierce polar bears.",["race:Tauren"]="To the Tauren, the hunt is a sacred spiritual trial of endurance and reverence, honoring the Earth Mother by respecting every beast that feeds and clothes the tribe.",["race:Orc"]="Orcish beastmasters honor ancestral survival traditions forged on harsh worlds, forming unbreakable combat bonds with loyal wolves and boars.",["race:Human"]="Human hunters learn their trade in Elwynn Forest from seasoned trackers like the dwarf Tordrin Sternblade in Northshire Valley and Josephine Carson in Goldshire.",["race:Skyborne"]="Skyborne hunters learn to track and tame the beasts of Zephras Isle, a floating land adrift in Skywall."},kw={"archery","aspects","beasts","hunter","marksmanship","pet","rifle","survival","tracking","traps"},rel={"topic:sentinels","topic:tauren","topic:dwarf","topic:night-elf","topic:horde","topic:alliance","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:gnome","topic:human","topic:skyborne"},src={"Hunter"}}
E["topic:juggernaught"] = {t="topic",n="Juggernaught",z="general",s="A Juggernaught is an enormous, heavily armored battleship bristling with heavy naval cannons. Originally engineered by ogres to serve as the iron core of the Old Horde's fleet during the Second War, these floating fortresses were built to pulverize coastal strongholds and trade routes. Their massive iron plating and siege-grade broadsides make them legendary instruments of naval warfare.",h="A Juggernaught is an enormous, heavily armored battleship bristling with heavy naval cannons.",sec={{t="Terror of the Second War",b="Orcs were not traditionally a seafaring culture, so when the Horde required a fleet capable of crossing the Great Sea and contesting the Alliance's naval superiority during the Second War, they relied heavily on ogre ingenuity. The resulting craft—the ogre juggernaught—became the backbone of the Horde armada. Though vastly slower and less nimble than swift troll destroyers, these behemoths carried immense plating and devastating broadside batteries that could shatter Alliance hulls and smash shore fortifications to rubble.",sp=0},{t="Ironclad Cove",b="Hidden beneath the hills of Westfall, deep within the subterranean caverns of the Deadmines, lies Ironclad Cove. There, secluded away from prying eyes and royal inspectors, Edwin VanCleef's craftsmen labored secretly to construct a fully armed juggernaught. By assembling a heavy warship in an underground cavern connected directly to the open sea, the Defias planned to bypass Stormwind's overland defenses and launch a surprise naval strike right into the heart of the kingdom.",sp=1,bt={"defias.hideout"}}},faq={{q="What is a juggernaught?",a="A juggernaught is a massive, heavily armored naval battleship armed with heavy bombardment cannons. Developed originally by the Horde with ogre aid during the Second War, it functions as a floating fortress built to overpower enemy fleets and shatter coastal defenses.",al={"juggernaut warcraft","orc battleship","ogre juggernaut"},it="identity"},{q="Why is there a huge ship inside the Deadmines?",a="Edwin VanCleef and the Defias Brotherhood used the secluded cavern of Ironclad Cove inside the Deadmines as a hidden shipyard. Constructing the juggernaught underground kept it safe from Alliance patrols while they worked on the vessel.",al={"ship in deadmines","why boat in mines","deadmines ship"},sp=1,it="why_here",bt={"defias.hideout"}},{q="What did Edwin VanCleef intend to do with the ship?",a="The ship is part of VanCleef's activities beneath Westfall. Investigating the Defias Brotherhood reveals what he intended for it.",al={"vancleef boat plan","why did defias build a ship","defias juggernaught purpose"},sp=1,it="motive"},{q="Who originally invented the juggernaught?",a="The Old Horde built juggernaughts with the help of ogres during the Second War. The orcs had little seafaring experience, and these massive warships became the heavy armament of their fleet.",al={"who made juggernauts","ogre ships lore","horde navy origin"},it="origin"},{q="How does the ship get out of the Deadmines cavern?",a="Ironclad Cove connects to the open waters of the Great Sea through a concealed coastal channel along the rocky shores of Westfall, permitting the warship to sail directly out into the ocean once completed.",al={"how does deadmines ship leave","ironclad cove exit","deadmines water exit"},sp=1},{q="Does the Horde still use juggernaughts?",a="Orcish battleships were still known as juggernaughts during the Third War. Their role was to provide the heavy firepower of the Horde fleet; the current deployment of such vessels is uncertain.",al={"modern horde juggernauts","horde battleships"},it="today"},{q="What is the history of the juggernaught?",a="Juggernaughts were first constructed during the Second War to serve as the iron core of the Old Horde's naval armada. Because the orcs possessed little naval tradition, they relied heavily on ogre craftsmanship and brute force to fashion these gargantuan floating fortresses. Bristling with heavy cannons and thick armor, they terrorized the seas of Azeroth and inflicted devastating losses upon the Alliance fleet. Though many were sunk or abandoned following the Horde's ultimate defeat, surviving vessels and salvageable designs continue to be prized by outlaws and warlords alike.",al={"juggernaught history","where did juggernaughts come from","second war juggernaughts"},it="history",add="f4436d36"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The discovery of a juggernaught under construction beneath Westfall exposes a grave threat to the safety of Stormwind and King Anduin's court.",["faction:Horde"]="Juggernaughts represent a storied chapter of the Horde's naval heritage from the Second War, proving that raw brute engineering could contest Alliance mastery of the seas.",["race:Human"]="Stormwind's rebuilders, betrayed and turned outlaw under VanCleef, applied their finest architectural and stonework skills into assembling a monstrous warship hidden beneath the hills of Westfall.",["class:Rogue"]="The juggernaught in the Deadmines represents the ultimate audacity of Edwin VanCleef—crafting a capital battleship right beneath the noses of SI:7 and the Stormwind guard."},kw={"battleship","deadmines","defias","ironclad cove","juggernaught","juggernaut","ogre juggernaught","ship","stormwind","vancleef"},rel={"subzone:ironclad-cove","zone:deadmines","npc:edwin-vancleef","topic:defias-brotherhood","zone:stormwind","topic:second-war","topic:horde","quest:65"},src={"Juggernaught"}}
E["topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand"] = {t="topic",n="Knights of the Silver Hand",z="general",s="The Knights of the Silver Hand are the legendary order of holy paladins founded after the First War to combine martial combat with the healing and smiting power of the Holy Light. Though their original northern chapter was broken during the fall of Lordaeron, surviving knights carry on their sacred vows in Stormwind and Ironforge, while scattered remnants battle the undead in the northern Plaguelands.",h="The Knights of the Silver Hand are the legendary order of holy paladins founded after the First War to...",sec={{t="Creation of the Paladins",b="Following the destruction of Stormwind during the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol recognized a dire flaw in the Alliance's defenses: the clerics of Northshire were devoted and skilled healers, but they lacked martial training and fell easily on the front lines. To ensure holy warriors could endure the brutal combat of the Second War, Faol and his premier student, Uther the Lightbringer, founded the Order of the Knights of the Silver Hand. Faol recruited both battle-hardened knights willing to learn devotion to the Light and devout clerics willing to bear plate armor and warhammers.",sp=0},{t="Glory in the Second War",b="Led by Uther the Lightbringer, the first paladins proved instrumental to the Alliance of Lordaeron. Riding into combat, the paladins lifted the morale of weary soldiers and countered the sinister necromancy of the Horde's death knights. Throughout the war, the Silver Hand broke peasant rebellions, reclaimed Caer Darrow, and helped defend Capital City against the siege of the orcs. In the years of peace that followed, the paladins guarded Lordaeron's borders, settled civil disputes, and served as shining exemplars of honor, humility, and justice.",sp=0},{t="The Fall and Fractured Remnants",b="Tragedy struck the order from within during the Third War. Prince Arthas Menethil, a decorated knight of the Silver Hand, clashed bitterly with his mentor Uther outside Stratholme and unlawfully suspended the paladins when Uther refused to butcher the infected citizenry. When Arthas later succumbed to corruption and led the Scourge against Lordaeron, Uther was slain, shattering the order's central leadership. The surviving knights in the north fractured under differing doctrines, eventually birthing the zealous Scarlet Crusade and the vigilant Argent Dawn, while the surviving chapters in Stormwind and Ironforge continue to train new paladins under the classic banner of the Silver Hand.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who founded the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order was created by Archbishop Alonsus Faol following the First War, with Uther the Lightbringer chosen as its very first knight.",al={"who made the silver hand","founder of paladins","who created paladins"},it="origin"},{q="Does the Silver Hand still exist?",a="Yes. While its northern high command was decimated during the Scourge's destruction of Lordaeron, thriving chapters continue to serve the Alliance out of Stormwind's Cathedral of Light and the dwarven halls of Ironforge.",al={"is silver hand alive","silver hand today","are paladins still around"},it="today"},{q="How are the Scarlet Crusade and Argent Dawn related to the Silver Hand?",a="After the death of Uther and Highlord Alexandros Mograine, the surviving paladins in Lordaeron fractured over ideological differences. Hardline fanatics formed the Scarlet Crusade to eliminate all outsiders, while more moderate veterans founded the Argent Dawn.",al={"scarlet crusade silver hand","argent dawn silver hand","how did the silver hand split"},it="ties"},{q="Can non-humans join the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="Yes. While initially composed of human knights and clerics from Lordaeron, dwarven priests and warriors of Ironforge were welcomed into the order during and after the Second War, bearing the Silver Hand's insignia and rites.",al={"dwarf paladins silver hand","who can be a silver hand paladin","can dwarves be silver hand"},it="residents"},{q="What is the Order of the Silver Hand?",a="The Knights of the Silver Hand are an order of holy warriors, commonly known as paladins. Formed as the martial branch of the Church of the Holy Light, they blend martial prowess with divine power. Armed with heavy plate, warhammers, and sacred incantations, they serve as paragons of virtue, dedicated to protecting the innocent, healing the wounded, and vanquishing evil.",al={"what are the knights of the silver hand","who are the silver hand","paladins of the silver hand"},it="identity",add="f98dc644"},{q="What is the history of the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="Archbishop Alonsus Faol established the order during the Second War after the clergy suffered heavy losses in the First War. He sought to create champions who could fight on the front lines while channeling the Light. Under Uther the Lightbringer, the paladins turned the tide against the Horde. For decades, they defended Lordaeron until Prince Arthas shattered their ranks during the Third War.",al={"history of silver hand","how did the silver hand start","silver hand origin story"},it="history",add="a6d23619"},{q="Who leads the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order lacks a single supreme leader. Lord Uther the Lightbringer originally commanded the knights until his tragic demise at the hands of Arthas. Highlord Alexandros Mograine briefly rallied the survivors in Lordaeron until his assassination. Today, the remnants in Lordaeron have splintered, while paladin leaders in Stormwind and Ironforge guide the brothers fighting under the Alliance banner.",al={"silver hand leader","who commands the silver hand","leader of paladins"},it="leader",add="1db4f553"},{q="Who are the Knights of the Silver Hand allied with?",a="The Silver Hand was established to defend humanity and fight for the Grand Alliance. While its original northern chapter fell into ruin during the Third War, active chapters in Stormwind and Ironforge remain staunch champions of the Alliance of Lordaeron's successor nations, fighting alongside humans, dwarves, and high elves against threats across Azeroth.",al={"who does the silver hand fight for","silver hand factions","silver hand alliance"},it="allies",add="0d7dcf69"}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Every human and dwarven paladin serving the Alliance traces their sacred oaths, librams, and martial tradition directly back to the laws laid down by Archbishop Faol and Uther the Lightbringer.",["class:Priest"]="The Silver Hand was born out of the priesthood's vulnerability during the First War, bridging the gap between divine ministry and heavy warfare.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were the ideological anchor of the Alliance of Lordaeron, inspiring soldiers across every human realm to stand firm against darkness.",["race:Human"]="Humanity views the Silver Hand as the pinnacle of virtue, honor, and courage, mourning the fall of its great northern masters while honoring their memory in Stormwind."},kw={"alonsus faol","arthas","holy light","paladin","silver hand","stratholme","tirion fordring","turalyon","uther"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:light","npc:tirion-fordring","subzone:stratholme","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:second-war","topic:argent-dawn","topic:scarlet-crusade"},src={"Knights of the Silver Hand"}}
E["topic:kobold"] = {t="topic",n="Kobold",z="general",s="In Azeroth's mines and caverns, kobolds guard the candles fixed to their heads. Small and rat-like, they dig in the dark and defend their tunnels in numbers.",h="In Azeroth's mines and caverns, kobolds guard the candles fixed to their heads.",sec={{t="Miners of the Dark",b="Kobolds stand barely four feet tall. Filthy scavengers, they spend their lives delving underground. Their low-light vision fails in the deepest shafts, so each kobold fixes a crude candle to its head. Alone, one is cowardly when cornered. Yet kobolds breed in massive numbers and work with relentless energy. When agitated, their rat-like tails whip as they rush intruders in frantic swarms. They wield heavy iron mining picks and beat foes to death with them.",sp=0},{t="You No Take Candle!",b="Kobolds fear pitch-black tunnels with a deep, primal terror. To them, a candle is more than light. Its flame shields them from a darkness they believe will consume them.",sp=0},{t="Subterranean Pests of the Kingdoms",b="Across the Eastern Kingdoms, kobolds often overrun working mines. In Elwynn Forest, a clan displaced from the Redridge Mountains seized the Echo Ridge, Fargodeep, and Jasperlode mines. Riverpaw gnolls and the Defias Brotherhood helped drive them there. In Loch Modan, the Tunnel Rat clan seized the Silver Stream Mine from the dwarven Miners' League.\n\nKobolds also plague industrial digs in Kalimdor. They labor for the Venture Company in Stonetalon and uncover strange minerals in Thousand Needles.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do kobolds care so much about their candles?",a="Kobolds feel deep, paralyzing terror at complete darkness. Their folklore treats it as a predator that snuffs out life and swallows miners whole. They guard their candles as their only defense.",al={"why candles","kobold candles","you no take candle meaning"},it="motive"},{q="Where do kobolds come from?",a="Kobolds' true origins are unknown. They have dwelt in tunnels and caverns beneath Azeroth for as long as anyone remembers, wherever earth can be dug.",al={"kobold origins","where do kobolds come from","kobold evolution"},it="origin"},{q="Are kobolds allied with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Kobolds were not willing partners of the Defias Brotherhood. In Elwynn Forest and Westfall, Riverpaw gnolls largely bullied and enslaved them. The Defias hired those gnolls to disrupt mining and secure mineral wealth.",al={"kobold defias","are kobolds working for defias","fargodeep defias"},it="allies"},{q="How do kobolds choose their leaders?",a="Kobolds have no single ruler. Each clan answers to its own bosses, including taskmasters who drive the diggers to work in the mines they claim.",al={"kobold leader","kobold boss","how do kobolds pick chiefs"},it="leader"},{q="Are kobolds found in Kalimdor as well as the Eastern Kingdoms?",a="Yes. Kobolds infested the underground caverns beneath Orgrimmar during the city's founding. They also work in the Venture Company's Windshear Mine in the Stonetalon Mountains and burrow throughout Thousand Needles.",al={"kobolds in kalimdor","stonetalon kobolds","orgrimmar kobolds"},it="place"},{q="What do kobolds eat?",a="Little is recorded about kobold diets. They are filthy scavengers who live on whatever they find in and around their tunnels.",al={"kobold diet","what do kobolds eat"}},{q="What are kobolds?",a="Kobolds are diminutive, rat-like subterranean humanoids standing barely four feet tall. Infamous for their cowardice and foul odor, they make their homes tunneling through caverns and abandoned mines. While individually weak and fearful of larger races, they reproduce rapidly and rely on overwhelming numbers when forced into a fight, swarming intruders with crude mining picks.",al={"what is a kobold","who are the kobolds","kobold info"},it="identity",add="31a27fce"},{q="Why are kobolds found infesting so many mines?",a="Kobolds are natural diggers and miners who prefer subterranean dens far from populated surface settlements. They naturally flock to pre-existing caves, quarries, and rich mineral seams. When humanoid settlements leave excavations unattended or lightly defended—such as the mines across Elwynn, Loch Modan, and Stonetalon—opportunistic kobolds quickly burrow in, expand the tunnels, and claim the veins for themselves.",al={"why are kobolds in mines","kobold mines why","where do kobolds live"},it="why_here",add="427afc17"},{q="Why are kobolds hostile to travelers?",a="Kobolds are skittish, paranoid, and distrustful of anything larger than themselves. Deep underground, they constantly fear cave-ins, starvation, and the absolute dark. When outsiders enter their territory, kobolds perceive them as an immediate threat come to rob their claims or snuff out their precious head-candles, driving them to attack in frantic, desperate mobs.",al={"why do kobolds attack","are kobolds always hostile","why do kobolds fight"},it="hostile",add="840836f4"},{q="What is known about the history of kobolds?",a="Kobolds have inhabited the tunnels beneath Azeroth for as long as anyone remembers, though their true origins are unknown. Largely splintered into local clans, they regularly collide with other races, such as when goblin workers digging beneath Orgrimmar during its construction accidentally broke into a kobold den.",al={"kobold history","how long have kobolds existed","where did kobolds come from history"},it="history",add="d2531c8b"}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="As a miner in human or dwarven lands, you will inevitably encounter kobold infestations. Kobolds gravitate toward rich veins of copper, tin, and silver.",["race:Human"]="For the Kingdom of Stormwind, kobolds are a constant economic headache. They have overrun vital local operations at Fargodeep, Jasperlode, and Echo Ridge.",["race:Dwarf"]="As a dwarf, you know the Miners' League regularly employs adventurers to clear Tunnel Rat kobolds from mountain digs like the Silver Stream Mine in Loch Modan.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you know kobold warrens beneath the dry earth caused chaos during Orgrimmar's founding. Clans like the Gravelsnouts still harass frontier outposts in Kalimdor."},kw={"candle","echo ridge","fargodeep","jasperlode","kobold","silver stream","trogg","tunnel rat","windshear"},rel={"quest:60","subzone:fargodeep-mine","subzone:jasperlode-mine","subzone:silver-stream-mine","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:trogg","quest:21"},src={"Kobold"}}
E["topic:kolkar-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Kolkar clan",z="general",s="The Kolkar are an aggressive clan of centaur native to central Kalimdor, claiming descent from the legendary first Khan, Kolk. They maintain strongholds in Desolace and across the Barrens, frequently raiding Horde settlements and fighting anyone who crosses their path.",h="The Kolkar are an aggressive clan of centaur native to central Kalimdor, claiming descent from the legendary...",sec={{t="Sons of Kolk",b="The Kolkar take their name from Khan Kolk, the legendary first centaur khan born from the union of Zaetar and Theradras. Driven by a deep hatred for other races and their fellow centaur clans alike, the Kolkar distinguish themselves by their red markings and an absolute refusal to form alliances. Historically based out of their ancient village in eastern Desolace, the restless spirit of Khan Kolk still wanders the subterranean depths of their ancestral lands.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge of the Barrens",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren endured brutal raids from the Kolkar as they traversed the arid Barrens. When the orcs arrived on Kalimdor during the Third War, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof to push the marauding centaur back and escort the tauren to safety in Mulgore. Defeat did not break the clan; instead, it fueled a lingering blood feud. The Kolkar entrenched themselves around all three vital water sources in the Barrens: the Forgotten Pools, the Lushwater Oasis, and the Stagnant Oasis.",sp=0},{t="Encroachment on Durotar",b="Emboldened by their fortified positions across the savanna, the Kolkar have pushed eastward past the Southfury River into Durotar itself. From Kolkar Crag in the south, raiding parties plot assaults against Sen'jin Village, the Valley of Trials, and the paths approaching Orgrimmar. While senior Horde leadership focuses on larger regional threats, young recruits are dispatched to cull the centaur advance before their raiding parties can strike the nascent orc capital.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Kolkar centaur?",a="The Kolkar are one of five major centaur clans in Kalimdor. Named after their founding leader, Khan Kolk, they are a fierce and xenophobic tribe recognized by their red armor. They maintain settlements in Desolace, control the Barrens' primary water oases, and run raiding camps in southern Durotar.",al={"kolkar clan","who are kolkar","kolkar lore","centaur in barrens"},it="identity"},{q="Why do the Kolkar hate the Horde so much?",a="The Kolkar hunted and harassed the tauren for generations across the Barrens. When Thrall's Horde arrived, the orcs joined Cairne Bloodhoof to drive the centaur back so the tauren could settle Mulgore. This alliance humiliated the Kolkar Khans, turning centuries of predatory hunting into a bitter war against both orcs and tauren.",al={"why do kolkar attack orcs","kolkar tauren feud","centaur vs horde"},it="hostile"},{q="Why are the Kolkar occupying the Barrens oases?",a="In the arid Barrens, controlling water means controlling survival. The Kolkar have occupied the Forgotten Pools, Lushwater Oasis, and Stagnant Oasis with command tents, tribal totems, and hyena packs, denying these precious watering holes to travelers, merchants, and the Horde.",al={"kolkar at oases","forgotten pools centaur","lushwater oasis centaur"},it="why_here"},{q="Who leads the Kolkar clan?",a="The clan's first ruler was Khan Kolk, whose spirit lingers in Maraudon. In recent times, the clan was led by Khan Dez'hepah and his officers from the ancient Kolkar Village in Desolace, while the Barrens camps answer to leaders such as Hezrul Bloodmark. Other khans, like the fallen Khan Gragtor, led warbands farther afield.",al={"kolkar leader","kolkar khan","who is the kolkar boss"},it="leader"},{q="What is Kolkar Crag?",a="Kolkar Crag is a rocky canyon outpost in southern Durotar. Centaur warbands established it as a forward staging ground to harass Horde initiates in the Valley of Trials, menace Sen'jin Village, and test the defenses of Orgrimmar.",al={"where is kolkar crag","durotar centaur camp","kolkar in durotar"},it="place"},{q="Do the Kolkar work with other centaur clans?",a="No. The Kolkar share no kinship or treaties with the other four centaur clans native to Desolace. Centaur society is violently fragmented, and the Kolkar wage war against other clans just as readily as they fight the Horde and Alliance.",al={"centaur clans alliance","kolkar allies","do centaur cooperate"},it="allies"},{q="Where did the Kolkar clan come from?",a="The Kolkar are one of the original centaur clans born in Desolace, tracing their bloodline to Khan Kolk, the son of Zaetar and Princess Theradras. Long before the Third War, their roaming warbands pushed east into the Barrens, violently driving the nomadic tauren away from their sacred hunting grounds. When Thrall's newly arrived orcs allied with Cairne Bloodhoof, the united Horde shattered the centaur army and opened the way for the tauren to settle Mulgore. Now fractured across Desolace, Durotar, and the Barrens, the Kolkar nurse a bitter grudge and wage constant territorial wars to reclaim their lost dominance.",al={"kolkar history","origin of kolkar","kolkar clan origins"},it="history",add="0aa5759f"},{q="How did the Kolkar clan get their name?",a="The clan takes its name from its founding father, Khan Kolk. As the legendary eldest son of the demigod Zaetar and the earth elemental Princess Theradras, Kolk stood as the first of the great centaur khans who led their brutish descendants out of Maraudon and claimed the barren reaches of central Kalimdor.",al={"why are they called kolkar","kolkar name origin","who is kolk"},it="name",add="a5b3ba08"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Kolkar hunted your people nearly to extinction across the Barrens before the orcs intervened, making every encounter with them a matter of ancestral survival.",["race:Orc"]="Your first pact with the tauren was forged in blood against Kolkar marauders, and their scouts at Kolkar Crag now threaten the Valley of Trials where young orcs come of age.",["faction:Horde"]="Kolkar control over the three Barrens oases cuts off critical trade and water supplies between Durotar, Mulgore, and the Crossroads.",["class:Hunter"]="Kolkar raiding parties rely heavily on trained, semi-domesticated hyenas to track intruders and guard the perimeters of their watering holes."},kw={"barrens","centaur","desolace","dezhepah","durotar","khan","kolk","kolkar","kolkar crag","maraudon","oasis"},rel={"topic:centaur","zone:barrens","zone:desolace","subzone:kolkar-crag","topic:tauren","topic:horde","quest:1362","subzone:lushwater-oasis"},src={"Kolkar clan"}}
E["topic:kul-tiras"] = {t="topic",n="Kul Tiras",z="general",s="Kul Tiras is an insular maritime human kingdom situated on an island archipelago in the Great Sea, west of Dun Morogh and Baradin Bay. Renowned for possessing the mightiest navy in Azeroth, it served as a cornerstone of the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War under Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. Following the fracturing of the old Alliance and tragic losses in Kalimdor, the island realm has largely receded into isolated waters.",h="Kul Tiras is an insular maritime human kingdom situated on an island archipelago in the Great Sea, west of...",sec={{t="A Nation Born of the Sea",b="Kul Tiras blossomed into an unmatched seafaring power, sustained by an industrious merchant marine, rich fishing grounds, and a naval armada without peer in human history.",sp=0},{t="The Second War and Isolation",b="When the Orcish Horde swept through the Eastern Kingdoms, Kul Tiras answered the call of King Terenas Menethil II and committed its armada to the Alliance of Lordaeron. Lord Admiral Daelin Proudmoore commanded the naval forces of humanity, waging bloody warfare across the open ocean to sever the Horde's sea lanes and protect coastal shores. However, following the war's conclusion and the eventual splintering of the northern kingdoms, Kul Tiras pulled back its fleets, withdrawing into heavy coastal defense and quiet insularity.",sp=0},{t="Shadows over the Proudmoores",b="Kul Tiras has felt the bitter sting of distant wars across the sea. When Lady Jaina Proudmoore led refugees across the ocean to Kalimdor to build Theramore, her father, Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore, sailed to Kalimdor in search of her, then launched a campaign against the Horde. His crusade brought ruin, culminating in his death at Theramore.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is Kul Tiras located?",a="Kul Tiras is an archipelago situated out in the Great Sea, west of Baradin Bay and the coast of Dun Morogh. It is a rugged, cold, and mountainous cluster of islands fortified by massive seawalls and heavy coastal garrisons.",al={"where is kul tiras","kul tiras map","kul tiras location"},it="place"},{q="Is Kul Tiras still part of the Alliance?",a="Kul Tiras was one of the core founding nations of the original Alliance of Lordaeron. However, since the old Alliance splintered, the island kingdom has largely withdrawn from continental affairs and maintains a posture of stern isolation.",al={"is kul tiras alliance","kul tiras alliance member"},it="allies"},{q="Who rules Kul Tiras?",a="Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore led the nation for decades until his fateful campaign in Kalimdor ended with his death at Theramore.",al={"kul tiras leader","who leads kul tiras","king of kul tiras"},it="leader"},{q="Why is Kul Tiras famous across Azeroth?",a="Kul Tiras is legendary for having the greatest maritime fleet, shipwrights, and sailors in the known world. Its warships and vast merchant convoys have dominated the sea lanes for centuries, and its navy was essential in holding off the Horde during the Second War.",al={"what is kul tiras known for","why is kul tiras famous"},it="fame"},{q="How is Kul Tiras connected to Jaina Proudmoore?",a="Lady Jaina Proudmoore is a daughter of Kul Tiras, being the child of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. Although born to her homeland's ruling family, she studied in Dalaran and later established the independent port of Theramore Isle on Kalimdor, distancing her from her homeland's leadership.",al={"jaina kul tiras","is jaina from kul tiras"},it="ties"},{q="What threats face Kul Tiras?",a="Beyond their deep-seated enmity for the Horde, the waters around Kul Tiras are continuously plagued by renegade pirate crews and hostile coastal beasts, requiring constant vigilance and fortified seagates to protect shipping lanes.",al={"kul tiras enemies","threats to kul tiras"},it="threat"},{q="What is Kul Tiras?",a="Kul Tiras is an island nation and archipelago situated in the Great Sea west of the Eastern Kingdoms. Renowned for their peerless maritime mastery and trading vessels, the people of Kul Tiras built one of the seven historic human kingdoms. They command the mightiest naval fleet in the world, built on a long tradition of shipwrighting, fishing, and open-ocean exploration.",al={"what is kul tiras","kul tiras kingdom","kul tiras nation"},it="identity",add="31ee9d0a"},{q="What is the history of Kul Tiras?",a="Kul Tiras grew into a powerful maritime nation whose merchant lanes reached across the world. It provided the core naval strength of the Alliance during the Second War under Daelin Proudmoore.",al={"history of kul tiras","how was kul tiras founded","kul tiras origin"},it="history",add="db90478b"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron owed its survival at sea to the Kul Tiran armada, though the island nation currently remains distant and guarded following the losses of the Third War.",["faction:Horde"]="Kul Tiran sailors under Admiral Proudmoore fought bitterly against the Horde in the Second War and later attacked Thrall's new homeland in Durotar, deepening an enduring blood feud.",["race:Human"]="Kul Tirans developed a distinct, hardy island culture famed for shipbuilding, trade, and resilience against the open ocean.",["profession:Mining"]="Kul Tiras's shipyards turn great quantities of metal into the armored warships of the mightiest navy in Azeroth."},kw={"alliance of lordaeron","daelin proudmoore","great sea","jaina proudmoore","kul tiras","tiragarde"},rel={"npc:jaina-proudmoore","topic:second-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:alliance","topic:horde","subzone:baradin-bay","zone:dunmorogh","topic:pirate"},src={"Kul Tiras"}}
E["topic:leper-gnome"] = {t="topic",n="Leper gnome",z="general",s="Leper gnomes are the tragic, mutated survivors of Gnomeregan's catastrophic irradiation during the Third War. Maddened by toxic fallout and lingering fumes, most have sworn fealty to the usurper Mekgineer Thermaplugg, fiercely defending their ruined city against all outsiders.",h="Leper gnomes are the tragic, mutated survivors of Gnomeregan's catastrophic irradiation during the Third War.",sec={{t="The Fall of the Great City",b="When trogg invaders breached Gnomeregan during the Third War, Mekgineer Thermaplugg advised High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque to flood the city with radioactive toxins. Thermaplugg claimed the poison would exterminate the troggs while sparing the gnomish population. Instead, the catastrophe wiped out eighty percent of the city's inhabitants. Those who survived within the sealed lower levels were warped in mind and flesh by toxic fallout, mutating into pale, pustulant wretches dubbed leper gnomes.",sp=0},{t="Thermaplugg's Enclave",b="Thermaplugg himself succumbed to the poisonous fallout, losing both his complexion and his sanity. Naming himself techno-overlord and King of Gnomeregan, he gathered the crazed survivors in the Tinkers' Court. In their fractured mental state, many leper gnomes perceive all surface dwellers as trogg intruders, driving them to attack returning rescue parties with frantic savagery. They pilot salvaged mechanical constructs and assemble volatile ordnance, guarding Thermaplugg's twisted kingdom with fanatical devotion.",sp=0},{t="Scattered Across Azeroth",b="While the majority remain trapped within Gnomeregan's contaminated corridors, a handful of leper gnomes have found their way into the wider world. Driven by desperation or dark ambition, individuals have turned up among the Dark Iron dwarves in Blackrock Depths, operated machinery for the Venture Company in Stonetalon, or taken up service as lab assistants under the Forsaken's Royal Apothecary Society in the bowels of the Undercity.",sp=0},{t="The Search for a Cure",b="Gnome scholars exiled in Ironforge refuse to abandon their irradiated kin. In Dun Morogh, engineers and tinkers such as Ozzie Togglevolt and Razzle Sprysprocket work tirelessly on 'Operation Recombobulation,' attempting to devise alchemical or mechanical means to reverse the mutation. Whether a mind broken by toxic fallout can truly be restored remains a painful and unanswered question.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What turned the gnomes into leper gnomes?",a="During the trogg invasion of Gnomeregan, Mekgineer Thermaplugg urged High Tinker Mekkatorque to release radioactive vents to purge the caverns. The toxic fallout failed to kill the troggs and instead irradiated the trapped gnomish citizenry, mutating their skin and destroying their sanity.",al={"why are leper gnomes green","how did they become leper gnomes","gnome radiation cause"},it="origin"},{q="Are leper gnomes undead?",a="No. Leper gnomes are living gnomes who have suffered severe radiation poisoning and physical mutation. While some serve alongside the Forsaken in Lordaeron due to their affinity for blight and toxins, their affliction stems from irradiated gnomish engineering, not necromancy.",al={"are leper gnomes zombies","are they undead","leper gnome scourge"}},{q="Can leper gnomes be cured?",a="Allied gnome inventors like Ozzie Togglevolt and Razzle Sprysprocket are actively testing treatments, such as recombobulation devices and alchemical washes. While some experimental devices show minor promise, no reliable cure currently exists to reverse both the physical rot and the deep mental degradation.",al={"can leper gnomes be saved","leper gnome cure","operation recombobulation"}},{q="Who leads the leper gnomes?",a="Mekgineer Thermaplugg acts as their despotic techno-overlord. Mutated by the very radiation he insisted on unleashing, he rules from the Tinkers' Court inside Gnomeregan, manipulating the survivors' fractured minds to maintain absolute control over the ruins.",al={"leper gnome leader","king of gnomeregan","thermaplugg boss"},it="leader"},{q="Why do leper gnomes attack anyone who enters Gnomeregan?",a="The prolonged exposure to radioactive gasses shattered their minds. In their paranoia and delirium, most leper gnomes view any outsider—even an uncorrupted gnome from the surface—as an invading trogg bent on their destruction.",al={"why are leper gnomes hostile","why do they fight us"},it="hostile"},{q="Why are some leper gnomes working with the Forsaken?",a="A few leper gnomes, such as Ganoosh in the Undercity's Apothecarium, have joined the Forsaken. The Royal Apothecary Society tolerates and employs them as research assistants, recognizing a shared appreciation for plague, blighted alchemy, and survival despite severe bodily degradation.",al={"leper gnomes in undercity","forsaken leper gnomes"},it="allies"},{q="What are leper gnomes?",a="Leper gnomes are gnomes who were twisted, diseased, and driven mad by the toxic radiation released within Gnomeregan. Marked by pale, greenish, lesion-covered skin and violent instability, most have devolved into deranged wretches who wander the ruined halls of their former capital, completely severed from civilized gnome society.",al={"what is a leper gnome","who are leper gnomes","leper gnome info"},it="identity",add="e1b9c7cf"},{q="Where did the leper gnomes come from?",a="During the Third War, troggs invaded Gnomeregan from subterranean depths. To repel them, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque—advised by Mekgineer Thermaplugg—opened the city's radioactive waste tanks. The plan proved disastrous: toxic radiation blanketed the underground metropolis, slaughtering eighty percent of the gnome population and horrifically mutating the survivors into the maddened leper gnomes seen today.",al={"leper gnome history","origin of leper gnomes","how did leper gnomes happen"},it="history",add="acba60f9"},{q="Why are they called leper gnomes?",a="They earn their name from their sickly, rotting appearance. Heavy exposure to irradiated waste left their flesh discolored, peeling, and pocked with vile sores and lesions, resembling the afflictions of classic leprosy. Combined with their slow physical and mental degradation, the title reflects their tragically diseased state.",al={"why leper gnomes","leper gnome name meaning","why are they named leper gnomes"},it="name",add="7550d48d"}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Every leper gnome is a tragic reminder of Gnomeregan's downfall and the heavy cost of Mekkatorque's trust in Thermaplugg. Exiled gnomes in Tinker Town view them with a mixture of profound sorrow and determination to find a cure.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken apothecaries find common ground with these irradiated outcasts, viewing their diseased forms without the disgust typical of living surface races and putting their mechanical skills to work in Undercity labs.",["profession:Engineering"]="Even in their state of utter madness, leper gnomes retain an uncanny aptitude for assembly lines, automated defense turrets, and high-voltage machinations."},kw={"dun morogh","gnomeregan","leper gnome","mekkatorque","radiation","recombobulation","thermaplugg","toxic"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","npc:sicco-thermaplugg","topic:gnome","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","quest:412","subzone:tinkers-court","topic:trogg","zone:undercity"},src={"Leper gnome"}}
E["topic:lich-king"] = {t="topic",n="Lich King",z="general",s="The Lich King is the lord and psychic master of the undead Scourge, ruling from the Frozen Throne far to the north in Icecrown. Originally forged by the demon Kil'jaeden from the spirit of the orc shaman Ner'zhul, the entity eventually lured Prince Arthas Menethil into corruption, culminating in their fateful union atop Northrend's icy peaks.",h="The Lich King is the lord and psychic master of the undead Scourge, ruling from the Frozen Throne far to the...",sec={{t="Birth of the Frozen God",b="When the orc shaman Ner'zhul attempted to flee the dying world of Draenor, he fell directly into the clutches of Kil'jaeden the Deceiver. The demon lord tore Ner'zhul's body to shreds but bound his tormented soul into a block of diamond-hard ice cast out of the Twisting Nether. Flung into Azeroth, the glacial monolith struck the northern wastes of Northrend, forming the Frozen Throne. Kil'jaeden intended the Lich King to forge an unstoppable undead vanguard that would dismantle the mortal kingdoms without succumbing to the infighting that ruined the Orcish Horde. Instead, armed with immense telepathic power, Ner'zhul immediately began plotting his own bid for freedom.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Lordaeron",b="Watching dreadlords kept close vigil over the entity, forcing Ner'zhul to act in secret. Reaching out with his mind, he ensnared the exiled Kirin Tor archmage Kel'Thuzad, who established the Cult of the Damned in Lordaeron. Through cauldrons of tainted grain, the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath across the northern kingdoms. The crisis drew in Prince Arthas Menethil, whose desperation to save his people led him into ruthless brutality, culminating in the infamous slaughter of Stratholme. Blinded by vengeance against the dreadlord Mal'Ganis, Arthas pursued his quarry into Northrend, where the cursed runeblade Frostmourne waited to steal his soul and turn him into the Scourge's premier death knight.",sp=0},{t="Treachery and Ascendance",b="Though forced to summon Archimonde and hand temporary command to the Burning Legion, the Lich King anticipated the demons' hubris. Arthas manipulated the demon hunter Illidan Stormrage into claiming the Skull of Gul'dan and slaying Tichondrius, ultimately contributing to Archimonde's ruin at Mount Hyjal. In the aftermath, a desperate Kil'jaeden tasked Illidan with destroying the Frozen Throne. As cracks in the icy spire weakened Ner'zhul's power—allowing Sylvanas Windrunner and her Forsaken to break free—the Lich King summoned his champion home. Arthas returned to Northrend, defeated Illidan at the foot of Icecrown, ascended the spire, and shattered the ice to merge with Ner'zhul into a single terrible consciousness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Lich King right now?",a="The Lich King is the merged entity of Prince Arthas Menethil and the orc shaman Ner'zhul. Arthas shattered the glacial prison atop the Frozen Throne in Icecrown and donned the Helm of Domination, uniting his mortal form and dark soul with Ner'zhul's spirit into a single absolute ruler of the Scourge.",al={"current lich king","who rules the scourge","is arthas the lich king"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the Burning Legion create him?",a="Kil'jaeden created the Lich King to weaken Azeroth's defenders before a full demonic invasion. The previous invasion failed due to internal squabbling among the mortal Orcish Horde; a telepathically controlled army of the dead promised absolute obedience and complete exhaustion of mortal armies.",al={"why make lich king","kiljaeden purpose","legion scourge origin"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Lich King located?",a="He sits upon the Frozen Throne atop the icy glacier of Icecrown, deep within the remote and frozen continent of Northrend, far across the Great Sea north of Lordaeron.",al={"where is frozen throne","lich king location","where does he live"},it="place"},{q="How did Sylvanas break free from his control?",a="When Illidan Stormrage attacked the Frozen Throne with powerful sorcery, the magical ice fractured and the Lich King's power began leaking out. As his telepathic grip weakened, a faction of undead regained their free will, rallied under Sylvanas Windrunner, and founded the Forsaken.",al={"how did forsaken break free","why sylvanas free","scourge loss of control"},it="history"},{q="What is Frostmourne?",a="Frostmourne is the cursed, soul-stealing runeblade sent out from the Frozen Throne to lure a mortal champion. It lured Prince Arthas Menethil into claiming it in Northrend, devouring the prince's soul and transforming him into the foremost death knight of the Scourge.",al={"what sword did arthas use","frostmourne lore","runeblade"},it="item"},{q="Who was Ner'zhul?",a="Ner'zhul was an elder orc shaman of Draenor. When he attempted to flee the dying homeworld through dimensional rifts, he was captured by the demon Kil'jaeden, who tore his flesh away and warped his spirit into the all-seeing consciousness that became the Lich King.",al={"nerzhul lore","who was nerzhul","original lich king"},it="history"},{q="Why are the Lich King and his Scourge hostile to all living travelers?",a="The Lich King seeks the utter extinction and subjugation of mortal life. Every living creature slain by his plague or his unholy legions can be reanimated under his absolute mental command, expanding his power exponentially. He sees mortal races not as neighbors, but as raw material to swell his eternal Scourge.",al={"why is lich king hostile","scourge hostility","why does the lich king attack the living"},it="hostile",add="66564786"},{q="Who is allied with the Lich King?",a="The Lich King works with no living nations. He commands the Undead Scourge directly through psychic domination, supported by mortal necromancers and zealots in the Cult of the Damned like Kel'Thuzad. Though created by the demon lord Kil'jaeden, he secretly betrayed the Burning Legion to pursue his own supreme dominion over Azeroth.",al={"lich king allies","who works for lich king","who does the lich king serve"},it="allies",add="b2423b94"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken exist solely because the Lich King's telepathic grip faltered, and Queen Sylvanas drives her people with a burning hatred toward their former master in the frozen north.",["race:Human"]="For humans, the Lich King represents the ultimate tragedy: Prince Arthas, heir to the throne of Lordaeron and paladin of the Silver Hand, murdered his own father and annihilated his kingdom in the entity's name.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand was shattered by the Lich King's corrupting power, which transformed their greatest champion into a death knight who struck down Uther the Lightbringer.",["class:Priest"]="The Scourge represents a grim perversion of the Light's sanctity, reminding holy practitioners of the terrible cost paid across Lordaeron's fallen parishes."},kw={"arthas","cult of the damned","frostmourne","frozen throne","helm of domination","icecrown","kiljaeden","lich king","nerzhul","scourge"},rel={"topic:arthas-menethil","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:cult-of-the-damned","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:lordaeron","topic:burning-legion","subzone:stratholme"},src={"Lich King"}}
E["topic:light"] = {t="topic",n="Light",z="general",s="The Light is a boundless primal cosmic force whose energy connects and suffuses all living things across creation. Manifested in the mortal realm as Holy magic, it responds to willpower, meditation, and unyielding faith to mend flesh, shield allies, and purge darkness.",h="The Light is a boundless primal cosmic force whose energy connects and suffuses all living things across...",sec={{t="A Primal Force",b="The Light is an omnipresent force of living energy. When brought into Azeroth, it manifests as Holy magic. Its power flows through living souls as a connective tissue, binding mortal hearts together. While mortals often personify it as a conscious protector, the Light fundamentally answers the absolute conviction of the soul calling upon it.",sp=0},{t="Instruments of Faith",b="To channel the Light requires intense self-abnegation, disciplined meditation, and unshakable willpower. Practitioners—most famously the priests of the Church of the Holy Light and the holy paladins—train for years to become clear, empty vessels for its radiance. Through their devotion, the Light manifests as restorative warmth to mend mortal wounds, brilliant wards to shelter the innocent, or searing fire to strike down demons and the risen undead.",sp=0},{t="Many Faces of Radiance",b="While humanity and the dwarves revere the Light through formal scripture, other cultures access its brilliance through different spiritual traditions. The night elves channel sacred moonlight through devotion to the goddess Elune, weaving holy magic that cleanses and protects. The tauren honor the sun deity An'she in their own spiritual traditions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Light?",a="The Light is a fundamental cosmic energy that exists across all creation. It is the primal source of life and positive energy, binding sentient souls together. Mortals call upon it to produce Holy magic for healing, protection, and warfare.",al={"holy light lore","what is the holy light","the light explained"},it="identity"},{q="Does the Light have a conscious will or mind?",a="The Light is a primal cosmic force rather than a god. While mortals frequently speak of the 'will of the Light' and treat it as a guiding patron, it does not possess mortal morality. It responds directly to the certainty, willpower, and faith of the caster.",al={"is the light alive","does light have a mind","is the light a god"}},{q="Why can evil zealots still use the Light?",a="Because the Light answers conviction, not abstract righteousness. If a caster possesses absolute faith that their cause is just and their spirit remains undivided, the Light will flow through them—even if their deeds are ruthless or misguided.",al={"why do scarlet crusade have light","can bad guys use the light","evil paladins light"}},{q="How does the Light affect the undead?",a="Holy magic is a powerful weapon against the Scourge, but it can also heal undead bodies. Channeling the Light or receiving its healing causes undead intense pain; it does not automatically burn away their flesh.",al={"light vs undead","why does light hurt scourge","can undead use light"}},{q="How do priests and paladins learn to wield it?",a="Wielding the Light requires rigorous mental discipline, emotional clarity, and years of meditation. The caster must quiet their personal ego and focus their spirit entirely, acting as a clear conduit through which the Holy magic can pour into reality.",al={"how to use holy magic","paladin magic source","priest light training"}},{q="Is Elune's magic connected to the Light?",a="Yes. The moonlight channeled by the priestesses of Elune manifests as a form of holy magic, sharing the same restorative, protective, and purifying capabilities as the spells wielded by paladins and church priests.",al={"is elune holy magic","elune and the light","moonlight holy light"}},{q="Where did the Light come from?",a="Its true origins are unknown. Priests of the Church of the Holy Light teach that the Light dwells within all living beings, but where the Light itself comes from remains a mystery.",al={"origin of the light","where did light come from","how was the light created","history of the holy light"},it="history",add="73e6193d"}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Paladins do not merely cast spells; they infuse their martial discipline and mortal bodies with the Light, wielding its incandescent fury against the Scourge and demonic evil.",["class:Priest"]="Priests serve as spiritual shepherds and conduits, practicing profound meditation and prayer to mend flesh, steady broken spirits, and shield allies.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Church of the Holy Light forms the spiritual bedrock of Stormwind and Ironforge, offering hope in an era darkened by the fall of Lordaeron.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, channeling the Light or receiving its healing brings intense pain, even when the spell mends their bodies.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elves channel holy energy not through church doctrine, but through reverence for the moon goddess Elune and her silver light.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren revere An'she, the golden right eye of the Earth Mother, through their traditional sun worship."},kw={"an'she","church","elune","faith","healing","holy light","holy magic","light","paladin","priest","scourge"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","npc:tirion-fordring","topic:elune","topic:lordaeron","topic:tauren","topic:alliance","quest:99134","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Light"}}
E["topic:llane-wrynn-i"] = {t="topic",n="Llane Wrynn I",z="general",s="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of Stormwind during the catastrophic First War, whose reign ended with the fall of the kingdom and his own tragic murder. A beloved and proud monarch, his steadfast refusal to believe his friend Medivh had betrayed humanity ultimately doomed his defense of the capital.",h="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of Stormwind during the catastrophic First War, whose reign ended with the...",sec={{t="A Prince of Reckless Valor",b="Born to King Barathen Wrynn, young Prince Llane grew up alongside Anduin Lothar and Medivh. In their youth, the three companions ventured into the jungles of Stranglethorn Vale and clashed with the Gurubashi trolls there. In time, Llane ascended to the throne as Stormwind's king.",sp=0},{t="The First War",b="When mysterious, green-skinned creatures emerged from the southern swamps, King Llane dispatched Lothar to investigate the Black Morass. What began as minor skirmishes erupted into the First War as the Horde overran the forests of Duskwood, Westfall, and Redridge. Messengers sent to Lordaeron and other northern human kingdoms were dismissed, their pleas undermined by Stormwind's historic isolationism and aristocratic intrigue. Despite these disadvantages, Llane organized an audacious counterattack alongside Lothar that broke the Horde's initial siege of Stormwind, dealing the invaders their first major defeat.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal in the Keep",b="Llane's steadfast nature ultimately blinded him to the true danger within his inner circle. Even when Medivh's apprentice Khadgar and the emissary Garona revealed that the Guardian himself had opened the Dark Portal, Llane refused to accept his childhood friend's treachery. Soon after, Orgrim Doomhammer unified the Horde and breached Stormwind's outer defenses. As Llane directed the defense from Stormwind Keep, Garona—compelled by the magical influence of the Shadow Council—murdered the King in his own throne room, carving out his heart and sealing the city's doom.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who was King Llane Wrynn I?",a="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind during the First War. He was the father of King Varian Wrynn and grandfather of young Prince Anduin Llane Wrynn. He died defending his people during the initial fall of Stormwind.",al={"who is llane","llane wrynn lore","king llane"},it="identity"},{q="How did King Llane die?",a="Llane was assassinated inside Stormwind Keep during the final siege of the First War. He was slain by Garona Halforcen, a half-orc emissary whom he considered a trusted friend and confidante, but who was mind-controlled by the Shadow Council.",al={"who killed king llane","llane death","how did llane die"},it="conflict"},{q="What was Llane's relationship to Medivh and Lothar?",a="Llane, Medivh, and Anduin Lothar were inseparable childhood companions who adventured together across southern Azeroth. Their lifelong bond proved tragic: Lothar became Llane's greatest champion, while Llane's total refusal to believe Medivh could betray him led to the monarch's downfall.",al={"llane medivh lothar","llane childhood friends"},it="allies"},{q="Why didn't the other human kingdoms help Llane during the First War?",a="Stormwind had long maintained an isolationist foreign policy that northern nations viewed as arrogant. When Llane sent warnings of the green-skinned invaders, leaders in Lordaeron dismissed the reports as an exaggerated civil rebellion, leaving Stormwind to fight alone.",al={"why didn't lordaeron help llane","alliance during first war"}},{q="How did Varian escape the fall of Stormwind?",a="Following King Llane's assassination and the breach of Stormwind Keep, Champion Anduin Lothar gathered the surviving citizens and Llane's young son, Prince Varian Wrynn. Lothar led them to the harbor and fled north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",al={"what happened to varian after llane died","varian escape stormwind"},it="history"},{q="What did Medivh give Llane at his ascension?",a="When Llane came of age as Prince of Stormwind, Medivh emerged from a long coma and presented him with an enchanted obsidian hourglass. The sand within trickled endlessly, with the upper chamber never emptying, symbolizing Stormwind's eternal prosperity.",al={"medivh gift to llane","llane hourglass"},it="item"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="King Llane's tragic murder and the fall of Stormwind forced the survivors north, directly provoking the creation of the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron to counter the Horde.",["race:Human"]="For the people of Stormwind, King Llane remains a tragic hero whose sacrifice is commemorated across the restored city, including in the naming of King Varian's son, Anduin Llane Wrynn.",["class:Warrior"]="King Llane fought alongside his Champion, Anduin Lothar, personally directing the martial defense of Stormwind from the front lines of the Keep.",["class:Rogue"]="The regicide of King Llane by the assassin Garona remains one of the most famous and devastating blade strikes in Azeroth's history."},kw={"barathen","first war","garona","gurubashi","kingslayers","llane","lothar","medivh","shadow council","stormwind","wrynn"},rel={"topic:varian-wrynn","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:first-war","topic:medivh","topic:stormwind-kingdom","subzone:stormwind-keep","topic:shadow-council","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Llane Wrynn I"}}
E["topic:lordaeron"] = {t="topic",n="Lordaeron",z="general",s="Lordaeron is the great northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms, once the glorious heart of human civilization and the Alliance. Following the devastation of the Third War, its former kingdoms lie in ruins, contested by the undead Scourge, Sylvanas Windrunner's Forsaken, and desperate remnants of the living.",h="Lordaeron is the great northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms, once the glorious heart of human...",sec={{t="Cradle of Northern Humanity",b="Lordaeron shares its name with the human city-state of Lordaeron, which rose in the north after the ancient Empire of Arathor and grew into the most powerful of the human kingdoms. Stretching from the rugged Arathi Highlands in the south across scenic forests and waterways up toward Quel'Thalas, the region nurtured thriving kingdoms like Lordaeron, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. It was historically celebrated for fertile farmlands in Tirisfal and Hillsbrad, mountain strongholds, and the sacred cradle of the Holy Light.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge and the Fall",b="During the Third War, Lordaeron became ground zero for the Lich King's invasion. Through tainted grain, the Cult of the Damned transformed countless northern citizens into mindless undead monstrosities. Prince Arthas Menethil fell to darkness, returning from Northrend to slay his father, King Terenas Menethil II, and orchestrate the total slaughter of the Capital City. The thriving heartland quickly rotted into the blighted nightmare known as the Plaguelands.",sp=0},{t="A Contested Wasteland",b="Today, Lordaeron is a brutal battleground defined by fractured loyalties. Beneath the ruins of the Capital City, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken have carved out the Undercity, establishing a sovereign realm of free-willed undead aligned with the Horde. To the east, the Lich King's mindless Scourge clings to corrupted keeps and strongholds. Meanwhile, human zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, surviving Alliance settlements in Hillsbrad, and wild forest trolls clash relentlessly over what remains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What exactly is Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron refers both to the entire northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms and specifically to the former human kingdom that once ruled its central territories. The land stretches from the Thandol Span at the Arathi border north toward Quel'Thalas, encompassing mountains, forests, and inland lakes.",al={"is lordaeron a continent","what does lordaeron mean","lordaeron geography"},it="identity"},{q="Who controls Lordaeron right now?",a="No single power controls the entire subcontinent. Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken hold Tirisfal Glades and Silverpine from their subterranean Undercity. The Scourge retains immense power in the Plaguelands under Kel'Thuzad, while the Scarlet Crusade, the Kingdom of Stormwind's allies in Hillsbrad, and various troll tribes control isolated frontiers.",al={"who rules lordaeron","who owns lordaeron now","current ruler of lordaeron"},it="in_charge"},{q="What happened to the original living humans of Lordaeron?",a="Most of Lordaeron's population perished during the Third War, struck down by the plague of undeath or Arthas's Scourge army. Many of those dead were raised into mindless servitude, and later regained their minds to become the Forsaken. The surviving living fled south to Stormwind, sailed to Kalimdor with Jaina Proudmoore, or joined militant enclaves like the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"where did the people of lordaeron go","did lordaeron humans die","what happened to lordaeron"},it="people"},{q="Why was the subcontinent named Lordaeron?",a="The land takes its name from the city-state of Lordaeron, which rose in the north after the ancient Empire of Arathor and grew into one of humanity's most powerful kingdoms, eventually lending its name to the whole northern subcontinent.",al={"origin of name lordaeron","who is lordaeron named after","lordain name"},it="name"},{q="Where is the Capital City of Lordaeron?",a="The Capital City of Lordaeron stands along the northern shore of Lordamere Lake in Tirisfal Glades. Though its surface streets and palace courtyards lie in silent ruins, the sprawling catacombs beneath have been converted by Sylvanas Windrunner into the Undercity, the Forsaken capital.",al={"where was lordaeron city","capital of lordaeron","ruins of lordaeron location"},it="place"},{q="Is Lordaeron part of the Alliance or the Horde?",a="It is bitterly contested. Historically, the Kingdom of Lordaeron founded and led the original Alliance of Lordaeron. Today, however, the Undercity is an official capital of the Horde, making western Lordaeron a Horde stronghold, while Alliance forces desperately fight to hold their few remaining outposts in Hillsbrad and Arathi.",al={"is lordaeron alliance or horde","who claims lordaeron"},it="side"},{q="What is the history of Lordaeron?",a="Originally settled by human tribes following the era of the Empire of Arathor, Lordaeron grew into one of humanity's most powerful kingdoms and gave its name to the entire northern continent. For generations, its realm encompassed rich farmlands and strong cities, standing as the bulwark of the Alliance during the Second War. That glory crumbled in the Third War, when the Scourge unleashed the plague of undeath upon its lands, tearing the kingdom apart and reducing its once-thriving territories to the blighted Plaguelands and contested ruins you see today.",al={"history of lordaeron","lordaeron lore","where did lordaeron come from"},it="history",add="ca72f255"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This was once your homeland in life. You walk the ruined courtyards of the Capital City not as visiting travelers, but as the rightful heirs of Lordaeron claiming what is yours under Lady Sylvanas.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the Alliance, Lordaeron is a sacred tragedy. It was the cradle of the Holy Light and the leader of the original Alliance; seeing its halls overrun by the undead is a constant source of grief and fury.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance once took its very name from this continent. Today, reclaiming footholds in Hillsbrad and combating the Scourge in the Plaguelands represents an agonizing struggle to recover human heritage.",["faction:Horde"]="Through their Forsaken allies, the Horde maintains a vital and contested foothold in the northern Eastern Kingdoms, surrounding Alliance outposts and confronting the remnants of the Scourge.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand was born in Lordaeron at the Chapel of Alonsus Faol. Every road and ruin in these northern lands is steeped in the history of the earliest paladins."},kw={"hillsbrad","lordaeron","menethil","plaguelands","scourge","silverpine","terenas","tirisfal","undercity"},rel={"topic:third-war","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal","zone:westernplaguelands","zone:easternplaguelands","zone:hillsbrad","zone:silverpine","zone:arathi"},src={"Lordaeron"}}
E["topic:mage"] = {t="topic",n="Mage",z="general",s="Mages are scholarly spellcasters who bend the fabric of reality through arcane study, hurling destructive fire, controlling frost, and raw arcane energy. While physically fragile and cloaked only in cloth, their mastery over teleportation, conjuration, and battlefield crowd control makes them formidable allies and terrifying foes. Their discipline requires immense mental dedication, balancing immense destructive potential against the perilous history of arcane misuse.",h="Mages are scholarly spellcasters who bend the fabric of reality through arcane study, hurling destructive...",sec={{t="Scholars of the Arcane",b="The path of the mage demands intellect, focus, and years of rigorous study. Rather than communing with spirits or relying on divine faith, magi treat magic as a precise cosmic science. By drawing upon arcane energies, a practitioner can weave raw destructive power, summon searing bursts of flame, or chill the surrounding air into solid ice and howling blizzards. Because heavy armor disrupts the delicate somatic gestures and mental focus required for incantations, mages wear light cloth, relying instead on wards, mana barriers, and evasive spells to protect themselves.",sp=0},{t="The Legacy of Dalaran and Arathor",b="Human magical heritage began nearly three millennia ago during the Troll Wars, when high elven sorcerers instructed one hundred humans of the Empire of Arathor to turn the tide against the Amani. As the number of human practitioners multiplied, fearful mundane citizens drove many magi to establish the magocracy of Dalaran. Under the governance of the Kirin Tor, Dalaran became the central archive of magical study for the northern kingdoms, formalizing the traditions of combat magic that later supported the Alliance of Lordaeron through the First and Second Wars.",sp=0},{t="Suspicion and Necessity",b="Arcane practitioners have never enjoyed uncomplicated trust. From the ancient cataclysm wrought by the Highborne to the catastrophic fall of Dalaran during the Third War, magic's volatile nature evokes widespread wariness. The Church of the Holy Light has historically treated wizardry with skepticism, and conservative knights have often viewed arcane spellcrafters as damned souls or a necessary evil on the battlefield. Despite this suspicion, civil and military leaders recognize that a capable magus offers invaluable battlefield supremacy, swift mass transportation, and essential countermeasures against supernatural threats.",sp=0},{t="Mages of the Horde and the High Order",b="The arcane is not the Alliance's alone. Troll and Forsaken mages serve the Horde, and orcs who take up the art begin under the Darkspear mage Mai'ah in the Valley of Trials, who teaches that patience and careful judgment matter more than raw power. Among the Skyborne, the High Order descends from the Highborne of Eldre'Thalas. Their ages in Skywall cost them much of their ancestors' arcane knowledge, and with the wind spirits gone from Zephras Isle they seek to recover it. Skyborne mages fight for the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What schools of magic do mages specialize in?",a="Mages focus on three core schools: Arcane, Fire, and Frost. Arcane magic alters time, space, and reality, allowing for polymorphing, teleportation, and focused bursts of pure power. Fire spells focus on swift, overwhelming combustive damage to incinerate targets. Frost magic slows, snares, and shatters foes, providing critical battlefield control to keep enemies from closing into melee distance.",al={"mage specs","mage magic types","arcane fire frost"}},{q="Why do mages only wear cloth armor?",a="Casting complex spells requires uninhibited physical movement, precise hand gestures, and uninterrupted mental focus. Heavy materials like leather, chainmail, or plate interfere with a spellcaster's somatic components and channel of magical energy. To compensate for their physical vulnerability, magi employ magical barriers, frost shielding, and spells that keep enemies at bay.",al={"why cloth","mage armor type","can mages wear leather"}},{q="How did humans first learn to cast arcane magic?",a="During the ancient Troll Wars roughly 2,800 years ago, the human Empire of Arathor allied with the high elves of Quel'Thalas against the Amani trolls. In exchange for military aid, elven magi trained one hundred humans in the fundamentals of the arcane. These original hundred pioneers proved devastating in battle and founded the human magical lineage that eventually established Dalaran.",al={"first human mages","troll wars mages","human magic origin"}},{q="What is the relationship between mages and the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The relationship is tense. For centuries, orthodox members of the Church of the Holy Light viewed arcane studies and the Kirin Tor with suspicion, sometimes treating them as heretical. Paladins and devout priests often consider wizardry an unpredictable, morally dubious practice, though one accepted out of necessity due to its strategic military power.",al={"church views on mages","paladins and mages","holy light arcane heresy"}},{q="Are Stormwind's mages part of Dalaran's Kirin Tor?",a="Historically, Stormwind had its own traditions, originally led by court conjurers who operated largely independent of Dalaran. While Dalaran's Kirin Tor was the undisputed center of magical academia in the north, southern wizards developed their own enclaves and royal advisory roles, though communication and common ties endured across both realms.",al={"stormwind mages dalaran","conjurers of azeroth","stormwind wizards"}},{q="Can mages create food and transport people instantly?",a="Yes. Beyond destructive spells, mages possess extensive utility magic. They can weave arcane energy to conjure fresh food and water from thin air, enchant allies' intellects, and bend space to instantly open portals connecting major cities across the world.",al={"mage utility","mage portals","conjure water food"}},{q="Which races can be mages?",a="Humans, gnomes and the Skyborne train as mages for the Alliance, and trolls, the Forsaken and orcs do for the Horde. Dwarves, night elves and tauren do not; the night elves shun the arcane after the ruin Queen Azshara's Highborne brought upon ancient Kalimdor.",al={"who can be a mage","mage races","can orcs be mages","orc mage","skyborne mage"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Your craft is one of meticulous intellectual discipline, mastering fire, frost, and arcane force while safeguarding the fragile balance of reality.",["race:Human"]="Humanity's arcane legacy traces back to the hundred original magi taught during the Troll Wars, whose descendants built Dalaran into a grand center of magical study.",["race:NightElf"]="Kaldorei society strictly shuns arcane magic, remembering all too well how Queen Azshara and her Highborne magi brought ruin upon ancient Kalimdor.",["class:Paladin"]="While you fight beside magi on the battlefield, the Knights of the Silver Hand have long regarded their worldly arcane methods with deep caution.",["class:Warlock"]="Many warlocks began their journey as mages before abandoning academic restraint and strict Dalaran edicts in pursuit of demonic power.",["race:Orc"]="Orc mages begin their studies under the Darkspear mage Mai'ah in the Valley of Trials, who teaches patience and careful judgment before power.",["race:Skyborne"]="Skyborne mages of the High Order seek to recover the Highborne spellcraft their ancestors carried out of Eldre'Thalas, hoping it can save their failing island."},kw={"arcane","conjuration","dalaran","fire","frost","kirin tor","mage","magus","mana","spellcaster","teleport","wizard"},rel={"subzone:dalaran","topic:highborne","npc:jaina-proudmoore","topic:medivh","topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","zone:stormwind","topic:skyborne","npc:mai-ah"},src={"Mage"}}
E["topic:medivh"] = {t="topic",n="Medivh",z="general",s="Medivh was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order tasked with defending Azeroth from demonic corruption. Bound from before his birth by the spirit of the Dark Titan Sargeras, he opened the Dark Portal and brought the orcish Horde into the world, setting off decades of catastrophic warfare.",h="Medivh was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order tasked with defending Azeroth from demonic...",sec={{t="A Cursed Heritage",b="Medivh was born to the powerful Guardian Aegwynn and the court conjurer Nielas Aran. Centuries earlier, Aegwynn had fought the fallen Titan Sargeras in Northrend, unaware that his dark essence slipped into her body to await an heir. When Medivh reached his fourteenth birthday, the vast magical inheritance bestowed by his mother manifested in a terrifying explosion of power, plunging the boy into a decade-long coma under the watch of the clerics at Northshire Abbey.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Karazhan",b="Awakening from his slumber, Medivh took residence in the isolated tower of Karazhan, serving as court magus to his childhood friend King Llane Wrynn of Stormwind. Yet the dark spirit within him slowly eroded his sanity. Seeking to unleash devastation, Medivh made contact across the stars with the orc warlock Gul'dan and the Shadow Council. Together, they channeled tremendous magic to tear open the Dark Portal, delivering the Horde upon the Eastern Kingdoms and sparking the First War. Medivh was ultimately slain in his tower by his closest friend, Anduin Lothar, and his apprentice, Khadgar.",sp=0},{t="The Prophet's Redemption",b="Freed from Sargeras by his mortal death, Medivh returned from beyond death to atone for his crimes. During the Third War, he disguised himself as an enigmatic raven-cloaked Prophet, whispering warnings of the Burning Legion's return to King Terenas Menethil and Warchief Thrall. Though Lordaeron ignored his pleas, he guided Thrall's Horde and Jaina Proudmoore's refugees across the Great Sea to Kalimdor, uniting orcs, humans, and night elves for the decisive stand at Mount Hyjal before fading into myth.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Medivh?",a="Medivh was the last human Guardian of Tirisfal, endowed with immense arcane power to protect Azeroth. He was the son of Magna Aegwynn and the architect behind the opening of the Dark Portal.",al={"who is medivh","medivh lore","the last guardian"},it="identity"},{q="Why did Medivh open the Dark Portal?",a="Medivh was possessed before birth by the spirit of the Dark Titan Sargeras. Under this malevolent influence, he bargained with the warlock Gul'dan, offering the location of the Tomb of Sargeras in exchange for the Horde invading Azeroth to bring ruin to the mortal kingdoms.",al={"why did medivh betray stormwind","medivh dark portal","why bring orcs"},it="motive"},{q="Who killed Medivh?",a="During the First War, Medivh was confronted in Karazhan by his close friend Anduin Lothar, his apprentice Khadgar, and the half-orc assassin Garona. Khadgar pierced Medivh's heart and Lothar severed his head, ending his life and casting Sargeras's spirit back into the Nether.",al={"how did medivh die","who killed the guardian","medivh death"},it="conflict"},{q="What was Medivh's relationship with Stormwind's royalty?",a="Medivh grew up alongside Prince Llane Wrynn and Anduin Lothar in the royal court of Stormwind. The trio fought side by side against Gurubashi trolls in their youth, and King Llane trusted Medivh completely, making his eventual betrayal devastating to the kingdom.",al={"medivh and llane","was medivh friends with lothar"},it="ties"},{q="Is Medivh still alive?",a="After guiding the mortal races to victory over Archimonde at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, Medivh left to take his place among the legends of the past. His whereabouts since are unknown.",al={"where is medivh now","is the prophet medivh","medivh status"},sp=1,it="today"},{q="What is Karazhan?",a="Karazhan is a spire built atop a potent intersection of ley lines in Deadwind Pass. Originally established by Aegwynn and later inherited by Medivh, the tower became heavily warped by arcane and demonic energies following the Guardian's fall and remains a haunted ruin.",al={"medivh tower","what happened to karazhan"},it="place"},{q="Where did Medivh come from?",a="Medivh was born to Magna Aegwynn, the Guardian of Tirisfal, and Nielas Aran, a mage of the Kirin Tor. Unknown to his parents, the dark titan Sargeras possessed Medivh in his mother's womb. Raised among the court of Stormwind as a close childhood friend to Prince Llane Wrynn and Anduin Lothar, his inherited magical powers violently manifested on the eve of his fourteenth birthday, casting Medivh into a decade-long coma.",al={"medivh history","medivh origin","medivh past"},it="history",add="121458c2"},{q="What does the name Medivh mean?",a="Medivh means \"keeper of secrets\" in Thalassian, the tongue of the high elves. Given to him at birth by his mother, Aegwynn, the name proved chillingly fitting for a Guardian who concealed both the boundless power of Tirisfal and the insidious corruption of Sargeras within his soul.",al={"what does medivh mean","medivh name meaning","meaning of medivh"},it="name",add="2cdc20e2"}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Medivh represents both the zenith and the ultimate peril of arcane mastery; every Kirin Tor scholar studies his catastrophic fall as a cautionary tale of unchecked power.",["class:Warlock"]="Medivh's communion with Gul'dan and his role as the mortal conduit for Sargeras remain legendary demonstrations of fel summoning and interplanar manipulation.",["faction:Alliance"]="The people of Stormwind view Medivh with bitter sorrow, remembering him as a beloved royal companion who ultimately invited the ruinous Horde into their lands.",["faction:Horde"]="To Thrall's Horde, Medivh is the human sorcerer whose bargain with Gul'dan opened the Dark Portal and first brought the orcs into this world."},kw={"aegwynn","atiesh","dark portal","guardian","karazhan","khadgar","lothar","medivh","sargeras","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:karazhan","subzone:dark-portal","topic:sargeras","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:first-war","topic:third-war","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Medivh"}}
E["topic:muradin-bronzebeard"] = {t="topic",n="Muradin Bronzebeard",z="multi",s="Muradin Bronzebeard was the brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and Brann Bronzebeard, as well as a renowned warrior, diplomat, and co-founder of the Explorers' League. Revered across Khaz Modan and Lordaeron alike, he is believed to have died in the frozen wastes of Northrend when Prince Arthas Menethil claimed the cursed runeblade Frostmourne.",h="Muradin Bronzebeard was the brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and Brann Bronzebeard, as well as a renowned...",sec={{t="A Brother and Ambassador",b="Muradin fought with distinction during the Second War, acting as the vital liaison between the mountain halls of Khaz Modan and the Alliance of Lordaeron. He helped hold Ironforge during the Horde's siege and marched alongside Alliance forces all the way through Blackrock Spire to the Dark Portal. In the peaceful years that followed, Muradin settled into Capital City as Ironforge’s official ambassador. Jovial yet formidable, he became a fixture in the palace, growing close to King Terenas Menethil and taking it upon himself to tutor young Prince Arthas in the arts of swordplay and combat.",sp=0},{t="The Explorers' League and Northrend",b="Beyond politics and war, Muradin possessed a restless curiosity. Alongside his brothers, he helped establish the dwarven Explorers' League, dedicated to uncovering lost titan relics and chronicling the distant corners of the world. He fell in love with the desolate, windswept continent of Northrend, making frequent journeys north to sketch ancient architecture and document its fierce inhabitants. It was Northrend's rugged mysteries that ultimately drew him on his final expedition, seeking out lore, relics, and the whispered legend of a forgotten runeblade.",sp=0},{t="The Claiming of Frostmourne",b="When Prince Arthas arrived in Northrend obsessed with hunting down the dreadlord Mal'Ganis, he reunited with Muradin and enlisted his dwarven expedition. Arthas sought the power of Frostmourne to turn the tide, ignoring Muradin's rising alarm as the prince betrayed his own mercenaries and abandoned all honor. At the altar of Frostmourne, Muradin read the warning inscribed upon its icy pedestal and pleaded with his former pupil to turn back. Arthas refused. As the prince seized the runeblade, the explosion of unholy ice struck Muradin down, leaving him apparently dead upon the cavern floor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Muradin Bronzebeard?",a="Muradin Bronzebeard was the middle brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and the explorer Brann. He was a veteran hero of the Second War, Ironforge's ambassador to Lordaeron, and a founding member of the Explorers' League.",al={"who is muradin","muradin lore","magni brother muradin"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Muradin in Northrend?",a="Muradin accompanied Prince Arthas to the cavern containing Frostmourne. When Arthas ignored Muradin's warnings and shattered the icy prison holding the runeblade, an exploding shard of enchanted ice struck Muradin, seemingly killing him on the spot. Arthas abandoned his body and took the blade.",al={"how did muradin die","muradin death","did arthas kill muradin"},it="history"},{q="What was Muradin's relationship with Prince Arthas?",a="While serving as Ironforge's ambassador in Capital City, Muradin took Arthas under his wing. He trained the young prince in bladed combat and went on many adventures with him, viewing Arthas almost like a surrogate son or beloved nephew until Arthas's dark descent in Northrend.",al={"did muradin know arthas","muradin arthas teacher","muradin and arthas"},it="ties"},{q="What was Muradin's role in the Second War?",a="Muradin served as the Alliance liaison between Lordaeron and Khaz Modan. He defended Ironforge during the Horde's siege, then marched south with the united Alliance army to fight at Blackrock Spire and the Dark Portal.",al={"muradin second war","muradin battles","did muradin fight the horde"},it="history"},{q="Why did Muradin go to Northrend in the first place?",a="As a founder of the Explorers' League, Muradin was deeply fascinated by Northrend's archaeology and ancient ruins. He returned there on an expedition to uncover forgotten history and search for relics, which ultimately led him to the trail of Frostmourne.",al={"why was muradin in northrend","muradin expedition","what was muradin looking for"},it="motive"},{q="How did King Magni react to Muradin's death?",a="King Magni and the entire Bronzebeard clan were devastated by Muradin's loss. Muradin's apparent demise severed the once-warm bond between Ironforge and Prince Arthas, turning grief into bitter fury toward the Scourge.",al={"magni muradin reaction","ironforge muradin grief"}},{q="Who was Muradin Bronzebeard allied with?",a="Muradin was fiercely loyal to the Alliance of Lordaeron and his own people of Khaz Modan. As a prince of the Bronzebeard clan, he answered directly to his brother, King Magni Bronzebeard, while serving as Ironforge's official ambassador to the royal court of Lordaeron. There, he developed strong ties with King Terenas Menethil II and Prince Arthas. Muradin also helped found the dwarven Explorers' League, leading intrepid teams of dwarven archaeologists and adventurers into the world's most remote corners under Ironforge's banner.",al={"muradin bronzebeard allies","who did muradin work for","muradin factions"},it="allies",add="3bcb591a"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Muradin is remembered by the dwarves of Ironforge as a paragon of their people—brave, fiercely loyal, and driven by an insatiable curiosity for the history of their makers.",["faction:Alliance"]="Muradin forged the bonds of camaraderie between Ironforge and the northern human kingdoms, standing as one of the Alliance's foundational heroes.",["class:Warrior"]="Muradin was considered one of the finest dwarven duelists and weapon masters of his era, having instructed Prince Arthas Menethil himself in the art of the blade."},kw={"arthas","bronzebeard","explorers league","frostmourne","ironforge","magni","muradin","northrend"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","npc:brann-bronzebeard","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:explorers-league","zone:ironforge","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:second-war"},src={"Muradin Bronzebeard"}}
E["topic:murloc"] = {t="topic",n="Murloc",z="general",s="Murlocs are amphibious, bipedal humanoids famous for their gurgling battle cries, territorial coastal villages, and sudden swarming tactics. Though historically dwelling in the deep ocean, they have steadily migrated inland along the shores, lakes, and rivers of Azeroth, acting with far greater coordination and cunning than their primitive appearance suggests.",h="Murlocs are amphibious, bipedal humanoids famous for their gurgling battle cries, territorial coastal...",sec={{t="Creatures of the Shallows",b="Murlocs dwell in densely populated settlements along coastlines, riverbanks, and freshwater lakes. Averaging roughly four feet in height, they possess bulbous, slime-coated bodies, wide mouths filled with sharp fangs, and keen vision capable of piercing pitch-black water. While individuals are rarely seen wandering far from their kin, encountering one murloc invariably means a dozen more lurk just beneath the surface. They rely on overwhelming numbers and sharp crude weaponry.",sp=0},{t="The Inland March",b="Though ancient lineages suggest murlocs have existed beneath the waves since before recorded history, their widespread encroachment onto the landmasses of Lordaeron and Kalimdor is relatively recent. Historians note that this inland migration coincided suspiciously with the reemergence of the serpentine naga from the ocean depths. Some tribes appear to worship deep-sea monstrosities waiting in the oceanic fathoms. Whether fleeing darker terrors or preparing the world for their masters' awakening, their coordinated movement into freshwaters poses a rising threat.",sp=0},{t="A Hidden Intellect",b="Because their garbled language sounds like nonsensical bubbling to outsiders, many dismiss murlocs as mere feral pests. However, their coordinated ambushes, mastery of earth and water magic, and communal structures hint at a sharper intellect. Renowned explorer Brann Bronzebeard has suggested that murlocs are far smarter than civilized races realize, intentionally concealing their cunning. Their impact on global history is undeniable: it was a murloc raid upon the Darkspear tribe that spurred Thrall to intervene, forging the alliance between the orcs and trolls that built the modern Horde.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do murlocs attack all at once?",a="Murlocs rarely fight alone. They live in tight-knit, crowded settlements and respond in groups to intruders, swarming enemies with overwhelming numbers.",al={"murloc swarming","why do murlocs group up","murloc aggro"},it="danger"},{q="Are murlocs actually intelligent?",a="Yes, far more than their comical appearance implies. While their guttural, gurgling dialect is incomprehensible to most, murlocs craft weapons, organize strategic ambushes, and wield elemental water and earth magic. Scholars like Brann Bronzebeard believe murlocs intentionally allow outsiders to underestimate their intellect.",al={"are murlocs smart","murloc intelligence","can murlocs think"},it="power"},{q="Why are murlocs moving inland from the sea?",a="Originally creatures of the ocean floor, murlocs have steadily advanced into freshwater lakes and riverbeds. Historians suspect this migration was triggered by the resurgence of the naga or by deep-sea entities slumbering in the abyss whose awakening commands murlocs to secure the land.",al={"why are murlocs on land","murloc migration","why are murlocs everywhere"},it="why_here"},{q="How did murlocs help form the Horde?",a="During the Third War, a tribe of murlocs serving the sea witch Zar'jira ambushed the Darkspear trolls on their island home. Warchief Thrall and his orc warriors fought alongside Sen'jin to defend the trolls. This battle forged the unbreakable bond between orcs and the Darkspear tribe, laying the foundation of the modern Horde.",al={"murloc horde origin","murlocs and darkspear","thrall murloc island"},it="conflict"},{q="What are mur'gul?",a="Mur'gul are murlocs encountered during the Third War. Despite their strange appearance and name, they are living creatures.",al={"mur'gul","warcraft iii murlocs","murgul identity"}},{q="What is that iconic sound murlocs make?",a="When engaging prey or raising the alarm, murlocs let out a loud, wet, undulating gargle (often rendered as 'Aaaaaughibbrgubugbugrguburgle' or 'RwlRwlRwlRwl'). It serves as both an aggressive psychological weapon and an instant call for their pack to converge.",al={"murloc noise","murloc battle cry","rwlrwlrwl"}},{q="What are murlocs?",a="Murlocs are a bipedal, amphibious race found clustering along shores, lakes, and rivers across Azeroth. Averaging around four feet in height, they possess bulbous bodies, slimy skin, clawed digits, and mouths lined with razor-sharp fangs. While often dismissed as primitive fish-beasts due to their gurgling tongue, they construct communal settlements, wield handcrafted weaponry, and channel earth and water magic.",al={"what is a murloc","murloc race","who are the murlocs"},it="identity",add="87c77fd7"},{q="Why are murlocs hostile to travelers?",a="Murlocs are deeply insular creatures that shun dry-land mortals and hold almost no tolerance for other races. They fiercely protect their nesting grounds, egg caches, and food stores from outsiders. Combined with their habit of living in tightly packed encampments, any trespasser venturing near their shoreline settlements is quickly swarmed and driven off by the entire tribe.",al={"why do murlocs attack","murloc aggression","why are murlocs aggressive"},it="hostile",add="033f2da0"},{q="Where did murlocs come from?",a="Though they only recently invaded Azeroth's inland shores, murlocs are an ancient people. Their early forebears inhabited the abyssal depths of the world's oceans long before surface-dwellers ever knew of them, possibly predating even the trolls. In recent years, they have migrated steadily out of the oceanic abyss and adapted to freshwater waterways across the continents.",al={"murloc origin","history of murlocs","where do murlocs come from"},it="history",add="630a3c8a"},{q="Who are murlocs allied with?",a="Murlocs generally isolate themselves, but scholars suspect their emergence is tied to darker ocean powers. Some tribes revere sea witches or possibly slumbering deep-sea monstrosities waiting in the oceanic fathoms, carrying out rituals in their name.",al={"who do murlocs work for","murloc masters","murloc allies"},it="allies",add="b6922777"}},ang={["race:Troll"]="The Darkspear tribe shares a bitter history with murlocs, having faced capture and ritual sacrifice by coastal tribes before joining Warchief Thrall.",["race:Orc"]="Thrall's warriors fought murlocs while rescuing the Darkspear trolls at sea, learning early on that their goofy demeanor hides savage brutality.",["faction:Alliance"]="Settlers across Westfall, Darkshore, and Lakeshire frequently find their shipping, farms, and docks besieged by encroaching murloc tribes.",["class:Shaman"]="Murloc mystics possess an innate, if crude, connection to water and earth elementals, bending the local spirits to bolster their shoreline villages."},kw={"brann bronzebeard","coastal","fin","mur'gul","murloc","rwlrwlrwl","shoreline","tadpole"},rel={"topic:naga","topic:darkspear-tribe","npc:thrall","zone:westfall","subzone:westfall-lighthouse","npc:brann-bronzebeard","topic:scourge","topic:gnoll"},src={"Murloc"}}
E["topic:naga"] = {t="topic",n="Naga",z="general",s="The naga are a monstrous, serpentine race dwelling beneath the ocean's depths, ruled by their ancient empress, Queen Azshara. Once the Highborne elite of the Kaldorei Empire, they were warped into amphibious horrors during the Great Sundering ten thousand years ago. Recently emerging from the dark waters, they have seized shorelines across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms to reclaim their forgotten surface empire.",h="The naga are a monstrous, serpentine race dwelling beneath the ocean's depths, ruled by their ancient...",sec={{t="Fall of the Highborne",b="Ten millennia ago, the Highborne nobility of the Kaldorei Empire ruled supreme from their grand capital of Zin-Azshari. Consumed by an obsession with arcane power, Queen Azshara and her inner court tapped the Well of Eternity to draw the gaze of the Dark Titan Sargeras, inviting the catastrophic invasion known as the War of the Ancients. When the Well of Eternity collapsed into ruin, triggering the Great Sundering, the palace slipped into the crushing abyss. Facing utter annihilation, Azshara bargained with the malevolent Old Gods deep below. In exchange for survival, she and her followers were transformed into cold, scaly, serpentine beings bound to the deeps.",sp=0},{t="Depths of Nazjatar",b="Submerged at the seafloor beneath the swirling fury of the Maelstrom, the survivors erected the aquatic empire of Nazjatar. Nursing a ten-thousand-year grudge against the surface world, the naga preserved their Highborne arrogance while mutating into distinct casts: hulking, brute-like warriors and multi-armed, spell-weaving sea witches. Under Azshara's unyielding gaze, they perfected their mastery over sea creatures, storm magic, and frost sorcery, slowly rebuilding their strength in wait for an opportunity to strike back against land-dwellers.",sp=0},{t="The Resurfacing",b="The naga broke their ten-thousand-year silence during the Third War. Thrall's Horde first encountered the sea witch Zar'jira in the Great Sea, while Lady Vashj answered the summons of Illidan Stormrage, marching naga forces across the ruins of Dalaran. Across the world today, naga encampments encroach aggressively upon coastlines in Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, Dustwallow Marsh, Hillsbrad, Arathi, and Stranglethorn. Driven by ancient contempt and whispers of the Old Gods, they forge quiet alliances with the Twilight's Hammer and savage satyrs to claim what was once theirs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What were the naga before they were transformed?",a="The naga were once the Highborne, the aristocratic upper class of the ancient night elven empire. Ten thousand years ago, during the Great Sundering that destroyed the Well of Eternity, Queen Azshara and her courtiers drowned in the collapsing sea. The Old Gods offered them salvation, mutating their bodies into serpentine, aquatic abominations.",al={"what are naga","naga origin","who were the naga"},it="origin"},{q="Who rules the naga?",a="The naga are ruled absolute by Queen Azshara, the former empress of the Kaldorei. From her underwater city of Nazjatar beneath the Maelstrom, she directs her sea witches and warriors. Even after thousands of years of monstrous transformation, the naga venerate her with fanatical devotion.",al={"naga leader","naga queen","who leads the naga"},it="leader"},{q="Why are naga attacking the beaches and coasts now?",a="After hiding beneath the waves for ten millennia, the naga resurfaced during the Third War. They believe all of ancient Kalimdor rightfully belongs to them as the true heirs of the Kaldorei Empire. Working under the guidance of Queen Azshara and the Old Gods, they seek to subjugate coastal territories and conquer the surface races.",al={"why are naga on land","naga invasion","why did naga return"},it="why_here"},{q="Why do male and female naga look so different?",a="Naga society has severe physical dimorphism. Naga females retain the intellectual and magical cunning of their Highborne ancestry, often possessing four arms and immense talent for arcane, frost, and storm spellcasting. Naga males are larger, hulking brutes covered in heavy scales and fins, built primarily for physical warfare and brute violence.",al={"male vs female naga","naga differences","naga sea witches"}},{q="What is the relationship between the naga and Illidan Stormrage?",a="Following the Third War, Illidan summoned deep-sea allies using powerful magic. Lady Vashj answered his call with an army of naga, honoring ancient Highborne ties. Naga forces assisted Illidan in the Broken Isles, Lordaeron, and beyond.",al={"why did naga help illidan","illidan naga","lady vashj illidan"},it="allies"},{q="Where can naga be encountered right now?",a="Naga hold beachheads along numerous coastlines. In Kalimdor, they heavily infest Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, and Dustwallow Marsh. In the Eastern Kingdoms, their raiding parties and altars can be discovered along the shores of Hillsbrad Foothills, Arathi Highlands, and Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"naga locations","where to find naga","naga zones"},it="place"},{q="What are the naga?",a="The naga are a cruel, serpentine race of aquatic beings who dwell in the ocean depths and have recently invaded the coasts of Azeroth. Once the aristocratic Highborne of the ancient night elf empire, they were transformed ten thousand years ago after the Great Sundering drowned their civilization. They possess formidable mastery over water, frost, and arcane sorcery, wielding heavy tridents and slithering upon muscular, scaled tails.",al={"who are the naga","what is a naga","naga race"},it="identity",add="443d0bbf"},{q="Why are naga hostile to land dwellers?",a="The naga harbor bitter resentment over their ancient banishment beneath the waves. Having lost the empire they once ruled as Highborne nobility, their minds and hearts were warped with hatred toward the surface world. They view air-breathing races as inferior usurpers of their rightful lands. Now driven to reclaim their place and serve their dark masters, they show no mercy to anyone trespassing upon the shores they claim.",al={"why do naga attack","why are naga aggressive","why do naga hate us"},it="hostile",add="b021c83f"},{q="What is the history of the naga?",a="Over ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara's Highborne courtiers brought about the War of the Ancients by reckless use of the Well of Eternity. When the Well collapsed during the Great Sundering, their capital sank into the ocean depths. Facing drowning, Azshara struck a pact with the Old Gods, who twisted the survivors into serpentine horrors. From the underwater city of Nazjatar beneath the Maelstrom, they spent millennia plotting their return.",al={"where did naga come from","naga history","history of naga"},it="history",add="14259e40"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="To the night elves, naga are a grotesque and agonizing reminder of the Highborne hubris that shattered Ancient Kalimdor. Seeing Azshara's former court slithering onto the shores of Darkshore and Ashenvale is a bitter call to purge their ancient kin.",["race:Troll"]="The Darkspear trolls hold an intense hatred for the naga ever since the sea witch Zar'jira drove them from their island home during the Great Sea voyage, an attack that claimed the life of their elder leader, Sen'jin.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance remembers the naga from the ruins of Dalaran, where Lady Vashj's creatures captured survivors and incited the division between the blood elves and Grand Marshal Garithos's forces.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall and the Horde fought off naga raiders from the Darkspear Isles to the shores of Dustwallow Marsh, where Lady Darkscale butchered human settlements and assaulted Durotar's outskirts."},kw={"azshara","highborne","naga","nazjatar","old gods","sea witch","sundering","vashj"},rel={"topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:old-god","zone:azshara","zone:darkshore","zone:dustwallow","topic:third-war"},src={"Naga"}}
E["topic:new-horde"] = {t="topic",n="New Horde",z="general",s="The New Horde is the modern coalition forged under Warchief Thrall, departing from the bloodthirsty, demon-corrupted Old Horde of the First and Second Wars. Founded upon shamanic tradition, honor, and mutual survival, it unites the liberated orcs, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren tribes of Kalimdor, and a tenuous pact with the Forsaken in Lordaeron.",h="The New Horde is the modern coalition forged under Warchief Thrall, departing from the bloodthirsty...",sec={{t="A Legacy Reborn",b="The original Horde was a weapon crafted by the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, fueling the orcs with demonic bloodlust and driving them across the Dark Portal. After the Second War ended in devastation, surviving orcs were rounded up into Alliance internment camps, sinking into a soul-draining lethargy. Through the efforts of Orgrim Doomhammer, Grommash Hellscream, and the young shaman Thrall, the orcs cast off their demonic shackles, rediscovered their ancestral shamanism, and fought their way to freedom.",sp=0},{t="Crossing the Great Sea",b="Following prophetic visions from the mysterious Prophet, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to the untamed continent of Kalimdor. Along the way, the Horde welcomed Sen'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls. Upon arriving on Kalimdor, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof and the nomadic tauren, helping drive back hostile centaur tribes. Together, these disparate peoples fought alongside humans and night elves at Mount Hyjal, breaking the Burning Legion's invasion and carving out their new home of Durotar.",sp=0},{t="The Modern Coalition",b="Today, the New Horde strives to build a durable society from their fortified capital of Orgrimmar. While anchored by the strong bond between orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren, survival has forced Thrall to accept uncomfortable alliances—chief among them Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken in the ruins of Lordaeron. Thrall pursues an uneasy peace with the Alliance, but old enmities, resource shortages, and hawkish elements among both factions keep borders simmering on the brink of war.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes the New Horde different from the Old Horde?",a="The Old Horde was ruled by demonic corruption, dark warlocks, and brutal conquest during the First and Second Wars. The New Horde, founded by Thrall, explicitly rejected the Burning Legion, renewed the orcs' lost shamanic roots, and emphasizes honor, cultural survival, and self-determination.",al={"old horde vs new horde","how is this horde different","thrall horde difference"}},{q="Who leads the New Horde?",a="Thrall, son of Durotan, serves as Warchief. He governs from the Valley of Wisdom in Orgrimmar, working in close council with High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren and Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear trolls.",al={"warchief","who is the leader","thrall's role"},it="leader"},{q="Which races belong to the New Horde right now?",a="The core of the New Horde consists of the orcs, the Darkspear tribe of trolls, and the tauren of Mulgore. Across the sea in the Eastern Kingdoms, the undead Forsaken under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner hold membership through a pragmatic military alliance.",al={"horde races","who is in the horde","horde members"},it="allies"},{q="Why did the Forsaken join the New Horde?",a="Surrounded by the Scarlet Crusade, hostile humans, and Scourge remnants, Sylvanas Windrunner sought powerful allies for survival. The tauren, led by Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, advocated for them in Orgrimmar, believing the Forsaken could be redeemed, convincing Thrall to accept them into a mutual pact of convenience.",al={"undead in the horde","why forsaken horde","why are undead with thrall"}},{q="Are the orcs still corrupted by demon blood?",a="No. When Grommash Hellscream and Thrall confronted the pit lord Mannoroth in Ashenvale, Grom struck down the demon at the cost of his own life. Mannoroth's demise shattered the blood curse once and for all, liberating every orc from demonic enslavement.",al={"demon blood","blood curse","are orcs still evil"}},{q="Is the New Horde currently at war with the Alliance?",a="Open, all-out war is officially held in check by an uneasy truce established between Thrall and Lady Jaina Proudmoore of Theramore. However, bitter skirmishes rage across Ashenvale, Alterac Valley, and the Arathi Basin, as mistrust and resource disputes continually threaten to erupt into open conflict.",al={"are horde and alliance at war","peace with alliance","horde alliance conflict"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the New Horde?",a="The New Horde is the coalition of races gathered under Warchief Thrall. Built upon the ideals of honor, shamanism, and mutual survival, it stands distinct from the bloodthirsty demonic puppet of the First and Second Wars. Today, its core consists of the free orcs who broke their demonic lethargy, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren of Kalimdor, and a tenuous pact with the Forsaken of Lordaeron.",al={"what is new horde","who are new horde","new horde faction"},it="identity",add="48a04cd3",nx={8}},{q="Where did the New Horde come from?",a="The seeds of the New Horde were sown when Thrall, Orgrim Doomhammer, and Grommash Hellscream liberated the lethargic orcs from human internment camps across Lordaeron. Guided by a prophetic vision, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to the forgotten continent of Kalimdor. Along their journey, they allied with Sen'jin's Darkspear trolls and later Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren, establishing a permanent home in the harsh lands of Durotar.",al={"history of new horde","origin of new horde","how was new horde founded"},it="history",add="7f9c7de9"},{q="Why is it called the New Horde?",a="The name distinguishes Thrall's renewed alliance from the Old Horde that invaded Azeroth through the Dark Portal. While the Old Horde was bound to the Burning Legion's fel corruption and endless slaughter, the New Horde seeks redemption through ancient shamanic traditions, honor, and freedom from demonic dominion.",al={"why new horde","new horde name meaning"},it="name",add="a76f0096"}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The New Horde is a sanctuary of outcasts. Membership offers mutual defense, cultural survival, and a shared oath of honor under Warchief Thrall.",["faction:Alliance"]="Many in the Alliance still view the New Horde through the blood-soaked memories of the First and Second Wars, struggling to believe orcs can ever abandon their violent past.",["race:Orc"]="The New Horde represents redemption: a chance to shed the lethargy of the internment camps, honor the sacrifice of Grom Hellscream, and live as free shamanic warriors.",["class:Shaman"]="Shamanism is the spiritual backbone of the New Horde, reviving ancient traditions connecting mortals with the elements that were almost lost during the demonic corruption."},kw={"darkspear","doomhammer","durotar","new horde","orgrimmar","shamanism","tauren","thrall","warchief"},rel={"topic:horde","npc:thrall","topic:old-horde","topic:tauren","topic:troll","topic:grommash-hellscream","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","quest:787"},src={"New Horde"}}
E["topic:night-elf"] = {t="topic",n="Night elf",z="general",s="The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient and reclusive race native to the forests of northern Kalimdor. Once the masters of a sprawling magical empire, they abandoned arcane magic following the catastrophic War of the Ancients and turned to the worship of Elune and the path of druidism. In the wake of the Third War, having sacrificed their immortality to stop the Burning Legion, they joined the Alliance to defend their ancestral lands from Horde encroachment.",h="The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient and reclusive race native to the forests of northern Kalimdor.",sec={{t="Children of the Stars",b="Millennia ago, an inquisitive nocturnal tribe gathered around the shores of the Well of Eternity, a vast font of raw arcane energy. Over generations, the Well's cosmic magic transformed them into tall, violet-skinned, long-lived beings who called themselves the kaldorei—'children of the stars.' Guiding their early society were the moon goddess Elune and the forest demigod Cenarius, who nurtured their deep connection to the living wilds of Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="The Empire and the Sundering",b="As centuries passed, the ruling caste—the Highborne, led by Queen Azshara—became obsessed with unlocking the Well's infinite arcane secrets. Their reckless use of sorcery built breathtaking wonders, but it ultimately caught the attention of the Burning Legion. The ensuing demonic invasion ended in the Great Sundering: the Well imploded, shattering ancient Kalimdor into fragmented continents and destroying Azshara's realm. Horrified by the calamity, the surviving kaldorei strictly outlawed arcane magic under penalty of death or exile.",sp=0},{t="The Long Vigil and the New Age",b="For ten thousand years, the night elves retreated into seclusion. Druids slumbered in the Emerald Dream to heal the world while the Priestesses of Elune and Sentinels stood guard over Kalimdor's woodlands beneath the World Tree Nordrassil. This isolation ended during the Third War when the Legion returned to attack Mount Hyjal. The kaldorei sacrificed their immortality to defeat Archimonde. Now, led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and Archdruid Fandral Staghelm, the night elves dwell in their young World Tree, Teldrassil, and fight alongside the Alliance to safeguard Ashenvale.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does 'kaldorei' mean?",a="In their native language of Darnassian, kaldorei translates to 'children of the stars.' It was the name adopted by the early night elves after settling around the Well of Eternity and dedicating themselves to the moon goddess Elune.",al={"kaldorei meaning","what is kaldorei","night elf native name"}},{q="Where did the night elves originate?",a="Long before the Sundering, a curious nocturnal tribe settled along the radiant banks of the Well of Eternity. Exposed to the lake's raw magical energies over generations, their bodies and minds transformed, turning them into the graceful, long-lived kaldorei.",al={"night elf origins","where do night elves come from","are elves evolved trolls"}},{q="Why don't night elves practice arcane magic?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne sorcerers drew the demonic Burning Legion to Azeroth through their reckless manipulation of the Well of Eternity. The resulting war shattered the world during the Great Sundering. Afterwards, the night elves outlawed arcane practice to prevent demons from ever finding Azeroth again.",al={"why no night elf mages","night elf arcane ban","why did night elves ban magic"}},{q="Are night elves still immortal?",a="No. The night elves gained their immortality and agelessness from the World Tree Nordrassil, which had been blessed by the Dragon Aspects. During the Battle of Mount Hyjal in the Third War, the kaldorei detonated Nordrassil's ancestral power to destroy the demon lord Archimonde, forfeiting their immortality in the process.",al={"are night elves immortal","did night elves lose immortality","how long do night elves live"}},{q="Where is the night elf capital located?",a="The night elf capital is Darnassus, constructed atop the massive canopy of Teldrassil, a colossal World Tree planted off the northwest coast of Kalimdor following the Third War.",al={"night elf capital","where do night elves live","darnassus location"}},{q="Why did the night elves join the Alliance?",a="After centuries of isolation, the kaldorei fought alongside humans and dwarves against the Burning Legion at Mount Hyjal. Following the war, the Horde began heavily logging Ashenvale's sacred forests for lumber, prompting the night elves to formalize an alliance with the Eastern Kingdoms to defend their borders.",al={"why are night elves alliance","night elf alliance allegiance","why join alliance"}},{q="Which classes can night elves be?",a="Night elves train as warriors, hunters, rogues, priests and druids. They shun the arcane, so there are no night elf mages or warlocks.",al={"night elf classes","what classes can night elves be"}}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Your people carry the wisdom and burdens of ten millennia, navigating a perilous new reality where mortality is real, your ancestral forests are under siege, and ancient traditions are tested.",["race:Troll"]="Troll history remembers the elves as ancient enemies, a bitter rivalry that stretches back thousands of years.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde's aggressive lumber operations in Ashenvale have made the fierce Sentinels and watchful druids of the kaldorei some of your most relentless and deadly adversaries on Kalimdor.",["class:Druid"]="Night elf tradition shaped modern druidism under the demigod Cenarius, binding kaldorei guardians to the Emerald Dream and the defense of the natural balance."},kw={"ashenvale","cenarius","darnassus","elune","kaldorei","night elf","nordrassil","sundering","teldrassil","well of eternity"},rel={"zone:darnassus","topic:elune","topic:cenarius","topic:nordrassil","topic:highborne","zone:ashenvale","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","npc:malfurion-stormrage"},src={"Night elf"}}
E["topic:nordrassil"] = {t="topic",n="Nordrassil",z="general",s="Nordrassil, the 'Crown of the Heavens,' is the original World Tree planted atop Mount Hyjal to shield the second Well of Eternity. Blessed by the Great Dragon Aspects, it granted the night elves ageless immortality and a deep connection to the Emerald Dream for ten thousand years. Its sacrifice during the climax of the Third War broke Archimonde's assault, leaving the tree wounded and the night elves mortal.",h="Nordrassil, the 'Crown of the Heavens,' is the original World Tree planted atop Mount Hyjal to shield the...",sec={{t="Crown of the Heavens",b="Ten thousand years ago, in the ashes of the Great Sundering, Illidan Stormrage cast vials of the original Well of Eternity into a mountaintop lake atop Mount Hyjal, birthing a new font of arcane power. To prevent this raw magic from corrupting the world or drawing the Burning Legion anew, the Dragon Aspect Alexstrasza planted an acorn from the Mother Tree, G'Hanir, directly over the waters. From it grew Nordrassil. The roots spread deep beneath Azeroth, sealing the Well's volatile energies and distributing gentle, life-giving vigor across the continents.",sp=0},{t="The Aspects' Gifts",b="Nordrassil became the spiritual and cultural heart of night elf civilization through the blessings of three Great Dragon Aspects. Alexstrasza imbued the tree with peerless vigor, extending vitality to the kaldorei. Ysera bound the tree to the Emerald Dream, allowing night elven druids to enter her slumbering realm with effortless clarity. Crucially, Nozdormu the Timeless One cast his blessing across its boughs: as long as the colossal tree stood, the night elves would never age or fall prey to natural disease, gifting an entire race with enduring immortality.",sp=0},{t="The Fall at Mount Hyjal",b="Nordrassil's tranquility ended with the Third War, when the demon lord Archimonde marched upon Mount Hyjal to tear the tree apart and claim the Well's infinite power. With mortal defenders pushed to the brink, Malfurion Stormrage made the ultimate choice. Sounding the Horn of Cenarius, he called upon thousands of nature spirits and unleashed the stored enchantments within Nordrassil itself. The ensuing cataclysmic blast obliterated Archimonde and decimated the Legion. Though Nordrassil survived to heal slowly, its enchantments shattered—stripping the night elves of their beloved immortality.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Nordrassil mean?",a="In Darnassian, Nordrassil translates to 'Crown of the Heavens.' It earned this name due to its monumental height, towering high above the summit of sacred Mount Hyjal.",al={"nordrassil translation","what does nordrassil mean in darnassian","nordrassil name meaning"},it="name"},{q="Where is Nordrassil located?",a="Nordrassil stands at the summit of Mount Hyjal in northern Kalimdor, rooted directly inside the waters of the second Well of Eternity created by Illidan Stormrage.",al={"nordrassil location","where is the original world tree","where is nordrassil"},it="place"},{q="Why did the night elves lose their immortality?",a="To defeat the demon lord Archimonde at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, Malfurion Stormrage unleashed the magical wards and blessings bound within Nordrassil. The resulting detonation obliterated Archimonde but burned away the blessings of the Dragon Aspects, ending Nozdormu's spell of eternal life.",al={"why aren't night elves immortal anymore","night elf mortality","loss of immortality"},it="power"},{q="How is Nordrassil related to Teldrassil?",a="Nordrassil is the original World Tree atop Mount Hyjal. Teldrassil is a much younger World Tree grown off the coast of Kalimdor by Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and the Circle of the Ancients, planted in a desperate bid to replace Nordrassil and reclaim their lost immortality.",al={"nordrassil vs teldrassil","difference between teldrassil and nordrassil","is teldrassil nordrassil"},it="ties"},{q="Did Nordrassil die when Archimonde was defeated?",a="No. While the blast severely scorched and wounded the World Tree, Nordrassil's roots survived. The tree remains standing atop Mount Hyjal, slowly mending its wounds over time, even though its ancient dragon blessings were dispelled.",al={"is nordrassil dead","did nordrassil survive","nordrassil destroyed"},it="today"},{q="Which dragons blessed Nordrassil?",a="Nordrassil was blessed by three Dragon Aspects: Alexstrasza the Life-Binder, who planted it and gave it vitality; Ysera the Dreamer, who linked it to the Emerald Dream; and Nozdormu the Timeless One, who granted the night elves eternal youth.",al={"who blessed nordrassil","dragon blessings world tree","which aspects blessed nordrassil"},it="allies"},{q="What is Nordrassil?",a="Nordrassil is the original World Tree of the night elves, standing atop Mount Hyjal above the second Well of Eternity. Grown to shield and contain the Well's raw arcane magic, its massive roots spread life-giving energy throughout Azeroth. For millennia, it served as the sacred heart of night elf civilization.",al={"what is nordrassil","who is nordrassil","nordrassil identity"},it="identity",add="1a91b9d6"},{q="What is the history of Nordrassil?",a="After the Great Sundering, the dragon Alexstrasza planted an acorn from the Mother Tree, G'Hanir, into the second Well of Eternity created atop Mount Hyjal. The Great Aspects Alexstrasza, Ysera, and Nozdormu blessed the growing tree, binding the night elves to nature, granting druids direct access to the Emerald Dream, and bestowing immortality upon the Kaldorei. Nordrassil stood unharmed for ten thousand years until the Third War, when Malfurion Stormrage detonated the tree's ancestral energies to obliterate the demon lord Archimonde.",al={"history of nordrassil","where did nordrassil come from","how was nordrassil created"},it="history",add="734b781a"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Nordrassil was the cradle of your civilization for ten millennia. Its sacrifice saved the world from Archimonde, but left you grappling with aging, sickness, and mortality for the very first time.",["class:Druid"]="Rooted above the second Well of Eternity, Nordrassil's bond with Ysera allowed druids to effortlessly cross into the Emerald Dream. Its scarring remains a stark reminder of the cost of defending nature.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance's presence at Mount Hyjal forged the desperate coalition that shielded Nordrassil from the Burning Legion, securing Kalimdor's salvation.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Thrall led the Horde to the slopes of Mount Hyjal alongside humans and night elves, buying Malfurion the time needed to spring the trap at the base of Nordrassil."},kw={"alexstrasza","archimonde","hyjal","immortality","malfurion","nordrassil","nozdormu","teldrassil","well of eternity","world tree","ysera"},rel={"quest:940","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:third-war","topic:emerald-dream","npc:fandral-staghelm","topic:druid","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius"},src={"Nordrassil"}}
E["topic:old-god"] = {t="topic",n="Old God",z="general",s="The Old Gods are colossal, malevolent horrors of flesh and madness who ruled Azeroth in its primordial dawn. Though vanquished and bound deep beneath the crust by the Titans, their ancient whispers still corrode mortal minds, twist nature, and drive doomsday cults to unearth their prisons.",h="The Old Gods are colossal, malevolent horrors of flesh and madness who ruled Azeroth in its primordial dawn.",sec={{t="Masters of the Primordial World",b="Long before mortal records began, Azeroth was dominated by the Old Gods, colossal entities of chaotic flesh such as C'Thun. They embedded themselves into the planet's crust, commanding elemental armies and insectoid swarms. When the Titans arrived, their armies fought an apocalyptic war against them. Rather than destroying them, the Titans bound the Old Gods in vast, subterranean prisons to keep them dormant forever.",sp=0},{t="Whispers from the Deep",b="Even chained beneath miles of stone and enchanted wards, the Old Gods have never truly slept. Their psychic malice seeps continually into the world, corrupting the vulnerable. It was their collective malice that slowly drove Neltharion the Earth-Warder mad during the War of the Ancients, twisting the noble dragon into Deathwing. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne drowned beneath the raging waters of the Great Sundering, an Old God answered their desperation, transforming them into the serpentine naga.",sp=0},{t="Stirrings in the Sands",b="In the modern era, mortal civilizations treat the Old Gods as myth, yet their prisons are fracturing. Nearly a millennium ago, the insectoid qiraji swarmed out of the southern desert of Silithus in the War of the Shifting Sands, serving their master C'Thun until the night elves and dragons sealed them behind the Scarab Wall. Today, ominous cults like the Twilight's Hammer gather in dark corners across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, performing foul rites to shatter the Titans' bindings and hasten the end of all mortal life.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What exactly are the Old Gods?",a="They are ancient, eldritch monstrosities of flesh and madness who dominated Azeroth in primordial times. They feed on chaos and seek to corrupt the world, driving mortals and elementals toward madness.",al={"what is an old god","old gods lore","who are the old gods"},it="identity"},{q="How many Old Gods were there on Azeroth?",a="No mortal knows for certain. The best known is C'Thun, imprisoned beneath Ahn'Qiraj in Silithus; others lie chained in subterranean vaults across the globe.",al={"how many old gods","list of old gods","names of old gods"}},{q="Why didn't the Titans just kill them all?",a="The Old Gods had burrowed so deeply into Azeroth's bedrock that tearing them out risked destroying the world itself, so the Titans built prisons instead.",al={"why didn't titans kill old gods","why imprison old gods","why spare old gods"}},{q="Are the Old Gods connected to Deathwing and the naga?",a="Yes. The Old Gods whispered into the mind of Neltharion the Earth-Warder until he snapped and became Deathwing. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne sank during the Great Sundering, an Old God answered their cries and remade them into the naga.",al={"did old gods make naga","old gods deathwing","who corrupted neltharion"},it="ties"},{q="Who are the Twilight's Hammer?",a="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to serving the Old Gods. They seek to unbind the imprisoned entities, unleash the elemental lords, and plunge the world into total ruin.",al={"twilights hammer cult","who worships old gods","old god cultists"},it="allies"},{q="Where are the Old Gods imprisoned?",a="Their known prisons are scattered across the world, entombed deep beneath Titan ruins. For instance, C'Thun lies trapped beneath Ahn'Qiraj in Silithus, while others were bound beneath the northern reaches and deep subterranean foundations.",al={"where are old gods","old god locations","c'thun location"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are the Old Gods hostile to mortals?",a="The Old Gods exist solely to corrupt reality. To these primordial horrors, mortal life is little more than insects to be twisted, driven mad, or utterly enslaved. They hold no concept of peaceful coexistence; their dark whispers seek only to undermine your sanity and bend every living soul to their will.",al={"why do old gods attack","old god hostility","why are old gods evil"},it="hostile",add="6f130189"},{q="Where did the Old Gods come from?",a="No mortal knows where the Old Gods came from. They were already entrenched deep within the planet's crust, ruling primordial Azeroth with elemental armies and insectoid swarms, when the Titans arrived to defeat and seal them beneath the earth.",al={"old god origins","origin of the old gods","how were the old gods created"},it="history",add="2cc577ae"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The kaldorei fought the qiraji minion hordes in the War of the Shifting Sands, sealing Silithus behind the Scarab Wall.",["class:Priest"]="Priests who delve into shadow magic walk a perilous path, wary of the maddening whispers said to rise from the ancient horrors chained beneath the earth.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold a sacred duty to guard the Emerald Dream against the Emerald Nightmare, an unnatural corruption whose true source remains unknown.",["class:Warlock"]="Though warlocks primarily draw from fel magic and demons of the Burning Legion, some foolishly seek the forbidden curses and sacrificial rites left behind by the Old Gods' servants."},kw={"ahn'qiraj","c'thun","old god","qiraji","silithus","twilight's hammer"},rel={"subzone:scarab-wall","zone:silithus","topic:twilight-s-hammer","topic:titan","topic:emerald-dream","topic:naga","npc:deathwing","topic:war-of-the-ancients"},src={"Old God"}}
E["topic:old-horde"] = {t="topic",n="Old Horde",z="general",s="The Old Horde was the blood-crazed coalition of orcish clans manipulated by demonic power into conquering Draenor and invading Azeroth. Led first by Blackhand and later by Orgrim Doomhammer, its path of destruction shaped the First and Second Wars until its decisive defeat by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Today, Thrall's New Horde seeks to overcome that dark legacy, while isolated remnants like the Dark Horde still cling to its bloody past.",h="The Old Horde was the blood-crazed coalition of orcish clans manipulated by demonic power into conquering...",sec={{t="Rise and Deception",b="Before it crossed into Azeroth, the Horde was forged on Draenor through demonic manipulation. The demon lord Kil'jaeden used the warlock Gul'dan and the elder shaman Ner'zhul to corrupt the proud, spiritual orc clans. As elemental spirits abandoned the warring orcs, shamanistic traditions were cast aside for warlock magic. Sealed by the consumption of the blood of Mannoroth, the clans surrendered to bloodlust, slaughtering the draenei and draining their homeland of life.",sp=0},{t="Invasion of Azeroth",b="With Draenor's lands withering away and resources exhausted, the warlock Gul'dan and the possessed human guardian Medivh opened the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The Horde poured through, igniting the First War against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Under Warchief Blackhand—and the covert strings of Gul'dan's Shadow Council—the Horde battered the southern realm. Toward the war's conclusion, Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand and purged the Shadow Council, taking command of the Horde and razing Stormwind to the ground.",sp=0},{t="Collapse and Demonic Hangover",b="Orgrim Doomhammer drove the Horde north across the seas into the Second War, forging alliances with forest trolls and goblin mercenaries. However, at the height of the siege of Lordaeron, Gul'dan deserted the war effort with his loyalists to seek the Tomb of Sargeras. Fractured and reeling from the betrayal, the Horde was pushed back to Blackrock Mountain and crushed by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Defeated, their portal destroyed, surviving orcs were herded into internment camps, afflicted with lethargy as the demonic blood haze faded.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Old Horde and the New Horde?",a="The Old Horde was driven by demonic corruption, warlock magic, and conquest under the influence of the Burning Legion and Gul'dan's Shadow Council. Thrall's New Horde, established after escaping the internment camps, returned to traditional shamanism and honor, forming a coalition of survivors rather than an invading army.",al={"old horde vs new horde","difference in hordes","thrall's horde vs old horde"},it="identity"},{q="Who led the Old Horde?",a="Warchief Blackhand initially led the Horde during the genocide of the draenei and most of the First War, though he served as a figurehead for Gul'dan. Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand in a duel, assumed the mantle of Warchief, and led the Horde through the end of the First War and the Second War.",al={"who was warchief","old horde leader","who led the first horde"},it="leader"},{q="Why did the orcs drink demon blood?",a="Gul'dan offered the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth to the clan chieftains. He promised them overwhelming power and victory, concealing the fact that drinking it bound their souls to the Burning Legion in reckless bloodlust.",al={"why did orcs drink fel blood","mannoroth blood","orc corruption reason"},it="motive"},{q="What was the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council was a secret order of warlocks, necrolytes, and assassins founded by Gul'dan. It controlled Warchief Blackhand and steered the Horde from the shadows according to the will of the Burning Legion until Orgrim Doomhammer uncovered and purged them.",al={"who were the shadow council","gul'dan council","shadow council role"}},{q="How was the Old Horde defeated?",a="During the Second War, Gul'dan abandoned the Horde at the gates of Lordaeron to claim godhood at the Tomb of Sargeras, fracturing the army. The Alliance of Lordaeron rallied, pushing Doomhammer back to Blackrock Mountain, capturing him, and herding the demoralized remnants into internment camps.",al={"how did the old horde lose","second war ending","defeat of the orcs"},it="history"},{q="Do any remnants of the Old Horde still exist?",a="Yes. Splinter factions such as the 'Dark Horde' holding Blackrock Spire, led by Rend Blackhand, reject Thrall's New Horde and cling to the old brutal ways of conquest, allying with dragons and forest trolls.",al={"is the old horde still around","dark horde","remnants of old horde"},it="today"},{q="Why is it called the Old Horde?",a="During its existence, it was simply known as the Horde or the Orcish Horde. Historians and travelers now call it the Old Horde or the first Horde to distinguish it from the New Horde founded by Thrall. Thrall's Horde broke free from demonic corruption and returned to the spiritual ways of shamanism, leaving behind the dark legacy of the original faction.",al={"why old horde","old horde name meaning","why called old horde"},it="name",add="354eaa00"},{q="Who did the Old Horde ally with?",a="Originally, the Old Horde consisted solely of the orcish clans of Draenor, though they were soon joined by ogres from their homeworld. During the Second War on Azeroth, their ranks expanded further to include the forest trolls of the Amani Empire and mercenary goblins who provided engineering, Zeppelins, and munitions to bolster their conquest.",al={"old horde allies","who fought with the old horde","old horde races"},it="allies",add="b4ccff16"}},ang={["race:Orc"]="For orcs, the Old Horde represents a shameful era of demonic slavery and ruin, whose bitter legacy Thrall strives to overcome through honor and shamanism.",["race:Human"]="Humanity still bears the deep scars of the Old Horde's invasion, which leveled Stormwind City and ravaged the lands of Lordaeron during the First and Second Wars.",["class:Shaman"]="The formation of the Old Horde led the elemental spirits of Draenor to turn their backs on the orcs, a spiritual breach healed only recently under Thrall's guidance.",["class:Warlock"]="Gul'dan and his Shadow Council spread warlock magic through the orc clans while building the Old Horde, drawing on the corrupting powers of the Burning Legion.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron was created specifically to unite the disparate mortal kingdoms against the existential threat posed by the Old Horde."},kw={"blackhand","dark portal","draenor","first war","gul'dan","mannoroth","old horde","orgrim doomhammer","second war","shadow council"},rel={"npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:shadow-council","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","subzone:dark-portal","topic:blackrock-clan","topic:new-horde"},src={"Old Horde"}}
E["topic:orc"] = {t="topic",n="Orc",z="general",s="Originally a shamanistic clan people hailing from the alien world of Draenor, the orcs were deceived and corrupted into a bloodthirsty conquering force by the demonic Burning Legion. After invading Azeroth and suffering eventual defeat and internment, they were rallied by Warchief Thrall to reclaim their spiritual heritage. In this new era, the orcs lead the Horde from their newly carved nation of Durotar, striving for honor while grappling with the lingering shadow of their demonic past.",h="Originally a shamanistic clan people hailing from the alien world of Draenor, the orcs were deceived and...",sec={{t="Children of Draenor",b="Before their fall, orcs lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers and spiritual clans across the rugged landscapes of Draenor. Their culture was grounded in ancestral reverence and deep communion with elemental spirits, guided primarily by revered shamans. Among the disparate clans were the Frostwolves, Warsongs, and Shadowmoon. Though physically formidable and deeply proud, traditional orcish society was defined by survival, honor, and harmony with the natural elements rather than mindless conquest.",sp=0},{t="Corruption and the Great Wars",b="The fate of the orcs shattered when Kil'jaeden and the warlock Gul'dan orchestrated their corruption. Tricked into drinking the blood of the demon Mannoroth, the orcs were seized by a supernatural bloodlust. Forged into the original Horde, they nearly eradicated Draenor's draenei before pouring through the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The First and Second Wars saw human kingdoms fall and orcish armies reach Lordaeron's gates, but internal treachery and the unified Alliance of Lordaeron eventually crushed the invaders, herding survivors into miserable internment camps.",sp=0},{t="A New Beginning in Kalimdor",b="Imprisonment stripped the orcs of their demonic frenzy, leaving them lethargic and broken until Thrall, an orc raised in human captivity, escaped. Reconnecting with shamanism, Thrall rallied the Frostwolf clan, freed his kin from the camps, and established a renewed Horde. Heeding prophetic warnings, he led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor. There, the sacrifice of Grom Hellscream finally broke Mannoroth's blood curse forever. Today, the orcs have carved out the arid realm of Durotar and built the fortress-city of Orgrimmar, striving to maintain their hard-won honor amidst renewed tensions with the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are orcs green?",a="No tale told in Azeroth says for certain. Orcs have been green-skinned for as long as this world has known them: every orc who came through the Dark Portal was green, and so are the orcs of Thrall's Horde today.",al={"why is orc skin green","orc green skin origin","brown orcs vs green orcs"}},{q="Where did the orcs come from?",a="Orcs are not native to Azeroth; they originated on the alien world of Draenor. They traveled to Azeroth through the Dark Portal when Gul'dan and the demon-possessed human sorcerer Medivh opened the rift between worlds.",al={"orc homeworld","are orcs native to azeroth","where are orcs from"}},{q="Who is the leader of the orcs?",a="The orcs are led by Warchief Thrall, son of Durotan. Raised by humans as a gladiator slave, Thrall rediscovered the ancestral art of shamanism, liberated the orc internment camps, and guided the new Horde to Kalimdor.",al={"orc warchief","orc faction leader","who leads the orcs"}},{q="What is the blood curse?",a="The blood curse was the demonic enslavement inflicted upon the orcs when they drank the blood of the annihilan Mannoroth. It instilled an uncontrollable bloodlust that fueled their wars, but it was finally shattered when Grom Hellscream sacrificed his life to slay Mannoroth.",al={"blood of mannoroth","orc bloodlust curse","mannoroth blood"}},{q="Why do the orcs live in Durotar?",a="Following the Third War, Thrall settled the orcs in the harsh, dry coastal realm he named Durotar in honor of his father, Durotan. Its punishing terrain serves as both penance for past sins and a forge to keep his people strong and resilient.",al={"why is durotar a desert","why orgrimmar","why durotar"}},{q="Are all orcs part of Thrall's Horde?",a="No. While Thrall leads the New Horde, rogue clans remain. The Dragonmaw clan still clings to their enslaver traditions in the Wetlands, while the Blackrock clan under Rend Blackhand continues to wage war from Blackrock Mountain, rejecting Thrall's peace.",al={"evil orcs","hostile orcs","are all orcs friendly to horde"}},{q="Which classes can orcs be?",a="Orcs train as warriors, hunters, rogues, shaman, mages and warlocks.",al={"orc classes","what classes can orcs be","can orcs be mages"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Orcs form the military and ideological backbone of the modern Horde, championing honor and survival alongside their troll, tauren, and Forsaken allies.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance races still view orcs with deep suspicion and lingering resentment, remembering the slaughter of the First and Second Wars and the destruction of Stormwind.",["class:Shaman"]="For orcs, shamanism is not merely magic—it represents their reclaimed cultural soul and spiritual redemption after decades of demonic servitude.",["class:Warlock"]="Orc warlocks are viewed with deep distrust and revulsion by their kin, as fel magic is the exact force that corrupted and enslaved their race."},kw={"bloodlust","draenor","durotar","fel","koshharg","mannoroth","orc","orcs","orgrimmar","shamanism","thrall"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:new-horde","zone:orgrimmar","zone:durotar","topic:burning-legion","subzone:dark-portal","topic:warsong-clan","topic:dragonmaw-clan"},src={"Orc"}}
E["topic:paladin"] = {t="topic",n="Paladin",z="general",s="Paladins are holy warriors who blend martial prowess with the power of the Holy Light. Clad in heavy plate, they protect the innocent, heal the wounded and strike down wicked foes. Humans and dwarves carry the Silver Hand's legacy for the Alliance, and among the Horde, Forsaken who served the Light in life still answer its call, though it burns them.",h="Paladins are holy warriors who blend martial prowess with the power of the Holy Light.",sec={{t="Origins in Lordaeron",b="The paladins were originally established during the Second War by Archbishop Alonsus Faol. Having witnessed the devastation wrought upon the pious Clerics of Northshire during the First War, Faol recognized that healers alone could not survive the brutality of orcish warfare. He gathered virtuous knights of Lordaeron and trained surviving clerics in the ways of martial combat and divine magic. Baptized as the Knights of the Silver Hand and led by Uther the Lightbringer, these champions provided both physical defense and spiritual renewal to the kingdoms of humanity.",sp=0},{t="Champions of the Holy Light",b="Where priests act as the voice of the Holy Light—preaching faith and tending the spirits of the common folk—paladins serve as its martial arm. Their profound conviction grants them miraculous powers: they project emboldening auras, seal sanctified blessings upon their allies, and mend fatal injuries. In combat against unholy horrors, the Light burns through their strikes, allowing paladins to incinerate undead and demonic entities with righteous fury.",sp=0},{t="Heirs of the Silver Hand",b="The fall of Lordaeron during the Third War scattered the original order, yet the ideals of the Silver Hand endure. In the south, the humans of Stormwind and the stalwart dwarves of Ironforge have taken up the mantle. Training in sanctuaries like the Cathedral of Light and the Hall of Mysteries, these paladins pledge their swords and shields to uphold justice and defend the Alliance from the gathering shadows.",sp=0},{t="Paladins of the Forsaken",b="Not every knight of Lordaeron stayed in the grave. Some Forsaken who served the Light in life still hold to their vows, and the Light still answers them, though it sears their dead flesh with every prayer. Aramis Hammerhand trains these risen paladins in the chapel at Deathknell, Shari Stilwell continues their instruction in Brill, and an order of Forsaken paladins, Tyr's Watch, holds Bandarion Keep in western Tirisfal. Theirs is a lonely road: they wield the very power that burned their people, and Sylvanas Windrunner tolerates their order while watching it closely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a priest and a paladin?",a="Priests serve as the voice of the Light, ministering to the faithful and offering pastoral guidance through prayer and ritual. Paladins serve as the hand and hammer of the Light, donning heavy plate armor and taking up weapons to directly enforce divine justice and defend the weak on the battlefield.",al={"paladin vs priest","difference between priest and paladin","how are paladins different from priests"}},{q="Who created the first paladins?",a="Archbishop Alonsus Faol created the order during the Second War. After seeing the Clerics of Northshire slaughtered during the First War, he recruited devout knights and trained surviving clerics to unite martial discipline with holy magic under the banner of the Knights of the Silver Hand.",al={"who made paladins","founder of paladins","origin of the silver hand"}},{q="Which races can be paladins?",a="Humans and dwarves train as paladins in the Alliance, and the Forsaken do in the Horde. The order began among humans, the dwarves of Ironforge embraced the Holy Light and joined it, and Forsaken who were knights and clerics in life still wield the Light after death, at the price of constant pain.",al={"can horde be paladins","who can be a paladin","paladin races","can undead be paladins","undead paladin","forsaken paladin"}},{q="Why are paladins effective against the undead?",a="The Holy Light is anathema to necrotic corruption and demonic energy. When paladins channel divine power, it purges blight and incinerates unholy creatures, making them the foremost champions against the Scourge and other foul beings.",al={"paladins vs undead","why does holy magic hurt undead","paladin scourge counter"}},{q="What happened to the original Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order suffered catastrophic losses during the Third War when the plague ravished Lordaeron. The brotherhood fragmented amid internal betrayal and political strife, though surviving members and new initiates in Stormwind and Ironforge continue to carry the title and ideals forward. Some of its fallen knights rose again as Forsaken, and a few still keep their oaths.",al={"what happened to silver hand","is silver hand gone","fall of the silver hand"}},{q="Can members of the Horde become paladins?",a="Only the Forsaken. Many were knights and clerics of Lordaeron before the plague, and some still keep their vows: the Light answers them, though it burns their dead flesh. Orcs, tauren and trolls follow shamans, druids and witch doctors rather than the knightly path of the Holy Light.",al={"horde paladins","can orcs be paladins","why no horde paladin","can forsaken be paladins","horde paladin races"}},{q="Why does the Light hurt Forsaken paladins?",a="The Holy Light opposes the dark energy that sustains the undead. A Forsaken paladin's faith and will let them channel it, but it burns through dead flesh and reawakens deadened nerves, so every blessing and every heal comes with searing pain. Their trainers teach them to endure it as proof that the Light still answers.",al={"does the light hurt undead paladins","undead paladin pain","forsaken paladin pain","can undead use the light"}}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Every novice paladin walks in the footsteps of Uther the Lightbringer and Alonsus Faol, binding martial readiness to an oath of selfless duty.",["class:Priest"]="Priests share a deep spiritual kinship with paladins, though they shepherd the flock with sermons and faith while paladins protect it with steel.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarves from Ironforge recently embraced the tenets of the Silver Hand, finding that the discipline of the Light aligns naturally with their unyielding nature.",["race:Human"]="For humans, paladins represent the enduring soul of the Alliance, carrying on the sacred crusade forged during the horrors of the Second War.",["faction:Alliance"]="Paladins stand as the moral anchor of the Alliance, rallying troops with sacred auras and inspiring hope across the battlefield.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken paladins are the rarest of the risen: knights who kept their vows past death and still wield the Light, paying for every prayer in pain.",["faction:Horde"]="Within the Horde, only the Forsaken raise paladins, and even among their own people the order is met with suspicion."},kw={"alonsus faol","auras","blessings","cleric","crusader","holy light","paladin","plate","silver hand","uther","forsaken paladin","tyr's watch","undead paladin"},rel={"topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:light","topic:priest","topic:warrior","topic:alliance","topic:second-war","zone:stormwind","zone:ironforge","npc:aramis-hammerhand","subzone:bandarion-keep","topic:forsaken"},src={"Paladin"}}
E["topic:pirate"] = {t="topic",n="Pirate",z="general",s="Pirates and buccaneers are lawless seafaring plunderers operating across the Great Sea and coastal waters of Azeroth. Preying on Alliance, Horde, and neutral merchant vessels alike, they band together under loose flags to seize wealth, capture cargo, and control coastal coves. Their violent presence drives constant naval skirmishes from the waters of Kalimdor to the shores of the Eastern Kingdoms.",h="Pirates and buccaneers are lawless seafaring plunderers operating across the Great Sea and coastal waters of...",sec={{t="Lords of the High Seas",b="Pirates—often called buccaneers, corsairs, or freebooters—are maritime raiders of nearly every race who make their living seizing ships and raiding coastal settlements. Their operations stretch far beyond simple robbery; crews regularly engage in smuggling, kidnappings, and mercenary contracts for whoever has coin. With the chaos left behind by the Third War and the collapse of northern kingdoms, naval enforcement has fractured, giving pirate factions the freedom to carve out fortified footholds on neglected islands and coastal coves.",sp=0},{t="A Ruthless Life",b="Despite common tales painting pirate life as one of romantic adventure, life aboard a buccaneer vessel is brutal and short. Crews endure squalid, rat-infested holds, harsh discipline, and the ever-present threat of scurvy, fought off only with supplies of limes. Betrayal is an everyday hazard, and captains maintain their command solely through strength and fear of punishment, which ranges from immediate execution to being marooned or fed to sharks. When battles end, buccaneers celebrate with roaring bouts of dice, song, and heavy rum.",sp=0},{t="Factions and Havens",b="While independent crews roam every sea lane, major organizations wield enough ships to contest sovereign navies. The Southsea Freebooters harass shipping throughout the waters surrounding Kalimdor, while the notorious Bloodsail Buccaneers menace the southern seas and wage open war against the Steamwheedle Cartel in Booty Bay.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a pirate, buccaneer, and freebooter?",a="The titles are largely used interchangeably along the sea lanes of Azeroth. While some groups prefer particular banners—such as the Southsea Freebooters or the Bloodsail Buccaneers—they all engage in maritime raiding, robbery, smuggling, and coastal plundering.",al={"buccaneer vs pirate","freebooter difference","pirate titles"},it="identity"},{q="Where are the main pirate bases located?",a="Pirate crews congregate in lawless anchorages and hidden harbors across the world. Major havens include Plunder Isle and coastal camps scattered throughout the shores of Tanaris, the Barrens, and Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"pirate towns","where do pirates live","pirate ports"},it="place"},{q="Who do pirates attack?",a="Most pirate crews are indiscriminate predators. They hunt merchant vessels, civilian transports, and military supply craft belonging to the Alliance, the Horde, and the neutral Steamwheedle Cartel alike. Any ship laden with gold, timber, or trade goods is a potential target.",al={"who do pirates target","are pirates alliance or horde"},it="target"},{q="How do pirate crews recruit new members?",a="Pirates expand their numbers by hiring desperate cutthroats and mercenaries in port towns, but they also rely heavily on press-ganging. Captured sailors and travelers are frequently given the choice between swearing fealty to the crew or being cast into the sea.",al={"joining pirates","how do they get crew"}},{q="How does Kul Tiras deal with pirates?",a="After the Second War, Kul Tiran fleets patrolled the trade lanes, hunting pirates and marauders on the high seas.",al={"kul tiras piracy","kul tiran pirate law"}},{q="Are there non-human pirates?",a="Yes. While human crews are common, buccaneer crews accept anyone willing to raid and follow orders. Crews include orcs, goblins, trolls, and dwarves.",al={"orc pirates","goblin pirates"},it="people"},{q="What is a pirate?",a="Pirates are cutthroat seafaring raiders who plunder ships along the high seas and coastlines of Azeroth. Rather than flying national banners, they operate independently for personal gain through looting, smuggling, kidnapping, and mercenary work. Crews consist of ruthless renegades from across the mortal races, feared by merchants and hunted as criminals by both the Alliance and Horde navies.",al={"what is a pirate","who are pirates","define pirate"},it="identity",add="d5e8dad6"},{q="Why are pirates hostile to travelers?",a="Pirates view anyone crossing their territory or sea lanes as fresh prey. They take what they want through bloodshed and intimidation, caring little for the lives or allegiances of those they ambush. Any passing adventurer or merchant vessel represents plunder, hostage ransoms, or cargo to strip and sell.",al={"why do pirates attack","why are pirates hostile","are pirates dangerous"},it="hostile",add="433b56ef"},{q="Where do pirates come from?",a="Piracy has troubled the Great Sea for generations, thriving wherever kingdoms falter or fail to patrol the waters. In the aftermath of the Second and Third Wars, the destruction of coastal kingdoms and the breakdown of naval enforcement allowed lawless outcasts, deserters, and opportunists to seize abandoned outposts and terrorize maritime trade routes.",al={"pirate history","origin of pirates","where did pirates come from"},it="history",add="41dff7f7"}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="The cutthroat tactics, blinding powder, and swift blade work of pirate crews closely mirror the practical, lethal fighting styles favored by roguish mercenaries.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance transport lines between Stormwind, Ironforge, and Theramore are under constant threat from buccaneers who prowl coastal trade routes.",["faction:Horde"]="The Southsea Freebooters maintain aggressive encampments along the coast of Durotar and the Barrens, raiding Horde caravans and landing parties.",["race:Tauren"]="Though plains dwellers by tradition, some tauren traveling Kalimdor's shores have ended up aboard pirate vessels, either impressed into crews or seeking fortune."},kw={"bloodsail","booty bay","buccaneer","corsair","freebooter","great sea","pirate","plunder","southsea"},rel={"subzone:booty-bay","subzone:baradin-bay","quest:887","topic:kul-tiras","topic:goblin","topic:alliance","topic:horde"},src={"Pirate"}}
E["topic:plague-of-undeath"] = {t="topic",n="Plague of Undeath",z="general",s="The Plague of Undeath is a supernatural contagion concocted by the Lich King under the direction of the Burning Legion to scourge northern Eastern Kingdoms. Transmitted through infected grain, airborne cauldrons, and tainted waterways, the sickness rapidly kills living mortals and raises them as mind-bound thralls of the Scourge.",h="The Plague of Undeath is a supernatural contagion concocted by the Lich King under the direction of the...",sec={{t="Origins in the Ice",b="The plague was devised by the fallen shaman Ner'zhul after he was transformed into the Lich King by Kil'jaeden. From his icy seat atop the Frozen Throne in Northrend, Ner'zhul perfected the sickness as an unholy vanguard for the Burning Legion's invasion. Testing his power on isolated human villages across Northrend, he discovered that he could snuff out life and psychically command the corpses that arose shortly after. Ner'zhul's telepathic summon reached across the Great Sea to the Kirin Tor mage Kel'Thuzad, who abandoned Dalaran to serve the entity. Kel'Thuzad organized the secretive Cult of the Damned, whose mortal infiltrators laid the groundwork to poison Lordaeron from within.",sp=0},{t="The Grain of Andorhal",b="Kel'Thuzad and the dreadlord Mal'Ganis chose Lordaeron's agricultural heartland to distribute the contagion. The cult secured distribution centers around Andorhal, packaging the plague into crates of grain destined for population centers across the north, including Brill and Stratholme. Unsuspecting villagers baked bread with the contaminated shipments, falling sick with burning joints, oozing sores, and milky eyes. Within three days, entire communities perished, only to immediately rise as flesh-eating zombies bound to the Lich King's psychic call. Prince Arthas Menethil raced to intercept the shipments, culminating in the horrific culling of Stratholme's citizens before the infected bread could transform them into Mal'Ganis's army.",sp=1},{t="A Land Corrupted",b="The blight extends far beyond humanity. While non-human fauna generally suffer sickening fevers or rot rather than rising as undead thralls, the land itself bears the brunt of demonic and necromantic decay. Across the Plaguelands, massive plague cauldrons pump alchemical and magical vapors directly into the atmosphere, corrupting the surrounding land, where carrion grubs and colossal spore-choked mushrooms thrive. The Argent Dawn actively battles this airborne corruption by sabotaging these cauldrons, reversing their catalytic mixtures to burn the Scourge rather than feed it. Meanwhile, in the Undercity, the Royal Apothecary Society experiments tirelessly to refine a New Plague designed to slay both living foes and the undead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How was the plague originally spread across Lordaeron?",a="Kel'Thuzad's Cult of the Damned infiltrated the distribution hub of Andorhal. They loaded grain supplies with the plague and shipped crates to towns throughout the kingdom, such as Stratholme and Brill. When the local populace consumed the contaminated flour, they succumbed to the sickness and rose as undead.",al={"how did plague spread","infected grain","plagued grain distribution","grain of andorhal"},it="history"},{q="What happens to someone infected by the plague?",a="Victims rapidly experience weakness, nausea, joint aches, and fever. Skin breaks out into irritated boils and leaking yellow-green lesions, while eyes turn milky. Severe hemorrhaging from the ears and eyes quickly follows. Most mortals die within three days, only to rise soon after as reanimated servants awaiting the commands of the Lich King.",al={"plague symptoms","what does the plague do","how fast does plague kill"},it="threat"},{q="Why does the plague affect humans differently than other races?",a="The Lich King deliberately engineered the plague to target humanity in order to obliterate the Kingdom of Lordaeron. Non-human wildlife and races exposed to the plague often suffer horrific rot, delirium, and fever, but rarely reanimate automatically as Scourge thralls without additional direct necromantic spells.",al={"why humans","does plague affect elves","are orcs immune to plague"},it="target"},{q="Can the Plague of Undeath be cured?",a="No established cure is known in the current era. Natural medicines cannot reverse the plague; once a victim has died and risen, the transformation cannot be undone.",al={"is plague curable","cure for the plague","healing the plague"},it="power"},{q="What are the giant cauldrons in the Plaguelands for?",a="The Scourge erected massive cauldrons across the former farmlands to vent vaporized plague directly into the air. This airborne delivery corrupts the soil, clouds the sky, and sickens passing beasts. The Argent Dawn works tirelessly to counteract these cauldrons by turning their reagents into holy or toxic counters against the Scourge.",al={"plague cauldrons","what are cauldrons doing","why are cauldrons in fields"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Forsaken's New Plague?",a="Developed in the bowels of the Undercity by the Royal Apothecary Society, the New Plague is a bespoke chemical and magical pathogen. Unlike the Scourge's version, the Forsaken design their formula specifically to kill both living mortals and the undead thralls of the Lich King.",al={"forsaken plague","royal apothecary society plague","new plague undercity"}},{q="What is the Plague of Undeath?",a="It is a potent magical disease conjured by Ner'zhul, the Lich King, under the direction of the demon lord Kil'jaeden. Engineered to drain life and spread undeath, the affliction was designed as a weapon to destroy mortal civilizations and reanimate their dead as mindless thralls under the Lich King's psychic dominion.",al={"what is the plague","plague of undeath explained","scourge plague"},it="identity",add="e29c4cee"},{q="Why is it called the Plague of Undeath?",a="It earned the name because death is not its conclusion. While natural diseases merely kill their hosts, this affliction reanimates those who succumb to it. Victims perish rapidly from agonizing symptoms only to rise again as mindless, walking undead bound to the will of the Lich King.",al={"why plague of undeath","origin of plague name","why is it called undeath"},it="name",add="8d7ecba0"}},ang={["race:Human"]="The Plague of Undeath wiped out the Kingdom of Lordaeron and tore families apart, standing as the direct cause of humanity's greatest historical catastrophe.",["race:Undead"]="Many Forsaken carry the physical scars of the plague, having lived through its agony and died only to break free from the Lich King's psychic grip years later.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand shattered trying to contain the contagion, leaving paladins to rely solely on the Holy Light to purge its foul residue.",["class:Priest"]="Priests face the ultimate corruption of body and spirit in the plague, driving their faith to burn away necrotic sickness before it festers.",["faction:Alliance"]="The loss of northern kingdoms to the plague remains an unhealed wound for the Alliance, driving their ongoing crusade to cleanse the Plaguelands.",["faction:Horde"]="While orcs and tauren viewed the northern sickness as a human curse, the Royal Apothecary Society's research has brought the horror of plague experimentation directly into the Horde."},kw={"andorhal","blight","cauldrons","grain","kel'thuzad","mal'ganis","ner'zhul","plague","scourge","stratholme","undeath"},rel={"topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:arthas-menethil","subzone:andorhal","subzone:stratholme","subzone:cauldron","topic:royal-apothecary-society"},src={"Plague of Undeath"}}
E["topic:priest"] = {t="topic",n="Priest",z="general",s="Across Azeroth, Priest traditions shape belief, mend flesh, and guard souls. Some draw on the radiant power of faith, while others reach into the terrifying depths of the Void. Their paths range from the Church of the Holy Light to the Sisterhood of the Moon Goddess and shadowy Forsaken cults.",h="Across Azeroth, Priest traditions shape belief, mend flesh, and guard souls.",sec={{t="Vessels of Faith and Devotion",b="Priests guide the spiritual lives of their communities. While warriors test their strength with steel, priests draw power from conviction. Their divine connection can soothe grievous wounds, lift fallen companions from death's threshold, and weave mystic barriers against harm. On the field, their presence can mean the difference between survival and annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Light and Shadow",b="The priesthood does not serve only benevolent ends. Life holds both light and darkness, and priests walk a perilous line between healing and devastation of the mind. Those who master the holy arts call down searing righteous vengeance upon their enemies. Practitioners of Shadow magic delve into the Void to dominate minds, twist willpower, and inflict agonizing pain from afar.",sp=0},{t="Many Paths of Faith",b="No single faith guides every priest in Azeroth. Among humans, dwarves and gnomes, the Church of the Holy Light teaches a philosophy of virtue. After the First War, it shaped its fragile Clerics of Northshire into a hardened order.\n\nIn Kalimdor, the night elven Sisterhood of Elune honors the Mother Moon with starlight and grace, while troll witch doctors beseech the primal Loa. Among the newly risen Forsaken, many have turned from former dogmas to the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow. Its followers preach that darkness and light must exist in delicate equilibrium.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a priest and a paladin?",a="During the First War, human clerics suffered immense slaughter on the battlefield because they lacked martial defense. In response, Archbishop Alonsus Faol and the Church formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, training armored holy warriors called paladins. Priests remain robed casters devoted to spiritual ministry, prayer, and deeper mastery of the mind and shadow.",al={"priest vs paladin","why play priest not paladin","paladin or priest lore"}},{q="Can priests use Shadow magic without losing their faith?",a="Yes. Spiritual balance holds that light cannot exist without darkness. Many priests study shadow to understand the perils threatening their flock, while others, like the Forsaken Cult of the Forgotten Shadow, embrace dark energies as necessary to existence. They argue that understanding both forces is the way to master the self.",al={"why do priests use shadow","shadow priest lore","shadow and holy balance"}},{q="Do all playable races worship the same thing as priests?",a="No. Humans, dwarves and gnomes primarily follow the Church of the Holy Light, a philosophy of virtue rather than worship of a deity. Night elves revere Elune through her Sisterhood, and trolls beseech spirits and Loa. Undead Forsaken practitioners follow the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow or struggle with their fractured bond to their former human faith.",al={"priest religions","what gods do priests worship","elune vs holy light priest"}},{q="Who leads the human priesthood in Stormwind?",a="After the fall of Lordaeron, the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind became the primary seat of the human church. Its clergy and surviving clerics from Northshire oversee humanity's spiritual recovery and guide the souls of the Alliance in these unsettled times.",al={"who leads church of holy light","stormwind cathedral leader","priest leader stormwind"}},{q="Why do the Forsaken still have priests?",a="Many undead retained memories and techniques from their living devotions in Lordaeron. After facing death's abandonment, some embraced the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow and wield dark energies while studying the balance between darkness and light. Others still call upon the Holy Light they served in life.",al={"undead priests lore","can undead use the light","forsaken shadow priests"}},{q="What is the Sisterhood of Elune?",a="The Sisterhood of Elune is an ancient order of night elf priestesses, established long before the War of the Ancients. Led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, they revere the moon goddess Elune. They serve as healers and spiritual guides, and as fiercely capable leaders within night elf society.",al={"night elf priests","priestesses of the moon","sisterhood of elune"}},{q="Which races can be priests?",a="Humans, dwarves, gnomes and night elves train as priests in the Alliance. Trolls and the Forsaken do so in the Horde. Orcs, tauren and the Skyborne do not walk the priest's path.",al={"who can be a priest","priest races","can gnomes be priests","gnome priest"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you walk between pure radiance and devastation of the mind. You command the healing mercy of prayer and the punishing horrors of the Void.",["race:Human"]="As a human priest, you inherit the resilient tradition of the Northshire Clerics. Through the Church of the Holy Light, you help form the spiritual bedrock of the Alliance.",["race:NightElf"]="As a night elf priestess, you honor the Sisterhood of Elune and channel starlight and lunar grace under High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind's guidance.",["race:Undead"]="As a Forsaken priest, you reshape your shattered faith through the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow. You embrace the balance of dark energies after former vows brought only ruin.",["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you share holy teachings with the priesthood. The paladins arose from the Church's need to protect robed clerics after the bloody lessons of the First War.",["race:Gnome"]="As a gnome priest, you serve the Holy Light alongside the dwarven clergy of Ironforge's Hall of Mysteries. You tend a people still scattered by Gnomeregan's fall."},kw={"cathedral","cleric","cult","elune","healing","holy light","priest","shadow","sisterhood","void"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:light","topic:elune","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:forsaken","topic:paladin","quest:5650","topic:gnome"},src={"Priest"}}
E["topic:quilboar"] = {t="topic",n="Quilboar",z="general",s="Quilboar are aggressive, boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor, renowned for their sharp spinal quills and relentless territorial savagery. Claiming descent from the fallen demigod Agamaggan, they dwell within dense mazes of razor-sharp brambles across Durotar, Mulgore, and the Barrens. Their fierce struggle over water, soil, and sacred ground makes them a constant threat to travelers and Horde settlements.",h="Quilboar are aggressive, boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor, renowned for their sharp spinal...",sec={{t="Children of the Great Boar",b="Quilboar revere Agamaggan, the colossal boar Ancient who perished battling the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. According to their sacred traditions, the quilboar are his mortal offspring. Where Agamaggan bled and died across central Kalimdor, enormous thorny vines erupted from the scorched earth, forming sprawling root-mazes that the quilboar treat as holy ground. His crystallized blood endures across the Barrens as crimson Blood Shards, which their geomancers dig up and revere as powerful talismans.",sp=0},{t="Bramble Harrows and Geomancy",b="Rather than building traditional towns of stone or timber, quilboar cultivate colossal briars that choke surrounding vegetation and render nearby ground barren. Tribes are typically guided by powerful geomancers—practitioners of an earthbound, crude shamanism who consult stones and crystals to direct the clan. These mystics call upon Agamaggan's spirit to summon spectral boars into combat. Because the arid central plains offer few oases, quilboar view water as the ultimate treasure; they stage lavish feasts when rare rains fall and defend water holes with vicious tenacity.",sp=0},{t="The War for the Barrens",b="For generations, quilboar clashed bitterly with the nomadic tauren and roaming centaur packs. When Thrall led the Orcish Horde into Kalimdor, orcs rapidly colonized Durotar and established trade routes through the Barrens, driving displaced tribes into open revolt. Quilboar war parties ambush supply wagons, are suspected of frightening tallstriders into wagon trains, and stage raids on outposts like Crossroads and Camp Taurajo. Two principal tribes dominate the open plains: the Razormane and the Bristleback, both engaged in simultaneous feuds against the Horde and each other.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow over Razorfen",b="The spiritual heart of quilboar civilization lies at the southern edge of the Barrens within Razorfen Kraul and the adjacent Razorfen Downs, tangled mazes formed directly from Agamaggan's gargantuan remains. An ambitious crone named Charlga Razorflank seized dominion over the Kraul, orchestrating aggressive assaults against rival clans and Horde territories. Worse still, the traditional quilboar capital of Razorfen Downs has fallen under the sway of the Scourge. In league with the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer, corrupted Death's Head quilboar have succumbed to undeath, turning the ancestral tomb into a plague-ridden redoubt.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why do quilboar hate the Horde so much?",a="Quilboar occupied the fertile oases and thorned ravines of Durotar and the Barrens long before the Horde arrived. When Thrall's people landed and claimed land to build Orgrimmar, they displaced native quilboar dens. Compounding this, the Horde allied with the tauren, whom the quilboar had fought for generations. Now, the tribes view every caravan, outpost, and water well as stolen ancestral ground.",al={"why do quilboar attack orcs","quilboar horde conflict","why are quilboar hostile"},it="hostile"},{q="What is the relationship between quilboar and Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan is a colossal boar demigod who fought the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. Quilboar claim to be his mortal descendants. The massive briars they inhabit sprouted where his blood soaked the earth, and the sacred tunnels of Razorfen Downs are said to be carved from his very remains.",al={"agamaggan quilboar god","who is agamaggan","quilboar demigod"},it="ties"},{q="What are the red stones quilboar carry?",a="Those crimson crystals are Blood Shards, formed from the petrified blood of Agamaggan. Quilboar dig them up throughout the Barrens, believing they contain the vitality and strength of their fallen ancestor. Geomancers use these stones as catalysts in earth rituals and combat enchantments.",al={"blood shards","red rocks quilboar","blood of agamaggan"},it="item"},{q="What kind of magic do quilboar use?",a="Quilboar practice geomancy, an elemental craft akin to crude shamanism focused on stone, earth, and thorns. They revere the reading of stones and gems above all other mystical arts. Tribal geomancers call upon earth spirits, summon brambles to entangle foes, and conjure boar spirits in Agamaggan's image.",al={"quilboar magic","quilboar geomancers","what is geomancy"},it="power"},{q="Why are quilboar so protective of water?",a="The Barrens is an arid wasteland where natural springs and oases are exceedingly scarce. In quilboar culture, water represents survival, wealth, and spiritual blessing. They celebrate even the lightest desert rains with tribal feasts and fiercely guard springs against encroaching caravans and herds.",al={"quilboar water","why quilboar hoard water","quilboar oases"},it="motive"},{q="What is the difference between Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs?",a="Both are massive briar mazes sprouting from Agamaggan's carcass in southern Kalimdor. Razorfen Kraul serves as the stronghold of the crone Charlga Razorflank, who rallies living quilboar against all outsiders. Razorfen Downs was the race's ancestral capital, but it has fallen into the hands of the Scourge lich Amnennar the Coldbringer, who raises deceased quilboar as undead minions.",al={"kraul vs downs","razorfen dungeons","where do quilboar live"},sp=1,it="place"},{q="What are quilboar?",a="Quilboar are a primitive, aggressive race of boar-like humanoids native to Kalimdor. Short, hunched, and muscular, their most striking feature is the array of razor-sharp spines that protrudes from their backs and heads. Believed to be the mortal descendants of the demigod Agamaggan, they revere massive bramble thorns and live in fiercely protective tribal societies governed largely by powerful geomancer shamans.",al={"what is quilboar","who are quilboars","quilboar race"},it="identity",add="606f2b6f"},{q="Why are quilboar found in the Barrens and Durotar?",a="Quilboar flock to areas dominated by colossal, thorn-covered roots, believing them to be sacred ground grown from the blood of their fallen god, Agamaggan. Central Kalimdor—spanning the Barrens, Durotar, and Mulgore—contains the heart of these brambles, culminating in the massive colonies of Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs. Their shaman intentionally cultivate and spread these choking thorns wherever they settle.",al={"why are quilboar here","quilboar locations","why do quilboar live in the barrens"},it="why_here",add="d22ef352"},{q="Who leads the quilboar?",a="Quilboar are divided into distinct tribes like the Bristleback, Razormane, and Death's Head, each led by its own chiefs and shamans. However, the crone Charlga Razorflank holds dominion over Razorfen Kraul and commands her followers. Under her tyrannical rule, quilboar stage aggressive strikes against rival tribes, the Horde, and travelers throughout the Barrens.",al={"quilboar leader","who is in charge of quilboar","quilboar crone"},it="leader",add="361cc79b"},{q="Who are the quilboar allied with?",a="Most quilboar tribes are fiercely independent and despise outsiders, fighting anyone who enters their territory. However, Charlga Razorflank and her Death's Head tribe forged a dark alliance with the Scourge. This pact allowed the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer into Razorfen Downs, converting the traditional capital of the quilboar into an undead stronghold bolstered by reanimated boar-folk.",al={"quilboar allies","do quilboar have allies","who do quilboar work for"},it="allies",add="b83eba15"},{q="Where did the quilboar come from?",a="Quilboar believe themselves to be the mortal offspring of Agamaggan, the colossal boar demigod who fought the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients. They were already abundant before the Great Sundering. When Agamaggan fell, massive thorn-ridden vines sprouted where his blood seeped into the earth, and the quilboar came to occupy these sacred brambles.",al={"quilboar origin","quilboar history","how were quilboar created"},it="history",add="5fc285c1"},{q="Why are they called quilboar?",a="Their name comes from their distinct appearance: boar-like humanoids bristling with sharp, defensive quills along their spines and skulls. These spines are central to their physiology and identity, used both defensively and launched in battle, with tribal names like Bristleback and Razormane reflecting their formidable quilled backs.",al={"quilboar name meaning","why the name quilboar","why are they called quillboar"},it="name",add="dac333bc"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Your ancestors fought the quilboar for watering holes and grazing land long before the orcs sailed west. Their expansionist brambles choke the natural earth that the Earth Mother sustains.",["race:Orc"]="Securing Durotar and the Barrens requires breaking the quilboar war bands, whose raids continually harass Horde trade wagons and outlying farmsteads.",["class:Shaman"]="Quilboar geomancy represents an earthy, ancient offshoot of shamanic tradition, though twisted toward territorial brutality and the conjuration of savage boar spirits.",["faction:Horde"]="Quilboar pose an immediate logistical hazard across central Kalimdor, ambushing vital caravans traveling between Orgrimmar, the Crossroads, and Thunder Bluff."},kw={"agamaggan","barrens","blood shards","brambles","bristleback","charlga razorflank","geomancer","quilboar","quillboar","razorfen","razormane"},rel={"topic:agamaggan","zone:barrens","zone:razorfenkraul","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","npc:charlga-razorflank","topic:tauren","quest:5052","quest:899"},src={"Quilboar"}}
E["topic:ravenholdt"] = {t="topic",n="Ravenholdt",z="general",s="Ravenholdt, also known as the Assassin's League, is an elite guild of rogues, thieves, and killers based out of a secluded mountain manor above the Hillsbrad Foothills. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the league maintains strict neutrality from faction politics while waging an unending shadow war against their bitter rivals, the Syndicate. They test and recruit only the most cunning operatives across Azeroth, valuing peerless discipline, stealth, and lethal efficiency above all else.",h="Ravenholdt, also known as the Assassin's League, is an elite guild of rogues, thieves, and killers based out...",sec={{t="The Hidden League",b="Tucked away in the craggy mountains connecting Hillsbrad to Alterac, Ravenholdt Manor serves as the nerve center for Azeroth's premier brotherhood of assassins. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the organization operates far beyond the jurisdiction of kings and warchiefs. Within its walls, master specialists hone every lethal art: Master Kang instructs disciples in close combat and assassination, the Thunderwood brothers brew rare and deadly poisons, and gnome craftsmen engineer intricate locks to test fledglings. To non-rogues, entering the estate is an overt flirtation with death, where an unwary step easily ends with a poisoned dagger in the ribs.",sp=0},{t="Shadow War with the Syndicate",b="For years, Ravenholdt has been locked in an escalating feud with the Syndicate, an aristocratic criminal cartel infesting Alterac and Arathi. While the League regards the Syndicate as clumsy, arrogant amateurs, their sheer numbers make them impossible to eradicate through simple slaughter. Instead, Ravenholdt employs psychological and structural disruption: by tasking contracted rogues with pickpocketing the signet emblems worn by Syndicate ringleaders, the League relies on the Syndicate's own paranoia and cutthroat hierarchy to execute disgraced members internally.",sp=0},{t="An Independent Guild of Shadows",b="Though Ravenholdt cooperates on occasion with other intelligence apparatuses like Stormwind's SI:7, they answer to no crown. Master Mathias Shaw occasionally finds Ravenholdt an ally in checking the Syndicate, yet Jorach Ravenholdt fiercely guards his autonomy. When SI:7 suffered heavy losses investigating the fallen ranger Nathanos Marris in the Plaguelands, Ravenholdt flatly refused Shaw's plea to intervene. The League picks its own battles, dedicating their resources to acquiring ancient relics, perfecting deadly arts, and keeping their blade at the throat of anyone foolish enough to cross them.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Ravenholdt?",a="Ravenholdt is an ancient, clandestine guild of thieves, assassins, and shadow-dancers governed by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt. Known informally as the Assassin's League, they operate from a secret estate nestled deep in the mountains above Hillsbrad Foothills, training promising rogues and offering deadly contracts.",al={"who are ravenholdt","what is the assassin's league","ravenholdt faction"},it="identity"},{q="Why are they fighting the Syndicate?",a="The Syndicate and Ravenholdt vie for dominance across the ruins of Alterac and the surrounding highlands. The League considers the Syndicate undisciplined, dishonorable brigands whose messy criminal operations threaten the subtle balance of power in the region. Rather than wipe them out through sheer attrition, Ravenholdt methodically undermines their leadership by stealing their emblems of rank.",al={"why syndicate vs ravenholdt","ravenholdt syndicate war","why do they hate the syndicate"},it="conflict"},{q="Can non-rogues visit Ravenholdt Manor?",a="Non-rogues can physically reach the manor, but the residents do not welcome outsiders. Guards and assassins make thinly veiled threats about poisoned drinks and daggers in the back to anyone who wanders in without shadowcraft credentials or business with Lord Ravenholdt.",al={"can other classes go to ravenholdt","warrior at ravenholdt manor","is ravenholdt rogues only"},gp=true},{q="Who is Lord Jorach Ravenholdt?",a="Lord Jorach Ravenholdt is the elusive master of the Assassin's League. Operating from his mountain estate, he maintains an extensive network of rogue trainers and informants across the continents, dispatching trusted disciples to recover powerful artifacts and neutralize rival threats.",al={"who leads ravenholdt","jorach ravenholdt","ravenholdt leader"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Rite of Cunning?",a="It is an introductory test for prospective disciples seeking Ravenholdt Manor. An initiate must use their ability to detect traps while passing through the mountain tunnel leading to the grounds; the danger is discovered during the rite.",al={"rite of cunning","ravenholdt rogue trial","ravenholdt tunnel test"}},{q="How does Ravenholdt view the Alliance and the Horde?",a="Ravenholdt is entirely neutral regarding factional politics, accepting skilled cutthroats regardless of whether they hail from Orgrimmar or Ironforge. While they maintain cordial lines of communication with organizations like Stormwind's SI:7, they refuse to act as pawns for any kingdom's war effort.",al={"is ravenholdt alliance or horde","ravenholdt alignment","who does ravenholdt work for"},it="side"},{q="Why is Ravenholdt Manor hidden in the Alterac Mountains?",a="Ravenholdt operates from a secluded manor concealed high in the mountains above Hillsbrad Foothills to guard its secrets and protect its members from outside scrutiny. Tucked away behind an easily overlooked mountain path, the estate provides an ideal stronghold for an elite assassin's guild, offering security against unwanted intrusions and placing them directly adjacent to the Syndicate forces they actively undermine across the region.",al={"why are they in alterac","ravenholdt manor location why","why hide in hillsbrad mountains"},it="why_here",add="d3774f53"},{q="Who are Ravenholdt allied with?",a="Ravenholdt operates independently as an autonomous league of assassins and thieves, strictly bound to its own interests and code. However, they maintain selective professional contacts with other clandestine circles. They have accepted cooperative aid from Stormwind's intelligence service, SI:7, specifically to root out the Syndicate affliction in the northern kingdoms, though they remain purely mercenary and will refuse requests that do not serve their guild's priorities.",al={"who does ravenholdt work for","ravenholdt allies","ravenholdt connections"},it="allies",add="eb92cd94"},{q="What is the history of Ravenholdt?",a="Formed as the Assassin's League under the leadership of Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the guild has long gathered the world's finest practitioners of stealth, poison, and theft. Years ago, their treasured artifact, the Horns of Nez'ra, was stolen by the Blackrock clan, sparking a relentless hunt across continents. In recent times, their primary focus has been a bitter, protracted shadow war against the noble-led Syndicate across Alterac and Arathi.",al={"history of ravenholdt","where did ravenholdt come from","origins of ravenholdt"},it="history",add="77e129fe"}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Ravenholdt is your global fraternity and proving ground. The trainers, poison-brewers, and shadowmasters here offer trials, rare weapon schematics, and recognition that cannot be earned anywhere else in Azeroth.",["faction:Alliance"]="While Mathias Shaw and SI:7 occasionally coordinate with Ravenholdt to suppress the Syndicate, Lord Ravenholdt owes no allegiance to Stormwind and will casually rebuff high-stakes Alliance requests.",["faction:Horde"]="Despite its noble human exterior and location in the northern highlands, Ravenholdt judges operatives solely by their prowess, welcoming Horde stalkers who prove their mettle."},kw={"alterac","assassin","fahrad","hillsbrad","jorach","lockpicking","manor","poisons","ravenholdt","syndicate"},rel={"subzone:ravenholdt-manor","npc:lord-jorach-ravenholdt","topic:syndicate","zone:hillsbrad","zone:alterac","quest:8249","npc:mathias-shaw","quest:2300"},src={"Ravenholdt"}}
E["topic:razorfen"] = {t="topic",n="Razorfen",z="general",s="Razorfen is the collective name associated with the predominant quilboar tribe of southern Kalimdor and their sprawling thorn-choked settlements. Carved into massive briars along the southern border of the Barrens and the edge of Thousand Needles, Razorfen encompasses the strongholds of Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs. It serves as both the spiritual heart and the central bastion of quilboar power.",h="Razorfen is the collective name associated with the predominant quilboar tribe of southern Kalimdor and their...",sec={{t="The Thorns of Agamaggan",b="According to legend, the colossal thorned brambles that make up the Razorfen sprouted ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients. When the giant boar Ancient, Agamaggan, fell in battle against the Burning Legion, his blood seeped into the earth wherever he bled and died. From that divine sacrifice grew massive vines of petrified bramble and iron-hard thorns, creating a natural fortress where his descendants, the quilboar, have dwelled ever since.",sp=0},{t="The Razorfen Tribe",b="The Razorfen tribe is among the most aggressive and entrenched of all quilboar populations. Rather than scattering into open warrens, they built complex labyrinthine communities directly within the colossal briars. From these fortresses, the tribe fiercely defends their ancestral territory against neighboring tauren tribes, Horde settlements in the Barrens, and any outsiders daring to trespass into their holy grounds.",sp=0},{t="A Divided Citadel",b="The realm of Razorfen is divided into two distinct bastions. Razorfen Kraul sits as the main stronghold and military encampment of the quilboar, guarded by their shamans and huntresses. Further south, bordering Thousand Needles, lies Razorfen Downs, the traditional capital, sacred ancestral city and burial grounds of the quilboar elders, currently shadowed by deeper necromantic corruption that threatens all surrounding lands.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Razorfen?",a="Razorfen refers to both the prominent quilboar tribe of the southern Barrens and their fortified settlements woven into massive briar labyrinths: Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs.",al={"what is razorfen","razorfen meaning","razorfen lore"},it="identity"},{q="Where did the giant thorns come from?",a="The thorns are the fossilized blood and remnants of the Wild God Agamaggan, the Great Boar. When he fell fighting the Burning Legion during the ancient War of the Ancients, massive thorned tendrils grew wherever his blood struck the earth.",al={"giant briars barrens","agamaggan thorns","why giant thorns"},it="origin"},{q="What is the difference between Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs?",a="Razorfen Kraul serves as the living city and main stronghold of the Razorfen tribe, filled with warriors and geomancers. Razorfen Downs is the ancestral burial ground and sacred resting place of quilboar elders, located further south toward Thousand Needles.",al={"kraul vs downs","difference between razorfen dungeons"}},{q="Why are the quilboar hostile to everyone?",a="Quilboar view the land of the Barrens and its great briars as their divine birthright granted by Agamaggan. They consider any outside races—especially the expanding Horde—to be thieving interlopers invading their holy ground.",al={"why do quilboar attack","quilboar lore"},it="hostile"},{q="Where is Razorfen located?",a="Razorfen's massive thorn networks lie in the far southern reaches of the Barrens, right along the high cliffs that descend into the salt flats and river canyon of Thousand Needles.",al={"razorfen map location","where is razorfen"},it="place"},{q="What kind of magic do the Razorfen quilboar use?",a="Traditionally, Razorfen quilboar practice earth and nature magic known as geomancy, invoking the power of stone and thorn. However, recent developments within Razorfen Downs show alarming incursions of dark necromantic arts.",al={"quilboar magic","razorfen shamans"},it="power"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Razorfen quilboar have clashed violently with tauren hunting bands and settlements across the Barrens for generations, making their eradication a matter of homeland security.",["faction:Horde"]="Controlling the southern Barrens is critical for Horde logistics, and the aggressive Razorfen raiders consistently harass caravans traveling between Mulgore, Crossroads, and the southern frontier.",["class:Druid"]="The briars of Razorfen sprouted from the sacrifice of the Ancient Agamaggan; druids view the desecration and corruption of such a sacred site with profound concern.",["class:Shaman"]="The Razorfen geomancers manipulate the spirits of earth and stone through crude elemental rites, demanding attention from shamans seeking balance."},kw={"agamaggan","barrens","brambles","downs","kraul","quilboar","razorfen","thorns"},rel={"topic:quilboar","subzone:razorfen-kraul-classic","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","zone:thousandneedles"},src={"Razorfen"}}
E["topic:refugee"] = {t="topic",n="Refugee",z="general",s="Throughout the violent history of Azeroth, constant warfare has repeatedly driven civilian populations from their homes. From the exodus of Stormwind during the First War to the tragic flight from Lordaeron in the Third War, refugees remain living testaments to the devastating costs of invasion, plague, and betrayal.",h="Throughout the violent history of Azeroth, constant warfare has repeatedly driven civilian populations from...",sec={{t="The Exodus of Stormwind",b="When the Orcish Horde swept through the kingdom of Stormwind during the First War, settlements like Goldshire, Moonbrook, and Grand Hamlet burned. Following the assassination of King Llane Wrynn, Sir Anduin Lothar took command of the kingdom's remnants. He gathered surviving soldiers and desperate civilians, escorting them to the harbor as the city burned. Lothar led a massive fleet across the Great Sea to the northern shores of Lordaeron. Arriving near Southshore, the exiled survivors—including the young Prince Varian Wrynn—were granted sanctuary by King Terenas Menethil II.",sp=0},{t="Flight from the Scourge",b="A generation after welcoming Stormwind's exiles, the people of Lordaeron faced ruin of their own. During the Third War, the Scourge plague and the legions of the undead systematically dismantled the kingdom. Prince Arthas Menethil slaughtered his own subjects as he swept across the realm, leaving survivors scattered and terrified. Lady Jaina Proudmoore managed to lead an expedition of refugees across the sea to Kalimdor, eventually establishing a settlement at Theramore. However, countless others remained behind in the Eastern Kingdoms, hunted relentlessly by the Scourge, the Forsaken, and monstrous horrors that arose in the ruins.",sp=0},{t="Turned Away at the Gate",b="Those fleeing the devastation of Lordaeron who could not escape across the sea desperately sought shelter wherever walls still stood. Many traveled south to the massive Greymane Wall, begging for entry into the isolationist kingdom of Gilneas. Instead of sanctuary, they found sealed gates and armed indifference. Left destitute on the borders of Silverpine Forest, these haggard survivors huddled in makeshift camps, supported only by selfless caretakers like Dalin Forgewright while facing starvation, undead raiders, and the predatory worgen roaming near Shadowfang Keep.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What happened to the people who fled Stormwind during the First War?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar led thousands of survivors aboard a desperate fleet across the Great Sea to the northern kingdom of Lordaeron. They made landfall at the port village of Southshore and were granted refuge by King Terenas Menethil II until Stormwind was eventually reclaimed and rebuilt.",al={"stormwind refugees first war","where did stormwind flee","exodus of stormwind"},it="history"},{q="Did all of Stormwind's citizens escape the First War?",a="No. Many died in the streets as the city fell, and uncounted survivors who never reached the ships were left to wander the wilderness of Azeroth on their own.",al={"stormwind survivors","who stayed in stormwind","cathedral of light refugees"},it="history"},{q="Where did the survivors of Lordaeron go?",a="Many boarded ships under Lady Jaina Proudmoore and sailed west to Kalimdor, eventually settling the island outpost of Theramore. Others scattered across the Eastern Kingdoms, seeking haven in places like Southshore, the Hinterlands, or futilely pleading at the sealed Greymane Wall.",al={"lordaeron refugees","where did lordaeron people go","survivors of the plague"},it="history"},{q="Why didn't Gilneas let the refugees from Lordaeron in?",a="Gilneas chose complete isolationism, closing and barring the massive Greymane Wall to cut itself off from the rest of the world and avoid external wars. When the desperate, sickly victims of the Scourge arrived at the gates, they were firmly turned away and left exposed in Silverpine Forest.",al={"greymane wall refugees","gilneas closed gates","why did greymane lock out survivors"},it="history"},{q="Who helped the refugees stranded outside the Greymane Wall?",a="Dalin Forgewright dedicated his life to caring for the haggard and sickly refugees trapped outside the gates of Gilneas, tending to their ailments and offering what solace he could in the perilous wilderness.",al={"dalin forgewright","who helped silverpine refugees"},it="people"},{q="Who took in the Stormwind exiles when they reached Lordaeron?",a="King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron welcomed the southern survivors when their fleet reached his shores near Southshore, granting the landless exiles sanctuary in his kingdom.",al={"southshore landing","marcus redpath refugees","who greeted stormwind"},it="allies"},{q="What is a refugee in Azeroth?",a="Refugees are survivors forced from their homes by the unending wars, plagues, and cataclysms that ravage Azeroth. Across the world, whole populations have been displaced when their kingdoms fell—from the citizens of Stormwind driven north across the Great Sea during the First War, to the survivors of Lordaeron fleeing the Scourge in the Third War. Stripped of shelter and safety, they wander in search of sanctuary.",al={"what is a refugee","who are refugees","refugee meaning"},it="identity",add="6f7a70cf"},{q="Why are there so many refugees across Azeroth?",a="You find displaced folk scattered across the world because successive conflicts have shattered entire nations in recent decades. The sacking of Stormwind by the Horde scattered survivors throughout the Eastern Kingdoms, while the Scourge's destruction of Lordaeron sent desperate remnants wandering toward places like the barricaded Greymane Wall or following Jaina Proudmoore overseas to Kalimdor.",al={"why are there refugees","where do refugees come from","refugees reason"},it="why_here",add="8e410108"}},ang={["race:Human"]="Almost every human family carries the trauma of displacement, whether fleeing the burning of Stormwind to Southshore a generation ago or escaping the butcher's work of Arthas in Lordaeron.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron was born directly from the plea of homeless Stormwind refugees, proving that shared survival and sanctuary are the foundations of the modern Alliance.",["class:Priest"]="The Holy Light compels clergy to aid the dispossessed, following the example of the caretakers who sheltered the wounded in ruined sanctuaries.",["race:Undead"]="Many of the Forsaken were once the very Lordaeron refugees who failed to outrun the plague and the Scourge, now inhabiting the ruined kingdom they died trying to flee."},kw={"exodus","greymane wall","jaina proudmoore","lordaeron","lothar","refugee","silverpine","southshore","stormwind"},rel={"topic:first-war","topic:third-war","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:lordaeron","subzone:southshore","subzone:greymane-wall","npc:jaina-proudmoore","quest:1476"},src={"Refugee"}}
E["topic:riverpaw-pack"] = {t="topic",n="Riverpaw pack",z="multi",s="The Riverpaw pack is a savage clan of gnolls inhabiting the borderlands of Elwynn Forest and Westfall. Known for their brutal raids and ruthless dominance over local kobolds, they have grown increasingly organized and brazen following the Third War. With Stormwind's military stretched thin, their presence threatens vital roads and settlements throughout the heartland.",h="The Riverpaw pack is a savage clan of gnolls inhabiting the borderlands of Elwynn Forest and Westfall.",sec={{t="A Plague on the Heartland",b="For decades, the Riverpaw clan prowled the fringes of human civilization, but their boldness has surged in recent years. In Elwynn Forest, packs led by the infamous brute Hogger prowl the southern woods around Forest's Edge and roam the shores of Stone Cairn Lake. In Westfall, they roam across the Dust Plains and along the coasts, ambushing solitary travelers and raiding homesteads. Deputy Rainer at Westbrook Garrison and Captain Danuvin at Sentinel Hill rely heavily on mercenary blades to cull their numbers, issuing standing bounties for painted armbands and severed paws.",sp=0},{t="Merciless Taskmasters",b="Beyond petty ambushes, the Riverpaws exhibit an unusual drive for conquest and labor. They drove a clan of kobolds out of the Redridge Mountains and forced them into the gold mines of Elwynn Forest and Westfall, working the small creatures into relentless exhaustion to extract ore. This cruel tyranny over the mines suggests the gnolls are no longer simple scavengers living hand-to-mouth, but a faction amassing wealth and consolidating strategic ground right under the crown's nose.",sp=0},{t="Shadows and Gold",b="The Riverpaw pack's sudden surge in discipline, armaments, and magic is not organic. Recovered ledgers and documents indicate that the gnolls are engaged in financial transactions with the Defias Brotherhood. While the Stormwind Army initially dismissed the pack as an animalistic nuisance, officials such as Wiley the Black and Captain Danuvin have come to realize the Riverpaws are being bankrolled and directed as part of a coordinated effort to destabilize the kingdom.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who leads the Riverpaw gnolls?",a="The most infamous leader of the Riverpaw pack is Hogger, a massive and ferocious gnoll stalking the southwestern woods of Elwynn Forest near Forest's Edge. The Stormwind authorities consider him an urgent threat and have posted a massive bounty for his head.",al={"riverpaw leader","who leads the riverpaw","hogger riverpaw"},it="leader"},{q="Where are Riverpaw gnolls found?",a="The Riverpaw pack primarily infests southwestern Elwynn Forest around Forest's Edge and Stone Cairn Lake, as well as the open expanses of Westfall, particularly throughout the Dust Plains and along the coastline.",al={"riverpaw locations","where to find riverpaws","riverpaw gnoll spawn"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are the Riverpaw gnolls working with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Documents recovered from Riverpaw scouts reveal that payments are exchanged between the clan and the Defias Brotherhood. The Defias fund and equip the Riverpaws to spread chaos, pillage resources, and tie down local militias across Westfall and Elwynn, weakening Stormwind's defense.",al={"riverpaw defias connection","defias paying gnolls","gnoll brotherhood connection"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="Why are kobolds working in the gold mines near the Riverpaw?",a="The Riverpaw gnolls conquered a clan of Redridge kobolds and enslaved them, forcing them into the mines of Elwynn Forest and Westfall. The gnolls serve as merciless overseers, compelling the kobolds to dig out precious gold to fuel their war efforts.",al={"gnolls and kobolds","why do gnolls have kobold slaves","riverpaw kobolds"}},{q="Why hasn't the Stormwind Army wiped out the Riverpaw pack?",a="The kingdom's official military is severely overextended, with few reinforcements sent to rural garrisons like Sentinel Hill and Westbrook. Local marshals must rely on passing adventurers and militia bounties to keep the gnoll population from completely overwhelming the territory.",al={"why aren't guards fighting gnolls","stormwind gnoll response"},it="danger"},{q="What is the historical relationship between the Riverpaws and Stormwind?",a="The Riverpaws have clashed with Stormwind for generations. In the years after the Third War, the pack grew bolder and more organized, raiding the farms and roads of Elwynn and Westfall while the kingdom's soldiers were stretched thin.",al={"riverpaw history","prince llane gnolls"},it="history"},{q="What is the Riverpaw pack?",a="The Riverpaw pack is an aggressive clan of gnolls indigenous to Elwynn Forest and Westfall. Ferocious, hyena-like scavengers and brigands, they roam the countryside raiding farms, plundering travelers, and clashing with local garrisons. Recently, they have demonstrated an alarming degree of organization, enslaving kobolds to mine gold and aligning themselves with human outlaws to destabilize Stormwind's territories.",al={"riverpaw gnolls identity","who are the riverpaw","what are riverpaws"},it="identity",add="dbbc58c8"},{q="Why are the Riverpaw gnolls hostile to travelers?",a="Like all gnolls, the Riverpaws are vicious, territorial scavengers driven by bloodlust and greed. They view any traveler through Elwynn or Westfall as prey, food, or plunder. Their hostility has only grown bolder and more coordinated as shadowy backers supply them with weapons, magic, and tactical direction to terrorize settlements and drive human defenders out of the countryside.",al={"why do riverpaw attack","riverpaw gnoll hostility","why are gnolls hostile"},it="hostile",add="b2a04ab0"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Riverpaws represent an immediate security crisis for the Alliance heartland, cutting off trade routes between Goldshire, Westfall, and Redridge while Stormwind's armies remain absent.",["race:Human"]="For the farmers and homesteaders of Westfall and Elwynn, the Riverpaws are a nightmare at their doorstep, slaughtering families, stealing livestock, and seizing local mines.",["class:Warrior"]="Town marshals pay well in coin and prestige for warriors willing to march into the brush to cull the Riverpaw brutes and duel their warlords like Hogger.",["class:Rogue"]="Intercepting Riverpaw messengers reveals that their savage violence is orchestrated by criminal syndicates, yielding valuable intelligence on the Defias network."},kw={"defias","dust plains","elwynn","forest's edge","gnolls","hogger","kobold slaves","riverpaw","westfall"},rel={"npc:hogger","zone:elwynn","zone:westfall","topic:defias-brotherhood","quest:176","quest:11","quest:102","subzone:westbrook-garrison"},src={"Riverpaw pack"}}
E["topic:rogue"] = {t="topic",n="Rogue",z="general",s="Rogues are masters of stealth, espionage, and sudden, lethal combat who dictate the terms of engagement from the shadows. Relying on light leather armor, dual-wielded blades, and brewed toxins, they strike unawares before vanishing back into the dark. Across Azeroth, they serve as scouts, assassins, pickpockets, and agents for factions and hidden syndicates alike.",h="Rogues are masters of stealth, espionage, and sudden, lethal combat who dictate the terms of engagement from...",sec={{t="Strike from the Shadows",b="Rogues eschew the heavy plate and open confrontation of traditional frontline warriors. Clad in lightweight leather to remain completely silent and mobile, they stalk their quarry unseen. When they engage, they seize the opening moments through precision and surprise, aiming for vital organs or exposed joints. Utilizing an ever-flowing pool of energy and rapid weapon combinations, a rogue softens up an enemy with carefully chained strikes before unleashing a catastrophic finishing blow.",sp=0},{t="Poisons and Street Smarts",b="A blade alone is rarely enough for a professional cutpurse or killer. Rogues are skilled alchemists of lethal concoctions, hand-crafting venomous coatings to numb limbs, slow escapes, or rapidly sap the vitality of their targets. Coupled with lockpicking tools, thrown weapons, and distracting trickery, they excel at slipping past fortified perimeters, disarming traps, and infiltrating vaults that would stump a company of soldiers.",sp=0},{t="Guilds, Crowns, and Syndicates",b="While many rogues operate as lone wolves, highwaymen, or street-level thugs, the finest operatives belong to organized intelligence networks and assassin guilds. Stormwind relies on the clandestine operatives of SI:7, and the Forsaken utilize the relentless Deathstalkers. In Orgrimmar, the shadowy remnants of the Shattered Hand serve the Warchief, while the elite, neutral League of Assassins answers to Lord Jorach Ravenholdt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What armor and weapons do rogues use?",a="Rogues wear leather armor to ensure they can move silently and swiftly without encumbrance. In combat, they dual-wield one-handed armaments, most notably daggers, swords, maces, and fist weapons. They also carry silent ranged weapons such as throwing knives, bows, or crossbows for utility and pulling marks.",al={"rogue gear","what can rogues equip","rogue armor type"}},{q="How does stealth work for a rogue?",a="Stealth is a disciplined blend of light-footed movement, exploiting shadows, and subtle mysticism. It renders a rogue practically invisible to distant onlookers, though moving too close to an alert guard or enemy can give away their presence. Entering stealth allows rogues to position behind prey to deliver devastating ambushes, garrotes, and disabling strikes.",al={"how does rogue stealth work","can rogues turn invisible","stealth lore"}},{q="What is the Ravenholdt League?",a="The League of Assassins, headquartered at Ravenholdt Manor in the Alterac Mountains, is an ancient, clandestine brotherhood led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt. Unlike faction-bound intelligence units, Ravenholdt is a guild dedicated purely to the discipline, honor, and business of the assassin's craft, taking contracts regardless of political allegiance.",al={"who are ravenholdt","assassins league","lord jorach ravenholdt"}},{q="What is SI:7?",a="SI:7 is the premier covert intelligence and reconnaissance wing of the Kingdom of Stormwind. Headquartered in Stormwind City, its field agents, snipers, and thieves handle espionage, high-profile assassinations, and border reconnaissance to protect Alliance interests across Azeroth.",al={"stormwind si7","si7","stormwind intelligence"}},{q="How do rogues fight in prolonged combat?",a="When an assassination cannot be completed in an initial strike, a rogue uses agility and footwork to evade return blows. They execute sequential, rapid attacks with their dual weapons to exploit weaknesses—building tactical openings that culminate in lethal finishing blows like ruptures or eviscerations.",al={"rogue combat style","combo points lore","how do rogues fight"}},{q="Do rogues use magic?",a="Most rogues rely primarily on physical conditioning, martial training, alchemical concoctions, and mundane stealth. However, advanced practitioners often weave minor mysticism or shadow magic into their repertoire, helping them conceal their physical form, slip into shadows in broad daylight, and vanish instantly from sight.",al={"is rogue stealth magic","do rogues cast spells"}},{q="Which races can be rogues?",a="Every race but the tauren: humans, dwarves, gnomes and night elves in the Alliance, orcs, trolls and the Forsaken in the Horde, and the Skyborne on both sides.",al={"who can be a rogue","rogue races","can tauren be rogues"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="For a rogue, every mission is an exercise in leverage, patience, and lethal precision, whether working as an operative for high command or picking pockets in city alleys.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance clandestine power runs through organizations like Stormwind's SI:7, which handles internal subversion and foreign threats.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde depends on the espionage of the Shattered Hand in Orgrimmar and Sylvanas Windrunner's feared Deathstalkers in the Undercity to scout enemy territory and eliminate threats quietly."},kw={"assassin","deathstalker","eviscerate","lockpicking","poisons","ravenholdt","rogue","shattered hand","si:7","stealth"},rel={"topic:ravenholdt","npc:lord-jorach-ravenholdt","quest:2300","quest:1858","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:forsaken","zone:orgrimmar","subzone:forlorn-cavern"},src={"Rogue"}}
E["topic:royal-apothecary-society"] = {t="topic",n="Royal Apothecary Society",z="general",s="The Royal Apothecary Society is an alchemical guild established under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and based in the Apothecarium beneath the Undercity. Composed of Forsaken researchers, its primary mission is compounding a lethal New Plague capable of eradicating the Scourge while claiming vengeance against the living. While Horde allies believe the society seeks a cure for undeath, its researchers actively harvest venoms, slimes, and toxic reagents across Azeroth to perfect weapons of mass destruction.",h="The Royal Apothecary Society is an alchemical guild established under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and based in...",sec={{t="Vengeance Through Alchemy",b="Founded in the wake of the Forsaken's liberation, the Royal Apothecary Society serves as the scientific and alchemical arm of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom. From their vats and subterranean laboratories in the Apothecarium, researchers seek to weaponize the very force that damned them. Their preeminent endeavor is the creation of a 'New Plague'—a concoction intended to eradicate the Scourge and destroy the Lich King. Unlike traditional human alchemy, which prioritizes elixirs of healing or transmutation, the Society experiments ruthlessly with foul pathogens, caustic acids, and necromantic blends, raising fallen warhorses as skeletal steeds and brewing deadlier strains of toxic blight.",sp=0},{t="A Calculated Deception",b="To the outside world, the Society presents an air of misunderstood scholars. When dealing with their allies in Orgrimmar and Thunder Bluff, Forsaken representatives suggest their alchemists toil tirelessly to uncover a remedy for the curse of undeath. In truth, curing the affliction is of little interest to Master Apothecary Faranell and the dark council. The Society works in absolute secrecy to hone toxins that spare no one, secretly testing their efficacy against both living and undead test subjects to ensure utter annihilation of their enemies.",sp=1},{t="Outposts Across Azeroth",b="The reach of the Apothecary Society extends far beyond the canals of the Undercity. Field apothecaries are dispatched to remote outposts across Lordaeron and Kalimdor. In Silverpine and Hillsbrad, agents like Apothecaries Renferrel and Lydon gather local floras and distill venoms. In the arid Barrens, Apothecary Helbrim catalogs strange desert flora and creatures, while Apothecary Zamah operates beneath Thunder Bluff in the Pools of Vision. Wherever natural toxins, shifting oozes, or virulent organisms exist, the Society sends acolytes to collect samples for the Apothecarium's cauldrons.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Royal Apothecary Society?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society is the Forsaken alchemical organization headquartered in the Apothecarium within the Undercity. Created under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner, its members study virulent pathogens, necromancy, and toxic reagents to craft chemical weapons for the Forsaken.",al={"what is the ras","who are the apothecaries","forsaken alchemy guild"},it="identity"},{q="What is the New Plague they are working on?",a="The New Plague is a bespoke alchemical pathogen developed by the Society under Sylvanas's direct orders. Its ultimate purpose is to possess the power to exterminate the Scourge and the Lich King, while also remaining lethal to living beings who threaten the Forsaken.",al={"new plague explained","what is the forsaken plague","why make a new plague"},it="purpose"},{q="Does the Horde know what the Society is really brewing?",a="No. The Forsaken maintain the facade that their research focuses on curing the affliction of undeath and finding a peaceful place for their kind. The Tauren and Orcs believe the apothecaries seek a medical remedy, unaware that the Society is engineering a lethal weapon of eradication.",al={"does thrall know about the plague","horde stance on apothecaries","do orcs know about the plague"},it="ties"},{q="Who leads the Royal Apothecary Society?",a="While the Society ultimately answers to Queen Sylvanas Windrunner, its daily scientific efforts and research within the Undercity are directed by senior overseers such as Master Apothecary Faranell.",al={"leader of the apothecaries","ras leader","who runs the apothecarium"},it="leader"},{q="Why are apothecaries stationed in places like the Barrens or Thunder Bluff?",a="Field apothecaries search for rare biological agents, exotic venom, fungi, and mutagenic oozes not found in Lordaeron. Agents like Helbrim in the Crossroads and Zamah in Thunder Bluff study local ecology and collect dangerous samples to send back to the laboratories in the Undercity.",al={"why are apothecaries in kalimdor","apothecaries outside tirisfal","apothecary zamah pools of vision"},it="why_here"},{q="How does the Society create Forsaken mounts?",a="Rather than allowing fallen warhorses to rot, the Royal Apothecary Society blends dark alchemy and necromancy to reanimate deceased steeds, raising them as skeletal mounts to serve Forsaken riders in battle.",al={"where do undead horses come from","forsaken mount lore","skeletal horse origin"}},{q="Who is the Royal Apothecary Society allied with?",a="The Society serves Lady Sylvanas Windrunner directly as an organ of the Forsaken state, working in close cooperation with her dreadlord enforcer Varimathras for supplies and subjects. Through the Forsaken, they are nominally part of the Horde, though their relationship with the rest of the faction is built on deceit: while the orcs and tauren believe the Society seeks a cure for undeath, the apothecaries operate purely in service to Sylvanas's vengeance.",al={"apothecary society allies","who do the apothecaries work for","who controls royal apothecary society"},it="allies",add="da4c33fe"},{q="How was the Royal Apothecary Society formed?",a="The Society was founded after Sylvanas established the Forsaken realm in the Undercity, to turn the plague that created the Forsaken into a weapon against their enemies. Today Master Apothecary Faranell directs its research from the Apothecarium beneath the capital.",al={"origin of the royal apothecary society","how did the apothecary society start","history of royal apothecary society"},it="history",add="ac67cb21"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Society represents the scientific might of your people, turning the very plague that butchered Lordaeron into an instrument of vengeance against the Scourge and all who oppose you.",["faction:Horde"]="Forsaken representatives claim their alchemical work seeks an antidote to restore their cursed condition, though their secluded cauldrons in the Apothecarium reek of deadlier ambitions.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The Society places an enormous premium on deadly flora, harvesting toxic fungi, stranglekelp, and grave moss from every corner of Azeroth to fuel their virulent cauldrons.",["race:Tauren"]="Out of pity for the cursed dead, the elders permitted Apothecary Zamah into the Pools of Vision, believing the Forsaken genuinely sought a cleansing cure for their affliction."},kw={"alchemy","apothecarium","faranell","helbrim","lydon","new plague","royal apothecary society","sylvanas windrunner","undercity","zamah"},rel={"subzone:apothecarium","npc:master-apothecary-faranell","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","quest:367","zone:undercity","topic:forsaken","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge"},src={"Royal Apothecary Society"}}
E["topic:ruins-of-gilneas"] = {t="topic",n="Gilneas",z="general",s="Beyond the Greymane Wall, Gilneas keeps the world at a distance. The human kingdom lies on a rugged peninsula south of Silverpine Forest, sealed away after the Second War by King Genn Greymane.",h="Beyond the Greymane Wall, Gilneas keeps the world at a distance.",sec={{t="A Nation Withdrawn",b="Gilneas once ruled the waves among the human kingdoms and traced its line to Arathor, the ancient human empire. King Genn Greymane entered the Second War reluctantly. He believed his realm could defend itself without ties to foreign powers.\n\nBitter over Gilnean deaths and unwilling to help pay for internment camps holding captured orcs, he broke with the Alliance of Lordaeron. Then he ordered a great stone wall along Gilneas's northern frontier to shield the kingdom from calamity beyond its borders.",sp=0},{t="The Severed Frontier",b="The Greymane Wall left two villages exposed. Its builders anchored the ramparts to natural mountain passes, leaving Ambermill and Pyrewood Village beyond the gate on the northern side. When the Scourge crossed Lordaeron during the Third War, King Greymane refused pleas for aid. He barred human refugees from entering, and the villages beyond the wall faced plague and undead alone.",sp=0},{t="A Looming Shadow",b="During the Third War, Dalaran archmage Arugal summoned feral worgen to aid the Kirin Tor against the Scourge. The beasts slew the undead, then turned on the wizards and besieged Baron Silverlaine's keep. They spread through the shadows of Silverpine. Gilneas remains sealed behind its gates and treacherous sea reefs, while eerie rumors and silence shroud the kingdom.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Can players enter Gilneas?",a="No. Gilneas lies behind the Greymane Wall at Silverpine Forest's southern border. Its iron gate is barred, and sheer sea cliffs and treacherous reefs guard the peninsula's coast.",al={"can you go past greymane wall","is gilneas open","get into gilneas"},it="arrival",gp=true},{q="Why was the Greymane Wall built?",a="King Genn Greymane believed Gilneas could stand on its own and resented spending lives and resources to aid other human nations. After the Second War, bitter over debts, internment camps and foreign conflicts, he built the wall to isolate Gilneas from the rest of Lordaeron.",al={"why did gilneas isolate","greymane wall purpose","why closed off"},it="purpose"},{q="What happened to Pyrewood Village and Ambermill?",a="The Greymane Wall followed defensible mountain ridges, leaving Pyrewood Village and Ambermill on its northern side, outside the kingdom. Once the gates were sealed, the villages were cut off from Gilneas.",al={"why is pyrewood outside wall","ambermill gilneas connection"},it="ruin"},{q="Is Gilneas part of the Alliance?",a="No. Genn Greymane withdrew Gilneas from the Alliance of Lordaeron. The kingdom has no diplomatic ties, military treaties or trade with Stormwind, Ironforge or any other Alliance power.",al={"gilneas faction","are gilneans in the alliance","gilneas alliance"},it="allies"},{q="What connection does Arugal have to Gilneas?",a="During the Third War, Dalaran archmage Arugal summoned feral worgen to aid the Kirin Tor against the Scourge. They slew the undead, then turned on the wizards, overran Shadowfang Keep and spread through Silverpine, driving Arugal mad.",al={"arugal gilneas","how did arugal get worgen"},it="ties"},{q="Who rules Gilneas?",a="King Genn Greymane rules Gilneas. He fiercely guards the kingdom's independence, leaving the world beyond its gates to fend for itself.",al={"gilneas leader","king of gilneas"},it="leader"},{q="What is Gilneas?",a="Gilneas is an ancient human kingdom situated on a rugged peninsula southwest of Silverpine Forest. Once an influential member of the original Alliance of Lordaeron, its rulers chose a path of fierce isolationism after the Second War. Today, the entire kingdom remains sealed away behind the towering Greymane Wall, cut off from the rest of Azeroth by land and guarded by stormy seas and jagged reefs.",al={"what is gilneas","ruins of gilneas","gilneas kingdom"},it="identity",add="54d2a8c9"},{q="What is the history of Gilneas?",a="Gilneas arose over two thousand years ago as an independent city-state during the expansion of the human empire of Arathor. Known for its self-reliance, the kingdom joined the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. Disillusioned with the costs of the conflict and unwilling to help pay for orcish internment camps, King Genn Greymane severed ties with his allies and constructed the Greymane Wall to shut out foreign troubles.",al={"gilneas history","origin of gilneas","how was gilneas founded"},it="history",add="8a1cf72e"}},ang={["race:Human"]="As a human of Lordaeron or Stormwind, you may see Gilneas as a bitter reminder of how pride and isolation broke the unity of the Seven Kingdoms before the Third War.",["race:Undead"]="As a Forsaken, you patrol southern Silverpine and watch the great gate. A whole human kingdom remains alive and hostile just across your border.",["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you know Archmage Arugal's summoning beneath the Greymane Wall as a grim warning of magic turned to war without restraint."},kw={"ambermill","arugal","genn","gilneas","greymane","greymane wall","pyrewood","silverpine","worgen"},rel={"subzone:greymane-wall","zone:silverpine","npc:arugal","subzone:pyrewood-village","subzone:ambermill","topic:worgen","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron"},src={"Ruins of Gilneas"}}
E["topic:sargeras"] = {t="topic",n="Sargeras",z="general",s="Sargeras is the fallen titan and supreme creator of the Burning Legion, bent on scouring all life from the cosmos. Once the noble champion and defender of the Pantheon, his descent into madness led to the Sundering of ancient Azeroth and nearly every demonic catastrophe since.",h="Sargeras is the fallen titan and supreme creator of the Burning Legion, bent on scouring all life from the...",sec={{t="Champion of the Pantheon",b="Before his fall, Sargeras was the greatest and noblest warrior among the titans. Entrusted by the Pantheon of Order to safeguard their grand creations across the Great Dark Beyond, he waged war against the chaotic demons spawned from the Twisting Nether. Incapable of conceiving pure evil, Sargeras trapped countless fiends away within a great prison. Yet eons of relentless warfare against cosmic horrors gradually wore down his altruistic spirit.",sp=0},{t="Despair and the Burning Crusade",b="Over eons of battle, Sargeras's faith in the titans' work eroded. Despairing at the evil he faced, he concluded that creation was fundamentally flawed and that order could never truly prevail. Turning his back on the Pantheon, he shattered the demons' prison, embraced the chaotic fel fire he had once fought, and marshaled the liberated demons into the unstoppable Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="Obsession with Azeroth",b="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless magic of Queen Azshara and her Highborne reached across the stars, alerting Sargeras to Azeroth's existence. Whispering through the depths of the Well of Eternity, the Dark Titan seduced Azshara into opening a portal for his vanguard. Though that ancient invasion culminated in the catastrophic Sundering, Sargeras never ceased scheming to consume Azeroth—orchestrating the opening of the Dark Portal and guiding the demonic corruption of the orcish Horde.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sargeras?",a="Sargeras is a fallen titan who once served as the Pantheon's greatest warrior. Driven by despair over cosmic corruption, he betrayed his kind and forged the Burning Legion to purge all life from the universe.",al={"sargeras backstory","who is the dark titan","creator of the burning legion"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Sargeras want to destroy the universe?",a="Eons of battling demons and corruption drove him to despair. He came to believe that creation itself was flawed and that order could never prevail, and chose to burn away all life rather than let it go on.",al={"sargeras motive","why burning crusade","why does sargeras want to destroy everything"},it="motive"},{q="What is Sargeras's connection to Queen Azshara?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara's reckless use of the Well of Eternity drew Sargeras's attention. Viewing him as a god, Azshara and her Highborne attempted to summon the Dark Titan through the Well, triggering the devastating War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering.",al={"sargeras and azshara","well of eternity sargeras","ancient invasion azshara"},it="ties"},{q="Did Sargeras create the demons?",a="No. Demons originally spawned naturally within the chaotic Twisting Nether. Sargeras fought and imprisoned them for ages before choosing to shatter their prison, uniting the demonic races under his command to form the Burning Legion.",al={"did sargeras make demons","where did the burning legion come from"},it="origin"},{q="What happened to the other titans?",a="Mortal scholars do not know. The titans departed Azeroth long ago after shaping it, and nothing is known on Azeroth of what became of them after Sargeras turned against his kin.",al={"where are the other titans","pantheon fate","did sargeras kill the pantheon"},it="history"},{q="How was Sargeras connected to the opening of the Dark Portal?",a="Long after the Sundering, Sargeras continued his designs on Azeroth from afar, orchestrating events to corrupt the orcs of Draenor and using the guardian Medivh to tear open the Dark Portal, bringing invasion once again to mortal shores.",al={"sargeras orcs","sargeras dark portal","did sargeras bring orcs"},it="conflict"},{q="Who are Sargeras's allies in the cosmos?",a="Sargeras commands the Burning Legion, a vast and terrifying horde of demonic entities he bound to his will. Rather than fighting alongside equal allies, he rules over corrupted races such as the eredar lords Archimonde and Kil'jaeden, dreadful pit lords like Mannoroth, cunning nathrezim dreadlords, and endless ranks of lesser demons. All in the Legion exist solely to serve his apocalyptic crusade.",al={"sargeras allies","who serves sargeras","sargeras burning legion"},it="allies",add="f96cad47"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For the kaldorei, Sargeras is the ultimate architect of the Great Sundering, whose seductive whispers corrupted Queen Azshara and shattered ancient Kalimdor ten millennia ago.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks draw upon the very fel powers and demon servitors bound together by the Dark Titan, wielding the ruinous sorceries that formed the Legion.",["race:Orc"]="The tragic history of the orcish clans—blood-curse, madness, and the invasion of Azeroth—traces directly back to the manipulations of Sargeras and his demonic lieutenants.",["class:Mage"]="Mages remember Sargeras as a cautionary terror, proving that unchecked arcane ambition will inevitably attract the gaze of the Burning Legion across the stars."},kw={"aggramar","azshara","burning legion","dark titan","fel","pantheon","sargeras","sundering"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:highborne","zone:azshara","subzone:dark-portal","topic:horde"},src={"Sargeras"}}
E["topic:satyr"] = {t="topic",n="Satyr",z="general",s="Satyrs are twisted, caprine demons born from the curse of the Highborne sorcerer Xavius during the ancient War of the Ancients. Once night elves, they were warped by Sargeras into horned, hooved beasts dedicated to spreading fel corruption, tormenting their former kin, and furthering the Burning Legion's will. Across the wilds of Kalimdor, scattered satyr sects corrupt pristine woodlands, desecrate moonwells, and subvert vulnerable mortal cults.",h="Satyrs are twisted, caprine demons born from the curse of the Highborne sorcerer Xavius during the ancient...",sec={{t="Origins of the Curse",b="Ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients, the Highborne councilor Xavius failed to keep the Burning Legion's initial gateway stable against Malfurion Stormrage. Enraged by this blunder, Sargeras dismantled Xavius's spirit and rebuilt him as a punishment and tool: his torso retained elven features, but he gained wicked horns, taloned claws, a leonine tail, and the cloven lower body of a goat. Sent back into Zin-Azshari, Xavius gleefully passed his fel affliction to other power-hungry Highborne. Though Xavius was struck down, his demonic progeny survived, continually seeking to reproduce their kind by corrupting others.",sp=0},{t="After the Sundering",b="Following the Great Sundering, the surviving satyrs continued to menace the shattered night elf civilization. Over the ages they were broken and driven off, their remnants fracturing into scattered sects that linger in dark forests and ancient ruins while harboring deep hatred for their kaldorei cousins.",sp=0},{t="Corrupters of Kalimdor",b="Today, satyr clans inhabit deep woodland enclaves across Kalimdor, from Darkshore and Ashenvale to Felwood, Azshara, and Feralas. They feed directly on the land's pure magic, leeching and spoiling nature until their hooves swell with drained arcane energy. Satyrs frequently turn peaceful furbolg tribes violent, taint ancient moonwells, and serve as covert liaisons for the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. While some work alongside Twilight's Hammer cultists or hide within ancient ruins like Dire Maul, all remain bound to the shadow of the Burning Legion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What exactly is a satyr?",a="A satyr is a demon transformed from a night elf through an ancient fel curse. They possess cloven hooves, horns, sharp claws, and a tail, retaining an elven torso while wielding dark fel sorcery and physical savagery.",al={"what are satyrs","satyr race","satyr lore"},it="identity"},{q="Who was the first satyr?",a="Lord Xavius, the Highborne sorcerer and chief advisor to Queen Azshara, was the first satyr. After Xavius failed to defend the Burning Legion's portal during the War of the Ancients, Sargeras remade his body into a demonic, horned form as both punishment and a dark gift.",al={"first satyr","who created satyrs","xavius satyr"},it="origin"},{q="Can satyrs make new satyrs?",a="Yes. Existing satyrs can inflict their transformative curse on other beings, primarily night elves, though the magic has weakened over the millennia and successful transformations are far rarer today. Scattered cultists occasionally revere Xavius hoping to earn the transformation.",al={"how are satyrs made","satyr curse spread","becoming a satyr"},it="power"},{q="Are there female satyrs?",a="Scattered reports speak of female satyrs, but scholars have yet to secure any irrefutable proof that they exist.",al={"female satyrs","are all satyrs male","girl satyr"},it="people"},{q="Why do satyrs target moonwells and forests?",a="Satyrs feed on purity and natural arcane magic, leeching it directly from pristine lands and night elf moonwells. They store this stolen energy in their hooves, which become swollen and uncloven as they absorb immense magical power.",al={"why do satyrs corrupt moonwells","satyr hooves lore","satyr magic"},it="motive"},{q="Where are satyrs found right now?",a="Satyrs are scattered in ruined and corrupted reaches across Kalimdor, including Satyrnaar and Xavian in Ashenvale, Bashal'Aran in Darkshore, Jadefire Run in Felwood, Sargeron in Desolace, the Haldarr and Legash encampments in Azshara, and within Dire Maul in Feralas.",al={"satyr locations","where do satyrs live","satyr zones"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are satyrs hostile to travelers?",a="Satyrs are thoroughly demonic beings consumed by cruelty and spite. They delight in inflicting physical and emotional pain upon mortals, harboring a deep, ancient hatred particularly for night elves. As servants of demonic corruption, they see any uncorrupted travelers as trespassers and prey, actively striving to taint the land and butcher anyone who stands in the way of spreading their fel corruption.",al={"why do satyrs attack","are satyrs hostile","why satyrs fight"},it="hostile",add="5dcac0d4"},{q="Who leads the satyrs?",a="They have no single overall ruler. In the ages after the War of the Ancients, their centralized hierarchy shattered completely, forcing them into fractured sects and cults. Each local band answers to its own warlord or sorcerer—such as Prince Xavalis in Felwood. Many continue to revere the memory of Xavius, the first satyr, hoping he will return to command them.",al={"satyr leader","who is the satyr king","satyr leadership"},it="leader",add="bac5d43e"},{q="Who are satyrs allied with?",a="Most satyrs remain bound to the will of the Burning Legion, operating alongside demonic remnants and demonic cults like the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. While some isolated sects act on their own or ally with other dark forces such as the Twilight's Hammer, they ultimately remain demonic thralls who will answer the call whenever the Legion commands them.",al={"satyr allies","who do satyrs serve","satyr factions"},it="allies",add="6fc26cea"},{q="What is the history of the satyrs?",a="Satyrs arose during the War of the Ancients when Sargeras punished the Highborne sorcerer Xavius by reshaping his soul and body into the first horned, cloven-hoofed demon. Xavius passed this demonic curse to other ambitious Highborne. After the Legion fell, the satyrs waged a desperate war against the night elves until they were broken and scattered, lingering in ruined forests as bitter, fragmented remnants.",al={"satyr history","how were satyrs created","where did satyrs come from"},it="history",add="5221b631"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Satyrs represent the ultimate betrayal of kaldorei heritage, serving as a bitter reminder of the Highborne's arrogance during the War of the Ancients.",["class:Druid"]="Satyrs actively poison the wilds, corrupt furbolgs, and defile sacred groves, making their destruction an urgent imperative for the Cenarion Circle.",["class:Warlock"]="As beings of pure fel energy and demonic malice, satyrs demonstrate the corruptive mastery of the Burning Legion's ancient curses.",["faction:Alliance"]="Sentinels and Alliance adventurers constantly fight to purge satyr encampments encroaching on night elven settlements throughout Ashenvale and Darkshore.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde clashes violently with satyr sects in Ashenvale and Desolace, rooting out their clandestine alliances with the Burning Blade."},kw={"corrupter","demon","fel","highborne","jadefire","sargeron","satyr","satyrnaar","xavian","xavius"},rel={"topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:burning-legion","zone:ashenvale","zone:felwood","subzone:satyrnaar","quest:1842"},src={"Satyr"}}
E["topic:scarlet-crusade"] = {t="topic",n="Scarlet Crusade",z="general",s="Born from the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical holy order obsessed with purging the undead from Lordaeron. Blighted by paranoia and radical zealotry, they view anyone outside their ranks as carriers of the plague and shoot outsiders on sight. From strongholds across Tirisfal Glades and the Plaguelands, their crimson banners wage a ruthless war against Scourge and living alike.",h="Born from the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical holy...",sec={{t="Rise from the Ashes",b="When Prince Arthas betrayed Lordaeron and murdered King Terenas Menethil II, the Order of the Silver Hand was broken. Surviving paladins and priests rallied under leaders like Alexandros Mograine, Saidan Dathrohan, and General Abbendis to reclaim their ruined homeland. Reorganizing their forces at Hearthglen and Tyr's Hand, they swore to cleanse the undead scourge with holy fire. Yet as the war dragged on, tragedy and suspicion festered within their ranks. What began as a desperate resistance soon devolved into an unyielding, xenophobic crusade.",sp=0},{t="The Great Schism",b="As the Crusade's dogma hardened into religious madness, moderate commanders recoiled. Lord Maxwell Tyrosus and his followers argued that retaking the north required alliances across Azeroth, seeking aid from the dwarves, gnomes, and night elves. Rejected by the zealots, Tyrosus and other disillusioned knights abandoned the order to form the Argent Dawn, remaining faithful to the pure, inclusive tenets of the Holy Light. Meanwhile, the Scarlet Crusade seized the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades as their heart of power, slaughtering travelers and refugees under the hysterical belief that all who walk the Plaguelands carry the Scourge's contagion.",sp=0},{t="A Puppet in Crimson",b="The Crusade's extreme descent into butchery was not entirely of their own making. During an assault on Stratholme, Grand Crusader Saidan Dathrohan was separated from his men and quietly murdered by the dreadlord Balnazzar. Possessing Dathrohan's corpse, the demon assumed direct control over the Scarlet ranks. Balnazzar subtly fueled their paranoia, orchestrating the murder of Highlord Alexandros Mograine through the jealousy of his son Renault. Behind grand speeches of purity and righteous fury, the crusaders serve as unknowing puppets to the very demonic darkness they believe the Light calls them to destroy.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade hostile to everyone?",a="The Crusade has succumbed to extreme paranoia after the fall of Lordaeron. They believe that anyone who has spent time in northern Lordaeron without being cleansed by their order is an active or latent carrier of the plague of undeath. To preserve their vision of pure humanity, they execute all outsiders on sight.",al={"why are scarlets hostile","are scarlets bad guys","why does scarlet crusade attack me"},it="hostile"},{q="Where did the Scarlet Crusade come from?",a="They originated from surviving knights and priests of the Knights of the Silver Hand after Lordaeron fell during the Third War. Regrouping in Hearthglen and the Scarlet Monastery under figures like Saidan Dathrohan and Alexandros Mograine, they pledged to eradicate the Scourge by any means necessary.",al={"scarlet crusade origin","who founded scarlet crusade","scarlet crusade history"},it="history"},{q="How is the Scarlet Crusade different from the Argent Dawn?",a="Both orders splintered from the original Silver Hand, but while the Argent Dawn accepts all races and remains true to the compassionate virtues of the Light, the Scarlet Crusade became a fanatical, human-supremacist cult that massacres refugees and travelers under the guise of holy cleansing.",al={"argent dawn vs scarlet crusade","difference between scarlet and argent dawn"},it="conflict"},{q="Where are the Scarlet Crusade's main bases located?",a="Their primary bastion is the Scarlet Monastery in northeastern Tirisfal Glades. They also control the fortified town of Hearthglen in the Western Plaguelands, Tyr's Hand in the east, and the Scarlet Bastion inside the ruins of Stratholme, alongside numerous scout towers across the region.",al={"scarlet crusade bases","scarlet locations","where are scarlet crusaders"},it="why_here"},{q="Who actually leads the Scarlet Crusade?",a="Outwardly, Grand Crusader Saidan Dathrohan commands the Crusade from Stratholme, supported by High Inquisitor Whitemane and Scarlet Commander Mograine at the Monastery.",al={"who is leader of scarlet crusade","scarlet crusade grand crusader"},sp=2,it="leader"},{q="Can the Scarlet Crusaders still use the Holy Light if they are evil?",a="Yes. In the cosmology of the Light, wielding its power does not require moral goodness, but absolute, unwavering conviction and faith. The crusaders genuinely believe their brutal crusade is pure and just, allowing them to channel holy wrath despite their cruelty.",al={"why can scarlets use the light","scarlet crusade paladins light","do scarlets have the light"},it="power"},{q="What is the Scarlet Crusade?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical religious order of human zealots dedicated to wiping out all undead from the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron. Originally formed from surviving paladins and priests of the Knights of the Silver Hand after the Third War, their righteous cause quickly devolved into paranoid extremism. Convinced that anyone outside their ranks could carry the plague, they violently purge any outsider they encounter.",al={"who are scarlet crusade","scarlet crusaders who are they","what are the scarlets"},it="identity",add="7fbcc7ca"},{q="Who is the Scarlet Crusade allied with?",a="They have no true allies. While born from the ruins of the human Alliance of Lordaeron, the Scarlet Crusade views all outsiders as potential carriers of the Scourge plague or traitors to humanity. They reject the Alliance of Stormwind, murder night elves, dwarves, and gnomes on sight, and wage relentless war against both the Scourge and the Forsaken.",al={"scarlet crusade allies","who supports scarlet crusade","are scarlets part of the alliance"},it="allies",add="6a9b40aa"}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="The Scarlet Crusade represents the darkest perversion of the Silver Hand's teachings, demonstrating how righteous conviction can twist into tyrannical fanaticism without humility and compassion.",["race:Undead"]="The Scarlet Crusade is the Forsaken's most immediate living nemesis, contesting the borders of Tirisfal Glades and actively hunting any undead who break free from the Lich King's grasp.",["faction:Alliance"]="Though they wear the armor and speak the language of fallen Lordaeron, the Scarlet Crusade considers the modern Alliance compromised and executes Alliance emissaries without hesitation.",["class:Priest"]="Studying the Crusade reveals the terrifying potency of faith stripped of moral restraint, where holy scripture is used to justify ruthless inquisitions and village purges."},kw={"argent dawn","ashbringer","hearthglen","saidan dathrohan","sally whitemane","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","silver hand","tyr's hand"},rel={"zone:scarlet","zone:tirisfal","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:argent-dawn","npc:sally-whitemane","subzone:stratholme","subzone:hearthglen","subzone:tyr-s-hand"},src={"Scarlet Crusade"}}
E["topic:scourge"] = {t="topic",n="Scourge",z="general",s="The Scourge is an apocalyptic army of undead monsters, necromancers, and enslaved spirits bound to the will of the Lich King. Originally created by the Burning Legion to destabilize Azeroth ahead of the Third War, the legion of walking corpses broke free from demonic control and shattered the kingdoms of Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas. Today, they wage unending war from their arctic stronghold in Northrend and their corrupt dominion over the Plaguelands.",h="The Scourge is an apocalyptic army of undead monsters, necromancers, and enslaved spirits bound to the will...",sec={{t="A Plague of Demonic Design",b="The Scourge was born as an instrument of destruction forged by the Burning Legion. The demon lords bound the spirit of the fallen orc shaman Ner'zhul to the Frozen Throne in distant Northrend, fashioning him into the Lich King. With terrifying telepathic power, he was commanded to prepare Azeroth for conquest. The Lich King unleashed the Plague of Undeath southward into the unsuspecting human realm of Lordaeron, relying on the mortal traitors of the Cult of the Damned to poison grain supplies. Those who perished arose not as restful dead, but as twisted thralls entirely subservient to the Lich King's iron mind.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the North",b="The Scourge achieved total devastation when Prince Arthas Menethil fell to the Lich King's whispers. Seeking vengeance in Northrend, Arthas claimed the cursed blade Frostmourne and forfeited his soul, becoming the Scourge's paramount death knight. Returning home, Arthas slew his father King Terenas, butchered Capital City, and unleashed the undead horde across Lordaeron. The Scourge swept through towns like Darrowshire and Andorhal, butchered the Silver Hand paladins, destroyed Dalaran to summon Archimonde, and carved a dead scar through Quel'Thalas to resurrect Kel'Thuzad as a lich.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal and the Schism",b="The Lich King never intended to serve the Burning Legion permanently. Through Arthas, Ner'zhul secretly engineered the Legion's defeat by goading Illidan Stormrage into slaying the dreadlord Tichondrius in Felwood. Enraged, the demon lord Kil'jaeden later ordered Illidan to obliterate the Frozen Throne. Illidan's assault fractured the icy prison, causing the Lich King's mental grip over his minions to waver. Seizing on this weakened control, a faction of free-willed undead led by the resurrected banshee Sylvanas Windrunner broke away, christened themselves the Forsaken, and seized the ruins of Lordaeron.",sp=0},{t="The Present Threat",b="Though weakened by the fracturing of the Frozen Throne and the rebellion of the Forsaken, the Scourge remains the deadliest force in the northern world. Led in the field by the lich Kel'Thuzad, its abominations, crypt fiends, and necromancers dominate the corrupted Eastweald and Westweald, now known as the Plaguelands. From grim bastions and blighted fields, they continuously wage war against the Forsaken of the Undercity, the fanatic zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, and the remaining enclaves of the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Scourge?",a="The Scourge is a vast empire of undead thralls, necromancers, and warped beasts bound mentally to the Lich King. They exist solely to destroy all living things and convert the corpses of the fallen into obedient servants.",al={"what is the scourge","undead scourge","who are the scourge"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Scourge?",a="The Scourge is commanded by the Lich King from his Frozen Throne in the distant, icy continent of Northrend. In the ruined lands of Lordaeron, his armies are directed locally by powerful lieutenants such as the lich Kel'Thuzad.",al={"who is the scourge leader","scourge master","who controls the scourge"},it="leader"},{q="How does the Scourge differ from the Forsaken?",a="The Scourge are mindless or dominated undead enslaved to the psychic will of the Lich King. The Forsaken are former Scourge victims who regained their free will when the Lich King's power weakened. Led by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Forsaken despise the Scourge and fight fiercely to annihilate them.",al={"scourge vs forsaken","are forsaken scourge","why are undead fighting each other"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Scourge attack Lordaeron?",a="The Burning Legion ordered the Lich King to spread the Plague of Undeath across Lordaeron to slaughter its people, weaken the Alliance, and clear away any resistance before the demons began their invasion of Azeroth during the Third War.",al={"why destroy lordaeron","scourging of lordaeron","why did scourge invade"},it="history"},{q="Who is the Cult of the Damned?",a="The Cult of the Damned is a mortal organization founded by the archmage Kel'Thuzad. Its members are living humans who willingly serve the Lich King, lured by promises of dark magical power and eternal life through undeath. They were responsible for spreading the tainted grain that initiated the plague.",al={"cult of the damned","scourge cultists","why do humans join scourge"},it="allies"},{q="How was Prince Arthas corrupted by the Scourge?",a="Driven to obsession while trying to save his kingdom from the plague, Prince Arthas pursued the dreadlord Mal'Ganis to Northrend. In desperation, Arthas took up the cursed runeblade Frostmourne, which stole his soul. Broken and twisted by the Lich King's whispers, he returned as the Scourge's premier champion and butchered his own homeland.",al={"how did arthas turn","arthas scourge","why did arthas betray his people"},it="history"},{q="Why is the undead army called the Scourge?",a="The necromancer Kel'Thuzad bestowed the name upon the army as he watched its ranks swell with freshly infected victims in northern Lordaeron. Beholding the monstrous host of mindless undead and devoted cultists rising under the Lich King's dark will, he proclaimed that the army would act as a scourge to sweep down upon the living and scour humanity from the face of the world.",al={"why called scourge","scourge name origin","how did scourge get its name"},it="name",add="8fc504d6"},{q="Why are the Scourge hostile to everyone?",a="The Scourge exists for a single horrific purpose: the eradication of all mortal life. Bound telepathically to the absolute will of the Lich King, its legions of rotting corpses, banshees, and monstrosities feel no mercy, exhaustion, or doubt. They view any living traveler as both an enemy to slaughter and fresh material to raise into their ever-expanding army of the dead.",al={"why does the scourge attack","why is scourge hostile","scourge aggressive why"},it="hostile",add="4bf4ad75"},{q="Where did the Scourge come from?",a="The Scourge was originally created by the demonic Burning Legion to soften Azeroth's defenses ahead of their invasion. The Legion took the spirit of the orc shaman Ner'zhul, bound him as the Lich King within the Frozen Throne in distant Northrend, and granted him dominion over necromancy. From his icy seat, he unleashed the Plague of Undeath southward into Lordaeron, subverting mortals into an unstoppable legion of undead servants.",al={"where did scourge come from","scourge origin","how was scourge created"},it="origin",add="03d58107"},{q="Why are the Scourge found throughout Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was ground zero for the Scourge's devastation of humanity. The Cult of the Damned contaminated the northern grain supplies with the Plague of Undeath, killing vast populations and raising them as mindless thralls. After Prince Arthas returned from Northrend as the Lich King's champion and murdered King Terenas, the Scourge swept across the kingdom, claiming its towns and transforming the Eastweald into the blighted Plaguelands you see today.",al={"why is scourge in plaguelands","scourge lordaeron why","why are undead in lordaeron"},it="why_here",add="51cb9edc"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every Forsaken was once an enslaved instrument of the Scourge, forced to butcher friends and kin until the Lich King's grip slipped. Eradicating the Scourge is not just a war of survival; it is a campaign of absolute personal vengeance.",["race:Human"]="The Scourge destroyed humanity's greatest kingdom, slaughtered the line of Menethil, and transformed the heartland of Lordaeron into a blighted wasteland.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were shattered by the Scourge when Prince Arthas turned on his own mentors and murdered Uther the Lightbringer. For any paladin, cleansing the Scourge with the Holy Light is a sacred duty.",["faction:Alliance"]="The memory of Lordaeron's fall remains an open wound for the Alliance, driving their ongoing military efforts to reclaim the Plaguelands and avenge the fallen.",["faction:Horde"]="With the Forsaken now accepted into the Horde, the entire coalition finds itself drawn into the brutal, ongoing war against the remnants of the Lich King's armies in the north."},kw={"arthas","cult of the damned","frostmourne","frozen throne","kel'thuzad","lich king","lordaeron","plague","plaguelands","scourge","undead"},rel={"topic:lich-king","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:forsaken","topic:lordaeron","topic:third-war","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Scourge"}}
E["topic:second-war"] = {t="topic",n="Second War",z="general",s="The Second War was the continent-spanning clash across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the newly forged Alliance of Lordaeron. Sparked after the sack of Stormwind, the war devastated Khaz Modan and northern human realms before turning at the gates of Capital City. It ended in the destruction of the Dark Portal and the dispersal of the old Horde.",h="The Second War was the continent-spanning clash across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the...",sec={{t="A Continent at War",b="Following the fall of Stormwind during the First War, Regent Anduin Lothar led human refugees north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. King Terenas Menethil II convened the Council of Seven Nations, forging the Alliance of Lordaeron alongside the high elves of Quel'Thalas, the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, and the gnomes of Gnomeregan. Meanwhile, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer consolidated the Horde, forging alliances with forest troll tribes and the Steamwheedle Cartel while subjugating red dragons through the Dragonmaw clan to invade the northern continents.",sp=0},{t="The Invasion of the North",b="The Horde launched devastating land and naval offensives, quickly overrunning Khaz Modan and driving the dwarves and gnomes behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. Advancing north across the Thandol Span and through the seas, orcish forces burned their way through Hillsbrad and the Hinterlands before laying siege to Capital City itself. The kingdom of Alterac betrayed the Alliance, guiding orc divisions through mountain passes. Despite devastating dragon attacks, the Alliance rallied around the newly created Knights of the Silver Hand and paladins who wielded the Holy Light against orcish warlocks and death knights.",sp=0},{t="The Turning Tide and the Dark Portal",b="The war turned when the warlock Gul'dan deserted the Horde with his followers to pursue personal power, forcing Doomhammer to divert critical forces from the front lines. The Alliance counterattacked, breaking the siege of Lordaeron and systematically driving the Horde back south across the Thandol Span, liberating Khaz Modan along the way. In the final mountain passes of the Burning Steppes, Anduin Lothar fell in single combat against Doomhammer, but his lieutenant Turalyon rallied the troops and routed the orcs. The Alliance pursued the survivors all the way to the Black Morass, shattering the remnants and demolishing the Dark Portal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How did the Second War begin?",a="After the Horde razed Stormwind during the First War, Orgrim Doomhammer recognized that the northern human realms would eventually retaliate. He decided to strike first before humanity could organize. At the same time, Stormwind refugees led by Anduin Lothar warned King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron, leading to the creation of the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"why did the second war start","how did it start","second war cause"},it="origin"},{q="Who won the Second War?",a="The Alliance of Lordaeron won a decisive victory. After repelling the Horde from Capital City and driving them south through Khaz Modan, Alliance forces defeated Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer at the foot of Blackrock Mountain and dismantled the Dark Portal in the Black Morass.",al={"who won","alliance or horde win","second war victor"},it="conflict"},{q="What happened to Khaz Modan during the fighting?",a="The Horde overran the surface lands of Khaz Modan to seize its rich mineral deposits and forges. The Bronzebeard dwarves and their gnomish allies blew up mountain passes and tunnels to slow the march, eventually retreating entirely behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. The Horde laid siege to Ironforge but could never breach its gates before the Alliance pushed south and liberated the realm.",al={"khaz modan second war","ironforge siege second war","what happened to the dwarves"},it="ruin"},{q="Why did the Horde lose when they reached Capital City?",a="At the height of the siege of Lordaeron's capital, the warlock Gul'dan abandoned the front line, taking the Stormreaver and Twilight's Hammer clans with him to hunt for ancient demonic power. Doomhammer was forced to split his army to punish the deserters, fatally weakening his siege lines just as the Alliance counterattack struck.",al={"why did the horde lose","gul'dan betrayal second war","why did doomhammer fail"},it="motive"},{q="Who was Anduin Lothar?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar was the Lion of Azeroth and the Regent of Stormwind after King Llane's assassination. He led the survivors north and was named Supreme Commander of the Alliance armies. Lothar was killed in single combat against Orgrim Doomhammer in the Burning Steppes near the end of the war, inspiring Turalyon to lead the Alliance to ultimate victory.",al={"who is lothar","anduin lothar death","supreme commander lothar"},it="leader"},{q="Where did the final battle take place?",a="The decisive army-level battle took place around Blackrock Mountain in the Burning Steppes, where Doomhammer was defeated. The surviving orcs fled to the Black Morass, where the Alliance fought a final engagement at the Dark Portal itself, demolishing the portal's stone framework and imprisoning or scattering the remaining Horde clans.",al={"last battle second war","dark portal battle","burning steppes second war"},it="place"},{q="What was the Second War?",a="The Second War was a massive, continent-spanning conflict fought across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the original Alliance of Lordaeron. Beginning shortly after the fall of Stormwind, it pitted humanity and their newfound allies—the dwarves of Khaz Modan, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas—against Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, bolstered by forest trolls, goblins, and enslaved red dragons.",al={"what is second war","second great war","who fought second war"},it="identity",add="1c320fa5"},{q="Who were the factions and allies in the Second War?",a="The war united races on both sides into vast coalitions. The Alliance of Lordaeron brought together the seven human kingdoms alongside Bronzebeard and Wildhammer dwarves, Gnomeregan's gnomes, and high elves. Opposing them was the Orcish Horde, joined by the Amani forest trolls, the goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel, and red dragons forced into servitude by the Dragonmaw clan using the Demon Soul.",al={"second war alliances","who fought with who second war","second war factions"},it="allies",add="b89daeed"},{q="Why is it called the Second War?",a="It is named the Second War because it immediately followed the First War, the initial conflict triggered when orcs first surged through the Dark Portal and razed the Kingdom of Stormwind. When the survivors fled north and Orgrim Doomhammer pushed across the continent to eradicate the remaining human kingdoms, the renewed clash became the second Great War between the two mortal factions.",al={"why called second war","second great war name","name second war"},it="name",add="5ecc4b82"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Second War was the defining crucible that forged the modern Alliance, uniting humans, dwarves, gnomes, and elves against an existential threat.",["faction:Horde"]="The conflict marked the collapse of the old blood-cursed Horde, leading directly to the internment camps and the eventual rebirth of the new Horde under Thrall.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were founded specifically during this conflict by Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Uther the Lightbringer to counter the fel magic and death knights of the Horde.",["race:Dwarf"]="Khaz Modan suffered widespread devastation during the war, enduring a protracted Horde siege behind the sealed mountain gates of Ironforge until Alliance armies lifted it."},kw={"alliance of lordaeron","blackrock mountain","dark portal","doomhammer","guldan","lothar","second war","silver hand","thandol span"},rel={"topic:first-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:anduin-lothar","subzone:dark-portal","topic:dragonmaw-clan","zone:ironforge","zone:burningsteppes"},src={"Second War"}}
E["topic:sentinels"] = {t="topic",n="Sentinels",z="general",s="Across Kalimdor, the Sentinels defend night elf lands and meet the Horde in battle. Formed after the War of the Ancients, this elite force is guided by the Sisterhood of Elune and led by General Shandris Feathermoon. Its mostly female ranks guard settlements and sacred ground from Ashenvale to Feralas, and watch over the night elves’ new home on Teldrassil.",h="Across Kalimdor, the Sentinels defend night elf lands and meet the Horde in battle.",sec={{t="Born from the Sundering",b="The Sentinels took shape after the War of the Ancients laid bare the failures of the old Kaldorei military. Before the Great Sundering, noble birth decided rank in the ancient Kaldorei Empire, while skill in battle counted for little. That order could not shield Azeroth from the war’s devastation.\n\nAfterward, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind remade night elf society. She looked to the Sisterhood of Elune, an order that had survived intact and knew tactics, combat, and healing. From its devoted ranks, Tyrande formed a standing army to guard the growing society, whatever a Sentinel’s heritage.",sp=0},{t="Structure and Warfare",b="Tyrande Whisperwind remains the Sentinels’ spiritual supreme commander. Her lieutenant, General Shandris Feathermoon, directs their active military from Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas. Sentinel discipline is known in the bow and the deadly moonglaive, and in their specialized forces.\n\nElite archers fight beside huntresses riding nightsabers. Scouts mounted on hippogryphs command the skies. Centuries before the Third War, the Sentinels fought under Archdruid Fandral Staghelm in the brutal War of the Shifting Sands in Silithus, where they halted the qiraji swarm. From Astranaar to Darnassus’s spreading branches, night elf settlements rely on Sentinel garrisons for protection.",sp=0},{t="Defending Sacred Soil",b="During the Third War, the Sentinels faced demons and early Horde incursions in desperate battles. They suffered immense losses when Cenarius fell in Ashenvale. They survived the Battle of Mount Hyjal and pursued Illidan across Lordaeron, then returned to a changed Kalimdor.\n\nNow danger presses from several fronts. On Teldrassil, garrisons face corrupted wildlife and enraged furbolgs. Farther south in Warsong Gulch, the Silverwing Sentinels fight the Warsong Outriders. They are determined to drive the Horde from Ashenvale and protect the ancient forests from further exploitation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads the Sentinels?",a="High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind guides the Sentinels politically and spiritually, and founded them from the Sisterhood of Elune. General Shandris Feathermoon commands the army day to day from Feathermoon Stronghold and directs its operations across Kalimdor.",al={"sentinel leader","who is in charge of sentinels","shandris or tyrande"},it="leader"},{q="Why are most Sentinels female?",a="Most Sentinels are female because night elf society traditionally divided spiritual and martial duties by gender. Women dominated Elune’s priesthood and formed the Sentinels, while men overwhelmingly joined the Cenarion Circle as druids. That division shaped the army for millennia, though society has slowly begun to shift since the Third War.",al={"why only female sentinels","women army night elf","can men be sentinels"},it="people"},{q="Where is the Sentinels' main base of operations?",a="The Sentinels’ primary fortress outside Darnassus is Feathermoon Stronghold, on an island off the coast of Feralas. They also keep prominent garrisons across Ashenvale, including Astranaar and Silverwing Outpost.",al={"sentinel base","sentinel headquarters","feathermoon stronghold"},it="place"},{q="What weapons do the Sentinels specialize in?",a="Sentinels are celebrated archers who use recurve bows, and the three-bladed moonglaive is their cultural hallmark. Huntresses wield glaives from nightsaber panthers, while riders strike from armored hippogryphs.",al={"sentinel weapons","moonglaive","what do sentinels use"}},{q="Why are the Sentinels fighting in Warsong Gulch?",a="The Silverwing Sentinels fight the Horde’s Warsong Outriders over Ashenvale. The orcs have cut heavily into the forest for lumber, violating woods the night elves hold sacred. The Sentinels are determined to drive out every invader.",al={"warsong gulch sentinels","silverwing sentinels","why ashenvale war"},it="conflict"},{q="Did the Sentinels fight in the Third War?",a="Yes. The Sentinels formed the main front-line defense when the Burning Legion and the Scourge marched on Kalimdor. Led by Tyrande, they held the line until they joined forces with human and orc refugees. The fighting ended in victory at the Battle of Mount Hyjal.",al={"sentinels third war","sentinels hyjal","sentinels wc3"}},{q="What are the Sentinels?",a="The Sentinels, or the Sentinel Army, are the standing military force and primary guardians of the night elf race. Deeply entwined with the Sisterhood of Elune, their ranks consist of devout, elite warriors—chiefly deadly archers, saber-mounted huntresses, and aerial hippogryph riders. They patrol ancient woodlands, guard night elven settlements, and defend kaldorei territories against all outside threats.",al={"who are sentinels","sentinel army","night elf sentinels"},it="identity",add="b427a0e4"},{q="Where are the Sentinels found across Kalimdor?",a="You will encounter Sentinels stationed in nearly every night elf settlement and wilderness outpost across northern and central Kalimdor. They maintain a heavy garrison guarding their new home in Teldrassil and their capital of Darnassus. Across Ashenvale, Darkshore, and Stonetalon, they actively repel invading Horde lumber expeditions, corrupted furbolgs, and demonic remnants, while maintaining their southern fortress of Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas.",al={"sentinel locations","where do sentinels patrol","why sentinels here"},it="why_here",add="3421f1ac"},{q="Why are the Sentinels hostile to the Horde?",a="If you march under the Horde banner, the Sentinels view you as an invading trespasser. Bitter hostility began during the Third War when the Warsong clan slew their revered demigod Cenarius. Today, the Horde continues to harvest timber from the sacred groves of Ashenvale, driving the Sentinels to violently defend their ancestral borders from all encroaching orcs and their allies.",al={"why sentinels attack me","sentinels kos horde","are sentinels hostile"},it="hostile",add="da0cb30d"},{q="Who are the Sentinels allied with?",a="The Sentinels serve Darnassus and answer directly to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and the Sisterhood of Elune. As the night elves joined the Grand Alliance, the Sentinels fight alongside humans, dwarves, and gnomes, deploying troops to assist allied campaigns and standing guard at joint outposts across the world.",al={"sentinel allies","who do sentinels fight for","are sentinels alliance"},it="allies",add="37da9748"},{q="How was the Sentinel Army formed?",a="The Sentinels were forged in the aftermath of the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. Following the collapse of the old empire—where military leadership had decayed into idle noble status—High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind reformed their martial forces. Drawing upon the battle-tested, disciplined ranks of the Sisterhood of Elune, she created a vigilant, standing army to ensure the night elves would never again fall unprepared to demonic invasion.",al={"sentinel origin","origin of sentinels","history of sentinels"},it="history",add="fe6bc7d9"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As a kaldorei, your hamlet relies on the Sentinels’ watch. Many young women aspire to join the army of Shandris and Tyrande.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you face lethal woodland skirmishers who strike from Ashenvale’s shadows and fight your logging efforts.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you see Sentinel archers and nightsaber-riding huntresses as masters of marksmanship and animal companionship on Kalimdor.",["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you share a bond between military duty and devotion to the Moon Goddess. The Sentinels were formed from the Sisterhood of Elune."},kw={"ashenvale","feathermoon","huntress","kaldorei","moonglaive","nightsaber","sentinels","shandris","silverwing","tyrande"},rel={"npc:shandris-feathermoon","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:night-elf","topic:elune","subzone:feathermoon-stronghold","zone:ashenvale","topic:war-of-the-ancients","npc:warsong-outrider"},src={"Sentinels"}}
E["topic:shadow-council"] = {t="topic",n="Shadow Council",z="general",s="The Shadow Council was the secret cabal of warlocks and necrolytes that orchestrated the corruption of the Old Horde on Draenor and guided its invasion of Azeroth. Founded by Gul'dan under the direction of the Burning Legion, the cult manipulated clan chieftains from behind the scenes until Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer purged their stronghold in Blackrock Mountain.",h="The Shadow Council was the secret cabal of warlocks and necrolytes that orchestrated the corruption of the...",sec={{t="Architects of the Blood Curse",b="Before the crossing into Azeroth, Gul'dan brought together a clandestine assembly of warlocks, necrolytes, and opportunistic chieftains to manipulate the orcish clans. By elevating Blackhand the Destroyer as a malleable figurehead, the Shadow Council ruled the Old Horde from the dark. They disseminated fel magic across Draenor and fed the blood of the demon Mannoroth to the clans, binding the orcs to the will of the Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="The Crossing and Blackrock Mountain",b="The Shadow Council entered a pact with Medivh—the Guardian possessed by Sargeras—to breach the barrier between worlds. Through the Dark Portal, the Council guided the Horde into the Kingdom of Stormwind. From their sanctum in Blackrock Spire, the warlocks oversaw the war effort.",sp=0},{t="Doomhammer's Purge and Death Knights",b="Following Medivh's death, Gul'dan fell into a comatose trance. Orgrim Doomhammer seized the moment: he slew Blackhand, interrogated Garona to expose the Council's inner sanctum, and launched an assault that obliterated most of the warlocks. Awaking to find his cult shattered, Gul'dan bargained for his life by offering Doomhammer a terrifying counter to human magic. Imbuing the spirits of slaughtered Shadow Council warlocks into the corpses of fallen human knights, Gul'dan created the first death knights.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who founded the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council was founded by the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Serving the demon lord Kil'jaeden, Gul'dan gathered like-minded practitioners of fel magic to pull the strings of the clans while using Warchief Blackhand as an unwitting puppet.",al={"who made shadow council","shadow council founder","guldan council"},it="origin"},{q="What happened to the Shadow Council during the First War?",a="When Gul'dan was incapacitated after Medivh's death, Orgrim Doomhammer killed Blackhand and took command of the Horde. Doomhammer tortured the assassin Garona to uncover the Council's hidden sanctuary in Blackrock Spire, leading a purge that wiped out nearly all of its warlocks.",al={"how did shadow council fall","doomhammer shadow council","purge of the shadow council"},it="history"},{q="How did the first death knights come from the Shadow Council?",a="After Doomhammer eradicated the Council, Gul'dan bargained for clemency by promising a dark weapon to match human sorcerers. He took the lingering souls of his slain warlocks—most notably Teron Gorefiend—and infused them into the preserved bodies of fallen Stormwind knights, creating the first generation of death knights.",al={"shadow council death knights","who became death knights","teron gorefiend council"}},{q="Did all orc warlocks belong to the Shadow Council?",a="Not necessarily. The Shadow Council was a secret inner circle of Gul'dan's most trusted warlocks, necrolytes, and chieftains who ruled the Horde from behind the scenes, while fel magic itself spread far more widely through the orc clans.",al={"were all warlocks shadow council","drekthar shadow council","nekros shadow council"}},{q="What was Cho'gall's role in the Council?",a="Cho'gall was Gul'dan's foremost apprentice and chief lieutenant. He led the Twilight's Hammer clan within the Council's orbit.",al={"chogall shadow council","chogall role","twilight hammer shadow council"}},{q="Does the Shadow Council still exist?",a="The original Council was shattered by Doomhammer, and its remnants further fractured when Gul'dan died at the Tomb of Sargeras. However, splintered cultists, surviving warlocks, and demonic agents continuing the Legion's work across Azeroth and the ruins of Draenor still trace their lineage to Gul'dan's organization.",al={"is shadow council around","shadow council today","shadow council present"},it="today"},{q="What is the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council is a clandestine cult of mortal warlocks dedicated to serving the demonic Burning Legion. Founded on Draenor by the orc warlock Gul'dan, the Council worked from the shadows to manipulate the Orcish Horde, corrupt its clans with fel magic, and coordinate the genocide of the draenei before steering the orcs through the Dark Portal into Azeroth.",al={"what is shadow council","who are the shadow council","shadow council meaning"},it="identity",add="5ec0e415"},{q="Who do the Shadow Council serve?",a="The Shadow Council serves the Burning Legion and its demon lords, notably Kil'jaeden. While mortal leaders like Gul'dan have directed its day-to-day machinations, the cult's ultimate purpose has always been carrying out the Legion's dark designs across both Draenor and Azeroth.",al={"who do shadow council serve","shadow council allies","who leads shadow council","shadow council demon masters"},it="allies",add="5b2501ce"},{q="Why are the Shadow Council hostile to travelers?",a="The Shadow Council operates entirely in secrecy to further the Burning Legion's schemes of destruction and demonic corruption. Anyone outside their demonic fold—whether Alliance or the new Horde—is an obstacle, an enemy, or fuel for their dark soul-draining rituals.",al={"why does shadow council attack","shadow council hostile why","why is shadow council evil"},it="hostile",add="26726978"}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="The Shadow Council formalized the study of fel sorcery and demonic pacts among mortals; modern warlocks draw directly upon the summoning rites and soul-harvesting techniques they pioneered.",["race:Orc"]="The Shadow Council engineered the ruin of Draenor and the blood pact that cursed the orcish race, leaving deep generational shame that Thrall's new Horde actively strives to purge.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer's brutal slaughter of the Shadow Council marked the turning point where the Horde first attempted to sever itself from demonic puppet masters.",["faction:Alliance"]="The kingdom of Stormwind fell largely to the covert operations of the Shadow Council, from the opening of the Dark Portal to the assassination of King Llane Wrynn."},kw={"blackhand","blackrock mountain","burning legion","cho'gall","dark portal","death knight","fel magic","gul'dan","mannoroth","shadow council","teron gorefiend"},rel={"npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:old-horde","subzone:blackrock-mountain","topic:burning-legion","subzone:dark-portal","topic:twilight-s-hammer","topic:medivh","topic:first-war"},src={"Shadow Council"}}
E["topic:shaman"] = {t="topic",n="Shaman",z="general",s="Shaman are spiritual mediators who commune with the primordial forces of earth, air, fire and water. Balancing raw nature through totems, lightning and ancestral healing, they are the spiritual core of orc, tauren and troll culture under Warchief Thrall. Dwarves walk the path too, as the Wildhammer long have, and the Skyborne Windshapers seek to restore their people's bond with the winds.",h="Shaman are spiritual mediators who commune with the primordial forces of earth, air, fire and water.",sec={{t="Mediators of the Elements",b="Unlike priests who revere the divine or mages who bend the arcane, shaman interact directly with the primal forces of nature. The elements of earth, air, fire, and water are inherently chaotic and locked in perpetual conflict. A shaman does not enslave these forces, but petitions and negotiates with them, serving as a balance keeper between primal fury and the mortal world. Through wooden and stone totems planted into the soil, shaman channel these elemental bargains to protect allies, hinder foes, or mend the wounded with cleansing water.",sp=0},{t="Loss and Rebirth Among Orcs",b="Shamanism was once the ancient foundation of orcish clan life on Draenor. However, Kil'jaeden and the Shadow Council deceived the shamans into war against the draenei, causing the elemental spirits to withdraw their favor. Stripped of their gifts, many former shaman succumbed to demonic corruption and became warlocks, nearly extinguishing the tradition entirely. Only after the Second War did Thrall, guided by Drek'Thar of the Frostwolf clan, rediscover the ancient rites. Thrall's ascension as Warchief revitalized shamanism, elevating it into the moral compass of the New Horde.",sp=0},{t="Traditions of the Horde",b="Across Kalimdor, shamanic traditions vary widely among the Horde's allied peoples. Tauren mystics treat communion with the earth and the spirits as a sacred cultural anchor. Darkspear trolls approach the discipline through reverence for the loa and ancestral wild spirits, weaving voodoo and nature rites together. Across all these tribal borders, shaman stand as essential community elders, advisers, and fearsome front-line defenders.",sp=0},{t="Beyond the Horde",b="The shaman's path does not belong to the Horde alone. Among the dwarves, the Wildhammer of Aerie Peak have long called on storm and stone, and Wildhammer earthseers such as Earthseer Farsen bring that far-seeing craft to Dun Morogh. Dwarves of Ironforge take up the totem as well and fight for the Alliance. Far above the world, the Windshapers are the spiritual leaders of the Skyborne. The wind spirits of Zephras Isle have fallen silent, and the Windshapers train new shaman in the hope of restoring that lost bond; Skyborne who join the Horde follow their path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Which races can become shaman?",a="Orcs, tauren and trolls of the Horde, whose traditions draw on elemental spirits, ancestors and, among trolls, the loa. Dwarves become shaman for the Alliance, and Skyborne who join the Horde train with the Windshapers.",al={"who can be shaman","shaman races","can alliance be shaman","can dwarves be shaman","dwarf shaman","alliance shaman","skyborne shaman"},it="people"},{q="How do shaman get their magic?",a="Shaman derive their power through communion with the elemental spirits of earth, air, fire, and water, as well as the spirits of their ancestors. Rather than commanding these energies by force, a shaman asks for their aid and balances their chaotic nature, often anchoring these connections through ritual totems.",al={"how does shaman magic work","shaman source of power","where do totems come from"},it="power"},{q="Why did the orcs lose their shaman powers before the First War?",a="The demon lord Kil'jaeden deceived the orc shamans into attacking the peaceful draenei. Offended by the senseless slaughter, the elemental spirits of Draenor severed ties and refused to answer calls. Left defenseless, many desperate shaman turned to fel magic and became warlocks.",al={"why did orc shaman become warlocks","draenor shaman fall","kiljaeden trick shaman"},it="history"},{q="Who brought shamanism back to the orcs?",a="Thrall, the son of Durotan, sought out the Frostwolf clan in the Alterac Mountains. Under the tutelage of the elder shaman Drek'Thar, Thrall learned the ancient spiritual bonds, breaking the demonic lethargy that plagued his people and reinstating shamanism as the heart of the New Horde.",al={"who revived shamanism","thrall and drek'thar shaman","shaman return"},it="history"},{q="What is the Earthen Ring?",a="The Earthen Ring is an order of shaman, with elders among the tauren of Thunder Bluff. Its practitioners dedicate themselves to maintaining harmony among the volatile elemental spirits and healing regions afflicted by spiritual unrest.",al={"earthen ring lore","who are earthen ring","shaman faction"},it="allies"},{q="How does a shaman differ from a druid?",a="Druids commune with the Emerald Dream, plant life, and animal spirits to safeguard the living cycle of nature. Shaman instead moderate the inanimate, primordial elements of the physical world—earth, fire, water, and wind—alongside the ancestral spirits of the dead.",al={"druid vs shaman","difference between druid and shaman","shaman or druid"},it="identity"},{q="What is a shaman in lore?",a="Shaman are spiritual guides and mortal mediators who commune directly with the natural elements: earth, air, fire, and water. Rather than serving as masters of the divine, they act as moderators between primal, chaotic elemental forces that are in unending conflict. In tribal societies like those of the orcs, tauren, and trolls, shaman serve as revered advisors, seers, and protectors. In battle, they call upon these elemental energies to unleash lightning, mend wounds with cleansing water, or empower allies through carved totems.",al={"what is a shaman","shaman lore","who are shamans"},it="identity",add="82a70d6c"},{q="What does it mean to become a shaman?",a="Walking the path of the shaman means learning to listen to the living world and earning the respect of its spirits. You do not command elemental forces through arcane mastery or sheer will; you commune with them, bringing balance to their volatile fury. As an initiate, you craft and carry totems that focus the elements of earth, fire, water, and air. Whether fighting on the front lines with weapon-imbued elemental force, healing allies through ancestral guidance, or calling down storms, your strength relies entirely upon keeping harmony with the primal spirits.",al={"becoming a shaman","why become a shaman","path of the shaman"},it="for_you",add="8fa4f120"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Your powers are granted through mutual respect with fickle elemental spirits and ancestors; maintaining balance is both your martial duty and your spiritual burden.",["faction:Horde"]="Shamanism represents the spiritual renaissance of the Horde, providing a path of honor and nature worship that replaced demonic corruption.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren have practiced shamanism for generations, serving as patient spiritual anchors for the younger cultures of the Horde.",["race:Orc"]="Reclaiming the path of the shaman allowed the orcs to overcome the lethargy of the internment camps and reclaim their ancestral heritage.",["race:Troll"]="Darkspear shaman channel the primal elements through their tribal traditions, bridging the reverence of nature spirits and ancient loa.",["faction:Alliance"]="To much of the Alliance, shaman are foes whose totems call lightning and tremors, yet dwarven shaman bring the same storm-born gifts to Ironforge's defense.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven shaman answer the stone and the storm, kin to the Wildhammer earthseers of Aerie Peak who have long heard the elements' voices.",["race:Skyborne"]="Skyborne shaman train with the Windshapers, who hope to bring the vanished wind spirits back to Zephras Isle."},kw={"air","ancestors","drek'thar","earth","earthen ring","elements","fire","primal","shaman","thrall","totem","water","wildhammer","windshapers","earthseer"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:tauren","topic:darkspear-tribe","topic:new-horde","topic:horde","topic:burning-legion","zone:durotar","topic:wildhammer-clan","topic:skyborne"},src={"Shaman"}}
E["topic:shan-do"] = {t="topic",n="Shan'do",z="general",s="Shan'do is a revered Darnassian honorific meaning \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" Traditionally used within night elven society and druidic circles, it denotes deep reverence toward a mentor who guides another along the path of wisdom and natural magic. Its direct counterpart is thero'shan, meaning \"honored student.\"",h="Shan'do is a revered Darnassian honorific meaning \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" Traditionally...",sec={{t="A Title of Deep Reverence",b="In the Darnassian language, shan'do translates directly to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" It is far more than an occupational label: addressing an elder or mentor as shan'do expresses solemn humility, filial devotion, and enduring respect for their guidance.",sp=0},{t="The Lineage of the Druids",b="The title is most famously intertwined with the history of the Cenarion Circle. The demigod Cenarius was the original shan'do to Malfurion Stormrage, instructing him in the ways of nature before the War of the Ancients. In turn, Malfurion became shan'do to generations of mortal druids, to the point where \"Shan'do Stormrage\" is his standard styling among night elves, forest keepers, and reverent allies across Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Mentors Beyond the Circle",b="While most common among Cenarion druids—such as disciples addressing Naralex or the slumbering Malfurion—the honorific is fundamentally Darnassian, not exclusively religious. The title represents the bond of transmission between master and pupil, irrespective of the school of thought.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the word shan'do actually mean?",a="In Darnassian, shan'do translates to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" It is used to show deep deference and gratitude toward a mentor.",al={"shando meaning","what does shando translate to","shando translation","darnassian shando"},it="name"},{q="What is the opposite or student version of shan'do?",a="The complementary honorific is thero'shan, which translates to \"honored student.\" Mentors address their apprentices as thero'shan to acknowledge their devotion and discipline.",al={"opposite of shando","student word in darnassian","theroshan meaning","what is thero'shan"}},{q="Who is most commonly called Shan'do?",a="Archdruid Malfurion Stormrage is the figure most commonly hailed as Shan'do by the night elves and the Cenarion Circle. Cenarius was also addressed as shan'do by his original pupils, and other prominent leaders such as Naralex carry the honorific from their followers.",al={"who is shando","shando stormrage","who has the title shando"},it="fame"},{q="Is the title shan'do only used by druids?",a="No. While it is deeply rooted in Cenarion druidic tradition, shan'do is a Darnassian linguistic courtesy. Any master of craft, magic, or wisdom can theoretically be addressed as shan'do by their student.",al={"can non druids be shando","is shando only for druids","shando outside druidism"}},{q="What is the meaning and role of a Shan'do?",a="Shan'do is an esteemed Darnassian courtesy title translating to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" It is bestowed upon revered mentors within night elven culture, most notably among druids who guide apprentices in the ways of nature. The title conveys deep reverence, placing the teacher in a position of sacred guidance and elder authority over their thero'shan, or honored student.",al={"what is shando","who is shando","shan'do meaning"},it="identity",add="9fa21e25"},{q="What is the history of the title Shan'do?",a="The honorific dates back millennia to the earliest teachings of nature and arcane magic. During the ancient era before the Great Sundering, the demigod Cenarius served as shan'do to Malfurion Stormrage, instructing him as the first mortal druid. Over the ages, it has endured as the quintessential Darnassian honorific for masters of profound spiritual and magical disciplines.",al={"history of shando","where did shando come from","origin of shando"},it="history",add="01c1f540"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As part of your native tongue, shan'do is among the most solemn courtesies you can offer an elder or teacher, carrying millennia of cultural weight.",["class:Druid"]="Every druid traces their teachings through a chain of mentors back to Shan'do Stormrage and Cenarius himself; uttering the title honors that unbroken chain.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren druids studying alongside the night elves in Moonglade recognize the title, using it respectfully when addressing mentors like Malfurion Stormrage."},kw={"cenarius","darnassian","druid","honored teacher","malfurion","shan'do","shando","thero'shan"},rel={"npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:cenarius","topic:druid","npc:naralex","topic:war-of-the-ancients","npc:remulos","quest:952","quest:963"},src={"Shan'do"}}
E["topic:skyborne"] = {t="topic",n="Skyborne",z="general",s="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, are an offshoot of high elves descended from rebels who fled Eldre'Thalas after opposing Prince Tortheldrin's demonic pacts. Striking a desperate bargain with wind spirits, their ancestors escaped Azeroth to dwell atop the floating Zephras Isle within Skywall. Today, with their elemental benefactors abruptly gone, their floating sanctuary faces internal fractures and physical collapse.",h="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, are an offshoot of high elves descended from rebels who fled Eldre'Thalas after...",sec={{t="Flight from Eldre'Thalas",b="Following the Sundering, the Shen'dralar of Eldre'Thalas languished without the Well of Eternity. Prince Tortheldrin devised a dark salvation: summon the demon Immol'thar and siphon its power. Horrified nobles and citizens resisted, fearing another Legion invasion. In response, Tortheldrin executed a bloody purge of dozens of houses. From the surviving shadows rose a resistance spearheaded by a masked figure known only as the Shal'nan ('the Veiled One'). When a desperate rebel raid on Tortheldrin's ritual sanctum descended into catastrophic chaotic magic, the Shal'nan rallied the survivors and fled west to the port of Eldre'narr, taking to the seas pursued by the Prince's fleet.",sp=0},{t="Sanctuary in the Sky",b="Cornered on the open ocean, the exiles forged a pact with primordial wind spirits. These beings lifted Zephras Isle straight into the skies, anchoring it safely within Skywall—the Elemental Plane of Air. Relieved of absolute rule, the Shal'nan stepped down during the consecration of the Shrine of the Four Winds, placing governance in the hands of an elected council. Over millennia in this alien realm, the night elf exiles evolved into fair, slender high elves. In recent generations, an elemental influence known as the Sky-Touched Blessing has emerged, causing a growing number of children to be born with pale blue skin.",sp=0},{t="A Shattering Calm",b="For centuries, life in Skywall flourished in isolation. The wind spirits strictly forbade mention of the Windlord, Al'Akir, brushing off questions with eerie unease. A few centuries ago, catastrophe struck: the elemental benefactors vanished without explanation, and the Shal'nan disappeared along with them. Deprived of their caretakers, the arcane pylons anchoring Zephras Isle began failing. Outlying landmasses broke away, refugees flooded what remained, and desperation fueled the rise of bandits and the Al'Aketh—a fanatical cult dedicated to Al'Akir. Today, society fractures between the High Order, who seek salvation in reclaiming ancestral arcane magic, and the Windshapers, who cling to elemental rites.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who are the Skyborne?",a="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei ('the hidden people'), are high elves descended from ancient night elves who rebelled against Prince Tortheldrin in Eldre'Thalas. To escape his vengeful fleet, they struck a pact with wind spirits, who relocated their island, Zephras Isle, into the Elemental Plane of Air.",al={"what are skyborne","who are shen'dorei","shendorei","skyborne lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why do some Skyborne have blue skin?",a="Millennia in the Elemental Plane of Air have subtly reshaped their biology. A recent phenomenon known as the Sky-Touched Blessing causes an increasing proportion of children—now roughly three out of ten—to be born with blue skin. Scholars predict their ancestral night elf traits will soon be entirely supplanted.",al={"sky-touched blessing","why are they blue","blue elves","sky touched"},it="origin"},{q="Who was the Shal'nan?",a="The Shal'nan, or 'the Veiled One', was the masked leader who spearheaded the resistance against Prince Tortheldrin's demon summoning in Eldre'Thalas. After leading the survivors to safety in Skywall, he abdicated to establish a democratic council, serving his people until mysteriously vanishing centuries ago alongside their elemental benefactors.",al={"who is the veiled one","shalnan","the masked rebel","leader of skyborne"},it="leader"},{q="What is happening to Zephras Isle?",a="Zephras Isle is held aloft within Skywall by magical pylons attuned to elemental power. When the wind spirits who maintained them abruptly disappeared centuries ago, the anchoring pylons began to fail. Portions of the landmass have broken off into the void, stranding survivors and driving displaced elves to banditry.",al={"why is zephras isle crumbling","zephras falling","what happened to skywall island"},it="ruin"},{q="What are the High Order and the Windshapers?",a="They are the two primary cultural factions of the Skyborne. The High Order descends from Highborne magisters and seeks to rebuild their crumbling society through disciplined arcane mastery. The Windshapers trace their roots to ordinary night elf citizens and revere the elemental and spiritual customs cultivated during their time in Skywall.",al={"skyborne factions","high order vs windshapers","windshaper lore"},it="people"},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a desperate cult of Skyborne who worship Al'Akir the Windlord. While their ancestors' elemental benefactors feared and avoided speaking of Al'Akir, the collapse of Zephras Isle led homeless and disenfranchised refugees to turn to the elemental lord for salvation and power.",al={"alaketh cult","cult of al'akir","skyborne cult"},it="threat"},{q="Where do the Skyborne stand in the world today?",a="Their civilization faces slow collapse. Centuries ago, the wind spirits and the Shal'nan vanished, destabilizing the ancient pylons holding Zephras Isle together. With parts of their realm crumbling into ruin, communication cut off from other settled islands, and refugees flooding the isle, surviving Skyborne have fractured. The High Order and Windshapers vie over whether arcane heritage or elemental traditions will save them, while desperate exiles turn to banditry or join the Al'Akir-worshipping Al'Aketh cult.",al={"skyborne current situation","state of the skyborne","where are the skyborne now"},it="today",add="10ab7a6f"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Skyborne share direct bloodlines with the ancient Shen'dralar and northern night elves, though millennia in Skywall have physically altered them into high elves with an increasing prevalence of elemental blue skin.",["class:Mage"]="The High Order among the Skyborne preserves ancient Highborne spellcraft brought out of Eldre'Thalas, seeking to salvage their crumbling island through rigorous arcane mastery.",["class:Shaman"]="The Windshapers represent an elven lineage deeply intertwined with the wind spirits of Skywall, relying on elemental communion rather than the arcane traditions of their Highborne forebears."},kw={"al'aketh","eldre'thalas","high order","shal'nan","shen'dorei","sky-touched","skyborne","skywall","windshapers","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","subzone:dire-maul","topic:highborne","topic:skywall","topic:elemental-plane","npc:ventaari-brightwish","quest:93552","zone:feralas"},src={"Skyborne"}}
E["topic:skywall"] = {t="topic",n="Skywall",z="general",s="Skywall is the vast, storm-swept domain of the air elementals within the Elemental Plane. Banished here by the titans in ancient days, the creatures of wind, cloud, and lightning dwell under the reign of Al'Akir the Windlord.",h="Skywall is the vast, storm-swept domain of the air elementals within the Elemental Plane.",sec={{t="A Prison in the Heavens",b="When the titans overthrew the Old Gods eons ago, they crafted the Elemental Plane as a secure dimensional prison to contain Azeroth's unruly elemental spirits. The lords of the elements fought fiercely amongst themselves, ultimately carving out distinct dominions. The realm of wind and storm became known as Skywall, ruled by Al'Akir the Windlord. While sealed away from the surface world, its tempests continue to swirl in eternal isolation.",sp=0},{t="The War of Elements",b="Banishment did not end the ancient animosity between the elemental rulers. Skywall has long waged border disputes against neighboring realms like the Firelands. One of the most infamous clashes was the Elemental Sundering, wherein Prince Thunderaan the Windseeker was ambushed and betrayed by Garr and Baron Geddon, the chief lieutenants of Ragnaros the Firelord. Ragnaros devoured the prince's essence and bound the fragments into a shattered talisman.",sp=0},{t="Isles of the Mist",b="Skywall is not composed solely of raging gales and djinn citadels. Among the cloudbanks drifts Zephras Isle, a sanctuary inhabited by the Shen'dorei. Mortals on Azeroth rarely pierce the veil into the domain of air, though reckless spellcasters and weapon-crafters across history have courted disaster attempting to siphon the living storms of the Windlord.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Skywall?",a="Skywall is the dominion of air elementals located within the dimensional Elemental Plane. Created by the titans to imprison the servants of the Old Gods, it is a realm formed entirely of endless skies, storms, clouds, and lightning.",al={"what is skywall","skywall lore","domain of air"},it="identity"},{q="Who rules Skywall?",a="Skywall is ruled by Al'Akir the Windlord, the supreme Elemental Lord of air. From within his tempestuous kingdom, he holds dominion over the djinn, cyclones, and living storms.",al={"who leads skywall","skywall boss","ruler of air"},it="leader"},{q="How was Skywall created?",a="After defeating the Old Gods in Azeroth's primordial past, the titan-forged fashioned the Elemental Plane to seal away the chaotic elementals. Within this dimensional prison, the air elementals seized their own vast territory, which became Skywall.",al={"origin of skywall","how was skywall made"},it="history"},{q="What happened to Prince Thunderaan?",a="During the Elemental Sundering, Prince Thunderaan was ambushed by Ragnaros the Firelord's lieutenants, Garr and Baron Geddon. Ragnaros consumed the air prince's essence and imprisoned what remained in a shattered talisman of elemental binding.",al={"thunderaan lore","what happened to thunderaan","windseeker fate"}},{q="Who lives in Skywall?",a="Skywall is populated by air elementals, powerful djinn lords, lightning revenants, and elemental storm drakes. It is also home to Zephras Isle, a sanctuary where the Shen'dorei dwell.",al={"skywall inhabitants","skywall denizens","who is in skywall"},it="residents"},{q="Can mortals reach Skywall?",a="Mortals cannot easily reach Skywall, as it exists in a separate dimensional prison. Historically, only powerful sorcerers manipulating elemental rifts or those seeking the isolated Shen'dorei on Zephras Isle have touched its skies.",al={"how to get to skywall","travel to skywall","skywall portal"},it="arrival"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Skywall is the primordial source of the raw, violent winds and lightning that shamans revere and beseech when communing with the spirits of air.",["class:Mage"]="Conjurors and arcanists have historically sought to tap the boundless atmospheric energies of Skywall, though such attempts to siphon Al'Akir's tempests often end in ruin."},kw={"air elemental","al'akir","elemental plane","shen'dorei","skywall","stormwall","thunderaan","windlord","zephras isle"},rel={"topic:elemental-plane","npc:thunderaan","zone:zephras","npc:highlord-demitrian","topic:titan","topic:old-god","subzone:uldum"},src={"Skywall"}}
E["topic:steamwheedle-cartel"] = {t="topic",n="Steamwheedle Cartel",z="general",s="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the largest and most influential goblin enterprise operating across modern Azeroth. Fiercely neutral, the cartel governs bustling commercial hubs like Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan, serving Alliance and Horde patrons alike so long as their coin is genuine. Their sprawling operations encompass cross-continental shipping, alchemy, salvaging, and cutthroat mercenary contracting.",h="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the largest and most influential goblin enterprise operating across modern Azeroth.",sec={{t="A Profitable Neutrality",b="Originating from the subterranean goblin capital of Undermine, the Steamwheedle Cartel initially allied with the Old Horde during the Second War, supplying war machines, sappers, and naval engineering. After the dust settled, the cartel's leaders realized that picking sides cost more than it earned. Embracing absolute neutrality, they expanded across both continents, opening trade networks that welcome Alliance and Horde alike. Today, as open tensions simmer, Steamwheedle ports provide vital common ground where anyone with gold can purchase passage, bank resources, and hire local specialists.",sp=0},{t="Ports and Outposts",b="The cartel's influence rests on strategic ports and frontier towns. In Stranglethorn Vale, Baron Revilgaz presides over Booty Bay, a fortified harbor town carved into the cliffs alongside the Blackwater Raiders. Across the Great Sea, Chief Engineer Gazlowe directs Ratchet on the coast of the Barrens, running a deep-water ferry line directly back to Booty Bay. Deep within the deserts of Tanaris lies the boomtown of Gadgetzan, while smaller operations like Steamwheedle Port and remote outposts in Desolace, Azshara, and Stonetalon Mountains secure regional resources.",sp=0},{t="Rivals and Reavers",b="Steamwheedle trade is constantly imperiled by opportunistic cutthroats. In the Southern Seas, the Bloodsail Buccaneers continually menace Booty Bay's naval lanes, while Southsea Freebooters raid the coastlines near Ratchet. On land, the cartel frequently clashes with their corporate arch-rivals, the Venture Company, competing violently for timber rights, oil fields, and shredder technology across Stonetalon and the Barrens. To maintain order and enforce trade contracts, cartel barons routinely hire passing mercenaries to settle grievances with extreme prejudice.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel is a massive, neutral goblin trading conglomerate based out of Undermine. They operate several major independent towns across Azeroth—including Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan—offering banking, engineering, transport, and auction houses to all factions.",al={"who are steamwheedle","steamwheedle goblins","what is steamwheedle"},it="identity"},{q="Are the Steamwheedle goblins part of the Horde or Alliance?",a="Neither. The cartel maintains strict political neutrality. While they previously worked with the Old Horde during the Second War, they severed formal faction allegiances to maximize profit. Today, they happily trade with both the Alliance and the Horde, enforcing peace in their towns with armed bruisers.",al={"is steamwheedle horde","is steamwheedle cartel neutral","steamwheedle faction"},it="side"},{q="Which major cities belong to the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="Their four principal towns are Booty Bay in southern Stranglethorn Vale, Ratchet on the eastern shore of the Barrens, Gadgetzan in Tanaris, and Everlook in Winterspring. They also maintain smaller hubs like Steamwheedle Port and isolated trading outposts.",al={"steamwheedle towns","cartel cities","goblin towns list"},it="place"},{q="Who leads the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="The cartel is run from Undermine, but its towns answer to local bosses such as Baron Revilgaz in Booty Bay and Chief Engineer Gazlowe in Ratchet.",al={"steamwheedle leader","trade prince steamwheedle","who runs steamwheedle"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Steamwheedle goblins enemies with the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a rival goblin enterprise known for cutthroat strip-mining and deforestation. The two factions constantly compete for the same territorial concessions, industrial blueprints, and natural resources across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"venture co vs steamwheedle","why hate venture co","venture company rivalry"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens if you fight inside a Steamwheedle port?",a="Steamwheedle towns employ armed goblin bruisers to keep the peace. Attacking other travelers can bring the guards down on you, while killing cartel guards or citizens damages your standing with the cartel.",al={"guards in booty bay","pvp in ratchet","ratchet bruisers"},it="danger",gp=true},{q="Where did the Steamwheedle Cartel come from?",a="Originating from the subterranean metropolis of Undermine on Kezan, the Steamwheedle Cartel is the oldest and largest merchant syndicate in goblin society. They seized control of Blackwater Cove in Stranglethorn Vale, transforming it into the bustling haven of Booty Bay. During the Second War, the cartel sold its services to Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, providing zeppelins, alchemists, and engineers for profit. When that conflict ended, the cartel recognized that strict neutrality across Azeroth offered vastly superior trade opportunities, prompting them to open neutral ports and trading outposts spanning both continents.",al={"steamwheedle history","origin of steamwheedle cartel","steamwheedle background"},it="history",add="ae8eebbb"},{q="Why are the Steamwheedle Cartel found in so many distant lands?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel pursues profit wherever trade routes, rare resources, or regional turmoil emerge. Establishing settlements like Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale, Ratchet along the Barrens coast, and Gadgetzan in Tanaris grants them dominion over maritime passage and frontier commerce between the continents. Their independent outposts and ventures allow them to trade openly with both the Alliance and the Horde while harvesting minerals, brokering salvage, and bankrolling lucrative contracts regardless of local peril.",al={"why is steamwheedle everywhere","why are goblins in ratchet and booty bay","steamwheedle locations why"},it="why_here",add="02088509"}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Cartel hubs like Ratchet and Gadgetzan are premier gathering spots for Goblin Engineering schematics, shredder parts, and experimental mechanical components.",["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance travelers, Steamwheedle ferry routes and desert outposts provide indispensable waystations and flight paths across remote, Horde-heavy wilderness.",["faction:Horde"]="While Gazlowe and his crew maintain historic working relationships with the orcs of Durotar, the cartel still demands cold hard coin for their services and neutrality.",["class:Rogue"]="The lawless fringes of Booty Bay and Ratchet offer bountiful employment for thieves, lockpicks, and covert contractors willing to fleece pirates and rivals alike."},kw={"booty bay","bruisers","cartel","gadgetzan","gazlowe","goblins","ratchet","revilgaz","steamwheedle","undermine"},rel={"subzone:ratchet","subzone:booty-bay","subzone:gadgetzan","subzone:steamwheedle-port","quest:1040","quest:887","quest:764","zone:barrens"},src={"Steamwheedle Cartel"}}
E["topic:stonefield-family"] = {t="topic",n="Stonefield family",z="general",s="The Stonefield family is a lineage of human farmers residing at the Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn Forest. Known for their long-standing rural roots, they tend their homestead while caught up in an ongoing rivalry with their neighboring peers, the Maclures. Despite the hostility between the two households, younger generations find themselves entangled across the family divide.",h="The Stonefield family is a lineage of human farmers residing at the Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn Forest.",sec={{t="Life on the Homestead",b="The Stonefields are quintessential Elwynn agrarian stock, working the soil south of the road leading to Westfall. The homestead revolves around Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, and young Tommy Joe Stonefield. Daily life on the farm involves tending crops and managing livestock while fending off local pests and dealing with nearby kobold incursions that plague the region's mines and borders.",sp=0},{t="The Maclure Feud",b="Separated only by a thin strip of woodland and pasture, the Stonefields and their neighbors, the Maclures, maintain a bitter and protracted grudge. Little love is lost between the older generations of both houses, with petty squabbles and territorial animosity coloring every interaction. Despite this fierce neighborhood dispute, a secret romance has blossomed between young Tommy Joe Stonefield and Maybell Maclure, forcing sympathetic travelers to act as discreet go-betweens.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where does the Stonefield family live?",a="The Stonefields reside at the Stonefield Farm, located in southwestern Elwynn Forest, just south of the main road heading toward the Westfall border.",al={"stonefield farm location","where are the stonefields","stonefield family location"},it="place"},{q="Who are the known members of the Stonefield family?",a="Prominent household members include matriarch Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, and the young Tommy Joe Stonefield.",al={"who is in the stonefield family","stonefield members","stonefield family tree"},it="people"},{q="Why do the Stonefields hate the Maclures?",a="The Stonefields and the Maclures are entangled in a classic rural family feud over old territorial grudges and neighborhood stubbornness, with both households stubbornly refusing to reconcile their grievances.",al={"stonefield maclure feud","why are stonefields fighting maclures","maclure rivalry"},it="conflict"},{q="How does the feud affect Tommy Joe Stonefield?",a="Tommy Joe Stonefield is secretly in love with Maybell Maclure from the rival farmstead. Because of the open hostility between their kin, the pair must rely on discreet intermediaries to exchange letters and tokens of affection.",al={"tommy joe maclure","tommy joe romance","who is tommy joe in love with"},it="ties"},{q="Is the family aligned with Stormwind?",a="Yes. As human residents of Elwynn Forest, they fall under the protection of the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Alliance, although they primarily concern themselves with the daily survival of their farm and regional threats.",al={"are stonefields alliance","stonefield loyalty"},it="side"},{q="What threats does the Stonefield family face?",a="Aside from their friction with the Maclure household, the Stonefields must deal with encroaching kobolds vermin, roving wildlife, and the general lack of Stormwind guards patrolling rural farmlands.",al={"stonefield dangers","problems at stonefield farm"},it="threat"},{q="Who are the Stonefields?",a="The Stonefields are an established human farming family residing in Elwynn Forest. Operating out of the Stonefield Farm southwest of Goldshire, they work the land while contending with surrounding wilderness threats and an ongoing, bitter feud with their neighbors, the Maclures.",al={"stonefield family lore","who are stonefield","stonefields"},it="identity",add="e2a78cbe"}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, the Stonefields reflect the resilient working-class backbone providing grain and produce to feed the kingdom.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers passing toward Westfall often handle the mundane chores and personal squabbles of the Stonefields to keep Elwynn's breadbasket stable."},kw={"elwynn forest","gramma stonefield","maclure","mildred stonefield","stonefield","stonefield family","stonefield farm","stonefields","tommy joe"},rel={"subzone:stonefield-farm","npc:tommy-joe-stonefield","npc:gramma-stonefield","npc:ma-stonefield","npc:maybell-maclure","zone:elwynn","topic:human","topic:alliance"},src={"Stonefield family"}}
E["topic:stonemasons-guild"] = {t="topic",n="Stonemasons Guild",z="general",s="The Stonemasons Guild was the collective of master builders and artisans responsible for reconstructing Stormwind City after the Second War. Following a bitter dispute over payment with the House of Nobles that ended in deadly riots, the guild was forcibly dissolved, driving its disenfranchised members into exile while leaving the true fate of its scattered artisans an enduring mystery.",h="The Stonemasons Guild was the collective of master builders and artisans responsible for reconstructing...",sec={{t="Architects of the Realm",b="Led by elected Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef, the artisans of the Stonemasons Guild demonstrated a mastery of masonry that rivaled the Royal Stonecutters Union of the dwarves. Following the devastation of the First and Second Wars, the House of Nobles hired the guild to embark upon the monumental task of rebuilding Stormwind City from ruins, trusting them to restore the capital to its ancient grandeur.",sp=0},{t="Toil and Betrayal",b="For years, VanCleef and an army of craftsmen toiled through Elwynn, raising the soaring spires of Stormwind Keep and the massive arches of the Cathedral of Light. However, royal finances collapsed under heavy military debts incurred in southern campaigns. Influenced by aristocratic politics, the House of Nobles refused to pay the guild their agreed salaries and fees. When VanCleef petitioned for restitution, the nobles answered by unilaterally disbanding the guild, sparking immense fury among the laborers who had bled to rebuild the city.",sp=0},{t="Blood in the Streets",b="The wage dispute erupted into violent unrest throughout Stormwind. The Crown cracked down ruthlessly, capturing key conspirators like VanCleef's lieutenant, Bazil Thredd, while VanCleef and hundreds of ruined stonemasons fled into the countryside of Westfall. Exiled and branded as traitors, the desperate artisans transformed their trade union into the militant Defias Brotherhood.",sp=1,bt={"defias.masons"}},{t="The Hidden Hand",b="The standoff between the nobility and the builders was not merely a matter of bankrupt coffers. Behind the scenes, the royal court advisor Lady Katrana Prestor actively fanned the embers of discord. She convinced the aristocracy that the Stonemasons' bills were extortionate and their work shoddy, while simultaneously provoking the craftsmen into outright rebellion. By paralyzing Stormwind with internal strife and cutting off supplies to Westfall, the black dragon Onyxia successfully fractured the kingdom from within.",sp=2}},faq={{q="What was the Stonemasons Guild?",a="The Stonemasons Guild was an association of human artisans, architects, and stonecutters led by Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef. They were renowned for rebuilding Stormwind City after the Second War.",al={"who were the stonemasons","stonemasons guild lore","what did the stonemasons do"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the Stonemasons Guild become the Defias Brotherhood?",a="When the rebuilding of Stormwind was complete, the House of Nobles refused to pay the guild's wages due to crippling debt and court intrigue. The resulting protests turned into deadly riots, and the exiled workers organized the Defias Brotherhood to claim their stolen wealth by force.",al={"why defias mad","why did stonemasons turn evil","origin of defias brotherhood"},sp=1,it="conflict",bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="Who was the leader of the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Edwin VanCleef served as the elected Guildmaster. Under his direction, the guild earned fame for its incredible stonework before he was exiled and founded the Defias Brotherhood.",al={"stonemasons guildmaster","vancleef guild","who led the stonemasons"},sp=1,it="leader",bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="What notable structures did the Stonemasons build?",a="The guild entirely rebuilt Stormwind City after the First War, including the Cathedral of Light and Stormwind Keep.",al={"what did stonemasons build","did stonemasons build stormwind"},it="fame"},{q="Did all the Stonemasons join the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Not all joined the rebellion. Notably, master craftsman Baros Alexston broke ties with Edwin VanCleef and stayed in Stormwind to accept the post of City Architect, earning the enduring hatred of his former guildmates.",al={"did all stonemasons become bandits","baros alexston stonemason","did any stonemasons stay in stormwind"},sp=1,it="people",bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="What happened during the Stonemason riots?",a="When the nobles disbanded the guild instead of paying it, the stonemasons rioted in the streets of Stormwind. The Crown cracked down, capturing VanCleef's lieutenant Bazil Thredd, while VanCleef and the other masons fled into Westfall.",al={"stonemason riots","what happened in the riots","stonemasons riot stormwind"}},{q="Who was Bazil Thredd in the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Bazil Thredd was Edwin VanCleef's trusted assistant and chief lieutenant during their time in the guild. Unlike VanCleef, Thredd was apprehended during the aftermath of the riots and remains locked inside the Stormwind Stockade.",al={"bazil thredd backstory","thredd stonemason","vancleef lieutenant"},it="people"},{q="What is the history of the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild was commissioned by the House of Nobles to rebuild Stormwind City from the ruins left by the Orcish Horde. Composed of skilled engineers and artisans, they spent years restoring the kingdom to glory, shaping landmarks like the Cathedral of Light and Stormwind Keep. However, rising royal debts and political maneuvering led the nobility to refuse payment, culminating in riots and the guild's exile into Westfall.",al={"stonemasons history","stonemasons background","origin of stonemasons"},it="history",add="1a605800"},{q="Why are the Stonemasons called that?",a="They were named for their craft as professional stonecarvers, builders, and architects. Functioning as a chartered trade union akin to Ironforge's Royal Stonecutters Union, the guild brought together masons and artisans whose masterwork masonry was capable of constructing massive stone fortresses, cathedrals, and city ramparts.",al={"why called stonemasons","stonemasons guild name meaning"},it="name",add="f8ddb796"}},ang={["race:Human"]="The beautiful white stone of Stormwind stands as a bitter testament to the kingdom's greatest internal tragedy, reminding human travelers of the cost of aristocratic hubris.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven masons deeply respected the Stonemasons Guild, noting that their precision and towering ramparts rivaled Ironforge's own Royal Stonecutters Union.",["class:Rogue"]="The transformation of master tradesmen into deadly highwaymen, lockpicks, and assassins proves just how far VanCleef's tactical cunning reached.",["faction:Alliance"]="The collapse of the guild left the breadbasket of Westfall defenseless and overrun by outlaws, straining the Alliance's supply lines right outside the capital."},kw={"alexston","defias","nethergarde","nobles","stonemasons","stormwind","thredd","vancleef"},rel={"topic:defias-brotherhood","npc:edwin-vancleef","topic:house-of-nobles","zone:stormwind","npc:baros-alexston","subzone:nethergarde-keep","npc:onyxia","quest:389"},src={"Stonemasons Guild"}}
E["topic:stormwind-army"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind Army",z="general",s="The Stormwind Army is the standing military force charged with defending the Kingdom of Stormwind and preserving the security of the Grand Alliance in the south. Though legendary for its resilient knights and marshals, the army is currently crippled by royal absence, political infighting, and crippling troop shortages across the provinces.",h="The Stormwind Army is the standing military force charged with defending the Kingdom of Stormwind and...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Iron and Blood",b="The army traces its martial lineage through centuries of brutal conflict, from early frontier clashes against gnolls to the desperate defense against the Gurubashi trolls. In the First War, under King Llane Wrynn I and Sir Anduin Lothar, the Knights of Stormwind mounted legendary ambushes against the Orcish Horde, yet fell when the capital burned. Surviving remnants evacuated to Lordaeron, spearheading the formation of the Alliance of Lordaeron and fighting through the Second War. After the Dark Portal's fall, Stormwind's military was rebuilt alongside the restored kingdom.",sp=0},{t="Structure and Ranks",b="Headquartered within Stormwind Keep, the military relies on dedicated regional garrisons overseen by the Stormwind Marshals. Within the stone walls of the capital, the Stormwind City Guard maintains civil order, while the elite Royal Guard attends the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Beyond the gates, the army deploys outposts to patrol Elwynn Forest, keep order along trade roads, and man defensive ramparts as far as the Redridge Mountains, assisted on the water by the Stormwind Fleet.",sp=0},{t="A Thin Blue Line",b="Despite its proud traditions, the modern army is stretched beyond its breaking point. Debts incurred by the House of Nobles to expand military reach into Stranglethorn fueled the bitter rift with the Stonemasons Guild, culminating in civil riots and the birth of the Defias Brotherhood. With King Varian missing, the House of Nobles has routinely withheld troops, funding, and supplies from outlying settlements like Westfall, Lakeshire, and Duskwood, forcing local marshals to deputize wandering adventurers to carry the realm's burdens.",sp=1,bt={"q123.3"}}},faq={{q="Why aren't more troops sent to Lakeshire and Sentinel Hill?",a="Stormwind's forces are stretched thin, and requests for provincial reinforcements have gone unmet. Westfall relies on the People's Militia, while Lakeshire still has royal soldiers who survived the loss of Stonewatch Keep. With too few defenders and dwindling supplies, its officers turn to traveling adventurers for help.",al={"where is the stormwind army","why no soldiers in westfall","why won't stormwind help"},it="motive"},{q="What is the difference between the Stormwind Army and the Stormwind Guard?",a="The Stormwind Army encompasses the broader national military, including frontier marshals, garrisons, and expeditionary knights across the kingdom. The Stormwind Guard is the specific branch dedicated to enforcing law, patrolling the streets, and defending the gates within Stormwind City and surrounding rural hubs.",al={"guard vs army","who are stormwind guards","stormwind military branches"}},{q="Who commands the Stormwind Army right now?",a="In the absence of the missing King Varian Wrynn, military authority officially rests with the boy-king Anduin Wrynn, guided directly by Regent Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. However, their strategic decisions are constantly contested and undercut by political machinations in the House of Nobles.",al={"who leads stormwind army","stormwind army commander","supreme commander stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="Why do marshals give out deputy badges to adventurers?",a="Because the Stormwind Army is critically short-staffed and denied reinforcements from the capital, regional marshals like McBride in Northshire and Dughan in Goldshire grant acting deputy status to competent outsiders so they can legally eliminate outlaws and defend borders.",al={"deputy status","why deputize players","deputy stormwind"},it="purpose"},{q="What happened to the army during the First War?",a="Led by Sir Anduin Lothar, the army held the Orcish Horde at bay with disciplined cavalry strikes, but was overwhelmed when King Llane was assassinated. Lothar gathered the surviving soldiers and civilians, fleeing across the Great Sea to Lordaeron, where those veterans formed the core of the original Alliance forces.",al={"first war stormwind army","did stormwind army lose","lothar stormwind army"},it="history"},{q="Where does the army currently maintain active garrisons?",a="The army retains formal garrisons throughout Elwynn Forest, including Northshire, Goldshire, and Westbrook Garrison. Westfall and Duskwood rely on local militias, while soldiers remain in Lakeshire after losing Stonewatch Keep and await reinforcements.",al={"stormwind garrisons","where is army stationed","stormwind troop locations"},it="place"},{q="What is the Stormwind Army?",a="The Stormwind Army is the primary military force of the human Kingdom of Stormwind and a core pillar of the Grand Alliance. Based in Stormwind City, the army includes foot soldiers, heavily armored knights, and officers known as marshals. In addition to defending the realm's core territories across southern Azeroth, its commanders oversee provincial garrisons and commission local militias and adventuring deputies to maintain order.",al={"what is stormwind army","who are stormwind army","stormwind military"},it="identity",add="bbf0b2a6"},{q="Who is the Stormwind Army allied with?",a="The army serves the Crown of Stormwind, upholding the authority of the House of Wrynn and the ruling council. Beyond the kingdom's borders, it is formally bound to the Grand Alliance, standing in common defense alongside Ironforge, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the night elves of Kalimdor. Domestically, it collaborates with the Stormwind Fleet and the capital's City Guard.",al={"stormwind army allies","who does the army serve","who do they work for"},it="allies",add="b7687992"}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="For human warriors, the Knights of Stormwind and the infantry of Lothar represent the gold standard of disciplined martial service, though many struggle with the nobility's abandonment of the frontier.",["class:Paladin"]="The army's heavy cavalry and leadership work closely with the Order of the Silver Hand and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon to bring the Light's justice to lawless lands.",["faction:Alliance"]="With Lordaeron shattered by the Scourge, Stormwind's army serves as the vital defensive backbone of the Grand Alliance in the Eastern Kingdoms.",["race:Human"]="To the citizenry of Stormwind, the royal army is both an enduring symbol of survival against impossible odds and a frustrating reminder of bureaucratic neglect in distant hamlets."},kw={"anduin lothar","bolvar fordragon","defias","deputy","garrison","knights of stormwind","marshals","stormwind army","stormwind guard"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:stormwind-guard","topic:house-of-nobles","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:varian-wrynn","zone:elwynn","npc:marshal-mcbride","quest:18"},src={"Stormwind Army"}}
E["topic:stormwind-guard"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind Guard",z="general",s="The Stormwind Guard comprises the regional garrisons and sentries tasked with enforcing the kingdom's laws and defending its surrounding territories. Stretched perilously thin by political gridlock and distant military campaigns, its scattered marshals struggle to hold back gnolls, orcs, and bandits across Elwynn and Redridge.",h="The Stormwind Guard comprises the regional garrisons and sentries tasked with enforcing the kingdom's laws...",sec={{t="Shield of the Crown",b="Operating as a vital branch of the Stormwind Army, the Stormwind Guard patrols the highways, crossroads, and stone watchtowers that bind the kingdom together. In outlying settlements, governance and defense fall to appointed marshals—such as Marshal McBride in Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris in Lakeshire. These officers muster recruits and maintain order, relying on trusty brown horses known for stamina to cover ground between fortified outposts like Westbrook Garrison.",sp=0},{t="A Border Stretched Thin",b="Following the Third War, the Guard successfully kept Elwynn Forest safe and quiet, but recent years have brought crisis. With King Varian Wrynn vanished and the kingdom's standing army tied down abroad, regional garrisons have received little aid. At Westbrook Garrison, Deputy Rainer can barely hold the western border against Riverpaw gnolls and Defias thieves, forcing local commanders to outsource crucial security tasks and bounties to visiting adventurers.",sp=0},{t="The Abandoned Territories",b="The Guard's true crisis stems from decisions made within the capital's royal court. Under the influence of Lady Katrana Prestor, the kingdom deemed Westfall too costly to garrison, pulling its troops out entirely and allowing Edwin VanCleef's Defias Brotherhood to seize the region. Duskwood suffered the same fate; its soldiers were withdrawn to Darkshire's despair, forcing the abandoned locals to establish the ragtag Night Watch for self-defense.",sp=1},{t="The Siege in the East",b="While Duskwood and Westfall were abandoned, the crown kept a tenuous military presence in the Redridge Mountains. Even so, the guard's position is desperate. Overwhelmed by Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs descending from the Burning Steppes, soldiers were forced to cede Stonewatch Keep. In Lakeshire, blacksmith Verner Osgood burns through iron merely keeping guards outfitted, while exhausted sentries scan the road waiting for a 2nd Battalion that never arrives.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Stormwind Guard and the City Guard?",a="While the Stormwind City Guard polices the capital inside its fortified walls, the Stormwind Guard—often organized as regional garrisons—is responsible for defending the crown's outer territories, patrolling the roads, and holding watchtowers across Elwynn Forest and Redridge.",al={"stormwind guard vs city guard","are they the same guards","city guard"}},{q="Why aren't there any Stormwind Guards in Westfall?",a="Influenced by Lady Katrana Prestor's counsel regarding state expenses, the royal court decided the upkeep was too burdensome and withdrew all official garrisons. This complete absence of military protection allowed Edwin VanCleef's Defias Brotherhood to rapidly overrun the territory's farms and roads.",al={"why did guards leave westfall","westfall army","where are westfall guards"}},{q="Why did the guard leave Duskwood?",a="Much like Westfall, Duskwood saw its royal garrisons recalled back toward the capital as resources dried up. Stripped of official crown protection amid rising dark magic and horrors, the remaining townsfolk of Darkshire had to form their own militia, the Night Watch, to survive.",al={"duskwood guards","why did stormwind abandon darkshire","darkshire garrison"}},{q="Who leads the local garrisons?",a="Local defense is governed by appointed regional marshals. Marshal McBride oversees Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan commands the forces centered in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris directs the desperate defense of Lakeshire in the Redridge Mountains.",al={"who is in charge","guard leaders","marshals"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the guards in Lakeshire in such bad shape?",a="The Redridge garrison was routed from Stonewatch Keep by invading Blackrock orcs and local gnolls. Trapped in Lakeshire, they suffer from acute equipment shortages—blacksmith Verner Osgood consumes nearly all available iron just rearming sentries—and are waiting for promised reinforcements that royal leaders have delayed.",al={"redridge guards losing","what happened at stonewatch","lakeshire garrison trouble"}},{q="Who is Marshal Haggard?",a="Marshal Haggard is a venerable retired commander who once led the Stormwind Guard during calmer days, when off-duty soldiers could still travel south to drink together at the Scarlet Raven Tavern in Darkshire.",al={"marshal haggard lore","retired marshal","old guard marshal"}},{q="What is the Stormwind Guard?",a="The Stormwind Guard, often known as the Stormwind Garrisons, is the territorial military branch of the Stormwind Army. Unlike the Stormwind City Guard who protect the capital's streets, these guards patrol the kingdom's outer territories—manning watchtowers, keeping roads secure, and enforcing royal law across Elwynn Forest and the Redridge Mountains.",al={"who are stormwind guards","stormwind garrison","what are stormwind guards"},it="identity",add="8131ff5c"},{q="Why are the Stormwind Guard stationed where they are?",a="They are stationed at outposts and scout towers every few miles along main roads to protect settlements and travelers from threats like gnolls, kobolds, and bandits. While they once maintained order across Westfall and Duskwood, court politics and resource shortages forced them to pull back, leaving active garrisons mainly in Elwynn Forest and Redridge.",al={"where are stormwind guards","guard locations","why guard towers"},it="why_here",add="fe3d1e2d"},{q="Who do the Stormwind Guard fight for?",a="They serve the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Alliance under the authority of the crown and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Locally, they answer to appointed marshals and cooperate closely with town magistrates, local militia, and Alliance adventurers willing to reinforce their hard-pressed outposts.",al={"stormwind guard allegiances","who do guards serve","stormwind army faction"},it="allies",add="b4df286f"},{q="What is the history of the Stormwind Guard?",a="Following the Third War, the Guard maintained widespread stability, keeping watchtowers manned to protect Elwynn, Duskwood, and Westfall. However, recent political intrigue and royal court maneuvering led by Lady Katrana Prestor caused funding and troops to be withdrawn from outlying regions. With much of the army abroad or held back, the remaining garrisons now struggle to hold basic border posts like Westbrook and Lakeshire.",al={"stormwind guard history","origins of the guard","history of stormwind garrison"},sp=1,it="history",add="7b82470a",bt={"prestor.onyxia"}},{q="Why are they called the Stormwind Guard?",a="They bear the name of the Kingdom of Stormwind, representing royal authority and military law beyond the capital's walls. They are also known as the Stormwind Garrisons because their ranks are split into regional outposts stationed in fortified towers and towns across the realm.",al={"why stormwind guard","stormwind guard name meaning","why the garrison name"},it="name",add="8669b7db"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Stormwind Guard represents the kingdom's front line, but its abandonment of sister settlements exposes the deep political cracks threatening the heart of the Alliance.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the southern farmlands, the guard's blue tabards once signified absolute safety, making their sudden absence in Westfall and Duskwood feel like a bitter betrayal.",["class:Warrior"]="From Northshire recruits to hardened veterans at Westbrook Garrison, human warriors cut their teeth marching alongside regional marshals against gnolls and bandits.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="A guard garrison is only as tough as its armor; in places like Lakeshire, local smiths exhaust entire iron reserves merely keeping the garrison's blades and shields serviceable."},kw={"garrison","goldshire","lakeshire","marshal dughan","marshal marris","marshal mcbride","stonewatch","stormwind guard","westbrook garrison"},rel={"topic:stormwind-army","topic:stormwind-kingdom","npc:marshal-dughan","npc:marshal-marris","zone:elwynn","zone:redridge","topic:night-watch","topic:defias-brotherhood"},src={"Stormwind Guard"}}
E["topic:stormwind-kingdom"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind (kingdom)",z="general",s="The Kingdom of Stormwind is the southernmost and currently foremost human realm on Azeroth, holding dominion over Elwynn Forest, Westfall, Duskwood, and the Redridge Mountains. Sacked and shattered during the First War, it was rebuilt through blood and labor after the Second, rising to become the military and cultural center of the Alliance following the tragic collapse of Lordaeron in the north. Today, the kingdom stands beset from within and without, ruled by the ten-year-old King Anduin Wrynn under the watchful guidance of Highlord Bolvar Fordragon while its crowned monarch remains missing.",h="The Kingdom of Stormwind is the southernmost and currently foremost human realm on Azeroth, holding dominion...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Defiance",b="Stormwind was established over a thousand years ago by descendants of the ancient Arathi empire who ventured south beyond the crags of Khaz Modan into fertile, forested valleys. Far from the northern kingdoms, the realm grew into a culture fiercely proud of its self-reliance and the Light.",sp=0},{t="Ashes and Restoration",b="That independence was nearly extinguished when the Orcish Horde poured through the Dark Portal. Stormwind City burned to ash in the First War, its King Llane slain. The surviving populace fled north across the Great Sea led by Sir Anduin Lothar, rallying the northern realms into the Alliance of Lordaeron. Once the Orcish threat was crushed in the Second War, refugees returned home, hiring the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild their magnificent capital of white stone and blue banners. When the Scourge wiped out Lordaeron during the Third War, Stormwind became humanity's greatest remaining bastion.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Realm",b="Beneath its soaring towers, Stormwind struggles to govern its vast southern holdings. While Elwynn Forest remains relatively secure, neighboring regions like Westfall are swarming with outlaws, Redridge faces constant orc and gnoll incursions, and the darkened woods of Duskwood have fallen out of the realm's control. A bitter dispute between the House of Nobles and the Stonemasons sparked violent riots in the capital, scattering displaced workers into open rebellion across the provinces.",sp=1},{t="The Hollow Throne",b="The kingdom's instability deepened with the sudden, quiet disappearance of King Varian Wrynn while sailing to a diplomatic summit with the Horde at Theramore. To preserve fragile public order, the crown was placed on his ten-year-old son, Anduin Wrynn, with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon named Lord Regent. Yet royal authority is divided and stalled: the House of Nobles, guided closely by the enigmatic Lady Katrana Prestor, repeatedly vetoes military deployments, leaving local towns to fend for themselves while dangerous secrets fester within Stormwind Keep.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who is currently ruling Stormwind?",a="Formally, the crown belongs to King Anduin Wrynn, who took the throne as a ten-year-old child. Because he is a minor, real governmental authority is wielded by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon as Lord Regent, with heavy legislative counsel and oversight from Lady Katrana Prestor and the House of Nobles.",al={"who is king of stormwind","current ruler","who runs stormwind","king of stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="What happened to King Varian Wrynn?",a="King Varian departed Stormwind on a confidential diplomatic voyage to Theramore to negotiate peaceful relations with the Horde. He vanished under mysterious circumstances along the way. His fate remains a guarded state secret to keep the kingdom's populace and external rivals from descending into chaos.",al={"where is varian","varian missing","what happened to the king","varian wrynn backstory"}},{q="Why was Stormwind destroyed in the past?",a="Stormwind was the first human nation encountered when the Orcish Horde invaded through the Dark Portal in the First War. Despite a stubborn defense led by King Llane and Sir Anduin Lothar, the city was breached, sacked, and razed, forcing the surviving population to evacuate north by sea to Lordaeron.",al={"first war stormwind","fall of stormwind","when did stormwind burn","stormwind destroyed"},it="ruin"},{q="Why are Westfall, Redridge, and Duskwood lacking royal soldiers?",a="The royal military is stretched thin and paralyzed by court politics. Lady Katrana Prestor and the House of Nobles frequently block requests to send garrisons outside Elwynn Forest, arguing that capital funds and defense forces cannot be wasted policing peripheral lands, leaving militias like the People's Militia and the Night Watch to fight alone.",al={"why no soldiers","why no army help","abandoned towns","outlying towns undefended"},it="conflict"},{q="What territory does the Kingdom of Stormwind claim?",a="Historically, Stormwind rules the southern continent of Azeroth, encompassing Elwynn Forest, the Redridge Mountains, Westfall, and Duskwood. It also previously maintained frontier posts reaching south into northern Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"stormwind borders","stormwind lands","what lands are stormwind"},it="place"},{q="Why did the Stonemasons turn against Stormwind?",a="After rebuilding Stormwind City into a masterpiece of white stone, the Stonemasons Guild presented their invoice. Influenced by noble politics, the House of Nobles refused full payment, while the stonemasons refused to compromise. The ensuing city riots drove the guild out to become the outlaw Defias Brotherhood.",al={"why defias mad","stonemasons riot","defias backstory","why did builders rebel"},sp=1,it="motive",bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="What is the Kingdom of Stormwind?",a="The Kingdom of Stormwind, also called the Kingdom of Azeroth, is the southernmost of humanity's Seven Kingdoms and the heart of the Grand Alliance. Settled by descendants of the ancient Arathor empire, it encompasses fertile territories stretching across Elwynn Forest, Westfall, the Redridge Mountains, and Duskwood. Following the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge in the north, Stormwind now stands as the strongest remaining bastion of human power and civilization in the world.",al={"what is stormwind","who is stormwind","stormwind kingdom"},it="identity",add="4ff36454"},{q="Who are the Kingdom of Stormwind allied with?",a="Stormwind is the pillar of the Grand Alliance, maintaining close ties with the Ironforge dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes of Khaz Modan, as well as the night elves across the sea in Kalimdor. Its people also share profound spiritual and historical bonds with the Church of the Holy Light. While it once stood isolated from the northern human kingdoms, Stormwind now leads the unified factions striving to defend civilization across Azeroth.",al={"stormwind allies","who is stormwind allied with","alliance of stormwind"},it="allies",add="4fc8efc2"},{q="What is the history of Stormwind?",a="Founded roughly twelve hundred years ago by Arathi migrants who journeyed south past Khaz Modan, Stormwind prospered as an independent, agrarian realm. It was utterly sacked by the Orcish Horde in the First War. Led by Lord Anduin Lothar, the survivors fled north to Lordaeron, returning after the Second War to rebuild their gleaming capital. With Lordaeron's recent fall to the Scourge, Stormwind has become humanity's paramount stronghold.",al={"stormwind history","history of stormwind","where did stormwind come from"},it="history",add="bf266b42"}},ang={["race:Human"]="Stormwind is humanity's beating heart and final sovereign stronghold after the ruin of Lordaeron, Alterac, and Dalaran in the north.",["faction:Alliance"]="As the primary military and economic anchor of the modern Alliance, Stormwind provides shelter, leadership, and resources to dwarven, gnomish, and night elven allies.",["class:Paladin"]="The kingdom honors the ancient holy traditions of the Church of the Holy Light and the legacy of the Silver Hand, housing consecrated orders within the Cathedral of Light.",["class:Rogue"]="Stormwind's bitter domestic strife and the shadow of the Defias uprising have filled its back alleys, sewers, and countryside with opportunistic cutpurses and political spies."},kw={"anduin","azeroth","bolvar","elwynn","first war","house of nobles","prestor","stonemasons","stormwind","varian","wrynn"},rel={"zone:stormwind","zone:elwynn","zone:westfall","zone:redridge","zone:duskwood","topic:varian-wrynn","topic:house-of-nobles"},src={"Stormwind (kingdom)"}}
E["topic:syndicate"] = {t="topic",n="Syndicate",z="general",s="Across northern Lordaeron, the Syndicate holds strongholds and fights to reclaim Alterac. Dispossessed nobles lead this human criminal organization against both the Alliance and the Horde, seeking to restore their lost aristocracy.",h="Across northern Lordaeron, the Syndicate holds strongholds and fights to reclaim Alterac.",sec={{t="Fallen Nobility",b="After King Aiden Perenolde's treachery during the Second War, the kingdom of Alterac collapsed in dishonor. Its aristocracy lost its lands and titles, but the surviving nobles refused to accept exile. They gathered under the late king's son, Prince Aliden Perenolde, and forged the Syndicate.\n\nThey marked themselves with orange bandanas and filled their ranks with hired thugs, highwaymen, and hedge wizards. To outsiders, they are brigands who raid caravans, ambush travelers, and extort people. Yet their crimes exist only to fund an organized bid to reclaim their ancestral lands and subjugate the surrounding regions.",sp=0},{t="Strongholds of the North",b="Across northern Lordaeron, the Syndicate holds fortified ground. In the snowy Alterac Mountains, they fortified the ruined town of Strahnbrad and set camps in the Uplands and Dandred's Fold. Farther south, they hold the crumbling Durnholde Keep in the Hillsbrad Foothills. From there, they harass Southshore and raid supply routes.\n\nFarther east, beyond Thoradin's Wall, Lord Falconcrest commands an occupying Syndicate army in the historic ruins of Stromgarde Keep. There, they battle the battered Stromgarde Militia for control of the Arathi Highlands.",sp=0},{t="Enemies on All Fronts",b="The Syndicate's ambitions reach beyond ordinary outlawry, earning them the hatred of nearly every regional faction. They call the Alliance their mortal enemy, believing it stole their rightful heritage. Their attacks often target Southshore officials such as Magistrate Henry Maleb.\n\nToward the Horde, their scorn is open. Following Aedelas Blackmoore's philosophy, they see orcs as beasts fit for slave labor and seek to restore the internment camps. Sylvanas Windrunner's Forsaken execute captured Syndicate members on sight. Ravenholdt's clandestine thieves' guild wages a shadow war against Syndicate operatives it considers sloppy and arrogant.",sp=0},{t="Puppets of the Shadow Council",b="The Syndicate's noble leaders believe they fight for human supremacy and their birthright. Yet cultists of the Argus Wake have infiltrated the upper ranks and steer men such as Lord Falconcrest toward seizing Stromgarde Keep.\n\nThe Argus Wake answers to the Shadow Council in Jaedenar, within Felwood. It uses the Syndicate's armies and stolen lands as a mortal vanguard, clearing the way for the Burning Legion.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who are the Syndicate?",a="The Syndicate is a criminal organization of dispossessed nobles, brigands, and mercenaries from fallen Alterac. Exiled aristocrats such as Prince Aliden Perenolde and Lord Falconcrest lead them. They use highway robbery, assassination, and military occupation to try to rebuild Alterac.",al={"what is the syndicate","syndicate background","who are these guys in orange"},it="identity"},{q="Why does the Syndicate hate the Alliance?",a="Alterac betrayed the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War, and the Alliance dismantled the kingdom and stripped its nobles of their lands. Syndicate leaders consider the Alliance their mortal enemy and sabotage its towns, trade caravans, and garrisons such as Stromgarde Keep.",al={"why does syndicate attack alliance","syndicate alliance relationship","alterac vs alliance"},it="conflict"},{q="Why does the Syndicate capture and enslave orcs?",a="The Syndicate follows the harsh philosophy of former Durnholde commander Aedelas Blackmoore, who viewed orcs as savage beasts fit only for unpaid servitude. Prince Aliden Perenolde sought to restore the Alliance internment camps, using enslaved orcs captured in the Hillsbrad Foothills and Alterac as forced labor.",al={"syndicate orc slaves","why are orcs in cages in strahnbrad","syndicate slavery"},sp=1,it="motive",bt={"q533.4"}},{q="What is the Syndicate's relationship with Ravenholdt?",a="Ravenholdt is a prestigious guild of professional assassins and thieves in the Alterac Mountains. Its members consider the Syndicate amateurish and undisciplined, and contract rogues to steal Syndicate emblems. The Syndicate's leaders ruthlessly cull operatives who lose theirs.",al={"ravenholdt vs syndicate","why does ravenholdt hate syndicate","syndicate emblems"},it="ties"},{q="Who is really pulling the strings behind the Syndicate?",a="The Syndicate publicly answers to exiled Alteraci nobles such as Prince Aliden Perenolde and Lord Falconcrest. They believe they are fighting to reclaim their lost titles. Yet whispers in the Arathi Highlands hint at darker hands behind their hold on Stromgarde, and travelers can look into it at the Syndicate's strongholds there.",al={"who leads the syndicate","argus wake syndicate","shadow council syndicate"},sp=2,it="power"},{q="Where are the Syndicate's primary strongholds?",a="The Syndicate's main strongholds lie across the Alterac Mountains, the Hillsbrad Foothills, and the Arathi Highlands. They hold Strahnbrad and Dandred's Fold in Alterac, Durnholde Keep in Hillsbrad, and Northfold Manor in Arathi. Under Lord Falconcrest, they also occupy a large part of Stromgarde Keep.",al={"where are syndicate bases","syndicate locations","where to find syndicate"},it="place"},{q="Why is the Syndicate hostile to travelers?",a="The Syndicate views all outsiders as threats to their criminal plots and territorial ambitions. They despise the Alliance for dismantling Alterac after the Second War, regard the Horde merely as brutes fit only for forced labor, and brook no interference in their banditry, assassinations, and raids.",al={"why do syndicate attack","syndicate hostile","why syndicate attack players"},it="hostile",add="1053e6ff"},{q="Who leads the Syndicate?",a="The organization was founded and led in the Alterac Mountains by Prince Aliden Perenolde, the son of Alterac's traitorous late king. In the Arathi Highlands, operations are commanded by Lord Falconcrest from the ruins of Stromgarde Keep, supported by regional overseers like Baron Vardus and Master Ryson.",al={"syndicate leader","who is in charge of syndicate","syndicate boss"},it="leader",add="8dd05730"},{q="Who is the Syndicate allied with?",a="Openly, they rely on rogue mercenaries, bandits, and corrupt human nobility while fighting nearly every established faction. In secret, however, the Syndicate is manipulated by the Argus Wake, a sinister warlock coven serving the Shadow Council at Jaedenar, which promised to restore Alterac's rule in exchange for footholds across northern Lordaeron.",al={"syndicate allies","who works with syndicate","syndicate partners"},sp=1,it="allies",add="c949a5e9"},{q="Where did the Syndicate come from?",a="The Syndicate was born from the ruins of the Kingdom of Alterac. After King Aiden Perenolde betrayed the Alliance during the Second War, Alterac was stripped of sovereignty and fell into ruin. Deposed nobles and disgruntled aristocrats banded together under Prince Aliden Perenolde, turning to smuggling, banditry, and terrorism to reclaim their lost lands.",al={"syndicate origin","syndicate history","how was syndicate formed"},it="history",add="ff7f041a"}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Ravenholdt pays thieves to slip behind enemy lines and steal emblems from Syndicate brigands, taking advantage of their fear of losing rank.",["race:Orc"]="The Syndicate sees your people as beasts destined for chains and internment. Freeing captured brethren in Strahnbrad and Durnholde is a matter of honor.",["faction:Alliance"]="Syndicate operatives murdered Southshore's former magistrate and keep pushing the beleaguered Stromgarde Militia toward extinction in the Arathi Highlands.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Thrall and Lady Sylvanas both maintain active bounties against the Syndicate. They send scouts to destroy its camps and recover stolen relics."},kw={"aliden perenolde","alterac","bandanas","durnholde keep","falconcrest","ravenholdt","strahnbrad","stromgarde","syndicate","uplands"},rel={"topic:alterac-kingdom","topic:ravenholdt","subzone:stromgarde-keep","subzone:durnholde-keep","subzone:strahnbrad","subzone:dandred-s-fold","zone:alterac","quest:5726"},src={"Syndicate"}}
E["topic:tauren"] = {t="topic",n="Tauren",z="general",s="Across central Kalimdor, the tauren are building a home in Mulgore while keeping their old ways. They call themselves shu'halo and honor the Earth Mother, shamanism, and the natural world. Centaur clans nearly destroyed them before they joined Thrall's New Horde. High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof now leads them from Mulgore and Thunder Bluff.",h="Across central Kalimdor, the tauren are building a home in Mulgore while keeping their old ways.",sec={{t="Nomads of the Barrens",b="For countless generations, the tauren roamed the arid Barrens with the seasons. They hunted the great kodo and listened for the Earth Mother's voice. Then the centaur tribes brought their peaceful wanderings to an end. Born from the tragic union of Zaetar and Princess Theradras, the fierce centaur waged a relentless war against them. Without a fortified homeland, and unwilling to fight without end, the tauren were driven near annihilation.",sp=0},{t="The New Horde and Thunder Bluff",b="An alliance from across the sea gave the tauren a chance to survive. During the Third War, Cairne Bloodhoof met Warchief Thrall and the orcs who had newly arrived. The leaders recognized a shared honor and longing for their shamanic heritage, and joined forces.\n\nWith the Horde's help, the tauren defeated the marauding centaur and claimed Mulgore's sheltered grasslands. Cairne founded Thunder Bluff on Mulgore's windswept mesas. In gratitude, the tauren marched with the Horde to Mount Hyjal and helped turn back the Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="Spiritual Guardians and Internal Tensions",b="The tauren bring a peaceful, steadying voice to the Horde. Their mastery of nature and the elements helped revive orcish shamanism. Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem also keeps rare diplomatic and spiritual ties with night elf druids as they investigate growing corruption within the Emerald Dream.\n\nYet unity among the tribes remains fragile. Most revere Cairne's wisdom, but the xenophobic and ruthless Grimtotem clan lives within Thunder Bluff. Its leader, the scheming elder crone Magatha, secretly plots to seize leadership and steer the shu'halo toward supremacy.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why did the tauren join the Horde?",a="Ruthless centaur clans nearly wiped out the tauren in the Barrens. During the Third War, Warchief Thrall and his orcs fought beside Cairne Bloodhoof, drove the centaur back, and helped secure Mulgore. Bound by honor and shared spiritual values, Cairne pledged his people's loyalty to the New Horde in gratitude.",al={"why join horde","tauren horde alliance","why are tauren in horde"}},{q="Who leads the tauren?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof leads the tauren and heads the Bloodhoof tribe. Respected for his strength and measured judgment, he unites the tribes from Thunder Bluff, with Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem assisting him.",al={"tauren leader","tauren chieftain","who rules thunder bluff"}},{q="What is the Earth Mother?",a="The Earth Mother is the central deity of tauren belief. The shu'halo believe she created the world, the sun, and the moon. They honor her through a life attuned to nature's balance, seasonal migration, hunting, and respect for the elements.",al={"earthmother","who is the earth mother","tauren religion"}},{q="Why do the tauren hate the centaur?",a="Centaur hunted the tauren for sport and warred with them for centuries across central Kalimdor. They saw the tauren's peaceful spirituality as weakness, drove them from place to place, and nearly wiped out the shu'halo before the orcs intervened.",al={"tauren centaur war","centaur feud"}},{q="Can tauren be druids?",a="Yes. Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem leads the tauren druids, who learn the ways of the wild alongside night elves. Tauren druids are a cornerstone of the Cenarion Circle.",al={"tauren druid lore","how did tauren become druids"}},{q="Are all tauren loyal to Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Most tauren tribes revere Cairne, but the Grimtotem are a bitter exception. Led by Magatha Grimtotem, they believe tauren are superior to all other races and despise Cairne's alliance with the Horde. They quietly work to undermine his peaceful reign.",al={"grimtotem loyalty","tauren civil war"}},{q="Which classes can tauren be?",a="Tauren train as warriors, hunters, shaman and druids.",al={"tauren classes","what classes can tauren be"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As shu'halo, your people's ancient nomadic wanderings have given way to a homeland in Mulgore, secured through the brotherhood between Cairne and Thrall.",["faction:Horde"]="The tauren give the Horde moral guidance and deep spiritual roots, tempering the aggressive instincts of orcs and trolls with ancient wisdom and honor.",["class:Druid"]="Through Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, tauren druids work alongside the night elves of the Cenarion Circle to heal the land and protect the Emerald Dream.",["class:Shaman"]="Tauren shamanic traditions are among Azeroth's oldest. Their teachings helped the orcs rediscover their sacred elemental bonds after the demonic taint of past wars."},kw={"barrens","cairne bloodhoof","centaur","earth mother","hamuul runetotem","kodo","magatha grimtotem","mulgore","shu'halo","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","zone:barrens","topic:earth-mother","topic:centaur","npc:magatha-grimtotem","quest:1489"},src={"Tauren"}}
E["topic:third-war"] = {t="topic",n="Third War",z="general",s="Across Lordaeron and Kalimdor, the Third War ended only after mortals stood against the Burning Legion. The war began with the Lich King's Plague of Undeath and brought down Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas before the defense of Mount Hyjal.",h="Across Lordaeron and Kalimdor, the Third War ended only after mortals stood against the Burning Legion.",sec={{t="The Plague and the Prince",b="The Third War began with plague in northern Lordaeron. Infected grain from Andorhal carried a sickness that killed its victims and raised them as ghouls bound to the Lich King's will. Prince Arthas Menethil and Jaina Proudmoore, a sorceress of the Kirin Tor, tried to stop it.\n\nDreadlords turned Arthas's grief and fear toward Stratholme. He purged its infected people, then pursued his foe to Northrend. There Arthas took Frostmourne, the cursed runeblade, and cast aside his soul. He returned south and murdered his father, King Terenas. Lordaeron then fell into the Scourge's hands.",sp=0},{t="The Invasion of the Legion",b="Arthas used the dead to crush Quel'Thalas and raise the necromancer Kel'Thuzad at the Sunwell. The Scourge then besieged Dalaran and seized the spellbooks needed to summon Archimonde, the demon lord, into Azeroth. With Archimonde on mortal soil, Dalaran was leveled by arcane devastation. The Scourge had served as the Legion's advance guard. Now the Burning Legion turned toward the eternal power across the Great Sea.",sp=0},{t="The Flight West and Mount Hyjal",b="The mysterious Prophet sent cryptic warnings to Warchief Thrall and Lady Jaina Proudmoore. Thrall led the New Horde across the Great Sea, while Jaina brought surviving Alliance refugees to safety. In ancient Kalimdor, the newcomers clashed with the reclusive night elves.\n\nThen the three factions recognized the threat before them. Atop Mount Hyjal, mortals joined forces beneath the World Tree Nordrassil. When Archimonde reached the tree, ancestral wisps destroyed him. The Legion's vanguard was broken, and the war ended.",sp=0},{t="A Scarred World",b="Archimonde's fall brought no lasting peace. Lordaeron's Alliance is gone. Stormwind and Ironforge now anchor the southern Alliance, while the old kingdom rots as the Plaguelands. In Kalimdor, Thrall's Horde has carved out Durotar, though tensions with the newly allied night elves remain volatile.\n\nThe Scourge still holds the northern ruins. The freed undead, called the Forsaken, hold the catacombs of Capital City. They fight to survive against their former masters and vengeful crusaders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="When did the Third War happen?",a="The Third War began when the Plague of Undeath broke out in Lordaeron and ended at the Battle of Mount Hyjal. The devastation is only four years past, so ruins are fresh and nearly every adult alive fought in the war or lost family to it.",al={"when was the third war","how long ago was the war","third war dates"},it="history"},{q="How was the Third War different from the First and Second Wars?",a="The First and Second Wars were chiefly struggles between the Old Horde and the Alliance kingdoms. The Third War brought a demonic invasion that shattered nations. Mortals fought the Burning Legion and its undead Scourge across three continents, forcing enemies into a desperate truce.",al={"difference between third war and second","why was third war different","horde vs alliance third war"},it="conflict"},{q="What happened to the Kingdom of Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was destroyed from within. The Cult of the Damned tainted the regional grain with the Plague of Undeath, killing people and raising them as monsters. Prince Arthas fell to corruption, murdered his father King Terenas II, and delivered the broken kingdom to the Scourge.",al={"fate of lordaeron","why is lordaeron destroyed","what happened to terenas"},it="ruin"},{q="Who was the Prophet that warned everyone to go to Kalimdor?",a="The Prophet was Medivh, the Last Guardian of Tirisfal. Seeking atonement for opening the Dark Portal, he traveled as a raven-cloaked wanderer. He warned that Lordaeron was doomed and urged its leaders to unite in Kalimdor.",al={"who was the prophet","raven prophet","medivh third war"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the night elves part of the Alliance now?",a="The night elves had lived in strict isolation for ten thousand years. The Third War brought the Legion back to their sacred forests and made them sacrifice their immortality to slay Archimonde at the World Tree. In a changed world, with resource conflicts growing between them and Thrall's Horde and Scourge corruption still present, they left their solitude and allied with the races of the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why did night elves join alliance","night elves third war","kaldorei joining alliance"},it="allies"},{q="How did the Horde escape the war in Lordaeron?",a="Warchief Thrall trusted Medivh's prophetic dreams. He rallied the freed clans from the internment camps, raided human ports for sailing ships, and crossed the Great Sea before the Scourge or Legion fully consumed Lordaeron.",al={"how did thrall leave lordaeron","orcs sailing west","horde kalimdor flight"},it="motive"},{q="What was the Third War?",a="The Third War was a catastrophic global conflict that saw the demonic Burning Legion launch its second invasion of Azeroth, aided by the undead Scourge. Beginning with the Plague of Undeath in northern Lordaeron and the betrayal of Prince Arthas Menethil, the war quickly spread across the Eastern Kingdoms and into Kalimdor. Unlike earlier conflicts between just orcs and humans, it engulfed nearly every major race on Azeroth before culminating in a desperate, united stand atop Mount Hyjal.",al={"what is the third war","third war summary","explain third war"},it="identity",add="b67a3dac"},{q="Why is it called the Third War?",a="It is named the Third War because it followed the First and Second Wars fought between the original orcish Horde and the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Because this invasion brought unprecedented global devastation and demon armies, it is also known as the Third Great War, the Chaos War, and the Reign of Chaos.",al={"third war name origin","why called third war","reign of chaos name"},it="name",add="4a458aae"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="Lordaeron's fall moved Alliance leadership south to Stormwind and Ironforge. Survivors look north, longing to reclaim their homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="For Thrall's Horde, the war brought redemption. Grommash Hellscream slew Mannoroth and broke the demonic blood curse, so the orcs could build a sovereign nation in Kalimdor.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken are Lordaeron's people, slain by the plague and forced to serve the Scourge. They broke free and claimed the Undercity.",["race:NightElf"]="Defending Nordrassil cost the kaldorei their immortality. It also opened their ancient homelands to the wider world for the first time in ten thousand years.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were torn apart in the war. Arthas disbanded them, murdered Uther the Lightbringer, and turned their holy halls into charnel houses."},kw={"archimonde","arthas","hyjal","mannoroth","medivh","nordrassil","scourge","stratholme","terenas","third war"},rel={"topic:scourge","topic:burning-legion","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:nordrassil","topic:lordaeron","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:medivh","topic:new-horde"},src={"Third War"}}
E["topic:titan"] = {t="topic",n="Titan",z="general",s="The titans are a primordial race of colossal, godlike beings who wander the cosmos bringing order and shaping worlds. In ancient antiquity, they came to Azeroth to vanquish the chaotic Old Gods, establish massive terrestrial facilities, and foster life before departing into the Great Dark Beyond.",h="The titans are a primordial race of colossal, godlike beings who wander the cosmos bringing order and shaping...",sec={{t="Architects of the Cosmos",b="The titans are cosmic giants forged from primordial matter and arcane magic, embodying the forces of order and creation. In their journeys across the Great Dark Beyond, they sought out slumbering worlds to cultivate, raising up continents, calming chaotic elemental forces, and designing life. To enforce balance, they established powerful constructs made of stone, iron, and sea to watch over their works.",sp=0},{t="The Ordering of Azeroth",b="Long ago, the Pantheon of titans discovered Azeroth in the grip of the chaotic elemental lords and the horrific Old Gods. The titans waged war against these parasitic horrors, banishing the elementals to pocket planes and sealing the monstrous Old Gods deep beneath the crust of the earth. Upon securing the world, they carved great biomes, established vault complexes like Uldaman, and entrusted five great dragons—the Dragon Aspects—with stewardship of the land, magic, time, and life itself before journeying back among the stars.",sp=0},{t="The Fallen Champion",b="Not all titans remained aligned with the Pantheon's vision of perfect order. Sargeras, once the greatest warrior and defender of the titans, grew despondent and terrified by the deep cosmic darkness and demonic malevolence he was charged with combating. Rejecting the work of his kin, Sargeras turned to nihilism, founding the demonic Burning Legion to extinguish all creation rather than allow it to be corrupted.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Are the titans still alive and on Azeroth?",a="The titans themselves departed Azeroth eons ago into the Great Dark Beyond after defeating the Old Gods and ordering the world. They left behind titanic watchers, stone and iron constructs, and vast buried vault complexes to oversee their design.",al={"where are the titans","are titans on azeroth","titan locations"},it="place"},{q="Why do the dwarves care so much about the titans?",a="Dwarves descend directly from the earthen, a race of subterranean stone beings fashioned by the titans to carve the foundations of the world. Through archaeology and the excavation of ruins like Bael Modan and Uldaman, dwarves seek to uncover their own forgotten lineage.",al={"dwarf titan connection","why dwarves dig","dwarves and the creators"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Pantheon?",a="The Pantheon is the governing council of the titans, led by the Highfather Aman'Thul. Other members include Khaz'goroth the shaper of worlds, Eonar the life-binder, Norgannon the keeper of celestial magics, Golganneth the lord of skies, and Aggramar, the champion who replaced Sargeras.",al={"titan pantheon","who leads the titans","titan leaders"}},{q="Did the titans create the Dragon Aspects?",a="Yes. Before leaving Azeroth, the Pantheon channeled their powers through the keepers to bless five proto-dragons. Alexstrasza, Ysera, Malygos, Nozdormu, and Neltharion were granted immense power to safeguard life, nature, magic, time, and the earth.",al={"titan dragons","aspect origins","who made the aspects"}},{q="Is Sargeras a titan?",a="Sargeras was once the champion of the Pantheon, defending the cosmos against chaos and demons. Driven to despair and madness by the darkness he witnessed, he broke away from his brethren to form the Burning Legion, dedicating himself to burning down the ordered universe.",al={"who is sargeras","is sargeras a god","sargeras origin"}},{q="Did a titan fall in Silithus?",a="Prophecies recorded by the Qiraji Prophet Skeram claim that a titan battled an ancient evil in the southern deserts of Silithus and fell. However, scholars and explorers debate whether this entity was a true titan or merely one of their titanic constructs.",al={"silithus titan","prophecy of c'thun titan","did c'thun kill a titan"},it="history"},{q="What are the titans?",a="The titans are colossal, godlike beings of the cosmos, the supreme champions of Order and creation. Wandering the Great Dark Beyond, they bring structure to formless worlds and foster life wherever they travel.",al={"who are the titans","what is a titan","titan lore"},it="identity",add="f6eb9ea0"},{q="Where did the titans come from?",a="No mortal knows where the titans came from. They are ancient beings of the Great Dark Beyond who travel the cosmos to bring order to newborn worlds, as they did when they came to Azeroth, defeated the Old Gods and shaped the land.",al={"titan origin","origin of titans","how were titans created"},it="history",add="23c22d21"},{q="Who leads the titans?",a="The titans are led by Highfather Aman'Thul, the eldest among them and the head of their governing council, the Pantheon. Wielding immense temporal and cosmic power, Aman'Thul guides their mission across the Great Dark Beyond to order worlds.",al={"who is the titan leader","leader of the titans","leader of the pantheon"},it="leader",add="91d84aea"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="The titans are revered as the divine Makers. Discovering their origins through titanic relics is the central mission of the Explorers' League and King Magni Bronzebeard.",["race:Gnome"]="Gnomes share a mechanical legacy originating from titanic creations, driving their natural affinity for clockwork design and excavation.",["class:Mage"]="Arcane magic is the fundamental lifeblood of the titans, and its ordered principles trace directly to the structural enchantments woven across the world by the Pantheon.",["profession:Mining"]="Miners dig deep into the bones of the earth, often unearthing pristine titan-forged metal nodes and architectural foundations that predate recorded history."},kw={"aman'thul","earthen","eonar","khaz'goroth","makers","norgannon","order","pantheon","titan"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:old-god","topic:sargeras","topic:burning-legion","topic:emerald-dream","npc:deathwing","zone:silithus","npc:greatfather-winter"},src={"Titan"}}
E["topic:trogg"] = {t="topic",n="Trogg",z="general",s="Troggs are brutish, subterranean humanoids descended from the earliest, failed titan-forged experiments sealed deep within the earth. Having recently clawed their way to the surface after dwarven excavations disturbed their ancient vaults, these relentless diggers have overrun dwarven quarries and driven the gnomes out of their beloved capital of Gnomeregan.",h="Troggs are brutish, subterranean humanoids descended from the earliest, failed titan-forged experiments...",sec={{t="The Flawed Predecessors",b="Long before mortal races recorded history, the titan-forged keepers sought to fashion servants from stone. Their initial design went awry, yielding stone-skinned brutes of low intelligence and unbridled savagery. Distressed by their botched creations, the keepers refined the matrix into the earthen race, who would eventually become the ancestors of the dwarves. Unwilling to annihilate the imperfect troggs, the giantess Ironaya sealed them deep underground within the vault of Uldaman, intending for them to remain trapped in eternal stasis.",sp=0},{t="Awakened by the Pickaxe",b="For countless millennia, the troggs lay dormant until the Explorers' League of Ironforge breached the ancient subterranean complexes of Khaz Modan. Waking in an unfamiliar era, swarms of furious troggs poured from the depths, pursuing their distant dwarven relatives with ancestral fury. Finding the surface bright and unwelcoming, they carved sprawling tunnel networks beneath Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and the Badlands, quickly ambushing quarries, archaeological dig sites, and trade outposts.",sp=0},{t="The Catastrophe at Gnomeregan",b="Guided by subterranean vibration and noise, trogg swarms tunneled directly up into Gnomeregan. Desperately outmatched by sheer numbers and savage strength, the gnomes fought a bitter war in the subterranean corridors. In a desperate gambit engineered by Mekgineer Thermaplugg, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque unleashed radioactive venting to exterminate the infestation. Tragically, the irradiated gas killed roughly eighty percent of the gnomish populace while merely enraging the troggs, leaving the grand techno-city an irradiated trogg stronghold.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What exactly are troggs?",a="Troggs are subterranean humanoids created as the first, flawed generation of stone servants by the titan-forged keepers. Distressed by their raw brutality, the keepers locked them in stasis vaults like Uldaman rather than destroying them, until dwarven miners inadvertently unearthed them thousands of years later.",al={"trogg origins","what is a trogg","trogg lore"},it="identity"},{q="Are troggs related to dwarves?",a="Yes. Both races were shaped by the titans' keepers. Troggs represent the keepers' unrefined first draft, while the earthen—the ancestors of the dwarves—were the perfected second iteration.",al={"are dwarves and troggs related","trogg dwarf connection"},it="ties"},{q="How did troggs capture Gnomeregan?",a="Drawn by the vibrations of heavy machinery, the troggs tunneled into Gnomeregan from below. Despite gnomish defensive inventions, the invaders overwhelmed the defenders through sheer numbers. A failed attempt to purge them with radioactive gas killed most of the gnomes instead, cementing trogg control over the city.",al={"why are troggs in gnomeregan","gnomeregan trogg attack"},it="conflict"},{q="Are troggs capable of speech or advanced culture?",a="Troggs possess very limited intellect, communicating mostly through guttural grunts and roaring. They value pure brute strength above all else, choosing tribal chieftains through violence. While some manifest rudimentary shamanistic spellcasting, their craftsmanship is crude, rarely advancing beyond crude clubs, stone arrows, and stolen relics.",al={"can troggs talk","trogg culture","trogg intelligence"},it="power"},{q="Why do troggs collect ancient relics?",a="Explorers believe their compulsion to hoard ancient titan relics is an instinctual remnant of their titan-forged origins. However, troggs rarely understand what they carry, using priceless artifacts as blunt cudgels or primitive good-luck charms.",al={"why do troggs hoard artifacts","trogg treasure"},it="purpose"},{q="Are there troggs outside of Khaz Modan?",a="Yes. Subterranean trogg clans have established nests across Azeroth, including the Ragefire troggs deep within Ragefire Chasm beneath Orgrimmar, the Gravelflint troggs in Darkshore, and groups throughout the Badlands and Alterac.",al={"trogg locations","troggs in durotar","troggs in kalimdor"},it="place"},{q="Why are troggs so hostile to other races?",a="Troggs are driven almost entirely by raw instinct, cunning, and sheer aggression. Upon being disturbed from their ancient slumber deep underground, their immediate impulse was to vent their fury on the surface dwellers, particularly their dwarven cousins who broke into their vaults. Simpleminded and territorial, they view other living beings either as threats to be crushed or as meals to be eaten, readily resorting to violence and even cannibalism without provocation.",al={"why troggs attack","are troggs aggressive","why do troggs hate dwarves"},it="hostile",add="0f8118fe"},{q="Who leads the troggs?",a="Troggs have no unified ruler or central civilization. Instead, they organize into scattered tribes and clans, such as the Rockjaw, Stonesplinter, and Stonevault troggs. Within each tribe, leadership belongs solely to the strongest, largest, and most vicious brute who can enforce rule through intimidation and bloodshed, such as Grimlok within Uldaman.",al={"trogg leader","trogg chieftain","trogg king"},it="leader",add="9c157b85"},{q="Do troggs have any allies?",a="Almost universally, troggs fight alone. Their savagery, cannibalistic tendencies, and hostility toward other races make diplomacy impossible, and they despise outsiders. A rare exception exists among the Stonesplinter clan, who have established loose ties with the kobolds of the Tunnel Rats, but genuine allegiances beyond their own kind are practically nonexistent.",al={"trogg allies","who works with troggs","trogg factions"},it="allies",add="21373546"},{q="Where did troggs come from?",a="Troggs were the first generation of titan-forged crafted by the titanic keepers. The design proved flawed and overly complex, producing stone-skinned, brutish creatures lacking higher intellect. Distressed by the result, the keepers refined their subsequent efforts into the earthen and sealed the defective troggs in stasis vaults like Uldaman. Over millennia, they slept beneath Azeroth until dwarven miners unwittingly excavated their tombs.",al={"origin of troggs","trogg history","how were troggs created"},it="history",add="638bdf0a"}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Troggs are the bitter bane of your people, responsible for shattering your underground home and scattering the surviving gnomes to Tinker Town in Ironforge.",["race:Dwarf"]="These savage cousins were unearthed by your own pickaxes in your quest to uncover titan ancestry, making their incursions across Khaz Modan a direct consequence of dwarven archaeology.",["profession:Mining"]="Trogg nests frequently occupy rich mineral veins and deep mines like Gol'Bolar, forcing miners to defend their claims with pickaxes and hammers.",["faction:Alliance"]="Clearing trogg infestations across Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and Gnomeregan remains a vital defensive effort to keep the mountain kingdom and its refugees safe."},kw={"caverndeep","earthen","gnomeregan","grubbis","oggleflint","rockjaw","stonesplinter","trogg","uldaman"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","subzone:uldaman","zone:dunmorogh","zone:lochmodan","zone:ragefire","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","npc:sicco-thermaplugg","topic:explorers-league"},src={"Trogg"}}
E["topic:troll"] = {t="topic",n="Troll",z="general",s="Trolls are among the oldest sentient races native to Azeroth, having arisen around the Well of Eternity long before the Sundering. Renowned for their incredible physical regeneration, worship of primal spirits known as loa, and mastery of voodoo, their fractured empires once ruled vast swaths of the ancient world. Today, while ancient kingdoms like the Gurubashi and Amani remain deeply hostile to outsiders, the Darkspear tribe has found a lasting haven within Thrall's Horde.",h="Trolls are among the oldest sentient races native to Azeroth, having arisen around the Well of Eternity long...",sec={{t="Children of the Wilds",b="Long before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, the primal energies of the Well of Eternity nurtured the rise of trollkind. Emerging as savage, highly agile hunter-gatherers, the early trolls possessed a unique physiology that granted them astonishing regenerative powers, allowing them to recover rapidly from grievous wounds and even regrow severed limbs over time. Settling around the sacred mountains of Zandalar, they directed their reverence toward primal spirits they called loa. Under the spiritual guidance of shadow hunters and witch doctors, these rituals coalesced into the practice of voodoo.",sp=0},{t="War Against the Aqir",b="In ancient times, the trolls waged a long war against the insectoid aqir, whose swarms threatened to overrun the continent. The Amani and Gurubashi empires led the fight and eventually drove the aqir to the far corners of the land: north into what became Azjol-Nerub and south into the deserts where Ahn'Qiraj would rise.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Legacy",b="With the insect swarms broken and contained, the trolls' common cause dissolved. Each great faction withdrew into its own sovereign empire, developing distinct cultures, physical traits, and tribal hierarchies adapted to forests, jungles, and frozen wastes. Over millennia of separation, rivalry, and territorial wars against younger races, troll dominion slowly decayed into ruin and insularity. Most remaining troll kingdoms view all other races with fierce, unyielding hostility, clinging bitterly to the ruins of their former greatness. Only the exile jungle trolls of the Darkspear tribe have chosen a new path, joining the Horde alongside orcs and tauren.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Can trolls really regenerate lost limbs?",a="Yes. Trolls possess an extraordinary regenerative physiology that allows them to recover from physical trauma at a speed unmatched by most other mortal races. Over time, a troll can even regrow lost fingers and entire limbs, making them exceptionally resilient in battle.",al={"troll healing","troll limb regrowth","how do trolls heal"}},{q="What are loa?",a="The loa are the primal deities worshipped by trollkind across virtually every tribe. Many are powerful animal spirits, which trolls honor through offerings, meditation, and ritual magic. Priests and shadow hunters call upon the loa to grant blessings, guidance, and devastating power in battle.",al={"who are loa","troll gods","what do trolls worship"}},{q="Why are most trolls hostile to players?",a="Most troll tribes, such as the forest trolls of the Amani or the jungle trolls of the Gurubashi, possess ancient, insular cultures that harbor deep hatred for outsiders. Centuries of conflict with encroaching races have made them fiercely protective of their ancestral territories, attacking trespassers on sight.",al={"why are trolls bad","hostile trolls","evil trolls"}},{q="How did the Darkspear join the Horde?",a="The Darkspear jungle trolls were driven from their ancestral lands and trapped on an island under siege by murlocs and sea witches. Warchief Thrall and his orc fleet intervened, saving the Darkspear from annihilation. In gratitude and shared necessity, their chieftain pledged the tribe's loyalty to the newly formed Horde.",al={"why are trolls horde","darkspear horde origin","thrall and trolls"}},{q="What is voodoo?",a="Voodoo is an ancient school of spiritual and dark magic practiced by troll witch doctors, shadow hunters, and priests. It weaves together ancestral communion, loa worship, curses, hexes, and potent herbal concoctions to harm foes and empower allies.",al={"troll voodoo","how does voodoo work","witch doctor magic"}},{q="Which classes can trolls be?",a="Trolls train as warriors, hunters, rogues, priests, shaman, mages and warlocks.",al={"troll classes","what classes can trolls be","can trolls be warlocks"}}},ang={["race:Troll"]="Your heritage stretches back further than almost any civilization on Azeroth. While your cousins in Zul'Gurub and Zul'Aman cling to bitter, isolationist pride, the Darkspear forge a surviving legacy through the strength of the Horde.",["faction:Horde"]="The cunning, agility, and spiritual strength of the Darkspear trolls provide essential scouts, shadow hunters, and shamans to the warchief's banner.",["race:NightElf"]="Elven history and ancient myths trace bitter rivalries back to troll kingdoms, viewing troll religious voodoo and sacrificial rites with severe wariness.",["class:Priest"]="Troll priesthood is inextricably tied to the loa and shadow hunting, communing directly with animal spirits and primal dark forces rather than the Holy Light."},kw={"amani","aqir","darkspear","gurubashi","loa","regeneration","troll","trolls","voodoo","zandalari"},rel={"topic:darkspear-tribe","npc:vol-jin","topic:horde","topic:old-god","topic:alliance","topic:titan"},src={"Troll"}}
E["topic:twilight-s-hammer"] = {t="topic",n="Twilight's Hammer",z="general",s="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to awakening the Old Gods and bringing about the end of Azeroth. Originally an orcish clan led by the ogre-mage Cho'gall, it has evolved into a secretive, multiracial order actively conducting dark rituals across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",h="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to awakening the Old Gods and bringing...",sec={{t="Origins in Shadow",b="The Twilight's Hammer began on Draenor as an orc clan of the Old Horde, led by the two-headed ogre-mage Cho'gall, one of Gul'dan's chief lieutenants in the Shadow Council. Under Cho'gall the clan embraced a grim, apocalyptic creed, coming to see the Horde as merely an engine of destruction meant to bring about the end of all things.",sp=0},{t="Arrival on Azeroth",b="When the Twilight's Hammer crossed the Dark Portal during the First War, their apocalyptic fanaticism only deepened. In the soil of Azeroth, the cultists sensed the genuine whispers of the Old Gods—ancient horrors chained beneath the earth. Blackhand tolerated their erratic, ritualistic behavior solely because Cho'gall managed their bloodlust, deploying them alongside Kilrogg Deadeye's Bleeding Hollow clan in early assaults on Stormwind Keep. The clan later operated deep within Grim Batol during the Second War, overseeing critical refineries until Gul'dan's betrayal fractured the Horde. Though Orgrim Doomhammer ordered the traitorous clan butchered near the Tomb of Sargeras, a core cadre of zealots survived.",sp=0},{t="A Spreading Cancer",b="Upon reaching the forgotten shores of Kalimdor, the cult transformed from a shattered orc clan into a clandestine, multiracial conspiracy. Seducing disillusioned members of both the Alliance and the Horde, the Twilight's Hammer recruits any soul broken enough to welcome oblivion. In northern Kalimdor, led by Twilight Lord Kelris, cultists have entrenched themselves within the submerged halls of Blackfathom Deeps, feeding sacrifices to Aku'mai, a monstrous servant of an Old God. Further north in Darkshore, their excavators disturb the colossal fossilized remains at the Master's Glaive. Across the southern deserts of Silithus, camps of robed fanatics tend abyssal windstones, listening for the stirring of ancient horrors entombed beneath the sands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Twilight's Hammer trying to achieve?",a="They seek the total annihilation of all life and order on Azeroth. Rather than seeking conquest or wealth, the cult views oblivion as a sacred blessing and actively works to release the ancient Old Gods chained beneath the world.",al={"what do cultists want","twilights hammer goal","twilight hammer purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Are they an orc clan or a cult?",a="They began as an orc clan on Draenor led by Cho'gall. After fleeing the Second War, they abandoned clan boundaries and transformed into a secret, multiracial cult, enlisting humans, night elves, dwarves, and anyone willing to worship the Void.",al={"are they orcs","is twilights hammer a clan","twilights hammer races"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Twilight's Hammer?",a="The cult was founded and overseen by Cho'gall, a cunning two-headed ogre-mage and former apprentice of Gul'dan. Local chapters operate under regional commanders, such as Twilight Lord Kelris in Blackfathom Deeps.",al={"who is twilight hammer boss","twilights hammer leader","cho'gall"},it="leader"},{q="Why are they in Blackfathom Deeps?",a="Led by Twilight Lord Kelris, the cultists occupied the sunken night elven temple along the Zoram Strand to worship and feed sacrificial victims to Aku'mai, a primordial three-headed hydra and servant of an Old God.",al={"blackfathom cultists","kelris bfd","what are they doing in ashenvale"},it="place"},{q="What are they doing at the Master's Glaive in Darkshore?",a="The Master's Glaive is a massive glaive embedded in the skull of a colossal creature; the Ancient of Lore Onu says an old, old lord of the earth fell there. Cultists congregate there to commune with its lingering dark energies and seek portents of their masters' awakening.",al={"masters glaive cultists","darkshore twilight hammer"},it="place"},{q="Why did Orgrim Doomhammer try to wipe them out?",a="During the height of the Second War, Cho'gall and Gul'dan abandoned the Horde's march on Lordaeron to seek godhood at the Tomb of Sargeras. Furious at the treachery that cost the Horde its victory, Warchief Doomhammer dispatched loyal troops to slaughter the defecting clans.",al={"doomhammer twilights hammer","second war betrayal"},it="history"},{q="Why are the Twilight's Hammer hostile to outsiders?",a="The Twilight's Hammer serves the Old Gods, seeking the utter annihilation of Azeroth. They view all outside civilizations—Alliance, Horde, and neutral alike—as obstacles to their apocalyptic grand design. Anyone not pledged to their nihilistic cause is an enemy to be eliminated or sacrificed in their dark rituals.",al={"why is twilight's hammer hostile","twilight hammer attack on sight","why do cultists attack me"},it="hostile",add="4ab30bd1"},{q="Who are the Twilight's Hammer working for?",a="Their true masters are the Old Gods, ancient beings of void and shadow imprisoned deep beneath Azeroth. The cult works to shatter those prisons, awakening entities like C'Thun in Silithus. They also revere the servants of these dark masters, such as the hydra Aku'mai in Blackfathom Deeps.",al={"who do twilight hammer serve","twilight hammer masters","who are twilight hammer allied with"},it="allies",add="301a24e8"},{q="Where did the Twilight's Hammer clan originate?",a="They began on Draenor as an orc clan of the Old Horde, led by the two-headed ogre-mage Cho'gall, one of Gul'dan's chief lieutenants. Under him the clan embraced an apocalyptic creed, and it crossed through the Dark Portal into Azeroth with the rest of the Horde.",al={"twilight hammer origin","where did twilight hammer come from","history of twilight's hammer"},it="history",add="23bd082e"},{q="Why is the Twilight's Hammer called that?",a="The name dates back to the clan's days in the Old Horde under Cho'gall. Its origin is not recorded, but it fits a creed bent on bringing the world to its end.",al={"why are they called twilight's hammer","twilight hammer name meaning","origin of twilight hammer name"},it="name",add="74c580c8"}},ang={["class:Priest"]="The Twilight's Hammer manipulates the same Void and shadow magic that shadow priests draw upon, serving as a dark warning of what happens when communion with the whispers overwhelms mortal sanity.",["class:Warlock"]="Though originally affiliated with Gul'dan's Shadow Council, the Twilight's Hammer abandoned demonic fel mastery in favor of the primordial madness of the Old Gods, making them unpredictable rivals rather than allies.",["faction:Horde"]="The cult is a shameful relic of the Old Horde's darkest days. Thrall's new Horde has rejected their madness entirely and actively executes any zealots attempting to seduce young orcs back into nihilism.",["race:NightElf"]="The defilement of holy sites like Blackfathom Deeps and the Master's Glaive by alien cultists represents a direct spiritual and physical assault on the sacred lands of northern Kalimdor."},kw={"aku'mai","blackfathom deeps","cho'gall","kelris","master's glaive","old gods","silithus","twilight's hammer","void"},rel={"npc:twilight-lord-kelris","npc:aku-mai","subzone:master-s-glaive","zone:blackfathom","topic:old-god","zone:silithus","topic:shadow-council","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Twilight's Hammer"}}
E["topic:varian-wrynn"] = {t="topic",n="Varian Wrynn",z="multi",s="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind, currently missing after vanishing en route to a diplomatic summit in Theramore. In his absence, his ten-year-old son Anduin wears the crown, guided by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and Lady Katrana Prestor.",h="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind, currently missing after vanishing en...",sec={{t="A Boyhood in Ash",b="Varian was just a boy when the First War shattered his life. He watched his father, King Llane Wrynn, die by the hand of the assassin Garona Halforcen as the Old Horde stormed the capital. Rescued by Anduin Lothar, the orphaned prince fled north with the kingdom's survivors to Southshore. In Capital City, King Terenas Menethil II took Varian in like a second son. The young prince grew up in exile alongside Prince Arthas Menethil, bearing the memory of a burned home until the Alliance of Lordaeron broke the Horde and reclaimed Stormwind.",sp=0},{t="A Troubled Restoration",b="Crowned at eighteen, Varian took on the immense task of resurrecting his ruined kingdom. To restore the capital, he commissioned the Stonemasons Guild under Edwin VanCleef. But when the grand work was finished, the corrupt House of Nobles refused to pay the promised wages, and the stonemasons refused to back down. Varian found himself paralyzed between the entrenched nobility and the furious artisans. The dispute erupted into violent riots in the streets of Stormwind.",sp=0},{t="The Vanished King",b="Seeking to defuse simmering tensions across the sea, Varian recently departed Stormwind on a private voyage to Theramore Isle for peace talks arranged by Lady Jaina Proudmoore. He never reached the summit. The king disappeared without a trace along the crossing, leaving Stormwind vulnerable, whispers of conspiracy spreading through the court, and the regency in the hands of Highlord Bolvar.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is King Varian right now?",a="Officially, no one in Stormwind knows. Varian vanished while sailing across the Great Sea to Theramore for secret diplomatic talks.",al={"where is varian","where did the king go","is varian alive","varian location"},it="place"},{q="Who is currently ruling Stormwind in his place?",a="His young son, Anduin Wrynn, holds the throne as the child-king. Because Anduin is only ten years old, real administrative and military authority rests with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who acts as regent, heavily influenced by the royal counselor Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"who rules stormwind","who is king now","stormwind leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What troubled Varian's rebuilding of Stormwind?",a="When the Stonemasons Guild finished rebuilding Stormwind, the House of Nobles refused to pay their promised wages. The dispute erupted into violent riots in the city's streets, and the exiled masons later became the Defias Brotherhood.",al={"varian stonemasons","stonemason riots varian","varian and the nobles"},sp=1,bt={"defias.masons"}},{q="Why was Varian going to Theramore?",a="Varian was traveling to Theramore Isle at the invitation of Lady Jaina Proudmoore to attend a high-level diplomatic summit aimed at easing hostilities between the Alliance and Thrall's Horde.",al={"why did varian leave","theramore summit","varian peace talks"},it="purpose"},{q="How did Varian escape the First War?",a="After King Llane was assassinated and the Horde burned Stormwind, High Commander Anduin Lothar evacuated Prince Varian and the remaining civilian survivors by sea. They sailed north to Lordaeron, where King Terenas Menethil II took the young prince into his court.",al={"varian first war","young varian","fall of stormwind varian"},it="history"},{q="What was Varian's relationship with Arthas Menethil?",a="They grew up as close companions and rivals in Lordaeron while Varian lived in exile. The two princes often sparred together. However, Arthas's descent into madness, the murder of his father Terenas, and his betrayal of Lordaeron turned that past friendship into bitter hatred.",al={"did varian know arthas","varian and arthas","were arthas and varian friends"},it="ties"},{q="Who is Varian Wrynn?",a="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind and the heir of the House of Wrynn. As a boy, he witnessed the fall of his kingdom and the assassination of his father, King Llane, during the First War. Exiled to Lordaeron, he returned years later after the Alliance reclaimed his homeland, taking the throne at eighteen to rebuild Stormwind. Recently, however, the king vanished under mysterious circumstances while traveling on a diplomatic voyage, leaving the kingdom in turmoil.",al={"who is varian","king varian","king of stormwind","who is king wrynn"},it="identity",add="4f50c804"},{q="Who are Varian Wrynn's allies?",a="Varian stands at the heart of the Grand Alliance. His closest counselors and protectors include Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who rules as regent during his absence, and the royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor. His greatest historical bonds lie with the kingdoms of the Alliance of Lordaeron, fostered by King Terenas Menethil II, who fostered young Varian after Stormwind's fall. As Stormwind's king, he commands its armies and knights.",al={"varian allies","who does varian work with","varian wrynn faction"},it="allies",add="4a21f8ea"}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, Varian is the beloved son of King Llane who returned from exile to rebuild their home; his sudden disappearance casts a shadow of dread over the entire realm.",["class:Warrior"]="Trained by veteran knights and hardened by the horrors of the First War, King Varian is admired across the Alliance as a formidable warrior and a fierce martial leader.",["faction:Alliance"]="Varian's absence leaves the Alliance without its strongest southern monarch, creating a political vacuum that emboldens dissidents and threatens border security from Westfall to Redridge."},kw={"anduin","king","missing","stonemasons","stormwind","theramore","varian","wrynn"},rel={"zone:stormwind","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:house-of-nobles","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:terenas-menethil-ii"},src={"Varian Wrynn"}}
E["topic:venture-company"] = {t="topic",n="Venture Company",z="general",s="The Venture Company is a notoriously ruthless goblin cartel focused on aggressive resource extraction, heavy industrial machinery, and logging. Unaligned with the major goblin ports like Booty Bay or Ratchet, the cartel strip-mines and clear-cuts territories across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms without regard for local populations or the natural world.",h="The Venture Company is a notoriously ruthless goblin cartel focused on aggressive resource extraction, heavy...",sec={{t="Industrial Plunder",b="Led overall by Mogul Razdunk from afar, the Venture Company operates by a simple corporate philosophy: if it can be chopped down, drilled into, or blasted out of a mountain, they will exploit it. The cartel deploys some of the most advanced heavy machinery on Azeroth, including massive lumber-harvesting shredders, drilling rigs, and industrial refineries. Unlike traditional merchant houses that focus on shipping or retail, the Venture Company specializes in seizing raw resources by force, ignoring treaties, borders, and local sovereign rights.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching on Kalimdor",b="The cartel's rapid expansion into Kalimdor has earned them widespread enmity. In Mulgore, Supervisor Fizsprocket pushed drilling and mining rigs into the eastern bluffs despite being formally denied mineral rights by the tauren, polluting pristine water wells across the plains. Farther north, the company turned the Barrens' Sludge Fen into a chemical sewer to power oil pumps, while claiming the Horde's former Boulder Lode Mine and running foul experiments with samophlanges and toxic brews. In Stonetalon's Windshear Crag, logging operations under the mad leper gnome Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle have decimated whole groves with colossal Super Reaper 6000 machines.",sp=1},{t="Corporate Rivalries and Shady Contracts",b="The Venture Company is widely despised even by fellow goblins. Baron Revilgaz of Booty Bay frequently contracts out strikes against their base camp and oil rig at Lake Nazferiti, seeking both to thwart their territorial encroachment and steal their cutting-edge shredder fuel regulators. The Tinkers' Union in Ratchet openly runs corporate espionage against them to pilfer engineering blueprints. Worse still, SI:7 and the Shattered Hand have discovered the company trafficking dangerous alchemical compounds: selling Zanzil's deadly mind-altering poisons to Defias necromancers in Westfall and utilizing toxic fumes to breed mutated drone laborers.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who leads the Venture Company?",a="The president of the cartel is Mogul Razdunk, who governs the organization's overarching corporate ventures from afar. Day-to-day work sites across Azeroth are overseen by regional supervisors, foremen, and hired overseers such as Supervisor Fizsprocket in Mulgore, Supervisor Lugwizzle at the Sludge Fen, and Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle in Stonetalon.",al={"venture co leader","venture company boss","who runs venture co","mogul razdunk"},it="leader"},{q="Why does the Horde hate the Venture Company?",a="The cartel operates illegal, aggressive resource sites on ancestral Horde lands. In Mulgore, they invaded the eastern mountains without permission and fouled the sacred water wells of the tauren. In the Barrens, they seized the Horde's Boulder Lode Mine and polluted the Sludge Fen into a noxious oil slick.",al={"tauren venture co","why horde hate venture co","mulgore venture company"},it="conflict"},{q="Are they affiliated with the Steamwheedle Cartel or Booty Bay?",a="No. The Venture Company is a separate, predatory organization. They are fierce corporate enemies of the Steamwheedle Cartel; Baron Revilgaz of Booty Bay frequently hires mercenaries to disrupt their logging in Stranglethorn Vale, and Sputtervalve of Ratchet regularly sends operatives to sabotage their shredders and steal their blueprints.",al={"are they steamwheedle","venture co booty bay","ratchet venture co"},it="ties"},{q="Why is a leper gnome running operations in Windshear Crag?",a="Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle was hired by the Venture Company for his ruthless mechanical genius, designing the massive Super Reaper 6000 logging machine. His hiring caused an uproar among the cartel's goblin engineers, such as Ziz Fizziks, and even angered the Trade Princes in Undermine, who disapproved of Razdunk consorting with the outcast gnome.",al={"gerenzo wrenchwhistle","gnome in stonetalon","why leper gnome venture"}},{q="What is the Venture Company doing in Westfall?",a="The Venture Company had an illicit trading arrangement with the Defias Brotherhood. They acquired Zanzilli poisons and sold the mixture to Defias operative Klaven Mortwake in Westfall, who used the toxic concoctions to manufacture mindless Defias Drones similar to the laborers found in Venture Company operations.",al={"venture co westfall","klaven mortwake venture co","venture defias"},sp=1,it="why_here"},{q="What is being produced at the Sludge Fen?",a="The cartel erected an industrial drilling rig directly atop what used to be a clear natural spring, pumping and refining oil while dumping toxic sludge across the northern Barrens. In addition to oil, researchers there conduct tests on complex mechanical samophlanges and synthesize toxic mixtures.",al={"sludge fen oil","barrens venture co oil rig","samophlange sludge fen"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a ruthless, goblin-run cartel dedicated to plundering Azeroth's natural resources for maximum profit. Specializing in heavy machinery, mining, and clear-cutting, their philosophy is simple: if you can dig it up, chop it down, or blow it out of a mountain, they will. They operate entirely outside local laws and traditional ethics, using shredders, drills, and armed enforcers to extract wealth from wilderness frontiers.",al={"who are venture co","what is venture company","venture co lore"},it="identity",add="f7d6a0aa"},{q="Why is the Venture Company hostile to travelers?",a="The Venture Company operates illegal, aggressive resource grabs across contested and sovereign lands, from the sacred hills of Mulgore to the forests of Stonetalon. Because they routinely trespass, steal claims, and cut out competitors, their operations are heavily fortified. Their foremen view any outsider as an industrial spy, a rival claimant, or a vengeful local come to sabotage their machinery, and they instruct their enforcers to shoot first.",al={"why does venture co attack","venture company aggro","why is venture company hostile"},it="hostile",add="36300444"},{q="Who are the Venture Company allied with?",a="The Venture Company primarily works for its own profit, but it frequently cuts shady deals with outside factions when coin is on the table. They have established under-the-table trade with the Defias Brotherhood, selling them Zanzil-derived alchemical mixtures and toxic concoctions. Aside from such mercenary transactions, they maintain few friends, routinely stepping on the toes of neutral cartels like the Steamwheedle Cartel and drawing outrage from the trade princes in Undermine.",al={"venture co allies","who does venture company work with","venture co partnerships"},it="allies",add="48d27ccb"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Venture Company invaded the sacred hills of Mulgore without permission, poisoning ancestral water wells and clear-cutting timber in defiance of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="Their heavy clear-cutting in Stonetalon Mountains and industrial pollution of natural springs in Mulgore and the Barrens represent blatant, unchecked desecration of the land.",["class:Rogue"]="Both the Shattered Hand and SI:7 keep close watch on the cartel's poison trafficking, infiltrating their towers and sabotaging their high-level foremen to acquire their deadly chemical mixtures.",["profession:Engineering"]="The cartel manufactures some of the finest, most advanced shredders and mechanical regulators on Azeroth, making their blueprints prized targets for corporate theft."},kw={"fizsprocket","gerenzo","puzik gallywix","razdunk","samophlange","shredder","sludge fen","super reaper","venture co","venture company","windshear crag"},rel={"zone:stonetalon","zone:barrens","zone:mulgore","zone:stranglethorn","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:goblin","quest:764","quest:1093"},src={"Venture Company"}}
E["topic:war-of-the-ancients"] = {t="topic",n="War of the Ancients",z="general",s="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless sorcery of Queen Azshara and her Highborne tore a portal through the Well of Eternity, unleashing the demonic Burning Legion onto Azeroth. Led by the young druid Malfurion Stormrage, his brother Illidan, and Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, a desperate coalition of night elves, dragons, and ancient forest demigods repelled the invaders. The resulting destruction of the Well triggered the Great Sundering, violently shattering the ancient continent of Kalimdor and permanently altering the face of the world.",h="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless sorcery of Queen Azshara and her Highborne tore a portal through the...",sec={{t="The Highborne's Folly",b="At the height of the ancient Kaldorei Empire, Queen Azshara and her elite caste of sorcerers, the Highborne, relentlessly drew upon the vast arcane depths of the Well of Eternity. Their reckless manipulation sent massive ripples into the Great Dark Beyond, attracting Sargeras, the master of the Burning Legion. Entranced by promises of ultimate power and perfection, Azshara and her advisor Xavius opened a trans-dimensional rift within the Well. Legions of felhounds, felguard, doomguard, and monstrous commanders poured into the capital city of Zin-Azshari, slaughtering all non-Highborne and beginning their crusade to purge the world of life.",sp=0},{t="The Kaldorei Resistance",b="Horrified by the slaughter, night elves outside the Highborne caste rallied to resist. The resistance was led by remarkable heroes: the first mortal druid Malfurion Stormrage, the devout priestess of Elune Tyrande Whisperwind, and Malfurion's gifted brother Illidan. Together they gathered the common night elves into a desperate frontline against the Legion's onslaught.",sp=0},{t="The Price of Victory",b="The war escalated to world-shattering proportions when the demigod Cenarius rallied the ancient Wild Gods and dragonflights into the fray. Determined to stop Sargeras himself from stepping foot onto Azeroth, Malfurion and the resistance made a desperate push to collapse the portal over the Well of Eternity. Their desperate gambit succeeded in casting the demonic host back into the Twisting Nether, but the strain proved too great. The Well violently imploded, triggering the Great Sundering that shattered Kalimdor into separate continents and flooded the center of the world, birthing the Great Sea and forever marking Kaldorei culture.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What caused the War of the Ancients?",a="Queen Azshara and her Highborne sorcerers relentlessly experimented with the arcane energies of the Well of Eternity. Their spells sent waves across the cosmos that attracted the dark titan Sargeras, who convinced the Highborne to open a gateway within the Well so the Burning Legion could enter Azeroth.",al={"why did the war start","who caused the war of the ancients","well of eternity portal"},it="origin"},{q="What was the Great Sundering?",a="The Great Sundering was the cataclysm that ended the war. To stop Sargeras from entering the world, the resistance collapsed the portal inside the Well of Eternity. The backlash caused the Well to violently implode, tearing the single supercontinent of Kalimdor apart into separate landmasses and forming the Great Sea.",al={"what is the sundering","how did kalimdor break","well of eternity explosion"},it="conflict"},{q="Who led the mortal forces against the Legion?",a="The young druid Malfurion Stormrage, his brother Illidan, and the priestess Tyrande Whisperwind rallied the night elves who stood against Azshara and the Legion, while the demigod Cenarius brought the ancient spirits of the wild to their side.",al={"who led the resistance","kaldorei resistance leaders"},it="leader"},{q="Did other races fight alongside the night elves?",a="Yes. The great dragonflights joined the struggle, and the woodland demigod Cenarius led his wild brethren into battle against the demonic invasion alongside the night elves.",al={"tauren in war of the ancients","who helped the night elves"},it="allies"},{q="What happened to Queen Azshara during the war?",a="Azshara remained inside her palace at Zin-Azshari until the bitter end, obsessed with bringing Sargeras into Azeroth. When the Well of Eternity imploded and the oceans swallowed the capital, the queen and her surviving Highborne loyalists sank into the churning depths.",al={"where is azshara","did queen azshara die"}},{q="Why is arcane magic mistrusted by night elves today?",a="The Kaldorei witnessed firsthand how the Highborne's unchecked thirst for arcane magic summoned the Burning Legion and obliterated ancient Kalimdor. In the aftermath, the night elves strictly outlawed arcane sorcery, turning instead to druidism and faith in Elune to protect their lands.",al={"why do night elves hate mages","kaldorei magic ban"},it="today"},{q="What was the War of the Ancients?",a="The War of the Ancients was Azeroth's first major war against the demonic Burning Legion, fought roughly ten thousand years ago. Corrupted by promises of godlike power, Queen Azshara and her Highborne cast spells through the Well of Eternity to bring Sargeras and his demonic legions into the world. The night elf resistance, joined by early mortal races, the dragonflights, and the Wild Gods, fought desperately to halt the invasion, culminating in the destruction of the Well and the Great Sundering.",al={"what is war of the ancients","war of the ancients explained","first legion invasion"},it="identity",add="c481acb5"},{q="Why is it called the War of the Ancients?",a="The conflict earned its name both from the ancient era in which it took place—ten millennia before the modern age—and the pivotal role played by the Ancients themselves. Demigods and Wild Gods like Cenarius, alongside the ancient dragonflights, marched to war alongside mortal defenders to save Azeroth from demonic annihilation.",al={"why called war of the ancients","name war of the ancients meaning","war of the ancients name"},it="name",add="9e83d11f"}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The war shaped every aspect of modern Kaldorei society, explaining their deep mistrust of arcane magic, their reverence for Cenarius, and the enduring leadership of Malfurion and Tyrande.",["class:Druid"]="The conflict marks the birth of mortal druidism, when Malfurion Stormrage learned the ways of the wild from Cenarius and proved that the power of nature could defeat cosmic ruin.",["class:Mage"]="Highborne reckless arrogance during the war serves as the ultimate historical warning across Azeroth about the catastrophic dangers of unchecked arcane draw.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren who honor Cenarius and the demigods of the wild remember that these same forest spirits fought the Legion alongside the night elves ten millennia ago.",["class:Priest"]="Tyrande Whisperwind's mastery of Elune's grace during the war elevated the Priesthood of the Moon into the heart of night elven military and civic leadership."},kw={"azshara","burning legion","cenarius","highborne","malfurion","sargeras","sundering","tyrande","war of the ancients","well of eternity"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","npc:malfurion-stormrage","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:sargeras","zone:azshara"},src={"War of the Ancients"}}
E["topic:warlock"] = {t="topic",n="Warlock",z="general",s="Across Azeroth, Warlocks wield forbidden powers that bring fear and distrust. They command demons and cast curses, fire, and shadow magic. Some turn these arts against the Burning Legion and its servants.",h="Across Azeroth, Warlocks wield forbidden powers that bring fear and distrust.",sec={{t="Forbidden Arts",b="Where ordinary spellcasters see ruin, warlocks see a path to victory. They strike from afar with shadow and fire, and drain the vital essence and souls of their enemies to fuel their fiercest rites. Brimstone falls from the sky, and agonizing curses break the mind.\n\nWarlocks wear simple cloth and have little strength to withstand a blow. They call horrors to meet danger and sacrifice demonic thralls to preserve their own lives.",sp=0},{t="Demonic Servitude",b="A warlock's hold over the Twisting Nether begins with small bargains and grows into iron dominion. Novices command mischievous imps to hurl flame. With deeper knowledge of fel magic, they bind voidwalkers from shadowy depths to bear physical punishment, succubi who beguile enemy minds, and ravenous felhunters that devour magical energy.\n\nEach summon demands dangerous focus. The Nether's denizens are ever testing their masters for weakness.",sp=0},{t="A Legacy of Ruin",b="Orcish demonology began on Draenor, where Kil'jaeden, a demon lord, tempted Gul'dan with the promise of supreme power. Gul'dan founded the secretive Shadow Council and turned many shaman into masters of fel magic. Demonic magic withered Draenor's fertile plains into a barren red wasteland, and the Old Horde invaded through the Dark Portal.\n\nAmong humans, the Kirin Tor forbade demonology and hunted illicit summonings. Yet ambitious mages still fell to the lure of untamed sorcery.",sp=0},{t="Tolerated Pariahs",b="Warlocks now walk a narrow path between usefulness and execution. In Stormwind, they meet in hidden, gloomy tavern basements, away from the Cathedral of Light's watchful eyes. In Orgrimmar, they gather in the shadowed chasms of the Cleft of Shadow, watched by Thrall's shamanistic regime.\n\nTheir methods inspire revulsion, yet neither the Alliance nor the Horde can dismiss the edge warlock magic brings to a hostile battlefield.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes a warlock different from a mage?",a="Mages use the ordered structure of arcane magic to shape fire, frost, and energy. Warlocks cross its safe bounds for chaotic fel magic, shadow, and demons. Many began as mages, then grew impatient with academic limits and sought forbidden shortcuts to power.",al={"warlock vs mage","difference between warlock and mage","mage or warlock"}},{q="Are all warlocks evil?",a="Not every warlock is wicked, though warlock magic is dangerous and corruptive. Many fall into madness or join demonic cults. Others pledge loyalty to their faction and wield dark sorcery, stolen souls, and bound demons against threats such as the Scourge and the Burning Legion.",al={"are warlocks evil","can warlocks be good","why does the alliance allow warlocks"}},{q="How do warlocks summon and control demons?",a="Warlocks draw creatures from the Twisting Nether with ritual circles, dark incantations, and soul energy. They must break each demon's will and bind it through sheer mental dominance. If their focus slips, the demon may slaughter its master.",al={"how do warlock pets work","demonic summoning lore","binding demons"}},{q="Where did orc warlocks come from?",a="Orc warlocks began on Draenor, when Kil'jaeden turned Gul'dan from shamanism to demonic sorcery. Gul'dan formed the Shadow Council and trained other orcs. Fel corruption spread across their homeland until Draenor's soil withered and died.",al={"how did orcs become warlocks","gul'dan warlocks","orc fel magic origin"}},{q="Why do the Alliance and Horde tolerate warlocks in their cities?",a="Neither faction trusts warlocks. In Stormwind and Ironforge, they practice in secret basements and forgotten tunnels to avoid public outrage. In Orgrimmar, they are kept in the Cleft of Shadow. Leaders tolerate them because their curses and summons are effective weapons in wartime.",al={"why are warlocks allowed","are warlocks legal","warlock legality"}},{q="What are soul shards used for?",a="Warlocks drain the living essence of defeated enemies to make crystallized soul shards. These fragments hold the raw power to bypass natural magical laws, summon monstrous demons, and cast their most destructive spells.",al={"why do warlocks take souls","soul shard lore","harvesting souls"}},{q="Which races can be warlocks?",a="Humans and gnomes practice as warlocks in the Alliance. In the Horde, orcs, trolls, and the Forsaken do. Dwarves, night elves, tauren, and the Skyborne do not. Wherever they live, warlocks are tolerated rather than trusted.",al={"who can be a warlock","warlock races","can trolls be warlocks","troll warlock"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="As a mage, you may view warlocks with contempt and fear. Their dealings with the Nether seem undisciplined and put all mortal life at risk.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you remember how Gul'dan's fel sorcery severed your ancestors' ties to the spirits and killed Draenor's land. That is why shaman despise warlock magic above all else.",["race:Orc"]="As an orc, you bear the bitter stain of the Old Horde's blood curse when you practice demonology. Some orcs keep these arts to survive in a hostile world.",["class:Paladin"]="As a paladin, you consider demonic summoning an abominable heresy. You watch warlocks who practice it with open hostility.",["race:Troll"]="As a troll, you begin warlock training beside orc initiates in the Valley of Trials. Thrall's Horde watches every wielder of fel magic with the same wariness."},kw={"affliction","demonology","destruction","fel","felhunter","gul'dan","imp","shadow council","succubus","twisting nether","voidwalker","warlock"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","topic:shadow-council","topic:first-war","topic:blackrock-clan","topic:mage","topic:shaman"},src={"Warlock"}}
E["topic:warrior"] = {t="topic",n="Warrior",z="general",s="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, mastering heavy plate armor, shields, and nearly every form of martial weaponry. Fueling their most devastating strikes with pure combat rage, they charge into the thick of battle to defend weaker allies or carve through enemy ranks. They are found across every race and civilization, serving as everything from stalwart city guards and royal champions to wandering mercenaries.",h="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, mastering heavy plate armor, shields, and...",sec={{t="Masters of Steel and Shield",b="Warriors rely neither on divine prayers nor arcane incantations. Their power stems entirely from physical endurance, battlefield discipline, and an exhaustive mastery of weaponry. Clad in the heaviest plate mail, they dictate the frontline. Some fight behind towering shields to withstand punishing assaults and draw peril away from spellcasters and archers. Others step forward with two-handed polearms, axes, and greatswords, or wade into melee wielding a blade in each hand to cleave through whole formations.",sp=0},{t="The Power of Battle Rage",b="In the furnace of battle, pain and blood ignite a warrior's inner fire. Known simply as rage, this resource swells as warriors inflict and absorb punishment. Rather than yielding to exhaustion or bleeding wounds, seasoned fighters channel this combat fury directly into bone-shattering strikes, sweeping whirlwinds of steel, and deafening battle cries that bolster nearby comrades while shattering enemy morale.",sp=0},{t="A Universal Tradition",b="Unlike classes tied to specific creeds or elemental patrons, the warrior tradition is universal to every race on Azeroth. Stormwind relies on its disciplined knights and footmen, Ironforge marshals its stout mountain guard, and the Horde rallies around orcish blademasters, tauren braves, and Forsaken Deathguards. Whether driven by honor, territorial defense, or cold coin, those who answer the call of arms form the unbroken backbone of every standing army.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What powers a warrior if they do not use magic?",a="Warriors rely on martial skill, tactical training, and rage. Rage builds directly from the clash of combat—both by striking enemies and absorbing incoming blows. This surging combat adrenaline fuels their most devastating maneuvers, such as sweeping weapon strikes, concussive shield bashes, and intimidating shouts.",al={"how do warriors fight","warrior resource","what is rage"},it="power"},{q="What races can become warriors?",a="Every playable race in Azeroth can be a warrior. The need for heavily armored frontline soldiers exists across all mortal cultures, from the stoic dwarven defenders of Khaz Modan to the savage orcish shock troops of Durotar.",al={"who can be warrior","warrior races","can every race be warrior"},it="people"},{q="What role do warriors serve in a combat party?",a="Warriors typically lead from the front. Stalwart protection warriors use shields and plate armor to absorb punishment and keep dangerous foes focused on them. Offensive arms and fury warriors instead bring crushing two-handed weapons or paired blades to overwhelm enemies through brutal physical damage.",al={"warrior role","do warriors tank","arms vs fury vs prot"},it="purpose"},{q="What weapons are warriors able to wield?",a="Warriors are masters of almost every weapon known to mortal warfare. They can wield swords, maces, axes, polearms, daggers, and staves, in both one-handed and two-handed grips, as well as heavy shields and ranged weapons like bows, guns, and crossbows.",al={"warrior weapons","can warriors use bows","what can warriors equip"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who are some famous warriors in Azeroth's history?",a="Renowned warriors include Lord Anduin Lothar, the Champion of Stormwind and Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron; Grommash Hellscream, chieftain of the Warsong clan; and Cairne Bloodhoof, High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes.",al={"famous warriors","legendary warriors","who are warrior heroes"},it="fame"},{q="Where do new warriors receive their formal training?",a="Warriors train anywhere soldiers gather. Human recruits drill in Northshire Abbey, dwarves and gnomes learn at Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley, night elves train in Shadowglen, orcs and trolls practice in the Valley of Trials, Forsaken fighters report to the Deathknell barracks, and the Skyborne begin on Zephras Isle.",al={"warrior trainers","where do warriors learn","warrior trainer locations"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is a warrior?",a="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, relying on sheer physical prowess, mastery of arms, and heavy armor rather than mystical arts. They come from every culture and background, serving as stalwart city guards, fearsome berserkers, mercenary soldiers, and seasoned commanders. Equipped with shields and a vast array of weaponry, warriors charge directly into the fray to draw the enemy's wrath and shield their more fragile companions from harm.",al={"what is a warrior in lore","warrior lore identity","who are warriors"},it="identity",add="2a49f247",nx={8}},{q="What does it mean to walk the path of a warrior?",a="Choosing the warrior's life means dedicating yourself to constant training, discipline, and uncompromising martial perfection. You step onto the battlefield without spells to shield you, putting your faith entirely in heavy plate, your chosen steel, and your instincts. Whether seeking glory, fortune as a mercenary, or defending your people from looming perils, you are the living wall that meets danger head-on, turning raw combat fury into decisive strikes.",al={"becoming a warrior","what does it mean to be a warrior","life of a warrior"},it="for_you",add="2e549509"}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Every battle you fight tests your stamina and nerve; by turning the pain of incoming blows into pure rage, you control the front line.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Warriors rely entirely on blacksmiths to hammer out the heavy plate mail, sturdy bucklers, and honed blades necessary to survive mortal combat.",["faction:Alliance"]="The footmen and knights of the Alliance form the defensive vanguard protecting human kingdoms, dwarven mountain holds, and elven forests.",["faction:Horde"]="For the Horde, martial prowess is a way of life, with grunt shock troops and tauren warriors leading every territorial advance."},kw={"arms","berserker","fury","melee","plate","protection","rage","shield","vanguard","warrior"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:alliance","topic:horde","topic:tauren","topic:second-war","topic:third-war","subzone:anvilmar"},src={"Warrior"}}
E["topic:warsong-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Warsong clan",z="general",s="The Warsong clan is one of the most ferocious and legendary fighting forces among the orcs, formerly led by the revered chieftain Grommash Hellscream. Having survived exile, the internment camps, and demonic corruption, the clan now operates primarily in Ashenvale as the Warsong Outriders, fiercely securing lumber for the fledgling Horde while clashing ceaselessly with the night elves.",h="The Warsong clan is one of the most ferocious and legendary fighting forces among the orcs, formerly led by...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Defiance",b="Long before crossing into Azeroth, the Warsong forged their reputation across the open plains of Draenor through mobile wolf-rider tactics. After the Horde's defeat, while other orcs languished in Alliance internment camps, the Warsong evaded capture, wandering the wilderness and clinging to their warlike spirit until Thrall united with Hellscream to forge the New Horde.",sp=0},{t="Blood and Redemption",b="Upon arriving in Kalimdor during the Third War, Thrall ordered Hellscream and his clan into Ashenvale to harvest timber and establish an outpost. The intrusion enraged the night elves and the forest demigod Cenarius. Pushed to the brink of slaughter, the Warsong succumbed once more to the pit lord Mannoroth's demonic blood to prevail, slaying Cenarius in a frenzy of fel corruption. Thrall and his allies managed to purge Hellscream of the curse, culminating in a fateful showdown where Grom gave his life to destroy Mannoroth. His heroic sacrifice broke the demon blood curse forever, cementing the Warsong clan's place in Horde history.",sp=0},{t="The War for Ashenvale",b="In the present day, the clan continues Grommash's mission under the banner of the Warsong Outriders. From strongholds like Splintertree Post and active logging encampments, they harvest timber crucial for building the young Horde nation in the arid wastes of Durotar. Their presence has sparked a bitter territorial war with the night elven Silverwing Sentinels. Driven by their ancestral zeal, the Warsong show no sign of relinquishing Warsong Gulch, cutting deep into the ancient forest despite endless ambushes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads the Warsong clan right now?",a="Following the death of Grommash Hellscream at the end of the Third War, the Warsong clan is largely integrated into Thrall's New Horde. Their operational forces in the field, particularly the Warsong Outriders in Ashenvale, answer to local commanders and the overall authority of Warchief Thrall.",al={"warsong leader","who is warsong chief","current warsong chieftain"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Warsong logging in Ashenvale?",a="The New Horde is based in the arid plains of Durotar and the Barrens, lands barren of substantial timber. The Warsong were sent into the rich forests of Ashenvale to gather lumber essential for constructing cities like Orgrimmar, maintaining military fortifications, and sustaining the expanding Horde.",al={"why harvest ashenvale","warsong lumber","orcs cutting trees ashenvale"},it="why_here"},{q="How did the Warsong kill Cenarius?",a="During the Third War, the demigod Cenarius led the night elves in a brutal defense of Ashenvale, driving the Warsong into desperation. The clan drank from a pool tainted with the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth, transforming into demon-maddened chaos orcs whose empowered fury was potent enough to overwhelm and slay Cenarius.",al={"cenarius death","who killed cenarius","warsong vs cenarius"}},{q="How did the Warsong escape the internment camps after the Second War?",a="Unlike many orc clans that were captured when the Second War ended, Grommash Hellscream and the Warsong evaded Alliance capture by hiding in the remote wilderness of Lordaeron. They waged guerrilla warfare and maintained their fighting spirit until Thrall found them.",al={"warsong internment camps","grom evasion","warsong escape"}},{q="What is Warsong Gulch?",a="Warsong Gulch is a canyon valley in southern Ashenvale where the Warsong Outriders clash directly with the night elven Silverwing Sentinels. The Outriders fight to secure their lumber operations, while the Sentinels battle to drive the orcs completely out of their sacred woodland.",al={"wsg lore","what is warsong gulch","warsong battlefield"}},{q="Who was Grommash Hellscream?",a="Grommash 'Grom' Hellscream was the legendary chieftain of the Warsong clan and a mentor to Thrall. Though he was the first orc to drink the demon blood on Draenor, he ultimately redeemed himself and freed all orcs from demonic bondage by slaying the pit lord Mannoroth, sacrificing his life in the deed.",al={"who is grom","grom hellscream","grommash"}},{q="What is the Warsong clan?",a="The Warsong clan is a fiercely martial orcish clan renowned for its aggressive nature and mobile wolf riders. Long led by the legendary chieftain Grom Hellscream, the clan now serves as an integral pillar of Thrall's Horde, spearheading logging and military campaigns in the dense forests of Ashenvale.",al={"who are the warsong clan","who are the warsong","warsong clan info"},it="identity",add="ab40120e"},{q="Where did the Warsong clan come from?",a="The Warsong originally hailed from the plains of Draenor, where they earned renown as fierce, mobile wolf riders, and crossed into Azeroth with the Horde under Grom Hellscream. Evading human internment camps after the Second War, they later followed Thrall across the Great Sea to Kalimdor.",al={"warsong clan history","warsong origin","where are the warsong from"},it="history",add="b8b233bd"},{q="Who is the Warsong clan allied with?",a="The Warsong clan is fully pledged to Thrall's New Horde. They fight shoulder-to-shoulder with their fellow orcs, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren of Mulgore, and the newly admitted Forsaken, harvesting vital lumber to supply the Horde's growing settlements across Durotar and the Barrens.",al={"warsong allies","who do the warsong fight for","warsong faction"},it="allies",add="f1dd8797"},{q="Why is the Warsong clan hostile to Alliance travelers?",a="As a central military force of the Horde, the Warsong clan views members of the Alliance as mortal enemies. Their deep-seated hatred is further inflamed in Ashenvale, where night elven Silverwing Sentinels and their allies fiercely contest the clan's aggressive clear-cutting of the sacred ancient woodlands.",al={"why do warsong attack","why are warsong hostile","are warsong enemy"},it="hostile",add="5b3f6a6c"}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The Warsong represent both the greatest shame and highest redemption of the orcish people: the first to drink the blood of Mannoroth, but also the clan whose chieftain died to shatter the demon's hold over all orcs.",["race:NightElf"]="The Warsong are viewed with intense hatred by the night elves, not only for deforesting sacred woodlands in Ashenvale, but for the unforgivable slaughter of the demigod Cenarius.",["faction:Horde"]="Without the lumber harvested by the Warsong Outriders in Ashenvale, the New Horde would lack the raw materials necessary to fortify its settlements and expand its holdings in Kalimdor.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Warsong embody the unrelenting aggression of the orcs, proving to many in the Alliance that peace between the two factions remains impossible so long as their axes swing in Ashenvale."},kw={"ashenvale","cenarius","grommash","hellscream","mannoroth","outriders","splintertree","warsong"},rel={"topic:grommash-hellscream","zone:ashenvale","npc:thrall","topic:cenarius","topic:new-horde","npc:warsong-outrider","subzone:splintertree-post","topic:sentinels"},src={"Warsong clan"}}
E["topic:wildhammer-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Wildhammer clan",z="general",s="The Wildhammer clan is a rugged, nature-dwelling branch of the dwarven race famed for taming wild gryphons and wielding storm-charged hammers. Driven from their ancestral fortress of Grim Batol centuries ago, they carved a fiercely independent home across the wooded peaks of the Hinterlands. Though reclusive by nature, they remain stalwart friends of the Alliance, defending the skies whenever called upon.",h="The Wildhammer clan is a rugged, nature-dwelling branch of the dwarven race famed for taming wild gryphons...",sec={{t="Exile from Grim Batol",b="Before the War of the Three Hammers, the Wildhammers lived in the foothills around Ironforge. When High King Modimus Anvilmar died, civil war fractured the dwarven kingdom, and the victorious Bronzebeards claimed Ironforge for themselves. Thane Khardros led his clan north to establish a magnificent hold inside the peak of Grim Batol. Yet peace was short-lived: the defeated Dark Irons soon laid siege to the fortress. While Khardros personally slew the Dark Iron sorceress Modgud, her dying curse blighted the mountain so deeply that the halls became uninhabitable. Refusing an invitation to move back under Bronzebeard rule, the Wildhammers chose self-reliance, marching north into the forests and rocky crags of the Hinterlands.",sp=0},{t="Riders of the Storm",b="In the lush highlands of their new realm, the Wildhammers built Aerie Peak. Unlike their subterranean cousins, these dwarves embraced the open sky and living rock, developing a deep, spiritual kinship with the native gryphons. Forging enchanted stormhammers in secluded aviaries, the Wildhammers became the finest aerial cavalry in Azeroth. During the Second War, when Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer pushed his forces toward Quel'Thalas and assaulted Aerie Peak, Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and the Alliance came to their aid. In gratitude, High Thane Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his gryphon riders to the coalition, dogfighting the Horde's enslaved red dragons across the skies of Lordaeron and the Great Sea.",sp=0},{t="Loyal to the End",b="When the Dark Portal reopened, Kurdran Wildhammer led his riders into Draenor with the Alliance Expedition, sacrificing his return to seal the rift—an act commemorated by a colossal statue in Stormwind's Valley of Heroes. Back in Aerie Peak, High Thane Falstad stepped up to lead the clan. Even as the human kingdoms squabbled and fractured in later years, the Wildhammers remained steadfast partners of King Terenas Menethil II. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron in the Third War, Wildhammer flyers took to the air once more, fighting desperately to protect allied lands and innocent refugees from the walking dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Wildhammer clan based right now?",a="Their primary bastion is Aerie Peak, located in the mountainous forest of the Hinterlands, north of the Wetlands and the Thandol Span.",al={"wildhammer home","where do wildhammers live","wildhammer location"},it="place"},{q="Why don't the Wildhammers live in Ironforge?",a="Following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar, the Wildhammer clan lost the civil war for control of Ironforge to the Bronzebeards. Thane Khardros led his clan north to build a new life in Grim Batol rather than bow to Bronzebeard rule.",al={"why did wildhammer leave ironforge","war of the three hammers wildhammer"},it="why_here"},{q="What happened to their fortress at Grim Batol?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Iron sorceress Modgud laid siege to Grim Batol. Thane Khardros slew her, but her dark sorcery infected the halls with foul corruption, making the mountain completely uninhabitable and forcing the clan into the Hinterlands.",al={"why is grim batol abandoned","grim batol curse","modgud grim batol"},it="ruin"},{q="Who leads the Wildhammer clan today?",a="High Thane Falstad Wildhammer leads the clan from Aerie Peak. He took command after his predecessor, Kurdran Wildhammer, joined the Alliance Expedition through the Dark Portal.",al={"wildhammer leader","high thane wildhammer","who rules aerie peak"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Wildhammers join the Alliance?",a="During the Second War, Orgrim Doomhammer attacked the Hinterlands while marching toward Quel'Thalas. Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar led the Alliance to defend Aerie Peak, driving off the Horde and forest trolls. Moved by their bravery, the Wildhammers joined the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"why are wildhammers alliance","wildhammer alliance history"},it="allies"},{q="Are the Wildhammer dwarves closely allied with Ironforge?",a="Yes. While the two clans fought during the War of the Three Hammers, they united to defeat the Dark Irons. To honor their friendship, they constructed the Valley of Kings in Loch Modan, and they have maintained a firm alliance ever since.",al={"bronzebeard wildhammer relationship","do ironforge and wildhammer get along"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Wildhammer clan?",a="The Wildhammer clan is one of the three great dwarven peoples of Azeroth, renowned for their close bond with nature, their independence, and their fearless aerial mastery. Unlike their Bronzebeard cousins who prefer subterranean halls, the Wildhammers dwell openly among high peaks and lush forests. Renowned across the world as peerless gryphon riders, they take to the skies wielding mystic, lightning-infused stormhammers to defend their homelands and aid their long-standing allies.",al={"who are the wildhammers","wildhammer clan overview","wildhammer dwarves"},it="identity",add="eadee6c6"},{q="Where did the Wildhammer clan come from?",a="Long ago, the Wildhammers lived in the crags and foothills around Ironforge under High King Modimus Anvilmar. Following his death, the civil strife of the War of the Three Hammers erupted. Banished from Ironforge by the victorious Bronzebeards, Thane Khardros led his clan north to carve out the grand fortress of Grim Batol. After surviving a harrowing siege by the Dark Irons that left Grim Batol cursed and uninhabitable, the Wildhammers migrated further north into Lordaeron, eventually settling throughout the forested mountains of the Hinterlands.",al={"wildhammer history","wildhammer clan origins","origin of the wildhammer clan"},it="history",add="f21b0698"}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For a dwarf of Ironforge, the Wildhammers are kin bound by old oaths. Though they prefer windy crags and open skies over deep mines, their bravery with the stormhammer commands the deepest respect.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance owes much of its aerial supremacy to the Wildhammers, whose lightning-infused gryphon riders turned the tide against the Horde's red dragons during the Second War.",["class:Hunter"]="The bond Wildhammer mountaineers share with their wild gryphons represents the pinnacle of beast mastery, built on lifelong partnership rather than simple dominion.",["class:Shaman"]="Wildhammer tradition channels raw storm energy through mystic hammers, maintaining a bond with elemental lightning and nature far older than the modern church traditions of Ironforge."},kw={"aerie peak","falstad","grim batol","gryphon","hinterlands","khardros","kurdran","stormhammer","wildhammer"},rel={"topic:dwarf","zone:hinterlands","topic:bronzebeard-clan","subzone:grim-batol","topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:second-war","subzone:valley-of-kings"},src={"Wildhammer clan"}}
E["topic:worgen"] = {t="topic",n="Worgen",z="general",s="Worgen are fierce, lupine humanoids cursed with uncontrollable primal rage, moving upright yet hunting with bestial swiftness. Once thought to be mere nightmares conjured from other planes, these savage pack-hunters now terrorize Silverpine Forest, Ashenvale, and Duskwood.",h="Worgen are fierce, lupine humanoids cursed with uncontrollable primal rage, moving upright yet hunting with...",sec={{t="Whispers and Folktales",b="Long before their recent emergence in Lordaeron and Kalimdor, wolf-men were whispered of in folktales and cautionary fables. Their true origins remain a mystery: scholars who have studied the beasts believe they were drawn into Azeroth from another realm, and both Archmage Arugal and the night elves' Scythe of Elune have proven able to call them forth.",sp=0},{t="Arugal's Folly",b="During the Third War, as the Scourge hordes overran Lordaeron and slain Kirin Tor wizards rose again as undead, Archmage Arugal sought a weapon to bolster Dalaran's dwindling ranks. Drawing from the forbidden research of the Dalaran wizard Ur, Arugal pierced the veil and summoned the beasts into Silverpine Forest. The summoned worgen devastated the undead invaders with savage abandon, but the archmage could not leash their fury. The beasts turned on the wizards themselves, spreading their curse through fang and claw. Driven mad by guilt and fascination, Arugal adopted the monsters as his children, seizing Baron Silverlaine's ancestral seat—now rechristened Shadowfang Keep—and cursing nearby villagers into monstrous nocturnal servitude.",sp=0},{t="The Scythe Awoken",b="Across the sea in Kalimdor, another summoning unleashed the worgen anew. Desperate to purge the lingering demonic remnants of the Burning Legion from Felwood, the night elf Sentinel Velinde Starsong unearthed the Scythe of Elune. Channelling the ancient relic, she called forth wolf-men to tear apart the demonic satyrs. Yet as battles raged, Velinde realized that the scythe had slipped beyond her command—the beasts began to manifest without her consent, slipping into the dark forests of Ashenvale and beyond. Now roaming in wild, feral packs, the worgen spread terror wherever the shadow of their moonlit hunts falls.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What are worgen?",a="Worgen are ferocious, upright lupine humanoids possessed of terrifying strength, predatory senses, and dark shadow magic. They primarily dwell in secluded woodlands and ruins, hunting in packs under the thrall of uncontrollable bestial fury.",al={"what is a worgen","worgen lore","wolf men"},it="identity"},{q="Where did the worgen originally come from?",a="No one knows for certain. The beasts appear to come from another realm entirely: Archmage Arugal summoned them into Silverpine from beyond, and the night elves' Scythe of Elune has drawn them forth in Kalimdor.",al={"worgen origin","who made worgen","how were worgen created"},it="origin"},{q="How did worgen end up in Silverpine Forest?",a="During the Third War, Archmage Arugal summoned them using the esoteric research of the late Dalaran wizard Ur. Arugal hoped they would bolster the Kirin Tor against the Scourge, but the unleashed beasts turned on everyone.",al={"silverpine worgen","why are worgen in silverpine","who summoned worgen"},it="why_here"},{q="How does someone become a worgen?",a="The condition spreads as a virulent supernatural affliction. A bite or scratch from an active worgen infects humanoids, eventually causing victims to succumb to feral urges and transform into wolf-beasts unless restrained by potent discipline or specialized sorcery.",al={"worgen curse","can you turn into a worgen","worgen bite"}},{q="What is the Scythe of Elune?",a="The Scythe of Elune is a powerful night elf relic. It holds deep, volatile dominion over the worgen and possesses the power to summon and agitate them.",al={"scythe of elune","scythe elune lore"}},{q="Who is Archmage Arugal?",a="Arugal was a Kirin Tor archmage of Dalaran. After summoning the worgen to fight the Scourge, he succumbed to madness and came to rule over the haunted ruins of Shadowfang Keep alongside his monstrous 'children.'",al={"arugal","who is arugal"}},{q="Why are worgen in Ashenvale and Kalimdor?",a="Worgen packs now prowl Ashenvale and Felwood, and the Sentinels of Darnassus are still searching for how they came to Kalimdor. Their investigation follows the trail of the vanished Sentinel Velinde Starsong and an ancient relic, the Scythe of Elune.",al={"ashenvale worgen","kalimdor worgen","terrowulf"}},{q="Why are worgen hostile to travelers?",a="Most worgen you encounter are consumed by feral, primal fury. The curse strips away reason and self-control, driving those afflicted into a frenzy where they view outsiders as prey or threats to be torn apart. Even those who follow the mad Archmage Arugal view mortals as enemies to be slaughtered or cursed into joining their packs.",al={"why do worgen attack","are worgen hostile","why are worgen aggressive"},it="hostile",add="d99a4888"},{q="Who leads the worgen?",a="The worgen do not answer to a single master, but gather in packs under powerful figures. In Silverpine Forest, many serve Archmage Arugal at Shadowfang Keep, who summoned them into the world. Independent packs run wild across Lordaeron, Duskwood, and Kalimdor, answering only to their alpha or primal instincts.",al={"worgen leader","who controls the worgen","worgen master"},it="leader",add="92f4d7ca"},{q="Who are the worgen allied with?",a="The worgen have no broad alliances. In Silverpine, certain packs answer to the renegade Archmage Arugal out of Shadowfang Keep. Otherwise, these feral beasts run rampant across the wilderness, attacking Scourge, Alliance, and Forsaken without distinction. They serve only their primal hunger and the leaders of their individual packs.",al={"worgen allies","who do worgen serve","worgen factions"},it="allies",add="52984533"},{q="Where did the worgen come from?",a="Their true origins are unknown. The beasts appear to come from another realm: Archmage Arugal summoned them into Silverpine from beyond during the Third War, and how packs came to prowl Kalimdor's forests is a mystery the night elves are still investigating.",al={"history of worgen","worgen backstory","how were worgen created"},it="history",add="b6544cd9"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The worgen are a cautionary tale of savage power summoned without the wisdom to control it, and their spread through Kalimdor's forests troubles the Cenarion Circle.",["race:NightElf"]="The worgen remain a troubling mystery for the kaldorei, whose Sentinels now find the beasts prowling Ashenvale and Felwood.",["race:Undead"]="In Silverpine Forest, the Forsaken battle feral worgen packs and Arugal's twisted cultists for mastery over the region south of the Ruins of Lordaeron.",["class:Mage"]="Dalaran's Kirin Tor long debated the research in Ur's mystical treatises; Arugal's catastrophic conjuring proved Ur's warnings that these nightmare creatures should never be summoned."},kw={"arugal","pyrewood","scythe of elune","shadowfang keep","silverpine","ur","velinde starsong","worgen"},rel={"npc:arugal","zone:shadowfang","zone:silverpine","quest:1043","topic:druid","topic:emerald-dream","npc:baron-silverlaine"},src={"Worgen"}}
E["topic:world-of-warcraft-forever"] = {t="topic",n="World of Warcraft: Forever",z="general",s="World of Warcraft: Forever takes place in the year after the Third War. Thrall's Horde has settled Durotar, and a regent rules Stormwind for its boy-king. The Forsaken hold the Undercity. The world reaches from the Skyborne of Zephras Isle to the contested Riverglades and Shen'dralas, sacred Mount Hyjal and the city of Dalaran rising from its ruins in the Alterac Mountains.",h="World of Warcraft: Forever takes place in the year after the Third War.",sec={{t="The World After the Third War",b="The Burning Legion has been driven back at Mount Hyjal, yet the world bears the hurt it left behind. Thrall's Horde of orcs, trolls and tauren has made its home in Durotar and Mulgore. The Forsaken under Sylvanas Windrunner have joined the Horde from the ruins of Lordaeron.\n\nIn Stormwind, Highlord Bolvar Fordragon rules as regent for the boy-king Anduin Wrynn while King Varian is missing. The dwarves of Ironforge, the exiled gnomes and the night elves of Teldrassil stand with the Alliance. The Scourge still holds the Plaguelands.",sp=0},{t="The Skyborne",b="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, descend from night elves who rebelled against Prince Tortheldrin of Eldre'Thalas. Wind spirits carried their island, Zephras Isle, into Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air, and the Skyborne escaped with it. Ages there made them high elves, and more are now born blue-skinned.\n\nThe spirits vanished centuries ago, and Zephras Isle is slowly breaking apart. The Skyborne look back toward Azeroth. Their airships reach the Alterac Mountains near Dalaran and Mulgore's Skywatcher Plateau. They fight for both the Alliance and the Horde.",sp=0},{t="Lands to Explore",b="Zephras Isle floats in Skywall as the Skyborne's homeland. East of the Burning Steppes, the Riverglades is an old Stormwind frontier lost in the First War. Loyalists at Farholde Keep hold out against the wilds, Horde outposts and the Twilight's Hammer at Sunnyglade.\n\nShen'dralas is a rugged borderland between Desolace, Feralas and Mulgore, contested by centaur, quilboar and outcasts. Mount Hyjal, sacred to the night elves, still bears the scars of Archimonde's defeat. In the Alterac Mountains, the Kirin Tor have lowered Dalaran's shield and are rebuilding their city. Off Kalimdor's coast, the Horde and the Alliance battle over the Darkspear Islands.",sp=0},{t="Dungeons and Raids",b="Danger waits beneath cities and in the world's far corners. There is the Hall of Thanes deep under Ironforge and the haunted Ruins of Lordaeron above the Undercity. An excavation site lies in the Wetlands, alongside the City of Dalaran itself.\n\nFarther afield are the Drowned City in Stranglethorn Vale, Krol'dok Stronghold in the Riverglades, Alcaz Prison in Dustwallow Marsh, Blackmaw Hold in Azshara and the Shaper's Terrace in Un'Goro Crater. On Mount Hyjal, the Barrow Deeps and Hyjal Summit test the mightiest war bands.",sp=0},{t="Who Walks Which Path",b="Warriors come from every race. Paladins are human, dwarf or Forsaken, while shaman are orc, tauren, troll, dwarf or Horde Skyborne. Priests are human, dwarf, gnome, night elf, troll or Forsaken. Mages are human, gnome, orc, troll, Forsaken or Alliance Skyborne.\n\nWarlocks are human, gnome, orc, troll or Forsaken. Hunters are human, dwarf, night elf, orc, tauren, troll or Skyborne. Rogues come from every race but the tauren. Druids are night elf, tauren or Skyborne.",sp=0}},faq={{q="When is World of Warcraft: Forever set?",a="World of Warcraft: Forever is set in the year after the Third War, once the Forsaken have claimed the Undercity. Thrall leads the Horde from Orgrimmar. Highlord Bolvar Fordragon is regent of Stormwind for the boy-king Anduin, and the Scourge still holds the Plaguelands.",al={"when is wow forever set","wow forever timeline","what year is wow forever","when does forever take place"}},{q="What lands can I explore in WoW Forever?",a="You can explore Zephras Isle, the Skyborne's floating home in Skywall, and the old Stormwind frontier of the Riverglades. Shen'dralas lies between Desolace, Feralas and Mulgore, while Mount Hyjal is sacred to the night elves. Dalaran is rising again in the Alterac Mountains, and the Horde and Alliance fight over the Darkspear Islands.",al={"what's new in wow forever","what's new in forever","new zones in wow forever","wow forever zones","what is in wow forever"}},{q="Who are the Skyborne?",a="The Skyborne are high elves descended from night elves who fled Eldre'Thalas ages ago and lived on Zephras Isle in Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air. With their island crumbling, they look back toward Azeroth and fight for both the Alliance and the Horde.",al={"skyborne","shen'dorei","new race wow forever"}},{q="Where is Dalaran?",a="Dalaran stands on the shore of Lordamere Lake in the Alterac Mountains. Archimonde shattered the city in the Third War. The Kirin Tor raised a shield over its ruins, and have now lowered it as they rebuild the city.",al={"dalaran wow forever","is dalaran rebuilt","dalaran alterac"}},{q="What dungeons and raids are there?",a="Among the dungeons are the Hall of Thanes under Ironforge, the Ruins of Lordaeron above the Undercity, an excavation site in the Wetlands and the City of Dalaran. Others include the Drowned City in Stranglethorn Vale, Krol'dok Stronghold in the Riverglades, Alcaz Prison in Dustwallow Marsh, Blackmaw Hold in Azshara and the Shaper's Terrace in Un'Goro Crater. The raids are the Barrow Deeps and Hyjal Summit on Mount Hyjal, and Onyxia's Lair in Dustwallow Marsh.",al={"wow forever dungeons","wow forever raids","new dungeons in wow forever","new raids"}},{q="Which classes can each race be?",a="Humans can be warriors, paladins, hunters, rogues, priests, mages or warlocks. Dwarves can be warriors, paladins, hunters, rogues, priests or shaman. Night elves can be warriors, hunters, rogues, priests or druids. Gnomes can be warriors, rogues, priests, mages or warlocks.\n\nOrcs can be warriors, hunters, rogues, shaman, mages or warlocks. Forsaken can be warriors, paladins, rogues, priests, mages or warlocks. Tauren can be warriors, hunters, shaman or druids. Trolls can be warriors, hunters, rogues, priests, shaman, mages or warlocks.\n\nSkyborne can be warriors, hunters, rogues or druids. Alliance Skyborne can also be mages, and Horde Skyborne can also be shaman.",al={"race class combinations","what class can i be","can undead be paladins","undead paladin","dwarf shaman","gnome priest","human hunter","orc mage","troll warlock","skyborne classes"}}},ang={["race:Skyborne"]="Your people have only begun to return to Azeroth. Both the Alliance and the Horde have taken Skyborne into their ranks.",["race:Undead"]="Some Forsaken still wield the Holy Light as paladins, though it burns them. Tyr's Watch holds Bandarion Keep in western Tirisfal.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarves of Ironforge walk the shaman's path as well as the Light's. They call on stone and storm."},kw={"class combinations","dalaran","darkspear islands","mount hyjal","riverglades","shen'dralas","skyborne","world of warcraft forever","wow forever","zephras isle"},rel={"topic:skyborne","zone:zephras","zone:riverglades","zone:shendralas","zone:hyjal","subzone:dalaran","zone:darkspearislands","topic:third-war","zone:hallofthanes","zone:ruinsoflordaeron"},src={"World of Warcraft: Forever","Template:Forever class by race"},fv=true}
